    "label": "4",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Charles Stewart Parnell was a remarkable leader of the Home Rule movement whose strengths in organisation, tactics and his ability to unite different strands of nationalism made the Irish Party a force at Westminster. His weaknesses, notably his aloofness, his dependence on the Liberals and above all his mishandling of his private life, brought about his fall and the split of 1890.\n\nParnell's first great strength was his ability to unite the different strands of nationalism. After joining Biggar's obstruction in the Commons from 1877 he won Fenian respect by saying that he did not believe any murder had been committed at Manchester, and in 1879 he allied with Michael Davitt and, at a distance, with John Devoy in the New Departure. This linked Fenians, land reformers and constitutional nationalists. As president of the Land League from October 1879 he turned the land agitation into a mass movement that reached every parish, and a fund-raising tour of America in 1880 brought money and publicity. His imprisonment in Kilmainham in October 1881 made him a hero, and the settlement of 1882 brought a measure of relief on arrears and steered him back to parliamentary politics.\n\nHis second strength was the party machine that he built. Chosen chairman of the Irish Parliamentary Party in May 1880, he insisted on the pledge, which bound MPs to vote together, and after the Irish National League replaced the Land League in 1882 he kept personal control of a nationwide organisation. His skill in Commons procedure and in obstruction made the party a nuisance that could not be ignored. At the general election of 1885 the party won 86 seats and held the balance of power between Liberals and Conservatives, which led Gladstone to adopt Home Rule and introduce the first Bill in April 1886. Although it was defeated in June, Parnell had made Home Rule a leading question in British politics.\n\nParnell also showed his strength when his enemies tried to ruin him. In 1887 The Times printed a letter supposedly signed by him that excused the Phoenix Park murders. A Special Commission was set up, and in February 1889 the forger Richard Pigott collapsed under cross-examination and later killed himself in Madrid. Parnell was cheered in the Commons and received damages from The Times, and in 1890 he stood at the height of his reputation, in alliance with Gladstone and in control of a disciplined party.\n\nHis weaknesses, however, were serious. He was aloof and autocratic, a Protestant landlord who ran the party as his own, and he was often away from Ireland and from the daily running of the party. He remained ambiguous about the violence of the Land War and did not endorse the Plan of Campaign in 1886. His strategy depended heavily on the Liberal alliance, so that the movement's fortunes rested on Gladstone and on the opinion of English non-conformists.\n\nHis private life proved his greatest weakness. Captain William O'Shea named him as co-respondent in the divorce case of November 1890, and Parnell did not contest it. The Irish Party re-elected him as chairman, but Gladstone made clear that he could not lead the Liberals if Parnell stayed, and the Catholic bishops condemned him on 3 December. His manifesto attacking the Liberals deepened the bitterness. In Committee Room 15, from 1 to 6 December, about 45 MPs walked out under Justin McCarthy, leaving about 28 with him. He lost by-elections in Kilkenny, Sligo and Carlow, married Katharine O'Shea in June 1891 and died at Brighton in October at the age of 45.\n\nOverall, Parnell's strengths were great enough to transform Irish politics, and he gave the movement discipline, unity and a place in British politics that it had lacked under Butt. His weaknesses lay in his personality and his private life, and his refusal to step aside split the party for a decade. He remains a leader of exceptional ability who failed to pass on what he had built.",
