    "label": "1",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The partition of Ireland in 1920 was the product of three factors working together: the determined and armed resistance of Ulster unionism, the repeated failure of British governments to find any all-Ireland settlement, and the political divergence of north-east Ulster from the rest of the island, which the 1918 election made plain. Because Britain was unwilling to coerce unionists, the Government of Ireland Act of December 1920 gave Ireland two parliaments rather than one.\n\nThe first and most important factor was unionist resistance. When Asquith introduced the Third Home Rule Bill in April 1912, Edward Carson and James Craig organised mass protest. On Ulster Day, 28 September 1912, about 237,000 men signed the Ulster Covenant pledging to resist Home Rule, and the Ulster Volunteer Force was formed in January 1913 to back the pledge with force. The Curragh incident of March 1914, when British cavalry officers said they would rather be dismissed than act against Ulster, and the landing of some 25,000 rifles at Larne in April 1914, which the government did not stop, showed that Home Rule could be imposed on the whole island only by civil war. Unionist resistance made some form of exclusion almost inevitable.\n\nSecondly, successive British proposals assumed that part of Ulster would be left out, so the principle of partition was accepted long before 1920. Asquith offered county option, by which Ulster counties could vote themselves out for six years, in March 1914. The Buckingham Palace Conference of July 1914 failed to agree a border, with Tyrone and Fermanagh the sticking point. When the Home Rule Act received royal assent in September 1914, it was suspended until the end of the war, with a promise of special treatment for Ulster. Redmond accepted temporary exclusion reluctantly, but Carson wanted permanent exclusion, and that gap was never bridged.\n\nThirdly, the war and the 1916 Rising changed the political landscape. Lloyd George's proposals of 1916 for immediate Home Rule with the exclusion of six Ulster counties collapsed because unionist ministers in the cabinet insisted that exclusion be permanent, while nationalists had been told it would be temporary. This damaged the credibility of Redmond's party, which had staked everything on Home Rule by consent, and the Irish Convention of 1917-18 also failed to produce agreement. Constitutional nationalism, the one force that might have negotiated a compromise, was fatally weakened.\n\nFourthly, the 1918 general election confirmed that Ireland had divided into two political cultures. Sinn Fein won the great majority of seats on the island, while unionists won 26 seats, mostly in the north-east. Only four of the nine Ulster counties, Antrim, Down, Armagh and Londonderry, had unionist majorities, and Tyrone and Fermanagh had narrow nationalist majorities. The unionists therefore chose six counties rather than nine, since this gave them a safer majority, about two-thirds Protestant of a population of some 1.25 million. Meanwhile Sinn Fein's abstention and the War of Independence made it unlikely that the south would accept any scheme that left the Crown in place.\n\nFinally, British policy made partition the practical outcome. Lloyd George's coalition contained a large Conservative element sympathetic to unionism and unwilling to coerce Ulster, while it was fighting a war in the south and needed a settlement quickly. The Government of Ireland Act of December 1920 set up a parliament for the six counties of Northern Ireland and another for the remaining 26 counties of Southern Ireland, with a Council of Ireland meant to link them. Only the northern parliament worked: the Unionists won 40 of 52 seats in May 1921 and Craig became Prime Minister, while in the south Sinn Fein won 124 of 128 seats and treated the election as one for the Dail.\n\nIn conclusion, partition was not planned by any one party but resulted from unionist willingness to resist by force, British reluctance to confront them, the collapse of constitutional nationalism and the electoral divide of 1918. Of these, unionist resistance backed by the threat of arms was the decisive factor, because it turned exclusion from a temporary concession into a permanent border.",
