    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "To a very large extent, language, religion and education were used to promote Irish cultural identity in the years 1920 to 1949, but the identity they promoted was a narrow one. In the Free State the government used the schools to revive Irish, the Catholic Church shaped both schooling and public morality, and the state defined Irishness as Gaelic and Catholic. In Northern Ireland the same three areas were used by unionists and nationalists to express separate identities, which deepened division rather than building a shared one.\n\nThe Irish language was the centrepiece of cultural policy in the Free State. Irish was made a compulsory subject in national schools in 1922, and many infant classes were taught wholly through Irish. A pass in Irish became necessary for State examinations at second level and for entry to the civil service and the Garda, so that the language was tied directly to jobs, and Ernest Blythe pressed civil servants to learn it. The Gaeltacht Commission of 1925 defined the Irish-speaking districts and recommended support for them, An Gúm was founded in 1926 to publish books in Irish, and Taibhdhearc na Gaillimhe opened as an Irish-language theatre in Galway in 1928. The 1937 constitution declared Irish the first official language. The state clearly saw language as the core of national identity.\n\nThe results, however, were limited. Census returns showed the number able to speak Irish rising from about 18 per cent in 1926 to about a quarter in 1936, but this was mostly the product of schooling and few used Irish in daily life. The Gaeltacht itself continued to shrink through emigration and poverty, and many parents and teachers resented the compulsion. Even so, Irish remained a powerful symbol of identity, and the state kept it as a school subject even where it failed as a spoken language. As a way of promoting identity the policy succeeded far better than as a way of reviving a living language.\n\nReligion, in the form of the Catholic Church, was equally central. The Church managed most national schools through local clergy, while the state paid the salaries and set the curriculum, and most secondary schools were run by religious orders. The School Attendance Act of 1926 made school compulsory from 6 to 14, so the Church's influence reached every child. The Vocational Education Act of 1930 provided technical education through local committees, though the Church kept its grip on the content of teaching. John Charles McQuaid, Archbishop of Dublin from 1940, became an influential voice on social and educational issues. Education in the South therefore transmitted a Catholic as well as a Gaelic identity.\n\nCatholic teaching also shaped the law. The Censorship of Films Act of 1923 and the Censorship of Publications Act of 1929 allowed films and books to be cut or banned, and writers such as Samuel Beckett, Kate O'Brien and Seán Ó Faoláin were among those banned. The Criminal Law Amendment Act of 1935 banned the sale of contraceptives, and the Dáil had stopped divorce bills in 1925, a decision that drew a famous protest from W.B. Yeats in the Seanad on behalf of the Protestant minority. The 1937 constitution banned divorce and recognised the special position of the Catholic Church. This promoted a conformist Catholic identity and left Protestants feeling excluded, and their numbers fell during these years.\n\nThe state promoted culture in other ways too. The Tailteann Games were held in 1924, 1928 and 1932, the Abbey Theatre received a state subsidy from 1925, and 2RN began broadcasting in 1926. The Irish Folklore Commission was set up in 1935, and in 1937 and 1938 schoolchildren collected local folklore. The GAA, through Rule 27, banned its members from foreign games, and in 1938 it removed Douglas Hyde as patron after he attended a soccer match. Artists such as Evie Hone, whose window My Four Green Fields won a prize at the New York World's Fair of 1939, gave Ireland a modern cultural presence abroad.\n\nIn the North, identity was expressed through education and religion in a divided way. The Londonderry Act of 1923 aimed at a non-denominational state system, but the Protestant churches objected and the Act was amended in 1925 to allow Bible instruction. The Catholic Church refused to transfer its schools to state control, and they stayed outside the system with only partial public funding. Unionist culture was expressed through Orange parades and the Union Jack, while nationalists turned to the GAA, Catholic schools and Irish classes that received little state support. Craig's statement of 1934 that Northern Ireland was a Protestant state matched the Catholic emphasis of the South.\n\nIn conclusion, language, religion and education were used extensively and deliberately to promote cultural identity on both sides of the border. Their success in giving the new state a distinct identity was real, but the identity was narrow, enforced by law and schooling, and it excluded minorities in the South while hardening the divide in the North.",
