    "marks": 100,
    "model": "De Valera's mission to the United States between June 1919 and December 1920 was important for the money and publicity it brought the Irish Republic, but it failed in its central aim of winning American recognition and it divided Irish America. Its significance lies in what it showed about the possibilities and the limits of diaspora politics, as much as in what it achieved.\n\nThe mission was undertaken at a critical time. Sinn Féin had won 73 of the 105 Irish seats in December 1918, and the Dáil declared an Irish Republic in January 1919. De Valera escaped from Lincoln Gaol in February 1919 and was elected head of the Dáil ministry in April. The Paris Peace Conference had refused to hear the Irish delegates, and President Wilson would not press Britain on self-determination. De Valera went to America, arriving in June 1919, to seek recognition for the Republic, to raise funds, and to influence the 1920 presidential election. His mission was therefore an attempt to bring international pressure on Britain.\n\nThe tour was a considerable public success. Harry Boland had prepared the way, and de Valera addressed huge rallies in cities with large Irish communities, including a vast crowd at Fenway Park in Boston on 29 June 1919. Irish-American organisations and parishes arranged his receptions, and the Senate passed a resolution on 6 June 1919 asking the Peace Conference to hear Irish representatives. This was sympathy rather than commitment, but the mission raised the profile of the Republic in American newspapers and at public meetings, and it was one of the first major efforts by the Republic to win international attention.\n\nFinancially the mission was its most successful part. The Dáil bond drive, launched in January 1920, sold bond certificates in the Irish Republic to Irish Americans, and it aimed at five million dollars, a figure it reached by 1921. This gave the Dáil government a financial base abroad that helped pay for the war and its administration, alongside the National Loan raised in Ireland by Michael Collins. Without these funds, the Republic's government would have been much weaker.\n\nThe mission also divided Irish America. John Devoy and Judge Cohalan of the Friends of Irish Freedom clashed with de Valera over who controlled the funds and the movement, and the quarrel was fought out in the press. De Valera's interview with the Westminster Gazette in February 1920, in which he suggested treating Ireland like Cuba under American influence, caused controversy among republicans and gave Cohalan and Devoy a weapon against him. The split weakened the Friends of Irish Freedom and led to a new body, the American Association for the Recognition of the Irish Republic, founded in November 1920, which claimed several hundred thousand members.\n\nThe main political aim failed. De Valera approached both parties at their 1920 conventions in Chicago and San Francisco. The Republicans ignored the request, and the Democrats offered only a resolution of sympathy. Warren Harding won in November 1920, and no American government recognised the Republic during the mission or in the years that followed. Meanwhile de Valera's absence left the Dáil without its president during the Anglo-Irish War, and relations between him and Collins became strained.\n\nEven so, American opinion shifted as events in Ireland unfolded. Reports of Black and Tan reprisals, the death on hunger strike of Terence MacSwiney in October 1920 and the burning of part of Cork in December 1920 won sympathy, and an American Commission on Conditions in Ireland investigated British conduct. These events helped the Irish cause as much as de Valera's speeches did.\n\nOverall, the mission was important mainly for the funds and publicity it generated, which sustained the Republic during the war. It was less important diplomatically, since recognition was never won, and it exposed the difficulty of running a movement from abroad.",
