    "model": "The main trends in Irish emigration between 1920 and 1966 were a sharp shift in destination from the United States to Britain, a pattern of emigration that rose and fell with economic conditions at home, and a growing concern in Irish society about its effects. Throughout the period emigrants were mostly young, poor and drawn from rural Ireland, and the flow only fell significantly when the economy began to grow in the 1960s.\n\nThe first trend was the American door closing. The Emergency Quota Act of 1921 limited immigrants of each nationality to 3 per cent of that nationality's foreign-born population in the 1910 census. The Immigration Act of 1924 then set quotas at 2 per cent of each group in the 1890 census, a base chosen to favour north-western Europeans. The Irish Free State received a quota of 28,567 a year, cut to 17,853 from 1929. The effect was to redirect emigration rather than end it, and the Irish who had once headed overwhelmingly for New York, Boston and Chicago now looked elsewhere. The Great Depression after 1929 reduced American demand for labour still further, and some emigrants even returned.\n\nThe second trend was the rise of Britain as the main destination. Britain was easy to enter, the fare was low, jobs were close and the Irish were never treated as aliens there. Large numbers crossed in the 1930s, and the Free State governments did nothing to restrict them, treating emigration as an economic problem they could not quickly solve. During the Emergency of 1939 to 1945 Irish people needed travel permits, but Britain recruited workers for war factories, building and farm work, and well over 100,000 people from the state went to work there, while tens of thousands more joined the British forces. The Ireland Act of 1949 and the Common Travel Area confirmed that Irish citizens could live, work and vote in the United Kingdom freely, and they were exempted from the Commonwealth Immigrants Act of 1962.\n\nThe third trend was the surge of the 1950s. Post-war reconstruction and the new National Health Service created huge demand for labour in Britain. Irish men worked as navvies on roads, building sites and power stations, while women trained as nurses or worked in factories and offices. The Irish clustered in London districts such as Kilburn and Camden Town and in Birmingham, Coventry, Luton, Manchester and Liverpool. About 400,000 people left the state between 1951 and 1961, and the 1961 census recorded the lowest population in the state's history, about 2.8 million. Domhnall Mac Amhlaigh, who went to England in 1951, described the navvy's life of hard work, lodgings and homesickness in Dialann Deoraí, published in 1960.\n\nThe fourth trend was the character of the emigrants and their experience. They were mostly young and single, from small farms and the west, and as in the earlier century women left in large numbers. Many arrived in Britain untrained and lived in lodging houses or navvy camps, working through subcontractors who paid cash in hand without insurance or security. Catholic chaplaincies, the Legion of Mary and Irish centres helped newcomers, and dance halls such as the Galtymore in Cricklewood became centres of emigrant social life. Because the journey home was short, many saw their stay as temporary, and historians such as Enda Delaney argue that emigrants kept strong ties with home while integrating into British working life.\n\nThe fifth trend was official and public concern. The government set up the Commission on Emigration and Other Population Problems in 1948, and it reported in 1954. It found the causes to be mainly economic, such as low incomes, unemployment and small farms, though it also noted the attraction of better living standards abroad, and it urged economic development rather than any ban on leaving. The Vanishing Irish, edited by John A. O'Brien in 1953, warned that the nation was dying out. The turning point came with T.K. Whitaker's Economic Development of 1958 and the First Programme for Economic Expansion pursued by Seán Lemass from 1959, which brought growth and cut emigration sharply in the 1960s. The US Act of 1965, abolishing the quotas from 1968, came too late to change the pattern, and favoured those with family ties.\n\nOverall, the dominant trend was the replacement of America by Britain as the Irish emigrant's destination, driven by American restriction and British labour demand, with the 1950s as the peak. Emigration in these decades was a safety valve that relieved rural poverty but it also took the young and kept the population falling until 1961, and it declined only when the Irish economy gave young people reasons to stay.",
