    "model": "The welfare state had a real but uneven impact on Northern Ireland between 1949 and 1969. In education and health it raised opportunities and living standards for Catholics and Protestants alike, while in housing it was undermined by local councils that allocated homes along sectarian lines. It therefore improved lives but left the central grievances of the Catholic minority untouched.\n\nThe welfare state reached Northern Ireland on a basis of parity with Britain. The Social Services Agreement of 1949 meant that National Insurance, unemployment benefit and pensions matched British levels, with the British Treasury covering what Northern Ireland could not raise from its own taxes. This was important because the province had the highest unemployment of any UK region for much of the period. Catholics received the same benefits as Protestants, but parity also meant that Stormont had little freedom to shape policy, and it did nothing to end discrimination in jobs and allocation.\n\nThe Education Act (Northern Ireland) 1947 was the most significant change in schooling. It provided free secondary education for all pupils and set up a tripartite system of grammar, technical and secondary intermediate schools, with children sorted at about eleven by the eleven-plus examination. Poorer children, many of them Catholic, now had access to qualifications that had been out of reach. State scholarships and grants extended to university study, so more Catholic students reached Queen's University Belfast from the 1950s, and by the 1960s an educated Catholic middle class was emerging. Many of its members would later lead the civil rights movement, so the Act had political consequences that Stormont had not intended.\n\nEducation also showed the limits of reform. Voluntary schools, mostly Catholic, kept their own management and received a state capital grant of 65 per cent towards building costs, so the Catholic Church had to find the rest. Schooling remained segregated by religion, with Protestant and Catholic children growing up apart. The eleven-plus also created a divide between those who went to grammar school and those who did not, so the opportunities were real but not equal.\n\nIn health, the National Health Service began in Northern Ireland on 5 July 1948, the same day as in the rest of the United Kingdom. Free medical care, funded through British taxation, improved the health of ordinary families regardless of religion. Health was the area where the welfare state came closest to being genuinely neutral, because the service was run on a UK model and there was little scope for local councils to discriminate in who was treated.\n\nHousing was the area where the welfare state worked least fairly. The Northern Ireland Housing Trust, set up in 1945, built estates across the province and allocated houses on a points system that was regarded as fair. But most council houses were allocated by local councils, and many unionist-controlled councils favoured Protestant families. Londonderry Corporation remained under unionist control although Catholics were a majority in the city, because the ward boundaries of 1936 packed most Catholic voters into one ward. In Fermanagh and Tyrone, where Catholics were about half the population, unionist councils also held power. Catholic and Protestant families were increasingly housed in separate estates, which deepened segregation in Belfast and Derry. Housing allocation became a leading grievance, taken up by Conn and Patricia McCluskey's Campaign for Social Justice, founded in Dungannon in 1964, and by NICRA after 1967.\n\nThe pressure of the civil rights movement did eventually change the system. O'Neill's five-point reform package of 22 November 1968 promised a points system for housing allocation, an ombudsman and the replacement of Londonderry Corporation by a commission. These reforms came late and under pressure, but they show that the problem was not the welfare state itself but who controlled its local delivery.\n\nOverall, the welfare state improved health, education and housing for the people of Northern Ireland, and its benefits to the Catholic minority in schooling and health were considerable. Yet because the Unionist government kept control of local allocation and refused to share power, the welfare state could not satisfy Catholic demands for equal treatment. Its greatest success was in education and health, and its greatest failure was in housing.",
