    "label": "2",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Terence O'Neill's contribution to Northern Ireland between 1949 and 1969 was that of a modernising Prime Minister who tried to reform the state on his own terms. He began economic planning as Minister of Finance, tried to build bridges with Catholics and Dublin, and conceded much of the civil rights programme. But he could not carry his own party, and his resignation in 1969 showed the limits of reform from above.\n\nO'Neill's first contribution was as Minister of Finance from 1956 to 1963, when he developed the economic planning that later shaped his premiership. Northern Ireland's economy was in trouble, with shipbuilding and linen declining, unemployment above the UK average and emigration draining young people. After becoming Prime Minister in March 1963, O'Neill made modernisation the centre of his programme. He drew on the Matthew Report of 1963 on regional planning around Belfast and the Wilson Plan of 1965 on economic development, which led to the new city of Craigavon in County Armagh. Projects such as Craigavon and the new university at Coleraine were part of his wider effort to show that Northern Ireland could be modern and prosperous.\n\nA second contribution was his policy of bridge-building. O'Neill made symbolic visits to Catholic schools and hospitals, which hardliners resented as concessions. On 14 January 1965 he received the Taoiseach, Seán Lemass, at Stormont, a meeting widely described as the first between the heads of the two governments since partition, and he returned the visit to Dublin in February 1965. These gestures improved the atmosphere. The Nationalist Party under Eddie McAteer agreed in 1965 to become the official opposition at Stormont for the first time, which was a real change after decades of abstention.\n\nHowever, O'Neill's gestures were not matched by changes in housing, jobs and voting, and Catholics judged progress too slow. He did not see Catholic grievances as primarily political, and he wished to reform on his own terms. Gerrymandering in Derry, the company vote and discrimination in housing allocation remained in place. This frustration helped fuel the civil rights movement, led by the Campaign for Social Justice from 1964 and NICRA from 1967, and the march in Derry on 5 October 1968 was banned by his Minister of Home Affairs, William Craig, leading to violence that the television cameras carried around the world.\n\nO'Neill's third contribution was to concede reform under pressure from London and the civil rights movement. On 22 November 1968 he announced a five-point package: a points system for housing, an ombudsman, replacement of Londonderry Corporation by a commission, withdrawal of the Special Powers Act when possible and an end to the company vote. On 9 December 1968 he made his Ulster at the Crossroads broadcast, explaining his policy and appealing for calm. He also dismissed William Craig in December 1968. By April 1969 his party had accepted one man, one vote in local elections. This was a substantial concession that met most of the civil rights programme, though many leaders saw it as forced by protest and too late.\n\nO'Neill's authority was undermined from within his own community. Ian Paisley, who founded the Free Presbyterian Church in 1951, led the O'Neill must go campaign from 1966 and opposed the Lemass meeting as a threat to the Union. Hardline unionists accused O'Neill of giving too much to nationalists. Violence added to the pressure: the UVF murdered three people in 1966, and in 1968 and 1969 there were disorders, the ambush of the People's Democracy march at Burntollet on 4 January 1969 and bomb attacks on water and electricity installations. After a poor showing in the February 1969 election, O'Neill resigned on 28 April 1969.\n\nO'Neill's contribution was therefore significant but ultimately limited. He modernised the economy and changed the tone of politics, and he began the reforms that Stormont had refused for decades. But his reforms were too slow for Catholics and too much for hardline unionists, so he satisfied neither community. Historians remain divided on whether he was a sincere reformer who was undermined or a leader whose reforms were too limited and too late, but few doubt that his fall opened the way to the Troubles.",
