    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The Apprentice Boys of Derry made a major contribution to the religious and cultural identity of the city's unionist minority. Through its annual parades, rituals and symbols it kept alive the memory of the siege of 1688 to 1689, giving Protestants in a Catholic-majority city a shared public identity. The same traditions were seen by nationalists as triumphalist, and this made them a source of conflict.\n\nThe organisation grew out of the siege itself. In December 1688 thirteen apprentice boys shut Derry's gates against a regiment loyal to the Catholic King James II. The siege proper lasted 105 days from April 1689, during which the city suffered severe hunger and disease, until the relief ship Mountjoy broke the boom across the River Foyle on 28 July 1689. Governor Robert Lundy, who favoured negotiating with James, became a byword for betrayal. For Protestants the siege represented the defence of civil and religious liberty against a Catholic monarch, and this gave them a founding story that linked their identity to loyalty to the Crown.\n\nThe Apprentice Boys of Derry Club was founded in 1814 to commemorate the siege, followed by the No Surrender Club in 1824. Its members were drawn largely from Protestant tradesmen and businessmen. It gave the minority an organised body of its own, with a General Committee set up in 1861 and a headquarters in the Memorial Hall in Society Street, opened in 1877 in honour of the thirteen apprentices. The organisation spread to other places in Northern Ireland and Britain, so Derry's Protestants were part of a wider network of loyal fraternal societies, which strengthened their sense of belonging.\n\nThe parades were the most visible contribution. The Relief of Derry commemoration in August follows a route around the city walls and is the largest event in the calendar. The December commemoration of the Shutting of the Gates includes a service at St Columb's Cathedral and the burning of an effigy of Lundy. The parades feature bands, banners, crimson flags, the firing of a cannon, wreaths and speeches. Symbols such as the walls, the cannon Roaring Meg and the slogan No Surrender expressed defiance and loyalty. Historians describe the unionist outlook this reinforced as a siege mentality, a sense of being surrounded and under threat, which was real for a minority in a city where Catholics were the majority.\n\nThe tradition also had a political side. Although Derry had a Catholic majority, unionists controlled Londonderry Corporation through gerrymandering, and nationalists saw the parades as part of the unionist power structure. The Bogside, a largely Catholic working-class area below the walls, lay close to the parade route, so the parades were a recurring source of tension. Critics called them triumphalist, a celebration of victory over another community, while the Apprentice Boys argued that they were a legitimate expression of heritage.\n\nThis conflict came to a head on 12 August 1969, when the parade passed near the Bogside and stone-throwing and clashes with the RUC began. The Battle of the Bogside lasted until 14 August, residents built barricades and the area became Free Derry, and British troops were deployed on 14 August. Unionists pointed out that the parade was legal and traditional, while nationalists called it a provocation. After 1969 the parades were restricted or rerouted in some years, and the IRA blew up the Walker Pillar on the walls in 1973. As Protestants moved from the west bank to the Waterside, the sense of siege grew, and the parades became even more important to those who remained.\n\nThe Apprentice Boys therefore preserved a commemorative tradition that gave Protestant Derry a shared public identity for more than a century and a half, and it was central to how unionists understood their history and their place in the city. Yet because the tradition expressed loyalty in a way that nationalists saw as exclusion, it was both a genuine act of commemoration and a political statement, and its meaning changed with the conflict around it.",
