    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Direct Rule was introduced in March 1972 because Brian Faulkner's Stormont government could not contain the violence and Westminster was unwilling to leave control of security in its hands. It lasted so long because unionists and nationalists could not agree on any form of devolved government that both would accept, so London had no partner to hand power back to.\n\nBy 1972 Stormont had lost the confidence of the Catholic community. The civil rights protests of the late 1960s, the Battle of the Bogside in August 1969 and the arrival of British troops had shown that the Unionist government could not keep order alone. The Hunt Report of October 1969 led to the disbanding of the B Specials, while the new Ulster Defence Regiment and the reforms of Chichester-Clark's government, such as the Housing Executive set up in 1971, came too late for nationalists and went too far for unionist hardliners. Violence grew as the Provisional IRA, formed after the split of December 1969, built a campaign against the British army, and loyalist groups such as the UDA, formed in 1971, answered in kind.\n\nThe immediate causes were internment and Bloody Sunday. Faulkner, who became Prime Minister in March 1971, persuaded London to authorise internment, which began on 9 August 1971. Almost everyone arrested was Catholic and no loyalist was interned until 1973, so the policy alienated nationalists, boosted IRA recruitment and was followed by a surge in deaths, including the Ballymurphy killings. The SDLP withdrew from Stormont in July 1971. On 30 January 1972 British paratroopers shot dead thirteen civilians at a banned civil rights march in Derry. The embassy in Dublin was burned on 2 February, and the Widgery Report, which largely accepted the army's account, convinced nationalists that the state could not be trusted with security.\n\nThe final step came when Edward Heath's government demanded control of security and Faulkner refused to hand it over. Faulkner and his ministers resigned on 24 March 1972, and Westminster suspended Stormont from 30 March under the Northern Ireland (Temporary Provisions) Act. William Whitelaw became the first Secretary of State. London believed that only direct control of the army and police could end the violence and create room for a settlement acceptable to nationalists, which the old system of one-party unionist rule had never offered. The Constitution Act of 1973 then formally abolished the Stormont parliament.\n\nDirect Rule was meant to be temporary but lasted, apart from a few months in 1974, throughout the period to 1993. The main reason was the failure of power sharing. The Sunningdale Agreement of December 1973 created an executive under Faulkner with Gerry Fitt of the SDLP as deputy, but unionists rejected the Council of Ireland, the Ulster Unionist Council voted against it in January 1974, and the Ulster Workers' Council strike from 15 May 1974 paralysed the province. Faulkner resigned on 28 May 1974 and Direct Rule was restored. The Constitutional Convention of 1975 produced only a unionist demand for majority rule, which Westminster rejected, and it was dissolved in 1976.\n\nViolence and mistrust kept the deadlock in place. The IRA campaign continued through the hunger strikes of 1981, the Brighton bombing of 1984 and the rise of Sinn Féin as an electoral force under Gerry Adams. The British government answered with Ulsterisation and criminalisation, which left little room for political experiment. When London and Dublin did act, in the Anglo-Irish Agreement signed by Thatcher and FitzGerald at Hillsborough in November 1985, unionists responded with the Ulster says No campaign and the resignation of their MPs, which made them even less ready to negotiate. Thatcher's rejection of the New Ireland Forum options in 1984 also showed how limited British flexibility was.\n\nDirect Rule was therefore introduced because Stormont had failed to contain the crisis and Westminster would not share control of security. It lasted over two decades because unionists would not accept an Irish dimension or nationalist partners in government, nationalists would not return to majority rule, and violence repeatedly destroyed the trust needed for talks. Only the Hume-Adams contacts and the diplomacy of the early 1990s began to move the deadlock.",
