    "model": "The political instability and economic problems of 1949 to 1959 were closely linked. Governments changed in 1951, 1954 and 1957, and the main reason was that no government, whether Fianna Fáil or coalition, had a programme that could reverse stagnation, emigration and balance of payments crises. Each time voters punished the party in power, and the new government repeated the same deflationary approach.\n\nThe instability began with the nature of the coalitions. The First Inter-Party Government of 1948 united Fine Gael, Labour, the National Labour Party, Clann na Poblachta, Clann na Talmhan and independents, and it had little in common beyond a wish to end sixteen years of Fianna Fáil rule. John A. Costello became Taoiseach because Richard Mulcahy's Civil War record made him unacceptable to some partners. The government contained conservatives and radicals, such as Seán MacBride and Noël Browne, and a coalition built on such different outlooks was always likely to split. The second coalition of 1954 to 1957 was equally fragile, as it relied on Clann na Poblachta supporting it from outside government. When that party withdrew its support in January 1957, after the start of the IRA Border Campaign, Costello had to call an election.\n\nThe Mother and Child crisis was the most dramatic political upheaval. Noël Browne proposed free care for mothers and for children up to sixteen, without a means test. The Irish Medical Association opposed it, and the Catholic hierarchy, advised by Archbishop McQuaid, said it was contrary to Catholic social teaching. MacBride asked Browne to resign, which he did on 11 April 1951, and Costello told the Dáil that the government must accept the bishops' decision. The affair damaged Clann na Poblachta, exposed the limits of state power against the church, and led to the general election of May 1951 in which the government lost office. De Valera's minority government that followed replaced the scheme with the means-tested Health Act 1953, and depended on independents, which again made it weak.\n\nThe economic problems explain why governments kept falling. Ireland remained dependent on cattle exports to Britain, with agriculture employing about 40 per cent of the workforce on small, low-productivity farms. Industry was sheltered behind tariffs and the Control of Manufactures Acts, which required Irish citizens to hold most shares in new firms and so discouraged foreign investment. The home market was too small for efficient production. While western Europe boomed, Irish growth was a fraction of the European rate, and the Irish pound, tied to sterling, meant Irish conditions followed British stop-go cycles.\n\nBalance of payments crises in 1951 to 1952 and 1955 to 1956 produced a pattern of deflation. MacEntee's 1952 budget cut food subsidies and raised duties, which pushed up prices and cost Fianna Fáil seats in 1954. Gerard Sweetman's response in the second coalition was higher taxes, credit restrictions and spending cuts, which reduced demand and raised unemployment. These measures cut imports but did nothing to build productive capacity, so the same crisis returned. Voters, angry at taxes and unemployment, swung from one side to the other, which is why governments alternated so quickly.\n\nThe human cost was emigration. The population fell from 2,960,593 in 1951 to 2,818,341 in 1961 despite a high birth rate, and net emigration in the decade was about 400,000, mostly young people leaving for Britain where construction, factories and the health service offered work. Emigration drained the country of its most energetic people, undermined confidence in every government and fed the sense of drift. There were some positive steps, such as the IDA in 1949, the Underdeveloped Areas Act 1952, Marshall Aid of about 146 million dollars, and Noël Browne's success against tuberculosis, but they were too limited to change the picture.\n\nOverall, political instability and economic failure fed each other. Weak coalitions and minority governments could not plan for growth, and each deflationary crisis made voters turn against whoever held office. Historians such as J.J. Lee speak of wasted years, and others stress Ireland's dependence on Britain, but both views agree that only the change of direction after 1957, with Whitaker's Economic Development and Fianna Fáil's win of 78 seats and an overall majority in the 1957 election, ended the cycle.",
