    "label": "2",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "I agree to a considerable extent that Seán Lemass transformed both the country and its foreign relations, though the change was clearer in the economy and in tone than in lasting results. At home he ended protectionism and made planning and foreign investment the basis of policy, and abroad he moved Ireland towards Europe, free trade with Britain and a more realistic approach to Northern Ireland. He left some problems unresolved, so transformation is best seen as a decisive change of direction rather than a complete solution.\n\nThe domestic transformation began before he became Taoiseach in June 1959. As Minister for Industry and Commerce from 1957 he worked with T.K. Whitaker, whose Economic Development of May 1958 argued that protection had failed and that investment should be productive and export-led. The government's First Programme for Economic Expansion, published in November 1958, removed the Control of Manufactures rule that Irish citizens must hold most shares, extended Export Profits Tax Relief, and gave IDA grants to new firms, with the best terms in the west. The Shannon industrial estate was set up in 1959. The result was growth of about 4 per cent a year to 1963, twice the target, and net emigration fell to about 80,000 in 1961 to 1966 from about 200,000 in the previous five years. The 1966 census recorded the first rise in population since the early 1950s.\n\nLemass also changed the style of government. He built a closer relationship with trade unions and employers, and the national wage rounds were an example. He set up the Committee on Industrial Organisation and, from 1963, adaptation councils to prepare firms for competition. He used the new Telefís Éireann to explain policy and project a modern state. The Second Programme of 1963 was more ambitious, but it was abandoned in 1967 after wage rises and a balance of payments deficit, a sign that the transformation had limits. Employment grew only modestly, farming and the west lagged behind, and rural poverty and housing problems remained.\n\nIn foreign relations the change was equally clear. Ireland applied to join the EEC on 31 July 1961, nine days before Britain, because Britain was the market for most Irish exports. When de Gaulle vetoed British entry on 14 January 1963, Lemass turned to the Anglo-Irish Free Trade Agreement, signed on 14 December 1965 and in force from July 1966, which removed tariffs between the two countries over ten years. This ended the old policy of economic nationalism, though it exposed small firms in textiles and footwear to competition, and many closed. Ireland also joined GATT in 1967, committing it to lower tariffs. Meanwhile Frank Aiken's independent line at the UN, including the 1961 resolution on nuclear non-proliferation, showed a small state with a voice, though that owed as much to Aiken as to Lemass.\n\nThe most striking change was towards Northern Ireland. On 14 January 1965 Lemass travelled to Stormont to meet Terence O'Neill, and O'Neill visited Dublin on 9 February, the first meetings of the leaders of the two parts of Ireland since partition. This implied that the North would not change without the consent of its people, a break from the anti-partition rhetoric of the 1950s. Ian Paisley and other unionists denounced the meetings, and no real progress followed, so the effect was more symbolic than political.\n\nOn balance, Lemass deserves the credit for a real transformation. He supplied the political will that turned Whitaker's analysis into policy, abandoned protection, sought entry to Europe and changed the tone of Irish politics. Critics note that world growth in the 1950s and 1960s helped, that earlier governments had begun some measures, and that partition and rural poverty were untouched. Even so, Ireland in 1966 was more confident, more open and more outward-looking than it had been in 1959, and that justifies the claim in the question.",
