    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Attitudes towards the Irish language and Irish culture changed greatly between 1949 and 1989. Early on, the language was treated as a national duty to be enforced through schools and the civil service, whereas by the 1970s compulsion was being abandoned and Irish was supported through broadcasting and institutions. Culture became more confident, popular and international, and was shaped by new media, though the church and the censor were slower to give way.\n\nAt the start of the period the language was protected by compulsion. The 1937 Constitution declared Irish the national and first official language, it was compulsory in school, a pass was needed for the Leaving Certificate, and Irish was required for entry to the civil service. Yet the Gaeltacht was in decline through emigration, and the state set up Gaeltarra Éireann in 1957 to encourage industry in Irish-speaking areas. The sense that the revival was struggling led to new approaches that tried to make Irish attractive rather than obligatory. Gael Linn, founded in 1953, used records, the Amharc Éireann newsreel from 1956 and the feature film Mise Éire in 1959, with a score by Seán Ó Riada, to link the language to modern media.\n\nBy the 1960s attitudes were turning against compulsion. The Commission on the Restoration of the Irish Language reported in 1963 and a government White Paper of 1965 accepted the aim of restoration but proposed a more flexible approach. The Language Freedom Movement, founded in 1966, argued that compulsory Irish turned pupils against the language, and its critics set up counter-campaigns. The change came in 1973, when the Fine Gael and Labour government under Richard Burke as Minister for Education ended the rule that students must pass Irish to gain the Leaving Certificate, and the civil service requirement was relaxed. This marked a shift from enforcement to choice, even though the language kept its symbolic status.\n\nThe Gaeltacht itself began to demand a voice. The Gaeltacht Civil Rights Movement, formed in 1969, wanted better services and Irish-language radio, and in 1970 it ran a pirate station, Saor Raidió Chonamara. Raidió na Gaeltachta began broadcasting from Connemara on Easter Sunday, 2 April 1972. Bord na Gaeilge followed in 1975 and Údarás na Gaeltachta in 1980, which took over economic and social development in Irish-speaking areas. These show a change from a top-down attempt to revive the language to support for communities that spoke it, but everyday speakers continued to decline outside the Gaeltacht. Writers such as Breandán Ó hEithir, whose novel Lig Sinn i gCathú appeared in 1976, presented Irish as a living medium for modern subjects, not as a duty.\n\nTraditional culture was revived and made popular. Comhaltas Ceoltóirí Éireann was founded in Mullingar in 1951, and the fleadh cheoil competitions from 1952 spread traditional music and dance. Ó Riada's group Ceoltóirí Chualann played traditional music in a concert style from 1961, and his Ceol an Aifrinn of 1968 linked the music to the reforms of Vatican II. The Chieftains were formed in 1962, and the Clancy Brothers, The Dubliners and later Planxty made Irish ballads popular at home and abroad. This reduced the old divide between rural tradition and urban popular culture and gave Irish culture a commercial and international dimension.\n\nNew media and changing religion also reshaped attitudes. Telefís Éireann began in 1961 and brought imported programmes alongside home-produced drama, sport and Irish-language material, although Irish took a small share of airtime. The Late Late Show, from 1962, became a forum for national debate. Some clergy and traditionalists feared that television threatened Irish values, while others welcomed a window on the world. Censorship was relaxed too: John McGahern's The Dark was banned in 1965, but the Censorship of Publications Act 1967 limited bans to twelve years and freed thousands of titles. Vatican II, held from 1962 to 1965, introduced the vernacular Mass in Ireland from 1965.\n\nOverall, attitudes moved from compulsion and defensive protection to choice, pluralism and openness. The language gained institutions and broadcasting but lost ground as a community language, while traditional music and literature gained a larger, younger and international audience. Historians see these years as the start of a more secular and diverse Ireland, in which Irish identity was no longer defined by a single language or a single set of values.",
