    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Irish membership of the EEC from 1 January 1973 transformed the economy and, to a lesser extent, society. Economically it brought guaranteed farm prices, large transfers from Brussels and a flood of foreign investment, but it also destroyed many protected industries and did not prevent high unemployment in the 1980s. Socially it forced equality reforms, widened horizons and reopened the debate on sovereignty.\n\nThe most immediate economic effect was on agriculture through the Common Agricultural Policy. The CAP guaranteed minimum prices for beef, milk and cereals and bought surplus produce into intervention stores. Irish farm prices were raised towards EEC levels during the transition and farm incomes rose sharply in the early years, especially for beef and dairy farmers. Land values climbed and farmers invested in machinery, buildings and drainage, helped by grants. The benefits were uneven, however. Larger, more productive farms in the east and south gained most, while small farms on poor land in the west gained far less, and the number working on the land continued to fall. Guaranteed prices also encouraged overproduction, and in 1984 the EEC introduced milk quotas that limited output on Irish dairy farms. Farmers therefore gained greatly, but membership also showed the limits of dependence on a single policy.\n\nSecond, membership brought substantial money from Europe. The European Regional Development Fund was created in 1975, partly because Britain, Ireland and Italy pressed for help for poorer regions, and Ireland received a considerable share. The European Social Fund paid for training and employment schemes, including those of AnCO, the state training authority. The money paid for roads, water and sewerage schemes, telecommunications and industrial estates. By the 1980s net payments from the EEC budget amounted to several per cent of national income, and the reform of the structural funds in 1988 doubled the money available, with Ireland a major beneficiary for 1989 to 1993. Ireland, once a poor relation, became a net recipient, and this helped to modernise the country's infrastructure.\n\nThird, membership transformed industry and trade. American and other foreign firms set up factories in Ireland to sell into the EEC, drawn by IDA grants, a young English-speaking workforce and low taxes. In 1981 a 10 per cent rate of corporation tax on manufacturing profits was introduced. Electronics, pharmaceuticals, chemicals and engineering became leading sectors, with firms such as Digital in Galway. Manufactured goods overtook farm produce in the value of exports, and trade with the continent grew while the share going to Britain fell, reducing dependence on a single market. Membership was also the context for Ireland's decision in March 1979 to join the European Monetary System, which ended the link between the Irish pound and sterling. The economy became more diversified and more open.\n\nHowever, the economic effects were not all positive. Tariffs on trade with other member states were phased out by 1977, and protected Irish industries such as clothing, textiles and footwear lost their shelter. Many firms closed, with heavy job losses, and employment in traditional manufacturing fell. The rise in unemployment of the late 1970s and 1980s showed that membership did not by itself solve the economy's weaknesses. The benefits of membership were real, but they came at a price for the workers and communities of the old protected industries.\n\nSocially, the most important effect was on equality. EEC directives on equal pay in 1975 and equal treatment in employment in 1976 required changes in Irish law, and they reinforced the Anti-Discrimination (Pay) Act 1974 and the Employment Equality Act 1977. The marriage bar in the public service was lifted in 1973, and a directive on equal treatment in social welfare, adopted in 1978, forced changes to payments to married women in the 1980s. Irish citizens also gained the right to live and work in other member states, which eased emigration, and young people had more contact with continental Europe. Ireland's outlook became less narrowly tied to Britain, and Patrick Hillery served as the first Irish member of the Commission while Ireland held the presidency of the Council of Ministers in 1975.\n\nMembership also raised questions about independence. EEC law took precedence over Irish law in the areas covered by the treaties, which was made possible by the Third Amendment to the Constitution in 1972, and the European Court of Justice became a route for individuals to challenge Irish law. Critics argued that Ireland had surrendered some sovereignty, and the debate returned in the Crotty case and the later referendums on European treaties. Fisheries were a particular grievance, since fishermen felt Ireland received a small share of the catch in its own waters under the Common Fisheries Policy agreed in 1983.\n\nOverall, membership of the EEC had a largely positive but uneven effect between 1973 and 1989. It secured better-priced markets for farmers, brought large transfers, attracted foreign industry and forced social reform, yet it also closed protected firms, left small farmers and fishermen behind and did not prevent unemployment. Historians generally accept that membership underpinned the transformation of the economy and society, while stressing that the full benefits came later, from the 1990s.",
