    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Robert Owen's village at New Lanark was a pioneering experiment in industrial welfare, combining shorter hours, schooling, decent housing and a humane system of discipline with continued profit. It was not generally adopted because it depended on Owen's personal control, because most employers relied on low wages and long hours for their profits, and because it met opposition from his partners and critics over its cost and his religious views.\n\nOwen, born in Newtown in 1771, learned the cotton trade in Manchester and became manager of New Lanark in January 1800 after marrying Caroline Dale, daughter of the owner David Dale. The mills on the River Clyde, founded in 1785 and powered by the Falls of Clyde, housed about 2,000 people, including some 500 pauper children from workhouses. Owen found a thirteen-hour working day, children as young as five or six working in the mills, dirty overcrowded houses, filthy streets and a workforce marked by drunkenness and theft. Influenced by the Enlightenment view that character is formed by environment, he set out to change the conditions in order to change the people.\n\nIn the mill itself, Owen stopped taking pauper children as apprentices and raised the minimum age of employment, so that children under ten no longer worked in the mills. By 1816 he had cut the working day to ten and three-quarter hours, arguing that output did not fall. When the American embargo of 1806 closed the mills, he paid wages to idle workers for about four months, which won their loyalty. He replaced fines and physical punishment with the Silent Monitor, a block of wood with four coloured sides, from black for bad conduct to white for excellent, turned each day to record each worker's behaviour without shouting or punishment.\n\nOutside the mill, Owen improved the whole community. He rebuilt and improved the housing, cleaned the streets and opened a village store selling good-quality goods at close to cost price, with the sale of strong drink controlled. In 1816 he opened the Institute for the Formation of Character, a hall that provided adult education, lectures, music and dancing, and an infant school for children from about one or two to six, which used songs, games and practical activities instead of rote learning. This was one of the earliest infant schools in Britain and attracted visitors from abroad, including statesmen and royalty. Owen treated this spending as a sound investment in the workforce, and the mills remained profitable.\n\nOwen also tried to spread his ideas. He published A New View of Society between 1813 and 1816, gave evidence to a parliamentary committee in 1816 and campaigned for legislation, which contributed to the Cotton Mills and Factories Act of 1819, barring children under nine from cotton mills and limiting the hours of older children. Yet the Act was weaker than he wanted, since it had no inspectors and relied on local magistrates, so it was poorly enforced. He also put forward plans for self-supporting co-operative villages in 1817 and 1820, and later founded New Harmony in Indiana in 1825, which collapsed within a few years after disputes among its members.\n\nSeveral reasons explain why the model was not adopted generally. Most mill owners made their profits from low wages and long hours, and few were willing to give up the level of control or spend on schools and welfare that Owen's scheme needed. The success of New Lanark depended heavily on his personal authority, which made it difficult to copy. His own partners, especially the Quaker William Allen, objected to the cost of his schemes, to the absence of formal religious instruction and to the dancing and music, and this conflict led to his departure, as he left for America in 1824 and ended his connection with the mills around 1829.\n\nOwen also damaged his cause by his radicalism. His public denunciation of all organised religions at a London meeting in August 1817 turned many clergy and conservative figures against him, and critics regarded his co-operative plans as impractical socialism. Later experiments such as the Equitable Labour Exchange of 1832 and the Grand National Consolidated Trades Union of 1834 also failed.\n\nIn the end, New Lanark showed that humane treatment and profit could go together, and its influence lived on in the infant school movement, the 1819 Act and the co-operative movement, notably the Rochdale Pioneers' store in 1844. Later employers, such as the builders of Saltaire in 1853, created model villages, but they were paternalist rather than co-operative. The model was therefore admired but not generally copied, because it demanded more of employers than their commercial interests would allow.",
