    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Music, art and literature and language all made a major contribution to cultural nationalism between 1815 and 1871. They gave peoples without their own state a shared past, a set of symbols and a common language, and so turned a vague sense of belonging into a demand for nationhood. This was especially important in Italy, Germany, Poland and the Czech lands.\n\nThe Romantic movement supplied the ideas behind this. Romantics rejected the cool rationalism of the Enlightenment in favour of feeling, imagination and the national past. Herder had developed the idea of the Volksgeist, the distinctive spirit of a people, and nationalists took it up. Folklore and medieval legend were collected because they seemed to reveal the true character of a nation, and this made culture a weapon in the hands of movements that had no government of their own.\n\nMusic was one of the most powerful forms. Chopin's mazurkas and polonaises drew on Polish dance rhythms, and after 1831 his music became a symbol of Polish feeling for a people whose homeland was ruled by foreigners and who lived in exile. Liszt's Hungarian Rhapsodies of the 1840s and 1850s drew on Hungarian and Romani music and presented Hungary's identity to audiences all over Europe. Smetana's opera The Bartered Bride (1866) and his cycle Ma vlast (1874-79) did the same for the Czechs through folk melody and landscape. Wagner used the Nibelungen legend and Norse sagas in the Ring cycle, first performed in full at Bayreuth in 1876, and so gave Germans a mythic national past.\n\nVerdi shows music at its most political. His opera Nabucco (1842) contained the chorus Va, pensiero, sung by Hebrew exiles, and Italian audiences under Austrian rule heard it as a call for their own liberation. By about 1859 the cry Viva VERDI was popularly said to be a coded slogan, since the letters could stand for Vittorio Emanuele Re d'Italia, the king nationalists wanted. Music thus reached ordinary people in theatres and streets in a way that political pamphlets could not.\n\nArt gave nationalism its images. Delacroix's Liberty Leading the People (1830) showed liberty leading the Parisian crowd over the barricades in the July Revolution and became a lasting picture of popular struggle. Caspar David Friedrich's landscapes, such as Wanderer above the Sea of Fog, expressed a Romantic feeling for nature and the German spirit. Romantic historical painting presented scenes from national history, which gave citizens a shared image of their past. Painting was therefore less direct than opera but it shaped how nations pictured themselves.\n\nLiterature and language had the deepest effect, because a common language is the basis of a nation. The Brothers Grimm published their folk tales in 1812 and 1815 as the voice of the German people and began a great German dictionary in 1838. Manzoni's novel I promessi sposi (1827) was revised in 1840-42 in the educated Tuscan of Florence and became a model for standard Italian, a vital step in a country where most people spoke local dialects. Mickiewicz's epic Pan Tadeusz (1834) celebrated the Polish past, and Pushkin's writing became a model for the Russian literary language. Jungmann's Czech dictionary of 1835-39 and Lonnrot's Kalevala (1835) for Finland showed how grammars, dictionaries and national epics created a single national community.\n\nOverall, cultural nationalism did not itself create states, which came through war and diplomacy. But music, art and literature built the sense of identity without which no movement could have won mass support. Where a nation had no state, as in Italy, Germany, Poland and Bohemia, culture was often the only means by which it could assert its existence, and so its contribution was fundamental.",
