    "label": "1",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "European powers sought colonies between 1871 and 1914 for a mixture of economic, strategic, nationalist, missionary and ideological reasons, and the rivalry between them fed on itself. No single motive explains the scramble, but the economic and nationalist motives were probably the strongest, since colonies were seen both as a source of wealth and as a badge of Great Power status.\n\nEconomic motives were central. Industrial states needed raw materials such as rubber, cotton, palm oil and copper, and wanted protected markets once tariffs rose across Europe from the 1870s. The Congo is a stark example, where the demand for rubber, driven by the bicycle and the motor car, led Leopold II's state to force Africans to collect it under quotas. J.A. Hobson argued in Imperialism: A Study (1902) that capitalists invested overseas because of surplus capital at home, and Lenin later built on this idea. Historians dispute the theory, since most European trade and investment went to areas outside the empires, so economics alone cannot be the whole explanation.\n\nStrategic considerations also drove expansion. Powers wanted naval bases, coaling stations and secure routes. Britain occupied Egypt in 1882 to protect the Suez Canal, its route to India, and then moved to control the Nile valley. This in turn led to its ambition of a belt of territory from the Cape to Cairo, promoted by Cecil Rhodes, while France sought to link its territories from Senegal to the Red Sea. The clash of these two ambitions at Fashoda in 1898 shows how strategic aims produced competition between powers.\n\nNationalism and prestige were equally important. Colonies were seen as a mark of Great Power status, and newspapers, colonial societies and the public cheered each acquisition. After its defeat in 1871 France sought compensation overseas under Jules Ferry, building French Indochina from Vietnam, Cambodia and Laos. Germany, newly united and industrial, claimed through Bulow 'a place in the sun' and acquired South West Africa, Togo, Cameroon and East Africa, along with Kiaochow in China. Italy took Eritrea and Somaliland and later Libya in 1911-12, partly to show that it was a real Great Power.\n\nMissionary and ideological motives gave imperialism a moral justification. Churches wanted to spread Christianity and end the slave trade, and explorers such as David Livingstone and H.M. Stanley made Africa known to the public. Ideas of racial and cultural superiority, including Social Darwinism, and the belief in a duty to civilise other peoples, as in France's mission civilisatrice or Kipling's poem The White Man's Burden (1899), were used to justify conquest. Yet these ideas were often a cover for other aims, as the brutality of Leopold's Congo Free State, condemned by Morel and Casement, made clear.\n\nFinally, technology and competition made the scramble easy and fast. Quinine against malaria, steamships, the telegraph and the Maxim gun gave Europeans overwhelming military advantage, so conquest was cheap. Once one power claimed territory, others felt that they had to do the same or be shut out. The Berlin Conference of 1884-85, called by Bismarck, set rules such as effective occupation, and it speeded up the partition: in 1880 about a tenth of Africa was under European control, but by 1914 about nine tenths had been taken, with only Ethiopia and Liberia independent.\n\nIn conclusion, European powers sought colonies because of a combination of motives, but I would argue that economic need and national prestige were the most powerful, backed by strategic fears and justified by religious and racial ideas, while new technology and fear of being left behind turned a series of claims into a race.",
