    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Stalin transformed the Soviet Union to a very great extent. Within twelve years of becoming undisputed leader he had turned a mainly peasant country into an industrial power with collectivised farming and a state-controlled society. The transformation was achieved at a terrible human cost, and in some respects, especially living standards, it fell short of its promises.\n\nThe first element was economic: rapid industrialisation through the Five-Year Plans. From 1928 Gosplan set targets for heavy industry such as coal, iron, steel, electricity and machinery. Stalin argued that the USSR was fifty to a hundred years behind the West and must close the gap in ten years or be crushed. Vast new projects were built, including the Dnieprostroi hydroelectric dam, the Magnitogorsk steel complex and the Moscow metro. Propaganda promoted heroes such as the miner Stakhanov in 1935 to raise output. Production of coal, steel and oil rose dramatically, and by 1939 the Soviet Union was among the world's leading industrial producers. This was a genuine economic transformation, and it helped the country survive the German invasion of 1941.\n\nThe plans, however, had serious weaknesses. Targets were often unrealistic, so managers falsified figures, quality was poor and waste was common. Consumer goods and housing were neglected, workers faced harsh discipline, long hours and overcrowded barracks, and forced labour in the Gulag camps was used on construction projects. Industrial growth was real, but it was lopsided and bought at the expense of ordinary comfort.\n\nThe second element was the transformation of agriculture through collectivisation, begun in 1929. Peasants were forced to merge their holdings into collective farms, or kolkhozy, supplied with machinery by Machine Tractor Stations. Stalin also ordered the liquidation of the kulaks as a class, and wealthier peasants were deported, shot or sent to camps. Many peasants slaughtered their livestock rather than hand it over. The result was a catastrophic famine in 1932 to 1933, especially in Ukraine and Kazakhstan, in which millions died, while the state continued to export grain to pay for industrial machinery. Collectivisation gave the state control of the countryside and of grain supplies for the cities, but it damaged agricultural output for years. It destroyed the independent peasantry and so changed rural society completely, yet it was economically a failure in the short term.\n\nThe third element was social transformation through terror and control. After the murder of Kirov in December 1934, Stalin launched the Great Purge. The show trials of 1936 to 1938 condemned old Bolsheviks such as Zinoviev, Kamenev and Bukharin to death, the army leadership was purged and the NKVD under Yezhov arrested millions, many of whom were shot or sent to the Gulag. Fear permeated all levels of society and no one was safe. A cult of personality presented Stalin as the infallible leader, culture was forced into the mould of socialist realism and religion was persecuted. The 1936 constitution promised rights and universal suffrage, but power stayed with Stalin and the Party.\n\nThere were also social gains. Education expanded, literacy rose, cities grew rapidly as millions moved from villages to the new industrial centres, and women entered the workforce in large numbers with access to training and jobs. New opportunities opened for ambitious and loyal people who replaced those purged. These changes reshaped Soviet society, although they were accompanied by heavy repression.\n\nOverall, Stalin's transformation was extensive. The economy became industrial and state-owned, the peasantry was brought under state control and society was reshaped by terror and propaganda. It succeeded in building military and industrial strength but at the cost of millions of lives and with limited improvement in ordinary living standards, so it was a transformation by force rather than by consent.",
