    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Britain dealt with the social and economic problems of the inter-war period with only partial success. Governments kept democracy stable and avoided extremism, and by the late 1930s there was real progress in housing and in the new industries of the south, but they failed to solve long-term unemployment in the older industrial regions, and their policies were cautious and often inadequate.\n\nThe economic problem was the decline of the staple industries on which Britain's nineteenth-century prosperity had rested: coal, shipbuilding, iron and steel, and textiles. Postwar competition and the loss of export markets left the north of England, south Wales, Clydeside and the north-east with heavy unemployment, which rose above two million by mid-1921. The government's response was largely orthodox. Churchill's return to the gold standard in 1925 made British exports more expensive and added to the difficulties of coal and other exporters. This policy was a failure, because it deepened the problems of the older industries.\n\nIndustrial conflict followed. When mine owners proposed lower wages and longer hours in 1926, the miners' lockout led to the General Strike of 3 to 12 May. The Baldwin government used volunteers and the Organisation for the Maintenance of Supplies to keep services running, and the TUC called off the strike, leaving the miners to fight on alone until they were forced to return. The government kept order and avoided revolution, which was a success of a kind, but the underlying problems of low wages and high unemployment in coal were not addressed, and the Trade Disputes Act of 1927 restricted union power.\n\nThe Great Depression after 1929 made things much worse. Unemployment rose to nearly three million by 1932 to 1933. In 1931 the Labour government split over cuts in the dole, and Ramsay MacDonald formed a National Government. It cut public spending, introduced the means test for unemployment relief, which humiliated families by examining household resources, and left the gold standard in 1931. Cheap money, with the bank rate falling to 2 per cent in 1932, and tariffs introduced in 1932 helped recovery. Unemployment was falling by the mid-1930s, so the government can be credited with a measure of recovery.\n\nThe regional divide shows the limits of this success. The south-east and Midlands saw new industries such as motor cars, electrical goods and chemicals grow, with prosperity and a housing boom. The old industrial areas remained depressed. The Special Areas Act of 1934 offered little help. The Jarrow March of October 1936, when about 200 men marched some 300 miles to London with a petition asking for new industry after the closure of Palmer's shipyard, showed how desperate some towns remained. Ellen Wilkinson, the town's MP, supported the march and presented the petition in the Commons, but it was not debated and there was no immediate relief for the town. Keynes's argument in The General Theory in 1936 that governments should spend to create demand was not accepted until later.\n\nOn the social side Britain did better. The Addison Act of 1919, the Wheatley Act of 1924 and the Greenwood Act of 1930 provided council housing and slum clearance, and about three million new houses were built during the 1930s, many of them by private builders in the suburbs. Unemployment insurance was extended in 1920, so the dole prevented the worst destitution even if its level was low. These measures raised living standards for most of the population, and those in work saw real wages rise as prices fell.\n\nIn conclusion, Britain's record was mixed. It preserved political stability, extended housing and welfare and achieved recovery in the south, but it failed the communities of the old staple industries, whose problems were solved only by rearmament and war after 1939. A fair verdict is that it was moderately successful for the majority but unsuccessful for the regions hit hardest.",
