    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Between 1920 and 1945 the technology of warfare was transformed on land, in the air, at sea and in the field of intelligence, and the period ended with the most destructive weapon ever devised, the atomic bomb. The developments fall into two phases: the theory and experiment of the inter-war years, and the application of those ideas on a massive scale during the Second World War.\n\nOn land the key development was the tank and the theory of armoured warfare. Writers such as J.F.C. Fuller and Basil Liddell Hart in Britain argued that tanks should be used in concentrated formations rather than spread among the infantry, but it was the German general Heinz Guderian, in Achtung - Panzer! (1937), who put the ideas into practice. From 1935 Germany formed Panzer divisions that used radio communication and combined armour with motorised infantry. Together with dive-bombers such as the Stuka, they produced blitzkrieg, or lightning war, which defeated Poland in about five weeks in 1939 and France in six weeks in 1940, when German armour bypassed the Maginot Line through the Ardennes and reached the Channel coast by 20 May. France had as many tanks as Germany, but it had spread them among infantry units, which shows how much the way technology was used mattered. Later the Soviet T-34 and the American Sherman were built in huge numbers and helped to turn the tide against Germany.\n\nThe second great area of change was air power. Theorists such as Giulio Douhet argued that bombers would decide future wars, and the bombing of Guernica in April 1937 by the German Condor Legion in the Spanish Civil War tested aircraft and tactics and shocked world opinion. Fighter design improved rapidly with the Hawker Hurricane, first flown in 1935, the Supermarine Spitfire of 1936 and Germany's Messerschmitt Bf 109. In 1940 the Battle of Britain became the first major campaign fought entirely in the air, and the Blitz, which began on 7 September 1940, showed the vulnerability of cities. Later in the war RAF Bomber Command and the United States Army Air Forces carried out strategic bombing of German cities and industry, with devastating raids on Hamburg in July 1943 and Dresden in February 1945, while American B-29 bombers attacked Japanese cities. Air power also changed war at sea, because carrier aircraft decided the Pacific war, as at Midway in June 1942 when four Japanese carriers were sunk.\n\nRadar and codebreaking showed that war was now also a contest of science and intelligence. Robert Watson-Watt demonstrated in 1935 that radio waves could detect aircraft, and the Chain Home stations were built along the east and south coasts from 1936. This early warning allowed the outnumbered RAF to use its fighters economically in 1940 and helped defeat the Luftwaffe, and later airborne and ship-borne radar helped to locate U-boats. At Bletchley Park British codebreakers, building on the work of Polish mathematicians who had broken the early Enigma machine in the 1930s, decrypted German Enigma messages. This intelligence, known as Ultra, was used in the Battle of the Atlantic and in North Africa. German U-boats sank huge numbers of Allied merchant ships, but the convoy system, escort groups, radar and codebreaking reduced losses from 1943.\n\nRockets and jets were the most advanced developments of the war, though they came too late to change its outcome. Frank Whittle patented the jet engine in 1930, and the German Heinkel He 178 became the first jet aircraft in August 1939. The Messerschmitt Me 262 jet fighter entered service in 1944. Wernher von Braun's rocket research, which began in the early 1930s, led to the V-2 at Peenemünde. Germany launched V-1 flying bombs against London from June 1944 and V-2 rockets from September 1944, and later against Antwerp. These weapons were the ancestors of the guided missile and the space rocket, but Germany lacked the time and resources to use them decisively.\n\nThe most decisive development of all was the atomic bomb, produced by the American Manhattan Project. The first test took place in New Mexico on 16 July 1945, and the United States dropped atomic bombs on Hiroshima on 6 August and Nagasaki on 9 August 1945, after which Japan announced its surrender on 15 August. A single weapon could now destroy a city, which ended the war in the Pacific but also opened the nuclear age.\n\nOverall, the period saw warfare become faster, more mechanised and more destructive. Tanks and aircraft brought mobility and attack from the sky, radar and Enigma decrypts made information a weapon, and the V-weapons and the atomic bomb pointed to the future. Each development increased the suffering of civilians as well as soldiers, and by 1945 total war was fought with science as much as with armies.",
