    "label": "1",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Opposition to the Sovietisation of Eastern Europe was widespread but, with one qualified exception, it was not successful. In the years 1945 to 1948 non-communist parties were broken one by one, and when ordinary people rose in revolt in 1953 and 1956 the Soviet Union either crushed them or conceded only limited change inside a communist system. Only Yugoslavia and, to a degree, Poland in 1956 achieved any lasting independence from Moscow.\n\nIn the first phase, from 1945 to 1948, political opposition was real but weak against the Red Army and the secret police. In Poland the Soviet Union backed the communist-dominated Lublin government against the London government-in-exile, and sixteen leaders of the Polish underground were arrested and tried in Moscow in 1945. The peasant leader Stanisław Mikołajczyk led the main democratic opposition but he left Poland in October 1947 after rigged elections. In Bulgaria the opposition leader Nikola Petkov was executed in 1947. Opposition in these states was therefore eliminated by fraud and force, and Stalin ignored the promises of free elections made at Yalta.\n\nHungary shows both the strength of popular opposition and the method used to defeat it. In the free election of November 1945 the communists won only about 17 per cent of the vote while the Smallholders won about 57 per cent. Yet Mátyás Rákosi used the police and the ministry of the interior to break the Smallholders slice by slice, the technique known as salami tactics, and a fraudulent election in 1947 made the communists the largest party. Democratic opposition had won the vote but still lost the country, which shows how limited its success was. Czechoslovakia confirms this. It was the last eastern state to remain democratic, and in February 1948 the non-communist ministers resigned in protest at police control. The gamble failed, because Klement Gottwald's communists took power without a general election, and the death of Jan Masaryk in March 1948 ended any hope of resistance.\n\nThe Church and the economy offered other lines of resistance, again with little success. In Hungary Cardinal Mindszenty was jailed in 1949 after a show trial, and communists such as László Rajk were executed after show trials in an attempt to remove any rival. Eastern states such as Poland and Czechoslovakia showed interest in Marshall Aid in 1947, but both withdrew under Soviet pressure and Stalin forbade eastern states to take part. They were bound instead into the Cominform in 1947 and Comecon in 1949. Attempts to escape the Soviet economic and political system were stopped within weeks.\n\nThe one clear success came from within the communist movement itself. Tito's Yugoslavia, which had come to power largely through its own wartime resistance rather than the Red Army, was expelled from the Cominform in June 1948 but survived and kept its independence. It showed that a communist state could break from Moscow, and Stalin's purges of suspected Titoists elsewhere, such as the Rajk trial, were a response to that example. However, Yugoslavia was an exception, as it had no Soviet troops on its soil.\n\nAfter Stalin's death in 1953 opposition moved from politicians to workers and the streets. East German workers rose on 17 June 1953 against higher work norms and shortages, and the rising spread to hundreds of towns before Soviet tanks crushed it. In Poland the Poznań workers protested in June 1956, and in October Władysław Gomułka returned to power after a confrontation with Moscow that was resolved without Soviet intervention. Poland won a new leadership, an end to forced collectivisation and the release of Cardinal Wyszyński, so this was a partial success, but the Polish party kept one-party rule and Gomułka later reimposed tight controls.\n\nThe greatest challenge came in Hungary in October 1956. A student demonstration on 23 October, encouraged by Khrushchev's secret speech and the Polish events, became a national uprising after the AVH fired on the crowd. Imre Nagy became prime minister, formed a coalition with non-communist parties and on 1 November declared Hungary neutral and withdrawn from the Warsaw Pact. This went beyond reform of communism, and on 4 November Soviet forces attacked Budapest in strength. Perhaps 2,500 to 3,000 Hungarians died and about 200,000 fled to Austria. Nagy was executed in 1958, and János Kádár's government was installed by Soviet troops.\n\nOverall, opposition to Sovietisation was brave and at times popular, but it was largely unsuccessful. Political parties were broken between 1945 and 1948, the revolts of 1953 and 1956 were crushed, and only Tito's Yugoslavia and Gomułka's Poland won any room to manoeuvre. Opposition succeeded only where it stayed within a communist framework and did not threaten Soviet security.",
