    "label": "1",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Britain contributed a great deal to tensions in the Middle East between 1945 and 1956, but not alone. Its contradictory promises and its handling of the Palestine Mandate created the conditions for the Arab-Israeli conflict, and its heavy-handed pursuit of imperial interests in Egypt culminated in the Suez disaster. Arab nationalism, the Holocaust and Cold War rivalry were also powerful causes of tension.\n\nThe roots of the problem lay in British policy before 1945. The Balfour Declaration of November 1917 promised a national home for the Jewish people in Palestine, provided the rights of existing non-Jewish communities were not prejudiced, while Britain also made promises to Arab leaders. When Britain took the Mandate, which came into force in 1923, it was responsible for reconciling claims that could not easily be reconciled. By 1939 the Jewish share of the population had risen from about 11 per cent to roughly 30 per cent, and the Arab Revolt of 1936-39 showed how bitter the rivalry had become. The White Paper of May 1939, which limited Jewish immigration to 75,000 and promised an independent Palestine within ten years, was rejected by the Zionists. Britain had therefore set Arab and Jewish hopes against one another before 1945.\n\nAfter the war British policy deepened Jewish bitterness. The Holocaust killed about six million Jews and made the demand for a Jewish state and for refugee entry to Palestine far more urgent. Yet Britain, with about 100,000 troops in the country, refused the Anglo-American Committee's recommendation of April 1946 that 100,000 refugees be admitted, and kept to its immigration limits. The turning back of the Exodus in 1947, with about 4,500 survivors sent back to Europe, drew world sympathy for the Zionists and hostility towards Britain. Groups such as the Irgun under Menachem Begin and Lehi turned to violence against the British, most notably the bombing of the King David Hotel in July 1946, which killed 91 people. British policy thus helped to produce the Jewish terrorism that made the situation worse.\n\nBritain's manner of leaving also contributed to the violence. Unable to reconcile the two communities, it handed the question to the United Nations in February 1947, and the UN passed the partition plan on 29 November 1947. Britain abstained, refused to implement partition itself and announced it would leave by 15 May 1948. This created a security vacuum in the spring of 1948, when civil war was already raging; Jewish forces seized Haifa, Jaffa and Tiberias. The Mandate ended on 14 May, the day Israel was proclaimed, and the Arab states invaded the next day. About 700,000 Palestinian Arabs fled or were expelled, and the refugee question has poisoned the region ever since. Britain's hasty withdrawal left the conflict to be fought out.\n\nIn Egypt, British policy was also a source of tension. Britain still held the Suez Canal base, and Nasser, who emerged after the Free Officers overthrew King Farouk in 1952, saw British influence as a relic of imperialism. Although the Anglo-Egyptian Agreement of 1954 provided for British withdrawal, completed in June 1956, Britain's sponsorship of the Baghdad Pact of 1955 appeared to Nasser to be an attempt to isolate Egypt. He responded by buying arms from the Soviet bloc through Czechoslovakia, bringing the Cold War into the region. When the United States and Britain withdrew the offer to fund the Aswan High Dam in July 1956, Nasser nationalised the Suez Canal Company on 26 July, setting off the crisis.\n\nBritain's response to nationalisation was the greatest single contribution to regional tension. Eden compared Nasser to the dictators of the 1930s, and in the secret Protocol of Sèvres of 24 October 1956 Britain, France and Israel planned an Israeli attack on Sinai followed by an Anglo-French ultimatum. Israel invaded on 29 October, and British and French paratroopers landed at Port Said on 5 November. The collusion was condemned by Gaitskell and led to the resignation of Anthony Nutting. The invasion was ended by American financial pressure, the threat of Soviet rocket attack and UN opposition. Nasser's prestige soared, Eden resigned in January 1957, and Britain's position collapsed. Britain's allies in the region also suffered, as the Hashemite monarchy in Iraq was overthrown in 1958.\n\nOverall, Britain contributed to tensions to a very large extent. Its Mandate promises, its immigration policy, its rushed withdrawal and the Suez invasion all inflamed the region. But the Holocaust, Arab nationalism under Nasser, the rejection of partition by the Arab states and superpower rivalry were also at work, so Britain was a major cause rather than the only one.",
