    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The former colonial powers both helped and hindered post-colonial Africa, but on balance their record was disappointing. They offered trade preferences, aid and relief, yet they left economies built to serve Europe, interfered politically, and attached conditions that often worsened hardship.\n\nColonial rule itself left a damaging legacy. Colonial economies were organised around exporting raw materials, such as cocoa in the Gold Coast, groundnuts in Senegal, cotton in Uganda and copper in Katanga. Railways and ports linked mines and farms to the coast rather than to each other, and companies such as Unilever's United Africa Company and the Union Minière kept control of trade and mining after independence. Dependence on one or two commodities made states vulnerable when copper prices fell in the 1970s and cocoa and coffee prices fell in the 1980s. Economists such as Prebisch and Singer argued that the terms of trade moved against primary producers, so Africa had to export more to buy the same manufactured goods. The powers had therefore created problems they did little to solve.\n\nOn the positive side, Europe did offer trade agreements. The Yaoundé Convention of 1963 gave 18 associated African states duty-free access to European markets. Lomé I, signed in February 1975 between the EEC and 46 African, Caribbean and Pacific states, gave largely duty-free access without requiring reciprocal concessions, which was a genuine innovation. It included STABEX, which compensated countries when earnings from agricultural exports fell, and a Sugar Protocol guaranteeing quotas at European prices. Lomé II added SYSMIN for mineral exporters. These measures helped, but most ACP exports stayed concentrated on a few primary commodities, and prices still fell in the 1980s, so the benefit was limited.\n\nAid and debt are more doubtful. Much aid was bilateral, from former rulers, and was often tied, meaning recipients had to spend it on the donor's goods, which reduced its value and reinforced old links. Aid also supported corrupt regimes such as Mobutu's Zaire, and it created dependence on donors. After the oil price rises of 1973 and 1979, many states borrowed heavily, and when interest rates rose and commodity prices fell in the early 1980s they could not repay. The IMF and World Bank then imposed structural adjustment: devaluation, cuts in public spending, removal of subsidies and privatisation. Critics argued that this cut spending on health and education, and UNICEF's report 'Adjustment with a Human Face' called for protecting the poor. Ghana's recovery programme under Rawlings from 1983 was an early success, though it brought hardship.\n\nFamine and humanitarian work show the helpful side of the West. Pictures of starving children in Biafra in 1968-70 brought relief, and Concern was founded in Dublin in 1968 in response, while Trócaire followed in 1973 and Médecins Sans Frontières in 1971. After Michael Buerk's BBC report of October 1984 on the Ethiopian famine, Bob Geldof's Band Aid and the Live Aid concerts of July 1985 raised about 150 million pounds. Health campaigns eradicated smallpox by 1977, and missionaries and volunteers built schools, hospitals and wells. Yet relief was criticised for distorting local markets and for feeding armed groups, and these efforts addressed symptoms more than causes.\n\nPolitical interference also hindered Africa. Belgium and its mining company supported Katanga's secession in 1960, and Belgian officers were present at Lumumba's murder, leaving the Congo in chaos. Cold War aid was given to allies rather than to the needy, and the West backed Mobutu because he was anti-communist. Meanwhile African governments carried blame too: Nyerere's villagisation in Tanzania disrupted farming and caused food shortages, as the Berg Report of 1981 argued that domestic policies were at fault.\n\nOverall, the former colonial powers did something to help through trade preferences, relief and health work, but their colonial legacy, tied aid, support for dictators and structural adjustment hindered at least as much. Most historians now combine colonial legacies with falling prices, Cold War pressures and African governments' own choices, and the powers must bear a large share of the responsibility.",
