    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Race relations became a major issue in both Britain and France because large-scale post-war migration from former colonies brought visible new minorities into societies that were slow to accept them. In Britain the issue crystallised in the 1950s and 1960s through colour bars, riots and restrictive legislation, and in France it exploded in the 1980s with the second generation, the Front National and the headscarf controversy. In both countries the economic downturn after 1973 turned migrants into a political problem.\n\nIn Britain the starting point was labour shortage and the British Nationality Act of 1948, which gave Commonwealth citizens the right to settle as British subjects. The Empire Windrush docked at Tilbury in June 1948 with about 1,000 passengers, mostly Jamaicans, and recruitment for the NHS, London Transport and the postal service followed, while migration from Punjab and Gujarat grew in the 1950s. Because migrants were concentrated in London, the West Midlands and Yorkshire, and met a colour bar in lodgings and exploitative landlords such as Peter Rachman in Notting Hill, race became a local and then a national question.\n\nViolence turned discrimination into a headline issue. In August and September 1958 white youths attacked West Indian residents in Notting Hill and Nottingham, the worst racial violence in post-war Britain, fuelled by overcrowded housing, competition for jobs and agitation by far-right groups such as Mosley's Union Movement. The murder of the Antiguan carpenter Kelso Cochrane in Notting Hill in 1959 increased pressure for a legal response. Race relations were now plainly a problem that government could not ignore.\n\nPolitics and law then made it a permanent issue. The Commonwealth Immigrants Act of 1962 introduced employment vouchers, and in 1968 a second act was rushed through in days to restrict Kenyan Asians holding British passports. Enoch Powell's Birmingham speech of April 1968 predicted violence from continued immigration; Heath sacked him from the Shadow Cabinet, yet London dockers marched in his support, showing how much feeling existed. The Immigration Act of 1971 introduced patriality, and the National Front, founded in 1967, campaigned against immigration, clashing with opponents at Lewisham in 1977 and Southall in 1979, where Blair Peach died. The Race Relations Acts of 1965, 1968 and 1976 banned discrimination in public places, then in housing and employment, and then indirect discrimination, but they were hard to enforce, which kept the issue alive.\n\nThe 1981 riots showed that disadvantage had not been removed. Heavy use of the sus law against young black men and Operation Swamp 81, in which nearly a thousand people were stopped in a week, preceded the Brixton riots of April 1981, and Toxteth and Moss Side followed in July, with CS gas used on the British mainland for the first time. The Scarman Report blamed racial disadvantage, unemployment and poor policing, and led to the Police and Criminal Evidence Act of 1984. Later the Rushdie affair of 1988-89, with copies of The Satanic Verses burned in Bradford, showed new tensions over religion and identity in a multicultural Britain.\n\nIn France the issue arose from the legacy of the Algerian war and the arrival of North and West African workers needed for reconstruction. Many lived in bidonvilles such as Nanterre, and on 17 October 1961 Paris police under Maurice Papon attacked an Algerian demonstration, killing dozens or more. After the oil shock of 1973 and the suspension of labour immigration in 1974, the children of migrants, the beurs, grew up in the banlieues facing high unemployment, poor housing and hostile policing. In the summer of 1981 car burnings and clashes with police at Les Minguettes near Lyon brought this to national television.\n\nThe 1980s made race a central political question in France. The Marche des Beurs began from Marseille in October 1983 and reached Paris in December with tens of thousands, and SOS Racisme, founded in 1984 with the slogan Touche pas à mon pote, mobilised young people. Against this, Le Pen's Front National broke through at Dreux in 1983, won about 11 per cent in the 1984 European elections, 35 seats in 1986 and over 14 per cent for Le Pen in the 1988 presidential election. Chirac's government under Pasqua tightened immigration law in 1986, and in 1989 the exclusion of three Muslim girls for wearing headscarves at Creil set laïcité against Muslim identity.\n\nOverall, race relations became a major issue because migration collided with housing, jobs, policing and national identity at a time of economic decline, and because far-right movements and riots forced governments to respond. Britain addressed it through anti-discrimination law and a multicultural approach, France through assimilation and universal citizenship, but neither ended disadvantage, and by 1990 race was central to politics in both countries.",
