    "label": "4",
    "marks": 20,
    "model": "No, the forecast was not accurate, because the GAA survived a crisis that had nearly destroyed it by 1891. The American tour of 1888 and the Parnell split had left membership and the number of clubs collapsed. However, the parish-based club system allowed local clubs to carry on even when the Central Council quarrelled. The patronage of Archbishop Croke and the clergy gave the association moral authority, and priests helped restore order in the clubs. The GAA recovered slowly in the 1890s, and by 1908 it had bought grounds at Jones's Road in Dublin, later Croke Park.",
