    "label": "B2",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "John Charles McQuaid was Archbishop of Dublin from 1940 to 1972 and the most influential churchman in the state in the 1950s. He advised the government in the Mother and Child crisis, and the bishops said the scheme was contrary to Catholic social teaching. He objected to the principle that the state, rather than parents and the church, should decide how mothers and children were cared for. He attended Vatican II but implemented its reforms slowly in Dublin and was wary of ecumenism.",
    "model_source": "haiku",
