    "label": "C4",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Napoleon III seized power in a coup on 2 December 1851, and a plebiscite approved it by a large majority. The 1850s brought rapid economic growth, helped by the Crédit Mobilier, which financed railways, industry and urban development. Paris was rebuilt under Haussmann with new boulevards, parks and sewers. The Cobden-Chevalier Treaty of 1860 with Britain reduced French tariffs and encouraged French industry to modernise. France helped Piedmont defeat Austria in 1859, and in 1860 it gained Nice and Savoy in return. His plan for a French-backed empire in Mexico collapsed, and French troops withdrew by 1867. Prussia's victory in 1866 weakened his prestige, and after he was captured at Sedan on 2 September 1870 the Second Empire fell two days later.",
