    "marks": 30,
    "model": "The Soviet Union's Obninsk reactor began supplying electricity to a public grid in June 1954, the first nuclear station to be connected to one. Calder Hall in Cumbria opened in October 1956 and was the world's first nuclear power station to supply electricity on a commercial scale. After the Messmer Plan of 1974 France built many reactors, and by the 1980s nuclear power provided most of its electricity. The Chernobyl explosion of 26 April 1986 released radioactive material across Europe, and Italy voted to end nuclear power in 1987.",
