    "label": "C2",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Jean Monnet was a French planner who, as head of the French planning commission from 1946, produced the Monnet Plan for modernising French industry. He drafted the Schuman Declaration of 9 May 1950, which proposed pooling French and West German coal and steel production under a joint authority. This proposal led to the Treaty of Paris of April 1951, which created the European Coal and Steel Community with supranational powers. Monnet was the first president of the High Authority of the Coal and Steel Community from 1952 to 1955, and the High Authority could set prices and quotas and impose fines on firms. In 1955 he founded the Action Committee for the United States of Europe to press for further integration. Historians see him as a supranational enthusiast who designed a method of gradual integration that shaped the later EEC.",
