    "label": "C3",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Sacrosanctum Concilium of 1963 allowed local languages in the Mass, and the Roman Missal of 1969 to 1970 completed the change so that the priest faced the congregation. Lay Catholics were encouraged to read the Scriptures and serve as ministers, and parish councils gave them a larger role. Lumen Gentium described the Church as the people of God and gave bishops a collective role alongside the pope. Nostra Aetate rejected the charge of collective Jewish guilt for the death of Christ, which improved Catholic–Jewish dialogue. Unitatis Redintegratio committed the Church to ecumenism, and in December 1965 the mutual excommunications of 1054 were lifted. Archbishop Lefebvre founded the Society of St Pius X in 1970 to reject the reforms, while many Catholics in Western Europe attended Mass less often after the 1960s.",
