    "label": "4",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "The Gaelic Athletic Association, founded at Thurles on 1 November 1884, faced serious difficulties in its first years, including internal quarrels, infiltration by the IRB, the unease of the Church, debt and the Parnell split. It dealt with them partly through its sound local organisation and the backing of powerful patrons, and partly by changing its leaders, so that by 1891 it was weakened but had survived.\n\nOne early difficulty was the lack of a national structure and of rules, since hurling had declined and survived under varying local rules. The GAA dealt with this by drawing up a national code. Maurice Davin, the first president, drew up rules for hurling and football in 1885, replacing the old local variations. The GAA also opened competition to all classes, rejecting the amateur rules that excluded labourers and artisans, and organised itself on the parish, with county committees, a Central Council and an annual convention. This parish structure was the Association's greatest source of strength, since local clubs could carry on even when the leaders quarrelled. In 1887 it held the first All-Ireland championships, and the first hurling final was played at Birr in April 1888, when Thurles beat Meelick, which gave the clubs a national competition.\n\nA second difficulty was the need for legitimacy and protection, since the Association was new and the authorities were suspicious. Michael Cusack won the patronage of Archbishop Croke of Cashel, whose letter of December 1884 attacked the imitation of English games and was widely printed, together with Parnell and Davitt. Croke's name gave the GAA moral authority among farmers, and many priests became club presidents and organisers. The Association also set up the ban on foreign games and on members of the RIC and the British forces, which defined it as a body committed to Irish games and made it a symbol of resistance to anglicisation.\n\nThe most dangerous difficulty was political. Members of the IRB joined in large numbers and used clubs and county boards to gain influence, and Dublin Castle reports described the GAA as a front for Fenianism. Cusack, who disliked the secret society, quarrelled with Davin and other officers and was removed as secretary in 1886. The conventions of 1886 and 1887 at Thurles were contests between moderates and IRB delegates for control of the Central Council. As the IRB's hold grew, Archbishop Croke and other bishops became uneasy and clergy withdrew from clubs in places. Davin stepped down as president in 1889. These disputes were settled by changing leaders rather than by breaking up the Association, which helped it to hold together, though the Church and the IRB remained at odds.\n\nFinancial and political blows then came together. In the autumn of 1888 about fifty hurlers and athletes toured the United States in the American Invasion, which lost money, left debts and left the 1888 championships unfinished. Then the O'Shea divorce and the split in the Irish Party in December 1890 divided members, with the IRB generally favouring Parnell and Church-minded members opposing him, and Parnell's death in October 1891 left the Association demoralised. By 1891 membership and the number of clubs had collapsed, and in many areas only a few county boards remained.\n\nOverall, the GAA coped with its difficulties only in part. Its parish clubs, the appeal of hurling and football, the patronage of Croke and the clergy and the willingness to change leaders kept it alive, but the IRB infiltration, the quarrel with the Church, the American tour and the Parnell split almost destroyed it. It survived mainly because local clubs and local rivalry did not depend on politics, and it recovered slowly in the 1890s alongside the Gaelic revival.",
