    "label": "2",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Government responses to the Famine between 1845 and 1849 were, on the whole, inadequate. Peel's measures in the first season were reasonably effective, but the Whig government that followed was bound by laissez-faire and by the belief that Irish property should pay for Irish poverty, and its policies were too limited and too slow for a crisis on this scale. About one million people died and over a million emigrated, and a richer United Kingdom could have done far more.\n\nPeel's response, from November 1845 to June 1846, was the most successful of the Famine. His Conservative government secretly bought about 100,000 pounds' worth of Indian corn in the United States through Baring Brothers and sold it at cost through government depots to keep prices down. A Relief Commission, later chaired by Sir Randolph Routh, coordinated hundreds of local relief committees, which raised subscriptions and bought food, and the Board of Works ran public works such as road building to give the poor wages. The maize was hard to grind and caused stomach pain if badly cooked, earning the nickname Peel's brimstone, but mortality in the first season was low. Peel also repealed the Corn Laws in June 1846, though this did little for Ireland immediately, and his party split and he resigned. His measures were adequate for the partial failure of 1845, but they were not tested by the total failure that followed.\n\nThe Whig government of Lord John Russell, which took office at the end of June 1846, was committed to laissez-faire, the belief that government should not interfere with trade or the food market. It refused to import food or control prices in most of Ireland, leaving supply to private merchants, and its depots opened only in the west and sold at market prices. Charles Trevelyan, the senior Treasury official who ran relief, saw the Famine as an act of Providence that would force change in the Irish land system, and he feared that generous relief would make the Irish dependent. Grain, cattle and butter continued to leave Irish ports while people starved, which fed nationalist bitterness, although from 1847 imports of maize were several times greater than exports. This ideology was the greatest single cause of inadequacy.\n\nThe public works were the central Whig measure and they were badly flawed. The Labouchere Act of August 1846 relaunched the works with the whole cost to be repaid from local taxes, and wages were set below local rates so as not to compete with private employers. Numbers rose to about 700,000 by March 1847, but payment was by task work, often late, and weakened, poorly clad people worked through the hard winter of 1846 to 1847. Wages could not keep up with food prices, which more than doubled, and the works produced many roads that led nowhere. Faced with rising deaths and costs, the government closed the works from March 1847.\n\nThe most effective relief came late and did not last. The Soup Kitchen Act of February 1847 set up kitchens run by local committees giving free rations, and by July about three million people a day received food, the cheapest and most effective relief of the whole Famine. But Trevelyan closed the kitchens by September 1847 and treated the crisis as over, even though hunger continued, since the small crop of 1847, though healthy, came from so few seed potatoes planted. This showed that direct feeding could work, which makes the earlier delay and the decision to end it all the more damning.\n\nFrom then on relief was thrown on the Poor Law. The Poor Law Extension Act of June 1847 made the poor law responsible for all further relief, so that Irish property would pay for Irish poverty. Its Gregory clause refused relief to anyone holding more than a quarter of an acre, forcing smallholders to surrender their land to qualify, and landlords used it, with rising poor rates, to clear estates, so that evictions rose sharply from 1847. Many western unions went bankrupt because their ratepayers were ruined, and workhouses, holding far more people than they were built for, became centres of fever. The Rate-in-Aid Act of 1849 imposed a national rate of sixpence in the pound to support about twenty-two bankrupt unions, which Ulster guardians resented, and the government treated the Famine as over by 1850 though high mortality continued in the west.\n\nOn balance, government responses were inadequate. Peel's maize, committees and works kept deaths low in 1845 to 1846, and the soup kitchens showed what was possible, but the Whigs' faith in laissez-faire, the poorly paid works, the burden placed on the poorest unions and the use of the Gregory clause turned a crisis into a catastrophe. John Mitchel claimed that the Almighty sent the blight but the English created the Famine. Historians such as Cormac O Grada and Christine Kinealy reject the charge of deliberate genocide but agree that the scale of death was not inevitable and that ideology, parsimony and indifference made it worse.",
