    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The coming of the railways transformed Irish society and economy between the 1830s and 1860s, but the change was uneven. They cut the cost and time of moving goods and people, tied farmers to British markets, reshaped towns and spread news and ideas. At the same time they helped to commercialise agriculture, left the south with little industry and gave the north-east a decisive advantage.\n\nThe first and most obvious impact was on transport itself. The Dublin and Kingstown Railway opened in December 1834, running about six miles from Westland Row to Kingstown, and it was the first line in Ireland. Growth was slow at first, with only about 65 miles open in 1845, but the network then expanded rapidly to nearly 1,000 miles by 1855 and was largely complete by about 1860. The Great Southern and Western reached Cork in 1849, the Midland Great Western reached Galway in 1851 and Belfast was linked to Dublin in the 1850s. Journeys that had taken days by road or canal now took hours, so the railways broke down the isolation of the provinces and made a national market possible for the first time.\n\nThe economic effect on agriculture was profound. Railways cut the cost of carrying cattle, butter, grain and fish, and together with the steamships they tied Irish farmers directly to the growing British towns. After the Famine, cattle were sent live to Britain by rail and boat, and this encouraged the shift from tillage to pasture and the rise of the stronger farmer with a large livestock holding. In this way the railways reinforced the commercialisation of Irish farming, which benefited the larger farmers while the labourers and small farmers of the west gained little and often emigrated.\n\nThe railways also changed the pattern of towns and trade. Fairs and markets moved to railway towns, and junctions such as Mallow, Limerick Junction and Athlone grew in importance. Some small ports and inland towns that were bypassed lost trade as goods moved by rail instead. Construction gave employment to thousands, and William Dargan, the leading contractor who built the Dublin and Kingstown line and many others, became a symbol of Irish enterprise. In 1853 he financed the Dublin Industrial Exhibition, which drew over a million visitors. Yet his work depended on railway investment rather than on a wider Irish industrial base, which shows the limits of the change.\n\nSociety and culture were affected as much as the economy. Newspapers, mail and ideas travelled faster, so national papers reached the provinces and political movements could organise on a national scale. Tourism grew, and the line to Killarney opened in 1853. The rail and steamer link to Britain also eased travel and emigration, making it simpler for people to reach Cork, Queenstown or Liverpool and leave Ireland in the years after the Famine. Ireland adopted its own broad gauge of 5 feet 3 inches, which set it apart from Britain, yet the railways in practice drew the country closer to the British economy.\n\nThe railways also helped to widen the gap between north and south. Belfast had no coal or iron of its own, but cheap imports by rail and sea made industry viable there, and linen, engineering and shipbuilding grew. The city's population rose from about 100,000 in 1851 to over 170,000 by 1871, and Harland and Wolff was formed in 1861. In the south, by contrast, local manufacturers faced competition from British factory goods carried cheaply by rail, and only brewing, distilling and food processing survived in Dublin and Cork. The same line that opened a market for Irish cattle therefore also opened Irish towns to British manufactured goods.\n\nOverall, the railways had a major impact because they integrated Ireland into one national and British market, speeding trade, news and travel and changing the shape of towns. Their benefits were uneven: they favoured the stronger farmer and industrial Belfast, hurt small ports and southern industry, and eased the emigration that drained the countryside. They modernised Irish life without creating a balanced economy.",
