    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Cardinal Paul Cullen was the most influential Catholic churchman of the mid-nineteenth century, and he shaped both Irish political attitudes and religious practice. Religiously, he was the architect of the Romanisation of the Irish Church, bringing discipline, uniformity and close loyalty to the pope. Politically, he made the Church distrustful of revolutionary and agitating movements and inclined it to co-operate with the Liberals.\n\nCullen was born in County Kildare in 1803, studied in Rome and became rector of the Irish College there in 1832. His experience of Rome made him an ultramontane, a believer in strong papal authority over national churches. In 1849 he was made Archbishop of Armagh and in 1850 Apostolic Delegate, the pope's special representative, and he presided at the Synod of Thurles in 1850. He became Archbishop of Dublin in 1852 and the first Irish cardinal in 1866, and by his death in 1878 he was the dominant voice of Irish Catholicism.\n\nThe Synod of Thurles was the centre of his religious influence. It was the first national synod of the Irish Church for about two centuries, and it ended the old situation in which each bishop ran his diocese with little central direction. The decrees laid down uniform liturgy and discipline, with the Roman rite followed, regulated clerical dress and behaviour and strengthened the bishops' authority over priests. Cullen also promoted church building, religious orders and parish missions, and encouraged devotions such as the rosary, novenas and devotion to the Sacred Heart and the Virgin Mary. Historians such as Emmet Larkin link this work to the devotional revolution, in which regular attendance at Mass became the norm among Catholics by the 1870s.\n\nEducation was another area in which Cullen shaped religious life. He opposed the non-denominational Queen's Colleges, which Rome had condemned in 1847, and the Synod forbade Catholics to attend them. He backed the Catholic University, which opened in Dublin in 1854 under John Henry Newman. It had no state-recognised degrees and few students, and Newman resigned in 1858, so this policy had limited success, but it showed Cullen's determination that Catholics should be educated under Church control. He also pressed for greater Catholic control of the national schools.\n\nIn politics Cullen's influence was conservative and cautious. He distrusted Young Ireland, the Tenant League and the involvement of priests in politics, and the Synod discouraged priests from political agitation at the altar and at election meetings. He opposed the Fenians, and in 1861 he refused to let the body of Terence Bellew MacManus lie in the Pro-Cathedral in Dublin. He preferred to work with the Liberals and supported Gladstone's disestablishment of the Church of Ireland. Because the Church was the main institution of the Catholic majority, his stand helped to turn Catholic opinion away from revolutionary nationalism for a generation.\n\nCullen's influence was not unchallenged. Archbishop John MacHale of Tuam, who had opposed the Queen's Colleges but resented Cullen's authority, and many nationalists and liberal Catholics resisted his control, while Protestants saw an alien and authoritarian power at work. Historians also debate whether Cullen shaped Catholic attitudes or reflected what the Church and the post-Famine farming class already wanted, since the Famine had removed the poorest, least observant Catholics and left a more prosperous and disciplined community.\n\nOverall, Cullen's influence on religious practice was deep and lasting, since he centralised the Church, introduced Roman devotions and discipline and raised the standing of the clergy. His political influence was real but more negative, restraining revolutionary movements and priests in politics. Together these made the Catholic Church the most powerful force in Irish public life.",
