    "label": "1",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Patrick Pearse had two great aims, to restore a Gaelic Irish identity and to win an independent Irish republic by force, and his achievements were greatest in the second, though in ways he did not live to see. As an educator and Gaelic Leaguer he had real if limited success, and as a revolutionary he failed militarily in 1916 but did more than anyone to turn opinion towards separatism.\n\nPearse's first aim was cultural. Born in Dublin in 1879 to an English father and an Irish mother, he joined the Gaelic League in 1896 and edited its paper An Claidheamh Soluis from 1903. He believed that Ireland could not be free in spirit without its own language and traditions, and he worked to make Irish a living language. His educational aim was linked to this. He founded St Enda's, a bilingual school, in 1908 to educate boys in Irish culture and a spirit of service, and in his essay The Murder Machine he attacked the state education system as one that crushed individuality and Irish identity.\n\nHis second aim was political, and it moved from cultural nationalism to armed rebellion. He joined the IRB in 1913 and became Director of Military Organisation of the Irish Volunteers, which had been founded in November 1913 in reply to the Ulster Volunteers. When Redmond urged the Volunteers to fight in the British army in September 1914, Pearse stayed with the minority under MacNeill that kept the separatist tradition alive. In 1915 he joined the Military Council, which planned a rising to take advantage of Britain's war. He came to believe that bloodshed could renew the nation, and his 1915 writings praised war as cleansing, which later critics called a cult of blood sacrifice.\n\nPearse's achievements in preparing the Rising were considerable. He used the funeral of the Fenian O'Donovan Rossa on 1 August 1915 as a show of strength, and in his graveside oration he declared that Ireland unfree would never be at peace. It was a speech that is still remembered as his best-known statement. When the Rising began on Easter Monday, 24 April 1916, he was President of the Provisional Government and Commandant-General, and he read the Proclamation of the Irish Republic outside the GPO. The Proclamation addressed Irishmen and Irishwomen, guaranteed religious and civil liberty and equal rights, and gave the republican cause a statement of principle.\n\nMilitarily, however, the Rising failed. The Aud was lost with its 20,000 German rifles, Casement was arrested, MacNeill's countermand reduced the numbers, and only about 1,200 took part. The rebels held no major target outside their garrisons, and the GPO had to be evacuated after fire. Pearse surrendered to Brigadier-General Lowe on 29 April to prevent more civilian deaths, since about 485 people had been killed.\n\nHis real achievement came through his death. Initially the Rising was unpopular, and some Dubliners jeered the prisoners. But Pearse, Tom Clarke and Thomas MacDonagh were shot at Kilmainham on 3 May, and 15 men were executed in all, which, with mass arrests, turned opinion towards the rebels. Bishop O'Dwyer of Limerick criticised the executions. By autumn Pearse was honoured as a martyr, and the Frongoch prisoners who returned became the core of the new Sinn Féin, which swept the 1918 election. Yeats's line that a terrible beauty was born summed up the change.\n\nOverall, Pearse's aims were to revive Gaelic culture and to win a republic, and his achievements lay in inspiring a generation, issuing the Proclamation and giving the republican movement its martyrs. Historians still differ, with Conor Cruise O'Brien criticising the cult of sacrifice and others stressing his work as an educator, but there is no doubt about his lasting influence on Irish separatism.",
