    "label": "2",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Anglo-Irish relations between 1923 and 1949 moved from the cooperative but frustrating relationship of the Cosgrave years, through the bitter conflict of de Valera's first years in office, to a gradual settlement and then the final break of 1949. Each stage involved Ireland widening its independence, and Britain for the most part accepting the changes, so that by 1949 the Treaty settlement had been dismantled.\n\nUnder Cosgrave the Free State accepted the Treaty but used the Commonwealth to widen its freedom. The Boundary Commission crisis of November 1925, after its expected findings were leaked to the Morning Post, ended with the Tripartite Agreement of 3 December 1925, which left the border unchanged but released the Free State from its share of the imperial debt. At the Imperial Conference of 1926 Kevin O'Higgins was a leading advocate of the Balfour Declaration, which described Britain and the dominions as equal in status and freely associated. The Statute of Westminster of 1931 then gave the dominions legislative independence. Cosgrave, though, kept the oath, the Governor-General and the Treaty ports, and held that independence should come by agreement with Britain.\n\nThe relationship changed sharply when de Valera became President of the Executive Council on 9 March 1932. Using the Statute of Westminster and his Dáil majority, he set out to remove the symbols of the Treaty. The Constitution (Removal of Oath) Act of 1933 ended the oath for members of the Oireachtas, and the Seanad was abolished in 1936. The abdication of Edward VIII in December 1936 gave him his opportunity, and on 11 December the Constitution (Amendment No. 27) Act removed all references to the Crown and the Governor-General. The Executive Authority (External Relations) Act the next day gave the King a role only in the appointment of diplomats and signing of treaties. Britain accepted these changes without military or economic action.\n\nHowever, de Valera's decision to stop paying the land annuities in 1932 led to the Economic War. The annuities, about £3 million a year, were repayments by tenant farmers under the Land Acts, and de Valera argued they were owed to the Irish government and not to Britain. Britain imposed special duties on Irish cattle, beef, butter and other exports from July 1932, and Ireland replied with duties on British goods such as coal, cement and iron and steel. The farming economy suffered badly and the government turned to self-sufficiency. The Coal-Cattle Pact of 1935 reduced the damage slightly, but the dispute lasted until 1938.\n\nThe tension was resolved by the Anglo-Irish Agreements signed in London on 25 April 1938. Under the defence agreement, the Treaty ports at Berehaven, Cobh and Lough Swilly were returned to Ireland, which removed the Treaty's last military restriction. The financial agreement ended the annuities dispute for a single payment of £10 million, far less than Britain had claimed, and the trade duties were ended. Chamberlain's government was anxious to improve relations as war approached, and Churchill opposed the return of the ports. The agreements were a major success for de Valera, although they did nothing about partition.\n\nThe handover of the ports made it possible for Ireland to remain neutral in the Second World War. Neutrality, adopted on 3 September 1939, was seen as proof of sovereignty, since a state that followed Britain into war would look like a dominion in practice. It caused friction, as Churchill complained about the loss of the bases in the battle of the Atlantic, and Britain restricted supplies of coal, wheat and shipping. The American Minister David Gray also criticised Ireland. Yet in practice neutrality leaned towards the Allies, and in his victory broadcast of 13 May 1945 Churchill attacked it and referred to the ports.\n\nThe final stage came after the election of February 1948, when John A. Costello became Taoiseach of an inter-party government. Seán MacBride regarded the External Relations Act as an anomaly, and Costello announced its repeal while on a visit to Canada in September 1948. The Republic of Ireland Act, passed in December 1948, came into force on Easter Monday, 18 April 1949, ending the link with the Commonwealth. Britain's Ireland Act of 1949 accepted this, but it said that Irish citizens would not be treated as foreigners and guaranteed Northern Ireland's position.\n\nIn conclusion, Anglo-Irish relations developed from dominion status to full republican independence in under three decades. Cosgrave laid the groundwork at the Imperial Conferences, de Valera removed the Treaty's symbols and secured the ports, and the inter-party government completed the process, though partition remained unresolved.",
