    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Between 1920 and 1949 cultural identity was promoted in both parts of Ireland, but in opposite directions. In the South the state built a Gaelic and Catholic identity through the Irish language, the schools, the Church and national institutions. In the North the Unionist government and the unionist community promoted a British and Protestant identity, while nationalists defended a separate Irish Catholic one.\n\nThe Free State made the revival of Irish the centre of its cultural policy. Irish was made compulsory in national schools in 1922, and many infant classes were taught wholly through it. A pass in Irish became necessary for State examinations and for entry to the civil service and the Garda, so the language was tied to jobs, and Ernest Blythe pressed civil servants to learn it. The Gaeltacht Commission of 1925-26 defined the Irish-speaking districts, An Gúm was founded in 1926 to publish books in Irish, Taibhdhearc na Gaillimhe opened in 1928, and the 1937 constitution declared Irish the first official language. The policy had mixed results. The share of people able to speak Irish rose from about 18 per cent in 1926 to about a quarter in 1936, but this came mostly from schooling, few used it daily, and the Gaeltacht kept shrinking through emigration. Irish worked better as a symbol of identity than as a spoken language.\n\nThe Catholic Church was the second pillar of the identity promoted in the South. Clergy managed most national schools, secondary education was largely in the hands of religious orders, and the state accepted this. Legislation reflected Catholic teaching: film censorship in 1923, the end of private divorce bills in 1925, the Censorship of Publications Act of 1929, the ban on contraceptives in 1935, and the 1937 constitution, which banned divorce and recognised the special position of the Catholic Church. Writers such as Samuel Beckett, Kate O'Brien and Seán Ó Faoláin were banned or criticised. This promoted a Gaelic and Catholic Ireland, but it left the Protestant minority and many urban people feeling excluded. Yeats's defence of that minority in the Seanad over divorce shows the unease. The Eucharistic Congress of 1932 showed the Church's public influence at its height. It drew a crowd at the Phoenix Park Mass often put at around a million, and de Valera's government gave it full support.\n\nThe state also promoted culture through institutions and games. The Tailteann Games were held in 1924, 1928 and 1932, the Abbey Theatre received a state subsidy from 1925, and 2RN began broadcasting in January 1926, with Athlone strengthening the service in 1932. The Irish Folklore Commission was set up in 1935, and the Schools' Collection of 1937-38 had children gather local folklore. The GAA played a major role in national identity, and its Rule 27 banned members from foreign games. In 1938 it removed Douglas Hyde as patron after he attended a soccer match. Artists such as Evie Hone, who won first prize for her stained glass window My Four Green Fields at the 1939 New York World's Fair, helped give independent Ireland a modern cultural face.\n\nIn Northern Ireland identity was promoted along unionist lines, and education showed the tension. The Londonderry Education Act of 1923 aimed at non-denominational schools under public control, but Protestant churches objected to the lack of religious instruction and the Act was amended in 1925. The Catholic Church refused to transfer its schools and stayed outside the system with only partial funding. The result was a divided school system that kept the communities apart. Orange parades on 12 July and loyalist bands enjoyed official protection, the Union Jack and royal ceremonies stressed the British link, and nationalist parades and republican emblems were more likely to be banned under the Special Powers Act. Nationalists kept their identity alive through Catholic schools, the GAA and Irish classes, though the Irish language had little state support. Craig's claim in 1934 that Northern Ireland was a Protestant state matched the Catholic character of the 1937 constitution in the South.\n\nOverall, cultural identity was actively promoted on both sides of the border, by the state in the South and by the dominant community in the North. In the South the policy gave Ireland a distinctive identity and preserved much culture, but it was narrow and conformist and the language did not revive. In the North it deepened the divide. Each state stressed one tradition at the expense of the other, which reinforced partition.",
