    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Belfast in 1945 was very different from Belfast in 1939 economically and physically, but in its politics and its religious divisions it was much the same. The war ended mass unemployment, brought new links with Britain and the United States, and destroyed much of the city's housing. It did not break unionist control or the separation of Catholic and Protestant communities.\n\nEconomically the change was dramatic. In 1939 Belfast was a city with high unemployment, as it had been since the 1920s. War contracts changed this. Harland and Wolff built warships and merchant ships and repaired thousands of vessels, Short Brothers built Stirling bombers, engineering firms made munitions and aircraft parts, and the linen mills and rope works produced parachutes, tents and uniforms. Unemployment fell sharply, wages rose, and women entered the factories in large numbers. By 1945 the city's industries were running at full capacity, though they depended on wartime orders. Farmers had also been ordered to plough more land, making Northern Ireland an important food supplier to Britain.\n\nPhysically, the city had been scarred by the Blitz. In 1939 the government assumed Belfast was too far from German airfields to be attacked, and the city had few public shelters, only a handful of heavy anti-aircraft guns and a small, poorly equipped fire service. On the night of 15-16 April 1941, Easter Tuesday, about 180 German bombers attacked, killing about 900 people, mostly in the working-class districts of north and east Belfast. A raid on 4-5 May hit Harland and Wolff, Short Brothers and the docks. In all about 1,000 people died, more than half the city's houses were damaged, and tens of thousands were left homeless. Up to 100,000 people left each night to sleep in the countryside, and fire brigades from Dublin, Dún Laoghaire, Dundalk and Drogheda crossed the border to help. Housing remained desperately short in 1945, and many families lived in temporary housing.\n\nSocially and politically there were some changes. The Blitz and the evacuation of children exposed the poverty and poor health in the slums, as similar evacuation had in Britain, and the Unionist government was criticised for its slow response. John Miller Andrews resigned in April 1943 and Basil Brooke replaced him. In February 1943 Jack Beattie won a Belfast West by-election at Westminster, and Labour made gains in 1945. From 1942 Belfast also became a base for Allied troops, and from 26 January 1942 American soldiers arrived in large numbers, bringing money, goods and popular culture. Wartime co-operation and the promise of a British welfare state tied Northern Ireland closer to Britain, and post-war reforms such as the Education Act of 1947 and the Health Services Act of 1948 followed.\n\nIn other respects Belfast had not changed. The Unionist Party still held power at Stormont, and the government made no changes to the electoral system or policing. Catholics and Protestants still lived largely apart, and the war had if anything strengthened the unionist case against the neutral South. The IRA's activity in the city, including the hanging of Tom Williams in 1942, deepened nationalist resentment.\n\nOn balance, the Belfast of 1945 was greatly different in its economy, its housing and its relationship with Britain, so the war was a real turning point there. But it was not a turning point in politics or in community relations, and the underlying divisions of 1939 survived intact.",
