    "model": "The main trends in Irish emigration between 1840 and 1920 were its enormous scale, its transformation from a flow of relatively well-off Protestants into a mass movement of the Catholic poor, and its settling into a permanent feature of Irish life after the Famine. Emigrants increasingly headed for the United States, and the experience of the journey and of settlement changed greatly over the period.\n\nBefore 1845 emigration was already established. Ulster Presbyterians had been the first large group to cross to America, and about a million people left between 1815 and 1845. Pre-Famine emigrants were often Protestant small farmers, weavers and tradesmen who could pay their own fares, though from the 1830s poorer Catholics from the south and west made up a growing share. British North America was the main transatlantic destination because fares were cheapest, while Britain took the poorest, since the crossing to Liverpool or Glasgow cost only a few shillings. Seasonal migrants from Mayo, Donegal and Galway also crossed each summer to harvest work and returned with cash for the rent.\n\nThe Famine turned a steady flow into a mass exodus. The potato crop failed almost completely in 1846, and about a million people died by 1851. Between 1.5 and 2 million emigrated between 1845 and 1855, the great majority to the United States and the rest to Britain and British North America, and the population fell from about 8.2 million in 1841 to about 6.5 million in 1851. These emigrants were mostly Catholic, poor, unskilled and often Irish-speaking, from the west and south-west, and travelled in families. About 300,000 destitute Irish landed at Liverpool alone in 1847. Some left under landlord-assisted schemes, like that of Major Denis Mahon of Strokestown, who sent 1,490 tenants to Quebec in 1847, but most were financed by pre-paid tickets sent by relatives abroad, which began the pattern of chain emigration.\n\nThe Famine journey showed the human cost of this flight. Passengers were crammed into the steerage of sailing ships for six weeks or more, and typhus killed so many that they were called coffin ships. Of about 100,000 who sailed for British North America in 1847, an estimated 17,000 or more died at sea, in quarantine or soon after. At Grosse Isle, the quarantine station below Quebec, about 40 ships with some 12,500 passengers lay queued by 31 May 1847, and more than 5,000 people were buried on the island that year. The US Passenger Acts of 1847 raised fares to America and diverted many of the poorest to Canada.\n\nAfter the Famine emigration became settled and routine. About 4 million left in the six decades after 1855, and the population fell from 6.5 million in 1851 to about 4.4 million in 1911. The United States took about four out of five, and the number of Irish-born there peaked at about 1.87 million in 1890. Emigrants were mostly young and single, aged 15 to 30, and unusually women left in numbers equal to men. The cause lay in rural society, since after the Famine one son inherited the farm and one daughter got a dowry, so the rest emigrated or stayed single, while late marriage, a shift to cattle and lack of industry outside the north-east pushed young people out. The agricultural depression of 1879 to 1882 caused a fresh surge from the west.\n\nThe way of leaving and of settling also changed. Steamships replaced sail from the 1860s, cutting the crossing from about six weeks to about two, and liners picked up emigrants at Queenstown and Moville. Emigrants landed at Castle Garden from 1855 and Ellis Island from 1892, where Annie Moore from Cork was the first processed. The American wake marked the farewell, since most would never return. Abroad the Irish settled in cities such as New York, Boston and Chicago, men as labourers on docks, railways and in mines, women in domestic service, and many rose into skilled trades, police forces, city politics and the professions. Remittances of well over a million pounds a year paid for passages, rent and dowries, and chain emigration meant kin found each other work and lodgings in the same parishes.\n\nOverall, the central trends were the Famine's mass flight of the poor, the shift towards the United States, and the post-Famine emigration of the young, driven by the structure of rural society and sustained by remittances and family networks. Emigration relieved poverty but drained Ireland of its young people, and by 1920 it had become an accepted part of Irish life.",
