    "label": "2",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "De Valera's mission to America from June 1919 to December 1920 was a partial success but ultimately a failure in terms of its central aim. It raised publicity and about five million dollars for the Dáil government, but it never won American recognition of the Irish Republic and it left Irish America divided.\n\nDe Valera went to America as head of the Dáil government after escaping from Lincoln Gaol in February 1919, with the aims of winning recognition of the Republic, raising funds, influencing the 1920 presidential election and pressuring Britain. The context seemed favourable. Sinn Féin had won 73 of the 105 Irish seats in December 1918 and the Dáil had declared a Republic in January 1919, but President Wilson had not pressed Britain at Paris and the Irish delegates were not heard. America was the obvious place to turn, with its large Irish population and the principle of self-determination associated with Wilson.\n\nThe tour was a success in terms of publicity. De Valera arrived secretly in June 1919, having crossed as a stowaway from Liverpool, and Harry Boland prepared the way. He toured cities with large Irish populations, and addressed a huge rally at Fenway Park in Boston on 29 June 1919. Crowds, receptions and parades followed in cities such as Chicago and New York. A few days before his arrival the US Senate had passed a resolution asking the Peace Conference to hear Irish representatives, which showed sympathy, but it committed the government to nothing. His appearances raised the profile of the Republic in the American press and showed the strength of Irish-American feeling, although the American government and much of the press stayed cautious.\n\nThe bond drive was the most tangible success. The Dáil authorised a loan, and bond certificates in the Irish Republic were sold from January 1920 to Irish Americans, with the aim of raising five million dollars, a target that was roughly reached by 1921. The drive used Irish-American parishes and clubs and gave the Dáil government a financial base alongside Michael Collins's National Loan at home. This money helped to pay for the war and the Dáil's administration, and historians such as Francis Carroll stress its importance. Yet there were disputes about who controlled the money and how it should be spent.\n\nThe failures were more serious. Most damagingly, de Valera quarrelled with John Devoy and Judge Daniel Cohalan of the Friends of Irish Freedom over control of funds and of the American movement. Their dispute was fought in the press, with Boland backing de Valera, and it split Irish America just when unity was needed. His Westminster Gazette interview of February 1920, suggesting Ireland might be treated as the United States treated Cuba, upset republicans and gave Cohalan and Devoy a weapon against him. In June 1920 he sought a plank recognising the Republic from both parties at their conventions. The Republicans ignored him and the Democrats offered only a resolution of sympathy with Irish aspirations for self-government. When Harding won in November 1920 his administration did not recognise the Republic.\n\nThere were some compensations. The American Association for the Recognition of the Irish Republic was founded in November 1920 to replace the Friends of Irish Freedom for his supporters, and it grew rapidly and claimed several hundred thousand members. American sympathy grew because of events in Ireland, particularly the Black and Tan reprisals, the death on hunger strike of Terence MacSwiney in October 1920, and the American Commission on Conditions in Ireland in December. But these gains owed as much to British actions as to de Valera, and his absence left the Dáil without its president during the war, straining his relations with Collins.\n\nOn balance the mission was a propaganda and fundraising success but a diplomatic failure. The Republic was never recognised by the United States, the movement in America was split, and de Valera's main objective was not achieved. The money and publicity were valuable, but as a judgement on what he set out to do, the mission must be considered a failure.",
