    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Irish participation in politics was extensive in both the United States and Britain between 1850 and 1966, but it took very different forms. In the United States the Irish built powerful city machines and eventually reached the highest national offices, while in Britain they used their votes to back first the nationalist cause and later the Labour Party. In both countries participation gave a poor immigrant community a real voice, though it also brought charges of corruption and clannishness.\n\nIn the United States the Irish turned their numbers into power through the Democratic Party. Most Irish Americans voted Democratic because the party defended immigrants against nativist attacks such as those of the Know-Nothings, who won major state victories in 1854 and wanted longer waiting periods before immigrants could vote and a bar on Catholics holding public office. In the cities, ward politics gave the new arrivals jobs, housing and legal help in return for votes, and the police and fire services became routes into local politics. Participation in the cities was therefore built on group loyalty, and it gave a despised Catholic minority a place in public life.\n\nThe clearest example was Tammany Hall in New York, which controlled the city through patronage, jobs and contracts and was led by Irish bosses from 1872, beginning with John Kelly. Richard Croker, born in Co. Cork in 1843 and brought to New York as a child, led it from the mid-1880s until 1902 and placed his allies in office, such as Mayor Robert Van Wyck. The Lexow Committee of 1894 and the Mazet Committee of 1899 exposed the corruption of the system, and reformers condemned it. Many Irish voters, however, saw Croker as a friend of the poor, so machine politics showed both the extent of Irish power and its limits.\n\nIrish politicians also won the highest local offices. William R. Grace became the first Irish Catholic mayor of New York in 1880 and Hugh O'Brien the first in Boston in 1885, and John F. Fitzgerald and later James Michael Curley dominated Boston politics in the early twentieth century. Al Smith, a New York Catholic of Irish descent, became governor of New York and was the Democratic presidential candidate in 1928. Although he lost, anti-Catholic feeling played a part in his defeat, which shows that prejudice still limited Irish advancement. Individuals such as Paul O'Dwyer, a lawyer from Bohola, Co. Mayo, who campaigned for civil rights and later ran for the US Senate in 1968, show a more liberal strand of participation.\n\nNational office came in the twentieth century. Joseph P. Kennedy, born in Boston in 1888 and the son-in-law of Mayor Fitzgerald, was the first chairman of the Securities and Exchange Commission in 1934 and in 1938 became the first Irish Catholic ambassador to Britain, though his support for appeasement damaged his reputation and he resigned in 1940. His son John F. Kennedy, a Catholic of Irish descent, was elected President in 1960, which marked the final breakthrough of Irish Catholics into the highest level of American politics.\n\nIn Britain Irish participation was easier in law, since Irish-born men were British subjects who could vote and stand for Parliament, but it was channelled into national parties rather than into city machines. The Irish National League of Great Britain, founded in 1883, organised Irish voters behind the Irish Parliamentary Party, and in 1885 Parnell told them to vote against the Liberals, so they voted Conservative. T. P. O'Connor was elected MP for Liverpool Scotland that year and held the seat until 1929, using newspapers such as The Star to promote Home Rule. After Gladstone's first Home Rule Bill in 1886 Irish voters in Liverpool and Glasgow largely turned to the Liberals, and from the early twentieth century they increasingly supported Labour, which also drew on working-class support.\n\nOverall Irish participation was extensive in both countries. It was more dramatic in the United States, where Irish bosses, mayors and finally a president emerged from an immigrant community, while in Britain it was more focused on Irish questions and then on class politics. In both places the Irish were able to use the vote to defend their interests against hostility, though in America the price was sometimes corruption and in Britain a political voice that depended on larger parties.",
