    "label": "1",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The welfare state brought real improvements to education, health and housing in Northern Ireland after 1945, and Catholics as well as Protestants benefited from free secondary schooling and the National Health Service. However, the reforms did not remove religious division in schools or the discrimination in housing allocation, and this limited their impact on relations between the two communities.\n\nThe Education Act (Northern Ireland) 1947 was the most important change. It provided free secondary education for all pupils and set up a system of grammar, technical and secondary intermediate schools, with pupils selected at about eleven by the eleven-plus examination. Children from poorer families, who had previously been shut out by fees, could now reach grammar schools and sit public examinations. The Act also extended state scholarships and grants for university study, so that more students, including many Catholics, reached Queen's University Belfast from the 1950s.\n\nThe Act had a particular impact on the Catholic community. Voluntary schools, which were mostly Catholic, kept their own management and received a state capital grant of 65 per cent towards building costs, so the Church could expand its schools. By the 1960s a new educated Catholic middle class was emerging, and many of its members would not accept second-class status. Some of the leaders of the civil rights movement came from this generation. Education nevertheless stayed segregated by religion, so Protestant and Catholic children grew up apart, with little regular contact with each other.\n\nThe National Health Service began in Northern Ireland on 5 July 1948, the same day as in the rest of the United Kingdom. Free medical care, hospital treatment and later improvements in child health raised living standards for ordinary families. It followed the principle of parity, formalised in the Social Services Agreement of 1949, by which benefits and services matched British levels and Britain met the cost through its subsidy. Because Northern Ireland could not have funded these services from its own revenue, the welfare state was a shared British settlement, and the Catholic minority gained from it as much as anyone.\n\nHousing was more mixed. The Northern Ireland Housing Trust, set up in 1945, built houses and estates in towns and cities and allocated them on a points system that was regarded as fair. But most council houses were allocated by local councils, and many unionist-controlled councils favoured Protestant families. Londonderry Corporation stayed in unionist hands although Catholics formed a majority of the city, because the ward boundaries drawn in 1936 put most Catholic voters into a single ward. In Fermanagh and Tyrone too, unionist councils held power in areas where Catholics were about half the population. Families were increasingly housed in separate estates, which made segregated housing a lasting feature of Belfast and Derry.\n\nHousing allocation therefore became the grievance that the civil rights movement took up in the 1960s, and the housing issue was one of the first it raised. The reforms made after 1969, including a points system for allocation and the creation of the Housing Executive in 1971, were an admission that the council system had failed.\n\nOverall, the welfare state raised living standards and widened opportunity, especially in education and health, and it gave Catholics benefits that they had not enjoyed before. But because the unionist government kept control of local government and the allocation of houses and jobs, and because schools stayed separate, it did not create equality or end sectarianism. Its greatest impact was in health and education, and its least in housing.",
