    "label": "2",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Stormont fell in March 1972 because the Unionist government lost control of security and the confidence of the nationalist community, and the power-sharing executive collapsed in May 1974 because most unionists would not accept the Council of Ireland and a loyalist strike showed that London would not defend it. Both failures came from the same cause, the inability to win consent from both communities for a way of governing Northern Ireland.\n\nThe long-term cause of Stormont's fall was the way it had governed. Unionists held a majority at every Stormont election from 1921 to 1972, so nationalists were a permanent opposition. Local government used gerrymandered boundaries, such as those drawn in Derry in 1936, and the business vote, and housing and jobs were allocated unfairly. The Special Powers Act and the almost wholly Protestant B Specials added to nationalist distrust. When the civil rights movement protested from 1968 onwards, the government's slow and half-hearted reforms under O'Neill and Chichester-Clark failed to satisfy either side.\n\nThe violence of 1969 to 1972 then destroyed the government's authority. After the Battle of the Bogside in August 1969 British troops were deployed, the Hunt Report led to the disbanding of the B Specials, and the Provisional IRA emerged to attack the army and the state. Under Brian Faulkner internment was introduced on 9 August 1971, and since it was aimed almost only at Catholics it drove recruitment to the IRA and made nationalists reject Stormont altogether, the SDLP having withdrawn in July 1971. Bloody Sunday on 30 January 1972, when paratroopers killed thirteen civilians in Derry, deepened the crisis.\n\nThe immediate cause was the dispute over security. Edward Heath's government insisted that Westminster take control of law and order, Faulkner refused, and he and his ministers resigned on 24 March 1972. Westminster suspended Stormont from 30 March and introduced Direct Rule under William Whitelaw. The Northern Ireland Constitution Act of 1973 abolished the parliament, so Stormont fell because it had failed to contain violence and because London judged that it could no longer be trusted to do so.\n\nThe British government then tried to create a settlement based on power sharing and an Irish dimension. The Assembly elected in June 1973 produced an executive that took office on 1 January 1974, with Faulkner as Chief Executive and Gerry Fitt of the SDLP as his deputy. The Sunningdale conference of 6 to 9 December 1973 agreed a Council of Ireland. But Faulkner's party was split. The Ulster Unionist Council rejected the Council of Ireland by 427 to 374 on 4 January 1974, and Faulkner resigned as party leader. Opponents combined in the United Ulster Unionist Council, which won eleven of the twelve Northern Ireland seats at Westminster in February 1974. The Boland case also damaged Faulkner, since it suggested that the Irish claim to the North had not really been dropped.\n\nThe final blow was the Ulster Workers' Council strike, which began on 15 May 1974 with the backing of loyalist paramilitaries. It cut power supplies and paralysed services through roadblocks and intimidation. The Labour government under Harold Wilson and Merlyn Rees, the Secretary of State, would not confront the strikers directly, and Wilson's remarks about 'spongers' on 25 May angered unionists. The Dublin and Monaghan bombings on 17 May, which killed 33 people, added to the tension. Faulkner resigned as Chief Executive on 28 May 1974 when his colleagues in the executive would not negotiate with the strikers, and the executive collapsed.\n\nStormont therefore fell because a one-party unionist state could not hold the loyalty of nationalists or contain the violence that followed. The power-sharing executive fell because it lacked majority unionist support and because Westminster would not use force to uphold it. Both failures showed that no settlement could last without the consent of both communities.",
