    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The Anglo-Irish Agreement of 1985 and the Downing Street Declaration of 1993 were both important, though in different ways. The Agreement gave the Irish government a formal and permanent role in Northern Ireland's affairs for the first time, while the Declaration set out the principles of consent and self-determination on which the peace process was built. Together they moved British and Irish policy from deadlock towards the negotiated settlement of 1998.\n\nThe Anglo-Irish Agreement was signed at Hillsborough Castle on 15 November 1985 by Margaret Thatcher and the Taoiseach, Garret FitzGerald. It followed the collapse of power-sharing in 1974, the failure of the Constitutional Convention and the hunger strikes of 1981. It also followed Thatcher's rejection of the three options of the New Ireland Forum in 1984, which she dismissed with the words 'Out, out, out'. By 1985 British policy had gone nowhere for a decade, and the Agreement showed that London accepted that Dublin had a legitimate interest in Northern Ireland.\n\nIts central importance lay in the Intergovernmental Conference of ministers, served by a permanent joint secretariat at Maryfield near Belfast. Through it the Irish government could put forward its views on matters such as security, the administration of justice and cross-border co-operation. The role was consultative only, but it was a bigger step than Sunningdale had taken. It was the biggest shift in British policy since 1973. Article 1 also confirmed that Northern Ireland's status would not change without the consent of a majority of its people, so the principle of consent was reaffirmed alongside the new Irish role.\n\nThe Agreement was important too because of how people reacted to it. Unionists felt betrayed because they had not been consulted. Under the slogan 'Ulster says No' they held a huge rally at Belfast City Hall on 23 November 1985, and a day of action followed in March 1986. Fifteen unionist MPs resigned their Westminster seats in January 1986 to force by-elections. The campaign, led by James Molyneaux and Ian Paisley, failed to have the Agreement scrapped. It showed that the British government was prepared to face down unionist opposition in order to pursue a settlement, and it showed how far unionist trust had fallen. The SDLP under John Hume welcomed the Agreement, which strengthened constitutional nationalism against Sinn Féin, while republicans rejected it because British jurisdiction remained.\n\nThe Agreement did not achieve all that Thatcher and FitzGerald hoped. Security co-operation did not improve as much as Thatcher had expected, the IRA campaign went on, and the Enniskillen bombing of 8 November 1987 killed eleven civilians. Even so, it survived the unionist campaign and kept Dublin permanently involved. It also established the idea, taken further in later negotiations, that the problem had to be solved by both governments working together, and it endured until it was superseded by the Good Friday Agreement.\n\nThe Downing Street Declaration was issued on 15 December 1993 by the British Prime Minister, John Major, and the Taoiseach, Albert Reynolds. It came after the Brooke and Mayhew talks of 1991 and 1992 had failed to produce agreement. It also followed the dialogue between John Hume and Gerry Adams, which began in 1988 and resumed in 1991, and Hume's influence on the text was strong. Its importance was that it tried to bring republicans into the political process.\n\nThe Declaration said that the Irish people as a whole had the right to self-determination, to be exercised by agreement between North and South, and that a united Ireland would need the consent of a majority in Northern Ireland. Britain declared that it had no selfish strategic or economic interest in Northern Ireland, an echo of what Peter Brooke had said in 1990. It would legislate for a united Ireland if a majority wished it. The Irish government committed itself to a forum for peace and reconciliation and to examining Articles 2 and 3 of its constitution. Republicans were invited to renounce violence and join talks, so the Declaration offered something to nationalists and unionists alike. Each could see its own principle recognised.\n\nReactions were mixed. Unionists suspected that Britain was moving towards a united Ireland, while republicans hesitated because the Declaration did not meet their demands outright. Yet it laid the foundation for what followed. The IRA declared a ceasefire in August 1994 and loyalist paramilitaries followed in October, and the principles of consent and self-determination became the basis of the 1998 agreement.\n\nOverall, both documents were significant, but in different ways. The Anglo-Irish Agreement was important because it institutionalised Dublin's role and showed that Britain would act despite unionist protest. The Downing Street Declaration was important because it set out principles that republicans, unionists and both governments could eventually accept, and it opened the way to the ceasefires. The Declaration probably did more to bring about peace, but it built on the ground that the Agreement had broken.",
