    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Ecumenism between 1965 and 1993 offered Northern Ireland real opportunities for reconciliation, but it also met serious problems. It built contacts between the churches and gave individuals a way to reach across the divide, yet it was opposed by hardliners on both sides and could not alter the political division between unionists and nationalists. Its achievements were therefore real but limited.\n\nThe main opportunity came from the new mood within the churches. The Second Vatican Council of 1962 to 1965 encouraged the Catholic Church to enter into dialogue with other Christian churches, which had been unthinkable a generation earlier. In a society where religion shaped schooling, marriage and social networks, and where most people had little contact across the divide, this offered a way to break down mutual suspicion. Ecumenism was backed by moderate church leaders in both traditions and gave them a platform from which to condemn violence.\n\nMuch of this opportunity was turned into practical initiatives. The Corrymeela Community was founded in 1965 by the Presbyterian minister Ray Davey at Ballycastle, County Antrim, as a centre for reconciliation where Catholics and Protestants, young people in particular, could meet and live together. The Irish School of Ecumenics was set up in Dublin in 1970 by the Jesuit Michael Hurley to promote interchurch dialogue. In 1973 the leaders of the Catholic and main Protestant churches met formally for the first time at Ballymascanlon, County Louth. These bodies created networks of people committed to understanding, and they kept channels open when politics offered few.\n\nEcumenism also offered opportunities during the worst of the violence. In December 1974 a group of Protestant clergy met IRA leaders at Feakle, County Clare, and the IRA declared a Christmas ceasefire soon afterwards, although it did not last. In September 1979 Pope John Paul II appealed for an end to violence during his visit to Drogheda. Church leaders gave a moral voice to the revulsion that followed atrocities, as with Gordon Wilson, who forgave his daughter's killers after the Enniskillen bombing in 1987. Some of the old barriers also weakened, because the Catholic rules on mixed marriages, which required children to be raised as Catholics and caused resentment among Protestants, were relaxed in 1970.\n\nThe problems were considerable, however. The strongest opposition came from hardline Protestants, led by Ian Paisley and his Free Presbyterian Church, which he founded in 1951. Paisley portrayed ecumenism as a betrayal of the Protestant faith and the Reformation. In 1966 he protested at the Presbyterian General Assembly when links with the World Council of Churches were being considered. He later attacked Pope John Paul II in the European Parliament in 1988, and was removed from the chamber. Because Paisley combined religious and political leadership, founding the DUP in 1971, his opposition made ecumenism part of the political quarrel over the Union. Unionists who saw it as a step towards Rome were hostile to it.\n\nResistance was not confined to Protestants. Clergy who took part in joint initiatives were sometimes denounced by their own congregations or hierarchies, and mixed marriages and joint worship were disapproved of in both communities. Religious identity was passed down through baptism and separate schooling, so ecumenical gestures at the top of the churches often failed to reach ordinary members. Orange parades and traditions of separation continued to emphasise difference. The practical impact was therefore limited to a minority of committed people.\n\nThe violence itself was the final problem. Sectarian killings undermined trust between the churches, and clergy were themselves sometimes targets of attack. Ecumenical work came to depend on the goodwill of people who were frequently isolated within their own communities. Most importantly, the dispute in Northern Ireland was political as much as religious, and better relations between churches could not settle the question of the border and of power.\n\nOn balance, ecumenism was a worthwhile but limited response. It provided opportunities in the form of dialogue, centres such as Corrymeela, a moral voice against violence and some channels to the paramilitaries. Its problems were hardline hostility, suspicion within the churches and the political nature of the conflict. It made a lasting contribution to reconciliation at a personal and community level, but it did not alter the political division.",
