    "label": "2",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Between 1949 and 1989 Irish governments transformed provision in education, health and social welfare. Education saw the greatest change, with free secondary schooling from 1967, while health moved slowly from means-tested services towards a national structure of health boards, and social welfare grew in range and generosity, especially after 1965.\n\nBefore the 1960s the state left most second-level education to the churches and to parents who could pay. Most children left school at 14 with only a primary education, secondary schools charged fees and were mostly owned by religious orders, and vocational schools had lower status. Economic planning after 1958 raised the question of whether the country was educating enough people with the skills it needed. Patrick Hillery, Minister for Education from 1959 to 1965, commissioned a survey with the help of the OECD. Investment in Education, published in 1965, showed wide differences in participation by social class and region and argued that education was an investment in growth. In 1963 Hillery had announced comprehensive schools, which offered academic and practical subjects to pupils of all abilities, and the first opened in 1966 in places such as Carraroe and Cootehill.\n\nThe breakthrough came when Donogh O'Malley became Minister for Education in July 1966 and announced on 10 September that free second-level education would begin the following year. The scheme began in September 1967 and abolished fees in participating schools, and it was accompanied by free school transport which allowed rural pupils to reach a school. Most secondary schools joined and enrolment rose steeply. The Primary Certificate was abolished in 1967 and the school-leaving age was raised from 14 to 15 in 1972. Further changes followed. Community schools were introduced in the early 1970s by merging secondary and vocational schools, Regional Technical Colleges opened from 1970 in towns such as Carlow, Dundalk, Athlone and Waterford, and a new primary curriculum in 1971 emphasised the needs and interests of the child rather than memorisation. At third level, the Higher Education Authority was set up in 1968, a grants scheme helped students from poorer homes, and the National Institute for Higher Education opened in Limerick in 1972. More women and more students from rural and working-class homes reached college than before.\n\nHealth policy moved more slowly and was marked by disputes over who should provide services. The Health Act 1953 replaced the Mother and Child Scheme with means-tested maternity and child services and set out eligibility for public hospital care according to income. In 1957 the Voluntary Health Insurance Board was set up to offer private insurance to those who earned too much to qualify for free hospital care. The Fitzgerald Report of 1968 recommended fewer, larger regional hospitals, a controversial proposal for local communities. The Health Act 1970 then created eight regional health boards, which began work in 1971 and took over the functions of the old county health authorities, and Comhairle na nOspidéal, set up in 1972, regulated the appointment of consultants and the structure of hospital services. Medical cards gave lower-income families free GP care and medicines, but better-off patients paid, so the system remained unequal.\n\nSocial welfare provision grew in range and level, particularly after 1965 as the economy expanded. The Social Welfare Act 1952 gathered the existing social insurance schemes into a single system covering unemployment, illness, widowhood and old age. Children's allowance, first introduced in 1944, was raised in value and coverage. Contributory old age pensions were introduced in 1961 for insured workers, free travel for pensioners followed in 1967 and an electricity allowance in 1968. The state also began to recognise groups who had been overlooked. Deserted wife's benefit came in 1970 and an unmarried mother's allowance in 1973, giving lone mothers a claim of their own, and in 1974 pay-related benefit tied payments for unemployed and sick workers to their previous earnings. Welfare spending rose as a share of national income in the 1970s, partly because unemployment grew after the oil crises.\n\nThese reforms had limits. The churches kept control of management in most schools, so compromises gave the bishops representation on boards of management. Many schools were overcrowded and short of qualified teachers after 1967, and free fees did not remove other costs such as books and uniforms, so class differences in attainment persisted. The VHI and the medical card system left health care unequal. Historians note that the reforms were driven as much by the economic need for skills as by social ideals, and that the middle classes benefited first.\n\nOverall, the period saw a major change of direction. Free post-primary education and transport widened access, a nationwide structure of health boards was created, and a more generous welfare system protected more families, including lone mothers. Education changed most dramatically, but in all three areas the state took on a much larger role in providing for its citizens.",
