    "model": "Nationalism in the Balkans was partly successful between 1815 and 1871. It won autonomy for Serbia, independence for a small Greek kingdom and a union of the Danubian principalities, but it left most Balkan Christians under Ottoman rule and depended heavily on the Great Powers. Success was real but limited.\n\nSerbia achieved autonomy through its own efforts. The first uprising began in 1804 under Karadjordje Petrovic against the Janissary garrison, and the Ottomans crushed it in 1813. A second rising in 1815 under Milos Obrenovic was more successful because Milos negotiated with the Ottoman government, and the Sultan recognised Serbian autonomy in 1830, with Milos as hereditary prince. This was a major gain, and Russia's backing at Adrianople in 1829 helped. However, Serbia stayed under Ottoman suzerainty and paid annual tribute, Ottoman garrisons remained in its fortresses until 1867, and full independence came only in 1878. Milos also ruled autocratically, and the liberal constitution of 1835 was suspended within weeks under pressure from the Powers.\n\nGreece won the clearest success. The Philiki Etaireia, founded at Odessa in 1814, planned a rising, and the revolt began in 1821 in the Danubian principalities and the Peloponnese. A national assembly at Epidaurus declared independence in January 1822. Ottoman massacres such as Chios in 1822 and the death of Byron at Missolonghi in 1824 aroused European philhellenism. The Greeks themselves were hard pressed when Ibrahim Pasha arrived in 1825, and Missolonghi fell in 1826. Independence was won because Britain, France and Russia destroyed the Ottoman and Egyptian fleet at Navarino in October 1827. The London Protocol of 1830 recognised Greece, and in 1832 it became a monarchy under Otto of Bavaria.\n\nThe Greek success had clear limits. The frontier of 1832, running from Arta to Volos, left most Greeks outside the kingdom, which fuelled the Megali Idea voiced by Kolettis in 1844. Kapodistrias, the first head of state, was assassinated in 1831, Otto and his Bavarian officials ruled autocratically until a revolt in 1843 forced a constitution, and he was deposed in 1862. Greece was a small state dependent on the Powers, who chose a Danish prince as King George I in 1863.\n\nIn the principalities, Moldavia and Wallachia elected the same prince, Alexander Cuza, in 1859 and the union was recognised by 1862. Cuza was deposed in 1866 and replaced by Carol of Hohenzollern. This was a real step towards a Romanian state, though the principalities were still formally Ottoman.\n\nThe wider picture was less favourable. Ottoman rule survived over most of the Balkans, and the Treaty of Paris of 1856 after the Crimean War guaranteed the Empire's territorial integrity. The Hatt-i Humayun of 1856 promised equality to non-Muslims, but it was a concession by the Sultan and the Powers, not a nationalist victory. Balkan nationalists could rarely succeed without Great Power support, and Russia, Austria and Britain each pursued their own interests in the Eastern Question.\n\nOverall, nationalism in the Balkans was successful in creating a precedent: Greece independent, Serbia autonomous and the principalities united. But by 1871 these gains were modest, and the real breakthrough for Balkan nationalism lay beyond that date.",
