    "label": "2",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The movement for Italian unification made progress in the years to 1871 through ideas and organisation, through the leadership of Piedmont, through skilful diplomacy, through military action and through the removal of the last obstacles at Venetia and Rome. The early attempts failed, but after 1852 each stage added territory until the Kingdom of Italy had Rome as its capital in 1871.\n\nThe early stage was one of ideas, and its practical results were poor. After 1815 Italy was divided into several states, with Austria ruling Lombardy and Venetia and the Pope and the Bourbons controlling the centre and south. The Carbonari spread liberal and nationalist ideas among officers and professionals, but their risings in Naples in 1820, Piedmont in 1821 and central Italy in 1831 were all crushed with Austrian help. Mazzini founded Young Italy at Marseille in 1831 to work for a unified republic, and his message of God and the People inspired a generation. His risings, such as the invasion of Savoy in 1834, failed, but he made unification a popular ideal.\n\nThe revolutions of 1848-49 were a major step, even though they ended in defeat. The Five Days of Milan drove out the Austrian garrison, Venice proclaimed the Republic of San Marco under Manin, and Pius IX granted a constitution. Charles Albert of Piedmont declared war on Austria but was beaten at Custoza and then at Novara in 1849. The Roman Republic under Mazzini and Garibaldi fell to French troops in July 1849. Yet the lesson was clear: Italy could not defeat Austria alone, and Piedmont, which kept its constitution, the Statuto Albertino, under Victor Emmanuel II, emerged as the natural leader.\n\nPiedmont's growth under Cavour, prime minister from 1852, was the decisive advance. He modernised the economy through free trade and railways, and by sending about 15,000 troops to the Crimean War he won Piedmont a seat at the Congress of Paris in 1856, where he raised the Italian question before the Powers. The Italian National Society, founded in 1857, backed Piedmont instead of Mazzini's republicanism. At Plombieres in July 1858 Cavour secured the promise of French military support against Austria.\n\nWar followed in 1859. French and Piedmontese forces won at Magenta and Solferino, and although Napoleon III made peace at Villafranca without consulting Cavour, Lombardy passed to Piedmont. In March 1860 Tuscany, Modena, Parma and the Romagna voted in plebiscites to join Piedmont. Then Garibaldi and the Thousand sailed from Quarto on 5 May 1860, landed at Marsala, took Palermo and entered Naples on 7 September. When Piedmontese troops entered the Papal Marches and Umbria, Garibaldi handed over his conquests to Victor Emmanuel II, and plebiscites approved annexation. The Kingdom of Italy was proclaimed on 17 March 1861.\n\nThe last stages came through the Great Powers' conflicts. Italy allied with Prussia in 1866, and although it lost at Custoza and Lissa, Venetia was ceded under the Treaty of Vienna. Garibaldi's attempts on Rome failed at Aspromonte in 1862 and Mentana in 1867, but when the French garrison left in August 1870 during the Franco-Prussian War, Italian troops entered Rome on 20 September 1870. A plebiscite approved annexation, and Rome became the capital in 1871.\n\nIn short, progress came in stages: ideas and conspiracy first, then Piedmont's leadership and Cavour's diplomacy, then Garibaldi's volunteers and finally international circumstances. The result was unity under a Piedmontese monarchy, short of Mazzini's republican ideal, but a united state nonetheless.",
