    "model": "Between 1815 and 1871 industrialisation, trade unionism and socialism were closely linked. Industrialisation transformed how Europe produced goods and where people lived, trade unionism was the workers' practical defence against its hardships, and socialism was the body of ideas that challenged the capitalist system itself. The main features of each were new technology and factories, legal struggle and organisation, and competing visions of a fairer society.\n\nThe first main feature of industrialisation was the rise of coal, iron and cotton, powered by steam, with Britain in the lead. James Watt's improved steam engine drove pumps and machinery, Arkwright's water frame and Crompton's mule transformed cotton spinning, and Darby's coke-smelted iron supplied rails, bridges and machines. Britain became the workshop of the world, a status displayed at the Great Exhibition of 1851, and Bessemer's converter of 1856 made steel cheap. Railways were the symbol of the age, from the opening of the Liverpool and Manchester line in 1830, so that industrial growth rested on a transport revolution as well as on factories.\n\nSecond, industrialisation spread unevenly to the continent. Belgium was first, with its coal in Hainaut and Liège. France industrialised more slowly, building most of its railways after the Railway Law of 1842, while in the German lands the Ruhr and Silesian coalfields grew, the first railway ran from Nuremberg to Fürth in 1835 and the Zollverein removed internal tariffs. Krupp's steelworks at Essen expanded from the 1850s. A telling difference was that continental industry relied more on banks and state support through tariffs and railways, whereas British growth was financed largely from private profits and was shaped by laissez-faire, which saw the repeal of the Corn Laws in 1846.\n\nThird, industrialisation created the factory system and the industrial city, with heavy human costs. Workers laboured twelve to fourteen hours a day under supervision, and women and children formed a large part of the textile workforce. Manchester grew from about 75,000 people in 1801 to about 300,000 in 1851, and the 1851 census showed that more than half the British people lived in towns. Overcrowding and open sewers brought cholera in 1831-32 and 1848-49, and Chadwick's report of 1842 and Engels's account of Manchester exposed the conditions. The state responded slowly, with the Factory Act of 1833, the Mines Act of 1842 and the Ten Hours Act of 1847. These problems explain why workers organised and why socialist ideas found an audience.\n\nTrade unionism had its own main features. Workers began in craft societies, but the Combination Acts of 1799 and 1800 made combining illegal until their repeal in 1824, and the act of 1825 then limited strikes. Persecution continued, as the sentencing of the Tolpuddle Martyrs to transportation in 1834 showed, and Robert Owen's Grand National Consolidated Trades Union of that year collapsed within months. A more durable model emerged with the Amalgamated Society of Engineers of 1851, a New Model union with high subscriptions, a central fund and sickness and unemployment benefits. Such unions were confined to skilled men, but the Trades Union Congress first met in 1868 and the Trade Union Act of 1871 gave unions legal recognition, so unionism moved from illegality to acceptance.\n\nSocialism was marked by a range of approaches. Utopian thinkers such as Saint-Simon and Fourier rejected competitive capitalism and planned co-operative communities, and Owen put the idea into practice at New Lanark and New Harmony. Louis Blanc's Organisation of Labour in 1839 urged state-backed workshops. Karl Marx gave socialism a scientific basis. With Engels he wrote the Communist Manifesto in 1848, arguing that history was the history of class struggle and that the working class would overthrow capitalism, and he developed the analysis of surplus value in the first volume of Das Kapital in 1867. He also dominated the First International, founded in London in 1864. Bakunin's anarchism, which rejected any state, split the International, and he was expelled in 1872.\n\nOverall, the main features of the period were the technological and social upheaval of industrialisation, the slow, hard-won legal recognition of trade unions and the growth of socialist thought from utopian experiment to Marxist theory. The three developments were connected, because each answered the problems the one before created, and together they shaped the modern industrial society that emerged by 1871.",
