    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Charles Darwin and Victor Hugo both made major contributions to the history of ideas in the nineteenth century, though in different fields. Darwin changed how Europeans understood nature and humanity through the theory of evolution by natural selection, while Hugo shaped literature, politics and the idea of liberty through his Romantic writing and his republican opposition to Napoleon III.\n\nDarwin was born in Shrewsbury in 1809 and studied medicine at Edinburgh and then divinity at Cambridge before joining HMS Beagle as naturalist. During the voyage of 1831 to 1836 he collected fossils, plants and animals in South America, the Galápagos Islands and Australia, and he observed how species varied from place to place. This gave him the evidence on which his later theory rested, and it shows that his contribution came from patient observation rather than speculation.\n\nHis central contribution was On the Origin of Species, published in November 1859. He had developed the theory in the years after his return but delayed publication for about twenty years, and he finally published after Alfred Russel Wallace reached the same idea. The book argued that species change over time through variation and natural selection, with individuals better suited to their environment surviving and reproducing more successfully, and that all living things share common ancestors. In The Descent of Man in 1871 he applied the theory to human origins. This replaced the view of fixed, separately created species with a natural explanation of life's diversity.\n\nThe impact of Darwin's ideas was immediate and divisive. Scientists such as Thomas Henry Huxley defended him, while Bishop Samuel Wilberforce and many churchmen opposed him at the Oxford debate of 1860, so evolution became a focus of the conflict between science and religion. Later work, notably that of Gregor Mendel on inheritance, which was rediscovered in 1900, supplied the mechanism that Darwin lacked. Darwin is now regarded as the founder of modern evolutionary biology, and he was buried in Westminster Abbey in 1882.\n\nHugo's contribution was first literary. He led French Romanticism, and his preface to Cromwell in 1827 served as a manifesto for Romantic drama. His play Hernani in 1830 provoked a riot between Romantic supporters and classical critics and marked the victory of the new style over the rules of classical theatre. Notre-Dame de Paris in 1831 revived interest in the Gothic past of Paris and helped to inspire the restoration of medieval buildings, and he entered the Académie française in 1841.\n\nHugo was also a political thinker who used literature to defend liberty. A monarchist in the 1820s, he became a republican by 1848, sat in the Constituent and Legislative Assemblies, and defended freedom of the press and education. After Louis-Napoléon's coup of December 1851 he went into exile, in Brussels, Jersey and then Guernsey. From there he attacked the Emperor in Napoléon le Petit in 1852 and in the satirical poems Les Châtiments in 1853, and he refused the amnesty of 1859 as a protest. Les Misérables, largely written on Guernsey and published in 1862, portrayed the poor of France through Jean Valjean and became one of the most widely read novels of the century. He returned to Paris in September 1870, the day after the Third Republic was proclaimed, and his funeral in 1885 drew huge crowds.\n\nIn judgement, Darwin's contribution was the more far-reaching in its challenge to established beliefs, since it changed science and the way humans saw their place in nature. Hugo's was equally significant in a different way, because he showed that a writer could shape public opinion and become a symbol of resistance to tyranny. Between them they illustrate the two great forces of nineteenth-century thought, scientific inquiry and the Romantic commitment to freedom.",
