    "model": "There was an economic and political crisis in Tsarist Russia after 1900 because a backward, autocratic state tried to modernise its economy without reforming its politics. Rapid industrialisation and a poor peasantry created discontent, the war with Japan exposed the regime's weakness, and the Tsar's refusal to share real power left the opposition with nowhere to go but revolution.\n\nThe first cause was the cost of Witte's industrialisation. As Finance Minister from 1892 to 1903, Sergei Witte drove a state-led great spurt of growth, building railways such as the Trans-Siberian, putting the rouble on the gold standard in 1897 and attracting French and Belgian loans. Coal, iron and steel grew in the Donbas, oil in Baku and engineering in St Petersburg. But the bill was paid by the peasants through heavy indirect taxes, and grain was exported even when people were hungry. The industrial depression of 1900 to 1903 then brought unemployment and strikes. Growth had created a crisis rather than solved one, because it depended on foreign capital and a desperately poor population.\n\nSecondly, the countryside was in turmoil. About 80 per cent of Russians were peasants, and they were burdened by redemption payments since the abolition of serfdom in 1861, by small plots, backward methods in the mir and a rapidly growing population. Land hunger grew. The famine of 1891 and the cholera epidemic of 1892 had already shown the slowness of government relief, and voluntary efforts by figures such as Tolstoy and Chekhov exposed the regime's failure. In 1902 peasants attacked landlords' estates in Kharkov and Poltava provinces, and the violence returned on a far larger scale in 1905 and 1906. The peasants wanted land, and the regime could not or would not give it to them.\n\nThirdly, industrialisation created a new and dangerous urban working class. Industry was concentrated in huge factories such as the Putilov works in St Petersburg, where tens of thousands of workers lived in poor conditions. Many were first-generation peasants who kept links with their villages, so discontent passed easily between town and country. Large workforces made organisation and strikes easier, and Marxist Social Democrats and the peasant-based Social Revolutionaries worked underground to win them over.\n\nPolitically, the autocracy gave no outlet for any of this. Nicholas II had called hopes of a constitution senseless dreams in 1895 and relied on the Church, the army, the nobility and the Okhrana. Liberals formed the Union of Liberation in 1903 to demand a constitution, and revolutionaries multiplied. Because every form of peaceful opposition was blocked, grievances built up until a single shock could set off an explosion.\n\nThat shock came with the Russo-Japanese War of 1904 to 1905. Plehve is reported to have wanted a short victorious war to stop revolution, but it did the opposite. Port Arthur surrendered in January 1905, the army was beaten at Mukden and the Baltic Fleet was destroyed at Tsushima in May. The defeat humiliated the regime and revealed the weakness of its army, transport and administration. On 22 January 1905, Bloody Sunday, troops fired on Father Gapon's peaceful procession to the Winter Palace, killing and wounding hundreds, and the people's faith in the Tsar as their protector was shattered. Strikes, peasant risings, the mutiny on the battleship Potemkin and a general strike in October followed, and the St Petersburg Soviet was formed.\n\nThe regime survived 1905 only by concession and force. Witte persuaded the Tsar to issue the October Manifesto, promising civil liberties and an elected Duma, and the army stayed loyal while the opposition split. Yet the Fundamental Laws of 1906 kept the Tsar an autocrat with control of ministers, the army and foreign policy and the power to dissolve the Duma. The First and Second Dumas were dissolved and the electoral law was rigged in 1907, so the political crisis was postponed, not resolved. Stolypin's land reform began to create independent farmers, but most peasants stayed in the mir, and strikes revived after the Lena goldfields shootings in 1912.\n\nOverall, the crisis after 1900 was both economic and political and each fed the other. Industrial and agrarian strain produced discontent, defeat in Asia turned it into revolution, and the Tsar's refusal to reform made it permanent. Nicholas II had the chance to change in 1905 and 1906 and threw it away, which is why the regime was so fragile when a far greater test came in 1914.",
