    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Wilfred Owen was honoured as a soldier because he served with courage and leadership and won the Military Cross, and as a poet because he gave the suffering of the Western Front its most powerful and truthful voice. The two honours are linked, since his poetry came from what he had endured as an infantry officer, and both were recognised largely after his death in November 1918.\n\nOwen was born in Oswestry in Shropshire in 1893, the eldest child of a railway official. He worked as a lay assistant to a vicar and as a teacher in France before the war. He enlisted in the Artists Rifles in October 1915 and was commissioned in the Manchester Regiment in 1916, so he went to war as a volunteer officer. He fought on the Somme and in the Beaumont-Hamel area in January 1917, where the conditions were brutal, and he suffered concussion after spending weeks in a shell-hole.\n\nHis experience of the front led to his breakdown and, indirectly, to his emergence as a poet. He was diagnosed with neurasthenia, or shell shock, and sent to Craiglockhart War Hospital near Edinburgh in June 1917. There in August he met Siegfried Sassoon, who read his drafts, advised him to write plainly and introduced him to literary circles including Robert Graves. This friendship turned a minor writer into a major one. Between autumn 1917 and 1918 Owen wrote or finished his best work, including Dulce et Decorum Est, Anthem for Doomed Youth, Strange Meeting, Futility and Exposure.\n\nThe poems were honoured because of what they said and how they said it. Dulce et Decorum Est attacked what Owen called the old Lie, that it is sweet and fitting to die for one's country, with graphic detail of gas, mud and death that destroyed the patriotic picture painted by earlier writers such as Brooke. He used pararhyme, consonance in place of full rhyme, to create a sense of unease and incompleteness that suited his subject. In a draft preface he wrote that the poetry is in the pity, showing that his purpose was compassion for the men he commanded rather than glory. This combination of craft and pity is why critics such as Jon Stallworthy stressed his skill and why he is seen as the main English voice of the war's suffering.\n\nOwen was honoured as a soldier too. He chose to return to the front in 1918, when he could have remained at home, and in October he won the Military Cross for his actions near Joncourt. He was killed on 4 November 1918 in an attack on the Sambre-Oise canal, a week before the armistice, at the age of 25. His death so close to the end of the war made his story especially poignant, and it gave his poems the authority of a man who had paid the price he wrote about.\n\nHis poetry was honoured largely after his death, because only a few poems appeared in his lifetime. Sassoon and Edith Sitwell published a first edition in 1919 to 1920 and Edmund Blunden produced a fuller edition in 1931. In the 1920s Owen was one of several war poets and the public were at first drawn to Brooke, but interest grew from the 1930s as pacifist feeling spread. Later historians, such as Dominic Hibberd, have studied his complex character, and his work became part of how the war is remembered in Britain and Ireland.\n\nOwen was therefore honoured as a decorated officer who led his men with courage and as a poet whose work expressed the pity and futility of the war. It should be remembered that most soldiers did not write such poetry and that the poets' war view was shaped by an educated officer class. Even so, Owen's combination of lived experience and artistic skill makes him the figure through whom the First World War is most widely understood.",
