    "model": "Between 1920 and 1940 the Third Republic of France faced political instability, financial and economic difficulties, the challenge of extremist leagues and a deeply divided society, and finally a military threat for which it was unprepared. Although the republic's institutions survived until 1940, the combination of these problems left France weak when Germany attacked.\n\nA first problem was chronic political instability. The Third Republic, founded in 1870, was governed by an elected Chamber of Deputies, and the President had only a limited, largely ceremonial role. Frequent votes of no confidence meant that ministers were often replaced after only a few months. The Bloc National, a conservative coalition, governed from the election of November 1919 until 1924, when the Cartel des Gauches of Radicals and Socialists under Édouard Herriot won power. The Cartel fell in April 1925 after a run on the franc, and governments changed rapidly thereafter. This revolving door of ministries made long-term policy almost impossible and led many French people to lose faith in parliamentary democracy.\n\nA second set of problems was financial and economic. France had lost about 1.4 million dead in the war, and the north-east was devastated, so reconstruction was costly and was supposed to be paid for by German reparations. When Germany failed to pay, Raymond Poincaré ordered the occupation of the Ruhr in January 1923, but this damaged relations with Britain and the United States and ended in 1925 without securing full reparations. Poincaré returned in 1926 with a national unity government and stabilised the franc through tax increases and spending cuts, and the Poincaré franc was fixed in law in 1928 at about a fifth of its pre-war value. The Depression reached France later than Britain or Germany, because small family farms and businesses cushioned the economy, but industrial production fell sharply from 1931. Governments kept the franc at its gold value until 1936 and used deflation to defend it, which hurt industry and farming.\n\nThe Stavisky affair of 1934 exposed a third problem, the lack of public trust in the republic. Alexandre Stavisky, a financier with political protection, was found shot dead in January 1934 after his fraudulent bond sales collapsed, and many on both left and right suspected he had been silenced. The Prime Minister, Camille Chautemps, resigned, and right-wing leagues such as the Croix-de-Feu, Action française and the Solidarité française used the scandal to attack the republic as corrupt. On 6 February 1934 they marched on the Chamber of Deputies, the police opened fire and about fifteen people were killed and over a thousand injured. The Daladier government resigned, and a national government under Gaston Doumergue was formed. Historians disagree about how far the leagues were a real fascist threat, but the riots frightened the left and showed how fragile the republic had become.\n\nThe riots in turn deepened the division of French society into left and right. The left responded with a general strike on 12 February 1934, and in July 1935 Socialists, Communists and Radicals united in the Popular Front. It won the elections of April and May 1936, and Léon Blum, a Socialist and the first Jew to hold the office, became Prime Minister. Strikes and factory occupations involving around two million workers led to the Matignon Agreements, which gave wage rises, union recognition and collective bargaining, followed by the 40-hour week and paid holidays. But the government faced capital flight and financial pressure, devalued the franc in October 1936, paused its reforms in February 1937 and fell in June 1937. The Popular Front was also split over the Spanish Civil War, and in 1938 Daladier undid the 40-hour week. Conservatives deeply resented Blum's reforms, and the cry of better Hitler than Blum is often quoted to show how bitter the divisions were.\n\nFinally, France faced the problem of defence and the German threat. It spent heavily on the Maginot Line, built between 1930 and 1938, but it did not extend through the Ardennes or along the Belgian frontier. When Germany attacked on 10 May 1940, German armoured divisions broke through the Ardennes, crossed the Meuse at Sedan on 13 and 14 May and reached the Channel coast by 20 May. The French spread their tanks among infantry units, and the generals had expected the Ardennes to be difficult terrain for armour. Paris fell on 14 June, an armistice was signed on 22 June, and on 10 July 1940 the National Assembly voted full powers to Marshal Pétain, which ended the Third Republic.\n\nIn conclusion, the Third Republic was undermined most seriously by weak and unstable government, financial and economic difficulties, bitter political division after 1934 and military unpreparedness. It survived the Depression and the leagues without a coup, but the divisions and the failure to modernise the army left it unable to withstand the German attack in 1940.",
