    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The Jarrow March of October 1936 was caused by the collapse of Jarrow's only major industry, which left the town with unemployment far above the national level and a government unwilling to help it, and its consequences were disappointing in the short term but significant in how the 1930s came to be remembered. The march won no jobs for Jarrow, yet it made the town a symbol of the Depression and shaped attitudes to unemployment for decades.\n\nThe deepest cause was the dependence of Jarrow on Palmers Shipbuilding and Iron Company, which had been founded in the town in 1851 and launched its thousandth ship in 1930. Almost the whole local economy rested on the yard, so when world trade collapsed after 1929 and shipbuilding orders dried up, shops, trades and families that depended on its wages collapsed with it. Palmers launched its last ship, the destroyer HMS Duchess, in July 1932 and a receiver was appointed in 1933. Jarrow was an extreme example of the problem of the staple industries of the north-east, South Wales and Clydeside, which had lost their export markets while the south-east and Midlands enjoyed relative prosperity.\n\nThe closure of the yard was not simply an accident of the slump but the result of deliberate policy. National Shipbuilders Security Ltd, formed in 1930 by the shipbuilders with the support of the Bank of England, bought and closed redundant yards to cut the industry's excess capacity. It bought the Palmers yard in 1934 and barred shipbuilding on the site for at least forty years, which ruled out any revival. A plan by the American businessman T. Vosper Salt to build a modern steelworks on the site was diluted by the British Iron and Steel Federation, which feared competition, until it was unworkable. Local people therefore felt that Jarrow had been sacrificed by the industry and ignored by the government, and Ellen Wilkinson, the town's Labour MP, later called it the town that was murdered.\n\nThe human cost provided the immediate cause. Well over half of insured workers in Jarrow were on the dole in the mid-1930s, and figures of around two-thirds are often quoted, against about 17 per cent in the north-east in 1936. The town had some of the worst housing, hunger and ill health in Britain. The Special Areas Act of 1934 gave Tyneside little real help, and the Unemployment Assistance Board paid the long-term unemployed by household means test at rates set in London that took no account of local conditions. Earlier hunger marches, such as the National Hunger March of 1932, had been organised by the Communist-led National Unemployed Workers' Movement and had been refused a hearing, so Jarrow's leaders decided on a respectable, cross-party appeal by the whole town, backed by the council under David Riley, with Wilkinson as the public voice in Parliament.\n\nThe march itself, from 5 to 31 October 1936, was the response to these causes, and its organisation helped to determine its consequences. About 200 men, chosen from more than a thousand volunteers and examined by a doctor, walked some 290 miles to London, carrying a petition of over 11,000 signatures in an oak box. They covered about 15 miles a day, slept in church halls and schools and were fed by supporters and Co-operative societies along the route. Press and newsreel coverage was largely sympathetic and the town became a national cause. Yet the Labour Party and the TUC, fearing Communist influence and preferring parliamentary pressure, did not back it, which limited its political weight.\n\nThe immediate consequences were bleak. Stanley Baldwin refused to meet a deputation on 29 October, pointing to falling unemployment, and Wilkinson presented the petition in the Commons on 4 November, but it was not debated and the government made no new commitment. The Unemployment Assistance Board reduced the marchers' benefit on the ground that they were not available for work while they marched, and the men went home by train on 5 November with nothing to show for their effort. Many felt it had failed, and Riley and some councillors briefly left the Labour Party afterwards.\n\nIn the longer term, a little industry did come to the site, as Sir John Jarvis helped attract small firms, and by 1939 about 100 men worked in a furniture factory and up to 500 in metal industries. The Special Areas (Amendment) Act of 1937 offered tax reliefs to firms in depressed areas, but it was already being prepared, and historians do not claim that the march caused it. Real recovery came through rearmament and war orders, not the march. Its lasting consequence was in memory: Labour adopted its banners as a symbol of the injustice of the 1930s, and it contributed to the postwar belief in government failure that supported full employment policy and the welfare state after 1945.\n\nThe causes of the march lay in the closure of Palmers, an industry that chose to shed its capacity, and a government that trusted general recovery to reach the distressed areas in time. Its consequences were a failure in its immediate aims but a lasting success in the politics of memory, which is why Jarrow is remembered far more than it achieved.",
