    "label": "1",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The Sovietisation of Eastern Europe and the Berlin Crisis of 1948-49 were two of the most important causes of Cold War tension in Europe. Sovietisation convinced the Western powers that Stalin intended to dominate the territory his army had occupied, while the Berlin Blockade turned that suspicion into a direct confrontation and led to the permanent division of Germany and the formation of rival military alliances.\n\nSovietisation was the process by which communist parties, backed by the Red Army and Soviet security police, took control of states that had been promised free elections at Yalta. The Red Army occupied Poland, Romania, Bulgaria, Hungary and eastern Germany by May 1945, so Soviet power on the ground shaped events. Communist parties first governed in coalitions but kept key posts such as the interior ministry and the police. They then removed rivals one thin slice at a time, a method known as salami tactics and associated with the Hungarian leader Mátyás Rákosi. This broke the promise of the Declaration on Liberated Europe and convinced Western leaders that Stalin's aim went beyond a defensive buffer.\n\nThe country-by-country record deepened the alarm. In Poland sixteen underground leaders were tried in Moscow in 1945, and the peasant leader Mikołajczyk fled in October 1947 after rigged elections. In Hungary the communists won only about 17 percent in the free election of 1945, yet fraud and police pressure later made them the largest party. King Michael of Romania was forced to abdicate in December 1947, and Nikola Petkov was executed in Bulgaria. The decisive shock was Czechoslovakia in February 1948, when non-communist ministers resigned and Gottwald's communists took power without an election. The death of Jan Masaryk in March 1948 shocked Western opinion. Churchill's Iron Curtain speech at Fulton in March 1946 had already named this division, and each new takeover made it harder to find agreement.\n\nSovietisation also split Europe economically and politically. Marshall aid was offered to all Europe in June 1947, but Stalin forbade eastern states to take part, and Poland and Czechoslovakia withdrew their interest. The Cominform was set up in September 1947 to bring the communist parties under Moscow's direction, and Comecon followed in January 1949. Europe now had two rival systems, and Truman's policy of containment, begun with the Truman Doctrine in March 1947, was the Western reply. Even Yugoslavia's expulsion from the Cominform in 1948 showed Stalin's determination to enforce loyalty.\n\nThe Berlin Crisis brought these tensions to a head. By 1947 the zones of Germany were drifting apart, and the British and American zones had been merged as Bizonia. When the Western powers introduced the Deutsche Mark on 20 June 1948, the Soviet authorities saw a threat to their zone and blockaded road, rail and canal access to West Berlin. Stalin's aim was to force the Western powers to abandon the new currency and their plans for a West German state, or to drive them out of a city that lay deep inside the Soviet zone.\n\nThe Western response raised tension further and made the crisis a test of will. Truman refused to withdraw, and General Clay and the Berlin mayor Ernst Reuter led the resistance. The airlift flew food and fuel into Tempelhof, Gatow and Tegel, delivering over two million tons, and planes sometimes landed about every minute. It was a Soviet humiliation, and Stalin lifted the blockade on 12 May 1949. The confrontation had lasted almost a year, and the risk of war throughout it was real.\n\nThe long-term results confirmed the divisions. The Federal Republic of Germany was founded on 23 May 1949 and the German Democratic Republic on 7 October 1949, so Germany was divided in two. The crisis also hastened the North Atlantic Treaty of April 1949, which built on the Brussels Treaty of March 1948 and committed the USA to defend Western Europe. The rival Warsaw Pact followed in 1955, after West Germany joined NATO. Soviet archives opened after 1991 showed more of Stalin's aims, but historians still debate how far Western actions such as the currency reform provoked him.\n\nOverall, Sovietisation created the mistrust and the divided Europe, while the Berlin Crisis made the division permanent and military. Sovietisation was the deeper cause, because it destroyed the wartime alliance's promise of free Europe, but Berlin was the moment when tension came close to open war and gave the Cold War its lasting shape.",
