    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Marriage, the family and the position of women in Europe changed profoundly between 1945 and 1992. In the late 1940s and 1950s the ideal was the stable nuclear family with a male breadwinner and a housewife mother, but from the 1960s the contraceptive pill, feminism, legal reform and rising female employment weakened that model. By 1992 marriage was easier to leave, women had much greater legal equality and control over their bodies, and family life was far more varied, though inequalities remained.\n\nThe years after the war saw a return to domestic stability. A baby boom ran from about 1946 to the mid-1960s, people married earlier and more often than before the war, and the nuclear family became the ideal. Housing shortages forced many young couples to live with relatives, and governments supported families through allowances, as in France in 1945-46. Churches and conservative parties promoted the family as the basis of a stable society after the upheaval of war, and films, magazines and later television presented the happy housewife surrounded by labour-saving appliances. Women's legal position remained weak: in France married women could only open a bank account or take a job without their husband's consent from 1965, and in West Germany a husband could decide whether his wife worked until 1977. The family of the 1950s was therefore traditional, and women were expected to be wives and mothers first.\n\nThis began to change as more women, including married women, entered paid employment from the 1950s, mainly in teaching, nursing, clerical and factory work. Rising affluence meant that families wanted a second income to buy cars, televisions and washing machines. Women remained poorly paid and barred from many professions, but employment gave them independence and a new sense of their rights. Equal pay was written into Article 119 of the Treaty of Rome, and the European Court of Justice enforced it in the Defrenne case of 1976. Britain passed the Equal Pay Act in 1970, which came fully into effect in 1975 together with the Sex Discrimination Act. Legal equality in the workplace therefore advanced considerably, even if pay gaps persisted in practice.\n\nControl over childbearing was another major change. The contraceptive pill, approved in the United States in 1960, spread through Western Europe in the 1960s and allowed women to plan their families and their careers. France legalised contraception in the Neuwirth Law of 1967. Britain's Abortion Act of 1967 permitted abortion on medical grounds, and in France the Veil Law of January 1975, sponsored by the health minister Simone Veil, legalised abortion in the first ten weeks. West Germany reformed its law in 1974-76 and Italy legalised abortion in 1978. These reforms were won against opposition from the Catholic Church, whose encyclical Humanae Vitae of 1968 reaffirmed the ban on artificial birth control, and they separated sex from reproduction as never before.\n\nFeminist ideas drove much of this change. Simone de Beauvoir's The Second Sex of 1949 argued that women were defined as the Other by a male-centred society and that their inferior position was created by history, law and education rather than biology. Betty Friedan's The Feminine Mystique of 1963 influenced a new generation, and second-wave feminism emerged in the late 1960s, with the Mouvement de liberation des femmes formed in France in 1970. In 1971 de Beauvoir and others signed the Manifesto of the 343, publicly declaring that they had had abortions. These movements demanded equal pay, nurseries and reproductive freedom, and they changed attitudes to what women could expect from marriage and work.\n\nMarriage itself became easier to end. Britain's Divorce Reform Act of 1969, in force from 1971, allowed divorce on grounds of irretrievable breakdown, and West Germany introduced no-fault divorce in 1977. Italy legalised divorce in 1970 and its voters upheld the law in a referendum in 1974, while Spain followed in 1981 and Ireland only after a referendum in 1995. Family law reforms, such as those in West Germany and Italy in 1975, replaced the husband as head of household with equal rights for spouses. Divorce rates rose in the 1970s and single-parent families became more common, which prompted conservatives to blame permissiveness for the decline of the traditional family.\n\nThe experience in the communist states was different. In East Germany and the Soviet Union far more women went out to work than in the West, supported by state nurseries and paid maternity leave, and in the Soviet Union women worked as doctors and engineers and Valentina Tereshkova became the first woman in space in 1963. Yet women carried the double burden of paid work and nearly all the housework, and independent feminist movements were not permitted.\n\nOverall, marriage, the family and the role of women changed more in these decades than in any comparable period. Women gained legal equality, access to contraception and abortion, and a place in the workforce, while marriage became less permanent and families more varied. The changes were uneven between countries and social classes, and women still earned less and did most domestic work in 1992, but the traditional pattern of 1945 had been transformed.",
