    "model": "Julius Nyerere's policy of ujamaa, launched in the Arusha Declaration of 5 February 1967, was only partly successful. It achieved real gains in education, health and national unity, but it failed in its central economic aim of building a prosperous, self-reliant rural socialism, and the compulsion used to enforce it left Tanzania poorer and more dependent on foreign aid by the early 1980s.\n\nThe aim was a distinctive African path. Nyerere rejected both Western capitalism and Soviet-style communism and held up ujamaa, or familyhood, as a model rooted in traditional African village life, where people shared work and land. The Arusha Declaration committed Tanzania to self-reliance and state control of the main means of production. The banks, major industries and insurance companies were nationalised in 1967, and 'Education for Self-Reliance' reshaped schooling around rural work and community values. The TANU Mwongozo guidelines of 1971 required party members to work for their living and avoid privilege. This was a coherent vision, and Nyerere's personal simplicity gave it credibility, which counts as one real success in itself.\n\nThe strongest achievements were social. Universal primary education, launched in 1974, aimed to put every child in school by 1977, and primary enrolment rose rapidly. Adult literacy rose to a high proportion of the population by the early 1980s, which was a remarkable record for a very poor country with limited resources. Health care also expanded in rural areas. Historians who defend Nyerere point to these gains as proof of what a government committed to its people could achieve, and they remained his most lasting legacy.\n\nNyerere also held Tanzania together politically. In a region where Nigeria and the Congo suffered civil war and secession, Tanzania avoided ethnic conflict, and Nyerere refused to rule by ethnic favour. The ideology of ujamaa and the single-party system under TANU gave a shared national purpose. His supporters stress this national unity as a major success, although critics reply that it was bought at the price of a one-party state in which opposition was not tolerated.\n\nThe economic record was far weaker. Ujamaa villages began as voluntary cooperative settlements, but few peasants moved of their own free will. From 1973-74 the government changed course and moved millions of people into villages, often by force, in the policy known as villagisation. This disrupted farming, separated people from their established land and crops, and produced food shortages. Agricultural output collapsed, and Tanzania, which had been able to feed itself, had to import food. Compulsion also contradicted the ideal of voluntary familyhood that Nyerere had preached, which damaged the moral case for the policy.\n\nNot all the blame lay with ujamaa. The oil price rise of 1973, drought, and the heavy cost of the war to overthrow Idi Amin of Uganda in 1978-79 all added to the crisis, and falling commodity prices hurt a country dependent on a few exports. Even so, by the early 1980s the economy was in decline, and the self-reliance that Nyerere had promised had given way to heavy dependence on foreign aid and lending. Tanzania relied on donors, including Scandinavian states and China, which built the TAZARA railway between 1970 and 1975, so the aim of self-reliance was never really achieved.\n\nIn the end Nyerere himself accepted the verdict. He stepped down as president in 1985, and his successor Ali Hassan Mwinyi agreed an IMF programme in 1986 that reversed many of the socialist policies. Ujamaa was therefore successful as a social and moral project, and Nyerere, known as Mwalimu, remained widely respected after his death in 1999. As an economic strategy it failed, because forced villagisation collapsed farm output and left Tanzania poorer. On balance it was a limited success, with real social gains but an economic failure that outweighed them.",
