    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Between 1960 and 1990 cultural diversity brought Britain both serious problems and real opportunities. Large-scale migration from the Caribbean and South Asia produced racism, discrimination, restrictive laws and violent unrest, but it also enriched British music, food, religion and politics, and it forced Britain to develop laws and policies that promoted equality.\n\nThe first problem was racism and discrimination in everyday life. Under the British Nationality Act of 1948 Commonwealth citizens had the right to enter Britain, and migrants from the Caribbean, India and Pakistan filled shortages in the National Health Service, London Transport and the factories of the Midlands and Yorkshire. Yet they met a colour bar in lodgings and jobs, and in Notting Hill exploitative landlords such as Peter Rachman packed newcomers into overcrowded housing. The racial violence of 1958 in Notting Hill and Nottingham, and the murder of Kelso Cochrane in 1959, showed how serious hostility had become, and far-right groups added to the tension.\n\nA second problem was the political reaction. The Commonwealth Immigrants Act of 1962 introduced employment vouchers, and a new Act in 1968 was rushed through Parliament in days to restrict Kenyan Asians holding British passports. In April 1968 Enoch Powell made his 'rivers of blood' speech in Birmingham, warning that immigration would bring violence; Edward Heath dismissed him from the Shadow Cabinet, but London dockers marched in his support, showing the depth of feeling. The Immigration Act of 1971 introduced patriality, and the National Front, founded in 1967, campaigned against immigration, with violent confrontations at Lewisham in 1977 and Southall in 1979, where the teacher Blair Peach died. When Idi Amin expelled Ugandan Asians in 1972, about 27,000 came to Britain, and the reception they received tested attitudes again.\n\nThe most serious social problem was the breakdown of relations between police and black communities. The 'sus' laws were used heavily against young black men, and unemployment and poor housing fell hardest on minority communities. After Operation Swamp 81 in April 1981 the Brixton riots broke out, followed by Toxteth in Liverpool and Moss Side in Manchester that July. The Scarman Report identified racial disadvantage, unemployment and poor policing as causes and recommended more accountable policing, leading to the Police and Criminal Evidence Act of 1984. Tensions also arose between religious identity and free speech, as the Rushdie affair of 1988-89 showed when copies of The Satanic Verses were burned in Bradford and Khomeini issued a fatwa.\n\nAgainst these problems, diversity created real opportunities. Cultural life was transformed: Caribbean communities shaped British music with reggae, ska and calypso, and Caribbean and South Asian food became part of everyday urban life. The Notting Hill Carnival, which began as a street festival in 1966, grew into one of the largest in Europe. Mosques, gurdwaras and Hindu temples appeared in Birmingham, Leicester, Bradford and London, making Britain a visibly multi-faith society, and Claudia Jones's indoor carnival of 1959 was an early expression of this cultural confidence.\n\nDiversity also drove political and legal progress. The Race Relations Acts of 1965, 1968 and 1976 banned discrimination, first in public places, then in housing and employment, and finally in indirect forms, creating the Race Relations Board and the Commission for Racial Equality. Roy Jenkins argued in 1966 that integration meant equal opportunity within cultural diversity rather than assimilation, which set a more tolerant direction for policy. Black and Asian candidates entered Parliament in 1987, including Diane Abbott, Paul Boateng, Bernie Grant and Keith Vaz, and reports such as Rampton in 1981 and Swann in 1985 urged schools to meet the needs of minority children. Anti-racist movements, such as the Anti-Nazi League and Rock Against Racism, showed that many white Britons also embraced diversity.\n\nOverall, cultural diversity was a source of conflict in the first decades, when racism, restrictive laws and police hostility dominated, but by 1990 Britain had become a more open multicultural society. The problems were real and the laws did not end disadvantage, yet the opportunities, in culture, politics and legal protection, proved lasting.",
