    "model": "McCarthyism and the anti-war movement both affected US foreign policy between 1945 and 1972, but in different ways and at different times. McCarthyism worked mainly in the early 1950s by narrowing the choices open to policymakers and weakening their expertise, while the anti-war movement worked from 1965 and helped to force the government to scale down and end its war in Vietnam.\n\nMcCarthyism grew out of the Red Scare that followed the communist victory in China in October 1949, the Hiss conviction and the arrest of the atomic spy Klaus Fuchs. Republicans charged that Truman and the State Department had lost China, and McCarthy took up the charge in his Wheeling speech of February 1950, claiming to hold a list of communists in the State Department. This made foreign policy a weapon in domestic politics. Truman, already under pressure, hardened his line, and Democrats found it politically risky to show any willingness to negotiate with communist states during the Korean and Chinese crises.\n\nThe effect on the State Department was direct. China specialists were moved or dismissed in the early 1950s, and Owen Lattimore was accused in 1950 of being a leading Soviet agent, though he was never charged with espionage. The loss of such experts meant that the government's knowledge of Asia was weakened just as Asia became the centre of US commitments. Eisenhower's administration adopted tighter security checks through Executive Order 10450 of April 1953, and in 1953 McCarthy's investigations of the Voice of America and the State Department's overseas libraries, with aides Roy Cohn and David Schine touring Europe, showed that foreign policy itself was under scrutiny. Eisenhower avoided challenging him in public, so officials played safe.\n\nThe influence of McCarthyism was limited, however. His power collapsed after the Army-McCarthy hearings of 1954, and the Senate censured him on 2 December 1954. Historians such as Ellen Schrecker argue that McCarthyism was a wider system, supported by the FBI under Hoover and by both parties, which McCarthy exploited rather than created, so anti-communism would have shaped policy with or without him. It reinforced a Cold War outlook that already existed rather than changing the direction of policy.\n\nThe anti-war movement, by contrast, grew from a small minority to a mass movement. The first major teach-in was held at the University of Michigan on 24 March 1965, and Students for a Democratic Society organised large marches. Draft resistance spread, Muhammad Ali refused induction in April 1967, and a large march on the Pentagon in October 1967 showed the scale of feeling. Because the draft deferment for college students meant that poorer and black men were more likely to be sent to Vietnam, the war's unfairness became a major theme. The Chicago convention in August 1968, where police beat protesters in front of television cameras, added to the sense that the government was losing control of opinion.\n\nThe effect on policy came from 1968. After the Tet Offensive and mounting opposition, Johnson limited the bombing and decided not to seek re-election in March 1968, and peace talks began. Nixon then pursued Vietnamization, withdrawing US troops, partly because he could not ignore the Vietnam Moratorium demonstrations of October and November 1969, to which he responded with an appeal to the silent majority. The invasion of Cambodia in April 1970 provoked the shootings at Kent State on 4 May, in which four students died, and Congress moved to limit the war, passing a ban on US ground troops in Laos and Thailand in December 1970. The Pentagon Papers, published in June 1971, deepened distrust of the government. The Gulf of Tonkin Resolution was repealed in January 1971.\n\nOverall, McCarthyism affected foreign policy by making it more rigid and less informed, while the anti-war movement affected it by making a prolonged war politically impossible. The anti-war movement had the more direct and decisive effect, since it contributed to Johnson's decision and Nixon's withdrawal, although the war went on for years and historians disagree on whether protest changed the outcome or only sharpened decisions that were already forming.",
