    "label": "2",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The Montgomery bus boycott of 1956 advanced the civil rights movement in several ways. It ended bus segregation in one Southern city, it showed that a disciplined black community could sustain mass non-violent protest, it produced Martin Luther King Jr. and the organisations that followed, and it brought the issue to national and international attention. Its limits, though, were also clear.\n\nThe boycott grew from long grievances. Segregation on Montgomery's buses was enforced by law, with the front rows reserved for white passengers and black passengers made to pay at the front and re-enter by the rear door, although black people made up most of the riders. The Brown v Board of Education decision of May 1954 raised expectations, and Jo Ann Robinson of the Women's Political Council warned the mayor that a boycott was possible. When Rosa Parks, secretary of the local NAACP, was arrested on 1 December 1955 for refusing to give up her seat, leaflets were printed that night and ministers announced a one-day boycott from the pulpits. It was almost completely observed on 5 December, and a mass meeting at the Holt Street Baptist Church voted to continue it.\n\nThe first way in which Montgomery advanced the cause was by proving the power of organised community action. The Montgomery Improvement Association was formed on 5 December 1955 and co-ordinated a car pool of volunteers and black taxi drivers, funded partly by churches and northern supporters, which kept about 40,000 riders off the buses. Many domestic workers walked long distances to work. The boycott lasted 381 days and hit the bus company's income and city centre shops hard, showing that economic pressure could be turned against segregation. This method was later used in other cities and in the sit-in campaigns.\n\nSecond, the boycott made non-violence the guiding principle of the movement. King, a 26-year-old pastor who had arrived in Montgomery only in 1954, was elected president of the MIA, and he combined Christian ideas of love with the methods of Gandhi, helped by advisers such as Bayard Rustin and Glenn Smiley. When his house was bombed on 30 January 1956 with his wife and baby daughter inside, he urged an angry crowd to go home and not retaliate. When 89 leaders were indicted in February 1956 under an Alabama law against boycotts, the sight of ministers lining up to be booked turned the arrests into a symbol of dignity. The disciplined response won sympathy in the North and showed up the violence of white resistance, including the bombing of homes and churches and the banning of the NAACP in Alabama in June 1956.\n\nThird, the boycott produced a legal victory and a lasting organisation. Fred Gray filed Browder v Gayle on 1 February 1956 on behalf of four black women, and on 5 June a federal court ruled by two votes to one that bus segregation broke the Fourteenth Amendment. The Supreme Court affirmed this on 13 November 1956, and the buses were desegregated on 21 December. Some historians argue that the courts, relying on Brown, rather than the boycott, ended bus segregation, but the boycott kept the case alive and made the ruling effective. In 1957 King and other ministers founded the Southern Christian Leadership Conference, which built on the MIA and the black churches and led later campaigns such as Birmingham and Selma. The boycott also gave the movement international publicity at a time when the Soviet Union used American racism as propaganda.\n\nIn conclusion, Montgomery advanced the movement a great deal, by giving it a method, a leader, an organisation and a first clear victory. Yet its direct gain was limited to buses, segregation remained in schools, housing and restaurants, black voters were still largely excluded, and violence followed the victory. Historians such as Aldon Morris argue that the movement drew on local leaders and church networks that already existed, so Montgomery was one stage in a longer struggle, though a vital one, and real change came only with the laws of 1964 and 1965.",
