    "marks": 30,
    "model": "Serfs were peasants bound to the land of their noble owners, who controlled their labour, marriage and movement and could buy or sell them. By the late 1850s privately owned serfs made up more than a third of Russia's population. The main obligations were barshchina, labour dues of several days a week on the lord's land, and obrok, a payment in money or goods. Serfdom held back farming because landlords had little incentive to invest, and Alexander II ended personal serfdom with the emancipation edict of 19 February 1861.",
