    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Soviet willingness to use force was decisive, because Moscow regarded Hungary's neutrality and departure from the Warsaw Pact as unacceptable. On 4 November 1956 a large Soviet force attacked Budapest, crushed the fighting and removed Nagy's government. The West did not intervene militarily, and the Suez crisis drew world attention away from Hungary at the crucial moment. The Soviet Union vetoed Security Council action on Hungary, and the General Assembly condemned the intervention without sending a UN force. The uprising had no organised armed leadership beyond local groups, so it could not defend itself once Moscow chose to use force. Nagy was taken from the Yugoslav embassy on 22 November and, after a secret trial, executed on 16 June 1958.",
