    "marks": 30,
    "model": "Development workers came from churches, voluntary bodies such as Britain's Voluntary Service Overseas, government programmes such as the American Peace Corps, and NGOs such as Concern and Trocaire. Their typical work included well-digging, agricultural advice, primary health care and vaccination, and the supply of food in famines. Many were young volunteers on short contracts who lived in rural communities with little access to medicine, transport and communications. Their problems included poor roads and supplies, cultural misunderstandings, the risk of violence during civil wars and the difficulty of keeping projects running after the donor left.",
