    "label": "B2",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "The Lome Conventions were trade and aid agreements between the EEC and African, Caribbean and Pacific states, beginning with Lome I, which was signed in Togo on 28 February 1975. Lome I gave ACP exports largely duty-free access to European markets without requiring reciprocal concessions, which was a new departure in trade relations. STABEX compensated ACP countries when their earnings from specified agricultural exports fell below a set level. Later Conventions in 1979, 1984 and 1989 added conditions on human rights and governance, before being replaced by the Cotonou Agreement in 2000.",
