    "label": "C1",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Gandhi returned from South Africa in 1915 and became the dominant Congress leader after 1920, building a mass movement among peasants and urban workers. He developed satyagraha, non-violent civil disobedience, which he first used in peasant campaigns at Champaran in 1917 and Kheda in 1918. He led the Non-Cooperation movement of 1920 to 1922 and the Salt March of 1930 to Dandi, which drew very large numbers of people into the independence struggle. At the London Round Table Conference of 1931 he was Congress's only representative, but the talks failed over separate electorates for minorities. He was imprisoned several times, including from August 1942 to May 1944, and the Quit India campaign showed the British that their authority could not be kept by force alone. However, his campaigns did not secure a united India, and he publicly opposed partition, which Congress and League leaders accepted in June 1947.",
