    "label": "C3",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "The secession tested whether the Congo could survive as one state, and the central government's eventual victory with UN help preserved the country's territory. Katanga, the Congo's richest mining province, was cut off from the rest of the state from July 1960 to January 1963. The crisis drew in the UN, which set up ONUC in July 1960 and later was authorised by Resolution 169 of November 1961 to act against the secession. The crisis left the Congolese state dependent on foreign military support, because Belgian officers stayed in the Katangan forces after Belgian troops left. The Simba rebellion of 1964 showed how much of the country remained outside central control. After the secession ended, Mobutu nationalised the Union Miniere's Katanga assets in 1966 to 1967 and created Gecamines, making the province's minerals the central state's main source of revenue.",
