    "label": "C4",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "By the early 1980s the children of North and West African migrants lived in the banlieues, where youth unemployment was far above the national average and relations with the police were poor. The Minguettes unrest of 1981 in Venissieux near Lyon, when young men burned cars and clashed with police, drew national attention to these conditions. A march for equality set out from Marseille in October 1983 after Toumi Djaidja was shot by a police officer, and it reached Paris in December with tens of thousands of supporters. The Front National, founded in 1972 by Jean-Marie Le Pen, campaigned for immigration restriction and national preference, and it won 35 seats in the National Assembly in 1986. The Pasqua laws of 1986 tightened immigration controls, allowed identity checks and made deportation easier. The 1989 headscarf affair at Creil showed the tension between laicite and Muslim identity in schools.",
