    "label": "B1",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "Johnson declared an unconditional War on Poverty in January 1964, and the Economic Opportunity Act created the Job Corps and community action programmes, although these were underfunded as the war in Vietnam grew. The official poverty rate fell from about 22 per cent in 1959 to about 12 per cent in 1969, but poverty rates among black Americans stayed far higher than among whites. Crack cocaine spread through poor urban neighbourhoods in the mid-1980s, producing a wave of violence and drug-related crime. The Anti-Drug Abuse Act of 1986 set mandatory minimum sentences that treated one gram of crack the same as one hundred grams of powder cocaine, a disparity that fell heavily on black defendants.",
