    "model": "William H. Whyte, an editor at Fortune magazine, published The Organization Man in 1956 as a study of white-collar workers in large corporations. Whyte argued that corporate employees had accepted a social ethic that valued belonging, teamwork and conformity over individualism and personal conscience. He studied Park Forest, a suburb of young corporate families in Illinois, and criticised the personality tests that companies used in hiring. Critics saw the Organization Man as a threat to individual creativity and independence, although later scholars argued that the book overstated the conformity of corporate life.",
