    "label": "C2",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "McCarthy's Wheeling speech of 9 February 1950 claimed that communists worked in the State Department, which made the department the main target of his charges. Republicans charged that Truman's government had lost China after the communist victory of October 1949, and McCarthy's attacks amplified this claim. In 1950 he accused the China expert Owen Lattimore of being a leading Soviet agent, and the State Department's China specialists were moved from their posts or dismissed in the early 1950s. His campaign made it politically risky for Democrats to negotiate with communist states, which constrained diplomacy during the Korean and Chinese crises. Eisenhower's administration was accused of appeasing him, and its State Department adopted the loyalty security checks that McCarthy had demanded. The Senate censured McCarthy on 2 December 1954 by 67 votes to 22, which finally ended his influence on policy.",
