    "model": "Massive funding made the programme possible, as Apollo cost about 25 billion dollars and NASA's budget peaked in 1965 and 1966 at about 4 to 5 per cent of federal spending. Sputnik led to the creation of NASA in 1958 and to the National Defense Education Act of 1958, which funded science teaching. The Mercury programme of 1958 to 1963 sent the first Americans into space, and the Gemini programme of 1965 and 1966 tested the rendezvous and docking needed for a Moon landing. NASA chose lunar orbit rendezvous in 1962, with a separate lunar module descending from the command module, and the Saturn V rocket was developed under Wernher von Braun. Major contractors such as North American Aviation, Grumman and Boeing built the spacecraft, and the Apollo Guidance Computer used integrated circuits. Apollo 11 landed in the Sea of Tranquillity on 20 July 1969.",
