    "model": "Charles Trevelyan and Asenath Nicholson made very different contributions to Irish affairs during the Great Famine. Trevelyan, as the senior civil servant at the Treasury, directed the government's relief policy and shaped how Ireland suffered through it, while Nicholson, an American woman, gave relief directly to the poor and left one of the most vivid eyewitness records of the catastrophe.\n\nCharles Trevelyan was born in Taunton in 1807 and served in the East India Company before becoming Assistant Secretary to the Treasury in 1840, its senior permanent official. From 1845 he ran the Treasury's Famine relief, controlling the purchase of Peel's maize, the public works, the food depots and the soup kitchens. Ministers such as Russell and Charles Wood set the principles, but it was Trevelyan who carried them out, so his contribution was central to what the government actually did in Ireland.\n\nTrevelyan's outlook was the key to his influence. He was an evangelical Protestant and a firm believer in laissez-faire and political economy, and he feared that generous relief would make the Irish dependent on the state. He saw the Famine as an act of Providence that would force reform of the Irish land system and population, and he blamed Irish landlords and the character of the people. Under the Whig government from 1846 the state refused to import food or control prices in most of the country, leaving supply to private merchants. The Labouchere Act of August 1846 relaunched public works with the cost to be repaid from local taxes, and by March 1847 about 700,000 people were on the works, working for wages that could not keep pace with food prices that had more than doubled.\n\nHe also insisted that Irish property should pay for Irish poverty. Government soup kitchens under the Temporary Relief Act of February 1847 fed about three million people a day by July, the most effective and cheapest relief of the Famine, yet they were closed by September 1847. The Poor Law Extension Act of June 1847 then threw the burden onto the unions, and its Gregory clause refused relief to anyone holding more than a quarter of an acre, which helped landlords clear estates. Many western unions went bankrupt. Trevelyan declared the Famine over and defended the policy in The Irish Crisis in 1848. He was knighted that year, but nationalists and many relief workers condemned his rigidity, and Cecil Woodham-Smith's The Great Hunger made him the villain of the Famine. Robin Haines has argued more recently that he was a scapegoat for ministers and worked hard within his orders. His later career, the Northcote-Trevelyan Report of 1854 that created a merit-based civil service, shows he was a capable administrator, but in Ireland his ideology cost lives.\n\nAsenath Nicholson's contribution was of a very different kind. Born in Chelsea, Vermont, in 1792, she was a teacher and a widow who promoted temperance and a vegetarian diet. She first came to Ireland in 1844 and walked through almost every county, staying in cabins and reading the Bible to the people she met. Her book Ireland's Welcome to the Stranger, published in 1847, described the poverty and dependence on the potato before the Famine, so she recorded the conditions that made the disaster so severe.\n\nWhen she returned in 1846 she became a relief worker. She set up a soup kitchen in Dublin's Liberties and then travelled to Ulster, Mayo, Erris and Arranmore, distributing food and clothing paid for by appeals in New York newspapers. She acted independently rather than through a committee, which let her reach remote places and see events at first hand. Where Trevelyan relied on reports and accounts, Nicholson stood in the cabins and gave out the food herself.\n\nHer writing gave her lasting importance. Lights and Shades of Ireland in 1850, whose Famine section appeared as Annals of the Famine in Ireland in 1851, condemned landlords, officials and proselytisers alike, and blamed the system rather than its victims. Some officials and clergy found her eccentric and suspected her as a Protestant Bible reader, though the poor welcomed her, and she left Ireland in 1848 and died in Jersey City in 1855. Historians now value her as one of the most sympathetic and vivid witnesses of the Famine.\n\nOverall, the two figures stand at opposite ends of the Famine story. Trevelyan was the powerful official whose adherence to laissez-faire and to Irish property paying for Irish poverty shaped the official response and left a bitter memory in nationalist Ireland. Nicholson had no power and little money, but her direct relief and honest testimony saved some lives and exposed the failures of the system he administered. Trevelyan's contribution was greater in scale and consequence, but Nicholson's is the one that gives the Famine its human face.",
