    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Young Ireland and Fenianism both made major contributions to Irish nationalism between 1842 and 1870. Young Ireland gave nationalism a cultural definition of the nation and the example of an armed attempt in 1848, while the Fenians built the first lasting organisation committed to winning an Irish republic by physical force. Together they created the cultural and physical force traditions that outlived the constitutional movement of O'Connell.\n\nYoung Ireland grew out of the Repeal movement. Charles Gavan Duffy, Thomas Davis and John Blake Dillon founded The Nation on 15 October 1842, and it became the most widely read paper in Ireland, passed from hand to hand and read aloud in reading rooms. It published history, ballads and essays, and the Library of Ireland series extended its educational work. Davis, a Protestant from Mallow, wrote songs such as A Nation Once Again and argued that the nation was a community of shared history, culture and territory that could unite Catholic, Protestant and Dissenter. He urged the study of Irish history and the Irish language. This cultural nationalism was Young Ireland's most lasting contribution, and historians credit Davis with laying its foundations and inspiring later figures such as Pearse and the Gaelic revival, even though his hope of winning Protestants was never realised.\n\nYoung Ireland also challenged the limits of O'Connell's constitutional methods. After clashes over federalism and the Queen's Colleges, the break came over the peace resolutions of July 1846, when Thomas Francis Meagher's Sword Speech refused to condemn force in all circumstances and the group left the Repeal Association. They formed the Irish Confederation in January 1847 under William Smith O'Brien. James Fintan Lalor argued that the land question should be joined to national independence, and John Mitchel, in the United Irishman of 1848, preached open revolution. The movement therefore introduced ideas of social and agrarian radicalism into nationalism.\n\nThe rising of July 1848 failed. After Mitchel's transportation and the suspension of habeas corpus, Smith O'Brien's small band besieged police in the Widow McCormack's farmhouse at Ballingarry on 29 July, and dispersed after two rebels were killed. The people were starving, the clergy were opposed and the leaders were unprepared. Yet the rising mattered, because James Stephens, wounded at Ballingarry, and John O'Mahony both took part and later founded the Fenians, so 1848 became a link in the physical force tradition and Young Ireland's martyrs entered nationalist memory.\n\nFenianism took that tradition much further. The Irish Republican Brotherhood was founded in Dublin on 17 March 1858 by James Stephens as an oath-bound secret society, and in New York John O'Mahony founded the Fenian Brotherhood the same year to raise money and men. The movement drew in artisans, clerks, shopkeepers and farmers' sons, and tried to recruit soldiers in the British army. The funeral of Terence Bellew MacManus in 1861 showed its strength, and its paper the Irish People, founded in 1863 with Luby, O'Leary and Kickham as editors, spread its ideas. Its contribution was a transatlantic, disciplined organisation committed to an Irish republic, which was a new departure.\n\nMilitarily the Fenians failed. The government raided the Irish People in September 1865 and arrested the leaders, the rising of March 1867 was crushed in bad weather, and informers and lack of arms doomed it. The Church, led by Cardinal Cullen, condemned the secret society. But their actions kept the movement in public view. The Manchester Martyrs, Allen, Larkin and O'Brien, were hanged in November 1867 after a police sergeant was killed in rescuing two prisoners, and were mourned across Ireland, while the Clerkenwell explosion that December turned British opinion against the Fenians. The threat to Britain made reform urgent and helped to prompt Gladstone's disestablishment of the Church of Ireland and his Land Act, and the campaign for the release of Fenian prisoners saw them freed between 1869 and 1871.\n\nYoung Ireland's greatest legacy was cultural, a vision of the nation that shaped later nationalism, with the example of 1848 as a footnote in the physical force tradition. Fenianism's contribution was organisational and political, since it kept republicanism alive in a lasting secret organisation that extended into 1916. Neither succeeded in its immediate aims, but between them they gave Irish nationalism its cultural and revolutionary traditions alongside the constitutional one.",
