    "model": "Between 1815 and 1870 Ireland made important contributions in both the creative arts and science, even in a period of poverty, famine and emigration. Writers, musicians and antiquarians drew on Irish history and folklore, while scientists such as Hamilton, Parsons and Boole won international recognition in mathematics, physics and astronomy.\n\nIn literature and music, Thomas Moore's Irish Melodies, published in numbers from 1808 to 1834, set Irish airs to words and were popular in Britain, Ireland and America, bringing Irish music to a wide audience. William Carleton, born in 1794 in Tyrone to a Catholic Irish-speaking tenant family, chronicled the peasantry. His Traits and Stories of the Irish Peasantry, published in 1830 and 1833, described wakes, pilgrimages, faction fights and hedge schools, and The Black Prophet of 1847 was a novel of famine. He is valued as a realist who showed both the violence and the humour of rural life, and he is an important source for pre-Famine society.\n\nThe Nation, founded in 1842, was the centre of a literary nationalism. Thomas Davis wrote ballads such as A Nation Once Again and urged the study of Irish history and language. James Clarence Mangan contributed poems including Dark Rosaleen of 1846, adapted from the Irish, and died in 1849 during the cholera of the Famine years. The Famine itself drew writers to the themes of hunger, eviction and emigration, so the arts recorded the national catastrophe.\n\nScholarship about the Gaelic past also advanced. George Petrie, painter and antiquarian, showed in an essay of 1845 that the round towers were Christian in origin and collected traditional music in The Ancient Music of Ireland in 1855. The Ordnance Survey of the 1830s, directed by Thomas Larcom, employed John O'Donovan and Eugene O'Curry to record place names and study manuscripts. O'Donovan edited the Annals of the Four Masters between 1848 and 1851, and O'Curry became professor of Irish history and archaeology at the Catholic University. The Royal Irish Academy and the Royal Dublin Society supported this work.\n\nIn science, Irish figures achieved world standing. William Rowan Hamilton, Royal Astronomer of Ireland at Dunsink, discovered quaternions on 16 October 1843 while walking by the Royal Canal at Broom Bridge, a major advance in mathematics. William Parsons, third Earl of Rosse, built the six-foot reflecting telescope known as the Leviathan of Parsonstown at Birr Castle, completed in 1845 and then the largest in the world. With it he observed the spiral structure of the galaxy later known as M51, the Whirlpool Galaxy.\n\nOther developments added to this. Nicholas Callan, a priest and professor at Maynooth, built an early induction coil in the 1830s and developed powerful batteries. George Boole, first professor of mathematics at Queen's College Cork from 1849, published The Laws of Thought in 1854, the basis of Boolean algebra. Robert Kane, first president of Queen's College Cork, wrote The Industrial Resources of Ireland in 1844, urging the use of science for economic development.\n\nThe key developments were the creation of a literature and scholarship that gave Irish nationalism and identity a cultural base, and a group of scientists whose work had lasting international importance. The Famine and emigration weakened the rural culture and language that cultural nationalists hoped to preserve, but the achievements of Moore, Carleton, Petrie, O'Donovan, Hamilton, Rosse and Boole show that Ireland's contribution to the arts and sciences in these years was substantial.",
