    "model": "The land agitation of 1870-1914 led to land reform to a very large extent. Agitation forced the state to concede the Three Fs in 1881 and then to finance the transfer of land to the tenants themselves, so that by 1914 landlordism had effectively ended. The reform fell short, however, of what the most radical agitators wanted, and it was driven as much by Conservative calculation as by pressure from the tenants.\n\nThe starting point was the failure of Gladstone's Land Act of 1870. It gave legal force to the Ulster Custom and offered compensation for improvements and for disturbance, but it did not fix rents. Landlords could still raise rents beyond what tenants could pay and then evict for non-payment, and the purchase clauses of the Act led to very little land being sold. Tenants were left without security, which was the root of the later agitation. Reform by legislation alone had therefore failed, and it took mass pressure to move Westminster.\n\nThat pressure came with the agricultural depression of the late 1870s. Bad weather, poor harvests and cheap American grain brought falling prices, rent arrears mounted and evictions rose from about 460 families in 1877 to over 2,000 in 1880. Michael Davitt, a Mayo man evicted as a child, organised the meeting at Irishtown in April 1879, and the Irish National Land League was founded in October 1879 with Parnell as president. Its methods were new and effective: mass meetings, rent strikes and, from Parnell's Ennis speech in 1880, boycotting, named after Captain Boycott, whose workers abandoned him in County Mayo. The Ladies' Land League under Anna Parnell kept the campaign alive when the men were jailed. Gladstone's government answered with coercion under Forster, but also recognised that the tenants could not be ignored.\n\nThe result was the Land Law (Ireland) Act of 1881, which granted the Three Fs: fair rents set by land courts, fixity of tenure and free sale. Judicial rents cut rents by about a fifth on average. This was a real reform and a direct response to the agitation. Yet it was limited. The landlord kept ownership, creating dual ownership, and tenants in arrears were excluded until the Arrears Act of 1882, which was part of the Kilmainham settlement. The Land League was suppressed after the No Rent Manifesto of October 1881 failed, so the agitation itself had faltered even as legislation was passed.\n\nWhen prices fell again in 1886 the Plan of Campaign, launched by William O'Brien, John Dillon and Timothy Harrington, sought reductions estate by estate, and it was applied on about 100 estates. Balfour replied with coercion in 1887, and at Mitchelstown three people were killed by police in September 1887. The Pope's condemnation of the Plan in 1888 and the Parnell split weakened it. Its direct achievements were modest, but the Conservatives now adopted constructive unionism, trying to defuse the land question with funds for purchase. The Ashbourne Act of 1885 advanced the full purchase price to tenants, Balfour's Act of 1891 extended it, and the Congested Districts Board of 1891 targeted the poor west. Here reform followed agitation, but it was a policy of killing Home Rule with kindness rather than a concession to the tenants' demands.\n\nThe decisive reform came with the Land Conference of 1902, where Lord Dunraven, William O'Brien and John Redmond agreed a scheme with the landlords. The Wyndham Act of 1903 offered loans of the full price over about 68 years and a bonus of 12 per cent to landlords who sold, and over 200,000 tenants bought farms within a few years. The Birrell Act of 1909 added compulsory purchase where landlords would not sell. By 1914 most tenants had bought or were buying their holdings. The ultimate goal of the Land League, peasant proprietorship, had been achieved.\n\nOverall, the agitation led to land reform to a great extent, since without the Land War of 1879-82 and the pressure that followed there would have been no Three Fs and no state-financed purchase. But the reform was shaped by compromise and by Conservative strategy, labourers and small western farmers gained least, and Davitt's land nationalisation was never realised. The agitation transformed who owned the land more than it solved rural poverty.",
