    "label": "2",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Ireland's urban poor, above all in Dublin, faced appalling housing, low and insecure wages and a very high death rate. Attempts to solve these problems came from trade unionists, relief organisers, reformers and some employers, but before 1914 they achieved only limited results, and the greatest effort, the unionisation of the unskilled in 1913, ended in defeat.\n\nThe worst problem was housing. Dublin's old Georgian townhouses had been divided into tenements, and the 1911 census showed more than 20,000 families living in one room each. Many houses were owned by landlords who collected high rents for rooms with no running water and a shared yard toilet, and some of these landlords sat on Dublin Corporation, which was slow to act. The danger was real: on 2 September 1913 two tenement houses in Church Street collapsed, killing seven people. Dublin also had one of the highest death rates in the United Kingdom, with tuberculosis and infant mortality especially severe.\n\nThe second problem was work. Dublin had few factories and a large pool of unskilled labour. Most men were casual labourers at the docks, in carting and in building, hired by the day at the stand with no security and very low pay. Women worked in laundries, textile mills, biscuit factories and domestic service for low wages. Belfast was somewhat better, with higher skilled wages and back-to-back terraces rather than tenements, but disease and low pay still afflicted the mill districts, and its prosperity relied on cheap female labour in the linen mills.\n\nThe first attempts to solve these problems came through trade unionism. Craft unions had ignored the unskilled, but from the 1890s the new unionism organised general workers. The Irish Trades Union Congress was founded in 1894, Connolly founded the Irish Socialist Republican Party in 1896, and in 1907 Larkin led the Belfast dockers and carters in a strike that briefly united Catholic and Protestant workers. In 1909 he founded the Irish Transport and General Workers' Union in Dublin, and in 1912 the Irish Labour Party was formed at Clonmel to give workers a voice in parliament. The ITGWU won disputes in Dublin and Cork and fought a lockout in Wexford in 1911.\n\nThe great test was the Dublin lockout of 1913. After William Martin Murphy challenged the union on the trams in August, about 400 employers demanded that workers sign the Document renouncing the ITGWU, and some 20,000 were out of work. British trade unions sent food ships, beginning with the Hare on 27 September, and relief was distributed from Liberty Hall. The Save the Kiddies scheme tried to send strikers' children to homes in Britain, but Archbishop Walsh condemned it and it was stopped. The Irish Citizen Army was founded in November 1913 to protect pickets. The Askwith inquiry condemned the Document and AE's open letter shamed the employers, but the British TUC refused sympathetic action in December and hunger forced a return to work in January 1914, mostly without recognition of the union.\n\nOther attempts addressed housing directly. A housing inquiry by the Local Government Board in 1913-14 exposed tenement conditions, and public sympathy increased pressure for slum clearance. Philanthropic bodies such as the Dublin Artisans' Dwellings Company and the Iveagh Trust built better homes, but these mainly housed skilled workers rather than the casual labourers in the tenements. The Church supported the poor in principle but opposed socialism and condemned the tactics of 1913.\n\nOverall, the problems of poor housing, low pay and disease were never solved before 1914. The unions and the Labour Party made the poor organised and politically visible, and the lockout kept the ITGWU alive and led to the Citizen Army, but the immediate attempts at reform were partial and the tenements remained.",
