    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "All three of the GAA, the Gaelic League and W. B. Yeats made important contributions to the cultural revivals of 1870-1914. The GAA brought sport and a mass organisation into the revival, the Gaelic League brought the language and a mass movement for education and culture, and Yeats gave Ireland a literature and theatre in English. Together they shaped a sense of national identity, though each had its limits.\n\nThe GAA was founded at Hayes' Hotel in Thurles on 1 November 1884 by Michael Cusack and Maurice Davin, after Cusack's article in United Ireland calling for a national body to revive Irish sports. Its aim was to revive hurling and Gaelic football and to open athletics to all classes, since the existing amateur rules excluded manual workers and farmers. It won the patronage of Archbishop Croke, Parnell and Davitt, which gave it respectability and linked it to the national movement. Davin drew up the first rules in 1885, replacing the local variations, and it organised clubs by parish, a structure that suited the Church and allowed priests to play a part. The first All-Ireland championships were held in 1887, with the hurling final at Birr in April 1888.\n\nThe GAA also promoted de-anglicisation through the ban on its members playing or attending foreign games such as rugby and cricket. Its contribution was great, but its early years were troubled. The IRB infiltrated its leadership, Cusack was removed as secretary in 1886, the Church grew uneasy, the American tour of 1888 lost money, and the Parnell split of 1890-91 nearly destroyed it. It recovered in the 1890s and bought grounds at Jones's Road in 1908, later Croke Park. Its lasting contribution was to give ordinary rural people a national and cultural organisation.\n\nThe Gaelic League was founded on 31 July 1893 by Douglas Hyde and Eoin MacNeill, following Hyde's lecture on the necessity for de-anglicising Ireland in November 1892. He argued that the Irish were neither English nor Irish, and called for the language and customs to be revived. The League was officially non-political and non-sectarian, and its branches ran Irish classes, with travelling teachers sent to rural areas. It held feiseanna, started the Oireachtas in 1897 and published An Claidheamh Soluis from 1899, edited by Pearse from 1903. It won real successes, with Irish allowed in primary schools by the Revised Programme of 1900 and made compulsory for entry to the National University from 1913. It drew women, teachers and the urban middle class, unlike the GAA.\n\nThe League did not make Irish an everyday language again, and its non-political stance eroded as IRB members joined from about 1910. Hyde resigned as president in 1915 when it declared for independence. D. P. Moran, through The Leader from 1900, gave the revival a narrower Irish Ireland stance, defining the nation as Gaelic and Catholic. Even so, the League created a generation of activists who later led the independence movement.\n\nYeats approached the revival from the Anglo-Irish tradition. His early poetry, such as The Wanderings of Oisin in 1889, drew on Irish mythology, and he co-founded the National Literary Society in 1892. With Lady Gregory and Edward Martyn he founded the Irish Literary Theatre in 1899, opening with The Countess Cathleen, and with Lady Gregory wrote Cathleen ni Houlihan, staged in 1902 with Maud Gonne. The Abbey Theatre opened on 27 December 1904 with Yeats, Lady Gregory and Synge as directors, giving Ireland a national theatre. He was also involved in controversy, as the Church attacked The Countess Cathleen and the Playboy riots of January 1907 showed nationalist objection to Synge's portrayal of rural Ireland. In September 1913 he attacked the narrow middle class for lacking generosity. He won the Nobel Prize in 1923.\n\nOverall, each made a distinct contribution. The GAA mobilised the masses through sport, the League revived interest in the language and Irish identity, and Yeats gave the revival a literary voice and an international reputation. The revival was divided between inclusive and narrow versions of Irish identity, but it created lasting institutions and gave the nationalist movement self-confidence.",
