    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "John Redmond was the more effective leader before 1914. Both men were forceful and both mobilised a large following, but Redmond won a Home Rule Act and land reform by constitutional means, whereas Carson, for all his success in obstructing Home Rule, ended the period with Ulster's future unsettled and the Union itself broken.\n\nRedmond's first achievement was to rebuild a party that had been shattered by the Parnell split of 1890-91. He had stayed loyal to Parnell and led the small Parnellite group after Parnell died in 1891, and he went on to lead the reunited Irish Parliamentary Party for eighteen years. Healing the division gave nationalism a disciplined parliamentary voice again. This mattered because the decade of bitterness after 1891 had wasted the gains of the Parnell years, and only a united party could exploit the political situation after 1910.\n\nHis second achievement was in land reform. Redmond and William O'Brien took part in the Land Conference of 1902-03, which brought landlords and tenant representatives together and led to the Wyndham Act, under which tenants were enabled to buy out their holdings with state-aided loans. This finished the work that the land agitation of the 1880s had begun and transformed the Irish countryside into one of peasant proprietors. It showed Redmond as a negotiator who could deliver concrete results, and it won him the confidence of the farming class who formed the backbone of the nationalist vote.\n\nRedmond's greatest success was to use the balance of power at Westminster. The two elections of 1910 left the Liberals dependent on the Irish Party, which held 82 seats in January and 84 in December. Redmond backed the Liberals after the Lords rejected the People's Budget, and the Parliament Act of 1911 removed the Lords' veto and left them only a two-year delay. Asquith introduced the Third Home Rule Bill in April 1912, it passed the Commons three times, and it received the royal assent on 18 September 1914. That Home Rule reached the statute book at all was a remarkable vindication of Redmond's constitutional strategy, something neither Butt nor Parnell had achieved.\n\nCarson's effectiveness lay in a different place. A Dublin-born barrister and MP for Dublin University, he became leader of the Irish Unionists in 1910 and, with James Craig as his organiser, turned the opposition to Home Rule into a mass movement. On Ulster Day, 28 September 1912, he was the first to sign the Solemn League and Covenant, and about 237,000 men signed it, with about 234,000 women signing a separate declaration. In January 1913 the Ulster Volunteer Force was formed, reaching perhaps 90,000 to 100,000 men, and in September 1913 he announced a Provisional Government of Ulster to take over if Home Rule became law. He won Conservative backing, as Bonar Law pledged at Blenheim in July 1912, and the Curragh incident of March 1914 showed that the army could not be relied on to coerce Ulster. The Larne gun-running of April 1914, with about 25,000 rifles landed, made the threat of force credible. Carson's campaign forced the government to accept the principle of Ulster's exclusion.\n\nYet Carson's effectiveness was limited. He could not stop the Third Home Rule Bill becoming law, and he rejected Asquith's offer of a six-year opt-out in March 1914 as a stay of execution. The Buckingham Palace Conference of July 1914 failed over Tyrone and Fermanagh, and his southern unionist followers were left without protection. His success was largely negative, a blocking tactic that depended on Conservative support and on the threat of civil war, and he later lamented that unionism had been sacrificed in the creation of Northern Ireland.\n\nRedmond, too, had his failures. He never solved the Ulster problem, and in March 1914 he accepted that Ulster counties might opt out for six years, a concession of the principle of partition. His reliance on Liberal goodwill was criticised by Griffith and the IRB, and his takeover of the Irish Volunteers in June 1914 and his Woodenbridge speech of September 1914 split the movement, with about 170,000 following him and about 11,000 staying with MacNeill. Even so, these weaknesses largely belong to the crisis of 1914 and the war, when the Home Rule Act was suspended.\n\nOn balance Redmond was the more effective leader in the period to 1914. Carson was a brilliant organiser of resistance who protected Ulster's position, but Redmond achieved a lasting land settlement, reunited his party and secured a Home Rule Act, results that stood at the end of the period while Carson's campaign only delayed and distorted what Redmond had won.",
