    "label": "1",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The Anglo-Irish Treaty of December 1921 was largely responsible for the Civil War, because it split the republican movement over the Crown, the oath and partition. But it was not the only cause. The way it was negotiated and signed, the divisions in the IRA, and the actions of both sides in the spring and summer of 1922 turned a political split into a war.\n\nThe terms of the Treaty were the main source of division. It made the Free State a dominion with the same status as Canada. Members of the Free State parliament had to swear faithfulness to the Constitution and to the King, a Governor-General represented the Crown, and Britain kept the Treaty ports of Berehaven, Queenstown and Lough Swilly. Northern Ireland could opt out of the Free State, with a Boundary Commission to settle the border. Republicans who had taken an oath to the Republic declared in 1919 saw this as a betrayal of the Republic. Supporters like Collins said it gave the freedom to achieve freedom, and that refusal meant renewed war with an IRA short of arms. The Dáil approved the Treaty on 7 January 1922 by only 64 votes to 57, and all six women deputies opposed it. The narrow vote shows how deep the division was.\n\nThe way the Treaty was reached made the split worse. De Valera chose the delegation but stayed in Dublin, and the delegates were named plenipotentiaries while their instructions told them to report back before signing. Under pressure from Lloyd George, who warned of immediate and terrible war, Griffith, Collins and the other delegates signed at about 2.10 am on 6 December without sending the final text to Dublin. De Valera was angry and opposed the Treaty on these grounds. The cabinet divided four to three on 8 December, and his alternative, Document No. 2, based on external association, was never put to the British. The personal and procedural dispute between the leaders made compromise harder.\n\nThe army was the second major factor. The IRA split, and in March 1922 most of its senior officers, led by Rory O'Connor and Liam Lynch, rejected the authority of the Dáil. The anti-Treaty IRA seized the Four Courts on 14 April 1922. A pro-Treaty National Army was recruited at the same time, so two armed forces existed side by side. Barracks were taken over as the British left, and this led to local clashes. The Collins-de Valera pact of 20 May aimed to avoid conflict but was repudiated by Collins before the election. Here the war was driven as much by the militarisation of the dispute as by the Treaty itself.\n\nThe final triggers came in June 1922. In the general election of 16 June pro-Treaty candidates won 58 seats and anti-Treaty candidates 36, a clear popular verdict for the Treaty. The assassination of Sir Henry Wilson on 22 June led the British government to press Collins to act against the Four Courts, and the kidnapping of General J.J. O'Connell by the garrison on 26 June added to the pressure. On 28 June the National Army shelled the Four Courts with British artillery, and the Civil War began. Partition also played a part, as Collins's support for the IRA in the North and the Boundary Commission issue kept the border central to the dispute.\n\nIn conclusion, the Treaty was the cause of the Civil War in the sense that without it the split over the Crown, the oath and the republic would not have happened. But it took the division of the IRA, the bitterness of the signing, the pressure from Britain and the events of June 1922 to turn a political disagreement into civil war.",
