    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The Eucharistic Congress of June 1932 was highly significant for the Irish Free State. It gave a young state the chance to present itself to the world as stable, devout and orderly ten years after the Civil War, it displayed the close alliance between the Catholic Church and the political leadership, and it brought credit to the new government of Eamon de Valera. It also exposed the religious divide between North and South and the unresolved status of the Crown's representative.\n\nThe Congress was an international gathering of Catholics, and the 31st was awarded to Dublin in 1932 because it was the 1,500th anniversary of the traditional date of St Patrick's arrival in 432. This tied Catholicism closely to Irish identity. The Church was already powerful, since the vast majority of the population was Catholic and the clergy managed most schools and hospitals. Archbishop Edward Byrne of Dublin led the organisation and the Pope sent a legate, Cardinal Lorenzo Lauri, so the event was treated as a national occasion by Church and state alike.\n\nThe Congress was significant first as a demonstration of the state's ability and stability. It ran from 22 to 26 June 1932. Dublin was decorated with altars, flags and illuminations, special trains and sailings carried pilgrims, and the Garda and the Army managed enormous crowds. On 26 June a closing Mass was held in the Phoenix Park before a crowd often put at about a million, and four processions then went to O'Connell Bridge, where Cardinal Lauri gave the benediction. The week passed without major disorder, which was a striking sign of public order only ten years after the Civil War. John McCormack sang at the Mass, and the Pope's message was broadcast by radio, using a station set up at Athlone that later became Radio Éireann. Foreign newspapers reported the event, giving the Free State international publicity.\n\nSecond, it showed the alliance of Church and state. De Valera, known for his personal piety, took a leading part, and he and Archbishop Byrne received the legate at Dún Laoghaire on 21 June, with about 36,000 schoolchildren lining the route to the Pro-Cathedral. The Congress had been planned under Cosgrave, but it took place under de Valera, who had been in office only since March 1932, and he gained much of the credit. It gave his new Fianna Fáil government the chance to show that it could work with the hierarchy, a relationship that shaped the Catholic character of the 1937 constitution. Historians have called the Congress the high point of the Church's public influence in the new state.\n\nThird, it had a political edge concerning sovereignty. The Governor-General, James McNeill, who represented the Crown, was not invited to the reception for the legate at Dublin Castle, and he responded by holding his own dinner and complaining to de Valera in writing in July. The affair formed part of the dispute that led to his dismissal in October 1932. De Valera used the occasion to present his government as Catholic and independent of the Crown.\n\nFinally, the Congress deepened the divide between North and South. Pilgrims travelling to Dublin were attacked in Northern Ireland, with trains stoned at places such as Portadown, Lisburn and Belfast, and police baton-charged at some stations. Unionists saw the Congress as proof that the Free State was a Catholic state, confirming their belief in partition, while northern nationalists felt pride in an Irish Catholic nation.\n\nOverall, the Congress was a great public triumph that strengthened national confidence, de Valera's prestige and the Church's standing, though its effects were largely symbolic. It expressed the Catholic and Gaelic identity of the 1930s and also hardened the divisions on the island, so its significance was both a source of national pride and a sign of the conformity of the new state.",
