    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Irish missionaries had a profound impact in Africa and Asia, above all in education and health care, and in building local churches that outlasted colonial rule. They also had a lasting effect on Ireland itself, where they gave the country a sense of a spiritual empire. Their impact was not wholly positive, since they were tied to colonial states, were slow to hand over leadership and were criticised for undermining local culture.\n\nThe missionary effort was large because the Irish church had grown strong after the Famine. The reforms of Archbishop Paul Cullen, called the devotional revolution by Emmet Larkin, produced more priests, brothers and nuns than Ireland needed, and the surplus went abroad. Societies were founded to channel them. The Maynooth Mission to China was set up in 1916 by Edward Galvin and Father John Blowick and became the Missionary Society of St Columban, with its headquarters at Dalgan Park, Navan. The Holy Rosary Sisters were founded at Killeshandra in 1924 by Bishop Joseph Shanahan, and the Medical Missionaries of Mary by Mary Martin in 1937. Irish writers linked all this to Columbanus and the island of saints and scholars, and the phrase Ireland's spiritual empire expressed it.\n\nEducation was the most important impact, and Nigeria shows it best. Joseph Shanahan, a Tipperary Holy Ghost priest who arrived in 1902, made the school the main instrument of conversion, building village schools staffed by catechists. After 1945 the mission opened new parishes, schools and training colleges across south-eastern Nigeria, and mission schools became the main route to education in the region. Under the grant-in-aid system the colonial government paid towards the cost of the schools, and many of the educated Nigerians who later served in the new state had passed through them. This gave the mission a lasting influence in the country's elite.\n\nHealth care was the second great contribution. Until 1936 the Church forbade sisters to practise obstetrics and surgery, and when that restriction was lifted Mary Martin founded the Medical Missionaries of Mary, with its mother house at Drogheda. The order trained doctors, nurses and midwives and set up hospitals and clinics where few medical services existed. The Holy Rosary Sisters likewise ran hospitals and dispensaries in rural Nigeria. For Irish women this was a rare chance of leadership in nursing and medicine, and the work delivered real benefits to people in areas where government services were scarce.\n\nThe missionaries also helped create local churches with African leadership, although slowly. The Catholic hierarchy was established in Nigeria in 1950, with the Irish Spiritan Charles Heerey as Archbishop of Onitsha. The Catholic population of the region grew strongly and local vocations rose. Michael Tansi, an Igbo, was ordained in 1937 and beatified in 1998, and in 1965 Francis Arinze became auxiliary bishop of Onitsha at the age of 32. After independence in 1960 leadership passed increasingly to Nigerian clergy. In Asia the story was harder. Galvin led the Hanyang mission in China from the 1920s, and its churches, schools, orphanages and clinics survived civil conflict and Japanese invasion, but after the Communist takeover of 1949 he was placed under house arrest, tried and expelled in 1952.\n\nThere were limits and criticisms. Mission education promoted Christian and Western values, and critics said it weakened local culture and languages. The missionaries were accused of paternalism because Irish leadership continued long after local people were qualified. Catholic and Protestant rivalry over schools and converts divided communities, and the dependence on colonial grants tied the mission to the state. The Biafran war, which began in 1967, damaged or closed many mission stations, though Irish Holy Ghost Father Anthony Byrne helped organise the relief airlift and the Irish relief agency Concern was founded in 1968 in response to the famine. After the war the state took over mission schools.\n\nOn balance the impact was considerable. Irish missionaries built schools, hospitals and churches that outlasted colonial rule, trained local teachers, nurses and priests, and helped produce educated African leaders. Their cultural influence and slowness in localising leadership qualify that achievement, but their practical contribution to education and health was their most enduring legacy.",
