    "label": "2",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The choice of Coleraine as the site of Northern Ireland's second university was controversial because it passed over Derry, the province's second city, which had a strong case and a mostly nationalist population. Nationalists, and many Derry unionists too, saw the decision as a test of whether Stormont would treat the west fairly, and they concluded that it had failed. The government insisted that Coleraine was a sound planning choice, so the dispute was about motives as much as geography.\n\nDerry's claim was strong on its merits. Magee College, founded by the Presbyterian Church in 1865, already stood in the city and taught degrees of Trinity College Dublin, though it lacked full university status. Derry was the second largest city in Northern Ireland, with higher unemployment and more emigration than the Belfast area. After the Robbins Report of 1963 recommended a major expansion of higher education across the United Kingdom, Derry argued that a university would bring jobs, investment and prestige to a city that had been neglected for decades. Passing over such a case was bound to cause anger.\n\nThe campaign to win the university was also unusually broad. The University for Derry Committee was chaired by John Hume, then a young teacher, and it drew support from nationalists, some Protestants and business figures in the city. This mattered because the decision could not be dismissed as a purely Catholic complaint. When the Lockwood Committee, appointed in 1963 under Sir John Lockwood, reported in February 1965 in favour of Coleraine, Derry supporters organised a motorcade to Stormont in protest. The size and cross-community character of the protest showed how deeply the decision was resented.\n\nThe controversy also turned on suspicion of the way the decision was made. The Stormont government accepted the Lockwood recommendation, and a unionist majority carried it against nationalist members and some Derry unionists. Derry representatives alleged that the choice was made on political grounds, and the phrase 'faceless men' became a popular slogan. The government replied that Coleraine, a smaller town about 30 miles from Derry, offered a central site, good access and room to expand, and that Derry's case had been fully considered. No proof of a plot was ever produced, but the choice of a small town over a large city weakened the claim that investment was evenly spread, so the allegation was widely believed in Derry.\n\nThe decision fed into the wider 'west of the Bann' grievance, which is the strongest reason it became so controversial. Nationalists argued that new industry and public investment were being placed east of the River Bann. They pointed to the designation of Craigavon in County Armagh in 1965 as the site of a new city, and to the closure of the Derry Road railway line in 1965, which left Derry without a direct rail link to Belfast. Derry also had unionist control of its corporation in a majority Catholic city, because the ward boundaries had been drawn in 1936 to concentrate Catholic voters, so the city's own representatives had little power to influence these decisions. Against this background Coleraine looked like one more decision against the west.\n\nThe long-term consequences also explain why the controversy mattered. The New University of Ulster opened at Coleraine in 1968, while the Derry campaign continued. Hume gained experience in cross-community organising and in public protest, and he later led the Derry Citizens' Action Committee after the march of 5 October 1968. The university issue was linked to housing and voting grievances, and fed directly into the demands of the civil rights movement in Derry in 1968 and 1969. Nationalist distrust of Stormont deepened as a result.\n\nOverall, the choice of Coleraine was controversial because Derry had a strong economic and educational case, because a united Derry campaign was rejected by a unionist majority, and because the decision seemed to confirm a pattern of favouring the east. Unionists saw a rational planning decision, but the lack of any explanation that satisfied Derry meant that the decision became a symbol of inequality in public investment, rather than just a question of where to build a university.",
