    "label": "2",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Sylvia Meehan and Mary Robinson made distinct but complementary contributions to the changing role of women in the Republic between 1949 and 1989. Meehan helped to turn the equality laws of the 1970s into working practice through her leadership of the Employment Equality Agency, while Robinson used the law and the Oireachtas to challenge discrimination and to open up debate on social reform. Neither worked alone, but both were central to a period in which women gained legal equality in many areas.\n\nTo understand their contribution it is necessary to see the position women started from. In 1949 women in the civil service and many other public posts had to resign when they married, under the marriage bar, and women were paid less than men for similar work. The bar was removed for primary teachers in 1958 and for the civil service in 1973, and the Commission on the Status of Women, chaired by Thekla Beere and set up in 1970, reported in 1972 with 49 recommendations on equal pay, employment, education and social welfare. Equal pay for the public service followed in 1973. This created the programme of reform that campaigners like Meehan and Robinson helped to carry out.\n\nSylvia Meehan, born in Dublin in 1929, was a teacher, vice-president of the Association of Secondary Teachers, Ireland and chairperson of the women's committee of the Irish Congress of Trade Unions. Her trade union background gave her direct experience of unequal treatment at work and a route to lobby for change. When the Employment Equality Act 1977 made it unlawful to discriminate on grounds of sex or marital status in recruitment, training and promotion, it set up the Employment Equality Agency, and Meehan was appointed its first chairperson in 1977, holding the post until 1992.\n\nIn that role Meehan's contribution was to make the law work. The Anti-Discrimination (Pay) Act 1974 had required equal pay for the same or similar work, but a law on paper changed little unless it was enforced. Under her the agency supervised research, investigated complaints and advised the government. Its studies of girls and women in education, pay, maternity leave and childcare provided evidence that helped to shape later legislation, such as the Maternity Protection of Employees Act 1981. She was regarded as a determined advocate, although employers and some politicians resisted the agency's findings and its powers were limited.\n\nMary Robinson, born in Ballina, Co. Mayo, in 1944, brought a different strength, that of a lawyer. She was called to the bar in 1967, studied at Harvard and became Reid Professor of Law at Trinity at the age of 25. Elected to the Seanad for the University of Dublin in 1969, she served until 1989 as an independent. In the early 1970s she introduced private members' bills to liberalise the law on contraception, which the Seanad refused to debate. They did not pass, but they placed a taboo subject before the public, and contraception was eventually legalised through the McGee case of 1973 and the Health (Family Planning) Act 1979.\n\nRobinson also campaigned for women to be allowed to serve on juries, a change enacted in the Juries Act 1976, and as a barrister she took cases for individuals against the State. These included a tax case brought by a married couple, decided in their favour by the Supreme Court in 1980, and the long case of David Norris against the law criminalising homosexual acts. She later left the Labour Party in 1985 in protest at the Anglo-Irish Agreement. Conservatives and the church attacked her views, but she was respected for her persistence, and historians credit her with creating a legal and political basis for reform that prepared the way for the changes of the 1990s and her election as President in 1990.\n\nTheir achievements need to be kept in proportion. Women remained a small minority in the Dáil, with only 14 elected by 1987, and pay gaps persisted. Divorce and abortion remained prohibited, and the church kept great influence. Still, married women's participation in the workforce rose from about 5 per cent in 1961 to about 17 per cent in 1981. Meehan and Robinson, working alongside the Irish Women's Liberation Movement and the Commission on the Status of Women, played a major part in that change. Meehan secured the practical enforcement of equality at work, and Robinson created the legal and political arguments for wider reform, so together they made a significant contribution to the changing role of women.",
