    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Jack Lynch was a reasonably effective Taoiseach in his handling of Ireland's internal and external affairs, though his record was stronger in external matters than in domestic ones. Externally he kept the state out of the Northern conflict and brought Ireland into the EEC, while internally he held Fianna Fáil together, won two elections and survived the Arms Crisis. However, his second government's economic policy was reckless, and in the end he lost control of events.\n\nLynch became Taoiseach on 10 November 1966 as a compromise choice between rival candidates, succeeding Lemass. A famous hurler with six All-Ireland medals and a barrister, he was popular and conciliatory, and he had served as Minister for Education, Industry and Commerce and Finance. He won the 1969 general election with a majority of seats, which showed his standing with the public, but he lost power in 1973 to the National Coalition of Fine Gael and Labour.\n\nInternally, Lynch's greatest test was the Arms Crisis of 1970. After being told by the Special Branch that senior ministers might be involved in a plan to import arms for Northern nationalists, he dismissed Charles Haughey and Neil Blaney on 6 May 1970, and Kevin Boland resigned in protest. Haughey and the other accused were acquitted in October 1970. Lynch preserved the authority of the government and the state's commitment to constitutional politics, but critics said he was slow to act and the crisis left a lasting division in Fianna Fáil between his supporters and Haughey's.\n\nIn 1977 Lynch's party won 84 of 148 seats, the largest majority in the history of the state, on a manifesto promising to abolish domestic rates and the tax on cars and to create jobs by increasing spending. The government cut taxes and increased spending, financed by borrowing, and the budget deficit and national debt rose sharply. Inflation, a second oil crisis after the Iranian Revolution of 1979 and the cost of the manifesto turned growth into crisis. Fianna Fáil lost two by-elections in Cork in November 1979, and Lynch resigned in December, when Haughey defeated George Colley for the leadership. This was the weakest part of his record.\n\nExternally, Lynch's main challenge was Northern Ireland. After violence in Derry and Belfast in August 1969 he said the state could not stand by and set up army field hospitals near the border, but he ruled out military intervention and sought unity by consent. He condemned internment in August 1971, took Britain to the European Court of Human Rights, and recalled the Irish ambassador after Bloody Sunday in January 1972. He met Edward Heath and Brian Faulkner at Chequers in 1971, arguing that only an Irish dimension could end the violence. In December 1972 he secured amendments to the Offences Against the State Act to deal with the IRA. By avoiding both military action and inaction he kept the state out of the conflict, even if he did not shape a settlement.\n\nHis greatest external success was entry to the EEC. Patrick Hillery led the negotiating team, the Treaty of Accession was signed in Brussels on 22 January 1972, and a referendum on 10 May 1972 produced about 83 per cent in favour. Ireland joined with Britain and Denmark on 1 January 1973, giving farmers access to a large market and attracting investment. Lynch's second government also took Ireland into the European Monetary System in March 1979, breaking the link with sterling for the first time since the 1820s.\n\nOverall, Lynch was effective in external affairs, where he kept Ireland out of the Troubles and secured EEC membership, and partly effective at home, where he preserved the unity of his party and his own popularity but allowed the economy to deteriorate after 1977. Contemporaries admired his decency and moderation but doubted his firmness, and that judgement is a fair one.",
