    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The communications revolution had a profound impact on Ireland between the 1960s and the 1980s. It reduced the isolation of rural communities, brought new ideas and entertainment into every home, undermined the control of the church and the censor, and changed the way that politics was conducted. Television, above all, made Ireland a more open, questioning society, although it largely accelerated changes that were already under way.\n\nThe revolution involved more than television. The number of cars on the road rose several times over between 1960 and 1980, and better roads, bus services and air travel reduced rural isolation. Telephone services, once poor, were improved, and Bord Telecom Éireann was set up in 1984. Newspapers changed too, with the Irish Times under Douglas Gageby from 1963 becoming a forum for new ideas and Magill, founded in 1977, expanding political journalism. Pirate radio challenged RTÉ's monopoly in the 1970s and 1980s, and local radio was licensed in 1988.\n\nTelevision was the most powerful change. Telefís Éireann began on 31 December 1961, and within a few years it reached most of the country, so that by the early 1970s most households had a set. It altered how people spent their evenings, since families stayed at home to watch rather than go to dances or visit neighbours, and it brought national and international news and sport to the countryside. Programmes such as The Riordans, a drama about a farming family that began in 1965, reflected rural life and its changes, and Telefís Scoile, from 1964, was among the earliest schools television services in Europe. RTÉ also raised standards for Irish drama and music, and gave writers and entertainers a national audience.\n\nTelevision also had a major impact on attitudes to religion and morality. The Late Late Show, presented by Gay Byrne from 1962, discussed sex, marriage, contraception, unmarried mothers and the church, subjects rarely spoken of in public. In 1966 the episode known as the Bishop and the Nightie caused uproar when a wife said she had not worn a nightdress on her honeymoon night, and Bishop Thomas Ryan of Clonfert condemned the programme, though many viewers defended it. The episode showed that the church's authority could now be debated openly. This was reinforced by Vatican II and the 1967 Censorship of Publications Act, which limited bans to twelve years and freed thousands of titles. Church leaders such as McQuaid worried about the influence of television on faith and morals, although the church also used it to broadcast Mass and the Angelus.\n\nTelevision changed politics, and also brought conflict with the government. Politicians had to learn to speak to voters through the medium, and campaigns began to focus on appearance and image. Current affairs programmes such as Seven Days, launched in 1966, investigated housing, poverty and emigration, and ministers resented the criticism. The Troubles made coverage of Northern Ireland a major source of conflict. In October 1971 the Minister for Posts and Telegraphs, Gerry Collins, issued the first Section 31 order, and in November 1972, after the This Week interview with Seán Mac Stíofáin, he dismissed the entire RTÉ Authority. Section 31 restrictions were extended and not ended until 1994. This showed that the state still tried to control the new medium.\n\nOverall, the communications revolution made Ireland less isolated, more informed and more willing to question authority in church and state. Television exposed viewers to arguments about contraception, divorce and the role of the church, and also brought British and American culture into Irish homes, which worried language activists. Historians such as Robert Savage argue that RTÉ both reflected changes already under way and helped to speed them, which seems to me the fairest conclusion.",
