    "label": "1",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The revolution in Germany in 1848 was caused by a combination of economic hardship, liberal and national frustration with the settlement of 1815, and the example of the revolution in Paris. Its results were disappointing for the liberals, since no united Germany was created and the old order returned, but it did bring some lasting gains, notably the end of feudal dues and the experience of a national parliament.\n\nThe economic causes were immediate. The potato blight from 1845 and poor grain harvests in 1846 and 1847 sharply raised food prices and caused hunger riots across the German states. A commercial and industrial depression in 1847 brought bankruptcies and unemployment, which hit artisans already threatened by factory competition. The Silesian weavers' rising of 1844 had already shown the desperation of hand workers. Distress among the poor therefore created a volatile population ready to join any rising.\n\nPolitical grievances provided the leadership and the programme. Liberals, mostly educated middle-class men, wanted constitutions, freedom of the press and assembly, trial by jury and a German national parliament, while nationalists wanted a nation state in place of the 39 states of the German Confederation. Peasants in the south and west wanted an end to feudal dues. Liberal opposition was growing, as in Prussia's United Diet of 1847, which refused a railway loan without constitutional concessions. The fall of Louis-Philippe in Paris in February 1848 supplied the spark, and it spread by newspapers, railways and the telegraph.\n\nThe revolution began with the March days. A mass meeting at Mannheim on 27 February demanded a free press, juries and a German parliament, and rulers in Baden, Württemberg, Saxony and Hanover appointed liberal ministries rather than risk overthrow. Ludwig I of Bavaria abdicated. In Berlin troops fired on a crowd on 18 March and barricade fighting left about 300 civilians dead, after which Frederick William IV withdrew the troops, honoured the dead and promised that Prussia would merge into Germany. A self-appointed Vorparlament met at Frankfurt, and the Frankfurt National Assembly opened in the Paulskirche on 18 May 1848.\n\nThe results began to emerge as the Assembly proved unable to act. It was the professors' parliament, with almost no peasants or workers, and it had no army, civil service or revenue. It passed the Basic Rights in December 1848 but split over whether a Germany should include Austria (Grossdeutsch) or be led by Prussia (Kleindeutsch). Its weakness was exposed in the Schleswig-Holstein crisis, when Prussia signed the Malmö armistice in August 1848 without consulting it and the Assembly had to accept. Meanwhile the old governments recovered, with Windischgrätz crushing Prague in June and retaking Vienna in October, and Frederick William dissolving the Prussian Assembly in December 1848 and imposing his own constitution.\n\nThe decisive result came in 1849. The Assembly offered the imperial crown to Frederick William IV, who refused it in April because he would not accept a crown from an elected assembly. Austria, Prussia, Bavaria, Saxony and Hanover rejected the constitution, so it never came into force. Radical risings in Saxony, the Palatinate and Baden were crushed by Prussian troops by July 1849, the rump parliament was dispersed at Stuttgart, and thousands of revolutionaries emigrated, many to the United States. Prussia's Erfurt Union failed too, and at Olmütz in November 1850 Prussia gave way to Austria and Russia and the old Confederation was restored in 1851.\n\nOverall, the revolution failed to create German unity or liberal government, and the Basic Rights were repealed in 1851. Yet it abolished serfdom and feudal dues, left Prussia with a constitution and parliament, and gave Germany its first national assembly and political parties. Its long-term lesson, which Bismarck later exploited, was that unity would come through Prussian power rather than through liberal debate.",
