    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Life was harsh for both agricultural and industrial workers in Europe between 1815 and 1871 because economic change moved faster than the protections of law, wages or public provision. Farm labourers lost land, rights and work to enclosure, mechanisation and bad harvests, while factory workers faced long hours, dangerous work and filthy housing in cities that grew far faster than their services. Governments, committed to laissez-faire, responded slowly and unevenly.\n\nIn Britain agricultural workers were hit by enclosure, carried out through thousands of Acts of Parliament, mostly between 1750 and 1850. Open fields and common land were replaced by consolidated farms, and many labourers lost common rights and their access to land, leaving them dependent on wages. The Corn Laws of 1815 kept grain prices high for the benefit of landowners, so bread was dear for the poor. Mechanisation, including the threshing machine, reduced the need for hand labour at harvest, and the anger this caused fed the Swing Riots of 1830 in southern England. Workers who tried to organise were punished: six Dorset labourers, the Tolpuddle Martyrs, were transported to Australia in 1834 for swearing a union oath.\n\nOn the continent the position of the rural poor was also difficult. In Prussia the Stein-Hardenberg reforms from 1807 abolished serfdom in stages, but many peasants had to surrender part of their land to the noble estates in order to gain ownership, and the great Junker estates of the east grew grain for the market. Peasants in France owned much land after 1789, but farms were small and families were vulnerable when the harvest failed. The crisis of 1846 to 1847, with poor harvests and the potato blight from 1845, caused high food prices and widespread hunger across western and central Europe, and it helped create the conditions for the revolutions of 1848. Many rural people left, with German emigration to the United States growing strongly in the late 1840s and 1850s.\n\nIndustrial workers faced the discipline of the factory system, with 12 to 14 hours a day common in British cotton mills. Children and women made up a large share of the textile workforce, and children could start work at an early age. Accidents, machinery injuries and poor ventilation were common, and little compensation was paid to the injured. The Sadler Committee of 1832 gathered evidence of the abuse of child workers, and reform came only gradually, through the Factory Act of 1833 barring children under nine from most textile factories, the Mines Act of 1842 banning women and children under ten from underground work, and the Ten Hours Act of 1847. Even these acts regulated hours and age but not wages.\n\nHousing and health in the new towns added to the misery. Manchester grew from about 75,000 in 1801 to about 300,000 in 1851, and the 1851 census showed over half of Britain's people living in towns. Overcrowded back-to-back houses, cellar dwellings such as Little Ireland in Manchester, open sewers and shared privies caused high death rates. Cholera struck Britain in 1831 to 1832 and 1848 to 1849 and Paris in 1832 and 1849, killing thousands. Engels in 1845 described the conditions of the Manchester working class, and Chadwick's report of 1842 linked bad living conditions to disease, but the Public Health Act of 1848 only began to address the problem.\n\nPolitical attitudes and the law helped keep life harsh. Laissez-faire doctrines, expressed by Adam Smith and later economists, held that the state should not interfere in the market, and the Poor Law Amendment Act of 1834 forced the poor to enter workhouses to get relief. The Combination Acts of 1799 and 1800 made it illegal for workers to combine until their repeal in 1824, and even afterwards unions were limited, the Grand National Consolidated Trades Union collapsed in 1834 and skilled workers alone benefited from the New Model unions of the 1850s. Workers excluded from the vote turned to Chartism, whose petitions were rejected in 1839, 1842 and 1848.\n\nLife was therefore harsh for both groups because their situations were shaped by the same forces: loss of land or traditional work, wages kept low by competition and government unwillingness to intervene. The rural labourer lost his land and the factory worker his health, and in both cases reform was slow. By the 1870s factory laws, public health measures and primary education had brought real improvements, but the costs of change were paid first and most heavily by the working poor.",
