    "model": "Bismarck managed Germany's external affairs better than its internal affairs. In foreign policy he kept the new Reich secure and France isolated for nearly twenty years, whereas at home several of his policies failed, and he left Germany with divisions he had deepened rather than healed.\n\nIn domestic affairs Bismarck's greatest weakness was the Kulturkampf. Fearing that Catholics owed loyalty to Rome after the First Vatican Council declared papal infallibility in 1870, he attacked the Church with the pulpit law of 1871, the expulsion of the Jesuits in 1872 and the May Laws of 1873 piloted by Falk, and made civil marriage compulsory in 1875. Bishops were imprisoned or exiled and many parishes had no priest, but Catholics resisted and the Centre Party doubled its seats to become the second-largest party. By 1878 to 1879 Bismarck was seeking peace with Pope Leo XIII, and most of the laws were relaxed or repealed in the 1880s. He had strengthened the very force he meant to crush and showed the limits of his power.\n\nHis campaign against socialism was no more successful. After attempts on the life of Wilhelm I in 1878, he used the public fear to pass the Anti-Socialist Law, banning socialist organisations, meetings and newspapers. Yet socialists could still stand for the Reichstag, and the SPD vote kept rising, reaching about 1.4 million and 35 seats in 1890. Bismarck's state insurance schemes of 1883, 1884 and 1889, the first of their kind in a major state, were an innovative attempt to win workers over, but they were limited in coverage and did not reduce support for the SPD. The carrot and the stick both failed.\n\nIn the middle of his chancellorship he also turned from the National Liberals to tariffs. The tariff of 1879 protected iron, steel and agriculture, and it did serve German industry and the landowners, but it split the National Liberals and left him dependent on Conservatives and the Centre. He kept control of the army budget by winning the 1887 election after the Reichstag rejected the Septennate, and in 1890 he even considered a coup against the constitution. A chancellor who governed by naming enemies of the Reich in turn, and who ended by quarrelling with a new Kaiser, had not solved Germany's internal problems.\n\nIn external affairs the record was far stronger. After 1871 Bismarck declared Germany a satiated power and set out to prevent a hostile coalition, above all one including France, which was embittered by the loss of Alsace and Lorraine. He backed French colonial expansion, as in Tunisia in 1881, to distract it from revanche. The Three Emperors' League of 1873 linked Germany with Austria-Hungary and Russia, and at the Congress of Berlin in 1878 he presided as honest broker and settled the Balkan crisis without a general war.\n\nWhen the League collapsed he built a system of treaties. The Dual Alliance with Austria-Hungary in 1879 became the permanent core of his policy and lasted until 1918. The Triple Alliance of 1882 added Italy, angered by the French occupation of Tunisia. After the Bulgarian crisis destroyed the League in 1887, he signed the secret Reinsurance Treaty with Russia, with each promising neutrality unless Germany attacked France or Russia attacked Austria-Hungary, and he encouraged the Mediterranean Agreements to keep Britain friendly. He avoided war with a major power, and France had no ally for the whole period. His colonial claims of 1884 to 1885 caused friction with Britain, but he used them skilfully to cooperate with France and to hold the Berlin Conference.\n\nThere were flaws in the system. The Reinsurance Treaty conflicted in spirit with the Dual Alliance, the Lombardverbot of 1887 pushed Russia towards French loans, and the whole structure depended on one man's skill, so that after his fall in March 1890 the treaty with Russia lapsed and Russia moved towards France. Even so, these weaknesses appeared when the system was in other hands.\n\nOverall, Bismarck was a master of diplomacy and a mixed success in domestic politics. His foreign policy achieved its aim of keeping Germany secure and France isolated while he remained in office, whereas the Kulturkampf and the Anti-Socialist Law were failures that left lasting hostility among Catholics and workers. His external affairs were therefore managed better.",
