    "label": "2",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "European states managed religion in very different ways between 1870 and 1914, but the common thread was that governments were rarely willing to leave it alone. In Germany and Italy the state clashed with the Catholic Church over authority and loyalty, in France the issue became part of a bitter national quarrel, and in Russia the state itself persecuted its Jewish minority. In most cases heavy-handed management failed to achieve what governments wanted.\n\nThe clearest example of a state confronting a church was Bismarck's Kulturkampf in Germany. The Reich was about two-thirds Protestant and one-third Catholic, and after the First Vatican Council declared papal infallibility in 1870 Bismarck feared that Catholics owed their first loyalty to Rome. He distrusted the Catholic Centre Party, founded in 1870, and the Polish Catholics in the east, while liberals welcomed an attack on the Church as a defence of modern secular society. In 1871 the pulpit law forbade clergy to criticise the state in sermons, the Jesuits were expelled in 1872, and schools were put under state inspection. The May Laws of 1873, introduced by the Prussian culture minister Adalbert Falk, forced priests to study at state universities and pass state exams, and made church appointments subject to state approval. Civil marriage became compulsory in 1875.\n\nThis policy failed. Pope Pius IX condemned the laws in 1875, bishops were jailed or exiled, and by 1876 many parishes had no priest. Catholics rallied behind their Church and the Centre Party doubled its seats to become the second-largest party in the Reichstag. By 1878 Bismarck needed the Centre's votes for tariffs and against the socialists, and he negotiated with the new pope, Leo XIII. Most of the laws were repealed or relaxed in the 1880s, though the ban on the Jesuits lasted until 1917. The state had asserted control over schools and marriage, but it had made the Centre a permanent force and exposed the limits of Bismarck's power.\n\nIn Italy the question was different, because the Pope had lost his territory. Italian troops entered Rome on 20 September 1870, ending the Papal States. The Law of Guarantees of 1871 declared the Pope's person inviolable and gave him the Vatican and Lateran palaces and an annual income, but Pius IX rejected it and called himself a prisoner in the Vatican. This Roman Question divided a liberal, anticlerical state from a largely Catholic population. The non expedit forbade Catholics to vote in or stand for parliamentary elections, so they were shut out of national politics while building their own organisations such as Catholic Action, banks, newspapers and cooperatives. The deadlock eased only gradually, with the Gentiloni Pact of 1913, when Catholic voters backed Giolitti's liberal candidates, and the issue was not settled until the Lateran Treaties of 1929.\n\nIn France, Leo XIII tried conciliation. His ralliement policy of the early 1890s urged Catholics to accept the Third Republic, but it largely failed, because many Catholics remained monarchist and because the Dreyfus Affair then drove Church and Republic apart. In 1894 Captain Alfred Dreyfus, a Jewish officer, was convicted of passing secrets to Germany and sent to Devil's Island. The army suppressed evidence in 1896 that Esterhazy was the real author of the leaked document, and in January 1898 Zola published J'accuse. France divided into Dreyfusards and anti-Dreyfusards, among whom were the army and much of the Catholic Church. Dreyfus was pardoned after the retrial at Rennes in 1899 and cleared in 1906. The anticlerical measures of the early 1900s followed, ending in the separation of church and state in 1905, so here state management of religion ended in a complete legal break.\n\nIn Russia the state itself was the persecutor. About five million Jews lived in the empire, most confined to the Pale of Settlement. After Alexander II was assassinated in 1881, pogroms swept the south and the authorities were slow to act. The May Laws of 1882 banned Jews from settling in rural areas and owning land, quotas limited their places in schools and universities from 1887, and Jews were expelled from Moscow in 1891-92. Officials such as Pobedonostsev backed this policy, and the Kishinev pogrom of April 1903 killed 49 Jews and caused international outrage. The policy drove about two million Jews to emigrate between 1881 and 1914, mostly to the USA, and pushed others towards the Bund and Zionism.\n\nOverall, states managed religion mainly by trying to subordinate it to national or secular goals, and they were largely unsuccessful. Bismarck had to retreat, Italy lived with a decades-long stand-off, and France ended up with separation. The treatment of Jews was worst where the state itself promoted discrimination, as in Russia, while in France the republic eventually corrected the injustice, though the prejudice remained.",
