    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "World War I had no single cause. Its roots lay in a system of rival alliances, militarism, imperial and naval rivalry and nationalism, which together made Europe highly unstable. The immediate spark was the assassination at Sarajevo in June 1914, and the way each government responded in the July crisis turned a Balkan quarrel into a general war.\n\nThe alliance system was the first underlying cause. Bismarck had built a network of agreements to keep France isolated, but after he was dismissed in 1890 Germany let the Reinsurance Treaty with Russia lapse. Russia turned to France, and the Franco-Russian Alliance of 1892-94 gave Germany the danger of a war on two fronts. Britain ended its splendid isolation with the Anglo-Japanese Alliance of 1902, the Entente Cordiale with France in 1904 and the Anglo-Russian Entente in 1907, creating the Triple Entente to face the Triple Alliance. The blocs were not fully rigid, since Italy had promised France neutrality in 1902, but each side came to see every crisis as a test of strength, and Germany spoke of encirclement. A local dispute could therefore draw in every Great Power.\n\nImperial and naval rivalry poisoned relations between the powers. The New Imperialism produced clashes at Fashoda in 1898 and in the Boer War, and Germany's Weltpolitik, summed up by Bülow's claim to a place in the sun, brought it into conflict with Britain and France. The two Moroccan crises showed this. In 1905 Wilhelm II landed at Tangier to challenge the new Entente, but at Algeciras in 1906 Germany found itself isolated and the Entente grew stronger. In 1911 the gunboat Panther was sent to Agadir, and Lloyd George warned in the Mansion House speech that Britain would not be ignored. Each crisis drew Britain and France closer and left German opinion feeling humiliated. Imperial disputes were mostly settled peacefully, so they raised tension more than they caused war directly.\n\nThe naval race with Britain was especially damaging. Under Tirpitz, Germany passed Navy Laws in 1898 and 1900, planning a fleet of 38 battleships on the theory that a strong fleet would deter Britain. Britain regarded this as a direct threat to its security, and responded by building the Dreadnought in 1906 and outbuilding Germany. The race cost huge sums, convinced British leaders that Germany was a rival rather than a partner, and pushed Britain towards France and Russia.\n\nMilitarism and mobilisation plans made war harder to avoid. The powers built mass conscript armies and the general staffs prepared rigid timetables. The most important was the Schlieffen Plan, which required Germany to defeat France quickly through Belgium before Russia could mobilise. Once Russia mobilised, German leaders felt that they had to attack at once, and this left little time for diplomacy. Nationalism was just as dangerous. French revanche looked to recover Alsace-Lorraine, pan-Slavism pushed Russia to support the Slav peoples of the Balkans, and South Slav nationalism threatened the existence of multinational Austria-Hungary.\n\nThe Balkans were where these pressures met. Serbia became independent in 1878 and after 1903 turned towards Russia. When Austria-Hungary annexed Bosnia in October 1908, Serbia and Russia were humiliated, and Russia, which backed down only because Germany warned it in 1909, resolved not to give way again. The Balkan Wars of 1912-13 doubled Serbia in size by the Treaty of Bucharest and alarmed Austria-Hungary, whose chief of staff Conrad von Hötzendorf pressed for war on Serbia. The Great Powers had contained these wars by conference diplomacy, but Austria-Hungary was left feeling that next time it would have to fight.\n\nThe immediate cause was the murder of Franz Ferdinand at Sarajevo on 28 June 1914 by Gavrilo Princip, a Bosnian Serb who had been armed with help from Serbian officers of the Black Hand. Germany gave Austria-Hungary its blank cheque on 5 and 6 July. Austria presented a harsh ultimatum to Serbia on 23 July and declared war on 28 July. Russia mobilised on 29 and 30 July, Germany declared war on Russia on 1 August and on France on 3 August, and its invasion of Belgium on 4 August brought Britain in under the Treaty of London of 1839. Within six weeks of the assassination, a murder in Bosnia had become a European war.\n\nHistorians still debate responsibility. Fritz Fischer argued that German leaders deliberately risked war in 1914 to win world-power status, while Christopher Clark in The Sleepwalkers stresses that leaders in all the capitals, including Belgrade, St Petersburg and Paris, stumbled into a war none fully intended. In my view the alliances, the arms race and nationalism created the danger, but the decisions of Austria-Hungary and Germany in July 1914, the blank cheque and the ultimatum, together with Russian mobilisation, were the links that made war certain.",
