    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Between 1871 and 1920 science and technology changed the way Europeans lived, were healed, communicated and travelled. The key developments were in medicine, where germ theory transformed the fight against disease, and in technology, where electricity, wireless, the motor car and the aeroplane created the modern world. The First World War then showed that the same advances could also be used for destruction.\n\nThe most important medical development was the germ theory of disease. Louis Pasteur showed in the 1860s that microbes cause decay and disease, and he developed vaccines against chicken cholera, anthrax in 1881 and rabies, which he used in 1885 to save the life of a boy, Joseph Meister, who had been bitten by a rabid dog. The Pasteur Institute was founded in Paris in 1887-88. In Germany Robert Koch developed methods to grow bacteria in pure culture and identified the anthrax bacterium in 1876, the tuberculosis bacillus in 1882 and the cholera bacterium in 1883, and he won the Nobel Prize for Medicine in 1905. Behring and Kitasato developed antitoxins against diphtheria and tetanus in 1890, and in 1909 Paul Ehrlich produced Salvarsan, the first effective drug against syphilis and the first modern chemotherapy.\n\nSurgery was transformed by Joseph Lister, who used carbolic acid on wounds, instruments and in the operating theatre from the mid-1860s and so cut deaths from infection. Combined with anaesthetics such as ether and chloroform, and with the sterile conditions of asepsis developed in the 1880s and 1890s, surgery became far safer and longer operations became possible. Public health improvements, including clean water and sewers, did much to reduce cholera and typhoid in cities. Wilhelm Röntgen's discovery of X-rays in 1895 gave doctors a way of seeing broken bones and bullets, and he won the first Nobel Prize for Physics in 1901.\n\nIn science, Marie Curie was the outstanding figure. After Becquerel found that uranium gave off rays in 1896, she studied them and called the phenomenon radioactivity. Working with Pierre Curie she discovered polonium and radium in 1898, and she shared the Nobel Prize for Physics in 1903, becoming the first woman to win one. She won the Chemistry prize in 1911, the first person to win in two sciences. In the First World War she organised mobile X-ray units, the petites Curies, for field hospitals. Her work also showed the limits of the knowledge of the time, since she died in 1934 of an illness probably caused by long exposure to radiation. Einstein's theory of special relativity of 1905 and Haber and Bosch's synthesis of ammonia, which produced artificial fertilisers and explosives, show the further advance of science.\n\nIn technology, electricity and communications changed everyday life. Bell patented the telephone in 1876, and Edison developed a long-lasting electric light bulb in 1879, followed by power stations, electric lighting and trams in cities. Marconi sent wireless signals from 1895 and claimed to receive a signal across the Atlantic in December 1901. Wireless was used to call for help when the Titanic sank in 1912 and became vital for military communication in the war.\n\nThe motor car was the most visible new technology. Nikolaus Otto built the four-stroke engine in 1876, and Daimler and Maybach developed a light, fast petrol engine in the 1880s. Karl Benz patented his Motorwagen in 1886, and in 1888 his wife Bertha made the first long-distance journey of about 106 km from Mannheim to Pforzheim. Dunlop's pneumatic tyre of 1888 improved the ride. France led early production with Panhard and Peugeot, but the decisive step came in the USA, where Henry Ford launched the Model T in 1908 and introduced the moving assembly line in 1913, cutting the cost of a car until ordinary people could afford one. In Europe, cars were still too expensive for most people by 1920.\n\nFlight also made rapid progress, from the Wright brothers' first powered flight at Kitty Hawk on 17 December 1903 to Blériot's Channel crossing in 1909 and Alcock and Brown's non-stop transatlantic flight in June 1919. The war speeded up the development of aircraft, motor lorries and the tank, and made engines more powerful. Overall, the greatest achievements of these years were in medicine and communications, which saved millions of lives and brought people closer, but the same science also produced deadlier weapons in 1914-18, so progress had a darker side.",
