    "label": "2",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Germany had the greater social and economic problems during the inter-war years. Britain suffered real hardship, above all in its old industrial regions, but its problems were largely those of long-term decline within a stable political system. Germany faced economic catastrophe twice, in 1923 and again after 1929, and each time the damage struck the whole of society and in the end destroyed the republic itself.\n\nBritain's difficulties began with the collapse of the short post-war boom. Prices fell sharply in 1920 and 1921, export markets were lost and unemployment rose above two million by mid-1921. The staple industries of coal, cotton, shipbuilding, iron and steel, which had been the basis of the Victorian economy, entered a long decline. Lancashire cotton lost markets to Japan and India, and the Tyne, Wear and Clyde shipyards were short of orders. In 1925 Churchill, as Chancellor, returned sterling to the gold standard at the pre-war rate. Keynes warned in The Economic Consequences of Mr Churchill that this would price British exports out of markets and force wages down. The coal owners did then demand wage cuts and longer hours, and this led to the General Strike of May 1926. The strike was the only nationwide one in British history, but it was called off after nine days and the miners were forced back on worse terms. Britain's industrial problems were therefore serious and left real bitterness.\n\nThe Depression made things worse, and the social cost in the depressed regions was heavy. Registered unemployment reached about three million, over 20 per cent of insured workers, in 1932 and 1933. In towns such as Jarrow and Merthyr Tydfil the figure was far higher, while the south-east and Midlands had much less. The household means test of 1931 was deeply resented because it took account of the income of every member of the family. Hunger marches such as the National Hunger March of 1932 showed the anger of the unemployed. Priestley, Orwell and the Pilgrim Trust survey Men Without Work recorded malnutrition and overcrowding. Yet for those in work real wages rose, prices fell, and suburban housing boomed, with about four million houses built between the wars. New industries making cars and electrical goods grew in the south and Midlands. Britain was two nations, and poverty was concentrated in particular regions rather than spread across the whole country.\n\nGermany's problems were more extreme from the start. The Treaty of Versailles took territory, limited the army and fixed war guilt on Germany, and the London Schedule of 1921 set reparations at 132 billion gold marks, a sum most Germans thought impossible. When Germany fell behind on deliveries, France and Belgium occupied the Ruhr in January 1923. The government funded passive resistance by printing money, and the result was hyperinflation. By November 1923 one US dollar was worth about 4.2 trillion marks. Workers were paid daily and rushed to spend their wages, and barter replaced money in places. The savings, pensions and war loans of the middle class were largely wiped out. This was a social disaster that Britain never faced, and it left millions resentful of the republic. The currency was only stabilised by the Rentenmark of November 1923 under Stresemann and Schacht.\n\nThe recovery between 1924 and 1929 rested on weak foundations, because the Dawes Plan loans were American money. When the Wall Street Crash came in October 1929 those loans were withdrawn. German unemployment rose from about 1.9 million in 1929 to about 6 million registered unemployed by early 1932. This was a far higher proportion of the workforce than in Britain, and the welfare system created in 1927 had been funded for far fewer claimants. Brüning governed by emergency decree under Article 48 and cut wages, pensions and welfare to balance the budget, which made the slump deeper. The banking crisis of July 1931 added to the collapse. Middle-class and farming groups were ruined by falling prices, and the collapse hit most of the nation rather than particular regions.\n\nThe political consequences also show how much greater Germany's problems were. In Britain the crisis of 1931 produced a National Government, tariffs under the Import Duties Act of 1932, cheap money, and the abandonment of gold in September 1931, and the parliamentary system survived untouched. In Germany proportional representation and Article 48 had already made stable government hard, and mass despair gave the Nazis their opening. They rose from 12 seats in 1928 to 107 in 1930 and 230 in July 1932, drawing on the unemployed, farmers and the ruined middle class. Hitler became Chancellor on 30 January 1933. Economic and social problems in Germany therefore ended in dictatorship, while in Britain they were managed within democracy.\n\nBoth countries suffered badly, and the unemployed of Jarrow or South Wales had little reason to think of themselves as lucky. However, Germany endured national humiliation, the destruction of savings in 1923, unemployment of around six million and the collapse of its political system. Britain's problems were serious but regional, and its government coped. Germany therefore had the greater social and economic problems.",
