    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Joseph Goebbels and Leni Riefenstahl both made major contributions to Nazi propaganda, but of different kinds. Goebbels was the organiser and director who built the propaganda machine and controlled what Germans saw and heard, while Riefenstahl was an artist whose films gave the regime its most powerful visual image. Goebbels's contribution was the greater, because he shaped the whole system, but Riefenstahl's films reached beyond Germany and lasted far longer.\n\nGoebbels's work began before the Nazis took power. Born in 1897 in Rheydt, he gained a doctorate and joined the Nazi party in 1924. Hitler appointed him Gauleiter of Berlin in 1926. There he built support in the capital through street campaigns, mass meetings and the newspaper Der Angriff. In the Reichstag campaigns of 1930 and 1932 he used posters, loudspeaker vans and radio. These were modern methods, and the party's vote rose from 2.6 per cent in 1928 to about 37 per cent in July 1932. In that sense he helped to win Hitler the power from which the regime's propaganda then grew.\n\nOnce Hitler was Chancellor, Goebbels became head of a state propaganda machine. The Ministry of Public Enlightenment and Propaganda was created on 13 March 1933 under him, with responsibility for the press, radio, film, theatre, music and the arts. The Editors' Law of October 1933 required editors to be members of approved professional bodies and excluded Jewish journalists. The Reich Chamber of Culture, set up in September 1933, meant that writers, painters, musicians and actors could not work unless they were admitted to it. Radio was brought under state control, and the cheap Volksempfänger, costing 76 Reichsmarks, was launched in August 1933 so that ordinary households could hear Hitler. Loudspeakers carried major speeches into factories. His control of the media was therefore close to total.\n\nGoebbels also staged the public ceremonies of the regime. The book burnings of 10 May 1933 destroyed works by Jewish, Marxist and liberal authors in Berlin and other university towns. The Degenerate Art exhibition of 1937 mocked modernist painters while approved art was displayed alongside it. His ministry used repetition of simple themes: national unity, the authority of the leader, the threat of communism and the Jews, and the glory of Germany. The cult of Hitler as saviour of the nation was promoted through the Heil Hitler greeting, portraits in public buildings and the Führer's birthday. Films such as Jud Süß of 1940 were explicitly anti-Semitic, and his ministry made the persecution of the Jews part of everyday messages. In the Sportpalast speech of 18 February 1943 he called for total war, and the speech was broadcast to the public.\n\nLeni Riefenstahl's contribution was cinematic. Born in Berlin in 1902, she trained as a dancer and actress and made mountain films before Hitler commissioned her to film the party's rallies. Her Triumph of the Will recorded the Nuremberg Rally of 1934, the first after the Night of the Long Knives. It opens with Hitler's aeroplane descending through clouds over Nuremberg and is built on the contrast between the single leader and the massed ranks of the party, the SA, the SS and the army. The film presented a picture of unity, order and devotion to Hitler, with Hitler and the party as the sole leadership. It won the German National Film Prize in 1935 and a gold medal at the Paris exposition of 1937.\n\nHer Olympia, released in 1938, covered the 1936 Berlin Olympics. It used innovative camera techniques to present athletic achievement as a national triumph. Since the regime had used the Games to show Germany as a peaceful and modern nation, the film carried that image to audiences abroad. After 1945 Riefenstahl claimed that her films were artistic documents rather than propaganda, but critics have always seen their propaganda purpose, and the rallies they recorded were themselves designed as propaganda for Nazi policies.\n\nOverall, Goebbels and Riefenstahl complemented each other. Goebbels created the structures, controlled the press, radio, film and the arts, and set the messages the people were fed. Riefenstahl turned the rallies and the Olympics into images of power that audiences in Germany and abroad found impressive. Goebbels's contribution was wider and more decisive, because without his ministry there would have been no system for her films to serve, but hers was the most memorable.",
