    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "World War II had a profound impact on the civilian population of both Britain and France. In Britain civilians were exposed to evacuation, rationing and bombing, but the war strengthened national unity and led to demands for social reform. In France defeat and occupation brought shortages, fear and divided loyalties, and the war left a long moral argument about collaboration and resistance.\n\nIn Britain the first major effect was evacuation. Operation Pied Piper began on 1 September 1939 and moved schoolchildren, mothers with young children, the disabled and teachers from London, Birmingham, Liverpool and other cities to the countryside. About 1.5 million people, most of them children, were evacuated in the first few days. The experience was uneven. Some host families welcomed evacuees, while others were shocked by poverty and poor hygiene among children from the cities. Many returned during the Phoney War when the bombing did not come, and the scheme was repeated in 1940. The gap it exposed between urban poverty and rural comfort was later used by reformers to argue for better health and education services.\n\nBombing and daily hardship were the heaviest burdens. The Blitz began on 7 September 1940, and London was bombed on 57 consecutive nights. Coventry, Liverpool, Birmingham and Belfast were also heavily bombed. About 40,000 civilians were killed in the Blitz of 1940 and 1941, over half of them in London. Families used Anderson shelters, Morrison shelters and Underground stations, and the blackout raised road deaths in the first winter. V-1 flying bombs and V-2 rockets killed thousands more in 1944 and 1945. Food was rationed from January 1940 under the Ministry of Food led by Lord Woolton, and clothing was rationed from June 1941. Because shortages were shared fairly, most people accepted them, and Dig for Victory encouraged families to grow their own vegetables. Rationing of meat continued until 1954.\n\nThe war also changed the lives of British civilians in work and society. Ernest Bevin, as Minister of Labour, directed workers into war industries. Women took up work in factories making aircraft and munitions, and the National Service Act of December 1941 required single women aged 20 to 30 to register for war work. Others joined the ATS, WAAF and WRNS, the Land Army or civil defence as wardens and fire watchers. The Home Guard was formed in May 1940. Shared danger helped to produce support for planning, and the Beveridge Report of December 1942, with its five giants of want, disease, ignorance, squalor and idleness, sold hundreds of thousands of copies. Its proposals were carried out through the National Insurance Act of 1946 and the National Health Service of 1948.\n\nFrench civilians had a harsher experience because their country was defeated and occupied. In May and June 1940 millions fled from the advancing German army and crowded the roads. The armistice of 22 June divided the country, and the south was occupied too in November 1942. Ration cards were issued by age and work category, and bread, meat, fat and fuel were short, so many city dwellers relied on the black market. German soldiers requisitioned buildings and food, and curfews, identity checks and the taking of hostages made life uneasy. France paid occupation costs of 400 million francs a day. About one and a half million prisoners of war were held in Germany, and many women ran family farms and businesses in their absence. Allied bombing before and after D-Day killed tens of thousands of French civilians.\n\nFor France the war also divided society. The Vichy regime under Pétain ended elections, banned strikes and dissolved trade unions, and it promoted its National Revolution. Vichy passed an anti-Jewish statute in October 1940, and about 76,000 Jews were deported from France. The Milice, founded in January 1943, hunted members of the Resistance. The compulsory labour service pushed many young men into hiding, and the maquis grew as a result. Most people were passive attentistes, but a minority joined the Resistance, which de Gaulle and Jean Moulin helped to unite in 1943. Pastor Trocmé and the villagers of Le Chambon-sur-Lignon sheltered thousands of Jewish refugees. After liberation in 1944 there were reprisals against collaborators, and historians since the 1970s have challenged the myth of a nation united in resistance.\n\nThe impact on civilians was therefore great in both countries, but different in kind. British civilians endured bombing, rationing and conscription of labour, yet their society emerged more united and more committed to reform. French civilians lived under foreign occupation, suffered persecution and divided loyalties, and carried the moral consequences of Vichy long after 1945.",
