    "label": "2",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Both Ho Chi Minh and Achmad Sukarno achieved a great deal for Asian nationalism, but I would argue that Ho Chi Minh achieved more. Both men led their peoples to independence from a European power and defeated efforts to restore colonial rule. Ho, however, defeated a major power on the battlefield, built a durable state and left a movement that eventually united his country, while Sukarno's later rule ended in failure and his own removal from power.\n\nBoth men built nationalist movements before 1945. Sukarno trained as an engineer, founded the Indonesian Nationalist Party in 1927, and was imprisoned by the Dutch in 1929 and later exiled to remote islands. Ho Chi Minh spent about thirty years abroad, presented a petition for Vietnamese rights to the Versailles conference in 1919, which the Allies ignored, joined the French Communist Party in 1920 and helped found the Indochinese Communist Party in 1930. In May 1941 he founded the Viet Minh, which united communists and non-communist nationalists to fight both the French and the Japanese. Ho's organisation was disciplined and national in scope, whereas Sukarno's depended heavily on his gifts as an orator and on Japanese tolerance.\n\nThe Japanese occupation gave both men their chance. Sukarno worked with the Japanese, who trained Indonesian forces such as PETA, and on 1 June 1945 he set out the Pancasila as the ideological basis for an independent state. He and Mohammad Hatta proclaimed independence in Jakarta on 17 August 1945. Ho's Viet Minh seized power in Hanoi in August 1945, Emperor Bao Dai abdicated, and on 2 September Ho proclaimed the Democratic Republic of Vietnam, quoting the American Declaration of Independence. Ho's claim to have led the August Revolution gave him a stronger moral position, since Sukarno was criticised by the Dutch and some Indonesians as a collaborator with Japan.\n\nThe struggle against the returning colonial powers is where the two differ most in method. Indonesia won sovereignty largely through diplomacy and international pressure. The Linggadjati Agreement of 1946 was reinterpreted by the Dutch, who launched police actions in July 1947 and December 1948, capturing Yogyakarta and imprisoning Sukarno and Hatta. The UN, and American pressure on the Netherlands, including threats over Marshall Plan aid, brought the Dutch to the Round Table Conference at The Hague, and sovereignty was transferred on 27 December 1949. Sukarno's suppression of the Madiun communist rising in 1948 had helped win American sympathy. Ho by contrast won his independence by force. After the failure of the Fontainebleau talks and the bombardment of Haiphong, war began in December 1946. With Chinese weapons from 1950 and the generalship of Vo Nguyen Giap, the Viet Minh overran the French garrison at Dien Bien Phu on 7 May 1954, capturing over 10,000 troops. Defeating a European army in open battle was a far greater feat and an inspiration to nationalists across Asia and Africa.\n\nSukarno's strongest claims lie in the years after independence. He gave a diverse archipelago a national language and a unifying ideology in the Pancasila, and at the Bandung Conference of 1955, bringing together 29 Asian and African states, he was the most prominent host, giving newly independent countries a voice in world affairs and promoting non-alignment. He also took over Dutch businesses from 1957 and secured West New Guinea, transferred in 1963. These were real achievements for Asian nationalism, and in the field of international leadership he arguably surpassed Ho.\n\nHowever, the limits of each man's achievement tell against Sukarno. Indonesia's parliamentary democracy was unstable after the 1955 election, and Sukarno introduced Guided Democracy in 1959, ending parliamentary rule. Regional rebellions, Konfrontasi against Malaysia and high inflation weakened the country, and his closeness to the Communist Party alarmed the army. After the attempted coup of 30 September 1965 and the killings that followed, the Supersemar order of March 1966 stripped him of power, and he died under house arrest in 1970. Ho also had failures: Vietnam was divided at the 17th parallel by the Geneva Accords of 1954, the 1956 elections were never held and his land reform of 1953-56 was violent. Yet he remained head of a state recognised by China and the Soviet Union until his death in 1969, and his government took control of the whole country in 1975.\n\nOverall, both leaders won independence and inspired others, but Ho Chi Minh achieved more. He defeated a major colonial power by arms, kept the loyalty of his movement until his death and saw his cause ultimately triumph, whereas Sukarno's diplomatic triumph of 1949 and Bandung were undone by his own authoritarianism and the collapse of his rule.",
