    "label": "C4",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Larkin was the central figure in the 1913 strike and lockout, as the organiser who built the ITGWU and led the Dublin workers. He founded the Irish Transport and General Workers' Union in Dublin in 1909, with Liberty Hall as its headquarters. When William Martin Murphy dismissed ITGWU members in mid-August 1913, the tram drivers and conductors walked off their cars on 26 August, and the union called out other workers who handled tram-related goods. Larkin defied the police ban by promising to speak in Sackville Street on 31 August, appearing in disguise on the balcony of the Imperial Hotel. His tactics of sympathetic strikes and refusal to handle tainted goods spread the dispute into a lockout of about 20,000 workers. Larkin was sentenced to seven months for sedition in October 1913, but his release after a public campaign made him a hero for Dublin workers.",
    "model_source": "haiku",
