    "label": "B4",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "Irish troops joined the UN force in the Congo in July 1960, and Irish observers had served in Lebanon from 1958. At Niemba in November 1960 an Irish patrol was ambushed and nine soldiers were killed. About 6,000 Irish soldiers served in the Congo until 1964, and twenty-six of them lost their lives. From 1964 Irish troops helped keep the Greek and Turkish Cypriot communities apart, and peacekeeping gave a neutral state a respected role in world affairs.",
