    "label": "C1",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "The main aim of the First Programme for Economic Expansion was to raise national output and living standards and to halt emigration by creating jobs in export industry and farming. It set a modest target of about 2 per cent growth in gross national product a year, deliberately low so that it could be achieved. It grew out of T.K. Whitaker's Economic Development of 1958, which argued that protection had failed to produce growth or jobs. The Industrial Development (Encouragement of External Investment) Act of 1958 removed the rule that Irish citizens must hold most shares in new firms. Grants through the IDA were offered to firms setting up new plant, with the best terms in the poorer western areas. An Foras Talúntais was set up in 1958, and farmers were encouraged to raise productivity and exports.",
