    "label": "B2",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "Mazzini's Young Italy, founded at Marseille in 1831, aimed at a unified republic, but its risings failed and Piedmont's armies were defeated at Custoza and Novara in 1848 and 1849. Cavour made Piedmont the leader of the national movement, and at Plombières in 1858 Napoleon III agreed to help Piedmont against Austria in return for Nice and Savoy. Garibaldi and his Thousand conquered Sicily and Naples in 1860, and the Kingdom of Italy was proclaimed on 17 March 1861 under Victor Emmanuel II. Venetia was added in 1866 after the Austro-Prussian War, and Rome was taken in September 1870 when the French garrison left.",
