    "label": "C1",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Potato blight and poor harvests from 1845 to 1847 raised food prices, and the commercial depression of 1847 brought unemployment to artisans. Liberals wanted constitutions and a German national parliament, while nationalists disagreed over whether the Habsburg lands should be included in a united Germany. Peasants wanted an end to feudal dues, and the Paris revolution of February 1848 inspired the March demands in the German states. The Frankfurt National Assembly opened on 18 May 1848 and passed the Basic Rights in December, but it had no army of its own. Austria regained control by retaking Prague in June 1848 and Vienna on 31 October, and Prussian troops entered Berlin on 10 November 1848. Frederick William IV refused the imperial crown on 28 April 1849, Prussian troops crushed the Baden and Palatinate risings, and Württemberg troops dispersed the rump parliament on 18 June 1849.",
