    "label": "C3",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Napoleon III appointed Haussmann Prefect of the Seine in 1853, and he directed the grands travaux for about seventeen years. The engineer Belgrand brought clean water from the Dhuis and Vanne valleys and built underground sewers, which greatly improved health and sanitation. Wide straight boulevards such as Sebastopol and Saint-Germain improved traffic, made it easier for troops to move, and gave the city a more regular plan. Parks such as the Bois de Boulogne, the Bois de Vincennes and the Buttes-Chaumont gave residents green space, including the working-class east. Public buildings and markets, such as Baltard's iron-and-glass Les Halles, and uniform stone facades gave Paris a more impressive appearance. The works also provided work for construction labourers, but the demolitions displaced the poor, and the cost of the debt led to his dismissal in 1870.",
