    "label": "B1",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "Radio became a mass medium in the 1920s and 1930s, when the British Broadcasting Company was founded in 1922 and became the BBC in 1927 under John Reith. Roosevelt began his fireside chats on the radio in March 1933, and the American networks NBC and CBS competed for advertisers. Cinema was the most popular mass entertainment of the 1930s, and Hollywood dominated the world market, with talking pictures spreading after The Jazz Singer of 1927. The Cinematograph Films Act of 1927 required British cinemas to show a quota of British films to protect the domestic industry against American competition.",
