    "label": "B2",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "The Lateran Treaties of 11 February 1929 ended the long dispute between the Italian state and the papacy and created Vatican City. The Italian state paid the Church compensation of 1.75 billion lire for the loss of the papal states, and Catholicism was recognised as the sole religion of the Italian state. In 1931 the regime clashed with Catholic Action over its youth groups, and Pope Pius XI protested in the encyclical Non abbiamo bisogno. An agreement that year limited Catholic Action to religious, cultural and charitable activities. The racial laws of 1938 excluded Jews from schools, the professions and the armed forces, and the Church opposed them in part but did not break with the regime.",
