    "label": "B3",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "John Maynard Keynes criticised the return to gold in his 1925 pamphlet The Economic Consequences of Mr Churchill, arguing that an overvalued pound would make British exports uncompetitive. In 1929 he co-wrote We Can Conquer Unemployment, which proposed large public works programmes to reduce unemployment. His General Theory of Employment, Interest and Money of 1936 argued that government spending could restore demand and employment when private investment was low. In How to Pay for the War of 1940 he proposed compulsory saving to prevent wartime inflation, and at the Bretton Woods conference of 1944 he argued for an international clearing union.",
