    "label": "C1",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "As prime minister from 1953 to 1955, Nagy pursued the New Course of consumer goods, farm relief and looser controls. Nagy was reappointed prime minister on 24 October 1956 and ordered Soviet troops out of Budapest, and the Soviet government was prepared to negotiate their withdrawal. On 30 October he formed a coalition with non-communist parties, reducing the Communist Party to a minority role. On 1 November he declared Hungary neutral and announced its withdrawal from the Warsaw Pact, a step Moscow would not tolerate. Within days his government had won Soviet withdrawal and a multi-party system, which no other satellite state had achieved, but he was naive about Soviet intentions and could not defend Hungary. He was taken from the Yugoslav embassy on 22 November, tried in secret and executed on 16 June 1958.",
