    "model": "The crisis originated in the Arab–Israeli conflict, when Egypt and Syria attacked Israel on 6 October 1973 and the USA resupplied Israel with arms. The Arab oil states cut output by 5 per cent a month and imposed an embargo on the USA and the Netherlands, which supported Israel. On 16 October OPEC's Gulf members raised the oil price by about 70 per cent, and prices roughly quadrupled in the following months. The economy suffered falling industrial production, rising unemployment and stagflation, as stagnation and rising prices occurred together. Society felt the crisis in fuel rationing, car-free Sundays in the Netherlands and West Germany, and Britain's three-day working week from January to March 1974. Governments abandoned the post-war commitment to full employment, and their main aim became defeating inflation.",
