    "model": "Katanga was the Congo's richest mining region, and the Belgian-controlled Union Miniere du Haut-Katanga ran its copper, cobalt and uranium mines. Moise Tshombe's Conakat party wanted the province to keep its own mineral revenue and campaigned for regional autonomy. Conakat argued that the Katangan people had a separate identity, and Tshombe claimed that the central government was controlled by outsiders and communists. Tshombe declared secession on 11 July 1960, eleven days after independence, with Belgian officers, advisers and mercenaries backing the Katangan forces. The secession tested the Congo's unity, and UN forces under ONUC helped end it when Tshombe announced the end of the secession at Kolwezi in January 1963. The crisis left the Congo dependent on foreign military support, and the Simba rebellion of 1964 showed how much of the country remained outside central control.",
