    "marks": 40,
    "model": "After proclaiming the Democratic Republic of Vietnam on 2 September 1945, Ho Chi Minh led his government through the First Indochina War against France from December 1946. His forces under Vo Nguyen Giap defeated the French at Dien Bien Phu in May 1954, and the Geneva Accords of July 1954 divided Vietnam at the 17th parallel. In North Vietnam his Lao Dong Party ran the state with Soviet and Chinese aid, and both China and the Soviet Union recognised his government. His land reform of 1953 to 1956 was violent, and it was followed by a public admission of errors. The planned 1956 elections were never held, which left Vietnam divided. He died in September 1969 before the war ended, but his government took control of the whole country in 1975, and many Vietnamese nationalists saw him as the leader who had made independence possible.",
