    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Cardinal Paul Cullen was the dominant figure in Irish Catholicism between 1850 and his death in 1878. He turned a Church that had run on diocese-by-diocese independence into a disciplined, centralised and Rome-focused institution, and he shaped its attitude to education and politics for a generation.\n\nHis first importance lay in the way he reached the top and what he was sent to do. Born in County Kildare in 1803, Cullen studied in Rome, was ordained there and became rector of the Irish College in 1832. He therefore knew the Roman view of Ireland better than any other Irish churchman. Rome named him Archbishop of Armagh in 1849 and Apostolic Delegate in 1850, giving him the pope's own authority to carry out reform. He became Archbishop of Dublin in 1852 and the first Irish cardinal in 1866. These posts gave him the power to impose his ideas on the bishops, and that is why his career mattered so much.\n\nThe Synod of Thurles in August and September 1850 was his greatest single achievement. It was the first national synod of the Irish Church since Kilkenny in 1642, and Cullen presided over it as Apostolic Delegate. It laid down uniform liturgy and discipline following the Roman rite, regulated clerical dress and conduct, and strengthened the bishops' authority over their priests. It also discouraged priests from political agitation at the altar and at election meetings. This was the beginning of the Romanisation of the Irish Church, and it made ultramontanism, the belief in strong papal authority over national churches, the guiding principle of Irish Catholicism.\n\nCullen was also central to what Emmet Larkin called the devotional revolution. He promoted parish missions, the rosary, novenas, benediction and devotion to the Sacred Heart and the Virgin Mary, and he encouraged religious orders such as the Christian Brothers and the Sisters of Mercy to build schools, hospitals and orphanages. Church building expanded and the clergy were better educated and more tightly controlled. Weekly Mass attendance rose from perhaps a third of Catholics before the Famine to the large majority by the 1870s. Historians debate how much of this was Cullen's own work, since the Famine had removed many of the poor who were least regular in practice, but he gave the change direction and authority.\n\nEducation was the second great battleground. Cullen followed Rome in condemning the Queen's Colleges of Belfast, Cork and Galway as godless, because they were non-denominational and had no chair of theology. The Synod forbade clergy to take posts in them and ordered a Catholic University, which opened in Dublin in 1854 with John Henry Newman as rector. In practical terms the project failed. It had few students and no state-recognised degrees, and Newman resigned in 1858. Most Catholics who wanted a degree still went to Trinity or the Queen's Colleges. Even so, Cullen's insistence on Catholic control of education set the Church's demands on schools and universities for decades.\n\nPolitically Cullen was conservative and suspicious of radicalism. He distrusted Young Ireland, the Tenant League and priests who involved themselves in politics, and he opposed the Fenians, refusing in 1861 to let the body of Terence Bellew MacManus lie in the Pro-Cathedral in Dublin. He preferred co-operation with the Liberals and backed Gladstone's disestablishment of the Church of Ireland, which took effect in 1871. Nationalists and bishops such as John MacHale of Tuam resented his authority, and some critics saw him as an alien, authoritarian figure who kept the Church aloof from the national movement.\n\nOverall, Cullen's importance was that he created the modern Irish Catholic Church, centralised, devout, ultramontane and firmly led by the hierarchy. His Catholic University failed and his politics divided opinion, but the discipline, the devotions and the institutions he promoted came to define Irish Catholic life well beyond his death in 1878.",
