    "label": "2",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The Dublin strike and lockout of 1913 was a failure in its immediate aims but a qualified success in its longer-term effects. The employers defeated the workers, who were starved back to work, but the Irish Transport and General Workers' Union survived, the labour movement was strengthened and the dispute helped to shape the politics of the years that followed.\n\nThe lockout was a failure in the short term because the workers lost. It began when William Martin Murphy, chairman of the Dublin United Tramways Company and owner of the Irish Independent, dismissed members of Larkin's ITGWU in August 1913. The tram drivers and conductors walked out on 26 August, the first day of Horse Show week. The police protected the replacement workers, and on 31 August a baton charge in Sackville Street injured hundreds on what became known as Bloody Sunday. On 3 September about 400 employers agreed to demand that workers sign the Document, a pledge to leave the ITGWU, and locked out those who refused. By autumn about 20,000 workers were out of work, with thousands more dependants affected.\n\nThe workers could not break this resistance because they lacked outside support. Food ships from British unions, beginning with the Hare on 27 September, and relief from Liberty Hall kept many families going. But a special congress of the British TUC in December 1913 rejected calls for a sympathetic strike or a boycott of Dublin goods, which was a crushing blow. The Save the Kiddies scheme of October 1913, to send strikers' children to homes in Britain, was condemned by Archbishop Walsh and blocked by priests and the Ancient Order of Hibernians, and it damaged the cause. By January 1914 hunger drove the workers back, and the ITGWU advised a return, with the dispute ending around 18 January. Some workers were blacklisted and employers kept the right to bar union members in some firms.\n\nNevertheless the employers did not wholly succeed. Many men returned to work without signing the Document, so the principle that a union could be destroyed by such a pledge was not established. The Board of Trade inquiry under Askwith condemned the Document in late 1913, and Murphy's hardness cost him public sympathy. George Russell (AE) wrote his Open Letter to the Masters of Dublin in the Irish Times on 7 October 1913, and Yeats, Connolly and Constance Markievicz all spoke for the strikers. Public attention was drawn to the appalling slum housing of Dublin, which increased pressure for housing reform.\n\nThe longer-term results were more positive for labour. The ITGWU had lost members but it survived and grew during the First World War, and it had kept a mass movement of unskilled workers together for five months, something never done before in Ireland. The dispute made labour a political force in Dublin. James Connolly's belief in an alliance of labour and nationalism was strengthened, though the nationalist movement itself was split, with Griffith's Sinn Fein and the Irish Party largely unsympathetic to the strikers.\n\nPerhaps the most significant result was the Irish Citizen Army. Founded at Liberty Hall on 23 November 1913 to protect pickets from the police, and drilled by Captain Jack White, it later took part in the Easter Rising of 1916 under Connolly. In this way the lockout contributed to the radicalisation that fed into the revolutionary period.\n\nOn balance the lockout was a clear defeat in its immediate objective, since the workers returned hungry and in some cases blacklisted, but the union survived, labour found a political voice and the Citizen Army emerged. It is best judged a failure in the short term and a success in the long term, a defeat that strengthened the labour movement.",
