    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Michael Davitt made a major contribution to both social and political reform in Ireland between 1870 and 1914. He organised the Land League, the greatest tenant movement of the century, and so forced Westminster to concede the Three Fs and, in time, to finance tenant ownership. He also drew Fenians into constitutional politics, argued for a more radical social order based on land nationalisation and labour rights, and refused to compromise his principles even when that cost him influence.\n\nDavitt's background shaped his outlook. He was born at Straide, Co. Mayo, in 1846 and was evicted with his family in 1850, after which they moved to Haslingden in Lancashire. At eleven he lost his right arm in a cotton-mill accident. He joined the IRB and was arrested for arms trafficking in 1870, receiving fifteen years. He served about seven years, mostly in Dartmoor, and was released on licence in December 1877. His experience of eviction and of factory work convinced him that Ireland's problems were social as much as political, and that the land question was the key to national revival.\n\nHis first great contribution was the New Departure. After his release he travelled to the United States in 1878 and met John Devoy of Clan na Gael. Out of these contacts came the offer of October 1878 to support Parnell if he combined self-government, peasant ownership and independent parliamentary action. Davitt was the driving force in bringing Fenians, who had rejected parliamentary politics, into co-operation with constitutionalists and tying the land question to the national one. This gave the Home Rule movement a mass base of tenant farmers that Isaac Butt had never reached, and was a political reform of method as much as of aim.\n\nHis most important achievement was the Land League itself. On 20 April 1879 he organised a meeting at Irishtown, Co. Mayo, against high rents, and in August he founded the Land League of Mayo. In October 1879 the Irish National Land League was founded in Dublin with Parnell as president, and Davitt as its leading organiser. The League's programme was to reduce rents and then to make tenants owners of their holdings, and it spread rapidly across the south and west in 1880 during the agricultural depression. Through rent strikes, boycotting and the support of Irish America, it protected tenants from eviction and gave small farmers confidence that collective action could defeat landlordism. Davitt also rejected violence against individuals, which gave the campaign moral authority.\n\nThe results of this agitation were substantial. Gladstone's Land Act of 1870 had not fixed rents and left tenants insecure, but the Land Law Act of 1881 granted fair rents set by land courts, fixity of tenure and free sale. Rents fell by about a fifth in the first judicial rounds. The Arrears Act of 1882 cleared arrears for poorer tenants. The pressure he helped to generate also led Conservative and later Liberal governments to fund tenant purchase through the Ashbourne Act of 1885, Wyndham's Act of 1903 and Birrell's Act of 1909, so that by 1914 most tenants owned their land. Davitt himself was jailed again in February 1881 and held at Portland until May 1882, but the campaign survived, and the Ladies' Land League helped to keep it going.\n\nDavitt's social vision went further than the settlement. He favoured land nationalisation and was dismayed that the League accepted peasant proprietorship, believing that the land should belong to the people and not simply pass from landlords to a class of farmers. In 1890 he founded the newspaper Labour World to promote the cause of workers, and he spoke for labourers and for English and Irish workers alike, a concern the Irish Party often neglected. He opposed Parnell's continued leadership after the O'Shea divorce in 1890, putting principle ahead of the party. In 1899 he resigned his seat in protest against the Boer War. His history of the movement, The Fall of Feudalism in Ireland, appeared in 1904, and he died in 1906.\n\nOverall, Davitt's contribution was large, though it was not what he intended. Nationalists regarded him as a hero of the tenants, while unionists saw a dangerous agitator, but historians stress his social radicalism and his role in joining Fenians and constitutionalists. He did more than any other figure to bring about the end of landlordism, and he gave the national movement a popular base. Yet land nationalisation was never achieved, labourers and small western farmers gained least, and Parnell, not Davitt, reaped the political credit. His lasting legacy was a social programme that was ahead of its time.",
