    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The development of Ulster Unionism between 1870 and 1914 was the transformation of a loose Protestant attachment to the Union into a highly organised and eventually armed movement. Its growth was driven by Gladstone's conversion to Home Rule in 1886, by Belfast's industrial interests and by religious fears. By 1914 unionists had defeated two Home Rule Bills and forced the British government to accept that Ulster could not be coerced into a Dublin parliament.\n\nUnionism was a coalition rather than a single party. Ulster had a slight Protestant majority, about 56 per cent in 1911, concentrated in Antrim, Down, Armagh and Londonderry, and Protestants of every class were unionist, from landlords and industrialists to Presbyterian tenant farmers and shipyard workers. Many Presbyterians had supported the Liberals before 1886, but Gladstone's decision to introduce the First Home Rule Bill pushed them into the unionist camp. Southern unionists, a small minority of landlords, professionals and clergy, were strongest in Dublin and parts of Munster and Leinster. Colonel Edward Saunderson led the Irish Unionist MPs from 1886 until 1906.\n\nThe reasons for opposition explain this development. Religion was central: Protestants feared that a parliament elected by a Catholic majority would be dominated by the Church, and the slogan was that Home Rule is Rome Rule. Economics mattered just as much. Belfast, with its linen mills, the Belfast Ropeworks, and the shipyards of Harland and Wolff and Workman Clark, depended on British and imperial markets, and its population had grown to 387,000 by 1911. Unionists feared tariffs and higher taxes under a parliament that would favour the agricultural south and west. Many Ulster Protestants also saw themselves as British, were proud of the empire, and believed that Home Rule was the first step towards separation.\n\nFrom 1885 unionists built organisations to match the National League. The Orange Order, founded in 1795, revived in the 1880s and gave unionism a mass base of lodges. The Irish Loyal and Patriotic Union was founded in 1885 by southern unionists, and the Irish Unionist Alliance followed in 1891. In June 1892, as Gladstone prepared a Second Home Rule Bill, about 12,000 delegates attended the Ulster Convention in Belfast to protest against it. The Ulster Unionist Council was founded in March 1905 as a permanent central body for the province, after the Irish Reform Association's devolution proposals alarmed unionists. The Ulster Women's Unionist Council, formed in 1911 under Lady Londonderry, mobilised tens of thousands of women.\n\nThe decisive phase came after the Parliament Act of 1911 removed the Lords' veto, so that the Third Home Rule Bill, introduced in April 1912, would become law within about two years. Edward Carson, a Dublin-born barrister who led the Irish unionists from 1910, and James Craig, a wealthy Belfast businessman, organised mass resistance. Andrew Bonar Law pledged Conservative support to Ulster at Blenheim Palace on 27 July 1912. On Ulster Day, 28 September 1912, about 237,000 men signed the Solemn League and Covenant, and about 234,000 women signed a separate declaration. The Ulster Volunteer Force was formed in January 1913 under Sir George Richardson, and it had perhaps 90,000 men by 1914. In September 1913 the Ulster Unionist Council announced a Provisional Government of Ulster with Carson as chairman, to be set up if Home Rule became law.\n\n1914 brought the crisis to its peak. Asquith's proposal in March that Ulster counties could vote themselves out for six years was rejected by Carson as a stay of execution. On 20 March, in the Curragh incident, General Hubert Gough and many officers offered to resign rather than act against Ulster, which showed that the army could not be relied upon. On the night of 24 to 25 April the UVF landed about 25,000 rifles at Larne, Donaghadee and Bangor, organised by Fred Crawford, and neither the police nor the army intervened. The Buckingham Palace Conference in July failed over the exclusion of Tyrone and Fermanagh, and the Home Rule Act, which received the royal assent on 18 September 1914, was suspended for the duration of the war.\n\nIn all, Ulster Unionism changed from a defensive reaction into a disciplined and armed political force. Its achievements were to defeat the first two Bills, to show that Home Rule could not be imposed on Ulster by force, and to make exclusion the central question. Its limits were that it could not stop the Third Bill becoming law and that southern unionists were left without protection. By 1914 these developments had made some form of partition very likely.",
