    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Between 1932 and 1945 de Valera managed Anglo-Irish relations considerably better than the economy. In relations with Britain he achieved most of what he set out to do, ending the Treaty's symbols of subordination and recovering the ports, while his economic record was damaged by the Economic War and by a policy of protection that created jobs but not prosperity.\n\nOn the economy, the first and most costly episode was the Economic War. De Valera stopped paying the land annuities of about £3 million a year in 1932, arguing that they were owed to the Irish government and not to Britain. Britain imposed special duties on Irish cattle, beef and butter, and the Free State replied with duties on British coal, cement and iron and steel. Cattle exports, the largest item, fell sharply, and large cattle farmers in Munster and Leinster suffered most, although the whole rural economy felt the fall in prices. The government paid bounties and subsidised wheat and beet, financed by higher taxes. The Coal-Cattle Pact of 1935 gave only slight relief, and exports had not returned to their former level before the war.\n\nSecond, Fianna Fáil turned from free trade to protection, with Seán Lemass as Minister for Industry and Commerce. Tariffs and quotas were placed on a wide range of imports, and the Control of Manufactures Acts of 1932 and 1934 required majority Irish ownership of manufacturing firms. The Industrial Credit Company and the Irish Sugar Company were set up in 1933, and semi-state bodies such as Aer Lingus followed in 1936. Industrial employment grew by tens of thousands, and supporters said this provided jobs that free trade would never have done. But many of the new firms were small and inefficient and depended on a small home market, and critics said protection raised prices without leading to exports.\n\nThird, the social record was mixed. The Housing Acts of 1932 and 1936 raised subsidies and local authorities built tens of thousands of houses, while the Unemployment Assistance Act of 1933 and the Widows' and Orphans' Pensions Act of 1935 extended help to the poor. Yet Dublin's tenements remained overcrowded, tuberculosis was common, and emigration rose again in the late 1930s and during the Second World War. The Emergency cut off imports and reinforced self-sufficiency, but living standards remained low. The caution of the Department of Finance under McElligott also limited investment. Overall the economy was stable rather than successful, and historians such as Joe Lee criticise it while crediting de Valera's political skills.\n\nIn Anglo-Irish relations, by contrast, the achievements were real. De Valera took power in March 1932 pledged to remove the oath and the symbols of the Treaty, and he did it by legislation. The Removal of Oath Act of 1933 ended the oath to the King, an Act that same year ended the appeal to the Privy Council, and the Seanad was abolished in 1936. He dismissed the Governor-General James McNeill in 1932 and replaced him with Domhnall Ua Buachalla. After the abdication of Edward VIII, the Constitution (Amendment No. 27) Act of 11 December 1936 removed the Crown and Governor-General from the Constitution, and the External Relations Act left the King only a role in diplomatic matters. Britain accepted these changes without military or economic action, partly because the Statute of Westminster supported his constitutional argument.\n\nThe crowning achievement was the set of Anglo-Irish Agreements of 25 April 1938. Under the defence agreement the Treaty ports at Berehaven, Cobh and Lough Swilly were handed to Ireland. The financial agreement ended the annuities dispute for a single payment of £10 million, far less than Britain had claimed, and the trade agreement ended the special duties. The 1937 constitution, Bunreacht na hÉireann, declared the state sovereign and independent and was accepted in practice by Britain. The ports made it possible to remain neutral in 1939, and neutrality during the Emergency was the clearest assertion of sovereignty. De Valera's failures were that he did not end partition, since Britain would not move on the border, and the 1938 settlement came only after six years of costly dispute.\n\nIt is fair to add that some of his gains rested on groundwork laid by Cosgrave at the Imperial Conferences, including the Balfour Declaration of 1926 and the Statute of Westminster. Even so, de Valera dismantled the Treaty settlement and secured the ports, while the Economic War damaged farmers, and protection fell short of its aims. Anglo-Irish relations were therefore the area in which he was far more successful than in managing the economy.",
