    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Between 1912 and 1945 cultural identity was promoted deliberately on both sides of the border, but in sharply different ways. In the Free State the government and Church used schools, publishing, broadcasting and the arts to build a Gaelic and Catholic national culture. In Northern Ireland the unionist state protected a Protestant and British identity, while the nationalist minority held on to its own through the GAA, Irish classes and Catholic schools.\n\nThe revival of the Irish language was the centrepiece of cultural policy in the South. Irish was made a compulsory subject in national schools in 1922, and many infant classes were taught entirely through it. A pass in Irish became necessary for State examinations and for entry to the civil service and the Garda, so language was tied directly to jobs, and civil servants were required to learn it. The Gaeltacht Commission of 1925 defined the Irish-speaking districts and recommended economic and educational support, and preparatory colleges trained teachers to teach through Irish. An Gúm was founded in 1926 to publish books in Irish, including translations and school texts, and Taibhdhearc na Gaillimhe opened in Galway in 1928 as an Irish-language theatre. The 1937 constitution then declared Irish the first official language. The results were mixed. Census figures showed the share able to speak Irish rising from about 18 per cent in 1926 to about a quarter in 1936, but the Gaeltacht itself kept shrinking through emigration and poverty, and many people resented the compulsion.\n\nThe state also promoted national identity through culture and the media. The Tailteann Games were held in 1924, 1928 and 1932 as a festival of sport and the arts modelled on the ancient games and open to people of Irish descent abroad. The Abbey Theatre received a state subsidy from 1925, the first for a national theatre in the English-speaking world, and the Gate Theatre was founded in 1928 to bring international drama to Dublin. The radio station 2RN began broadcasting on 1 January 1926, with a high-power transmitter at Athlone from 1932, and it became Radio Éireann in 1938. The Irish Folklore Commission was set up in 1935, and the Schools' Collection of 1937-38 had children gather local folklore. The GAA, with its Rule 27 banning members from foreign games, enforced a cultural boundary, and in 1938 it removed President Douglas Hyde as patron for attending a soccer match. Artists such as Evie Hone, who won first prize for her window My Four Green Fields at the 1939 New York World's Fair, helped give Ireland a modern identity.\n\nA Catholic identity was promoted alongside the Gaelic one. The Church managed most schools, and the state accepted this, while legislation reflected Catholic teaching. The Censorship of Films Act of 1923 and the Censorship of Publications Act of 1929 allowed films and books to be banned, and writers such as Samuel Beckett and later Seán Ó Faoláin were among those affected. Divorce was effectively closed off in 1925 and banned outright by the 1937 constitution, contraceptives were banned in 1935, and Article 44 recognised the special position of the Catholic Church. The Eucharistic Congress of 1932, with a closing Mass in the Phoenix Park attended by a crowd put at around a million, presented Ireland to the world as a devout Catholic nation. Critics argued that this vision excluded Protestants and urban people and encouraged conformity.\n\nIn Northern Ireland the state backed a unionist and Protestant identity. Orange Order parades on 12 July and loyalist bands enjoyed official protection, and the Union Jack and royal occasions, such as the opening of the Northern parliament by George V in 1921 and the Stormont buildings in 1932, symbolised the British link. Craig's statement in 1934 that he boasted of a Protestant parliament and a Protestant state matched the Catholic emphasis of the South. The Special Powers Act allowed the banning of processions and republican emblems, and nationalist parades were more likely to be banned. The Irish language received little state support.\n\nEducation shows the contest most clearly. The Londonderry Act of 1923 tried to create a non-denominational system under local education committees, but Protestant churches objected and the Act was amended in 1925 to allow Bible instruction. The Catholic Church refused to transfer its schools to state control, so they stayed outside the system with only partial funding. Nationalists turned instead to Catholic schools, the GAA and Irish classes to maintain their own culture, and the result was a divided school system that deepened segregation.\n\nOverall, attempts to promote cultural identity were energetic and, in the South especially, state-led, and they gave the new state institutions that survive today. But in both parts of the island the identities promoted were narrow. The South linked Irishness to Gaelic and Catholic values and restricted individual choice, while the North linked its identity to Protestantism and the Crown and left the minority on the margins. The effect was to strengthen the cultural division between North and South as well as within the North.",
