    "model": "Irish emigration between 1840 and 1914 was the product of conditions at home that pushed people out and opportunities abroad that drew them in. At home the potato failure, land hunger, evictions and a lack of industry left millions with little prospect, while abroad higher wages, cheap passages and the help of relatives already settled made leaving both attractive and possible.\n\nThe most dramatic factor at home was the Great Famine. Potato blight destroyed about a third of the crop in 1845 and almost all of it in 1846, and the poor, who depended on the potato, faced starvation. About one million people died, and between 1.5 and 2 million emigrated between 1845 and 1855, mostly to the United States, with large numbers to Britain and British North America. This was panic flight rather than planned movement: in 1847 about 300,000 destitute Irish people landed in Liverpool alone. Ireland's population fell from about 8.2 million in 1841 to about 6.5 million in 1851, which shows that the Famine turned a steady flow into a mass exodus.\n\nGovernment policy and landlord action pushed emigration further. The Poor Law Extension Act of 1847, with its Gregory clause, denied relief to anyone holding more than a quarter acre unless they gave up the land, and rising poor rates gave landlords a reason to clear smallholders, so evictions rose sharply between 1847 and 1852. Some landlords found it cheaper to pay passages than to support tenants in the workhouse: Major Denis Mahon of Strokestown sent 1,490 tenants to Quebec in 1847, and Lord Lansdowne paid for several thousand tenants from Kenmare in the early 1850s. The Encumbered Estates Act of 1849 allowed bankrupt estates to be sold, and many new owners cleared tenants to consolidate farms. Emigration was therefore often forced rather than freely chosen.\n\nThe pressures at home did not end with the Famine. Afterwards farms were no longer subdivided: one son inherited the holding and one daughter received a dowry, so the other children had to emigrate or stay unmarried. Late marriage, the move from tillage to cattle grazing and the lack of industry outside the north-east pushed young people out, and about four million left in the six decades after the Famine. The population of the island fell from about 6.5 million in 1851 to about 4.4 million by 1911. Agricultural depression and evictions around 1879 to 1882 caused a fresh surge from the west, so emigration became a settled part of rural life, with the poorest western counties sending the highest proportion of their people.\n\nFactors abroad were just as important in explaining where and how people went. Industrial Britain and North America offered far higher wages and constant demand for labour, in factories, on railways and canals, in mines and in domestic service. Britain was close and the fare was only a few shillings, which is why the poorest went there, while Quebec fares were cheaper than New York, so many Famine emigrants went to Canada. Colonial governments in Australia and New Zealand subsidised passages to attract settlers, so emigration there was largely assisted. Steamships replaced sail from the 1860s and cut the Atlantic crossing from about six weeks to about two, making the voyage far safer and encouraging more to risk it.\n\nThe networks that emigrants themselves built abroad kept the flow going. Relatives in America bought pre-paid tickets from shipping agents and posted them home, so people with no money could still leave. Remittances ran to well over a million pounds a year in the later nineteenth century, much of it from servant girls and labourers, and they paid the passages of younger brothers and sisters. Chain-emigration meant that new arrivals stayed with kin, found work through them and settled in the same parishes. The American letter, often containing dollars, told households in the west of wages and jobs abroad, and Ireland was unusual in sending about as many women as men.\n\nIn conclusion, emigration was driven first by home conditions, above all the Famine, evictions and the land inheritance system, but it was made possible and sustained by what lay abroad: jobs, cheap passages, assisted schemes and the support of relatives. Home pressures explain why people left, while the pull of America and Britain, strengthened by chain-emigration, explains why the flow continued long after the Famine had ended.",
