    "label": "2",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Anti-Irish sentiment in the nineteenth century in both the United States and Britain rested on religion, poverty, competition for work and a belief that the Irish were an inferior people. It was strongest where large numbers of poor Catholic Irish arrived quickly, as they did in the Famine years, and it was reinforced by cartoons and newspapers that spread stereotypes.\n\nReligion was the deepest cause. Most Irish emigrants were Catholic, and Protestant Americans and Britons often saw Catholicism as foreign and a threat to their institutions, with the Pope as a rival authority. In the United States this fear fuelled nativism, the movement against immigrants and Catholic influence, which peaked in the 1840s and 1850s. The Philadelphia riots of 1844 followed a dispute about the use of the Bible in schools, and nativist mobs attacked Irish Catholic churches and homes. The Know-Nothings, formally the American Party, won major state victories in 1854, including Massachusetts. They wanted longer waiting periods before immigrants could become citizens and voters, and to bar Catholics from public office. They feared that Catholic voters would take orders from their priests.\n\nIn Britain religious hostility was mixed with sectarian rivalry. The Stockport riots of 1852 saw Irish Catholic homes and churches attacked after a Catholic procession, and the Murphy riots of 1867 followed the anti-Catholic lectures of William Murphy and brought attacks on Irish districts in towns such as Birkenhead and Wolverhampton. In Liverpool and Glasgow, Protestant Orange lodges and Catholic Irish communities clashed, so sectarian rivalry shaped local politics and street life.\n\nPoverty and disease supplied a second cause. The Irish who arrived in the Famine years were destitute, and they crowded into the poorest slums of cities such as Liverpool, Glasgow, Manchester, New York and Boston. Outbreaks of typhus and cholera were blamed on them, and writers in Britain blamed them for low wages and crime in industrial towns. Irish migrants were also blamed for drunkenness and disorder and were over-represented in arrest figures. Native residents associated the Irish with dirt, crime and disease rather than seeing the conditions that forced them into these districts.\n\nEconomic competition added to the resentment. Irish labourers and servants competed with native-born and other workers for jobs, and their willingness to accept low wages made them unpopular, with tensions over strike-breaking. In America job notices were said to have stated No Irish Need Apply, although historians debate how common written signs really were. Whatever the extent of such signs, the fear that cheap Irish labour would undercut wages was real, and it gave hostility a practical edge.\n\nRacial stereotyping and political fear completed the picture. The Irish were often portrayed as a separate and inferior race. Punch magazine presented them in the 1840s and 1860s as simian, violent and backward, and Thomas Nast's cartoons in Harper's Weekly in the 1870s portrayed Irish Catholics, especially Irish politicians, as ape-like and corrupt. Political suspicion grew as Irish Americans used the vote through the Democratic Party and machines such as Tammany Hall, which made nativists fear Catholic influence on city government. In Britain, Fenian activity heightened fears: after the Manchester Martyrs were executed in November 1867 and the Clerkenwell prison bomb killed about a dozen people that December, many believed that all Irish people were a security threat, and Irish districts were heavily policed.\n\nIn conclusion, hostility to the Irish was not the product of one cause. Religion gave it its fervour, poverty and competition for work gave it its everyday grievances, and racial caricature and political fear made it respectable. Irish communities responded by building their own parishes, schools and newspapers and by using the vote, which in the longer run turned hostility into political strength.",
