    "label": "1",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Neither leader fully solved the problem of a divided state, but O'Neill was the more effective leader because he recognised that Northern Ireland had to change and began the process, whereas Brookeborough managed the state for twenty years without addressing its weaknesses. Brookeborough was effective at keeping the Unionist Party in power and the Union secure. O'Neill was ambitious in his aims but ultimately failed to carry his own community with him.\n\nBrookeborough, Prime Minister from 1943 to 1963, delivered stability and the benefits of the post-war welfare state. The National Health Service began in Northern Ireland on 5 July 1948, the same day as in Britain, and the Education Act of 1947 gave free secondary education to all. This opened grammar schools and Queen's University to Catholic children, and by the 1960s an educated Catholic middle class was emerging. The Social Services Agreement of 1949 guaranteed parity with Britain, with the British Treasury meeting much of the cost. These reforms raised living standards, but they owed more to Westminster's policy than to Brookeborough's initiative.\n\nBrookeborough was also effective in defending the constitutional position. The Ireland Act of 1949 guaranteed that Northern Ireland would remain in the United Kingdom unless Stormont consented, and the Unionist Party won 37 of 52 seats in the February 1949 election. When the IRA launched Operation Harvest in December 1956, the government introduced internment in 1957, and the campaign collapsed and was called off in February 1962 having won little nationalist support. For unionists this was real success, and it explains why he is remembered so warmly in his own community.\n\nHowever, his effectiveness ended where the interests of the Catholic minority began. His government kept the Special Powers Act of 1922 and the almost wholly Protestant B Specials, and it resisted reform of local government and the franchise. Gerrymandering in Derry, where Catholics were the majority but unionists controlled the Corporation through ward boundaries drawn in 1936, went unchecked, as did the company vote and discrimination in housing and jobs. Brooke had himself urged Protestants to employ Protestants in the 1930s. The economy also stagnated. Shipbuilding and linen declined, unemployment was the highest in the United Kingdom, emigration was heavy, and new industry went mostly to the east of the Bann. By 1963 he had left a legacy of unresolved grievance.\n\nO'Neill, Prime Minister from March 1963, set out to address the economic weakness and the sectarian divide. He had been Minister of Finance since 1956, and he promoted planning through the Matthew Report and the Wilson Plan, which led to the new city of Craigavon and the university at Coleraine. He made gestures to Catholics, visiting their schools and hospitals, and on 14 January 1965 he received the Taoiseach, Seán Lemass, at Stormont, the first meeting of the two heads of government since partition. In response the Nationalist Party under Eddie McAteer became the official opposition in 1965. This bridge-building showed a vision that Brookeborough never had.\n\nO'Neill's weakness was that he could neither deliver enough nor hold his party together. His economic projects were seen in Derry and the west as favouring the east, and the choice of Coleraine over Derry for the university was resented. Catholics found his gestures welcome but his reforms too slow. Hardliners led by Ian Paisley ran an 'O'Neill must go' campaign and accused him of betraying the Union. When the civil rights movement began marching and the RUC batoned marchers in Derry on 5 October 1968, O'Neill was forced to respond under British pressure. His five-point package of 22 November 1968 promised a points system for housing, an ombudsman, the replacement of Londonderry Corporation and an end to the company vote. His party accepted one man, one vote only in April 1969.\n\nO'Neill resigned on 28 April 1969 after a divided February election and bomb attacks on water and electricity installations, and the violence of August 1969 followed. Brookeborough kept order for twenty years but did so by ignoring the problem, and the grievances he left unaddressed fuelled the civil rights movement. O'Neill's tragedy was that he began reform too late and without his party's support. Because he at least tried to modernise the economy, open dialogue with Dublin and begin reform of discrimination, I judge him the more effective leader, though not the more successful one in terms of outcomes, since Brookeborough left office in better order than O'Neill did.",
