    "label": "2",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Both the Coleraine decision and the Apprentice Boys' activities were divisive, but to different degrees. The Coleraine controversy was a political dispute within a formal process, and it deepened nationalist suspicion of Stormont. The Apprentice Boys' parades were more deeply divisive because they expressed a historical identity that each community interpreted in opposite ways, and in August 1969 they were the occasion for serious violence.\n\nThe controversy over the second university arose from the expansion of higher education following the Robbins Report of 1963. Derry had Magee College, founded in 1865, and was the second city of Northern Ireland with high unemployment and emigration. A University for Derry Committee, chaired by the young John Hume, drew support from nationalists and some unionists and business people in the city. When the Lockwood Committee reported in February 1965 in favour of Coleraine, a much smaller town, and the government accepted this, Derry supporters organised a motorcade to Stormont in protest. The unionist majority carried the decision, and the slogan of 'faceless men' reflected the belief that politics lay behind the choice.\n\nThis decision was divisive because it was seen as part of a pattern. Derry nationalists linked it to the closure of the Derry Road railway line in 1965 after the Benson Report, to the choice of Craigavon in County Armagh as a new city, and to the unionist control of Londonderry Corporation through gerrymandered ward boundaries. The 'west of the Bann' grievance, that investment went east, drew in more people than the sectarian argument alone. The campaign also gave Hume experience of organising that he would later use in the civil rights movement and the SDLP. However, the case against Stormont was less clear-cut than nationalists claimed. The decision followed a formal committee process, the government argued that Coleraine offered a central site and room to expand, and the allegation of a plot was never proved. Some Derry unionists had also supported the Derry bid, so the divide was not purely religious.\n\nThe Apprentice Boys of Derry were founded in 1814 to commemorate the siege of 1688 to 1689, when thirteen apprentices shut the gates against the army of James II and the city held out for 105 days until the Mountjoy broke the boom on the Foyle on 28 July 1689. For unionists the annual parades in August and the December Shutting of the Gates, with the burning of an effigy of Governor Lundy, celebrate the defence of Protestant liberty. The slogan 'No Surrender' and the siege mentality it expressed were part of the unionist identity, and the Memorial Hall, opened in 1877, was its centre.\n\nFor nationalists the same events looked very different. The parade route along the walls above the Bogside, a Catholic and working-class area in a city with a Catholic majority, was a reminder of Protestant ascendancy in a city where unionists held power by gerrymandering. Critics called the parades triumphalist and a provocation. This made them a recurring source of tension, and the dispute linked cultural symbols to the housing and voting grievances of the civil rights campaign.\n\nThe consequences of the parade on 12 August 1969 show how divisive they could be. Stone-throwing and clashes with the RUC near the Bogside developed into the Battle of the Bogside, which lasted from 12 to 14 August. Residents built barricades and declared Free Derry, and British troops were deployed on 14 August 1969, a turning point in the Troubles. The Apprentice Boys maintained that their parade was legal and traditional and that disorder was started by others, but the effect was the same. In later years routes were restricted or negotiated, the IRA blew up the Walker Pillar in 1973, and the departure of many Protestants from the west bank of Derry deepened the sense of siege.\n\nOverall, both episodes were significantly divisive. Coleraine reinforced the belief that Stormont would not treat the west fairly, but it was a single decision and a matter of dispute rather than violence. The Apprentice Boys' parades touched the identity of both communities and contributed directly to the outbreak of violence in 1969. They were the more divisive of the two, though the tradition was also a genuine commemoration that its members valued, and much of the division lay in how it was interpreted.",
