    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The Troubles had profound social and economic effects. Economically, they deterred investment, damaged tourism and left Northern Ireland dependent on British public money and a growing public sector. Socially, they caused thousands of deaths, deepened residential and educational segregation, and placed lasting strain on health and everyday life. The costs were borne by both communities, although working-class areas suffered most.\n\nThe most direct social effect was the loss of life. Between 1969 and 1993 about 3,000 people died, and the total for the whole conflict is about 3,500, with tens of thousands injured. The year 1972 was the worst, with nearly 500 deaths. About half of the dead were civilians, and the violence was concentrated in Belfast, Derry and the border counties, in working-class areas such as Ardoyne, the Falls and the Shankill. The Dublin and Monaghan bombings of 1974 also showed that the effects were felt beyond the north.\n\nThe violence also damaged the economy by discouraging investment and tourism. Foreign firms were reluctant to build factories in a province where bombs and disruption were a risk, and visitors stayed away. Belfast city centre was enclosed behind the security cordon known as the ring of steel, which harmed trade. Businesses faced high insurance premiums, some moved to Britain or the Republic, and security checks added to the cost of moving goods and people. Compensation for bomb damage added to public spending. The De Lorean car factory at Dunmurry, which opened in 1981 with large British grants and loans to provide about 2,000 jobs, went into receivership in 1982 and became a symbol of the difficulty of attracting investment.\n\nThe structure of the economy also shifted. Traditional industries such as shipbuilding, linen and engineering continued to lose jobs, and several man-made fibre plants cut back or closed. The government took Harland and Wolff into public ownership in 1975 and Short Brothers in 1977 to protect jobs. At the same time public-sector employment grew, with jobs in health, education and administration, and in the security sector, which included the RUC, the Ulster Defence Regiment and the prison service. British subvention rose and sustained living standards above what local output could support, which left the economy exposed to changes in British government spending.\n\nUnemployment remained the highest of any UK region for much of the period, and Catholic unemployment was consistently higher than Protestant, in some surveys roughly twice as high. Deprivation was most severe in west Belfast and Derry. Government responded with the Fair Employment Act of 1976, which set up a Fair Employment Agency, and the stronger Act of 1989, which created a Fair Employment Commission and required employers to monitor their workforce. These were real steps, but they did not close the gap between the communities.\n\nSocially, the Troubles drove the separation of the two communities. Intimidation forced families out of mixed areas into segregated estates, despite the allocation policy of the Northern Ireland Housing Executive, set up in 1971 to take over housing from the councils. Peace walls were built from 1969 between Protestant and Catholic streets, and people had less contact with the other community. Schools stayed largely segregated, although parents formed All Children Together in 1974, Lagan College opened in 1981 as the first integrated secondary school, and the Education Reform Order of 1989 gave statutory support to integrated education.\n\nHealth and daily life were also affected. The Royal Victoria Hospital in Belfast developed expertise in gunshot and blast injuries, and the demands on staff were heavy. Stress and trauma were widespread among those who had lived through bombings and shootings. Checkpoints, searches and sealed-off town centres became routine, and children grew up among the security forces and memories of violence.\n\nOverall, the Troubles left Northern Ireland poorer, more divided and more dependent on Britain, with the heaviest costs in the working-class areas of Belfast and Derry. Some reforms in housing, employment and education improved matters, but the divisions in housing, schooling and opportunity were deepened rather than healed.",
