    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The Sunningdale Agreement of 1973, the Anglo-Irish Agreement of 1985 and the Downing Street Declaration of 1993 were each important because they tried to break the political deadlock in Northern Ireland by balancing unionist consent with an Irish dimension. Sunningdale failed within months, but it set out the template of power sharing and an Irish dimension that later settlements followed. The Anglo-Irish Agreement gave Dublin a formal role for the first time, and the Downing Street Declaration set out the principles of consent and self-determination on which the peace process was built.\n\nSunningdale grew out of the British decision to suspend Stormont and impose Direct Rule in 1972. A border poll in March 1973 produced a 98.9 per cent vote to remain in the United Kingdom, though nationalists boycotted it and turnout was only about 59 per cent. The White Paper of 1973 then proposed an Assembly elected by proportional representation and an executive in which unionists and nationalists shared power. After the Assembly election of June 1973, Brian Faulkner became Chief Executive with Gerry Fitt of the SDLP as his deputy, and John Hume became Minister of Commerce. This was the first time in the history of the state that nationalists held office in a Northern Ireland government. That was the first part of its importance.\n\nThe conference at Sunningdale Park in December 1973, attended by Edward Heath, Liam Cosgrave and the Northern Ireland parties, added the Irish dimension. A Council of Ireland was to be set up with a council of ministers of seven from each side and a consultative assembly of sixty members. The Irish government accepted that Northern Ireland's status would not change without the consent of a majority there, and a Law Enforcement Commission was established to deal with fugitive offenders. For nationalists this recognised their Irish identity. For many unionists, however, the Council of Ireland looked like a first step towards a united Ireland, and the fact that Dublin did not change Articles 2 and 3 of its constitution deepened their suspicion, especially after the Boland case.\n\nThe weakness of Sunningdale was that Faulkner could not carry his own community. The Ulster Unionist Council rejected the Council of Ireland by 427 votes to 374 in January 1974 and Faulkner resigned the party leadership. In the Westminster election of February 1974 the anti-agreement United Ulster Unionist Council won eleven of the twelve Northern Ireland seats. The Ulster Workers' Council strike, which began on 15 May 1974, cut electricity and paralysed the province, and the new Labour government under Harold Wilson and Merlyn Rees would not confront the strikers. Faulkner resigned on 28 May and the executive collapsed. Sunningdale's importance therefore lay partly in its failure, since it taught later negotiators that any settlement needed unionist consent and a government willing to defend it.\n\nThe Anglo-Irish Agreement, signed at Hillsborough on 15 November 1985 by Margaret Thatcher and Garret FitzGerald, was the biggest shift in British policy since Sunningdale. It set up an Intergovernmental Conference with a permanent secretariat at Maryfield near Belfast, through which the Irish government could put forward its views on Northern Ireland. Article 1 reaffirmed that there would be no change in status without the consent of a majority. Thatcher's motives were to improve security cooperation across the border and to strengthen the SDLP against Sinn Féin, which had grown after the hunger strikes of 1981. The Agreement mattered because it gave Dublin a permanent, formal consultative role that did not depend on unionist cooperation.\n\nUnionists reacted with fury, because they had not been consulted. The 'Ulster says No' rally at Belfast City Hall on 23 November 1985 drew huge crowds, and fifteen unionist MPs resigned their Westminster seats in January 1986 to force by-elections. Yet the Agreement survived, which showed that unionists could no longer veto British policy. Republicans rejected it because British jurisdiction remained, and violence continued, so it did not bring peace. Its importance was that the two governments now worked together on Northern Ireland, and that cooperation underlay later progress.\n\nThe Downing Street Declaration of 15 December 1993, issued by John Major and Albert Reynolds, built on years of dialogue, including the talks between John Hume and Gerry Adams. Britain declared that it had no selfish strategic or economic interest in Northern Ireland and would legislate for a united Ireland if a majority there wished it. The Irish people as a whole had the right of self-determination, to be exercised by agreement between North and South, and a united Ireland required the consent of a majority in Northern Ireland. Dublin promised a forum for peace and reconciliation and a review of Articles 2 and 3, and republicans were invited to renounce violence and join talks. Unionists feared it moved Britain towards neutrality, but it gave the republican movement a route into politics, and the IRA ceasefire of 1994 followed.\n\nOverall, Sunningdale was the least successful in the short term, but it fixed the basic ideas of power sharing and an Irish dimension. The Anglo-Irish Agreement gave Ireland a lasting institutional voice, and the Downing Street Declaration was the most directly important for peace, because it set out consent and self-determination in a form both governments and the republican movement could work with. Together they were stepping stones to the Good Friday Agreement of 1998.",
