    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Between 1949 and 1989 Irish education was transformed from a system that left most children with only primary schooling into one open to all, and attitudes to the Irish language moved from state compulsion towards a more realistic and flexible approach. Both changes reflected the economic modernisation and the growing questioning of old ideas that marked the period from the 1950s onwards.\n\nBefore the 1960s the state left most second-level education to the churches and to parents who could pay. Most children left school at 14 with only primary education, secondary schools charged fees and were mostly owned by religious orders, and vocational schools, set up in 1930, had lower status. Planning for growth after 1958 raised the question of whether the country was educating enough people with the skills it needed. Patrick Hillery, Minister for Education from 1959 to 1965, commissioned a survey with the help of the OECD. Its report, Investment in Education (1965), showed wide differences in participation by social class and region and a shortage of trained teachers and technical skills, and it argued that education was an investment in growth and not only a cultural or religious matter.\n\nTwo ministers turned the findings into reform. In 1963 Hillery announced comprehensive schools, state-funded schools offering both academic and practical subjects to pupils of all abilities, and the first opened in 1966 in places such as Carraroe and Cootehill, areas that lacked second-level schools. Donogh O'Malley, Minister from July 1966, announced on 10 September 1966 that free second-level education would begin the following year. The scheme started in September 1967, abolished fees in schools that joined it, and was accompanied by free school transport that allowed rural pupils to reach a school. Most schools joined and enrolment rose steeply. The Primary Certificate was abolished in 1967 and the school-leaving age was raised from 14 to 15 in 1972.\n\nThe reforms went on into the 1970s. Community schools, usually formed by merging a secondary and a vocational school, offered a full range of subjects to all pupils and served adults. Regional Technical Colleges opened from 1970 in towns such as Carlow, Dundalk, Athlone and Waterford, the Higher Education Authority was set up in 1968 and a higher education grants scheme was introduced the same year to help students from poorer homes. The National Institute for Higher Education opened in Limerick in 1972. A new primary curriculum in 1971 replaced a curriculum built on memorisation with one centred on the needs of the child, with more activity-based learning, art, music and drama. The number of third-level students grew strongly, and more women and more students from rural and working-class homes reached university than before.\n\nThere were limits. The churches kept control of management in most schools and the bishops were wary of comprehensive and community schools, so they gained representation on boards. Many schools were overcrowded and short of qualified teachers after 1967, free fees did not remove the costs of books, uniforms and travel, and the west had fewer places than the east. Historians note that the reforms were driven as much by the economic need for skills as by social ideals, and that the middle classes benefited first, but all accept that they were a major change of direction.\n\nAttitudes to the Irish language changed in a different way. The 1937 Constitution declared Irish the national and first official language, and governments backed its revival through the schools, the civil service and the Gaeltacht. Irish was compulsory in school, a pass was required for the Leaving Certificate and the language was needed for civil service entry. Yet it was losing ground as a community language, and the Gaeltacht declined through emigration. Gael Linn, founded in 1953, tried to promote Irish by popular means, including records, the Amharc Éireann newsreel from 1956 and the film Mise Éire in 1959, and Gaeltarra Éireann was set up in 1957 to encourage industry in Irish-speaking areas.\n\nFrom the 1960s public opinion turned against compulsion. The Commission on the Restoration of the Irish Language reported in 1963 and a White Paper of 1965 accepted restoration as an aim but proposed a more flexible approach. The Language Freedom Movement, founded in 1966, argued that compulsion turned pupils against the language and provoked counter-campaigns. In 1973 the Fine Gael-Labour government, with Richard Burke as Minister for Education, ended the rule that students must pass Irish to gain the Leaving Certificate, and the civil service requirement was relaxed.\n\nAt the same time the language gained new institutions. The Gaeltacht Civil Rights Movement of 1969 ran a pirate station in 1970, and Raidió na Gaeltachta began broadcasting from Connemara on 2 April 1972. Bord na Gaeilge was created in 1975 and Údarás na Gaeltachta in 1980. Traditional music also revived, through Comhaltas Ceoltóirí Éireann from 1951, Seán Ó Riada's work and groups such as The Chieftains. Even so, the number of everyday speakers continued to fall outside the Gaeltacht.\n\nOverall, education changed more fundamentally, because free post-primary schooling, new school types and wider access to college altered the life chances of a generation. The language kept its symbolic status and gained broadcasting and institutions, but it lost its compulsory position and its decline as a community language continued, while traditional music enjoyed a real revival.",
