    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Archbishop McQuaid, Garret FitzGerald and Mary Robinson each left a real mark on Irish society, but in different ways. McQuaid was the embodiment of Catholic power over social policy, FitzGerald tried and largely failed to make the state more pluralist, and Robinson created the legal and political groundwork for reforms that only came in the 1990s. Taken together they show a society slowly moving from clerical control towards liberalism.\n\nJohn Charles McQuaid, Archbishop of Dublin from 1940 to 1972, was the most influential churchman in the state. Born in Cootehill, Co. Cavan, and formerly president of Blackrock College, he had personal access to ministers, and had advised de Valera on the religion article of the 1937 Constitution. His impact was greatest on social policy. In the Mother and Child crisis of 1950-51, Noël Browne proposed free care for mothers and for children up to sixteen, without a means test. McQuaid advised Browne and Costello that the scheme conflicted with Catholic social teaching and parental rights, and the bishops wrote to Costello to say so. MacBride asked Browne to resign, which he did on 11 April 1951, and Costello told the Dáil that the government was bound to accept the bishops' decision. The affair helped to bring down the first Inter-Party Government and the Health Act 1953 replaced the scheme with a means-tested system. It showed that in the 1950s the church could defeat the elected government on a matter of social policy.\n\nMcQuaid's influence extended into education and welfare. He oversaw the building of new churches and schools in the growing Dublin suburbs and founded charitable bodies such as the Catholic Social Service Conference, so he shaped everyday life as well as politics. He attended Vatican II, but on his return in December 1965 he reassured Dubliners that the council would not disturb their faith. He introduced liturgical reforms slowly and was wary of ecumenism and of lay initiatives outside clerical control. Admirers praised his efficiency and devotion, while critics, notably his biographer John Cooney, saw him as the ruler of Catholic Ireland whose authoritarian style came to seem out of date after his retirement in 1972. His impact was therefore to slow the pace of change in Irish society for a generation.\n\nGarret FitzGerald, an economist and the son of Desmond FitzGerald, a minister in the first Free State government, was Minister for Foreign Affairs from 1973 to 1977 and Taoiseach from 1981 to 1982 and from 1982 to 1987. In 1981 he announced a constitutional crusade to make the Republic more pluralist and so more attractive to Northern unionists, with divorce reform and the removal of Catholic influences from the law. The results were disappointing. The 1983 referendum inserted the Eighth Amendment banning abortion, in a form FitzGerald had not wished to see, and the 1986 divorce referendum was defeated. These outcomes showed that the church still had great influence over social questions in the 1980s. He did achieve some change, since in 1985 his government allowed contraceptives to be sold without prescription to people over 18.\n\nFitzGerald's wider impact lay in putting new ideas on the agenda. He set up the New Ireland Forum in 1983 and negotiated the Anglo-Irish Agreement with Margaret Thatcher in 1985, which gave the Irish government a consultative role in Northern Ireland. His economic record was weaker, as unemployment and debt kept rising and Labour left the government in January 1987 over spending cuts. Historians credit him with opening Irish politics to pluralism, while judging that he could not deliver the social change he sought.\n\nMary Robinson was born in Ballina, Co. Mayo, in 1944, was called to the bar in 1967 and became Reid Professor of Law at Trinity at the age of 25. As an independent senator for the University of Dublin from 1969 to 1989 she used the Oireachtas and the courts to challenge discriminatory law. She introduced bills in the early 1970s to liberalise the law on contraception, which the Seanad refused to debate, and later sought reform of the law on homosexuality. She campaigned for women to serve on juries, which was enacted in the Juries Act 1976, and as a barrister she took cases against the State, including that of David Norris against the law criminalising homosexual acts. Many of her efforts failed at the time, but she put issues of rights and equality on the public agenda and was attacked by conservatives and the church for doing so. Her persistence prepared the way for the reforms of the 1990s and for her election as President in 1990, which was itself a sign of a changing society.\n\nOverall, McQuaid's impact was greatest in the 1940s, 1950s and 1960s, when he entrenched church influence over health, education and morality. FitzGerald and Robinson, working in the 1970s and 1980s, challenged that influence, though neither won quick victories. Their combined effect was to expose the limits of clerical power and to lay the foundations for a more liberal and pluralist Irish society.",
