    "label": "2",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Robert Owen and Robert Peel were both effective reformers, but in very different ways and to different degrees. Peel was the more effective in the practical sense, because he changed the law, the police, the currency system and the tax and tariff structure of the whole country from within government. Owen was a pioneer whose model at New Lanark achieved real improvements for one community and inspired later movements, but his wider schemes failed and his direct influence on legislation was limited.\n\nOwen's reforms at New Lanark were remarkable for their time. When he took over the mills in 1800 the village of about 2,000 people included some 500 pauper children, the working day was about thirteen hours, the houses were dirty and overcrowded and discipline relied on fines and punishment. Owen stopped taking pauper children as apprentices, raised the minimum age of employment so that children under ten no longer worked in the mills, and by 1816 had reduced the working day to ten and three-quarter hours. He replaced punishment with the Silent Monitor, a block of wood with four coloured sides hung beside each worker to record conduct. During the American embargo of 1806 he kept paying wages to idle workers for about four months. He rebuilt the housing, cleaned the streets, opened a village store selling good-quality goods near cost price and controlled the sale of drink.\n\nOwen's educational work was especially innovative. The Institute for the Formation of Character and the infant school, both opened in 1816, offered adults lectures, music and classes, and took children from the age of one or two to six, using songs, games and practical activities rather than rote learning. The infant school was among the earliest of its kind in Britain and attracted visitors from abroad. Owen also showed that these reforms did not bankrupt the business, since New Lanark stayed profitable under his management, and he argued that investing in workers was sound economics. This was effective as a demonstration that humane conditions and profit could go together.\n\nHowever, Owen's effectiveness beyond New Lanark was limited. He campaigned for factory legislation and gave evidence to a parliamentary committee in 1816, but the Cotton Mills and Factories Act of 1819 was weaker than he wanted, barring children under nine and limiting hours for those aged nine to sixteen to twelve a day, with no inspectors, so it was poorly enforced. His plans for self-supporting co-operative villages, presented in 1817 and 1820, were rejected, and his reputation was damaged in 1817 when he denounced all organised religions. His ventures failed one after another: New Harmony in Indiana, founded in 1825, collapsed within a few years amid disputes, the Equitable Labour Exchange of 1832 lasted only a few years, and the Grand National Consolidated Trades Union of 1834, which he led, collapsed within months. New Lanark was not widely copied, because few owners would accept such control and most mill profits depended on low wages and long hours.\n\nPeel, by contrast, reformed the state itself. As Home Secretary he founded the Metropolitan Police in 1829, a professional force that became a model for others. With Wellington he conceded Catholic emancipation in 1829 to avoid conflict in Ireland, at the cost of angering many Tories. His Tamworth Manifesto of 1834 set out a moderate programme of reform within the Conservative tradition and became the basis of the modern Conservative Party. As Prime Minister from 1841 to 1846 he reintroduced income tax in 1842 to pay for tariff cuts, and his budgets of 1842 and 1845 lowered duties on many imports. The Bank Charter Act of 1844 regulated the issue of banknotes by the Bank of England, and the Factory Act of 1844 limited hours for women and children.\n\nPeel's greatest act was the repeal of the Corn Laws in 1846, prompted partly by the Irish famine and supported by Cobden and Bright of the Anti-Corn Law League. It established free trade, and it showed that he put what he saw as the national interest above party, but it split the Conservatives and ended his government. Disraeli attacked him as a traitor, although historians such as Norman Gash rank him among the greatest British statesmen of the century.\n\nOwen worked outside government to create model communities based on co-operation and communal ownership, while Peel reformed from within and wanted to preserve private property while making the existing order more efficient. Owen's reforms were confined to one community, whereas Peel's measures affected the entire country and were accepted by much of the middle class. Owen did leave an important legacy, because his writings inspired the co-operative movement, and the Rochdale Pioneers opened their store in 1844.\n\nOn balance, Peel was the more effective reformer, since his changes were national, lasting and carried into law. Owen was more original and showed what was possible, and his influence on education, factory reform and co-operation was significant, but most of his own projects failed.",
