    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Both Italy and Germany moved towards unity through a combination of nationalist feeling, failed revolutions, the leadership of one strong state and the use of war and diplomacy. In Italy Piedmont under Cavour, with French help and Garibaldi's volunteers, created the Kingdom of Italy in 1861 and added Venetia and Rome by 1871. In Germany Prussia under Bismarck defeated Denmark, Austria and France in turn and created the German Empire in 1871.\n\nItaly in 1815 was divided into several states, most of them under Austrian influence. Austria ruled Lombardy and Venetia directly, Habsburg princes ruled Tuscany, Modena and Parma, the Pope governed the Papal States and the Bourbons held the Kingdom of the Two Sicilies. Only Piedmont-Sardinia, under the House of Savoy, was ruled by a native dynasty. Early nationalism came from the Carbonari, whose revolts of 1820 in Naples and 1821 in Piedmont were crushed by Austrian troops, and from Giuseppe Mazzini, who founded Young Italy in 1831 to work for a united republic. His conspiracies largely failed, but he gave the movement an ideal. The revolutions of 1848 and 1849 also ended in defeat, because Charles Albert of Piedmont was beaten at Custoza and Novara, Pius IX withdrew from the war, and French troops crushed the Roman Republic in July 1849. Italians learned that they could not defeat Austria without outside help.\n\nPiedmont became the leader of unification after 1849, because it alone kept its constitution, the Statuto Albertino. Cavour, Prime Minister from 1852, modernised the economy through free trade and railways, and sent about 15,000 troops to the Crimean War in 1855, which won him a place at the Congress of Paris in 1856, where he raised the Italian question. At Plombières in July 1858 he agreed with Napoleon III that France would support Piedmont against Austria in return for Nice and Savoy. War followed in 1859, and the French and Piedmontese won at Magenta and Solferino. Napoleon III then made peace at Villafranca without consulting Cavour, and Piedmont gained only Lombardy, but in March 1860 Tuscany, Modena, Parma and the Romagna voted in plebiscites to join Piedmont.\n\nThe south was won by Garibaldi. He sailed from Quarto with about 1,000 volunteers on 5 May 1860, landed at Marsala, won at Calatafimi and took Palermo, then crossed to the mainland and entered Naples in September 1860 as Francis II withdrew. Piedmontese troops invaded the Papal Marches and Umbria, and Garibaldi handed his conquests to Victor Emmanuel II. Plebiscites approved annexation and the Kingdom of Italy was proclaimed on 17 March 1861. Venetia came in 1866 after Italy allied with Prussia against Austria, and Italian troops entered Rome on 20 September 1870 once the French garrison was withdrawn during the Franco-Prussian War.\n\nGermany in 1815 was a loose Confederation of 39 states under Austrian presidency, designed to resist revolution rather than promote unity. Liberal and national feeling grew through the Burschenschaften, the Wartburg and Hambach festivals and the Frankfurt Parliament of 1848, but this attempt failed. A more lasting step was economic: the Zollverein, in force from 1834, united most German states in a customs union without Austria, so Prussia became the economic leader. By the 1850s the coal and iron of the Ruhr and Silesia, railways and Krupp's steelworks at Essen gave Prussia a strength that Austria's indebted, protectionist empire could not match.\n\nBismarck, appointed Minister-President in September 1862 to break the deadlock over Roon's army reforms, used war to achieve unity. In 1864 Prussia and Austria together defeated Denmark and took Schleswig, Holstein and Lauenburg, and the Convention of Gastein of 1865 divided their administration, which created a quarrel he could exploit. In 1866 Bismarck allied Prussia with Italy and went to war with Austria. Prussia, armed with the breech-loading needle-gun and Moltke's rail mobilisation, won the decisive battle of Königgrätz on 3 July. The Treaty of Prague dissolved the Confederation and excluded Austria, Prussia annexed Hanover and other states, and the North German Confederation was formed in 1867 with the Prussian king as president and a Reichstag elected by universal male suffrage. Bismarck deliberately avoided humiliating Austria, to keep it as a future friend.\n\nThe final step was war with France. After the Hohenzollern candidacy for the Spanish throne, Bismarck published an edited version of the Ems telegram of July 1870, which made France appear insulted, and France declared war. The southern states joined Prussia under their treaties, and the French were beaten at Sedan on 2 September 1870, where Napoleon III was captured. The German Empire was proclaimed at Versailles on 18 January 1871 with William I as emperor, and the Treaty of Frankfurt gave Germany Alsace and part of Lorraine.\n\nIn both countries unity came less through popular revolution, which failed in 1848, than through the leadership of a strong state, the weakness of Austria and the diplomacy of Cavour and Bismarck. Italy depended more on foreign help and Garibaldi's volunteers, whereas Germany was achieved mainly by Prussian military and industrial strength.",
