    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Bismarck was clearly the more effective handler of German affairs. He created a stable Reich, governed it for nineteen years and kept Germany at peace, whereas Wilhelm II, for all the energy and ambition he brought, left Germany isolated, encircled and heading for war by 1914. Bismarck's record was not flawless, particularly at home, but his achievements were far more solid.\n\nIn foreign policy Bismarck's skill was outstanding. After 1871 he declared Germany a satiated power and set out to keep France isolated, since he assumed that France, embittered by the loss of Alsace and Lorraine, would never be a friend. He built a web of agreements: the Three Emperors' League of 1873, the Dual Alliance with Austria-Hungary in 1879, the Triple Alliance with Italy in 1882, and the secret Reinsurance Treaty with Russia in 1887, which promised neutrality if either was at war with a third power. At the Congress of Berlin in 1878 he acted as an honest broker and helped avoid a European war over the Balkans. The result was that Germany fought no war after 1871 and never faced a war on two fronts while he held office. The system was complicated and depended on his personal skill, and the Reinsurance Treaty sat uneasily with the Dual Alliance, but it worked as long as he ran it.\n\nAt home Bismarck's record was more mixed, but he still achieved a great deal. He consolidated the new state with a common currency, the mark, in 1873 and a central bank, the Reichsbank, and he governed with a Reichstag that could not remove him. His Kulturkampf against the Catholic Church, begun with the May Laws of 1873, failed and left the Catholic Centre Party stronger, and the Anti-Socialist Law of 1878 did not stop the SPD, which won about 1.4 million votes in 1890. Yet in 1883, 1884 and 1889 he introduced sickness, accident and old age insurance, the first national scheme of its kind in a major state, and his tariff of 1879 protected German industry. His methods were harsh and left a divided society, but the Reich was stable and respected.\n\nWilhelm II's handling of German affairs began with an act that showed his ambition. In 1890 he forced Bismarck to resign because he wanted to rule personally. He promised a new course at home, easing the Anti-Socialist Law and sponsoring workers' protection laws, but when the SPD kept growing he turned against it, so the policy gained nothing. His own personality was a liability. His Hun speech of 1900 and the Daily Telegraph interview of 1908 offended foreign and German opinion alike, and after the latter the Reichstag demanded constitutional restraint and Bülow resigned in 1909. A ruler who was supposed to guide the state proved impulsive and unreliable.\n\nThe gravest failures came in foreign affairs. Caprivi and Holstein allowed the Reinsurance Treaty to lapse in 1890, and Russia turned to France, producing the Franco-Russian Alliance of 1894 and exactly the two-front threat that Bismarck had feared. Wilhelm's Weltpolitik was clumsy: the Kruger telegram of 1896 angered Britain, and the landing at Tangier in 1905 and the Agadir crisis of 1911 ended in humiliation at Algeciras and in a settlement that gave Germany little. Tirpitz's naval laws of 1898 and 1900 produced the arms race with Britain, particularly after HMS Dreadnought in 1906, and drove Britain into the Entente with France and Russia. Germany had the second-largest fleet in the world, but it never overtook Britain, strained the finances of the Reich and did not deter anyone. In July 1914 Wilhelm gave Austria-Hungary the blank cheque and could not stop the mobilisations that followed.\n\nWilhelm II did have achievements. Germany grew into the leading industrial power in Europe, gained colonies, Kiaochow and the Baghdad Railway concession, and the navy and the colonial movement won him popularity and brought Conservatives and National Liberals together. The defeat of 1918, the mutinies at Kiel and Wilhelmshaven that began the revolution, and his abdication on 9 November 1918 show where his policies led. Bismarck's achievements were more lasting and his foreign policy far more effective, and although his domestic settlement left problems for his successors, he gave Germany security while Wilhelm II lost it.",
