    "label": "2",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The New Imperialism, the rush of European powers from about 1880 to acquire colonies and spheres of influence, had several causes: economic, strategic, nationalist, missionary and ideological, all made possible by new technology. Its effects on Europe were significant. It created rivalries and crises that raised tension, led the powers to settle some disputes and form new groupings, and exposed the brutality of European rule, although the war of 1914 itself came from Europe's own quarrels.\n\nEconomic motives were important. Industrial states needed raw materials such as rubber, cotton, palm oil and copper, and wanted protected markets when tariffs rose in Europe from the 1870s. J.A. Hobson argued in 1902 that capitalists invested overseas because of surplus capital at home, and Lenin built on this in 1916, though historians point out that most trade and investment went to areas outside the empires. Strategic concerns mattered as well: Britain occupied Egypt in 1882 to protect the Suez Canal and the route to India, and powers wanted naval bases and coaling stations.\n\nNationalism and ideas drove expansion too. Colonies were a sign of Great Power status, and newspapers and colonial societies cheered every acquisition. After defeat in 1871 France under Jules Ferry looked for compensation overseas, and once one power claimed land the others felt they must follow or be shut out. Missionaries and explorers such as Livingstone and Stanley made Africa known and campaigned against the slave trade, while Social Darwinism and the belief in a duty to civilise other peoples, expressed in France's mission civilisatrice and Kipling's White Man's Burden of 1899, were used to justify conquest. Technology made it easy: quinine against malaria, steamships, the telegraph and weapons like the Maxim gun gave Europeans overwhelming advantage.\n\nThe first effect on Europe was the Scramble for Africa. In 1880 about a tenth of the continent was under European control; by 1914 about nine tenths was, and only Ethiopia, which defeated Italy at Adwa in 1896, and Liberia stayed independent. The Berlin Conference of 1884-85, called by Bismarck, set rules of notice and effective occupation and recognised Leopold II's Congo Free State, and it made the partition quicker among the powers. Colonial claims became a source of friction among them and Germany acquired South West Africa, Togo, the Cameroons and East Africa, which brought quarrels with Britain.\n\nColonial rivalry produced crises that increased tension between the powers. At Fashoda in 1898 Marchand's French expedition met Kitchener on the Upper Nile, and war was feared until France backed down. The Boer War of 1899-1902 damaged Britain's reputation and aroused hostility across Europe, which pushed Britain to end its splendid isolation. In Morocco, Wilhelm II's landing at Tangier in 1905 and the Agadir crisis of 1911, when the gunboat Panther was sent, were attempts to test France and the Entente, and both ended badly for Germany, deepening German resentment and drawing Britain and France closer, with secret military talks from 1906.\n\nImperialism also helped to rearrange alliances. The Entente Cordiale of 1904 settled colonial disputes, recognising British control of Egypt and French control of Morocco, and the Anglo-Russian Entente of 1907 divided Persia and settled Afghanistan and Tibet. These agreements made the Triple Entente, which faced the Triple Alliance, and each bloc became more suspicious of the other, with Germany complaining of encirclement. In this way colonial rivalries hardened Europe into two camps, even though many colonial quarrels were solved by compromise.\n\nThe New Imperialism also had effects on European society and conscience. The Congo Free State, where Leopold II's rubber quotas were enforced by the Force Publique with hostage-taking and mutilation, caused a reform campaign led by E.D. Morel and Roger Casement, whose report appeared in 1904, and the Belgian parliament took over the colony in 1908. Conrad's Heart of Darkness shaped opinion. Rivalry in Asia, such as the concessions in China after 1895, and Japan's defeat of Russia in 1904-05, turned Russia back towards the Balkans, and the imperial race fed naval and military competition.\n\nThe New Imperialism arose from economic need, strategic interest, national pride and a belief in European superiority, backed by superior technology. Its effect on Europe was to create rivalries, make alliances rigid and raise suspicion, particularly between Britain and Germany, but it was also settled peaceably in many cases, so it was one cause of the tension before 1914 rather than the direct cause of the war.",
