    "model": "Anti-Semitism had a deep impact in both France and Russia between 1871 and 1914, but in different ways. In France it split the nation during the Dreyfus Affair and weakened the army and the Church, although the Republic survived. In Russia it was backed by the state, led to pogroms and discriminatory laws and forced millions of Jews to emigrate.\n\nIn France, anti-Semitism grew after the humiliation of 1871 as nationalists looked for scapegoats and blamed Jews and Freemasons for the country's weakness. Edouard Drumont's La France juive of 1886 was a bestseller portraying Jews as a threat to France, and in 1892 he founded the newspaper La Libre Parole. The word anti-Semitism itself had been coined by Wilhelm Marr in Germany in 1879, which shows that racial rather than merely religious prejudice was spreading across Europe. Prejudice was thus a part of French political life before the Dreyfus Affair began.\n\nThe Dreyfus Affair brought this prejudice into the open. In October 1894 Captain Alfred Dreyfus, a Jewish artillery officer from Alsace, was arrested on suspicion of passing secrets to Germany and was convicted by a secret court martial in December 1894 and sent to Devil's Island. In 1896 Colonel Picquart found that the real author of the leaked memorandum was Major Esterhazy, but the army suppressed this and Esterhazy was acquitted in January 1898, and a forgery by Colonel Henry was used to support the case until it was exposed. Dreyfus was convicted largely because he was a Jew and an easy target, and the army put its own reputation ahead of justice.\n\nThe affair divided France. On 13 January 1898 Emile Zola published J'accuse in L'Aurore, accusing the army of a cover-up, and he was convicted of libel and fled to England. Dreyfusards, including republicans, socialists such as Jaures, and many intellectuals, faced anti-Dreyfusards, who included the army, much of the Catholic Church, nationalists and monarchists. A second court martial at Rennes in 1899 found Dreyfus guilty again with extenuating circumstances, and President Loubet pardoned him. Not until 1906 did the court of appeals clear him and reinstate him in the army.\n\nThe longer-term impact in France was political. The army's reputation was damaged, and the Church, which had backed the anti-Dreyfusard side, lost standing, which led to the anticlerical measures of the early 1900s and the separation of church and state in 1905. The republican side won, but the prejudice remained strong. The affair also had an influence beyond France: Theodor Herzl covered the trial as a journalist, published Der Judenstaat in 1896, and the First Zionist Congress met at Basel in 1897.\n\nIn Russia the impact was more violent because the state itself supported discrimination. About five million Jews, the largest Jewish community in the world, lived in the Russian Empire, and most were confined to the Pale of Settlement, which was set up in 1791. After Alexander II was assassinated in March 1881, rumours blamed Jews and pogroms spread through southern Russia, beginning at Elizavetgrad in April 1881, and the police and troops were slow to intervene. The May Laws of 1882 banned Jews from settling in rural areas of the Pale and from owning land, quotas limited their places in schools and universities from 1887, and Jews were expelled from Moscow in 1891-92. Officials such as Pobedonostsev supported discrimination and Russification.\n\nThe persecution continued into the new century. The Kishinev pogrom of 6-7 April 1903 killed 49 Jews and injured hundreds, and caused international outrage, and further pogroms followed in 1905-06 during the revolution. The Protocols of the Elders of Zion, a forgery claiming a Jewish plot to dominate the world, was first published in Russia in 1903 and later spread widely. The Orthodox Church and nationalist groups such as the Black Hundreds spread anti-Semitic propaganda. The results were mass emigration, with about two million Jews leaving the Russian Empire between 1881 and 1914, most to the USA, and the growth of Jewish movements such as the Bund of 1897 and Zionism.\n\nOverall, the impact in France was a political and moral crisis that ended in justice for one man and a republican victory, while in Russia it was a policy of persecution that destroyed the security of millions and drove them abroad. In both countries anti-Semitism proved deeply rooted, and it was Russia, where the state encouraged it, that suffered the worse consequences for the Jewish population.",
