    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "My study of Douglas Haig, Wilfred Owen and women in the workforce taught me that the First World War was a war of attrition fought at terrible human cost, that it was experienced and remembered very differently by those who took part, and that it changed the lives of civilians as well as soldiers.\n\nDouglas Haig showed me the nature of the fighting on the Western Front. Born in Edinburgh in 1861, he served in the Sudan and the Boer War, commanded a corps in 1914 and succeeded Sir John French as commander-in-chief of the British Expeditionary Force in December 1915. He faced a front where machine guns, barbed wire and heavy artillery gave the defender the advantage, and he still believed that a breakthrough could be won by offensive. The result was the Somme in 1916, a joint British-French offensive planned partly to relieve the French at Verdun. On the first day, 1 July, the British Army lost about 57,000 casualties, of whom around 19,000 were killed, the worst day in its history. The battle advanced the line only a few kilometres and cost over a million casualties across all armies. This taught me that the war was one of attrition, in which success was measured by wearing down the enemy rather than by territory.\n\nHaig's later career taught me how difficult it is to judge a commander. Passchendaele in 1917, fought in rain and mud around Ypres, gained only about eight kilometres, and Lloyd George distrusted him and thought his battles wasteful. After the war Lloyd George's memoirs and later Alan Clark's The Donkeys helped to fix the image of brave men led by incompetent generals. Yet Haig issued his 'backs to the wall' order in April 1918 and led the Hundred Days advance of 1918, in which British forces played the leading part. Historians such as John Terraine and Gary Sheffield argue that he learned from experience and won by combining tanks, aircraft and artillery, while Denis Winter and Gerard De Groot portray him as callous and unimaginative. I learned that his strategy was shaped by the strength of the defences and the need to support France, so that he can be seen both as a symbol of waste and as the architect of victory.\n\nWilfred Owen taught me what the war felt like to the soldier. He enlisted in the Artists Rifles in October 1915, fought on the Somme and near Beaumont-Hamel, and was sent to Craiglockhart War Hospital in 1917 with shell shock. There he met Siegfried Sassoon, who advised him to write plainly, and he wrote Dulce et Decorum Est, Anthem for Doomed Youth and Strange Meeting. His poems described gas, mud and the death of young men, and he attacked 'the old Lie' that it is sweet and fitting to die for one's country. This contrasted sharply with the patriotic idealism of Rupert Brooke in 1914 and showed me how enthusiasm gave way to disillusionment. He won the Military Cross in October 1918 and was killed on 4 November at the Sambre-Oise canal, a week before the armistice, aged 25. Only a few poems appeared in his lifetime, and his reputation grew after Blunden's edition of 1931 as pacifist feeling spread, though critics note that the 'poets' war' view came from an educated officer class and that most soldiers did not write such poetry.\n\nWomen in the workforce showed me that the war transformed the home front. Before 1914 most working women were young, many were in domestic service, and they were paid half or less of what men earned. The shell shortage of 1915 led the new Ministry of Munitions to turn to women, and hundreds of thousands became 'munitionettes' filling shells at places such as Woolwich Arsenal. Their work was dangerous: TNT poisoning turned their skin yellow, so that they were called 'canary girls', and the explosion at Chilwell in July 1918 killed over 130. Women also worked as clerks and farm labourers in the Women's Land Army formed in 1917, and as nurses, as Vera Brittain did in the VADs, while Edith Cavell was executed in Brussels in 1915 for helping Allied soldiers escape.\n\nThe long-term effects were mixed, which taught me the limits of the war's impact. The Representation of the People Act of 1918 gave the vote to women over 30 who met a property qualification, and Asquith said in 1917 that women had earned it by their war work, though Gail Braybon argues that pre-war campaigning mattered as much. Yet pay stayed below men's, the Restoration of Pre-War Practices Act of 1919 reinstated the old trade rules and forced many women out of their jobs, and women did not get the vote on equal terms until 1928.\n\nOverall, I learned that the First World War was a costly war of attrition on the Western Front, that it was remembered through the voices of men such as Owen as much as through generals such as Haig, and that it opened new roles for women while leaving many old attitudes in place.",
