    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Between 1920 and 1945 Britain faced a series of serious social and economic challenges: the decline of its old staple industries, mass unemployment and regional poverty, financial crises and industrial conflict, and then the strains of total war. Britain remained a democracy throughout, but it had to deal with the problem of two Britains in the inter-war years and with the demands of mobilising and sustaining the whole society from 1939.\n\nThe first challenge was the post-war slump. A brief boom in 1919 and 1920 gave way to collapse, and unemployment rose above two million by mid-1921 as export markets were lost. The Geddes Axe of 1922 cut public spending on education, housing and the armed services. The coal industry was returned to private ownership in 1921, and when the owners tried to cut wages the miners were left to be defeated on Black Friday, 15 April 1921, when the railwaymen and transport workers of the Triple Alliance refused to strike in support.\n\nThe decision to return to the gold standard in April 1925 made matters worse. Churchill, as Chancellor, fixed sterling at the pre-war parity of about 4.86 dollars, and Keynes warned in The Economic Consequences of Mr Churchill that an overvalued pound would make exports dearer and force wages down. The coal owners announced wage cuts and longer hours, and the TUC called the General Strike of 4 to 12 May 1926 in support of the miners. It was the only nationwide strike in British history, and it was called off after nine days, while the miners stayed out for months and went back on worse terms. The Trade Disputes Act of 1927 then banned sympathetic strikes, so the labour movement was weakened.\n\nThe deepest long-term problem was the decline of the staple industries of coal, cotton, shipbuilding, and iron and steel. Lancashire cotton lost markets to Japan and India, and shipbuilding on the Tyne, Wear and Clyde collapsed when world trade fell. Unemployment was concentrated in South Wales, the north of England and central Scotland, where it could stay above 30 per cent in many towns and was higher still in places such as Jarrow and Merthyr Tydfil, while the south-east and Midlands prospered with new industries such as motor vehicles and electrical goods. The result was a divided country, with real wages rising for those in work but long unemployment bringing malnutrition and overcrowding in the depressed areas, as Priestley, Orwell and the Pilgrim Trust survey showed.\n\nThe Wall Street Crash and the Depression brought a financial and political crisis. Ramsay MacDonald's second Labour government of 1929 to 1931 struggled with rising unemployment, and the May Committee in 1931 forecast a large deficit and recommended cuts in benefit. The Cabinet split over the cuts, and MacDonald formed a National Government in August 1931, for which Labour expelled him. Britain left the gold standard on 21 September 1931, a few days after the Invergordon mutiny against pay cuts, and the National Government then introduced tariffs under the Import Duties Act of 1932, imperial preference at Ottawa and cheap money, which helped recovery in the south-east. Registered unemployment still peaked at about three million in 1932 and 1933 and never fell below a million before 1940.\n\nThe unemployed faced the means test, introduced in November 1931, which looked at the income of the whole household, and many families lost assistance when a son or daughter earned money. The National Unemployed Workers' Movement organised hunger marches, and the Unemployment Act of 1934 and the cuts to scales in 1935 caused protests that forced the government to suspend them. Government help for the depressed regions was modest. The Special Areas Act of 1934 offered limited grants, and the regions did not recover fully until rearmament from 1936 created new demand. At the same time, about four million houses were built between the wars, so housing improved for many.\n\nThe Second World War created a new set of challenges. Britain had to finance and organise total war, and Keynes proposed compulsory saving in How to Pay for the War in 1940 to prevent inflation. Civilians faced rationing, evacuation of children from the cities and bombing during the Blitz, while women and men were mobilised into war work. The war also brought full employment, but it left Britain with heavy debt, and Keynes had to negotiate an American loan in 1945. It also raised demands for social reform, shown in the Beveridge Report of 1942.\n\nIn conclusion, the main challenges were mass unemployment concentrated in the old industrial regions, industrial conflict and the strains of currency and budget crises, and then the burdens of a total war. Britain handled them without losing its democratic system, and prosperity grew in the south, but the long failure to reduce unemployment, and the hardship of the means test, left a legacy that shaped the demand for a welfare state after 1945.",
