    "label": "1",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The main crises of the Cold War affecting Europe fell into three groups: the Berlin crises, which put the continent's most exposed city at the centre of superpower rivalry; the crises within the Soviet bloc, such as Hungary in 1956 and Czechoslovakia in 1968; and the nuclear confrontations, from Cuba in 1962 to the Euromissile dispute of the early 1980s. Each brought Europe close to conflict, but none produced direct war between the superpowers.\n\nThe first great crisis was the Berlin Blockade of 1948-49. After the Western powers introduced a new currency in their zones of Germany, Stalin closed road and rail access to West Berlin in June 1948, hoping to starve the city into submission or force the West to abandon it. The Americans and British answered with the Berlin Airlift, flying in food and fuel for nearly a year, and Stalin lifted the blockade in May 1949. The crisis confirmed the division of Germany into two states, the Federal Republic and the German Democratic Republic, and it pushed the Western powers into NATO in April 1949.\n\nBerlin became a flashpoint again under Khrushchev. In November 1958 he issued an ultimatum giving the Western powers six months to agree to end their occupation of West Berlin. Talks, including Khrushchev's visit to the USA in 1959, achieved little, and the Paris summit of May 1960 collapsed after the U-2 spy plane piloted by Gary Powers was shot down over the Soviet Union. At the Vienna summit in June 1961 Khrushchev pressed the new president, John F. Kennedy, again. East Germany had lost around 2.7 million people to the West since 1945, so on 13 August 1961 it began building the Berlin Wall. The Western powers did not use force against it, accepting the Wall as the price of avoiding war, and it became the most enduring symbol of Europe's division.\n\nCrises also arose inside the Soviet bloc, because Moscow would not tolerate a loss of control. In Hungary in October 1956 the uprising led by Imre Nagy demanded democratic reform and withdrawal from the Warsaw Pact. Soviet tanks entered Budapest on 4 November, thousands were killed and many thousands fled, and the West, preoccupied with the Suez crisis, offered only words. In 1968 Alexander Dubcek's Prague Spring, an attempt at socialism with a human face, was ended when Warsaw Pact forces invaded Czechoslovakia on 20-21 August. Brezhnev then set out the doctrine that Moscow could intervene wherever socialism was threatened. Both events showed that the Cold War in Europe was a contest of spheres of influence and that neither side would cross the line into the other's sphere.\n\nThe most dangerous moment of all was the Cuban Missile Crisis of October 1962. When American reconnaissance photographs showed Soviet missiles being installed in Cuba, Kennedy announced a naval quarantine on 22 October. For about a week the superpowers were on the brink of nuclear war. On 28 October Khrushchev agreed to withdraw the missiles in return for an American pledge not to invade Cuba, and the Americans secretly agreed to remove their Jupiter missiles from Turkey. Although it was fought outside Europe, the crisis mattered greatly to Europeans, who saw that decisions on nuclear war would be taken in Washington and Moscow. Its lasting effect was caution: a hotline was set up in 1963, and the Partial Test Ban Treaty followed in August of that year.\n\nAfter a period of detente, marked by Brandt's Ostpolitik, SALT I in 1972 and the Helsinki Final Act of 1975, tension returned in the late 1970s. The Soviet Union deployed SS-20 intermediate-range missiles aimed at Western Europe, and in December 1979 NATO decided to deploy Pershing II and cruise missiles unless talks succeeded. The Soviet invasion of Afghanistan on 24 December 1979 and Reagan's hard line after 1980, including his description of the Soviet Union as an evil empire in 1983, deepened the second Cold War. Large peace demonstrations took place in West Germany, Britain and the Netherlands, and the NATO exercise Able Archer 83 was misread by Soviet leaders as a possible first strike. The crisis eased only under Gorbachev, with the Intermediate-Range Nuclear Forces Treaty of December 1987.\n\nOverall, the Berlin crises and the Cuban Missile Crisis were the most serious, because they brought the superpowers closest to war, while Hungary and Czechoslovakia showed the limits of each bloc's tolerance. Together these crises shaped Europe for forty years: they divided Germany, created permanent alliances and, by exposing the danger of nuclear war, drove the arms control agreements that eventually helped bring the Cold War to an end.",
