    "label": "2",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Europe's retreat from empire in Africa between 1945 and 1990 brought independence, but its social and economic consequences were mixed and often damaging. Colonial economic structures survived, new states were burdened by debt and famine, and ethnic divisions and war cost millions of lives, although some governments achieved real gains in education and health.\n\nEconomically, African states inherited economies built to export raw materials to Europe. Cocoa in the Gold Coast, groundnuts in Senegal, cotton in Uganda and copper in Katanga dominated, and railways ran from mines and farms to ports rather than linking African states to each other. Multinational firms such as Unilever's United Africa Company, Shell-BP and the Union Miniere kept control of trade, oil and mining after independence. Dependence on one or two commodities left governments exposed to price falls, as with copper in the 1970s and cocoa and coffee in the 1980s, and economists such as Prebisch and Singer argued that the terms of trade moved against primary producers.\n\nEurope tried to manage this relationship through trade agreements. The Yaounde Convention of 1963 gave associated African states duty-free access to European markets, and Lomé I, signed in February 1975 between the EEC and 46 African, Caribbean and Pacific states, gave largely duty-free access without requiring reciprocal concessions. It also set up STABEX to compensate states when export earnings fell. These were real innovations, yet exports stayed concentrated on a few primary commodities, and commodity prices fell sharply in the 1980s, so Africa remained dependent on Europe.\n\nDebt was a further economic consequence. The oil price rises of 1973 and 1979 raised import costs, and many governments borrowed heavily. When interest rates rose and commodity prices fell in the early 1980s, they struggled to repay. The IMF and World Bank made new loans conditional on structural adjustment, meaning devaluation, cuts in public spending, removal of subsidies and sale of state enterprises. Critics, including UNICEF in its 1987 report, argued that this cut health and education spending and hurt the poor, although Ghana under Rawlings was cited as an early success. Aid also created dependence and sometimes supported corrupt rulers such as Mobutu in Zaire.\n\nSocially, the most terrible consequences were war and famine. In Nigeria, regional parties drew support along ethnic lines, coups followed in 1966, and the eastern region declared the Republic of Biafra on 30 May 1967. In the war that lasted until January 1970 a federal blockade caused famine, and estimates of the dead range from several hundred thousand to over two million, mostly from starvation and disease. In the Sahel drought of the early 1970s and the Ethiopian famine of 1983 to 1985, which killed hundreds of thousands, drought combined with state policy and civil war. The BBC report of Michael Buerk in October 1984 led to Band Aid and Live Aid, which raised about 150 million pounds, and NGOs such as Concern, founded in Dublin in 1968 after Biafra, and Trócaire in 1973 grew out of these crises.\n\nPolitical collapse produced further social disruption. The Congo, which had been given no preparation for independence, fell into crisis in 1960 with the secession of Katanga and the Lumumba affair, and then endured Mobutu's rule. Angola's war of independence from Portugal ended in civil war because the three movements had different ethnic bases and foreign backers. Colonial rulers had fixed ethnic identities through indirect rule and census categories, so tribalism undermined national unity in many states.\n\nThere were social achievements. Tanzania under Nyerere stands out. The Arusha Declaration of 1967 committed the country to ujamaa and self-reliance, and universal primary education launched in 1974 raised enrolment rapidly, with adult literacy rising to a high level by the early 1980s. Yet villagisation from 1973 to 1974 moved millions of people, often by force, farm output collapsed and food had to be imported. Smallpox was also eradicated by 1977, showing what international co-operation could achieve.\n\nOverall, the consequences for Africa were largely negative in the short and medium term. The economic legacy of colonial rule, debt, famine and ethnic conflict outweighed the gains in schooling and health, and Europe's retreat left states politically independent but economically dependent, with deep social damage that lasted well beyond 1990.",
