    "label": "C4",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "After 1945 the mission opened new parishes, schools, hospitals and training colleges across south-eastern Nigeria. Mission schools became the main route to education in the region, and teacher training colleges created a growing educated class. Local vocations grew, and Michael Tansi, an Igbo, was ordained in 1937. In 1965 Francis Arinze, aged 32, became auxiliary bishop of Onitsha. The mission's schools and churches outlasted colonial rule and produced many of the educated Nigerians who held office after independence. Its success was limited, because leadership stayed with Irish missionaries long after local people were qualified, and the Biafran war damaged many stations.",
