    "label": "B1",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "The welfare reforms of the 1940s were extended to Northern Ireland on a basis of parity with Britain, so National Insurance, unemployment benefit and pensions were run on a UK-wide basis. The Social Services Agreement of 1949 formalised parity, and the British Treasury met the cost of services that Northern Ireland could not fund from its own revenue. The Education Act (Northern Ireland) 1947 provided free secondary education, although the eleven-plus decided who went to a grammar school, and Catholic students gained access to a wider range of qualifications. The National Health Service began in Northern Ireland on 5 July 1948, but the reforms did not remove discrimination in the allocation of council houses and jobs.",
