    "label": "B2",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "Ireland applied for UN membership in 1946, but a Soviet veto blocked it, citing Ireland's neutrality in the Second World War, until Ireland was admitted on 14 December 1955. Irish resolutions from 1958 led to Resolution 1665 in 1961, which called on all states not to transfer or acquire nuclear weapons. Ireland was the first state to sign the 1968 Treaty on the Non-Proliferation of Nuclear Weapons. Irish troops joined the UN force in the Congo in 1960, and about 6,000 served there until 1964, when twenty-six had died.",
