    "label": "B3",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "John Charles McQuaid was born in 1895 in Cootehill, Co. Cavan, and was Archbishop of Dublin from 1940 to 1972. He advised the government on the Mother and Child Scheme in 1951, arguing that it conflicted with Catholic teaching on the family, and he objected to the state, not parents and the church, deciding how mothers and children were cared for. He had direct access to ministers and advised de Valera on the 1937 Constitution and its article on religion. He attended Vatican II but implemented its reforms slowly in Dublin and was wary of ecumenism and lay initiatives.",
