    "label": "C2",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Telefís Éireann began broadcasting on 31 December 1961, and by the early 1970s most households had a television. The Late Late Show, first broadcast in July 1962, became a forum for national debate about sex, marriage, unmarried mothers and the church. Gay Byrne presented the show until 1999 and generally avoided taking sides, but his willingness to air taboo subjects had a liberalising effect. Seven Days, launched in 1966, investigated housing, poverty and emigration, and its 1969 moneylending programme led to a government inquiry. On 1 October 1971 the minister Gerry Collins issued the first Section 31 order, and in November 1972 the whole RTÉ Authority was dismissed. Overall RTÉ entertained its viewers, challenged authority and speeded up change in attitudes to religion and sexuality.",
