    "label": "C3",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Membership from 1973 gave farmers guaranteed prices under the Common Agricultural Policy, and farm incomes rose sharply in the early years, especially for beef and dairy farmers. The European Regional Development Fund was created in 1975, and Ireland received a considerable share for roads, water and industrial estates. American firms set up factories to sell into the EEC, drawn by IDA grants and a young English-speaking workforce. Protected clothing, textile and footwear firms lost their tariff protection, and many closed with heavy job losses. EEC directives on equal pay in 1975 and equal treatment in 1976 forced changes in Irish law on the status of women. On balance membership transformed the economy and society, though its benefits were uneven and the full gains came from the 1990s.",
