    "label": "B2",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "Serfs were peasants bound to the land of their noble owners, who controlled their labour, marriage and movement. Their main obligations were barshchina, labour dues of several days a week on the lord's land, and obrok, a payment in money or goods. By the late 1850s privately owned serfs made up more than a third of Russia's population. Alexander II issued the emancipation edict of 19 February 1861, which freed serfs from personal bondage, but landlords kept much of the best land and peasants paid redemption payments for 49 years.",
