    "label": "C2",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "The Frankfurt Assembly had no army, money or administration, so it depended on the very governments it was trying to limit. The rulers kept control of their armies and bureaucracies, which stayed loyal and were used to retake Prague, Vienna and Berlin. Liberals feared a social revolution and a republic more than they feared the princes, so they divided from the radicals and workers. Peasants lost interest once governments abolished most feudal dues in 1848, as Austria did in September 1848. Months of debate on the Basic Rights and the Grossdeutsch and Kleindeutsch question gave Austria and Prussia time to recover. Frederick William IV refused the imperial crown in April 1849, which left the constitution without the one state strong enough to enforce it.",
