    "label": "C3",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Bismarck, Prussian Minister-President from 1862, believed that national questions would be decided by blood and iron rather than by majority votes. He isolated Austria by winning Russian goodwill after the Alvensleben Convention of 1863 and Napoleon III's neutrality after their meeting at Biarritz in 1865. He joined Austria to invade Denmark in 1864 and win Schleswig and Holstein, and the Gastein arrangement then set up a dispute with Austria. Prussia's victory at Königgrätz in 1866 led to the Treaty of Prague, which dissolved the German Confederation and excluded Austria from German affairs. He formed the North German Confederation in 1867 under Prussian leadership, and in 1870 he used the Ems telegram to provoke France into war, which drew in the southern states. The German Empire was proclaimed at Versailles on 18 January 1871, and the Chancellor answered to the emperor, not to the Reichstag.",
