    "label": "B2",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "Wilhelm II wanted a battle fleet to match Britain's navy and give Germany the status of a world power. Tirpitz's First Navy Law of 1898 authorised 19 battleships, and the Second Navy Law of 1900 raised the plan to 38. Tirpitz's risk theory argued that a strong German fleet would deter Britain from war, and the German Navy League grew to over a million members. Britain responded with HMS Dreadnought, launched in 1906, which made existing battleships obsolete and started a costly naval race. Naval talks failed because Germany would not limit shipbuilding and Britain would not promise neutrality, and the German fleet remained largely in harbour during the war.",
