    "label": "B3",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "Anti-Jewish prejudice grew in France after 1870, as nationalists blamed Jews and Freemasons for the country's weakness, and Édouard Drumont's La France juive of 1886 was a bestseller. In 1894 Captain Alfred Dreyfus, a Jewish artillery officer, was convicted by a secret court martial and sent to Devil's Island. The army covered up evidence that Major Esterhazy was the real author of the leaked memorandum, and Émile Zola's open letter J'accuse of 1898 denounced the cover-up. France divided into Dreyfusards, such as republicans, socialists and intellectuals, and anti-Dreyfusards, including the army, much of the Catholic Church and nationalists. The Affair weakened the army and the Church and led to anticlerical laws in the early 1900s.",
    "model_source": "haiku",
