    "model": "The Soviet Union's Obninsk reactor, which began supplying a public grid in June 1954, was the first nuclear station connected to one. Calder Hall in Cumbria, opened in October 1956, was the world's first nuclear power station to supply electricity on a commercial scale. Eisenhower's Atoms for Peace address to the UN in December 1953 promoted civil nuclear power and led to the International Atomic Energy Agency in 1957. The Treaty of Rome of 1957 created Euratom to coordinate civil nuclear energy in the six EEC states. After the Messmer Plan of 1974 France built many reactors, and by the 1980s nuclear power provided most of its electricity.",
