    "label": "C3",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Her government pursued monetarism and cut public spending, and inflation was lower by the mid-1980s. The Employment Acts of 1980 and 1982 and the Trade Union Act of 1984 reduced the power of the trade unions, and the miners' defeat in 1985 weakened the union movement. The Falklands victory of June 1982 helped her win the general election of 1983, and she also won the elections of 1979 and 1987. She sold state assets including British Telecom in 1984 and gave council tenants the right to buy their homes in 1980. Unemployment reached about three million in 1982, and deindustrialisation hit the north, Scotland and Wales hardest, which critics saw as a heavy and lasting cost. She became the longest-serving British prime minister of the twentieth century, but she resigned on 22 November 1990 after a challenge within her party.",
