    "label": "C4",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Jacques Delors was a French socialist who became finance minister under Mitterrand from 1981 to 1984 and president of the European Commission in January 1985. He drove the Single European Act of 1986, which set a deadline of 1992 for completing a single market without internal border controls. The Commission's White Paper of 1985 listed nearly 300 measures to remove barriers to the single market. He chaired the committee whose 1989 report set out a three-stage route to economic and monetary union, which became the basis of the Maastricht Treaty. He served as Commission president until 1995, the longest tenure in the Commission's history at that time. Historians credit him with relaunching European integration in the 1980s, although Margaret Thatcher opposed his federalist ideas.",
