    "label": "B2",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "The Balfour Declaration of 1917 promised a national home for the Jewish people in Palestine, but the Mandate's promises to Arabs were never reconciled with it, which created conflicting claims. Jewish immigration rose sharply in the 1930s, and the Jewish share of Palestine's population grew from about 11 per cent in 1922 to roughly 30 per cent by 1939, which provoked the Arab Revolt of 1936 to 1939. The UN General Assembly passed Resolution 181 on 29 November 1947 to partition Palestine, but the Arab states rejected it and fighting began. When Israel was proclaimed on 14 May 1948, Arab armies entered former Mandate territory, and about 700,000 Palestinian Arabs fled or were expelled, creating a refugee problem that remains unresolved.",
