    "model": "Charles Stewart Parnell made a decisive contribution to both land reform and the Home Rule movement. He gave the Land War a national leader and helped to win the Three Fs in 1881, and he turned the Irish Party into a disciplined machine that held the balance of power at Westminster and brought Gladstone to introduce the First Home Rule Bill in 1886. His contribution had limits, notably his distance from the Plan of Campaign and his fall in 1890, but his place as the leading figure in both causes is secure.\n\nParnell's contribution to land reform began in the agricultural depression that followed 1877, when bad weather and cheap American grain brought falling prices, and evictions rose from about 460 families in 1877 to more than 2,000 in 1880. At Westport in June 1879 he told tenants to keep a firm grip of their homesteads, his first major public identification with the agitation. When the Irish National Land League was founded in Dublin in October 1879 he became its president, with Michael Davitt as the leading organiser. His prestige gave a local movement of the west a national leadership, and he secured American money by touring the United States with John Dillon in early 1880.\n\nHe also shaped the methods of the Land War. At Ennis in September 1880 he urged that anyone who took a farm from which a tenant had been evicted should be shunned by the whole community, and the first victim was Captain Boycott in Mayo. Parnell obstructed Forster's coercion bill in the Commons in early 1881, a sitting of about 41 hours that ended only when the Speaker intervened. The result was the Land Act of August 1881, which granted fair rents set by land courts, fixity of tenure and free sale. Parnell was arrested in October 1881 after he tested the Act by sending selected cases to the courts, and the No Rent Manifesto, issued from Kilmainham, failed. In the Kilmainham settlement of April and May 1882 he secured the Arrears Act, which cleared arrears for poorer tenants.\n\nHis role in later land reform was more limited. He did not endorse the Plan of Campaign of 1886, because he feared that evictions and violence would alarm the Liberals whose alliance mattered most to him. Nor did he follow Davitt's belief in land nationalisation, favouring instead peasant proprietorship. This approach showed that for Parnell land was above all the engine of the national movement, and he used the agitation to give the party a mass base among tenant farmers.\n\nOn Home Rule, Parnell's first contribution was to turn it from a small parliamentary group into a force that Westminster could not ignore. He joined Joseph Biggar's obstruction from 1877, and in August 1877 he replaced Butt as president of the Home Rule Confederation of Great Britain. After the 1880 election, when about 60 Home Rulers were returned, he was elected chairman of the Irish Parliamentary Party, defeating William Shaw by 23 votes to 18. Isaac Butt's followers had been undisciplined Liberals, with no whip or pledge, and Parnell ended that.\n\nParnell built the machinery that made the party effective. The Irish National League, founded in October 1882, replaced the Land League and put Home Rule first, with branches in nearly every parish. In 1884 candidates were required to sign a pledge to sit, act and vote with the party, and conventions chose them. The newspaper United Ireland, from 1881, gave the party a voice. The result was seen in 1885, when his party held the balance of power in the Commons, and in 1886 Gladstone introduced the First Home Rule Bill. It was defeated in the Commons in June 1886 when about ninety Liberals voted against it, but Home Rule had become the central question of British politics.\n\nParnell's reputation reached its height in 1889, when the forgeries of Richard Pigott were exposed before the Special Commission, and in 1890 he won £5,000 in damages from The Times. Then the O'Shea divorce case of November 1890 led Gladstone and the Catholic bishops to condemn him, and the party split in Committee Room 15 in December. He died in October 1891, aged 45. Overall, Parnell's achievement was to link land reform and Home Rule in one movement and to give it discipline and a mass following, even though he did not live to see either completed. Land purchase was finished by Wyndham's Act of 1903, and Home Rule was passed only in 1914, but both were reached by the path he had marked out.",
