    "label": "2",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Between 1870 and 1914 Belfast and Dublin developed in sharply different ways. Belfast became an industrial giant, built on linen, shipbuilding and engineering, while Dublin remained a city of administration, trade, brewing and distilling, and its social problems, above all its slums, grew worse than the economy could solve. Both cities saw growth in population, rising labour organisation and deep social division.\n\nBelfast's economic transformation was the most dramatic. Its population rose from a town of about 20,000 around 1800 to about 255,000 in 1891, when it passed Dublin, and 387,000 by 1911. Linen was the first great industry. The mills made Belfast known as Linenopolis, and they employed tens of thousands, most of them women and girls. The American Civil War cut cotton supplies in the 1860s and boosted linen, and power looms replaced handloom weaving. The Belfast Ropeworks, founded in 1873, was among the largest in the world, and tobacco firms such as Gallaher and engineering works such as Sirocco added to the city's wealth. Belfast's economy depended on exporting to British and imperial markets.\n\nShipbuilding on Queen's Island made the city world famous. Edward Harland bought a small yard in 1858 and went into partnership with Gustav Wolff in 1861. William Pirrie became a partner in 1874 and chairman after Harland's death in 1895, and he was made Lord Pirrie in 1906. Harland and Wolff built the White Star liners Olympic and Titanic, the biggest ships in the world when launched. Titanic was launched in 1911 and lost on her maiden voyage in April 1912, and the yard employed about 15,000 men by 1912. Workman Clark, founded in 1880, was a second major yard, and the Arrol Gantry of 1908-09 allowed Olympic and Titanic to be built side by side. This industry brought skilled men wages that were higher than in Dublin and a sense of prosperity tied to the Union.\n\nThe social cost of Belfast's growth was considerable, and religion shaped who benefited. Mill workers laboured long hours in hot, damp rooms full of flax dust, which caused lung disease, for low wages. Shipyard work was dangerous and labourers were laid off between contracts. The skilled jobs were mostly held by Protestants, often through family and Orange Order connections, while Catholics, who made up about a quarter of the city by 1911 and lived around the Falls Road, were mainly labourers. Sectarian riots broke out in 1857, 1864 and 1886, and in July 1912 Protestant workers drove Catholic workers out of the yards. Larkin's dock strike of 1907 united Catholic and Protestant workers briefly, but the unity did not last. Belfast's prosperity therefore deepened its divisions and strengthened its unionism, since Protestant workers feared that Home Rule would bring tariffs and Catholic dominance.\n\nDublin's story was one of poverty rather than industrial boom. Apart from large employers such as Guinness at St James's Gate and Jacob's, the city had few factories and a large pool of unskilled labour. Most men were casual labourers at the docks, in carting and in building, hired by the day with no security. Women worked in laundries, mills, biscuit factories and domestic service. The housing was among the worst in the United Kingdom. The 1911 census showed more than 20,000 families living in one room each in tenements, which were old Georgian houses divided into single rooms, often with no running water and a shared yard toilet. Dublin's death rate was among the highest of any British city, with tuberculosis and infant mortality especially severe. On 2 September 1913 two tenement houses collapsed in Church Street, killing seven people.\n\nThis poverty produced Dublin's most important social development, the growth of unionisation among the unskilled. Craft unions had ignored unskilled workers, but James Larkin founded the Irish Transport and General Workers' Union in 1909, with its headquarters at Liberty Hall, and it won disputes and recruited thousands. James Connolly helped found the Irish Labour Party in 1912. William Martin Murphy and the employers resisted, and in August 1913 the tram strike grew into the Dublin Lockout, in which about 20,000 workers were out of work. Police baton-charged crowds on Bloody Sunday, 31 August, and employers demanded that workers sign a document renouncing the union. Help came from British unions, and the Irish Citizen Army was founded in November 1913, but by January 1914 hunger forced workers back. The union lost the dispute but survived, and it made labour a political force and drew public attention to the slums.\n\nOverall, Belfast saw the more impressive economic development, as an industrial city of international importance, but it was built on cheap female labour and sectarian division. Dublin's economy stagnated by comparison, yet the city produced the more significant social and political changes, through mass unionisation and the pressure for housing reform. The two cities showed that the Ireland of 1914 was not one economy but two, and that wealth and poverty in each were bound up with the political divisions between unionist and nationalist.",
