    "model": "The Gaelic Athletic Association was the more effective organisation. Both bodies were products of the cultural nationalism of the late nineteenth century and both set out to reverse anglicisation, but the GAA built a bigger, more durable and more popular mass movement, while the Gaelic League, though influential in education and in politics, failed in its central aim of making Irish the everyday language.\n\nThe GAA was founded at Hayes' Hotel in Thurles on 1 November 1884 by Michael Cusack and Maurice Davin, with Davin as first president. Before then organised sport followed English amateur rules that excluded labourers and artisans, and hurling had declined since the Famine. The GAA's aim was to revive native games and to open athletics to all classes. This was an immediate success. It won powerful patrons in Archbishop Croke of Cashel, Parnell and Davitt, and Croke's letter of December 1884 condemned the imitation of English games and made the association respectable. Priests became club presidents and organisers, and the parish-based clubs soon spread across the south and west and then the midlands.\n\nThe GAA's organisation was the secret of its effectiveness. Davin drew up national rules for hurling and football in 1885, replacing the old local variations. The parish club, with county committees and a Central Council above, put an organisation in every community. The first All-Ireland championships began in 1887, and the first hurling final was played at Birr in 1888 when Thurles beat Meelick. Matches drew large crowds, often with bands and processions. The association survived severe difficulties: the infiltration of the Irish Republican Brotherhood, a quarrel with the Church, the loss-making American tour of 1888 and the Parnell split of 1890-91, which left membership collapsed by 1891. Yet the parish structure and the appeal of local rivalry meant that it recovered in the 1890s, and in 1908 it bought the grounds at Jones's Road, later Croke Park. Few Irish bodies of the period have lasted so well.\n\nThe Gaelic League was founded on 31 July 1893 by Douglas Hyde and Eoin MacNeill, following Hyde's 1892 lecture on the necessity for de-anglicising Ireland. It was officially non-political and non-sectarian, and it grew into a mass movement, with several hundred branches by 1904, travelling teachers called timirí, feiseanna and from 1897 the annual Oireachtas. Its newspaper, An Claidheamh Soluis, was launched in 1899 and edited by Patrick Pearse from 1903. It won real victories. Irish was allowed in primary schools by the Revised Programme of 1900, and the League won its campaign to make Irish compulsory for matriculation to the National University, decided in 1910. It also popularised Irish names, signs and the celebration of St Patrick's Day.\n\nHowever, the League's main aim, the revival of Irish as a spoken language, was not achieved. The decline of Irish since the Famine continued, and the classes taught many people some Irish without making it the language of the home. The League also drew mostly on teachers, women and the urban middle class, whereas the GAA reached farmers and workers in rural parishes. Its non-political stance did not survive: from about 1910 IRB members joined in growing numbers and pushed it towards politics, and in 1915 it declared for Irish independence, at which point Hyde resigned as president. The League's influence on the nationalist leadership was large, but this was an influence on politics rather than on the language itself.\n\nThe two bodies were closely connected, sharing the aim of de-anglicisation and many members, and the GAA's Irish-only, amateur identity, including its ban on foreign games, was a clear expression of that idea. Both gave nationalism self-confidence, and both trained a generation of activists who later took part in the independence struggle. The Church, which backed the GAA through Croke, was more cautious towards the League, as the dismissal of Fr O'Hickey from Maynooth in 1909 showed.\n\nOn balance, the GAA was more effective. It achieved exactly what it set out to do, creating a national association open to all classes and securing the place of hurling and Gaelic football in Irish life, and it endured as a mass organisation long after 1914. The Gaelic League achieved important educational gains and an inspiring cultural movement, but failed to restore the language it was founded to save.",
