    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Edward Carson was a highly successful leader of unionism in the sense that mattered most between 1910 and 1914. He built a mass movement, made the threat of armed resistance credible and forced the British government to accept that Ulster could not be coerced into Home Rule. He was less successful in keeping the whole of Ireland in the Union, and his success ended in partition rather than the defeat of Home Rule.\n\nCarson was a Dublin-born barrister, educated at Trinity College, who had been Solicitor General for Ireland in 1892 and Solicitor General for England from 1900 to 1906. He was MP for Dublin University and had cross-examined Oscar Wilde in 1895. In 1910 he became leader of the Irish Unionist parliamentary party. His southern background made him an unlikely champion of Ulster, but it allowed him to speak for all Irish unionists, and his reputation as a lawyer and orator gave unionism a formidable public voice.\n\nHis greatest success was mobilisation. With James Craig as chief organiser, he led a campaign built on the Ulster Unionist Council, founded in 1905, and on the Orange lodges and the Ulster Women's Unionist Council. A huge demonstration was held at Craigavon in September 1911. On Ulster Day, 28 September 1912, Carson was the first to sign the Solemn League and Covenant at Belfast City Hall, and about 237,000 men signed, with about 234,000 women signing a separate declaration. Conservative backing was secured when Bonar Law pledged support for Ulster in resistance at Blenheim Palace in July 1912. Unionism had never before shown such unity or organisation.\n\nCarson also made the threat of force credible. The Ulster Volunteer Force was formed in January 1913 under Sir George Richardson and had perhaps 90,000 to 100,000 men by 1914. In September 1913 the Ulster Unionist Council announced a Provisional Government of Ulster with Carson as chairman if Home Rule became law. The government did not prosecute. The Curragh incident of March 1914, when Brigadier-General Gough and many officers offered to resign rather than act against Ulster, showed that the army could not be relied upon to coerce unionists. In April 1914 the UVF landed about 25,000 rifles at Larne, Donaghadee and Bangor, and the authorities did not intervene. In doing so, Carson turned a political protest into a threat of civil war.\n\nThese tactics forced concessions. In March 1914 Asquith offered that Ulster counties could vote themselves out of Home Rule for six years, and although Carson rejected this as a stay of execution, the principle of exclusion had been conceded. At the Buckingham Palace Conference in July 1914 Carson and Craig negotiated with Asquith, Redmond and others, though it failed over Tyrone and Fermanagh. Carson's strategy meant that by 1914 Home Rule could not be imposed on the north-east.\n\nYet there were failures. Unionists could not stop the Third Home Rule Bill, which became law after the Parliament Act of 1911 removed the Lords' veto, and the Home Rule Act received the royal assent on 18 September 1914, although it was suspended for the war. Southern unionists, whom Carson also represented, were left without protection, and some historians argue that his threat made partition inevitable and that he abandoned them. Others suggest that he never intended to fight and bluffed to protect the Union. He himself lamented in 1921, when he resigned the leadership, that unionism had been sacrificed in the creation of Northern Ireland. His dependence on the Conservatives also exposed unionism when they later compromised.\n\nOverall, Carson was very successful as a leader of Ulster unionism, in organisation, unity and in securing exclusion, and less successful as a leader of Irish unionism as a whole. He failed to preserve the Union in all of Ireland, but he achieved the more realistic goal of protecting Ulster's place within it.",
