    "label": "1",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "At Grosse Isle the Irish experienced the worst of the Famine emigration: disease, overcrowding and death at the very edge of the New World, made worse by an unprepared system, though also met by courage and kindness from those who helped them. Grosse Isle was the quarantine station for the port of Quebec, and in 1847 it became a place of mass suffering and a lasting memorial to the Famine dead.\n\nGrosse Isle is an island in the St Lawrence River about 48 km downstream from Quebec City. The quarantine station was opened in 1832 to stop cholera reaching Quebec from emigrant ships. Every ship bound for Quebec had to stop there, where a medical officer inspected the passengers, the sick were landed at the hospital and the healthy were held in sheds or on board. The medical superintendent was Dr George Mellis Douglas, who had held the post since 1836. The station was built for the normal flow of a season, and its hospital sheds held only a few hundred patients when the emigration of 1847 began.\n\nThe flood of Famine emigrants overwhelmed it. The first ship of the season, the Syria, arrived in mid-May 1847 with fever on board, and the hospital was full within days. By 31 May about 40 ships carrying some 12,500 passengers lay at anchor off the island in a queue stretching down the river. Ships waited days or weeks for inspection, and the sick and dead lay in the holds alongside the healthy, so typhus spread among people who had been well. Many of these emigrants were already weakened by hunger, and some had been sent out by landlords or had crossed on overcrowded and poorly provisioned vessels.\n\nOn the island conditions were dreadful. Tents and new sheds were put up, but sick and dying people lay on the ground and on the beaches, and healthy passengers were crowded into sheds where many caught typhus. Dr Douglas had warned the authorities in advance and asked for more help, and his reports are a major source for the season. The staff themselves suffered, since several doctors caught typhus and some died. Catholic priests from the diocese of Quebec, many of them French-Canadian, and Anglican clergy took turns on the island, and several caught typhus and some died, which shows the courage shown by those who tried to help.\n\nThe death toll was severe. More than 5,000 people were buried on the island in 1847, most of them Irish, and some estimates are higher. The disease did not stay on the island, because passengers passed as healthy carried typhus up the river, and about 6,000 Irish emigrants died in the fever sheds at Montreal in 1847. Montreal's mayor, John Mills, died of typhus while helping the sick. Hundreds of children orphaned at Grosse Isle and in the fever sheds were adopted by French-Canadian families, many of whom let them keep their Irish surnames, an act of generosity that is still remembered.\n\nThe memory of the experience has also shaped how it is understood. The medical staff put up a monument on the island in 1853 to the dead of 1847, and the Ancient Order of Hibernians erected a large Celtic cross on the highest point, unveiled on 15 August 1909, with inscriptions in Irish, English and French. The Irish text blames the Famine on British rule more bluntly than the English text. Nationalists from John Mitchel onwards used Grosse Isle as evidence that British policy, not only the blight, killed the Irish poor, while others stressed landlord clearances and the limits of colonial resources. The station closed in 1937, and after Irish-Canadian campaigning the island became, in the 1990s, Grosse Île and the Irish Memorial National Historic Site.\n\nThe Irish experience at Grosse Isle was therefore one of catastrophe, shown in the quarantine queue, the fever sheds and the thousands of graves. It revealed the human cost of unregulated mass emigration and the failure of governments on both sides of the Atlantic to prepare, but also the generosity of French-Canadian Quebec. It is now the leading memorial to the Famine dead outside Ireland, even though most Famine emigrants went to the United States and Britain.",
