    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The main trend in Irish emigration between 1920 and 1966 was that the flow never stopped, but its direction changed. Emigration to the United States was cut by American law in the 1920s, and from then on Britain became the great destination, taking the great majority of those who left. The numbers rose and fell with economic conditions at home and abroad, and they reached a peak in the 1950s before collapsing in the 1960s.\n\nThe first trend was restriction in the United States, which redirected emigration rather than ending it. The Emergency Quota Act of 1921 limited immigrants of each nationality to 3 per cent of that nationality's foreign-born population in the 1910 census. The Immigration Act of 1924, the Johnson-Reed Act, tightened this to 2 per cent of the 1890 census, a base chosen to favour north-western Europeans. The Irish Free State received a quota of 28,567 a year, cut to 17,853 from 1929. Emigration to the USA fell sharply as a result, and the old pattern of the American wake and the passage from Queenstown gave way to a shorter journey.\n\nBritain filled the gap. The fare was low, jobs were close, there were no quotas, and the Irish could enter freely. The Great Depression after 1929 reduced American demand for labour and cut Irish emigration to the USA still further, and some emigrants even returned home. The Free State governments did not try to restrict emigration. They treated it as an economic problem they could not quickly solve, so the Irish moved to Britain in large numbers in the 1930s.\n\nThe Second World War brought a new phase. During the Emergency, Irish people needed travel permits and identity documents to go to Britain, and both governments controlled who could travel. Yet Britain recruited Irish workers for war factories, building and farm work, and well over 100,000 people from the state went to work there, while tens of thousands more joined the British forces. Wartime demand therefore confirmed Britain as the main destination, and the habit of going there stayed with a generation.\n\nThe post-war years saw emigration rise to its highest level. Reconstruction created huge demand for labour in Britain. Irish men worked as navvies on roads, building sites and power stations, while Irish women trained as nurses in the new National Health Service of 1948 or worked in factories and offices. The Ireland Act of 1949 confirmed that Irish citizens in the United Kingdom were not treated as aliens and could live, work and vote there, and the Common Travel Area allowed free movement between the islands. The Irish clustered in London, in places such as Kilburn and Camden Town, and in Birmingham, Coventry, Luton, Manchester and Liverpool. Domhnall Mac Amhlaigh, who went to England in 1951, recorded the navvy life of lodgings, long hours, dance halls and homesickness in his diary, Dialann Deoraí, published in 1960.\n\nThe 1950s were the crisis decade. About 400,000 people left the state between 1951 and 1961, most for Britain, and the census of 1961 recorded the lowest population in the history of the state, about 2.8 million. The Commission on Emigration and Other Population Problems, set up in 1948 and reporting in 1954, blamed mainly economic causes such as low incomes, unemployment and small farms, though it also noted the attractions of better living standards abroad. It accepted that emigration could not be ended quickly. John A. O'Brien's The Vanishing Irish, published in 1953, expressed the fear that the nation was dying out. The emigrants themselves were mostly young, and many came from rural areas and arrived in Britain with little training, which left them dependent on Catholic chaplaincies and Irish centres for help.\n\nThe last trend was the sharp fall in the 1960s. Economic Development of 1958, drawn up by T.K. Whitaker, and the First Programme for Economic Expansion, pursued by Seán Lemass as Taoiseach from 1959, brought growth that cut emigration sharply. At the same time the American Immigration and Nationality Act of 1965 abolished the old quotas from 1968 but favoured family ties and skills, which made entry harder for Irish people without American relatives.\n\nOverall, emigration from 1920 to 1966 was shaped by two forces: American restriction, which closed one door, and British demand, which opened another. Its peak in the 1950s reflected the economic failure of the independent state, and its decline in the 1960s showed how closely emigration followed Irish prosperity. Historians such as Enda Delaney see it as a choice by individuals within family strategies, but for many contemporaries it was a sign of a nation failing to keep its young.",
