    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Cultural nationalism was the use of music, art, literature and language to create and express a sense of national identity, and in nineteenth-century Europe it flourished in all of these. It was strongest where a people lacked its own state, as in Italy, Germany, Poland and the Czech lands, and it gave nationalist movements the myths, symbols and languages that political leaders then used.\n\nThe intellectual root was Romanticism, which rejected the rational order of the Enlightenment in favour of emotion, imagination and the national past. Johann Gottfried Herder had argued in the late eighteenth century that every people has its own spirit, the Volksgeist, expressed in its language, folklore and history. Nationalists took up this idea, and artists and writers set about collecting folklore and reviving medieval legends, which were claimed to reveal the true character of a people.\n\nMusic was one of the most powerful vehicles. Frédéric Chopin wrote mazurkas and polonaises based on Polish dance rhythms, and after 1831 his music, composed in exile, became a symbol of Polish national feeling. Franz Liszt, born in Hungary, composed the Hungarian Rhapsodies in the 1840s and 1850s, drawing on Hungarian and Romani music and presenting Hungary's identity to audiences across Europe. In Italy, Verdi's opera Nabucco (1842) included the chorus Va, pensiero, sung by Hebrew exiles, which Italian audiences came to hear as a cry for liberation from foreign rule. Around 1859 the slogan 'Viva VERDI' was popularly said to stand for Vittorio Emanuele Re d'Italia, the king the nationalists wanted. In Bohemia Bedřich Smetana's opera The Bartered Bride (1866) and his cycle of symphonic poems Má vlast (1874-79) drew on Czech folk music and landscape to assert a distinct Czech identity within the Habsburg Empire.\n\nArt played a smaller but real part. Romantic historical painting showed scenes from the national past, giving citizens a shared image of their history. Caspar David Friedrich's landscapes, such as Wanderer above the Sea of Fog (about 1818), expressed Romantic feeling for nature and the German spirit. Delacroix's Liberty Leading the People (1830), showing liberty leading the Parisian crowd over the barricades of the July Revolution, turned a political event into a lasting patriotic image. Art therefore helped to build a national memory, although it was less directly tied to specific nationalist movements than music or literature.\n\nLiterature was central. The Brothers Grimm published their Children's and Household Tales in 1812 and 1815, presenting German folklore as the expression of the German people, and began a great German dictionary in 1838. Alessandro Manzoni's novel I promessi sposi (1827), set in Lombardy under Spanish rule, was read as a comment on Italy's foreign domination. The Polish poet Adam Mickiewicz wrote the national epic Pan Tadeusz (1834), celebrating a Polish past at a time when Poland did not exist as a state. Pushkin's writing established a Russian literary language and became a central part of Russian national culture, while Elias Lonnrot's Kalevala (1835) did the same for Finland. Such works gave peoples a sense of a shared past and destiny.\n\nLanguage was where cultural nationalism was most practical. Manzoni's revised edition of 1840-42, written in the educated Tuscan of Florence, became a model for standard Italian. Josef Jungmann's Czech dictionary (1835-39) helped to revive Czech as a literary language, and grammars, dictionaries and schools in other lands did likewise. A standardised language created a single national community out of scattered dialects and regions, which was the necessary basis of any later demand for a state.\n\nOverall, cultural nationalism was a powerful force that preceded and prepared the way for political nationalism. It gave national movements symbols, languages and histories, and it reached ordinary people through opera houses, books and schools in a way that political pamphlets could not. Its limits were that it often idealised the past and excluded minorities, but in all its forms it helped to create the sense of national belonging that drove the unifications and national movements of the period.",
