    "model": "Between 1871 and 1920 Russia faced a series of connected challenges: an autocratic government unwilling to reform, a poor and land-hungry peasantry, the strains of rapid industrialisation, military defeat in two wars, and finally the collapse of the state in 1917 and a civil war that lasted until 1920. Together they ended three hundred years of Romanov rule.\n\nThe first challenge was political. The Tsar ruled as an autocrat, with no parliament, backed by the nobility, the Orthodox Church, the army and the Okhrana. Alexander II's limited reforms did not satisfy the revolutionaries, and he was assassinated by People's Will in March 1881. Alexander III and Nicholas II upheld autocracy, and Nicholas called hopes of a constitution 'senseless dreams' in 1895. This left liberals, Social Revolutionaries and Marxist Social Democrats working underground, and the regime had no way of channelling discontent.\n\nThe second challenge was the poverty of the peasantry, who made up about 80 per cent of the population in 1897. After the emancipation of 1861 they paid redemption payments for forty-nine years, held land in the mir with backward farming methods, and suffered from land hunger as families grew. The harvest failure of 1891 caused a famine across the Volga region, followed by cholera, and the slow government response was filled by volunteers such as Tolstoy and Chekhov. Stolypin's reforms after 1906 let peasants leave the commune and buy land, but by 1914 only about a quarter of households had done so, and he was assassinated in 1911.\n\nThe third challenge was industrialisation. Sergei Witte, Finance Minister from 1892 to 1903, drove a great spurt of growth with railways such as the Trans-Siberian, the gold standard in 1897 and French and Belgian loans. By 1914 Russia was the fifth industrial power. The cost fell on the peasants through heavy taxes and grain exports, and it created a discontented working class in huge factories such as the Putilov works. The depression of 1900-03 brought unemployment and strikes, so modernisation increased the pressure on the regime.\n\nThe fourth challenge was war and revolution in 1905. Defeat by Japan, with the fall of Port Arthur and the destruction of the Baltic Fleet at Tsushima in May 1905, exposed the weakness of the army and administration. On Bloody Sunday, 22 January 1905, troops fired on Father Gapon's peaceful procession, destroying trust in the Tsar, and strikes, mutinies and the Petersburg Soviet followed. The October Manifesto promised a Duma, but the Fundamental Laws of 1906 kept the Tsar's autocratic powers, and the first two Dumas were dissolved. The regime survived because the army stayed loyal and the opposition split, but it had not solved its problems.\n\nThe fifth challenge, the First World War, proved fatal. Russia mobilised over fifteen million men but was short of rifles and shells, and suffered defeats at Tannenberg in 1914 and in the great retreat of 1915. Railways broke down, inflation rose and the cities were short of food and fuel. Nicholas II took command of the army in 1915 and left the government to the Tsarina and Rasputin, whose murder in December 1916 showed the regime's isolation. On 8 March 1917 women textile workers in Petrograd demanded bread, the garrison joined the crowds, and Nicholas abdicated a week later.\n\nFinally, the new government could not meet the challenges either. The Provisional Government under Lvov and then Kerensky shared power with the Soviet, continued the war and delayed land reform. The Kerensky offensive failed in July, and Lenin's Bolsheviks, promising peace, land and bread, seized power on 6-7 November 1917. They then accepted the harsh Treaty of Brest-Litovsk in March 1918, losing Poland, the Baltic provinces and Ukraine, and fought a civil war against the Whites in which Kolchak, Denikin and Wrangel were defeated by 1920 under War Communism and the Red Terror.\n\nThe greatest challenge was the weakness of an autocracy that could not reform at the pace modernisation demanded. Industrial and agrarian problems built up pressure, but it was war that broke the regime, and the years from 1917 to 1920 showed how deeply the country was divided.",
