    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The First World War transformed both the economies and the societies of Europe. It was the first total war, so governments took control of industry, labour and food, and the cost in lives, debt and disruption changed the continent permanently. Economically it left Europe poorer and indebted, while socially it killed and maimed millions, strengthened workers and women and left lasting unrest.\n\nThe most immediate economic effect was state control of the economy. Because the war was long and consumed huge quantities of shells, the old belief in free markets was abandoned. In Britain the Defence of the Realm Act of August 1914 gave the government power over property, labour and the press, the railways were taken over at the outbreak of war, and the coal mines and shipping followed. After the Shell Crisis of 1915, which helped to bring down the Liberal government, the new Ministry of Munitions under Lloyd George took charge of supplying war material. Germany did the same through Walther Rathenau's War Raw Materials Department in 1914 and the Hindenburg Programme of 1916. The war therefore taught governments that they could direct whole economies, an idea that outlived the conflict.\n\nSecond, the war created a financial revolution and damaged European prosperity. States paid for the fighting through borrowing, war loans and printing money rather than taxation alone, so national debts multiplied several times over. Britain, once the world's greatest creditor, became a debtor to the United States, which replaced Europe as the leading financial power. Prices rose faster than wages and savings lost value. In Germany, where the war was financed mainly by loans and money creation, this laid the ground for the later inflation. Peacetime trade collapsed and Europe lost export markets to the United States and Japan, while northern France and Belgium were devastated by fighting. In Russia the economy collapsed under the strain, which contributed to revolution in 1917.\n\nThird, civilians suffered shortages that they had never faced in earlier wars. The British naval blockade and the failed harvest of 1916 brought the Turnip Winter of 1916 to 1917 in Germany, and hundreds of thousands of civilians died of malnutrition-related causes. Germany introduced bread rationing in 1915, while Britain, after the German submarine campaign cut supplies, introduced compulsory rationing of sugar, meat and butter in early 1918. Governments also used propaganda and censorship to keep civilians committed, so the war reached into everyday life far more than before.\n\nFourth, the human cost was enormous and shaped society for a generation. About nine to ten million soldiers died, including around 1.4 million French, about 2 million Germans and about 750,000 British. Many survivors were disabled and there were hundreds of thousands of war widows. The influenza pandemic of 1918 to 1919 then struck young adults weakened by the war and killed tens of millions worldwide. Returning soldiers often found unemployment, and demobilisation brought strikes and unrest in 1919.\n\nFifth, the war changed the position of workers and of women. Governments needed the cooperation of the trade unions, and British union membership roughly doubled between 1914 and 1920. Women took on men's work on a scale never seen before: the Treasury Agreement of March 1915 accepted dilution of skilled jobs, and hundreds of thousands of munitionettes filled shells in factories despite long shifts, the risk of explosions such as Silvertown in 1917 and Chilwell in 1918, and the TNT poisoning that turned them into canary girls. Women's auxiliary services were created in 1917 and 1918, and the Representation of the People Act of 1918 gave the vote to women over 30 in Britain, while German women over 20 won the vote in November 1918. Yet the gains were limited: many women were dismissed on demobilisation, pay stayed below men's, and France did not give women the vote until 1944. Class barriers also loosened somewhat, and reforms such as the Fisher Education Act of 1918 followed.\n\nOverall, the war's impact on the economy was largely negative, leaving debt, inflation, lost markets and devastated regions, while its impact on society was both destructive and transforming. Governments became more involved in economic life, unions and women gained a new role and recognition, but the scale of loss and instability meant that Europe emerged weaker and less stable than in 1914.",
