    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Between 1871 and 1920 national identity was expressed in literature and the arts in two main ways. Before 1914 composers, writers and monument builders presented history, myth, folk tradition and landscape as proof that each people had a distinct character, often when it was seeking unity or independence. During the First World War writers then first expressed patriotic national feeling, and later challenged it.\n\nIn Germany the arts celebrated the new Reich. Richard Wagner's operas drew on Germanic myth, and his Ring cycle was first staged in full at his own theatre in Bayreuth in 1876. After his death in 1883 the Bayreuth Festival continued as a shrine of German culture, so that his music became a symbol of a distinctively German identity. Monuments did the same in stone: the Hermann Monument in the Teutoburg Forest, completed in 1875, honoured the Germanic leader who defeated the Romans, and the Monument to the Battle of the Nations at Leipzig was unveiled in 1913. These works gave the young German state a heroic past.\n\nFor peoples without their own state, the arts expressed identity as a form of resistance. Bedrich Smetana's cycle of symphonic poems Ma vlast, written between 1874 and 1879 and including The Moldau, celebrated Bohemian history and landscape at a time when the Czechs sought more autonomy within Austria-Hungary. Jean Sibelius's Finlandia of 1899 to 1900 became a symbol of Finnish resistance to Russian control. Edvard Grieg used Norwegian folk music in Peer Gynt in 1876. In Ireland the same impulse found expression in the Gaelic League, founded by Douglas Hyde in 1893, and in the Abbey Theatre, opened in 1904, which staged plays drawn from Irish life and legend. In each case culture was used to show that a nation existed before it had political independence.\n\nIn imperial states the arts expressed national confidence. Edward Elgar's Pomp and Circumstance March No. 1 of 1901 and the Coronation Ode of 1902, with its Land of Hope and Glory, expressed British imperial pride. Rudyard Kipling celebrated British soldiers and the Empire in poems such as Recessional in 1897 and The White Man's Burden in 1899. Across Europe, governments and nationalist societies also founded national museums, galleries and theatres, taught national history in schools and promoted national languages, so the arts became a tool of state policy as well as of individual genius.\n\nThe First World War turned national identity into a theme of literature itself. In 1914 and 1915 Rupert Brooke's sonnets, including The Soldier, expressed idealism about dying for England, and he was celebrated as a hero when he died on the way to Gallipoli in April 1915. John McCrae's In Flanders Fields wrote in a similar spirit. By 1917 the mood had changed. Siegfried Sassoon, who had won the Military Cross, published a statement condemning the prolongation of the war, and Wilfred Owen, in Dulce et Decorum Est, attacked what he called the old Lie that it is sweet and fitting to die for one's country. Owen was killed on 4 November 1918, a week before the armistice. This was a challenge to national identity as patriotic duty, but it still shaped how the British remembered the war.\n\nOther nations produced their own versions. Henri Barbusse's Le Feu of 1916, which won the Prix Goncourt, described the lives of French infantry in the trenches, while Ernst Junger's Storm of Steel of 1920 presented a German officer who found meaning in combat. The contrast shows that each nation's literature expressed a different national experience of the same war.\n\nIn conclusion, between 1871 and 1920 literature and the arts were one of the main ways in which national identity was expressed. Before the war they glorified national myth, landscape and empire, and they gave a voice to peoples seeking independence; during and after the war the best writing questioned patriotism, but it still helped define how each nation understood itself.",
