    "label": "1",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "German governments had mixed success in dealing with social and economic problems between 1920 and 1939. Weimar governments overcame hyperinflation and built a welfare state but failed utterly against the Depression, while the Nazi regime ended mass unemployment and rebuilt the economy, but by methods that served rearmament and at the cost of freedom, living standards and the exclusion of those it despised.\n\nThe first great problem was hyperinflation. Germany fell behind on reparations, which had been set at 132 billion gold marks in 1921, and in January 1923 France and Belgium occupied the Ruhr. The government of Chancellor Cuno called for passive resistance and paid for it by printing money. By November 1923 a US dollar was worth about 4.2 trillion marks, wages were paid daily, and the savings, pensions and war loans of the middle classes were wiped out. Stresemann, who became Chancellor in August 1923, ended passive resistance in September, and the Rentenmark introduced on 15 November, with Hjalmar Schacht keeping its supply tightly limited, ended the inflation. This was a real success, but the middle classes were never fully compensated and remained resentful of the republic.\n\nBetween 1924 and 1929 Weimar governments achieved a genuine if fragile recovery. The Dawes Plan of 1924 scheduled reparations according to Germany's ability to pay and provided American loans, and the French left the Ruhr by 1925. The Young Plan of 1929 reduced the total and spread payments to 1988. Socially, the republic built a welfare state, with the eight-hour day, large municipal housing programmes in cities like Berlin and Frankfurt funded by a tax on those who had profited from inflation, and the Unemployment Insurance Act of 1927. However, this prosperity depended on American loans, so Germany was vulnerable to any shock.\n\nThat shock came with the Wall Street Crash of October 1929, and here Weimar governments failed. American loans were called in, exports collapsed, and unemployment rose from about 1.9 million in 1929 to about 6 million by early 1932. The unemployment insurance system had been designed for roughly 800,000 claimants and was overwhelmed. The coalition collapsed in March 1930, and Chancellor Brüning, governing by emergency decree under Article 48, cut wages, pensions and benefits to balance the budget. This deflationary policy deepened the slump. The Voluntary Labour Service of 1931 and the job-creation schemes of Papen and Schleicher in 1932 were too small and too late. Millions lived on poor relief, and desperate young men joined the SA, which gave them food and uniforms. The failure to solve unemployment brought the Nazis 37 per cent of the vote in July 1932 and led to Hitler's appointment in January 1933.\n\nThe Nazi regime was more successful in tackling unemployment. Under Schacht, the Reichsbank financed public works and rearmament through Mefo bills, which concealed military spending. Autobahn building, housing and military construction put men to work, conscription was reintroduced in March 1935, and the Reich Labour Service became compulsory for young men. Unemployment fell from about six million in early 1933 to under one million by 1937, giving way to labour shortages. The Four Year Plan of 1936 under Göring aimed at self-sufficiency and war readiness. Yet the figures excluded Jews and many women, wages were held down, trade unions were abolished in May 1933 and replaced by the German Labour Front, and the recovery was geared to war and not to consumers.\n\nSocially, the Nazis tried to create a national community. Strength Through Joy offered cheap holidays and cruises, and the Volkswagen savings scheme promised workers a car, though the factory was turned to military production and savers never received one. The Hitler Youth became the only legal youth organisation in 1936, and women were encouraged to be mothers through marriage loans and the Mother's Cross, although labour shortages later drew many back to work. But this community excluded people. Jews lost their rights under the Nuremberg Laws of 1935, and disabled people were forcibly sterilised under a law of 1933. Social problems were therefore solved for some by victimising others.\n\nOverall, German governments were successful in the short term at ending hyperinflation in 1923, at building welfare and housing in the later 1920s, and, in the Nazi case, at removing mass unemployment. Yet the Weimar governments failed to respond to the Depression, and the Nazi recovery rested on rearmament, repression and exploitation that was leading Germany towards war. I would judge that German governments were only partly successful, with real achievements that were fragile and in the Nazi case morally compromised.",
