    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Anglo-American popular culture had a large impact on Europe between 1920 and 1945, mainly through radio, cinema and music. Hollywood films and American and British broadcasting reached millions and shaped leisure, taste and fashion, especially among young and working-class people. The impact was also contested, as governments and critics tried to limit it and to use it for their own ends.\n\nThe most important influence was cinema. The Jazz Singer, released by Warner Brothers in 1927, introduced synchronised sound and the talkies spread rapidly. The Hollywood studio system of the 1930s, dominated by MGM, Paramount, Warner Brothers and Fox, produced and distributed most of the films shown in Britain and Europe. By the late 1930s Hollywood films filled most of the screens in Britain and much of Europe, and going to the cinema was the weekly habit of millions, including young women and the working class. American stars shaped fashion and ideas of glamour, and cinema became the most popular mass entertainment of the decade.\n\nIndividual figures show this reach. Charlie Chaplin, born in London in 1889, went to the United States in 1913 and made the Tramp one of the most recognised characters in the world, in films such as The Kid, The Gold Rush and City Lights. His Modern Times of 1936 satirised mechanisation, and The Great Dictator of 1940 parodied Hitler and was a major commercial success. Bing Crosby, whose relaxed crooning style suited radio and records, became the most popular entertainer of his age. His White Christmas, released in 1942, became one of the best-selling songs of all time, and he entertained Allied troops in Britain and France in 1944. Such stars carried Anglo-American entertainment into homes across Europe.\n\nRadio was the second great carrier. The BBC, founded as a company in 1922 and made a corporation in 1927 under John Reith, offered a public service model, while the American networks NBC and CBS were commercially financed. Roosevelt's fireside chats from March 1933 showed how directly radio could reach listeners. In the war the BBC became a trusted source of news, in contrast to German propaganda such as the broadcasts of Lord Haw-Haw, and its broadcasts to occupied Europe carried coded messages to resisters. The American Forces Network, from 1942, played American popular music to a wide audience in Britain and Europe, and Vera Lynn's We'll Meet Again became a symbol of hope for servicemen and families.\n\nThe cultural impact also showed in resentment and resistance. Critics in Britain worried about the effect of Hollywood and American radio on language, taste and morals, and the Cinematograph Films Act of 1927, requiring a quota of British films, together with the BBC's public service ethos, was partly a response. Fascist Italy imposed quotas on American films and built Cinecittà in 1937 to boost home production. In Germany American films were restricted from the mid-1930s and in effect excluded by 1940, and the Nazis condemned jazz and swing as degenerate. Even so, swing youth in Hamburg and other cities defied the ban and were met with arrests. When American troops arrived in Britain from 1942 with swing, chewing gum and cheap goods, they caused both admiration and resentment, summed up in the phrase overpaid, oversexed and over here.\n\nGovernments also used popular culture to sustain morale. Britain's Ministry of Information backed films such as In Which We Serve in 1942, and the radio show ITMA kept up spirits. Hollywood made Mrs Miniver and Casablanca in 1942, and Frank Capra's Why We Fight series explained the war to American troops. Entertainment thus served the war effort as well as providing escape.\n\nIn conclusion, Anglo-American popular culture transformed European leisure by 1945. It made cinema and radio mass habits, spread American music and style among the young, and provoked protective measures even in democracies. Its influence survived the efforts of the dictatorships to restrict it, and its lasting consequence was the dominance of American film and popular music in postwar Europe.",
