    "label": "1",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Communist governments were imposed on Eastern Europe between 1945 and 1956 through a combination of Soviet military occupation, gradual political manipulation and terror, backed by economic and ideological control. Stalin wanted a buffer of friendly states against invasion from the west, and the Red Army's presence made it possible. The imposition was usually slow and disguised at first, and when a satellite tried to break free, as Hungary did in 1956, Soviet force ended it.\n\nThe Red Army was the foundation of the process. By May 1945 it occupied Poland, Romania, Bulgaria, Hungary, eastern Germany and much of Czechoslovakia, so Soviet power on the ground limited what the Western Allies could demand. At Yalta in February 1945 Stalin accepted a Declaration on Liberated Europe promising free elections and a Polish government broadened to include non-communists, but he did not honour these promises. In October 1944 Churchill and Stalin had also informally agreed spheres of influence in the Balkans, giving the Soviet Union the lead in Romania and Bulgaria. Poland's case was typical: the Soviet Union backed the communist-dominated Lublin government, and sixteen leaders of the Polish underground were arrested and tried in Moscow in 1945.\n\nBetween 1945 and 1947 communists generally ruled through coalitions with peasant, socialist and liberal parties while taking control of key posts, especially the interior ministry and the police. The method became known as salami tactics, attributed to the Hungarian leader Matyas Rakosi, who boasted of slicing away his opponents one thin piece at a time. In Hungary the communists won only about 17 per cent in the free election of November 1945 against about 57 per cent for the Smallholders. Rakosi then used the police to break the Smallholders, and fraud in 1947 gave the communists the largest share. In Poland the peasant leader Mikolajczyk fled in October 1947 after rigged elections, and in Bulgaria the opposition leader Nikola Petkov was executed the same year.\n\nBy 1947 and 1948 the final stage came, in which coalition was replaced by one-party rule. King Michael of Romania was forced to abdicate on 30 December 1947. In Czechoslovakia, the only eastern state to remain democratic until 1948, non-communist ministers resigned in February 1948 over police control and Klement Gottwald's communists took power without a general election. The death of the foreign minister Jan Masaryk in March 1948 shocked Western opinion. In Poland the Socialist and Workers' Parties were merged into the Polish United Workers' Party in December 1948, ending independent socialist opposition.\n\nSoviet policy also worked by tying the satellites into a closed bloc. After the Truman Doctrine of March 1947 and the Marshall Plan of June 1947, Stalin forced the eastern states to refuse American aid. Czechoslovakia and Poland had shown interest before withdrawing in July 1947. The Cominform, founded in September 1947, linked the communist parties under Soviet direction, and Comecon, set up in January 1949, directed the satellites' trade towards Moscow. Soviet-style institutions followed: central planning, nationalisation of industry, collectivisation of farms in most states, and Soviet reparations and joint companies that took resources out of countries such as Hungary, Romania and East Germany.\n\nTerror and show trials eliminated rivals, including those within the communist parties. After Tito's Yugoslavia was expelled from the Cominform in June 1948, leaders suspected of independent nationalist views were purged. The Hungarian foreign minister Laszlo Rajk was tried and executed in 1949, and Cardinal Mindszenty was jailed the same year. The secret police, such as Hungary's AVH, spied on ordinary citizens, and the churches were attacked as centres of independent loyalty. In this way the regimes removed any organised opposition, not only the non-communists but also communists who did not follow Moscow.\n\nThe limits of this control were shown after Stalin's death in 1953. Khrushchev's secret speech of February 1956 exposed Stalin's crimes, and Hungarian reformers, encouraged by the Polish October of 1956, demanded change. When the uprising began on 23 October 1956 Imre Nagy was reinstated as prime minister, formed a coalition with non-communist parties, and on 1 November declared Hungary neutral and announced its withdrawal from the Warsaw Pact. The Soviet Union intervened again on 4 November, crushing the revolt. About 2,500 to 3,000 Hungarians were killed, around 200,000 fled, and Nagy was executed in 1958. The Western powers, distracted by the Suez crisis, gave no help.\n\nIn conclusion, communism was imposed above all by Soviet military power, which made it possible, and by the patient manipulation of coalitions, rigged elections, purges and terror, which turned it into reality by 1948 and 1949. Economic and institutional integration kept it in place, and the crushing of Hungary in 1956 showed that the Soviet Union would use force to preserve the system. These governments rested on Soviet backing rather than on popular consent.",
