    "label": "2",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The years 1973 to 1992 brought a sharp break in the social and economic life of Europe. The oil crisis ended the long post-war boom, bringing stagflation, unemployment and the decline of older industries in the West, while the communist economies fell into stagnation and then collapsed. Socially, family life, women's roles and the consumer society continued to change, and the end of communism in 1989 to 1991 redrew the map of Europe.\n\nThe starting point was the oil crisis. The Yom Kippur War began on 6 October 1973, and on 16 October the Gulf members of OPEC raised the price of oil by about 70 per cent. Arab producers announced output cuts and an embargo on the USA and the Netherlands, and over the following months prices roughly quadrupled. Western Europe, which depended on imported oil for around half of its energy, suffered fuel rationing, car-free Sundays in West Germany and the Netherlands, and a three-day working week in Britain in early 1974. The second shock after the Iranian revolution in 1979 doubled prices again and caused a second recession in 1980 to 1982.\n\nThe result was stagflation, a combination of stagnation and rising prices that conventional Keynesian policies could not cure. Unemployment across the EEC rose from low levels in the early 1970s to above 10 per cent by the mid-1980s, and the post-war commitment to full employment was abandoned, with governments making the defeat of inflation their main aim. Heavy industries such as coal, steel and shipbuilding declined sharply, and the EEC introduced steel quotas in 1980. Governments sought new energy sources: France launched its nuclear programme in 1974 and drew about 70 per cent of its electricity from nuclear power by the late 1980s, while North Sea oil made Britain self-sufficient by 1980. West Germany moved its industry up-market into machinery and cars.\n\nIn Britain these pressures produced the most dramatic change in policy. Margaret Thatcher, Prime Minister from May 1979, pursued monetarism, cut public spending, and curbed the trade unions through the Employment Acts of 1980 and 1982 and the Trade Union Act of 1984. She privatised state assets such as British Telecom, introduced the right to buy council houses in 1980, and deregulated the City in 1986. The cost was unemployment of about three million in 1982 and the collapse of manufacturing in the north, Scotland and Wales, with riots in English cities in 1981. The miners' strike of 1984 to 1985 ended in defeat for the National Union of Mineworkers. Supporters saw a reversal of decline, while critics pointed to lasting regional inequality.\n\nThe communist economies faced a different crisis. Central planning had built heavy industry but produced shortages, poor quality and slow innovation, and the arms race and the war in Afghanistan from 1979 absorbed much of the Soviet Union's output. Under Brezhnev growth slowed in what became known as the era of stagnation, and the leadership postponed reform by relying on oil and gas revenues, which vanished when oil prices fell in the mid-1980s. Eastern Europe fell behind the West, and Hungary's limited market reforms under Kadar, known as goulash communism, were only a partial answer. Gorbachev's perestroika, begun in 1986, introduced limited private enterprise but could not rescue the system.\n\nThe social changes of these years continued trends from the 1960s. Divorce became easier and more common, with West Germany introducing no-fault divorce in 1977 and Spain legalising divorce in 1981, and single-parent families became a growing category of households. More married women took paid work, although they were paid less than men, and the equal pay and sex discrimination laws of the 1970s gave them a legal basis for equality. Church attendance continued to fall in the West, and immigrant communities, including Muslims from North Africa, Turkey and South Asia, made European societies more diverse. Youth culture reflected the economic mood, as punk in 1976 and 1977 expressed the anger of unemployed young people, and Live Aid in 1985 showed the reach of television and pop music.\n\nThe period ended with political and social upheaval. Mass protest, helped in East Germany by church-led prayers in Leipzig in 1989, brought down the communist regimes, and the Soviet Union itself was dissolved in 1991. In the West the EEC deepened integration through the single market from 1986, and the Maastricht Treaty was agreed in 1991 to 1992.\n\nOverall, the main developments were the end of the post-war boom and its replacement by unemployment, industrial restructuring and free-market policies in the West, and the failure of central planning that led to the collapse of communism in the East. Socially the period saw the continued loosening of family and religious ties and growing diversity. The oil crisis of 1973 and the revolutions of 1989 were the turning points.",
