    "marks": 100,
    "model": "British withdrawal from India in August 1947 both helped and hindered the country. It helped by ending nearly two centuries of foreign rule and giving Indians control of their own government, and the new state avoided a break-up and survived as the world's largest democracy. It hindered because the speed and manner of the departure produced Partition and its terrible violence, and left lasting conflict with Pakistan. On balance independence was a great gain, but the manner of withdrawal made it much more costly than it needed to be.\n\nThe most important benefit was political freedom. The British Raj had ruled India directly since 1858, and Indians had campaigned for self-rule for decades through Gandhi's Non-Cooperation movement, the Salt March of 1930 and Quit India in 1942. Independence on 15 August 1947 fulfilled that struggle. Nehru became prime minister, and India became a republic on 26 January 1950. Its leaders chose a democratic constitution and a policy of non-alignment in the Cold War, which gave India an independent voice in world affairs that it could never have had under British control.\n\nIndia also gained by remaining connected to Britain on its own terms. The London Declaration of April 1949 allowed India to become a republic while staying in the Commonwealth, with the King recognised only as head of the association. India stayed a major trading partner of Britain within the sterling area, so independence did not cut it off from economic links. Britain's departure also removed the colonial relationship in which, by 1945, Britain actually owed India more than a billion pounds in sterling balances for wartime supplies. In that sense withdrawal reversed the old financial dependence.\n\nAgainst this, the withdrawal was rushed. Britain was exhausted and heavily in debt after the war, the Royal Indian Navy mutiny of 1946 showed that the forces could no longer be relied on, and Attlee's government announced in February 1947 that rule would end by June 1948. Mountbatten then brought the date forward to August 1947. Congress and the Muslim League had failed to agree at the Cabinet Mission of 1946, and Jinnah's demand for Pakistan, first set out in the Lahore Resolution of 1940, could not be met without dividing Punjab and Bengal. The haste left only weeks to divide the army, civil service and railways between the two new dominions.\n\nThe human cost was enormous. Cyril Radcliffe, a barrister who had never visited India, drew the boundary lines, and the Radcliffe Line was only announced on 17 August, two days after independence. Between ten and fifteen million people fled across the new borders, and communal violence killed hundreds of thousands, with some estimates far higher. The killings had already begun in Calcutta on Direct Action Day in August 1946, which suggests that the British had known the danger. Gandhi, who had fought for a united India, was shot dead by a Hindu nationalist in January 1948.\n\nPartition also left unresolved disputes. Over 500 princely states had to choose between India and Pakistan, and the Maharaja of Kashmir signed an Instrument of Accession to India in October 1947, which led to the first Indo-Pakistani war and a ceasefire only in January 1949. Pakistan itself was created in two parts separated by about 1,600 km of Indian territory, a flaw that contributed to later conflict. Kashmir has remained a source of tension between the two states since then.\n\nIn conclusion, withdrawal helped India fundamentally by giving it self-government, a democratic constitution and a place in the world. However, the rush to leave and the decision to partition left a legacy of violence and rivalry that has lasted to the present. The principle of withdrawal was right, but the way it was carried out hindered India.",
