    "label": "2",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Development workers in Africa faced problems that came from the physical conditions in which they worked, from the poverty and political instability of the countries themselves, and from weaknesses in the aid system. Whether they were missionaries, volunteers or staff of agencies such as Concern, Trocaire or GOAL, they often found that good intentions were not enough to bring lasting change.\n\nThe first group of problems was practical and physical. Many workers, such as the young volunteers sent out by Voluntary Service Overseas or the American Peace Corps, lived in remote rural communities on short contracts. Roads were poor, supplies were slow to arrive, and access to medicine, transport and communications was limited. Their typical work, including digging wells, giving agricultural advice, running primary health care and vaccination, and training local staff, depended on equipment and fuel that were hard to obtain. Climate added to the difficulty, as drought in the Sahel from the late 1960s to the mid-1970s and in Ethiopia in 1983 to 1985 destroyed livestock and crops and meant that development workers were suddenly forced to deliver emergency food rather than long-term projects.\n\nA second problem was war and political violence. Development workers often worked in countries going through civil conflict. During the Biafran War of 1967 to 1970, the blockade caused famine in Biafra, and relief workers, including Irish missionaries, had to operate under fire and in the face of political controversy. Concern was founded in Dublin in 1968 in response to that famine, and Medecins Sans Frontieres grew from the experience of doctors in Biafra. In the Congo after 1960 the army mutiny and the secession of Katanga put foreign staff at risk. Relief was also open to misuse, since critics argued that food aid could go to armed groups and prolong wars rather than end suffering.\n\nA third problem was cultural misunderstanding and unsuitable projects. Many early projects of the 1960s and 1970s were designed top-down by outsiders and imposed on communities without consulting the people they were meant to help. Workers who did not know local languages or customs could misjudge needs, and some African critics saw them as agents of dependence on foreign money. Missionary orders such as the Holy Ghost Fathers and the Medical Missionaries of Mary ran schools, hospitals and clinics in Nigeria, Kenya and Tanzania, but they too had to adapt to the local society. In the 1980s, agencies increasingly moved towards projects planned with local people for this reason.\n\nSustainability was another difficulty. A well or clinic often failed once the donor left, because local staff lacked training, money or spare parts to keep it going. Aid was frequently tied, requiring recipients to spend it on goods from the donor country, which raised costs and reduced its value. Aid could also support corrupt governments such as Mobutu's Zaire, which meant that workers were helping to maintain a system that wasted resources. Furthermore, debt and the structural adjustment programmes imposed by the IMF and World Bank from the early 1980s forced governments to cut spending on health and education, undermining the services that development workers were trying to build.\n\nIn conclusion, development workers faced a mixture of practical hardship, violence, cultural gaps and structural obstacles. Many of them achieved real results, such as the worldwide eradication of smallpox by 1977 and improved access to water and schooling, but they worked in a system with weaknesses that limited what even the most dedicated individual could achieve.",
