    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "On balance Britain managed race relations better than France between 1945 and 1990. Britain faced serious racism and violence, but it passed anti-discrimination laws, accepted that its society was now multi-racial and learned from its failures after the 1981 riots. France, tied to a republican ideal that ignored ethnic difference, left its second generation of migrants poorly protected and saw the rise of the Front National.\n\nBritain's first strength was that it was willing to legislate against discrimination. The Race Relations Act of 1965 made discrimination on grounds of colour, race or ethnic origin illegal in public places and set up a Race Relations Board. The 1968 Act extended the ban to housing and employment, areas where migrants had met a colour bar in lodgings and Rachmanite exploitation in Notting Hill, and the 1976 Act covered indirect discrimination and created the Commission for Racial Equality. These laws were hard to enforce, since discrimination was difficult to prove, but they gave minorities legal rights and a state body to turn to, and they marked official disapproval of racism.\n\nSecondly, Britain adopted a policy of integration that respected cultural difference. Roy Jenkins said in 1966 that integration meant equal opportunity combined with cultural diversity, not assimilation. Under this multicultural approach mosques, gurdwaras and temples were built in Birmingham, Leicester and London, the Notting Hill Carnival grew into one of the largest street festivals in Europe, and the Swann Report of 1985 urged schools to recognise the needs of ethnic minority children. Black and Asian politicians such as Diane Abbott, Bernie Grant and Keith Vaz entered Parliament in 1987, which showed that minorities could win a voice inside the political system.\n\nBritain was far from perfect, and a fair argument must say so. Immigration controls tightened in 1962, 1968 and 1971, the 1968 Act was rushed through to keep out Kenyan Asians, and Enoch Powell's rivers of blood speech of April 1968 found support among London dockers. The National Front campaigned against immigration and the death of Blair Peach in Southall in 1979 showed the violence it provoked. The Notting Hill riots of 1958 and the murder of Kelso Cochrane in 1959 had already shown the depth of hostility. Yet the National Front never won a parliamentary seat, and counter-movements such as the Anti-Nazi League and Rock Against Racism mobilised many young people against it.\n\nThe worst failure came in 1981. Heavy use of the sus laws and Operation Swamp 81 sparked the Brixton riots of April, followed by Toxteth and Moss Side in July. However, Britain's response was an open inquiry. The Scarman Report identified racial disadvantage, unemployment and poor policing as causes and recommended community consultation and more accountable policing, which led to the Police and Criminal Evidence Act of 1984. A state that investigates its own failings in public and reforms is managing race relations better than one that avoids the subject.\n\nFrance's republican model offered equality in theory but little in practice. Universal citizenship and laïcité meant the state did not recognise ethnic communities, and France collected no statistics on ethnic origin, which made discrimination hard to measure or challenge. Its main anti-racist law, the Pleven law of 1972, only criminalised incitement to racial hatred. Migrants from North Africa lived in bidonvilles such as Nanterre and in Sonacotra hostels, the harkis were kept in camps, and the police attack on Algerian demonstrators in Paris on 17 October 1961 under Maurice Papon killed dozens and was long buried. Labour immigration was suspended in 1974, and the government even offered payments after 1977 to migrants who would go home.\n\nIn the 1980s the second generation, the beurs, were French by birth but trapped in rundown banlieue estates with high unemployment and tense relations with the police. Unrest in Les Minguettes in 1981 and the shooting of Toumi Djaïdja led to the March for Equality and against Racism of 1983, which reached Paris with tens of thousands of supporters. Mitterrand's government regularised about 130,000 undocumented migrants and introduced the ten-year residence card, and SOS Racisme, founded in 1984 with its slogan Touche pas à mon pote, mobilised young people. Yet Mitterrand's promise of local voting rights for foreign residents was never kept, and critics said SOS Racisme ignored social causes of disadvantage.\n\nMeanwhile the Front National turned hostility to immigrants into political success. Founded by Jean-Marie Le Pen in 1972, it won about 17 per cent at Dreux in 1983, 11 per cent in the 1984 European elections, 35 seats in the National Assembly in 1986 and over 14 per cent for Le Pen in the 1988 presidential election. Britain's far right never came close to this.\n\nOverall, both countries struggled, and France deserves credit for its anti-racist marches and regularisations. But Britain's laws, its acceptance of cultural diversity, its minority MPs and its willingness to learn from Scarman gave it a more effective framework, and far-right extremism there remained marginal.",
