    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Julius Nyerere and Gamal Abdul Nasser were two of the most significant leaders in post-colonial Africa, and Nadine Gordimer was one of its most important writers. Nyerere showed how a new state could achieve national unity and social progress, Nasser made Egypt the centre of Arab nationalism and defied the old colonial powers, and Gordimer exposed the moral failure of apartheid. Their significance was real, though each also left a mixed record.\n\nNyerere's first achievement was to lead Tanganyika to independence peacefully and to hold a diverse country together. He founded the Tanganyika African National Union in 1954, led it to a majority in the 1960 elections and became prime minister when Tanganyika became independent on 9 December 1961, and president of the republic a year later. Tanganyika united with Zanzibar in April 1964 to form Tanzania, a state with more than a hundred ethnic groups and no dominant majority. Nyerere avoided ethnic parties and made Swahili the national language and the language of primary education, which reduced ethnic conflict at a time when Nigeria was torn apart by the Biafran War. He also showed independence abroad: he asked Britain to put down the army mutiny of January 1964, yet broke off relations with Britain in 1965 over its failure to act in Southern Rhodesia.\n\nHis second claim to significance was his attempt to build African socialism through ujamaa. The Arusha Declaration of 5 February 1967 committed Tanzania to self-reliance and state control of the main means of production, and banks and major industries were nationalised that year. Nyerere's Education for Self-Reliance tied schooling to rural life, and Tanzania made impressive gains in health and literacy, so that adult literacy rose to a high level by the early 1980s. However, from 1973-74 the voluntary ujamaa villages became compulsory villagisation, and millions were moved, often by force. Farm output collapsed, food had to be imported, and drought, the oil price rise and the cost of the war against Idi Amin worsened the crisis. Nyerere stepped down in 1985, and his successor accepted an IMF programme in 1986 that reversed much of his socialism. Even so, he is remembered as Mwalimu, the teacher, for his integrity and his refusal to rule by ethnic favour.\n\nNasser's significance lay in his challenge to Western control of the Middle East and North Africa. Born in Alexandria in 1918, he was one of the Free Officers who overthrew King Farouk in July 1952, and he became prime minister in 1954 and president in 1956. He secured the withdrawal of British troops from the Suez Canal base in 1954 to 1956, attended the Bandung Conference in 1955 and bought Soviet-bloc arms. After the United States and Britain withdrew their offer to fund the Aswan High Dam, he nationalised the Suez Canal Company on 26 July 1956. Britain, France and Israel invaded in October, but American and Soviet pressure forced them to withdraw, and although Nasser's army was beaten in Sinai he emerged with the canal under Egyptian control. It was a decisive blow to Anglo-French prestige in the region and made him a hero across the Arab world.\n\nNasser used that prestige to promote Arab nationalism, forming the United Arab Republic with Syria in 1958 and backing anti-colonial movements such as the Algerian struggle. At home he pursued land reform, industrialisation and the Aswan High Dam. His record had serious flaws: one-party rule, the suppression of the Muslim Brotherhood, a costly commitment in the Yemeni civil war from 1962, and above all the catastrophic defeat of June 1967, when Egypt lost Sinai after he had blocked the Straits of Tiran. He offered to resign, was kept in office by mass demonstrations and died in September 1970.\n\nNadine Gordimer's significance was cultural and moral. A white South African born at Springs in 1923, she used novels such as A World of Strangers, The Conservationist, Burger's Daughter and July's People to expose the divisions and hypocrisies of apartheid society. Several of her books were banned, she supported the African National Congress and helped Nelson Mandela with his speech for the Rivonia trial in 1964. Her Nobel Prize in Literature in 1991 brought international attention to the struggle against apartheid, though some black writers felt white liberal authors had a limited perspective.\n\nOverall, Nyerere's significance lay in unity and social progress despite economic failure, Nasser's in giving the Arab and African world a symbol of defiance against colonial powers despite military and political failure, and Gordimer's in showing through literature the human cost of racial rule. Each shaped how post-colonial Africa saw itself.",
