    "label": "1",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Berlin, Korea and Cuba were each of great importance for US foreign policy in the Cold War. Berlin showed the American commitment to defend Western Europe, Korea showed that containment would be applied by force in Asia, and Cuba brought the United States to the edge of nuclear war and shaped the way it handled the superpower rivalry thereafter.\n\nBerlin first tested American resolve in Europe. Germany was divided in 1949 into the Federal Republic in the West and the German Democratic Republic in the East, but Berlin, deep inside the Soviet zone, was divided into four sectors. On 24 June 1948 the Soviet Union blocked road, rail and canal access to West Berlin, hoping to force the Western powers out. Truman refused to withdraw and instead ordered an airlift, which carried more than two million tons of food, coal and other supplies before the blockade was lifted on 12 May 1949. This showed that the United States would risk confrontation to support free Europeans, and it helped to push the creation of NATO, signed on 4 April 1949, with Article 5 promising that an attack on one member would be treated as an attack on all.\n\nBerlin was important again in 1958 to 1963. Khrushchev demanded in November 1958 that the Western powers leave, and the issue was debated at the Vienna summit with Kennedy in June 1961. Over two million East Germans had fled west between 1949 and 1961, mostly through Berlin, and the Wall was built from 13 August 1961 to stop them. Kennedy accepted the Wall rather than risk war, but in October 1961 American and Soviet tanks faced each other at Checkpoint Charlie, and in June 1963 he visited West Berlin and declared 'Ich bin ein Berliner'. The crisis taught the United States that the division of Germany was something both sides would live with, while the symbol of American support for West Berlin remained.\n\nKorea was important because it made containment a military policy in Asia. After the Communist victory in China in October 1949 and the adoption of NSC-68 in April 1950, North Korea invaded the South on 25 June 1950. Truman sent American forces under a United Nations flag, without a declaration of war, treating the operation as a police action. General MacArthur's landing at Inchon in September 1950 turned the war, but when UN forces advanced north of the 38th parallel, Chinese forces entered the war, driving them back. Truman's dismissal of MacArthur on 11 April 1951 after he argued for attacking China established the principle of civilian control and the policy of limited war.\n\nThe consequences of Korea for American policy were far-reaching. The armistice signed at Panmunjom on 27 July 1953 left the frontier close to the 38th parallel, and about 36,000 Americans had died. US troops remained in South Korea afterwards, and defence spending rose after NSC-68 was adopted, so the Cold War was militarised. Korea also confirmed the belief that communism in Asia had to be resisted, a belief that led the United States towards Vietnam.\n\nCuba was the most dangerous of the three. Fidel Castro took power on 1 January 1959 and nationalised American-owned property in 1960, and Eisenhower responded by cutting the sugar quota and imposing a partial embargo, breaking off relations in January 1961. The Bay of Pigs landing by CIA-trained exiles on 17 April 1961 was defeated within three days, a humiliation for Kennedy. In October 1962 a U-2 photograph taken on 14 October revealed Soviet missile sites on Cuba, ninety miles from Florida. Kennedy and his advisers in ExComm rejected an air strike and announced a naval quarantine on 22 October. After Black Saturday, when a U-2 was shot down on 27 October, Khrushchev agreed on 28 October to remove the missiles in return for a US pledge not to invade Cuba. Kennedy also secretly agreed to withdraw Jupiter missiles from Turkey.\n\nThe missile crisis was important because it showed both sides how close nuclear war could come. A hotline between Washington and Moscow was installed in 1963, and relations began to ease, which led towards arms control. It also left Cuba as a continuing Soviet ally close to American shores.\n\nOverall, each crisis shaped American policy, but Cuba had the greatest effect because it brought the world closest to nuclear war and led to a more careful management of the Cold War. Berlin showed American will in Europe and Korea extended containment to Asia, but only the missile crisis forced Washington and Moscow to recognise the need for communication and restraint.",
