    "label": "2",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Between 1945 and 1968 the United States enjoyed the greatest economic boom in its history, and the causes lay in the strength of its wartime economy, its dominance of the postwar world, heavy government spending and an eager consumer market. The boom transformed American society, creating mass suburban living, consumer culture and a large middle class, but it also left many groups, especially black Americans and the poor, outside its benefits.\n\nThe starting point was the Second World War. American factories turned out vast quantities of aircraft, ships, tanks and munitions between 1941 and 1945, and by 1945 the United States produced roughly half of the world's manufactured goods while its European and Japanese rivals lay in ruins. Wartime wage and price controls and the shortage of consumer goods meant that households had built up large savings, and once rationing ended there was a pent-up demand for cars, houses and appliances. The government had also built factories during the war and sold them cheaply to private firms in industries such as aluminium, synthetic rubber and aircraft. The boom therefore began from an unrivalled industrial base and a public ready to spend.\n\nSecond, American leadership of the international economy fuelled growth. The Bretton Woods conference of 1944 created the International Monetary Fund and the World Bank and made the dollar, convertible to gold at 35 dollars an ounce, the central currency of the world. The Marshall Plan of 1948 to 1952 gave about 13 billion dollars to Western Europe, and much of it was spent on American goods, so aid abroad became orders for American factories. GATT in 1947 committed states to reducing trade barriers, which suited the world's largest exporter. American multinationals such as General Motors, IBM and Coca-Cola expanded overseas, especially after the Treaty of Rome in 1957 opened the European market, and this tied the American economy to global growth.\n\nThird, government spending and public investment sustained the boom. The Korean War and NSC-68 raised the defence budget from about 14 billion dollars in 1950 to about 50 billion by 1953, and high Cold War spending continued through the 1950s and 1960s, supplying contracts to firms such as Lockheed, Boeing and General Dynamics. Eisenhower himself warned of the military-industrial complex in January 1961. Defence research funded jet aircraft, electronics and computers and encouraged the growth of Silicon Valley, while the space programme after Sputnik employed thousands of engineers. The Federal-Aid Highway Act of 1956 funded about 41,000 miles of interstate highway, boosting steel, construction and car production. The 1964 tax cut, urged by Walter Heller and cutting the top rate from 91 to 70 per cent, stimulated demand and growth through the mid-1960s. Boom was thus as much a product of the state as of the free market.\n\nThe impact on society was striking, beginning with the suburbs and the American Dream. The GI Bill of 1944 and federal mortgage guarantees allowed millions to buy homes with little deposit, and William Levitt built about 17,000 standardised houses at Levittown on Long Island from 1947. By 1960 more Americans lived in suburbs than in the central cities or on farms. The baby boom, which peaked in 1957 at about 4.3 million births, drove demand further, and the Sun Belt grew rapidly. Yet the suburbs were largely closed to black families by covenants and lending practices, and white flight left the inner cities poorer and more segregated.\n\nConsumerism and the world of work were also reshaped. Credit cards such as Diners Club in 1950 and BankAmericard in 1958 allowed families to buy on instalments, and television, in about 9 per cent of homes in 1950 and about 87 per cent in 1960, brought advertising into the living room. Dishwashers, freezers and washing machines became common in middle-class homes. White-collar work grew faster than manufacturing, and William Whyte's The Organization Man of 1956 described a corporate employee who valued loyalty and conformity. Strong unions secured gains such as the 1950 Treaty of Detroit, which tied wages to productivity and gave pensions and health insurance to car workers. Women's roles were affected too: the ideal of the suburban housewife dominated the 1950s, yet women's share of the labour force rose from about 30 per cent in 1950 to about 38 per cent in 1970, and Betty Friedan's The Feminine Mystique in 1963 challenged the domestic ideal.\n\nThe boom's limits were equally important. John Kenneth Galbraith's The Affluent Society in 1958 argued that private wealth had left public services neglected, and Michael Harrington's The Other America in 1962 showed that tens of millions still lived in poverty. Black Americans and many southern workers faced discrimination in jobs, housing and access to GI Bill benefits, and the riots at Watts in 1965 and Detroit in 1967 revealed the anger this produced. Overall, the boom rested on temporary American dominance and heavy public spending, and it gave most white middle-class Americans a standard of living unknown to earlier generations, but it did not deliver the American Dream to all.",
