    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The Moon landing of July 1969 was an overwhelming triumph for the United States, and its greatest impact lay in the Cold War, where it gave America a decisive victory over the Soviet Union in the space race. At home its impact was more limited and short-lived, lifting national pride at a time of turmoil but failing to end the divisions or sustain public support for the space programme.\n\nIn foreign affairs the landing was above all a Cold War victory. The space race began with Sputnik in October 1957, and Soviet firsts such as Yuri Gagarin's flight in April 1961 raised fears that the Soviet system was technologically superior. Kennedy responded on 25 May 1961 by committing the United States to a landing before the decade's end, and at Rice University in 1962 he said the nation chose to go to the Moon because it was hard. When Apollo 11's Eagle landed in the Sea of Tranquillity on 20 July 1969 and Neil Armstrong stepped out, the United States had won the race. The Soviet Moon rocket, the N1, failed in every test, and the Soviet crewed lunar programme was abandoned. Soviet media played down the achievement, and the Luna 15 probe crashed on the Moon on 21 July.\n\nThe landing also enhanced American prestige and power across the world. It was a global television event and an image of American technology, and the crew's plaque reading we came in peace for all mankind, along with the 1967 Outer Space Treaty banning national claims, presented the United States as a responsible leader. The same technology that took astronauts to the Moon, such as the Saturn V and the powerful rockets behind the intercontinental ballistic missile, underlined American military strength. The success restored confidence in American leadership abroad at the very moment when Vietnam was damaging its reputation. It also contributed to the thaw in relations that followed: the Moscow summit of 1972 saw the first major arms agreements, and the Apollo-Soyuz joint flight of July 1975 was the first joint American-Soviet space mission, a symbol of détente.\n\nDomestically, the landing gave a powerful boost to national pride at a bitter moment. 1968 had brought the assassinations of Martin Luther King and Robert Kennedy, riots in the cities, the chaotic Democratic convention in Chicago and a deeply divisive war in Vietnam. Apollo 11 offered a moment of shared achievement, and Nixon spoke to the astronauts by telephone from the White House. The mission was a triumph of organisation, employing hundreds of thousands of people through contractors such as North American Aviation, Grumman, Boeing and IBM, and it cost about 25 billion dollars. It spurred developments in technology, since the Apollo Guidance Computer was one of the first to use integrated circuits, which helped the electronics industry.\n\nYet the domestic impact had clear limits. Some commentators contrasted the technical triumph with the troubles at home, and critics such as Amitai Etzioni and some in Congress argued the money would be better spent on poverty. The landing did nothing to end the anti-war movement or racial tension. Once the race was won, public interest and political support declined, and NASA's budget fell substantially in the early 1970s. Apollo 17 in December 1972 was the last crewed lunar mission and later flights, such as Apollo 18 to 20, were cancelled. Skylab in 1973 and the Space Shuttle, first flown in 1981, never recaptured the same excitement, and the Challenger disaster of 1986 showed the limits of American ambitions.\n\nOverall, the Moon landing had its greatest impact on foreign affairs, where it secured a clear victory over the Soviet Union and enhanced American prestige at a time of difficulty. At home it gave a real but temporary lift to national morale, and it did not resolve the deeper social and political problems of the era.",
