    "label": "B2",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "Pope John XXIII opened the Second Vatican Council on 11 October 1962, and it closed on 8 December 1965, with Irish bishops including McQuaid attending. The council allowed the Mass to be said in the vernacular, and the first Masses in English in Ireland were held in 1965. It promoted ecumenism, so the Catholic ban on attending Trinity College Dublin was lifted in 1970 and the first inter-church talks were held at Ballymascanlon in 1973. It declared religious freedom a right, which weakened the claim that a Catholic state should have a special position in law.",
