    "label": "C1",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "As Home Secretary, Robert Peel founded the Metropolitan Police in London in 1829, a professional force that became a model for others. In 1829 he conceded Catholic emancipation with the Duke of Wellington to avoid civil conflict in Ireland. He reintroduced income tax in 1842 to pay for cuts in tariffs, and his budgets of 1842 and 1845 reduced tariffs on many imports. The Bank Charter Act of 1844 regulated the issue of banknotes by the Bank of England, and the Factory Act of 1844 limited hours for women and children. The Tamworth Manifesto of 1834 set out a moderate programme of reform that became the basis of the modern Conservative Party. He repealed the Corn Laws in 1846, supported by free traders such as Cobden and Bright, which split the Conservative Party and ended his ministry.",
