    "model": "Napoleon III appointed Haussmann Prefect of the Seine in 1853, and he directed the grands travaux for about seventeen years. Belgrand built aqueducts from the Dhuis and Vanne valleys and an underground sewer network, which brought clean water and improved sanitation. Straight wide boulevards such as Sebastopol and Saint-Germain opened up the old city, improved traffic and let troops move quickly. New parks such as the Bois de Boulogne, the Bois de Vincennes and the Buttes-Chaumont gave residents green space, including in the working-class east. Public buildings and markets, such as Victor Baltard's iron-and-glass Les Halles, and uniform tree-lined facades gave the city an impressive appearance. The works gave employment and made the city safer, but the demolitions displaced the poor, and the cost of the debt led to his dismissal in 1870.",
