    "label": "B4",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "Wilfred Owen was born in Oswestry in 1893 and enlisted in the Artists Rifles in October 1915, later being commissioned in the Manchester Regiment. He fought on the Somme and was sent to Craiglockhart War Hospital in June 1917 with shell shock. At Craiglockhart Siegfried Sassoon advised him to write plainly, and he wrote poems such as Dulce et Decorum Est and Anthem for Doomed Youth. His poems used pararhyme and graphic detail of gas and mud to show the reality of trench warfare, and he wrote that the poetry is in the pity. Owen was killed on 4 November 1918 in an attack on the Sambre-Oise canal, a week before the armistice, and only a few of his poems were published in his lifetime.",
