    "label": "C1",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Wilhelm II wanted a navy to match Britain's, and Tirpitz's Navy Laws of 1898 and 1900 planned a large battle fleet for the North Sea. Tirpitz's risk theory aimed to deter Britain from war, because a fleet strong enough to damage the Royal Navy would make Britain think twice. The German Navy League, founded in 1898, grew to over a million members and campaigned for the fleet with the support of Krupp and heavy industry. Britain responded with HMS Dreadnought in 1906, which made existing battleships obsolete and forced Germany to match its building programme. The Haldane mission of February 1912 failed because Germany wanted a British promise of neutrality, while Britain wanted slower German building. The naval race turned Britain from a possible friend into an opponent, and helped push it into the Entente with France and Russia.",
    "model_source": "haiku",
    "answerStatus": "written",
    "type": [
     "long-answer"
    ],
    "diagramRelation": "none",
