    "label": "C1",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Hungary under Rákosi was a rigidly Stalinist one-party state with show trials, the hated AVH secret police and forced collectivisation. Living standards fell because the economy was run on Soviet lines and heavy industry was favoured, which fuelled resentment among workers and peasants. Khrushchev's secret speech of February 1956 exposed Stalin's crimes and gave Hungarian reformers a precedent for criticising their own leaders. On 23 October a student demonstration in Budapest turned into a national uprising when the AVH opened fire on the crowd outside the radio building. The West did not intervene, as the Suez crisis drew attention away from Hungary and Eisenhower publicly ruled out military action. The uprising had no organised armed leadership beyond local groups, so it could not defend itself once Moscow sent a large second intervention on 4 November 1956.",
