    "label": "B1",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "The United States produced roughly half of the world's manufactured goods in 1945, and American companies used this strength to expand abroad from the 1950s. Firms such as General Motors, IBM, Coca-Cola and ITT had large overseas operations by the 1960s, and Coca-Cola's bottling networks spread across Europe, Latin America and Asia. After the Treaty of Rome of 1957 created the European Economic Community, US companies invested heavily in Western Europe to use its large single market. Critics such as Jean-Jacques Servan-Schreiber, in The American Challenge (1967), argued that these companies were gaining control of key European industries.",
