    "label": "C2",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "At home Johnson's Civil Rights Act of 1964 banned segregation in public places and discrimination in employment on grounds of race, colour, religion, sex or national origin. The Voting Rights Act of 6 August 1965 banned literacy tests and sent federal examiners to register black voters. Medicare and Medicaid were signed into law on 30 July 1965, and federal aid to schools and colleges followed in the same year. Abroad, he used the Tonkin Gulf Resolution of 7 August 1964 to escalate the war, with bombing of North Vietnam from March 1965 and combat troops in place by the end of that year. The war cost about 58,000 American lives, ended without victory in 1973, and drained money from the Great Society. His approval fell below 40 per cent by late 1967, and he did not seek re-election in 1968.",
