    "label": "C3",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Kennedy committed the United States to landing a man on the Moon before the end of the decade in a speech to Congress on 25 May 1961. The Mercury and Gemini programmes tested the flight skills and the rendezvous and docking techniques needed for the landing, and NASA chose lunar orbit rendezvous in 1962. The Saturn V rocket, developed under Wernher von Braun, and the Apollo spacecraft were built with major contractors such as Boeing and North American Aviation. Apollo 11 landed in the Sea of Tranquillity on 20 July 1969, and Armstrong and Aldrin spent about two and a half hours on the surface. The landing was watched live by about 600 million people and was a Cold War victory over the Soviet Union, whose Moon rocket had failed in every test. The Apollo Guidance Computer used integrated circuits, which made the programme a major driver of new computing technology.",
