    "model": "Between 1815 and 1870 the political structures of Ireland changed in important ways, though the basic framework of the Union, with government from Westminster and Dublin Castle, remained in place. The main changes were the admission of Catholics to Parliament and office in 1829, repeated changes to who could vote, the reform of local government and policing, the creation of new elected bodies for poor relief, and the ending of the Church of Ireland's position as the state church in 1869.\n\nIn 1815 Ireland was governed under the Act of Union of 1800. It sent 100 MPs to Westminster, and the Lord Lieutenant, Chief Secretary and Under-Secretary ran the Irish executive from Dublin Castle. Local power lay with Protestant landowners: grand juries chosen by the sheriff levied the county cess, unpaid magistrates tried minor cases, and town corporations such as Dublin were closed Protestant bodies. Catholics could vote but could not sit in Parliament or hold senior office, because MPs had to take an oath that no Catholic could swear.\n\nThe first great change came in 1829. O'Connell's Catholic Association, funded by the Catholic Rent from 1824 and organised through the parishes, and his victory in the Clare election on 5 July 1828, left Wellington and Peel fearing civil strife. The Roman Catholic Relief Act of April 1829 allowed Catholics to sit in Parliament and to hold almost all civil and military offices, although the Lord Lieutenancy and the Lord Chancellorship stayed closed to them. Yet a companion Act raised the county franchise from forty shillings to £10 and cut the Irish electorate from about 216,000 to about 37,000. Catholics gained access to power, but the mass of voters lost it.\n\nThe franchise was altered again in the following decades. The Irish Reform Act of 1832 raised Ireland's seats from 100 to 105 and gave the borough vote to £10 householders, but the electorate stayed small compared with Britain's. The Irish Franchise Act of 1850 gave the county vote to occupiers rated at £12 or more and the borough vote to those rated at £8, which greatly enlarged the county electorate. Voting stayed open until the Ballot Act of 1872, so landlords and priests could still influence it. The 1852 election, when more than 40 MPs were returned pledged to independent opposition, shows that the Tenant League could use the larger county electorate.\n\nLocal government and administration changed as well. Peel's Peace Preservation Force of 1814 and the 1822 county constabulary were merged under the Constabulary Act of 1836 into a single armed, centrally controlled Irish Constabulary, which became the Royal Irish Constabulary in 1867. Under the Whig administration of Mulgrave, Morpeth and Drummond from 1835, Catholics were appointed to office and paid magistrates were used. The Municipal Corporations (Ireland) Act of 1840 abolished most of the closed Protestant corporations and gave the rest an elected council on a £10 householder franchise, and O'Connell became Lord Mayor of Dublin in 1841. The Irish Poor Law Act of 1838 created 130 unions, each run by a board of guardians elected by ratepayers, which introduced a new form of elected local government.\n\nFinally, Gladstone's Irish Church Act of 1869 disestablished the Church of Ireland from 1 January 1871, ending its status as the state church of a mainly Catholic country. The Union's religious structure was thus changed, and the tithe that had provoked the Tithe War of the 1830s was finally gone, after the Tithe Rentcharge Act of 1838 had only shifted it onto landlords.\n\nOverall, the structures changed substantially: Catholics could sit in Parliament, local bodies became more representative, policing became centralised and the established church lost its privileged place. Yet Ireland was still governed from London and Dublin Castle, the franchise remained narrow, voting was open, and Repeal was never achieved, so the change was real but limited.",
