    "model": "The Great Famine began in September 1845, when potato blight, a fungus-like organism probably carried from North America, was first reported in Ireland. It lasted so long because the blight returned for several years, because Irish society had no cushion against the loss of the potato, and because the government's relief policy, driven by laissez-faire thinking, was inadequate and was cut back in 1847 while hunger continued.\n\nThe blight found a country that was dangerously dependent on a single crop. The population had risen from about five million around 1800 to about 8.2 million by the 1841 census, and nearly half of all holdings were under five acres after generations of subdivision. Millions of cottiers, labourers and smallholders lived on potatoes, and many grew the heavy-cropping but poor-quality lumper variety, which could not be stored from one season to the next. About 40% of houses were one-roomed mud cabins. The poor had no savings, no secure tenure and no alternative food, so any failure of the crop was bound to bring disaster.\n\nThe weakness of the countryside had deeper roots. After the French Wars ended in 1815 grain prices fell, rents fell more slowly, and indebted landlords cleared smallholders to make larger pasture farms. Rural industry such as hand spinning and weaving collapsed outside north-east Ulster. Tenants had no legal security and no compensation for improvements, which discouraged investment, and the Devon Commission of 1843-45 found extreme poverty and insecurity but led to no reform. Partial famines in 1816-17, 1822 and the 1830s had already shown how fragile the system was. The blight therefore began as a natural disaster, but the poverty it struck was man-made.\n\nThe first season, 1845, was serious but not fatal. Between a third and a half of the crop was lost and there were few deaths. Peel's Conservative government secretly bought about 100,000 pounds of Indian corn through Baring Brothers, sold it at cost through depots, set up a Relief Commission and local committees, and ran public works under the Board of Works. Peel also repealed the Corn Laws in June 1846. These measures kept mortality low, but the failure was only partial, and Peel's party split and he fell from office just as the crisis deepened.\n\nThe Famine lasted so long first because the blight kept returning. In 1846 almost the entire crop was destroyed. In 1847, Black '47, the crop was healthy but tiny, because so few seed potatoes had been planted, so hunger continued while deaths peaked. The blight returned in 1848 and failed the crop again, and 1849 brought further failure in the west and a cholera epidemic. Most victims died of typhus, relapsing fever, dysentery and scurvy rather than simple starvation, and the weakened people could not resist them. The worst hit counties were Mayo, Sligo, Galway, Roscommon, Clare and west Cork.\n\nThe response of Lord John Russell's Whig government, in office from June 1846, prolonged the crisis. Committed to laissez-faire, it refused to import food or control prices and left supply to private merchants, even though grain, cattle and butter continued to leave Irish ports. The Labouchere Act of August 1846 revived the public works with costs to be paid from local taxes, but wages were set low, food prices more than doubled, and weak and poorly clad people were left to work through the winter of 1846-47. Charles Trevelyan, the Treasury official who ran relief, saw the Famine as a providential lesson and insisted that Irish property must pay for Irish poverty.\n\nWhen the works failed, the soup kitchens of 1847 fed about three million people a day, the most effective and cheapest relief of the whole Famine. Yet they were closed by September 1847, and responsibility passed to the Poor Law under the Poor Law Extension Act of June 1847. Workhouses were overcrowded and became centres of fever, and many western unions went bankrupt. The Gregory clause refused relief to anyone holding more than a quarter of an acre, forcing smallholders to give up their land, and landlords used it, along with rising rates, to evict tenants. The Rate-in-Aid Act of 1849 then imposed a national rate to prop up the bankrupt western unions. The government treated the Famine as over by 1850, although distress continued in the west.\n\nThe result was about one million deaths and over a million emigrants, and the population fell from about 8.2 million in 1841 to about 6.6 million in 1851. The Famine began with a fungus, but it lasted about five years because the potato had been made the only food of a very poor population, because the blight recurred, and because a government wedded to laissez-faire and economy provided too little, then withdrew relief too soon. Historians such as Cormac O Grada and Christine Kinealy agree that a rich United Kingdom could have saved far more lives.",
