    "model": "Charles Kickham and James Stephens both made major contributions to Fenianism, but in different ways. Stephens built the organisation and gave the movement its structure and leadership, while Kickham gave it a voice, through his journalism and novels, that reached the rural Irish people.\n\nJames Stephens, born in Kilkenny in 1825, took part in the failed rising of 1848 at Ballingarry under William Smith O'Brien and escaped to Paris. He returned to Ireland in 1856 and travelled the country sizing up opinion. The experience of 1848, the bitterness left by the Famine and the disillusion with parliamentary politics after the collapse of the Independent Irish Party all fed his belief that only force could win independence. On 17 March 1858 he founded the Irish Republican Brotherhood in Dublin with Thomas Clarke Luby and others, as an oath-bound secret society whose aim was an Irish republic.\n\nStephens also gave the IRB its organisation. It was arranged in circles under local leaders who reported to him, and he took the title of Chief Organiser of the Irish Republic. He recruited artisans, clerks, shopkeepers and farmers' sons, and he tried to win over Irish soldiers in the British army. He worked with John O'Mahony's Fenian Brotherhood in New York, founded in the same year, which supplied money and men, including veterans of the American Civil War. This gave Fenianism a transatlantic reach that earlier movements lacked. In 1863 he launched the Irish People newspaper in Dublin to spread the movement's message.\n\nStephens's leadership failed at the decisive moment. He was arrested in November 1865 after the police raided the Irish People offices, but escaped from Richmond Prison within a fortnight with the help of warders. He then went abroad and, in 1866, failed to launch the rising he had promised, and American Fenian leaders removed him in December. His indecision and secrecy contributed to the poorly armed and informer-ridden rising of March 1867, which was quickly crushed. Even so, he is credited with building the first lasting revolutionary organisation in Irish history.\n\nCharles Kickham, born in Mullinahone, County Tipperary, in 1828, was a different kind of Fenian. An accident with gunpowder in boyhood left him almost deaf and with poor sight, so he was a writer rather than a conspirator. He wrote for The Nation, and when the Irish People began in 1863 he was one of its editors alongside Luby and John O'Leary, with Jeremiah O'Donovan Rossa as manager. The paper made the Fenian case to ordinary readers, and Kickham's contribution to it gave the movement intellectual credibility and a popular voice.\n\nKickham was arrested in 1865 and sentenced in 1866 to fourteen years' penal servitude. He was released in 1869 on grounds of ill health, after a campaign for the release of Fenian prisoners. His courage and endurance in prison won wide admiration, and his imprisonment helped to keep the amnesty movement alive. His novels gave Fenian nationalism a place in popular culture. Sally Cavanagh (1869) dealt with the eviction of a tenant family, while Knocknagow, or The Homes of Tipperary, was very popular for its portrait of rural Ireland. Through them he linked nationalism to the experience of tenant farmers and to literature. In the 1870s he was a leading member of the IRB's Supreme Council.\n\nKickham was a practising Catholic who was caught between his faith and the bishops' condemnation of Fenianism, and in that way he helped show that devout Catholics could be Fenians despite Cardinal Cullen's hostility. Historians see him as the movement's voice for rural Ireland.\n\nOverall, Stephens supplied the organisation, the republican aim and the transatlantic links, though he failed as a military leader, while Kickham supplied the literary and moral weight that made Fenianism more than a conspiracy. Together they created a movement that failed in 1867 but kept physical force republicanism alive, helped to prompt Gladstone's reforms, and left a legacy that lasted into 1916.",
