    "marks": 100,
    "model": "William Carleton, Thomas Davis and Cardinal Paul Cullen each shaped cultural or religious identity in Ireland, in very different ways. Carleton recorded the culture of the Catholic peasantry, Davis argued for a nation defined by shared history and culture rather than religion, and Cullen gave the Catholic Church a strongly Roman identity that came to define Irish Catholicism.\n\nWilliam Carleton was born in 1794 at Prillisk, County Tyrone, the son of a Catholic Irish-speaking tenant farmer. He went to Dublin about 1817 and began writing, at first for the Christian Examiner, an evangelical Protestant paper, and his early stories had an anti-Catholic edge. His Traits and Stories of the Irish Peasantry (1830, with a second series in 1833) described wakes, pilgrimages, faction fights and hedge schools, preserving the customs and beliefs of a rural society that the Famine was about to shatter. Fardorougha the Miser (1839) and The Black Prophet (1847), a novel of famine, explored the lives of the poor. His contribution was to document the culture and religious practice of ordinary people, with its violence and poverty as well as its humour. He was a witness rather than a political actor, and some Catholic nationalists resented his early writing, but historians value him as a realist and a source on pre-Famine life.\n\nThomas Davis, born in Mallow in 1814 into a Protestant family and educated at Trinity College Dublin, shaped the cultural idea of the Irish nation. In 1842 he co-founded The Nation with Charles Gavan Duffy and John Blake Dillon. His songs and ballads, including A Nation Once Again and The West's Asleep, put history and patriotism into popular verse that could be sung by people who had never read a political pamphlet. He urged the study and teaching of Irish history and the Irish language, and he edited the Library of Ireland series, which brought national history to a wide readership.\n\nDavis's most important contribution to identity was his belief that the nation should unite Catholic, Protestant and Dissenter in a shared Irish identity based on history, culture and language rather than religion. This was in contrast to O'Connell's mainly Catholic movement. He welcomed the mixed education of the Queen's Colleges, which O'Connell condemned as godless, and this was one of the disagreements that divided Young Ireland from O'Connell. He died of scarlet fever in September 1845, aged only thirty, but historians credit him with laying the cultural foundations of Irish nationalism, although his appeal to Protestants was limited.\n\nCardinal Paul Cullen, born in County Kildare in 1803, was trained in Rome and became rector of the Irish College there in 1832. Appointed Archbishop of Armagh and Apostolic Delegate in 1850, he presided at the Synod of Thurles, held from 22 August to 9 September 1850, the first national synod of the Irish Catholic Church for about two centuries. Before it each bishop ran his own diocese with little central direction, and practice varied widely. The Synod laid down uniform liturgical practice and church discipline, increased the bishops' authority over priests, regulated clerical conduct and discouraged priests from political agitation at the altar and at election meetings.\n\nThe Synod also condemned the Queen's Colleges as dangerous to faith and ordered the founding of a Catholic University, which opened in 1854 under Newman, though it attracted few students and gave no state-recognised degrees. As Archbishop of Dublin from 1852 and the first Irish cardinal in 1866, Cullen championed ultramontanism, devotion to Rome and the pope, church building, religious orders, parish missions and Catholic schools. This was the Romanisation of the Irish Church, which Emmet Larkin called the devotional revolution. He also distrusted Young Ireland and opposed the Fenians, refusing in 1861 to let the body of Terence Bellew MacManus lie in the Pro-Cathedral. Nationalists and some bishops, notably MacHale, resented his authority.\n\nTaken together, the three men show competing visions of Irish identity. Carleton preserved the folk culture of the peasantry, Davis proposed an inclusive cultural nationalism that was later taken up by the Gaelic revival, and Cullen made a disciplined, Rome-centred Catholicism the dominant religious identity of the Irish majority. Cullen's influence was probably the most lasting in the short term, since his Church shaped daily life for generations, but Davis's ideas shaped how Irish nationalists thought about the nation.",
