    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Douglas Hyde and W.B. Yeats were two of the most important figures in the cultural revival in Ireland between about 1890 and 1914. Hyde launched the movement to revive the Irish language and de-anglicise the country, while Yeats, with Lady Gregory and others, created a national literature and theatre drawing on Irish mythology and speech. Both Protestants, they gave Irish people a sense of cultural identity that fed into the nationalism of the period.\n\nHyde's first major contribution was intellectual. On 25 November 1892 he delivered The Necessity for De-Anglicising Ireland to the National Literary Society in Dublin. He argued that Ireland had been anglicised in language, names, dress, games and ideas, and that the Irish were failing both to be English and to be Irish. The lecture gave the revival its programme: Irish people should stop imitating England and return to their own language and customs. It built on the example of the GAA, founded in 1884, and within a year it led to the founding of an organisation to carry out its aims.\n\nThat organisation was the Gaelic League, founded in Dublin on 31 July 1893 by Hyde, Eoin MacNeill and others, with Hyde as its first president. It was officially non-political and non-sectarian, and welcomed Catholics, Protestants, nationalists and unionists alike. Branches ran Irish classes, and travelling teachers called timirí were sent to rural districts, and there were several hundred branches by 1904. The League organised feiseanna and, from 1897, the annual Oireachtas, and its paper An Claidheamh Soluis, begun in 1899, was edited by Patrick Pearse from 1903. Hyde himself was a scholar who collected songs and folktales, publishing Love Songs of Connacht in 1893, and he led the League for twenty-two years.\n\nThe League won real successes. The Revised Programme of 1900 allowed Irish to be taught in primary schools, and after a long campaign Irish became compulsory for matriculation to the National University, decided in 1910 and applied from 1913. St Patrick's Day became a national occasion, and many people took Irish names and used Irish on signs. The League also became a training ground for future leaders, and from about 1910 IRB members joined and pushed it towards politics, so that Hyde resigned as president in 1915 when it declared for independence.\n\nYeats's contribution was literary and theatrical. Born in Sandymount, Dublin, in 1865, he helped found the National Literary Society in 1892 with Hyde. His early poetry, such as The Wanderings of Oisin in 1889, drew on Irish mythology. In 1899 he founded the Irish Literary Theatre with Lady Gregory and Edward Martyn, its first production being his own The Countess Cathleen. In 1902 Yeats and Lady Gregory's Cathleen ni Houlihan was staged with Maud Gonne in the title role, a patriotic play that linked the revival to nationalist feeling.\n\nThe greatest institutional achievement was the Abbey Theatre, which opened on 27 December 1904 with funds from Annie Horniman, Yeats, Lady Gregory and J.M. Synge as directors. It gave Ireland a national theatre staging plays about Irish life and legend, including Synge's work, which used the speech of the Aran Islands and the west. Yeats also stood up for artistic freedom in the face of controversy: The Countess Cathleen was attacked by Catholic critics in 1899, and the Playboy riots of January 1907 showed the clash between the Abbey and nationalists who wanted a flattering picture of Irish life. In September 1913 he condemned the narrow outlook of the Catholic middle class in his poem on the Hugh Lane dispute. He won the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1923.\n\nIn short, Hyde and Yeats made complementary contributions. Hyde mobilised a mass movement to restore the language and break the habit of imitating England, and Yeats gave Ireland a literature and theatre of international standing. Between them they helped create the cultural confidence that underpinned the political change of the following years.",
