    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "James Larkin was largely effective in winning improvements for workers, though the results were uneven and the greatest test of his methods, the Dublin lockout of 1913, ended in defeat. His real achievement was to organise unskilled workers who had never had a voice, to win them real gains before 1913, and to leave behind a union and a labour movement that survived. His intransigence and the limits of his tactics, however, meant that the gains of the lockout year were won at a heavy cost.\n\nThe need for his work was obvious. Dublin had few factories and a large pool of unskilled labour. Most men were casual labourers at the docks, in carting and in building, hired by the day with no security and very low pay. Many families lived in tenements, and the 1911 census showed more than 20,000 families living in one room each. Dublin's death rate was among the highest in the United Kingdom. Craft unions, mostly branches of British bodies, organised skilled tradesmen and had little interest in the unskilled, so the workers who were worst off had no protection.\n\nLarkin first showed his effectiveness in Belfast in 1907, when, as an organiser for the National Union of Dock Labourers, he led a strike of dockers and carters. For a time it united Catholic and Protestant workers behind a common cause, which was a remarkable achievement in that city, although riots in August left two dead. When he fell out with the British union he founded the Irish Transport and General Workers' Union in Dublin in 1909, with Liberty Hall as its headquarters. This was the first mass organisation of unskilled Irish workers, and it made the new unionism a reality in Ireland.\n\nThe ITGWU won disputes in Dublin and Cork and fought a lockout in Wexford in 1911. Larkin's methods, known as Larkinism, included the sympathetic strike and the refusal to handle tainted goods from firms in dispute. They were aggressive, but they gave poorly paid men a power they had never had. He also founded the weekly Irish Worker in 1911, which carried the workers' case to the public, and he supported the founding of the Irish Labour Party in 1912, so that workers could seek representation in parliament. By the summer of 1913 his union had won gains from several Dublin firms, and he had made himself the champion of the poor.\n\nThe lockout of 1913 shows both his effectiveness and his limits. William Martin Murphy, the owner of the Irish Independent and chairman of the Dublin United Tramways Company, led the employers in an attempt to destroy the union. When the tram men struck on 26 August, the first day of Horse Show week, Larkin defied the police ban on a meeting in Sackville Street, appearing in disguise on the balcony of the Imperial Hotel on 31 August. The police baton charge that followed, known as Bloody Sunday, injured hundreds and turned public opinion towards the workers. Larkin held about 20,000 workers together for five months against 400 employers who demanded that every worker sign the Document, renouncing the union. The Askwith inquiry condemned the Document, George Russell published his Open Letter to the Masters of Dublin, and food ships such as the Hare brought supplies from British trade unions. Larkin also survived a sentence of seven months for sedition, being released after a public campaign in November 1913.\n\nYet by any practical test the lockout failed. The British TUC refused the sympathetic action that Larkin wanted at its special congress in December 1913, and hunger drove the men back in January 1914, many returning without recognition for the union. Some were blacklisted, and employers kept the right to bar ITGWU members in some firms. Historians also argue that Larkin's temper and his extension of the dispute into a general conflict made compromise harder. The Save the Kiddies scheme was condemned by Archbishop Walsh, and the Church opposed his tactics. He left for the United States in October 1914, so the union was led by Connolly, who built on Larkin's work through the Irish Citizen Army.\n\nOn balance, Larkin was highly effective as an organiser and an inspirer, because he created a union for the unskilled, won gains before 1913 and made labour a political force in Ireland, while the ITGWU survived and grew during the First World War. He was less effective as a negotiator, and the lockout ended in defeat. His long-term effect on workers' lives was greater than his short-term results.",
