    "label": "1",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Patrick Pearse, Arthur Griffith and Countess Markievicz each made a major contribution to Irish affairs between about 1905 and 1922. Griffith supplied a political programme and a party, Pearse supplied the ideology and the leadership of the 1916 Rising, and Markievicz showed what women could do in revolutionary politics and government. Together they helped to move Irish nationalism from cultural revival to a republican revolution.\n\nPearse's first contribution was in cultural nationalism and education. Born in Dublin in 1879, he joined the Gaelic League in 1896 and edited its paper An Claidheamh Soluis from 1903. In 1908 he founded St Enda's, a bilingual school, and in The Murder Machine he attacked the state education system. This work helped to create a generation committed to Irish identity and ready for more radical action.\n\nHis second and greater contribution was the Rising. He joined the IRB in 1913, became Director of Military Organisation of the Irish Volunteers and sat on the Military Council of 1915. At the funeral of O'Donovan Rossa on 1 August 1915 he gave the graveside oration that Ireland unfree would never be at peace, a speech that is still remembered. On Easter Monday 1916 he was President and Commandant-General in the GPO and read the Proclamation of the Irish Republic, which guaranteed religious and civil liberty and equal rights. He ordered the surrender on 29 April and was executed on 3 May at the age of 36. The Rising was a military failure, but his execution and those of his comrades turned opinion, and he was honoured as a martyr by the autumn. Critics such as Conor Cruise O'Brien have condemned the cult of sacrifice that his writings encouraged, but his contribution to the republican tradition was beyond doubt.\n\nArthur Griffith made his contribution mainly through politics and ideas. In 1905 he founded Sinn Féin, which stood for economic self-reliance, abstention from Westminster and a dual monarchy of Britain and Ireland on the Austro-Hungarian model. This policy of self-reliance, set against the Irish Parliamentary Party's reliance on Westminster, gave the nationalist movement an alternative strategy. Although Sinn Féin was small before 1916, the British called the Rising the Sinn Féin rebellion, and in 1917 the party became the vehicle for separatists of every kind. At the Ard Fheis of October 1917 Griffith stood aside for de Valera as president, which helped hold the movement together. He was arrested in the German Plot of 1918, and he served in the first Dáil as Minister for Home Affairs and later Foreign Affairs.\n\nGriffith's last contribution was in the Treaty negotiations. He led the Irish delegation in London in 1921 and signed the Treaty in December, accepting dominion status and the Boundary Commission as the best available terms. He supported it in the Dáil and was elected President of Dáil Éireann in January 1922, and he died in August 1922, before the Free State's first government was formed. He gave the new state its leadership at a crucial moment, though his acceptance of the settlement split the movement he had founded.\n\nConstance Markievicz made her contribution in several fields. Born Constance Gore-Booth and raised at Lissadell, she joined Inghinidhe na hÉireann and Sinn Féin in 1908, and in 1909 helped Bulmer Hobson found Fianna Éireann, the republican youth organisation. She joined the Irish Citizen Army and in the 1913 Lockout ran the soup kitchen at Liberty Hall. In 1916 she was second-in-command to Michael Mallin at St Stephen's Green and the College of Surgeons. Her death sentence was commuted because of her sex, and she was released in the amnesty of 1917.\n\nShe then achieved a series of firsts. In December 1918, in Holloway prison, she won St Patrick's, Dublin, and became the first woman elected to the House of Commons, though she did not take her seat. From April 1919 she was Minister for Labour in the Dáil ministry, the only woman in the cabinet and one of the first women in Europe to hold cabinet rank. Her work was limited by long periods in jail, and she opposed the Treaty, but she opened political life to women.\n\nTaken together, all three left lasting marks. Pearse's contribution was the greatest in its immediate effect, because the Rising and his execution changed Irish politics, while Griffith provided the party and the practical statesmanship that carried the movement to the Treaty. Markievicz was a pioneer who showed that women could fight, be elected and govern. Each contributed in a different way to the achievement of independence.",
