    "label": "2",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The Irish government of 1922 to 1932 consolidated democracy by defeating armed opposition, building the institutions of a working state, asserting civilian control over the army and, above all, by handing power over peacefully in 1932. W.T. Cosgrave's Cumann na nGaedheal governments were cautious and conservative, but they ensured that the ballot box rather than the gun became the accepted route to power.\n\nThe first task was to win the Civil War and to establish the authority of the elected government. After the Treaty split, the National Army, using British artillery, shelled the Four Courts in June 1922 and then took the towns in the summer, forcing the anti-Treaty IRA into guerrilla warfare. After the deaths of Griffith and Collins in August 1922, Cosgrave took charge and the government used the Army Emergency Powers Resolution to set up military courts. About 80 anti-Treaty prisoners were executed, including Erskine Childers and, in reprisal for the killing of Seán Hales, Rory O'Connor and three others in December 1922. These measures were harsh and left lasting bitterness, but they showed that the state would not be overthrown by force, and Frank Aiken's order to dump arms in May 1923 ended the war. Democracy had survived its first test.\n\nThe government then built the institutions of a stable state. The Constitution of the Irish Free State, in force from December 1922, provided for a two-chamber Oireachtas, proportional representation and an Executive Council responsible to the Dáil. The Civic Guard, renamed the Garda Síochána in 1923, was an unarmed force under Eoin O'Duffy, and it absorbed the Dublin Metropolitan Police in 1925, giving the people a police force that was accepted rather than feared. The Courts of Justice Act of 1924 created a new system of District, Circuit, High and Supreme Courts, replacing both the British and the republican courts. The civil service and local government were kept largely as they had been, which gave the new state continuity and efficiency, and the Ministers and Secretaries Act of 1924 set up the government departments. A working administration and an independent legal system gave the state the authority of an established government.\n\nThe government also had to defend democratic control against threats from within the state's own forces. The Army Mutiny of March 1924, led by former IRA and IRB officers such as Liam Tobin and Charles Dalton who objected to demobilisation, was ended when the Army Council and the Minister for Defence, Richard Mulcahy, resigned and Kevin O'Higgins reorganised the army. This established firm civil control over the military, something that many new states have failed to achieve. In the same way, the government survived the Boundary Commission crisis of November 1925, when the leak of its likely findings to the Morning Post led to the resignation of Eoin MacNeill. The Tripartite Agreement of December 1925 left the border unchanged but removed the Free State's share of the imperial debt, and the crisis did not shake the government.\n\nThe assassination of Kevin O'Higgins on 10 July 1927 was the most severe test, and the response ensured that republicans had to enter parliamentary politics. The government passed the Public Safety Act of 1927 and the Electoral Amendment Act, which required candidates to promise to take their seats and so the oath. De Valera had already left Sinn Féin in March 1926 and founded Fianna Fáil in May 1926, accepting the oath as an empty formula. Fianna Fáil won 44 seats in June 1927 and entered the Dáil in August 1927. After this the largest anti-Treaty party was working inside the constitutional system, and in September 1927 Cumann na nGaedheal won 62 seats to Fianna Fáil's 57 in a fiercely contested but peaceful election.\n\nThe government also showed that the state could deliver. The Land Act of 1923 completed the transfer of land to tenants, the Free State joined the League of Nations in 1923 and opened a legation in Washington in 1924, and the government helped to win wider independence through the Imperial Conference of 1926 and the Statute of Westminster of 1931. The Shannon Scheme, approved in 1925 and built by Siemens-Schuckert, led to the creation of the Electricity Supply Board in 1927 and the opening of Ardnacrusha in 1929. These achievements gave citizens reason to accept the state, although the government's low spending did little for urban poverty.\n\nThe decisive proof came in 1932. After the Depression cost Cumann na nGaedheal support, Fianna Fáil won 72 seats to 57 in the February election, and de Valera was elected President of the Executive Council on 9 March with Labour's votes. Cosgrave handed over power peacefully, and the army and police accepted the change. In a state that had fought a civil war only nine years before, the transfer of power to former opponents was the clearest sign that democracy had been consolidated, and the credit belongs largely to the Cosgrave government, despite the repression with which it began.",
