    "model": "The Irish who went to Britain between 1920 and 1966 faced a combination of economic insecurity, poor housing, social prejudice and isolation. Britain was easy to reach and offered work, but most emigrants arrived young and unskilled, and they filled the hardest and least secure jobs.\n\nThe first challenge was the kind of work available. After American quotas in 1921 and 1924, and the Great Depression after 1929, Britain became the main destination, and the Irish moved there in large numbers in the 1930s. During the Emergency of 1939 to 1945 well over 100,000 people from the Irish state went to work in British war factories, building and farms, while tens of thousands joined the British forces. After 1945 reconstruction created a huge demand for labour, and Irish men worked as navvies on roads, building sites and power stations. Many arrived with no training, so they were confined to heavy manual labour.\n\nThe terms of that work added to their difficulties. Navvy employment was often arranged through subcontractors who paid cash in hand, which left workers without insurance or job security. Long hours and hard drinking were common in the navvy camps, and the work could end suddenly. Irish women had somewhat better prospects, as many trained as nurses in the new National Health Service of 1948 or worked in factories and offices, but they too were mostly in low-paid and subordinate roles.\n\nHousing was a second major problem. Many young emigrants lived in lodging houses or camps, and they clustered in areas such as Kilburn, Camden Town and Cricklewood in London, and in Birmingham, Coventry, Luton, Manchester and Liverpool. Overcrowded and poor accommodation was typical of the early years of a stay. The notices reading No Irish, widely remembered from lodging-house windows in the 1950s and 1960s, symbolised the difficulty of finding a home, although historians disagree about how common they were.\n\nPrejudice and cultural isolation were a third challenge. The Irish remained a visible Catholic working-class minority in a mostly Protestant country, and old stereotypes of the Irish as heavy drinkers and unskilled labourers persisted. Homesickness was strong, and many saw their stay as temporary. The Gaeltacht emigrant tended to lose the Irish language within a generation, and Domhnall Mac Amhlaigh's diary, An Irish Navvy, written about his years from 1951 on building sites in Northampton, vividly shows the mixture of hard work, loneliness, homesickness and both welcome and prejudice that the Irish met.\n\nAgainst these difficulties the Irish had some real legal advantages and support. The Ireland Act of 1949 confirmed that Irish citizens were not aliens in the United Kingdom and could live, work and vote there freely, and the Common Travel Area allowed free movement, while Irish people were exempt from the Commonwealth Immigrants Act of 1962. Catholic chaplaincies, the Legion of Mary and Irish centres helped newcomers to find lodgings and work, and dance halls such as the Galtymore in Cricklewood and the Gresham on the Holloway Road gave emigrants a social world of their own. Enda Delaney has argued that the emigrants kept strong ties with home while integrating into British working life.\n\nOverall, the main challenges were economic, as the Irish took the lowest rungs of the labour market, and social, in the form of poor lodgings, prejudice and isolation. These were severe, especially for the generation that left in the 1950s, when about 400,000 left the state, mostly for Britain. Yet open access and community networks meant that the Irish in Britain could cope and gradually settle, which explains why many stayed for life.",
