    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Irish missionaries were remarkably successful in Africa and Asia in building schools, hospitals and churches, and in creating local clergy, especially in Nigeria. Their success was uneven, however, since their China mission ended in expulsion and their work in Africa was criticised as paternalistic and culturally damaging.\n\nThe scale of the effort was itself a sign of success. After the Famine, Archbishop Paul Cullen's reforms, which Emmet Larkin called the devotional revolution, produced more priests, brothers and nuns than Ireland needed, and Maynooth and other seminaries sent the surplus abroad. The Maynooth Mission to China was founded in 1916 and became the Columbans, while the Kiltegan Fathers, the SMA, the Holy Ghost Fathers and the Loreto and Presentation sisters also worked in Africa and Asia. The idea of an Irish spiritual empire gave this effort a national meaning, and parishes at home raised money and vocations in support of it.\n\nThe Holy Ghost mission to south-eastern Nigeria is the best example of success. The Spiritans began work at Onitsha in 1885, and Joseph Shanahan, who arrived in 1902, made the school the main instrument of conversion, building village schools staffed by catechists. After 1945 the mission opened parishes, schools, hospitals and training colleges across the region. The Archdiocese of Onitsha was created in 1950, and mission schools became the main route to education, training teachers who created a growing educated class. The Catholic population grew strongly, and local vocations followed. Michael Tansi was ordained in 1937 and beatified in 1998, and in 1965 Francis Arinze, aged 32, became auxiliary bishop of Onitsha.\n\nMedical work was another solid achievement. Mary Martin, who went to Nigeria in the 1930s, founded the Medical Missionaries of Mary in 1937 after a Vatican instruction of 1936 allowed sisters to practise obstetrics and surgery. Her order, with its mother house at Drogheda, trained doctors, nurses and midwives and set up hospitals and clinics where few medical services existed. The Holy Rosary Sisters, founded by Shanahan in 1924, also taught and nursed. Such institutions gave rural people care that government services did not, and they outlasted the colonial era.\n\nIn Asia the picture was more mixed. Bishop Edward Galvin of Cork founded the Maynooth Mission to China with John Blowick, and he led the mission in Hanyang, where it ran churches, schools, orphanages and clinics through civil conflict and the Japanese invasion. Yet few Chinese priests were ordained before the 1940s, and after the Communist takeover of 1949 the missionaries were restricted, and Galvin was tried and expelled in 1952. Chinese authorities denounced the missionaries as agents of foreign influence, so the long work in China ended abruptly.\n\nThere were also real limits in Africa. The mission relied on colonial grants-in-aid for its schools, which tied it to government standards and to the colonial state. Critics said the mission was paternalistic because Irish missionaries held leadership long after local people were qualified for it, and that its education weakened local culture and languages. Rivalry with Methodist and Anglican missions over schools and converts also divided communities. The Biafran war from 1967 to 1970 damaged or closed many mission stations, and afterwards many foreign missionaries left the east and the state took over the schools, although Holy Ghost Father Anthony Byrne helped organise the relief airlift and the war led to the founding of Concern in 1968.\n\nIn conclusion, Irish missionaries were most successful in Nigeria, where schools, hospitals and a Nigerian-led church outlasted colonial rule, but less so in China, where their work was ended by the Communist government. Historians stress both the practical contribution to health and education and the cultural and political costs, and that balanced verdict suggests the missionaries were highly successful by their own aims, though not without lasting criticism.",
