    "label": "1",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Between 1949 and 1989 the state moved from a passive role to active provision in education, health and social welfare. The decisive changes came in the 1960s, when economic planning created a demand for skills and the state widened access to schooling, built a national structure of health services and increased welfare payments, though inequalities and church influence remained.\n\nBefore the 1960s the state left most second-level education to the churches and to parents who could pay. Most children left school at 14 with only a primary education, secondary schools charged fees and were mostly owned by religious orders, and vocational schools set up under the 1930 Vocational Education Act had lower status. Planning for growth after 1958 raised the question of whether the country was educating enough people with the skills it needed. Patrick Hillery, Minister for Education, commissioned a survey with OECD help, and Investment in Education, published in 1965, showed wide differences in participation by class and region and a shortage of trained teachers and technical skills. It argued that education was an investment in growth.\n\nTwo ministers turned the findings into reforms. Hillery announced comprehensive schools in 1963, offering academic and practical subjects to pupils of all abilities, and the first opened in 1966 at places such as Carraroe in Galway and Cootehill in Cavan. Donogh O'Malley, who became Minister for Education in July 1966, announced on 10 September 1966 that free second-level education would begin the following year. The scheme started in September 1967, accompanied by free school transport that allowed rural pupils to reach a school, and enrolment rose steeply. The Primary Certificate was abolished in 1967 and the school-leaving age rose from 14 to 15 in 1972. Community schools, usually formed by merging a secondary and a vocational school, appeared from the early 1970s, and Regional Technical Colleges opened from 1970 in Carlow, Dundalk, Athlone and Waterford. At third level the Higher Education Authority was set up in 1968, a grants scheme helped poorer students, and the National Institute for Higher Education opened in Limerick in 1972. A new primary curriculum in 1971 replaced memorisation with an emphasis on the needs and interests of the child.\n\nHealth policy moved gradually from a means-tested service towards wider provision. The Health Act 1953 replaced the Mother and Child Scheme with means-tested maternity and child services and set out eligibility for public hospital care by income. The Voluntary Health Insurance Board was set up in 1957 to insure those who earned too much for free hospital care. The Fitzgerald Report of 1968 recommended fewer, larger regional hospitals, which proved controversial locally. The Health Act 1970 created eight regional health boards, which began work in 1971 and took over the functions of the old county health authorities, while Comhairle na nOspidéal, set up in 1972, regulated the appointment of consultants. Medical cards gave lower-income families free GP care and medicines.\n\nSocial welfare grew in range and level, especially after 1965. The Social Welfare Act 1952 gathered existing social insurance schemes into a single system covering unemployment, illness, widowhood and old age, and contributory old age pensions were introduced in 1961. Free travel for pensioners came in 1967 and an electricity allowance in 1968. Newer groups were recognised: deserted wife's benefit was introduced in 1970 and an unmarried mother's allowance in 1973, which treated mothers with children outside marriage as claimants in their own right. Pay-related benefit in 1974 tied payments for unemployed and sick workers to previous earnings. Welfare spending rose as a share of national income in the 1970s, partly because unemployment grew after the oil crises.\n\nThere were limits. The churches kept control of management in most schools, and the hierarchy was wary of comprehensive and community schools, so bishops were given places on boards of management. Free fees did not remove the cost of books, uniforms and travel, so poorer families still faced barriers, and rural areas in the west had fewer places. The VHI and medical card system left health care unequal.\n\nOverall, these decades saw a major change of direction. Free post-primary education widened access, the health boards gave the country a national structure, and welfare protected more families, including lone mothers. Historians note that the reforms were driven as much by economic need as by social ideals and that the middle classes benefited first, but they accept that Ireland in 1989 was a very different society in the provision made for its citizens.",
