    "label": "2",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Membership of the EEC affected the Republic of Ireland far more than membership of the United Nations. The UN, which Ireland joined in 1955, gave a small state a voice, a reputation and a role in peacekeeping, and it shaped Irish foreign policy and self-image. The EEC, which Ireland joined in 1973, changed the economy, agriculture, industry, the law and even social attitudes in ways that touched ordinary people's daily lives.\n\nIreland applied to join the UN in 1946 but the Soviet Union vetoed it, citing Irish neutrality in the Second World War and the absence of diplomatic relations. The deadlock ended in December 1955, when a package deal allowed sixteen states, including Italy, Spain and Austria, to enter together. Membership gave Ireland a platform on world affairs and a way of showing that it was independent of Britain. It was a recognition of statehood more than a change in the way the country was run.\n\nWithin the UN Ireland pursued an independent line, largely through Frank Aiken, Minister for External Affairs for most of 1957 to 1969. From 1957 Ireland refused to support the American-led move to block debate on the seat of China, and in 1959 it helped sponsor a resolution criticising the suppression of human rights in Tibet. Aiken's greatest achievement was in nuclear non-proliferation. Irish resolutions from 1958 led to Resolution 1665 in 1961, and Ireland was the first state to sign the Non-Proliferation Treaty in 1968. Frederick Boland, Ireland's representative, was elected President of the General Assembly in 1960, a remarkable honour for a state that had joined only five years earlier. These successes raised Ireland's prestige, but they brought no economic benefit to the Irish people.\n\nPeacekeeping was the most tangible effect of the UN. Irish troops served in Lebanon from 1958, in the Congo from 1960, in Cyprus from 1964 and again in Lebanon from 1978. In the Congo nine soldiers were killed at Niemba in November 1960, and 155 men were besieged at Jadotville in 1961. About 6,000 Irish soldiers served there and twenty-six died. This service gave the army a role, fed national pride and linked neutrality to practical international service. Yet it involved a small number of people and left the structure of Irish society unchanged.\n\nThe EEC had a much deeper effect, beginning with agriculture. After applications in 1961 and 1967, both blocked by de Gaulle's vetoes of British entry, Ireland signed the Treaty of Accession in January 1972. A referendum in May 1972 approved entry by about 83 per cent. Under the Common Agricultural Policy farmers received guaranteed prices for beef, milk and cereals, and farm incomes rose sharply in the early years. Land values rose and farmers invested in machinery and drainage. The gains were uneven, since small western farms benefited least, and milk quotas in 1984 showed that guaranteed prices also encouraged overproduction.\n\nMembership also transformed industry and the wider economy. Foreign firms, particularly American ones in electronics and pharmaceuticals, set up in Ireland to sell into the EEC, attracted by IDA grants and low taxes. Protected industries such as clothing, textiles and footwear lost their tariff protection and many firms closed. Exports to Britain fell as a share of the total, which reduced dependence on one market. The Regional and Social Funds paid for roads, water schemes and training, and a reform in 1988 doubled the structural funds, with Ireland a major beneficiary. Membership led Ireland to join the European Monetary System in 1979 and break the link with sterling.\n\nThe EEC also changed law and society. Directives on equal pay in 1975 and equal treatment in 1976 forced Irish law to change, and the marriage bar in the public service was lifted in 1973. Irish citizens gained the right to work in other member states, and EEC law took precedence over Irish law. The cost to sovereignty was real, as the Crotty case of 1987 showed when the Supreme Court ruled that the Single European Act required a constitutional amendment.\n\nOverall, the UN gave Ireland a respected international voice and a lasting part of its identity, but its effect was mainly on foreign policy and national self-image. The EEC changed the economy, the farm, the factory, the law and the position of women, and it reduced dependence on Britain. For that reason membership of the EEC affected the Republic of Ireland considerably more.",
