    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Charles Haughey and Garret FitzGerald dominated Irish politics in the 1980s, alternating as Taoiseach through three elections in eighteen months. Haughey contributed energy, a nationalist outlook and, eventually, financial discipline, though his career was marked by scandal. FitzGerald contributed a vision of a modern, pluralist Ireland and a serious approach to Northern Ireland, but his achievements were limited by referendums and by a weak economic record.\n\nHaughey was born in Castlebar in 1925, trained as an accountant, married a daughter of Seán Lemass and became very wealthy. He served as Minister for Justice, Agriculture and, from 1966 to 1970, Finance. His rise was interrupted by the Arms Crisis of 1970, when Jack Lynch dismissed him over a plan to import arms for Northern nationalists. He was charged with conspiracy but was acquitted in October 1970. The affair left a lasting division in Fianna Fáil, but Haughey spent the 1970s rebuilding support and defeated George Colley for the leadership in December 1979 by 44 votes to 38.\n\nAs Taoiseach, Haughey's early record was poor. On 9 January 1980 he told the country on television that it was living beyond its means, yet spending and borrowing continued to rise. His governments of 1981 and 1982 were unstable and the 1982 government was overshadowed by scandals, including phone-tapping and the arrest of Malcolm MacArthur in the apartment of the Attorney General. Conor Cruise O'Brien summed these up with the acronym GUBU. His government fell in November 1982 when the Workers' Party and Tony Gregory withdrew support, although Gregory had won investment for inner-city Dublin as the price of his vote.\n\nHaughey's most important contribution came after he returned to power in March 1987. Leading a minority government, he cut public spending sharply under Ray MacSharry as Minister for Finance, helped by Fine Gael's Tallaght Strategy under Alan Dukes. In October 1987 the Programme for National Recovery was agreed with unions, employers and farmers, holding pay increases low in return for a pledge to reduce the deficit. This helped to bring the national debt under control, which had risen above 100 per cent of national income, and laid the base for recovery in the 1990s. Later tribunals found that he had received large sums from businessmen, so historians see him as gifted but flawed.\n\nGarret FitzGerald, born in 1926, was the son of Desmond FitzGerald, a minister in the first Free State government. He worked as an economist and was Minister for Foreign Affairs from 1973 to 1977 before becoming leader of Fine Gael. He was Taoiseach from June 1981 to March 1982 and from December 1982 to March 1987. In 1981 he announced a constitutional crusade to make the Republic more pluralist, partly to make it more attractive to Northern unionists. The results were limited. The Eighth Amendment, banning abortion, was passed in 1983 by about two to one, in a form he had not wished, and the divorce referendum of 1986 was defeated by a similar margin. In 1985, however, his government liberalised contraception, allowing sale without prescription to people over 18.\n\nFitzGerald's greatest contribution was on Northern Ireland. He set up the New Ireland Forum in 1983, which brought together nationalist parties from the Republic and the SDLP, and in 1985 he negotiated the Anglo-Irish Agreement with Margaret Thatcher. This gave the Irish government a formal consultative role in Northern affairs and was a landmark in Anglo-Irish relations. His economic record was weaker: unemployment rose from about 7 per cent in 1979 to over 15 per cent in the mid-1980s, the debt kept growing, emigration returned and Labour left his government in January 1987 over spending cuts.\n\nIn conclusion, both men left a mixed legacy. Haughey was a forceful leader whose later financial discipline and social partnership prepared recovery, yet he was tainted by scandal and extravagance earlier on. FitzGerald put pluralism and the Northern question on the agenda and showed integrity, but could not deliver social change in referendums or economic revival. Historians generally judge that FitzGerald contributed more to ideas and relations with Britain, and Haughey more to the eventual recovery of the public finances.",
