    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "T.K. Whitaker, Gay Byrne and Mary Robinson each helped to change attitudes in the Republic between the late 1950s and the 1980s, but in different ways. Whitaker altered attitudes about the economy and the role of the state, Byrne opened up public discussion of subjects that had been taboo and challenged the authority of the church, and Robinson used law and politics to challenge discriminatory attitudes towards women and minorities. Together they helped to move Ireland from an inward-looking, pessimistic society towards a more open one.\n\nWhitaker was born in Rostrevor in 1916 and became Secretary of the Department of Finance in 1956. By the 1950s protection was widely seen to have failed, emigration was about 400,000 for the decade and the population was falling. In 1958 he published Economic Development, which argued that protection had produced neither growth nor jobs and that emigration was a symptom of a stagnant economy. It urged a shift towards productive investment, the welcoming of foreign capital and export-led growth. The government accepted the analysis and published the First Programme for Economic Expansion in November 1958. This was a change of attitude, as the Department of Finance had traditionally resisted spending, and it replaced the pessimism of the 1950s with confidence in planning and growth.\n\nThe results supported that change of mind. Output grew by about 4 per cent a year from 1958 to 1963, double the target, and emigration fell sharply, to about 80,000 in 1961 to 1966. The population rose in the 1966 census for the first time in decades. Whitaker's work led to the end of the Control of Manufactures Acts, the opening to foreign investment and, ultimately, to an outlook that looked towards free trade and the EEC. Historians stress that Lemass supplied the political will and the world economy was booming, yet Whitaker is seen as the architect of the new approach.\n\nGay Byrne presented The Late Late Show from its start in July 1962 until 1999, and it became the most important forum for public debate in the country. Television had arrived in 1961 and brought viewers a window on the wider world. The show discussed sex, marriage, contraception, unmarried mothers and the church, subjects rarely spoken of in public, in an open-minded, humorous but probing style. In 1966 an item for married couples, in which a wife said she had not worn a nightdress on her honeymoon, caused an uproar. Bishop Thomas Ryan of Clonfert condemned the programme, but many viewers defended Byrne. The row showed that television could provoke arguments about morality and the limits of church authority. His morning radio programme from 1973 extended his audience to every part of the country, and people in rural areas, once isolated, could hear the same debates as people in the cities.\n\nMary Robinson, born in Ballina in 1944, became Reid Professor of Law at Trinity at the age of 25 and a senator for Dublin University in 1969. She introduced private members' bills in the early 1970s to liberalise contraception law, which the Seanad refused to debate, and later sought reform of the law on homosexuality. She campaigned for women to serve on juries, enacted in the Juries Act 1976. As a barrister she took cases against the State, including a tax case brought by a married couple that was won in 1980, and the case of David Norris against the law criminalising homosexual acts. Conservatives and the church attacked her views, yet her persistence made such reforms seem reasonable and created a legal and political basis for the changes of the 1990s, including her election as President in 1990.\n\nIn conclusion, each of the three contributed to a real change in outlook. Whitaker changed attitudes to economic policy, Byrne changed attitudes to discussion of morality and the church, and Robinson changed attitudes to rights and equality. Their influence had limits: the divorce referendum of 1986 was lost and the church kept great influence. Even so, they helped to prepare a more open and confident Ireland.",
