    "model": "Robert Owen's aims at New Lanark were to prove that industrial workers could be reformed by improving their living and working conditions, and to show that humane treatment was compatible with profit. To a considerable extent he succeeded, since New Lanark stayed profitable, won international fame and influenced legislation. But his success depended on his personal control, and his wider schemes and his relations with his partners ended in failure.\n\nOwen was born in Newtown, Montgomeryshire, in 1771 and learned the cotton trade in Manchester, where he was managing a mill by about nineteen. He became a partner in the Chorlton Twist Company, and in 1799 he married Caroline Dale, daughter of David Dale, the owner of the New Lanark mills on the Clyde. When the Chorlton partners bought New Lanark, Owen took over as manager in January 1800. His outlook was shaped by Enlightenment environmentalism, the belief that character is formed by surroundings. His aim was therefore to change the environment in order to change the people, and he later set this out in A New View of Society (1813-16).\n\nThe conditions he found made the need clear. The village housed about 2,000 people, including some 500 pauper children from the workhouses of Edinburgh and Glasgow. The mill day was about thirteen hours, children of five or six worked before attending evening classes when they were exhausted, houses were dirty and overcrowded, and Owen described the workforce as marked by drunkenness, theft and distrust. He blamed these conditions on the environment, not on the people. His aims were to end child labour, shorten hours, improve housing and sanitation, replace punishment with reward and educate both children and adults, while still making the mills pay.\n\nHe achieved a great deal in the mills. He stopped taking pauper children as apprentices and raised the minimum working age so that children under ten no longer worked there. By 1816 he had cut the day to ten and three-quarter hours, arguing that output did not fall. During the American embargo of 1806 he kept paying wages to idle workers for about four months, which won their loyalty. He opposed physical punishment and used the Silent Monitor, a block of wood with four coloured sides, turned each day to show a worker's conduct, so that behaviour was recorded without shouting or beating. In the village he rebuilt housing, cleaned the streets, opened a store selling good goods at close to cost price and controlled the sale of drink.\n\nHis educational work was the most original part. The infant school, opened in 1816, took children from about one or two to six and used songs, games and practical activities rather than rote learning, which made it one of the earliest of its kind in Britain. The Institute for the Formation of Character, also opened in 1816, provided adult classes, lectures, music and dancing. Thousands of visitors, including statesmen and royalty, came in the 1810s and 1820s to see New Lanark, and Owen's profits, which he said were maintained, were used to show that welfare was not a drain on the business. In 1813 he and new backers, including Jeremy Bentham and William Allen, bought out the earlier partners and agreed to a fixed 5 per cent return, leaving the rest of the profit for the community.\n\nOwen also tried to spread his model through law. He gave evidence to a parliamentary committee in 1816 and campaigned for restrictions on child labour, which contributed to the Cotton Mills and Factories Act of 1819. That Act barred children under nine from cotton mills and limited older children to twelve hours a day, but it was weaker than Owen wanted, since it had no inspectors and depended on magistrates, and it was poorly enforced. His plans for self-supporting co-operative villages, presented in 1817 and 1820, won little backing, and in 1817 he damaged his reputation by denouncing all organised religion at a public meeting.\n\nThe limits of his success were clear. His Quaker partner William Allen objected to the cost of his schemes, to the absence of religious teaching and to the dancing and music, and these quarrels led Owen to leave for the United States in 1824, finally ending his connection with New Lanark about 1829. New Harmony, which he founded in Indiana in 1825, collapsed within a few years. New Lanark itself was not widely copied, because few owners would accept the control required and most mills depended on low wages and long hours. Critics said it relied on his personal authority, and historians still debate whether it was true welfare or paternalism.\n\nOn balance, New Lanark was a significant success as an experiment in industrial welfare. It proved that shorter hours, schooling and decent housing could go with profit, it helped to shape the 1819 Act and the infant school movement, and it inspired the co-operative movement, including the Rochdale Pioneers of 1844. It failed, however, to become a general model, and Owen's wider schemes failed, so his aims were achieved at New Lanark but not beyond it.",
