    "label": "2",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Marx, Bakunin and O'Connor all wanted to transform the society produced by industrial capitalism and the narrow political order of their day, but they sought change by very different means. Marx relied on theory, organisation and the eventual revolution of the working class, Bakunin on the destruction of the state itself, and O'Connor on a mass campaign for the vote within the British political system.\n\nKarl Marx sought change first by providing a theory of why it must come. Born in Trier in 1818, he became editor of the Rheinische Zeitung in Cologne in 1842, was expelled from Paris in 1845, and with Friedrich Engels wrote the Communist Manifesto, published in London in February 1848. It declared that all history was a history of class struggles and ended with a call to working men of all countries to unite. His theory of historical materialism held that economic relations and class conflict drive historical change, and that the working class would overthrow capitalism. In this way he tried to give workers confidence that change was historically inevitable and to give them a clear programme.\n\nMarx also tried to bring about change through direct involvement and organisation. During the revolutions of 1848 he edited the Neue Rheinische Zeitung in Cologne, which was closed in 1849 when he was expelled from Prussia. He then settled in London, where he lived until his death in 1883. There he analysed capitalism in the first volume of Das Kapital in 1867, explaining how capitalists profit from the surplus value produced by labour. In 1864 he helped to found the International Workingmen's Association in London, wrote its inaugural address and became the dominant figure on its General Council. The International was an attempt to link workers across national borders. Yet his predictions of revolution in advanced capitalist countries were not fulfilled in his lifetime, and his followers only became a mass movement after his death.\n\nMikhail Bakunin sought change by a much more violent and immediate route. Born into a noble family in Russia in 1814, he left the army and became a revolutionary in the 1840s. He took part in the Slav Congress at Prague in 1848 and in the Dresden rising of May 1849, for which he was sentenced to death, a sentence commuted to life imprisonment. He was handed over to Austria and then to Russia in 1851, held in the Peter and Paul Fortress, exiled to Siberia in 1857 and escaped in 1861 by way of Japan and the United States to London. His career shows a belief in insurrection and conspiracy as the means of change.\n\nBakunin's aim was also different from that of Marx. He argued for the abolition of the state and of all authority, a position that became known as anarchism, and he rejected Marx's belief that a workers' state was needed after the revolution. This quarrel split the First International, and Bakunin was expelled at the Hague Congress in 1872, after which the General Council moved to New York. He died in 1876. Governments saw him as a dangerous conspirator, and his influence on the mass labour movement was less than that of Marx, though he remains a major figure in the anarchist tradition.\n\nFeargus O'Connor, born in Co. Cork in 1796, worked for change through peaceful agitation for democratic reform. A barrister and a supporter of Daniel O'Connell, he was elected MP for Cork in 1832 and became the leading figure in Chartism, the first mass working-class political movement in Britain. Its programme was the People's Charter of 1838, which demanded universal male suffrage, secret ballots, annual parliaments, equal electoral districts, no property qualification for MPs and payment of MPs. In 1837 he founded the Northern Star in Leeds, which became the most widely read radical newspaper in Britain and held the movement together. He took part in the petitions of 1839, 1842 and 1848, and as a leader of the National Charter Association he helped to organise local meetings and campaigns across the country.\n\nO'Connor also tried practical schemes and mass demonstrations. He led the National Land Company, founded in 1845, which bought estates and let subscribers draw lots for smallholdings that would also give them a vote in the counties, but it collapsed after financial mismanagement and was wound up in 1851. On 10 April 1848 he presented the great petition to Parliament after the demonstration on Kennington Common, which dispersed peacefully when the authorities banned a procession to Westminster. Parliament rejected each petition, and his quarrels with moral-force Chartists such as Lovett divided the movement. A mental breakdown in 1852 ended his influence and he died in 1855.\n\nOverall, none of the three achieved his aims in his own lifetime. Marx had the most lasting impact because his analysis of capitalism shaped later economics and politics, Bakunin's anarchism inspired a minority tradition, and O'Connor's Chartism kept the demand for the vote alive, although universal male suffrage in Britain came only in 1918. Marx used ideas and organisation, Bakunin conspiracy and insurrection, and O'Connor peaceful mass agitation, and in each case the methods were more influential than the immediate results.",
