    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Between 1815 and 1871 Italy moved from division under Austrian and papal influence to a single kingdom under Victor Emmanuel II. The moves towards unity came in stages: secret societies and failed revolts before 1848, the revolutions of 1848-49, then the diplomacy of Cavour, the war of 1859, Garibaldi's conquest of the south in 1860, and finally the acquisition of Venetia in 1866 and Rome in 1870.\n\nAfter the Congress of Vienna Italy was divided into several states. Austria ruled Lombardy and Venetia directly, Habsburg princes ruled Tuscany, Modena and Parma, the Pope ruled the Papal States, and the Bourbons ruled the Kingdom of the Two Sicilies from Naples. Only Piedmont-Sardinia was ruled by a native dynasty, the House of Savoy. The first moves towards unity came from secret societies, above all the Carbonari, who spread liberal and nationalist ideas among army officers and professionals. They took part in the revolts in Naples in 1820 and Piedmont in 1821, but Austrian troops crushed them. Risings in Modena, Parma and the Papal Legations in 1831 met the same fate, and Ciro Menotti was executed. These failures showed that Italians alone could not defeat Austria, but they kept the idea of unity alive.\n\nGiuseppe Mazzini gave the movement an ideology. Born in Genoa in 1805, he founded Young Italy at Marseille in 1831, a secret society aiming at a unified republic, with the motto God and the People. He believed the people's own insurrection and education would achieve unity, not foreign help or kings. His invasion of Savoy in 1834 was easily defeated, but his network of exiles and his ideas shaped the Risorgimento. In 1848-49 he was one of the triumvirs of the Roman Republic.\n\nThe revolutions of 1848-49 were the next great move. Risings began at Palermo in January 1848 and spread to Milan and Venice, where the Five Days of Milan drove Radetzky's garrison out of the city in March. Charles Albert of Piedmont declared war on Austria, but was defeated at Custoza in July 1848 and again at Novara in March 1849, and abdicated in favour of Victor Emmanuel II. The Roman Republic, defended by Garibaldi, fell to French troops in July 1849, and Venice under Manin surrendered in August 1849. The revolutions failed, but Piedmont kept its constitution, the Statuto Albertino, and so became the hope of Italian liberals.\n\nUnder Cavour, who became Prime Minister of Piedmont in 1852, the movement succeeded by diplomacy. He modernised the economy through free trade and railways, and sent about 15,000 troops to the Crimean War in 1855, which won Piedmont a seat at the Congress of Paris in 1856, where he raised the Italian question before the Great Powers. At Plombieres in July 1858 he secured Napoleon III's promise of French help against Austria in return for Nice and Savoy. War followed in 1859, with French and Piedmontese victories at Magenta and Solferino. Napoleon III then made the armistice of Villafranca without consulting Cavour, who resigned. Piedmont gained Lombardy, but Venetia stayed Austrian. In March 1860 plebiscites in Tuscany, Modena, Parma and the Romagna voted to join Piedmont.\n\nThe most dramatic move was Garibaldi's expedition in 1860. He sailed from Quarto near Genoa on 5 May with about 1,000 volunteers, landed at Marsala on 11 May, won at Calatafimi and took Palermo. He crossed to the mainland and entered Naples on 7 September as the Bourbon king withdrew. The Piedmontese army meanwhile invaded the Papal Marches and Umbria, and at Teano in October Garibaldi handed his conquests to Victor Emmanuel II. Plebiscites in the south approved annexation, and the Kingdom of Italy was proclaimed on 17 March 1861. Cavour died in June that year.\n\nUnification was completed in two stages. Italy allied with Prussia in 1866 and, although it was beaten at Custoza and Lissa, Prussia's victory gained Venetia for Italy under the Treaty of Vienna in October 1866. Garibaldi's march on Rome in 1862 had been stopped at Aspromonte by Italian troops, and his attempt in 1867 was defeated at Mentana by French troops protecting the Pope. The French garrison was withdrawn in August 1870 when the Franco-Prussian War began, and Italian troops entered Rome on 20 September 1870. A plebiscite approved annexation, and Rome became the capital in 1871, although Pius IX rejected the Law of Guarantees and declared himself a prisoner of the Vatican.\n\nIn conclusion, the moves towards unity passed from conspiracy and revolt to the statecraft of Piedmont, supported by French and Prussian power and by Garibaldi's volunteers. The result was a centralised monarchy with a very narrow franchise, which fell short of Mazzini's popular republican ideal, but it achieved the unification of the peninsula that the Risorgimento had aimed at since the 1820s.",
