    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Napoleon III handled France's internal affairs more effectively than its external affairs. At home he restored order, delivered economic growth and gradually liberalised his regime, but in foreign policy early successes in the Crimea and Italy gave way to the Mexican failure and the disaster of 1870, which destroyed the Empire.\n\nInternally, Napoleon III first established authority after the instability of the Second Republic. He was elected president in December 1848 with about three-quarters of the vote by appealing to peasants and to the memory of his uncle. When the Assembly refused to allow his re-election, he carried out the coup of 2 December 1851, and a plebiscite approved it by a very large majority. A plebiscite in November 1852 approved the restoration of the Empire, and he took the title Napoleon III on 2 December 1852. In the 1850s the regime was authoritarian: the press was censored, opponents were exiled, and elections were controlled by official candidates. This was effective in the short term in ending the cycle of revolutions, though it cost him the loyalty of republicans such as Victor Hugo, who went into exile and attacked him in Napoleon le Petit and Les Chatiments.\n\nHis economic policy was his greatest internal success. The Credit Mobilier, founded in 1852 by the Pereire brothers, financed railways, industry and urban development, and rapid growth followed in the 1850s. The Paris Exposition of 1855 and the rebuilding of Paris under Haussmann showed the regime's commitment to modernisation and prestige. The Cobden-Chevalier Treaty of 1860 with Britain reduced tariffs and pushed French industry to modernise. Supporters praised this prosperity, and revisionist historians such as Theodore Zeldin and Roger Price stress his interest in economic and social reform.\n\nFrom 1860 Napoleon III also showed flexibility by liberalising. The legislature was given more freedom to debate, the right to strike was legalised in 1864, although trade unions remained illegal, and the press law of 1868 relaxed censorship. Emile Ollivier formed a liberal ministry in 1870, and a plebiscite in May 1870 approved the reforms by a large majority. These steps suggest that the regime could adapt, but they also show it had needed to concede power because opposition was growing.\n\nIn external affairs his record began well. France fought in the Crimean War of 1854-56 as Britain's ally, and the Congress of Paris in 1856 restored French prestige after the humiliation of 1815. In 1859 he backed Piedmont against Austria after the secret meeting with Cavour at Plombieres, and French victories at Magenta and Solferino followed. France gained Nice and Savoy in 1860. But he made the armistice of Villafranca without consulting Cavour, after Prussian mobilisation and Catholic anger at home, and left Italy only half satisfied. French troops also stayed in Rome to protect the Pope, which antagonised Italian nationalists.\n\nThe later record was a series of failures. His plan for a French-backed empire in Mexico under the Habsburg Archduke Maximilian collapsed, French troops withdrew by 1867, and Maximilian was executed by Mexican republicans. The Prussian victory over Austria in 1866 changed the balance of power in Europe and badly damaged his prestige. Finally he led France into war with Prussia in 1870, was captured at Sedan on 2 September 1870, and went into exile in England, where he died in 1873. A republic was proclaimed in Paris on 4 September.\n\nOverall, Napoleon III was effective at home, where he gave France stability, prosperity and a modernised capital, but ineffective abroad, where overreach and miscalculation lost him his throne. Because the Empire depended on his personal authority and prestige, the failure in foreign policy brought down the whole regime.",
