    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "My study of the Krupp family, the early motor car and Marie Curie taught me that science and technology were driving forces of change between 1871 and 1920, that they were closely tied to national prestige and to war, and that the breakthroughs often came through determined individuals, sometimes against social prejudice.\n\nThe Krupps showed how technology could create industrial and military power. Friedrich Krupp founded a cast steel works at Essen in 1811, and his son Alfred (1812-87) made it a major enterprise. He exhibited a flawless two-ton ingot of cast steel at the Great Exhibition in London in 1851, developed seamless railway tyres and, using the Bessemer process, steel rails. He moved into breech-loading steel guns, which Prussia adopted in the 1860s and which proved effective against France in 1870-71, and his sales to foreign governments earned him the name the Cannon King. Technology was therefore linked directly to the strength of the state.\n\nUnder Friedrich Alfred and then Gustav von Bohlen und Halbach the firm expanded into naval armour plate, the Germania shipyard at Kiel in 1902, and, in 1914, the 42 cm howitzer known as Big Bertha, used against the Belgian forts at Liege and Namur. During the war the firm made guns, shells, armour plate and submarines, and its workforce grew to well over 100,000, many of them women. Krupp also showed the social side of industrial technology. Alfred built housing, schools, shops and sickness and pension funds, though historians call this paternalism because it also demanded loyalty and discouraged political activity, and socialists attacked the family as merchants of death.\n\nThe motor car showed how invention developed in stages and by competition. Nikolaus Otto built the first practical four-stroke internal combustion engine in 1876, and Gottlieb Daimler and Wilhelm Maybach developed a light, high-speed petrol engine in the 1880s. Karl Benz, working independently in Mannheim, built a three-wheeled Motorwagen and applied for a patent on 29 January 1886. His wife Bertha proved its usefulness by driving about 106 km from Mannheim to Pforzheim in August 1888. France led early production through Panhard et Levassor and Peugeot, and John Boyd Dunlop's pneumatic tyre of 1888 made travel smoother. Yet early cars were luxury goods, and it was Henry Ford, with the Model T of 1908 and the moving assembly line at Highland Park in 1913, who made the car a mass product. The lesson was that Germany and France invented the car, but the USA, through mass production, made it a consumer product, and war then showed its value, as when Paris taxis carried troops to the Marne in 1914.\n\nMarie Curie taught me about pure science and the role of a determined individual. Born Maria Sklodowska in Warsaw in 1867, she could not attend university there and went to the Sorbonne in 1891. Following Becquerel's discovery, she coined the term radioactivity and, with Pierre Curie, announced polonium and radium in 1898 after processing tonnes of pitchblende in a shed. She shared the 1903 Nobel Prize for Physics, the first woman to win one, and won the 1911 Nobel Prize for Chemistry, the first person to win in two sciences. Her work had practical results: during the First World War she organised mobile X-ray units, the petites Curies, for field hospitals. She faced prejudice, as the French Academy of Sciences rejected her in 1911 and the press attacked her partly on xenophobic grounds, and she died in 1934 of aplastic anaemia probably caused by radiation, showing the dangers of the science.\n\nOverall, I learned that science and technology brought great progress, in industry, transport and medicine, and brought national prestige, but that they were also used for war, as with Krupp's guns, the motor lorry and the X-ray, and that their benefits and dangers were not always understood at the time.",
