    "label": "2",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Mussolini was highly effective at the first thing he wanted, which was power. Within four years of becoming Prime Minister he had built a dictatorship, and his propaganda and public works won him real popularity. He was far less effective at his larger aims. His economic policies mostly failed to deliver what they promised, and his foreign policy brought prestige in the 1920s but ended with Italy in an alliance with Germany, a war it could not fight and the collapse of his regime.\n\nThe destruction of democracy was his greatest internal success. He became Prime Minister in October 1922 after the March on Rome, when King Victor Emmanuel III refused to sign a state of siege and invited him to form a government. His first cabinet was a coalition, but the Acerbo Law of 1923 gave two-thirds of the seats to the largest list, and in the violent election of April 1924 the Fascist-led list won about 65 per cent. The murder of the Socialist deputy Giacomo Matteotti in June 1924 nearly brought him down, but the opposition walked out in the Aventine Secession and the King did nothing. On 3 January 1925 Mussolini accepted responsibility for the violence and began an open dictatorship. The Leggi Fascistissime banned other parties, censored the press, replaced elected mayors with appointed podesta, created the OVRA secret police and a Special Tribunal, and sent opponents into internal exile on islands such as Lipari. By 1928 voters could only accept or reject a single list. In controlling Italy this was effective, though it relied on a compliant King and army, and Mussolini never became the complete master that Hitler was.\n\nHis economic policy was far less successful. The corporate state, set up through the Rocco Law of 1926 and the Charter of Labour of 1927, banned strikes and lockouts and put workers and employers into state-recognised bodies, but in practice it served employers and cut workers' wages while the corporations of 1934 did little real planning. The Battle for Grain from 1925 raised wheat output and cut imports, but it pushed farming onto poor land and neglected other crops. Quota 90, the revaluation of the lira in 1926 to 90 to the pound, was a decision made for prestige. It made exports dearer, deflated the economy and cut wages. The Battle for Births rewarded large families and taxed bachelors, yet the birth rate kept falling. The draining of the Pontine Marshes was a genuine and much-publicised achievement, and the creation of the IRI in 1933 saved banks and industry after the Wall Street Crash. But these were rescues that left the state owning much of Italian banking, steel and shipbuilding, and they did not make Italy self-sufficient.\n\nMussolini was more successful at winning consent, through propaganda and social organisation. The Opera Nazionale Balilla enrolled children in a state youth movement, the Dopolavoro gave workers cheap leisure, and the Istituto Luce filmed Il Duce at rallies, harvests and building sites. Slogans such as 'Mussolini is always right' spread the cult of the leader. His greatest political triumph came with the Lateran Treaties of 1929, which settled the dispute between the Italian state and the papacy that had lasted since 1870. The treaties created the Vatican City, made Catholicism the state religion and compensated the Church, and they brought Catholic Italians behind the regime. Yet this control had limits. The Church kept its own youth organisations, and only 12 of about 1,200 university professors refused the loyalty oath of 1931, which shows how many accepted the regime for the sake of their careers rather than from belief.\n\nIn foreign policy his early actions were effective. In 1923 he bombarded and occupied Corfu after the murder of General Tellini and withdrew only when Greece paid compensation, and in 1924 Italy gained Fiume by treaty with Yugoslavia. These gave him prestige at little cost. The invasion of Abyssinia in October 1935 brought a victory when Addis Ababa fell in May 1936, and the League of Nations sanctions did not stop it. But the cost was heavy. The war exposed the weakness of the League, drove Italy away from Britain and France and towards Germany, and drained Italy's resources. Mussolini announced the Rome-Berlin Axis in November 1936, accepted the Anschluss in 1938, invaded Albania in April 1939 and signed the Pact of Steel with Hitler in May 1939. He was then tied to a stronger partner.\n\nHis foreign policy ended in disaster. Italy stayed out of the war in 1939 because its army and economy were not ready, and entered on 10 June 1940 only when France was already beaten. Defeats in Greece from October 1940 and in North Africa left Italy dependent on German help. The Allied landing in Sicily in July 1943 led the Grand Council to vote against Mussolini, and the King dismissed and arrested him on 25 July. He was rescued by German commandos, headed the puppet Salo Republic and was shot by partisans in April 1945.\n\nOverall, Mussolini's internal policies were effective in creating a dictatorship and a measure of popular support, but largely ineffective in the economy, and his external policies, which began with cheap successes, destroyed the regime. A leader who promised a new Roman Empire left Italy defeated, occupied and divided in civil war.",
