    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The growth of European unity between 1945 and 1992 had far-reaching economic effects, as it created a vast free-trading area with common policies, and important social effects, as it changed where people worked, how farmers lived and what it meant to be a European citizen. The effects were uneven, and the costs of some policies, notably the Common Agricultural Policy, were as striking as the gains.\n\nThe first economic effect was the pooling of key industries. The Schuman Declaration of May 1950, drafted by Jean Monnet, led to the Treaty of Paris of 1951 and the European Coal and Steel Community, which came into force in 1952. France, West Germany, Italy and the Benelux countries put coal and steel under a supranational High Authority that could set prices and quotas and fine firms. This gave each state access to more resources at lower cost, and it made war between France and Germany materially harder, which created the stable conditions that investment needed. The ECSC was therefore the first step in an economic recovery that rested on cooperation rather than rivalry.\n\nThe Treaties of Rome, signed in March 1957 and in force from January 1958, had far greater economic consequences. The EEC set out to remove internal tariffs, erect a common external tariff and allow free movement of goods, services, capital and workers. The customs union was completed in July 1968, eighteen months ahead of schedule, and trade between the members grew rapidly. This fed the boom of the 1950s and 1960s, in which West Germany, France and Italy grew faster than Britain, which had stayed outside. The speed of this success was so striking that Britain and Ireland applied to join in 1961, which shows how clearly outsiders judged the economic benefits of membership.\n\nThe Common Agricultural Policy, begun in 1962, had the most direct social effect on ordinary people. It set common prices for farm produce, so farmers across the Community had guaranteed incomes and a reason to modernise. For rural families in France, Italy and later Ireland this brought security and rising living standards. However, the policy quickly became the largest item in the EEC budget, and it caused a bitter dispute over its financing that contributed to de Gaulle's empty chair crisis in 1965. Consumers paid higher food prices, and the budget burden fell unevenly, which fed Britain's long dispute until the rebate agreed at Fontainebleau in 1984.\n\nFree movement of workers changed the social map of Western Europe. Labour shortages in the boom drew Italian workers to West Germany and Belgium, and the EEC gave citizens of member states the right to live and work across the Community. Alongside this came guest workers from Spain, Greece and Turkey, and migrants from former colonies, so that cities in Germany, France and Britain became multicultural. Immigration brought tensions and discrimination, but it also supplied the labour that kept factories and construction running, and many families who were expected to return home settled permanently.\n\nEnlargement and the relaunch of the 1980s widened both the economic and social impact. Britain, Ireland and Denmark joined in 1973, Greece in 1981, and Spain and Portugal in 1986. Ireland, which voted by about 83 percent in favour in 1972, gained access to larger markets and farm support, and poorer regions received money from the structural funds, which began to narrow the gap with richer areas. The Delors Commission's White Paper of 1985 listed nearly 300 measures to remove physical, technical and fiscal barriers, and the Single European Act of 1986 set the goal of a single market by 1992. The Social Charter of 1989 set out workers' rights, which gave the Community a social as well as an economic dimension, though Margaret Thatcher opposed it.\n\nThe Treaty of Maastricht in February 1992 carried these effects further. It created the European Union, set a timetable for economic and monetary union following the European Monetary System of 1979, and introduced European citizenship. For the first time Europeans held a shared status as well as shared markets, though Britain secured opt-outs from the single currency and the social chapter, which showed the limits of agreement.\n\nOverall, the economic effects of unity were more clearly positive than the social effects: trade grew, industry and farming were transformed and poorer regions were helped. The social effects, from migration and mobility to citizenship, were real but more contested, and the costs of the CAP and the budget disputes showed that gains were unevenly shared. Even so, by 1992 the Community had changed the working lives of millions of Europeans.",
