    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Margaret Thatcher, Prime Minister from May 1979 to November 1990, had a profound impact on Britain's internal affairs by breaking with the post-war consensus through monetarism, privatisation and the defeat of trade union power, and she changed Britain's external position by restoring national confidence, tying Britain closely to the United States and setting the terms of its dispute with the European Community. Her record was divisive, and the costs of her domestic policies were heavy.\n\nIn economic policy she adopted monetarism, influenced by Keith Joseph, aiming to defeat inflation by controlling the money supply and cutting public spending rather than by protecting full employment. Exchange controls were abolished in 1979 and the City was deregulated in 1986. The early 1980s brought recession: unemployment reached about three million in 1982, and manufacturing in the north, Scotland and Wales was hit hardest, with riots in English cities in 1981. Inflation was lower by the mid-1980s, but deindustrialisation left lasting regional inequality, so her economic legacy was one of both a more flexible economy and deep social cost.\n\nPrivatisation and the sale of council houses reshaped British society. Her government sold state assets, including British Telecom in 1984, and the right to buy, introduced in 1980, let tenants purchase their homes. These policies spread share and home ownership and reduced the role of the state that Labour had built in 1945. Supporters saw a popular capitalism, while critics argued that the sale of public assets and housing stock benefited those who could already afford to buy.\n\nHer confrontation with the trade unions was the most dramatic internal change. The Employment Acts of 1980 and 1982 and the Trade Union Act of 1984 restricted secondary picketing and the closed shop and required ballots. The miners' strike of March 1984 to March 1985, begun when Ian MacGregor's National Coal Board announced pit closures, was the severest test. The government had built up coal stocks and used the police, notably at Orgreave in June 1984, and the strike ended without a settlement. Arthur Scargill's National Union of Mineworkers was defeated, the union movement was weakened and pit closures accelerated.\n\nExternally, Thatcher's most striking moment was the Falklands War. After Argentina invaded the islands on 2 April 1982, she sent a task force, and the British victory on 14 June 1982 restored national pride and helped her re-election in 1983. Her close relationship with President Reagan was central to her foreign policy: she allowed American aircraft to use British bases in the 1986 raid on Libya and backed the deployment of cruise missiles at Greenham Common. She also played a role in ending the Cold War, for after meeting Gorbachev in December 1984 she said he was someone she could do business with and helped persuade Reagan that he was sincere.\n\nHer relations with the European Community showed her determination to defend national interests. She won the Fontainebleau rebate in June 1984, which reduced Britain's net contribution to the budget, and she supported the Single European Act of 1986 because it advanced the single market. But in her Bruges speech of 20 September 1988 she attacked plans for a federal Europe, and she opposed Delors's Social Charter and economic and monetary union. Britain joined the Exchange Rate Mechanism only in October 1990. Her stance on Europe contributed to her fall, when Geoffrey Howe resigned over it and Michael Heseltine challenged her, and she resigned on 22 November 1990.\n\nShe also dealt with Northern Ireland, signing the Anglo-Irish Agreement with Garret FitzGerald on 15 November 1985, which gave the Irish government a consultative role in the North, a significant departure for a Prime Minister who was otherwise a strong unionist.\n\nThatcher's impact was therefore great at home and abroad. Domestically she transformed the economy and weakened the unions at the price of high unemployment and regional decline, and externally she restored British confidence and strengthened the Atlantic alliance while setting Britain apart from its European partners. Whether she reversed Britain's decline or deepened its divisions remains debated, but her influence on both internal and external affairs was lasting.",
