    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The Second Vatican Council and Pope John Paul II were both of major historical importance, though in different ways. The Council changed how the Catholic Church worshipped, understood itself and related to other faiths, while John Paul II used the authority of the papacy to defend traditional teaching and, in doing so, became a significant political figure in the ending of communism in Europe.\n\nThe Council was called by Pope John XXIII, who was elected in October 1958 and was regarded as a caretaker pope. He announced it in January 1959 and used the word aggiornamento, meaning updating, to describe its aim of renewing the Church and bringing it closer to the modern world, which was marked by secularisation and declining practice in Western Europe. It opened on 11 October 1962 with about 2,500 bishops. At the first session the bishops rejected the drafts prepared by Roman Curia officials, and so the Council took its own direction. After John XXIII died in June 1963, Paul VI kept it going until it closed on 8 December 1965.\n\nThe most visible result was the reform of worship. Sacrosanctum Concilium in 1963 allowed the Mass to be said in local languages, and the new Missal of 1969 to 1970 completed the change, so that the priest faced the congregation and Latin was no longer the norm. Lay Catholics were encouraged to take an active part, reading the Scriptures and serving as ministers. This changed the experience of ordinary Catholics more directly than any other reform and was important in the life of every parish.\n\nThe Council also changed the Church's understanding of itself and its relations with others. Lumen Gentium described the Church as the people of God, gave greater importance to the laity and affirmed collegiality, the sharing of authority between bishops and pope. Unitatis Redintegratio committed the Church to ecumenism, and observers from Orthodox, Anglican and Protestant churches attended the sessions. In December 1965 Paul VI and Patriarch Athenagoras lifted the excommunications of 1054. Nostra Aetate rejected the charge of collective Jewish guilt for the death of Christ and became the basis for dialogue with Judaism, while Dignitatis Humanae affirmed religious liberty, which ended the ideal of the confessional state.\n\nThe Council's effects were, however, contested. Traditionalists such as Archbishop Lefebvre, who founded the Society of St Pius X in 1970, rejected the new liturgy and religious liberty, and he was excommunicated in 1988. Progressives felt it had not gone far enough on women and collegiality. Paul VI's encyclical Humanae Vitae of July 1968 reaffirmed the ban on artificial contraception, even though a papal commission had favoured relaxation, and it was widely rejected in the West, which opened a gap between the hierarchy and lay Catholics. Mass attendance fell steeply in Western Europe and vocations declined, though historians note that Protestant churches suffered similar decline, which suggests wider social causes.\n\nKarol Wojtyła, who had taken part in the Council and helped draft Gaudium et Spes, became pope on 16 October 1978, the first non-Italian pope since 1523. He travelled more than any previous pope, making over a hundred foreign journeys. His visit to Ireland in September 1979, when about a million people attended Mass in the Phoenix Park, was the first by a pope, and at Drogheda he appealed to paramilitaries to end violence. He launched World Youth Days and reaffirmed traditional teaching on contraception, abortion and the priesthood, opposed the ordination of women and liberation theology, and disciplined theologians through the Congregation for the Doctrine of the Faith under Cardinal Ratzinger.\n\nHis political importance was greatest in Eastern Europe. His visit to Poland in 1979 linked the Church to Polish national identity, and his support for Solidarity made him a challenge to communist authority. He survived being shot by Mehmet Ali Ağca in St Peter's Square on 13 May 1981, and he met Gorbachev at the Vatican on 1 December 1989, a sign that the Church had outlasted the communist regimes. He also improved relations with Judaism, visiting the Great Synagogue of Rome in April 1986 and calling Jews the elder brothers of Catholics, which developed the work of Nostra Aetate.\n\nBoth were therefore of great importance. Vatican II renewed Catholic worship and opened the Church to other faiths, though it coincided with falling practice, while John Paul II gave the Church a global presence and played a part in the fall of communism, though critics argue that he resisted reform. Together they shaped the Church's role in the late twentieth century.",
