    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Gandhi, Sukarno and Achebe were each significant, but in different ways. Gandhi and Sukarno led the political struggles that ended British and Dutch rule in India and Indonesia, while Achebe's importance lay in the cultural decolonisation that followed. Gandhi was probably the most influential of the three, because his method of non-violent mass protest transformed the Indian independence movement, though his significance was limited by his failure to prevent partition.\n\nGandhi's first significance was that he turned the Indian National Congress into a mass movement and showed the British that their authority rested on consent. Born in Porbandar in 1869 and trained as a barrister in London, he developed satyagraha, or non-violent civil disobedience, in South Africa and used it at home in the peasant campaigns at Champaran in 1917 and Kheda in 1918. After the Amritsar massacre of 1919 turned many Indians against British rule, he led the Non-Cooperation movement of 1920-22, urging boycotts of British goods, courts and schools. His simple life and promotion of hand-spinning made independence a cause that peasants and townspeople alike could share. The Salt March of 1930, a walk of about 240 miles to Dandi where he made salt in defiance of the government monopoly, drew enormous numbers into protest and won world attention, and the unrest after the Quit India resolution of August 1942 showed that British rule could not be maintained by force alone. Historians such as Judith Brown stress this skill in mass mobilisation, although others argue that war exhaustion, the Indian National Army trials, the naval mutiny of 1946 and Labour's policy under Attlee did more to end the Raj.\n\nGandhi's significance also had real limits. He aimed at Hindu-Muslim unity and the end of untouchability, yet Jinnah accused him of speaking only for Hindus, and B. R. Ambedkar was among his sharpest critics, even if the Poona Pact of 1932 resolved the dispute over separate electorates for Dalits. He opposed partition, but it was accepted by Congress and the League in June 1947, and independence on 15 August 1947 came amid terrible communal violence. Gandhi spent that day fasting in Calcutta rather than celebrating, having earlier walked through the villages of Noakhali to calm the killings there. His method did not secure a united India.\n\nSukarno was significant as the voice and unifier of Indonesian nationalism. Under Japanese occupation he and Mohammad Hatta were allowed to speak publicly, and on 1 June 1945 he set out the Pancasila, five principles intended to balance nationalists, Muslims and communists in one state. With Hatta he proclaimed independence in Jakarta on 17 August 1945. When the Dutch returned, he led a republic that survived two police actions, the second of which in December 1948 captured Yogyakarta and imprisoned him. International pressure, helped by the Republic's suppression of the communist rising at Madiun, led to the transfer of sovereignty at The Hague on 27 December 1949. As first president he gave an island nation a national language and a shared ideology, and at Bandung in April 1955 he hosted twenty-nine Asian and African states and promoted non-alignment and anti-colonialism, giving new nations a voice in world affairs.\n\nSukarno's later record was far more mixed. Parliamentary politics proved unstable, and in 1959 he introduced Guided Democracy by decree, ruling with the army and his Nasakom formula uniting nationalism, religion and communism. Economic decline, heavy spending on confrontation with Malaysia and withdrawal from the United Nations in 1965 followed. The attempted coup of 30 September 1965 brought Suharto to power, mass killings of communists, and Sukarno's removal from real authority in 1966. He therefore achieved independence and unity but undermined his own democratic legacy.\n\nChinua Achebe's significance was cultural rather than political. His novel Things Fall Apart, published in 1958, portrayed an Igbo community before and during the arrival of British rule through the story of Okonkwo, answering colonial writing that treated Africans as primitive. It became one of the most widely read African novels in English. In his 1965 essay on the African writer and the English language he argued, against critics like Obi Wali, that English could be remade to carry African experience, so shaping a major debate about language and identity. He also examined the failures of the independent states in No Longer at Ease, Arrow of God and A Man of the People, which dealt with corruption, and he later supported Biafra during the Nigerian civil war. Unlike Gandhi and Sukarno he did not win independence, but he helped Africans reclaim their own story.\n\nOverall, Gandhi was the most significant, since his method made mass politics and the end of British rule possible across the empire, even if it could not secure a united India. Sukarno achieved independence and national unity but weakened them by his later authoritarian rule, and Achebe changed how the decolonised world saw itself. Each shaped the post-colonial world, in politics, in diplomacy and in literature.",
