    "label": "B1",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "In 1956 T.K. Whitaker became Secretary of the Department of Finance and gathered a group of younger officials to examine the economy. His report Economic Development, published in May 1958, argued that protection had failed to create growth or jobs and that emigration showed the economy was not expanding. It recommended moving public money from housing and hospitals into productive investment in agriculture, industry and tourism, and it welcomed foreign capital. Whitaker was the main author of the First Programme for Economic Expansion, published in November 1958, which turned these ideas into growth targets for 1959 to 1963.",
    "model_source": "haiku",
    "answerStatus": "written",
