    "label": "C1",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Truman took office on 12 April 1945 and in September 1945 sent Congress a 21-point programme continuing the New Deal, although reconversion, inflation and strikes blocked most of it. The Housing Act of 1949 and a rise in the minimum wage were among his main domestic successes under the Fair Deal. The Truman Doctrine of March 1947 committed the United States to aid free peoples resisting communism, and the Marshall Plan gave about 13 billion dollars of aid to Western Europe. He helped to found NATO in April 1949, an alliance in which an attack on one member is an attack on all. Executive Order 9981 of July 1948 ordered the desegregation of the armed forces, the start of his civil rights programme. He sent American forces to defend South Korea in 1950 without a congressional vote, and in 1948 he won an upset victory over Thomas Dewey.",
