    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Education, the railways and industrial development in Belfast all affected Irish economy and society before 1870, but in different ways. The railways tied the country into a national and British market, education spread literacy at the cost of the Irish language and deepened religious divisions, and Belfast's industrialisation set the north-east apart from an agricultural south.\n\nThe national school system was established in 1831 by a letter from the Chief Secretary, Edward Stanley, and was run by a board of Commissioners of National Education. It aimed to give combined literary and moral education to children of all denominations, with religious instruction given separately by each church. The state paid grants, supplied textbooks and inspected schools, and a training college for teachers opened in Dublin. School attendance and literacy rose steadily, and by 1870 roughly a million children were on the rolls, so that most of the population could read and write, which made newspapers like The Nation and political movements more effective.\n\nThe social effects of education were more mixed. The churches contested the system, as the Church of Ireland set up its own Church Education Society in 1839 and the Catholic bishops were divided, with Archbishop Murray co-operating and Archbishop MacHale of Tuam opposed. In practice most schools became denominational because local clergy managed them and most pupils in a district were of one faith. Lessons were in English, and since parents saw English as the key to a job or to emigration, the system reinforced the decline of the Irish language. Higher education divided on religious lines as well. The Queen's Colleges, founded in 1845 and opened in 1849, were non-denominational, and were condemned by Rome in 1847 and forbidden to Catholics by the Synod of Thurles in 1850. The Catholic University opened in 1854 under John Henry Newman but had few students and no recognised degrees. The Queen's College in Belfast, by contrast, served the Presbyterian middle class and was the most successful of the three.\n\nThe railways had the most direct economic effect. The Dublin and Kingstown Railway, built by William Dargan, opened in December 1834 as the first in Ireland, and although only about 65 miles were open in 1845, the network had grown to nearly 1,000 miles by 1855 and was largely complete by about 1860. The Great Southern and Western reached Cork in 1849, the Midland Great Western reached Galway in 1851, and Belfast was linked to Dublin in the 1850s. Railways cut the cost and time of moving cattle, butter, grain and fish, tied farmers to British markets through the ports, and helped the shift from tillage to the live cattle trade after the Famine, which benefited the stronger farmers most. Fairs and markets moved to railway towns, and some small ports lost trade as goods moved inland.\n\nThe railways also changed society beyond the economy. Newspapers, mail and ideas travelled faster, so that national papers reached the provinces and political movements could organise nationally. Tourism grew, with the line to Killarney opening in 1853, and the link to the ports eased travel and emigration, which was heavy in the 1850s. Dargan's Dublin Industrial Exhibition of 1853 showed the confidence of this period, although his work depended on railway investment more than on a wider Irish industrial base.\n\nThe industrial development of Belfast was the most distinctive change. Mechanised flax spinning arrived in the 1820s and 1830s and linen became the dominant industry of Belfast and the Lagan valley, and the cotton famine caused by the American Civil War from 1861 raised demand and prices for linen. Harbour works, including Dargan's Victoria Channel, allowed larger ships to reach the port, engineering firms grew to serve the mills, and in 1861 Edward Harland and Gustav Wolff formed Harland and Wolff on Queen's Island. Belfast had no coal or iron of its own, but skilled labour, capital from the linen trade and strong links with Britain made industry viable. Its population grew from about 100,000 in 1851 to over 170,000 by 1871, more quickly than any other Irish town. The society that resulted was divided, since Catholics, about a third of the population and mostly migrants from the countryside, were concentrated in unskilled jobs and districts such as the Pound, and sectarian riots broke out in 1857 and 1864.\n\nOverall, the three developments changed Ireland in uneven ways. Education brought literacy but weakened Irish and deepened denominational divisions, the railways created a national market that served the commercial farmer, and Belfast became an industrial city while the south declined under British competition and remained dependent on agriculture and emigration. Industrial development in Belfast had the most lasting effect, because it created an economic and political division between north-east and the rest of the island.",
