    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Between 1815 and 1870 Ireland produced scientists and writers of real international standing, even though the country was poor, divided and, from 1845, devastated by famine. The main developments were the breakthroughs in mathematics, astronomy, physics and logic, and a flowering of literature, music and antiquarian scholarship that fed the growth of a cultural idea of the Irish nation.\n\nIn mathematics and astronomy the outstanding figure was William Rowan Hamilton, Royal Astronomer of Ireland at Dunsink Observatory. On 16 October 1843, while walking along the Royal Canal at Broom Bridge, he discovered quaternions, a new kind of algebra that later proved vital to physics and computing. This was one of the great mathematical discoveries of the century and it brought Irish science worldwide respect. Alongside Hamilton stood George Boole, the first professor of mathematics at Queen's College Cork from 1849, whose The Laws of Thought of 1854 laid the foundation of Boolean algebra and modern logic.\n\nAstronomy and engineering of a practical kind were also advanced. William Parsons, third Earl of Rosse, built the six-foot reflecting telescope at Birr Castle, known as the Leviathan of Parsonstown and completed in 1845, then the largest telescope in the world. With it he observed the spiral structure of the galaxy later called the Whirlpool Galaxy, M51, a discovery that changed how astronomers understood nebulae. In physics, Nicholas Callan, a priest and professor at Maynooth, built an early induction coil in the 1830s and developed powerful batteries, work that contributed to the early history of electrical technology.\n\nScience was also given an organised footing and a practical purpose. Robert Kane, first president of Queen's College Cork, wrote The Industrial Resources of Ireland in 1844, arguing that scientific knowledge could be turned to the country's economic development. The Royal Dublin Society and the Royal Irish Academy supported research and scholarship, and the Queen's Colleges of 1845 opened university science to a wider public. Even so, the benefit to the Irish economy was limited, and much of this work depended on a few gifted individuals, some of them wealthy landowners like Rosse.\n\nIn literature, William Carleton, born in 1794 to a Catholic tenant farmer in Tyrone, became the leading chronicler of the pre-Famine peasantry. His Traits and Stories of the Irish Peasantry (1830 and 1833) described wakes, pilgrimages, faction fights and hedge schools with realism, while The Black Prophet (1847) dealt with famine. Thomas Moore's Irish Melodies, published in numbers between 1808 and 1834, set Irish airs to words and won popularity in Britain, Ireland and America. Young Ireland, through The Nation founded in 1842, gave poetry a political purpose: Thomas Davis wrote ballads such as A Nation Once Again, and James Clarence Mangan produced Dark Rosaleen in 1846.\n\nScholarship rediscovered the Gaelic past. The Ordnance Survey of the 1830s, directed by Thomas Larcom, employed John O'Donovan and Eugene O'Curry to record place names and study manuscripts. O'Donovan edited the Annals of the Four Masters in 1848-51 and O'Curry became professor at the Catholic University. George Petrie, painter and antiquarian, showed in 1845 that the round towers were Christian in origin and collected traditional tunes in The Ancient Music of Ireland. Together these writers gave Irish people a sense of a shared history and helped lay the cultural foundations of later nationalism.\n\nOverall, the period was one of remarkable achievement, especially in mathematics, astronomy and literature, where figures such as Hamilton, Rosse, Boole, Carleton and Moore won international recognition. Its limits were equally clear: the Famine and emigration weakened the rural culture and the Irish language that the cultural nationalists wished to save, and scientific progress did little for the mass of the people.",
