    "model": "My study of the suffrage movement, Isabella Tod and Hanna Sheehy-Skeffington taught me that Irish women between 1870 and 1914 were excluded from political life, but built organisations and won real improvements in education, property rights and local government. They also learned that the vote itself came late, in 1918, and that the movement was divided by the national question and by the choice between peaceful and militant methods.\n\nThe early suffrage movement was respectable and constitutional. Women had no parliamentary vote, and under common law a married woman's property belonged to her husband. Anna Haslam, a Quaker, and her husband Thomas founded the Dublin Women's Suffrage Association in 1876, and she led it for over forty years. The early societies petitioned MPs, held drawing-room meetings and sent speakers, and they worked alongside the campaigns for girls' education and against the Contagious Diseases Acts. They won gains step by step: women ratepayers got the municipal vote in 1887, and women were first elected as poor law guardians in 1896. The Local Government Act of 1898 gave women the vote in county and district council elections and allowed them to stand for district councils and boards of guardians.\n\nIsabella Tod shows what one determined woman could achieve. Born in Edinburgh in 1836, she settled in Belfast and organised a suffrage society there in the early 1870s, the first in Ulster. She helped found the Ladies' Institute in 1867 for girls' education, and campaigned for the Married Women's Property Acts. She also worked for the inclusion of girls in the Intermediate Education Act of 1878 and in the Royal University's charter of 1879, which opened examinations and degrees to women. She co-founded the Belfast Women's Temperance Association in 1874 and opposed the Contagious Diseases Acts. As a Liberal Unionist she opposed Home Rule in 1886, which shows that suffragists were found on both sides of the national question, and her unionism limited her influence among nationalists. Historians see her as a pioneer who broke the exclusion of women from public debate.\n\nFrom 1900 the movement grew, but it learned how hostile the political establishment was. The Irish Women's Suffrage Federation of 1911 linked local societies and used peaceful methods. When a Conciliation Bill proposing a limited vote for women was defeated in March 1912, the Irish Party mostly voted against it. Redmond and Dillon feared that a franchise bill would complicate Home Rule, and Redmond personally opposed votes for women. Neither the government nor the Irish Party would add women's votes to the Third Home Rule Bill. Women therefore learned that parliamentary parties would not give them the vote for the asking.\n\nHanna Sheehy-Skeffington represents the militant response. Born in Kanturk in 1877, the daughter of the nationalist MP David Sheehy, she took an MA from the Royal University in 1902 and married Francis Skeffington in 1903. In 1908 she helped found the Irish Women's Franchise League with Margaret Cousins, to win votes for women on the same terms as men. The League published the weekly Irish Citizen from May 1912. In June 1912 she was among eight League members arrested for breaking windows at government buildings in Dublin, and she was jailed again in 1913, being released after a hunger strike under the Cat and Mouse Act. Hostile crowds attacked suffragettes, and many nationalists and trade unionists thought militancy counter-productive, so the tactics won attention but little change.\n\nHanna also showed that the suffrage cause was linked to the national question. She argued that independence would be incomplete without votes for women, and she criticised Cumann na mBan, founded in 1914, for putting women in an auxiliary position. When her husband was shot without trial by Captain Bowen-Colthurst in April 1916 she campaigned for an inquiry and refused compensation. Unionist women, by contrast, signed their own declaration on Ulster Day in 1912, about 234,000 of them, so women took different sides.\n\nThe history of women in these years was one of real but limited progress. By 1914 women could vote and stand in local elections, take degrees, and run campaigns and newspapers, yet there was no parliamentary vote until 1918, and then only for women over thirty with property qualifications. The movement was divided by nationalism and unionism and ignored by the Irish Party, but it gave women organisational experience that they used in later politics.",
