    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The Eucharistic Congress of June 1932 was significant because it was the greatest public display of Catholic Ireland, showed the close alliance between the Church and the state, and gave the Free State an international profile ten years after independence and a civil war. It also deepened the divide between North and South. Its effects were mostly symbolic, but they were important.\n\nThe Congress was an international Catholic gathering and the 31st was awarded to Dublin. It was held in 1932 because that year was the 1,500th anniversary of the traditional date of St Patrick's arrival in 432, which tied Catholicism to Irish identity. The planning began under Cosgrave's government, with Archbishop Edward Byrne leading the organisation, but it took place under de Valera, who became head of government on 9 March 1932. The Pope sent Cardinal Lorenzo Lauri as legate. Dublin was decorated with altars, flags and illuminations, and thousands of volunteers acted as stewards.\n\nThe events were on a huge scale. The Congress ran from 22 to 26 June, and some 36,000 schoolchildren lined the route from Dún Laoghaire to Dublin to welcome the legate. About 100,000 children attended a Mass in the Phoenix Park on 25 June. The closing Mass on 26 June was said before a crowd often put at about a million, though the estimate may be exaggerated, and John McCormack sang Panis Angelicus. The message of Pope Pius XI was broadcast live from the Vatican, and four processions went to O'Connell Bridge, where Cardinal Lauri gave the benediction. The event showed the extent of popular devotion in a state where the vast majority were Catholic.\n\nThe Congress also showed the alliance between Church and state. De Valera, known for his personal piety, and his ministers took a leading part and gave full support, and in this way the new Fianna Fáil government showed that it could work closely with the hierarchy. This relationship shaped the 1937 constitution, with its ban on divorce in Article 41 and recognition of the special position of the Catholic Church in Article 44, and the social legislation of the 1930s. Historians describe the Congress as the high point of the Church's public influence and a sign of the conformity of the 1930s. The Congress was also a political success for de Valera, whose prestige grew at home and abroad, although Cumann na nGaedheal noted that Cosgrave's government had planned it and Fianna Fáil got the credit.\n\nThe Congress had a further political meaning because of the Governor-General, James McNeill. He was not invited to the reception for the legate at Dublin Castle, and he replied with a dinner to which de Valera and the ministers were not invited, then complained in a letter on 7 July. The affair was part of the conflict that led to McNeill's dismissal in October 1932. De Valera was able to present his government as both Catholic and independent of the Crown, which showed that the Congress had a role in the assertion of sovereignty.\n\nInternationally, the Congress gave the Free State important publicity. Foreign newspapers reported it, and it presented a stable, devout and independent state only ten years after the Civil War. The state's organisation was tested, with special trains, buses and sailings, the Garda and army managing the crowds, loudspeakers carrying the ceremonies, and a new radio station at Athlone that later became Radio Éireann in 1938. It passed without major disorder, which was a strong demonstration of public order after the Civil War, and it united people across party lines at a time of political change.\n\nIn Northern Ireland the Congress was seen very differently. Catholic pilgrims travelling to and from Dublin were attacked, trains were stoned at places such as Portadown and Lisburn, and police made baton charges at some stations to protect them. Unionists saw the Congress as proof that the Free State was a Catholic state, which reinforced their case for partition, while nationalists in the North felt pride in belonging to an Irish Catholic nation. It increased the distrust between North and South and between Catholic and Protestant.\n\nOverall, the Congress was a great success and a triumph for both Church and nation, but it changed no laws or institutions. Its significance lay in what it revealed and symbolised: a Church at the height of its influence, a government anxious to be both Catholic and sovereign, and a state which, ten years after independence, could stage an event watched by the world. It also showed how far Catholic identity had become the identity of the state, a point that later generations judged more critically.",
