    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Domhnall Mac Amhlaigh (1926 to 1989) reflected the experience of Irish emigrants in the 1950s through both his life and his writings. He was a native of the west who went to England as a labourer, and his diary of navvy life is the best-known first-hand account of Irish emigration to Britain in that decade. In his life he was a typical emigrant, and in his writing he gave voice to what such emigrants felt.\n\nHis life followed the common pattern of the time. Born in Galway in 1926 and raised in Kilkenny, he faced poor prospects at home, and in 1951 he went to England like many thousands of his generation. In the 1950s emigration was the answer to rural poverty and unemployment, and it kept the population falling until 1961. He settled in Northampton and worked as a labourer on building sites and roads, taking the hard manual work in which Irish men were concentrated in Britain, as navvies and building labourers. He stayed in England for the rest of his life and died in Northampton in 1989, which reflects how many emigrants intended a temporary stay and never returned.\n\nHis writings were the product of that life. His diary of 1951 to 1957, Dialann Deoraí, was published in 1960, and Valentin Iremonger translated it as An Irish Navvy: The Diary of an Exile in 1964. The title itself reflects the sense of exile that many emigrants felt, and that Mac Amhlaigh shows, as someone doing the hardest work far from home and feeling himself an exile. He was a participant and not an observer, so the diary has the authority of lived experience. That he wrote in Irish also shows how emigrants from the Gaeltacht and the west carried their language and identity with them.\n\nThe diary describes in detail the world of the Irish emigrant. It covers the work itself and the lodgings where the men lived, the pubs and dance halls where they spent their leisure, and the company of other Irish navvies. It shows a generation that worked, socialised and kept its identity in an Irish world inside English cities. It also describes homesickness, one of the central emotions of emigration, and the mixture of welcome and prejudice that the Irish met in England, so it reflects both the opportunities and the difficulties of life abroad.\n\nMac Amhlaigh also wrote novels and short stories in Irish, such as Schnitzer O Se in 1974, and journalism. Contemporaries in Ireland valued the diary as a frank picture of a life that official Ireland preferred not to discuss, since emigration was an uncomfortable reminder of the failure of the state to provide for its young people. Historians now treat it as a key source on the Irish in post-war Britain.\n\nIn conclusion, Mac Amhlaigh's life and writings reflected the emigrant experience very closely, in the departure caused by poor prospects, the labouring work, the loneliness, the sense of exile and the community of fellow countrymen. It must be remembered that he is a single author and cannot stand for every emigrant, since many gained better wages and better lives. But his diary gives the most vivid picture of the generation of the 1950s who left Ireland and made their lives in England.",
