    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Anglo-Irish relations developed from bitterness in 1949, when the Republic left the Commonwealth and Britain guaranteed partition, to regular co-operation by 1989 through the Anglo-Irish Agreement and joint membership of the EEC. The progress was not steady: it was punctuated by crises over the Troubles, and the basic dispute over Northern Ireland remained.\n\nThe period opened with a rupture. The Republic of Ireland Act came into force on 18 April 1949, ended the link with the Crown and took Ireland out of the Commonwealth. Britain replied with the Ireland Act 1949, which said the Republic was not a foreign country, so Irish citizens in Britain kept their rights to vote, work and receive benefits, but also stated that Northern Ireland would not leave the United Kingdom without the consent of the Northern Ireland parliament. Dublin saw this as a guarantee of partition. The coalition government then ran an anti-partition campaign, with speeches abroad, especially in the United States, but Britain and the Stormont government refused to discuss the question and it achieved nothing. The Common Travel Area, however, continued, which mattered to the many Irish emigrants in Britain.\n\nFrom the late 1950s relations became more practical and focused on economics. About three-quarters of Irish exports went to Britain, and the Irish pound was tied to sterling, so Ireland could not afford to quarrel. Lemass applied to join the EEC in 1961, nine days before Britain, and after de Gaulle's veto in January 1963 turned to the Anglo-Irish Free Trade Agreement, signed in December 1965. He also broke with anti-partition rhetoric by meeting Terence O'Neill at Stormont on 14 January 1965, followed by O'Neill's visit to Dublin in February. This was the first meeting of the two heads of government for decades and implied that the North would not change without the consent of its people.\n\nThe outbreak of the Troubles in 1969 brought a sharp deterioration. Lynch said the state could not stand by, but he refused to send troops across the border, and he argued at Chequers in 1971 that only an Irish dimension could end the violence. The Irish government condemned internment in August 1971 and took Britain to the European Court of Human Rights. After Bloody Sunday on 30 January 1972, when paratroopers shot dead thirteen civilians in Derry, Dublin recalled its ambassador and a crowd burned the British embassy in Merrion Square. Yet Heath's suspension of Stormont and the introduction of direct rule in March 1972, which Dublin welcomed, showed that the two governments were moving closer in outlook.\n\nJoint entry into the EEC on 1 January 1973 helped, because ministers now met regularly in Brussels. That December Heath, Cosgrave and the northern leaders agreed the Sunningdale Agreement, which set up a power-sharing executive and a Council of Ireland, and Dublin accepted that the status of Northern Ireland would not change without the consent of a majority there. The agreement collapsed in May 1974 after the Ulster Workers' Council strike, while loyalist bombs in Dublin and Monaghan killed thirty-three people. The 1970s ended with the killing of Lord Mountbatten in August 1979, which prompted greater security co-operation.\n\nThe early 1980s were difficult. Haughey and Thatcher met in Dublin in December 1980 and spoke of studying the totality of relationships within these islands, but the hunger strikes of 1981, in which ten republican prisoners died, strained relations. During the Falklands War in 1982 Haughey's government withdrew support for sanctions on Argentina after the sinking of the General Belgrano, which angered Thatcher, and trust did not recover while he was in office.\n\nThe high point came under FitzGerald. The New Ireland Forum of 1983 to 1984 offered a unitary state, a federal state or joint authority, and Thatcher rejected all three in November 1984. Nevertheless, negotiations led to the Anglo-Irish Agreement, signed at Hillsborough on 15 November 1985. Both governments affirmed the consent principle, and an Intergovernmental Conference with a secretariat at Maryfield gave Dublin a formal consultative role in Northern Ireland. Unionists protested fiercely, and fifteen MPs resigned their seats, while Fianna Fáil opposed it and Desmond O'Malley left to found the Progressive Democrats. Haughey nevertheless left it in place after 1989.\n\nIn conclusion, relations moved from a rupture over the constitutional link in 1949 to institutionalised co-operation by 1989. Joint EEC membership, regular meetings and the Hillsborough machinery made contact closer than ever, and Dublin had moved from demanding an end to partition to accepting the consent principle. However, the violence continued and the fundamental dispute remained, so the agreement is best seen as a foundation for the Downing Street Declaration of 1993 and the Good Friday Agreement of 1998.",
