    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The EEC had a mixed impact on Irish fisheries, bringing new investment and a share of a large sea area but also small quotas, foreign competition and lasting grievance, while television, which began in 1961, had a far wider impact on Irish society, entertaining the country, loosening the grip of church and government on public debate and ending the isolation of the countryside.\n\nBefore 1973 Irish fishing was small and under-developed. Most boats were old and inshore, the industry employed only a few thousand full-time fishermen in places like Donegal, Kerry, Cork and Galway, and Irish people ate little fish outside Fridays. Foreign trawlers from France, Spain, Belgium, the Netherlands and Britain fished close to the coast under historic rights. Bord Iascaigh Mhara, set up in 1952, had begun to modernise the fleet with grants and loans, but fish was a tiny share of Irish exports. This explains why the government treated fishing as less important than the farming terms when it negotiated entry.\n\nThe accession terms showed this. The EEC had agreed in 1970 that all member states' fleets should have equal access to community waters, just before Ireland's negotiations began. Ireland won only a derogation, an exclusive six-mile zone and a twelve-mile zone in parts of the coast, for ten years to the end of 1982. Critics said the government had given away too much in order to secure the farming terms. When member states extended their limits to 200 nautical miles in 1977, Ireland gained control of a huge area of rich sea, but the Common Fisheries Policy, finally agreed in January 1983, set total allowable catches divided into national quotas on the principle of relative stability, based on past catches. Irish quotas were small compared with the value of the fish in Irish waters, and fishermen complained that Ireland received a small share of the fish caught in its own waters.\n\nEEC and state money did bring real gains. BIM financed larger vessels and the fleet grew in the late 1970s and 1980s. Killybegs in Donegal became the leading fishing port with trawlers, processing factories and service industries, Castletownbere became a major whitefish port, and harbours such as Dunmore East, Howth, Rossaveel and Kilmore Quay were improved. Fish processing and exports to continental markets expanded. However, small inshore fishermen often lost out to larger vessels and fewer ports, foreign fleets still fished Irish waters and the Naval Service had to arrest those who broke the rules. Fishing remained a grievance, and the Common Market Defence Campaign pointed to it as a loss of control over a national resource. On balance, the EEC modernised part of the industry but gave Ireland less than its waters seemed to deserve.\n\nTelevision had a much wider effect. Telefís Éireann began broadcasting on 31 December 1961, financed by licence fees and advertising and run by an Authority appointed by the government under the Broadcasting Authority Act 1960. Within a few years it reached most of the country and by the early 1970s most households had a set. It changed how people spent their evenings, since families stayed at home to watch rather than go to dances or visit neighbours, and it reduced the isolation of rural communities by bringing national and international news and sport into every home. The Riordans, which began in 1965, dealt with the changes in farming life and was hugely popular, and Telefís Scoile, which began in 1964, was one of the earliest schools television services in Europe.\n\nThe most important impact was on attitudes. The Late Late Show, first broadcast in July 1962 and presented by Gay Byrne until 1999, discussed sex, marriage, contraception, unmarried mothers and the church, subjects rarely talked about in public. In 1966 a married woman's remark about what she had worn on her honeymoon night caused uproar, Bishop Thomas Ryan of Clonfert condemned the programme, and the argument showed that television could provoke public debate about morality and the limits of church authority. Seven Days, launched in 1966, investigated housing, poverty and emigration. Historians accept that television reflected changes already under way, but it speeded them up and exposed viewers to arguments that censorship had kept out of print.\n\nTelevision also tested state power. Lemass described RTÉ as an instrument of public policy, but ministers resented criticism, and a Seven Days programme on illegal moneylending in 1969 led to an inquiry and a row about independence. Minister Gerry Collins issued the first Section 31 order in October 1971, and in November 1972, after the broadcast of an interview with the IRA chief of staff, Seán Mac Stíofáin, he dismissed the entire RTÉ Authority. This showed that the broadcaster's independence was fragile, although television had already established the habit of national debate.\n\nIn conclusion, the EEC's impact on fisheries was real but limited, with investment in ports and boats offset by small quotas and a sense of lost control, whereas television transformed how Irish people lived, entertained themselves and questioned church and state, making it the more far-reaching change.",
