    "model": "Revolution broke out in Germany in 1848 because economic distress, liberal frustration with the system of 1815 and the demand for national unity all came together just as news arrived of revolution in Paris. It failed because the Frankfurt Assembly had no power of its own, the old rulers kept their armies, and the revolutionaries were divided among themselves.\n\nEconomic hardship was an immediate cause. Potato blight from 1845 and poor grain harvests in 1846 and 1847 sharply raised food prices and led to hunger riots. A commercial and industrial depression in 1847 brought bankruptcies and unemployment, and artisans already threatened by factory competition were hit hardest. The Silesian weavers' rising of 1844 had shown how desperate hand workers had become. Peasants in the south and west also resented the feudal dues and services they still owed to landlords. This distress gave the revolution mass support in the towns and countryside.\n\nPolitical frustration was a deeper cause. The Congress of Vienna had created a German Confederation of 39 states with no elected parliament, and Metternich's Carlsbad Decrees and the measures of 1832 had censored the press and banned political associations. Educated middle-class liberals wanted constitutions, freedom of the press and assembly, trial by jury and a national parliament. Opposition had grown in the southern state parliaments, and in Prussia the United Diet of 1847 refused to vote a railway loan without constitutional concessions. Nationalists, meanwhile, wanted a united German nation state in place of the Confederation, and the Zollverein and the memory of the Burschenschaften and the Hambach Festival had kept the idea alive.\n\nThe spark came from France, where Louis-Philippe fell in February 1848. A mass meeting at Mannheim on 27 February demanded a free press, jury trial, citizens' militias and a German parliament, and the March demands spread across the states. Rulers gave way rather than risk overthrow. Liberal ministries were appointed and censorship was lifted in Baden, Württemberg, Saxony and elsewhere, and Ludwig I of Bavaria abdicated. In Berlin, troops fired on a crowd on 18 March, and about 300 civilians died in a night of barricade fighting. Frederick William IV withdrew the troops, honoured the dead and on 21 March rode through Berlin wearing the black, red and gold colours. The fall of Metternich in Vienna on 13 March showed that the old order could be overthrown.\n\nThe revolution then failed, first of all because the Frankfurt National Assembly, which opened in the Paulskirche on 18 May 1848, had no army, no civil service and no revenue. Its regent, Archduke John, headed a government with no real power. When Prussia signed the Malmö armistice with Denmark in August 1848 without consulting Frankfurt, the Assembly first rejected it and then accepted it on 16 September, which exposed its weakness. Radicals rioted in Frankfurt and two conservative deputies were murdered, and Prussian and Austrian troops had to protect the Assembly.\n\nThe liberals were also divided and slow. The deputies were mostly lawyers, officials and professors, with almost no peasants or workers, and they feared social revolution and a republic as much as the princes. Peasants lost interest once feudal dues were largely abolished. Months were spent on the Basic Rights of December 1848 and on whether to choose a Grossdeutsch solution including Austria or a Kleindeutsch one under Prussia. National rivalries with Danes, Poles and Czechs split the revolutionaries as well, and Jelačić's Croats helped the Habsburg army to win.\n\nMeanwhile the old governments recovered. Windischgrätz bombarded Prague in June, and Radetzky defeated Piedmont at Custoza in July. Vienna was retaken on 31 October, and Robert Blum, a Frankfurt deputy, was shot there on 9 November. In Berlin, General Wrangel's troops entered unopposed on 10 November, and on 5 December the king dissolved the Prussian Assembly and imposed a constitution of his own. Schwarzenberg's insistence in March 1849 that the whole Habsburg Empire must join Germany ended the Grossdeutsch plan.\n\nThe decisive blow came in April 1849. The Assembly offered the imperial crown to Frederick William IV, but he formally refused it on 28 April because he would not accept a crown from an elected assembly rather than from the princes. Austria, Prussia, Bavaria, Saxony and Hanover rejected the constitution, so it never came into force. Risings in Saxony, the Palatinate and Baden were crushed by Prussian troops by July 1849, and Austria forced Prussia to abandon its Erfurt Union at Olmütz in November 1850. The Confederation was restored in 1851.\n\nIn conclusion, the revolution came about because hunger, liberal anger and nationalist hope all peaked together in 1848. It failed because the rulers kept their armies while the Assembly had only words, and the revolutionaries were split by class and nationality. Its achievements, such as the end of feudal dues and Germany's first national parliament, were real, but German unity would instead come through Prussian power.",
