    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Music, art, literature and language all contributed to cultural nationalism in nineteenth-century Europe by giving peoples without their own state a shared sense of identity, history and purpose. Romantic artists rejected the rationalism of the Enlightenment and celebrated feeling, the past and the spirit of a people, an idea known as the Volksgeist that Herder had developed. This was most powerful where a nation lacked its own state, as in Italy, Germany, Poland and the Czech lands. I will discuss music, art, and literature and language.\n\nMusic was one of the strongest vehicles of national feeling. Frédéric Chopin wrote mazurkas and polonaises based on Polish dance rhythms, and his music became a symbol of Polish national feeling in exile after the failed rising of 1831. Franz Liszt, born in Hungary, wrote the Hungarian Rhapsodies, which drew on Hungarian and Romani music and presented Hungary's identity to European audiences. Bedřich Smetana's opera The Bartered Bride of 1866 and his cycle of symphonic poems Má vlast of the 1870s used Czech folk music and landscape to express Czech national feeling. Music reached audiences who could not read, and it linked national feeling to melody and emotion.\n\nOpera in particular could carry political meaning. Giuseppe Verdi's Nabucco of 1842 included the chorus Va, pensiero, sung by Hebrew exiles, and Italian audiences came to hear it as a call for national liberation from Austrian rule. Around 1859 the cry of Viva VERDI was popularly said to be a coded slogan, since the letters could stand for Vittorio Emanuele Re d'Italia, the king the nationalists wanted. Richard Wagner meanwhile drew on German myths and legends, and his Ring cycle, based on the Nibelungen legend and Norse sagas and first performed in full at Bayreuth in 1876, gave Germans a mythical image of their own past.\n\nArt gave nationalism its images and symbols. Eugène Delacroix's Liberty Leading the People of 1830 showed a personification of liberty leading the Parisian crowd over the barricades during the July Revolution, and it became one of the most famous images of revolutionary nationalism. Caspar David Friedrich's landscapes, such as Wanderer above the Sea of Fog of about 1818, expressed a Romantic feeling for nature and the German spirit. Romantic historical painters showed scenes from national history, which gave citizens a shared picture of their past. Art in this way turned abstract ideas of nation and liberty into visual symbols that ordinary people could recognise.\n\nLiterature recovered the national past and presented it as the property of the people. The Brothers Grimm published their Children's and Household Tales in 1812 and 1815, presenting German folklore as the expression of the German people, and they began a great German dictionary in 1838. Alessandro Manzoni's novel I promessi sposi of 1827 was set in Lombardy under foreign rule, which Italian readers could apply to their own situation. Adam Mickiewicz's Pan Tadeusz of 1834 was a national epic that celebrated the Polish past, and Pushkin's work was a central part of Russian national culture. Victor Hugo's Hernani of 1830 marked the victory of Romanticism in French theatre.\n\nLanguage was the foundation of all of these. Nationalists argued that a people was defined by its language, and standardising languages through grammars, dictionaries and schools helped to create a sense of a single national community. Josef Jungmann's Czech dictionary of 1835 to 1839 helped to revive Czech as a literary language in a Habsburg Empire where German dominated. Elias Lönnrot's Kalevala of 1835 did the same for Finland, and Manzoni's revised novel of 1840 to 1842, written in the educated Tuscan of Florence, became a model for standard Italian. Pushkin's Russian likewise became the model for later writers. By giving a people one agreed language, these writers made a nation easier to imagine.\n\nOverall, cultural nationalism gave national movements their symbols, languages and histories. Music and art won emotional loyalty, and literature and language created a sense of belonging that did not depend on a state. It could not by itself create nation states, which needed political action and force, but it prepared the ground by convincing peoples that they were nations, and without it the political nationalism of Italy, Germany, Poland and the Czech lands would have had much weaker foundations.",
