    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Robert Owen, Feargus O'Connor and Robert Peel all tried to change Britain between 1815 and 1871, but by very different roads. Owen worked outside politics, building a model community and campaigning for factory laws. O'Connor tried to change the political system itself by leading the Chartist demand for the vote. Peel reformed the state from inside government, through police, finance and trade. Of the three, Peel changed the country most, while Owen and O'Connor were more important as pioneers.\n\nOwen sought to change Britain by showing that industry could be run humanely. Born in Newtown, Montgomeryshire, in 1771, he learned the cotton trade in Manchester and took over the New Lanark mills on the Clyde in 1800. He believed that character is formed by environment, so he set out to improve the environment. He stopped taking pauper children as apprentices, raised the minimum age of employment so that children under ten no longer worked in the mills, and by 1816 had cut the working day to ten and three-quarter hours. He replaced fines and beatings with the Silent Monitor, a block of coloured wood turned each day to show a worker's conduct. He rebuilt the housing, cleaned the village and sold goods at close to cost price. This was change by example, and it showed that welfare and profit could go together.\n\nOwen also tried to spread his ideas beyond New Lanark. In 1816 he opened the Institute for the Formation of Character and one of the earliest infant schools in Britain, which used songs and games instead of rote learning and attracted thousands of visitors. He published A New View of Society (1813-1816), gave evidence to a parliamentary committee in 1816 and campaigned for a law to protect children. The result was the Cotton Mills and Factories Act of 1819, which barred children under nine from cotton mills. It was weaker than he wanted, since it had no inspectors and was poorly enforced. His later schemes largely failed: New Harmony in Indiana collapsed within a few years, and the Grand National Consolidated Trades Union of 1834 collapsed within months. Yet his ideas inspired the co-operative movement, and the Rochdale Pioneers opened their store in 1844.\n\nFeargus O'Connor sought a different kind of change, political democracy for working men. Born in Co. Cork in 1796, he trained as a barrister and entered Parliament as a supporter of Daniel O'Connell. He became the leading Chartist of the 1840s. The People's Charter of 1838 demanded universal male suffrage, the secret ballot, annual parliaments, equal electoral districts, no property qualification for MPs and payment of MPs. The Reform Act of 1832 had left most workers without a vote, and O'Connor channelled their disappointment, and anger at the New Poor Law, into a mass movement. His greatest tool was the Northern Star, which he founded in Leeds in 1837 and which became the most widely read radical newspaper in Britain. Through it and the National Charter Association of 1840 he gave Chartism a national organisation.\n\nO'Connor's methods, however, brought little immediate success. Petitions to Parliament in 1839, 1842 and 1848 were all rejected, and the 1848 petition was found to contain far fewer signatures than claimed. The Kennington Common demonstration of 10 April 1848 dispersed peacefully when a march to Westminster was banned. He was imprisoned for seditious libel in 1840-41, and his National Land Company, which aimed to settle workers on smallholdings that would give them a vote, collapsed in failure and was wound up in 1851. He also quarrelled with moral-force leaders such as William Lovett, which divided the movement. His breakdown in 1852 ended his influence. Still, he kept parliamentary reform on the agenda, and later governments conceded several Chartist demands, such as the secret ballot in 1872.\n\nRobert Peel changed Britain from the centre of power. As Home Secretary he founded the Metropolitan Police in 1829, a professional force that became a model for others, and in the same year he conceded Catholic emancipation to avoid conflict in Ireland. His Tamworth Manifesto of 1834 set out a moderate programme of reform that became the basis of the modern Conservative Party. As Prime Minister from 1841 he reintroduced income tax in 1842 to pay for tariff reductions, cut tariffs in his budgets of 1842 and 1845, and passed the Bank Charter Act of 1844 to regulate the issue of banknotes. The Factory Act of 1844 limited hours for women and children. Finally, in 1846 he repealed the Corn Laws, partly in response to the Irish famine, with the support of Cobden and Bright of the Anti-Corn Law League. It split his party and ended his government, but it was a decisive step towards free trade.\n\nThe three men therefore differ in both means and results. Owen worked outside the state to create a model, O'Connor tried to win power for the people through mass agitation, and Peel used the machinery of government to make the existing order more efficient while keeping private property and the social order intact. Peel's measures affected the whole country at once, whereas Owen's work was confined largely to one community and O'Connor's movement failed in its own time. In my judgement Peel achieved the most direct change, but Owen and O'Connor sowed ideas, co-operation and democracy, which proved important later.",
