    "model": "Wilhelm II's policy of Weltpolitik damaged international relations badly between 1897 and 1914. It set Germany against Britain through the naval race, provoked crises over Morocco that humiliated Germany, and helped to push Britain, France and Russia together into the Triple Entente. By 1914 Europe was divided into two suspicious blocs, and Germany's own policy had done much to create the encirclement it feared.\n\nWeltpolitik was a deliberate break with Bismarck's caution. Bernhard von Bülow, Foreign Secretary from 1897 and Chancellor from 1900, told the Reichstag in December 1897 that Germany wanted its 'place in the sun'. Germany seized Kiaochow in China in 1897 after two missionaries were killed and leased it for 99 years, bought Pacific islands from Spain in 1899, and sought influence in the Ottoman Empire through the Baghdad Railway concession. Wilhelm's own gestures were often clumsy. The Kruger telegram of January 1896 congratulated the Transvaal president on defeating the Jameson Raid and outraged British opinion. A Germany that wanted to be a world power was announcing itself as a rival to Britain, Russia and France in every region at once.\n\nThe most damaging effect was the naval race with Britain. Admiral Tirpitz became head of the Navy Office in 1897 and held that a German battle fleet strong enough to hurt the Royal Navy would deter Britain from war with Germany. The First Navy Law of 1898 and the Second of 1900 planned a fleet of 38 battleships, and the Navy League built mass support for it. Since 1889 Britain had held to the two-power standard, a navy at least equal to the next two largest combined, so it saw the fleet as a direct challenge. Admiral Fisher brought ships home from distant stations to face the North Sea, and HMS Dreadnought, launched in 1906, made every earlier battleship obsolete. Germany answered with its own dreadnoughts and a faster building programme in 1908, and the British public demanded more ships with the cry 'We want eight and we won't wait'. Tirpitz's risk theory therefore had the opposite of its intended effect: the fleet did not deter Britain but turned a possible friend into an opponent. Attempts to end the race, such as the Haldane mission of 1912, failed because Germany wanted a British promise of neutrality while Britain wanted the building slowed.\n\nWeltpolitik also drove Britain out of its isolation and into agreements with Germany's rivals. After the Boer War and the new German fleet, Britain made an alliance with Japan in 1902, the Entente Cordiale with France in 1904 and an entente with Russia in 1907. These settled colonial quarrels, but they left Germany facing a grouping that, together with the Franco-Russian Alliance of 1894, seemed to hem it in. Germany's response was to speak of encirclement, yet its own naval and colonial ambitions had provoked the very bloc it complained about.\n\nThe Moroccan crises show how German diplomatic pressure strengthened the opposite camp. In March 1905 Wilhelm landed at Tangier and declared support for Moroccan independence, hoping to test the new Entente and win concessions. Instead the Algeciras Conference of 1906 was a diplomatic defeat: only Austria-Hungary supported Germany, while Britain, Russia, Italy and the United States backed France. British and French military staffs began secret talks. In 1911 Germany sent the gunboat Panther to Agadir, and Lloyd George warned at the Mansion House that Britain would not be ignored. The crisis ended with Germany accepting French control of Morocco in return for a strip of the French Congo, which German opinion saw as another humiliation. Britain and France drew closer again, and in 1912 their navies agreed a division of responsibilities in the Channel and the Mediterranean.\n\nThere were some limits to this deterioration. Anglo-German relations improved a little in 1913 and 1914 over colonial matters and the Baghdad Railway, and many historians argue that the war itself grew out of the Balkans and the alliance system rather than the navy. Even so, Weltpolitik was a serious strategic mistake. It cost Germany vast sums, never produced a fleet to overtake Britain's, gained only small and unprofitable colonies, and left Germany isolated except for Austria-Hungary. It made European relations more rigid and more hostile, so that each later crisis seemed a trial of strength between the blocs.",
