    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Leo XIII, Rosa Luxemburg and Woodrow Wilson each shaped European affairs in a different way. Leo XIII reshaped the relationship between the Catholic Church and the modern state and addressed the social question. Luxemburg was a revolutionary voice who challenged both militarism and the compromises of European socialism. Wilson, from outside Europe, helped to determine the peace settlement of 1919. Of the three, Wilson's impact was the most direct and the most lasting, though each left a real mark.\n\nLeo XIII, pope from 1878 to 1903, succeeded Pius IX, whose confrontational stance had left the Church isolated. His first major achievement was to end the Kulturkampf in Germany. Bismarck's campaign against the Church since 1871 had seen the Jesuits expelled, schools placed under state inspection and bishops imprisoned or exiled. It had failed, since the Catholic Centre Party doubled its seats and became the second-largest party in the Reichstag. When Bismarck needed Centre support for tariffs and against socialism, Leo negotiated with him, and most of the laws were repealed or relaxed in the 1880s. This showed that a skilled diplomat could secure Catholic rights without confrontation.\n\nLeo's most influential act was the encyclical Rerum Novarum of 15 May 1891, on the condition of the working classes. It defended private property and rejected socialism and class war, but it upheld the right of workers to a just wage and decent conditions and their right to form trade unions, and it called on states to protect the poor. It became the foundation of modern Catholic social teaching and encouraged Catholic trade unions and Christian democratic parties in Germany, Belgium, Italy and elsewhere, giving Catholic workers an alternative to socialism. Leo was less successful elsewhere. His policy of ralliement in 1890-92 urged French Catholics to accept the Third Republic, but monarchist Catholics largely refused, and the anticlerical laws of the early 1900s, ending in the separation of church and state in 1905, undercut it. He also refused to accept the loss of the Papal States, so the Roman Question remained open until 1929.\n\nRosa Luxemburg, born in Russian Poland in 1871, made her mark on the European left. In Reform or Revolution (1899) she attacked Eduard Bernstein's revisionism, insisting that socialism would come through revolution rather than gradual reform. In The Accumulation of Capital (1913) she analysed imperialism economically. Before 1914 she opposed war within the Second International, and she and Lenin helped to amend the Stuttgart resolution of 1907 so that socialists would use a war to hasten the fall of capitalism. When the SPD voted for war credits on 4 August 1914 and the International collapsed, she condemned this as betrayal. With Karl Liebknecht she formed the Spartacus League in 1916 and wrote the Junius pamphlet denouncing the war as imperialist, much of it from prison.\n\nHer influence at the end of the war was dramatic but short-lived. Freed by the revolution of November 1918, she helped found the Communist Party of Germany at the end of December. She thought the Spartacist rising of January 1919 premature, but on 15 January 1919 she and Liebknecht were murdered by Freikorps officers. Her death removed a major independent voice from the German left. She welcomed the Bolshevik seizure of power but criticised its suppression of democracy, and she is remembered as a critic of both militarism and dictatorship. Her practical contribution to European affairs was small, but her ideas outlived her.\n\nWoodrow Wilson's contribution was the most direct. After keeping the United States neutral until the resumption of unrestricted submarine warfare and the Zimmermann Telegram, he asked Congress to declare war in April 1917, which brought American resources to the Allied side. On 8 January 1918 he set out the Fourteen Points, calling for open diplomacy, freedom of the seas, reduced armaments, self-determination for peoples and an independent Poland. Point 14 proposed an association of nations, which became the League of Nations. Germany asked for an armistice in October 1918 on the basis of these points, and Wilson's insistence that Germany end its military autocracy encouraged the fall of the Kaiser.\n\nAt the Paris Peace Conference, Wilson was one of the Big Four with Clemenceau, Lloyd George and Orlando, and he insisted that the League Covenant be part of the Treaty of Versailles. New states such as Poland and Czechoslovakia emerged on national lines. But he compromised, accepting reparations, Article 231 and the Saar to win agreement on the League, and self-determination was applied unevenly, as Ireland's case showed when Sinn Fein's delegate was refused a hearing. At home, he refused to accept the reservations proposed by Henry Cabot Lodge, and after his stroke in October 1919 the Senate rejected the treaty in 1919 and 1920. The League began without the United States and was weakened from the start.\n\nOverall, Wilson contributed most to European affairs, because his ideas and his presence shaped the peace settlement and the League, even though American withdrawal undermined his work. Leo XIII's contribution was real and lasting in ending the Kulturkampf and creating Catholic social teaching, while Luxemburg's was more intellectual than practical.",
