    "model": "The main challenges facing France between 1920 and 1945 were political instability and financial weakness in the 1920s, the Depression and the threat from the extreme right in the 1930s, military defeat in 1940, and then the moral and physical burdens of occupation, collaboration and liberation. Some of these challenges were of France's own making, while others were imposed from outside, but together they destroyed the Third Republic.\n\nThe first challenge was reconstruction and security after the First World War. France had lost about 1.4 million dead and large areas of the north-east lay devastated. The Bloc National government that took office after the election of November 1919 expected Germany to pay for reconstruction, and when reparations were not forthcoming Raymond Poincaré ordered the occupation of the Ruhr in January 1923. This damaged relations with Britain and the United States, which opposed it, and it ended in 1925 without securing full payment. France therefore remained anxious about German revival and isolated from its wartime allies.\n\nA second challenge was chronic political and financial instability. The Third Republic gave real power to the Prime Minister and a fragmented parliament, and governments changed frequently. The Cartel des Gauches under Édouard Herriot won the 1924 election but fell in April 1925 after a run on the franc. Stability returned only when Poincaré formed a national unity government in 1926 and stabilised the franc through tax rises and spending cuts. The Poincaré franc was fixed in law in 1928 at about a fifth of its pre-war value, a measure of how much the war and its aftermath had cost the French economy.\n\nThe Depression created a third set of problems. France was hit later than Britain or Germany because its economy rested on small family farms and businesses, but from 1931 industrial production fell sharply. Governments clung to the gold value of the franc until 1936 and used deflation to defend it, which hurt both industry and farming. At the same time vast sums went into the Maginot Line, built along the German border between 1930 and 1938. Economic hardship fed a political crisis, which broke in the Stavisky affair of January 1934. The financier Alexandre Stavisky was found shot dead, the left and right both suspected a cover-up, and the Radical Prime Minister Chautemps resigned. On 6 February 1934 right-wing leagues such as the Croix-de-Feu and Action française marched on the Chamber of Deputies, police opened fire and about fifteen people were killed. The republic had survived, but it was seen by many as corrupt and under threat from fascism.\n\nThe response to this threat exposed deep social divisions. The left answered with a general strike and in July 1935 Socialists, Communists and Radicals formed the Popular Front, which won the 1936 election under Léon Blum. The Matignon Agreements of June 1936 gave wage rises, union recognition and collective bargaining after about two million workers had struck, and the 40-hour week and paid holidays followed. Yet the government faced capital flight and a failing economy, devalued the franc in October 1936, and split over non-intervention in the Spanish Civil War. Blum paused his reforms in 1937 and resigned, and by November 1938 Daladier's decrees had undone the 40-hour week. France entered the war in 1939 divided between left and right.\n\nThe greatest challenge was military defeat. France declared war on 3 September 1939 but its army under Gamelin stayed passive during the Phoney War. When Germany attacked on 10 May 1940 its armoured divisions broke through the Ardennes, which the French generals had thought difficult for tanks, crossed the Meuse at Sedan on 13 and 14 May and reached the Channel by 20 May. France had as many tanks as Germany but spread them among infantry units, and the Maginot Line did not cover the Ardennes. Paris fell on 14 June, an armistice was signed at Compiègne on 22 June, and on 10 July the National Assembly voted by 569 to 80 to give full powers to Marshal Pétain, ending the Third Republic. About 90,000 French soldiers had died in six weeks.\n\nDefeat brought the challenge of occupation. Pétain's Vichy regime, with its National Revolution and the motto of Travail, Famille, Patrie, ruled the unoccupied south until German troops took it over in November 1942. France bore heavy occupation costs, suffered rationing and a black market, and saw about 1.5 million prisoners held in Germany. Vichy itself collaborated actively: it passed anti-Jewish laws in October 1940 without being asked, its police took part in the Vél' d'Hiv round-up of July 1942, and about 76,000 Jews were deported from France. It created the Milice in January 1943 and sent young men to work in Germany under the Service du Travail Obligatoire, which drove many into the maquis. Against this, de Gaulle's appeal of 18 June 1940 began the Free French movement and Jean Moulin united the Resistance in 1943. Paris was liberated on 25 August 1944 and France faced the task of rebuilding itself as the Fourth Republic.\n\nOverall, France's greatest challenges were not one crisis but a sequence of them. The weakness of the parliamentary system and the divisions of the 1930s left it unprepared for the German attack, and defeat then forced the French to confront collaboration and resistance, challenges that shaped the country long after 1945.",
