    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Hitler's foreign policy between 1933 and 1939 was largely responsible for the outbreak of the Second World War in September 1939, because it was aimed at overturning Versailles and winning Lebensraum in the east, and it was Germany's invasion of Poland that began the war. However, it was not the only factor, since the weakness of the League, the policy of appeasement and the Nazi-Soviet Pact allowed Hitler to pursue his aims for so long without a general war.\n\nThe case for Hitler's responsibility rests first on his aims. In Mein Kampf he argued that Germany needed living space in Eastern Europe, to be won by force and settled by a racially pure population, with the Slavs subjugated. At the meeting recorded in the Hossbach Memorandum on 5 November 1937 he told his military and foreign policy chiefs that Germany's space problem could be solved only by force, probably around 1943 to 1945. Intentionalist historians see these as evidence of a deliberate plan for war. Rearmament supports this reading: Schacht financed it through Mefo bills, conscription returned in March 1935, and Göring's Four Year Plan of 1936 aimed to make Germany ready for war within four years.\n\nHitler's actions between 1933 and 1936 steadily broke down the post-war settlement. In October 1933 he left the League of Nations and the Geneva Disarmament Conference. In March 1935, after the Saar had voted over 90 per cent to return to Germany, he announced conscription and an air force in breach of Versailles. The Stresa Front condemned this but did nothing, and the Anglo-German Naval Agreement of June 1935 then gave him a navy about 35 per cent the size of Britain's and divided Britain from France. On 7 March 1936 German troops reoccupied the Rhineland, breaching Locarno, and again no one acted. Each success showed Hitler that he could act with impunity and made a general war more likely.\n\nThe move from revising Versailles to open expansion came between 1938 and 1939. The Anschluss of 12 March 1938 followed pressure that forced Schuschnigg to resign, and German troops entered Austria. Then Hitler used Konrad Henlein's Sudeten German Party, funded from Berlin, to demand the Sudetenland, and at Munich on 29 and 30 September 1938 Britain and France agreed to its transfer without consulting Czechoslovakia. The decisive step was the entry into Prague on 15 March 1939. This broke the Munich promises and showed that Hitler's claims were not limited to Germans, which led Britain to abandon appeasement and guarantee Poland on 31 March. Hitler had made war likely by choosing to push on against a guarantee he doubted would be honoured.\n\nYet other factors contributed. Britain and France bear real responsibility through appeasement, which rested on the belief that Versailles had been unfair and that another war must be avoided. Chamberlain's claim to have secured peace for our time was widely welcomed, but Munich removed Czechoslovakia's defences and left Hitler convinced the western powers would not fight. The Soviet Union, excluded from Munich, signed the Nazi-Soviet Pact of 23 August 1939, which removed the threat of a war on two fronts and gave Hitler the confidence to invade Poland on 1 September. Functionalist historians and A.J.P. Taylor argue that Hitler was an opportunist responding to the chances offered, and that the competition between Nazi agencies and the choices of other powers shaped how and when war came.\n\nThese arguments have limits. Taylor's view of Hitler as an ordinary German statesman was widely challenged, and nobody forced Hitler to invade Poland. The expansion also depended on the restraint of the western powers, which German generals feared would fight, and a Germany strained by rearmament had reason to be cautious. That Hitler took risks regardless shows his own determination.\n\nIn conclusion, Hitler's foreign policy was the main cause of the war, since his ideology, rearmament and repeated aggression drove events and it was his decision to attack Poland that brought Britain and France in on 3 September 1939. But appeasement and the Nazi-Soviet Pact gave him the opportunity, so he was the principal rather than the only cause.",
