    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Anti-Semitism and the Holocaust affected Europe in three ways between 1920 and 1945. Nazi racial ideology gave a long-standing prejudice the force of state policy, it drove the persecution of Jews across Germany and then the whole continent, and it ended in the murder of about six million Jews, which destroyed Jewish communities and left Europe morally and politically changed.\n\nAnti-Semitism was old in Europe, combining religious hostility, economic resentment and, from the late nineteenth century, racial theories that claimed Jews were a separate and inferior race. What the Nazis did was turn it into the core of a racial doctrine. In Mein Kampf of 1925 Hitler blamed Jews for Germany's defeat in 1918, for communism and for the Versailles settlement, and the idea of a herrenvolk, a Germanic master race, required Jews to be defined as aliens. Der Stürmer and Nazi propaganda spread the stereotypes among ordinary people. This was not confined to Germany, for Italy introduced racial laws in 1938 excluding Jews from schools, the professions and the armed forces. Anti-Semitism was therefore an ideology that shaped the politics of Europe's dictatorships, even though Jews were under one per cent of Germany's population and were well integrated into its economic and cultural life.\n\nFrom 1933 the Nazi state excluded Jews from German society step by step. A boycott of Jewish shops was ordered on 1 April 1933, and the civil service law of that month dismissed Jewish officials, with later laws shutting Jews out of law, medicine and journalism. The Nuremberg Laws of 15 September 1935 defined Jews by descent, stripped them of citizenship so that they became subjects without political rights, and banned marriage and sexual relations with people of German blood. Jewish businesses were Aryanised, sold or seized at low prices. The effect on Europe was that tens of thousands of Jews became refugees, and the laws gave a legal definition used in every later stage of persecution.\n\nViolence escalated in November 1938. After Herschel Grynszpan shot the German diplomat Ernst vom Rath in Paris, Goebbels used the news to launch Kristallnacht on 9 and 10 November. Over 250 synagogues were destroyed, thousands of shops and homes wrecked and about 90 Jews killed, and about 30,000 Jewish men were sent to Dachau, Buchenwald and Sachsenhausen. Jews were then fined one billion marks collectively. The international response showed how little Europe was prepared to do. The Evian Conference of July 1938 was attended by 32 countries, but few agreed to take more refugees, and Britain and the United States kept strict immigration quotas. The Kindertransport saved about 10,000 children, mostly Jewish, by bringing them to Britain, but it was an exception to a general failure.\n\nThe war carried the persecution across Europe. After the invasion of Poland in 1939 Jews were forced into ghettos, the largest being Warsaw, sealed in November 1940 with about 400,000 people, where starvation, disease and forced labour killed thousands. When Germany invaded the Soviet Union in June 1941 the Einsatzgruppen followed the army and shot Jews in huge numbers, as at Babi Yar near Kiev, where about 33,000 were killed on 29 and 30 September 1941. The Wannsee Conference of 20 January 1942, chaired by Reinhard Heydrich, coordinated the Final Solution, the deportation of Jews from every country under German control to camps in the east. Roma, disabled people and Soviet prisoners of war were also murdered in large numbers.\n\nThe death camps were the final stage. Auschwitz-Birkenau used Zyklon B and about 1.1 million people were killed there, around a million of them Jews, selected on arrival for labour or immediate death. Treblinka, Belzec and Sobibor were pure killing centres, and Treblinka alone accounted for over 800,000 deaths. About 430,000 Hungarian Jews were deported to Auschwitz in under two months in 1944. The losses were enormous: about 90 per cent of Polish Jews were killed, about three-quarters of Dutch Jews, among them Anne Frank, and about a quarter of French Jews. Centuries-old Jewish communities in Poland, the Baltic states and the Netherlands were largely wiped out, which changed the human make-up of Europe permanently.\n\nThe response of Europeans varied, which is part of how the Holocaust affected the continent. Jews resisted in the Warsaw ghetto uprising of April 1943 and at Treblinka and Sobibor, and many kept schools and diaries going. Collaborators, including police units in France, the Netherlands and Lithuania, helped to round up Jews, and many bystanders did nothing or profited from seized property. Others rescued Jews: Oskar Schindler saved about 1,200 workers, and in October 1943 Denmark ferried over 7,000 Jews to Sweden. The Allies knew of the killings by late 1942 and declared they would punish those responsible, but did not bomb Auschwitz or the railways leading to it.\n\nOverall, anti-Semitism and the Holocaust devastated Europe's Jewish population and exposed the failure of most governments and societies to act. It was a deliberate, state-organised genocide, and its legacy was the principle, established at the Nuremberg trials, that crimes against humanity could be prosecuted. Few events had such a lasting effect on Europe's conscience.",
