    "label": "1",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Western Europe moved towards unity after 1945 because the continent needed security against another Franco-German war, protection against Soviet power, and a rapid economic recovery. Unity went far in the economic sphere, with a common market created by 1968 and membership enlarged to nine by 1973. It went much less far in the political and military spheres, where national governments, led by de Gaulle, kept control.\n\nThe first motive was the wish to end the cycle of war between France and Germany. Churchill called at Zurich in 1946 for a kind of United States of Europe built on reconciliation between the two. The Hague Congress of 1948 brought together European political figures and led to the Council of Europe in 1949. Robert Schuman's declaration of 9 May 1950, drafted by Jean Monnet, proposed pooling French and West German coal and steel under a joint authority. Since coal and steel were the raw materials of war, the pooling made a new conflict materially hard to imagine. The Treaty of Paris of 1951 created the European Coal and Steel Community, whose High Authority could set prices and quotas and fine firms without the consent of governments. This was a real step towards supranational unity.\n\nThe Cold War was the second driving force. Soviet control over Eastern Europe and strong communist parties in France and Italy made Western governments want to stand together. The USA encouraged this. Marshall Aid of about 13 billion dollars from 1948 to 1952 was channelled through the OEEC, and recipients had to cooperate on trade and recovery. Konrad Adenauer welcomed the Schuman Plan because it treated West Germany as an equal and ended international control of the Ruhr, so unity also brought Germany back into the Western family. Yet the Cold War also showed the limits of unity. The European Defence Community, proposed by Pleven in 1950, was rejected by the French National Assembly in August 1954, and West Germany joined NATO instead in 1955. Defence stayed a national and Atlantic matter.\n\nEconomic self-interest was the third and most successful motive. After Messina in 1955 and the Spaak Report, the Treaties of Rome were signed on 25 March 1957 by the six ECSC states and came into force in January 1958. They created the EEC, with a customs union, a common external tariff and free movement of goods, services, capital and workers. The customs union was completed in July 1968, ahead of schedule. The Common Agricultural Policy, begun in 1962, guaranteed prices for farmers. Trade between the members grew rapidly and the Six shared in the boom of the 1950s and 1960s, which showed that unity paid. Britain, which had stayed out and led EFTA in 1960, applied to join in 1961, proof of how attractive the EEC had become.\n\nThe extent of unity was limited by those who wanted a Europe of nations rather than a federation. Charles de Gaulle vetoed British entry in January 1963 and again in November 1967, arguing that Britain was too closely tied to the USA. In the empty chair crisis from June 1965, France withdrew from the Council of Ministers over CAP financing and the powers of the Commission. The Luxembourg Compromise of January 1966 let any state veto a decision touching vital national interests, which weakened majority voting. Federalists such as Monnet and Hallstein, the first Commission president, saw their hopes checked. The Council of Europe, meanwhile, was only a forum with no power to make binding laws.\n\nUnity advanced again once de Gaulle had gone. At the Hague summit of December 1969 the Six agreed to enlarge the Community and work towards monetary union. Britain, Ireland and Denmark joined on 1 January 1973, Ireland after a referendum in 1972 in which about 83 per cent voted yes, while Norway's voters rejected membership. The Community now had nine members and a larger market.\n\nOverall, Europe moved towards unity for compelling reasons of peace, security and prosperity, and it achieved a great deal economically. The EEC by 1973 was a working customs union that had made war between France and Germany unthinkable. But the unity was partial. Defence, foreign policy and the final say in decisions remained with national governments, and the federal Europe of Monnet's dreams had not arrived.",
