    "label": "2",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The 1973 oil crisis ended the long post-war boom in Western Europe. It brought sharp price rises, inflation combined with rising unemployment, balance of payments deficits and the decline of old industries. It also forced governments to rethink their economic policies and their energy supplies, so its impact was both immediate and lasting.\n\nThe economies it hit had grown for almost thirty years on cheap energy. Oil supplied about half or more of Western Europe's energy by 1973, most of it imported from the Middle East at around three dollars a barrel. The Bretton Woods system had collapsed in 1971 and inflation was already rising. The Yom Kippur War began on 6 October 1973 when Egypt and Syria attacked Israel. On 16 October the Gulf members of OPEC raised the posted price by about 70 per cent, and on 17 October the Arab producers announced output cuts of 5 per cent a month and then embargoes on the USA and the Netherlands, the latter because of its pro-Israeli stance and because Rotterdam was Europe's main oil port. By the beginning of 1974 prices were roughly four times the pre-war level. The embargo was lifted in March 1974 but the higher prices stayed.\n\nThe immediate impact was a sense of emergency. Governments introduced fuel-saving measures, including car-free Sundays in West Germany and the Netherlands, lower speed limits and appeals to households to turn down their heating. In Britain, a miners' dispute combined with power shortages led to a three-day working week for industry from January to March 1974. Industrial production fell, especially in heavy industry, and unemployment rose from the low levels of the 1960s.\n\nThe deeper impact was stagflation, the combination of stagnation and rising prices. Higher energy costs fed through into all prices and wage claims, while demand and output fell. The Keynesian policies that had managed demand since 1945 struggled to cope, because spending more to cut unemployment made inflation worse. Oil-importing states ran balance of payments deficits as they paid much more for imports, while the Arab producers earned huge revenues, the petrodollars, which were recycled through Western banks. A second shock followed the Iranian revolution of 1979, when prices doubled between 1978 and 1980 and brought a second recession in 1980 to 1982. Unemployment across the EEC rose from low levels in the early 1970s to over 10 per cent by the mid-1980s, with Spain and Ireland even higher.\n\nThe crisis also reshaped industry and energy policy. Older industries such as shipbuilding, steel and coal declined sharply across Europe, and the EEC introduced steel quotas in 1980 under the Davignon Plan. Britain suffered deindustrialisation in the north, Scotland and Wales. West Germany moved up-market into machinery and cars. France launched the Messmer Plan of 1974 and by the late 1980s drew about 70 per cent of its electricity from nuclear power. North Sea oil began to flow in the mid-1970s, and by 1980 Britain was self-sufficient in oil and Norway a major exporter, cushioning both from the second shock. The International Energy Agency was set up in 1974 to coordinate stocks and policy among importers.\n\nPolitically, the crisis helped end the post-war consensus on full employment. Governments made the defeat of inflation their main aim. In Britain, Margaret Thatcher, Prime Minister from 1979, pursued monetarism, cut public spending and curbed the unions, and unemployment passed three million in 1982.\n\nOverall, the oil crisis was a turning point. It did not alone cause the end of the boom, since inflation and the dollar's weakness were already straining the system, but it ended cheap energy, brought a decade of high unemployment and inflation, and forced Western Europe to restructure its industry, diversify its energy and change its economic thinking.",
