    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "My study of Khrushchev, Solzhenitsyn and Gorbachev taught me that the Soviet Union was a system that could loosen its controls but never solved its basic problems. Khrushchev opened it up after Stalin and met limits, Solzhenitsyn exposed the cost of its terror, and Gorbachev's reforms ended in the collapse of the state itself.\n\nKhrushchev showed how the Soviet system tried to leave Stalinism behind. After Stalin died in March 1953 he became First Secretary in September, saw off Beria, who was arrested in June 1953 and executed that December, and defeated the anti-party group of Molotov, Malenkov and Kaganovich in 1957. In his secret speech to the 20th Party Congress on 25 February 1956 he denounced Stalin's cult of personality and terror. De-Stalinisation released many political prisoners, reduced the role of the secret police and allowed more open discussion in literature. He also argued that war with capitalism was not inevitable, which became known as peaceful coexistence.\n\nHis rule also showed the limits and failures of the system. The Virgin Lands campaign from 1954 gave good early harvests in Kazakhstan and Siberia, but droughts and poor management caused the harvest failure of 1963, and grain had to be imported. His 1957 decentralisation of industry into regional councils was later reversed. The Novocherkassk strike of 1962 over food prices was suppressed by troops who fired on the crowd. Abroad, the Berlin Wall was built by East Germany in August 1961 with his backing, and the Cuban Missile Crisis of 1962 ended with the removal of the missiles, which his colleagues saw as a humiliation. On 14 October 1964 the Central Committee removed him while he was on holiday. His fall taught me that the Soviet elite would not tolerate one man dominating policy, and that reform from the top was risky.\n\nSolzhenitsyn taught me about the human cost of the Stalinist system. Born in 1918, he was an artillery officer in the Second World War and was arrested in 1945 for criticising Stalin in private letters. He spent eight years in labour camps and then internal exile in Kazakhstan. One Day in the Life of Ivan Denisovich appeared in Novy Mir in 1962 with Khrushchev's approval and brought the camps into public discussion, which showed the extent of the thaw. After Khrushchev fell, censorship tightened. The First Circle and Cancer Ward could be published only abroad in 1968 and Solzhenitsyn was expelled from the Writers' Union in 1969, a year before he won the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1970.\n\nHis later fate showed that the regime still would not tolerate open criticism. The Gulag Archipelago, published in Paris in 1973 after the KGB seized a copy of the manuscript, documented the camp system through testimony and history and had a major impact in the West. He was arrested on 12 February 1974 and flown to West Germany the next day. He lived in Switzerland and then in Vermont from 1976, and he returned to Russia in 1994. For me his career shows that the Soviet system rested on terror and censorship, and that one writer could damage its moral authority.\n\nGorbachev taught me how the system finally came apart. He became General Secretary in 1985 and, when his early slogan of acceleration failed, turned to perestroika, a restructuring of the economy combining market elements with planning, and glasnost, openness and criticism of the past. The Chernobyl explosion of April 1986 and the concealment that followed strengthened the case for openness. In 1989 the first partly free elections to the Congress of People's Deputies were held, and in 1990 the party gave up its constitutional monopoly of power. Abroad, he signed the INF Treaty in December 1987, which eliminated intermediate-range missiles, and withdrew troops from Afghanistan by February 1989.\n\nHis most important decision was not to use force in eastern Europe. Communist rule fell in 1989 in Poland, Hungary, East Germany, where the Berlin Wall opened on 9 November, and Czechoslovakia, with Romania the only violent case. At home, nationalism grew in the republics and the economy worsened. A hardline coup on 19 to 21 August 1991 failed after Boris Yeltsin rallied resistance in Moscow, but it left Gorbachev weakened. The Soviet Union was dissolved on 26 December 1991, a day after Gorbachev resigned.\n\nTaken together, the three men showed me that the Soviet Union could not reform without endangering itself. Khrushchev's thaw was reversed, Solzhenitsyn's truth-telling was punished, and Gorbachev's openness, meant to save communism, set off its end.",
