    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Between 1945 and 1992 Europe's religious life, its youth culture and its mass media all changed profoundly. Religious practice declined in the West and was either persecuted or tested in the East, young people emerged as a distinct group with their own music, fashion and politics, and television became the dominant medium that carried these changes to a mass audience.\n\nIn Western Europe the most important development in religion was secularisation. From the 1960s church attendance fell sharply in Britain, the Netherlands, France and Scandinavia, while Ireland, Poland, Spain and Italy stayed observant for longer. In the Netherlands the proportion of Catholics attending Mass dropped dramatically between the 1960s and 1980s, and by the 1970s seminaries and religious orders were recruiting far fewer men and women. The causes were rising affluence, wider education, urban life, television and the welfare state, which took over much of the social work once done by churches, together with a general questioning of authority in the 1960s. Immigration added a new element, as Muslim communities from North Africa, Turkey and South Asia built mosques and prayer rooms in cities such as Paris, Cologne and Birmingham.\n\nThe Catholic Church tried to respond to this decline through the Second Vatican Council. Pope John XXIII, elected in 1958, announced it in January 1959 and spoke of aggiornamento, the updating of the Church. It opened in October 1962 with about 2,500 bishops and was completed under Paul VI in December 1965. It allowed the Mass to be said in local languages, described the Church as the people of God with a bigger role for the laity, committed Catholics to ecumenism and affirmed religious liberty. Cooperation among Christians grew generally, as with the World Council of Churches founded in 1948 and the meeting of Paul VI and Patriarch Athenagoras in 1964. Vatican II brought the Church closer to ordinary believers, but it did not halt the fall in attendance.\n\nBehind the Iron Curtain the story was different. Churches were persecuted or controlled, yet some gained moral authority. Cardinal Mindszenty was jailed in Hungary in 1949 and later sheltered in the American legation in Budapest, the Catholic Church in Czechoslovakia was suppressed after 1948, and thousands of Russian Orthodox churches were closed under Khrushchev between 1959 and 1964. In Poland, however, the Church under Cardinal Wyszynski kept mass support, and the election of the Polish pope John Paul II in 1978 and his visit to Poland in June 1979 encouraged people to organise independently, which fed into Solidarity. In East Germany Protestant churches sheltered peace groups, and the prayers for peace in Leipzig in 1989 became the starting point for mass protest.\n\nYouth and popular culture were transformed in these decades. Young people became a distinct market with money to spend, helped by longer secondary education and the cheap 45 rpm single. Rock 'n' roll reached Europe in the mid-1950s, when Bill Haley's Rock Around the Clock charted in Britain in 1955 and Elvis Presley's hits followed in 1956. The Beatles then gave British pop a worldwide audience, with their appearance on the Ed Sullivan Show in February 1964 and albums such as Sgt. Pepper in 1967 showing that pop could be an art form. Youth identity was expressed through style: Teddy boys, Mods and Rockers, whose clashes at Brighton, Margate and Clacton at Easter 1964 caused a moral panic, the miniskirt and Carnaby Street, hippies and then punk in 1976 and 1977 as unemployed young people expressed their anger. Jeans became a symbol of the West that young people in the East prized.\n\nYouth culture also became political. In May 1968 student protest at Nanterre and the Sorbonne in Paris led to the Night of the Barricades and a general strike of about ten million workers, and de Gaulle faced a crisis that ended with his resignation in 1969. In West Germany Rudi Dutschke led protests against the Vietnam War and the Nazi past, and in Prague and Warsaw students backed reform and were suppressed in Poland. The movements overthrew no government, but they changed attitudes to authority and sexuality and fed the women's, peace and environmental movements. In 1985 Bob Geldof's Live Aid showed youth culture turned to humanitarian purposes.\n\nThe mass media, above all television, carried all of this. Television spread from a few sets in 1950 to most Western households by the 1970s, with the Coronation of 1953 persuading many British families to buy one. ITV broke the BBC's monopoly in 1955, pirate stations such as Radio Caroline played the pop music official radio neglected, and the Eurovision network allowed shared events across borders. The Beatles' All You Need Is Love in 1967 was seen on the first live global satellite broadcast, and the 1966 World Cup and Live Aid showed television creating a common European and global audience. It also changed politics, since leaders now had to perform on screen.\n\nOverall, the period saw the decline of institutional religion in the West, resisted or reinvented in the East, the invention of the teenager and a youth culture that challenged authority, and the rise of television as the force that spread new ideas and tastes. The common thread was a weakening of traditional authority, whether church, parent or state, and the key agents of that change were affluence and the mass media.",
