    "model": "Harry Truman handled the challenges he faced in a mixed but, on balance, impressive way. He was an unlikely president, thrown into office on Roosevelt's death in April 1945 with little preparation, and he was most successful in foreign policy, where he built the structure of containment, and less successful at home, where Congress and a divided party blocked much of what he wanted.\n\nHis first challenge was to take over from Roosevelt and to manage the end of the war. He was not told of the atomic bomb until he became president, and at Potsdam in July 1945 he met Stalin for the first time. He authorised the atomic bombings of Japan shortly afterwards, a decision that ended the war and that historians still debate. He showed decisiveness, and the sign on his desk, saying that the buck stops here, summed up his view that the president must take final responsibility.\n\nAt home he faced reconversion from a war economy to peace. Strikes, shortages and inflation in 1945 and 1946 made him unpopular, and critics joked that it was a case of to err is Truman. His 21-point programme of September 1945, which continued the New Deal, was largely blocked, and the Republicans won control of Congress in 1946. His Fair Deal of January 1949 proposed national health insurance, a higher minimum wage, wider Social Security and public housing, but the conservative coalition of Republicans and southern Democrats defeated most of it. Only the Housing Act of 1949 and a higher minimum wage were notable successes, so as a domestic reformer he achieved much less than he hoped.\n\nHe did better on civil rights, where he took real political risks. The report To Secure These Rights in October 1947 set out the injustice facing black Americans, and in July 1948 Executive Order 9981 ordered the desegregation of the armed forces. This cost him the support of southern Democrats, and Strom Thurmond's Dixiecrats broke away in 1948. In that election he also faced Henry Wallace on the left, and most observers expected Dewey to win. Truman toured the country by train, attacked the Republican Congress, and won 303 electoral votes to Dewey's 189, an upset which showed his political skill, though the Chicago Daily Tribune had printed the headline Dewey Defeats Truman.\n\nHis greatest achievements were in foreign policy, where he faced the challenge of Soviet expansion. The Truman Doctrine of March 1947 pledged support for free peoples against communist pressure, and the Marshall Plan, announced in 1947, provided about 13 billion dollars of aid to Western Europe between 1948 and 1952, helping recovery and weakening the appeal of communism. When the Soviet Union blockaded West Berlin in June 1948, he chose the airlift rather than an armed convoy, and over two million tons of supplies were flown in until the blockade ended in May 1949. NATO followed in April 1949, binding the United States to the defence of Western Europe. These decisions handled the crisis without war and set the course of American policy for decades.\n\nKorea was a harder test. When North Korea invaded in June 1950 he sent American forces without a declaration of war, and the UN force under MacArthur recovered the South after the landing at Inchon. But the advance north of the 38th parallel brought Chinese forces into the war in late 1950 and drove the UN back. Truman then dismissed MacArthur in April 1951 for demanding a wider war with China, upholding civilian control of the military and limited war, though at great cost in popularity. The armistice came only in July 1953, after he had left office, and the war cost about 36,000 American lives.\n\nThere were also failures. His loyalty programme of March 1947 fed the Red Scare and damaged civil liberties, and he was attacked by Republicans over the fall of China and spy scandals. By 1952, because of Korea, rising prices and corruption scandals, his approval had fallen to about 22 per cent, and he announced that he would not seek re-election.\n\nOverall Truman handled his challenges well in foreign affairs and fairly in domestic ones. Contemporaries judged him harshly, but historians since the 1970s, and David McCullough's biography, have ranked him among the stronger presidents for his decisiveness and his civil rights initiatives. His handling of the Cold War in Europe was a success, while his domestic record was limited by Congress and his loyalty programme was a lasting blemish.",
