    "label": "4",
    "marks": 20,
    "model": "The All-India Muslim League demanded separate Muslim states, a claim set out in the Lahore Resolution of 1940, and Jinnah argued that Hindus and Muslims were two nations. Congress and the League never agreed on a shared structure for India, and the Cabinet Mission of 1946 failed to bring them together. Communal violence grew worse after the League's Direct Action Day in August 1946, when thousands were killed in Calcutta. Britain was exhausted by the war and could no longer rely on its army and police after the INA trials and the 1946 naval mutiny. Mountbatten judged that a united India was no longer possible and feared civil war if he delayed, so he brought the date of independence forward to 15 August 1947.",
