    "label": "C4",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "The Sunningdale Agreement, reached at a conference in Berkshire from 6 to 9 December 1973, set up a Council of Ireland with a council of ministers of seven from each side and a consultative assembly of 60 members drawn equally from the Dáil and the Northern Ireland Assembly. The Irish government accepted that Northern Ireland's status would not change without the consent of a majority there. A power-sharing executive took office on 1 January 1974 under Brian Faulkner, with Gerry Fitt of the SDLP as Deputy Chief Executive. Unionists rejected the Council of Ireland as a step towards a united Ireland, and the Ulster Unionist Council voted 427 to 374 against it on 4 January 1974. The Ulster Workers' Council strike from 15 May 1974 cut electricity supplies and paralysed services, and the British government did not confront the strikers. Faulkner resigned as Chief Executive on 28 May 1974 and the executive collapsed.",
