    "label": "B1",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "Ireland was admitted to the United Nations on 14 December 1955 after the Soviet Union had blocked its application with a veto. Frederick Boland was elected President of the General Assembly for its 1960 session, an honour for a small state that had joined only five years earlier. Frank Aiken, Minister for External Affairs, took an independent line and refused to support the American move to block debate on the seat of China from 1957. Irish troops served in the Congo from 1960, in Cyprus from 1964 and in Lebanon from 1978, which gave the Defence Forces a good reputation abroad.",
