    "model": "Telefís Éireann began broadcasting on 31 December 1961 and reached most households by the early 1970s. The Late Late Show, presented by Gay Byrne from 1962, discussed contraception, unmarried mothers and the church, subjects that were rarely aired in public. The Bishop and the Nightie controversy of 1966 caused uproar and showed that television could provoke national debate about morality. Programmes such as The Riordans, a drama about farming life from 1965, reflected rural life and reduced the isolation of country communities.",
