    "label": "C2",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Ireland's accession terms gave Irish boats an exclusive six-mile zone and a 12-mile zone in parts of the coast until the end of 1982. The 1976 Hague agreement extended fishing limits to 200 nautical miles from 1977 and recognised that Ireland needed preferential treatment. The Common Fisheries Policy agreed in January 1983 set total allowable catches and national quotas based on past catches. The government state grants and EEC money helped BIM finance larger new boats, and the Irish fleet grew through the late 1970s and 1980s. Killybegs became Ireland's leading fishing port, with larger trawlers and processing factories. On balance membership helped fishing through investment, but the quotas were small compared with the value of the fish in Irish waters, which limited what Irish boats could land.",
