    "label": "C2",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Palmers shipyard in Jarrow collapsed between 1932 and 1933, leaving most of the town's working men without work in a town that depended on the yard. National Shipbuilders Security bought the yard in 1934 and barred shipbuilding there for forty years, and a proposed steelworks was diluted until it was unworkable. Unemployment in Jarrow was far above the national average, and the Special Areas Act of 1934 gave the town little help. About 200 men marched roughly 290 miles from Jarrow to London from 5 to 31 October 1936, carrying a petition of over 11,000 signatures for new industry. Baldwin refused to meet the marchers, and Ellen Wilkinson presented the petition in the Commons on 4 November 1936, but it was not debated and no new commitment followed. The march achieved no immediate results, since recovery came with rearmament, but it made Jarrow a national symbol of the distressed areas.",
