    "label": "C1",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "The uprising of 1956 was caused by the Stalinist rule of Mátyás Rákosi, whose secret police, the AVH, were hated, and by falling living standards. Khrushchev's secret speech of February 1956 exposed Stalin's crimes, and Poland's October settlement encouraged Hungarian students and intellectuals to demand change. On 23 October students in Budapest made sixteen demands, and the AVH firing on the crowd outside the radio building sparked a national revolt. Nagy declared Hungary neutral and announced withdrawal from the Warsaw Pact on 1 November, which crossed a line Moscow would not tolerate. The uprising failed because Soviet forces returned in strength on 4 November 1956 and crushed the fighting. The West did not intervene, because the Suez crisis drew attention away from Hungary and the Soviet Union vetoed action in the UN Security Council.",
