    "label": "C2",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "The Oil Crisis of 1973 ended the steady growth of the post-war years and brought fuel rationing and falling production to Western Europe. Arab oil producers imposed an embargo on the Netherlands and the USA, and OPEC prices roughly quadrupled, so oil-importing states faced large balance-of-payments deficits. Social life changed as governments asked households to turn down heating and banned Sunday driving in West Germany and the Netherlands. Britain's three-day working week in 1974 showed how far the crisis reached into ordinary life, and unemployment rose from low levels to above 10 per cent across the EEC by the mid-1980s. Inflation and stagflation pushed governments to make defeating inflation their main aim. The crisis also led to new energy policies, including nuclear power in France, North Sea oil and the International Energy Agency set up in 1974.",
