    "label": "C3",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "The Second Vatican Council changed the Catholic Church in several ways. Mass was said in local languages from 1964, and the new Roman Missal of 1969 to 1970 completed the reform. Lay Catholics were encouraged to read the Scriptures and serve as ministers, and the Council gave a larger role to the laity and to bishops collectively. The Church began formal dialogue with other Christian churches, and Unitatis Redintegratio committed it to ecumenism. Nostra Aetate improved relations with Judaism, and Dignitatis Humanae accepted religious liberty. However, the changes divided Catholics, because Paul VI's Humanae Vitae of 1968 was widely rejected in the West, and Archbishop Lefebvre founded the Society of St Pius X in 1970 to oppose the reforms.",
