    "label": "C4",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Billy Graham, a Baptist minister trained at Wheaton College, became a national figure after his Los Angeles crusade of 1949 was extended because Hearst newspapers reported it widely. He founded the Billy Graham Evangelistic Association in 1950 and began the radio programme Hour of Decision, which later reached a worldwide audience. He co-founded the magazine Christianity Today in 1956 to give a conservative evangelical voice alongside the mainstream press, and in 1957 he held a crusade at Madison Square Garden that ran for about sixteen weeks. He advised presidents from Eisenhower to Nixon and preached at presidential events, which tied his faith to the Cold War patriotism that critics saw in his work. He refused to hold segregated crusades from 1953 and invited Martin Luther King Jr. to pray at his New York crusade, though he urged patience rather than endorsing the mass protests. He declined to join Jerry Falwell's Moral Majority in 1979 and organised the Lausanne Congress in 1974, making him a leader of world evangelicalism.",
