    "model": "Between 1815 and 1850 Irish society and the Irish economy were shaped by a rapidly growing population, a rigid system of land tenure, a deep dependence on the potato and a long agricultural depression. These pressures produced widespread poverty and unrest, new attempts by the state to deal with destitution, and finally the catastrophe of the Great Famine, which transformed Ireland.\n\nRural society was a ladder with a small, mainly Protestant landlord class at the top and millions of poor at the bottom. About 10,000 landlords owned nearly all the land, and many were absentees who left their estates to agents, so rents left the locality and little was invested in improvement. Below them were middlemen, strong farmers with 30 acres or more, smallholders, cottiers who paid rent in labour, and landless labourers who rented conacre for a single season to grow potatoes. Most tenants held at will, with no security of tenure, and any improvement they made belonged to the landlord, which discouraged investment. The Devon Commission of 1843-45 confirmed this insecurity and the lack of compensation, yet no reform followed.\n\nPopulation growth deepened these problems. The population rose from about 5 million around 1800 to about 8.2 million at the census of 1841. Early marriage was common because a couple needed only a cabin and a potato patch, and holdings were subdivided among sons, so that by 1841 nearly half of all holdings were under 5 acres. About three million people depended almost entirely on the potato, often the poor-quality lumper variety, and the 1841 census classed about 40 per cent of houses as fourth-class, one-roomed mud cabins. A people so dependent on a single crop had no protection if it failed.\n\nThe economy was weak. Wartime demand had pushed up grain prices and rents, but after Waterloo in 1815 prices fell steeply while rents fell slowly, so tenants fell into arrears and indebted landlords could not invest. Many landlords began clearing smallholders and middlemen to create larger farms, especially for more profitable pasture. Rural industry collapsed in the face of factory competition: hand spinning and weaving of linen outside the north-east declined, and the cotton industry suffered after protective duties ended in 1824. Only the north-east around Belfast built a modern industrial base. Harvest failures in 1816-17, 1822 and the early 1830s brought partial famines and fever, and about a million people emigrated between 1815 and 1845, while others went to Britain seasonally for the harvest.\n\nSocial tension showed itself in agrarian violence and in protest. Secret societies such as the Whiteboys, Rockites and Ribbonmen attacked landlords, agents and tenants who took farms after evictions. In the Tithe War of 1830-38 farmers, organised by Catholic clergy, refused to pay the tithe to the Church of Ireland, and clashes such as Newtownbarry in 1831, Carrickshock the same year and Gortroe in 1834 left many dead. The Tithe Rentcharge Act of 1838 reduced the charge and made landlords responsible for it, ending the open conflict. The state also met the problem of poverty with the Irish Poor Law of 1838, which divided the country into 130 unions, each with a workhouse run by elected guardians. Relief was given only inside, in deliberately harsh conditions, and the workhouses held about 100,000, enough for normal destitution but not mass famine.\n\nThe potato blight, first reported in September 1845, exposed all these weaknesses. The crop was almost entirely destroyed in 1846, deaths peaked in Black '47, and most victims died of typhus, dysentery and other diseases rather than outright starvation. Connacht and west Munster suffered most. About one million people died and over a million emigrated, so the population fell from 8.2 million in 1841 to about 6.6 million in 1851. Evictions rose after the Poor Law Extension Act of 1847 and its Gregory clause, which forced smallholders to give up land to qualify for relief.\n\nOverall, the years 1815 to 1850 were marked by growing numbers, poverty and insecurity on the land, with little industrial development outside the north-east. The Famine did not create these conditions, but it was the terrible result of them, and it swept away the cottiers and labourers and began the consolidation of farms and the habit of mass emigration.",
