    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Daniel O'Connell was the greatest Irish political leader of his age, an extraordinary organiser and orator who won Catholic Emancipation and created modern mass politics. Yet his weaknesses were real: he relied on bluff, leaned too heavily on the Whigs, ruled his movement tightly and, in the end, failed to win Repeal of the Union.\n\nHis first great strength was his ability to turn a gentry petitioning campaign into a mass movement. When he and Richard Lalor Sheil founded the Catholic Association in 1823, it was a small body of lawyers and gentry. In 1824 he introduced associate membership at a penny a month, the Catholic Rent, which drew in tenant farmers, labourers and townspeople in their hundreds of thousands. The Rent was collected through the parishes under the priests, so the Church's network became his organisation. This was the first mass political movement in Irish or British history, and it gave the Catholic poor a voice for the first time.\n\nHe also showed outstanding tactical skill. O'Connell insisted on peaceful, legal methods while warning that the government could not ignore millions of organised Catholics. He evaded the suppression of 1825 by reforming the Association, and at the general election of 1826 the forty-shilling freeholders, led by their priests, defied their landlords in Waterford and elsewhere. His boldest stroke was to stand in the Clare by-election of 1828, though as a Catholic he could not take his seat. He won on 5 July by 2,057 votes to 982, defeating a sitting minister. Wellington and Peel feared civil strife and conceded the Catholic Relief Act in April 1829. He was also a gifted lawyer and parliamentarian who, at Westminster after 1830, backed the Whigs through the Lichfield House Compact of 1835, helping to secure Catholic appointments under Drummond and the Municipal Corporations Act of 1840. He became Lord Mayor of Dublin in 1841, the first Catholic to hold the post for generations, and he supported religious liberty for Jews and dissenters and the abolition of slavery.\n\nHis weaknesses became clearer after 1829. The same Act that gave Emancipation raised the county franchise from forty shillings to £10, cutting the Irish electorate from about 216,000 to about 37,000, and he had accepted this price. His Repeal motion of 1834 was lost by 523 votes to 38, and his alliance with the Whigs brought only limited reform, so critics said he had traded Repeal for favours. His movement was also overwhelmingly Catholic, which alienated Protestants, especially in Ulster, and his control of it was rigid, with posts and patronage going to members of his family.\n\nThe campaign of 1843 exposed his greatest weakness. About forty monster meetings were held, and the one at Tara on 15 August drew supporters' claims of up to a million. But Peel declared he would resist the break-up of the empire, and when the government banned the Clontarf meeting on 8 October, O'Connell cancelled it to avoid a massacre. That showed his principled commitment to moral force, but it also showed that his threats were a bluff and that he would not go beyond the law. Convicted of conspiracy in 1844, he was freed when the House of Lords quashed the verdict, but his health and energy declined, and his talk of a federal alternative confused his followers.\n\nHis last years brought the break with Young Ireland, who disliked his Whig alliance and his condemnation of the Queen's Colleges. In July 1846 his peace resolutions, declaring force never justified, led Thomas Francis Meagher to reply with the Sword Speech and the Young Irelanders to walk out. The Famine then destroyed the Repeal Rent and his following, and he died at Genoa on 15 May 1847 on his way to Rome.\n\nOn balance, O'Connell's strengths outweighed his failings. He won Emancipation, taught the Irish to organise peacefully and founded constitutional nationalism. His weaknesses, above all his reliance on bluff and his Catholic-centred movement, explain why he could not win Repeal, but they do not diminish his achievement as the Liberator.",
