    "model": "Fenianism and the Liberal reforms of 1869 and 1870 are best seen as two halves of one story, but if change is measured by what was actually altered in Irish life, the Liberal reforms brought the greater change. Fenianism failed in its stated aim of an Irish republic, yet it shook British politics and kept a revolutionary tradition alive. The Liberal reforms were concrete, legal and immediate, though they fell short of what tenants and nationalists wanted.\n\nFenianism grew out of disillusion. The Famine, the failed rising of 1848 and the collapse of the Independent Irish Party after Keogh and Sadleir took office in 1852 convinced many young men that parliamentary politics were useless. The Irish Republican Brotherhood was founded in Dublin on 17 March 1858 by James Stephens as an oath-bound secret society, and in New York John O'Mahony founded the Fenian Brotherhood the same year to raise money and men. The IRB organised in circles, recruited artisans, clerks, shopkeepers and farmers' sons, and tried to win over soldiers in the British army. This was a new thing in Irish politics: a disciplined, transatlantic movement committed to physical force republicanism, and its newspaper, the Irish People, founded in 1863 under editors such as Luby, O'Leary and Kickham, spread its ideas.\n\nMilitarily, however, Fenianism achieved almost nothing. In September 1865 the police raided the Irish People and arrested its leaders, and Stephens, though he escaped from Richmond Prison, postponed the promised rising and was removed by the American Fenians. Habeas corpus was suspended in 1866. The rising of March 1867 was fought in bad weather, undermined by informers and short of arms and trained leaders, and the small risings in Dublin, Cork, Limerick and Tipperary were quickly crushed. The American raids into Canada, such as Ridgeway in 1866, also failed. The Church condemned the movement too: Cardinal Cullen and most bishops denounced it as a secret society, and he refused to let the body of the 1848 rebel Terence Bellew MacManus lie in the Pro-Cathedral in 1861. On its own terms, Fenianism changed little.\n\nIts real impact was indirect, through events in England. In September 1867 Fenians freed two prisoners from a police van in Manchester and a police sergeant was killed; William Allen, Michael Larkin and Michael O'Brien were hanged in November as the Manchester Martyrs and were mourned across Ireland. In December the Clerkenwell explosion killed about a dozen people. These events convinced British politicians that Irish discontent could threaten Britain itself. When Gladstone became prime minister in December 1868 he declared that his mission was to pacify Ireland, and the Fenian scare made reform politically urgent. Fenianism therefore acted as a catalyst, and its long-term legacy, through the idea of the republic and the later IRB, ran as far as 1916.\n\nThe Liberal reforms, by contrast, changed the law. The Irish Church Act of 1869 disestablished the Church of Ireland from 1 January 1871. A church of a small minority, supported by the state, lost its privileged position and most of its endowments, and it ended a grievance that went back to the tithe war and beyond. This was a real transformation in the relationship between church and state in Ireland, achieved by a British parliament without a shot being fired.\n\nThe Irish Land Act of 1870 was the first attempt to recognise tenants' claims. It gave legal force to the Ulster custom of tenant right, allowing outgoing tenants to sell their interest, and gave compensation for disturbance and for improvements. Its Bright clauses offered loans for tenants to buy their holdings, though few did. Its weakness was that landlords could still evict for non-payment and could raise rents, so it gave no true security of tenure. Even so, it established the principle that the landlord's rights were not absolute, which opened the way to the later land acts. Alongside these measures, Fenian prisoners were released between 1869 and 1871 as the amnesty movement grew, and Isaac Butt founded the Home Government Association in 1870, showing that reform opened a space for constitutional nationalism.\n\nOn balance, Fenianism did less to change Ireland directly but did more to change British attitudes, while the Liberal reforms did most to change Irish society. Fenianism showed that violence could force reform; reform showed that grievances could not be fully removed by half-measures. Since the Church Act ended an established church and the Land Act began to limit landlord power, whereas the rising of 1867 was crushed within days, I would argue that the Liberal reforms brought the greater change, even though without Fenianism they might never have come when they did.",
