    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Between 1870 and 1914 land agitation was far more successful than the 1913 strike and lockout. Agitation forced the state to concede the Three Fs and then to finance the transfer of land from landlords to tenants, so that landlordism had effectively ended by 1914. The Dublin workers, by contrast, were defeated and returned to work after five months of hunger, although their struggle left a legacy that mattered later.\n\nThe land campaign began from real grievances. Most farmers were tenants at will, and Gladstone's 1870 Act, which gave compensation for improvements and for disturbance but did not fix rents, left them without security. When bad weather and cheap American grain brought an agricultural depression from 1877, rent arrears and evictions rose, with more than 2,000 families evicted in 1880. Michael Davitt organised a meeting at Irishtown, Co. Mayo, in April 1879, and the Irish National Land League was founded in October 1879 with Parnell as president. Its tactics, notably the boycotting of Captain Charles Boycott in 1880, mobilised whole communities, and the Ladies' Land League under Anna Parnell kept the campaign going when the men were jailed.\n\nThe results were considerable. The Land Law Act of 1881 granted fair rents set by land courts, fixity of tenure and free sale, the Three Fs that tenants had demanded, and judicial rents reduced rents by about one fifth on average. The Arrears Act of 1882 helped those in debt. It is true that the Act created dual ownership, excluded some tenants initially, and that the No Rent Manifesto failed and the League was suppressed in October 1881. The Plan of Campaign of 1886 to 1891 also met coercion under Balfour, the shooting at Mitchelstown in 1887 and a papal condemnation in 1888. But the pressure had changed the government's thinking.\n\nThe decisive success came through land purchase. The Conservatives, practising constructive unionism, passed the Ashbourne Act of 1885, which advanced the full price to tenants, and set up the Congested Districts Board in 1891 for the poor west. Then, after the Land Conference of 1902 brought together landlords and tenant leaders including Lord Dunraven, William O'Brien and John Redmond, the Wyndham Act of 1903 offered loans for the full price over about 68 years with a 12 per cent bonus for landlords. More than 200,000 tenants bought within a few years, and the Birrell Act of 1909 allowed compulsory purchase. A landlord class that had dominated Irish society was gone as a political force, and the Land League also made Parnell's party a mass national movement. Limits remained, since labourers and small western farmers gained least, though the Labourers Act of 1906 built cottages for some.\n\nThe 1913 dispute began when William Martin Murphy, owner of the Irish Independent and the Dublin tramways, dismissed ITGWU members in August. Dublin's working class lived in terrible conditions, with over 20,000 families in single-room tenements and very high death rates, and Larkin's union, founded in 1909, had organised unskilled workers. The tram strike of 26 August grew into a lockout after about 400 employers on 3 September demanded that workers sign the Document renouncing the union. Around 20,000 were out of work. Police baton charges killed two men on 30 August and injured hundreds on Bloody Sunday, and the Irish Citizen Army was founded in November. Support came from the British unions' food ships, beginning with the Hare in September, and from AE's open letter.\n\nYet the outcome was a clear defeat. The Askwith inquiry condemned the Document, but the employers rejected its proposals. The Save the Kiddies scheme was blocked by Archbishop Walsh and the clergy, and a special British TUC congress in December refused a sympathetic strike. By January 1914 hunger forced the men back, many returning without recognition for their union, and some were blacklisted. The ITGWU survived and grew during the war, labour became a political force, and the Citizen Army fought in 1916. These are long-term achievements, but they were not victories in the dispute itself.\n\nOn any measure of what was actually achieved within the period, land agitation was more successful. It won specific legal rights, then a transfer of ownership that settled the central grievance of rural Ireland. The 1913 workers won sympathy and kept a movement alive, but lost the struggle for recognition.",
