    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "All three movements provoked controversy, though in different ways. The GAA aroused argument over politics, secret societies and the Church; the suffrage movement offended both the Irish Party and many nationalists through its tactics and its demands; and the Anglo-Irish Literary Revival caused public rows over religion and the image of the Irish people.\n\nThe GAA was founded at Hayes' Hotel in Thurles on 1 November 1884 by Michael Cusack and Maurice Davin to revive Irish games and open athletics to all classes, which the old amateur rules had excluded. Its rules were controversial from the start. It banned members from playing or attending foreign games such as cricket, rugby and soccer, and barred members of the RIC and the British forces, which many saw as narrow and sectarian in effect. Dublin Castle watched it closely, and police reports described it as a front for Fenianism, while unionist newspapers called it seditious.\n\nThe sharpest controversy was the struggle with the IRB. Fenians joined in large numbers and fought moderates for control of the Central Council at the Thurles conventions of 1886 and 1887. Cusack, who disliked the secret society, quarrelled with Davin and others and was removed as secretary in 1886. Archbishop Croke, who had been a patron since December 1884 along with Parnell and Davitt, grew uneasy with the Fenian influence, and some clergy withdrew from clubs. The American tour of 1888 lost money and left the championships unfinished, and the Parnell split of 1890 divided members again, so that by 1891 the association had almost collapsed.\n\nThe suffrage movement's controversies were of another kind. Early campaigners such as Isabella Tod in Belfast and Anna Haslam in Dublin, who founded the Dublin Women's Suffrage Association in 1876, worked respectably, but the Irish Party was hostile. Redmond personally opposed votes for women and, with Dillon, feared that the issue would complicate Home Rule, so the party mostly voted against the Conciliation Bill of March 1912. Suffragists then argued that no Home Rule settlement was legitimate without votes for women, which angered nationalists. They were also divided among themselves, since many Ulster suffragists were unionists.\n\nThe Irish Women's Franchise League, founded in 1908 by Hanna Sheehy-Skeffington, Margaret Cousins and others, brought militancy. In June 1912 eight members were jailed for breaking windows at government buildings, and during Asquith's visit in July 1912 the English suffragette Mary Leigh threw a hatchet at the carriage in which he and Redmond sat. Prisoners went on hunger strike and were force-fed in Mountjoy, which caused public protest, and hostile crowds attacked suffragettes at meetings. Many nationalists and trade unionists thought the tactics counter-productive, and Sheehy-Skeffington's criticism of the Irish Party and of Cumann na mBan's auxiliary status caused further argument.\n\nThe Literary Revival, led by W.B. Yeats, Lady Gregory and Edward Martyn, was controversial because it was written in English by a mainly Protestant group and presented its own picture of Ireland. The Irish Literary Theatre opened in 1899 with Yeats's The Countess Cathleen, which Catholic critics attacked as heretical, and Cardinal Logue condemned it without seeing it. The Abbey Theatre opened in December 1904, and in January 1907 Synge's The Playboy of the Western World provoked riots as nationalist audiences objected to its picture of rural Irish life as immoral. The clash was between artistic freedom and a demand for a flattering national image. Yeats's September 1913, published in the Irish Times, grew out of the Hugh Lane gallery dispute and attacked the narrow middle class.\n\nIn each case controversy flowed from a clash between a cultural or social aim and the competing demands of politics, religion and national respectability. The GAA's was the most damaging in the short term, but the Playboy riots and the suffrage campaign were the most publicly dramatic.",
