    "model": "Irish emigrants to North America between 1840 and 1920 faced severe challenges, from the dangers of the crossing and extreme poverty to hostility and dangerous work. They also found genuine opportunities in jobs, political influence and the chance to rise, and for most the opportunities eventually outweighed the hardships.\n\nThe first challenge was the journey itself. During the Famine the poorest emigrants, mostly Catholic, unskilled and often Irish-speaking, were crammed into the lower decks of sailing ships for six weeks or more, with poor ventilation and little water. Typhus, or ship fever, spread by lice, killed thousands, and ships with high death rates became known as coffin ships. Of about 100,000 people who sailed for British North America in 1847, an estimated 17,000 or more died at sea, in quarantine or soon after arrival. At Grosse Isle, the quarantine station below Quebec, about 40 ships carrying some 12,500 passengers lay at anchor by 31 May 1847, and more than 5,000 were buried on the island that year. The fever travelled upriver with passengers passed as healthy, and about 6,000 Irish died in the fever sheds at Montreal. Later steamships cut the crossing to about two weeks by the 1870s and made the voyage much safer.\n\nOn arrival, emigrants faced poverty, overcrowding and discrimination. The Famine Irish were poor, crowded into city slums and blamed for cholera and typhus, for crime and drink, and for taking jobs by accepting low wages. Above all they were Catholics in Protestant societies. In the United States, nativism produced the Philadelphia riots of 1844, in which Irish churches and homes were attacked, and the Know-Nothings, who won major state victories in 1854 and wanted longer waiting periods before immigrants could vote and a bar on Catholics holding office. Notices reading No Irish Need Apply were widely remembered, although historians debate how common the written signs were, and Thomas Nast's cartoons of the 1870s portrayed Irish politicians as ape-like and corrupt. Work was hard and often dangerous. The Molly Maguires, Irish miners in the Pennsylvania coalfields, worked in dangerous conditions for low pay, and after the trials of 1876 to 1878 twenty were hanged, ten of them on 21 June 1877.\n\nEmigrants nonetheless found real opportunities in work and settlement. Most Irish settled in cities such as New York, Boston, Philadelphia and Chicago, and in mill and mining towns. Men began as unskilled labourers on docks, railways, canals and building sites, but many moved up into skilled trades, police forces and fire departments. Women dominated domestic service in cities like New York and Boston, which gave them board, lodging and wages they could save and send home. By 1900 the second generation was entering teaching, clerical work and the professions. In Canada many Irish Protestants settled in Ontario. Remittances, amounting to well over a million pounds a year in the later nineteenth century, show that emigrants earned far more than they could at home and used it to bring out brothers and sisters and support families.\n\nPolitics offered the greatest opportunity. Most Irish Americans voted Democratic, because the party defended immigrants and offered jobs and help. Through ward politics and organisations such as Tammany Hall, led by Irish bosses from 1872, they turned numbers into power. William R. Grace became the first Irish Catholic mayor of New York in 1880 and Hugh O'Brien the first in Boston in 1885, and Richard Croker, born in Co. Cork, ran Tammany until 1902. The Irish also built their own parishes, schools, newspapers and societies. The record was mixed, since machine politics depended on patronage and corruption, and reformers condemned Croker, but his career showed how an immigrant child from a poor Irish district could rise to real power.\n\nSupport networks helped emigrants face the challenges. Chain-emigration meant that new arrivals stayed with kin, found work through them and settled in the same parishes, so emigrant communities reproduced the networks of home. Pre-paid tickets and letters spread information about wages and jobs. The Catholic parish and its schools, the English language and the size of the Irish community aided assimilation, and by 1914 the second generation increasingly thought of itself as American.\n\nOverall, the Irish in North America met disease, poverty, prejudice and dangerous work, but they also gained employment, upward mobility and political power. The challenges were greatest for the Famine generation, while opportunities grew for their children, which is why the United States remained the destination of about four out of five emigrants.",
