    "label": "2",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Irish emigration between 1920 and 1966 was marked by a shift in destination from the United States to Britain, a peak in the 1950s, and a sharp decline in the 1960s once the economy began to grow. Throughout, the main cause remained economic weakness, with governments accepting emigration rather than trying to stop it.\n\nThe first trend was restriction in the United States and the redirection of emigrants. The Emergency Quota Act of 1921 limited immigrants of each nationality to 3 per cent of that nationality's foreign-born population in the 1910 census, and the Immigration Act of 1924 set quotas at 2 per cent of the 1890 census. The Irish Free State received a quota of 28,567 a year, cut to 17,853 from 1929. These laws redirected emigration rather than ending it, and emigration to the USA fell sharply from the 1920s. The Great Depression after 1929 cut American demand for labour still further, and some emigrants returned.\n\nBritain became the main destination. It was easy to enter, jobs were close and the fare was low, and the Irish moved to Britain in large numbers in the 1930s. The Free State governments did not try to restrict emigration, which they treated as an economic problem they could not quickly solve. During the Emergency of 1939 to 1945 travel permits and identity documents were needed, and both governments controlled who could travel, yet Britain recruited Irish workers for war factories, building and farm work. Well over 100,000 people from the Irish state went to work in Britain during the war, and tens of thousands more joined the British forces.\n\nAfter the war the flow to Britain grew. Reconstruction created huge demand for labour. Irish men worked as navvies on roads, building sites and power stations, while women trained as nurses in the new National Health Service of 1948 or worked in factories and offices. The Ireland Act of 1949 and the Common Travel Area allowed free movement between the two islands, and Irish citizens were not treated as aliens. The Irish clustered in London, in areas such as Kilburn and Camden Town, and in Birmingham, Coventry, Luton, Manchester and Liverpool. Domhnall Mac Amhlaigh, who went to England in 1951 and wrote of navvy life in his diary, published in 1960 as Dialann Deorai, described hard work, lodgings, homesickness and a mixture of welcome and prejudice. Catholic chaplaincies and dance halls such as the Galtymore in Cricklewood helped emigrants keep an Irish world inside English cities.\n\nThe 1950s were the crisis decade. About 400,000 people left the state between 1951 and 1961 on net, most of them for Britain, and the 1961 census recorded the lowest population in the history of the state, about 2.8 million. The Commission on Emigration and Other Population Problems, set up in 1948 and reporting in 1954, found that the main causes were low incomes, unemployment and small farms, though the attractions of better living standards and a livelier social life abroad also played a part. It accepted that emigration could not be ended quickly and urged economic development rather than any ban on leaving. The Vanishing Irish (1953) warned that the nation was dying out through emigration and late marriage.\n\nThe final trend was a sharp fall in the 1960s. Economic Development (1958), drawn up by T.K. Whitaker, and the First Programme for Economic Expansion, pursued by Sean Lemass as Taoiseach from 1959, brought growth that cut emigration sharply in the 1960s. Meanwhile the US Immigration and Nationality Act of 1965 abolished the national-origins quotas from 1968 but favoured family ties and skills, which made it harder for Irish people without American relatives to enter.\n\nOverall, the key trends were the decline of the United States as a destination after the quota laws, the dominance of Britain, the peak of the 1950s and the fall that followed economic planning. Emigration acted as a safety valve that relieved rural poverty and brought remittances, but it kept the population falling until 1961.",
