    "model": "Mother Mary Martin and the Holy Ghost mission to Nigeria were two of the most significant strands in the Irish Catholic Church's work overseas between the 1930s and the 1960s. Mother Mary Martin pioneered women-led medical missions through the Medical Missionaries of Mary, while the Holy Ghost Fathers built a network of schools, hospitals and parishes in south-eastern Nigeria that made the Church a central institution in the region. Both gave concrete meaning to the idea of Ireland's spiritual empire.\n\nBoth stories belong to the wider flowering of Irish missionary work. After Archbishop Paul Cullen's reforms of the post-Famine Church, which Emmet Larkin called the devotional revolution, Irish seminaries and novitiates produced more priests, brothers and nuns than Ireland itself needed, and the surplus went to the diaspora and the missions. Societies such as the Columbans, the Kiltegan Fathers and the Holy Ghost Fathers carried the faith to Africa and Asia, and Irish people at home supported them through collections and missionary magazines. A country with no empire of its own could thus present itself as a leader in spreading the faith without the taint of colonialism.\n\nMother Mary Martin, born in Dublin in 1892, trained as a nurse and midwife and went to Nigeria in the 1930s to work in mission medicine. Her role was made possible by a change in Church law, since until 1936 religious sisters were forbidden to practise obstetrics and surgery, and a Vatican instruction of that year lifted the restriction. She founded the Medical Missionaries of Mary, dated to 1937 in Nigeria, with its mother house at Drogheda, Co. Louth. This was a significant step for women in the Church, because it allowed nuns to work as fully qualified nurses, midwives and doctors rather than only as teachers or carers.\n\nThe order trained doctors, nurses and midwives and set up hospitals, clinics and nursing schools in areas where few medical services existed. In south-eastern Nigeria its sisters ran rural hospitals and dispensaries alongside the Holy Rosary Sisters, where government provision was scarce. Martin directed the order's expansion and the training of its members, and it later spread to other African countries. She received public recognition at home as the first woman to receive the Freedom of the Borough of Drogheda in 1966, and she died in 1975. Her role was to show that the Church's mission could serve the body as well as the soul, and her order became a pioneer of women-led medical missions.\n\nThe Holy Ghost Fathers, or Spiritans, a French-founded congregation with a large Irish province, had begun work at Onitsha on the lower Niger in 1885. The decisive Irish figure was Joseph Shanahan, born in Co. Tipperary in 1871, who arrived in Nigeria in 1902 and made the school the main instrument of conversion, building village schools staffed by catechists among the Igbo. He became prefect apostolic in 1920 and a bishop, recruited from Irish seminaries, and founded the Holy Rosary Sisters in 1924. By 1945 the mission already had parishes, schools and a few hospitals, and the postwar period built on this foundation.\n\nBetween 1945 and 1966 the mission expanded into a large network of parishes, schools, teacher training colleges and hospitals, staffed by growing numbers of Irish priests, brothers and sisters. The Church hierarchy was established in Nigeria in 1950, with the Irish Spiritan Charles Heerey as Archbishop of Onitsha and Joseph Whelan as the first bishop of Owerri. Mission schools became the main route to education in the region, and the Catholic mission ran a large share of primary and secondary schools, so that many of the Nigerians who later served in public life had passed through them. The Catholic population grew strongly, and local vocations increased: Michael Tansi, an Igbo, was ordained in 1937 and beatified in 1998, and in 1965 Francis Arinze became auxiliary bishop of Onitsha at the age of 32.\n\nThe mission's relationship with the colonial state shaped its role. The government paid grants towards mission schools in return for inspection and standards, which made education cheaper but tied the schools to official requirements, and missionaries sometimes disagreed with officials over curriculum and the place of religion. After independence on 1 October 1960 the mission adapted to a state run by Nigerians, and leadership passed gradually to Nigerian priests and bishops. Critics argued that mission education weakened local culture and that Irish leaders were slow to hand over, and the Biafran war from 1967 damaged many mission stations, though Holy Ghost Father Anthony Byrne helped organise the relief airlift.\n\nOverall, both Mary Martin and the Holy Ghost mission played major roles in the Catholic Church's history. Martin opened a new field for women and gave medical care to areas that lacked it, while the Holy Ghost Fathers created an education system and a local Church with Nigerian leadership that outlasted colonial rule. The weakness was a degree of paternalism and a close tie to the colonial state, but their achievements in health, education and faith were lasting.",
