    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "John F. Kennedy had a considerable impact on both Ireland and the United States, though in different ways. In the United States his election in 1960 as the first Catholic president marked the acceptance of Irish Catholics in American public life, while in Ireland his visit in June 1963 produced an outpouring of national pride and strengthened ties with the emigrant community. His impact was greatest in what he symbolised, and historians debate how far he changed things rather than celebrated them.\n\nKennedy's family story explains why he mattered so much. His great-grandfather Patrick Kennedy had left Dunganstown, Co. Wexford, in 1849 during the Famine emigration, and the family rose through business and politics in Massachusetts. His mother's family, the Fitzgeralds, also had Irish roots. Kennedy therefore embodied the journey of the Irish emigrant from poverty to the highest office in America, and both Irish people and Irish Americans saw their own story in his.\n\nIn the United States, Kennedy's greatest impact was to break a barrier of religious prejudice. Irish Catholics had faced anti-Irish and anti-Catholic sentiment since the Famine generation, and when Al Smith, a Catholic, ran for president in 1928 he was defeated. When Kennedy was elected in 1960 as the first Catholic president, many Irish Americans saw it as proof that Catholics could hold the highest office and were fully accepted as American. His election also confirmed the long rise of Irish Americans through city politics and the Democratic Party, and it gave them a new confidence in their place in the country. His assassination on 22 November 1963 turned him into a figure of national mourning in America as well as in Ireland.\n\nIn Ireland the main impact came with his visit from 26 to 29 June 1963, when he went to Dublin, Wexford and Dunganstown, Cork, Galway and Limerick. On 28 June he addressed the Oireachtas in Dublin, the first time a foreign head of state of that kind had done so. Huge crowds turned out and the press covered every moment, and the visit was widely celebrated as a moment of Irish pride. His return to the family homestead at Dunganstown brought the emigrant story vividly to life, and it encouraged Irish people to see their emigrant relatives abroad as part of a shared story rather than as people who had been lost.\n\nThe visit also had an impact on Irish self-image and on Ireland's relationship with America. Ireland in the 1950s had been marked by mass emigration, stagnation and a sense of failure, and in the early 1960s Seán Lemass's government was opening the economy to the world after the Whitaker plan. The sight of a descendant of an emigrant from Wexford as the leader of the most powerful country in the world suited a more confident Ireland. It also encouraged interest in roots and heritage tourism among Americans of Irish descent, and his portrait hung in many Irish homes after his death.\n\nHistorians, however, urge caution about the scale of his impact. They argue that the visit fitted the self-confidence of the Lemass government rather than causing it, and that Kennedy's Irishness was one part of his image rather than the basis of his policy. In the United States the significance was symbolic, since a Catholic president did not end anti-Catholic feeling overnight, and in Ireland the celebration was of a link rather than of any change in policy.\n\nOverall, Kennedy's impact was greatest as a symbol. For Irish Americans he proved that Irish Catholics had arrived at the heart of American life, and for Ireland he provided a moment of pride and a closer link with its diaspora at the moment the country was becoming more outward-looking. His death in 1963 cut short his presidency, but his place in the memory of both countries has remained very strong.",
