    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Republican and Loyalist terrorism had a devastating impact on Northern Ireland, causing thousands of deaths, deepening sectarian division, undermining every political initiative and damaging the economy and daily life of the province. It also shaped the British and Irish governments' policies and hardened attitudes in both communities.\n\nThe most immediate impact was the loss of life. About 3,500 people were killed between 1969 and 2001, roughly half of them civilians. Republican paramilitaries, above all the Provisional IRA, were responsible for about 60 per cent of the deaths, loyalists for about 30 per cent and the security forces for about 10 per cent. The worst year was 1972, with nearly 500 killed, including the nine people who died on Bloody Friday, 21 July 1972, when more than twenty IRA bombs exploded across Belfast. Tens of thousands more were injured, and the violence fell most heavily on working-class areas such as the Falls, the Shankill and Ardoyne.\n\nThe violence became increasingly sectarian and brutal. The IRA's campaign included the Kingsmill massacre of ten Protestant workmen in January 1976, the Enniskillen bombing on Remembrance Day 1987, which killed eleven people, and the Shankill Road bomb of 23 October 1993, which killed ten. Loyalist groups such as the UVF and the UDA, with its killing wing the UFF, targeted Catholic civilians. McGurk's Bar was bombed in December 1971, killing 15 Catholics, the Miami Showband was attacked in 1975, the Shankill Butchers murdered Catholics at night between 1975 and 1977, and in 1993 the UFF killed eight people at Greysteel a week after the Shankill bomb. Each atrocity brought retaliation, producing a cycle of revenge that locked the communities into mutual fear.\n\nTerrorism also destroyed political progress. The loyalist Ulster Workers' Council strike of May 1974, backed by paramilitaries, brought down the power-sharing executive, and the Dublin and Monaghan bombings of 17 May 1974, carried out by the UVF, killed 33 people in the Republic in the worst loss of life on a single day of the Troubles. Republican violence continued during the executive's term and made moderate unionists more fearful about security. Moderates on both sides, such as Brian Faulkner and the SDLP, found it hard to defend compromise while their communities were under attack, and the extremists gained support from the fear and anger that each outrage created.\n\nThe impact on the economy and society was heavy. Foreign investors were reluctant to build factories in a province where bombs were going off, tourism fell, and the centre of Belfast was surrounded by a security cordon, the so-called ring of steel. Businesses faced high insurance costs, and manufacturing continued to decline while the public sector and security jobs grew, making Northern Ireland increasingly dependent on British subvention. Segregation increased as families moved into single-identity areas, and checkpoints and searches became part of daily life.\n\nTerrorism also drew in the British and Irish governments and affected relations between them. Britain deployed the army, introduced internment in 1971 and later direct rule, while republican bombs in England, at Guildford and Birmingham in 1974, at Brighton in 1984 and at Warrington in 1993, killed civilians and hardened British opinion, yet also led to miscarriages of justice such as those of the Guildford Four and Birmingham Six, who were freed in 1989 and 1991. Security operations such as Loughgall in 1987 and Gibraltar in 1988 raised allegations of a shoot to kill policy, which alienated many nationalists and fed republican recruitment. In the Republic, the Irish government was alarmed by the spread of violence and responded by tightening security, and the Dublin bombings showed that the Republic itself was vulnerable.\n\nOverall, republican and loyalist terrorism left Northern Ireland a more divided, poorer and more militarised society. It destroyed the Sunningdale experiment, deepened sectarian hatred and cost thousands of lives, and it was only when the cost of violence became clear to the paramilitaries themselves and the governments that a political path to ceasefires opened up in the 1990s.",
