    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Religious affiliation and cultural identity, the Apprentice Boys of Derry and Seamus Heaney were all significant for Northern Ireland, because each shaped how its two communities understood themselves and each other. Religion and identity formed the underlying division, the Apprentice Boys showed how historical memory could become a flashpoint, and Heaney showed how culture could try to understand the conflict.\n\nReligious affiliation and cultural identity were the basis of the divide. Catholics made up about a third of the population, 35 per cent in the 1961 census, and Protestants the majority, and political allegiance largely followed religion, with most unionists Protestant and most nationalists Catholic. Unionist identity combined Protestantism, loyalty to the Crown and attachment to the Union, while nationalist identity combined Catholicism, Irish heritage and support for a united Ireland. Separate schools, churches, marriage patterns and social networks meant that many people had little regular contact across the divide, and identity was passed on from generation to generation.\n\nEach community had its own cultural institutions, which reinforced separateness. The Orange Order, founded in 1795 after the Battle of the Diamond, held parades on 12 July to mark the Boyne, and was closely linked to the Ulster Unionist Party. Its members could not marry Catholics, and its marches through Catholic areas created recurring tension. Catholic life centred on the Church, the Gaelic Athletic Association, founded in 1884, and the Irish language. The GAA's Rule 21, which banned members of the security forces, lasted until 2001 and was a point of contention with unionists. Nationalists also felt that the Stormont state had not respected their identity, for example in the Flags and Emblems Act. Attempts at bridge-building, such as the Corrymeela Community founded in 1965 and the ecumenical meetings at Ballymascanlon in 1973, had value but were opposed by hardliners such as Ian Paisley, and they did not change the political division.\n\nThe Apprentice Boys of Derry were significant as the most potent symbol of the siege mentality of Ulster Protestants. The club was founded in 1814 to commemorate the siege of 1688 to 1689, when thirteen apprentices shut the gates against the army of the Catholic James II, and the city held out for 105 days until the relief ship Mountjoy broke the boom on the Foyle on 28 July 1689. Its annual parades, the August Relief of Derry commemoration around the walls and the December commemoration of the Shutting of the Gates with the burning of an effigy of Governor Lundy, celebrated resistance and carried the slogan No Surrender. For unionists this represented the defence of Protestant liberty, but for nationalists in the Bogside, below the walls of a city with a Catholic majority controlled by unionists through gerrymandering, the parades were triumphalist.\n\nThat tension had major consequences. The Apprentice Boys' parade of 12 August 1969 passed the Bogside, and the clashes that followed became the Battle of the Bogside, which led to the deployment of British troops and is often seen as the start of the Troubles. The parade thus showed how a commemorative tradition, harmless to its members, could act as a trigger for violence when it intersected with civil rights grievances over housing and voting in Derry.\n\nSeamus Heaney was significant as the most important cultural voice to respond to the Troubles. Born in 1939 near Castledawson, County Derry, to a Catholic farming family, he was educated at St Columb's College and Queen's University Belfast. His early collections, Death of a Naturalist in 1966 and Wintering Out in 1972, were followed by North in 1975, in which poems such as Punishment used Iron Age bog bodies to reflect on ritual killing and tribal violence in Northern Ireland. He was a director of the Field Day Theatre Company, founded in 1980, which sought to explore Irish identity, and he moved to County Wicklow in 1972 while continuing to write about the North. He won the Nobel Prize for Literature in 1995, and gave Northern Ireland international recognition and a way of expressing grief, guilt and the search for an identity beyond sectarianism. Critics, such as Ciaran Carson, argued that North mythologised the violence, and his rejection of being called British, in his poem An Open Letter, reminds us that he did not stand outside the divisions of identity.\n\nIn conclusion, religious affiliation and cultural identity were the root of the division in Northern Ireland, the Apprentice Boys showed how the memory of the past could harden that division and even help spark violence, and Heaney showed that culture could explore the conflict from within. Each was significant for the way it shaped the lives of the people of the province and the course of the Troubles.",
