    "label": "1",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Metternich succeeded to a considerable extent in holding back change between 1815 and 1848, but only for a time and only by suppressing its causes rather than removing them. For over thirty years he preserved the Habsburg Empire and the conservative order, crushed most revolts and prevented a general European war. Yet his system suffered real defeats in Greece, Belgium and the Zollverein, and it collapsed in a few days in March 1848. His success was therefore partial and temporary.\n\nMetternich's greatest success was in building a framework for conservative Europe at and after the Congress of Vienna. Legitimacy restored the Bourbons and other deposed dynasties, while Austria gained Lombardy-Venetia and Habsburg relatives ruled Tuscany, Modena and Parma, making Austria the dominant power in Italy. In Germany the settlement replaced the Holy Roman Empire with a Confederation of 39 states, whose Diet at Frankfurt had no elected parliament and was presided over by Austria. Because decisions needed large majorities and the states kept their own rulers, the Bund could do little that Austria did not want. Nationalist and liberal hopes had been ignored, and Metternich worked to keep them that way.\n\nHe then used the Congress System to defend the settlement against revolution. At Troppau in 1820 Austria, Prussia and Russia claimed the right to intervene against revolutions in other states, and at Laibach in 1821 Austrian troops were authorised to crush the revolution in Naples; Austria also put down the Piedmontese rising that year. The Holy Alliance bound the eastern monarchies together. Austrian troops again put down risings in Modena, Parma and the Papal Legations in 1831. In these cases Metternich plainly succeeded: revolutionary change in Italy was stopped by force for a generation.\n\nWithin the German states he relied on repression. After the murder of Kotzebue by a radical student in March 1819, he drew up the Carlsbad Decrees, adopted by the Diet that September. They banned the Burschenschaften, placed universities under government supervisors and imposed press censorship, and a commission at Mainz hunted for conspirators. After the Hambach Festival of 1832, which brought some 20,000 to 30,000 people to demand unity and a free press, the Six Articles and further measures banned political meetings and associations. Inside Austria, police, informers and censorship silenced critics, and the empire survived intact until 1848. Such measures kept liberal and national movements weak and divided.\n\nHowever, there were important limits. The Congress System broke down in the 1820s when Britain refused to follow the eastern powers, and Canning kept Britain out of the system after the Congress of Verona. The Greek War of Independence, which began in 1821, broke the principle of non-intervention: Britain, France and Russia destroyed the Turkish-Egyptian fleet at Navarino in 1827, and Greece became independent. The revolutions of 1830 were a further blow. Charles X was overthrown in France, and Belgium broke away from the Netherlands and was recognised as independent, with Leopold of Saxe-Coburg as king in 1831. Metternich opposed all of this and could not stop it. In Germany, Prussia's Zollverein of 1834 created an economic union without Austria and began to shift loyalty towards Berlin.\n\nThe final test came in 1848. News of the February revolution in Paris sparked demonstrations in Vienna, and on 13 March, after troops fired on the crowd, the court forced Metternich to resign and he fled to England. Revolutions followed across the Habsburg lands and German states, with Hungarian, Czech and Italian demands for self-rule. This showed that repression had only delayed change. His admirers, such as Srbik and Kissinger, see a statesman who gave Europe a stable peace, and there was no general war until the Crimean War of 1853. But that peace owed much to exhaustion after the Napoleonic Wars and to British naval power.\n\nOverall, Metternich succeeded in postponing liberal and national change for over thirty years and in preserving Austria, but he failed to stop it permanently or to prevent some defeats along the way. His system depended on force, on the cooperation of Prussia and Russia, and on the weakness of his opponents. When these supports weakened, it fell almost at once.",
