    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Baron Haussmann and Charles Darwin each achieved something of lasting importance, Haussmann by physically remaking Paris and Darwin by transforming the way people understood life itself. Haussmann solved many of the practical problems of a crowded capital, though at a social cost, while Darwin's theory of natural selection founded modern evolutionary biology and provoked a lasting debate about religion and science.\n\nHaussmann's first achievement was the transformation of the street plan of Paris. Appointed Prefect of the Seine by Napoleon III in June 1853, he served for about seventeen years and used the expropriation law of 1841, extended in 1852, to clear whole blocks of the old medieval centre. Through the grands travaux he cut straight, tree-lined boulevards through the maze of narrow streets around the Île de la Cité and Les Halles. The Boulevard de Sébastopol ran north to south and linked the Seine with the railway stations, the Rue de Rivoli was extended, and the Boulevards Saint-Germain and Saint-Michel crossed the Latin Quarter. The Étoile became the centre of twelve radiating avenues, and building regulations fixed the height of facades, which gave Paris its uniform look. The result was a city in which traffic could move, fire engines could reach buildings and troops could move quickly, which also made barricade fighting such as that of 1830 and 1848 much harder.\n\nHis second achievement was in public health and sanitation. Working with the engineer Eugène Belgrand, Haussmann brought clean spring water into Paris from the Dhuis and Vanne valleys, stored it in covered reservoirs, and built a large underground sewer network that carried waste away from the centre. Paris had been among the worst-served capitals in Europe for water and drainage, and these works reduced the disease that had flourished in the overcrowded districts. This was arguably the most lasting benefit of his work for ordinary Parisians.\n\nThird, Haussmann gave Paris parks, markets and public buildings. With the engineer Alphand he redesigned the Bois de Boulogne in the west, laid out the Bois de Vincennes in the east to balance it, and turned a former quarry into the Buttes-Chaumont park, which opened in 1867. Victor Baltard built the new iron-and-glass pavilions of Les Halles, the railway stations were rebuilt and linked by the new streets, and Garnier's Opéra, begun in 1861, became a symbol of the imperial city. In 1860 he also oversaw the annexation of the suburbs, which raised the number of arrondissements from twelve to twenty and let him plan the whole urban area. Paris became the model for city planning across Europe, as Vienna's Ringstrasse and Barcelona's Eixample show.\n\nHaussmann's achievement had serious limits. Demolition displaced many working-class families, rents rose in the new central districts, and the poor were pushed into eastern districts such as Belleville, sharpening the division between east and west. The works were paid for by heavy borrowing, and Jules Ferry attacked the hidden accounts in 1868. Haussmann was dismissed in January 1870 when the liberal ministry of Ollivier would not keep so unpopular a prefect.\n\nCharles Darwin's achievement was of a different kind. Born in Shrewsbury in 1809, he served as naturalist on HMS Beagle from 1831 to 1836, collecting fossils and specimens in South America, the Galápagos and Australia, and observing how species varied. After years of study he developed the theory of evolution by natural selection, which holds that individuals with variations suited to their environment survive and reproduce more successfully. He delayed publication for about twenty years, until Alfred Russel Wallace reached the same idea, and then published On the Origin of Species in November 1859. It argued that all living things share common ancestors and that species change over time.\n\nDarwin extended his case in The Descent of Man in 1871, which applied the theory to human origins. His ideas were fiercely contested. Thomas Henry Huxley defended him, while Bishop Wilberforce and many churchmen opposed him at the Oxford debate of 1860, so that Darwin forced a lasting debate about the relationship between science and religion. His theory was later strengthened by genetics, which explained inheritance, and he was buried in Westminster Abbey in 1882, a mark of the respect he had won.\n\nBoth men achieved a great deal. Haussmann's work was immediate and visible, solving real problems of health and traffic and setting the pattern for modern cities, though at the expense of the poor and the public purse. Darwin's was the greater intellectual achievement, since he changed biology permanently and altered how humanity saw its place in nature, and it is his legacy that has proved the more enduring.",
