    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Between 1871 and 1920 all three of these themes show a new, more modern Europe straining against old institutions. In Italy the loss of Rome by the papacy created a lasting quarrel between state and Church. In France anti-Semitism exploded into the Dreyfus Affair and divided the Republic. In Russia a backward peasant economy, pushed into rapid industrialisation, produced a crisis that helped bring down the tsarist regime.\n\nChurch and State in Italy began with the completion of unification. On 20 September 1870 Italian troops entered Rome and the Pope lost his temporal power. In May 1871 the Law of Guarantees declared the Pope's person inviolable and gave him the Vatican and Lateran palaces and an annual income. Pius IX rejected it, refused to recognise the Italian state and called himself a prisoner in the Vatican. This deadlock became known as the Roman Question. It divided Italians between a liberal, anticlerical state and a largely Catholic population.\n\nThe tension shaped Italian politics for decades. The non expedit instruction, issued in 1868 and reinforced in 1874, forbade Catholics to vote in or stand for parliamentary elections, so most Catholics stayed out of national politics, though they remained active in local government. The state responded by expanding civil marriage and secular education and by closing religious orders, while Catholics built their own world of Catholic Action, banks, newspapers and cooperatives. Leo XIII, pope from 1878, kept the prisoner stance, so the question stayed open. The ban was relaxed only gradually, in the Gentiloni Pact of 1913 when Catholic voters backed Giolitti's liberal candidates, and in 1919 when Don Luigi Sturzo founded the Catholic Popular Party. The dispute was finally settled by the Lateran Treaties of 1929, long after the period ends.\n\nIn France anti-Semitism grew after the humiliation of 1871, as nationalists blamed Jews for the country's weakness. Edouard Drumont's La France juive (1886) was a bestseller, and in 1892 he founded the paper La Libre Parole. The crisis came in October 1894 when Captain Alfred Dreyfus, a Jewish artillery officer from Alsace, was arrested for passing secrets to Germany. He was convicted by a secret court martial in December 1894 and sent to Devil's Island. In 1896 Colonel Picquart discovered that the real author of the incriminating memorandum was Major Esterhazy, but the army suppressed the evidence and Esterhazy was acquitted in January 1898.\n\nThe Affair then divided France. On 13 January 1898 Emile Zola published J'accuse in L'Aurore, accusing the army of a cover-up, and he was convicted of libel and fled to England. Dreyfusards, mainly republicans, socialists such as Jaures and many intellectuals, faced anti-Dreyfusards, who included the army, much of the Catholic Church, nationalists and monarchists. A second court martial at Rennes in 1899 found Dreyfus guilty again with extenuating circumstances, and President Loubet pardoned him. In 1906 the court of appeals cleared him and he was reinstated. The Affair damaged the reputation of the army and the Church and led to the anticlerical measures that ended in the separation of Church and state in 1905. It also influenced Theodor Herzl, who published Der Judenstaat in 1896, and the First Zionist Congress at Basel in 1897.\n\nEconomic crisis in Tsarist Russia had deep roots. Serfdom was abolished in 1861, but peasants had to pay redemption payments over 49 years and most remained in the mir, with small plots and backward methods. About 80 per cent of the population were peasants. Sergei Witte, Finance Minister from 1892, then drove a great spurt of industrialisation. The Trans-Siberian Railway began in 1891, the rouble went onto the gold standard in 1897 and French loans financed mines and ironworks, with coal and iron growing in the Donbas and oil at Baku. But the cost fell on the peasants through heavy indirect taxes, and grain was exported even when people were hungry. The harvest failure of 1891 brought a famine across the Volga region, followed by cholera, and slow government relief weakened trust in the regime. The depression of 1900-03 added unemployment and strikes in huge factories such as the Putilov works.\n\nThis economic strain fed political crisis. Defeat by Japan in 1904-05 and Bloody Sunday on 22 January 1905 led to the 1905 Revolution, and the October Manifesto promised a Duma. Stolypin's land reform of 1906 tried to create prosperous peasant proprietors, and about a quarter of households had left the communes by 1914, but most peasants stayed in the mir. The First World War then overloaded the railways, caused inflation and left cities short of food and fuel by winter 1916-17. Bread riots by women in Petrograd in March 1917 brought the Tsar's abdication.\n\nOverall, the three cases show how unresolved tensions could dominate a period. In Italy the Roman Question was a long, slow conflict that was managed rather than solved. In France the Dreyfus Affair ended in justice for one man and a republican victory, though the prejudice remained. In Russia the economic crisis was the most serious, because the regime's attempt to modernise without reform ended in revolution in 1917.",
