    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The motor car had a profound effect on Europe between its invention in the 1880s and 1920, but the effect was greater in industry, technology and war than in everyday life. Before 1914 it was mainly a luxury for the rich and a spur to economic and legal change. By 1920 it had altered transport and warfare, though its mass social impact belonged largely to the years after 1920.\n\nThe car was a European invention. Nikolaus Otto built the first practical four-stroke internal combustion engine in 1876. Gottlieb Daimler and Wilhelm Maybach developed a light, high-speed petrol engine and fitted it to a motorcycle in 1885 and a carriage in 1886. Karl Benz, working independently in Mannheim, built a three-wheeled Motorwagen and patented it in 1886, the first practical car designed as a whole. In August 1888 Bertha Benz drove one about 106 km from Mannheim to Pforzheim, buying fuel at a pharmacy in Wiesloch, and the publicity helped to prove the car's usefulness. Germany and France led early production: Panhard et Levassor and Peugeot began building cars with Daimler engines in 1890-91, and Louis Renault built his first car in 1898. John Boyd Dunlop's pneumatic tyre of 1888, and the Michelin detachable tyre of 1891, made motoring smoother and more practical.\n\nThe car had a major economic effect. The new industry created demand for steel, rubber, glass, petrol and machine tools, and it created work in manufacturing, garages and repair. Oil companies and tyre firms expanded. In 1901 the Mercedes, with a lighter engine and lower body, set the pattern for later cars, and Rolls-Royce's Silver Ghost of 1906 made Britain a leader in luxury cars. The greatest change in method came from the United States, where Henry Ford introduced the moving assembly line at Highland Park in 1913. It cut the time to build a chassis from over twelve hours to about ninety minutes and brought the Model T, launched in 1908, from about 850 dollars to under 300 by the early 1920s. In January 1914 he raised pay to five dollars a day. European firms studied and copied these methods of mass production.\n\nGovernments and society had to adapt. Roads were improved and tarred for motor traffic, and states introduced traffic laws, licences and speed limits. In Britain the red flag law kept vehicles to walking pace until the Locomotives on Highways Act of 1896 raised the limit to 12 mph, celebrated by the Emancipation Run from London to Brighton. The Motor Car Act of 1903 brought registration and a 20 mph limit in Britain and Ireland, and the Gordon Bennett race was held in Kildare and Carlow that year. Early motorists faced hostility from pedestrians, farmers and cyclists. Cars were status symbols for the rich, giving rise to motoring clubs and early tourism, though doctors, salesmen and country priests also used them before 1914.\n\nThe First World War showed the car's military importance. About 600 Paris taxis carried troops to the battle of the Marne in September 1914, and motor lorries kept Verdun supplied along the Sacred Way in 1916. Cars and lorries served as ambulances and transport, and the war led to the tank and armoured car and to more powerful engines. After the war, ex-army lorries and cheap cars allowed bus services and road haulage to compete with the railways in the 1920s.\n\nOverall, the effect on Europe by 1920 was real but limited. The car boosted industry, forced governments to modernise roads and laws, and transformed military transport. Yet it remained too expensive for most Europeans and was far less common than in the United States, where the Model T made it part of everyday life. Germany and France invented the car, but the American mass production method made it a mass consumer product, and its full social effect on Europe came after 1920.",
