    "model": "The fascist regimes of inter-war Europe, above all Mussolini's Italy and Hitler's Germany, shared a set of characteristics. They were built on extreme nationalism and a single all-powerful leader, they ran one-party totalitarian states which destroyed opposition by violence and terror, and they used propaganda and mass organisations to win consent. They also glorified war and expansion and promised to replace class conflict with national unity.\n\nFascism grew where war, economic crisis and the fear of revolution had left people frightened and angry. In Italy the sense of a mutilated victory, weak coalition governments (five prime ministers between 1919 and 1922) and the strikes and factory occupations of the Biennio Rosso led landowners, industrialists and the middle classes to look to Mussolini. In Germany the Treaty of Versailles, inflation and the Depression played the same role for Hitler. Both movements drew on ex-servicemen and the lower middle class, and both were fiercely hostile to communism, socialism and liberal democracy, which they blamed for class conflict and national weakness. Extreme nationalism was the heart of fascism, placing the nation above the individual.\n\nA second characteristic was the leader principle. Mussolini became Il Duce and Hitler became Führer, each presented as the embodiment of the nation. Mussolini's rule was based on his own responsibility only to the King after the Leggi Fascistissime of 1925 and 1926, which allowed him to rule by decree. After Hindenburg died on 2 August 1934, Hitler combined the offices of Chancellor and President as Führer, and soldiers swore a personal oath of loyalty to him rather than to the constitution. A personality cult surrounded both men, with Mussolini shown harvesting grain and making balcony speeches from the Palazzo Venezia, and slogans such as 'Mussolini is always right'.\n\nBoth regimes were one-party, totalitarian states. Mussolini's formula was 'everything within the state, nothing outside the state, nothing against the state'. Opposition parties were banned in Italy in 1925 and 1926 and the press was censored, while elected mayors were replaced by appointed podesta. In Germany the Enabling Act of 23 March 1933 let the cabinet legislate without the Reichstag, trade unions were abolished in May 1933 and the law of 14 July made the NSDAP the only legal party. Gleichschaltung brought the civil service, courts, universities and state governments under Nazi control. By 1928 Italian voters could only accept or reject a single list drawn up by the Fascist Grand Council, so elections were without real choice.\n\nViolence and terror kept these regimes in place. The squadristi beat up socialists and trade unionists before 1922, and the murder of the socialist deputy Giacomo Matteotti in 1924 showed what happened to critics. Mussolini created the OVRA secret police, a Special Tribunal and internal exile on islands such as Lipari. In Germany the Gestapo under Himmler and Heydrich could arrest without legal challenge, Dachau opened in March 1933 as the first concentration camp, and the Night of the Long Knives, from 30 June to 2 July 1934, saw Röhm and other opponents murdered. Fascists glorified violence, and uniformed squads, black shirts and brown shirts, were a feature of both movements.\n\nPropaganda and control of young people were the means of building consent. In Italy the Opera Nazionale Balilla brought children into state youth groups, the Dopolavoro supervised workers' leisure and Minculpop controlled the press, radio and film from 1937. In Germany Goebbels used rallies, film and radio, and Hitler Youth organised the young. Both regimes also made use of anti-democratic ideas about race, and the Nazis in particular made racial hatred a central doctrine, passing the Nuremberg Laws of 1935 against Jews, while Italy introduced its own racial laws in 1938.\n\nEconomic and foreign policy followed from the same ideas. Mussolini's corporate state, with the Rocco Law of 1926 and the Charter of Labour of 1927, banned strikes and claimed to unite employers and workers, though it was more theory than practice. Nazi Germany pursued rearmament and autarky through the Four Year Plan of 1936. Both regimes sought expansion: Italy invaded Abyssinia in October 1935, and Hitler's drive for living space led to the Anschluss, the occupation of Czechoslovakia and the invasion of Poland. The Rome-Berlin Axis of 1936 and the Pact of Steel of 1939 bound them together.\n\nThe characteristics of fascist regimes were therefore extreme nationalism, a dictator and cult of the leader, a one-party totalitarian state, terror, propaganda, state control of the economy and aggressive foreign policy. The Nazi regime went furthest, especially in racial policy, but the Italian model showed the pattern first.",
