    "label": "2",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Stalin transformed the Soviet economy through the Five-Year Plans and collectivisation, which turned the Soviet Union into an industrial power at enormous human cost. He also used show trials and purges to remove rivals and scapegoats, and together these gave him a position of total personal power by 1938.\n\nStalin's rise to power gave him the means to change the economy. He became General Secretary in April 1922, which gave him control of party appointments, and he outmanoeuvred his rivals after Lenin's death in January 1924. He first allied with Zinoviev and Kamenev against Trotsky, who was expelled from the party in 1927 and exiled in 1929, and he then used the Right Opposition of Bukharin, Rykov and Tomsky against the Left. In 1928 and 1929 he turned against the Right and adopted the Left's policy of rapid industrialisation. By 1929 both factions were removed from political influence, so he was free to end the New Economic Policy and launch the First Five-Year Plan.\n\nThe Five-Year Plans were the first part of the economic transformation. Gosplan drew up the First Five-Year Plan, launched in October 1928, with high targets for coal, steel, electricity, oil and machinery, and heavy industry was given priority over consumer goods. New industrial cities were built, such as Magnitogorsk in the southern Urals. The Stakhanovite movement, begun after Alexei Stakhanov was reported to have cut 102 tonnes of coal in a shift in August 1935, was used to push workers to higher output. Steel output rose from about 4 million tonnes in 1928 to about 18 million in 1940, though official figures were inflated. The costs were heavy, with wages falling, housing overcrowded, quality poor and forced labour used on major projects. The industrial base nonetheless helped the Soviet Union to produce the weapons that defeated Germany.\n\nCollectivisation transformed agriculture. In late 1929 Stalin announced the liquidation of the kulaks as a class, and peasants were forced into collective farms (kolkhozy) where land and livestock were pooled under state control. Richer peasants were deported or killed in dekulakisation. Peasants resisted by slaughtering their livestock, and grain requisitions left villages short of food. The harvest failures and requisitions of 1932 and 1933 produced a famine that killed several million, mainly in Ukraine, the North Caucasus and Kazakhstan, and the authorities denied the famine and restricted movement to stop starving peasants reaching the towns. Collectivisation gave the state control of the grain supply to feed the cities and fund industry, but output of grain and meat fell for several years.\n\nThe human and political costs of these policies led to the show trials. The failures and shortages of the plans created a need for scapegoats, and earlier trials, such as the Shakhty trial of 1928 and the Industrial Party trial of 1930, blamed engineers for sabotage. The shooting of Sergei Kirov, the Leningrad party leader, on 1 December 1934 gave Stalin the pretext for a wider repression. A decree issued the same day ordered terrorism cases to be heard in days, with no defence and immediate execution. Stalin claimed that a conspiracy existed, and he used the claim to justify the trials, even though historians still disagree about whether he arranged the murder.\n\nThree great Moscow trials followed. In August 1936 Zinoviev and Kamenev, two of Lenin's closest colleagues, were tried with fourteen others for forming a terrorist centre under Trotsky and for murdering Kirov; all confessed and were shot. In January 1937 Pyatakov and Radek were tried for industrial sabotage, and thirteen were shot. In March 1938 Bukharin, Rykov and the former NKVD chief Yagoda were among twenty-one defendants tried for treason and spying, and eighteen, including Bukharin, were shot. The trials were stage-managed by the prosecutor Vyshinsky and the NKVD, who obtained confessions through sleep deprivation, threats against families and false promises of leniency. In secret, the army was purged too, and Marshal Tukhachevsky was shot in June 1937 after a closed trial, with perhaps 30,000 to 35,000 officers dismissed or arrested.\n\nThe trials achieved Stalin's purpose of removing the old Bolsheviks who could have been an alternative leadership, and they frightened everyone else into obedience, while blaming the economic problems on enemies. The 1936 Constitution and a cult of personality, with the NKVD and Gulag behind it, completed the totalitarian state. By 1938 Stalin had remade the Soviet economy by force and used terror to ensure that no one could challenge him.",
