    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Britain between 1920 and 1945 faced two very different sets of challenges. In peacetime the challenge was economic and social: mass unemployment, the decline of the old staple industries, industrial conflict and the hardship of the unemployed. In war the challenge was survival: defeat in France, invasion and bombing, the mobilisation of an entire population and the question of what kind of country should emerge from the conflict.\n\nThe first peacetime challenge was the collapse of the post-war boom. Demand surged in 1919 and 1920, but prices then collapsed and unemployment rose above two million by mid-1921 as export markets were lost. The government responded with economy rather than expansion, and the Geddes Axe of 1922 cut spending on education, housing and the armed forces. Industrial relations made matters worse. When the coal owners tried to cut wages in 1921, the railwaymen and transport workers of the Triple Alliance refused to strike in support on Black Friday, 15 April, and the miners were left to be defeated.\n\nThe second challenge was the crisis of the staple industries and the conflict that came with it. In April 1925 Churchill, as Chancellor, returned sterling to the gold standard at the pre-war rate of about 4.86 dollars to the pound. Keynes attacked the decision, arguing that an overvalued pound would make exports uncompetitive and force wages down. The coal owners did demand lower wages and longer hours, the miners were locked out, and the TUC called the General Strike from 4 to 12 May 1926, the only nationwide strike in British history. The government used volunteers and the Organisation for the Maintenance of Supplies, the TUC called the strike off after nine days, and the miners were forced back on worse terms. The Trade Disputes Act of 1927 then banned sympathetic strikes.\n\nThe third challenge was the Depression and the regional divide it exposed. Coal, cotton, shipbuilding and iron and steel declined sharply, and unemployment was concentrated in South Wales, the north of England and central Scotland, often above 30 per cent in the early 1930s. Nationally it peaked at about three million in 1932 and 1933. The May Committee forecast a deficit of about 120 million pounds in 1931, the Labour Cabinet split over cutting unemployment benefit, and Ramsay MacDonald formed a National Government in August. Britain left the gold standard on 21 September 1931. The government's answers were modest: tariffs under the Import Duties Act of 1932, cheap money with Bank Rate at 2 per cent, and the Special Areas Act of 1934 with limited funds. The household means test, hated by the unemployed, and the hunger marches of the National Unemployed Workers' Movement showed how deep the hardship was, while the south-east prospered with new light industries. Recovery in the depressed regions only came with rearmament from 1936.\n\nWar brought a different order of challenge. After the fall of France in May and June 1940 Britain stood alone, and Churchill, Prime Minister from 10 May, had to hold the country together. Operation Dynamo brought over 330,000 troops off the beaches of Dunkirk, and his speeches, such as their finest hour, sustained morale. The Battle of Britain from July to October 1940 was won by Fighter Command with Spitfires, Hurricanes and the Chain Home radar system, and Operation Sea Lion was abandoned. Britain then had to survive the Blitz, which began on 7 September 1940, bombed London for 57 consecutive nights and killed about 40,000 civilians, with cities such as Coventry, Liverpool and Belfast also heavily hit. The U-boat campaign threatened supplies in the Atlantic until convoys and Ultra codebreaking at Bletchley Park reduced losses from 1943.\n\nThe Home Front posed its own challenges, and the state met them by organising society on an unprecedented scale. Children were evacuated from the cities, the blackout was enforced, Anderson and Morrison shelters were issued, and the Home Guard and air raid wardens were recruited. Rationing and the Ministry of Food spread scarce supplies fairly. Ernest Bevin as Minister of Labour directed workers into war industry, women were conscripted under the National Service Act of 1941, and Land Girls raised home food production. The Beveridge Report of December 1942 then addressed the future, naming want, disease, ignorance, squalor and idleness as the giants to be defeated, and its ideas were largely carried out by the Labour government after 1945.\n\nOverall, the interwar challenges of unemployment, industrial conflict and regional decline were handled only partly, with unemployment staying above a million for most of the 1930s, while the challenges of war were met with far greater resolve and organisation. The irony is that the war achieved what peacetime policy had not: it ended mass unemployment and produced the planning and welfare state that the failures of the interwar years had made people demand.",
