    "label": "1",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The Cold War in Europe developed in stages. It began as a breakdown of the wartime alliance between 1945 and 1949 and hardened into two armed blocs and a divided Germany. It then moved through dangerous crises, notably Berlin and Cuba, into a period of détente in the 1970s, a second Cold War in the early 1980s, and finally an end brought about by Gorbachev's reforms and the revolutions of 1989.\n\nThe origins lay in the strains between the Allies as Germany was defeated. At Yalta in February 1945 Churchill, Roosevelt and Stalin agreed on occupation zones for Germany and promised free elections in liberated Europe, but the Red Army occupied Poland, Romania, Bulgaria, Hungary and eastern Germany, and Stalin wanted a buffer of friendly states after invasions in 1914 and 1941. At Potsdam in July 1945 Truman and Stalin argued over reparations and the German-Polish border. Churchill's Iron Curtain speech at Fulton in March 1946 and Kennan's Long Telegram named the Soviet Union as a threat, while Stalin treated the West as hostile. Communists in the East used coalitions and then force to take over, with the February 1948 coup in Czechoslovakia the clearest case.\n\nThe Cold War was then hardened by American commitments and the division of Germany. The Truman Doctrine of March 1947 gave aid to Greece and Turkey, and the Marshall Plan of June 1947 offered about 13 billion dollars to rebuild Western Europe. Stalin forbade eastern states to accept and set up the Cominform in 1947 and Comecon in 1949. The Western zones were merged, a new Deutsche Mark was introduced in June 1948, and the Soviet Union blockaded West Berlin. The Western airlift supplied the city until May 1949, and the crisis led directly to NATO in 1949 and to the creation of the Federal Republic in May and the German Democratic Republic in October 1949. Europe was now split into two camps.\n\nThe Cold War deepened in the 1950s as the blocs were militarised. The Korean War from 1950 persuaded Western governments that communism was expansionist and led to NATO rearmament and the push to rearm West Germany. When West Germany joined NATO in 1955 the Soviet Union formed the Warsaw Pact. The Hungarian Uprising of 1956 showed what that meant, because the Soviet Union crushed it by force, and the West did nothing despite its talk of liberation. Khrushchev's talk of peaceful coexistence after 1956 therefore sat alongside real confrontation.\n\nThe most dangerous phase came between 1958 and 1962. Khrushchev's ultimatum on Berlin of November 1958 was followed by the collapse of the Paris summit in May 1960 after the U-2 was shot down, and by the building of the Berlin Wall on 13 August 1961 to stop East Germans, some 2.7 million of whom had left since 1945, from fleeing to the West. In October 1962 the discovery of Soviet missiles in Cuba brought the superpowers to the brink of nuclear war until Khrushchev agreed on 28 October to withdraw them in return for a pledge not to invade Cuba. The crisis showed how close war had come, and it produced the Moscow-Washington hotline in 1963 and the Partial Test Ban Treaty that August.\n\nThe tension then eased into détente. Willy Brandt's Ostpolitik produced the Treaty of Moscow in 1970, the Four Power Agreement on Berlin in 1971 and the Basic Treaty between the two Germanies in 1972, and the superpowers signed SALT I in May 1972. The Helsinki Final Act of August 1975, signed by 35 states, recognised existing borders and committed them to human rights, which gave dissidents in the East a standard to appeal to, as Charter 77 later did. Détente survived even the invasion of Czechoslovakia in 1968, which confirmed the Brezhnev Doctrine, because both sides wanted stability.\n\nDétente collapsed at the end of the 1970s. Soviet SS-20 missiles aimed at Western Europe led NATO in December 1979 to decide to deploy Pershing II and cruise missiles, the Soviet Union invaded Afghanistan on 24 December 1979, and Reagan took a hard line, calling the Soviet Union an evil empire in 1983 and announcing the Strategic Defense Initiative. Huge peace demonstrations followed in West Germany and Britain, and the Able Archer exercise of November 1983 caused real Soviet alarm. The Cold War then ended quickly. Gorbachev, in power from March 1985, met Reagan at Geneva and Reykjavik and signed the INF Treaty in December 1987, and he refused to use force in eastern Europe, so communist rule collapsed in 1989 and the Berlin Wall fell. Germany was reunified in October 1990, and the Soviet Union dissolved in December 1991.\n\nIn short, the Cold War developed from an alliance breakdown into a divided continent, passed through its peak in Berlin and Cuba, was eased by détente and revived in the early 1980s, and ended because the Soviet economy and system could no longer sustain the competition and Gorbachev would not use force to preserve it. Its central feature throughout was confrontation without direct war between the superpowers.",
