    "label": "2",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Communist governments in Europe faced the same basic economic challenges from 1945 to 1992: rebuilding after the war, keeping up with the West, feeding and supplying their populations, and paying for the arms race. They first handled these by copying Stalin's central planning, then tried partial reforms, and in the 1980s they were overwhelmed, because they were unwilling or unable to change the system itself.\n\nTheir first response was to impose the Soviet model. Gosplan drew up five-year plans that set output targets and gave priority to heavy industry, steel, coal and armaments over consumer goods and housing. In eastern Europe industry, banks and large estates were nationalised in 1945 to 1948, plans on the Soviet pattern began in 1949 to 1951, and new industrial towns such as Nowa Huta in Poland and Stalinstadt in East Germany were built. Farms were collectivised in most countries, although most Polish farms stayed private. After Stalin refused Marshall aid for the East, Comecon was founded in January 1949 to coordinate trade, and eastern Europe's trade turned towards the Soviet Union, which supplied cheap raw materials and took manufactured goods. This brought rapid industrial growth and reconstruction, but at a cost, since reparations and dismantling slowed recovery in East Germany and the economies were made dependent on Moscow.\n\nThe model produced chronic problems that governments struggled to solve. Targets set in gross output encouraged factories to make heavy, low-quality goods that consumers did not want, prices were fixed by the state, and managers had little incentive to innovate. Shortages of meat, housing and cars, queues and black markets became normal, and East Germans waited years for a Trabant. Agriculture was unproductive, and the Soviet Union had to import grain from the 1960s. Heavy industry also caused severe pollution, in northern Bohemia and Upper Silesia for example. The economies grew by adding workers and raw materials, and when new labour ran out in the 1970s they could not shift to growth based on technology and productivity.\n\nSeveral governments responded with reform, but nearly always in a limited way. After Stalin's death Khrushchev promised to catch up with the USA, launched the Virgin Lands campaign in 1954 and a mass housebuilding programme, and in 1957 decentralised industry to regional councils, but harvests failed, notably in 1963 when grain had to be imported, and his decentralisation was reversed after his fall in 1964. Kosygin's reform of 1965 gave enterprises more control of profits but left central control of investment and was abandoned within a few years. Hungary's New Economic Mechanism of 1968 under Kadar allowed limited market prices and private enterprise in services, and this goulash communism gave Hungarians more consumer goods than other bloc citizens. Czechoslovakia's New Economic Model of the 1960s, devised by Ota Sik, was ended by the invasion of 1968, which also discouraged experiment elsewhere. Reform was therefore tolerated only where the party's control was not threatened.\n\nUnder Brezhnev, from 1964 to 1982, the leadership avoided reform altogether and growth slowed in what became known as stagnation. The Soviet Union used hard-currency earnings from oil and gas exports after the price rises of 1973 and 1979 to buy Western grain and technology and postpone change, while military spending, including the burden of the war in Afghanistan from 1979, consumed a large share of output. Corruption, blat and the black market became part of daily life, and living standards improved only slowly. Poland tried another route, borrowing from Western banks. Gierek, who replaced Gomulka after the food price riots in Gdansk in December 1970, imported machinery and consumer goods, but the new factories were not competitive, and by 1980 the debt exceeded 20 billion dollars. When prices rose again in July 1980 strikes spread to Gdansk and led to Solidarity.\n\nThe last attempt came from Gorbachev, who became General Secretary in March 1985. After uskorenie, or acceleration, failed, he introduced perestroika, a restructuring that combined limited market elements with planning, together with glasnost. Falling oil prices in the mid-1980s removed the support the Soviet budget had relied on, and the half-reforms produced dislocation without growth. The economic gap with the West had grown too wide to hide, since eastern Europeans could see how much better Western consumers lived. When Gorbachev would not use force to defend communist governments, the economic failure turned into political collapse in 1989, and the Soviet Union itself dissolved in December 1991.\n\nOverall, communist governments met their economic challenges with a mixture of mobilisation, partial reform and borrowing, but never abandoned the central planning that caused the problems. Reforms were abandoned when they threatened party control, and Western loans and Soviet energy exports postponed rather than solved the difficulty. That failure to adapt was a main reason why the communist system fell between 1989 and 1991.",
