    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Margaret Thatcher's leadership was highly effective in transforming Britain's economy and in raising Britain's standing abroad, but the price was high. At home she reversed the post-war consensus, broke trade union power and restored the market, though at the cost of mass unemployment and deindustrialisation. Abroad she achieved real successes with the Falklands and the Cold War alliance, while her stance on Europe was effective in winning a budget rebate yet ended by dividing her party and bringing her down.\n\nThe central aim of her domestic policy was to defeat inflation, which had risen sharply after the oil shocks of 1973 and 1979. Thatcher, who became Prime Minister on 4 May 1979, adopted monetarism, the belief that controlling the money supply was the key to stable prices. Public spending was cut, and the government abandoned the post-war commitment to full employment. By the mid-1980s inflation had fallen considerably, so on its own terms the policy was effective. However, the recession of the early 1980s hit manufacturing in the north, Scotland and Wales hardest, and unemployment passed three million in 1982, with riots in English cities in 1981. Her economic leadership therefore succeeded in its main goal but at a heavy social cost.\n\nThatcher was also determined to reduce the power of the trade unions, which she blamed for Britain's decline. The Employment Acts of 1980 and 1982 and the Trade Union Act of 1984 restricted secondary picketing and the closed shop and required ballots. The decisive test was the miners' strike of March 1984 to March 1985. When Ian MacGregor of the National Coal Board announced pit closures, Arthur Scargill's National Union of Mineworkers struck without a national ballot. The government had built up coal stocks beforehand and used the police, as at Orgreave in June 1984, and the strike ended in defeat for the miners. This was perhaps her most effective act of leadership, since it permanently weakened the union movement, though it also hastened the closure of mines and left bitter divisions in mining communities.\n\nA third strand of her domestic programme was to roll back the state and spread ownership. State assets were sold off, including British Telecom in 1984, and tenants were allowed to buy their council houses under the right to buy of 1980. Exchange controls were removed in 1979 and the City was deregulated in 1986. These measures created a more flexible, market-driven economy and widened share and home ownership, and the Conservatives won the elections of 1979, 1983 and 1987, making her the longest-serving British prime minister of the twentieth century. Critics, however, argue that the decline of manufacturing was a lasting cost and that regional inequality widened. Historians also debate whether she made a decisive break with the post-war settlement or accelerated trends already under way.\n\nIn foreign affairs her leadership was decisive in the Falklands War. After Argentina invaded the islands on 2 April 1982, she sent a task force and Britain won on 14 June 1982. The victory restored national confidence and helped to secure her re-election in 1983. She also built a close partnership with President Ronald Reagan, backing the deployment of American cruise missiles at Greenham Common and allowing American aircraft to use British bases in the 1986 raid on Libya. Nicknamed the Iron Lady by the Soviet press in 1976, she nonetheless showed flexibility towards Moscow, meeting Gorbachev in December 1984 and saying he was someone she could do business with, and she helped to persuade Reagan that he was sincere. This was skilful diplomacy that raised Britain's influence beyond its real economic weight.\n\nHer record in Europe and Ireland was more mixed. She was effective in the budget dispute, negotiating the Fontainebleau rebate of June 1984, which reduced Britain's net contribution to the EEC. She accepted the Single European Act in 1986 because it advanced the single market. Yet in her Bruges speech of 20 September 1988 she attacked plans for a federal Europe, and she opposed economic and monetary union and the Social Charter, while Britain joined the Exchange Rate Mechanism only in October 1990. In November 1985 she signed the Anglo-Irish Agreement with Garret FitzGerald, giving Dublin a consultative role in Northern Ireland. Her stand on Europe eventually isolated her: Geoffrey Howe resigned in November 1990, Michael Heseltine challenged her, and she resigned on 22 November 1990, with John Major succeeding her.\n\nOverall, Thatcher was a very effective leader in the sense that she set clear goals and achieved most of them: inflation was reduced, the unions were defeated, the economy was liberalised and Britain regained prestige abroad. Her effectiveness was greatest where she faced opponents head-on, in the miners' strike and the Falklands. It was weakest where her conviction became rigidity, in the human cost of unemployment and in the disputes over Europe that finally ended her premiership.",
