    "label": "2",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Harry Truman was the more successful handler of US foreign policy. Truman built the framework of containment that held Soviet power in check in Europe, and although Korea ended in stalemate it did not become a wider war. Lyndon Johnson, by contrast, turned Vietnam into an American war that he could not win, and it destroyed his presidency. Truman's record has flaws, but Johnson's foreign policy was a failure on its own terms.\n\nTruman's greatest achievement was to commit the United States to resisting Soviet expansion after 1945. The Truman Doctrine of 12 March 1947 pledged support to free peoples resisting armed minorities or outside pressure, beginning with about 400 million dollars of aid to Greece and Turkey. It followed George Kennan's Long Telegram of 1946 and gave containment its practical form. The Marshall Plan of 1948 to 1952 then poured about 13 billion dollars into Western Europe, rebuilding economies that might otherwise have turned to communism and creating markets for American goods. The decision to end isolation and act decisively was a clear success.\n\nTruman also handled the crises in Europe with skill. When Stalin blockaded West Berlin on 24 June 1948, Truman chose an airlift rather than an armed convoy that might have started a war. More than two million tons of supplies were flown in, and the Soviets lifted the blockade on 12 May 1949, a humiliation for Moscow. The North Atlantic Treaty of April 1949 gave Western Europe a collective defence guarantee in Article 5. Truman had stopped Soviet expansion in Europe without a shot being fired, which is a striking contrast with Johnson's experience.\n\nIn Asia Truman's record was more mixed. China fell to Mao Zedong in October 1949, and Republicans attacked him with the question of who had lost China. When North Korea invaded in June 1950 he sent American forces under a United Nations flag, without a declaration of war, and saved South Korea. The war went badly after Chinese intervention in late 1950, and Truman dismissed General MacArthur in April 1951 for demanding a wider war against China, a decision that cost him popularity. The armistice at Panmunjom in July 1953 left the frontier near the 38th parallel and about 36,000 Americans dead. Yet South Korea survived and Truman kept the war limited, so Korea can be judged a limited success.\n\nJohnson inherited a commitment to South Vietnam, with about 16,000 advisers, and a government in chaos after Diem's overthrow in November 1963. He feared that losing Vietnam would repeat the damage done to Truman over China, and he chose escalation. The Gulf of Tonkin Resolution of August 1964, passed 416 to 0 in the House and 88 to 2 in the Senate, gave him wide powers, though the second attack on which it rested probably never happened. Operation Rolling Thunder began in March 1965 and Marines landed at Da Nang that month. By July 1965 he announced troop increases without asking Congress for a declaration of war, and numbers rose to over 500,000 by 1968.\n\nThe strategy failed. Westmoreland's search and destroy and the body count could not break an enemy supplied down the Ho Chi Minh Trail and able to replace its losses. The Saigon government remained weak and the war was limited by fears of China, so Hanoi was never forced to negotiate. The Tet Offensive of 30 January 1968 was a military defeat for the communists but shattered the optimism Westmoreland had expressed in November 1967. The credibility gap grew, Walter Cronkite called the war a stalemate, and on 31 March 1968 Johnson halted part of the bombing and announced that he would not seek re-election. The war also cost him his Great Society, since guns and butter produced deficits and inflation.\n\nJohnson's real achievements, the Civil Rights Act of 1964 and the Voting Rights Act of 1965, were domestic. In foreign policy his legacy was about 58,000 American dead, a war ended without victory in 1973 and a lasting loss of public trust. Truman's policies defined the Western alliance for forty years and he avoided disaster in Berlin and Korea. Truman was therefore much the more successful, with Korea his weakest point, while Johnson's handling of Vietnam was a failure.",
