    "model": "Katanga broke away because its leaders wanted to keep the revenue from the copper and cobalt mines run by the Belgian company Union Minière. Moïse Tshombe's Conakat party also argued that the Katangan people had a separate identity, and Tshombe feared a central government controlled by Lumumba and Communists. Belgium sent troops back into the Congo and supported the secession with officers, while foreign mercenaries served in the Katanga Gendarmerie. The Soviet Union supplied transport aircraft to Lumumba's government, which heightened Western concern about Cold War involvement in the Congo. The UN Security Council authorised the ONUC operation to restore the Congo's territory, and UN forces fought the secession until Tshombe ended it in January 1963.",
