    "label": "C3",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Carson opposed Home Rule because he feared that a Dublin parliament would be dominated by the Catholic Church, the so-called Rome Rule. He also feared economic loss, since Belfast's linen and shipbuilding industries depended on British and imperial markets and could suffer from tariffs. He believed Home Rule was the first step towards separation from Britain, and he defended the Protestant minority, who would be vulnerable under nationalist rule. He spoke at the huge unionist rally at Craigavon near Belfast in September 1911 and led the Ulster Day rally on 28 September 1912. On Ulster Day about 237,000 men signed the Solemn League and Covenant to defeat Home Rule, and Carson signed it first at Belfast City Hall. In 1913 he backed the Ulster Volunteer Force, and the Ulster Unionist Council announced a Provisional Government of Ulster, with Carson as chairman, that would take over if Home Rule became law.",
