    "label": "C4",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "William Butler Yeats co-founded the National Literary Society in Dublin in 1892, and his early poetry, such as The Wanderings of Oisin (1889), drew on Irish mythology. In 1899 he co-founded the Irish Literary Theatre with Lady Gregory and Edward Martyn, and its first production was his play The Countess Cathleen. Yeats and Lady Gregory's Cathleen ni Houlihan was staged in 1902 with Maud Gonne in the title role. He helped found the Abbey Theatre, which opened in Dublin on 27 December 1904 as a national theatre for Irish plays. In his poem September 1913 he condemned the narrow Catholic middle class of Dublin for lacking the generosity of earlier Irish rebels. He served as a senator from 1922 to 1928 and won the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1923.",
