    "label": "C3",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Many unionists rejected the Council of Ireland because they saw it as a first step towards a united Ireland, and the Ulster Unionist Council voted 427 to 374 against the agreement on 4 January 1974. The Unionist Party split, with Faulkner's supporters on one side and Paisley's Democratic Unionists and William Craig's Vanguard on the other. In the Westminster election of February 1974 the anti-Sunningdale UUUC won eleven of the twelve Northern Ireland seats. The Ulster Workers' Council called a general strike on 15 May 1974, backed by loyalist paramilitaries, which cut electricity supplies and paralysed services. The British government under Harold Wilson, with Merlyn Rees as Secretary of State, did not confront the strikers, so unionists believed London would not defend the executive. Faulkner resigned as Chief Executive on 28 May 1974 after the SDLP and Alliance rejected his proposal to delay the Council of Ireland, and the executive collapsed.",
