    "marks": 30,
    "model": "The accession terms gave Ireland an exclusive six-mile zone and a 12-mile zone in parts of the coast until the end of 1982. In January 1983 the Common Fisheries Policy set annual total allowable catches and divided them into national quotas. With EEC and state grants, BIM financed larger new boats, and Killybegs grew into Ireland's leading fishing port. Irish fishermen complained that the quotas were small compared with the value of the fish in Irish waters.",
