    "label": "C4",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Thomas Kenneth Whitaker, Secretary of the Department of Finance from 1956, wrote Economic Development in May 1958. The report argued for productive investment and export-led growth, and it welcomed foreign capital. The First Programme for Economic Expansion, published in November 1958, turned these proposals into targets for 1959 to 1963. John Charles McQuaid, Archbishop of Dublin from 1940 to 1972, advised Browne and Costello on the church's objections to the Mother and Child Scheme in 1951. He insisted that the scheme conflicted with Catholic teaching on the family, and historians judge that the bishops' stance was decisive in ending it. He was wary of ecumenism and lay initiatives, and he built schools and welfare services in the Dublin suburbs.",
