    "label": "C3",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Robert Peel reformed British government from within through legislation and institutions. As Home Secretary he founded the Metropolitan Police in London in 1829, a professional force that became a model for others. He reintroduced income tax in 1842 to pay for cuts in tariffs on many imports. The Bank Charter Act of 1844 regulated the issue of banknotes by the Bank of England. The Factory Act of 1844 limited the hours of women and children in factories. He repealed the Corn Laws in 1846, which had kept British grain prices high, as a victory for free trade. The Tamworth Manifesto of 1834 set out a moderate programme of reform that became the basis of the modern Conservative Party.",
