    "model": "During the period up to 1914 the Unionist movement was largely successful in its central aim of resisting Home Rule, but its success was partial. It defeated the first two Home Rule Bills, built powerful organisations, and by 1914 had forced the government to accept the principle that Ulster might be excluded. However, it failed to stop the Third Home Rule Bill becoming law, and southern unionists were left exposed.\n\nUnionism began as a broad coalition of Protestants, including landlords, industrialists, Presbyterian farmers and Belfast workers, united by fear of a Dublin parliament. Their arguments were religious, with the slogan that Home Rule was Rome Rule, economic, because Belfast linen, shipbuilding and engineering depended on British and imperial markets, and a matter of identity, as Ulster Protestants saw themselves as British. Gladstone's conversion to Home Rule in 1886 pushed many Presbyterians who had been Liberals into the unionist camp, giving the movement a mass base in the north-east.\n\nUnionists were successful in blocking the first two Home Rule Bills. The First Bill of 1886 was defeated in the Commons when Liberal unionists, among them Joseph Chamberlain, voted against it, and the Belfast riots of that year showed the strength of feeling. The Second Bill passed the Commons in September 1893 by 301 votes to 267, but the House of Lords rejected it on 8 September by 419 votes to 41. Unionists had mobilised against it, notably at the Ulster Convention in Belfast in June 1892, attended by about 12,000 delegates. The Conservative governments of 1895 to 1905 also pursued constructive unionism, including the Wyndham Act, which they hoped would weaken nationalism.\n\nUnionists also built effective organisation. The Orange Order revived in the 1880s and gave unionism lodges across the province. The Irish Loyal and Patriotic Union was founded in 1885, the Irish Unionist Alliance in 1891, and the Ulster Unionist Council in March 1905, which brought together Orange lodges, constituency associations and MPs. The Ulster Women's Unionist Council of 1911 mobilised tens of thousands of women. Under Colonel Saunderson and then Edward Carson, who became leader of the Irish Unionist parliamentary party in 1910, with James Craig as organiser, unionism spoke with a single voice.\n\nThe Third Home Rule crisis from 1911 to 1914 was unionism's greatest effort. After the Parliament Act of 1911 removed the Lords' veto, the Conservative leader Bonar Law pledged support for Ulster at Blenheim Palace in July 1912. On Ulster Day, 28 September 1912, about 237,000 men signed the Solemn League and Covenant and about 234,000 women signed a separate declaration. The Ulster Volunteer Force was formed in January 1913 and by 1914 had perhaps ninety thousand men, and the Ulster Unionist Council planned a Provisional Government. In the Curragh incident of March 1914 General Gough and many officers offered to resign rather than act against Ulster, and in April about 25,000 rifles were landed at Larne without the police or army intervening. The government could no longer assume that Home Rule could be imposed on Ulster by force.\n\nThese pressures achieved a great deal. Asquith proposed in March 1914 that Ulster counties could opt out for six years, and the Buckingham Palace Conference of July 1914 argued over the area to be excluded, especially Tyrone and Fermanagh. The principle of exclusion, which unionists had demanded, was conceded, and Carson and Craig became decisive figures in later negotiations. Against this, unionism did not prevent the Home Rule Act receiving the royal assent on 18 September 1914, though it was suspended for the duration of the war. The Conservative alliance also proved risky, because unionism depended on a party that might compromise, as it did in 1920 and 1921. Southern unionists, a small minority of about a tenth of the population outside Ulster, had no protection and were effectively abandoned in the drive to save Ulster.\n\nOn balance, the Unionist movement was successful up to 1914. It killed two Home Rule Bills, made it clear that Home Rule could not be forced on Ulster, and showed that the north-east could not be coerced. But it did not defeat Home Rule in principle, and it won its victory at the price of partition and the sacrifice of southern unionism.",
