    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The Dublin strike and lockout of 1913 was not a total failure. The workers were defeated in the narrow sense that the employers broke the strike and the Irish Transport and General Workers' Union failed to win recognition. But the union survived, the labour movement gained organisation and political significance, and the dispute left consequences that outlasted the defeat.\n\nThere is no denying the scale of the defeat. William Martin Murphy, chairman of the Dublin United Tramways Company and owner of the Irish Independent, led the employers' attempt to destroy Larkin's union. After the tram strike began on 26 August 1913, about 400 employers agreed on 3 September that workers must sign the Document, pledging to leave the ITGWU. Up to about 20,000 workers were out by autumn, with thousands of dependants in the poorest tenements in the United Kingdom. The Board of Trade inquiry under Askwith condemned the Document, but the employers refused its proposals. The British TUC, at a special congress in December 1913, rejected a sympathetic strike or boycott of Dublin goods, and by January 1914 hunger drove men back to work. The union lost members, some workers were blacklisted, and the employers kept the right to bar ITGWU members in some firms.\n\nYet the union was not destroyed, which had been Murphy's aim. Many men returned without signing the Document, and the ITGWU survived and grew during the First World War. Larkin had founded it in 1909 and it had organised thousands of unskilled workers who had never been unionised before. To keep that mass movement alive for five months, against the most powerful employers in the city, was a considerable achievement. Strike pay and relief were distributed from Liberty Hall, with soup kitchens and food parcels, and British unions sent food, beginning with the ship Hare in September 1913.\n\nThe lockout also won wide public sympathy and moved opinion against the employers. Baton charges on 30 August killed two men, and the police baton charge on Bloody Sunday, 31 August 1913, injured hundreds. The collapse of two tenement houses in Church Street on 2 September, which killed seven people, exposed the conditions in which workers lived. George Russell (AE) published his Open Letter to the Masters of Dublin in October 1913, attacking the employers for starving workers into submission, and Yeats wrote September 1913. Public pressure and a housing inquiry in 1913 and 1914 increased the demand for slum clearance in the city.\n\nThe lockout also had lasting political effects that the workers' defeat does not capture. The Irish Citizen Army was founded at Liberty Hall in November 1913 to protect pickets from the police, and under James Connolly it took part in the Easter Rising of 1916. The dispute made labour a political force in Dublin and began the alliance of labour and nationalism that Connolly favoured. It also divided nationalist opinion, because the Irish Party and Griffith's Sinn Fein were largely unsympathetic while Connolly, Pearse's circle and Constance Markievicz supported the strikers. Even though the Save the Kiddies scheme was condemned by Archbishop Walsh and stopped by priests and the Ancient Order of Hibernians, the controversy brought the poverty of Dublin to national attention.\n\nIn the end, the immediate result favoured the employers, and no honest account can call the outcome a victory for labour. The Document was never universally enforced and the union was never crushed, however, and the men who had stood out for five months gained a sense of solidarity that Larkin and Connolly would build on. The lockout was a defeat, but not a total failure, because it left the ITGWU alive, labour organised and the Irish working class a force in Irish politics.",
