    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The cultural revival, the consolidation of Catholic identity and the Anglo-Irish Literary Revival were all of great importance in the period 1870-1914, because together they gave Ireland a sense of cultural identity and helped prepare the ground for the political revolution that followed. Hyde's Gaelic League created a mass movement for the Irish language, Yeats and his colleagues created a national literature and theatre, and the Catholic Church's tightening grip gave Irish nationalism much of its character. Their importance was real but also limited, since they disagreed with one another about who belonged to the Irish nation.\n\nDouglas Hyde gave the revival its programme. On 25 November 1892 he delivered The Necessity for De-Anglicising Ireland to the National Literary Society in Dublin. He argued that Ireland had been anglicised in language, names, dress, games and ideas, and that the Irish were failing to be either English or Irish. He pointed to the sharp decline of Irish as a spoken language since the Famine and urged that it be cultivated and used in schools. Crucially, he insisted that this was a cultural aim compatible with a view that was not separatist. The lecture was important because it turned a vague regret into a call for action, and within a year it led to the founding of the Gaelic League.\n\nThe Gaelic League, founded on 31 July 1893 by Hyde, Eoin MacNeill and others, was the most successful vehicle of that programme. It was officially non-political and non-sectarian and welcomed Catholics, Protestants, nationalists and unionists. It ran Irish classes through its branches, of which there were several hundred by 1904, and sent travelling teachers, the timirí, into rural districts. It organised feiseanna and, from 1897, the annual Oireachtas, and its paper An Claidheamh Soluis, begun in 1899 and edited by Patrick Pearse from 1903 to 1909, spread its ideas. The League won real victories: Irish was allowed in primary schools under the Revised Programme of 1900, and its campaign made Irish compulsory for matriculation to the National University, decided in 1910 and applied from 1913. The importance of Hyde and the League lay in the mass interest in the language they created and in the generation of activists they trained, though Irish did not revive as an everyday tongue and many members, increasingly IRB men from about 1910, pushed it towards politics. Hyde resigned as president in 1915 when the League declared for Irish independence.\n\nThe Anglo-Irish Literary Revival was important in a different way, creating a national literature in English. The National Literary Society was founded in 1892 with Hyde and Yeats among its leaders. W.B. Yeats, Lady Gregory and Edward Martyn founded the Irish Literary Theatre in 1899, and Yeats and Lady Gregory's Cathleen ni Houlihan was staged in April 1902 with Maud Gonne in the title role. The Abbey Theatre opened on 27 December 1904 with Yeats, Lady Gregory and J.M. Synge as directors, giving Ireland a national theatre. Yeats's early poetry, such as The Wanderings of Oisin, drew on Irish mythology, and Synge used the speech of the west of Ireland. Their work gave nationalism powerful images and self-confidence. Yet it was often controversial: Catholic critics attacked The Countess Cathleen in 1899, and the Playboy of the Western World provoked riots at the Abbey in January 1907 because nationalists objected to its picture of rural life. The writers were mainly Protestant and Anglo-Irish, so the Literary Revival showed that the cultural movement could not be claimed by one tradition alone.\n\nThe consolidation of Catholic identity was in some ways the deepest change of all. After the Famine the Church experienced what historians call a devotional revolution, with rising Mass attendance, parish missions, devotions such as the Rosary and the Sacred Heart, and a boom in church building paid for by a more prosperous farming and trading middle class. The Church controlled most national schools through clerical managers and ran secondary education through the Christian Brothers, Jesuits and convent orders, while Maynooth trained a large clergy of high social standing. Organisations such as the Pioneer Total Abstinence Association, founded by Fr James Cullen in 1898, extended its influence. From 1885 the bishops generally supported Home Rule, tying Catholic identity to nationalism, and the Irish Party was largely Catholic. The Ne Temere decree of 1908 angered Protestants, and unionists responded to this overlap of faith and politics with the slogan Rome Rule. This consolidation therefore shaped the politics of the period more directly than the language movement did.\n\nThe revivals also clashed. D.P. Moran, who founded The Leader in 1900 and published The Philosophy of Irish Ireland in 1905, defined the nation as Gaelic and Catholic, and attacked both Yeats's circle and the Irish Party as West Britons. This divided the movement between Hyde's inclusive approach and a narrower Catholic nationalism, a tension that survived into independence.\n\nOverall, all three were important, but in different ways. Hyde and the Gaelic League were most important in creating mass cultural participation, Yeats and the Abbey in giving Ireland a literature and a confident image, and Catholic consolidation in shaping the political identity of nationalists. Together they supplied the language and symbols of the national movement, even if historians still debate whether they caused the revolution of 1916 or merely provided a vocabulary for politics that had other causes.",
