    "model": "The main trends in Irish emigration between 1840 and 1920 were a shift from a steady flow to a mass exodus during the Famine, followed by a permanent, settled pattern of emigration which took about four million people out of Ireland in the decades after 1855. Over the period the typical emigrant changed from a Protestant small farmer with some capital to a poor Catholic from the west, then to a young, single person leaving as a normal stage of life, and the United States became the dominant destination.\n\nBefore the Famine emigration was already established. Ulster Presbyterians had sailed to the American colonies in the 18th century, and about one million people left between 1815 and 1845. Many pre-Famine emigrants were Protestant small farmers, weavers and tradesmen who could pay their own fares, though from the 1830s a growing share were poorer Catholics from the south and west. British North America was the main transatlantic destination because fares were cheapest, and Britain took the poorest, since the crossing to Liverpool or Glasgow cost only a few shillings. Seasonal migration also took labourers from Mayo, Donegal and Galway to English and Scottish harvests each summer.\n\nThe Famine turned this flow into a flood. Blight destroyed most of the potato crop in 1846, and about one million people died between 1845 and 1851, while between 1.5 and 2 million emigrated in the decade to 1855. The population fell from about 8.2 million in 1841 to about 6.5 million in 1851. Famine emigrants were mostly Catholic, poor and unskilled, often from the west and south-west, and many travelled as whole families. It was panic flight, shown by the arrival of about 300,000 destitute people at Liverpool alone in 1847, and by 1851 about 730,000 Irish-born people lived in Britain.\n\nThe Famine years also saw new methods of leaving. Some landlords paid the passages of tenants, because it was cheaper than paying poor rates, as with Major Mahon's 1,490 tenants sent from Strokestown to Quebec in 1847, and the Earl Grey scheme sent about 4,000 workhouse orphan girls to Australia. But most emigration was financed by relatives abroad through pre-paid passages, which began the chain-emigration that sustained the movement for decades. The voyage was often deadly: typhus killed thousands on the so-called coffin ships, and the quarantine station at Grosse Isle on the St Lawrence saw more than 5,000 burials in 1847. The American Passenger Acts of 1847 raised the cost of the US route, which diverted the poorest to Canada.\n\nAfter 1855 emigration became a settled feature of rural life. About four million people left in the following six decades, and the population of the island fell to about 4.4 million by 1911. The United States took about four out of five emigrants, and the number of Irish-born there peaked at about 1.87 million in 1890. Emigrants were now mostly young and single, aged roughly 15 to 30, and Ireland was unusual in sending as many women as men. Emigration was heaviest from the west, and the post-Famine custom of impartible inheritance, in which one son took the farm and one daughter a dowry, left the other children to emigrate or stay unmarried. A fresh surge came in the agricultural depression around 1879 to 1882.\n\nThe settlement pattern also changed. Irish emigrants in the United States went overwhelmingly to cities such as New York, Boston, Philadelphia and Chicago, working first as unskilled labourers while Irish women dominated domestic service. By 1900 the second generation was moving into skilled trades, city politics, teaching and the professions. In Britain the Irish clustered in Liverpool, Glasgow, London and Manchester, while Australia and New Zealand attracted assisted emigrants. Steamships from the 1860s cut the crossing from about six weeks to about two weeks and made it much safer, and the American wake marked the farewell to someone who would probably never return. Remittances of well over a million pounds a year paid for passages, rent and dowries, and the American letter kept families connected.\n\nOverall, the key trends were the mass scale of the Famine exodus, the shift to the United States and to young single emigrants, the continued importance of Britain for the poorest, and the self-sustaining chain of kin and remittances that made emigration a normal expectation. It was a response to poverty, a lack of industry outside the north-east and a farming system that could not support every child, and it left Ireland the only country in Europe whose population fell for so long.",
