    "label": "2",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Jack Lynch was a moderately effective Taoiseach. In external affairs he was at his most successful, because he kept the state out of the Northern conflict and led Ireland into the EEC. In internal affairs his record was more mixed: he held a divided Fianna Fáil together through the Arms Crisis, but his second government left the economy in serious difficulty.\n\nLynch's greatest test was the Troubles. After the rioting in Derry and Belfast in August 1969 he declared in a broadcast that the state could not stand by, and the army set up field hospitals near the border, but he ruled out military intervention. This was a sensible and restrained response. Sending troops across the border would have risked war and the collapse of the state, and his policy of seeking unity by consent and opposing violence kept the Republic out of the conflict. He condemned internment in August 1971 and recalled the Irish ambassador from London after Bloody Sunday in January 1972, which showed that he could protest forcefully without losing control. In December 1972, after bombs in Dublin, he secured amendments to the Offences Against the State Act to deal with the IRA. His handling of the North was therefore firm, moderate and effective in protecting the state.\n\nLynch also pursued diplomacy with London. In 1971 he met Edward Heath and Brian Faulkner at Chequers, where he argued that only an Irish dimension could end the violence. Heath suspended Stormont and introduced direct rule in March 1972, a step the Irish government welcomed. The meeting did not bring peace, but it put the Irish dimension on the agenda and prepared the ground for Sunningdale after he left office.\n\nHis greatest external achievement was EEC membership. Under his government, Ireland re-applied in 1967 and, after de Gaulle's veto and resignation, negotiations opened in 1970 with Patrick Hillery leading the Irish team. Ireland won a five-year transitional period for industry and terms on farm prices, and the Treaty of Accession was signed in Brussels on 22 January 1972. In the referendum of 10 May 1972 about 83 per cent voted in favour on a turnout of about 71 per cent, and Ireland joined on 1 January 1973. Lynch's government thus ended the country's economic dependence on Britain and opened the way to CAP prices and regional funds, a lasting success.\n\nInternally, Lynch faced the most serious split in Fianna Fáil during the Arms Crisis. In April 1970 the Special Branch told him that ministers might be involved in a plan to import arms for Northern nationalists. On 6 May 1970 he dismissed Charles Haughey and Neil Blaney, and Kevin Boland resigned in protest. Haughey and others were charged with conspiracy, but they were acquitted in October 1970. Lynch preserved his authority and the rule of law, but critics said he had been slow to act, and the division between his supporters and Haughey's lasted into the 1980s. He also won the 1969 election, though he lost power in 1973 to the Fine Gael-Labour coalition. His government set up the Commission on the Status of Women in 1970, which was a progressive step on social policy.\n\nHis second term was far less effective. In the 1977 election Fianna Fáil promised to abolish domestic rates and the car tax and to create jobs by spending more, and it won 84 of the 148 seats, the largest majority in the history of the state. The government cut taxes and increased spending, financed by borrowing, but inflation, the second oil crisis of 1979 and the cost of the manifesto turned growth into crisis, and the national debt rose sharply. After Fianna Fáil lost two by-elections in Cork in November 1979 his authority was weakened, and he resigned in December 1979, handing over to Haughey, who defeated George Colley by 44 votes to 38.\n\nOverall, Lynch was very effective in external affairs and in preserving the state from the Northern crisis, and he held a fractious party together by conciliation. On the economy his record was poor, and his indecision over Haughey and Blaney and the reckless programme of 1977 show the limits of his leadership. He was a skilful manager of men rather than a decisive reformer, but the state's safe entry into Europe and its escape from the Troubles are real achievements.",
