    "label": "2",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The main social and economic developments in Europe between 1871 and 1914 were rapid industrialisation, led by Germany, the growth of cities and a vast industrial working class, the rise of organised labour and socialism, and the tensions this created between old landed elites and new industrial classes. Governments responded with a mixture of welfare and repression, and by 1914 Europe was richer but more socially divided than in 1871.\n\nGermany was the most striking case of economic growth. After unification it combined coal and iron in the Ruhr, Saar and Silesia, and the ore of Lorraine taken from France in 1871, with a single market, a common currency (the mark, 1873) and the Reichsbank. Railways grew from about 21,000 km in 1871 to over 60,000 km by 1913, universal banks such as Deutsche Bank lent to industry for the long term, and the population rose from about 41 million to 67 million, giving both labour and a home market. Coal output rose from about 30 million tonnes to about 190 million, and Germany overtook Britain in several industries. The long depression after the crash of 1873 encouraged cartels such as the Rhenish-Westphalian Coal Syndicate and tariffs, so growth was not smooth, but the overall trend was dramatic.\n\nFrom the 1880s a second industrial revolution based on steel, chemicals and electricity transformed the economy, and Germany led it. The Thomas-Gilchrist process of 1878 allowed Lorraine ore to be turned into steel, BASF, Bayer and Hoechst dominated dyes and pharmaceuticals, and Siemens and AEG dominated electrical engineering, while Benz, Daimler and Diesel developed the motor car and diesel engine. The Krupp family of Essen symbolised this growth. Alfred Krupp made steel guns and railway tyres, and the firm grew into the largest industrial enterprise in Europe, supplying the army and, under Friedrich Alfred, the navy. Krupp also built housing, schools and sickness funds for its workers, an example of paternalism designed to win loyalty. Britain and France also industrialised, but Germany's pace showed how quickly economic power could shift.\n\nIndustrialisation brought urbanisation and a new working class. By 1910 around 60 per cent of Germans lived in towns, compared with about a third in 1871, and Berlin grew from under a million to about two million people. Workers lived in overcrowded tenements, the Mietskasernen, with poor sanitation and disease, and faced long hours and strict discipline. Real wages rose slowly in the 1890s and 1900s, and laws such as that of 1891 limited Sunday work and the hours of women and children. Large strikes, such as the Ruhr miners' strike of 1889 involving about 90,000 men, showed that the workers were becoming organised and confident. The social cost of rapid growth was therefore high, even if living standards eventually improved.\n\nThis in turn produced socialism and trade unionism. The SPD was formed at Gotha in 1875, was banned by Bismarck's Anti-Socialist Law of 1878 until 1890, and then adopted the Erfurt Programme of 1891. By 1912 it won about 4.25 million votes and 110 seats, making it the largest party in the Reichstag, while the Free trade unions had about 2.5 million members by 1913. Employers answered with associations, lockouts and blacklists, and the state used the courts and police. Bismarck tried to win workers away from socialism through the first national social insurance laws, covering sickness (1883), accidents (1884) and old age pensions (1889). Welfare did not stop the SPD growing, but it showed that the state now accepted some responsibility for social conditions.\n\nEconomic change also sharpened conflict between social classes. The Junkers, the landowners of eastern Prussia, dominated the army and the Prussian state, but were hit by cheap American and Russian grain. The tariff of 1879 created an alliance of iron and rye between heavy industry and landowners, and Miquel's Sammlungspolitik of 1897 tried to rally them against the left. The tariffs raised food prices and angered workers and consumers. Historians such as Wehler argue that the middle class imitated the nobility and so blocked democratic reform, though Eley and Blackbourn replied that Germany was a modern society with a confident bourgeoisie. In Russia the picture was different. Under Witte the state drove industrialisation through railways, including the Trans-Siberian, and foreign loans, but the peasantry remained poor, the new factory workers were crowded in cities such as St Petersburg, and discontent led to the revolution of 1905.\n\nOverall, the period was one of remarkable economic expansion, led by Germany's steel, chemical and electrical industries, which changed where people lived and worked. That growth created a large, organised working class and exposed deep social divisions between landowners, industrialists and workers, which neither paternalism nor welfare nor repression resolved. Europe in 1914 was more prosperous and more urban than in 1871, but also more politically tense.",
