    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Anti-Semitism had a major impact in both France and Russia between 1871 and 1920, but in different ways. In France it split the republic during the Dreyfus Affair and ended with the victory of the republican side, while in Russia it was encouraged by the state, producing legal discrimination, pogroms and the emigration of about two million Jews. In both countries it left Jews insecure and shaped wider politics.\n\nIn France, anti-Semitism grew from the humiliation of defeat in 1871 and from economic anxiety, as nationalists blamed Jews and Freemasons for the country's weakness. The word anti-Semitism itself was coined in Germany by Wilhelm Marr in 1879, showing that racial prejudice was spreading across Europe. Edouard Drumont's La France juive (1886) became a bestseller portraying Jews as a threat to the nation, and in 1892 he founded the newspaper La Libre Parole to spread the message. This created a climate in which a Jewish officer could easily be blamed for betrayal.\n\nThe most dramatic impact came with the Dreyfus Affair. In October 1894 Captain Alfred Dreyfus, a Jewish artillery officer from Alsace, was arrested and accused of passing secrets to Germany. He was convicted by a secret court martial in December 1894 and sent to Devil's Island. In 1896 Colonel Picquart found that the real author of the memorandum was Major Esterhazy, but the army suppressed the evidence, Esterhazy was acquitted in 1898 and a forgery by Colonel Henry was used against Dreyfus. On 13 January 1898 Emile Zola published his open letter J'accuse in L'Aurore, accusing the army of a cover-up, and was convicted of libel. The affair showed how prejudice could corrupt the army and the courts.\n\nThe Affair divided France into Dreyfusards, including republicans, socialists such as Jaures and many intellectuals, and anti-Dreyfusards, including the army, much of the Catholic Church, nationalists and monarchists. A second court martial at Rennes in 1899 found Dreyfus guilty again with extenuating circumstances, and President Loubet pardoned him; in 1906 the court of appeals cleared him and he was reinstated in the army. The consequences were political. The army and the Church lost reputation, and the republicans, strengthened by the outcome, passed the anticlerical measures of the early 1900s, ending in the separation of church and state in 1905. The Affair also influenced Theodor Herzl, who reported on the case and published Der Judenstaat in 1896, and the First Zionist Congress met at Basel in 1897. So in France the impact was a deep political crisis that ended in a republican victory, though prejudice remained.\n\nIn Russia the impact was far more violent and lasting, because anti-Semitism was supported by the state. About five million Jews lived in the empire, most confined to the Pale of Settlement and many poor. After the assassination of Alexander II in March 1881 rumours blamed the Jews, and pogroms swept southern Russia, beginning at Elizavetgrad in April 1881, with police and troops slow to intervene. The May Laws of 1882 banned Jews from settling in rural areas of the Pale and from owning land, quotas limited their places in schools and universities from 1887, and Jews were expelled from Moscow in 1891-92. Officials such as Pobedonostsev supported discrimination and Russification, and the Orthodox Church and the Black Hundreds spread propaganda.\n\nViolence returned in the new century. The Kishinev pogrom of 6-7 April 1903 killed 49 Jews and injured hundreds, causing international outrage, and further pogroms followed in 1905-06 during the revolution. The Protocols of the Elders of Zion, a forgery claiming a Jewish plot to dominate the world, was first published in Russia in 1903 and spread widely after 1917. The effects on Jewish life were profound: between 1881 and 1914 about two million Jews emigrated, most to the USA but also to Britain and Palestine, while others joined the Bund, founded in 1897, and socialist and Zionist movements.\n\nIn conclusion, the impact in France was political, since the Dreyfus Affair divided the nation, weakened the army and Church and ended in a victory for justice, while in Russia the impact was social and human, producing legal persecution, pogroms and mass emigration. The French case showed that a republic could defeat anti-Semitism in the courts, but the Russian case showed how dangerous it became when tolerated by the state.",
