    "label": "2",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Church-state relations developed very differently under the two dictators, although in both cases the regime sought to use or neutralise the Church for its own ends. Mussolini reached a settlement with the papacy in 1929 that brought him Catholic support, though it was strained by disputes in 1931 and 1938. Hitler signed a Concordat in 1933 but broke it within a few years and tried to subordinate the churches to Nazi ideology, which provoked a limited but real Christian resistance.\n\nMussolini's greatest achievement in this field was the Lateran Treaties, signed on 11 February 1929 with Cardinal Gasparri, the Pope's Secretary of State. Since 1870, when the Italian state seized Rome and ended the papal states, the popes had regarded themselves as prisoners in the Vatican, and the \"Roman Question\" had divided Catholics from the state. The treaties created the independent state of Vatican City and the Italian government paid the Church compensation of 1.75 billion lire for the loss of the papal states. In return the Church recognised the Kingdom of Italy with Rome as its capital. This gave Mussolini a huge propaganda victory, because the Church's endorsement helped legitimise the regime in the eyes of the Catholic majority of Italians.\n\nThe Concordat attached to the treaties gave the Church real benefits. Catholicism was recognised as the sole religion of the state, religious marriage was given civil effect, religious teaching was provided in state schools and priests were paid by the state. Mussolini, once a fierce anti-clerical, had calculated that these concessions were a small price for ending the Church's hostility. Relations were not smooth for long, however. In 1931 the regime clashed with Catholic Action, the Church's lay organisation, because its youth groups rivalled the Fascist youth movement. Fascist squads attacked Catholic centres, and Pius XI protested in the encyclical Non abbiamo bisogno. An agreement later that year confined Catholic Action to religious, cultural and charitable activity and kept its youth out of politics and sport, so Mussolini largely won the dispute.\n\nA further strain came in 1938 when Mussolini published the Manifesto of the Race and introduced racial laws that excluded Jews from schools, the professions and the armed forces. The Church objected in part, because the laws contradicted the Catholic belief in a common humanity, but Pius XI rarely challenged Mussolini openly and the Vatican did not break with the regime. Church-state relations in Italy therefore remained a working partnership, with private protest rather than confrontation.\n\nHitler's approach began in a similar spirit. The Reich Concordat with the Holy See was signed on 20 July 1933 and was supposed to protect Catholic schools and institutions in return for the Church's withdrawal from politics. The Catholic Centre Party had dissolved itself on 5 July 1933, just as the agreement was being concluded, so Hitler removed political Catholicism at the same moment as he gained international respectability. Within a few years the regime violated the Concordat by closing Catholic schools and youth organisations and arresting priests and religious.\n\nHitler also sought to control German Protestantism. The pro-Nazi German Christians won about two-thirds of the seats in the Protestant church elections of July 1933, and Ludwig Müller was appointed Reich Bishop in September 1933 to head a single Reich Church. The plan was to end the independence of the regional churches, to exclude Jewish Christians from church office and to replace Christian doctrine with an ideology of race and national blood. This was a direct challenge to Christian teaching, and it was resisted.\n\nOpposition came from the Confessing Church, whose Barmen Declaration of 31 May 1934 rejected the state's claim to control the Church's teaching. Martin Niemöller, a Protestant pastor and former U-boat commander, led the resistance and was arrested in 1937 and held in concentration camps until 1945. Dietrich Bonhoeffer joined the opposition and was executed at Flossenbürg in April 1945. On the Catholic side Pius XI issued the encyclical Mit brennender Sorge on 14 March 1937, which criticised Nazi violations of the Concordat and the racial ideology and was read from pulpits in Germany. In 1941 Bishop von Galen of Münster preached against the murder of the disabled under the euthanasia programme, and the programme was halted in August of that year.\n\nOverall, Mussolini's relations with the Church moved from a successful bargain in 1929 to a wary coexistence, while Hitler's moved from a deceptive agreement in 1933 to open conflict and persecution of church institutions. In neither state did the Church seriously threaten the dictator, because its protests mostly defended its own institutions and it did not challenge the persecution of Jews on the same scale. Yet the contrast is clear: Mussolini needed the Church's blessing and paid for it, whereas Hitler wanted to subordinate it and was checked only in the few areas where the churches were willing to speak out.",
