    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The fortunes of France between 1920 and 1945 swung from the confidence of a victorious but exhausted nation, through political instability and the Depression, to the catastrophe of defeat in 1940 and the humiliation of occupation, before liberation in 1944 restored the country to the ranks of the victors. It was a period of sharp decline followed by recovery, and the lowest point was the fall of the Third Republic.\n\nIn the 1920s France emerged from the First World War victorious but having lost about 1.4 million dead, with the north-east devastated. The Bloc National government that took office after the 1919 election expected Germany to pay for reconstruction. Raymond Poincaré, Prime Minister from 1922, ordered the occupation of the Ruhr in January 1923 when Germany defaulted, but this damaged relations with Britain and the United States and ended in 1925 without securing full reparations. The Cartel des Gauches won the 1924 election, but it fell in 1925 after a run on the franc. Poincaré returned in 1926 with a government of national unity and stabilised the franc through tax rises and spending cuts, and the Poincaré franc was fixed in law in 1928. So the 1920s ended with financial recovery, though governments changed with great frequency.\n\nThe Depression reached France later than Britain or Germany because the economy of small farms and family businesses cushioned the shock. Industrial production fell sharply from 1931, and the government's policy of defending the franc at its gold value meant deflation that hurt industry and farming until the devaluation of October 1936. Political confidence collapsed with the Stavisky affair. Alexandre Stavisky, a financier with political protection, was found shot dead in January 1934 after his fraudulent bond sales collapsed, and the scandal forced the Prime Minister Chautemps to resign. On 6 February 1934 right-wing leagues, including the Croix-de-Feu and Action française, marched on the Chamber of Deputies, and about fifteen people were killed when the police opened fire. The republic survived, but it was shaken by fears of fascism.\n\nThat fear produced the Popular Front, the alliance of Socialists, Communists and Radicals, which won the 1936 election and made Léon Blum Prime Minister. The Matignon Agreements of June 1936 followed a wave of sit-down strikes involving about two million workers, and gave wage rises, union recognition and collective bargaining. Laws brought in the 40-hour week and two weeks of paid holidays. Blum also dissolved the far-right leagues. These were real gains for French workers, but the economic programme was weakened by capital flight and financial pressure. The coalition split over non-intervention in the Spanish Civil War, Blum resigned in June 1937, and Daladier's decrees of November 1938 undid the 40-hour week. France was divided and its army was unprepared as war approached.\n\nThe nadir came in 1940. After the Phoney War, the German invasion of 10 May 1940 brought armoured divisions through the Ardennes, across the Meuse at Sedan and to the Channel coast within about ten days. France lost about 90,000 killed in six weeks and well over a million men were taken prisoner. Paris was occupied on 14 June, and an armistice signed on 22 June at Compiègne divided the country into an occupied zone and an unoccupied zone. On 10 July the National Assembly voted 569 to 80 to give full powers to Marshal Pétain, which ended the Third Republic. Contemporaries blamed the moral decadence of the Third Republic, but historians such as Ernest May stress poor intelligence, slow command and German skill, and the French spreading of their tanks among infantry units and the move of their best forces into Belgium left the Ardennes open to the German breakthrough.\n\nUnder the Vichy regime France lived through occupation and division. Pétain's National Revolution replaced Liberté, Égalité, Fraternité with Travail, Famille, Patrie. The regime passed anti-Jewish laws in 1940 without German demand, and about 76,000 Jews were deported from France, including those arrested in the Vél' d'Hiv round-up of July 1942. Under Laval collaboration deepened, the Milice hunted the Resistance and compulsory labour service from February 1943 sent young men to Germany. The French paid heavily for the occupation, with shortages, rationing and about 1.5 million prisoners of war held in Germany.\n\nFrance's fortunes began to recover through resistance and liberation. De Gaulle's broadcast from London on 18 June 1940 began the Free French movement, and Jean Moulin united the main resistance groups in the National Council of the Resistance in 1943. After the Normandy landings of June 1944, Paris was liberated on 25 August and de Gaulle led the procession down the Champs-Élysées. Pétain was sentenced to death in 1945, though the sentence was commuted, Laval was shot, and women voted for the first time in 1945.\n\nOverall, France's fortunes were poor for most of the period. A country that had begun with victory in 1918 suffered political instability, economic strain, defeat and occupation, and only the Resistance and Allied victory restored its place in the world.",
