    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The Soviet Union faced two kinds of challenge between 1924 and 1945. In peace, the challenges were political and economic: deciding who would succeed Lenin, building an industrial economy from a backward one, feeding the people and keeping control of the party and society. In war, the challenge was survival against the German invasion of 1941. Stalin met them with ruthless methods, so that the Soviet state survived and became a superpower at enormous human cost.\n\nThe first challenge was the succession to Lenin, who died on 21 January 1924 without a clear heir. Stalin had been General Secretary since April 1922, which gave him control of party appointments, and Lenin's Testament, which criticised him and called for his removal, was not published. Stalin allied first with Zinoviev and Kamenev against Trotsky, who was expelled from the party in 1927 and exiled from the Soviet Union in 1929. He then turned on Zinoviev and Kamenev, and used the Right Opposition of Bukharin to beat the Left before adopting the Left's policy of rapid industrialisation and defeating Bukharin in 1928-29. By 1929 his rivals were removed from political influence.\n\nThe second challenge was economic backwardness. The New Economic Policy had restored food supplies after the civil war, but many Communists regarded it as a retreat. The First Five-Year Plan, drawn up by Gosplan and launched in 1928, set high targets for coal, steel, electricity and machinery, with priority for heavy industry. New cities such as Magnitogorsk were built, and steel output rose from about 4 million tonnes in 1928 to about 18 million in 1940, though official figures were inflated. The costs were high: wages fell, housing was overcrowded, quality was poor and forced labour was used on projects such as the White Sea-Baltic Canal. The plans transformed the economy, but at the expense of consumers.\n\nThe third challenge, feeding the towns and controlling the peasants, led to collectivisation. In late 1929 Stalin announced the liquidation of the kulaks as a class, and peasants were forced into collective farms. Kulaks were deported or killed, and peasants resisted by slaughtering livestock. Grain requisitions of 1932 and 1933 left villages short of food and produced a famine that killed several million people, mainly in Ukraine, the North Caucasus and Kazakhstan. The authorities denied the famine. Collectivisation brought agriculture under state control but caused lower output of grain and meat for several years.\n\nThe costs of these policies created a fourth challenge, which Stalin met with terror. The failures of the plans needed scapegoats, and Stalin feared old Bolsheviks who might offer an alternative leadership. After the murder of Sergei Kirov in Leningrad on 1 December 1934, which Stalin exploited, a decree allowed terrorism cases to be heard in days with no defence and immediate execution. In August 1936 Zinoviev and Kamenev were tried publicly, confessed and were shot, and further show trials in 1937 and 1938 followed, with Pyatakov and Radek in January 1937. The Terror spread to the army, the party and ordinary people, and most delegates to the Congress of Victors of 1934 did not survive it. This removed any internal challenge to Stalin but weakened the state's officials and the armed forces on the eve of war.\n\nThe greatest challenge came on 22 June 1941, when Germany invaded in Operation Barbarossa and the Red Army suffered heavy losses in the first months. Stalin chaired the State Defence Committee from 30 June and became Supreme Commander in August, staying in Moscow during the German advance of autumn 1941. About 1,500 factories were moved east of the Urals in 1941 and 1942, and output of tanks, aircraft and munitions rose sharply, helped by Lend-Lease supplies from Britain and the United States. Order 227 of July 1942, \"Not one step back\", brought blocking detachments and penal battalions to enforce discipline. In 1943 Stalin eased pressure on the Orthodox Church and appealed to Russian nationalism in the Great Patriotic War.\n\nThe turning point came at Stalingrad, where the German Sixth Army surrendered in February 1943, and the victory at Kursk in July 1943 was followed by the Red Army's advance to Berlin in April and May 1945. The cost was about 27 million Soviet dead, more than any other combatant.\n\nOverall, the Soviet Union met every challenge, but by force and with immense suffering. Stalin secured his own power, industrialised the country and defeated Germany, and in 1945 the USSR emerged as a superpower controlling Eastern Europe, though the price was famine, terror and war losses on a scale unmatched elsewhere.",
