    "label": "1",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "By the time of the Hungarian Uprising in 1956 the Soviet Union had succeeded to a large extent in sovietising Eastern Europe, in that every state had a communist government, a Soviet-style economy and membership of Soviet-led institutions. The success was incomplete, however, because it rested on force and had provoked resistance in several states, and the uprising itself showed that sovietisation could be maintained only by the Red Army.\n\nPolitically, the Soviet Union had achieved its aim of friendly governments along its western frontier. The Red Army occupied Poland, Romania, Bulgaria, Hungary and much of Czechoslovakia by May 1945, and Stalin used this position to install communist control step by step. In Hungary the communists won only about 17 per cent in the free election of November 1945, but Matyas Rakosi used the police and the interior ministry to break the Smallholders, and fraud in 1947 gave the communists the largest share. In Romania King Michael was forced to abdicate in December 1947. In Bulgaria the opposition leader Nikola Petkov was executed in 1947. In Czechoslovakia, the last democracy, the communists under Klement Gottwald took power in February 1948. The death of Jan Masaryk in March 1948 shocked Western opinion. In Poland the socialists were merged into the Polish United Workers' Party in December 1948. By 1949 one-party rule was established across the region.\n\nThe Soviet model was also imposed through institutions and terror. Show trials and purges eliminated rivals, as in the execution of the Hungarian foreign minister Laszlo Rajk in 1949, and secret police forces such as the AVH in Hungary spied on ordinary citizens. Cardinal Mindszenty was jailed in 1949 in a show trial. Economically, the states adopted central planning, collectivisation of farms and a focus on heavy industry. The Soviet Union refused Marshall aid on behalf of its satellites, forcing Czechoslovakia and Poland to withdraw in July 1947, and it set up Comecon in January 1949 to coordinate trade. The Cominform of 1947 linked the communist parties under Moscow's direction. Soviet reparations and bilateral trade agreements tied the satellite economies to Moscow.\n\nAs regards security, the Soviet bloc was bound together militarily. The Soviet Union kept control of the Eastern European armies, and in 1955, after West Germany joined NATO, the Warsaw Pact was created. Germany had also been divided. After the Berlin Blockade failed in 1949 the Soviet zone became the German Democratic Republic on 7 October 1949, with the Socialist Unity Party formed by merging the communists and social democrats in 1946. On these measures, the Soviet Union had built a defensive buffer and a bloc that functioned as a single system.\n\nYet the policy had clear limits. Yugoslavia under Tito, who came to power largely through his own wartime resistance, was expelled from the Cominform in June 1948 and survived outside the bloc, showing that a communist state could break from Moscow. Albania was also largely independent in its origins. After Stalin's death in March 1953 resistance emerged: East German workers rose on 17 June 1953 against higher work norms and Soviet tanks put the rising down. Khrushchev's secret speech of 25 February 1956 denouncing Stalin's terror undermined the authority of the Stalinist leaders. Workers in Poznan protested in June 1956, and in October 1956 Wladyslaw Gomulka returned to power in Poland after a confrontation with Moscow that ended without Soviet intervention, ending forced collectivisation.\n\nHungary was the clearest failure. Rakosi's rigid Stalinism, falling living standards and the hated AVH produced a national revolt after 23 October 1956. Imre Nagy declared Hungary neutral and announced withdrawal from the Warsaw Pact on 1 November, which the Soviet leaders could not tolerate. They sent in the Red Army on 4 November, and Nagy was later executed in June 1958. Some 200,000 Hungarians fled the country. The Soviet Union had shown it would use force to keep its bloc, which was a kind of success.\n\nOverall, sovietisation had succeeded in establishing communist rule, centralised economies and a Soviet-led alliance system across the region. But it had not won popular consent. By 1956 it depended on terror and Soviet troops, and the events in East Germany, Poland and Hungary showed that the satellites were held by force rather than loyalty.",
