    "model": "Both the Suez Crisis of 1956 and the secession of Katanga from the Congo became international affairs because they involved former colonial powers acting to protect their interests, the rival superpowers of the Cold War and the United Nations, whose authority was tested in each case.\n\nThe Suez Crisis was international from the start because the canal was a vital world waterway. Opened in 1869, it was run by the Suez Canal Company, in which Britain had held a large shareholding since 1875, and about two-thirds of Western Europe's oil imports passed through it. When Nasser nationalised the company on 26 July 1956, after the United States and Britain had withdrawn their offer to fund the Aswan High Dam, he challenged British and French interests directly. Britain and France then colluded with Israel in the secret Protocol of Sèvres of 24 October. Israel invaded Sinai on 29 October, and Britain and France issued an ultimatum and bombed Egyptian airfields before landing at Port Said on 5 and 6 November. A conflict that began between Egypt and the former imperial powers had become a war involving four states, with the world's attention on it.\n\nThe Cold War gave the crisis its decisive international dimension. Nasser had bought Soviet-bloc arms through Czechoslovakia in 1955, which made Egypt a point of superpower rivalry. President Eisenhower opposed the invasion, and with Britain facing a run on the pound the United States refused financial support through the IMF until Britain agreed to withdraw. The Soviet Union threatened rocket attacks on London and Paris on 5 November, and it had just crushed the Hungarian rising, which weakened the Western protest against Soviet behaviour. The United Nations then created the United Nations Emergency Force on Lester Pearson's proposal, and the ceasefire took effect at midnight on 6-7 November. The outcome showed that Britain and France could no longer act against American wishes, and Eden resigned in January 1957.\n\nKatanga's secession on 11 July 1960, only eleven days after Congolese independence, became international for similar reasons. The province contained the copper, cobalt and uranium mines worked by the Belgian-controlled Union Minière, and Belgium supported Tshombe's regime with officers and advisers, even though no state recognised Katanga. Belgian troops returned to the Congo on 10 July. Lumumba appealed to the United Nations, and the Security Council authorised the ONUC operation, which grew to about 20,000 troops and was the largest UN operation of its time.\n\nThe Cold War also drew in the superpowers. When the UN refused to use force against Tshombe, Lumumba turned to the Soviet Union for aircraft and trucks, which alarmed the West. Mobutu, backed by the United States, intervened in September 1960, and Lumumba was murdered in Katanga in January 1961 with Belgian officers present, provoking protests in Moscow and across Africa. Khrushchev attacked Hammarskjöld and demanded a three-man leadership of the UN. Hammarskjöld himself died in an air crash near Ndola on 18 September 1961 while on his way to negotiate with Tshombe. Irish soldiers served with the UN, suffering nine dead at Niemba and the siege of Jadotville in September 1961. The secession ended in January 1963.\n\nIn both cases the international dimension arose because local disputes touched on wealth, strategy and the balance of the Cold War. At Suez the great powers confronted one another directly, while in Katanga the UN tried to protect a new state's territory while Belgium, the United States and the Soviet Union backed different sides. Both showed how decolonisation could draw the whole world into a single territory's affairs.",
