    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Ho Chi Minh, Achmad Sukarno and the race relations crisis in France in the 1980s were all important, though in different ways. Ho Chi Minh and Sukarno led two of the great independence struggles against European rule, while France in the 1980s showed that decolonisation also left a legacy at home, as the children of former colonial subjects demanded equality inside the old imperial power.\n\nHo Chi Minh was important first as the organiser of Vietnamese nationalism. Born in 1890, he spent some thirty years abroad, petitioned the Versailles peace conference in 1919 for Vietnamese rights and was ignored, and then joined the French Communist Party in 1920. In 1930 he helped found the Indochinese Communist Party, and in 1941 he founded the Viet Minh, which united communists and non-communist nationalists. The Viet Minh fought the Japanese and, when Japan surrendered, seized power in Hanoi in the August Revolution of 1945. On 2 September 1945 Ho proclaimed the Democratic Republic of Vietnam, quoting the American Declaration of Independence, so he gave the nationalist cause a government and a legitimacy that France could not simply remove.\n\nHis greater importance lay in the long war that followed. The talks with France in 1946, including the March agreement and the Fontainebleau conference, failed, and the shelling of Haiphong and the fighting in Hanoi in December 1946 began the First Indochina War. Under Ho's political leadership and with Vo Nguyen Giap commanding the army, the Viet Minh, helped by Chinese weapons and advisers from 1950, besieged the French at Dien Bien Phu from March to May 1954 and forced the surrender of more than 10,000 men. The Geneva Accords of July 1954 ended French rule, though they divided Vietnam at the 17th parallel, and Ho then led North Vietnam. The division led on to the Vietnam War, and the whole country came under his government's rule in 1975, six years after his death. He was therefore central both to ending French rule and to the wider conflict that followed.\n\nAchmad Sukarno was equally important in Indonesia. A trained engineer, he founded the Indonesian Nationalist Party in 1927, was imprisoned by the Dutch in 1929 and later exiled. Under the Japanese occupation he worked with the occupiers, which critics called collaboration, but this allowed him to speak publicly and mobilise people. On 1 June 1945 he set out the Pancasila, five principles meant to unite nationalists, Muslims and communists, and with Mohammad Hatta he proclaimed independence on 17 August 1945. He became the first president of the Republic and the symbol of national unity, often called Bung Karno.\n\nSukarno's republic survived the Dutch return. The Dutch launched two police actions, in July 1947 and December 1948, and in the second they captured Yogyakarta and imprisoned Sukarno and Hatta. But Indonesian resistance continued, the UN became involved, and American pressure on the Netherlands, including threats over Marshall Plan aid, helped force the Round Table Conference at The Hague. Sovereignty was transferred on 27 December 1949. Sukarno also gave Indonesia a voice abroad as host of the Bandung Conference of 1955, which brought together 29 African and Asian states and promoted non-alignment and anti-colonialism. His later record was weaker. He introduced Guided Democracy in 1959, ended parliamentary rule, pursued Konfrontasi against Malaysia, and presided over economic decline. After the attempted coup of 30 September 1965 General Suharto took control, the killings of the PKI and its supporters followed, and Sukarno lost real power through the Supersemar order of March 1966.\n\nRace relations in France in the 1980s were important for a different reason. The children of North African migrants, known as beurs, lived in run-down banlieue estates with high youth unemployment and poor relations with the police. The Minguettes estate near Lyon saw car burning and clashes with police in 1981. In October 1983, after Toumi Djaïdja was shot and wounded by a police officer, the March for Equality and against Racism left Marseille with about thirty people, reached Paris on 3 December with tens of thousands, and was received by President Mitterrand, who promised a ten-year residence card, introduced in 1984. SOS Racisme, founded in 1984 with its slogan Touche pas à mon pote, drew huge crowds, including at the Place de la Concorde in 1985.\n\nThere was also a hostile reaction. Jean-Marie Le Pen's Front National broke through at Dreux in 1983, won about 11 per cent in the 1984 European elections, 35 seats in the Assembly in 1986 and about 14.4 per cent in the 1988 presidential election. Chirac's government under Charles Pasqua tightened immigration law in 1986 and had to abandon its plan to change nationality rules after mass protest. The headscarf affair at Creil in 1989 showed the clash between laïcité and Muslim identity. These events were important because they showed that France had to decide whether the descendants of its former empire were equal citizens.\n\nOverall, Ho Chi Minh was the most important of the three in world terms, because his victory over France changed the whole of Indochina and fed into the Vietnam War. Sukarno won sovereignty and unity for the world's largest archipelago but ruined much of it by authoritarian rule. The French case was less dramatic, but it showed that the ending of empire raised lasting questions of identity and equality in Europe itself.",
