    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The American economy between 1945 and 1989 passed through two very different phases. The first, to about 1968, was a long boom built on wartime strength, government spending and consumer demand. The second, from the late 1960s, brought inflation, oil shocks, industrial decline and large deficits, followed by recovery under Reagan that left lasting problems.\n\nThe starting point was the wartime base. In 1945 the United States produced roughly half of the world's manufactured goods while its rivals lay in ruins, and Americans had large wartime savings and a pent-up demand for cars, houses and appliances. The GI Bill of 1944 gave veterans loans and education, creating a mass market for housing. Internationally, the Bretton Woods conference of 1944 made the dollar the central currency, convertible to gold at 35 dollars an ounce, and Marshall Aid of about 13 billion dollars in 1948 to 1952 was largely spent on American goods.\n\nGovernment spending was a second pillar of the boom. The Korean War and NSC-68 raised the defence budget from about 14 billion dollars in 1950 to about 50 billion by 1953, and defence contracts sustained aircraft, electronics and missile firms, giving rise to what Eisenhower called the military-industrial complex in his farewell address of January 1961. The Federal-Aid Highway Act of 1956 funded about 41,000 miles of interstate highway, boosting cars, steel and suburban growth, and the space programme after Sputnik employed thousands more. Under Kennedy and Johnson the Revenue Act of 1964 cut the top income tax rate from 91 to 70 per cent and was followed by strong growth.\n\nThird, the period saw the rise of the multinational corporation and the corporate economy. General Motors, IBM, Coca-Cola and ITT built large overseas operations, especially in Western Europe after the Treaty of Rome of 1957, and General Motors owned Opel and Vauxhall. Critics such as Servan-Schreiber in The American Challenge in 1967 feared American control of European industry. At home organised labour shared in the gains, as the Treaty of Detroit of 1950 tied wages to productivity and gave pensions and health insurance to autoworkers, although the boom left many behind, as Michael Harrington's The Other America showed in 1962.\n\nThe turning point came with Vietnam. Johnson funded both the war and the Great Society without raising taxes until 1968, so deficits grew and inflation, about 1 per cent in 1964, passed 4 per cent in 1968. The balance of payments worsened as dollars flowed abroad, and on 15 August 1971 Nixon suspended the convertibility of the dollar into gold, ending the Bretton Woods system. In 1971 the United States imported more goods than it exported for the first time since 1893.\n\nThe oil shocks and industrial decline followed. The Arab oil embargo of October 1973 quadrupled oil prices and produced petrol queues, and the recession of 1973 to 1975 was the deepest since the 1930s, with unemployment of about 9 per cent. A second shock after the Iranian Revolution pushed inflation above 13 per cent in 1980, and stagflation became the defining problem. Meanwhile Japanese and European competitors took over markets in steel and cars. The Youngstown steel mill closed in 1977, Japan overtook the United States as the largest car producer in 1980, and Chrysler needed federal loan guarantees of 1.5 billion dollars in 1979. The rust belt declined while the Sun Belt and Silicon Valley grew.\n\nFinally, the Reagan years brought supply-side policy and a changed economy. Paul Volcker raised interest rates to bring down inflation, causing the severe recession of 1981 to 1982 in which unemployment passed 10 per cent. The tax cuts of 1981 and 1986 and deregulation encouraged recovery, and the expansion from late 1982 created about 18 million jobs. Yet defence spending and tax cuts produced huge deficits, the national debt nearly tripled to almost 3 trillion dollars, and in 1985 the United States became a net debtor nation. The crash of October 1987 and widening inequality showed the fragility of recovery.\n\nOverall, the most significant developments were the postwar boom driven by American dominance and public spending, and the reversal after 1968 into inflation, deindustrialisation and debt. The strength of 1945 to 1968 could not last once Europe and Japan recovered, and the economy of 1989 was more service-based, more open to the world and more indebted than that of 1945.",
