    "model": "Arthur Griffith chaired the Irish delegation and led the negotiations on the Crown and Ulster, and he was prepared to accept dominion status with a Boundary Commission. Michael Collins handled the military and financial issues in London and built a good relationship with Lord Birkenhead and Winston Churchill. On 2 December Griffith gave Lloyd George a written assurance that he would not let the talks break on the Ulster question, provided the Boundary Commission was set up. Griffith and Collins then argued that the alternative to signing was war, and the Treaty was signed at about 2.10 am on 6 December 1921.",
