    "label": "B2",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "Charles Trevelyan, an Assistant Secretary to the Treasury from 1840, ran the Treasury's Famine relief from 1845. He controlled the purchase of Peel's maize, the public works, the food depots and the soup kitchens. He was an evangelical Protestant and a firm believer in laissez-faire, who feared that generous relief would make the Irish dependent. He insisted that relief costs must fall on Irish property, and he closed the soup kitchens in 1847 and declared the Famine over.",
