    "label": "B2",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "Serfs were peasants bound to the land of their noble owners, who controlled their work, marriage and movement and could buy or sell them. By the late 1850s privately owned serfs made up over a third of Russia's population. Serfs owed labour dues called barshchina, working the lord's land for several days a week. Serfdom held back farming because serfs had no reason to improve land they did not own. Alexander II's edict of 19 February 1861 freed the serfs from personal bondage, but peasants then had to pay redemption payments over 49 years.",
