    "label": "C4",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Giuseppe Mazzini founded Young Italy at Marseille in 1831, a secret society whose members swore to work for an independent, united republic. He organised a failed invasion of Savoy in 1834 and founded Young Europe to link revolutionary movements. In 1849 he governed the Roman Republic as one of its three triumvirs. His belief that Italy must be freed by the people's own insurrection inspired many ordinary followers. He planned risings and sent agents into Italy, but his conspiracies largely failed. Mazzini rejected the monarchical Italy of 1861 as a betrayal, and the Italian National Society backed Piedmont's leadership instead. Historians see his ideas as a key influence on the Risorgimento, although his republic was never achieved.",
