    "label": "B1",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "Marshall Aid was announced by US Secretary of State George Marshall in a speech at Harvard on 5 June 1947. The European Recovery Programme of 1948 to 1952 provided about 13 billion dollars, mostly as grants rather than loans. The Organisation for European Economic Co-operation, set up in April 1948, coordinated the aid among sixteen European states. Britain, France and West Germany received the largest sums, and the Soviet Union forbade eastern European states to take part, which divided Europe into two economic systems.",
