    "label": "C1",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "The uprising of 1956 was caused by the Stalinist rule of Mátyás Rákosi, whose secret police, the AVH, were hated, and by economic hardship under Soviet-style planning. Khrushchev's secret speech of February 1956 and the Polish October encouraged Hungarian reformers to press for change. On 23 October students in Budapest demanded free elections and the return of Imre Nagy, and when the AVH opened fire on the crowd outside the radio building, fighting spread across the city. Nagy declared Hungary neutral and announced withdrawal from the Warsaw Pact on 1 November, which Moscow could not accept. Soviet forces returned on 4 November and crushed the uprising, and Nagy was executed in 1958 after a secret trial. About 200,000 Hungarians fled to the West, and Kádár's government then ruled with harsh repression before moving to a policy of consolidation.",
