    "label": "C2",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "The Second Vatican Council was important because it changed the Church's relations with the modern world. Pope John XXIII called it, opening it on 11 October 1962, because he wanted aggiornamento, meaning the updating of the Church's life and teaching. The Council allowed local languages in the Mass and enlarged the role of the laity and of bishops collectively. Nostra Aetate of 1965 rejected the charge of collective Jewish guilt for the death of Christ and improved relations with Judaism. Dignitatis Humanae accepted religious liberty, which changed the Church's attitude to the state. Unitatis Redintegratio committed the Church to ecumenism, and in 1965 the mutual excommunications of 1054 between Rome and the Orthodox churches were lifted. Its effects were controversial, because Humanae Vitae of 1968 reaffirmed the ban on contraception and many Catholics in the West did not follow it.",
