    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Solzhenitsyn's One Day in the Life of Ivan Denisovich was published in 1962 and brought the Gulag camps into Soviet public discussion. His Gulag Archipelago, published in Paris in 1973, documented the Soviet camp system through testimony and history, and he had won the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1970. He criticised both Soviet communism and Western materialism in his 1978 Harvard address, and he was expelled from the Soviet Union in 1974. De Beauvoir's novel The Mandarins portrayed the Parisian intellectual left and won the Prix Goncourt in 1954. Her essay La vieillesse of 1970 attacked how society treated the elderly, and in 1960 she signed the Manifesto of the 121 in support of conscripts who refused to fight in Algeria.",
