    "model": "Britain's war left its economy exhausted, and it owed India over one billion pounds in sterling balances, so the Raj had lost much of its economic value. Labour's government of 1945 was committed to Indian self-rule, and Attlee announced on 20 February 1947 that British rule would end by June 1948 at the latest. Decades of nationalist agitation, from non-cooperation to the Quit India campaign of 1942, made British rule impossible to keep without a repression the British public would not accept. The INA trials and the 1946 naval mutiny led officials such as Wavell to doubt that the Indian Army could be relied on to suppress a mass rising. Britain's withdrawal came as Congress and the League failed to agree, and partition followed, with communal killings in the Punjab and Bengal and mass migration across the new border. India became independent on 15 August 1947 as a dominion, and it became a republic on 26 January 1950 under Nehru, while remaining in the Commonwealth.",
