    "label": "B1",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "In 1945 the United States produced roughly half of the world's manufactured goods, while its European and Japanese rivals had been devastated by war. Wartime savings and pent-up demand for cars, houses and appliances fuelled spending once rationing ended. The GI Bill of 1944 provided government-guaranteed home loans to veterans, which created a mass market for housing and consumer goods. The Bretton Woods system made the dollar the central currency, and Marshall Plan aid of about 13 billion dollars was largely spent on American goods.",
