    "label": "B3",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "Betty Friedan, born in Peoria, Illinois, in 1921, wrote The Feminine Mystique in 1963 after surveying her former Smith College classmates. The book argued that American women were trapped by a domestic ideal that denied them fulfilment, and it is widely credited with starting the second wave of feminism. In June 1966 she founded the National Organization for Women and became its first president. She organised the Women's Strike for Equality on 26 August 1970, but younger radical feminists criticised her as too moderate.",
