    "label": "C1",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Truman inherited a wave of strikes in 1945 and 1946 as the wartime economy was converted to peacetime production, and he sent Congress a 21-point reform programme in September 1945 that was mostly blocked. After the Republicans won both houses of Congress in 1946, his Fair Deal of 1949, which proposed national health insurance, was largely defeated. Abroad he faced Soviet pressure, which he answered with the Truman Doctrine of March 1947 and the Marshall Plan, which gave about 13 billion dollars of aid to Western Europe. When the Soviet Union blocked access to West Berlin in June 1948, he answered with an airlift that lasted until May 1949. When North Korea invaded the South in June 1950, he sent US forces without a congressional declaration of war, and he dismissed MacArthur in April 1951 after the general argued for attacking China. By 1952 the Korean stalemate and rising prices had cut his popularity sharply, and he announced that he would not seek re-election.",
