    "marks": 40,
    "model": "President Kennedy committed the United States in a speech to Congress on 25 May 1961 to landing a man on the Moon before the end of the decade. NASA chose lunar orbit rendezvous in 1962, in which a separate lunar module would descend from the command module orbiting the Moon. The Gemini programme of 1965 and 1966 tested the rendezvous and docking techniques that a Moon landing needed. Apollo 11 was launched on 16 July 1969, and Neil Armstrong and Edwin Buzz Aldrin landed the Eagle in the Sea of Tranquillity on 20 July 1969. The landing was broadcast live to an estimated 600 million people, and the crew splashed down safely in the Pacific on 24 July 1969. The landing was important as a Cold War victory over the Soviet Union, a triumph of American technology, and a national and global achievement that proved US technical strength.",
