    "label": "C4",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Billy Graham's Los Angeles crusade of 1949, extended after Hearst newspapers reported it, made him a national figure. He founded the Billy Graham Evangelistic Association in 1950, and his radio programme Hour of Decision later reached a worldwide audience. He co-founded Christianity Today in 1956 to give a conservative evangelical voice alongside the mainstream press, and in 1957 he held a crusade at Madison Square Garden that ran for about sixteen weeks. He took his crusades abroad to London in 1954 and Moscow in 1982, and he organised the Lausanne Congress in 1974. He advised presidents from Eisenhower to Nixon and preached at presidential events, which brought religion close to the centre of political life. He refused to hold segregated crusades from 1953 and invited Martin Luther King Jr. to pray at his New York crusade, though he urged patience rather than endorsing the protests.",
