    "label": "4",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "The Eucharistic Congress of June 1932 was significant mainly as a symbol rather than as a turning point in law or policy. It showed the Irish Free State to the world as a stable, devout and independent country, displayed the close alliance between the Catholic Church and the political leadership, and strengthened de Valera's standing at home and abroad. It also deepened the religious divide between North and South.\n\nIts first significance was religious and national. The 31st International Eucharistic Congress was held in Dublin to mark the 1,500th anniversary of St Patrick's traditional arrival in 432, which tied Catholicism to Irish identity. It ran from 22 to 26 June 1932, with Cardinal Lorenzo Lauri attending as the Pope's legate. On 25 June about 100,000 children attended a Mass in the Phoenix Park, and on 26 June the closing Mass was held before a crowd often put at around a million, about a quarter of the population of the state. John McCormack sang Panis Angelicus and a message from Pope Pius XI was broadcast live from the Vatican. Four processions then went to O'Connell Bridge for the benediction. The Congress presented Ireland as a Catholic nation, in line with the Gaelic and Catholic ideal of the 1930s.\n\nIt was also significant for church and state relations. The Congress had been planned under Cosgrave, but de Valera became head of government on 9 March 1932 and took a leading part. He and Archbishop Byrne received the legate at Dun Laoghaire and his government gave full support. The event showed co-operation between the hierarchy and the political leadership that later shaped the 1937 constitution and social legislation. Historians describe it as the high point of the Church's public influence and a sign of the conformity of the 1930s. The Governor-General, James McNeill, was not invited to the reception for the legate at Dublin Castle, and the dispute that followed formed part of the conflict that led to his dismissal in October 1932, so the Congress also showed the government's wish to diminish the Crown's representative.\n\nInternationally and politically it was a success. Only ten years after independence and a civil war, the state organised a huge event with special trains and sailings, loudspeakers and the Garda and Army managing the crowds, and it passed without major disorder. The radio station set up at Athlone for the Congress later became Radio Eireann in 1938. Foreign newspapers reported the event, giving the Free State publicity as a Catholic country, and de Valera's prestige grew. Cumann na nGaedheal noted that Cosgrave's government had planned it while Fianna Fail gained the credit.\n\nIn Northern Ireland the Congress had very different effects. Pilgrims travelling to and from Dublin were attacked, and trains were stoned at places such as Portadown, Lisburn and Belfast, with the police baton-charging at some stations. Unionists saw the Congress as proof that the Free State was a Catholic state, which reinforced their view that partition was necessary. For nationalists in the North it was a source of pride, so it deepened the distrust between the two communities on the island.\n\nOverall the Congress was a great public triumph, but its significance was largely symbolic. It changed no law or institution, yet it revealed the identity of church, state and nation in 1930s Ireland, raised de Valera's standing and hardened the division between North and South.",
