    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Government and private responses to the Great Famine were both only partly effective. Each saved lives at particular moments, but neither matched a crisis that killed about a million people between 1846 and 1851. Peel's early measures and the 1847 soup kitchens worked reasonably well, whereas the Whig faith in laissez-faire and the decision to make Irish property pay were failures. Private relief was swift and compassionate, but it was too small and short-lived to replace the state.\n\nPeel's response to the first, partial failure of 1845 was the most effective. His government secretly bought about £100,000 of Indian corn through Baring Brothers and sold it at cost from depots to hold down prices. A Relief Commission under Sir Randolph Routh coordinated local relief committees, and the Board of Works ran public works to give wages. Mortality was low in the first season, so these measures were later judged the most successful of the whole Famine, although the failure of 1845 was only partial. Peel also repealed the Corn Laws in 1846, though that did little for Ireland at once.\n\nRussell's Whig government, in office from June 1846, was far less effective. Committed to laissez-faire, it refused to import food or control prices and left supply to private merchants, while grain, cattle and butter still left Irish ports. The Labouchere Act of August 1846 relaunched public works with costs to be repaid locally. About 700,000 were on the works by March 1847, but wages were set low, were often late and lagged behind food prices, which more than doubled. Weakened people worked through a hard winter on roads that led nowhere. Charles Trevelyan at the Treasury carried out this policy, believing relief must not make the Irish dependent, and he was condemned for it by nationalists.\n\nThe Soup Kitchen Act of February 1847 was a real success, since by July about three million people a day received rations in the cheapest and most effective relief of the Famine. Yet the kitchens were closed by September 1847 and the Poor Law Extension Act of June 1847 made Irish property pay for Irish poverty. Its Gregory clause denied relief to anyone holding more than a quarter of an acre, forcing smallholders off their land, and landlords used it and the rates to clear estates. Western unions went bankrupt, workhouses became centres of fever and the Rate-in-Aid Act of 1849 taxed the whole country for them. The government treated the Famine as over by 1850, though death continued in the west. Government spending of about £7 million, half of it as loans, was small for the wealthiest state in the world.\n\nPrivate responses filled some of the gaps. The Society of Friends set up the Central Relief Committee in Dublin in November 1846, opened soup kitchens in Cork and elsewhere and gave food and money regardless of religion. Quaker visitors like William Forster and James Hack Tuke wrote reports that gave British readers trusted eyewitness accounts. The British Relief Association, founded in January 1847, raised about £470,000 and fed schoolchildren in the poorest unions through Count Strzelecki. Help also came from America, including the warship Jamestown, from the Choctaw Nation, from Pope Pius IX and from emigrants. Catholic and Church of Ireland clergy, nuns, and individuals like Asenath Nicholson gave direct help, and many died of fever.\n\nBut private relief had serious limits. Giving fell sharply after 1847 through donor fatigue, the British commercial crisis and the belief that the Famine was over, and it was weakest in the west where there were fewest gentry and merchants. The Quakers concluded that only the state could feed millions and withdrew from mass feeding, so by 1848 relief depended on the Poor Law. Some responses did harm: souperism, whereby some evangelical missions linked food to Protestant worship, left lasting bitterness, and many landlords cleared tenants or shipped them abroad, as Major Mahon did to about 1,500 tenants in 1847, many of whom died on the voyage.\n\nOverall, responses were ineffective compared with the scale of need. Private charity was quick, humane and non-sectarian at its best, but small and short-lived, while government relief was decisive in 1845-46 and in the spring of 1847 and then abandoned in favour of ideology and parsimony. Historians such as Ó Gráda and Kinealy agree that the scale of death was not inevitable.",
