    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The impact of land agitation and land reform between 1870 and 1909 was profound. It forced the state to concede the tenants' main demands, transformed Ireland from a country of landlords and tenants into one of peasant owners, and helped build the mass nationalist movement of Parnell. However, its impact was uneven, since labourers and the poor farmers of the west gained least, and it did not deliver Home Rule.\n\nThe first impact was to expose the weakness of the old system and to produce the 1881 settlement. Most farmers were tenants at will or on short leases, and their three demands became known as the Three Fs: fair rent, fixity of tenure and free sale. Gladstone's Land Act of 1870 gave compensation for improvement and disturbance, but it did not fix rents, so landlords could still raise them and evict for non-payment. When the agricultural depression of the late 1870s brought bad weather, cheap American grain and rising evictions, Michael Davitt organised the meeting at Irishtown in April 1879 and the Irish National Land League was founded in October 1879 with Parnell as president. Collective action, such as the boycotting of Captain Boycott in 1880, kept pressure on landlords, and the Ladies' Land League under Anna Parnell kept the campaign going when the men were jailed. The Land Law (Ireland) Act of August 1881 then granted fair rents fixed by land courts, fixity of tenure and free sale, and the first judicial rents cut rents by about one fifth on average. The Arrears Act of 1882 helped poorer tenants.\n\nA second impact was political. The Land League mobilised a mass base among the tenant farmers, who had been outside the electorate and the early Home Rule movement of Butt. It linked land to national politics through the New Departure and brought American funds and Fenian support to Parnell. His party became a national force, and the later National League, with branches in nearly every parish, grew out of the same network. Land agitation therefore gave the Home Rule party the strength to hold the balance of power in 1885.\n\nThird, it forced the government to pursue purchase, so that the long-term impact was the end of landlordism. The 1881 Act left dual ownership, with landlord and tenant sharing the holding. Conservative governments tried constructive unionism, or killing Home Rule with kindness: the Ashbourne Act of 1885 advanced the full purchase price to tenants, Balfour's Act of 1891 extended the funds, and the Congested Districts Board of 1891 aided the poor west. The Plan of Campaign of 1886 on about 100 estates, with its stand-offs, Balfour's coercion and the killing of three at Mitchelstown in September 1887, kept the question alive. The decisive step came after the Land Conference of December 1902, where Lord Dunraven, William O'Brien and John Redmond agreed a scheme. Wyndham's Land Act of 1903 offered loans of the full price over about 68 years and a bonus of 12 per cent to landlords, and more than 200,000 tenants bought within a few years. The Birrell Act of 1909 allowed compulsory purchase where landlords would not sell. By 1914 most tenants had bought or were buying their holdings.\n\nThe impact on Irish society was therefore a new class of farmer owners, conservative and attached to property, and landlords who had lost their economic and political power. Yet there were limits. Labourers gained little until the Labourers Act of 1906 provided cottages, and the small farmers of the west remained poor. Violence such as moonlighting, the failure of the No Rent Manifesto of 1881 and the papal condemnation of the Plan of Campaign in 1888 weakened the movement, and Parnell himself was never close to the Plan. Davitt's vision of land nationalisation was never realised, since the settlement came through state funding and compromise.\n\nIn conclusion, the impact was greatest in the social sphere, where the land was transferred to the tenants, and significant in politics, where the movement gave Parnell his mass base. Unionists said concessions were extracted by intimidation and nationalists said only pressure moved Westminster; historians still debate whether constructive unionism worked, since it removed a major grievance but did not stop the growth of nationalism.",
