    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Unionists opposed self-government for Ireland for reasons of religion, economics and identity, and they carried that opposition through by building mass organisations, winning Conservative support and, by 1913, threatening armed resistance. Between 1886 and 1914 this combination defeated two Home Rule Bills and forced the British government to accept that Ulster could not simply be coerced.\n\nThe first reason was religion. Protestants of every class feared that a parliament elected by a Catholic majority would be under the influence of the Catholic Church, and the slogan Home Rule is Rome Rule summed up the fear. The role of the hierarchy in selecting Irish Party candidates in 1885, and later the Ne Temere decree of 1908, seemed to confirm it. Protestants were a small minority in the south, about a tenth of the population outside Ulster, but in Ulster they were a slight majority of about 56 per cent in 1911, concentrated in Antrim, Down, Armagh and Londonderry. They did not intend to become a minority in a Catholic-dominated state.\n\nThe second reason was economic. The industrial north-east, with Belfast's shipyards, linen and engineering, depended on British markets and raw materials, whereas the agricultural south and west had a very different economy. Unionists argued that a Dublin parliament would favour farming interests and could impose tariffs that would damage Ulster industry. Landlords and Protestants who held a disproportionate share of Ireland's land, wealth and professions also feared for their property after the Land War and the Land Acts, and believed that nationalists, with their links to the Fenians, would not respect it.\n\nThe third reason was identity. Ulster Protestants saw themselves as British, were proud of the empire, and believed that Home Rule was the first step towards separation. Liberal unionists such as Chamberlain argued that it threatened the Union itself, not only the Ulster minority. Many Presbyterians who had supported the Liberals before 1886 moved into the unionist camp when Gladstone converted to Home Rule, and this widened unionism into a coalition of landlords, industrialists, farmers and Protestant workers.\n\nHow they opposed it begins with organisation. The Orange Order, founded in 1795 and revived in the 1880s, gave Ulster unionism a mass base of lodges. The Irish Loyal and Patriotic Union was founded by southern unionists in 1885, and the Irish Unionist Alliance in 1891 united the unionist bodies. In June 1892 about 12,000 delegates met at the Ulster Convention in Belfast to protest against the Second Home Rule Bill. The Ulster Unionist Council, founded in 1905 after unionists reacted against devolution proposals, gave the province a permanent central body, and the Ulster Women's Unionist Council of 1911 mobilised tens of thousands of women. Colonel Saunderson led the unionist MPs at Westminster from 1886, and the Conservative alliance meant the House of Lords could be relied on to wreck the first two Bills.\n\nThe campaign reached its peak after the Parliament Act of 1911 removed the Lords' veto and the Third Home Rule Bill was introduced in April 1912. Edward Carson, a Dublin-born barrister, became leader and James Craig his organiser. Bonar Law pledged Conservative support for Ulster at Blenheim Palace in July 1912. On Ulster Day, 28 September 1912, about 237,000 men signed the Solemn League and Covenant, pledging to use all means to defeat Home Rule, and about 234,000 women signed a separate declaration. In January 1913 the Ulster Volunteer Force was formed under General Sir George Richardson and grew to perhaps 90,000 men, and in September 1913 the Ulster Unionist Council announced a Provisional Government of Ulster to take over if Home Rule became law.\n\nIn 1914 resistance became more direct. The Curragh incident of March 1914, when Hubert Gough and many officers offered to resign rather than act against Ulster, showed that the army could not be relied on to coerce unionists. On the night of 24 and 25 April the UVF landed about 25,000 rifles and several million rounds at Larne, Donaghadee and Bangor, organised by Fred Crawford, and the authorities did not intervene. Asquith's offer of six years' exclusion in March was rejected by Carson, and the Buckingham Palace Conference of July 1914 failed over Tyrone and Fermanagh.\n\nOverall, unionists opposed Home Rule because they feared Catholic power, economic damage and the loss of their British identity, and they fought it by organisation, political alliance and the threat of force. They could not prevent the Third Bill passing, which became law in September 1914 though suspended for the war, but they had defeated two earlier Bills and made the exclusion of Ulster unavoidable. Their success, however, left southern unionists without protection, and it paved the way for partition.",
