    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The Gaelic Athletic Association and the suffrage movement both made a real contribution to Irish affairs between 1870 and 1914. The GAA gave Ireland a national sporting organisation open to all classes and fed the cultural nationalism of the 1880s, while the suffrage movement won women a place in local government, education and public debate, even though the parliamentary vote came only in 1918.\n\nThe GAA was founded by Michael Cusack and Maurice Davin at Hayes' Hotel in Thurles on 1 November 1884. At that time organised athletics followed English amateur rules that excluded artisans and labourers, and rugby and cricket were played by the gentry and middle class, while hurling had declined since the Famine. The Association's first contribution was to open sport to all classes, and Davin drew up the first rules for hurling and Gaelic football in 1885, replacing old local variations with a national code. It organised clubs on the parish, which gave it a network across the country, with county committees and a Central Council above them, and in 1887 it began the All-Ireland championships. The first hurling final was played at Birr in April 1888, when Thurles beat Meelick.\n\nThe GAA also contributed to national feeling. Archbishop Croke of Cashel became patron in a letter of December 1884 attacking the imitation of English games and manners, and Parnell and Davitt also agreed to be patrons. The rule banning members from playing or attending foreign games such as rugby, cricket and soccer, and barring the police and the British forces, made Gaelic games a symbol of de-anglicisation. The Association became part of the Irish Ireland idea that was later developed by the Gaelic League of 1893. Its weaknesses were also real, since the IRB infiltrated its leadership, Cusack was removed as secretary in 1886, Davin stepped down in 1889, the costly American tour of 1888 left debts, and the Parnell split of 1890 and 1891 almost destroyed it. Yet the parish structure and the appeal of the games kept it alive.\n\nThe suffrage movement began as a small, respectable campaign. Isabella Tod founded a suffrage society in Belfast in the early 1870s, and Anna Haslam and her husband Thomas founded the Dublin Women's Suffrage Association in 1876. These women also campaigned for property rights and for girls' education, and Tod helped win girls' inclusion in the Intermediate Education Act of 1878. Women ratepayers won the municipal vote in 1887, and the Local Government Act of 1898 gave women the vote in county and district council elections and the right to sit on district councils and boards of guardians. The Royal University admitted women to degrees from the early 1880s, and in 1877 the Dublin College of Physicians began licensing women.\n\nAfter 1900 the movement became more assertive. The Irish Women's Franchise League, founded in 1908 by Hanna Sheehy-Skeffington and Margaret Cousins, was more militant, and it published the weekly Irish Citizen from 1912. In June 1912 eight members were imprisoned for breaking windows, and prisoners went on hunger strike. The Irish Party under Redmond and Dillon refused to support votes for women, fearing it would complicate Home Rule, and a Conciliation Bill was defeated in 1912. The movement was also divided between nationalists and unionists, and militancy won little immediate change.\n\nOn balance, both movements left a lasting mark. The GAA created a mass organisation that anticipated the cultural revival and later supplied recruits and a network for nationalism. The suffrage movement did not win the parliamentary vote before 1918, but it opened local government and education to women, trained a generation of organisers, and insisted, through figures like Sheehy-Skeffington, that independence must include equality.",
