    "label": "1",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Grosse Isle, the quarantine station for the port of Quebec, played a central role in the history of Irish emigration because it was the first North American landfall for tens of thousands of Famine emigrants and the place where the human cost of mass emigration was most starkly exposed. Its role was medical, as a barrier against disease, but in 1847 it became a scene of mass death, a test of official preparedness and, later, a lasting memorial to the Famine dead abroad.\n\nGrosse Isle was an island in the St Lawrence River, about 48 km downstream from Quebec City, and the station was opened in 1832 to stop cholera reaching Quebec from emigrant ships. Every ship bound for Quebec had to stop there, where a medical officer inspected the passengers, the sick were landed at the hospital and the healthy were held in sheds or on board. Its importance grew because British North America was the cheapest transatlantic destination, and after the US Passenger Acts of 1847 limited passenger numbers and raised fares on the American route, even more of the poorest and sickest emigrants were diverted to Canada. The station was built for a normal season and held only a few hundred patients, so it was never designed for the flood that followed.\n\nThe Famine turned the station into a catastrophe. Emigrants of 1847 were poor, weakened by hunger and often travelled on overcrowded coffin ships, where typhus, spread by lice in the crowded holds, killed many at sea. Of about 100,000 people who sailed for British North America that year, an estimated 17,000 or more died at sea, in quarantine or soon after arrival. The first ship of the season, the Syria, arrived in mid-May with fever on board and the hospital was full within days. By 31 May about 40 ships carrying some 12,500 passengers lay at anchor in a queue stretching down the river. Ships waited days or weeks, and the sick and dead lay in the holds beside the healthy, so fever spread among people who had been well. Grosse Isle therefore shows how the quarantine system was overwhelmed by the scale of Famine emigration.\n\nThe response on the island showed both failure and heroism. The medical superintendent, Dr George Mellis Douglas, had warned the authorities in advance and asked for more help, but tents and new sheds were not enough, and sick and dying people lay on the ground and on the beaches while healthy passengers crowded into sheds where many caught typhus. Several doctors caught typhus and some died, and Catholic priests from the diocese of Quebec, many of them French-Canadian, and Anglican clergy took turns on the island, with several catching the fever and some dying. More than 5,000 people were buried on the island in 1847, most of them Irish, and some estimates are higher. The role of Grosse Isle was thus also to reveal the failure of governments on both sides of the Atlantic to prepare, and the generosity of French-Canadian Quebec.\n\nThe effects spread well beyond the island. Passengers passed as healthy carried typhus up the river, and about 6,000 Irish emigrants died in the fever sheds at Montreal in 1847, including the mayor, John Mills, who died of typhus while helping the sick. Hundreds of children orphaned at Grosse Isle and in the fever sheds were adopted by French-Canadian families, many of whom let them keep their Irish surnames, which created a lasting bond between Irish and French Canada. Grosse Isle was not unique, since Partridge Island at Saint John, New Brunswick, faced a similar crisis in 1847, and New York received far more Famine emigrants than Quebec. But Grosse Isle became the most famous because so many died there in such a short time.\n\nGrosse Isle also had a long afterlife in memory and politics. Medical staff erected a monument in 1853 to the dead of 1847, and the Ancient Order of Hibernians unveiled a large Celtic cross on the island's highest point on 15 August 1909, with inscriptions in Irish, English and French, the Irish text blaming the Famine on British rule more bluntly than the English. Nationalists from John Mitchel onwards used Grosse Isle as evidence that British policy, not only the blight, killed the Irish poor, while others stressed landlord clearances, such as Major Mahon's shipment of 1,490 Strokestown tenants to Quebec in 1847, and the limits of colonial resources. The station closed in 1937, and after Irish-Canadian campaigning the island became, in the 1990s, Grosse Ile and the Irish Memorial National Historic Site.\n\nGrosse Isle was both a quarantine station and a symbol. In practice it was the first point of contact with North America for tens of thousands of destitute emigrants, and in 1847 it exposed the dangers of unregulated mass emigration and the unpreparedness of governments. In memory it became the leading memorial to the Famine dead outside Ireland, though the great majority of Famine emigrants went to the United States and Britain rather than to Quebec.",
