    "label": "2",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Terence O'Neill and Ian Paisley both shaped Northern Ireland profoundly. O'Neill tried to modernise the state and to reconcile the two communities, and in doing so set off a crisis that ended his career. Paisley, who opposed him at every turn, became the voice of hardline unionism and in later decades a leading figure in unionist resistance to compromise.\n\nO'Neill became Prime Minister in March 1963 after serving as Minister of Finance since 1956. His main aim was economic modernisation, and he drew on the Matthew Report of 1963 and the Wilson Plan of 1965 to promote planning, which led to the new city of Craigavon and the new university at Coleraine. He wanted to show that Northern Ireland could be prosperous and modern, and that better jobs and living standards would draw the communities together. The weakness was that new investment went mostly to Belfast and the east, so the largely Catholic west gained little.\n\nHis second contribution was bridge-building. He visited Catholic schools and hospitals, and on 14 January 1965 he received the Taoiseach, Seán Lemass, at Stormont, the first meeting between the heads of the two governments since partition. He returned the visit to Dublin the following month. In 1965 the Nationalist Party under Eddie McAteer became the official opposition, a sign that these gestures had some effect. But he failed to tell most of his cabinet about the Lemass meeting, and he did not see Catholic grievances as political, so his gestures did not touch housing, voting or jobs.\n\nThird, O'Neill's approach helped to unleash the civil rights movement. The Campaign for Social Justice, founded in 1964, and the Civil Rights Association, formed in 1967, pushed for one man, one vote and fair housing. After the police broke up the Derry march of 5 October 1968, O'Neill came under pressure from London and announced a five-point package on 22 November 1968. It promised a points system for housing, an ombudsman, an end to the company vote and the replacement of Londonderry Corporation by a commission. He appealed for calm in the 'Ulster at the Crossroads' broadcast on 9 December 1968. He sacked William Craig in December, but his party accepted one man, one vote only in April 1969. His reforms came too late for Catholics and went too far for many unionists, and he resigned on 28 April 1969, so the limits of reform from above were clear.\n\nIan Paisley, born in Armagh in 1926, founded the Free Presbyterian Church in 1951 and became the most visible opponent of O'Neill's policies. He denounced the Lemass meeting as a threat to the Union and to Protestant identity, and he led the 'O'Neill must go' campaign from 1966, setting up the Ulster Constitution Defence Committee and the Ulster Protestant Volunteers. He was jailed for three months in 1966, and in the February 1969 election he opposed O'Neill in Bannside, losing by 1,414 votes. His rallies mobilised grassroots Protestant opinion and made it much harder for moderates to support reform, and historians argue that he helped to push unionist politics towards the hardline position of the 1970s. He won O'Neill's old Bannside seat in April 1970.\n\nPaisley's role did not end with O'Neill's fall. He founded the Democratic Unionist Party in 1971 and opposed power-sharing and the Sunningdale Agreement in 1973 and 1974. In 1985 he joined James Molyneaux in the 'Ulster says No' campaign against the Anglo-Irish Agreement, signed on 15 November 1985, which gave Dublin a consultative role. Paisley's contribution therefore lay in resistance, in blocking compromise, and unionists who felt abandoned by London looked to him for leadership. Through the DUP he built a lasting political base.\n\nOverall, both men had great influence but neither achieved what he wanted. O'Neill's attempt to reform on his own terms failed because it satisfied neither Catholics nor hardline unionists, but it did start the process of change and put reform on the agenda. Paisley's contribution was more negative, since he was effective in stopping change and in shaping hardline unionism for a generation. I would argue that O'Neill's was the more constructive contribution, while Paisley's proved the more enduring in politics.",
