    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The Sunningdale Agreement of December 1973 was a settlement between the British and Irish governments and the pro-power-sharing parties of Northern Ireland, setting out a power-sharing executive, a Council of Ireland and a guarantee that the constitutional position would not change without the consent of a majority. The executive it underpinned collapsed in May 1974, after only five months, because most unionists rejected the Council of Ireland and a loyalist general strike paralysed the province while the British government declined to confront it.\n\nThe background lay in the suspension of Stormont in 1972 and the British attempt to find a new basis for government. A border poll in March 1973 produced a 98.9 per cent vote to remain in the United Kingdom, though nationalists boycotted it and turnout was only about 59 per cent, so it settled little. The White Paper of March 1973 proposed an Assembly elected by proportional representation, an executive in which unionists and nationalists shared power, and a Council of Ireland. The Assembly election of June 1973 used the single transferable vote and returned unionists, nationalists and Alliance members, but the Ulster Unionist Party was already split between Brian Faulkner's supporters and opponents such as Paisley's Democratic Unionists and William Craig's Vanguard, which was a weakness from the start.\n\nThe Sunningdale conference met at Sunningdale Park in Berkshire from 6 to 9 December 1973, attended by Edward Heath, the Taoiseach Liam Cosgrave and the Northern parties of Faulkner's Unionists, the SDLP under Gerry Fitt and John Hume, and Alliance. The agreement created a Council of Ireland with a council of ministers, seven from each side, and a consultative assembly of sixty members drawn equally from the Dail and the Northern Ireland Assembly. The Irish government accepted that Northern Ireland's status would not change without the consent of a majority there, and Britain declared that it would support a united Ireland if a majority wished it. A Law Enforcement Commission was set up to deal with fugitive offenders, and both governments promised policing acceptable to both communities. The power-sharing executive took office on 1 January 1974, with Faulkner as Chief Executive and Fitt as his deputy, and Hume, Austin Currie and Paddy Devlin among the SDLP ministers.\n\nThe first and most important cause of collapse was unionist hostility to the Council of Ireland, which many saw as a first step towards a united Ireland. This was reinforced by the Boland case, in which the Irish government argued that its declaration on status did not alter Articles 2 and 3 of its constitution, which seemed to prove that the territorial claim remained. On 4 January 1974 the Ulster Unionist Council rejected the Council of Ireland by 427 votes to 374, and Faulkner resigned as party leader, leaving him as head of an executive without the backing of his own party. In the Westminster election of February 1974 the United Ulster Unionist Council, formed to oppose Sunningdale, won eleven of the twelve Northern Ireland seats, which gave anti-agreement unionists a strong claim that the executive did not represent the majority.\n\nThe immediate cause was the Ulster Workers' Council strike, which began on 15 May 1974, the day after the Assembly had voted 44 to 28 to support the Sunningdale policy. Backed by loyalist paramilitaries, workers in power generation and other essential industries cut electricity supplies and paralysed services, and roadblocks and intimidation enforced the stoppage. The Dublin and Monaghan bombings of 17 May, which killed 33 people in the Republic, added to the tension. The strike showed that a determined loyalist protest could stop Northern Ireland's economy and government, and it was presented as the voice of the majority against an imposed settlement.\n\nBritish weakness was the third factor. The Heath government had fallen in February 1974 and Harold Wilson's Labour government, with Merlyn Rees as Secretary of State, was reluctant to use the army against the strikers. Wilson's broadcast of 25 May, in which he said the strikers were sponging on British democracy, angered unionists further without breaking the strike. Faulkner proposed delaying the later stages of the Council of Ireland, but when the SDLP and Alliance rejected this he resigned on 28 May 1974 and the executive collapsed. Continuing republican violence also made moderate unionists more fearful about security and about nationalist intentions.\n\nOverall, Sunningdale failed because it was built on a narrow base. It had the support of the two governments, the SDLP and Alliance, but only a divided minority of unionists, and it had no means of winning the consent of the unionist majority for the Irish dimension. The strike supplied the means of destruction, but unionist opposition and British hesitation made it fatal. Its legacy was real, since the principles of consent, power sharing and an Irish dimension returned in the Belfast Agreement of 1998, which Seamus Mallon called Sunningdale for slow learners, but in 1974 it was an experiment that arrived before the unionist community was ready for it.",
