    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The Troubles had a heavy social and economic impact on Northern Ireland between 1969 and the early 1990s, and they also produced a rich cultural response in poetry, drama and the work of church and community groups. The economy suffered from lost investment and rising dependence on the state, society became more segregated and more traumatised, and writers and reconcilers tried, with limited political effect, to respond to the violence.\n\nThe human cost was the starting point. About 3,000 people died between 1969 and 1993, and about 3,500 by 2001, with 1972 the worst year at nearly 500 deaths. About half of the dead were civilians, and tens of thousands were injured. The violence fell most heavily on working-class areas of Belfast and Derry such as Ardoyne, the Falls and the Shankill, where the two communities lived side by side. The Royal Victoria Hospital in Belfast developed special expertise in gunshot and blast injuries, while stress and trauma became widespread among people who had lived through bombings, shootings and displacement.\n\nSocially, the Troubles deepened the divisions between the communities. Intimidation drove tens of thousands of families from their homes in Belfast between 1969 and 1973, and they moved to areas where their own community was in the majority. Peace walls were built between Protestant and Catholic streets from 1969, and housing became more segregated despite the Northern Ireland Housing Executive, set up in 1971 with a points system to end the discrimination the civil rights movement had criticised. Schooling remained divided, although parents formed All Children Together in 1974 and Lagan College opened in 1981 as the first integrated secondary school, followed by the Education Reform Order of 1989 giving integrated schools statutory support. Everyday life was shaped by checkpoints, searches and town centres sealed off after bombings, and children grew up among the sights and sounds of conflict.\n\nEconomically, the violence deterred investment and tourism. Foreign investors were wary of building factories in a province at risk of bombing, visitors stayed away, and central Belfast was surrounded by a security cordon known as the ring of steel which harmed trade. Businesses faced high insurance costs and some moved to Britain or the Republic. Manufacturing in shipbuilding, linen and engineering kept shrinking, and Harland and Wolff and Short Brothers were taken into public ownership in 1975 and 1977 to protect jobs. Public-sector and security employment grew, with the RUC, the Ulster Defence Regiment and the prison service all large employers, and British subvention rose, leaving the economy dependent on London. The De Lorean car factory at Dunmurry, opened in 1981 with heavy government grants to create about 2,000 jobs, went into receivership in 1982 and became a symbol of how hard it was to attract lasting investment.\n\nUnemployment remained the highest in the United Kingdom for much of the period, and Catholic unemployment was consistently higher than Protestant, in some surveys about twice as high. The Fair Employment Act of 1976 set up the Fair Employment Agency, and the Act of 1989 created a Fair Employment Commission and required employers to monitor their workforces, while the MacBride Principles of 1984 pressed American firms to follow fair practice. These measures helped, but deprivation remained deepest in west Belfast and Derry, and youth unemployment fed recruitment to paramilitaries.\n\nCulturally, the Troubles inspired writers and artists. Seamus Heaney, from a Catholic farming family in County Derry, explored violence and the inheritance of the past in collections such as North in 1975, using the Iron Age bog bodies in poems such as Punishment as images of ritual killing, and he won the Nobel Prize for Literature in 1995. Field Day Theatre Company was founded in 1980 by Brian Friel and Stephen Rea, and its first production, Friel's Translations, opened at the Guildhall in Derry in September 1980, dealing with the renaming of Irish placenames and the loss of language. Other works included Bernard MacLaverty's Cal, Frank McGuinness's play on the Somme, and murals in Catholic and Protestant areas, including the Free Derry gable. Critics such as Ciaran Carson and Edna Longley questioned whether these works mythologised violence or underplayed the unionist tradition.\n\nChurch and community responses sought reconciliation. The Corrymeela Community was founded in 1965 by the Presbyterian minister Ray Davey, the Irish School of Ecumenics in 1970, and church leaders met at Ballymascanlon in 1973. Protestant clergy met IRA leaders at Feakle in December 1974, and the Cultural Traditions Group of 1988 tried to promote mutual understanding. Yet Ian Paisley and the Free Presbyterians attacked ecumenism, and these initiatives reached mainly the educated and moderate, leaving the political division untouched.\n\nOverall, the Troubles left a costly legacy of death, segregation, dependence and unequal opportunity, with the heaviest burden on the poorest areas. The cultural response was genuine and often brilliant, giving grief and identity a voice, but it could not by itself resolve a political conflict, and it sometimes reflected the separate traditions it sought to bridge.",
