    "label": "1",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The system of Metternich was the policy, pursued by the Austrian foreign minister and later state chancellor from 1809 to 1848, of defending the settlement of 1815 and the autocratic order of Europe against the twin threats of liberalism and nationalism. It was a system of repression at home and intervention abroad, and it was attacked repeatedly, by students, revolutionaries, nationalists and economic change. It held for over thirty years, but it never removed the causes of unrest and it collapsed in March 1848.\n\nThe system rested on the settlement made at the Congress of Vienna, where Metternich was the dominant figure. The Final Act of June 1815 restored legitimate rulers such as the Bourbons, built buffer states around France such as the enlarged Netherlands and a strengthened Piedmont, and gave Austria Lombardy-Venetia and influence over Tuscany, Modena and Parma. In Germany the Holy Roman Empire was replaced by a loose Confederation of 39 states with a Diet at Frankfurt under Austrian presidency, designed to guard against France and revolution rather than to produce unity. Nationalist and liberal hopes were ignored, because Metternich believed that nationalism would tear apart the multinational Habsburg Empire.\n\nMetternich defended this order abroad through the Congress System. The Quadruple Alliance of 1815 and the Holy Alliance bound the eastern monarchies together, and at Troppau in 1820 Austria, Prussia and Russia claimed the right to intervene against revolutions in other states. At Laibach in 1821 Austrian troops were authorised to crush the revolution in Naples, and Austria also put down the rising in Piedmont. Austrian troops again suppressed risings in Modena, Parma and the Papal Legations in 1831. The system was therefore an active instrument of intervention, not just a statement of principles.\n\nAt home and in the German states it was a system of police, censorship and control. Austria had no constitution or parliament, and informers, intercepted post and strict censorship silenced critics. After the murder of the conservative writer Kotzebue by the student Karl Sand in 1819, the Carlsbad Decrees banned the Burschenschaften, placed the universities under government supervisors, allowed liberal professors to be dismissed and imposed press censorship, and a commission at Mainz hunted conspirators. After the Hambach Festival of 1832 the Six Articles and further measures banned political associations and meetings and restricted state parliaments. Metternich could rely on the cooperation of Prussia and Russia to enforce all this.\n\nThe system was challenged almost from the start. The Burschenschaften, founded at Jena in 1815, spread liberal and nationalist ideas, and the Wartburg Festival of 1817 called for German unity. The revolutions of 1820 and 1821 in Spain, Naples and Piedmont forced kings to grant constitutions, though they were reversed by Austrian and French troops. The Hambach Festival of 1832 drew about twenty thousand or more people to demand unity, a free press and popular sovereignty. These attacks were largely contained, but they show how much repression was needed to hold the order together.\n\nSome attacks succeeded in breaking the settlement. The Greek War of Independence from 1821 divided the powers, and Britain, France and Russia destroyed the Turkish-Egyptian fleet at Navarino in 1827, leading to an independent Greece, which Metternich opposed as support for rebels against a legitimate ruler. Britain had already left the Congress System after Verona in 1822, leaving only the eastern autocracies. The July Revolution of 1830 overthrew Charles X in France, and the Belgian revolt of 1830 broke up the Kingdom of the Netherlands, with Belgian independence recognised and Leopold of Saxe-Coburg made king in 1831. Only in Poland, where Nicholas I crushed the 1830 rising, did the system succeed.\n\nEconomic change also undermined Austria's position. Prussia's customs union, the Zollverein, came into force in 1834 with 18 states and excluded Austria, which could not join because of its protected industries. It accustomed the smaller German states to looking to Berlin rather than Vienna, and Metternich warned that it was building Prussian influence.\n\nIn the end the system was under attack to a very large extent, and it failed to survive. It kept Europe free of a general war until the Crimean War and delayed change by over thirty years, but this owed something to exhaustion after 1815 and to British naval power. When news of the February 1848 revolution in Paris reached Vienna, students and workers demonstrated on 13 March, troops fired on the crowd and Metternich was forced to resign that evening and fled to England. A system built on repression had silenced the demands of liberals and nationalists without answering them, and they returned with greater force in 1848.",
