    "label": "2",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Between 1815 and 1871 both agriculture and industry in Europe were transformed, though at different speeds. Britain led the way in both, with enclosure, new crops, machinery and the factory system, while France and the German lands changed more slowly and by different routes. The most dramatic change was the spread of coal, iron, steam and railways, which by 1871 had made Britain, and increasingly Germany, industrial societies.\n\nIn British agriculture the change was one of consolidation and improved method. Enclosure replaced open fields and common land with consolidated farms, mostly through Acts of Parliament passed between 1750 and 1850, and the General Enclosure Acts of 1801 and 1845 simplified the process. The Norfolk four-course rotation of wheat, turnips, barley and clover kept the soil fertile without leaving land fallow, and Tull's seed drill and Bakewell's selective breeding raised yields of grain, meat and wool. The Corn Laws of 1815 protected grain growers from cheap imports until their repeal under Peel in 1846. From the 1840s fertilisers such as guano and Lawes's superphosphate raised yields further.\n\nThese changes had heavy human costs. Many labourers lost common rights and access to land and moved to towns or emigrated, and mechanisation such as the threshing machine reduced the need for hand labour, which provoked the Swing Riots of 1830 in southern England. Agricultural change thus fed the growth of industrial towns by driving people off the land.\n\nOn the continent the changes in farming were different. In France the Revolution had abolished feudal dues and divided many estates, so the peasantry owned much of the land and farming remained small-scale. In Prussia the Stein-Hardenberg reforms from 1807 abolished serfdom in stages and allowed peasants to become owners, though many had to give up part of their land to the noble estates, and the great Junker estates of the east were modernised to grow grain for export and for the growing cities. The potato blight from 1845 and poor harvests in 1846-47 caused hunger and high prices across western and central Europe, helping to create the conditions for the revolutions of 1848, and emigration to the United States grew strongly.\n\nIndustrial change began in Britain, based on coal, iron and cotton. Watt's improvements to the steam engine in the 1760s and 1770s powered mines and factories, Arkwright's water frame and Crompton's mule transformed cotton spinning, and Darby's coke-smelting at Coalbrookdale produced iron on a larger scale. Railways were the great symbol. Stephenson's Rocket won the Rainhill Trials in 1829, the Liverpool and Manchester Railway opened in 1830, and the network expanded rapidly in the 1840s. Bessemer's converter of 1856 made steel cheaply, and the Great Exhibition of 1851 presented Britain as the workshop of the world.\n\nIndustrialisation then spread to the continent. Belgium was first, with coal and iron in Hainaut and Liege. France industrialised more slowly, with textiles in Alsace and Normandy and a railway network built mainly from the 1840s, following the Railway Law of 1842. In the German lands the first railway ran from Nuremberg to Furth in 1835, the Ruhr and Silesian coalfields grew rapidly, and the Zollverein, which began in 1834, removed internal tariffs and encouraged trade and investment. Krupp's steelworks at Essen expanded from the 1850s. Continental industry was often financed by banks, while British industry relied mainly on private profits, and governments in Prussia and France were more willing to promote industry through tariffs, railways and state-backed banks.\n\nThe factory system also changed society. Workers laboured twelve to fourteen hours a day in cotton mills, with women and children forming a large part of the workforce. The state gradually intervened, through the Factory Act of 1833, the Mines Act of 1842 and the Ten Hours Act of 1847, though British governments, guided by laissez-faire ideas, would not regulate wages. Towns grew enormously, with Manchester rising from about 75,000 in 1801 to about 300,000 in 1851, and the census of 1851 showed that for the first time more than half the British people lived in towns.\n\nOverall, agriculture changed through consolidation, new methods and in places the ending of serfdom, while industry changed through steam, iron, steel and railways, moving from Britain to Belgium, France and above all the German lands. By 1871 Europe's economy was far more productive and more urban, but the benefits were uneven and the social costs severe.",
