    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "City planning developed in this period from a response to the crisis of rapidly growing cities into a deliberate, state-directed remaking of the urban landscape, and its most famous example was Haussmann's reconstruction of Paris under Napoleon III from 1853. Paris became the model that other cities followed, though planning elsewhere often took different forms, and Haussmann's work solved many physical problems at a high social cost.\n\nPlanning developed because industrial and urban growth had overwhelmed existing cities. Millions of people moved into towns, and the old streets, water supplies and housing could not cope. Narrow medieval streets hindered traffic, overcrowded and unsanitary districts bred disease, with cholera reaching Britain in 1831-32 and again in 1848-49 and striking Paris in 1832 and 1849, and crowded housing was a fire risk. Governments also feared revolution in districts where barricades could be built quickly in narrow streets. In Britain Edwin Chadwick's report of 1842 linked poor living conditions to disease and led the sanitary reform movement to press for drainage, water supply and public health legislation.\n\nParis before 1853 had about a million people and was divided between the wealthy west and the crowded working-class east and north. The central districts around the Ile de la Cite and Les Halles were a maze of narrow streets with poor drainage, and the revolutions of 1830 and 1848, including the June Days, had all been fought behind barricades in them. Napoleon III, who had admired London's parks and squares during his exile, wanted to improve health, provide work through public works and make the city easier to control. In June 1853 he appointed Baron Haussmann Prefect of the Seine, and Haussmann held the post for about seventeen years. The expropriation law of 1841, extended in 1852, gave him the legal power to buy property compulsorily and clear whole blocks.\n\nThe most visible part of the grands travaux was the cutting of broad, straight, tree-lined boulevards through the old city. The Rue de Rivoli was extended, the Boulevard de Sebastopol was cut north to south and linked the Seine with the railway stations, and the Boulevards Saint-Germain and Saint-Michel were driven through the Latin Quarter. The Etoile became the centre of twelve radiating avenues, and the Avenue de l'Opera was laid out to frame the new Opera. Building regulations fixed the height of facades, giving the city its uniform appearance. The boulevards eased traffic and opened vistas, and they also allowed troops to move quickly and artillery to fire along straight streets.\n\nHaussmann's reforms also tackled health and leisure. The engineer Eugene Belgrand built new aqueducts and reservoirs bringing clean water from the Dhuis and Vanne valleys, and an underground sewer network carried waste away, greatly improving sanitation in a city that had been among the worst served in Europe. Jean-Charles Alphand designed the parks, including the Bois de Boulogne in the west, the Bois de Vincennes in the east to give the working-class districts a counterpart, the Buttes-Chaumont opened in 1867, the Parc Monceau and the Parc Montsouris, along with small squares in the districts. Public buildings were added too, such as Baltard's iron-and-glass pavilions at Les Halles, the rebuilt Gare du Nord and Garnier's Opera, begun in 1861 and opened in 1875. In 1860 the suburbs were annexed, increasing the arrondissements from twelve to twenty and allowing the whole urban area to be planned.\n\nThe costs were considerable. The demolition displaced many working-class families to the outer districts of the east and north, rents rose in the new centre, and the division between the wealthy west and the working-class east sharpened, an effect Zola satirised in La Curee. The works were paid for by large loans and the city's debt grew, and critics such as Jules Ferry attacked the hidden borrowing in 1868. Others, like Baudelaire, mourned the loss of old Paris. Haussmann was dismissed in January 1870 when the liberal ministry of Emile Ollivier would not keep so unpopular a prefect, and the barricades of the Commune in 1871 showed that the boulevards had not ended working-class unrest.\n\nPlanning developed elsewhere in different ways. Vienna's Ringstrasse, decreed in 1857, replaced the old city walls with a grand boulevard lined with public buildings, with plots sold to private developers. In Barcelona Ildefons Cerda's plan of 1859 laid out the Eixample on a regular grid, and Berlin's Hobrecht Plan of 1862 organised the city's expansion. In Britain, which relied more on public health reform than on a central authority, Joseph Chamberlain cleared slums in Birmingham in the 1870s and built Corporation Street.\n\nIn conclusion, city planning moved in this period from piecemeal reaction to large-scale design, and Haussmann's Paris was its fullest expression. It improved water, sanitation, traffic, parks and public buildings and became a model for Europe, but it did so by serving the interests of the regime and the wealthy and by displacing the poor.",
