    "label": "2",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Both economic crises and wars badly damaged Tsarist Russia between 1871 and 1920. Economic weakness and hardship fed discontent, the war with Japan helped to cause the revolution of 1905, and the First World War destroyed the regime in 1917. The Bolsheviks then had to fight a civil war to hold power, so crises and wars together brought down the Romanovs.\n\nRussia in 1871 was poor and overwhelmingly rural, with about 80 per cent of its people peasants. Serfdom had ended in 1861, but redemption payments left peasants in debt, and the mir discouraged improvement. The harvest failure of 1891 caused famine across the Volga and the black earth provinces, followed by cholera in 1892. Relief was slow, and volunteers such as Tolstoy and Chekhov did much of the work, so the famine weakened trust in the regime and encouraged opposition groups such as the Union of Liberation and the Social Revolutionaries.\n\nThe drive to industrialise created its own strains. Witte, Finance Minister from 1892, put the rouble on the gold standard in 1897 and used foreign loans, tariffs and heavy taxation of the peasantry to build railways, including the Trans-Siberian, and heavy industry in the Donbas, Baku and St Petersburg. Growth was rapid, but it was paid for by the peasants, who were taxed while grain was exported, and it created a discontented working class in huge factories such as the Putilov works. The depression of 1900-03 brought unemployment and strikes, and peasant attacks on estates in 1902 showed the countryside was restless.\n\nWar then turned this discontent into revolution. Russia and Japan clashed over Manchuria and Korea, and Japan attacked Port Arthur in February 1904. Russia lost at Mukden and Port Arthur, and its Baltic Fleet was destroyed at Tsushima in May 1905. The defeat humiliated the autocracy and exposed its weak army and administration. On Bloody Sunday, 22 January 1905, troops fired on Gapon's peaceful march to the Winter Palace, and strikes, mutinies such as that on the Potemkin, and peasant risings followed. The October general strike forced Nicholas II to issue the October Manifesto, promising civil liberties and a Duma. The regime survived because the army stayed loyal, the opposition split and a French loan arrived in 1906, and Stolypin's reforms and repression then bought some stability.\n\nThe First World War was far more destructive. Russia mobilised over fifteen million men, but its army was short of rifles, shells and boots, and defeats at Tannenberg and in the great retreat of 1915 cost millions of casualties. The economy broke down: railways were overloaded, inflation rose as money was printed, farm output fell as men were taken from the land, and by the winter of 1916-17 food and fuel were short in the cities. Nicholas II took command at the front in 1915, leaving the government to Alexandra and Rasputin, whose murder in December 1916 showed the regime's isolation. On 8 March 1917 women textile workers in Petrograd demanded bread, the strike became general and the garrison joined the crowds. The tsar abdicated on 15 March.\n\nThe war also destroyed the Provisional Government, which continued it and delayed land reform. The failed July offensive increased desertion, and the Bolsheviks, promising peace, land and bread, took power on 6-7 November 1917. Lenin accepted the harsh Treaty of Brest-Litovsk in March 1918, losing Poland, the Baltic provinces, Ukraine and much of Russia's coal and industry. The civil war of 1918-20 between Reds and Whites, with limited foreign intervention, brought further ruin, and War Communism meant requisitioning grain and nationalising industry.\n\nIn conclusion, economic crises weakened the tsarist system and made it unpopular, but war was the decisive blow. Defeat by Japan nearly ended it in 1905, and the strain of the First World War brought it down in 1917, when an army worn down by defeat and cities short of bread would no longer defend it.",
