    "model": "European states managed their relations with the churches and with religious minorities such as the Jews in very different ways between 1871 and 1914. Germany and France tried to curb the power of the Catholic Church, Italy lived with an unresolved quarrel with the papacy, and Russia persecuted its Jews. Where states attacked religious groups, as Bismarck did, the result was usually to strengthen them.\n\nIn Germany, Bismarck launched the Kulturkampf against the Catholic Church. About a third of Germans were Catholic, and after the First Vatican Council declared papal infallibility in 1870 he feared that they owed their loyalty to Rome and saw the Catholic Centre Party as an enemy of the new Reich. He also feared Polish Catholics in the east, and liberals welcomed an attack on church influence. The pulpit law of 1871 punished clergy who criticised the state, the Jesuits were expelled in 1872, and schools were put under state inspection. The May Laws of 1873, piloted by Falk, required priests to be trained at state universities, and civil marriage became compulsory in 1875.\n\nThe Kulturkampf failed. Pius IX condemned the laws, bishops were imprisoned or exiled, and many parishes had no priest, while Catholics resisted and the Centre Party rose from 63 seats in 1871 to 91 in 1874, becoming the second-largest party. Bismarck needed its votes for tariffs and against socialism, so he negotiated with the new pope, Leo XIII, and most of the laws were repealed or relaxed in the 1880s, although the Jesuit ban lasted until 1917. His attempt to manage the church by force strengthened Catholic unity and showed the limits of his power.\n\nIn Italy the state could not come to terms with the papacy. Italian troops entered Rome on 20 September 1870, and the Law of Guarantees of 1871 gave the Pope the Vatican and Lateran palaces and an income. Pius IX rejected it and called himself a prisoner in the Vatican, and the non expedit forbade Catholics to vote in national elections. The state expanded civil marriage and secular education, while Catholics built their own organisations, such as Catholic Action, banks and newspapers. The Roman Question was only softened by the Gentiloni Pact of 1913, when Catholic votes helped Giolitti's liberals, and it was not settled until the Lateran Treaties of 1929.\n\nFrance had a bitter and more lasting conflict. Leo XIII urged French Catholics to accept the Republic in his ralliement policy of the early 1890s, but monarchist Catholics refused, and the Dreyfus Affair divided the country. Captain Alfred Dreyfus, a Jewish officer, was convicted of treason in 1894 on weak evidence, and Zola's 'J'accuse' of January 1898 accused the army of a cover-up. Anti-Semitism was strong among nationalists, such as Drumont, whose La France juive appeared in 1886, and the army, much of the Church and the nationalists opposed Dreyfus, while republicans and socialists such as Jaurès defended him. He was found guilty again at Rennes in 1899 but pardoned, and cleared in 1906. The Republic responded to the Church's role in the Affair with anticlerical laws and the separation of church and state in 1905.\n\nIn Russia the state itself persecuted the Jews. About five million lived in the Empire, mostly confined to the Pale of Settlement. After the assassination of Alexander II in 1881, pogroms swept the south, beginning at Elizavetgrad, and police and troops were slow to act. The May Laws of 1882 forbade Jews to settle in rural areas of the Pale, quotas limited their places in schools and universities from 1887, and Jews were expelled from Moscow in 1891-92. Pobedonostsev, adviser to the tsars, supported discrimination, and the Kishinev pogrom of 1903 killed 49 Jews and caused international outrage. About two million Jews emigrated between 1881 and 1914, mostly to the United States, and many joined the Bund or the Zionist movement.\n\nOverall, states that tried to control or exclude religious groups, whether Bismarck against Catholics or Russia against Jews, gained little and produced resistance, emigration or political organisation. Only compromise, as Bismarck and Leo XIII reached in the 1880s, brought a settlement.",
