    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Between 1871 and 1920 Europe saw remarkable developments in all three fields. Medicine was transformed by germ theory, antiseptics and vaccines, science and technology changed communication, power and transport, and the motor car passed from an experiment in Germany and France to a commercial product that the United States turned into a mass consumer good.\n\nThe most important medical development was the acceptance of germ theory. Louis Pasteur showed in the 1860s that microbes cause fermentation and decay, and argued that they cause disease. He developed vaccines using weakened microbes against chicken cholera, anthrax in 1881 and rabies, and in July 1885 he saved the life of a nine-year-old boy, Joseph Meister, who had been bitten by a rabid dog. In Germany Robert Koch developed methods of isolating bacteria in pure culture and identified the anthrax bacterium in 1876, the tuberculosis bacillus in 1882 and the cholera bacterium in 1883, winning the Nobel Prize for Medicine in 1905. Once the cause of a disease was known, prevention and cure became possible, so germ theory was the foundation of modern medicine.\n\nGerm theory was put to practical use in surgery and in the treatment of disease. Joseph Lister, a Scottish surgeon, used carbolic acid on wounds, instruments and in the operating theatre from 1865-67, which cut deaths from infection sharply. Anaesthetics such as ether and chloroform had already made longer operations possible, and asepsis, or sterile conditions, developed in Germany in the 1880s and 1890s, made surgery far safer. Emil von Behring and Kitasato developed antitoxins against diphtheria and tetanus in 1890, and Behring won the first Nobel Prize for Medicine in 1901. In 1909 Paul Ehrlich produced Salvarsan, the first effective drug against syphilis and the first modern chemotherapy.\n\nScience and technology brought changes just as far-reaching. Wilhelm Röntgen discovered X-rays in Würzburg in 1895, and doctors quickly used them to find broken bones and bullets. Marie Curie, born in Warsaw and working in Paris, coined the term radioactivity and, with Pierre Curie, announced polonium and radium in 1898. She shared the Nobel Prize for Physics in 1903 and won the Chemistry prize in 1911, the first person to win in two sciences. In the First World War she organised mobile X-ray units, the 'petites Curies', for field hospitals.\n\nElectricity and communications changed daily life. Alexander Graham Bell patented the telephone in 1876, and exchanges spread through cities in the 1880s and 1890s. Thomas Edison developed a long-lasting electric light bulb in 1879, and power stations followed in the 1880s, bringing electric lighting, trams and underground railways. Guglielmo Marconi, whose mother was Irish, developed wireless telegraphy from 1895 and in December 1901 claimed to receive a signal across the Atlantic from Cornwall. Flight advanced too: the Wright brothers made the first powered flight in 1903, Blériot crossed the Channel in 1909, and Alcock and Brown flew the Atlantic non-stop in 1919, landing in Co. Galway.\n\nThe motor car was the best-known new technology of the age. Nikolaus Otto built the first practical four-stroke engine in 1876, and Gottlieb Daimler and Wilhelm Maybach developed a small, light, high-speed petrol engine in the 1880s. Karl Benz, working separately in Mannheim, built a three-wheeled Motorwagen that ran in 1885 and was patented in 1886, regarded as the first practical car designed as a whole. His wife Bertha proved its usefulness in August 1888 by driving it about 106 km from Mannheim to Pforzheim. France then led production, with Panhard et Levassor and Peugeot using Daimler engines from 1890-91. John Boyd Dunlop of Belfast patented the pneumatic tyre in 1888, making travel smoother, and the Mercedes of 1901 set the pattern for the modern car.\n\nThe decisive step was taken in the United States, where Henry Ford wanted a cheap car for ordinary people. His Model T, launched in 1908, was simple and strong, and in 1913 the moving assembly line at Highland Park cut the time to build a chassis from over twelve hours to about ninety minutes. The price fell from about 850 dollars to under 300 as output rose, and the five-dollar day of 1914 kept workers and made them customers. The First World War then showed the importance of motor transport, as Paris taxis carried troops to the Marne in 1914 and lorries supplied Verdun along the Sacred Way in 1916. Even so, cars were still too costly for most Europeans in 1920.\n\nOverall, the period 1871-1920 was one of real progress. Medicine saved millions of lives through germ theory, antiseptics and vaccines, new science and technology transformed communications and transport, and the car moved from Benz and Daimler's workshops to Ford's assembly line. Yet the same advances served war, since aircraft, lorries, chemistry and wireless made the First World War deadlier, so the gains were great but not unmixed.",
