    "label": "1",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Between 1920 and 1939 Germany faced two great waves of hardship, the inflation and reparations crisis of the early 1920s and the mass unemployment of the Depression, along with deep social strains in between. The Weimar governments dealt with the first crisis successfully and the second disastrously, while the Nazis offered a different answer after 1933 that cut unemployment but at the cost of freedom and with the economy geared to war.\n\nThe first great problem was the burden of defeat. The Treaty of Versailles took territory, limited the army and in 1921 the London Schedule fixed reparations at 132 billion gold marks, a sum many Germans thought impossible. When Germany fell behind on deliveries of coal and timber, France and Belgium occupied the Ruhr in January 1923. Chancellor Cuno called for passive resistance and paid the striking workers by printing money. The result was hyperinflation. By November 1923 a US dollar was worth about 4.2 trillion marks, wages were paid daily and rushed to the shops, and barter replaced money in many places. Savings, pensions and war loans were wiped out, so the middle classes who had supported the old order grew bitterly resentful of the republic, while speculators and borrowers such as Hugo Stinnes profited.\n\nThis crisis was dealt with decisively by Gustav Stresemann, who became Chancellor in August 1923. He called off passive resistance in September, and on 15 November the Rentenmark, backed by a mortgage on land and industry, was introduced. Hjalmar Schacht kept its supply tightly limited, and it was replaced by the Reichsmark in 1924. The Dawes Plan of 1924 tied reparations to Germany's capacity to pay and brought an American loan of 800 million gold marks, and the French left the Ruhr by 1925. The Young Plan of 1929 reduced the total again and spread payments to 1988. These measures ended the inflation and gave Germany five years of recovery, but the middle classes who lost their savings were never fully compensated, and the recovery rested on American money that could be withdrawn.\n\nWeimar also tackled social problems directly. The Stinnes-Legien agreement of 1918 recognised trade unions and gave the eight-hour day. The Hauszinssteuer, a tax on house owners who had gained from inflation, paid for large municipal housing estates in Berlin and Frankfurt in the later 1920s. The Unemployment Insurance Act of 1927 created a national system of benefit. This was a real welfare state, but it was designed for about 800,000 claimants and was soon overwhelmed. The political violence of the early years, from the Kapp Putsch of 1920 to the murders of Erzberger and Rathenau, also showed a state that could not protect itself, and judges who punished right-wing killers far more lightly than left-wing ones.\n\nThe Wall Street Crash of October 1929 produced the worst problem of all. American loans were called in, exports collapsed and unemployment rose from about 1.9 million in 1929 to about six million by early 1932. The banking crisis of 1931, after the failure of the Danat Bank, deepened the slump. The coalition government broke down in March 1930 over unemployment insurance contributions, and Heinrich Brüning governed by Article 48 emergency decrees. His answer was deflation: he cut wages, pensions and benefits and raised taxes to balance the budget. This made unemployment worse and made the government look like the cause of the crisis. Families lived on reduced benefit or poor relief, and thousands of unemployed young men joined the SA, which gave them a uniform, meals and shelter. Voluntary Labour Service schemes in 1931, Papen's tax certificates for employers in 1932 and Schleicher's job-creation programme of December 1932 came too late to take effect before Hitler was appointed in January 1933.\n\nHitler claimed to offer the solution, and his government did bring unemployment down quickly. Public works, including the autobahns, the first section of which opened in 1935, and the Reich Labour Service, compulsory for young men from 1935, took men off the register. Conscription in 1935 and above all rearmament did more. Schacht financed it through Mefo bills, which hid the military spending, and the Four Year Plan of 1936 under Göring aimed at self-sufficiency and readiness for war. Unemployment fell from about six million to under one million by 1937, and labour shortages followed. The regime also managed the social problem through the German Labour Front, which replaced the free unions, and its Strength Through Joy programme offered cheap holidays and cruises.\n\nThese successes were limited and costly. The figures left out Jews and many women, and wages were held down. The Volkswagen savings scheme never gave workers a car, as the factory turned to military production. Workers lost their independent unions, women were pushed out of the professions in 1933, and Jews and others judged inferior were excluded from the national community by the Nuremberg Laws and by forced sterilisation. The recovery rested on rearmament that Germany could not sustain without expansion.\n\nOverall, Weimar solved the hyperinflation crisis impressively but failed against the Depression, because its deflationary policies and weak parliamentary base deepened the suffering that destroyed it. The Nazis cut unemployment faster than any Weimar government, but by suppressing the unions, restricting wages and committing the economy to war, not by solving Germany's social and economic problems on a lasting basis.",
