    "model": "Europe was profoundly affected by both developments. Between 1945 and 1957 the idea of unity moved from speeches to institutions, ending the cycle of Franco-German rivalry and creating the Common Market. The oil crisis of 1973 ended the long post-war boom and forced European states into recession, rationing and a rethink of their economic policies.\n\nThe drive for unity after 1945 came from three needs: to end war between France and Germany, to contain Soviet power, and to rebuild shattered economies, with American Marshall Aid encouraging cooperation. Churchill's Zurich speech of 1946 called for a kind of United States of Europe, and the Hague Congress of 1948 led to the Council of Europe, founded in 1949. Its effect was limited, however. It was a forum for debate in Strasbourg without power to make binding law, which disappointed federalists, though its European Convention on Human Rights of 1950 was a lasting achievement.\n\nThe real breakthrough was the Schuman Plan. On 9 May 1950 the French foreign minister Robert Schuman, with a draft by Jean Monnet, proposed pooling French and West German coal and steel under a joint authority. The Treaty of Paris of 1951 created the European Coal and Steel Community of France, West Germany, Italy and the Benelux states. Its High Authority was genuinely supranational, able to set prices and quotas and fine firms. Because coal and steel were the raw materials of war, pooling them made a new Franco-German war materially hard to imagine, and Adenauer welcomed it as treating West Germany as an equal. Europe was affected by a new method of integration, step by step.\n\nIntegration did not advance smoothly. The plan for a European Defence Community, with a supranational army, was killed when the French National Assembly rejected it in August 1954 out of fear of German rearmament. West Germany instead joined NATO in 1955. The failure showed that defence remained a national matter and pushed integration towards economic union, which was the area where it succeeded.\n\nThe Messina Conference of 1955 and the Spaak Report relaunched the project, and on 25 March 1957 the six signed the Treaties of Rome, which came into force in January 1958. The EEC aimed at a customs union with a common external tariff and the free movement of goods, services, capital and workers, while Euratom pooled civil nuclear energy. The customs union was completed in 1968, ahead of schedule, and trade between members grew rapidly. Europe was also divided, as Britain stayed outside and formed the looser EFTA in 1960.\n\nThe oil crisis of 1973 hit a Europe that had grown for thirty years on cheap imported oil. After the Yom Kippur War began in October 1973, the Arab oil producers cut output, embargoed the USA and the Netherlands as supporters of Israel, and the price of oil roughly quadrupled within months. The immediate effects were fuel rationing, car-free Sundays in West Germany and the Netherlands, and Britain's three-day working week early in 1974. The longer effects were stagflation, with stagnant output and rising prices together, rising oil import bills, and unemployment that climbed above 10 percent across the EEC by the mid-1980s. A second shock after the Iranian revolution of 1979 deepened the recession. Governments abandoned the post-war commitment to full employment and made defeating inflation their priority.\n\nThe crisis also changed the structure of European economies and energy policy. France launched the Messmer nuclear programme in 1974 and drew about 70 percent of its electricity from nuclear power by the late 1980s. North Sea oil made Britain self-sufficient by 1980, the International Energy Agency was set up in 1974 to coordinate importers, and heavy industries such as coal, steel and shipbuilding were cut back, so that regions in northern Britain suffered badly from deindustrialisation. Historians add that inflation and the end of Bretton Woods had already weakened the boom, so the shock hastened its end rather than causing it alone.\n\nOverall, moves towards unity transformed Europe for the better, giving it lasting peace between old enemies and a common market, while the oil crisis was a harsh setback that ended the boom. The two were linked, for the pressures of the 1970s strengthened the case for cooperation and led to the European Monetary System of 1979.",
