    "marks": 100,
    "model": "To a considerable extent, both Britain and France became multi-racial societies between 1945 and 1990, in the sense that large minority communities settled permanently and changed national life. Britain, however, went much further in recognising and accommodating that diversity, while France, committed to assimilation, accepted the presence of minorities but not their separate identities. In both countries acceptance was partial, and racism, restrictive laws and social disadvantage limited how far real equality was achieved.\n\nBritain's multi-racial character began with the British Nationality Act of 1948, which gave Commonwealth citizens the right to enter and settle as British subjects. The SS Empire Windrush docked at Tilbury on 22 June 1948 with about 1,000 passengers, mostly from Jamaica, and became the symbol of this migration. Labour shortages led the National Health Service, London Transport and the postal service to recruit Caribbean workers, and from the 1950s migrants from Punjab and Gujarat settled in Birmingham, Leicester, Bradford and London. By 1961-62, when people rushed to enter before restrictions took effect, there were substantial Caribbean and South Asian communities concentrated in London, the West Midlands and Yorkshire.\n\nThese communities met hostility. Newcomers faced a colour bar in lodgings and discrimination in jobs, and landlords such as Peter Rachman in Notting Hill exploited them with overcrowded lettings. In 1958 white youths attacked West Indian residents in Notting Hill and Nottingham in the worst racial violence in post-war Britain. Parliament then restricted entry through the Commonwealth Immigrants Acts of 1962 and 1968, the latter rushed through to limit Kenyan Asians with British passports, and the Immigration Act of 1971 introduced patriality. Enoch Powell's 'rivers of blood' speech of April 1968 and the National Front, founded in 1967, showed that a section of opinion rejected a multi-racial Britain. Even so, the Front never won a parliamentary seat, and about 27,000 Ugandan Asians expelled by Idi Amin in 1972 were resettled in Britain.\n\nBritain also built a legal framework for a multi-racial society. The Race Relations Acts of 1965, 1968 and 1976 outlawed discrimination in public places, then in housing and employment, and then indirect discrimination, creating the Race Relations Board and the Commission for Racial Equality. Roy Jenkins declared in 1966 that integration meant equal opportunity with cultural diversity rather than assimilation. The limits were clear, however. The laws depended on complaints and were hard to enforce, and the Brixton riots of April 1981, which followed Operation Swamp 81, and those at Toxteth and Moss Side showed how unemployment, disadvantage and policing of young black men through the 'sus' laws remained grievances. The Scarman Report of 1981 accepted these causes and led to the Police and Criminal Evidence Act of 1984. By the 1980s Notting Hill Carnival, mosques, gurdwaras and temples, reggae and Caribbean food were part of British life, and Diane Abbott, Paul Boateng, Bernie Grant and Keith Vaz entered Parliament in 1987.\n\nFrance became multi-racial through a different route. Algerians held French nationality until 1962, and post-war reconstruction drew workers from Portugal, Spain, Italy, Morocco, Tunisia and West Africa into factories, mines and construction. Many lived in bidonvilles such as Nanterre, and the harkis who came after 1962 faced hostility and poor housing. The Franco-Algerian accord of 1968 gave Algerians preferential rights, but France suspended labour immigration in July 1974 and allowed family reunification in 1976. By the 1982 census about 800,000 Algerians lived in France, and a second generation, the beurs, grew up in the banlieues. Violence marked this history too, notably the attack by Paris police on Algerian demonstrators on 17 October 1961.\n\nThe French republican model did not recognise ethnic communities. Based on universal citizenship and laïcité, it expected migrants to assimilate and did not collect statistics by origin, which made discrimination harder to measure. Racial tension surfaced in the Minguettes unrest of 1981 and the Marche des Beurs of 1983, which reached Paris with tens of thousands of supporters and was received by Mitterrand, who promised a ten-year residence card. SOS Racisme, founded in 1984, popularised the slogan 'Touche pas à mon pote'. Against this, Jean-Marie Le Pen's Front National won about 11 per cent in the 1984 European elections, 35 seats in 1986 and over 14 per cent in the 1988 presidential election, and the headscarf affair at Creil in 1989 exposed the tension between laïcité and Muslim identity.\n\nOverall, both countries became multi-racial in demographic fact, with settled and growing minorities and a second generation born there. Britain went further by recognising multiculturalism and legislating against discrimination, though riots and prejudice showed the limits, while France's assimilationist model made diversity less visible and its ethnic minorities remained concentrated in disadvantaged banlieues. Multi-racial societies existed in both, but genuinely equal and fully accepted ones did not by 1990.",
