    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "David Ben-Gurion, Gamal Abdul Nasser and Nadine Gordimer were each important in the story of the post-colonial world, though in different ways. Ben-Gurion helped found a new state, Nasser challenged the old colonial powers and became the voice of Arab nationalism, and Gordimer used literature to expose the injustice of apartheid.\n\nDavid Ben-Gurion was the leading figure in creating Israel. Born in Płońsk in 1886, he emigrated to Ottoman Palestine in 1906 and led the executive of the Jewish Agency from 1935. He accepted the United Nations partition plan of 1947 as a first step towards a Jewish state, and on 14 May 1948 he proclaimed the State of Israel in Tel Aviv and became its first prime minister. He oversaw the creation of the Israel Defence Forces and ordered the shelling of the Irgun ship Altalena in June 1948 to establish a single army under state control. Israel survived the invasion by Egypt, Jordan, Syria, Iraq and Lebanon, though about 6,000 Israelis died.\n\nBen-Gurion's importance was also controversial. About 700,000 Palestinian Arabs fled or were expelled in 1948, and historians such as Benny Morris argue that he backed the expulsions from Lydda and Ramle in July 1948. For most Jews he was the founder of their state, while Arabs saw him as the architect of a state that displaced Palestinians. As prime minister in 1948-54 and 1955-63 he also took part in the secret talks at Sèvres with Britain and France in October 1956. His significance lies in building the institutions of a state and in shaping the Arab-Israeli conflict that followed.\n\nGamal Abdul Nasser was important as the leader of Egypt from 1954 to 1970. He emerged from the Free Officers movement that overthrew King Farouk on 23 July 1952, and he pursued land reform, industrialisation and the Aswan High Dam. His Arab nationalism opposed Western alliances such as the Baghdad Pact of 1955, and he bought arms from the Soviet bloc in 1955. When the United States and Britain withdrew funding for the dam in July 1956, he nationalised the Suez Canal Company on 26 July.\n\nThe nationalisation provoked the Protocol of Sèvres and an Israeli attack on Sinai on 29 October 1956, followed by Anglo-French landings at Port Said. Nasser's army was defeated in Sinai, yet he won a political victory. American and Soviet pressure halted the invasion, Anthony Eden resigned in January 1957, and Egypt kept control of the canal. Nasser's prestige in the Arab world rose sharply, and the crisis showed that Britain and France could no longer act as great powers without American consent. He formed the United Arab Republic with Syria in 1958 and backed anti-colonial movements such as Algeria's. His reputation suffered after the Six-Day War of 1967, when Egypt lost Sinai, and critics point to his one-party rule.\n\nNadine Gordimer's importance was cultural and moral. A white South African born at Springs in 1923, she wrote in English about the lives of black and white South Africans under apartheid. Her novels, including A World of Strangers (1958), which was banned in South Africa for twelve years, and Burger's Daughter (1979), which was also banned for a time, examined the divisions of her society and the limits of liberal opposition. The Conservationist shared the Booker Prize in 1974, and July's People (1981), imagining a revolution in which a white family takes refuge with its black servant, was widely read abroad.\n\nGordimer also acted on her convictions. She supported the African National Congress and helped Nelson Mandela with his speech at the Rivonia trial in 1964. She was awarded the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1991 and remained an opponent of censorship after apartheid ended. Some black writers argued that white liberal writers had a limited perspective, but her work made the realities of apartheid known to an international audience.\n\nOverall, Ben-Gurion and Nasser had the greater political impact, one by founding Israel and the other by defying Britain and France at Suez and reshaping Arab politics. Gordimer's importance was of a different kind, as a witness against apartheid whose writing helped the world understand the system.",
