    "label": "2",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The Montgomery bus boycott took place because black people in the city suffered daily humiliation on segregated buses and had a ready-made network to resist. It was carried out through an almost complete refusal to ride, organised by the Montgomery Improvement Association with a car pool and non-violent leadership. It was largely successful in ending bus segregation after 381 days, though its direct gains were limited to buses.\n\nThe causes lay in the system of segregation on Montgomery's buses. Front seats were kept for white passengers, and the driver could move the dividing line back to make black riders stand or give up their seats. Black passengers had to pay at the front and then re-enter by the rear door, and drivers, who carried guns, often insulted them or drove off. Black people made up the large majority of the riders, many of them maids and labourers, so the rules affected most of the black working population. Expectations had been raised by the Supreme Court's Brown v Board of Education ruling in May 1954, and the Women's Political Council under Jo Ann Robinson wrote to the mayor that month warning of a boycott. Earlier arrests, such as Claudette Colvin in March 1955 and Mary Louise Smith in October, had not been used as test cases.\n\nThe immediate trigger was the arrest of Rosa Parks on 1 December 1955 for refusing to give up her seat. Parks was a seamstress and secretary of the Montgomery NAACP, and she had attended a workshop at the Highlander Folk School, so she was no accidental protester. E. D. Nixon bailed her out and recognised that a respectable adult would command wide support. That night the WPC and students at Alabama State College ran off tens of thousands of leaflets calling for a one-day boycott on Monday 5 December, the day she was tried and fined, and the black ministers announced it from their pulpits.\n\nThe boycott was carried out with remarkable discipline. It was almost totally observed on 5 December, and that evening about 5,000 people at the Holt Street Baptist Church voted to continue. The Montgomery Improvement Association was formed that day with the 26-year-old Martin Luther King Jr. as president. The demands were at first modest: courteous treatment, first-come seating and black drivers on mainly black routes. A car pool of several hundred cars, helped by black taxi drivers charging the ten cent fare and by churches that bought station wagons, carried people to work, while many others walked long distances. Northern unions and churches sent money. King insisted on non-violence, and advisers Bayard Rustin and Glenn Smiley helped him apply Gandhian methods. About 40,000 bus riders stayed off the buses, which hit the bus company's income and city centre shops.\n\nWhite Montgomery fought back. The police arrested car pool drivers on minor charges, King was arrested on 26 January 1956 for speeding, and his house was bombed on 30 January, when he urged the angry crowd not to retaliate. In February a grand jury indicted 89 leaders under an Alabama law against boycotts, and King was convicted in March and fined. The white Citizens' Councils backed resistance. These attacks, however, only produced national press coverage and more sympathy for the protesters.\n\nThe decisive blow was legal. On 1 February 1956 the lawyer Fred Gray filed Browder v Gayle for four black women, including Colvin and Smith. A federal court ruled on 5 June 1956 by two votes to one that bus segregation broke the Fourteenth Amendment, and on 13 November the Supreme Court affirmed the decision, relying on Brown. The order was served on 20 December, the boycott ended, and the buses were desegregated on 21 December 1956, when King and Ralph Abernathy rode with white passengers.\n\nThe boycott was therefore a success in its immediate aim, and the Montgomery victory defeated determined resistance after 381 days. It produced a national leader in King, a lasting method of non-violent mass action and, in 1957, the Southern Christian Leadership Conference. It also won publicity that mattered in the Cold War. But the limits were real: the gain applied only to buses, schools, housing and voting stayed segregated, violence followed the victory when bombs damaged four black churches in January 1957, and some historians argue the courts rather than the boycott ended bus segregation. Still, the boycott kept the case alive and gave the movement a model for the Birmingham and Selma campaigns, so it was a major, though partial, success.",
