    "label": "C4",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Daniel Mannix, born in Charleville, Co. Cork, in 1864, was president of Maynooth College from 1903 to 1912 before becoming archbishop of Melbourne in 1917. He opposed conscription in Australia during the First World War and took a leading part in the campaign against the 1916 and 1917 referendums, both of which were defeated. His stand brought conflict with Prime Minister Billy Hughes and with imperial loyalists, who called him disloyal. He supported Irish independence and criticised British policy in Ireland after 1916, which made him a target for loyalists in Australia. In August 1920 a British naval vessel took him off his ship at sea and landed him in England, and he was banned from entering Ireland. Irish Australians and many Catholics admired him, so he represented the Church as a defender of its people against imperial and Protestant hostility.",
