    "label": "B2",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "On 13 August 1969 Taoiseach Jack Lynch broadcast that the Irish government 'can no longer stand by' while Catholics were attacked, and he called for a United Nations peacekeeping force. The Arms Crisis of May 1970 followed allegations that ministers had planned to import arms for Northern nationalists, and Lynch dismissed Charles Haughey and Neil Blaney from the government on 6 May 1970. After Bloody Sunday the Irish government declared a day of mourning and recalled its ambassador from London. In 1972 the Republic passed the Offences against the State (Amendment) Act and reactivated the Special Criminal Court to try paramilitary offences without a jury.",
