    "label": "B3",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "Seamus Heaney was born in 1939 at Mossbawn, near Castledawson in County Derry, the eldest of nine children of a Catholic farming family. His collection North, published in 1975, used Iron Age bog bodies to reflect on the violence in Northern Ireland. He moved to County Wicklow in the Republic in 1972 and was awarded the Nobel Prize for Literature in 1995. In his 1983 poem 'An Open Letter' he objected to being described as British, writing that his passport was green.",
