    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Television reached most households in the 1960s and early 1970s, and it reduced the isolation of rural communities by bringing national news, sport and entertainment into every home. The Late Late Show, presented by Gay Byrne from 1962, discussed sex, contraception, unmarried mothers and the church, subjects rarely raised in public before. The 1966 Bishop and the Nightie controversy showed that television could start a public argument about morality and the limits of church authority. Seven Days, launched in 1966, investigated housing, poverty and emigration and challenged government policy. Programmes such as The Riordans, begun in 1965, showed the realities of farming life and proved hugely popular with rural viewers. The government used Section 31 from October 1971 to restrict coverage of the Troubles, and the RTÉ Authority was dismissed in November 1972 after a broadcast interview with a Provisional IRA leader, which showed that the state tried to control what the public could see.",
