    "label": "C4",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Haussmann cut wide straight boulevards through the old city, such as the Boulevard de Sébastopol, which linked the Seine with the northern railway stations. Engineer Eugène Belgrand built new aqueducts that brought clean spring water into the city from the Dhuis and Vanne valleys. Belgrand also built an underground sewer network that carried waste away from the centre and reduced disease. Parks were laid out, including the Bois de Boulogne, the Bois de Vincennes and the Buttes-Chaumont. Les Halles were rebuilt as iron-and-glass pavilions by Victor Baltard, and the Gare du Nord was rebuilt in the 1860s. Building regulations fixed the height of facades along the new streets, giving Paris a uniform look. The annexation of the suburbs in 1860 raised the number of arrondissements from twelve to twenty.",
