    "label": "B1",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "About five million Jews lived in the Russian Empire in the late 1800s, most of them confined to the Pale of Settlement set up in 1791. After Alexander II was assassinated in 1881, pogroms swept southern Russia, and the May Laws of 1882 banned Jews from settling in rural areas and from owning land. The Kishinev pogrom of April 1903 killed 49 Jews and caused international outrage. The Protocols of the Elders of Zion was first published in Russia in 1903, and between 1881 and 1914 about two million Jews emigrated from the empire.",
