    "label": "C2",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Church-state relations in Italy were strained after 1870, when the popes regarded themselves as prisoners in the Vatican, until the Lateran Treaties of 11 February 1929 created Vatican City and gave the Church 1.75 billion lire in compensation. Catholicism was made the sole religion of the state, and religious marriage was given civil effect, so the regime gained Catholic support. In 1931 the regime clashed with Catholic Action over its youth groups, and Pope Pius XI protested in the encyclical Non abbiamo bisogno. The 1931 agreement limited Catholic Action to religious, cultural and charitable activities, and the Church otherwise made its objections in private. The racial laws of 1938 caused tension, since they challenged Catholic belief in a common humanity, but the Church did not break with the regime.",
