    "label": "C2",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "The Oil Crisis of 1973 ended the steady growth of Western Europe after the war. OPEC's Gulf members raised the posted oil price by about 70 per cent in October 1973, and the Arab producers' embargo followed, so prices roughly quadrupled. Governments introduced fuel rationing, car-free Sundays and, in Britain, a three-day working week from January to March 1974. Unemployment rose above 10 per cent across the EEC by the mid-1980s, and heavy industries such as shipbuilding, steel and coal declined sharply. Inflation rose with stagflation, so governments gave up full employment as their main aim. The second oil shock of 1979, after the Iranian revolution, caused a further recession, and Europe responded with the Messmer Plan for nuclear power, North Sea oil and the International Energy Agency set up in 1974.",
