    "label": "C3",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "The Second Vatican Council changed Catholic worship when the Mass was said in local languages from 1964, and the new Roman Missal of 1969 to 1970 completed the reform so that the priest faced the congregation. Lay Catholics were encouraged to take a more active part, reading the Scriptures and serving as ministers, and the Council gave a larger role to bishops collectively. Priests and religious orders were asked to return to their founders' ideals, and many nuns changed their habits. The Church began formal dialogue with other Christian churches, and in 1965 the mutual excommunications of 1054 between Rome and the Orthodox churches were lifted. Nostra Aetate changed relations with Judaism, and Dignitatis Humanae accepted religious liberty. These changes were the most visible results of the Council.",
