    "label": "C4",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Solzhenitsyn was arrested in 1945 for criticising Stalin in private letters, and he spent eight years in labour camps before internal exile. His One Day in the Life of Ivan Denisovich was approved for publication in 1962, but after Khrushchev fell the authorities tightened censorship. The Gulag Archipelago was published in Paris in 1973 without Soviet permission, after the KGB had seized a copy of the manuscript. His novels The First Circle and Cancer Ward could appear only abroad from 1968, and he was expelled from the Writers' Union in 1969. His 1978 Harvard address criticised Western materialism as well as communism, and he was expelled from the Soviet Union in February 1974. The Soviet leadership treated him as a traitor because he published abroad and challenged its system.",
