    "label": "C4",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Nadine Gordimer was born on 20 November 1923 at Springs near Johannesburg, and she was a white South African who wrote in English. Her early novels, The Lying Days (1953) and A World of Strangers (1958), explored the social divisions between white and black South Africans. The Conservationist shared the Booker Prize in 1974. Several of her books were banned under apartheid, including A World of Strangers, which was banned for twelve years, and she remained an opponent of censorship. July's People (1981) imagined a South African revolution and was widely read internationally. She supported the African National Congress and was awarded the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1991.",
