    "label": "C1",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Joseph McCarthy, a Republican senator from Wisconsin, claimed in a speech at Wheeling, West Virginia, on 9 February 1950 that he held a list of communists working in the State Department. His methods of guilt by association, unnamed lists and shifting numbers fuelled the Republican charge that Truman's State Department had lost China, and in 1950 he accused the China expert Owen Lattimore of being a leading Soviet agent. His attacks helped to defeat Senator Millard Tydings in the November 1950 election. The climate he created made it risky for Democrats to negotiate with communist states, and State Department China specialists were moved from their posts. In 1954 he turned his inquiries on the army, and the televised Army-McCarthy hearings, in which the lawyer Joseph Welch asked him whether he had no sense of decency, turned public opinion against him. The Senate voted 67 to 22 on 2 December 1954 to censure him, and his influence collapsed after that vote.",
