    "label": "C2",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "On 5 December 1955 King, a 26-year-old pastor of Dexter Avenue Baptist Church, was elected president of the Montgomery Improvement Association, which coordinated the bus boycott. At the Holt Street meeting he combined a demand for justice with a commitment to non-violence and Christian love, which launched him as the movement's voice. He insisted on non-violence based on Christian teaching and Gandhi's methods, and after a bomb was thrown on his porch in January 1956 he urged his supporters not to retaliate. In 1957 he became the first president of the Southern Christian Leadership Conference, which coordinated church-based protest across the South. He led the Birmingham campaign of 1963 and the Selma campaign of 1965, and his speech at the March on Washington in 1963 became the defining statement of the movement. He won the Nobel Peace Prize in 1964 at the age of 35, and in his last years he linked civil rights to poverty and the Vietnam War.",
