    "model": "Between 1815 and 1870 the Belfast region developed in a way that set it apart from almost all the rest of Ireland. While the south and west remained overwhelmingly agricultural, and lost population and industry, Belfast and the Lagan valley industrialised around linen, engineering and shipbuilding and grew into the only truly industrial city on the island.\n\nThe first great difference was the mechanisation of linen. Outside north-east Ulster, the hand spinning and weaving of linen collapsed against factory competition after 1815, and the Irish cotton industry declined once protective duties ended in 1824. Around Belfast the opposite happened. Mechanised flax spinning arrived in the 1820s and 1830s and linen became the dominant industry of the city and the Lagan valley. The region therefore turned the very change that destroyed rural industry elsewhere into the basis of its prosperity, and the American Civil War of 1861 to 1865 added to this, because the cotton famine cut off supplies of cotton and raised the demand and prices for linen.\n\nSecondly, Belfast developed heavy industry that the rest of Ireland never acquired. Harbour works, notably William Dargan's Victoria Channel, allowed larger ships to reach the port, and the dredged material created Queen's Island. Edward Harland took over Robert Hickson's shipyard there in 1858, and in 1861 he joined with Gustav Wolff to form Harland and Wolff. Engineering firms grew up to serve the mills and the yards. This is the more striking because Belfast had no local coal or iron, but cheap imports by sea and rail made industry viable. Dublin and Cork, by contrast, kept little more than brewing, distilling and food processing, while the rest of the south declined under competition from British factory goods once tariffs had gone.\n\nThirdly, the population and the character of the town changed rapidly. Belfast grew from about 100,000 in 1851 to over 170,000 by 1871, drawing in migrants from the Ulster countryside and from Famine-hit areas, and it grew faster than any other Irish town. This was the opposite of the national pattern, where the population fell from about 8.2 million in 1841 to about 5.4 million in 1871 through death and emigration. Belfast was also less affected by the Famine itself, because the east and north-east suffered least, and it offered work to people who elsewhere would have emigrated.\n\nFourthly, the region had advantages that explain why it developed differently. Ulster had skilled Protestant labour, capital built up in the linen trade, local business networks and strong trade links with Britain. Railways linked Belfast to Dublin in the 1850s, and Dargan built the Ulster Railway and the Ulster Canal. The Queen's College opened at Belfast in 1849 and, as the most successful of the three Queen's Colleges, served the Presbyterian middle class of Ulster. The city's economy and its leading class were thus oriented to commerce and industry and to Britain, whereas the south's was oriented to land.\n\nFinally, Belfast developed a distinctive and divided society. Catholics, mostly migrants from the countryside, made up about a third of the population, concentrated in unskilled jobs and in districts such as the Pound, while skilled work lay mainly with Protestants. Rivalry for housing and work, together with Orange marches, produced sectarian riots in 1857 and 1864. In the rest of Ireland the stronger farmers were the gainers of the post-Famine years, with fewer, larger farms and a shift from tillage to pasture, and the small farmers of the west stayed close to subsistence.\n\nIn conclusion, the Belfast region differed from the rest of Ireland in its industry, its growth, its trade and its social structure. It was an industrial city of linen, shipbuilding and engineering with a rising population and a Protestant business class, while the remainder of the island depended on agriculture and on exporting its people. Industrialisation was confined to the north-east, and this contrast was one of the deepest divisions within Ireland by 1870.",
