    "label": "2",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Daniel O'Connell had two great aims in his career: to win Catholic Emancipation, and then to win Repeal of the Act of Union, together with a series of practical reforms along the way. He fully achieved the first, partly achieved some of the reforms and failed to achieve Repeal, so his overall success was substantial but limited.\n\nHis first aim was to remove the disabilities that kept Catholics out of Parliament and high office. Catholics had the vote after 1793 but could not sit as MPs, because they could not take the oath of supremacy, and they were barred from the senior judiciary and high office. After the veto controversy had stalled the cause, O'Connell founded the Catholic Association with Richard Lalor Sheil in May 1823. In 1824 he introduced the Catholic Rent of a penny a month, collected through the parishes under the priests, which turned a gentry petitioning body into the first mass political movement in Irish or British history. He insisted on legal, non-violent methods, although the implied threat of a mass movement getting out of control was part of his pressure.\n\nThis strategy succeeded. In 1826 the forty-shilling freeholders, led by their priests, elected a pro-Emancipation candidate in Waterford and unseated a Beresford, and in 1828 O'Connell himself stood in Clare against Vesey-FitzGerald, a government minister, and won by 2,057 votes to 982. Wellington and Peel feared civil strife in Ireland, where many soldiers were Catholics, and conceded the Roman Catholic Relief Act in April 1829. The achievement was real, but it had limits, because the companion act raised the county franchise from forty shillings to £10 and cut the electorate from about 216,000 to about 37,000, removing many of the voters who had won the victory, and Protestants still dominated the magistracy and Dublin Castle. Even so, Emancipation was O'Connell's greatest success, because its aim was limited enough to be granted within the Union.\n\nO'Connell's next aims were reform of Irish grievances, notably the Church of Ireland tithe, local government and the administration of justice. At Westminster after 1830 he worked with the Whigs, and the Lichfield House Compact of 1835 kept Melbourne in office in return for reforms. Under Drummond and Mulgrave, Catholics were appointed to office, stipendiary magistrates were used and the police were centralised. The Municipal Corporations Act of 1840 abolished most of the closed Protestant corporations, and O'Connell became Lord Mayor of Dublin in 1841, the first Catholic in the office since the time of James II. The Tithe War reduced the burden: the Tithe Rentcharge Act of 1838 cut the charge by about a quarter, but tithe survived until disestablishment in 1869, and the Poor Law of 1838 and its workhouses were far from what he wanted. These gains were real but partial.\n\nHis final and greatest ambition was Repeal of the Union. He founded the Repeal Association in 1840 with a Repeal Rent and declared 1843 Repeal Year. About forty monster meetings were held, the largest at Tara on 15 August 1843, where supporters claimed up to a million attended. But Peel declared he would accept even war rather than the dismemberment of the empire, and banned the meeting at Clontarf in October 1843. O'Connell cancelled it, fearing a massacre, which saved his principle of moral force but showed that he would not go beyond the law. He was convicted of conspiracy in 1844, and although the House of Lords quashed the verdict he was old and his health declined.\n\nRepeal failed because both British parties and Protestant Ulster opposed it, the movement was overwhelmingly Catholic, the government combined coercion with concessions such as the Devon Commission and the Queen's Colleges, and the Young Ireland group split from him. Then the Famine destroyed the Repeal Rent and the mass following, and he died in 1847 with Repeal further away than ever.\n\nIn conclusion, O'Connell fully achieved his Emancipation aim and made mass democratic politics possible, achieved part of his reform agenda through the Whig alliance, and failed completely over Repeal. His methods, rather than his results after 1829, are his enduring legacy.",
