    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The Poor Law, the government responses to the Famine and Asenath Nicholson all contributed to the relief of distress in Ireland, but in very different degrees. The Poor Law gave a permanent but harsh framework, the government eventually reached millions with direct feeding but too late and with too many conditions, and Asenath Nicholson gave small-scale but vivid personal relief and bore witness to the suffering.\n\nThe Irish Poor Law Act of July 1838 was the first statutory system of relief in Ireland. Following the Nicholls report, which rejected the more costly proposals of the Whately commission, the country was divided into 130 unions, each with a workhouse run by a board of guardians elected by ratepayers and financed by a poor rate. Relief was given only inside the workhouse, conditions were made deliberately harsher than the life of the poorest labourer, and families were separated on entry. The workhouses had room for about 100,000 people and were designed for ordinary destitution, not mass famine. They gave real shelter to many, but the stigma was deep and they became centres of fever once overcrowded.\n\nGovernment responses in the Famine itself passed through several stages. Peel's government secretly bought about £100,000 of Indian corn in 1845 and sold it at cost through depots, while a Relief Commission under Sir Randolph Routh coordinated local committees and the Board of Works provided wages on public works. Mortality in the first season was low, so these measures were judged the most effective of the Famine. Russell's Whig government, committed to laissez-faire, refused to control food prices, and the Labouchere Act of August 1846 put the cost of public works on local taxes. About 700,000 people were on the works by March 1847, but wages could not keep up with food prices, which more than doubled, and weakened people worked in a hard winter.\n\nThe most effective relief came with the Temporary Relief Act of February 1847, the Soup Kitchen Act, under which free rations were given by local committees. By July 1847 about three million people a day were fed, the cheapest and most effective relief of the Famine. But the kitchens were closed by September, and the Poor Law Extension Act of June 1847 made the Poor Law responsible for all further relief, allowing outdoor relief for the able-bodied. Its Gregory clause refused relief to anyone holding more than a quarter acre of land, which forced smallholders to surrender their holdings, and landlords used it and the rising rates to clear estates. Many western unions went bankrupt, and the Rate-in-Aid Act of 1849 imposed a national rate of 6d in the pound to support them. Government relief therefore saved many lives, but was too late, too dependent on local funds and on the Treasury view of Charles Trevelyan, and by 1850 the Famine was declared over while distress continued in the west.\n\nAsenath Nicholson, an American teacher born in Vermont in 1792, made a different contribution. She came to Ireland in 1844 and walked through almost every county, then returned in 1846 to give relief. She set up a soup kitchen in the Liberties in Dublin and travelled to Ulster, Mayo, Erris and Arranmore, distributing food and clothing paid for by appeals in New York newspapers. She worked independently, not through a committee, which let her reach remote places and the very poor whom officials missed. Her relief was small beside the millions fed by the government, but its value lay in the fact that she gave it directly and without religious conditions, and she is remembered more for her books, Lights and Shades of Ireland (1850) and Annals of the Famine in Ireland (1851), which condemned landlords, officials and proselytisers alike and gave a vivid eyewitness account.\n\nIn conclusion, the greatest relief came from the state, notably the soup kitchens of 1847 and Peel's early measures, while the Poor Law gave an inadequate base which collapsed under the strain. Nicholson's relief was small in scale but compassionate and important as testimony, and none of these was enough to prevent about one million deaths.",
