    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Thomas Davis, James Stephens and Charles Kickham each shaped Irish nationalism, but in different ways. Davis gave it a cultural, non-sectarian definition of the nation in the 1840s. Stephens gave it an oath-bound revolutionary organisation in the 1850s and 1860s, and Kickham gave that physical force republicanism a voice in literature and in the countryside. Together they carried nationalism from O'Connell's constitutional tradition towards the cultural and republican traditions that lasted to 1916.\n\nDavis was a Protestant barrister from Mallow, educated at Trinity and called to the bar in 1838. He joined the Repeal Association in 1841 and in October 1842 founded The Nation with Charles Gavan Duffy and John Blake Dillon. It quickly became the most widely read paper in Ireland, passed from hand to hand and read aloud in reading rooms. Its great contribution was to define the nation by shared history, culture and, ideally, the Irish language rather than by religion. Davis argued that a people without a language of its own is only half a nation, and he held that Protestants and Catholics belonged equally to the Irish nation. This was a major shift from O'Connell's mass movement, which was overwhelmingly Catholic, and it created the idea of cultural nationalism.\n\nDavis also popularised nationalism through ballads and history. His songs, including A Nation Once Again and The West's Asleep, made romantic nationalism attractive to young people, and the Library of Ireland, a series of cheap books on Irish history and literature launched by Duffy, extended the paper's educational work. He promoted self-reliance and backed mixed, non-denominational education, welcoming the Queen's Colleges in 1845 and clashing publicly with O'Connell, who condemned them as godless. Davis died of scarlet fever in September 1845, aged only thirty, but his writing outlived him. It inspired the Gaelic revival and later figures such as Pearse, although his hope of uniting Protestant and Catholic under one national banner was never realised.\n\nJames Stephens contributed a different strand, physical force republicanism. Born in Kilkenny in 1825, he fought in the Young Ireland rising at Ballingarry in 1848 under William Smith O'Brien and escaped to Paris. On 17 March 1858 he founded the Irish Republican Brotherhood in Dublin as an oath-bound secret society aiming at an Irish republic, taking the title Chief Organiser. Working with John O'Mahony's Fenian Brotherhood in New York, founded the same year, he built a transatlantic movement that raised money and men from Irish exiles, including veterans of the American Civil War. The IRB was organised in circles, drew its members from artisans, clerks, shopkeepers and farmers' sons, and tried to recruit soldiers in the British army. The Famine bitterness, disillusion after the collapse of the Independent Irish Party and the experience of emigration gave it fertile ground, and it kept alive the idea that only force could win independence.\n\nStephens also showed the weaknesses of the tradition. The Irish People, the movement's newspaper, began in 1863, but in September 1865 the police raided its offices and arrested Luby, O'Leary, Rossa and Kickham. Stephens was arrested in November and escaped from Richmond Prison, but in 1866 he postponed a promised rising and American leaders removed him in December. The rising of March 1867 was badly compromised by informers and the lack of arms and leaders, and was crushed. Yet Fenianism still mattered. The Manchester executions of November 1867 and the Clerkenwell explosion that December convinced Gladstone that Irish discontent had to be addressed, and the Irish Church Act of 1869 and the Land Act of 1870 followed. The IRB survived to organise the Easter Rising of 1916.\n\nCharles Kickham, born in Mullinahone, Co. Tipperary, in 1828, linked nationalism to the countryside and to literature. Almost deaf and with poor sight after a gunpowder accident in boyhood, he wrote for The Nation, then became an editor of the Irish People and a Fenian leader. His novels Sally Cavanagh (1869), about the eviction of a tenant family, and Knocknagow, or The Homes of Tipperary, were hugely popular, and they gave rural Ireland a sympathetic picture of itself and connected national feeling to land grievances. He was sentenced in 1866 to fourteen years' penal servitude and was released in 1869 because of ill health after a campaign for Fenian prisoners, which helped make the cause popular. He became a leading member of the IRB's Supreme Council in the 1870s. As a practising Catholic opposed by the bishops, he also showed how hard it was to reconcile Fenianism with the Church.\n\nOverall, the three men contributed in complementary ways. Davis supplied the ideal of a cultural, inclusive nation, Stephens the organisation and the republican goal, and Kickham the literary and rural voice that made Fenianism a popular tradition. Davis's influence was the most lasting in cultural terms, since it underlay the Gaelic revival, but Stephens had the greater political effect, because the IRB forced British reform and carried the physical force tradition to 1916. Their weakness was that none of them won the Protestant community or the Catholic Church to the cause.",
