    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Between 1870 and 1914 Unionism was very successful in resisting Home Rule, since it helped to defeat two Home Rule Bills and ensured that the third never took effect before the First World War. But its success was partial, because it could not prevent the principle of Home Rule from being accepted by the Liberals and eventually passed into law, and it won only the survival of the Union in the north-east rather than in the whole of Ireland.\n\nUnionism began as a defensive reaction in 1886. Gladstone's conversion produced the First Home Rule Bill, and about 93 Liberals under Hartington and Chamberlain voted against it, so it was defeated on 8 June 1886 by 341 votes to 311. The Liberal Unionists then allied with the Conservatives and helped them to dominate government for most of the next twenty years. Lord Randolph Churchill played the Orange card in the Ulster Hall in February 1886, and the Orange Order revived. Unionists also built organisations to match the National League, among them the Irish Loyal and Patriotic Union in 1885 and the Ulster Loyalist Anti-Repeal Union in 1886, and Colonel Saunderson led the Irish unionist MPs at Westminster.\n\nThe Second Home Rule Bill of 1893 shows how unionism used the House of Lords. It passed the Commons, but the Lords rejected it by 419 votes to 41. Ulster unionists held a great convention in the Botanic Gardens, Belfast, in June 1892, with about 12,000 delegates, and the Irish Unionist Alliance had been formed in 1891 to unite the movement. In 1905 the Ulster Unionist Council gave Ulster a permanent organisation, drawing together the Orange lodges, constituency associations and MPs. For a generation the Lords' veto and Conservative strength meant that Home Rule could not pass.\n\nThe situation changed with the Parliament Act of 1911, which removed the veto and meant that the Third Home Rule Bill, introduced in April 1912, would become law within about two years. Unionists then moved to direct resistance. Under Carson and James Craig, who organised the campaign, they staged mass demonstrations, and Bonar Law pledged Conservative support at Blenheim in July 1912. On Ulster Day, 28 September 1912, about 237,000 men signed the Solemn League and Covenant, with a separate women's declaration. The Ulster Volunteer Force was formed in January 1913, reaching perhaps 90,000 men, and in September the Ulster Unionist Council planned a Provisional Government.\n\nIn 1914 unionist pressure had a decisive effect on British policy. Asquith proposed that Ulster counties could vote themselves out for six years, but Carson called this a stay of execution. In the Curragh incident in March Brigadier-General Gough and many officers offered to resign rather than act against Ulster, which showed that the army could not be relied on to coerce unionists. In April the UVF landed about 25,000 rifles at Larne, Donaghadee and Bangor without being stopped. The Buckingham Palace Conference in July failed over Tyrone and Fermanagh. The Home Rule Act received the royal assent in September 1914 but was suspended for the duration of the war.\n\nThe limits of unionist success are clear. The Third Home Rule Act was on the statute book, and the Liberal and Irish Party alliance had delivered the principle of self-government. Southern unionists, a small minority, had less influence than their Ulster allies, and Carson himself later felt that southern unionism had been sacrificed. Unionism in the long run could only secure exclusion for part of Ulster, which prepared the way for partition.\n\nOn balance, unionism was highly successful in the short term, since it frustrated Home Rule in 1886, 1893 and 1914, and by 1914 had made armed resistance credible enough to cause the government to hesitate. But it succeeded by delay and by the threat of force rather than by winning the argument, and what it preserved was the position of the north-east, not the Union as a whole.",
