    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The Gaelic League and the Anglo-Irish Literary Revival made a lasting contribution to Irish life. Between them they gave Ireland a language movement, a national literature and theatre, and a stronger sense of cultural identity, although they also exposed deep disagreements about what that identity should be.\n\nThe Gaelic League gave the revival its programme and its inspiration. In November 1892 Douglas Hyde delivered his lecture The Necessity for De-Anglicising Ireland to the National Literary Society, arguing that Ireland had adopted English language, dress, games and ideas and was failing to be either English or Irish. Within a year, on 31 July 1893, Hyde, Eoin MacNeill and others founded the Gaelic League in Dublin. Its contribution was to turn a scholarly argument into a mass movement, and because it was officially non-political and non-sectarian it welcomed Catholics, Protestants, nationalists and unionists alike. This gave Irish people a cultural cause that cut across traditional divisions, at least until the League became more politicised after 1910.\n\nThe League's practical work was impressive. Branches ran Irish classes, and travelling teachers, the timirí, carried the language to rural areas, so that there were several hundred branches by 1904. It organised feiseanna and, from 1897, the annual Oireachtas, where music, dance and language were celebrated together. Its weekly paper An Claidheamh Soluis, begun in 1899 and edited by Patrick Pearse from 1903, spread its ideas and gave writers in Irish an outlet. The League therefore created institutions and a culture of Irish names, signs and St Patrick's Day processions that gave ordinary people a way to express their identity.\n\nThe League also achieved real educational gains. Irish was allowed into primary schools under the Revised Programme of 1900, and a campaign to make Irish compulsory for matriculation to the National University succeeded in 1910, taking effect in 1913. These successes put the language into the education system and ensured it would be a subject of national importance. Even so, the League did not restore Irish as an everyday spoken language, and this must be recognised as a limit to its achievement, particularly as the Church itself sometimes held back, as when Fr Michael O'Hickey was dismissed from Maynooth in 1909 after campaigning for compulsory Irish.\n\nThe Anglo-Irish Literary Revival made a different contribution, creating a national literature in English drawn from Irish myth, folklore and the speech of the countryside. Led largely by Protestant writers, it began with the National Literary Society of 1892 and the Irish Literary Theatre founded in 1899 by W.B. Yeats, Lady Gregory and Edward Martyn, whose first production was Yeats's The Countess Cathleen. Yeats's early poetry, such as The Wanderings of Oisin, drew on mythology, while Lady Gregory collected folklore and J.M. Synge used the speech of the Aran Islands and the west. Cathleen ni Houlihan, staged in 1902 with Maud Gonne in the title role, showed that literature could stir national feeling.\n\nThe opening of the Abbey Theatre on 27 December 1904, with Yeats, Lady Gregory and Synge as directors, gave Ireland a permanent national theatre and a lasting cultural institution. The Abbey also made Irish life a subject of serious debate. Synge's The Playboy of the Western World provoked riots in January 1907 because nationalist audiences objected to its picture of rural Ireland, and The Countess Cathleen had earlier been attacked by Catholic critics. These disputes were part of the contribution too, since they forced the country to argue about artistic freedom and who had the right to define Irish identity. Later Yeats won the Nobel Prize in 1923, bringing international recognition to Irish writing.\n\nThe two movements shared the aim of giving Ireland confidence in its own culture, but they were not always in harmony. D.P. Moran, through The Leader from 1900 and his book The Philosophy of Irish Ireland, defined the nation as Gaelic and Catholic, and attacked the Anglo-Irish writers as too English. Hyde's inclusive approach therefore competed with a narrower Catholic and Gaelic nationalism, and unionists suspected that the revival disguised separatism. The Gaelic League also attracted IRB members from about 1910, and Hyde resigned in 1915 when it adopted an explicit political aim.\n\nOverall, the Gaelic League and the Literary Revival gave Ireland lasting institutions, symbols and national self-confidence, and prepared the ground for the nationalism that followed, even if historians still debate whether they caused the revolution of 1916 or merely supplied a language for politics with other causes. The League's mass organisation and the Abbey's drama were their greatest legacies, while their failure to agree on one inclusive identity was their most significant limit.",
