    "label": "1",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The Easter Rising began at noon on Monday 24 April 1916, when about 1,200 Irish Volunteers and members of the Irish Citizen Army seized strong points across Dublin and proclaimed an Irish Republic. Militarily it was a quick failure, but its aftermath transformed Irish politics, so that Ireland was changed profoundly, though not \"utterly\", as Yeats put it.\n\nThe Rising had been planned by the seven-man Military Council of the IRB, which included Patrick Pearse, Joseph Plunkett, Éamonn Ceannt, Tom Clarke, Seán MacDiarmada, James Connolly and Thomas MacDonagh. Their plans were damaged before a shot was fired. The German ship, the Aud, carrying about 20,000 rifles, was intercepted by the Royal Navy on Good Friday and scuttled in Cork Harbour, while Roger Casement was arrested after landing at Banna Strand. Eoin MacNeill, who had been misled about the plans, issued a countermand that was published in the Sunday Independent, so the Rising was put back a day and only a part of the Volunteers turned out. It was always a gamble against heavy odds.\n\nOn Easter Monday the rebels seized the General Post Office in O'Connell Street as their headquarters, with Pearse as President and Commandant-General and Connolly commanding the Dublin forces. Pearse read the Proclamation of the Irish Republic outside the GPO, addressed to Irishmen and Irishwomen and guaranteeing religious and civil liberty and equal rights. The other garrisons were Boland's Mill under Éamon de Valera, the South Dublin Union under Ceannt, Jacob's biscuit factory under MacDonagh, St Stephen's Green and the College of Surgeons under Michael Mallin and Countess Markievicz, and the Four Courts under Edward Daly. The rebels failed to take Dublin Castle, and Trinity College and the docks stayed in British hands.\n\nThe British response was overwhelming. Martial law was proclaimed on 25 April and General Sir John Maxwell took command on 28 April, with reinforcements from the Curragh and from Britain. At Mount Street Bridge a handful of Volunteers inflicted heavy casualties on the Sherwood Foresters, but artillery and the gunboat Helga shelled the city and fires wrecked much of O'Connell Street. The GPO was evacuated on Friday after fire, the garrison retreating to Moore Street, where The O'Rahilly was killed. Pearse surrendered to Brigadier-General Lowe on Saturday 29 April and the other garrisons followed. About 485 people died, the majority of them civilians, which shows the cost of the week to Dubliners. Outside the capital there were only limited actions, such as Thomas Ashe's victory at Ashbourne, Co. Meath, and Liam Mellows's poorly armed rising in Galway.\n\nAt first the Rising was deeply unpopular. Some Dubliners jeered the prisoners as they were marched away, and the Irish Parliamentary Party and most newspapers condemned it. Opinion turned because of British policy. About 3,500 people were arrested and about 1,800 interned, many at Frongoch in Wales. Ninety men were sentenced to death by court-martial and fifteen were shot between 3 and 12 May. Pearse, Clarke and MacDonagh were shot on 3 May, and Connolly, wounded in the fighting, was shot while seated in a chair on 12 May. Bishop O'Dwyer of Limerick criticised Maxwell publicly, and Birrell resigned as Chief Secretary. The slow drip of executions turned the leaders into martyrs and the Proclamation into a founding document.\n\nIreland was changed in important ways. Redmond's party, which had delivered only a suspended Home Rule Act and tied itself to the British war effort, lost authority to those who had kept their separatist principles. Lloyd George's attempt at a settlement in mid-1916 failed when unionist ministers insisted on permanent exclusion of the six counties, so Home Rule was dead in practice. The Frongoch prisoners, released at Christmas 1916, with a general amnesty in June 1917, became the core of the new Sinn Féin, which would sweep the country in 1918. The Rising also reopened the argument about armed force and legitimacy that shaped republican claims for decades.\n\nYet \"changed utterly\" overstates it. Ulster unionists were unmoved and their determination to remain in the United Kingdom was if anything hardened, leading to partition. Tens of thousands of Irishmen continued to serve in the British army, with the 36th Ulster Division suffering heavily on the Somme on 1 July 1916. Revisionist historians stress the small support the Rising had in April 1916 and the civilian dead. My judgement is that the Rising failed as a military operation, but through the executions and the British reaction it changed the direction of nationalist politics, making Yeats's \"terrible beauty\" a fair description of a profound but not total change.",
