    "label": "1",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "John Devoy, Archbishop Daniel Mannix and Éamon de Valera in America each helped to turn Irish nationalism into an international force, by using emigrant money, newspapers, churches and public opinion abroad to pressure Britain. Devoy supplied the organisation and funds, Mannix supplied a powerful voice in Australia and Britain, and de Valera tried to win diplomatic recognition in the United States. Their combined achievement was large in publicity and money, but limited in the political recognition they sought.\n\nJohn Devoy, born in Kildare in 1842, was the great link between Fenianism and the Rising of 1916. Arrested in 1866 for recruiting Fenians in the British Army, he was sentenced to fifteen years and released in 1871 on condition of exile, sailing to New York. There he became a leading figure in Clan na Gael, the secret republican society founded in 1867, which became the strongest republican body in the United States. In 1876 he and Clan na Gael organised the rescue of six Fenian prisoners from Western Australia by the American whaler Catalpa, a daring act that showed what Irish-American money and organisation could do. His influence therefore extended Irish nationalism across the Atlantic and Pacific.\n\nDevoy also bridged republican and constitutional politics. In 1878 he proposed the New Departure with Michael Davitt, under which Fenians would cooperate with Parnell's constitutional nationalists for land reform and self-government. This brought American money and republican energy behind the Land League of 1879. Parnell toured America in 1880 and addressed the House of Representatives, while Patrick Ford's Irish World raised large sums for evicted tenants, and the American Ladies' Land League was founded in New York. Devoy's role in creating this alliance made American support a direct force in Irish agrarian politics.\n\nDevoy's most important contribution came before 1916. In 1903 he founded the Gaelic American, which kept Irish-American opinion behind the republican cause. In 1914 he and Judge Daniel Cohalan met the German ambassador, Count Bernstorff, in Washington to seek German help for a rising, and Clan na Gael sent money to the IRB. The Irish Race Convention in New York in March 1916 launched the Friends of Irish Freedom under Devoy and Cohalan. After the executions of the leaders, Irish-American opinion swung strongly behind the Republic, and the Friends raised money and lobbied Congress. Devoy's career showed how an exile network could sustain a revolutionary movement for half a century.\n\nArchbishop Daniel Mannix gave Irish nationalism an international voice from a different quarter. Born in Charleville, Co. Cork, in 1864 and president of Maynooth from 1903, he became archbishop of Melbourne in 1917. He led the campaign against conscription in the Australian referendums of 1916 and 1917, both of which were defeated, which brought him into conflict with the prime minister, Billy Hughes, and imperial loyalists who called him disloyal. After 1916 he openly condemned British policy in Ireland and supported Irish independence, which made Irish-Australian Catholics a significant pro-nationalist body. In August 1920 a British naval vessel took him off his ship at sea on his way to Ireland and landed him in England, banning him from entering Ireland; the incident gave nationalists a powerful propaganda gift, and he spoke to Irish Catholic communities in Britain instead. His clashes with the Orange Order and Protestant bodies also showed how sectarian rivalry travelled abroad.\n\nÉamon de Valera's mission to America, from June 1919 to December 1920, was the most direct attempt to win international recognition for the Republic. After escaping from Lincoln Gaol in February 1919 and being elected head of the Dáil ministry, he arrived in New York and toured cities with large Irish communities, addressing a huge rally at Fenway Park in Boston on 29 June 1919. The most concrete success was the Dáil bond drive launched in January 1920, which raised about five million dollars from Irish Americans for the Dáil government and the war effort. The mission also produced wide publicity, helped by events such as the hunger-strike death of Terence MacSwiney in October 1920 and the reprisals by the Black and Tans.\n\nBut the mission also exposed the limits of diaspora politics. De Valera quarrelled bitterly with Devoy and Cohalan over control of funds and of the American movement, splitting the Friends of Irish Freedom and leading him to found the American Association for the Recognition of the Irish Republic in November 1920. His Westminster Gazette interview of February 1920, comparing Ireland to Cuba under American influence, caused controversy among republicans. Neither the Republican nor Democratic convention of 1920 recognised the Republic, and President Harding's administration did not either.\n\nOverall, all three contributed significantly, but in different ways. Devoy built the organisation and funding that made Irish nationalism a transatlantic force, Mannix gave it a moral and political voice in the Empire itself, and de Valera showed both the possibilities and the limits of appealing to foreign governments. They won money, publicity and sympathy, but not recognition, and the decisive struggle remained in Ireland.",
