    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The efforts of Ulster Unionists to lobby international support among Ulster emigrants were only moderately successful. They built real sympathy, moral backing and some money among Ulster-Scots in North America and Orangemen in Canada and Australia, and these networks gave unionism a wider identity. But this support had little direct effect on British policy, which was decided at Westminster and in Ulster itself.\n\nThe unionists' strongest asset was the Ulster-Scots, or Scotch-Irish, identity of the descendants of Ulster Presbyterians in the United States and Canada. These emigrants kept a sense of Ulster origins and a Protestant heritage that was distinct from Irish Catholic nationalism. A Scotch-Irish Society of America held its first congress in 1889, and several American presidents, including Andrew Jackson and Woodrow Wilson, were claimed as Ulster-Scots. Unionists could point to this to argue that Ulster Protestants had contributed greatly to the New World and were not part of a separate Irish nation. This was a useful propaganda point, but it was cultural rather than political and carried little weight with American governments.\n\nThe Orange Order was the most organised network abroad. Founded in Co. Armagh in 1795, it spread to Canada with soldiers and settlers, and the Grand Orange Lodge of British America was formed in 1830. In Ontario and Toronto, sometimes called the Belfast of Canada, Orangemen were politically influential. Sir John A. Macdonald, Canada's first prime minister, was an Orangeman, and Mackenzie Bowell, prime minister from 1894 to 1896, was a Grand Master. Orange lodges in New South Wales and Victoria likewise supported the British connection and opposed Home Rule. This gave unionists genuine political friends in the Empire, and meant that the Home Rule struggle was discussed in Dominion politics.\n\nThe Home Rule crisis of 1912 to 1914 was the high point of this lobbying. The Ulster Covenant of 28 September 1912 was signed by about half a million unionists, and it became a symbol of resistance that was reported across the Empire. Orange lodges in Canada gave moral support to Ulster during the crisis, and emigrants in Canada and Australia supported the British connection. A key personality was Andrew Bonar Law, born in New Brunswick in 1858, the son of an Ulster Presbyterian minister. As leader of the Conservatives from 1911, he told a rally at Blenheim Palace in July 1912 that he could imagine no length of resistance to which Ulster could go that he would not support. His Ulster-Scots roots, though he grew up in Glasgow, made him a living example of the overseas link, and his support made the exclusion of Ulster a Conservative goal.\n\nYet the limits are clear. Unionist lobbying among emigrants was largely moral and, to an extent, financial. It did not match the enormous funds that Irish-American nationalists raised for the Land League or the Dáil, and Ulster Unionists never needed to win foreign recognition because their cause was British loyalty. Orange loyalty in Canada and Australia was a sign of attachment to the Crown and the Empire, not a campaign to change the policy of foreign governments. The Orange parades in New York in 1870 and 1871 that led to riots also showed that sectarian rivalry could damage rather than help the image of the movement among native Americans.\n\nIn the end, unionist success came from the strength of Ulster itself, the support of the Conservative Party and the threat of force, not from emigrants. Partition in 1920 was achieved through the Government of Ireland Act, passed at Westminster, rather than through pressure from overseas Ulstermen. The efforts abroad gave encouragement, a wider identity and some resources, so they were not a failure, but their influence on events was small. I would judge them modestly successful as a source of moral support and unsuccessful as a decisive political force.",
