    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Between 1920 and 1966 so many Irish emigrants chose Britain because American doors were closing while Britain's were open, and because jobs, fares and family ties made it the obvious destination. Their assimilation was real in working life but only partial in identity, since they remained a visible Catholic working-class minority who kept strong links with home.\n\nThe first reason was the American restrictions. The Emergency Quota Act of 1921 limited immigrants of each nationality to 3 per cent of its foreign-born population in the 1910 census. The Immigration Act of 1924 then set quotas at 2 per cent of the 1890 census, and the Irish Free State received a quota of 28,567 a year, cut to 17,853 from 1929. The Great Depression after 1929 cut American demand for labour still further. These measures redirected rather than ended emigration, and Britain became the main destination. The US Immigration and Nationality Act of 1965 later favoured family ties and skills, which made entry harder for Irish people without American relatives.\n\nThe second reason was how easy it was to go to Britain. The fare was low, the journey short, and a return home was easy, so many emigrants saw their stay as temporary. The Common Travel Area allowed free movement between the two islands, and the Ireland Act of 1949 confirmed that Irish citizens in the United Kingdom were not treated as aliens and could live, work and vote there. Irish people were also exempted from the Commonwealth Immigrants Act of 1962. During the Emergency, Britain recruited Irish workers for war factories, building and farm work, and well over 100,000 people from the state went to work there.\n\nThe third reason was economic. Post-war reconstruction created huge demand for labour in Britain, while Ireland had low incomes, unemployment and small farms. Irish men worked as navvies on roads, building sites and power stations, and Irish women trained as nurses in the new National Health Service of 1948 or worked in factories and offices. The Commission on Emigration, reporting in 1954, found that low incomes and unemployment were the main causes. In the 1950s about 400,000 people left the state, most for Britain, and the census of 1961 recorded the lowest population in the history of the state, about 2.8 million. Family networks and chain emigration, with relatives finding lodgings and jobs, made Britain still more attractive.\n\nTurning to assimilation, in several ways the Irish integrated well. They spoke English, could vote, and took part in British working life, and Enda Delaney has argued that Irish emigrants kept strong ties with home while integrating into British working life. Irish nurses, builders and factory workers became essential to post-war Britain, and Catholic parishes, schools and chaplaincies helped new arrivals settle, as did the Legion of Mary and Irish centres in London and other cities.\n\nBut assimilation was limited. Many young emigrants arrived with no training and lived in lodging houses or navvy camps, working through subcontractors who paid cash in hand, leaving them without insurance or job security. Signs reading No Irish are widely remembered from lodging-house windows in the 1950s and 1960s, though historians disagree about how common they were. The Irish clustered in areas such as Kilburn, Camden Town, Birmingham, Coventry and Liverpool, and their social life centred on Irish dance halls like the Galtymore in Cricklewood and the Gresham on the Holloway Road. Irish-speaking emigrants tended to lose the language within a generation. The diary of Domhnall Mac Amhlaigh, a navvy in England in the 1950s, shows a generation that worked, socialised and kept its identity in an Irish world inside English cities.\n\nOverall, Britain was chosen because it was the open, near and available destination once America closed, and because work and family drew people there. Assimilation was fastest in work and slowest in identity: the Irish were integrated economically but remained a distinct community, with the expectation of returning home shaping how they lived. In the USA the second generation increasingly thought of itself as American by 1914, but in Britain the closeness of home kept Irishness alive for longer.",
