    "model": "The Holy Ghost mission to Nigeria, which the Spiritans began at Onitsha in 1885 and which Irish priests, brothers and sisters came to lead in the south-east, was a great success in education, health and the growth of a local Catholic church between 1945 and 1966. Its failures lay in its paternalism, its cultural impact, its dependence on the colonial state and the ruin that the Biafran war brought to its work.\n\nThe most obvious success was education. Joseph Shanahan, a Tipperary Spiritan who arrived in 1902, had made the school the main instrument of conversion, building village schools staffed by catechists. After 1945 the mission opened new schools and training colleges across the region, and it ran a large share of the primary and secondary schools. Mission schools became the main route to education for the Igbo, and teacher training colleges created a growing educated class. Many of the Nigerians who later held office in the independent state had passed through these schools, which gave the mission an influence far beyond the church.\n\nA second success was medical care. Hospitals and dispensaries run by the Holy Rosary Sisters, whom Shanahan founded in 1924, and by the Medical Missionaries of Mary, founded by Mother Mary Martin in 1937, brought treatment to rural areas where government services were few. Irish women gained a leadership role in nursing and medicine that was hard to find at home, and the mission hospitals and nursing schools trained Nigerian nurses. In health, as in education, the mission filled a gap that the colonial state left.\n\nThe mission also succeeded in building a church that was growing and increasingly Nigerian. The hierarchy was established in 1950, with the Irish Spiritan Charles Heerey as the first archbishop of Onitsha and Joseph Whelan as first bishop of Owerri. The Catholic population grew strongly, recruits came from Irish seminaries and novitiates in the 1950s, and local vocations increased. Michael Tansi, an Igbo, was ordained in 1937 and later beatified, and in 1965 Francis Arinze became auxiliary bishop of Onitsha at the age of 32. After independence on 1 October 1960 leadership passed steadily to Nigerian priests and bishops.\n\nAgainst this, the mission had real failings. It was closely tied to the colonial government, which paid grants towards mission schools under the grant-in-aid system in return for inspection and standards. This made education cheaper, but it bound the schools to government requirements and sometimes brought disputes over curriculum and religious teaching. When the regional government introduced free primary education in the 1950s, demand for schools rose and strained the mission's resources. The mission was also slow to hand over power: critics said it was paternalistic because Irish missionaries held the leadership long after local people were qualified for it.\n\nCritics also argued that mission education promoted Christian and Western values at the expense of local culture and languages. Rivalry with Methodist and Anglican missionaries over schools and converts divided communities, so the success of the Catholic mission came with sectarian competition in the same villages.\n\nThe greatest blow came with the Biafran war. The eastern region declared itself the Republic of Biafra on 30 May 1967, and the war lasted until January 1970. Many mission stations, schools and hospitals were damaged, closed or abandoned and missionaries were displaced. After the war many foreign missionaries left the east, including Bishop Whelan, and the state took over mission schools. Yet the war also showed the mission's humanity, since the Holy Ghost Father Anthony Byrne helped organise the relief airlift, and the famine moved Irish people so deeply that Concern was founded in 1968.\n\nOn balance the mission was a considerable success in what it built: schools, hospitals and parishes that outlasted colonial rule, and a Nigerian-led church and educated élite. Its failures were real but mostly of attitude and timing, in paternalism, dependence on the colonial state and cultural insensitivity, and the ruin of 1967 to 1970 was a catastrophe it did not cause. Its achievements were the more lasting.",
