    "label": "1",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The government of Northern Ireland had a mixed record between 1949 and 1969. It handled the economy and the welfare state reasonably well, and it kept political stability for most of the period. It was largely unsuccessful in dealing with community relations, because it would not tackle discrimination against the Catholic minority, and that failure brought the state to crisis by 1969.\n\nIn social policy the Unionist governments did deliver real change. Stormont accepted the British welfare settlement, and the National Health Service began in Northern Ireland on 5 July 1948, the same day as in Britain. The Education Act of 1947 gave free secondary education to all pupils, with grammar schools entered through the eleven-plus. Voluntary schools, mostly Catholic, received a state grant of 65 per cent of building costs. State scholarships brought more Catholic students to Queen's University Belfast, and by the 1960s an educated Catholic middle class was emerging. The Social Services Agreement of 1949 guaranteed parity with British benefits, paid for largely by the British Treasury. These reforms raised living standards for both communities and were a genuine success.\n\nThe economy was a much harder problem. Harland and Wolff and the linen industry, the twin pillars of Belfast's prosperity, declined after the war, and unemployment stayed above the UK average, while emigration drained young people from the province. Governments under Brookeborough and later O'Neill responded with grants and incentives that brought man-made fibre plants and aircraft production at Short Brothers. O'Neill, Prime Minister from 1963, went further with economic planning through the Matthew Report of 1963 and the Wilson Plan of 1965, which produced the new city of Craigavon. This was an active response, but its success was limited. Unemployment remained high, and the benefits were unevenly spread, so the economy never matched Britain's.\n\nThe distribution of new investment turned an economic problem into a community relations problem. Most new industry and public projects were placed in Belfast and east of the River Bann, leaving the largely Catholic west, including Derry, with fewer jobs. The decision in 1965 to site the new university at Coleraine rather than Derry, despite a cross-community campaign chaired by John Hume, convinced many nationalists that Stormont treated the west unfairly. The closure of the Derry Road railway line in 1965 and the focus on Craigavon added to the sense of neglect. Economic policy therefore failed to heal divisions and deepened a grievance that the civil rights movement later took up.\n\nOn community relations the record was poor. Catholics were about a third of the population but were under-represented in the RUC, the senior civil service and many large employers. Local government was shaped by gerrymandering, most notoriously in Derry, where ward boundaries drawn in 1936 gave unionists control of a council in a city with a Catholic majority. The business vote and the ratepayer franchise strengthened unionist control, and councils often allocated housing in ways that favoured Protestants. The Special Powers Act and the B Specials remained in place, and the Flags and Emblems Act of 1954 irritated nationalists. Brookeborough's government saw no need to reform a system that kept unionists in power, and the Nationalist Party only accepted the role of official opposition in 1965.\n\nO'Neill tried to improve relations through bridge-building, including the meeting with Seán Lemass at Stormont in January 1965 and visits to Catholic institutions. His efforts were sincere, but they were symbolic and did not touch housing, jobs or the franchise. The Campaign for Social Justice, founded by Conn and Patricia McCluskey in 1964, gathered evidence of discrimination, and the Civil Rights Association followed in 1967. When the Derry march of 5 October 1968 was banned and broken up by the RUC on television, O'Neill was forced into reform. His five-point package of November 1968 promised a points system for housing, an ombudsman and the replacement of Londonderry Corporation, but it came late and split his own party. The attack at Burntollet in January 1969 and his resignation in April 1969 showed how far the system had failed.\n\nOverall, Stormont was successful in extending the welfare state and keeping stability, and it partly met the economic challenge, but it failed on community relations. Its refusal to share power or end discrimination meant that economic and social progress could not satisfy the Catholic community, and by 1969 the state was heading into the Troubles.",
