    "label": "2",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Controversy arose in both cases because each was seen as a symbol of the unequal relationship between unionists and nationalists. The siting of the new university at Coleraine was seen in Derry as political discrimination against the west and the Catholic majority, while the Apprentice Boys' parades were seen by nationalists as triumphalist celebrations of Protestant victory held on their doorstep.\n\nThe university controversy grew from the Robbins Report of 1963, which recommended expanding higher education and led Northern Ireland to plan a second university beside Queen's in Belfast. Derry had Magee College, founded in 1865, and was the second city of Northern Ireland, with high unemployment and heavy emigration. Its people, nationalists and many unionists alike, believed it deserved the university after years of neglect. The location was therefore a test of whether Stormont would treat the west fairly.\n\nThe Lockwood Committee, appointed in 1963, was to advise on the site. The University for Derry Committee, chaired by the young teacher John Hume, campaigned across the community and argued that Derry had the population, the existing college and the need for jobs. When the committee reported in February 1965 in favour of Coleraine, a much smaller town about 30 miles away, there was anger. Supporters travelled to Stormont in a motorcade to protest, and the government accepted the recommendation with a unionist majority vote. Ministers argued that Coleraine had a central site, good access and room for expansion, but Derry people alleged that faceless men had made a political decision.\n\nThe decision was controversial because it fitted a pattern. The Benson Report led to the closure of the Derry Road railway line in 1965, and the Matthew Report and the new city of Craigavon seemed to draw investment to the east. This gave rise to the 'west of the Bann' grievance. The fact that Derry's own Corporation was controlled by unionists through gerrymandering, despite a Catholic majority in the city, meant that its people could do nothing to change these decisions. The affair fed directly into the housing and voting grievances that produced the civil rights protests in Derry in 1968, and it gave Hume his first experience of campaigning that he later used in the civil rights movement and the SDLP.\n\nThe controversy around the Apprentice Boys had deeper historical roots. The club was founded in 1814 to commemorate the siege of Derry of 1688 and 1689, when thirteen apprentices shut the gates against the army of the Catholic King James II. The defenders held out for 105 days until the relief ship Mountjoy broke the boom across the Foyle on 28 July 1689. For Protestants this was a story of resistance and the defence of civil and religious liberty, and the slogan 'No Surrender' expressed a siege mentality.\n\nThe Apprentice Boys' ceremonies were the source of contention. Each August the Relief of Derry parade marched around the city walls, above the Catholic Bogside, with bands, banners and crimson flags, and each December the burning of an effigy of the traitor Governor Lundy marked the shutting of the gates. Nationalists saw these as triumphalism, a celebration of conquest in a city where Catholics were a majority but were denied political control. Unionists replied that the parades were legal and traditional. The route past the Bogside was a repeated point of dispute, and the tension exploded on 12 August 1969, when stone-throwing and clashes with the RUC began and led to the Battle of the Bogside. Residents built barricades and declared Free Derry, and British troops were deployed on 14 August 1969.\n\nBoth controversies arose because decisions and traditions that unionists regarded as normal were seen by nationalists as signs of an unfair state. Coleraine added to the sense of discrimination in public investment, and the Apprentice Boys parade gave that resentment a place and a date on which it turned to violence.",
