    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Bernadette Devlin, John Hume and Seamus Heaney each left a lasting mark on Northern Ireland, but in different ways. Devlin gave the civil rights movement a youthful and uncompromising voice and carried its cause to Westminster. Hume shaped constitutional nationalism and the search for a peaceful settlement based on consent. Heaney's impact was cultural: he gave the Troubles a language of grief, memory and identity that reached far beyond Ireland.\n\nBernadette Devlin was born in Cookstown, County Tyrone, in 1947 and studied psychology at Queen's University Belfast. She became a leader of People's Democracy, the student civil rights group formed in October 1968 after the police baton charge on the Derry march. In January 1969 she took part in the march from Belfast to Derry, which was ambushed by a loyalist crowd at Burntollet Bridge on 4 January, among whom were off-duty B Specials. The attack, and the failure of the RUC to protect the marchers, deepened Catholic distrust of the police and kept the issue of discrimination in the public eye.\n\nIn April 1969 she won the Mid Ulster by-election as a unity and civil rights candidate, becoming at twenty-one the youngest woman elected to Westminster up to that time. This brought the grievances of Northern Ireland's Catholics directly into the House of Commons, at a time when British politicians had paid little attention to the province. Her memoir, The Price of My Soul, published in 1969, presented the struggle in her own words. She was imprisoned in 1970 for incitement to riot after the Battle of the Bogside, and the day after Bloody Sunday she struck the Home Secretary, Reginald Maudling, in the Commons. Nationalists saw her as a symbol of defiance, unionists as a dangerous agitator. She lost her seat in 1974, so her impact was greatest in 1969, when she made the civil rights case heard in Britain, though her radicalism was never in line with the moderate nationalism that came to dominate.\n\nJohn Hume had a far longer and more constructive influence. A teacher from Derry, he became vice-chairman of the Derry Citizens' Action Committee after the events of October 1968. He helped found the Social Democratic and Labour Party in 1970, giving nationalists a constitutional party that rejected violence. He was a leading figure in the Sunningdale talks of 1973 and served as Minister of Commerce in the power-sharing executive in 1974, which fell after the Ulster Workers' Council strike. That failure convinced him that any settlement needed the consent of both unionists and nationalists and an Irish dimension.\n\nHume became leader of the SDLP in 1979 and was elected to the European Parliament the same year, using Europe as a model of divided peoples working together. He supported the Anglo-Irish Agreement of 1985, which gave the Irish government a consultative role in Northern Ireland. His most controversial act was the dialogue with Gerry Adams, begun in 1988 and renewed from 1991 to 1993. Unionists and many nationalists condemned him for talking to Sinn Féin while the IRA campaign continued, but he argued that republicans had to be persuaded that violence was a barrier to Irish unity. Historians credit his work with Adams and with the Irish government with influencing the Downing Street Declaration of December 1993 and so the road to the ceasefires. He shared the Nobel Peace Prize in 1998, and the settlement of that year reflected his insistence on consent and on agreement between the two communities and between North and South.\n\nSeamus Heaney, born in 1939 at Mossbawn near Castledawson, County Derry, into a Catholic farming family, made his mark in literature. His early collections, Death of a Naturalist in 1966 and Door into the Dark in 1969, drew on rural life. As violence grew, his work turned to the Troubles. North, published in 1975, used Iron Age bog bodies, notably in the poem 'Punishment', to explore ritual killing and tribal loyalty, so that the violence of the present was linked to something ancient. Field Work in 1979 and Station Island in 1984 continued his reflection on violence, memory and the Catholic tradition.\n\nHeaney's impact was complicated. He moved to County Wicklow in 1972 and refused to be labelled British in an anthology, writing in 'An Open Letter' in 1983 that his 'passport's green'. He was a director of the Field Day Theatre Company, founded in 1980 by Brian Friel and Stephen Rea, which explored questions of language and identity. Critics such as Ciaran Carson argued that North mythologised violence and made it seem inevitable. Yet his work gave a shared language for grief and guilt, and his Nobel Prize for Literature in 1995 brought international attention to the Northern Irish experience. His influence was on how people understood the conflict rather than on its politics.\n\nOverall, Hume had the deepest political impact, shaping the peace process and the principle of consent, while Devlin's influence was brief but dramatic in the crucial year of 1969, and Heaney's was cultural and lasting. Together they show that the response to the Troubles came through protest, politics and art.",
