    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The EEC had the greater impact on Irish society, although Vatican II was important in changing the religious life of Catholics. Vatican II altered worship and encouraged questioning, but the church still held strong influence over law and public life into the 1980s. Membership of the EEC from 1 January 1973 transformed the economy, the farm sector, the position of women and the way in which Irish people saw themselves and their place in Europe.\n\nVatican II, held in Rome from October 1962 to December 1965, certainly changed Irish Catholic life. The Mass was now said in the vernacular instead of Latin, with the first Masses in English in Ireland in 1965, priests faced the congregation and laypeople could act as readers and distribute communion. The council also promoted ecumenism: the ban on Catholics attending Trinity College Dublin was lifted in 1970, and the first inter-church talks took place at Ballymascanlon, Co. Louth, in 1973. Its declaration that religious freedom is a right weakened the claim that a Catholic state should have a special place in law, and it encouraged people to think for themselves on moral questions. Seán Ó Riada's Ceol an Aifrinn in 1968 showed how the changes could inspire cultural renewal.\n\nHowever, the impact of Vatican II on society as a whole was limited and slow. Archbishop McQuaid assured Dubliners on his return in 1965 that the council would not disturb their faith, implemented reforms slowly, and was wary of ecumenism and lay initiatives. A survey in 1973 and 1974 still found about nine in ten Catholics attending Mass weekly. Humanae Vitae in 1968 reaffirmed the ban on artificial contraception, which many married Catholics found hard to accept, and vocations began to fall from the late 1960s. The church kept strong influence on law, as the 1983 abortion referendum and the 1986 divorce referendum showed. Historians stress that the decline in church power came from social change, such as television, education and emigration, as much as from the council itself.\n\nThe EEC, by contrast, changed the economy in ways that touched nearly every household. The Common Agricultural Policy guaranteed minimum prices for beef, milk and cereals, and farm incomes rose sharply in the early years of membership, with land values rising and farmers investing in machinery, buildings and drainage. The benefits were uneven, since larger farms in the east and south gained most, and milk quotas arrived in 1984, but agriculture was transformed. Regional and social funds, including the European Regional Development Fund created in 1975, paid for roads, water and sewerage schemes, telecommunications and training schemes, and the 1988 reform of the structural funds doubled the money available, with Ireland a major beneficiary.\n\nMembership also changed industry and Ireland's international position. American and other foreign firms set up factories to sell into the EEC, drawn by IDA grants, a young English-speaking workforce and low taxes, including a 10 per cent corporation tax rate on manufacturing profits from 1981. Electronics, pharmaceuticals and engineering became leading sectors, with Digital in Galway and later Intel at Leixlip, and manufactured goods overtook farm produce in the value of exports. Dependence on Britain fell as trade with the continent grew, and Ireland's decision in March 1979 to join the European Monetary System ended the link with sterling. There were costs too: protected industries such as clothing, textiles and footwear closed with heavy job losses, and the fishing industry gained less than farming, as the Common Fisheries Policy of 1983 gave Ireland small quotas compared with the value of the fish in its waters.\n\nMost directly for ordinary people, the EEC changed the legal position of women and individuals. Directives on equal pay in 1975 and equal treatment in employment in 1976 required changes in Irish law and reinforced the Anti-Discrimination (Pay) Act 1974 and the Employment Equality Act 1977. The marriage bar in the public service was lifted in 1973, and a 1978 directive on equal treatment in social welfare forced changes to payments to married women in the 1980s. Irish citizens gained the right to live and work in other member states, which eased emigration, and the European Court of Justice became a route for individuals to challenge Irish law. A change of this kind in economic and legal rights was more far-reaching than the change of worship brought by Vatican II.\n\nOn balance, the EEC had the greater impact. Vatican II changed how Catholics worshipped and began to loosen the church's authority, but its effects on society were gradual and the church remained powerful. The EEC changed incomes, jobs, industry, legal rights and Ireland's place in the world, and historians generally accept that membership underpinned the transformation of the economy and society.",
