    "model": "Balkan nationalism developed between 1815 and 1871 from local resistance to Ottoman rule in Serbia and Greece into a wider movement for national states, and it threatened European stability mainly by drawing the Great Powers into rivalry over Ottoman decline. The threat was real but limited, since the powers contained it, and the Crimean War was the one clear exception.\n\nIn 1815 the Balkans were ruled by the Ottoman Sultan through local officials and the millet system, which gave Christian communities religious autonomy but no political rights. Ottoman decline raised the Eastern Question of which Great Power would benefit. Russia claimed to protect Orthodox Christians, Austria feared Russian expansion and wanted the Ottoman Empire kept as a barrier, and Britain wished to protect its route to India. Any rising by Balkan Christians therefore concerned all of Europe. Serbia led the way. The First Serbian Uprising began in 1804 under Karadjordje Petrović against the Janissaries and was crushed in 1813, but a second rising in 1815 under Miloš Obrenović was more successful. Miloš negotiated autonomy, recognised by a decree of the Sultan in 1830, and became hereditary prince, although Serbia paid tribute and Ottoman garrisons stayed in its fortresses until 1867.\n\nGreece followed and gained more. The Philiki Etaireia, founded in Odessa in 1814, planned a rising, the Peloponnese rose in March 1821, and a national assembly at Epidaurus declared independence in January 1822. The Ottoman massacre on Chios in 1822 and the fall of Missolonghi in 1826 produced a wave of philhellenism, and Lord Byron died at Missolonghi in 1824. When Ibrahim Pasha brought Egyptian forces in 1825, Britain, France and Russia intervened and destroyed the Ottoman and Egyptian fleet at Navarino on 20 October 1827. The London Protocol of 1830 recognised Greek independence and the Treaty of London of 1832 made Greece a monarchy under Otto of Bavaria, who was forced to grant a constitution in 1843 and was deposed in 1862.\n\nThe movement also grew in ambition. Greece's first frontier, from Arta to Volos, left most Greeks outside the kingdom, so the Megali Idea, voiced by Kolettis in 1844, aimed to bring all Greeks into one state. In the same year Garašanin's Načertanije planned a larger South Slav state under Serbian leadership. In 1859 Moldavia and Wallachia elected the same prince, Alexander Ioan Cuza, and their union was recognised by 1862, with Carol of Hohenzollern replacing Cuza in 1866. National movements in the Balkans thus won autonomy or independence in stages, usually with the support or restraint of the Great Powers.\n\nThe threat to stability was most serious when the powers disagreed. The Greek war broke the principle of non-intervention that Metternich defended, since Britain, France and Russia supported rebels against a legitimate ruler, and this helped to break up the Congress System. The Crimean War of 1853 to 1856 was the clearest result of the Eastern Question. It arose from disputes over the Holy Places and Russian claims, notably Menshikov's mission of 1853 to claim a protectorate over the Sultan's Orthodox subjects, and Britain and France went to war against Russia. The Treaty of Paris of 1856 guaranteed Ottoman integrity and neutralised the Black Sea, but Balkan crises in the 1850s and 1860s kept the powers in a state of tension.\n\nYet the threat should not be exaggerated. Serbia and Greece were small and their gains depended on the powers, who settled most crises by agreement, as at Navarino and in the London settlements. The Crimean War was the only war among the great powers over the Balkans before 1871, and the biggest changes to the European balance in these years came from Italy and Germany. Balkan nationalism therefore developed from Serbian and Greek risings into a wider movement and made Ottoman decline a permanent source of tension, but it threatened stability less by itself than by involving the Great Powers, and its full danger belonged to the later decades of the century.",
