    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Charles Darwin and Victor Hugo were two of the towering figures of nineteenth-century Europe, one transforming science and the other literature and political thought. Darwin's achievement was the theory of evolution by natural selection, while Hugo became the most celebrated writer of French Romanticism and a courageous opponent of Napoleon III. Both changed how Europeans saw themselves, and both were honoured by their countries at their deaths.\n\nDarwin's first achievement was the scientific groundwork he laid on HMS Beagle. Born in Shrewsbury in 1809 and educated at Edinburgh and Cambridge, he joined the Beagle as naturalist, and between 1831 and 1836 he collected fossils, plants and animals in South America, the Galapagos Islands and Australia. His careful observation of how species varied gave him the evidence on which his later theory rested, and it showed that a great scientific idea could be built from patient fieldwork rather than speculation.\n\nHis central achievement was On the Origin of Species, published in November 1859. He had worked on the idea for about twenty years and delayed publication until Alfred Russel Wallace reached the same conclusion independently. The book argued that species change over time through variation and natural selection, in which individuals suited to their environment survive and reproduce more successfully, and that all living things share common ancestors. This overturned the older view that each species had been separately created and transformed biology. In The Descent of Man in 1871 he applied the theory to human origins, which forced Europeans to confront questions about religion and humanity's place in nature. Thomas Henry Huxley defended him, while Bishop Samuel Wilberforce and many churchmen opposed him at the Oxford debate of 1860, at which Darwin was not present. He was buried in Westminster Abbey in 1882, and genetics later explained the inheritance he could not.\n\nHugo's first achievement was to lead the Romantic movement in French literature. His preface to the play Cromwell in 1827 served as a manifesto for Romantic drama, and Hernani, staged in 1830, provoked a riot between Romantic supporters and classical critics and marked the victory of Romanticism in the French theatre. His novel Notre-Dame de Paris in 1831 revived interest in the Gothic past of the city and helped to inspire the restoration of medieval buildings, and his election to the Academie francaise in 1841 confirmed his standing as a literary figure.\n\nHis second achievement was his political courage. A monarchist in the 1820s, he was a republican by 1848, was elected to the Constituent Assembly and the Legislative Assembly, and defended freedom of the press and education while opposing restrictions on the franchise. After Louis-Napoleon's coup of December 1851 he was forced to leave France, going first to Brussels, then to Jersey and from 1855 to Guernsey. His pamphlet Napoleon le Petit in 1852 and his satirical poems Les Chatiments in 1853 attacked the new ruler and made Hugo a symbol of republican resistance, and he refused the amnesty of 1859 and stayed in exile as a protest. His exile also produced Les Contemplations in 1856, written partly in memory of his daughter Leopoldine, and Les Miserables in 1862, which followed the convict Jean Valjean from 1815 to the Paris rising of 1832, portrayed the poor of France with compassion and became one of the most widely read novels of the century. He returned to Paris on 5 September 1870, the day after the Third Republic was proclaimed.\n\nOverall, both men achieved greatness in different ways. Darwin's achievement was the more far-reaching intellectually, because natural selection changed science and the human self-image for ever. Hugo's was the more public and moral, since he joined literary genius to principled resistance to tyranny and spoke for the poor and for liberty. Together they show the breadth of nineteenth-century cultural and scientific achievement.",
