    "model": "I agree, with qualifications. Bismarck was not a pacifist and could be ruthless, but after 1871 he used diplomacy to reduce tension and keep the peace, whereas Wilhelm II's foreign policy after 1890 was boastful, clumsy and aggressive, and it pushed the other Great Powers into opposing camps. Measured by its effects on European stability, Wilhelm's policy was far more provocative.\n\nBismarck's aim after 1871 was to protect the new Reich, which he called a satiated power with no further territorial ambitions in Europe. He feared a hostile coalition, above all one including a France embittered by the loss of Alsace and Lorraine, so his strategy was to keep France isolated. The Three Emperors' League of 1873 linked Germany, Austria-Hungary and Russia, the Dual Alliance of 1879 bound Germany to Austria-Hungary, and the Triple Alliance of 1882 added Italy. When Russia turned away, he replaced the League with the secret Reinsurance Treaty of 1887, which promised neutrality if either power fought a third state, except if Germany attacked France or Russia attacked Austria-Hungary. These agreements were defensive, and their purpose was to prevent a war, not to start one.\n\nBismarck did take actions that were tough, and critics point to them. The War-in-Sight crisis of 1875 suggested that Germany might attack France to stop its rearmament, and Britain and Russia warned him off. His alliance system was also complicated and deceptive, since the Reinsurance Treaty was kept secret from Austria-Hungary and sat uneasily beside the Dual Alliance, and he let Franco-German tension simmer during the Boulanger crisis and the Schnaebele affair of 1887. However, he always drew back, and Germany fought no war after 1871 while he held power. At the Congress of Berlin in 1878 he acted as an honest broker and helped settle the Balkan crisis without a European war. He even backed French colonial expansion in Tunisia in 1881 to turn France away from Alsace-Lorraine, and he took colonies only in 1884-85.\n\nWilhelm II reversed this approach. He forced Bismarck out in 1890 because he wanted to rule personally, and the Reinsurance Treaty was allowed to lapse. This was a serious mistake, because it drove Russia towards France, and the Franco-Russian alliance of the early 1890s left Germany facing the two-front danger that Bismarck had worked for twenty years to avoid. Weltpolitik, the drive after 1897 for a place in the sun, was provocative in itself. The Kruger telegram of 1896 congratulated the Transvaal on defeating the Jameson Raid and outraged British opinion. Germany seized Kiaochow in China in 1897 and bought Pacific islands from Spain in 1899. The Kaiser's speeches, such as the Hun speech of 1900 and the Daily Telegraph interview of 1908, added to the impression of an unpredictable ruler. In Morocco, Germany challenged France at Tangier in 1905 and sent the gunboat Panther to Agadir in 1911, and both times it was left isolated while Britain and France drew closer.\n\nThe most provocative step was the naval programme. Driven by Tirpitz, who became state secretary of the Navy Office in 1897, Germany passed the Navy Laws of 1898 and 1900, planning a fleet of 38 battleships. Tirpitz's risk theory held that a fleet strong enough to damage the Royal Navy would deter Britain, but in practice it alarmed her. The launch of HMS Dreadnought in 1906 began an arms race, and Britain responded by ending its isolation through agreements with Japan in 1902, France in 1904 and Russia in 1907. The Haldane mission of 1912 failed because Germany wanted British neutrality in return for slowing its building. Bismarck's careful diplomacy against encirclement had given way to a policy that produced it.\n\nBismarck was a hard-headed realist who sometimes bluffed, but he aimed to preserve the settlement of 1871 and succeeded for nineteen years. Wilhelm's policy unsettled it by challenging Britain at sea, France in Morocco and Russia by neglect, so that Germany faced a hostile coalition in 1914. Wilhelm II's foreign policy was clearly the more provocative of the two.",
