    "model": "Soviet Russia was highly successful in controlling Eastern Europe for most of the period, but the success rested on force and not on consent, and it ended abruptly in 1989 when Moscow stopped being willing to use that force. Between 1945 and 1989 Soviet power was imposed, defended against revolt and made the basis of a bloc, yet by 1992 the system had collapsed, which shows how shallow the control really was.\n\nControl was built quickly between 1945 and 1949. The Red Army occupied Poland, Romania, Bulgaria, Hungary, eastern Germany and much of Czechoslovakia by May 1945, and Stalin wanted a buffer of friendly governments after the invasions of 1914 and 1941. Communist parties first governed in coalitions but held key posts such as the police, and removed their rivals slice by slice, the method Rakosi called salami tactics. Mikolajczyk fled Poland in October 1947 after rigged elections, King Michael of Romania was forced to abdicate in December 1947, and in February 1948 Klement Gottwald's communists took power in Czechoslovakia without an election. Show trials, such as that of Laszlo Rajk, executed in 1949, eliminated rivals. The Cominform of 1947 tied the parties to Moscow, Marshall Aid was refused, and Comecon was set up in 1949. Control was not complete, since Tito's Yugoslavia was expelled from the Cominform in 1948 and survived, and the Berlin Blockade failed when the Western airlift defeated it.\n\nThe Soviet Union showed again and again that it would use force to keep its satellites. When East German workers rose on 17 June 1953 against higher work norms, Soviet tanks crushed the rising. In Hungary in 1956 Imre Nagy formed a coalition, declared Hungary neutral and announced its withdrawal from the Warsaw Pact on 1 November. Moscow could not accept this, and on 4 November Soviet forces attacked. Thousands of Hungarians died, around 200,000 fled, and Nagy was executed in 1958. The West did nothing, as Suez distracted Britain and France. In 1968 the pattern was repeated: Dubcek's Prague Spring, with its promise of socialism with a human face, was ended by the Warsaw Pact invasion of 20-21 August, and the Brezhnev Doctrine claimed a right to intervene whenever socialism was threatened. These were clear Soviet successes in control.\n\nYet this control hid deep weaknesses. Central planning, exported to the satellites, favoured heavy industry and produced shortages and poor quality goods, while East Germans waited years for a Trabant and the West grew rich. Dissent kept reappearing: Charter 77 in Czechoslovakia, and above all Solidarity in Poland, which after the Gdansk agreement of August 1980 had about ten million members and was encouraged by the election of Pope John Paul II in 1978. General Jaruzelski's martial law of December 1981 suppressed it, but it was Polish forces and not Soviet troops that did so, and the union was never destroyed. A system that needed tanks and martial law to survive had not won loyalty.\n\nThe end came when the Soviet Union itself stopped enforcing its control. Gorbachev, General Secretary from 1985, introduced glasnost and perestroika, and the so-called Sinatra Doctrine meant that each state could choose its own path. In 1989 Solidarity won the elections of June, Hungary opened its border with Austria, Honecker fell in October, the Berlin Wall was opened on 9 November and Ceausescu was executed in December. No Soviet tank moved. The Warsaw Pact and Comecon were dissolved in 1991 and the Soviet Union itself ceased to exist in December 1991.\n\nOverall, Soviet Russia was very successful in controlling Eastern Europe in the short term, from the takeovers of 1945-48 to the crushing of Hungary and Czechoslovakia, but it was a control that depended on fear, a failing economy and the threat of force. When Gorbachev would no longer supply the force, the whole structure fell within a few months.",
