    "label": "2",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Marshall Aid and the moves towards European unity both had a major impact on the western economies. Marshall Aid supplied the dollars and the framework that started recovery after the war, while the European communities, from the coal and steel pool to the EEC, created the larger market that sustained the long boom to 1973. Together they were two of the main reasons why Western Europe prospered as it did.\n\nWestern Europe in 1947 was in serious difficulty. Much industrial capacity was destroyed, transport had to be rebuilt, and Europe had a large trade deficit with the United States, so it could not pay for the American machinery and raw materials it needed because it lacked dollars. Marshall's speech at Harvard on 5 June 1947 offered a solution. Under the European Recovery Programme, which ran from 1948 to 1952, about 13 billion dollars was provided, mostly as grants, to sixteen European states, with Britain, France and West Germany receiving the largest sums.\n\nThe aid paid for imports of raw materials, food, fuel and machinery, and counterpart funds were used for investment in industry and housing. Its impact was not only financial. Recipients had to draw up recovery plans and cooperate through the Organisation for European Economic Co-operation, set up in 1948, and the conditions encouraged them to remove trade barriers and control inflation. The European Payments Union of 1950 allowed currencies to be settled through a clearing system and revived trade between members. Marshall Aid also aimed to stop economic collapse from strengthening the communists in France and Italy, and it promoted American productivity methods.\n\nThe move towards unity gave a second, longer-lasting boost. Jean Monnet drafted the Schuman Declaration of 9 May 1950, which proposed pooling French and West German coal and steel under a joint authority. The European Coal and Steel Community began in 1952 and gave its members a common market in the industries that made war possible, while beginning to end Franco-German rivalry, which was essential to stable growth. The Treaty of Rome of 1957 then created the European Economic Community, which began on 1 January 1958. It removed tariffs between its members on industrial goods by July 1968, set a common external tariff and introduced the Common Agricultural Policy, which supported farm incomes. Larger markets allowed firms to produce on a bigger scale, and trade between the six grew very fast. Britain, which had set up the rival European Free Trade Association in 1960, joined with Ireland and Denmark on 1 January 1973, and Ireland gained access to a large market and to farm support under the CAP.\n\nHistorians qualify the impact of both. Alan Milward argued that Marshall Aid was a modest share of total investment and that domestic policy did much of the work, and West Germany's Wirtschaftswunder owed a great deal to Ludwig Erhard's currency reform of June 1948 and his social market economy. Cheap oil, American technology, refugee and guest-worker labour and government commitment to full employment all helped. Even so, Western Europe's growth between 1950 and 1973 was far higher than in any earlier period, with West Germany averaging over 7 percent a year in the 1950s.\n\nOverall, Marshall Aid was the vital start, since it solved the dollar shortage and taught cooperation, while European unity was the lasting framework that kept the markets open and the economies growing until the oil crisis of 1973. Neither alone explains the boom, but both had a deep and positive impact on the western economies.",
