    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Between 1945 and 1992 affluence and the consumer society developed from post-war austerity into a mass way of life in Western Europe, while nuclear power developed from a military technology into a major source of electricity that provoked growing opposition. Both were shaped by the Cold War and by the great growth of the western economies, and both were met with doubts as well as enthusiasm.\n\nAffluence grew out of the economic boom of the 1950s and 1960s. Rising wages and full employment meant that ordinary families could buy goods that had once been luxuries, such as refrigerators, washing machines and televisions. Car ownership expanded sharply as cheap models such as the Fiat 500, the Citroen 2CV and the Mini became affordable. Self-service supermarkets spread in the 1960s, and hire purchase and consumer credit let people buy before they could afford goods outright. Advertising and television encouraged consumption, and this made a consumer society in which buying goods was central to economic life.\n\nLeisure time grew with affluence. Working hours fell, the five-day week spread, and paid holidays were extended. Television became the centre of the home, while cinema, spectator sport and record-buying became mass leisure industries. Package holidays to Spain, Italy and the Greek islands grew rapidly in the 1960s and 1970s, with the Costa Brava a symbol of the trend. The contrast with the East was sharp: consumer goods there were scarce, East Germans waited years for a Trabant, and travel to the West was restricted. Critics also questioned the western model. Vance Packard in The Hidden Persuaders in 1957 argued that advertising manipulated consumers, Galbraith in The Affluent Society in 1958 said private wealth went with public squalor, and Marcuse in One-Dimensional Man in 1964 claimed consumerism encouraged conformity.\n\nNuclear power developed from the same post-war confidence in science. Eisenhower's Atoms for Peace address of December 1953 promoted civil nuclear energy, and the Soviet Obninsk reactor began supplying a public grid in June 1954. Calder Hall in Cumbria opened in October 1956 as the first nuclear station to supply electricity on a commercial scale, although it also produced plutonium for weapons. The Treaty of Rome of 1957 created Euratom to coordinate nuclear energy among the six EEC states, though the Windscale fire of October 1957 showed the risks.\n\nThe oil crisis of 1973 gave nuclear power a major boost, because governments wanted to reduce their dependence on imported oil. In 1974 the Messmer Plan committed France to building a large number of reactors, which came to provide most of its electricity, and the Soviet Union built reactors across its bloc, including the RBMK reactors at Chernobyl.\n\nOpposition grew alongside the programmes. The Three Mile Island accident in Pennsylvania on 28 March 1979 halted new American orders for years, and in Ireland the plan for a plant at Carnsore Point in Wexford was dropped after large protests in 1978-79. The decisive blow was the explosion at Chernobyl in Soviet Ukraine on 26 April 1986, which spread radioactive material across Europe, forced the evacuation of Pripyat and created a 30-kilometre exclusion zone. The slow Soviet admission of it strengthened the case for glasnost, and Italy voted in 1987 to end nuclear power.\n\nOverall, both developments were products of post-war prosperity and technology. Affluence and the consumer society spread steadily and changed everyday life, mostly welcomed, while nuclear power grew quickly into a source of cheap energy and then met a lasting loss of public trust, so that by 1992 it was a technology that Europe depended on and feared.",
