    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Alexander Solzhenitsyn, Simone de Beauvoir and John Lennon each left a lasting mark on the twentieth century in very different fields. Solzhenitsyn exposed the Soviet camp system to the world, de Beauvoir challenged the way Western society treated women, the elderly and the individual conscience, and Lennon gave the youth culture of the 1960s a global voice and a message of peace. I will deal with all three, since each achieved something of real significance.\n\nSolzhenitsyn's greatest achievement was to break the silence about the Gulag. Born in 1918, he served as an artillery officer in the Second World War but was arrested in 1945 for criticising Stalin in private letters, and he spent eight years in labour camps followed by internal exile in Kazakhstan. His experience became his writing. One Day in the Life of Ivan Denisovich, published in Novy Mir in 1962 with Khrushchev's approval, brought the camps into open Soviet discussion for the first time, which showed that a single book could force a state to face its past. After Khrushchev's fall censorship tightened, and The First Circle and Cancer Ward could appear only abroad in 1968.\n\nHis most important work was The Gulag Archipelago, published in Paris in 1973 after the KGB seized a copy of the manuscript. It documented the camp system through testimony and history and had a major impact in the West, undermining the moral standing of Soviet communism among many intellectuals. He had already won the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1970, and when the authorities arrested him on 12 February 1974 and flew him into exile the next day, the expulsion only made him more famous. He returned to Russia in 1994. Historians regard his work as central to Western understanding of Soviet terror, although some question the reliability of his testimony and dislike his later nationalism.\n\nSimone de Beauvoir's achievements lay in literature, philosophy and social criticism. With Jean-Paul Sartre she founded the review Les Temps modernes in 1945, which became the leading journal of left-wing intellectuals in France, and she was a leading voice of existentialism, the idea that people are free and responsible for their own choices. Her novel The Mandarins, portraying the Parisian intellectual left after the war, won the Prix Goncourt in 1954, and her memoirs, including Memoirs of a Dutiful Daughter in 1958, gave an honest account of her youth and her partnership with Sartre, a relationship by choice without marriage. Her feminist writing, above all The Second Sex, did much to shape the later women's movement.\n\nShe was also a committed public critic. In 1960 she signed the Manifesto of the 121 supporting French conscripts who refused to fight in Algeria, and in 1962 she campaigned against torture by the French army, taking up the case of Djamila Boupacha. Her essay on old age, The Coming of Age, published in 1970, attacked the way society neglected the elderly. Conservative critics attacked her for challenging traditional morality, but historians now rank her among the most influential critics of the century.\n\nJohn Lennon's achievements belong to popular culture and the peace movement. Born in Liverpool in 1940, he formed the Quarrymen in 1957, which became The Beatles, and he and Paul McCartney wrote most of the group's songs. Their first UK number one came in 1963, their appearance on The Ed Sullivan Show on 9 February 1964 gave them a mass American audience, and albums such as Rubber Soul and Sgt. Pepper showed that pop could be an art form. In June 1967 they performed All You Need Is Love on the first live global satellite broadcast, watched by hundreds of millions, which made them the first global pop stars and a model for later artists who wrote their own songs.\n\nLennon then used his fame for political causes. In 1969 he and Yoko Ono staged their bed-in protests for peace, which produced Give Peace a Chance, an anthem of the anti-war movement, and in November 1969 he returned his MBE in protest at British support for the United States in Vietnam. His solo album Imagine in 1971 was both radical and commercially successful. The American authorities tried to deport him in 1972 because of his anti-war activity, but he won the right to stay and received a green card in 1976. His murder in New York on 8 December 1980 was mourned across Europe and America.\n\nAll three achieved a great deal, but in different ways. Solzhenitsyn's achievement was the most directly political, since he shattered the silence around Stalin's terror and helped to discredit Soviet communism. De Beauvoir changed how society thought about women, the old and moral freedom, and Lennon changed popular culture and gave voice to a generation. Taken together they show how writers and artists could shape ideas and attitudes in the second half of the twentieth century.",
