    "model": "To a considerable extent, Britain contributed to tensions in the Middle East between 1945 and 1966. Its Mandate in Palestine rested on promises to both Arabs and Jews that could not be reconciled, its handling of Jewish refugees provoked Zionist violence, its withdrawal in 1948 left a security vacuum, and its efforts to hold on to influence in Egypt ended in the Suez Crisis of 1956. Arab rejection of partition and Nasser's nationalism were also real causes, so Britain was a major contributor rather than the sole one.\n\nThe roots of the tension lay in the Mandate itself. The Balfour Declaration of 1917 promised British support for a national home for the Jewish people in Palestine, provided the rights of existing communities were not prejudiced, and Britain held the Mandate from 1923. Jewish immigration rose sharply after the Nazis took power in 1933, and the Jewish share of the population grew from about 11 per cent in 1922 to roughly 30 per cent by 1939. Britain crushed the Arab Revolt of 1936-39, and its White Paper of May 1939 limited Jewish immigration to 75,000 over five years, which the Zionists rejected. By 1945 Britain had alienated both communities and had no policy acceptable to either.\n\nAfter the war Britain's treatment of Jewish refugees turned the Zionist movement against it. With the Holocaust fresh in the world's mind, Britain refused to admit 100,000 refugees as the Anglo-American Committee recommended in April 1946, and it turned back refugee ships, so that in 1947 about 4,500 survivors on the Exodus were sent back to Europe, a scene that drew worldwide condemnation. The Irgun under Menachem Begin and Lehi attacked British targets, most notoriously in the bombing of the King David Hotel on 22 July 1946, which killed 91 people. With about 100,000 troops tied down in Palestine, Britain was now a target for Jewish militants and a source of resentment for Arabs, which shows how its own policy fuelled the violence.\n\nBritain's exit in 1947-48 deepened the conflict further. Unable to find a settlement, it referred the problem to the United Nations in February 1947, and the General Assembly voted for partition on 29 November 1947 by 33 votes to 13. Britain abstained, refused to implement partition itself and announced that it would withdraw by 15 May 1948. Fighting between Arabs and Jews had already begun in December 1947, so Britain left no authority to keep order. The Mandate ended on 14 May 1948, the day Ben-Gurion proclaimed Israel, and the Arab states invaded the next day. The war produced about 700,000 Palestinian refugees and an unresolved conflict, so Britain's withdrawal contributed directly to the origins of the Arab-Israeli conflict, though Arab rejection of partition and atrocities on both sides, such as Deir Yassin, show that others shared the blame.\n\nBritain's efforts to keep influence in Egypt created fresh tension in the 1950s. After the Free Officers overthrew King Farouk in 1952, the Anglo-Egyptian Agreement of 1954 provided for the withdrawal of British troops from the canal base, completed in June 1956. Yet Britain also helped form the Baghdad Pact of 1955 with Iraq, Turkey, Iran and Pakistan, which Nasser saw as an attempt to isolate Egypt, and he bought arms from the Soviet bloc through Czechoslovakia, drawing the Cold War into the region. When the United States and Britain withdrew their offer to fund the Aswan High Dam on 19 July 1956, Nasser nationalised the Suez Canal Company on 26 July. By the secret Protocol of Sèvres of 24 October, Britain and France agreed with Israel that Israel would invade Sinai and that they would then intervene as supposed peacekeepers. Israel attacked on 29 October and British and French paratroops landed at Port Said on 5 November. This collusion united Arab opinion behind Nasser, and the United States forced a ceasefire by refusing support while the pound came under pressure. Eden resigned in January 1957, Britain's standing in the Arab world collapsed, and the Hashemite monarchy in Iraq, its closest Arab ally, was overthrown in 1958.\n\nOverall, Britain contributed greatly to Middle East tensions in these years through its contradictory promises in Palestine, a refugee policy that provoked Jewish violence, a hasty withdrawal and the reckless adventure at Suez. But the deeper cause of the Arab-Israeli conflict was the clash of Jewish and Arab nationalisms, which Britain did not create, and Nasser's ambitions and Cold War rivalry also drove events. Britain therefore made a bad situation much worse rather than being its sole cause.",
