    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Britain managed race relations better than France between the 1950s and the 1980s. Neither country avoided serious racism and urban violence, but Britain built a legal framework against discrimination, accepted that its society was becoming multi-racial and answered disorder with a public inquiry, while France relied on an assimilation model that ignored ethnic difference and left the problems of the banlieues largely unaddressed. Britain's record was far from perfect, but it was the stronger of the two.\n\nThe starting points were similar, since both countries recruited migrants from former colonies to fill labour shortages after 1945. In Britain the Nationality Act of 1948 gave Commonwealth citizens the right to settle, the Empire Windrush docked in June 1948 with about 1,000 Caribbean passengers, and workers were recruited for the NHS and London Transport. France recruited from Portugal, Spain, Italy, Morocco, Tunisia and West Africa, and Algerians moved freely until 1962. Both groups met discrimination: Caribbean migrants faced a colour bar in housing and exploiters like Rachman, while many migrants in France lived in bidonvilles such as Nanterre. Both states also restricted immigration, Britain in the Acts of 1962, 1968 and 1971 and France by suspending labour immigration in 1974.\n\nBritain's strongest advantage was its anti-discrimination legislation. After the Notting Hill and Nottingham violence of 1958 and the murder of Kelso Cochrane in 1959, Parliament passed the Race Relations Act of 1965, which banned discrimination in public places and created a Race Relations Board. The Act of 1968 extended the ban to housing and employment, and that of 1976 covered indirect discrimination and set up the Commission for Racial Equality. Roy Jenkins described the aim in 1966 as integration, meaning equal opportunity with cultural diversity rather than assimilation. These laws were hard to enforce, but they gave migrants a legal means to challenge unfair treatment. France had only the Pleven law of 1972 against incitement to racial hatred, and it collected no statistics on ethnic origin, which made discrimination harder to measure and debate.\n\nBritain also accepted a multi-racial society more openly. Multiculturalism allowed communities to build mosques, gurdwaras and temples, Notting Hill Carnival, begun in 1966, became one of Europe's largest festivals, and Diane Abbott, Paul Boateng, Bernie Grant and Keith Vaz entered Parliament in 1987. Commonwealth migrants had the vote from arrival. France's model of universal citizenship and laïcité treated citizens as individuals and expected migrants to keep their identities private. This promised equality in theory, but it left the second generation, the beurs, with high unemployment and poor housing in the banlieues, non-citizen residents had no vote, and Mitterrand's promise of local voting rights was never kept. The headscarf affair at Creil in 1989 showed how rigid laïcité clashed with Muslim identity.\n\nThe handling of disorder points the same way. Britain had serious failures: heavy use of the sus law and Operation Swamp 81 led to the Brixton riots of April 1981, followed by Toxteth and Moss Side. Yet the government commissioned the Scarman Report of November 1981, which identified racial disadvantage, unemployment and poor policing as causes and led to the Police and Criminal Evidence Act of 1984. In France the Minguettes unrest of 1981 brought largely social programmes such as Priority Education Zones and Banlieues 89, criticised as too limited, and the police killing of Malik Oussekine in December 1986 deepened bitterness. French migrants had to organise themselves, as in the March for Equality and against Racism of 1983, which reached Paris on 3 December with tens of thousands of supporters.\n\nThe far right was a danger in both countries but was far more successful in France. The British National Front, founded in 1967, never won a parliamentary seat and was met by the Anti-Nazi League and Rock Against Racism. Le Pen's Front National broke through at Dreux in 1983, won about 11 per cent in the European elections of 1984, took 35 seats in 1986 and won about 14.4 per cent in the presidential election of 1988. The Pasqua laws of 1986 made expulsion easier, and the Chirac government tried to end automatic nationality for French-born children of foreign parents before dropping the plan after mass protests. French anti-racism did have achievements, notably SOS Racisme, founded in 1984, and the regularisation of about 130,000 migrants in 1981-82.\n\nOn balance, Britain managed race relations better. Powell's speech, the National Front and the 1981 riots count against it, but Britain gave legal protection against discrimination, recognised diversity and investigated its failures, whereas France's colour-blind model hid inequality and the far right gained far more ground there.",
