    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Nadine Gordimer and Chinua Achebe made a major contribution to post-colonial literature by writing in English about the realities of colonial and post-colonial societies. Achebe gave Africans a voice in the world's literature and showed that English could carry African experience, while Gordimer used her fiction to dissect apartheid and the limits of white liberalism. Both were widely read abroad, and both changed how Africa was seen.\n\nAchebe's greatest contribution was Things Fall Apart, published by Heinemann in 1958. Born in 1930 at Ogidi in eastern Nigeria and educated at University College Ibadan, he told the story of Okonkwo to portray an Igbo community before and during the arrival of British rule. European writers had usually shown Africans as primitive and without culture. Achebe showed a society with its own laws, religion and values, which was then undermined by missionaries and colonial administrators. The title, taken from a line by W. B. Yeats, signalled the collapse of that world. The book became one of the most widely read African novels in English and is now studied as a foundational text of post-colonial literature, answering the colonial story with an African one.\n\nAchebe also looked critically at independent Africa, not only at the colonisers. No Longer at Ease (1960), Arrow of God (1964) and A Man of the People (1966) examined corruption and the failures of the new states, and Anthills of the Savannah (1987) dealt with military rule in a fictional West African state. Post-colonial literature therefore did not simply blame the past; it held the new elites to account. During the Biafran War he supported Biafra and travelled abroad to plead its cause, later writing about it in There Was a Country in 2012.\n\nHis contribution went beyond his own novels. As the first editorial adviser to Heinemann's African Writers Series from 1962, he helped make African novels widely available in schools and universities across the English-speaking world and helped to publish many other writers. He also took part in the debate about language. Critics such as Obi Wali argued that African literature in European languages was a dead end, and Ngugi wa Thiong'o later argued in Decolonising the Mind that European languages cut writers off from peasants and workers. Achebe replied in 1965 that English could be remade to carry African experience. In 1975 he attacked Joseph Conrad's Heart of Darkness for portraying Africa as the opposite of Europe, a charge still debated.\n\nNadine Gordimer made a different contribution from a different position. Born in 1923 at Springs near Johannesburg, she was a white South African who wrote in English and lived in Johannesburg. Her novels, from The Lying Days (1953) and A World of Strangers (1958), explored the divisions between black and white South Africans and the moral compromises of life under apartheid. The state treated her work as dangerous: A World of Strangers was banned in South Africa for twelve years, and Burger's Daughter (1979), about the child of a communist activist, was banned for a time. Her career is therefore also a record of how censorship tried to silence criticism of the regime.\n\nHer most striking work imagined the end of apartheid. July's People (1981) pictured a revolution in which a white family takes refuge in a village with their black servant, and the power relationship between them is reversed. It was widely read internationally and made foreign readers think about what apartheid had created. The Conservationist (1974) shared the Booker Prize, and My Son's Story (1990) explored the politics of the apartheid years through a family's divided loyalties. She supported the African National Congress and helped Nelson Mandela with his speech for the Rivonia trial in 1964. She won the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1991 and continued to oppose censorship after apartheid ended, although some black writers argued that white liberal writers had a limited perspective.\n\nOverall, the two writers contributed in complementary ways. Achebe restored African dignity and proved that African writers could use English on their own terms, while Gordimer showed the human cost of racial rule and defied the censors. Achebe's influence on the language debate and on African publishing was probably the more far-reaching, but Gordimer's Nobel Prize and political courage gave South African literature a global audience.",
