    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The American economy between 1945 and 1989 had remarkable strengths, especially in the long boom up to the late 1960s, but it also had serious weaknesses that became obvious from the late 1960s onwards. Its strengths lay in industrial power, consumer demand and technology, while its weaknesses were inflation, trade deficits, industrial decline, deficits and persistent poverty.\n\nThe first great strength was the position the United States held at the end of the Second World War. Its factories had expanded during the war while those of Europe and Japan were devastated, and it produced roughly half of the world's manufactured goods in 1945. Wartime savings and the end of rationing released a huge pent-up demand for cars, houses and appliances. The GI Bill of 1944 paid for education and gave loans to veterans, which created a mass market for housing. The Bretton Woods system made the dollar the central world currency, and the Marshall Plan, about 13 billion dollars to Western Europe between 1948 and 1952, was largely spent on American goods, which boosted exports. This gave American business a secure base from which to grow.\n\nGovernment spending was a second strength. The Korean War and NSC-68 raised defence spending from about 14 billion dollars in 1950 to about 50 billion by 1953, and the Cold War kept it high, giving steady contracts to firms such as Lockheed and Boeing and to electronics and computing, which helped to build Silicon Valley. The Federal-Aid Highway Act of 1956 funded about 41,000 miles of interstate highway, stimulating car, steel and construction industries and the growth of the suburbs. After Sputnik, NASA and the National Defense Education Act funded space and science. Eisenhower himself warned of the military-industrial complex in 1961, which shows how dependent the economy had become on such spending.\n\nThe result was a wave of affluence for the majority in the 1950s and 1960s. Wages rose, ownership of cars, televisions and suburban homes spread, and large multinational corporations expanded abroad. Large corporations employed a growing white-collar workforce, which William Whyte described in The Organization Man in 1956. This was the best record of growth in American history, and it brought a standard of living far above that of any other country.\n\nThe weaknesses began to show in the later 1960s. Johnson chose to pay for both the Vietnam War and the Great Society without raising taxes until the surtax of 1968, so spending outran output and inflation rose from about 1 per cent in 1964 to over 4 per cent in 1968. The balance of payments worsened as dollars flowed abroad. In August 1971 Nixon suspended the convertibility of the dollar into gold, ending Bretton Woods, and 1971 was the first year since 1893 in which the United States imported more than it exported. The OPEC oil embargo of October 1973 quadrupled oil prices, and the recession of 1973 to 1975, the deepest since the 1930s, brought unemployment to about 9 per cent. Inflation and stagnation together, stagflation, passed 13 per cent in 1980 after a second oil shock. Ford's Whip Inflation Now campaign and Carter's measures failed to cure it.\n\nAmerican industry also lost its lead. Japanese and European producers took markets in steel and cars: the Youngstown Sheet and Tube mill in Ohio closed in 1977, losing about 5,000 jobs, and small, fuel-efficient Japanese cars took about a quarter of the American market by the early 1980s, while Japan overtook the United States as the largest car producer in 1980. Chrysler needed a federal rescue in 1979. The old manufacturing cities of the rust belt, such as Detroit and Cleveland, lost jobs and population. Behind the affluence, too, lay poverty: Michael Harrington's The Other America in 1962 showed tens of millions living in poverty, mostly unseen by the affluent majority, and urban decay in the inner cities was linked to riots in the 1960s.\n\nReagan's policies had mixed results. Tax cuts, deregulation and the tight money policy of Paul Volcker brought inflation down and growth returned strongly from 1983, with new service and high-technology jobs. But the 1981 to 1982 recession pushed unemployment above 10 per cent, the highest since the Depression, and tax cuts combined with high defence spending produced huge deficits. The national debt nearly tripled to almost 3 trillion dollars, the United States became a net debtor in 1985, and the trade deficit reached about 150 billion dollars in 1987. The stock market crash of October 1987, when the Dow fell by about 22 per cent in a day, showed how fragile the recovery was.\n\nOverall, the economy was much stronger than any rival's until the late 1960s, when its strengths rested on the unusual conditions of 1945, and it then showed real weaknesses. By 1989 it was still the largest in the world, but it was heavily in debt, less competitive in manufacturing and unequal in its rewards.",
