    "label": "C1",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Conn and Patricia McCluskey set up the Dungannon Homeless Citizens League in 1963 and founded the Campaign for Social Justice in Dungannon in January 1964. Conn, a local doctor, gathered evidence of discrimination in housing allocation and local jobs, especially by councils in Tyrone and Fermanagh. The campaign published its findings and lobbied British MPs. This lobbying helped to lead to the Campaign for Democracy in Ulster being formed at Westminster in 1965. Their work provided models and evidence for the civil rights movement that followed, including the Northern Ireland Civil Rights Association founded in January 1967. Historians credit the Campaign for Social Justice with turning discrimination into a public issue before NICRA was formed.",
