    "label": "C3",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "The Lockwood Committee, appointed in 1963 to advise on the new university, reported in February 1965 and recommended Coleraine. The government argued that Coleraine offered a central site, good access and room for expansion, and that Derry's case had been fully considered. Derry had the larger population, the existing Magee College buildings and high unemployment, and its supporters argued that these justified a university in the city. The new university was planned as a single institution, so choosing one town meant that no other town could host it. Stormont accepted the recommendation in 1965, with the decision carried by a unionist majority. Derry's nationalists saw the choice as proof of discrimination, though the 'faceless men' allegation was never backed by proof.",
