    "model": "Margaret Thatcher refused political status to the hunger strikers in 1981, saying that crime is crime is crime. In November 1984 she rejected all three options put forward by the New Ireland Forum, saying 'Out, out, out'. She signed the Anglo-Irish Agreement with Garret FitzGerald at Hillsborough Castle on 15 November 1985, giving the Irish government a formal consultative role. John Hume became leader of the SDLP in 1979 and supported that Agreement as giving nationalists a role in Northern Ireland's government. From 1988 he began a dialogue with Gerry Adams, arguing that the IRA campaign must end, and he took part in the Brooke and Mayhew talks of 1991 and 1992. His work with Adams and the Irish government influenced the Downing Street Declaration of 1993.",
