    "label": "B1",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "Before the 1960s the state left most second-level education to the churches and to parents who could pay, and most children left school at 14. The 1965 report Investment in Education, commissioned by Patrick Hillery, showed wide differences in participation between social classes and regions. Donogh O'Malley announced free second-level education in September 1966, and the scheme began in September 1967 with free school transport. Comprehensive schools, first opened in 1966, offered academic and practical subjects to pupils of all abilities. The school-leaving age was raised from 14 to 15 in 1972.",
