    "label": "C1",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Bismarck's foreign policy was largely successful, since Germany fought no war after 1871 and he avoided a two-front war while he was in office. He kept France without allies by keeping the other powers friendly or at odds with each other. The Dual Alliance of 1879 with Austria-Hungary guarded against Russia, and the Triple Alliance of 1882 added Italy, which was aimed chiefly at France. At the Congress of Berlin in 1878, Bismarck acted as an honest broker and avoided a European war, although Russia felt cheated and the Three Emperors' League was weakened. The secret Reinsurance Treaty of 1887 kept Russia friendly, but it was not renewed in 1890 and Russia turned towards France. The system was complicated and depended on Bismarck personally, so it was not a lasting success.",
