    "label": "C2",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Nikolaus Otto built the first practical four-stroke engine in 1876, burning gas, and it became the basis of the petrol engine. Gottlieb Daimler and Wilhelm Maybach developed a small, light, high-speed petrol engine in the 1880s and fitted it to a motorcycle in 1885. Karl Benz built a three-wheeled Motorwagen with a single-cylinder engine in 1885, which he patented in 1886 and which is regarded as the first practical car designed as a whole. In August 1888 Bertha Benz drove it about 106 km from Mannheim to Pforzheim, proving that the car was useful. France led early production, with Panhard et Levassor and Peugeot building cars from 1890-91 using Daimler engines. Ford's Model T of 1908 and the moving assembly line of 1913 then made the car a mass product, bringing the price down to under 300 dollars by the early 1920s.",
