    "label": "C1",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Soviet willingness to use force was decisive, because Moscow regarded Hungary's withdrawal from the Warsaw Pact as unacceptable to its security. Nagy declared neutrality and announced Hungary's withdrawal from the Warsaw Pact on 1 November 1956, which crossed the line that Soviet leaders would tolerate. Soviet forces returned in strength on 4 November 1956, crushed the fighting in Budapest and removed Nagy's government. The uprising had no organised armed leadership beyond local groups, and talks with Soviet commanders failed to produce a lasting arrangement. The West did not intervene: the Suez crisis drew attention away, the Soviet Union vetoed UN Security Council action, and no Western government sent troops, despite the hopes raised by Radio Free Europe. Kadar, backed by Soviet troops, formed a rival government, and Nagy was later executed in June 1958.",
