    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Gorbachev became General Secretary in March 1985 and first tried to speed up economic growth with his slogan of uskorenie, which failed. He then introduced perestroika, a restructuring of the economy that mixed market elements with planning, and glasnost, greater openness about the past. The Law on State Enterprise of 1987 gave factories more freedom, and the 1988 law on cooperatives allowed private business, but these weakened central control without creating a working market. Falling oil prices in 1986 cut Soviet hard currency earnings, and by 1989 to 1990 the economy faced shortages, a growing budget deficit and falling output. Political reform brought partly free elections in March 1989, and in 1990 the Communist Party gave up its constitutional monopoly of power. Rising nationalism in the republics, which the failed August 1991 coup accelerated, led to the dissolution of the Soviet Union on 26 December 1991.",
