    "label": "C1",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "The Second World War left Britain financially exhausted, and it owed India over one billion pounds in sterling balances, so the Raj had lost much of its economic value. Labour's government of 1945 was committed to Indian self-rule, and Attlee announced in February 1947 that British rule would end by June 1948 at the latest. Decades of nationalist agitation, from non-cooperation to Quit India, made British rule impossible to keep without a repression the British public would not accept. The INA trials and the 1946 naval mutiny led officials such as Wavell to doubt that the Indian Army could suppress a mass rising. The British withdrawal led to the partition of India, which caused communal violence in the Punjab and Bengal and mass migration across the new border. India became independent on 15 August 1947 and a republic on 26 January 1950, with Nehru as prime minister.",
