    "label": "C2",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "The Women's Political Council and students ran off leaflets calling for a one-day bus boycott on 5 December 1955, after Rosa Parks was arrested on 1 December for refusing to give up her seat. The Montgomery Improvement Association, with Martin Luther King Jr. as president, ran a car pool of volunteer cars and black taxi drivers who charged the bus fare of ten cents. Thousands of black riders walked to work, and churches raised money to buy station wagons for the car pool, so the boycott hit the bus company's income hard. The city responded with arrests, a bomb on King's porch on 30 January 1956, and the indictment of 89 leaders under a 1921 state law. The federal case Browder v Gayle was upheld by the Supreme Court on 13 November 1956, and the buses were desegregated on 21 December 1956. The boycott was successful because it ended bus segregation after 381 days, but segregation remained in schools, housing and voting, and violence followed.",
