    "marks": 40,
    "model": "The war was limited, because the United States avoided invading North Vietnam or using nuclear weapons, which limited the pressure on Hanoi. North Vietnam and the Viet Cong could use bases in Laos and Cambodia that the US was reluctant to attack, so they could withdraw and rebuild. The South Vietnamese government was weak and corrupt, and the South Vietnamese army could not hold the territory cleared by American forces. Westmoreland's attrition strategy and body count measure of success were misleading, because the communists could replace their losses and choose when to fight. The Tet Offensive of January 1968 shattered American optimism and deepened the credibility gap between official statements and events. Johnson's gradual escalation kept options open while costs and divisions at home grew, and he played the war down in public to protect the Great Society.",
