    "model": "Fenianism had real strengths as a political movement: it was well organised, drew support on both sides of the Atlantic and kept the republican idea alive. Its weaknesses were equally serious, since it was riddled with informers, divided in its leadership, condemned by the Catholic Church and militarily helpless when it finally rose in 1867. On balance it failed in its own aim of a republic, but it succeeded in shaping later Irish politics.\n\nThe first strength was the scale and discipline of the organisation. The Irish Republican Brotherhood was founded in Dublin on 17 March 1858 by James Stephens as an oath-bound secret society, organised in circles under local leaders who reported to Stephens as Chief Organiser of the Irish Republic. In New York in the same year John O'Mahony founded the Fenian Brotherhood, which raised money and men in the United States. A transatlantic movement of this kind was something no earlier Irish revolutionary body had achieved, and it gave Fenianism resources and an exile base that the Young Irelanders of 1848 had lacked.\n\nA second strength lay in its roots and its appeal. Fenianism drew on Famine bitterness, on the memory of the failed 1848 rising and on disillusion with parliamentary politics after the Independent Irish Party collapsed when Keogh and Sadleir took office in 1852. It recruited artisans, clerks, shopkeepers and farmers' sons, and even soldiers in the British army. It also built public strength: the funeral of Terence Bellew MacManus in Dublin in 1861 was a large show of support, and the newspaper the Irish People, founded in 1863 under Luby, O'Leary and Kickham, spread the message. American Fenians who were Civil War veterans raised hopes of trained military leadership.\n\nThe movement's weaknesses began with security. The government was well informed by spies and informers, and in September 1865 police raided the Irish People offices and arrested Luby, O'Leary, Rossa and Kickham. Stephens was arrested in November and escaped from Richmond Prison, but habeas corpus was suspended in February 1866, allowing arrest without trial. The organisation was crushed before it could act, and the leadership was in prison or in exile at the decisive moment.\n\nLeadership and division were a further weakness. Stephens was gifted but autocratic and secretive, and he promised a rising in 1865 and again in 1866 without delivering. In December 1866 the American Fenian leaders removed him. The split between the Irish and American wings undermined confidence, and the movement depended on exile money and exile officers that it could not fully control.\n\nThe rising of March 1867 exposed the military weakness. It took place in bad weather, with small outbreaks at Tallaght, in Cork, Limerick and Tipperary, which were quickly crushed because informers had compromised the plans and the Fenians lacked arms and trained leaders. American raids into Canada, at Ridgeway in 1866 and again in 1870, also failed to win any territory. Opposition from the Church compounded this, since Cardinal Cullen and most of the bishops condemned Fenianism as a secret society, and Cullen refused to let MacManus's body lie in the Pro-Cathedral. This cut the movement off from the Catholic majority.\n\nYet the Fenians achieved more in defeat than their military record suggests. The execution of the Manchester Martyrs, Allen, Larkin and O'Brien, in November 1867 after a police sergeant was killed in the rescue of two prisoners, was mourned across Ireland and gave the movement martyrs. The Clerkenwell explosion in December 1867 turned British opinion against the Fenians, but it also convinced Gladstone that Irish discontent threatened Britain. He declared his mission was to pacify Ireland and passed the Church Act of 1869 and the Land Act of 1870, and Fenian prisoners were released between 1869 and 1871.\n\nOverall, Fenianism was a strong organisation but a weak political movement in the short term. It was betrayed by informers, divided at the top and opposed by the Church, and its rising was a failure. But it built the first lasting physical force republican organisation, kept republicanism alive and influenced British reform, and its legacy reached as far as 1916.",
