    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The period from 1815 to 1870 saw significant developments in both the creative arts and science in Ireland, even though it was also the age of the Famine. Writers, musicians and antiquarians drew on Irish history and folklore and helped shape a national culture, while Irish scientists such as Hamilton, Parsons and Boole won international recognition. The Famine and emigration damaged the rural culture behind much of this work, but achievement was real.\n\nIn literature, Thomas Moore's Irish Melodies, published in numbers from 1808 to 1834, set Irish airs to words and were popular in Britain, Ireland and America, giving Irish music an international audience. William Carleton, born in Tyrone in 1794, wrote Traits and Stories of the Irish Peasantry in 1830 and a second series in 1833, describing wakes, pilgrimages, faction fights and hedge schools. His novel Fardorougha the Miser appeared in 1839 and The Black Prophet, a novel of famine, in 1847. Carleton is valued as a realist who showed the violence and poverty of rural life as well as its humour.\n\nThe Nation, founded in 1842 by Thomas Davis, Charles Gavan Duffy and John Blake Dillon, used poems, ballads and history to teach a cultural idea of the nation as a community of shared history, which could include Catholic, Protestant and Dissenter. Davis wrote ballads such as A Nation Once Again and edited the Library of Ireland series before dying of scarlet fever in 1845, aged 30. James Clarence Mangan contributed poems to the paper, including Dark Rosaleen in 1846, adapted from the Irish, and died in 1849 in the cholera of the Famine years. Charles Kickham later wrote Knocknagow and Sally Cavanagh, which dealt with the eviction of a tenant family.\n\nAntiquarian scholarship was another strong development. George Petrie, a painter and antiquarian, showed in an essay of 1845 that the round towers were Christian in origin and collected traditional music in The Ancient Music of Ireland in 1855. The Ordnance Survey of the 1830s, directed by Thomas Larcom, employed John O'Donovan and Eugene O'Curry to record place names and study manuscripts. O'Donovan edited the Annals of the Four Masters between 1848 and 1851, and O'Curry became professor of Irish history and archaeology at the Catholic University. The Royal Irish Academy and the Royal Dublin Society supported this work. These developments recovered Ireland's past, although the Irish language itself declined, with the 1851 census finding only about a quarter of the population speaking it.\n\nIn science the achievement was outstanding. William Rowan Hamilton, Royal Astronomer of Ireland at Dunsink, discovered quaternions on 16 October 1843 while walking by the Royal Canal at Broom Bridge, a breakthrough in mathematics. William Parsons, third Earl of Rosse, completed the six-foot reflecting telescope at Birr Castle in 1845, known as the Leviathan of Parsonstown and then the largest in the world, and with it he observed the spiral structure of the galaxy now known as M51, the Whirlpool Galaxy.\n\nOther developments followed. Nicholas Callan, a priest and professor at Maynooth, built an early induction coil in the 1830s and developed powerful batteries. George Boole became the first professor of mathematics at Queen's College Cork in 1849 and published The Laws of Thought in 1854, the basis of Boolean algebra. Robert Kane, first president of Queen's College Cork, wrote The Industrial Resources of Ireland in 1844, urging the use of science for economic development. The Queen's Colleges, the Royal Dublin Society and the Royal Irish Academy supported such work, giving it an institutional base.\n\nOverall, the period was one of real creative and scientific achievement. Moore, Carleton, Petrie, O'Donovan, Hamilton, Rosse and Boole all won recognition beyond Ireland, and cultural nationalism gave the arts a political purpose. The limits were the Famine and emigration, which weakened the rural culture and language that the nationalists wished to preserve, but the best of the work endured.",
