    "label": "1",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The Home Rule movement developed from a small, respectable, constitutional body under Isaac Butt into a disciplined mass party under Charles Stewart Parnell. Butt founded the movement and won it a place in Irish politics, but it was Parnell who gave it organisation, a mass base and real power at Westminster by 1886.\n\nUnder Butt the movement began with the Home Government Association, founded in Dublin in May 1870 and bringing together Protestant Conservatives, Liberals, Repealers and Catholic moderates. Butt, a Protestant barrister who had defended Young Irelanders and Fenian prisoners, favoured a federal solution, with an Irish parliament for domestic affairs while Westminster kept control of the empire, defence and foreign policy. His success at the Limerick by-election of 1871 showed that Home Rule could win Catholic votes, and the Ballot Act of 1872 freed voters from landlord pressure. In November 1873 the Association became the Home Rule League, which committed its MPs to sit as an independent party.\n\nThe high point of Butt's leadership came at the 1874 general election, when about 60 of the 103 Irish seats went to Home Rulers. This made them a significant bloc, yet the movement did not develop as Butt hoped. Many of the MPs were Liberals who used the label for local reasons and there was no pledge or whip to bind them. Butt's motions were rejected by large majorities, since neither Disraeli's Conservatives nor the Liberals took polite argument seriously. The party also had little money and almost no organisation among tenant farmers, so it remained largely a body of MPs and lawyers. Butt, burdened by debt and ill health, died in May 1879, but his control had already slipped.\n\nThe movement changed character through obstruction. Joseph Biggar, MP for Cavan, began to jam Commons business with long speeches and endless amendments, and Parnell, who entered parliament for Meath in 1875, joined him from 1877. Parnell made his break with Butt's caution clear when he declared that he did not believe murder had been committed at Manchester, which won Fenian respect, and in August 1877 he replaced Butt as president of the Home Rule Confederation of Great Britain. Obstruction forced the House to notice Irish MPs, and in 1881 a sitting of about 41 hours against a coercion bill ended with the Speaker intervening and the Commons later adopting the closure rule.\n\nParnell's next step was to link Home Rule to the land question and to Fenian support through the New Departure of 1878 to 1879. Michael Davitt, released from Dartmoor in 1877, met John Devoy of Clan na Gael, and in October 1878 Devoy offered Fenian support if Parnell would press self-government, peasant ownership and independent parliamentary action. Parnell never formally accepted, but he moved towards the alliance with Davitt, and the Irish National Land League, founded in October 1879 with Parnell as president, turned the agricultural depression into a mass movement. His American tour with John Dillon in early 1880 brought money and publicity, and in May 1880 he became chairman of the Irish Parliamentary Party.\n\nBetween 1880 and 1885 Parnell built the party into a machine. His imprisonment in Kilmainham in October 1881 and the understanding reached with Gladstone in 1882, before the Phoenix Park murders on 6 May nearly wrecked it, showed that he could bargain with the government. After the Land League was suppressed, the Irish National League of October 1882 put Home Rule first and gave the party branches in nearly every parish, with priests often as officers. United Ireland provided a party newspaper, and in 1884 candidates had to sign a pledge to sit, act and vote with the party, chosen at conventions, which ended the independence of the old Home Rule MPs.\n\nThe results came at the general election of 1885, when Parnell's party won 86 seats and held the balance of power in the Commons. Gladstone's conversion to Home Rule followed, and the first Home Rule Bill was introduced in 1886. It was defeated in June when about ninety Liberals voted against it, and the Liberal government fell, but Home Rule was now the central issue of British politics.\n\nIn conclusion, Butt created the movement and made Home Rule respectable, but he lacked discipline, money and a mass following, and won no concession. Parnell, by combining obstruction, the land agitation, the Fenians, American funds and a pledge-bound party, made it a force that governments could not ignore. Butt prepared the ground and Parnell harvested it.",
