    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Land reform and the co-operative movement were both of great importance in Irish social and economic history between 1870 and 1914. Land reform transformed who owned the soil of Ireland and ended the landlord class as a political force, while the co-operative movement improved the way Irish farmers produced and sold their goods. One changed ownership, the other changed methods, and together they reshaped rural Ireland.\n\nThe importance of land reform began with the grievances of the tenants. Most farmers rented their holdings, often without a lease, from a small landlord class that was frequently Protestant and sometimes absentee. They demanded the Three Fs: fair rent, fixity of tenure and free sale. Gladstone's Land Act of 1870 gave legal force to the Ulster Custom and offered compensation for disturbance and improvements, but it did not fix rents, so landlords could still raise rents and then evict for non-payment. The Act's failure to give security of tenure was the main reason the land question exploded again a few years later.\n\nThe agitation of the Land War showed the power of mass organisation. Bad weather, poor harvests and cheap American grain from 1877 brought falling prices, rising arrears and a sharp increase in evictions. Michael Davitt, the son of an evicted Mayo tenant, organised the meeting at Irishtown in April 1879, and the Irish National Land League was founded in Dublin in October 1879 with Parnell as president. At Ennis in 1880 Parnell urged that anyone who took an evicted farm be shunned, and the tactic became known as boycotting after Captain Boycott, the agent at Lough Mask. The Ladies' Land League under Anna Parnell kept the campaign going when the men were jailed under Forster's coercion act. The movement was important because it united small farmers behind a single cause and made Parnell's party a national force.\n\nThe agitation won the 1881 Land Act, which granted the Three Fs. Fair rents were set by land courts for fifteen years, and the first judicial rents reduced rents by about one fifth. The landlord kept ownership but shared control with the tenant in what became known as dual ownership. Tenants in arrears were helped by the Arrears Act of 1882. The Act was a landmark because the state had accepted that the landlord's power over the tenant should be limited by law, and it was passed in response to pressure from below.\n\nThe next stage was the transfer of ownership itself. Conservative governments pursued constructive unionism, hoping to kill Home Rule with kindness. The Ashbourne Act of 1885 advanced the full purchase price to tenants, the Congested Districts Board was set up in 1891 to help the poor west, and after the Land Conference of 1902 the Wyndham Act of 1903 offered loans repayable over about 68 years together with a bonus of 12 per cent to landlords who sold. More than 200,000 tenants bought their farms within a few years, and the Birrell Act of 1909 allowed compulsory purchase. By 1914 landlordism had effectively ended, and Ireland was becoming a country of owner-occupiers. Labourers and small western farmers gained least, which limited the achievement.\n\nThe co-operative movement was important in a different way, because it tackled the weakness of Irish farming itself. Horace Plunkett, an Anglo-Irish landowner who returned from ranching in Wyoming, saw that Irish farmers suffered from poor marketing, dependence on shopkeepers and moneylenders, and competition from better-made Danish butter. He promoted farmer-owned co-operative creameries, the first of which is usually dated to 1889 at Dromcollogher, Co. Limerick. Farmers pooled their milk, used mechanical separators and were paid by the quality of the milk, which raised the standard of Irish butter and increased incomes.\n\nPlunkett organised the movement through the Irish Agricultural Organisation Society, founded in 1894 with the motto better farming, better business, better living. Credit societies on the German Raiffeisen model lent small sums on the guarantee of neighbours and reduced dependence on gombeen men, and the Irish Agricultural Wholesale Society followed in 1897. His Recess Committee of 1895 brought unionists and nationalists together and led to the Department of Agriculture and Technical Instruction in 1899, with Plunkett as its first vice-president. By the First World War there were close to 1,000 societies with about 100,000 members. The movement had limits: it was strongest in Munster, nationalists distrusted a unionist leader, the clergy were irritated by his criticism in Ireland in the New Century, and the poorest western farmers gained little.\n\nOverall, land reform was the more important of the two, because it settled the greatest social grievance of the period, gave the tenant the ownership of his farm and weakened the base of the landed class. The co-operative movement was less dramatic but it taught farmers self-help and improved their living standards. Both showed that change in rural Ireland could come through organisation, whether through agitation and legislation or through voluntary co-operation.",
