    "model": "Between 1922 and 1939 de Valera achieved more in Anglo-Irish relations than Cosgrave. Cosgrave won real gains through patient work within the Commonwealth, but he left the main symbols and the Treaty ports in place. De Valera, building on Cosgrave's work, removed the oath, the Crown and the Governor-General, ended the land annuities dispute and recovered the ports, although at a heavy cost through the Economic War.\n\nCosgrave's governments accepted the Treaty and used the Commonwealth to widen Irish independence step by step. In 1924 the Free State registered the Treaty with the League of Nations as an international agreement, over British objections, which treated it as a matter between two states. The Boundary Commission crisis ended in the Tripartite Agreement of 3 December 1925, after the report was leaked to the Morning Post and Eoin MacNeill resigned. The border stayed as it was, but the Free State was released from its share of the imperial debt and the Council of Ireland was ended. This outcome was disappointing for nationalists, though the agreement did settle the question for the time being.\n\nCosgrave's greatest success came through the Imperial Conferences. At the conference of 1926 the Balfour Declaration described Britain and the dominions as autonomous communities, equal in status and freely associated, and Kevin O'Higgins was one of its principal advocates. This was followed by the Statute of Westminster in December 1931, which gave the dominions legislative independence and removed the British Parliament's power to override their laws. Cosgrave's patient method therefore produced the legal tools with which independence could be completed. Yet he accepted the oath, the Governor-General and the Treaty ports, and the Free State continued to pay the land annuities to the British Exchequer, so by 1932 many symbols of subordination remained.\n\nDe Valera used these tools quickly. He became President of the Executive Council in March 1932, dismissed the Governor-General James McNeill, and in May 1933 the Constitution (Removal of Oath) Act ended the oath of fidelity to the King. Appeals to the Privy Council were ended in 1933 and the Seanad was abolished in 1936. The abdication of Edward VIII gave him his chance: in December 1936 an amendment removed all references to the Crown and the Governor-General from the Constitution, and the Executive Authority (External Relations) Act left the King only a role in the appointment of diplomats. The 1937 constitution, Bunreacht na hÉireann, named the state Éire and declared it sovereign and independent. Britain accepted all this without military or economic action, which shows how far the Statute of Westminster had changed the position.\n\nThe annuities dispute was more costly. De Valera stopped paying the annuities of about 3 million pounds a year in 1932, arguing that they were owed to the Irish government, and Britain imposed duties on cattle, beef and butter. The Economic War damaged cattle farmers and lasted until 1938, and the Coal-Cattle Pact of 1935 gave only slight relief. But in April 1938 three agreements were signed in London. The financial agreement ended the dispute for a single payment of 10 million pounds, far below what Britain had claimed, the trade agreement removed the duties, and the defence agreement handed over the Treaty ports at Berehaven, Cobh and Lough Swilly. Churchill opposed this, but it ended the last military restriction of the Treaty and made neutrality in 1939 possible.\n\nOn balance, de Valera achieved more. Cosgrave deserves credit for the Balfour Declaration and the Statute of Westminster, but without de Valera's use of them the Free State would have remained in effect a dominion. De Valera removed the symbols, secured the ports and the financial settlement, and gave the state a constitution of its own. His failures were partition, which Britain would not discuss, and the price paid by farmers in the Economic War, but in terms of Anglo-Irish relations his achievement was clearly the greater.",
