    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The 1932 Eucharistic Congress and the state's language and education policies both did a great deal to shape Irish identity between 1922 and 1949. They gave it a clear shape, Gaelic and Catholic, and the state worked to make that shape the national norm. The identity they built was distinctive and widely supported, but it was also narrow, and it left Protestants, unionists and many urban people at its edges.\n\nThe revival of Irish was the most deliberate attempt to define the nation. The Free State made Irish compulsory in national schools in 1922, and many infant classes were taught wholly through it. A pass in Irish became necessary for State examinations at second level and for entry to the civil service and the Garda, so the language was tied to jobs. Ernest Blythe pressed the civil service to learn it, the Gaeltacht Commission of 1925-26 defined the Irish-speaking districts, and the 1937 constitution made Irish the first official language. The message was that to be truly Irish was to have the language of the nation, and that Irish identity was separate from British culture.\n\nThe state backed the language with cultural institutions. An Gúm was founded in 1926 to publish books and school texts in Irish, Taibhdhearc na Gaillimhe opened in 1928 as an Irish-language theatre, and preparatory colleges trained teachers who could teach through Irish. The Irish Folklore Commission of 1935 and the Schools' Collection of 1937-38, in which children gathered local folklore, tied ordinary families to the national tradition. Yet the results were mixed. The share of the population able to speak Irish rose from about 18 per cent in 1926 to about a quarter in 1936, but this was mostly the result of schooling. The Gaeltacht itself kept shrinking through emigration and poverty, and many parents and teachers resented compulsion. Irish worked better as a symbol of identity than as a daily language.\n\nEducation also carried the Catholic side of the identity. The Church managed most national schools through the local clergy, while the state paid the salaries and set the curriculum. Secondary schools were mostly fee-paying and run by religious orders. The School Attendance Act of 1926 made school compulsory from 6 to 14, so every child passed through a system that taught the language, history and religion together. The state accepted the Church's role rather than challenging it, and this made Catholic teaching part of what it meant to be Irish.\n\nThe Eucharistic Congress of 22-26 June 1932 was the greatest public expression of that Catholic identity. It marked the 1,500th anniversary of St Patrick's arrival in 432, and Cardinal Lauri came as the Pope's legate. About 100,000 children attended a Mass in the Phoenix Park on 25 June, and the closing Mass on 26 June drew a crowd often put at around a million, with John McCormack singing Panis Angelicus and Pius XI's message broadcast by radio. The Athlone transmitter, built for the occasion, carried the ceremonies across the country. De Valera, in power only since March, worked closely with Archbishop Byrne, and the occasion showed Church, state and nation moving together. It presented Ireland to the world as a devout, orderly and independent Catholic country ten years after the Civil War.\n\nThe Congress also showed how identity could exclude. Catholic pilgrims travelling north were attacked, and trains were stoned at Portadown, Lisburn and Belfast. Unionists took it as proof that the Free State was a Catholic state, which confirmed their case for partition. In the South, the same Catholic ethos appeared in the Censorship of Publications Act of 1929, the ban on contraceptives in 1935 and Article 41 of the 1937 constitution, which banned divorce, while Article 44 recognised the Church's special position. Protestants in the Free State declined in number and many felt excluded. Meanwhile the GAA's Rule 27 banned foreign games, and in 1938 it removed Douglas Hyde as patron for attending a soccer match, showing how strictly the Gaelic boundary was policed.\n\nOverall, both strands contributed a great deal to Irish identity, but in different ways. The language and education policies were the more sustained, because they reached every child, yet they were the less successful in practice, since Irish did not become the everyday language. The Eucharistic Congress was a single event, but it expressed the Catholic identity of the majority more powerfully than any policy. Together they created a confident Gaelic and Catholic national self-image, but one that critics such as Seán Ó Faoláin saw as narrow and conformist, and that deepened the gap between North and South.",
