    "model": "The Second World War, which the Southern government called the Emergency, affected both parts of Ireland deeply, but in opposite ways. In the South it brought neutrality, shortages and tight state control, while in Northern Ireland it brought war production, full employment and the terrible Belfast Blitz. The war confirmed the independence of the South and strengthened the place of the North within the United Kingdom, so it widened the political gap on the island.\n\nIn the South the most important effect was neutrality. The Dáil declared an emergency on 2 September 1939, and Ireland stayed out of the war. Neutrality showed that the state was sovereign, since a state that followed Britain into war would look like a dominion in practice. It was made possible by the return of the Treaty ports in 1938, and it had broad support, as it avoided reopening Civil War divisions and Ireland was in any case militarily weak. Britain's offer of Irish unity in principle, brought by Malcolm MacDonald in June 1940 in return for joining the Allies, was rejected by de Valera, as was the American Note of February 1944 asking him to expel the Axis diplomats. De Valera's reply to Churchill's victory broadcast in May 1945 was widely praised at home. In practice neutrality leaned towards the Allies, through shared intelligence and the weather report from Blacksod lighthouse in June 1944, and some 50,000 to 70,000 people from the Free State served in the British forces.\n\nThe war also changed daily life in the South. The Emergency Powers Act of September 1939 gave ministers wide powers, and censorship forbade reports on troop movements, ship sinkings and even the weather. The IRA was dealt with firmly: the 1940 Offences Against the State Amendment Act introduced internment, and hundreds were held at the Curragh. Shortages were severe because of shipping losses. Seán Lemass's Department of Supplies rationed tea, sugar, butter and fuel, compulsory tillage orders increased the wheat harvest, and turf replaced coal. The Wages Standstill Order of 1941 froze wages while prices rose, so living standards fell, and thousands of workers emigrated to jobs in Britain. The state also suffered bombing, most seriously at the North Strand in Dublin on 31 May 1941, when about thirty people were killed. So neutrality protected the South from invasion, but at a heavy price in hardship, isolation and lost freedom of expression.\n\nNorthern Ireland, as part of the United Kingdom, was at war from 3 September 1939. The war ended the mass unemployment that had marked the region since the 1920s. Harland and Wolff built and repaired warships and merchant ships, Short Brothers made Stirling bombers, and the linen mills and rope works made parachutes and uniforms, while farmers ploughed more land to feed Britain. Women entered the factories in large numbers, and wages rose. From January 1942 American troops arrived in Belfast, numbering well over 100,000 by 1944, and Northern Ireland became a base for the Atlantic convoys and for D-Day.\n\nThe Belfast Blitz was the most painful effect in the North. The government had assumed that the city was out of range, and shelters and anti-aircraft guns were few. The raid of 15-16 April 1941, Easter Tuesday, by around 180 bombers killed about 900 people, and the attack of 4-5 May killed about 150 more. In all about 1,000 people died and more than half the houses in the city were damaged. Up to 100,000 left the city each night to sleep in the countryside, and the evacuees revealed the poor housing and health of the slums. Fire brigades from Dublin, Dún Laoghaire, Dundalk and Drogheda crossed the border on de Valera's authorisation to help. The criticism of the unionist government's response contributed to the fall of Andrews in 1943, and Basil Brooke took over.\n\nThe war also had longer-term political effects in the North. It strengthened the link with Britain, and the promise of the Beveridge Report led to the Education Act of 1947 and the Health Services Act of 1948. Unionists contrasted their loyalty with the South's neutrality, which strengthened the case against a united Ireland. Yet the Unionist Party kept control and Catholic exclusion was unchanged, and Tom Williams's hanging in September 1942 deepened nationalist resentment.\n\nOn balance, the war's impact in the South was mainly political and economic hardship, offset by the achievement of keeping independence, while in the North it was an economic and social transformation that left politics largely unchanged. The two parts of the island ended the war further apart than they began it.",
