    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Irish missionaries in Africa and Asia between 1945 and 1966 were highly successful in building lasting institutions, above all schools, hospitals and a native clergy, and in winning converts in Nigeria. Their success was uneven, however. In China the Columban mission ended in expulsion, and even in Nigeria the work was marked by paternalism, close ties to the colonial state and religious rivalry.\n\nThe clearest success was the Holy Ghost mission in south-eastern Nigeria. The Spiritans had begun work at Onitsha in 1885, and Joseph Shanahan, a Tipperary man who arrived in 1902, made the school the main instrument of conversion by building village schools staffed by catechists. After 1945 this foundation was expanded dramatically. New parishes, schools, hospitals and training colleges opened across the region, and Irish priests, brothers and sisters arrived in growing numbers from Irish seminaries and novitiates. In 1950 the Catholic hierarchy was established in Nigeria, with the Irish Spiritan Charles Heerey as Archbishop of Onitsha and Joseph Whelan as first Bishop of Owerri. Catholic missions came to run a large share of the primary and secondary schools in the region, which shows how far the mission had become part of the fabric of Igbo society.\n\nEducation also produced a lasting result, namely an educated Nigerian class. Teacher training colleges prepared local teachers, and many of the Nigerians who later served in the new state had passed through mission schools. This gave the mission a lasting role in the country's elite, well beyond the numbers it baptised. When the regional government of Eastern Nigeria introduced free primary education in the 1950s, demand for schools rose sharply, though this also strained mission resources, so success brought its own pressures.\n\nMedical work was a second major success, and here Irish women were central. The Holy Rosary Sisters, founded by Shanahan in 1924 at Killeshandra, and the Medical Missionaries of Mary ran hospitals and dispensaries in rural areas where government services were few. Mary Martin, a Dublin nurse, founded the Medical Missionaries of Mary in Nigeria in 1937 after the Vatican in 1936 lifted the ban on sisters practising obstetrics and surgery. The order had its mother house at Drogheda and trained doctors, nurses and midwives. Martin's own recognition, the Freedom of the Borough of Drogheda in 1966, reflected how highly the work was valued in Ireland. Medical missionaries therefore improved health care in places that had little, and presented women with a leadership role hard to find at home.\n\nA further measure of success was the growth of a local church. The Catholic population of the region grew strongly, and local vocations increased. Michael Tansi, an Igbo, had been ordained in 1937, and in 1965 Francis Arinze became auxiliary Bishop of Onitsha at the age of 32. After Nigerian independence on 1 October 1960 leadership passed increasingly to Nigerian priests and bishops. Yet this localisation was slow, and critics said the Irish held leadership long after local people were qualified for it, which was paternalistic.\n\nThere were other limits. The mission depended on the colonial government's grant-in-aid for its schools, which tied it to inspection and state standards and sometimes brought disagreements over curriculum and religious teaching. Critics argued that mission education promoted Western and Christian values and weakened local culture and languages. Rivalry with Methodist and Anglican missionaries over schools and converts also divided communities.\n\nThe Columbans in Asia show the weaker side. Edward Galvin, a Cork priest, had founded the Maynooth Mission to China in 1916 and became Bishop of Hanyang in 1927. His mission ran churches, schools, orphanages and clinics, but few Chinese priests were ordained before the 1940s. After the Communist takeover of 1949 the missionaries were treated as agents of foreign influence, and Galvin was placed under house arrest, tried and expelled in 1952. He died at Dalgan Park in 1956. In China, therefore, the work was brought to an end abruptly by political events outside missionary control.\n\nOverall, Irish missionaries were very successful by their own measures. The idea of Ireland's spiritual empire gave their work a national meaning and kept Irish parishes giving money and vocations, and in Nigeria they left schools, hospitals and a Nigerian-led church that outlasted colonial rule. The success was real but not complete. It was limited by paternalism, dependence on colonial structures, and in China by expulsion, so it was greatest in Nigeria and in medicine and education.",
