    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "From my study I learned that film and music created images of Ireland abroad that were often sentimental, rural and stereotyped, and that tourism and heritage then turned those images into practical links between Ireland and the wider Irish community. Both showed Ireland to the world, and both reflected the interests of emigrant audiences as much as the reality of Irish life.\n\nHollywood inherited the stage Irishman, a nineteenth-century theatrical figure who was drunken, quick-tempered, sentimental and comic. Harrigan and Hart's comedies in New York in the 1870s and 1880s made him familiar to American audiences. Films usually showed the Irish as rural, Catholic and emotional, and rarely showed urban or industrial Ireland. Going My Way, released in 1944, presented the Irish-American priest, and actors such as Pat O'Brien and Barry Fitzgerald played stock Irish characters, while Disney's Darby O'Gill and the Little People in 1959 offered leprechauns and banshees. John Ford, born in Maine to parents from Galway, won the Academy Award for Best Director for The Informer in 1935, which dealt with the revolutionary period in Dublin.\n\nThe most influential film image was Ford's The Quiet Man, filmed around Cong in Co. Mayo and in Connemara in 1951 and released in 1952. It starred John Wayne and Maureen O'Hara, and won Academy Awards in 1953 for Ford's direction and for colour cinematography. Its romantic rural Ireland shaped what American visitors expected to find. Irish critics objected that it presented rural people as comic and drunken, and the picture also contrasted sharply with a real Ireland suffering mass emigration in 1952. Maureen O'Hara, born in Ranelagh in 1920, helped build the image of the spirited Irish heroine, though historians point out that this was partly a Hollywood creation.\n\nMusic carried similar images. Songs such as When Irish Eyes Are Smiling in 1912 and Danny Boy, written in 1910 by an English lyricist to an Irish air, came out of the Irish-American song industry and presented Ireland as a land of exile and longing. John McCormack, the Athlone-born tenor, took Irish ballads into mainstream concert halls in America and Britain. Céilí bands and Irish dancing in parish halls and clubs gave emigrant communities a shared cultural life. The folk revival then changed the picture. The Clancy Brothers and Tommy Makem became popular in America after appearing on the Ed Sullivan Show in 1961, and the Dubliners from 1962 brought Irish songs to a young audience, giving Irish culture a more confident voice abroad than the sentimental ballad had.\n\nTourism and heritage turned these images into real links with the Irish abroad. Shannon Airport opened in 1945 as a transatlantic stop and opened the world's first duty-free shop in 1947, and Aer Lingus began transatlantic flights in 1958. The state set up Bord Fáilte under the Tourist Traffic Act of 1952 and launched An Tóstal, a festival of Irish culture, in 1953. The Quiet Man's rural comic Ireland suited the tourist board, and the film's locations, such as the bridge at Cong, drew American visitors who wanted to see what they had seen on screen. Tourism was also valued as an export industry that earned dollars and sterling.\n\nHeritage and roots tourism connected the state directly to emigrant descendants. Visits to ancestral homes, church records and genealogy services grew in the 1950s and 1960s. John F. Kennedy's visit from 26 to 29 June 1963 was the high point. His great-grandfather had left Dunganstown, Co. Wexford, in 1849, and Kennedy visited Dublin, Wexford, Cork, Galway and Limerick and addressed the Oireachtas on 28 June. The visit drew large crowds and encouraged Irish people to see emigrants and their descendants as part of one shared story, although historians argue it fitted Lemass's self-confident Ireland rather than causing it.\n\nOverall I learned that these images made Ireland widely known and strengthened ties with emigrant descendants, but they were an Ireland made for export, rural and sentimental, which left out urban and industrial life. Film fixed that picture, while the folk revival and the Kennedy visit showed a more modern and confident Ireland to the wider Irish community.",
