    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Between 1949 and 1989 each of these three forces mattered a great deal, but in different ways. Attitudes to the Irish language moved from official compulsion towards flexibility and choice, RTÉ opened Irish homes to new ideas and to public argument, and Archbishop McQuaid embodied a Catholic authority that was powerful in the 1950s but came to seem out of date. Together they show a country slowly shifting from a closed and deferential society to a more questioning one.\n\nThe language was the first official language under the 1937 Constitution, and governments backed its revival through schools, the civil service and the Gaeltacht. A pass in Irish was needed for the Leaving Certificate and for civil service entry. Yet the Gaeltacht was shrinking because of emigration, which is why Gaeltarra Éireann was set up in 1957 to bring industry to Irish-speaking areas. Gael Linn, founded in 1953, tried a more popular approach through records, the Amharc Éireann newsreel from 1956 and the film Mise Éire in 1959. The importance of these efforts was that they presented Irish as a living, modern language and not only a school duty, but they could not reverse its decline as a community language.\n\nBy the 1960s public attitudes were turning against compulsion. The Commission on the Restoration of the Irish Language reported in 1963 and the 1965 White Paper accepted restoration as an aim while proposing a more flexible approach. The Language Freedom Movement, founded in 1966, argued that compulsion turned pupils against Irish, and it provoked counter-campaigns. In 1973 the Fine Gael-Labour government, with Richard Burke as Minister for Education, ended the rule that students had to pass Irish for the Leaving Certificate. The Gaeltacht Civil Rights Movement and its pirate station, Saor Raidió Chonamara, led to Raidió na Gaeltachta in 1972, and Údarás na Gaeltachta followed in 1980. The language gained institutions and a broadcasting voice, but everyday speakers continued to decline, so its importance became more symbolic than practical.\n\nRTÉ was probably the most powerful agent of change. Telefís Éireann began on 31 December 1961 under the Broadcasting Authority Act 1960, and by the early 1970s most households had a set. Television reduced rural isolation and brought national and international news, sport and entertainment into every home. The Riordans, from 1965, dealt with the real issues of farming life, while Telefís Scoile, from 1964, was one of the earliest schools television services in Europe. Politicians had to learn to use the medium, and Lemass used it to explain policy and project a modern state. RTÉ therefore did not only entertain, it changed how Irish people saw the world and each other.\n\nRTÉ also challenged authority. The Late Late Show, presented by Gay Byrne from 1962, discussed sex, marriage, contraception and the church, subjects rarely aired in public, and in 1966 the episode known as the Bishop and the Nightie led Bishop Thomas Ryan of Clonfert to condemn the programme while many viewers defended it. Seven Days, from 1966, investigated housing and poverty, and its 1969 programme on illegal moneylending led to a government inquiry and a row about independence. The limits were shown when Gerry Collins issued the first Section 31 order in October 1971 and then dismissed the whole RTÉ Authority in November 1972 after the Seán Mac Stíofáin interview on This Week. Television was important because it made national debate possible, even though the state kept strong powers over it.\n\nArchbishop John Charles McQuaid, Archbishop of Dublin from 1940 to 1972, was the most influential churchman of the period. In 1950 and 1951 he advised the government that Noël Browne's Mother and Child Scheme conflicted with Catholic social teaching, and when Browne resigned in April 1951 Costello and the cabinet accepted the bishops' view. This showed how far the state deferred to the hierarchy on social policy. His influence also reached education, where he built schools and founded charitable bodies such as the Catholic Social Service Conference, and in the case of John McGahern, whose novel The Dark was banned in 1965 and who lost his teaching job after the archbishop's intervention.\n\nHis importance changed after Vatican II. He attended the council, but on his return in December 1965 he assured Dubliners that it would not disturb their faith, and he introduced liturgical reform slowly and was wary of ecumenism and of lay initiatives outside clerical control. His authoritarian style came to seem out of date as television, education and contact with Europe encouraged people to think for themselves, and he retired in January 1972. Critics such as John Cooney portray him as the ruler of Catholic Ireland, while others stress his work in building schools and welfare services and argue that the church's decline owed as much to social change as to his policies.\n\nOverall, all three were important, but not equally. The language policy shows a state slowly accepting that compulsion had failed, McQuaid shows the peak and then the decline of church power, and RTÉ was the most decisive because it helped to cause the changes in attitude to religion, authority and the language itself. Historians rightly add that television reflected changes already under way, but it speeded them up and gave them a national audience.",
