    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The status of women changed substantially between 1949 and 1989 in law, in employment and in public debate, but the change was uneven and incomplete. By 1989 women had legal equality in pay and employment and access to contraception, yet they remained scarce in politics and senior posts, and divorce and abortion were still prohibited. The change was real but limited.\n\nIn 1949 women's position was heavily restricted by the marriage bar, which forced women in the civil service and other public posts to resign on marriage, and by lower pay for similar work. Many employers, including banks, imposed their own bars. Married women were a very small part of the workforce, with a participation rate of only about 5 per cent in 1961. This was a society in which the Catholic Church's teaching on the family strongly shaped expectations, as the Mother and Child crisis of 1951 had shown. The starting point for change was therefore low.\n\nThe 1970s brought organised pressure. The Irish Women's Liberation Movement, formed in 1970 by Mary Kenny, Nell McCafferty, June Levine and Nuala Fennell among others, published Chains or Change in 1971, demanding equal pay, equal education, contraception and legal changes for unmarried mothers and deserted wives. On 22 May 1971 its members travelled by train to Belfast, bought contraceptives that were illegal to import and returned openly to Connolly Station, which made contraception a public issue. Women's Aid, founded in 1974, and the Well Woman Centre, opened in 1977, provided services that the state did not. At the same time the Commission on the Status of Women, chaired by Thekla Beere, was set up in 1970, and its report of December 1972 made 49 recommendations on equal pay, employment, education, family law and social welfare.\n\nLegislation then transformed women's legal position. The marriage bar was removed for the civil service in 1973, and equal pay in the public service followed. The Anti-Discrimination (Pay) Act 1974 required equal pay for the same or similar work, in line with the EEC directive, and the Employment Equality Act 1977 made it unlawful to discriminate on grounds of sex or marital status in recruitment, training and promotion. The Employment Equality Agency, headed by Sylvia Meehan, was set up to enforce this. The Juries Act 1976, for which Mary Robinson had campaigned, ended the exclusion of women from jury service, and the Family Home Protection Act 1976 stopped a husband selling the family home without his wife's consent. The Maternity Protection of Employees Act 1981 gave women the right to leave and to return to work. EEC membership clearly drove much of this change.\n\nContraception was the most controversial issue. In McGee v Attorney General in December 1973 the Supreme Court held by four to one that the ban on importing contraceptives infringed the right to marital privacy. A government bill in 1974 was defeated when Taoiseach Liam Cosgrave voted against it, and the Health (Family Planning) Act 1979, introduced by Haughey, allowed sale only on prescription to people with a genuine need, an Irish solution to an Irish problem. Sale to people over 18 without prescription was allowed only in 1985. The church opposed artificial contraception, reaffirmed by Pope Paul VI in 1968, though many married Catholics ignored the rule, and falling family size suggests that women's lives were changing in practice faster than in law.\n\nThe limits were still very clear. Women remained a small minority in the Dáil, with six after 1977 and 14 by 1987. Eileen Desmond became Minister for Health and Social Welfare in 1981, the first woman in cabinet since Constance Markievicz, and Gemma Hussey was Minister for Education from 1982, but these were exceptions. Pay gaps persisted and the Employment Equality Agency's powers were limited. Divorce and abortion remained prohibited, and the referendums of 1983 and 1986 showed that traditional views were still strong.\n\nIn conclusion, the status of women changed to a considerable extent between 1949 and 1989. The end of the marriage bar, equal pay and employment legislation, access to contraception and a growing women's movement marked a real break with the 1950s. But the change was driven as much by EEC law and economic development as by Irish attitudes, it was uneven, and Ireland in 1989 remained more conservative than many European states. A fair verdict is that women's legal status changed greatly, while their social and political position changed more slowly.",
