    "model": "Between 1815 and 1848 Metternich was the dominant diplomat in Europe, and his contribution was to shape and defend a conservative international order. He helped to design the Vienna settlement, used the Concert of Europe and the congress system to suppress revolution, and kept the great powers at peace for decades. His diplomacy had real successes, but it also had clear limits, and it could not outlast the pressures of liberalism and nationalism.\n\nHis first contribution came at the Congress of Vienna, which he hosted and dominated. Representing Austria alongside Castlereagh of Britain, Tsar Alexander I, Hardenberg of Prussia and Talleyrand of France, he worked for a settlement based on legitimacy and the balance of power. Rulers deposed by Napoleon were restored, and a ring of buffer states was built around France, including the enlarged Netherlands, a strengthened Piedmont-Sardinia and a neutral Switzerland. Austria gave up the Austrian Netherlands but gained Lombardy-Venetia, and Habsburg relatives ruled Tuscany, Modena and Parma, which made Austria the leading power in Italy. The Final Act was signed in June 1815, and Metternich also secured a loose German Confederation of 39 states, with a Diet at Frankfurt under Austrian presidency, that served Austrian interests by keeping Germany divided.\n\nHis second contribution was to turn the alliances of 1815 into an instrument for managing Europe. The Quadruple Alliance of Britain, Russia, Austria and Prussia, signed in November 1815, bound the powers to uphold the settlement and to meet at intervals, and it produced the Concert of Europe. The Holy Alliance of Russia, Austria and Prussia, which Metternich privately called empty, was useful to him because it bound the eastern monarchs together. At Aix-la-Chapelle in 1818 France was admitted to the Concert, which showed that the system could reintegrate a defeated power and strengthened general stability.\n\nThird, Metternich made the congress system a weapon against revolution. At Troppau in 1820 Austria, Prussia and Russia signed a protocol claiming the right to intervene against revolutions that threatened other states, though Britain and France refused to sign. At Laibach in 1821 Austrian troops were authorised to crush the revolution in Naples, and Austria also put down the Piedmontese rising that year. At Verona in 1822 the eastern powers backed a French invasion of Spain to restore Ferdinand VII, which took place in 1823. Austrian troops again crushed risings in Modena, Parma and the Papal Legations in 1831. In these years Metternich proved that diplomacy backed by force could defend legitimate rulers.\n\nHowever, the system had limits that Metternich could not overcome. Britain refused to join the Holy Alliance and withdrew from the congress system at Verona after Castlereagh's death, when Canning became foreign secretary, leaving the alliance as a combination of the three eastern autocracies. The Greek War of Independence, which began in 1821, broke the principle of non-intervention. Britain, France and Russia destroyed the Turkish-Egyptian fleet at Navarino in 1827, and Greece was recognised as independent in 1830 and became a monarchy under Otto of Bavaria in 1832, despite Metternich's opposition to aiding rebels against a legitimate ruler. The revolutions of 1830 added further blows when Belgium broke away from the Netherlands, and the Treaty of London in 1839 guaranteed its neutrality.\n\nHis system also depended on Prussia and Russia, and Prussia built a rival economic influence through the Zollverein, which came into force on 1 January 1834 and excluded Austria. Metternich warned that it was building Prussian influence, but he could not stop it. Leadership of the customs union gave Prussia an economic lead in Germany.\n\nOn balance, Metternich's contribution was substantial. The great powers fought no general war between 1815 and the Crimean War of 1853, though this owed something to exhaustion after the Napoleonic wars and to British naval power as well as to his diplomacy. He delayed liberal and national change for more than thirty years, but he did not remove its causes, and his system collapsed within days of the Vienna rising of March 1848, when he resigned and fled to England.",
