    "label": "2",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Germany achieved the greater unity and strength between 1815 and 1871. Both countries were united by a strong kingdom that used war and diplomacy, but Germany became a single, powerful and industrially advanced empire, while Italy became a poorer and more divided kingdom that depended on foreign help. By 1871 Germany was the leading power on the continent, and Italy, though unified, was still struggling to hold together.\n\nItaly's unity was a real achievement, won through Piedmontese leadership. In 1815 Italy was divided, with Austria ruling Lombardy and Venetia, Habsburg princes in Tuscany, Modena and Parma, the Pope in the Papal States and the Bourbons in the Two Sicilies, and the revolutions of 1820, 1821, 1831 and 1848 all failed. Cavour became Prime Minister of Piedmont in 1852, modernised its economy and sent troops to the Crimean War, which won Piedmont a place at the Congress of Paris in 1856. At Plombieres in 1858 he secured French support, and the war of 1859 brought victories at Magenta and Solferino, though Napoleon III made peace at Villafranca and only Lombardy went to Piedmont. Plebiscites in 1860 brought in Tuscany, Modena, Parma and the Romagna, Garibaldi's Thousand landed at Marsala in May 1860 and took Naples in September, and the Kingdom of Italy was proclaimed on 17 March 1861. Venetia came in 1866 and Rome in September 1870.\n\nYet Italy's strength was limited. It owed Venetia to Prussia's victory, since its own armies were defeated at Custoza and Lissa in 1866, and Rome came only when the French garrison withdrew in 1870. The new state was a centralised monarchy under Piedmontese control, and the franchise was so narrow that only about 2 per cent of the population could vote. The southern peasantry gained little, and brigandage in the south had to be suppressed by the army after 1861. The Pope rejected the Law of Guarantees and declared himself a prisoner of the Vatican, so Church and state remained hostile, and Trentino and Trieste stayed under Austria.\n\nGermany's unification was driven by a state with far greater resources. In 1815 Germany was a loose Confederation of 39 states with a Diet at Frankfurt under Austrian presidency, but Prussia's Zollverein, in force from 1834, brought most German states except Austria into an economic union. The Ruhr and Silesian coal and iron, the railways and Krupp's steelworks at Essen gave Prussia an industrial power that Italy could not match, while Austria was weakened by debt and by its defeat in Italy in 1859. When William I appointed Bismarck in 1862, Prussia already had an army reformed under Roon and a general staff under Moltke, and Bismarck carried the reforms through without an approved budget.\n\nBismarck then used war and diplomacy in three steps. The Danish War of 1864, fought jointly with Austria, won Schleswig, Holstein and Lauenburg. In 1866 he allied with Italy, and the needle-gun and rapid rail mobilisation brought a decisive victory at Koniggratz on 3 July; the Treaty of Prague dissolved the German Confederation and excluded Austria, Prussia annexed Hanover, Hesse-Kassel, Nassau, Frankfurt and Schleswig-Holstein, and the North German Confederation was formed in 1867 with Bismarck as Chancellor. He had halted at Nikolsburg to avoid humiliating Austria. In 1870 he edited the Ems telegram, France declared war, and the southern states joined Prussia, and after Sedan on 2 September the German Empire was proclaimed at Versailles on 18 January 1871. It was a federal state with a Reichstag elected by universal male suffrage, and it took Alsace, part of Lorraine and an indemnity of five billion francs from France. Its limits were real, since Prussia dominated, the Chancellor answered to the emperor and there were Polish, Danish and French-speaking minorities.\n\nOverall Germany achieved the greater unity and strength. It won its unity largely by its own army and economy, defeated two great powers and became the strongest state on the continent, whereas Italy depended on French and Prussian help, remained poorer and more divided, and was weaker in 1871.",
