    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Trade unionism and socialism responded more effectively to social change in Europe between 1815 and 1871 than mass politics did, although neither delivered everything its supporters wanted. They addressed the real conditions of industrial workers and built lasting organisations and ideas, whereas mass politics mobilised great support but achieved little in the period itself.\n\nTrade unionism won real, if limited, ground. In Britain the Combination Acts of 1799 and 1800 made it illegal for workers to combine to raise wages or change conditions. They were repealed in 1824, and an act of 1825 restored the right to organise while limiting strikes and violence. Setbacks followed: six Dorset labourers, the Tolpuddle Martyrs, were sentenced to transportation in 1834 for swearing a union oath, and the Grand National Consolidated Trades Union under Robert Owen collapsed within months. Yet a public campaign secured their pardon in 1836, which showed that workers could defend themselves by direct organisation.\n\nThe New Model unions gave this response permanence. The Amalgamated Society of Engineers, founded in 1851, charged a high subscription, held a central fund and paid benefits to sick and unemployed members, protecting skilled men from the worst insecurity of industrial life. The Trades Union Congress first met in 1868 and the Trade Union Act of 1871 gave unions legal recognition. The weakness was that unions were mostly confined to skilled workers, so they answered social change for a minority only.\n\nSocialism offered an explanation of industrial society and an alternative to it. Utopian socialists such as Saint-Simon and Fourier planned co-operative communities, Robert Owen showed at New Lanark that a co-operative model could work in practice, and Louis Blanc urged state-backed workshops in 1839. Marx and Engels published the Communist Manifesto in London in February 1848, presenting history as a history of class struggles, and Marx helped to found the International Workingmen's Association in 1864 and published the first volume of Das Kapital in 1867. These ideas gave workers an international framework for their grievances, but the quarrel between Marx and Bakunin split the International, and the predicted revolutions did not come. Socialism's effectiveness lay in ideas and organisation rather than immediate results.\n\nMass politics, by contrast, aroused huge hopes but won little. Chartism, the first mass working-class political movement in Britain, grew from the depression of 1837, hatred of the New Poor Law and disappointment that the Reform Act of 1832 left only about one adult man in five with a vote. Its People's Charter of 1838 demanded universal male suffrage, the secret ballot and annual parliaments, yet its petitions of 1839, 1842 and 1848 were all rejected and the Newport Rising of 1839 was crushed. The movement was split between moral-force men such as Lovett and physical-force leaders, and Feargus O'Connor's National Land Company collapsed in 1851. After the peaceful dispersal at Kennington Common in April 1848 it lost momentum.\n\nThere were genuine advances elsewhere. The Second Republic in France introduced universal male suffrage in 1848, the Second Reform Act of 1867 enfranchised many urban working men, and the North German Confederation adopted universal male suffrage in 1867. Peel's reforms, from the Metropolitan Police of 1829 to the repeal of the Corn Laws in 1846, showed the state adapting from within. But these were concessions by governments rather than victories won by popular movements, and in Britain the secret ballot came only in 1872 and universal male suffrage in 1918.\n\nOn balance, trade unionism and socialism were the more effective responses to social change. They produced legal recognition, permanent organisations and a lasting body of ideas, whereas mass politics delivered mainly defeats and partial reforms handed down by governments.",
