    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Between 1815 and 1871 both cultural nationalism and Haussmann's Paris were important, but in different ways. Cultural nationalism gave nationalist movements their symbols, languages and sense of a shared past, and so helped prepare the unification of Italy and Germany. Haussmann's Paris transformed the health and appearance of a great capital and became a model for city planning, although at a heavy social cost.\n\nCultural nationalism drew on Romanticism, which rejected the rational order of the Enlightenment and celebrated feeling and the national past. Herder developed the idea of a Volksgeist, a national spirit expressed in a people's language and folklore, and nationalists took it up. It was most powerful where a nation lacked its own state, as in Italy, Germany, Poland and the Czech lands. The Brothers Grimm published their folk tales in 1812 and 1815 as the expression of the German people and began a great German dictionary in 1838. Such work gave people who lived under many different rulers a feeling of belonging to one community.\n\nMusic, painting and literature did the same. Chopin's mazurkas and polonaises drew on Polish dance rhythms and became a symbol of Polish feeling in exile after 1831. Verdi's Nabucco in 1842 included the chorus Va, pensiero, sung by Hebrew exiles, which Italian audiences came to hear as a call for national liberation, and Delacroix's Liberty Leading the People of 1830 gave the July Revolution a lasting image. Manzoni's I promessi sposi, revised in 1840 to 1842 in the educated Tuscan of Florence, became a model for standard Italian, Mickiewicz's Pan Tadeusz of 1834 celebrated the Polish past, and Jungmann's Czech dictionary of 1835 to 1839 helped revive the Czech language. These works reached people who never read a political pamphlet. Yet cultural nationalism could not create a state by itself, as the failure of the 1848 revolutions showed; its importance lay in preparing minds.\n\nHaussmann's Paris was important for quite different reasons. Napoleon III appointed Haussmann Prefect of the Seine in June 1853, and he held the post until January 1870. The aims were to improve health, provide work through public works and make the city easier to control, since the revolutions of 1830 and 1848 had been fought behind barricades in narrow streets. Using the expropriation law of 1841, extended in 1852, he cleared whole districts and cut broad, straight boulevards such as the Boulevard de Sebastopol and the Avenue de l'Opera, lined with uniform buildings. The annexation of the suburbs in 1860 raised the arrondissements from twelve to twenty and let him plan the whole urban area.\n\nThe health benefits were the most lasting. The engineer Eugene Belgrand built aqueducts bringing spring water from the Dhuis and Vanne valleys and an underground sewer network, in a city that had been among the worst served in Europe. Parks such as the Bois de Boulogne, the Bois de Vincennes and the Buttes-Chaumont gave residents green space, and Baltard's iron-and-glass Les Halles gave Paris a modern look. Paris became a model that Vienna's Ringstrasse, Cerda's plan for Barcelona and Hobrecht's plan for Berlin followed in different forms.\n\nThe costs were severe. Demolition displaced working-class families to the east and north, rents rose in the centre, and the division between the wealthy west and the working-class east deepened, an effect Zola satirised in La Curee. The works were paid for by heavy borrowing, which Jules Ferry attacked in 1868, Haussmann was dismissed in 1870, and the Commune of 1871 showed that the boulevards did not end revolutionary danger.\n\nOverall, both were important, but cultural nationalism had the deeper long-term effect, since it shaped the ideas that helped to remake the map of Europe. Haussmann's Paris was the more immediate and visible achievement, solving real problems of health and traffic and setting the pattern for modern planning, though at the expense of the poor.",
    "model_source": "sonnet-long-fix",
    "answerStatus": "written",
