    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Between 1871 and 1914 industrialisation transformed Germany from a newly unified country into the second industrial power in the world, reshaping its economy, society, politics and foreign policy. The invention and early history of the motor car, in which German engineers led the way, was one part of the new industries of the period. Its impact on Germany before 1914 was smaller but it showed the country's inventive and technical strength.\n\nGermany industrialised quickly after 1871 because it combined coal and iron with a large home market. The population rose from about 41 million to about 67 million, the Ruhr was the heart of heavy industry, and Lorraine's iron ore had been gained from France in 1871. Unification brought a common currency in 1873, the Reichsbank and uniform commercial law, while railways grew from about 21,000 km to over 60,000 km. Universal banks such as Deutsche Bank lent to industry over long periods, and technical colleges trained engineers and chemists. The results were dramatic: coal output rose from about 30 million tonnes to about 190 million, and by 1913 Germany produced more than twice as much steel as Britain.\n\nThe second industrial revolution gave Germany leadership in new industries. The Thomas-Gilchrist process of 1878 let Lorraine ores be turned into steel. BASF, Bayer and Hoechst led the world in dyes and pharmaceuticals, and Haber and Bosch's ammonia synthesis made artificial fertiliser possible. Siemens and AEG dominated electrical engineering. Cartels such as the Rhenish-Westphalian Coal Syndicate of 1893 organised these industries, helped by tariffs, and German firms competed with British goods across the world.\n\nThe motor car belonged to this world of invention. Nikolaus Otto built the first practical four-stroke engine in 1876. Gottlieb Daimler and Wilhelm Maybach developed a light, high-speed petrol engine and fitted it to a motorcycle in 1885 and a carriage in 1886. Karl Benz, working independently at Mannheim, ran his three-wheeled Motorwagen in 1885 and patented it in January 1886; it is regarded as the first practical car designed as a whole. In August 1888 his wife Bertha drove it about 106 km from Mannheim to Pforzheim, buying fuel at a pharmacy in Wiesloch, and the publicity proved the car's usefulness. Rudolf Diesel patented his engine in 1892, and in 1901 Daimler's firm produced the Mercedes. The impact on Germany was therefore first of all one of prestige and technical leadership, and of new firms and skilled work in engineering.\n\nThe limits must be stated honestly. Before 1914 cars were handmade luxury goods for the rich, and France led early production through Panhard and Peugeot, while Henry Ford's moving assembly line of 1913 gave the USA the mass market. The social effects of the car in Germany came mostly after 1920. Its immediate impact was to add to demand for steel, rubber and machine tools, and to give Germany's industrial reputation a modern, innovative face.\n\nIndustrialisation had much deeper social consequences. People moved from the countryside to the cities, and by 1910 around 60 per cent of Germans lived in towns, against about a third in 1871. Berlin passed two million and the Ruhr towns grew many times over. Working-class families lived in crowded tenements, and factory work meant long hours and discipline. The Ruhr miners' strike of 1889 involved about 90,000 men. The SPD, formed at Gotha in 1875, grew despite the Anti-Socialist Law of 1878-90, and in 1912 it won about 4.25 million votes and 110 seats to become the largest Reichstag party, while the Free trade unions had about 2.5 million members by 1913.\n\nIndustry also strengthened the state and its ambitions. The Krupp works at Essen, built up by Alfred Krupp as the Cannon King, supplied steel guns, then naval armour under Friedrich Alfred, and the Big Bertha howitzer in 1914, illustrating the links between heavy industry, the army and the state. The navy and army were large customers, and industrial strength fed Wilhelm II's Weltpolitik.\n\nOverall, industrialisation made Germany a rich, urban, powerful but socially divided country, and gave it the confidence and the means to challenge Britain. The motor car was a symbol of German inventiveness rather than a force that changed everyday life before 1914, but together they show a country whose technological leadership was remarkable.",
