    "label": "2",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The New Imperialism, the rush of European states to acquire colonies in Africa and Asia from about 1880, had a mix of economic, strategic, nationalist, religious and ideological causes. Its effects on Europe were equally large: it intensified rivalry between the powers, produced diplomatic crises, shaped the alliance system and stirred public opinion, and its abuses, as in the Congo, caused a moral reaction at home.\n\nEconomic motives were important. Industrial states needed raw materials such as rubber, cotton, palm oil and copper and wanted protected markets when tariffs rose in Europe from the 1870s. J.A. Hobson argued in 1902 that capitalists invested abroad because of surplus capital at home, and Lenin built on this in 1916, though historians point out that most trade and investment went to areas outside the empires. Economic motives therefore mattered but did not alone explain the scramble.\n\nStrategic and nationalist causes were at least as strong. Powers wanted naval bases, coaling stations and secure routes: Britain occupied Egypt in 1882 to protect the Suez Canal and the route to India, and then pushed into the Nile valley. Colonies were seen as a sign of Great Power status, and newspapers and colonial societies cheered each acquisition. France, defeated in 1871, sought compensation overseas under Jules Ferry. Competition fed on itself, because once one power claimed land others feared being shut out.\n\nIdeas and technology completed the picture. Missionaries and humanitarians such as Livingstone wanted to spread Christianity and end the slave trade. Social Darwinism and a belief in a duty to civilise other peoples, in France's mission civilisatrice or Kipling's White Man's Burden of 1899, were used to justify conquest. Quinine against malaria, steamships, the telegraph and the Maxim gun gave Europeans overwhelming military advantage. The outcome was that, from about a tenth of Africa under European control in 1880, about nine tenths was taken by 1914, after the Berlin Conference of 1884-85 set rules of notice and effective occupation.\n\nThe effects on Europe began with rivalry and near war. Britain's Cape to Cairo ambitions under Rhodes crossed France's plan to link Senegal to the Red Sea, and at Fashoda in 1898 Marchand's small French expedition faced Kitchener's much stronger force. France backed down in November, but Anglo-French relations were poisoned for years. Colonial disputes also entered the German-French relationship in the Moroccan crises of 1905-06 and 1911, which each made Europe seem closer to war.\n\nThe diplomatic effect was far-reaching. The Boer War of 1899-1902 showed that Britain, hostile opinion all around, had few friends, and with Germany building a large navy Britain ended splendid isolation. It signed the Anglo-Japanese Alliance in 1902, then the Entente Cordiale of 8 April 1904, settling colonial disputes by recognising Britain in Egypt and France in Morocco, and the Anglo-Russian Entente of 1907 settled Asian rivalries. Ironically, imperialism, which had divided the powers, thus helped create the Triple Entente facing the Triple Alliance, and Germany spoke of encirclement. Public opinion was inflamed: the Boer War aroused anger across Europe, and British burning of farms and camps in which over 20,000 died shocked many.\n\nImperial rule also provoked a moral reaction. Leopold II's Congo Free State forced Africans to collect rubber, with hostage-taking, beatings and mutilation, and the population fell by millions. E.D. Morel and Roger Casement, whose 1904 report documented the abuses, led the Congo Reform Association, Conrad wrote Heart of Darkness in 1899, and in 1908 the Belgian parliament took over the colony. Europe's own conscience was thus stirred by what it had done abroad.\n\nIn conclusion, the New Imperialism was driven by a mixture of economic need, strategy, national pride and belief in racial superiority, made possible by technology. Its main effect on Europe was to move great power rivalry overseas and then back into Europe itself, hardening alliances and raising suspicion, so that it helped prepare the atmosphere in which war came in 1914, while also exposing Europe to criticism of its own methods.",
