    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Between 1900 and 1920 war and revolution transformed Russia. Defeat by Japan sparked the revolution of 1905, which shook but did not topple the autocracy, whereas the far greater strain of the First World War destroyed the Romanov monarchy in 1917. The Bolshevik seizure of power and a savage civil war then created a one-party state, at an enormous cost in territory, lives and economic strength.\n\nThe Russo-Japanese War of 1904-05 was the first blow. Russia and Japan were rivals in Manchuria and Korea, and the interior minister Plehve is reported to have wanted a short victorious war to head off revolution. The opposite happened. Port Arthur surrendered in January 1905, the army was beaten at Mukden, and the Baltic Fleet, which had sailed around the world, was destroyed at Tsushima in May. By the Treaty of Portsmouth Russia gave up southern Sakhalin and Port Arthur and conceded a free hand in Korea. The defeat humiliated the regime and exposed the weakness of its army, railways and administration, so it was the immediate trigger of revolution.\n\nOn 22 January 1905 troops fired on a peaceful procession led by Father Gapon that was carrying a petition to the Winter Palace, and Bloody Sunday destroyed the people's trust in the tsar as their protector. Strikes, peasant risings and the mutiny on the battleship Potemkin followed, and a general strike in October paralysed the cities while the Petersburg Soviet emerged, with Trotsky as a leading member. Witte persuaded Nicholas II to issue the October Manifesto, promising civil liberties and an elected Duma. The regime survived because the army stayed loyal, the opposition split between liberals and socialists, and a French loan in 1906 freed the government from dependence on the Duma. The Fundamental Laws of 1906 kept the Tsar's autocratic powers, so the outcome was limited change with deep grievances unresolved.\n\nThe First World War was far more destructive. Russia mobilised the largest army in Europe, but it was short of rifles, shells and boots and badly led, and defeats at Tannenberg and the Masurian Lakes in 1914 and the great retreat of 1915 cost millions of casualties and prisoners. Over fifteen million men were taken from the land, railways were overloaded, and the government printed money, so inflation rose and food and fuel ran short in the cities by the winter of 1916-17. When Nicholas II took personal command of the army in 1915 he left government to the Tsarina and Rasputin, and Rasputin's murder in December 1916 showed how isolated the regime had become. On 8 March 1917, International Women's Day, women textile workers in Petrograd demanded bread. Strikes became a general strike, the garrison soldiers joined the crowds rather than firing on them, and Nicholas abdicated on 15 March, ending three centuries of Romanov rule. The contrast with 1905 is clear: after three years of defeat the army had lost its loyalty. The Provisional Government shared power with the Petrograd Soviet, but by continuing the war and delaying land reform it lost support, and the Bolsheviks gained from Lenin's promise of peace, land and bread.\n\nOn 6-7 November 1917 the Bolsheviks seized power. They had won majorities in the Petrograd and Moscow Soviets, Kornilov's failed coup had given them arms and prestige, and Trotsky's Military Revolutionary Committee took the key points of the capital and the Winter Palace. The new government issued decrees on peace and land, but when the Constituent Assembly, in which the SRs had the most votes, met in January 1918 it was dissolved by force. Lenin then accepted the harsh Treaty of Brest-Litovsk in March 1918, by which Russia lost Poland, the Baltic provinces, Ukraine and Finland, with a vast share of its population, coal and industry.\n\nThe civil war of 1918-20 completed the transformation. Trotsky built the Red Army, which defeated the divided Whites under Kolchak, Denikin, Yudenich and Wrangel, while limited intervention by Britain, France, Japan and the USA failed. The Reds held the central heartland with its railways and industry, and many peasants feared a return of the landlords. Victory came through War Communism, with grain requisitioning, nationalised industry and the Red Terror. Finland, Poland and the Baltic states became independent, and Russia's economy lay in ruins.\n\nOverall, war swept away an autocracy that had survived 1905 only because its army stayed loyal, and replaced it with a Bolshevik dictatorship. The cost was huge territorial loss and economic ruin, and the period showed that war turned the weaknesses of an unreformed monarchy into collapse.",
