    "model": "British withdrawal from India in 1947 was of great importance for both countries. For Britain it ended the Raj, the largest and most valuable of its possessions, reduced its role in Asia and made the Commonwealth its main link with former colonies. For India it meant independence after decades of struggle, but also partition, mass migration and communal violence that left a lasting legacy of conflict with Pakistan.\n\nFor Britain, the first importance was the loss of the Raj itself. India had been ruled directly by the Crown since 1858 and was the greatest prize of the empire. Its departure ended Britain's claim to be a global imperial power in Asia. The decision was driven by weakness as much as principle: Britain had borrowed about 3.75 billion dollars from the United States in 1945-46 and owed India over one billion pounds in sterling balances, which reversed the old financial relationship and reduced the economic value of the Raj. Attlee's Labour government, elected in a landslide in July 1945, was committed to Indian self-rule and could not hold India against the will of its people.\n\nSecondly, withdrawal set a pattern for the rest of the empire. Burma and Ceylon became independent in 1948, and the Gold Coast became Ghana in 1957, the first British colony in tropical Africa to do so. Britain also found a new form of association in the London Declaration of April 1949, which allowed India to become a republic while remaining in the Commonwealth, so that membership no longer depended on accepting the monarch as head of state. India stayed a major trading partner through the sterling area. Britain could therefore present withdrawal as a managed transfer rather than a defeat, in contrast to the colonial wars France later fought in Indochina and Algeria.\n\nFor India, the first importance was that it achieved independence on 15 August 1947 after a long struggle. Congress, founded in 1885, had been turned by Gandhi into a mass movement through satyagraha, the Salt March of 1930, non-cooperation and the Quit India resolution of 1942. The INA trials of 1945-46 and the Royal Indian Navy mutiny of February 1946 showed that the armed forces could no longer be relied on. India became a republic on 26 January 1950 with Jawaharlal Nehru as prime minister, and under him it followed a policy of non-alignment, giving it a place of its own in world affairs.\n\nThe second importance for India, however, was partition. The Muslim League under Jinnah had called for separate Muslim states in the Lahore Resolution of 1940, and the Cabinet Mission of 1946 failed to bring Congress and the League together. Direct Action Day on 16 August 1946 brought killings in Calcutta, and Mountbatten, who arrived in March 1947, concluded that a united India was no longer possible. His plan of 3 June 1947 accepted partition and brought the date forward to August. Pakistan was created in two parts, West Pakistan and East Bengal, separated by about 1,600 km of Indian territory, and the boundaries were drawn by Cyril Radcliffe, a barrister who had never visited India, in a line announced two days after independence.\n\nThe human cost was enormous. Between 10 and 15 million people were displaced as Hindus and Sikhs moved into India and Muslims into Pakistan, and estimates of the dead range from about 200,000 to 2 million. Over 500 princely states had to choose between the two dominions, and the dispute over Kashmir, where the Maharaja acceded to India on 26 October 1947, led to war that ended in a ceasefire on 1 January 1949 and left a lasting division. Gandhi, who opposed partition and fasted to stop the killing, was shot dead by a Hindu nationalist, Nathuram Godse, on 30 January 1948. Critics argue that the rushed timetable worsened the violence, while defenders say delay would have meant more.\n\nOverall, withdrawal was a turning point for both countries. For Britain it marked the beginning of the end of empire and a shift to the Commonwealth, while for India it brought freedom and a place in world politics at the price of partition. Independence was the greater achievement for India, yet the legacy of division was the more lasting consequence for the whole subcontinent.",
