    "label": "2",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Sukarno's Indonesia faced serious internal problems of political instability, regional division, army-communist rivalry and economic decline, together with external problems arising from the Dutch and the neighbouring states. His attempts to solve them were only partly effective: he gave the country a national language and a unifying ideology and won international standing, but his methods ended parliamentary democracy and left Indonesia isolated and in crisis by 1965.\n\nThe first internal problem was holding together a vast and diverse archipelago after the war of independence. Sukarno, born in 1901 and founder of the Indonesian Nationalist Party in 1927, answered this with the Pancasila, the five principles he set out on 1 June 1945 to balance nationalists, Muslims and communists in one state. Indonesian was also promoted as a national language. These were real achievements in giving a diverse people a common identity. Yet political life was unstable. The first free election of September 1955 left four parties of similar size, the PNI, Masjumi, Nahdlatul Ulama and the PKI, and weak coalition cabinets followed.\n\nA second problem was regional resentment of Java's domination. In 1957-58 the PRRI in Sumatra and Permesta in Sulawesi rebelled, and the army put them down. This preserved national unity but strengthened the army's political role, which later threatened Sukarno himself. His answer to instability was Guided Democracy, introduced by decree on 5 July 1959, which returned to the 1945 constitution and ruled with a National Front and the army. He had himself named president for life in 1963. This ended the chaos of coalition cabinets in the short term, but it ended parliamentary government and made Indonesia depend on his personal balancing act.\n\nThat balancing act was the third and most dangerous problem. Sukarno promoted Nasakom, a union of nationalism, religion and communism, and relied on the PKI, which had about three million members by the mid-1960s and was the largest communist party outside the communist states. The army distrusted it. At the same time the economy declined in the early 1960s, with high inflation and heavy spending on confrontation with Malaysia. Here Sukarno's attempts failed. After the attempted coup of 30 September 1965, in which six generals were killed, General Suharto took control of the army, anti-communist killings followed with estimates from about 500,000 to a million or more, and Sukarno lost real power by the Supersemar order of 11 March 1966. He was placed under house arrest and died in 1970.\n\nExternally, the first problem was the legacy of Dutch rule. Indonesia won sovereignty on 27 December 1949 after the police actions of 1947 and 1948, UN intervention and American pressure on the Netherlands, but at The Hague it took over about 4.3 billion guilders of Dutch debt, and the Dutch kept West New Guinea. Sukarno responded by taking over Dutch businesses from 1957 and by pressing for West New Guinea, which passed to a UN administration in 1962 and to Indonesia on 1 May 1963. The Act of Free Choice in 1969 confirmed Indonesian rule, though it was held among about 1,000 chosen representatives and has been widely criticised. Here he was largely successful, and he had already abolished the weak Netherlands-Indonesian Union in 1954.\n\nIn wider foreign policy Sukarno won prestige at first. He hosted the Asian-African Conference at Bandung in April 1955, which brought together 29 countries and promoted non-alignment and anti-colonialism, giving newly independent states a voice. But his later policy was reckless. Konfrontasi, his opposition to the new Federation of Malaysia from 1963 to 1966, involved armed raids into Malaysian Borneo and brought no gain. In January 1965 he withdrew Indonesia from the United Nations and moved closer to China, leaving the country isolated.\n\nOverall, Sukarno was more effective in solving the problems of identity and decolonisation, where the Pancasila, the national language, West New Guinea and Bandung were real successes, than in the problems of government and economy, where Guided Democracy, dependence on the PKI and confrontation ended in collapse. Many historians therefore judge his later rule harshly, while crediting him with creating a national idea.",
