    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Julius Nyerere and the policy of ujamaa, together with the story of trade, aid and famine, show how hard it was for newly independent African states to turn political freedom into economic well-being. Nyerere tried a distinctive African road to development in Tanzania, while across the continent the colonial economic legacy, unequal trade, dependence on aid and recurring famine limited what any government could achieve.\n\nNyerere, known as Mwalimu or teacher, led Tanzania to independence and in the Arusha Declaration of 5 February 1967 committed the country to ujamaa, meaning familyhood or African socialism, and to self-reliance. The state took control of the main means of production, so the banks, insurance companies and major industries were nationalised in 1967. He also introduced Education for Self-Reliance, which shaped schooling around rural work so that education would serve village life rather than create an elite. The aim was a fairer society that did not depend on foreign capital, and the TANU Mwongozo guidelines of 1971 required party members to work for their living and avoid privilege.\n\nThe heart of ujamaa was the rural village. At first the ujamaa villages were voluntary cooperative settlements, but few peasants joined in large numbers. From 1973-74 the government moved millions of people into villages, often by force, in a policy called villagisation. This disrupted farming and food shortages followed. The oil price rise, drought and the cost of the war to overthrow Idi Amin of Uganda in 1978-79 added to the strain, so Tanzania had to import food and its economy declined by the early 1980s. The policy therefore showed the danger of compulsion and state-directed development.\n\nYet Nyerere's record was not simply one of failure. Tanzania made real gains in health and literacy, and the universal primary education launched in 1974 sent enrolment rising rapidly. He also refused to rule by ethnic favour and kept Tanzania united, and he chose Swahili as the national language and the language of primary schooling. When he stepped down in 1985 his successor Ali Hassan Mwinyi accepted an IMF programme in 1986 that reversed many socialist policies. Supporters therefore stress education, health and national unity, while critics stress collapsing farm output, compulsion and the one-party state.\n\nTanzania's difficulties were part of a wider problem of trade and aid. Colonial economies had been built around exporting cash crops and minerals, such as cocoa in the Gold Coast, groundnuts in Senegal and copper in Katanga, and independent states inherited dependence on one or two commodities. Economists such as Prebisch and Singer argued that the terms of trade moved against primary producers, and commodity prices fell sharply in the 1980s. The European Community tried to respond. The Yaoundé Convention of 1963 gave duty-free access to European markets for associated African states, and Lomé I, signed in February 1975 with 46 African, Caribbean and Pacific states, gave largely duty-free access without requiring reciprocal concessions. STABEX compensated states when export earnings from agricultural products fell. Even so, ACP exports stayed concentrated on a few primary commodities.\n\nAid and debt brought further problems. Oil price rises in 1973 and 1979 pushed governments to borrow heavily, and when interest rates rose and commodity prices fell in the early 1980s many could not repay. The IMF and World Bank then made loans conditional on structural adjustment, with devaluation, spending cuts and privatisation, and critics argued this reduced spending on health and education. Aid was often tied to purchases from the donor country and sometimes supported corrupt rulers such as Mobutu in Zaire.\n\nFamine showed how fragile food supply remained. Biafra suffered a famine in 1968-70 caused by blockade and war, and the Concern organisation was founded in Dublin in 1968 in response. A drought in the Sahel from the late 1960s to the mid-1970s hit Mali, Niger, Chad and other states. The Ethiopian famine of 1983-85 killed hundreds of thousands, as drought combined with the Derg's policies and civil war, and Michael Buerk's BBC report of October 1984 led to Band Aid and Live Aid in 1985, which raised about 150 million pounds. NGOs such as Trócaire, founded in 1973, and GOAL, founded in 1977, grew in importance.\n\nOverall, Nyerere's ujamaa was an idealistic attempt to avoid dependence that foundered on compulsion and falling output, though it left a legacy in education and unity. Trade agreements such as Lomé, aid and relief eased but did not remove the economic weakness inherited from colonialism, and most historians now explain Africa's difficulties by combining the colonial legacy, falling commodity prices, Cold War pressures and the choices of African governments.",
