    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Between 1945 and 1990 religion was a powerful force in shaping both Africa and Europe. In Africa, Christianity and Islam spread and were reshaped by African societies, influencing education, politics and identity. In Europe, migration from former colonies made Islam an increasingly visible faith, raising questions about integration and freedom of expression.\n\nIn colonial Africa the main agents of Christian expansion were Catholic and Protestant missions. Orders such as the White Fathers, founded in Algiers in 1868 and prominent in East and Central Africa, and societies such as the Church Missionary Society built churches, schools and hospitals. Mission schools taught literacy, scripture and European history, and produced the clerks, teachers and interpreters of the colonial system, so many later nationalist leaders were mission-educated. Irish missionary orders such as the Holy Ghost Fathers, the Society of African Missions and the Medical Missionaries of Mary ran schools and clinics in Nigeria, Kenya and Tanzania, which linked Ireland closely with Africa. The importance of Christianity therefore lay in education, health care and the creation of a new elite.\n\nChristianity was also changed by Africans themselves. African Independent Churches broke away from mission churches, and the Kimbanguist Church, which grew out of the preaching of Simon Kimbangu in the Belgian Congo from 1921, became one of the largest, while the Aladura churches grew in Yorubaland in Nigeria. These churches showed that Africans were not passive recipients, since they blended Christian belief with African practice and expressed cultural independence. Samuel Ajayi Crowther, a freed slave from Yorubaland who became the first African Anglican bishop in 1864, was an early sign of African leadership. Many Africans also combined Christian or Islamic belief with older religious traditions.\n\nIslam spread through different routes, mainly trade and Sufi brotherhoods. On the East African coast it had been established for centuries and shaped Swahili language and culture, and in West Africa it had spread across the region through trade. The Mouride brotherhood in Senegal, founded by Amadou Bamba and linked with the groundnut economy, became a major political and economic force despite his exile by the French. In northern Nigeria the Sokoto Caliphate, founded after Usman dan Fodio's jihad from 1804, had spread Islamic law, and Lugard's indirect rule worked through the Muslim emirs and barred most Christian missions from the emirates. The result was that western education spread much faster in the south than in the north, a difference that influenced Nigerian politics at independence and afterwards.\n\nReligion and decolonisation were also linked through language and culture. Colonial education spread European ideas, but religious movements and cultural nationalists such as Senghor challenged the idea that European culture was superior. Tanzania chose Swahili as its national language, tied to the coast's Islamic heritage, as a way to unite different communities. Religion therefore gave Africans organisations of their own, such as independent churches and brotherhoods, that colonial rulers could not fully control.\n\nIn Europe the importance of Islam grew with migration. The Grand Mosque of Paris was inaugurated in 1926 to honour the Muslim soldiers who died for France in the First World War, and it symbolised the relationship between the French state and Islam in its empire. From the 1950s and 1960s Muslim migration from South Asia to Britain and from Algeria, Morocco and Tunisia to France led to mosques being built in cities such as Birmingham, Bradford and London and to Islamic cultural centres. By the 1980s France had the largest Muslim population in Western Europe, between two and three million, and Islam became France's second religion.\n\nThe Rushdie affair showed the tensions this could cause. Salman Rushdie's The Satanic Verses was published in September 1988, and many Muslims in Britain objected to its portrayal of the Prophet Muhammad. Muslims in Bradford burned copies in January 1989, and on 14 February 1989 Ayatollah Khomeini issued a fatwa calling for Rushdie's death, so that he went into hiding under police protection. The affair raised questions about freedom of expression, religious identity and Britain's blasphemy law, which protected only Christianity. It was debated alongside the headscarf question in France.\n\nThe spread of Christianity and Islam in Africa shaped education, elites, politics and cultural identity, and often did so in distinctively African forms. In Europe, the growth of Islam through migration changed the religious map and created lasting debates about multiculturalism, showing that religion was among the most important legacies of empire and decolonisation.",
