    "model": "The Field Day Theatre Company was founded in Derry in 1980 by Brian Friel and Stephen Rea, and its first production, Friel's Translations, dealt with the English renaming of Irish placenames and the loss of the Irish language. Seamus Heaney's collection North, published in 1975, used Iron Age bog bodies to reflect on the violence in Northern Ireland. Murals in Catholic and Protestant areas of Belfast and Derry became a popular form of political expression, including the gable in the Bogside reading 'You are now entering Free Derry'. The Cultural Traditions Group was set up in 1988 to recognise both unionist and nationalist traditions and to encourage sharing across the community divide.",
