    "label": "C3",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "The Sunningdale Agreement of December 1973 set up a power-sharing executive in which unionists and nationalists shared power, and it took office on 1 January 1974 with Brian Faulkner as Chief Executive and Gerry Fitt as Deputy Chief Executive. It also agreed a Council of Ireland, and the Irish government accepted that Northern Ireland's status would not change without the consent of its majority. The executive failed mainly because unionists saw the Council of Ireland as a first step towards a united Ireland. Unionist opponents formed the UUUC in January 1974, and it won eleven of the twelve Northern Ireland seats at the February Westminster election. The Ulster Workers' Council strike from 15 May 1974 paralysed electricity and essential services. The British government did not confront the strikers, so Faulkner resigned on 28 May 1974 and the executive collapsed.",
