    "label": "C4",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Bernadette Devlin was a leader of People's Democracy, the student group formed at Queen's University Belfast in October 1968 that organised the Belfast to Derry march. The march was attacked at Burntollet Bridge on 4 January 1969, and the RUC entered the Bogside that night. Devlin was elected MP for Mid Ulster at the by-election of 17 April 1969, aged twenty-one, which brought the civil rights movement into Westminster. In the same month O'Neill's party agreed to accept one man, one vote in local elections, a key civil rights demand. She was convicted of incitement to riot after the Battle of the Bogside in August 1969. On 31 January 1972, the day after Bloody Sunday, she struck the Home Secretary Reginald Maudling in the House of Commons, bringing the conflict into Parliament itself.",
