    "model": "Haughey's government in 1987 was a minority government, supported by Fine Gael under Alan Dukes's Tallaght Strategy, and Ray MacSharry cut public spending sharply. The Programme for National Recovery, agreed in October 1987 with unions, employers and farmers, kept pay rises low in return for a cut in the deficit. The Tenth Amendment, approved by referendum on 26 May 1987, allowed Ireland to ratify the Single European Act. After the 1989 election he kept the Anglo-Irish Agreement in place when he formed a coalition with the Progressive Democrats. Historians judge that his cuts after 1987, together with the Programme for National Recovery, helped to prepare the recovery of the 1990s. He also held the Dublin summit with Thatcher in December 1980, which produced a statement on studying the totality of relationships within the islands.",
