    "marks": 30,
    "model": "The number of married women in paid employment rose steadily from the 1950s, especially in service and clerical work. The contraceptive pill, approved in the USA in 1960, gave women control over childbearing and became widely used in Western Europe in the 1960s. France's Veil Law of 17 January 1975 legalised abortion in its first ten weeks, while Britain's Equal Pay Act of 1970 required equal pay for work of equal value. Even so, women still did most of the housework and childcare, and pay gaps persisted.",
