    "marks": 30,
    "model": "Computers moved from wartime code-breaking machines, such as Colossus at Bletchley Park, to personal machines in the 1980s. The IBM Personal Computer, launched on 12 August 1981, spread personal computing into offices and homes. Computers automated routine office and factory work, reducing demand for some manufacturing jobs while service jobs in banking and administration grew. Tim Berners-Lee proposed the World Wide Web at CERN in 1989, and it was launched publicly in 1991.",
