    "label": "C1",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Khrushchev rose to power after Stalin's death in 1953, defeating rivals such as Beria and the anti-party group of Molotov, Malenkov and Kaganovich by 1957. In his secret speech of 25 February 1956 he denounced Stalin's cult of personality and terror, and de-Stalinisation released many political prisoners. He pursued peaceful coexistence and agreed to the Austrian State Treaty in 1955, and the hotline and the Partial Test Ban Treaty of 1963 followed the Cuban crisis. The Virgin Lands campaign produced good early harvests, but droughts and poor management caused failures, most seriously in 1963 when grain had to be imported. The Berlin Wall of August 1961 became the lasting symbol of his era, and Soviet leaders saw the Cuban settlement as a retreat. Removed in October 1964, he was only partly successful: he opened Soviet society but never built a lasting alternative to Stalinist planning.",
