    "label": "C2",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Oil had supplied about half of Western Europe's energy by 1973, at around three dollars a barrel, so the price rises hit economies hard. OPEC's price rises of October 1973 roughly quadrupled prices, and the Arab embargo targeted the USA and the Netherlands. Several countries introduced fuel-saving measures such as car-free Sundays, and Britain had a three-day working week from January to March 1974. Unemployment across the EEC rose from the low levels of the 1960s to above 10 per cent by the mid-1980s, with the sharpest increases in heavy industry such as shipbuilding, steel and coal. Stagflation combined stagnant output with rising prices, so governments abandoned full employment as an aim and made defeating inflation their main goal. The crisis led France to the Messmer Plan for nuclear power, and it strengthened the case for the European Monetary System of 1979.",
