    "label": "2",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "I agree. Charles Stewart Parnell's achievements between 1875 and 1891 were large: he built a disciplined national party, led the Land War and brought Gladstone to Home Rule. His failures were just as significant, because he never secured Home Rule and his own conduct split the movement and wasted a decade.\n\nParnell's first major achievement was to turn a loose group of Home Rule MPs into a disciplined force. Isaac Butt's party of about 60 had no whip and little organisation, and Butt's polite motions were always crushed. Parnell joined Joseph Biggar in obstruction from 1877, jamming Commons business, and in 1881 Irish MPs obstructed the coercion bill for about 41 hours until the Speaker intervened. He became chairman of the Irish Parliamentary Party in 1880. In 1884 candidates were required to sign a pledge to sit, act and vote with the party, and constituency conventions chose them. By 1885 the party held the balance of power at Westminster, which no Irish party had managed before.\n\nSecond, he united the forces of nationalism and gave the movement a mass base. Through the New Departure he drew Fenians such as Devoy and Davitt into co-operation with constitutional politics, and as president of the Land League from 1879 he tied the tenant-farmer movement to the national cause. His American tour in 1880 raised funds and publicity, and the National League of 1882 put a branch in nearly every parish. The Land War produced the 1881 Land Act, which conceded fair rent, fixity of tenure and free sale. Parnell's own record here is mixed, since he insisted on testing the Act and was jailed in Kilmainham, but the agitation he led forced the government to act, and the Kilmainham understanding of May 1882 brought the Arrears Act.\n\nThird, Parnell made Home Rule a serious question for British politics. His party's strength in the 1885 election, when it won about 85 seats, helped convince Gladstone to adopt Home Rule, and the first Home Rule Bill followed in 1886. His alliance with the Liberals was the foundation of that policy. His reputation reached its height in 1889 when the forgeries of Richard Pigott, published by The Times in 1887 to link him to the Phoenix Park murders, collapsed under cross-examination. Pigott fled to Madrid and shot himself, and Parnell was cheered in the Commons and later won damages from The Times.\n\nYet his failures were serious. The 1886 Bill was defeated in the Commons, and the Second Bill of 1893 was thrown out by the Lords, so that for all his power Parnell never delivered Home Rule. His strategy depended wholly on the goodwill of the Liberals, which left him exposed when they turned against him. He also never resolved the problem of Ulster unionism, and his policy on land was ambiguous, hostile to the Plan of Campaign yet closely tied to agitation that bred boycotting and moonlighting, which his unionist enemies used against him.\n\nHis greatest failure was personal. He lived for years with Katharine O'Shea, and when her husband filed for divorce and Parnell did not contest it in November 1890, the Irish Party re-elected him chairman, but Gladstone made clear that he could not lead the Liberals if Parnell stayed. The Catholic bishops condemned Parnell. After six days of debate in Committee Room 15 in December 1890, about 45 MPs walked out and only about 28 stayed with him. Parnell's manifesto attacking the Liberals deepened the bitterness. He lost by-elections in Kilkenny, Sligo and Carlow, married Katharine in June 1891 and died at Brighton that October, aged 45. The party split for a decade and voters became disillusioned.\n\nOverall, both sides of Parnell's career were significant. His achievements created the modern Irish party, a mass movement and a Liberal commitment to Home Rule, while his failures, above all the divorce split and his dependence on the Liberals, showed the limits of his leadership and delayed Home Rule. I therefore agree that his achievements and failures were both significant, with the achievements the more lasting.",
