    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "James Connolly and James Larkin both made major contributions to the Irish labour movement, and they were complementary. Larkin organised unskilled workers on a mass scale and led them in the great struggles of 1907 and 1913, while Connolly gave the movement its political direction and ideas and tied it to the national question.\n\nLarkin's first contribution was to bring trade unionism to unskilled workers, whom the craft unions had ignored. Born in Liverpool to Irish parents, he came to Ireland as an organiser for the National Union of Dock Labourers. In 1907 he led a strike of Belfast dockers and carters that united Catholic and Protestant workers for a time. After breaking with his British union he founded the Irish Transport and General Workers' Union in Dublin in 1909, with Liberty Hall as its headquarters. The union won disputes in Dublin and Cork, and in 1911 Larkin founded the weekly Irish Worker to give workers a voice.\n\nSecond, Larkin introduced a militant style of unionism, known as Larkinism. He used the sympathetic strike and the refusal of union members to handle goods from firms in dispute, and he was a powerful speaker who inspired Dublin workers who had lived in tenements with high death rates and casual, low-paid labour. His most famous campaign was the lockout of 1913. After William Martin Murphy dismissed ITGWU members in August and tram workers struck on 26 August, Larkin defied a ban on a meeting in Sackville Street, and on 31 August police baton-charged the crowd in what was called Bloody Sunday. About 20,000 workers were locked out when employers demanded that they sign the Document, a pledge to leave the union. Larkin kept the workers out for about five months, helped by food ships from British unions.\n\nThe outcome of 1913 was a defeat, as the British TUC refused sympathetic action and hunger forced men back in January 1914. Larkin's intransigence may have made compromise harder, and he left for the United States in October 1914. Even so, the ITGWU survived and the lockout left a lasting legacy, including the Irish Citizen Army, founded in November 1913 to protect pickets, and a labour movement that had become a political force in Dublin.\n\nConnolly's contribution was different, and was mainly intellectual and political. Born in Edinburgh to Irish parents, he moved to Dublin in 1896 and founded the Irish Socialist Republican Party, with its paper the Workers' Republic. He argued that socialism and national independence were inseparable, so that political freedom without social change would be meaningless. In Labour in Irish History (1910), written after years in the United States organising for socialist groups and the Industrial Workers of the World, he set this out and reinterpreted Irish history from the workers' side.\n\nConnolly also built the organisation of Irish labour. Returning in 1910, he became Ulster organiser of the ITGWU and helped found the Irish Labour Party at the Irish TUC meeting in Clonmel in 1912, so that workers had political representation. During the lockout he was Larkin's lieutenant, and when Larkin was jailed and then left Ireland, Connolly led the union and the Citizen Army, becoming its commander in 1914.\n\nConnolly's final contribution was to bring labour into the national revolution. He led the Citizen Army into the Easter Rising of 1916 and was executed that May. Some historians ask whether this showed that he put nation before class, but it made Irish labour inseparable from the independence movement in later memory.\n\nOverall, Larkin created the mass union movement and showed what unskilled workers could do, while Connolly supplied ideology, a political party and a link to nationalism. Together they transformed the labour movement from a minor force of craft unions into an organised, political and nationally significant movement.",
