    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Between 1870 and 1914 the Gaelic Athletic Association and Douglas Hyde both did a great deal to shape Irish identity. The GAA gave ordinary people a native sporting culture and a mass national organisation, while Hyde provided the intellectual case for de-anglicisation and led the Gaelic League in reviving the Irish language. Both defined Irishness as something distinct from English culture.\n\nThe GAA was founded at Hayes' Hotel in Thurles on 1 November 1884 by Michael Cusack and Maurice Davin, with Davin as president. Irish sport was then dominated by English games and by amateur rules that excluded labourers and artisans, and hurling had declined since the Famine. Cusack called for a national body in an article in United Ireland in October 1884. The GAA's aim was to revive Irish games and open competition to all classes, so sport became an expression of national self-respect, not something imitated from England.\n\nSecond, the GAA gained legitimacy and reach through powerful patrons and a parish-based structure. Archbishop Croke of Cashel, a supporter of the tenants, agreed to be patron in December 1884 and attacked the imitation of English games and manners, while Parnell and Davitt also agreed to be patrons, linking it to the Home Rule and land movements. Clubs were organised by parish, with county committees and a Central Council, which suited the Church's own structure, and many priests became club officials. Davin's rules of 1885 gave hurling and Gaelic football a national code, and the first All-Ireland championships were held in 1887.\n\nThird, the GAA deliberately separated Irish and English identity through its bans. Members were prohibited from playing or attending foreign games such as rugby, cricket and soccer, and members of the RIC and the British forces were barred. This made sporting loyalties a statement about national allegiance. The IRB also joined in large numbers and Dublin Castle saw the GAA as a front for Fenianism, which tied the association to separatist politics. The GAA nearly collapsed after the expensive American tour of 1888 and the Parnell split of 1890-91, but it recovered through the 1890s, and by 1914 it was one of the most widespread nationalist organisations in the country.\n\nDouglas Hyde's role began in his lecture The Necessity for De-Anglicising Ireland, delivered to the National Literary Society in Dublin on 25 November 1892. He argued that Ireland had been anglicised in language, names, dress, games and ideas, and that the Irish were failing both to be English and to be Irish. He pointed to the decline of Irish as a spoken language since the Famine and urged that it be cultivated and used in schools. Born in Roscommon, the son of a Church of Ireland rector, he learned Irish from local people, and his Love Songs of Connacht (1893) showed the richness of the tradition.\n\nHyde then turned the argument into action. The Gaelic League was founded in Dublin on 31 July 1893 by Hyde, Eoin MacNeill and others, with Hyde as president. It was officially non-political and non-sectarian, and welcomed Catholics, Protestants, nationalists and unionists. It sent travelling teachers into rural areas, ran classes, organised feiseanna and from 1897 the Oireachtas, and had several hundred branches by 1904. Its paper An Claidheamh Soluis began in 1899, and it won the use of Irish in primary schools under the Revised Programme of 1900 and compulsory Irish for matriculation to the National University, decided in 1910. It also made Irish names and the celebration of St Patrick's Day popular.\n\nThe limits of Hyde's influence show how contested identity was. D.P. Moran and his paper The Leader, from 1900, defined the nation as Gaelic and Catholic, a narrower view than Hyde's. Many IRB men joined the League after about 1910 and pushed it towards politics, and Hyde resigned in 1915 when it declared for independence.\n\nOverall, the GAA and Hyde shaped Irish identity in complementary ways. The GAA gave it a mass, sporting and organisational form, while Hyde gave it a language and a programme for rejecting anglicisation. Between them they produced a cultural nationalism that shaped the generation that went on to lead the revolution.",
