    "label": "1",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The Sinn Féin movement developed from a small, marginal party into the dominant force in Irish nationalism between 1912 and 1923. In that period it changed its aims from a dual monarchy to a republic, won a landslide election, set up its own government and then split over the Treaty. Its rise owed much to events it did not control, but it used them skilfully.\n\nIn 1912 Sinn Féin was a small party. Arthur Griffith had founded it in 1905 on economic self-reliance, abstention from Westminster and a dual monarchy of Britain and Ireland on the Austro-Hungarian model. The big political question was the Third Home Rule Bill, introduced in April 1912, and Redmond's Irish Parliamentary Party was the main voice of nationalism. Sinn Féin played no leading part in the Volunteers, founded in November 1913, or in the Rising of 1916. Even so, the British called the Rising the Sinn Féin rebellion, and this gave the name to the whole separatist movement and made it the natural home for the survivors.\n\nThe rise began in 1917. The executions of the leaders, the release of the Frongoch prisoners at Christmas 1916 and a general amnesty in June 1917 provided a pool of activists. Count Plunkett won North Roscommon in February 1917, Joseph McGuinness won South Longford in May by 37 votes under the slogan 'Put him in to get him out', and de Valera won East Clare in July by a large majority. The death of Thomas Ashe after forcible feeding in September 1917 produced huge crowds at his funeral. At the Ard Fheis of October 1917 de Valera replaced Griffith as president, and the movement adopted a compromise constitution aiming at an independent republic, after which the people could choose their form of government. This united republicans, Griffith's followers, Gaelic Leaguers and young priests in one organisation.\n\nThe conscription crisis of 1918 was the next turning point. The Military Service Act of 18 April 1918 threatened to extend conscription to Ireland, and nearly all nationalist opinion united in resistance, with the bishops backing a pledge signed at church doors and a one-day general strike on 23 April. Sinn Féin took the credit. The government never applied conscription, but its arrest of about 70 Sinn Féin leaders in May on the claim of a German Plot removed the older leaders and made martyrs of them. Redmond's death in March 1918 had already weakened the Irish Parliamentary Party.\n\nThe general election of 14 December 1918 confirmed the transformation. The franchise had been widened so that the electorate rose from about 700,000 to about 1.9 million. Sinn Féin won 73 of the 105 seats, with about 47 per cent of the vote, while the Unionists won 26 and the Irish Parliamentary Party only six. Constance Markievicz, elected from Holloway prison, became the first woman elected to the House of Commons. The party had won a democratic mandate for its republic, though the vote was not a vote for war.\n\nSinn Féin then acted as a government. The First Dáil met at the Mansion House on 21 January 1919 and adopted a Declaration of Independence and the Democratic Programme. De Valera was elected Príomh Aire in April 1919, Collins organised the Dáil Loan, and Dáil courts and republican police replaced British institutions in many areas. Sinn Féin won most of the councils in the 1920 local elections. The Volunteers swore allegiance to the Dáil in August 1920 and became the IRA, whose guerrilla campaign, with flying columns, Collins's intelligence network and events such as Bloody Sunday, led Britain to a truce on 11 July 1921. The movement now had both an elected assembly and an army, and it drew its strength from both.\n\nThe final phase was division. The Treaty of December 1921 gave dominion status and an oath of allegiance, and it split the Dáil, Sinn Féin and the IRA. A pact between Collins and de Valera in May 1922 to run a joint panel of candidates was repudiated by Collins on the eve of the poll. The Civil War began in June 1922 and ended when Frank Aiken ordered the arms dump on 24 May 1923. In the August 1923 election the pro-Treaty Cumann na nGaedheal, founded that year, won 63 seats, and the Republicans won 44 but abstained.\n\nOverall, Sinn Féin developed through three stages: a marginal party before 1916, a mass movement with a mandate from 1917 to 1921, and a broken movement after the Treaty. Its success depended on its ability to absorb the energy of 1916, but the compromise that held it together in 1917 could not survive the Treaty.",
