    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Between 1922 and 1949 successive governments tried hard to make independent Ireland a Gaelic and Catholic society, and they were far more successful in the second aim than the first. The Catholic ethos was written into law and the constitution and was accepted by most of the population, while the Gaelic aim produced a wide knowledge of Irish through the schools but very little everyday use of the language.\n\nThe Free State made the revival of Irish the centre of its cultural policy and used the schools as its main instrument. Irish became a compulsory subject in national schools in 1922, and many infant classes were taught wholly through Irish. A pass in Irish was made necessary for state examinations at second level and for entry to the civil service and the Garda, so that the language was tied to jobs, and Ernest Blythe pressed civil servants to learn it. Preparatory colleges trained teachers who could teach through Irish. The Gaeltacht Commission, set up in 1925, defined the Irish-speaking districts and recommended support for them, and An Gúm was founded in 1926 to publish books in Irish and supply school texts. Taibhdhearc na Gaillimhe opened in Galway in 1928, and the 1937 constitution declared Irish the first official language.\n\nThe results were limited. Census returns showed a rise in those able to speak Irish from about 18 per cent in 1926 to about a quarter in 1936, but this came mostly from schooling and did not mean that people used it at home. The real Irish-speaking districts, the Gaeltacht, continued to shrink because of emigration and poverty, and compulsion was resented by many parents and teachers while some pupils learned little. Irish remained a powerful symbol of national identity and the state kept it as a school subject, but as a spoken language the revival largely failed.\n\nThe state also encouraged a wider Gaelic culture. The Tailteann Games were held in 1924, 1928 and 1932, the Abbey Theatre received a state subsidy from 1925, 2RN began broadcasting in 1926, and the Irish Folklore Commission was set up in 1935, with children collecting local folklore for the Schools' Collection of 1937 to 1938. The GAA's Rule 27 banned its members from playing foreign games such as soccer and rugby, and in 1938 it removed Douglas Hyde as patron after he attended a soccer match. These measures built a strong sense of cultural identity, although they were not popular with everyone, and the Gate Theatre, founded in 1928, showed that international influences persisted.\n\nThe Catholic character of the state was achieved far more fully. The Church ran most schools, with national schools managed by local clergy while the state paid the teachers and set the curriculum, and secondary education was largely in the hands of religious orders. The state accepted this role, and John Charles McQuaid, Archbishop of Dublin from 1940, became an influential voice on education and social issues. Legislation followed Catholic teaching. The Censorship of Films Act of 1923 and the Censorship of Publications Act of 1929 allowed films and books to be banned, and writers such as Samuel Beckett, Kate O'Brien and Seán Ó Faoláin were among those banned. In 1925 the Dáil stopped private bills for divorce, the Public Dance Halls Act of 1935 responded to clerical concerns about dances, and the Criminal Law Amendment Act of 1935 banned the sale and import of contraceptives.\n\nThe 1937 constitution gave this ethos its fullest expression. Article 41 recognised the family as the foundation of society, spoke of the woman's life within the home and banned divorce, and Article 44 recognised the special position of the Catholic Church as guardian of the faith of the majority, while also naming other denominations. De Valera drafted it with advice from clergy, including the Archbishop of Dublin. The result was a state in which the majority religion shaped law and education with little opposition, although the cost was borne by minorities. The Protestant population of the Free State fell in the 1920s and 1930s, partly through emigration, the Ne Temere rule on mixed marriages and Civil War violence, and Yeats spoke in the Seanad in defence of the minority in 1925.\n\nIn conclusion, the Gaelic and Catholic ideal succeeded unevenly. The Catholic side was a real success, in that Church and state worked together and the constitution, the censorship laws and the school system reflected it. The Gaelic side achieved its goal of making Irish a symbol of identity and an education requirement, but not its goal of making Ireland an Irish-speaking country. The ideal also excluded many Protestants and urban people, so even its successes were partial.",
