    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "I have chosen the Apprentice Boys of Derry and Margaret Thatcher. The Apprentice Boys provoked controversy because their parades commemorated a Protestant victory beside a Catholic district, and Margaret Thatcher provoked it because her uncompromising stand on the hunger strikes and then the Anglo-Irish Agreement angered republicans, nationalists and unionists in turn.\n\nThe Apprentice Boys of Derry were founded in 1814 to commemorate the siege of 1688 to 1689, when thirteen apprentice boys shut the city gates against the regiment of the Catholic King James II. The siege lasted 105 days and was ended on 28 July 1689 when the ship Mountjoy broke the boom across the Foyle. Governor Robert Lundy, who favoured negotiating, became a byword for betrayal, and his effigy is burned each December at the Shutting of the Gates commemoration, a custom said to date from 1832. Membership was confined to Protestant men, and the Memorial Hall in Society Street, opened in 1877, became its centre. For unionists the relief of 1689 represented the defence of Protestant liberty, but the slogan 'No Surrender' and the banners and crimson flags struck nationalists as triumphalism.\n\nThe controversy grew from the city's geography and politics. Derry had a Catholic majority, yet unionists controlled the Londonderry Corporation through gerrymandering, and the August parade of the Relief of Derry marched along the walls above the Bogside, a Catholic and working-class district. Nationalists saw the parade as a celebration of conquest and Protestant ascendancy, and the route was a recurring point of dispute. The issue exploded on 12 August 1969, when stone-throwing and clashes with the RUC began as the parade passed the Bogside. The Battle of the Bogside lasted until 14 August, residents built barricades and declared Free Derry, and British troops were deployed. The Apprentice Boys said it was a legal, traditional march and that others began the disorder, while nationalists called it a provocation. Either way, the parade became tied to the outbreak of the Troubles, and the authorities restricted or rerouted the parades in some years afterwards.\n\nMargaret Thatcher, Prime Minister from May 1979 to November 1990, put security and the defeat of terrorism at the centre of her Northern Ireland policy, and her record caused repeated controversy. The first major test was the H-Block protest. Special category status had been ended on 1 March 1976, and Thatcher declared 'crime is crime is crime; it is not political' and refused to restore it. Bobby Sands began a hunger strike on 1 March 1981 and died on 5 May after 66 days, nine others followed, making ten deaths in all, and the strike ended on 3 October without political status. Nationalists accused her of intransigence. The strike also backfired politically: Sands was elected MP for Fermanagh and South Tyrone, Owen Carron won the by-election, and Sinn Féin adopted its electoral strategy.\n\nHer response to the New Ireland Forum angered nationalists again. The Forum report of May 1984 offered three options, a unitary state, a federal or confederal state and joint authority, and on 19 November 1984 she rejected all three with the words 'Out, out, out'. Her government had meanwhile come under direct attack, as the IRA bombed the Grand Hotel in Brighton on 12 October 1984, killing five people while she escaped. The IRA also killed Lord Mountbatten in August 1979.\n\nThen, in a reversal, she signed the Anglo-Irish Agreement with Garret FitzGerald at Hillsborough Castle on 15 November 1985, giving the Irish government a formal consultative role in Northern Ireland through an Intergovernmental Conference. It was the biggest British policy shift since Sunningdale, and unionists were outraged at not being consulted. Ian Paisley and James Molyneaux led the 'Ulster says No' campaign, with a rally at Belfast City Hall on 23 November 1985 and the resignation of fifteen unionist MPs from Westminster in January 1986. Historians differ on her motives, some stressing improved security cooperation with Dublin, others the aim of stopping the growth of Sinn Féin, and she later acknowledged that the security cooperation she had hoped for did not materialise.\n\nBoth the Apprentice Boys and Thatcher show how symbols and decisions could divide the communities. The Apprentice Boys' parades were a controversy of identity and territory, their commemoration read as pride by one side and as triumphalism by the other, while Thatcher's controversies arose from her hard line, then her shift, each of which alienated a different community.",
