    "model": "Bismarck was Chancellor of Germany from 1871 to 1890, and his record shows great strengths and real weaknesses. His strengths were a diplomatic mastery that kept Germany at peace and free of a two-front war, and the creation of a working unified state with pioneering social insurance. His weaknesses were a diplomatic system that depended on him alone, domestic campaigns against Catholics and socialists that failed, and a constitution that left parliament weak and the Reich divided.\n\nHis greatest strength was his diplomacy. After the Treaty of Frankfurt of 1871 took Alsace and part of Lorraine from France, Bismarck declared Germany a satiated power and set out to keep France isolated, since he assumed revanche would make it an enemy. He built the Three Emperors' League of 1873 with Austria-Hungary and Russia, and chaired the Congress of Berlin in 1878 as an honest broker, defusing the Balkan crisis without a European war. He signed the Dual Alliance with Austria-Hungary in 1879, added Italy in the Triple Alliance of 1882, and in 1887 made the secret Reinsurance Treaty with Russia. Germany fought no war after 1871, and Europe respected him as a master diplomat.\n\nA second strength was his management of the new state. The Empire had been proclaimed at Versailles in January 1871, and under Bismarck it gained a common currency, the mark, in 1873. He handled the Reichstag skilfully, relying on the National Liberals until 1878 and then, after the tariff of 1879 protected iron, steel and agriculture, on the Conservatives and the Centre Party. In 1887 the Reichstag rejected the army bill, but he called an election and the Kartell of Conservatives and National Liberals won a majority. This showed his skill in controlling a parliament that could not dismiss him.\n\nHis social insurance schemes were a third strength. To win workers away from socialism he introduced sickness insurance in 1883, accident insurance in 1884 and old age and invalidity insurance in 1889, making Germany the first major state to create such a system, which other countries later copied. However, the schemes were limited in coverage and benefits and failed in their political purpose, since the SPD vote kept rising.\n\nIn domestic policy his weaknesses are clear. In the Kulturkampf of the 1870s he attacked the Catholic Church, but it failed to break the Church, strengthened the Centre Party and was largely abandoned. After attempts on the Kaiser's life in 1878 he passed the Anti-Socialist Law, banning socialist organisations, meetings and newspapers, but socialists could still stand for the Reichstag, and by 1890 the SPD won about 1.4 million votes and 35 seats. Historians such as Wehler argue that this negative integration, uniting the Reich against supposed enemies, left a divided society and a weak parliamentary tradition, with a Chancellor responsible to the Kaiser rather than the Reichstag.\n\nHis foreign system also had weaknesses. It was so complicated that it depended on Bismarck personally, since the Reinsurance Treaty and the Dual Alliance pulled in opposite directions and a successor could not keep all the balls in the air. The Dual Alliance committed Germany to Austria-Hungary in a Balkan region where Habsburg and Russian interests clashed. Russians felt cheated at the Congress of Berlin, and his ban on Russian bonds in 1887 pushed Russia towards France for loans, which helped bring the two powers together. His fall in 1890 revealed these limits. He clashed with the young Wilhelm II over the anti-socialist laws and resigned on 18 March 1890, and the Reinsurance Treaty was not renewed.\n\nOverall, Bismarck's strengths outweighed his weaknesses in his own lifetime, because he united Germany, kept the peace and built a system of diplomacy that Europe respected. But his weaknesses were serious, since they left an unreformed constitution, a divided society and an alliance system that his successors could not manage.",
