    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "International tensions were a central cause of the outbreak of war in 1914. In the twenty years before it Europe divided into two rival alliance blocs, the Great Powers clashed over colonies and naval strength, and the Balkans became the point where Austria-Hungary and Russia collided. These tensions made every crisis a test of prestige, so a local assassination at Sarajevo became a continental war.\n\nThe alliance system hardened Europe into two camps. After Bismarck's fall Germany let the Reinsurance Treaty lapse in 1890, and Russia turned to France, producing the Franco-Russian Alliance, ratified in January 1894. This gave Germany the danger of a war on two fronts, which later shaped the Schlieffen Plan. Britain ended its isolation after the Boer War with the Anglo-Japanese Alliance of 1902, the Entente Cordiale of 1904 and the Anglo-Russian Entente of 1907. Germany spoke of encirclement while Britain and France feared German ambitions. Because of these links a quarrel between two states could draw in the rest.\n\nColonial rivalry then tested the blocs. Britain and France nearly came to blows at Fashoda in 1898 but settled their differences in 1904. Germany challenged the new Entente over Morocco. Wilhelm II landed at Tangier in March 1905, but at the Algeciras Conference in 1906 only Austria-Hungary supported Germany. In 1911 the gunboat Panther was sent to Agadir and Lloyd George warned at the Mansion House that Britain would not be ignored. Germany accepted French control of Morocco in return for part of the French Congo, and German opinion saw this as a humiliation. The crises drew Britain and France closer and left Germany feeling isolated and resentful.\n\nThe Anglo-German naval race poisoned relations further. Under Wilhelm II and Tirpitz, Germany pursued Weltpolitik and built a large battle fleet, which Britain saw as a direct threat to its security and its empire. Britain built more warships and concentrated its navy nearer home, relying on its new friends to cover distant waters. Haldane's mission to Berlin in 1912 failed, and Britain and France agreed a division of naval responsibilities that year. The race made Britain and Germany suspicious of each other and made it harder for Britain to stay out of a continental war.\n\nThe most dangerous tension lay in the Balkans. Serbia sought to unite the South Slavs, which threatened Austria-Hungary, while Russia saw itself as protector of the Slavs. When Austria annexed Bosnia in October 1908, Russia felt cheated and Serbia was furious. Germany forced Russia to back down in March 1909, and Russia resolved not to be humiliated again and accelerated its rearmament. The Balkan Wars of 1912 and 1913 doubled the size of Serbia, alarmed Austria-Hungary and led Conrad von Hotzendorf to press for war against it. Each side feared that backing down would cost it Great Power status.\n\nThese tensions came together in the July crisis. After Gavrilo Princip shot Franz Ferdinand on 28 June 1914, Germany gave Austria-Hungary its blank cheque on 5 and 6 July, and Austria's ultimatum of 23 July was answered by Serbia with most of the demands accepted but not all. Austria declared war on 28 July, Russia mobilised, and Germany declared war on Russia on 1 August and on France on 3 August. The Schlieffen Plan meant the invasion of Belgium on 4 August, and Britain, bound by the Treaty of London of 1839 and anxious about the balance of power, declared war that day. In my judgement international tensions made the war possible but not certain. Many colonial quarrels had been settled peacefully and the Balkan crises of 1908 and 1913 had been contained. What changed in 1914 was that Austria-Hungary and Germany chose to risk war while Russia and France were determined not to give way, and the rival alliances, the naval race and the Balkan rivalries made those choices lead to a general war.",
