    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Cinema between the wars was shaped by three very different figures. Charlie Chaplin made comedy into a serious art and used it to comment on society and on dictatorship. Leni Riefenstahl showed that film could be an instrument of political propaganda, while Bing Crosby showed how film, radio and popular song could combine in mass entertainment.\n\nChaplin's first contribution was the creation of the Tramp, the most famous screen character of the silent era. Born in London in 1889, he trained in the music hall before going to the United States and becoming a film star. The Tramp first appeared in 1914 and was developed in The Kid (1921) and The Gold Rush (1925). Chaplin showed that slapstick could carry real emotion, mixing comedy with pathos so that audiences laughed at the poor little man and sympathised with him. His precise physical timing and mime needed no translation, so his films appealed to audiences all over the world.\n\nHis second contribution was to use film to examine society and confront tyranny. City Lights (1931) was released when talking pictures had already spread after The Jazz Singer of 1927, yet Chaplin stayed with a largely silent form and made a great success of it, proving that his art could survive the arrival of sound. Modern Times (1936) satirised industrial life, with the Tramp caught in the machinery of a factory, and spoke to audiences who knew unemployment and the pressures of mechanisation in the Depression. In The Great Dictator (1940), his first full sound film, he played both a Jewish barber and Adenoid Hynkel, ruler of Tomania, a parody of Hitler. It was a major commercial success in the United States and brought the danger of Nazism to a huge audience through ridicule. Chaplin later said he would not have made it had he known the full horror of the camps.\n\nLeni Riefenstahl's contribution was of a different kind, since she turned film into propaganda. Born in Berlin in 1902, she began as a dancer and actress and made mountain films before Hitler commissioned her to film the party's rallies. Her Triumph of the Will (1935) recorded the 1934 Nuremberg Rally, the first after the Night of the Long Knives, and showed mass parades of the party, the SA, the SS and the army. It won the German National Film Prize in 1935 and a gold medal at the Paris exposition of 1937. Through her camera work she gave the regime its most powerful visual image and carried the rallies to cinema audiences in Germany and abroad.\n\nRiefenstahl was also a technical innovator. Her film Olympia (1938), covering the 1936 Berlin Olympics, used moving cameras, unusual angles and dramatic editing to present athletic achievement as national triumph. The techniques she pioneered have been copied in sports filming ever since, and her films remain among the most studied examples of film propaganda. After 1945 she claimed that her work was art and denied knowledge of the regime's crimes; she was classified as a fellow traveller in 1952, and historians still debate where art ends and propaganda begins in her work.\n\nBing Crosby's contribution lay in popular entertainment. An American singer and actor, he made his relaxed crooning style famous through radio and records before becoming a major film star. He made the Road films with Bob Hope from 1940 and won the Academy Award for Best Actor for Going My Way (1944). His recording of White Christmas, written by Irving Berlin and introduced in the film Holiday Inn in 1942, became one of the best-selling songs of all time, and its longing for home made it hugely popular with troops. Crosby also entertained Allied forces through radio broadcasts and a tour of Britain and France in 1944, raising morale and spreading American popular culture across Europe.\n\nOverall, Chaplin made the greatest contribution to cinema as an art, creating a character of universal appeal and daring to confront dictatorship through humour. Riefenstahl showed most disturbingly how powerfully film could shape political opinion, while Crosby helped build the modern entertainment industry in which film, radio and music worked together.",
