    "model": "Europe moved a considerable way towards unity between 1945 and 1973, but the unity was mainly economic and confined to Western Europe. It was driven by the wish to prevent another Franco-German war, the Soviet threat and the need for recovery, but its extent was limited by national sovereignty, the failure of defence union and the opposition of de Gaulle.\n\nThe first reason was the wish to end the rivalry between France and Germany, which had caused three wars in seventy years. Winston Churchill, speaking at Zurich in September 1946, called for a kind of United States of Europe built on reconciliation between the two. The Hague Congress of May 1948 led to the Council of Europe in May 1949, founded by ten states with its seat at Strasbourg. This body went only a short way. It could debate but not make binding law, which disappointed federalists such as Spinelli and Spaak, since the British and Scandinavian governments preferred cooperation between states. Its most lasting achievement was the European Convention on Human Rights, opened for signature in 1950.\n\nThe second reason was the Cold War and the need for recovery. Soviet domination of Eastern Europe and the division of Germany made Western Europeans look for strength in cooperation. The USA backed integration, and Marshall Aid from 1948 required recipients to plan together through the Organisation for European Economic Co-operation. Western Europe's economies were damaged and dependent on dollars, and pooling resources promised faster recovery.\n\nThese motives produced the first real step in the Schuman Declaration of 9 May 1950, largely drafted by Jean Monnet, which proposed putting French and West German coal and steel under a common authority. The Treaty of Paris of April 1951 created the European Coal and Steel Community, signed by France, West Germany, Italy and the Benelux countries, which came into force in 1952. Its High Authority was supranational, able to set prices and quotas and fine firms without the approval of governments. Since coal and steel were the raw materials of war, pooling them made war between France and Germany harder, and Adenauer welcomed it because West Germany was treated as an equal. Britain's Labour government declined to join, which showed the limits of the movement.\n\nThe limits were clear in 1954, when the French National Assembly rejected the European Defence Community on 30 August, fearing German rearmament and loss of sovereignty. Defence stayed national and West Germany joined NATO in 1955. Integration turned to economics. After the Messina Conference of 1955, the Treaties of Rome were signed on 25 March 1957 and created the European Economic Community from 1 January 1958. It aimed at a customs union and free movement of goods, services, capital and workers, and it completed its customs union in July 1968, eighteen months early. The Common Agricultural Policy of 1962 gave farmers common prices. Britain and its partners in EFTA, founded in 1960, chose a looser association, so Europe was divided economically as well.\n\nThe extent of unity was most sharply tested by Charles de Gaulle, who wanted a Europe of nations rather than a supranational one. He vetoed British membership on 14 January 1963 and again in November 1967. In June 1965 France began the empty chair crisis, boycotting the Council of Ministers over the funding of the CAP and the powers of the Commission, and it ended with the Luxembourg Compromise of January 1966, which gave every state a veto on vital national interests. This weakened majority voting and the federalist vision of Monnet. Only after de Gaulle's departure did The Hague summit of December 1969 agree on enlargement, and Britain, Ireland and Denmark joined on 1 January 1973, Ireland after a referendum in which about 83 percent voted in favour.\n\nOverall, Europe achieved substantial unity by 1973: reconciliation between France and West Germany, an economic community that grew from six to nine members and rapid growth in trade. But it was unity only of Western Europe, centred on economics, and national governments kept their sovereignty, so the move was real but limited.",
