    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Germany was divided because the wartime Allies agreed to occupy it in separate zones and their conflicting aims meant that the zones never came back together. Berlin became the focus of Cold War tension because it lay deep inside the Soviet zone yet was shared by all four powers, so it was a Western outpost that the Soviet Union tried repeatedly to remove.\n\nThe starting point was wartime planning. The Allies agreed occupation zones in 1944, and at Yalta in February 1945 Churchill, Roosevelt and Stalin confirmed that Germany would be divided, with the Soviet Union taking the east, the USA the south and centre-west, Britain the north-west and France the south-west. Berlin was itself divided into four sectors. At Potsdam in July 1945 Truman, Stalin and later Attlee set the aims of demilitarisation, denazification, democratisation and decentralisation, with Germany to be run as one economic unit. These were meant to be temporary arrangements, but the Red Army held the east, and that fact shaped what the West could demand.\n\nIn practice each power ran its own zone, and the dispute over reparations pulled them apart. The Soviet Union dismantled factories and shipped machinery east, and it demanded reparations from the industrial Ruhr, which the Western powers refused because they wanted their zones to recover. By 1947 the Western powers concluded that a single German economy could not be restored, and the Allied Control Council in Berlin achieved little. Behind this lay a clash of aims, since Stalin wanted a weak Germany and a buffer of friendly states after the invasions of 1914 and 1941, while the West wanted a prosperous one. Churchill's Iron Curtain speech in 1946, the Truman Doctrine and the Marshall Plan in 1947 hardened the split, and Stalin forbade eastern states to accept Marshall aid.\n\nThe Western zones now moved towards a state of their own. The American and British zones merged into Bizonia on 1 January 1947, and the French zone was added in 1948-49. The decisive step was the currency reform of 20 June 1948, when the Western zones introduced the Deutsche Mark. Stalin saw this as the creation of a West German state, and he blockaded road, rail and canal access to West Berlin to force the Western powers to drop the currency and their plans, or to drive them out of the city. They refused. General Clay and the Berlin mayor Ernst Reuter led the resistance, and the airlift flew food and fuel into Tempelhof, Gatow and Tegel, delivering over two million tons before the blockade ended on 12 May 1949. Stalin had failed, and the division became formal with the Federal Republic founded on 23 May under Adenauer and the German Democratic Republic proclaimed on 7 October. West Berlin was tied to the Federal Republic and East Berlin became the capital of the GDR.\n\nBerlin remained the most dangerous flashpoint because West Berlin was an island of freedom and prosperity inside East Germany. It was a window to the West through which millions of East Germans escaped, draining the GDR of skilled people and embarrassing communism. On 27 November 1958 Khrushchev issued an ultimatum giving the Western powers six months to agree to end their occupation of West Berlin. At the Vienna summit in June 1961 he pressed Kennedy hard on the question, and the crisis deepened over the summer. On 13 August 1961 East German forces began building the Berlin Wall, closing the border between the two halves of the city. The West did not use force, accepting the Wall as the price of avoiding war, and people who tried to cross over the following decades were shot or arrested. The Wall was a propaganda gift to the West, but it ended the crisis by stabilising the division.\n\nOverall, Germany was divided because Allied cooperation could not survive the clash between Soviet security fears and Western plans for recovery, and the division was made permanent by the currency reform, the blockade and the founding of two states in 1949. Berlin focused tension because it was the one place where the two systems touched, so every challenge to the division, from the blockade of 1948 to the ultimatum of 1958 and the Wall of 1961, risked a war that neither side wanted.",
