    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Alexander Solzhenitsyn and Simone de Beauvoir each challenged widely held views, Solzhenitsyn the official picture of Soviet communism and de Beauvoir the accepted place of women in society. Both did so through writing, and both met official hostility or ridicule, but both came to be seen as among the most influential voices of the twentieth century.\n\nSolzhenitsyn challenged the Soviet state's claim to have built a just society. Born in 1918, he served as an artillery officer in the Second World War and was arrested in 1945 for criticising Stalin in private letters. He was sentenced to eight years in labour camps and then internal exile in Kazakhstan, and this experience of the Gulag became the basis of his writing. Because he wrote from his own experience, his account carried an authority that official propaganda could not match. His first great success came in 1962, when One Day in the Life of Ivan Denisovich was published in the Soviet journal Novy Mir with Khrushchev's approval and brought the camps into public discussion for the first time, during the thaw after Stalin's death.\n\nAfter Khrushchev fell, censorship tightened. His novels The First Circle and Cancer Ward could be published only abroad in 1968, and he was expelled from the Writers' Union in 1969, though the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1970 gave him an international standing that protected him to some extent. His greatest challenge was The Gulag Archipelago. The KGB seized a copy of the manuscript in 1973, which led him to authorise its publication in Paris, and it documented the whole system of camps through testimony and history. It damaged the reputation of communism among sympathetic Western intellectuals, and the authorities treated him as a traitor. He was arrested on 12 February 1974 and expelled the next day, living first in Switzerland and from 1976 in Vermont. He challenged Western views too, since his Harvard address in 1978 criticised Western materialism as well as communism.\n\nSimone de Beauvoir challenged the view that women's inferior position was natural. Born in Paris in 1908, she passed the competitive agrégation in philosophy in 1929, coming second to Sartre, and was among the first women to teach philosophy in French lycées. Her book The Second Sex, published in 1949, argued that women were defined as the Other by a male-centred society. Its best-known line is that one is not born, but rather becomes, a woman, which separated biological sex from social gender. She argued that women's subordination was created by history, law, religion and education, not by biology, and that women should win economic independence.\n\nThe reaction showed how far she cut against accepted opinion. Many male intellectuals and conservative commentators mocked the book, and the Catholic Church put it on the Index of Forbidden Books in 1956, though it sold very widely and was translated into English in 1953. Younger women read it as a guide to their own position. In the 1970s she joined the Women's Liberation Movement, and in April 1971 she signed the Manifesto of the 343, in which women declared that they had had abortions and called for their legalisation. Historians now treat the book as a founding text of second-wave feminism.\n\nHer challenge went beyond women's issues. She co-founded Les Temps modernes with Sartre in 1945, won the Prix Goncourt in 1954 for The Mandarins, and in 1960 signed the Manifesto of the 121 supporting French conscripts who refused to fight in Algeria. She also challenged conventional morality by living her partnership with Sartre outside marriage.\n\nBoth writers therefore challenged the orthodoxies of their societies, one the claims of a communist state and the other the assumptions of a patriarchal society. Solzhenitsyn's impact was the more immediate, since his work damaged the moral standing of the Soviet system and brought about his expulsion. De Beauvoir's was slower but longer lasting, because her ideas shaped the feminist movement for decades.",
