    "model": "British withdrawal from India in 1947 had causes that built up over decades and became decisive between 1942 and 1946: a strong nationalist movement, Britain's wartime exhaustion, a loss of control over the army and administration, and the communal division between Congress and the Muslim League. Its consequences were independence for two new states, the violent partition of the Punjab and Bengal, and lasting effects on Britain, the Commonwealth and South Asia.\n\nThe nationalist movement was the first cause. The Indian National Congress, founded in 1885, moved under Gandhi from petitioning to mass civil disobedience. The Amritsar massacre of April 1919 turned many Indians against British rule, the Non-Cooperation movement of 1920-22 and the Salt March of 1930 mobilised millions, and after the Government of India Act 1935 Congress won majorities in five of eleven provinces in 1937. The Quit India resolution of August 1942 demanded British withdrawal, and although the leaders were arrested, widespread unrest followed. By 1945 it was clear that Britain could rule India only through repression that British opinion would not accept.\n\nThe Second World War transformed Britain's position. The fall of Singapore in February 1942 destroyed British prestige in Asia, and Japan's slogan of Asia for the Asians encouraged nationalists. Subhas Chandra Bose raised the Indian National Army from prisoners captured at Singapore, and the trials of its officers at the Red Fort in 1945-46 caused a public outcry, making punishment politically dangerous. The Bengal famine of 1943, in which two to three million are estimated to have died, further damaged British reputation. Meanwhile Britain was financially drained, owing India over one billion pounds in sterling balances and borrowing about 3.75 billion dollars from the United States in 1945-46, so it could not afford to garrison a hostile India.\n\nThe Labour government of Clement Attlee, elected in a landslide in July 1945, was committed to independence, and events in 1946 showed that the instruments of British control were failing. In February 1946 ratings of the Royal Indian Navy mutinied at Bombay, showing the armed forces could no longer be relied on. The Cabinet Mission of 1946 failed to win agreement between Congress and the League, and communal violence worsened.\n\nThe communal question explains both the timing and the form of withdrawal. The Muslim League under Jinnah adopted the Lahore Resolution in March 1940 and argued that Hindus and Muslims were two nations. It won most Muslim seats in the 1945-46 elections, giving Jinnah a claim to speak for Muslim India. The League's Direct Action Day of 16 August 1946 led to killings in Calcutta that left thousands dead. Attlee announced on 20 February 1947 that British rule would end by June 1948, and Lord Mountbatten, who arrived in March, judged that a united India was impossible. His plan of 3 June 1947 accepted partition and the date was brought forward to 15 August 1947.\n\nThe immediate consequence was independence and partition. The Indian Independence Act of July 1947 created the dominions of India and Pakistan, with Pakistan in two parts separated by about 1,600 km. Cyril Radcliffe, who had never visited India, drew the boundaries through Punjab and Bengal, and the line was announced on 17 August, two days after independence. Between 10 and 15 million people were displaced as Hindus and Sikhs fled into India and Muslims into Pakistan. Estimates of the dead range from about 200,000 to about 2 million. The princely states had to join one dominion or the other, and Kashmir's accession to India in October 1947 led to war and a ceasefire from 1 January 1949 that left the state divided.\n\nThere were political consequences too. Gandhi, who had fasted to stop communal violence, was shot dead in January 1948 by Nathuram Godse, a Hindu nationalist, and Jinnah died in September 1948. India became a republic on 26 January 1950 under Nehru but remained in the Commonwealth by the London Declaration of April 1949, showing that membership did not depend on accepting the monarch as head of state. For Britain the loss of the Raj, which had ruled directly since 1858, reduced its role in Asia and made the Commonwealth its main link with former colonies. Burma and Ceylon became independent in 1948, and the Gold Coast followed as Ghana in 1957, so the precedent encouraged decolonisation elsewhere.\n\nOverall, Britain left because war, debt, nationalist pressure and the unreliability of its own forces made rule impossible, while the communal conflict decided that withdrawal would end in partition. The consequences were a tragedy of mass migration and killing, the creation of two hostile states, and the start of the decline of the British Empire.",
