    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The Arab-Israeli conflict originated in the competing claims of Jewish and Arab nationalism over Palestine, which Britain encouraged and then could not reconcile. Israel survived in the period 1945 to 1956 because it won the war of 1948 and 1949 against its neighbours, because its armed forces were better organised than its enemies, and because of great power and international support, including at the time of the Suez Crisis.\n\nThe origins lay in Zionism, the movement for a Jewish national homeland led politically by Theodor Herzl from the first Zionist Congress in 1897. In the Balfour Declaration of 2 November 1917 Britain supported a national home for the Jewish people in Palestine, provided that the rights of existing non-Jewish communities were not prejudiced. Britain received the Mandate for Palestine, in force from 1923, and Jewish immigration grew sharply, especially after the Nazis came to power in 1933. The Jewish share of the population rose from about 11 per cent in 1922 to roughly 30 per cent by 1939. Arab opposition produced the Arab Revolt of 1936 to 1939, which British forces crushed. The Peel Commission of 1937 proposed partition and Arab leaders rejected it. The White Paper of 1939 limited Jewish immigration to 75,000 over five years, which the Zionists rejected. Britain had made promises it could not keep to both sides.\n\nThe period after 1945 sharpened the conflict. The Holocaust, which killed about six million Jews, intensified the demand for a Jewish state and for the admission of refugees. Britain, with about 100,000 troops in Palestine, refused to admit 100,000 refugees as the Anglo-American Committee of 1946 recommended, and it sent the Exodus refugees back to Europe in 1947. The Irgun under Menachem Begin and Lehi attacked British targets, and the Irgun bombing of the King David Hotel on 22 July 1946 killed 91 people. In 1947 Britain referred the problem to the United Nations. The General Assembly passed Resolution 181 on 29 November 1947 by 33 votes to 13, partitioning Palestine, with the Jewish state given about 55 per cent although Jews were about a third of the population. The Arab states and the Arab Higher Committee rejected it and fighting began in December. Britain's Mandate expired on 14 May 1948, leaving a security gap.\n\nIsrael's survival was first tested in 1948 and 1949. David Ben-Gurion proclaimed the state in Tel Aviv on 14 May 1948, and Egypt, Jordan, Syria, Iraq and Lebanon invaded the following day. Israel survived because the Haganah, the Jewish Agency's defence force, was well organised, and because Jewish forces had already taken Tiberias, Haifa and Jaffa under Plan Dalet in April and May 1948. The United States recognised Israel within minutes and the Soviet Union three days later, and both had supported partition. About 6,000 Israelis died, roughly one per cent of the Jewish population, but the 1949 armistices left Israel with more territory than the UN plan had given it, while Jordan held the West Bank and Egypt administered Gaza. About 700,000 Palestinian Arabs fled or were expelled, and the refugee question became a lasting source of conflict.\n\nThe threat continued after 1949. Egypt barred Israeli ships from the Suez Canal, and fedayeen raids from Gaza led to Israeli reprisals such as the Gaza raid of February 1955. Nasser, who came to power after the revolution of July 1952, made Arab nationalism hostile to Israel and bought arms from the Soviet bloc through Czechoslovakia in 1955. Israel then joined Britain and France in the Protocol of Sevres of 24 October 1956 and invaded Sinai on 29 October. Its forces defeated the Egyptians, and although American and Soviet pressure forced a withdrawal in early 1957, Israel gained assurances about the Straits of Tiran and a UN force, UNEF, on the frontier.\n\nThe conflict therefore originated in the incompatible promises of the Mandate period and was hardened by the Holocaust and by Britain's withdrawal. Israel survived 1945 to 1956 through military strength, superpower recognition and, in 1956, by exploiting Anglo-French action against Nasser, although the refugee problem and Arab hostility remained unresolved.",
