    "marks": 100,
    "model": "President Harry Truman was only partly effective. Abroad he met the Soviet challenge with decisive and lasting policies, and these were his greatest achievements. At home he was far less successful, because a hostile Congress blocked most of his programme, although he did take important steps on civil rights and won a famous election victory in 1948.\n\nTruman came to office on 12 April 1945 with little preparation, and was not even told of the atomic bomb project until he became president. His first domestic problem was the conversion from wartime to peacetime. Strikes swept the country in 1945 and 1946, prices rose after controls ended, and his 21-point programme of September 1945 was largely blocked. In the 1946 midterms the Republicans won both houses of Congress for the first time since 1928 under the slogan Had enough, and the joke To err is Truman captured his unpopularity. In this phase he handled his problems poorly.\n\nHis dealings with Congress remained difficult. The Taft-Hartley Act of 1947, restricting union tactics, was passed over his veto in June 1947. In January 1949 he set out the Fair Deal, calling for national health insurance, a wider Social Security system, a higher minimum wage and public housing. Conservative southern Democrats, voting with Republicans, blocked national health insurance and most of the rest, though the Housing Act of 1949 and a rise in the minimum wage were real successes. Overall the Fair Deal was a limited achievement. The Supreme Court also ruled in Youngstown Sheet and Tube v. Sawyer in 1952 that he had exceeded his powers by seizing the steel mills.\n\nHe was more effective on civil rights. His Committee on Civil Rights reported in October 1947 in To Secure These Rights, and on 26 July 1948 Executive Order 9981 ordered the desegregation of the armed forces. This cost him politically, since southern Democrats walked out in 1948 and formed the Dixiecrats under Strom Thurmond. His courage was rewarded in the election that year, when, with the Democrats split between Thurmond on the right and Henry Wallace on the left, he won 303 electoral votes to Dewey's 189 after a cross-country whistle-stop campaign attacking the do-nothing Congress. That victory showed political skill, even if his legislation did not follow.\n\nAbroad, Truman's record was stronger. He authorised the atomic bombing of Hiroshima and Nagasaki in August 1945, then, guided by Kennan's idea of containment, announced the Truman Doctrine on 12 March 1947 with aid to Greece and Turkey. The Marshall Plan gave about 13 billion dollars to Western Europe between 1948 and 1952, strengthening economies against communism, and NATO was founded in April 1949 on the principle of collective defence. When the Soviet Union blockaded West Berlin from June 1948, he answered with the airlift rather than force and the blockade ended in May 1949. These were successes for American leadership.\n\nHis foreign policy also had failures. The Communist victory in China in October 1949 allowed Republicans to ask Who lost China, and the Soviet atomic bomb of August 1949 ended the American monopoly. In Korea, he sent forces after the invasion of June 1950 without asking Congress for a declaration of war, and after MacArthur's Inchon landing the advance north of the 38th parallel provoked Chinese intervention. He dismissed MacArthur in April 1951, causing a storm at home. The war ended in stalemate in 1953, and about 36,000 Americans died.\n\nThe Cold War also damaged his record at home. His loyalty programme of March 1947 required checks on federal employees and fed the Red Scare, which Senator McCarthy exploited from 1950. By 1952, with Korea, rising prices and scandals in federal agencies, his approval fell to about 22 per cent and he decided not to run again. His domestic troubles were partly the cost of his foreign commitments.\n\nOverall, Truman was effective abroad, where he created the framework of containment that lasted for decades, but only moderately so at home, where he was often frustrated by Congress. He left office deeply unpopular, but historians such as David McCullough have ranked him among the stronger presidents for his decisiveness and his civil rights initiatives.",
