    "label": "2",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "American involvement in Vietnam mattered enormously to both countries. For the United States it was the costliest failure of the Cold War, one that shattered the consensus behind containment, divided society and wrecked a presidency. For Vietnam it meant a war of immense destruction and, in the end, a communist victory that reunited the country on Hanoi's terms.\n\nFor the United States the war was first a test of containment. Truman had paid for most of France's war in Indochina by 1954, and Eisenhower described the domino theory that year, arguing that if Vietnam fell the rest of Southeast Asia would follow. After the Geneva Accords divided the country at the 17th parallel, America backed Ngo Dinh Diem and joined SEATO. Kennedy raised the number of advisers to about 16,000, and Lyndon Johnson, who feared the political damage Truman had suffered over the loss of China, inherited a commitment he believed he could not abandon. Vietnam showed the limits of containment, because a commitment that began with advisers grew to over half a million troops by 1968 without producing victory.\n\nThe war transformed the presidency and American politics. After the disputed Gulf of Tonkin incidents of August 1964 Congress passed the Tonkin Gulf Resolution by 416 votes to 0 in the House and 88 to 2 in the Senate, giving Johnson wide powers without a declaration of war. Rolling Thunder began in March 1965, Marines landed at Da Nang, and troop numbers rose to about 540,000 in 1969. Johnson played the war down in public, and the gap between official optimism and events, the credibility gap, destroyed trust in him. The Tet Offensive of January 1968, although a military defeat for the communists, convinced many Americans that the war could not be won. After the New Hampshire primary Johnson announced on 31 March 1968 that he would not seek re-election. The Pentagon Papers of 1971 and the later Watergate crisis deepened distrust of government, and Congress reacted with the War Powers Resolution of 1973, passed over Nixon's veto.\n\nThe war also divided American society and damaged the economy. The draft, which spared most college students, made the war a burden on poorer and black families, and Muhammad Ali's refusal of induction in 1967 gave resistance a famous face. The anti-war movement grew from the Pentagon march of 1967 to the shooting of four students at Kent State in May 1970. Johnson tried to pay for both the war and the Great Society, a policy of guns and butter, which fed deficits and inflation and forced a tax surcharge in 1968. War spending thus undermined the War on Poverty and contributed to the economic troubles of the 1970s. About 58,000 Americans died, and the defeat left a lasting reluctance to commit forces abroad, known as the Vietnam syndrome.\n\nNixon's handling of the war showed how it distorted policy even in withdrawal. Vietnamisation and the Nixon Doctrine reduced troop numbers from about 540,000 to under 30,000 by late 1972, but the invasion of Cambodia in 1970 and the Christmas bombing of Hanoi in 1972 showed that the war was still being widened to secure a settlement. The Paris Peace Accords of January 1973 allowed American withdrawal, and when North Vietnam's offensive overran the South, Congress refused Ford's request for further aid. Saigon fell on 30 April 1975.\n\nFor Vietnam the importance was even greater. American firepower, including napalm and the defoliant Agent Orange, killed enormous numbers of civilians and devastated the countryside, and the bombing of the North under Rolling Thunder and later Linebacker destroyed much of its infrastructure. Atrocities such as My Lai in March 1968 showed the human cost, and peasants were driven into government camps or towards the Viet Cong. Well over a million Vietnamese died, and the war left the country shattered. The Strategic Hamlet programme and the persecution of Buddhists under Diem had already alienated the South's own people, and the American-backed governments that followed Diem's killing in November 1963 never won real loyalty.\n\nThe political outcome was equally decisive. The elections promised at Geneva for 1956 were never held, and the war settled the question of Vietnam's future by force. After Saigon's fall in 1975 the country was reunited under communist rule, and Cambodia and Laos also came under communist governments that year. Ho Chi Minh and his successors had achieved the national unity they had fought for since the struggle against the French, though at a terrible price.\n\nOverall, the war was a national turning point for both countries. For America it was a humiliating defeat that undermined confidence in presidents and in containment, while for Vietnam it was a catastrophe that nevertheless ended in the unification and independence its leaders had sought. The cost was borne far more heavily by the Vietnamese, but its effects on American politics and foreign policy were profound and lasting.",
