    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Marilyn Monroe and Billy Graham made very different contributions to American life in the post-war years. Monroe was one of the most popular film stars of the 1950s and an enduring image of glamour and of the pressures placed on women, while Graham was the best-known preacher of his age and a leader of the evangelical revival. Both reached mass audiences through the new media and reflected the tensions of the time.\n\nMonroe's film career made her a national and international star. After small roles in the late 1940s she won notice in Asphalt Jungle and All About Eve in 1950, and became a star in 1953 with Gentlemen Prefer Blondes and How to Marry a Millionaire. The Seven Year Itch in 1955 confirmed her as one of the biggest box office attractions of the decade. Her screen image, glamorous, blonde and sexually appealing, was widely copied and shaped the way Hollywood marketed women. Her contribution was therefore to the entertainment industry and to the popular culture that made Hollywood a symbol of American life abroad.\n\nHer image was also closely tied to the consumer culture of the affluent 1950s. She appeared in magazines and advertising that sold products by association with her appeal, and she became a symbol of the glamour of the decade. Yet she also showed the constraints on women. Later feminist critics argued that Hollywood exploited her as a sex symbol and limited women to decorative roles, a view that fits the ideal of femininity that Betty Friedan attacked. Monroe herself fought this typecasting. She studied at the Actors Studio in New York, founded Marilyn Monroe Productions in 1955, married the playwright Arthur Miller in 1956 and won critical praise for her comic performance in Some Like It Hot in 1959. Her death at the age of 36 in August 1962, ruled a probable suicide, made her an enduring legend.\n\nBilly Graham's contribution lay in religion. Born in 1918 and trained as a Baptist minister, he became a national figure after his Los Angeles crusade of 1949, which was extended from three weeks to eight after newspapers reported it widely. He founded the Billy Graham Evangelistic Association in 1950 and began a radio programme, Hour of Decision, and in 1956 he co-founded the magazine Christianity Today. His huge crusades, such as the sixteen weeks at Madison Square Garden in 1957, brought evangelical Protestantism into the mainstream of the religious revival of the Cold War, when faith gave many Americans a common identity against godless communism. Admirers saw him as a unifying figure, while critics saw him as a tool of Cold War patriotism.\n\nGraham also influenced politics and race relations. He was a friend and adviser of Eisenhower and later of Nixon, and he preached at presidential events, which placed religion close to the centre of public life. From 1953 he refused to hold segregated crusades, an early sign of commitment to integration, and he invited Martin Luther King to pray at his New York crusade of 1957, though he was criticised for urging patience and not backing the protests of the early 1960s. His closeness to Nixon compromised him, and after Watergate he said he had been used and would avoid partisan politics. He declined to join Jerry Falwell's Moral Majority in 1979 and warned against mixing religion with party politics. He took his message abroad, to London in 1954 and to Moscow in 1982, and he organised the Lausanne Congress in 1974.\n\nIn conclusion, Monroe and Graham contributed in contrasting ways. Monroe entertained millions and became a symbol of both the glamour and the limits of women's lives, while Graham revived mass evangelicalism and linked faith to national life. Graham's influence on American religion was probably the more lasting, but Monroe's image has proved just as enduring in popular culture.",
