    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Advances in space travel and information technology between 1945 and 1989 were highly significant. They became a field of Cold War rivalry in which the United States sought to prove its superiority, they transformed military and civilian life, and they helped shape the economy and the geography of American industry. The two developments were also linked, since space and military research drove progress in computing.\n\nThe space race began with Soviet successes. The launch of Sputnik on 4 October 1957 alarmed Americans about Soviet technology and education, and it led to the creation of NASA in 1958 and to the National Defense Education Act. Yuri Gagarin became the first man in space in April 1961, which increased the pressure on Kennedy, and on 25 May 1961 he committed the nation to landing a man on the Moon before the end of the decade. The Mercury and Gemini programmes, with Alan Shepard and John Glenn in the early 1960s, prepared the way for Apollo. The significance was that space became a test of national strength and prestige, and the race was run as part of the Cold War.\n\nThe Apollo programme was the largest peacetime technological effort in American history. It was directed by NASA under James Webb with major contractors, and the giant Saturn V rocket flew without losing a crew. Apollo 11 launched on 16 July 1969, and on 20 July Neil Armstrong and Buzz Aldrin landed in the Sea of Tranquillity while Michael Collins orbited above. An audience estimated at 600 million watched on television. The landing was a triumph for the United States over the Soviet Union, whose N1 Moon rocket failed in every test and whose lunar programme was cancelled. It restored American confidence at a time when Vietnam and urban unrest were causing doubt, although critics argued the money would have been better spent on poverty. Interest then declined, and Apollo 17 in December 1972 was the last crewed lunar flight.\n\nSpace technology also had military and civilian effects. Intercontinental missiles such as the Atlas and Minuteman used the same rocketry, and reconnaissance satellites from 1960 let the United States watch Soviet missile sites. Civilian satellites changed daily life, with the weather satellite TIROS 1 in 1960 and Telstar in 1962 relaying live television across the Atlantic. Space spending also tied the growth of the Sun Belt to federal contracts in Florida, Texas, Alabama and California. Later, the Space Shuttle flew in 1981, but the Challenger disaster of January 1986, which killed all seven crew, showed the limits of the programme.\n\nInformation technology was significant in a different way, because it transformed the economy and society over the long term. ENIAC was unveiled in 1946, and the transistor, invented at Bell Laboratories in 1947, made computers smaller and more reliable. UNIVAC became the first commercial computer in 1951, and IBM's System/360 of 1964 set the standard for business machines. The integrated circuit of 1958 and 1959 and Intel's microprocessor of 1971 made the personal computer possible, and the Apple II of 1977 and the IBM PC of 1981 brought computing into homes and offices.\n\nDefence spending was central to this progress. The Pentagon's research agency ARPA, set up after Sputnik, funded the ARPANET, which sent its first message in October 1969, and the Apollo Guidance Computer made the space programme a major customer for integrated circuits. Silicon Valley grew around Stanford University and firms such as Fairchild and Intel with the help of defence contracts. By the 1980s computing was creating new industries and firms such as Microsoft and Apple, supporting the service economy and giving the United States a technological lead.\n\nOverall, both advances were of great significance. Space travel gave the United States a Cold War victory and a symbol of national achievement, but its lasting effects were narrower and it was a short burst of spending. Information technology had the deeper and more lasting effect, because it reshaped the economy and everyday life, and it grew in part from the same Cold War research that drove the space race.",
