    "label": "B1",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "Terence O'Neill became Prime Minister in March 1963 and promoted economic planning, including the new city of Craigavon. He met the Taoiseach Seán Lemass at Stormont in January 1965, the first such meeting since partition, as part of his bridge-building policy. Under pressure from London and the civil rights movement he announced a five-point reform package on 22 November 1968, including a points system for council housing and an ombudsman. Unionist hardliners, including Ian Paisley, opposed him, and he resigned on 28 April 1969 after a party crisis.",
