    "label": "C2",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "The Sunningdale Agreement was reached at a conference from 6 to 9 December 1973 and set up a power-sharing executive with Brian Faulkner as Chief Executive. It created a Council of Ireland with a council of ministers and a consultative assembly drawn equally from the Dáil and the Northern Ireland Assembly. The Irish government accepted that Northern Ireland's status would not change without the consent of a majority there. It also set up a Law Enforcement Commission to examine how to deal with fugitive offenders and a possible all-Ireland court. Unionists saw the Council of Ireland as a step towards a united Ireland, and the Ulster Unionist Council voted by 427 to 374 to reject it in January 1974. The Ulster Workers' Council strike from 15 May 1974 paralysed services, and the British government did not confront the strikers, so Faulkner resigned on 28 May 1974.",
