    "label": "C4",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "John Hume helped found the SDLP in August 1970 and was a leading negotiator for the party at Sunningdale, where he served as Minister of Commerce in the power-sharing executive. He became leader of the SDLP in 1979 and supported the Anglo-Irish Agreement of 1985, arguing that it gave nationalists a role in Northern Ireland's government. In 1988 he began a public dialogue with Gerry Adams, arguing that the IRA campaign must end if nationalists were to achieve their goals through dialogue. He took part in the Brooke-Mayhew talks of 1991 and 1992, which tried to set up a new devolved settlement. His work with Adams and with the Irish government influenced the Downing Street Declaration of December 1993. Historians credit him with shaping the nationalist case for a peace process based on consent and dialogue.",
