    "label": "B2",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "The Combination Acts of 1799 and 1800 made it illegal for workers to combine to raise wages in Britain, until they were repealed in 1824. Six Dorset labourers, the Tolpuddle Martyrs, were sentenced to transportation in 1834 for swearing a union oath, and a public campaign secured their pardon in 1836. The Amalgamated Society of Engineers, founded in 1851, was a New Model union with a high subscription, a central fund and benefit payments for sick and unemployed members. The Trade Union Act of 1871 gave unions legal recognition.",
