    "label": "C3",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Hugo's preface to his play Cromwell in 1827 served as a manifesto for Romantic drama. The staging of Hernani in 1830 provoked a riot between Romantic supporters and classical critics, marking the victory of Romanticism in French theatre. His novel Notre-Dame de Paris, published in 1831, revived interest in the Gothic past of Paris and helped inspire the restoration of medieval buildings. Les Contemplations (1856) expressed grief and faith after the death of his daughter Léopoldine, who drowned in 1843. Les Misérables (1862) portrayed the poor of France and became one of the most widely read novels of the century. He was elected to the Académie française in 1841, a sign of his standing as a national poet. Historians regard him as one of the greatest French writers.",
