    "label": "C3",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "In my view the naval policy of Wilhelm II was bad for Germany and for Europe. The fleet cost vast sums that could have gone to the army, at a time when France and Russia threatened Germany's land frontiers, and it raised the national debt and led to the finance crisis of 1908-09. Tirpitz's risk theory failed to deter Britain, which saw the fleet as a threat and moved into the Entente with France and Russia. HMS Dreadnought of 1906 started a costly arms race, and the Haldane mission of 1912 failed to secure any naval agreement. Its Weltpolitik was clumsy, and the Tangier and Agadir crises ended in humiliation for Germany. The navy spent most of the war in harbour, and the mutinies at Wilhelmshaven and Kiel in 1918 helped to trigger the revolution that overthrew the Kaiser.",
