    "label": "C4",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Before 1914 most women who earned wages were young and unmarried, and worked in narrow, badly paid jobs such as domestic service. During the war women took over many men's jobs, and Britain's Ministry of Munitions turned to them after the shell shortage, so hundreds of thousands made shells and fuses by 1918. Women also worked as clerks, bank staff, tram conductors and postal workers, and the Women's Land Army was formed in Britain in 1917. In France, Albert Thomas encouraged women to work in armaments factories from 1915, while in Germany women bore the brunt of food shortages and led protests and riots in 1915-17. Once the war ended, however, many women were dismissed from munitions and some returned to domestic service. The war's effects on women were significant but temporary, and it helped to win the vote in Britain and Germany.",
