    "label": "B1",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "John Maynard Keynes criticised the return to the gold standard in 1925 in The Economic Consequences of Mr Churchill, arguing that the overvalued pound would make British exports uncompetitive and force down wages. In 1929 he co-wrote We Can Conquer Unemployment with Hubert Henderson, proposing large public works programmes to reduce unemployment. His General Theory of Employment, Interest and Money of 1936 argued that government spending could restore demand and employment when private investment was low. Many Labour and Liberal politicians welcomed his arguments, although the Treasury and the Bank of England were sceptical of deficit spending. Historians regard his work as a turning point in economic thought.",
