    "label": "C1",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "The Lateran Treaties of 11 February 1929, signed by Mussolini and Cardinal Pietro Gasparri, ended the dispute between the Italian state and the papacy and created the independent state of Vatican City. The Italian state paid the Church compensation of 1.75 billion lire for the loss of the papal states. Catholicism was recognised as the sole religion of the Italian state, and religious marriage was given civil effect. In return the Church gained religious teaching in state schools and state-paid priests, and Mussolini gained the support of the Catholic majority. In 1931 the regime clashed with Catholic Action over its youth and student groups, and Pope Pius XI protested in the encyclical Non abbiamo bisogno. An agreement that year limited Catholic Action to religious, cultural and charitable activities. The Church opposed the racial laws of 1938 in part, but it did not break with the regime.",
