    "marks": 40,
    "model": "The embargo and the price rises of October 1973 roughly quadrupled oil prices, which ended the long boom of cheap energy. The second oil shock after the Iranian revolution doubled prices between 1978 and 1980, and it caused a second recession from 1980 to 1982. Unemployment across the EEC rose above 10 per cent by the mid-1980s, with Spain and Ireland suffering even higher rates. Stagflation led governments to abandon full employment as an aim, making the defeat of inflation their main priority. Governments turned to nuclear power, with France's Messmer Plan making nuclear electricity the core of its supply, while North Sea oil made Britain self-sufficient by 1980. Heavy industries such as shipbuilding, steel and coal declined sharply, and deindustrialisation changed the regional economies of northern Britain and Scotland.",
