    "label": "B3",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "In the summer of 1981 young men at the Minguettes estate near Lyon burned cars and clashed with police, which drew national attention to the living conditions of second-generation migrants in the banlieues. The Socialist government of Francois Mitterrand created Priority Education Zones and the Banlieues 89 regeneration project, although critics said these were too limited to tackle the causes of disadvantage. The March for Equality and against Racism set out from Marseille in October 1983 and reached Paris on 3 December with tens of thousands of supporters, and Mitterrand promised a ten-year residence card. The Front National won 35 seats in the 1986 parliamentary elections, and the Pasqua laws of that year tightened immigration controls.",
