    "label": "C2",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Ben-Gurion led the Jewish Agency's executive from 1935 and accepted the 1947 UN partition plan as a first step towards a Jewish state. On 14 May 1948 he proclaimed the State of Israel in Tel Aviv and became its first prime minister. In May 1948 he oversaw the creation of the Israel Defence Forces, and in June 1948 he ordered the shelling of the Irgun ship Altalena to establish one army. He held the defence ministry alongside the premiership, serving as prime minister in 1948-54 and 1955-63. In October 1956 he attended the secret talks at Sèvres with Britain and France that prepared the Israeli attack on Sinai. Historians such as Benny Morris argue that he backed the expulsions from Lydda and Ramle in July 1948, which made him a controversial figure for Arab opinion.",
