    "label": "C3",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "On 13 May 1958 settlers and army officers seized government buildings in Algiers and formed a Committee of Public Safety, and the National Assembly invested Charles de Gaulle as prime minister on 1 June 1958. In Algiers and at Mostaganem in June 1958 he said Je vous ai compris and Vive l'Algerie francaise, which led the settlers to believe he would keep Algeria French. The constitution approved on 28 September 1958 created the Fifth Republic and the French Community, which gave the colonies self-government but kept foreign affairs, defence and currency with France. On 16 September 1959 he announced that Algerians would be offered self-determination, a choice between independence, integration with France and association. He defeated the settler revolt of Barricades Week in January 1960 and the Algiers putsch of April 1961 with the army, and the referendum of 8 January 1961 approved self-determination by about 75 per cent of votes cast. The Evian Accords of 18 March 1962 ended the war, and Algeria became independent on 5 July 1962, though about 800,000 settlers left.",
