    "label": "B1",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "Joseph McCarthy was a Republican senator from Wisconsin who used fear of communism to build a national following. In a speech at Wheeling, West Virginia, on 9 February 1950 he claimed to hold a list of communists working in the State Department. His methods of guilt by association, unnamed lists and personal smears drew criticism, and the Army-McCarthy hearings of 1954 were televised, showing his conduct to the nation. The Senate voted 67 to 22 to censure him on 2 December 1954, and his influence collapsed after that vote.",
