    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The world economic crisis of the 1930s and World War II both had a major impact on Northern Ireland, but in opposite ways. The depression deepened the unemployment that had already hit its shipbuilding and linen industries and strained the region's social peace, while the war brought full employment and economic recovery at the price of the Belfast Blitz and a closer dependence on Britain.\n\nNorthern Ireland entered the inter-war period with an economy that depended on a few export industries, above all shipbuilding, linen and engineering, centred on Belfast. Partition had left the heavy industry of the island in the North. When world trade collapsed after 1929, these industries were hit hard. Orders for ships at Harland and Wolff dried up, linen exports fell, and unemployment rose to levels far above the British average, with roughly a quarter of insured workers out of work in the worst years of the early 1930s. Agriculture, on small farms, was also hit by falling prices.\n\nThe social impact was severe. Because the region's government under Lord Craigavon had little power over the economy, and relief was tied to Poor Law rules, many of the unemployed relied on outdoor relief, which was low and strictly limited for single men. In October 1932 unemployed workers in Belfast, Catholic and Protestant together, protested and rioted over relief rates, and two people were killed in the clashes. The government conceded higher rates, but the unity was short-lived, and sectarian tensions soon returned in the riots of 1935. The crisis showed that economic hardship could cross the sectarian divide, but also that the unionist government's priorities were stability and loyalty rather than reform.\n\nWorld War II reversed the economic position. As part of the United Kingdom, Northern Ireland was at war from 3 September 1939, and its industry was drawn into the war effort. Harland and Wolff built warships and merchant ships and repaired thousands of vessels for the Admiralty, and Short Brothers in Belfast built Stirling bombers. Engineering firms made munitions, and the linen mills and rope works made parachutes, tents and uniforms. Farmers were ordered to plough more land and the region became an important supplier of food to Britain. Unemployment fell sharply, wages rose, and women entered factories in large numbers. Industrial unrest still occurred over pay and conditions in the shipyards and engineering works.\n\nThe war also exposed weaknesses and brought great suffering. The government assumed that Belfast was too far from German airfields to be attacked, and civil defence was weak. Before April 1941 there were few public shelters, anti-aircraft defences were slight, and the fire service was small. In four raids the city was devastated. On the night of 15 to 16 April, Easter Tuesday, about 180 German bombers attacked, and about 900 people were killed, mostly in the working-class districts of north and east Belfast. A raid on 4 to 5 May hit the shipyard and docks and killed about 150. Altogether about 1,000 people died, more than half the city's houses were damaged, and up to 100,000 people left the city each night to sleep in the countryside, known as ditchers. Fire brigades from Dublin, Dún Laoghaire, Dundalk and Drogheda crossed the border on de Valera's authorisation to help, a striking gesture given Southern neutrality.\n\nThe Blitz had political results. Evacuated children revealed the poverty and poor health of the slums, adding to calls for better housing and public health. The Unionist government was criticised for its slow response, and the elderly cabinet of John Miller Andrews, who had succeeded Craig in November 1940, was blamed. Andrews resigned in April 1943 and Basil Brooke took over, leading until 1963. The war also strengthened unionist loyalty to Britain, which contrasted with the South's neutrality, and the plan to extend conscription to Northern Ireland in 1941 was dropped after opposition from nationalists and the bishops. The IRA's activity, including the execution of Tom Williams in September 1942, deepened nationalist resentment.\n\nIn conclusion, the depression exposed the fragility of Northern Ireland's industrial base and brought unemployment and unrest, while the war restored prosperity and full employment but cost about a thousand lives in the Blitz, and left the region closer to Britain and more divided from the South.",
