    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Anglo-Irish relations between 1923 and 1949 developed from a state that accepted the Treaty and worked within it, through a period of confrontation under de Valera, to a complete break with the Crown and the Commonwealth. Over the period Ireland moved step by step from dominion status to full sovereignty, and Britain accepted nearly every change without taking military action.\n\nUnder Cosgrave the Free State used the Commonwealth itself to widen its independence. In 1924 the government registered the Treaty with the League of Nations as an international agreement, which Britain objected to because it regarded the Treaty as an internal imperial matter. At the Imperial Conference of 1926 Kevin O'Higgins was one of the leading advocates of the Balfour Declaration, which described Britain and the dominions as autonomous communities, equal in status and freely associated. This was followed by the Statute of Westminster of December 1931, which gave the dominions legislative independence and removed the British Parliament's power to override their laws. The Cosgrave government still accepted the oath, the Governor-General and the Treaty ports, however, because it believed independence should come by agreement with Britain, not by confrontation.\n\nThe Boundary Commission crisis showed the limits of this approach. In November 1925 the expected findings of the Commission were leaked to the Morning Post, Eoin MacNeill resigned, and the Free State government agreed to the Tripartite Agreement of 3 December 1925. The border was left unchanged, but the Free State was released from its share of the imperial debt. The hopes nationalists had placed in the Commission were shown to have been unrealistic, and partition was settled for a generation.\n\nThe relationship changed sharply when de Valera became President of the Executive Council in March 1932. He was determined to remove the symbols of the Treaty, and he used the Statute of Westminster and Fianna Fáil's majority in the Dáil to do it. He dismissed the Governor-General, James McNeill, in 1932 and replaced him with Domhnall Ua Buachalla, a Fianna Fáil figure with no official role. The Removal of the Oath Act of May 1933 ended the oath of fidelity to the King, and the right of appeal to the Privy Council was abolished the same year. The abdication of Edward VIII in December 1936 gave him his opportunity: the Constitution (Amendment No. 27) Act removed all references to the Crown and Governor-General, and the Executive Authority (External Relations) Act of 12 December 1936 gave the King a role only in the appointment of diplomats and the signing of international agreements. Britain protested but did nothing, which showed how effective the constitutional route had been. The 1937 constitution, Bunreacht na hÉireann, named the state Éire and declared it sovereign and independent, although it claimed the whole island in Articles 2 and 3.\n\nDe Valera's withholding of the land annuities in 1932, about three million pounds a year, led to the Economic War. Britain regarded the payments as obligatory and imposed special duties on Irish cattle, beef and butter, and Ireland retaliated with duties on British goods such as coal. The dispute damaged the farming economy and was only partly eased by the Coal-Cattle Pact of 1935. It ended with the three Anglo-Irish Agreements signed in London on 25 April 1938. The annuities dispute was settled for a single payment of 10 million pounds, far less than Britain had claimed, the special duties were ended, and the Treaty ports at Berehaven, Spike Island and Lough Swilly were handed back to Ireland. The return of the ports was the most important result, because it made neutrality possible in 1939. De Valera had linked the ports to partition, but Britain would not move on the border.\n\nThe Second World War tested relations again. Ireland declared neutrality, and Britain, led by Churchill who had opposed the surrender of the ports, resented the loss of the bases in the Battle of the Atlantic. In June 1940 Malcolm MacDonald offered de Valera a declaration in principle for a united Ireland if Ireland joined the Allies, but de Valera refused because he doubted that Britain could deliver unity. In February 1944 Britain and the United States presented the American Note asking for the expulsion of the Axis diplomats, and de Valera refused again. In practice neutrality leaned towards Britain, since Allied airmen were released and weather reports from Blacksod helped the D-Day decision, but relations were strained. Churchill attacked Irish neutrality in his victory broadcast of May 1945, and de Valera's reply on Radio Éireann was widely praised at home.\n\nThe last stage came under the inter-party government that took office in February 1948 under John A. Costello. Seán MacBride regarded the External Relations Act as an anomaly, and Costello announced its repeal while in Canada in September 1948. The Republic of Ireland Act came into force on Easter Monday, 18 April 1949, ending the link with the Commonwealth. Britain responded with the Ireland Act of 1949, which accepted the change but declared that Northern Ireland would not leave the United Kingdom without the consent of its own parliament, and so strengthened partition. Overall, relations developed from cooperation within the Commonwealth to confrontation in the 1930s and then to formal separation. Independence had largely been won before 1949, thanks to the groundwork of Cosgrave at the Imperial Conferences and the legislation of de Valera, but partition remained unresolved.",
