    "model": "Grosse Isle taught me that Famine emigration was often a desperate flight rather than a planned move, that it carried a terrible human cost, and that the governments on both sides of the Atlantic failed to prepare for it. It also showed me the generosity of ordinary people in Quebec, and why the island became the leading memorial to the Famine dead outside Ireland.\n\nThe first lesson is about the scale and character of the exodus. Repeated failures of the potato, almost total in 1846 and followed by the small crop of 1847 and the return of blight in 1848, turned a steady flow of emigrants into a mass flight. Between 1.5 and 2 million people left Ireland between 1845 and 1855. Unlike the pre-Famine emigrants, who were often Protestant small farmers and tradesmen with some capital, those who reached Quebec in 1847 were poor, unskilled and Catholic, and many came from the west and south-west as whole families. The fares to Quebec were cheaper than to New York, and the US Passenger Acts of 1847 raised the cost of the American route, so the poorest and sickest were diverted to Canada. Of about 100,000 people who sailed for British North America in 1847, an estimated 17,000 or more died at sea, in quarantine or soon after arrival.\n\nSecondly, Grosse Isle showed me how emigrants were pushed out as well as drawn away. Some landlords found it cheaper to pay passages than to pay poor rates, and the Poor Law Extension Act of 1847, with its Gregory clause, encouraged clearances. Major Denis Mahon of Strokestown sent 1,490 tenants to Quebec in 1847, and hundreds of them died on the crossing or at Grosse Isle. Mahon was shot dead on 2 November 1847, which shows how bitter feeling had become. Yet the case study also reminds me that many emigrants travelled on pre-paid tickets sent by relatives abroad, so family networks and chain-emigration were at work alongside the force of landlords.\n\nThe third lesson is about the horror of the voyage. Steerage passengers were crammed into the lower decks of sailing ships for six weeks or more, with poor ventilation, little water and less food than the law required. Typhus, or ship fever, spread by lice, killed thousands at sea, and ships with high death rates became known as coffin ships. By the time they reached the St Lawrence the passengers were already weakened, so the disease arrived with them.\n\nThe fourth lesson is about the failure of the quarantine system under pressure. Grosse Isle, the quarantine station for Quebec, had been opened in 1832 to keep cholera out and was built for a normal season, with hospital sheds holding only a few hundred patients. When the Syria arrived in mid-May 1847 with fever on board, the hospital was full within days. By 31 May about 40 ships carrying some 12,500 passengers lay at anchor in a queue down the river. The sick and the dead lay in the holds beside the healthy, and even healthy passengers who were landed in crowded sheds caught typhus. Dr George Mellis Douglas, the medical superintendent, had warned the authorities and asked for more help, but the preparations were inadequate. More than 5,000 people were buried on the island that year, and the disease spread beyond it, since about 6,000 Irish died in the fever sheds at Montreal, where the mayor, John Mills, died of typhus while helping the sick.\n\nThe fifth lesson is about the courage and kindness shown by others. Doctors and Catholic and Anglican clergy, many of them French-Canadian priests from the diocese of Quebec, worked on the island, and several caught typhus and died. Hundreds of orphaned children were adopted by French-Canadian families, many of whom let them keep their Irish surnames, which is why so many Quebeckers have Irish names today.\n\nFinally, Grosse Isle taught me about memory and interpretation. The medical staff put up a monument in 1853, and in 1909 the Ancient Order of Hibernians unveiled a great Celtic cross, whose Irish inscription blames British rule more bluntly than the English one. Nationalists from John Mitchel onwards used the island as proof that British policy, and not only the blight, killed the Irish poor, while others stress landlord clearances and the limits of colonial resources. The station closed in 1937, and the island is now Grosse Île and the Irish Memorial National Historic Site. Yet I also learned to keep it in proportion, since most Famine emigrants went to the United States and Britain rather than Canada.\n\nOverall, Grosse Isle showed me the human cost of unregulated mass emigration, the failure of governments to prepare, and the generosity of Quebec. It is the clearest single window onto what Famine emigration meant for those who lived through it.",
