    "model": "Terence O'Neill differed from Lord Brookeborough to a considerable extent in style, economic policy and willingness to reach out to Catholics and the Republic. On the deeper question of reforming the discrimination at the heart of the Northern Ireland system, however, the difference was much smaller than it appeared, and O'Neill's reforms were limited by his own outlook and his own party.\n\nBrookeborough, Prime Minister from 1943 until 1963, put stability and loyalty to the Crown above all. His government resisted reform of local government and the franchise, kept the Special Powers Act of 1922 in force with its power of detention without trial, and relied on the almost wholly Protestant B Specials and a Royal Ulster Constabulary with few Catholics. He had urged loyalists in the 1930s to employ Protestants rather than Catholics, and nationalists never forgot it. This is the clearest contrast with O'Neill, who did not speak in that way and who tried to present himself as a Prime Minister for the whole community.\n\nThe two men also differed sharply on the economy. Under Brookeborough, shipbuilding and linen declined, unemployment stayed the highest in the United Kingdom and emigration drained young people. His government offered grants to attract firms such as Short Brothers and man-made fibre plants, but without any broad plan. O'Neill, Minister of Finance from 1956, made economic planning the centre of his premiership, drawing on the Matthew Report of 1963 and the Wilson Plan of 1965, which led to the new city of Craigavon and the new university at Coleraine. Yet the pattern of investment east of the Bann continued, and decisions such as the closure of the Derry Road railway in 1965 left Catholic Derry feeling that O'Neill's modernisation was not for them.\n\nThe sharpest difference lay in outlook towards Catholics and the Republic. Brookeborough kept contact with Dublin to a minimum. O'Neill visited Catholic schools and hospitals, and on 14 January 1965 he received the Taoiseach, Seán Lemass, at Stormont, the first meeting of the two heads of government since partition, before returning the visit to Dublin in February. His policy of bridge-building won a response: in 1965 the Nationalist Party under Eddie McAteer accepted the role of official opposition at Stormont for the first time. Such a gesture would have been unthinkable under Brookeborough, when nationalists abstained for long periods.\n\nOn the welfare state and on discrimination, however, the two men had more in common. The Education Act of 1947, the National Health Service of 1948 and welfare parity with Britain were inherited by both, and raised living standards for all, while the Catholic share of the benefits grew with free secondary education. But neither man tackled gerrymandering in Londonderry, the business vote, or the allocation of council houses by unionist-controlled councils. O'Neill did not see Catholic grievances as primarily political, and his gestures were symbolic before November 1968. The result was that Catholics welcomed his tone but judged real change too slow.\n\nThe consequences of each man's leadership also differed. Brookeborough's era was quiet on the surface: he faced no serious challenge to the Union and the IRA border campaign of 1956 to 1962 failed, with internment on both sides of the border. O'Neill's attempt at change split his own party. Ian Paisley, who founded the Free Presbyterian Church in 1951, led the 'O'Neill must go' campaign, and William Craig opposed him in cabinet. Meanwhile the Civil Rights Association, founded in 1967, took up the grievances left unresolved. After the banned Derry march of 5 October 1968 was broken up by the RUC, O'Neill announced the five-point reform package of 22 November 1968, and he conceded one man, one vote in April 1969. The violence at Burntollet in January 1969 and a divided party finished him, and he resigned on 28 April 1969.\n\nO'Neill therefore differed from Brookeborough greatly in approach, in planning and in reconciliation, but very little in his reluctance to dismantle the structures that favoured unionists. Brookeborough preserved the system by refusing to change it; O'Neill tried to soften it, but found that even modest reform was more than his party would accept and less than Catholics now demanded.",
