    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "I will discuss the Sunningdale Agreement of 1973 and the Anglo-Irish Agreement of 1985. Both mattered because each tried to break the deadlock by combining the consent principle with a role for the Irish government. Sunningdale shows how far a settlement could go and why it failed without unionist consent, while the Anglo-Irish Agreement showed that Britain and Ireland could act together over unionist objections.\n\nThe Sunningdale conference was held at Sunningdale Park, Berkshire, from 6 to 9 December 1973, attended by the British Prime Minister, Edward Heath, the Taoiseach, Liam Cosgrave, and the pro-Assembly parties: Faulkner's Unionists, the SDLP and Alliance. It followed the border poll of March 1973 and the Assembly election of June, and it produced a power-sharing executive that took office on 1 January 1974, with Brian Faulkner as Chief Executive and Gerry Fitt of the SDLP as his deputy. Its importance lay in the fact that this was the first time unionists and nationalists governed together in Northern Ireland.\n\nThe agreement had two further key elements. A Council of Ireland was to link the two parts of the island through a council of ministers, seven from each side, and a consultative assembly of sixty members. The Irish government accepted that Northern Ireland's status would not change without the consent of a majority there, and Britain declared it would support a united Ireland if a majority wished it. This consent principle was important because it lasted through later agreements. A Law Enforcement Commission and a promise of policing acceptable to both communities addressed nationalist fears.\n\nIts failure was just as important. Unionists saw the Council of Ireland as a step towards a united Ireland, and the Boland case, in which the Irish government defended its constitutional claim, damaged Faulkner. The Ulster Unionist Council rejected the Council of Ireland by 427 to 374 on 4 January 1974 and Faulkner resigned as party leader. The United Ulster Unionist Council won eleven of the twelve Northern Ireland seats in the February 1974 Westminster election. The Ulster Workers' Council strike from 15 May 1974 paralysed electricity and services, and the Labour government under Harold Wilson with Merlyn Rees did not confront it. Faulkner resigned on 28 May and the executive collapsed after five months. The lesson was that no settlement could survive without the consent of a majority of unionists, and the SDLP's Seamus Mallon later called the 1998 agreement 'Sunningdale for slow learners'.\n\nThe Anglo-Irish Agreement was signed at Hillsborough Castle on 15 November 1985 by Margaret Thatcher and the Taoiseach, Garret FitzGerald. It followed the 1981 hunger strikes and the rise of Sinn Féin, and Thatcher's rejection of the New Ireland Forum options in 1984. Its importance lay in giving the Irish government a formal consultative role through an Intergovernmental Conference with a permanent secretariat at Maryfield near Belfast. Article 1 affirmed that there would be no change in the status of Northern Ireland without the consent of a majority, so the Agreement protected the Union while recognising an Irish interest. Thatcher hoped for improved security cooperation and to win nationalists back from Sinn Féin, though she later acknowledged that cooperation did not improve as she hoped, and FitzGerald wanted to strengthen the SDLP.\n\nThe reaction showed how deeply unionists were opposed. Under the slogan 'Ulster says No' a huge rally was held at Belfast City Hall on 23 November 1985, and fifteen unionist MPs resigned their seats in January 1986 to force by-elections. Yet the Agreement survived, unlike Sunningdale, because it was an agreement between two governments and needed no unionist cooperation to stay in force. Republicans rejected it because it did not end British jurisdiction, but John Hume supported it as a real step towards Irish involvement.\n\nBoth agreements were therefore of lasting importance. Sunningdale tried to build a settlement with local parties and failed for lack of unionist consent, while the Anglo-Irish Agreement endured but did not restore devolved government. Between them they established consent and an Irish dimension as the foundations on which the Downing Street Declaration and the later peace process were built.",
