    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Religion and identity were the fault line of Northern Ireland between 1949 and 1993. I learned how religious affiliation and cultural identity were bound up with political allegiance, how ecumenism tried to cross that divide, and how Ian Paisley, more than anyone, embodied the resistance to it. The three themes are closely linked, because Paisley's career shows both how deep the divisions went and why efforts to bridge them achieved so little.\n\nReligious affiliation and political allegiance overlapped, so that most unionists were Protestant and most nationalists were Catholic, although the boundary was not absolute. Catholics made up about a third of the population, 35 per cent in the 1961 census, and the Presbyterians were the largest Protestant denomination. Unionist identity combined Protestantism, loyalty to the Crown and attachment to the Union, while nationalist identity combined Catholicism, Irish cultural heritage and support for a united Ireland. Religion shaped schooling, marriage and social networks, and because children were separated in schools and baptised into their denominations, the divisions were handed on from generation to generation. Most people had little regular contact with the other community.\n\nEach tradition had institutions that expressed its identity. The Orange Order, founded in 1795 after the Battle of the Diamond in County Armagh, was the great Protestant fraternal body, closely tied to the Ulster Unionist Party through its delegates to the Ulster Unionist Council. Its 12 July parades, commemorating the Boyne of 1690, were central to unionist culture, but marches through Catholic areas were a constant source of tension, and members were forbidden to marry Catholics or attend Catholic services. Catholic identity drew on the Church, the Irish language and the Gaelic Athletic Association, founded in 1884. The GAA's Rule 21 banned members of the RUC and the British army and stayed in force until 2001, which unionists resented and nationalists saw as a mark of their separateness. Parades, flags and games were therefore symbols of identity that also marked out division, and the Flags and Emblems Act of 1954 showed how the state itself treated nationalist symbols with suspicion.\n\nEcumenism, the movement for dialogue between the churches, was the main attempt to bridge this divide. It was encouraged by the Second Vatican Council of 1962 to 1965 and by local initiatives. The Presbyterian minister Ray Davey founded the Corrymeela Community at Ballycastle in 1965 as a centre of reconciliation, and the Jesuit Michael Hurley founded the Irish School of Ecumenics in Dublin in 1970. In 1973 church leaders held their first formal inter-church meeting at Ballymascanlon in County Louth, and in December 1974 Protestant clergy met IRA leaders at Feakle in County Clare, after which the IRA declared a Christmas ceasefire. In September 1979 Pope John Paul II appealed for an end to violence at Drogheda. These efforts built contacts and gave moderates a voice, which was no small thing in the worst years of the Troubles.\n\nThe limits of ecumenism are best seen through Ian Paisley. He founded the Free Presbyterian Church in 1951 and made opposition to Catholicism and ecumenism the core of his ministry, protesting in 1966 when the Presbyterian General Assembly considered links with the World Council of Churches. He moved into politics in the 1960s, opposing Terence O'Neill's meeting with Seán Lemass in 1965 as a betrayal of Protestant identity, leading the 'O'Neill must go' campaign and being jailed for three months in 1966. He founded the Democratic Unionist Party in 1971 and led it until 2008, winning Bannside at Stormont and North Antrim at Westminster in 1970. He supported the 1974 strike against Sunningdale and led protests against the Anglo-Irish Agreement, and in 1979 he topped the poll in the first direct European election in Northern Ireland. In October 1988 he shouted at the Pope in the European Parliament in Strasbourg and was removed from the chamber. Supporters admired his forcefulness; opponents condemned his rhetoric, and historians such as Steve Bruce argue that his evangelical Protestantism was central to his politics. Either way, his religious politics made dialogue costly for any clergyman or politician who attempted it.\n\nCultural responses to the Troubles also reflected these questions of identity. Seamus Heaney explored violence and the inheritance of the past in collections such as Death of a Naturalist and Wintering Out, and in 1980 Brian Friel and Stephen Rea founded the Field Day Theatre Company in Derry, whose first production, Translations, dealt with the loss of the Irish language and the renaming of Irish places. The Cultural Traditions Group, set up in 1988, tried to promote respect for both unionist and nationalist heritage. Yet critics such as Edna Longley argued that Field Day downplayed the unionist tradition, and such initiatives reached mainly educated audiences.\n\nOverall, I learned that religion and culture were the foundation of political identity in Northern Ireland, not merely a background to it. Ecumenism and cultural initiatives created valuable dialogue and eased suffering at the margins, but hardline opposition, led by Paisley, and the continuing segregation of schools, housing and social life meant that they could not alter the political division between unionists and nationalists.",
