    "model": "Germany evolved from a loose Confederation into a unified Empire between 1815 and 1871 mainly because Prussia, under Bismarck, defeated Austria and France and then drew the other German states into a Prussian-led state. The change came from above, through economic strength, military reform and diplomacy, after the liberal attempt to achieve unity from below had failed in 1848.\n\nIn 1815 the Congress of Vienna created the German Confederation of thirty-nine states, with Austria presiding over its Diet at Frankfurt. It was designed to keep Germany divided and conservative, and Metternich enforced this through the Carlsbad Decrees of 1819, which censored the press and supervised the universities. Yet the first steps towards unity were economic. Prussia's Zollverein, in force from 1834 and eventually including most German states except Austria, created a large common market and accustomed the smaller states to looking to Berlin rather than Vienna.\n\nThe revolution of 1848 showed that unity could not be won by liberal idealism alone. The Frankfurt Parliament offered the imperial crown to the King of Prussia, but he refused it in 1849 because it came from an elected assembly and not from the princes, and the revolution was crushed. The lesson that Bismarck later drew was that unity would be decided by power rather than by parliamentary debate.\n\nIn the 1850s and early 1860s the balance shifted decisively towards Prussia. The Ruhr and Silesia supplied coal and iron, railways linked the Prussian regions and Krupp's works at Essen produced guns and rails, while Austria was heavily in debt, conservative, defeated in Italy in 1859 and without Russian goodwill after the Crimean War. Meanwhile Roon and Moltke reformed the army, and when the liberal Landtag refused to pay for the reforms, William I appointed Bismarck Minister-President in September 1862. Bismarck declared that the great questions would be decided by blood and iron, and he collected taxes without a budget, arguing that the constitution had a gap.\n\nBismarck then used three wars to remove obstacles to unity. In 1864 Prussia and Austria together defeated Denmark over Schleswig and Holstein, and the Convention of Gastein in 1865 divided the administration of the duchies, creating a quarrel he could exploit. He secured Russian goodwill, Napoleon III's neutrality after Biarritz and an alliance with Italy in April 1866, and at Königgrätz on 3 July 1866 the needle-gun, Moltke's planning and rapid mobilisation by rail won a decisive victory. By the Treaty of Prague the Confederation was dissolved and Austria was excluded from German affairs. Bismarck persuaded the king not to march on Vienna, so that Austria would not become a permanent enemy.\n\nThe settlement of 1866 created the North German Confederation in 1867. Prussia annexed Hanover, Hesse-Kassel, Nassau, Frankfurt and Schleswig-Holstein, and the king of Prussia became president with Bismarck as Chancellor. Its Reichstag, elected by universal male suffrage, won over many liberals, who accepted Bismarck's leadership in return for national progress, and the Indemnity Bill of 1866 ended the constitutional conflict. The southern states made secret military alliances with Prussia and sent deputies to the Customs Parliament of 1868, so Germany was now divided only by the River Main.\n\nThe last step came through the war with France in 1870. After the Hohenzollern candidature for the Spanish throne alarmed France and Leopold withdrew, Bismarck edited the Ems telegram so that it seemed a French insult to the Prussian king. France declared war on 19 July 1870, and Baden, Württemberg and Bavaria fought alongside Prussia under their treaties. After the French defeat at Sedan on 2 September, where Napoleon III was captured, the southern states agreed to join in November, and the German Empire was proclaimed at Versailles on 18 January 1871 with William I as Emperor. The Treaty of Frankfurt gave Germany Alsace and part of Lorraine and an indemnity of five billion francs.\n\nGermany therefore evolved from a Confederation designed to prevent unity into an Empire dominated by Prussia, through economic leadership and war rather than through the liberal nationalism of 1848. Unity was achieved at a price: Austria was excluded, Polish, Danish and French-speaking minorities were included, and the Chancellor answered to the emperor and not to the Reichstag.",
