    "model": "Robert Peel was the more effective reformer. Robert Owen showed what could be done in one village and inspired later movements, but Peel, who reformed from inside government, brought changes that affected the whole country and lasted.\n\nOwen's achievements at New Lanark were impressive. He took charge of the mills in 1800 and found long hours, dirty overcrowded houses, drunkenness and some 500 pauper children brought from workhouses. He stopped taking pauper apprentices, raised the age at which children could work so that those under ten no longer worked in the mills, and by 1816 had cut the working day to ten and three-quarter hours. He replaced fines and punishment with the Silent Monitor, a block of wood with four coloured sides turned daily to show each worker's conduct, and he paid wages to idle workers for about four months during the American embargo of 1806. He also spent profits on cleaner housing, a village store selling goods at close to cost price, and the Institute for the Formation of Character and an infant school, both opened in 1816, which drew visitors from Britain and abroad.\n\nOwen also tried to spread his ideas. He wrote A New View of Society, arguing that character is formed by circumstances, and he campaigned for factory legislation, which contributed to the Cotton Mills and Factories Act of 1819. That Act barred children under nine from the cotton mills and limited the hours of older children to twelve a day, but it had no inspectors and was poorly enforced. Owen's wider schemes were less successful. New Harmony in Indiana, founded in 1825, collapsed within a few years, the Equitable Labour Exchange of 1832 failed, and the Grand National Consolidated Trades Union of 1834, which he led, collapsed within months. His quarrels with his partners, notably William Allen, and his public attack on organised religion in 1817 damaged his credibility with the establishment.\n\nPeel's achievements were broader because he used the power of the state. As Home Secretary he founded the Metropolitan Police in London in 1829, a professional force that became a model for the rest of the country. In the same year he and Wellington conceded Catholic emancipation to avoid civil conflict in Ireland. The Tamworth Manifesto of 1834 set out a programme of moderate reform that became the basis of the modern Conservative Party. As Prime Minister from 1841 to 1846 he reintroduced income tax in 1842 to pay for cuts in tariffs, and his budgets of 1842 and 1845 lowered duties on many imports, which helped trade and the economy.\n\nPeel also legislated on banking and on factory conditions. The Bank Charter Act of 1844 regulated the issue of banknotes by the Bank of England and gave Britain a more stable financial system. The Factory Act of 1844 limited the hours of women and children. His most famous act was the repeal of the Corn Laws in 1846, supported by Cobden and Bright of the Anti-Corn Law League and prompted partly by the Irish potato famine. It established free trade as national policy and cut the cost of bread, although it split the Conservative Party and Peel resigned in June 1846.\n\nThe comparison shows a difference of scale and method. Owen worked outside government, aiming at co-operation and communal ownership, and his success depended on his personal control of one community, which few employers would copy. Peel worked within government and aimed to preserve private property and the existing order while making it more efficient, so that his measures applied nationwide and were accepted by much of the middle class. Owen's influence was real, since his ideas inspired the co-operative movement and the Rochdale Pioneers opened their store in 1844, but this came through later movements rather than through his own schemes.\n\nPeel must therefore be judged the more effective reformer. Owen was the more original and humane thinker, and New Lanark remains a pioneering experiment, but its direct impact was limited and his wider plans failed. Peel's police, tax, banking and tariff reforms changed how Britain was governed and worked, and historians such as Norman Gash rank him among the great statesmen of the century.",
