    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The period 1815 to 1871 saw major developments in science and technology, above all in electricity and communication, in biology and the understanding of life, in medicine and in transport. Together they changed how Europeans lived, worked and understood themselves, and several of them reached well beyond science itself.\n\nThe most important advances were in electricity. Michael Faraday discovered electromagnetic induction in 1831, showing that a changing magnetic field produces an electric current, and this made the generator and the transformer possible. His laws of electrolysis in 1833-34 showed that the amount of substance deposited at an electrode is proportional to the charge passed. In 1865 James Clerk Maxwell put forward his theory of the electromagnetic field, which showed that light is an electromagnetic wave and brought electricity, magnetism and light into a single theory. Werner von Siemens's dynamo of 1866 then made the large-scale generation of electricity possible, so that the laboratory discoveries of Faraday began to point towards industrial use.\n\nCommunication was transformed by the electric telegraph. William Cooke and Charles Wheatstone patented their system in Britain in 1837 and it was soon used on the railways, while Samuel Morse demonstrated his system in 1844 on a line between Washington and Baltimore, and Morse code was widely adopted. A submarine cable was laid across the Atlantic in 1858 but failed after a few weeks; a lasting cable was completed in 1866. Messages that once took days or weeks could now cross states and oceans almost instantly, which helped governments, businesses and newspapers to operate on a new scale. Photography was a further new medium, with the daguerreotype announced in 1839 and Fox Talbot's calotype following in 1841.\n\nThe greatest intellectual development was in biology. Charles Darwin sailed on HMS Beagle from 1831 to 1836, collecting fossils and animals in South America and the Galapagos Islands, and after about twenty years of delay he published On the Origin of Species in November 1859, arguing that species change through variation and natural selection and share common ancestors. His Descent of Man in 1871 applied the theory to humans. The theory provoked fierce controversy, with Huxley defending it and Bishop Wilberforce attacking it at the Oxford debate of 1860, and it challenged the traditional religious account of creation. Gregor Mendel's experiments on pea plants, published in 1866, revealed patterns of inheritance, although they were ignored until 1900, which shows that not every advance had an immediate impact.\n\nMedicine also advanced greatly. Louis Pasteur's work in the late 1850s showed that fermentation and decay are caused by micro-organisms, and his swan-neck flask experiments of 1861 disproved spontaneous generation, giving a foundation to the germ theory of disease. James Young Simpson introduced chloroform anaesthesia in 1847, which made longer operations bearable, and Joseph Lister's use of carbolic acid from 1867 began antiseptic surgery and reduced deaths from infection. Pasteur and Lister between them changed medicine from guesswork towards a science, although the full benefits came after 1871.\n\nTechnology in transport reshaped economies and trade. The steam railway, the telegraph and the steamship linked nations in a faster network. Brunel's iron screw steamship, the SS Great Britain of 1843, showed what iron and steam could do on the ocean, and the opening of the Suez Canal in 1869 shortened the sea route between Europe and Asia. Cheaper and faster movement of goods, people and news fed industrial growth and urban expansion, and it drew distant parts of the world into one trading system.\n\nOverall, the key developments of 1815-1871 were Faraday and Maxwell in electricity, the telegraph in communication, Darwin and Pasteur in biology and medicine, and the railway and steamship in transport. Their impact was greatest in the speed of communication, the health of populations and the challenge to traditional belief, though some, such as Mendel's work and the full use of electric power, only bore fruit in later decades.",
