    "label": "1",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The main causes of international tension between 1871 and 1914 were the unresolved hostility between France and Germany, the alliance system that divided Europe into armed blocs, imperial rivalry, Germany's Weltpolitik and naval challenge to Britain, and above all the instability of the Balkans. No single cause was enough alone, but together they turned each crisis into a test of strength between the blocs.\n\nThe first source of tension was created by German unification itself. By the Treaty of Frankfurt in May 1871 France lost Alsace and part of Lorraine and had to pay an indemnity of five billion francs. French desire for revanche never faded, and it flared up in the Boulanger crisis of 1886-87. Bismarck assumed France could never be reconciled, so his whole policy aimed at keeping her isolated. The War-in-Sight crisis of 1875 showed how quickly a Franco-German war could be feared, and it left Europe's balance of power permanently unsettled. A united Germany in the centre of the continent had upset the old order, and French bitterness ensured it was never fully restored.\n\nThe alliance system, begun by Bismarck to keep the peace, ended by dividing Europe. He built the Dual Alliance with Austria-Hungary in 1879, the Triple Alliance with Italy in 1882 and the secret Reinsurance Treaty with Russia in 1887. After his fall Germany let the Reinsurance Treaty lapse, and Russia turned to France in the alliance of 1894. Britain then abandoned isolation, agreeing with Japan in 1902, France in 1904 and Russia in 1907. By 1907 Europe was split between the Triple Alliance and the Triple Entente, and each bloc feared the other, so every dispute came to be seen as part of a struggle between them.\n\nImperial rivalry added friction. From the 1880s the powers partitioned Africa, so that about nine tenths of the continent was under European control by 1914, driven by markets, prestige and strategic bases. At Fashoda in 1898 British and French forces met and war was feared, and the Boer War brought hostility to Britain from much of Europe. Yet most colonial quarrels were settled by compromise, as the 1904 and 1907 agreements show, so imperialism mattered mainly because it embittered relations and, through the Moroccan crises of 1905 and 1911, hardened the blocs.\n\nGerman policy under Wilhelm II made matters worse. After dismissing Bismarck in 1890 the Kaiser pursued Weltpolitik, a claim to world power status. The Kruger telegram of 1896 outraged Britain, and the Naval Laws of 1898 and 1900 began a great battle fleet, which Britain saw as a threat to its survival. The launch of HMS Dreadnought in 1906 set off an expensive arms race. German diplomacy in Morocco was clumsy, ending in defeat at Algeciras in 1906 and the Agadir crisis of 1911, which drew Britain and France closer. Germany's behaviour helped create the encirclement it feared, and the naval race poisoned Anglo-German relations for a decade.\n\nThe most dangerous cause was the Balkans, where Ottoman decline left a vacuum that Austria-Hungary and Russia both wished to fill. Serbia, independent since 1878, turned towards Russia after the murder of King Alexander in 1903, and its ambition to unite the South Slavs threatened the Habsburg empire. Austria's annexation of Bosnia in 1908 humiliated Russia and Serbia, and Germany forced Russia to back down in 1909. The Balkan Wars of 1912-13 left Serbia stronger and Austria-Hungary determined to act. When Franz Ferdinand was shot at Sarajevo on 28 June 1914, Germany gave Austria a blank cheque, and the alliance system dragged in Russia, Germany, France and Britain.\n\nFranco-German hostility, rival blocs, imperial competition and the naval race created a Europe primed for conflict, but the Balkans were the trigger, because only there did Austro-Russian rivalry meet a nationalism that could not be contained. The alliance system turned a local crisis into a European war, and German policy after 1890 did most to make that system so dangerous.",
