    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Stalin's handling of the Soviet economy, the show trials and the wartime alliances shows a leader who was ruthless, determined and ready to pay any human price for his goals, but who was also a shrewd operator who understood power better than his rivals. In each case he set an aim and drove it through by force.\n\nHis handling of the economy showed his willingness to impose radical change from above. Having defeated Trotsky and then Bukharin's Right Opposition by 1929, he abandoned the NEP and launched the First Five-Year Plan in October 1928, drawn up by Gosplan, with heavy industry given priority. New cities such as Magnitogorsk were built, and the Stakhanovite movement of 1935 was used to push workers to higher output. Steel production rose from about 4 million tonnes in 1928 to about 18 million by 1940, so Stalin did drag a backward, peasant country towards industrial power. But the statistics were inflated, wages fell, housing was overcrowded and forced labour was used on projects such as the White Sea-Baltic Canal. Collectivisation showed the same brutality. Peasants were forced into collective farms, and the grain requisitions of 1932 and 1933 produced a famine that killed several million people, mainly in Ukraine, the North Caucasus and Kazakhstan. Stalin put speed and state control of grain ahead of human life.\n\nThe show trials revealed him as a suspicious and vengeful politician who used terror to remove every possible rival. The murder of Sergei Kirov in Leningrad on 1 December 1934 gave him his pretext, and a decree issued that same day ordered that terrorism cases be heard in days, with no defence lawyer and no appeal. In August 1936 Zinoviev and Kamenev, two of Lenin's closest colleagues, were tried in public on charges of forming a terrorist centre under Trotsky, and all sixteen defendants confessed and were shot within hours of the verdict. Pyatakov was condemned in January 1937, and in March 1938 Bukharin, Rykov and the former NKVD chief Yagoda were shot. The prosecutor Vyshinsky and the NKVD extracted confessions through sleep deprivation, threats against families and later torture. The trials were scripted and approved at the top, which shows how completely Stalin controlled the system.\n\nThe trials were only the visible part of a wider Terror, and it was turned on the army too. Marshal Tukhachevsky and other senior officers were arrested in 1937, and some 30,000 to 35,000 officers were dismissed or arrested between 1937 and 1939. This shows Stalin's paranoia, but also its cost, since it left the Red Army poorly led when the Winter War against Finland began in 1939 and when Germany invaded in 1941. His leadership removed every old Bolshevik who could challenge him, but it weakened the country he led.\n\nStalin's wartime record shows a calculating realist. The Nazi-Soviet Pact of August 1939 bought him time and a buffer, allowed the Red Army into eastern Poland on 17 September 1939 and led to the annexation of the Baltic states in 1940. He was caught out by the German invasion of June 1941, but he recovered, and about 1,500 factories were moved east of the Urals in 1941 and 1942. Order 227 of July 1942, 'Not one step back', enforced discipline with blocking detachments behind the front line. Within the Grand Alliance he accepted Lend-Lease supplies from Britain and the United States, and at Yalta in 1945 he joined Churchill and Roosevelt and emerged with the USSR in control of Eastern Europe.\n\nOverall, the evidence shows Stalin as ruthless, calculating and domineering. His economic policies built an industrial base that helped the Soviet Union survive the war, and his diplomacy won it superpower status, but the price was millions of deaths from famine, terror and war, and a system held together by fear.",
