    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Developments in technology had a decisive effect on the way the Second World War was fought. They transformed the speed of land campaigns, the nature of air and sea warfare and the use of intelligence, and in the end the atomic bomb brought the war against Japan to a close.\n\nOn land, the tank and the aeroplane combined to produce blitzkrieg. The ideas had been developed by theorists such as Heinz Guderian, whose Achtung - Panzer! appeared in 1937, and Germany formed Panzer divisions from 1935. Using radio communication, concentrated armour, motorised infantry and close air support from dive-bombers such as the Stuka, the Germans defeated Poland in about five weeks in September 1939. In May 1940 their armoured divisions advanced through the Ardennes, bypassing the Maginot Line, and reached the Channel coast at Abbeville by 20 May, and France was defeated in six weeks. Technology made the early war one of rapid movement rather than the stalemate of the trenches. Later, however, tanks such as the Soviet T-34 and the American Sherman were produced in huge numbers, and industrial output began to count as much as tactical brilliance.\n\nAir power was the second great change. The Battle of Britain in 1940 was the first major campaign fought entirely in the air, and it was won by the RAF's Hurricanes and Spitfires. They were guided by radar, which Britain had developed from 1935, when Robert Watson-Watt showed that radio waves could detect aircraft, and the Chain Home stations gave early warning of the Luftwaffe's approach. Technology thus allowed a smaller force to defend effectively. Air power was also used offensively, as RAF Bomber Command and the United States Army Air Forces bombed German cities, with Hamburg in July 1943 and Dresden in February 1945 among the most devastating raids. This brought war to civilians on a new scale.\n\nAt sea, technology produced a contest between attack and defence. German U-boats sank large numbers of Allied merchant ships in the Atlantic, but the convoy system, escort groups and airborne and ship-borne radar reduced losses from 1943. In the Pacific the aircraft carrier replaced the battleship as the main weapon, and at Midway in June 1942 the Americans sank four Japanese carriers, ending Japan's advance. Carrier air power and submarines then supported the island-hopping advance towards Japan, and B-29 bombers attacked Japanese cities. Intelligence was equally important. At Bletchley Park the British decrypted German Enigma messages, building on methods that Polish mathematicians had passed to Britain and France in July 1939, and the resulting intelligence, known as Ultra, was used in the Battle of the Atlantic and in North Africa.\n\nGermany tried to use new weapons to reverse its fortunes. The V-1 flying bomb was launched against London from June 1944 and the V-2 rocket, developed by Wernher von Braun's team, from September 1944, and it was also used against Antwerp. The Messerschmitt Me 262 jet fighter entered service in 1944. These were technological marvels, but they came too late and in too small numbers to change the outcome. Allied superiority in producing and applying technology mattered more than German innovation.\n\nThe most dramatic development was the atomic bomb. Developed by the United States in the Manhattan Project and first tested in New Mexico on 16 July 1945, it was dropped on Hiroshima on 6 August and Nagasaki on 9 August, and Japan announced its surrender on 15 August 1945. A single bomb could now destroy a city, and warfare had entered a new age. Overall, technology gave Germany its early victories through blitzkrieg, helped Britain survive through radar and codebreaking, shifted the balance at sea and in the air towards the Allies, and ended the war with a weapon that changed the nature of war for ever.",
