    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The Cold War developed in Europe out of the collapse of the wartime alliance between the USA, Britain and the Soviet Union, hardened into two armed blocs divided by the Iron Curtain, and ended between 1985 and 1991. Its origins lay in Stalin's determination to control Eastern Europe, and its end came when Mikhail Gorbachev's reforms and his refusal to use force allowed communism to collapse in Eastern Europe and then in the Soviet Union itself.\n\nThe alliance began to break down as soon as Germany was defeated. At Yalta in February 1945 Stalin promised free elections in liberated Europe, but the Red Army occupied Poland, Romania, Bulgaria and Hungary, and he wanted a buffer of friendly states after invasions in 1914 and 1941. At Potsdam, Truman and Stalin disputed reparations and the German-Polish border, and the American atomic bomb deepened Soviet suspicion. In March 1946 Churchill at Fulton, Missouri, declared that an iron curtain had descended from Stettin to Trieste, and George Kennan's Long Telegram urged that Soviet expansion be contained. In March 1947 the Truman Doctrine promised aid to Greece and Turkey, and in June Marshall Aid offered about 13 billion dollars to rebuild Western Europe. Stalin forbade the satellite states to accept it, set up the Cominform in September 1947 and Comecon in 1949, and communists sliced away their opponents by salami tactics, culminating in the Czechoslovak coup of February 1948. By 1949 Europe was formally divided.\n\nGermany became the focus of the conflict. When the Western zones introduced the Deutsche Mark in June 1948, Stalin blockaded West Berlin, but the Western powers kept the city alive through an airlift that delivered over two million tons of supplies before the blockade ended in May 1949. The result was the Federal Republic in May 1949, the German Democratic Republic in October, and NATO in April 1949. When West Germany joined NATO in 1955 the Soviet Union answered with the Warsaw Pact, so that two armed alliances now faced one another.\n\nThe Cold War then developed through crises that brought the world close to nuclear war. In 1956 Soviet tanks crushed the Hungarian Uprising, showing that Moscow would use force to hold its bloc together. After Khrushchev's Berlin ultimatum of 1958, the Berlin Wall was built in August 1961, which the West accepted rather than risk war. In October 1962 the discovery of Soviet missiles in Cuba produced the closest confrontation of all, ended when Khrushchev withdrew them in return for a pledge not to invade Cuba. The fear it caused led to the hotline and the Partial Test Ban Treaty of 1963, and then to détente, with Ostpolitik, SALT I and the Helsinki Final Act of 1975. Even so, the invasion of Czechoslovakia in 1968 and the Brezhnev Doctrine showed that Moscow would not tolerate reform. Détente collapsed after the Soviet invasion of Afghanistan in 1979, and Reagan's talk of an evil empire and the Strategic Defense Initiative in 1983 opened a second Cold War.\n\nThe end came with Gorbachev, General Secretary from March 1985. He met Reagan at Geneva in 1985 and Reykjavik in 1986 and signed the INF Treaty in December 1987, eliminating a class of missiles. He withdrew from Afghanistan by February 1989, and glasnost and perestroika exposed the failures of a command economy that could no longer sustain the arms race. Most important, he abandoned the Brezhnev Doctrine, which Gennady Gerasimov described as replaced by the Sinatra Doctrine. Opposition movements such as Solidarity in Poland and Charter 77 in Czechoslovakia therefore faced no Soviet tanks. Solidarity won the June 1989 elections, the Berlin Wall fell on 9 November, Havel came to power in the Velvet Revolution and Ceausescu was executed in December. Germany was reunified in October 1990, the Warsaw Pact was dissolved in July 1991 and, after the failed coup of August 1991, the Soviet Union itself was dissolved on 26 December.\n\nThe Cold War therefore grew from Stalin's imposition of communism on Eastern Europe and Western determination to resist it, and was deepened by Berlin, Hungary and Cuba. It ended not through military defeat but because the Soviet system could no longer afford to hold its empire by force and its last leader chose not to. Western firmness played a part, but the weakness of communist economies and the courage of opposition movements were the decisive causes.",
