    "label": "2",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Movements towards European unity between 1945 and 1973 and the Oil Crisis of 1973 had a profound social and economic impact, though in opposite directions. Unity brought the members of the EEC a quarter-century of growing trade, rising living standards and new rights, while the Oil Crisis ended the long post-war boom, bringing inflation, unemployment and a painful restructuring of industry.\n\nUnity began as an effort to prevent another war and rebuild shattered economies. The Schuman Declaration of 9 May 1950, drafted by Jean Monnet, proposed pooling French and West German coal and steel under a common authority, and the Treaty of Paris of 1951 created the European Coal and Steel Community of France, West Germany, Italy and the Benelux states. Coal and steel were the raw materials of war, so pooling them made another Franco-German conflict materially hard to imagine. Its greatest social impact was to end the old hostility between France and Germany and bring West Germany back into European life as an equal partner.\n\nThe Treaties of Rome, signed on 25 March 1957 and in force from January 1958, created the European Economic Community. It aimed at a customs union with the removal of internal tariffs and a common external tariff, and at the free movement of goods, services, capital and workers. The customs union was completed in July 1968, ahead of schedule, and trade between the members grew rapidly. This fuelled the prosperity of the 1950s and 1960s, which brought families cars, refrigerators and washing machines. The Treaty also contained Article 119 on equal pay for men and women, an important basis for later change in the position of women.\n\nThe Common Agricultural Policy from 1962 had a mixed impact. By setting common prices for farm products it gave farmers guaranteed incomes after the shortages of the war years. Yet it quickly became the largest item in the Community budget, and its cost was one cause of the empty chair crisis, when de Gaulle withdrew France from the Council of Ministers in June 1965. Enlargement was delayed by his vetoes of British membership in 1963 and 1967, until Britain, Ireland and Denmark joined on 1 January 1973. Ireland, which entered after a referendum in May 1972 with about 83 percent in favour, gained access to larger markets and farm price support.\n\nThe Oil Crisis of 1973 then struck an economy that had grown for almost thirty years on cheap energy. After the Yom Kippur War began on 6 October 1973, the Arab oil states raised prices and embargoed the USA and the Netherlands, and the price of oil roughly quadrupled. The social effects were visible in daily life, as West Germany and the Netherlands introduced car-free Sundays, speed limits were lowered, and Britain was put on a three-day working week in early 1974 as power supplies ran short.\n\nThe economic effects were more lasting. The shock produced stagflation, a combination of stagnation and rising prices that Keynesian policy could not easily cure, and importing states fell into balance of payments deficits. Unemployment rose from the low levels of the 1960s to above 10 percent across the EEC by the mid-1980s, and the second oil shock of 1979 caused another recession. Heavy industries such as steel, shipbuilding and coal declined, leaving deep regional hardship, and governments moved away from the post-war commitment to full employment towards the fight against inflation. The crisis also changed policy, as France launched its nuclear programme under the Messmer Plan, the International Energy Agency was founded in 1974, and the case for common action led to the European Monetary System in 1979.\n\nOverall, European unity had a largely positive impact, laying the basis for peace, trade and prosperity, whereas the Oil Crisis brought a harsh end to that prosperity. Historians point out that inflation and the end of the Bretton Woods system were already weakening the boom, so the oil shock hastened rather than caused its end, but its cost in unemployment and industrial decline was very real.",
