    "label": "1",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Mohandas Gandhi and David Ben-Gurion both made a decisive contribution to the affairs of their countries, though in very different ways. Gandhi turned the Indian National Congress into a mass movement and gave Indian nationalism its method of non-violent resistance, while Ben-Gurion led the Jewish community in Palestine through the struggle with Britain and the Arabs and proclaimed the State of Israel. I will argue that each was the central figure of his national movement, but that both fell short of some of their aims.\n\nGandhi's first major contribution was to change the character of Indian politics. Returning from South Africa in 1915, where he had developed satyagraha, or non-violent civil disobedience, he became the dominant Congress leader after 1920. He built a movement among peasants and urban workers, wore simple dress and promoted hand-spinning as a symbol of self-reliance. He tested his methods in the peasant campaigns at Champaran in 1917 and Kheda in 1918. Before him Congress had mostly petitioned the British; under him it became a force that millions of ordinary Indians could join.\n\nSecondly, Gandhi led the great campaigns that made British rule harder to sustain. After the Amritsar massacre of 1919 he led the Non-Cooperation movement of 1920 to 1922, urging Indians to boycott British goods, courts, schools and councils, although he called it off after the violence at Chauri Chaura. In 1930 the Salt March, about 240 miles to Dandi, where he made salt in defiance of the British monopoly, drew in vast numbers and gained world attention. In August 1942 he backed the Quit India resolution demanding British withdrawal, and was imprisoned until 1944. These campaigns showed the British that their authority could not be maintained by force alone, so they were a real contribution to independence.\n\nGandhi also tried to reshape Indian society, with mixed success. He sought Hindu-Muslim unity and an end to untouchability, calling the untouchables Harijans. In 1932, after Ambedkar won separate electorates for Dalits, Gandhi fasted in Yerwada jail and the Poona Pact replaced them with reserved seats in the general electorates. Critics such as Ambedkar felt he did too little on caste. His greatest failure was that he could not prevent partition. He publicly opposed it, yet in June 1947 the Congress and League leaders accepted it, and independence on 15 August 1947 came amid communal killings in which hundreds of thousands died. On independence day he was in Calcutta, fasting rather than celebrating, and he was shot dead by the Hindu nationalist Nathuram Godse on 30 January 1948 after trying to stop the violence.\n\nBen-Gurion's contribution began with his leadership of the Jewish community in Palestine. Born in Plonsk in 1886, he emigrated to Palestine in 1906 and became a labour activist. From 1935 he led the executive of the Jewish Agency, which was linked to the Haganah, the main Jewish defence force. He steered the community through the White Paper of 1939, which limited Jewish immigration and which the Zionists rejected, and through the pressure of the Holocaust, which intensified the demand for a state. In the years after the war he faced British restrictions on refugee ships, the Exodus affair of 1947 and the attacks by the Irgun and Lehi, and he kept the mainstream Zionist leadership focused on building a state.\n\nHis most important decision was to accept the United Nations partition plan of November 1947 as a first step towards a Jewish state, even though the Arab leaders rejected it. When Britain ended the Mandate, he proclaimed the State of Israel in Tel Aviv on 14 May 1948 and became its first prime minister, which was perhaps the most decisive single act of either man. The next day the armies of Egypt, Jordan, Syria, Iraq and Lebanon attacked, and he oversaw the war that left Israel with more territory than the UN plan had allotted, though about 700,000 Palestinian Arabs fled or were expelled and the refugee question remained unresolved. Historians such as Benny Morris argue that he backed the expulsions from Lydda and Ramle in July 1948, and Arab opinion sees him as the architect of a state that displaced Palestinians.\n\nAs prime minister, in 1948 to 1954 and 1955 to 1963, and also as defence minister, he built the institutions of the state. He created the Israel Defence Forces in May 1948, ordering the shelling of the Irgun ship Altalena in June to establish that there would be one army. Under his government the Law of Return of 1950 gave Jews the right to immigrate, and he took Israel into the Suez affair, attending the secret talks at Sevres with Britain and France in October 1956. Most Jews regard him as the founder of the state.\n\nBoth men therefore made an enormous contribution, but of different kinds. Gandhi created the method and the mass base that made independence possible, though he could not hold India together, whereas Ben-Gurion was a state-builder whose decisions secured Israel's survival, though at a cost that left the conflict with the Arabs unresolved. Gandhi was the moral leader of a movement, and Ben-Gurion the practical founder of a state, and in each case the country's affairs would have been very different without him.",
