    "label": "2",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Katanga seceded from the Congo on 11 July 1960 because of the region's mineral wealth, its local politics and the backing of Belgian interests, and the secession drew in the United Nations, Belgium and the Cold War powers because it threatened the survival of the newly independent Congo and became entangled with the struggle between East and West. It lasted about two and a half years and ended only after the UN used force.\n\nThe first cause was Katanga's wealth. It was the copper-mining heart of the Congo and also produced cobalt, zinc and uranium. The Union Miniere du Haut-Katanga, a company with Belgian shareholders, ran the mines and gave the province a far higher revenue base than any other. Katangan leaders could argue that the province should keep its own revenue rather than share it with a poorer country, so the economic motive for secession was very strong.\n\nThe second cause was regional and ethnic politics. Moise Tshombe founded the Conakat party in 1958 to represent the Lunda and related peoples of southern Katanga, and it campaigned for regional autonomy. Its rival, the Balubakat party under Jason Sendwe, drew on the Baluba of the north and Baluba migrants in the mining towns. In the provincial elections of May 1960 Conakat won the most seats and Tshombe became provincial president. He presented secession as protection against a central government in Leopoldville that he said was controlled by outsiders and Communists, and Lumumba's allies had won many Katangan seats in the national elections, which made the new central government a real threat to Tshombe's local power.\n\nThe third cause was Belgian support. Belgian mining interests and officials had backed Conakat's autonomy campaign, and when the mutiny of the Force Publique on 5 to 6 July 1960 led Belgium to send troops back on 10 July, Tshombe declared the Katangan State the next day, only eleven days after independence. Belgian officers, advisers and paid foreign mercenaries trained and led the Katanga Gendarmerie. The Congo had very few trained Congolese, since by 1960 only a handful held university degrees, and independence had been fixed at the Brussels Round Table only five months earlier, so the central government was too weak to resist. No state recognised Katanga, which shows how much it depended on Belgian help.\n\nInternational involvement began at once. Lumumba, faced with the secession and the Belgian return, appealed to the United Nations, and the Security Council set up the ONUC operation in July 1960, which grew to about 20,000 troops and was the largest UN operation of its time. The Council called on Belgium to withdraw on 9 August and UN troops entered Katanga on 12 August. But the UN would not use force against Tshombe, which frustrated Lumumba, and he turned to the Soviet Union for aircraft and advisers. This brought the Cold War into the Congo, because the West feared a Communist foothold in the heart of Africa. Kasa-Vubu dismissed Lumumba in September, Mobutu intervened, and Lumumba was flown to Katanga and murdered on 17 January 1961 with Belgian officers present, which caused riots and protests across the world and Resolution 161 of February 1961 authorising force as a last resort.\n\nThe UN then became directly engaged against the secession. Irish troops were part of the force. Nine were killed in an ambush by Baluba tribesmen at Niemba in November 1960, and in September 1961 A Company under Commandant Pat Quinlan was besieged at Jadotville by gendarmes and mercenaries, and surrendered after five days without a single Irish death. Conor Cruise O'Brien, the UN representative in Katanga, pressed for a firmer line, and Operation Morthor on 13 September 1961 tried to arrest the foreign officers but failed. Resolution 169 of November 1961 explicitly authorised action against the secession and the mercenaries.\n\nThe crisis cost the UN its Secretary-General. Dag Hammarskjold died in an air crash near Ndola in Northern Rhodesia on 18 September 1961 while travelling to negotiate a ceasefire with Tshombe. The Soviet Union had already attacked his handling of the Congo and demanded a troika. Fighting in December 1961 brought the Kitona Declaration, which Tshombe later repudiated, and finally Operation Grand Slam in December 1962 defeated the Katangan forces, so Tshombe announced the end of the secession on 14 January 1963.\n\nKatanga therefore seceded because mineral wealth, ethnic politics and Belgian backing combined in a weak new state, and the secession became international because it involved the UN's first large peacekeeping effort, Belgian and Soviet interests and Cold War rivalry. Its end preserved the Congo's unity, but at the cost of Hammarskjold's life, and the instability helped Mobutu seize power in November 1965.",
