    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Decolonisation was only partly a positive experience for former colonies. Independence gave Africans control of their own governments and in some places brought real gains in unity and social progress, but in others it was followed by civil war, dependence on former rulers and economic hardship. Looking at Tanzania, Nigeria, the Congo and Angola, I will argue that the experience was positive in principle but very uneven, depending largely on how peaceful the transfer of power was and how well the new state was prepared.\n\nThe clearest positive case is Tanganyika, later Tanzania. Independence came peacefully in 1961 under Julius Nyerere, whose TANU won the 1960 elections. Tanganyika joined with Zanzibar in 1964 to form the United Republic. It had more than a hundred ethnic groups and no dominant one, and Nyerere made Swahili the national language and avoided ethnic parties, which helped avoid the conflicts that scarred other states. The Arusha Declaration of 1967 committed Tanzania to ujamaa, or African socialism, and the country made important gains in health and literacy, with universal primary education launched in 1974 and rapidly rising enrolment. Independence therefore gave Tanzanians national unity and better social services.\n\nHowever, Tanzania's economic record shows the limits of this success. From 1973 to 1974 the government moved millions of people into villages, often by force, in the policy of villagisation. Farming was disrupted and food shortages followed, and the oil price rise, drought and the cost of the war against Idi Amin in 1978 to 1979 deepened the crisis, so that by the early 1980s the economy was declining. Nyerere stepped down in 1985 and his successor accepted an IMF programme in 1986. Even the best case of decolonisation was therefore a mixed one.\n\nNigeria's experience shows both promise and disaster. It became independent on 1 October 1960 as a federation of three regions under Prime Minister Abubakar Tafawa Balewa, which was a smooth transfer. But Britain had joined the northern and southern territories in 1914, and ethnic rivalry between the regions undermined the federation. Coups in 1966 were followed by massacres, and the Eastern Region declared itself the Republic of Biafra under Colonel Ojukwu on 30 May 1967. The Biafran War lasted until January 1970 and caused a famine in which several hundred thousand people, perhaps more than two million by some estimates, died. After the war Gowon's policy of reconciliation, rehabilitation and reconstruction, with no victor and no vanquished, did hold the country together, which was a significant achievement.\n\nThe Congo was the worst case, because Belgium prepared it so poorly. Belgium ran the colony for profit, and by 1960 only a handful of Congolese had a university degree and the Force Publique had no Congolese officers. Independence was fixed for 30 June 1960 only five months after the Brussels Round Table, and within days the army mutinied, Belgian troops returned and Katanga seceded with Belgian backing. Prime Minister Lumumba was dismissed, arrested and murdered in January 1961, and the UN operation, which at its height had about 20,000 troops, was drawn into a Cold War crisis. Mobutu seized power in 1965 and ruled Zaire as a one-party dictatorship until 1997, relying on Western aid, so for most Congolese independence brought instability and then authoritarian rule rather than freedom.\n\nAngola shows the cost of delayed decolonisation. Portugal under Salazar regarded it as an overseas province and refused independence, so a war began in 1961 that lasted until the Carnation Revolution in Lisbon in April 1974 overthrew the regime. Independence came on 11 November 1975, but the MPLA, UNITA and FNLA were already rivals, and a civil war followed, with Cuban troops and Soviet aid for the MPLA and South African and American support for its opponents, which lasted until 2002. Most of the roughly 300,000 Portuguese settlers left, taking skills and capital with them, so the new state had few trained administrators.\n\nThe economic legacy was also mixed. Colonial economies had been built around exporting cash crops and minerals, so new states stayed dependent on a few commodities whose prices fell in the 1980s. The Lome Convention of 1975 gave 46 African, Caribbean and Pacific states duty-free access to European markets, but most exports stayed concentrated on a few primary products, and debt and structural adjustment forced cuts in public services in the 1980s. Famines in Biafra, the Sahel and Ethiopia in the 1970s and 1980s showed how fragile food supply remained.\n\nOverall, decolonisation was positive in that it ended foreign rule and gave Africans their own states, and where it was peaceful and the leadership united the country, as in Tanzania, there were real gains. But where it was rushed, as in the Congo, or delayed, as in Angola, or where colonial borders ignored ethnic divisions, as in Nigeria, the result was war, dependence and hardship. It is fair to say that independence was a necessary achievement, but not by itself a guarantee of a better life.",
