    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The problems that emerged in multi-racial Britain and France after 1945 were similar in their roots and symptoms: both countries recruited migrants from former colonies to fill labour shortages, and both then met discrimination, racial violence, urban disadvantage and the rise of far-right politics. They differed in the legal status of the migrants, in the official philosophy of integration and in the form that conflict took, because Britain developed multiculturalism while France insisted on assimilation.\n\nThe origins of migration were alike. After the war Britain needed workers for the National Health Service, London Transport and the postal service, and the British Nationality Act of 1948 gave Commonwealth citizens the right to settle. The Empire Windrush docked at Tilbury in June 1948 with about 1,000 Caribbean passengers, and migration from Punjab and Gujarat grew in the 1950s towards Birmingham, Leicester, Bradford and London. France likewise had reconstruction labour shortages, and migrants from Portugal, Spain, Italy, Morocco, Tunisia and West Africa filled jobs in factories, mines and construction. In both states the newcomers were concentrated in a few cities and in the poorest housing. Caribbean migrants met a colour bar in lodgings and the exploitation of landlords such as Peter Rachman in Notting Hill, while many migrants in France lived in bidonvilles such as Nanterre, and later in run-down HLM estates in the banlieues.\n\nThe legal position, however, differed. Most migrants to Britain were Commonwealth citizens with the right to enter, vote and live there, so the British response was to restrict that right by law: the Commonwealth Immigrants Act of 1962 introduced employment vouchers, the 1968 Act was rushed through Parliament to limit Kenyan Asians holding British passports, and the Immigration Act of 1971 introduced patriality. France drew its migrants from southern Europe as well as North and West Africa, and Algerians held French nationality until independence in 1962 and could move freely, which meant their migration was not legally immigration before then. The Franco-Algerian accord of 1968 gave them preferential rights. Yet both countries ended up in the same place in the 1970s, since France suspended labour immigration in July 1974 and allowed only family reunification from 1976, just as Britain tightened its controls.\n\nRacism and violence were common to both. In August and September 1958 white youths attacked West Indian residents in Notting Hill and Nottingham, and Kelso Cochrane was murdered in Notting Hill in 1959. Enoch Powell's Birmingham speech of April 1968 gave respectability to anti-immigrant feeling. In France the police attack on Algerian demonstrators in Paris on 17 October 1961 under Maurice Papon killed dozens or more, and the legacy of the Algerian war shadowed the treatment of North Africans and of the harkis, who were housed in camps. Both states also saw clashes between young people and the police. In Britain the heavy use of the sus laws and Operation Swamp 81 led to the Brixton riots of April 1981 and to disorder in Toxteth and Moss Side, and the Scarman Report blamed racial disadvantage, unemployment and poor policing. In France the summer of 1981 brought car burnings and clashes with police in the Minguettes estate at Vénissieux near Lyon. In each country second-generation youth faced unemployment well above average and a sense of exclusion.\n\nThe far right also grew in both. The National Front, founded in 1967, campaigned for an end to immigration and fought the 1974 and 1979 general elections, but never won a seat and was confronted by the Anti-Nazi League and Rock Against Racism. In France the Front National of Jean-Marie Le Pen, founded in 1972, did far better: it won about 17 per cent at Dreux in 1983, about 11 per cent and ten seats in the European elections of 1984, 35 Assembly seats in 1986, and Le Pen took about 14.4 per cent in the presidential election of 1988. The French system of proportional voting in 1986 helped the extreme right in a way that Britain's first-past-the-post system never did, which is a real difference in outcome.\n\nThe sharpest difference lay in the official response. Britain developed multiculturalism, recognising ethnic communities within a common legal framework, and tackled discrimination through the Race Relations Acts of 1965, 1968 and 1976, which created bodies such as the Commission for Racial Equality. Roy Jenkins described integration in 1966 as equal opportunity with cultural diversity. Mosques, gurdwaras and temples were built, the Notting Hill Carnival grew, and black and Asian MPs such as Diane Abbott and Keith Vaz entered Parliament in 1987. France adhered to assimilation and laïcité, treating all as equal citizens and recording no ethnic statistics, so discrimination was harder to measure. The Pleven law of 1972 outlawed incitement to racial hatred, and the headscarf affair at Creil in 1989 and the Pasqua laws of 1986 showed the strain between the republican model and Muslim identity. French protest also took a different form, in the March for Equality of 1983 and SOS Racisme, with its slogan Touche pas à mon pote.\n\nIn the end the two countries faced very similar problems of disadvantage, discrimination and extremism, arising from a colonial past and economic need, but they diverged in law and philosophy. Neither model ended disadvantage, which is why both Britain's riots of 1981 and France's banlieue tensions show that legal equality alone did not secure integration.",
