    "label": "1",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The United States became involved in war in both Korea and Vietnam for the same underlying reason, the policy of containment, which held that communism had to be checked wherever it threatened to spread. The how differed. In Korea, America went to war within days of a clear invasion, under a United Nations flag. In Vietnam, involvement crept forward from financial aid to advisers to a huge ground war.\n\nContainment shaped both decisions. George Kennan's Long Telegram of 1946 and the Truman Doctrine of March 1947 committed America to supporting free peoples against communist pressure. The successes of the late 1940s, the Marshall Plan, NATO and the Berlin airlift, made Washington confident, but 1949 shook that confidence: Mao proclaimed the People's Republic of China on 1 October and the Soviet Union tested an atomic bomb in August. Republicans asked \"Who lost China?\", so Truman feared that any further loss in Asia would be politically fatal. NSC-68 in April 1950 called for a large rise in defence spending to meet the threat. Korea and Vietnam were fought in this atmosphere of fear that a communist advance anywhere would damage American credibility everywhere.\n\nThe immediate cause of the Korean war was the North Korean invasion of the South across the 38th parallel on 25 June 1950. Korea had been divided at that line in 1945, and Syngman Rhee in the South and Kim Il Sung in the North each claimed the whole country. American troops had left in 1949, and Dean Acheson's speech of January 1950 left Korea outside the American defence perimeter, which communist leaders may have read as a green light. Truman saw the attack as a test of containment, a Soviet-backed move that had to be answered. With the Soviet delegate absent, the UN Security Council condemned the attack and recommended help for South Korea. Truman sent American forces without asking Congress for a declaration of war, calling it a police action, and General MacArthur led the UN forces.\n\nHow the war was fought shows how involvement deepened. MacArthur's landing at Inchon on 15 September 1950 cut North Korean supply lines and led to the recapture of Seoul. The decision to cross the 38th parallel in October turned a defensive war into an attempt to liberate the North, and it brought Chinese forces in large numbers from late October and November, driving the UN back. Truman then chose a limited war and dismissed MacArthur on 11 April 1951 when he argued publicly for attacking China. The armistice at Panmunjom on 27 July 1953 left the frontier near the 38th parallel, with about 36,000 American dead. Containment had succeeded in saving South Korea, but at a heavy cost.\n\nInvolvement in Vietnam began with the French war in Indochina. From 1950 Truman gave aid to France, and by 1954 America was paying about three quarters of the cost. After the French defeat at Dien Bien Phu in May 1954, Eisenhower described the domino theory, that if Vietnam fell to communism the rest of Southeast Asia would follow. The Geneva Accords of July 1954 divided the country at the 17th parallel, and the promised elections of 1956 were never held. America backed Ngo Dinh Diem in the South, joined SEATO in 1954 and trained the South Vietnamese army. The National Liberation Front, or Viet Cong, was formed in December 1960 to overthrow Diem, so America was now committed to a government under armed challenge.\n\nKennedy deepened the commitment without sending combat troops. He raised the number of advisers to about 16,000 by late 1963 and backed the unpopular Strategic Hamlet programme. Diem's persecution of Buddhists, including the self-immolation of Thich Quang Duc in June 1963, made him a liability, and he was overthrown and killed on 2 November 1963 in a coup that US officials had tacitly encouraged. That left America responsible for the chaos that followed. Johnson, who feared a repeat of the \"loss of China\" and a right-wing backlash that would wreck his Great Society, told his ambassador that he would not lose Vietnam.\n\nThe decisive steps came under Johnson. The Gulf of Tonkin incidents of August 1964, one of them probably never happened, produced the Tonkin Gulf Resolution on 7 August by 416 to 0 in the House and 88 to 2 in the Senate, giving him a blank cheque without a declaration of war. After a Viet Cong attack at Pleiku in February 1965, Rolling Thunder bombing began on 2 March, and Marines landed at Da Nang on 8 March. On 28 July 1965 Johnson announced that forces would rise from 75,000 to 125,000, with more to follow. Numbers reached about 385,000 by the end of 1966 and over 500,000 in 1968. Westmoreland's strategy of attrition kept demanding more men, so each step made the next harder to refuse.\n\nOverall, America entered both wars through containment, but Korea followed a clear act of aggression and a decisive Truman decision, while Vietnam grew gradually from the domino theory, fear of political attack at home and a series of small steps that left Johnson committed to a war he could not win.",
