    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The United States achieved the Moon landing of July 1969 through a presidential commitment, an enormous national organisation and years of step by step testing. Its significance was national, as a triumph of American technology and unity, and international, as a Cold War victory over the Soviet Union and a global spectacle.\n\nThe programme began as a response to Soviet success. The Soviet launch of Sputnik on 4 October 1957 alarmed Americans about Soviet technology and education, and led to the creation of NASA in 1958 and the National Defense Education Act. Yuri Gagarin became the first man in space on 12 April 1961, and days later the Bay of Pigs failure humiliated Kennedy, who decided that the Moon was a field where America could win. On 25 May 1961 he told Congress that the United States would land a man on the Moon and return him safely before the end of the decade, and at Rice University in September 1962 he said the nation chose to go to the Moon because it was hard. Political will came first, since the aim was set publicly with a deadline.\n\nSecond, the landing depended on vast organisation and money. The Apollo programme cost about 25 billion dollars, and NASA's budget peaked at about 4 to 5 per cent of federal spending in 1965 and 1966. NASA directed hundreds of thousands of people and contractors such as North American Aviation, Grumman, Boeing and IBM, with James Webb managing the politics and contracts and Mission Control in Houston directing flights. Wernher von Braun developed the Saturn V at the Marshall Space Flight Center, the most powerful rocket built until then, and the Apollo Guidance Computer at MIT was one of the first to use integrated circuits. NASA chose lunar orbit rendezvous in 1962, with a separate lunar module landing while the command module stayed in orbit.\n\nThird, success came through careful testing, and through learning from failure. The Mercury flights of Alan Shepard (1961) and John Glenn (1962) showed that Americans could fly and orbit, and Gemini in 1965 and 1966 tested rendezvous and docking. The Apollo 1 fire on 27 January 1967 killed Grissom, White and Chaffee and led to a redesign of the spacecraft. Apollo 8 in December 1968 first took men round the Moon, and Apollo 9 and 10 in 1969 tested the lunar module, Apollo 10 flying to within about 15 kilometres of the surface. Meanwhile the Soviet N1 rocket failed in every test, so the rival programme collapsed.\n\nApollo 11 was launched from Florida on 16 July 1969 with Neil Armstrong, Buzz Aldrin and Michael Collins. The lunar module Eagle landed in the Sea of Tranquillity on 20 July. The descent was risky: computer alarms sounded and Armstrong took manual control to avoid a boulder field, landing with very little fuel left. Armstrong and Aldrin spent about two and a half hours outside, collected about 21 kilograms of rock and left a plaque reading We came in peace for all mankind. The crew splashed down in the Pacific on 24 July, recovered by USS Hornet, so Kennedy's goal was met with months to spare.\n\nNationally, the landing was a triumph of American confidence and technology at a bad moment, in the year of the anti-war movement and urban unrest. It justified the huge spending, boosted pride, and tied the growth of the Sun Belt in Florida, Texas, Alabama and California to federal contracts, and Nixon telephoned the astronauts from the White House. Critics such as Amitai Etzioni had called the programme a waste, and some in Congress argued the money would be better spent on poverty, so national unity was never complete. After 1969 public interest and the NASA budget declined, and Apollo 17 in December 1972 was the last crewed Moon mission.\n\nInternationally, the landing was a Cold War victory. It answered Sputnik and Gagarin, and Soviet media played down the achievement, while the Luna 15 probe crashed on the Moon on 21 July 1969. About 600 million people watched it live on television, making it one of the most watched events in history, and the image of American technology was carried to the whole world. The Americans also presented it as for all mankind, and did not claim the Moon, since the Outer Space Treaty of 1967 banned national claims. Later the Apollo-Soyuz flight of July 1975 showed cooperation with the Soviet Union.\n\nOverall, the landing was achieved by presidential determination, enormous resources and gradual testing, and its significance was greater internationally, where it decided the space race, than nationally, where it lifted pride but could not remove the divisions of the late 1960s.",
    "model_source": "sonnet-long-fix",
