    "label": "C1",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Redmond stayed loyal to Parnell in 1890 and led the small Parnellite group after Parnell's death in 1891. The parties reunited in January 1900, and Redmond was elected chairman in February 1900. He took part in the Land Conference of 1902 to 1903, which led to the Wyndham Act, giving tenants loans to buy their farms. In 1910 the Irish Party held the balance of power, and it backed the Liberals against the House of Lords, so the Parliament Act of 1911 removed the Lords' veto. Asquith introduced the Third Home Rule Bill in April 1912, and it became law on 18 September 1914 but was suspended for the war, which Redmond accepted. In March 1914 he accepted that the Ulster counties could opt out for six years, a concession he had previously refused.",
