    "label": "C4",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Edward Carson was born in Dublin in 1854 and became leader of the Irish Unionist parliamentary party in 1910. He was the chief unionist campaigner against Home Rule from 1911 to 1914, and he led the Ulster Day rally on 28 September 1912. On Ulster Day about 237,000 men signed the Solemn League and Covenant, pledging to use all means to defeat Home Rule, and about 234,000 women signed a separate declaration. The Parliament Act of 1911 had removed the Lords' veto, so the Third Home Rule Bill would become law within about two years. Unionists opposed Home Rule because they feared a Dublin parliament dominated by the Catholic Church, summed up in the slogan Home Rule is Rome Rule. Belfast's economy also depended on British and imperial markets, which unionists feared would suffer under a Dublin parliament.",
