    "model": "McElligott joined the Department of Finance in 1923 as an Assistant Secretary and was its Secretary from 1927 to 1953. He then served as Governor of the Central Bank from 1953 to 1960. He favoured financial orthodoxy, with balanced budgets, low taxes and limited state borrowing, and he was sceptical of high-spending social programmes. He was also wary of industrial protection as practised by Seán Lemass, and Ronan Fanning argues that Finance's caution held back growth until the late 1950s.",
