    "label": "C3",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Gerry Adams led Sinn Féin's move into electoral politics after he became its president in November 1983. In 1981 Sinn Féin adopted an electoral strategy alongside the armed campaign, and it won five seats in the 1982 Assembly election. In June 1983 Adams won the West Belfast seat at Westminster, although Sinn Féin members refused to take their seats there. He had earlier been released in July 1972 to take part in secret talks with William Whitelaw in London. He began a dialogue with John Hume in 1988, which historians see as an early step towards the peace process. The broadcasting ban of October 1988 kept his voice off British television, so broadcasters used actors' voices instead.",
