    "label": "C4",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Margaret Thatcher was Prime Minister from 1979 to 1990 and made security and the defeat of terrorism central to her policy in Northern Ireland. She refused to grant political status to the hunger strikers in 1981, and ten prisoners died during the protest. In November 1984 she rejected all three options of the New Ireland Forum report, a decision that set the limits of British policy. She signed the Anglo-Irish Agreement with Garret FitzGerald at Hillsborough Castle on 15 November 1985, giving the Irish government a formal consultative role. Unionists regarded her as having betrayed the Union, and they staged a major rally at Belfast City Hall. Historians debate her motives, with some stressing better security cooperation with Dublin and others the aim of limiting Sinn Féin's growth.",
