    "label": "C4",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Louis-Napoléon carried out a coup on 2 December 1851, arresting deputies and dissolving the Assembly, and a plebiscite approved it by a very large majority. The constitution of January 1852 gave the president a ten-year term and wide powers, and in December 1852 he proclaimed the Second Empire as Napoleon III. The early Empire was authoritarian, with the press censored, opponents exiled and elections controlled by official candidates. From 1860 the legislature was given more freedom to debate, and in 1864 the right to strike was legalised. The press law of 1868 relaxed censorship. Émile Ollivier formed a liberal ministry in 1870, but Napoleon III was captured at Sedan on 2 September 1870 and the Empire fell two days later.",
