    "label": "C3",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Leo XIII, pope from 1878, sought to influence European affairs through diplomacy, social teaching and scholarship, and he encouraged the study of Thomas Aquinas in Aeterni Patris of 1879. He ended the Kulturkampf by negotiation with Bismarck in the 1880s, and the Centre Party's cooperation helped to repeal most of the anti-Catholic laws. His encyclical Rerum Novarum of 1891 defended private property and the workers' right to a just wage and trade unions, while rejecting socialism and class war. The encyclical encouraged Catholic trade unions and Christian democratic parties in Germany, Belgium and Italy. His ralliement policy of 1890-92 urged French Catholics to recognise the Third Republic, but monarchist Catholics largely refused. He never reconciled himself to the loss of the Papal States, which left Catholics and the Italian state divided, so the Roman Question remained open.",
