    "model": "Hollywood studios such as MGM, Paramount, Warner Brothers and Fox produced most films shown in Britain and Europe, and by the late 1930s their films filled most screens there. American popular music reached Allied troops through the American Forces Network, which broadcast from 1942. American troops arriving in Britain brought swing, chewing gum and cheap goods, and the cinema was the weekly habit of millions. Nazi Germany condemned jazz and swing as degenerate, but swing youth in Hamburg defied the ban, and the regime responded with arrests.",
