    "label": "C1",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "The Jarrow March brought to light the collapse of a one-industry town after Palmers shipyard closed, leaving most of its working men without work. Unemployment in Jarrow was far above the national average, with well over half of insured workers on the dole in the mid-1930s. The loss of the yard's wages made shops, trades and families collapse with it. Jarrow had one of the worst records in Britain for poor housing, hunger and ill health. The National Shipbuilders Security scheme bought the Palmers site in 1934 and barred shipbuilding there for forty years, which ruled out any revival of the yard. The Special Areas Act of 1934 gave the town little help, showing the weakness of government policy for the distressed areas.",
