    "label": "C3",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "The Nuremberg Rallies used ritual to create a sense of sacred purpose, with the consecration of party banners, torchlight processions and oaths of loyalty. Mass parades of the party, the SA, the SS, the Hitler Youth and the army showed the movement's unity and strength to participants and to the German public. Speer's stone architecture, massed banners and, from 1934, about 130 searchlights known as the cathedral of light made each rally a spectacle. Hitler's speeches, usually given at night, expressed party doctrine when crowds were most receptive. Riefenstahl's Triumph of the Will of 1935 recorded the 1934 rally and presented Hitler and the party as the sole leadership. Her Olympia of 1938 presented the 1936 Olympics as a national triumph, and newsreels and cinemas carried the rallies to millions.",
