    "label": "C2",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Hungary under Rakosi was a rigidly Stalinist one-party state, with show trials, the AVH secret police and forced collectivisation, and living standards were falling. Khrushchev's secret speech of 25 February 1956 exposed Stalin's crimes and gave Hungarian reformers a precedent for criticising their own leaders. Poland's October 1956 change encouraged Hungarian intellectuals, and on 23 October crowds pulled down Stalin's statue while the AVH opened fire on the crowd outside the radio building. Soviet troops entered Budapest, but Nagy declared neutrality and announced withdrawal from the Warsaw Pact on 1 November, although the Soviet leadership had already decided on 31 October to intervene again. The main Soviet attack began on 4 November, crushing the fighting in Budapest and removing Nagy's government. Kadar was installed with Soviet backing, and Nagy was later executed in June 1958.",
