    "label": "C3",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "At home Gorbachev wanted to revive the economy through perestroika and to open public life through glasnost, and the 1987 Law on State Enterprise gave factories more freedom. He hoped democratisation would give reform popular support, and the first partly free elections were held in March 1989. Abroad he sought arms control, meeting Reagan at Geneva in 1985 and Reykjavik in 1986, which led to the INF Treaty of December 1987. He withdrew Soviet troops from Afghanistan by February 1989 and told the United Nations in 1988 that the armed forces would be cut by 500,000 men. He chose not to use force in eastern Europe, and communist regimes collapsed across the region in 1989. He was only partly successful: the economy worsened, nationalism grew in the republics, and the Soviet Union was dissolved on 26 December 1991.",
