    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Ben-Gurion accepted the 1947 UN partition plan as a first step towards a Jewish state, although the Arab states rejected it and fighting began in December 1947. On 14 May 1948 he proclaimed the State of Israel in Tel Aviv and became its first prime minister, and Egypt, Jordan, Syria, Iraq and Lebanon sent armies into former Mandate territory the next day. In May 1948 he oversaw the creation of the Israel Defence Forces, and in June 1948 he ordered the shelling of the Irgun ship Altalena to establish a single army. Historians such as Benny Morris argue that he backed the expulsions from Lydda and Ramle in July 1948, during a war in which about 700,000 Palestinian Arabs fled or were expelled. The 1949 armistice agreements left Israel with more territory than the UN plan had allotted it. In October 1956 he attended the secret Sevres talks with Britain and France that planned the attack on Egypt during the Suez Crisis.",
