    "label": "C3",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Chinua Achebe's novel Things Fall Apart, published in 1958, gave the world a widely read African account of Igbo life before and during colonial rule through the story of Okonkwo. The African Writers Series, begun by Heinemann in 1962 with Achebe as its first editorial adviser, made African novels available in schools and universities across the English-speaking world. His later novels, including A Man of the People (1966), examined corruption and the failures of the new independent states. In his 1965 essay on the African writer and the English language he answered critics such as Obi Wali by arguing that English could be remade to carry African experience. During the Biafran War he supported Biafra and travelled abroad to argue its cause. His 1975 lecture An Image of Africa criticised Conrad's Heart of Darkness, and his work is now studied as a foundational text of post-colonial literature.",
