    "label": "C4",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "After 1945 post-war labour shortages brought migrants from Portugal, Spain, Italy, Morocco, Tunisia and West Africa to work in factories, mines and construction, and Algerians held French nationality until 1962. The Franco-Algerian accord of 27 December 1968 gave Algerian nationals preferential rights to enter and work in France. In July 1974 the government suspended labour immigration, and in 1976 it allowed family reunification, so migrant families settled permanently. The second generation, known as the beurs, grew up in the banlieues, where youth unemployment was far above the national average. After unrest at the Minguettes estate in 1981, the Mitterrand government created Priority Education Zones and the Banlieues 89 project, which critics said were too limited. The March for Equality and against Racism of 1983 reached Paris with tens of thousands of supporters and secured Mitterrand's promise of a ten-year residence card, and the Pasqua laws of 1986 then tightened immigration control.",
