    "label": "C2",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Johnson took office in November 1963 and confirmed the policy of supporting South Vietnam, keeping Kennedy's advisers such as McNamara and Rusk. After the Gulf of Tonkin incidents of August 1964, Congress passed the Tonkin Gulf Resolution by 416 votes to 0 in the House and 88 to 2 in the Senate. Operation Rolling Thunder, a sustained bombing campaign against North Vietnam, began in March 1965, and the first US Marine combat units landed at Da Nang on 8 March 1965. US troops rose from about 184,000 at the end of 1965 to over 500,000 in 1968, but Westmoreland's search and destroy strategy did not break communist resolve. Senator Fulbright's televised hearings of 1966 and the teach-ins showed growing opposition, while the credibility gap widened between official statements and events. The Tet Offensive of January 1968 shocked American opinion, and on 31 March 1968 Johnson halted part of the bombing and announced that he would not seek re-election.",
