    "model": "In 1966 Ali said he had no quarrel with the Viet Cong, and on 28 April 1967 he refused induction into the army as a Muslim minister and conscientious objector. Boxing authorities stripped him of his title and licence, and in June 1967 he was convicted of draft evasion and sentenced to five years in prison. Many white Americans and sports writers condemned him, while many young black Americans and anti-war activists saw him as a principled leader. The Supreme Court overturned his conviction on 28 June 1971 by 8 votes to 0. His stand made him one of the most prominent critics of the war, and he was barred from boxing for over three years. Historians such as David Remnick treat him as an international symbol of black pride and resistance to the Vietnam War.",
