    "label": "4",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "American involvement in Vietnam wrecked Lyndon Johnson's presidency. It diverted money and attention from his Great Society, divided American society, destroyed public trust in his word, and in March 1968 forced him to announce that he would not seek re-election.\n\nJohnson inherited the commitment from Kennedy. When he became president in November 1963 there were about 16,000 American advisers in South Vietnam and the government had just been shaken by Diem's overthrow. Johnson kept Kennedy's advisers, including McNamara and Rusk, and feared that losing Vietnam would repeat the damage done to Truman's party over the loss of China and would provoke a right-wing backlash against his domestic programme. In August 1964 North Vietnamese torpedo boats attacked the USS Maddox in the Gulf of Tonkin, and a second reported attack, probably imagined, led Congress to pass the Tonkin Gulf Resolution by 416 to 0 in the House and 88 to 2 in the Senate. This gave Johnson wide powers, and in the 1964 election he won about 61 per cent of the vote against Barry Goldwater while presenting himself as the candidate of restraint.\n\nIn 1965 he chose escalation. After the Pleiku attack in February, Operation Rolling Thunder began on 2 March, and Marines landed at Da Nang on 8 March. On 28 July he announced that troops would rise from 75,000 to 125,000 with more to follow, without a declaration of war and without calling up the reserves, so as not to endanger the Great Society legislation passing through Congress. American numbers reached about 385,000 by the end of 1966 and over 500,000 in 1968. Westmoreland's search and destroy strategy, measured by body counts, did not break an enemy who could replace losses and be supplied along the Ho Chi Minh Trail. Johnson was now trapped in a war that he could neither win nor leave.\n\nThe war damaged his domestic programme. Johnson tried to pay for both the war and the Great Society, which critics called guns and butter, and he delayed asking for a tax increase until 1967. Deficits and inflation rose, and Congress passed a 10 per cent surtax in June 1968. The War on Poverty was cut or diverted as military spending grew. The Civil Rights Act of 1964 and the Voting Rights Act of 1965 were passed before the costs became acute and remained his great achievements, but by 1967 the cost of the war and the urban riots had turned opinion against new domestic spending.\n\nThe war also cost Johnson public trust. Journalists spoke of a credibility gap between official statements and reality. Senator Fulbright held televised hearings in 1966, student teach-ins began in 1965, and in April 1967 Martin Luther King spoke against the war at Riverside Church. In October 1967 about 50,000 people marched on the Pentagon. The draft spread the war's cost through families, and American dead passed 30,000 by the end of 1968. Johnson became an embattled president who could hardly appear in public without protest.\n\nThe Tet Offensive of January 1968 was the turning point. Although it was a military defeat for the communists, attacks on more than a hundred towns, including a raid on the US embassy in Saigon, contradicted Westmoreland's claims that the end was in view. Cronkite called the war a stalemate, Clark Clifford advised against sending more troops, and the Wise Men told Johnson the war could not be won at an acceptable cost. Eugene McCarthy's strong showing in New Hampshire and Robert Kennedy's entry into the race exposed his weakness within his own party. On 31 March 1968 Johnson partially halted the bombing, offered talks and declared he would not seek re-election.\n\nVietnam therefore turned Johnson from the architect of the Great Society into a president broken by an unwinnable war. It ended his political career and helped bring Richard Nixon to power in 1968.",
