    "label": "1",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The Tithe War and the Liberal reforms were both important because each showed how the Union dealt with Catholic grievances. The Tithe War proved that organised mass resistance could make an unjust system unworkable, while the Liberal reforms of 1869 and 1870 showed that a British government could remove grievances, though only partly, when it feared the consequences of ignoring Ireland.\n\nTithe was a tax of about a tenth of agricultural produce, paid in money by the occupiers of land to support the clergy of the Church of Ireland, the church of a small minority. Catholics resented paying for a church they did not attend, and the Tithe Composition Act of 1823 made matters worse by turning the tax into fixed money payments and extending it to pasture, which brought graziers and strong farmers into the protest. The campaign of non-payment began at Graiguenamanagh in Kilkenny in 1830 and 1831, where farmers organised by Catholic clergy refused to pay and protected each other's cattle from seizure. When tithe proctors and process servers arrived with police and soldiers, crowds gathered at the sound of horns and chapel bells to block them.\n\nThe conflict became violent. At Newtownbarry in Wexford in June 1831 yeomanry fired on a crowd and killed more than a dozen people. At Carrickshock in Kilkenny in December 1831 a crowd killed a process server and more than ten of his police escort. At Gortroe near Rathcormac in Cork in December 1834 soldiers fired on a crowd trying to stop the seizure of a widow's goods and killed about twelve. These events show how bitter the war was, and they turned the tithe from an economic burden into a symbol of Protestant ascendancy in a Catholic country.\n\nIts importance lay in what it achieved. Tithe became uncollectable in much of the south, arrears mounted and the government had to advance money to clergy left without income. The Whigs, divided over whether surplus Church revenues should be appropriated for non-religious purposes, delayed a settlement, but the Tithe Rentcharge Act of 1838 cut the charge by about a quarter and made landlords responsible for it as part of the rent. This ended open conflict, though tithe was only abolished with disestablishment. The war also showed the power of the parish network and of the clergy, the same strength that had won Emancipation, and it kept alive the demand that Catholics should not be taxed for the Church of Ireland.\n\nThe Liberal reforms took that logic further after 1868. William Gladstone became prime minister in December 1868 and declared that his mission was to pacify Ireland. The Fenian rising of 1867, the hanging of the Manchester Martyrs and the Clerkenwell explosion had shown that Irish discontent could threaten Britain, and this made reform urgent. The Irish Church Act of 1869 disestablished the Church of Ireland from 1 January 1871, ended its status as the state church and took most of its endowments, although serving clergy received compensation. This removed the grievance of the Tithe War at its root and ended the Protestant church's privileged position, an important symbolic victory for Catholics.\n\nThe Irish Land Act of 1870 was more limited. It gave legal force to the Ulster custom of tenant right, allowed outgoing tenants to sell their interest, and gave compensation to tenants evicted for reasons other than non-payment of rent, and for improvements. Its Bright clauses offered loans to help tenants buy their holdings, but few did. Because landlords could still raise rents and evict for non-payment, it did not give security of tenure, and demands for fair rent, fixity of tenure and free sale remained unanswered. Even so, it was the first time the state recognised tenants' claims against landlords, and it opened the way to later land legislation.\n\nOverall, the Tithe War was important because it showed the force of organised resistance and compelled a partial retreat, while the Liberal reforms were important because they accepted the principle that British government should remove Irish grievances. The Church Act was a real achievement, the Land Act a limited one, and together they gave Gladstone's reforms the greater lasting significance, though the pattern of reform following pressure, from tithe resistance to Fenian violence, was the lesson many nationalists took from the period.",
