    "label": "2",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "I agree that the campaign for Catholic Emancipation between 1823 and 1829 had both political and religious dimensions. It was a campaign to win political rights, a seat in Parliament and access to office, but it was also a Catholic movement, organised through the Church and led by the issue of religion, and the two aspects cannot be separated.\n\nThe political dimension began with the Catholic Association, founded by Daniel O'Connell and Richard Lalor Sheil in May 1823. At first it was a small body of lawyers and gentry paying a guinea a year. In 1824 O'Connell introduced associate membership at a penny a month, the Catholic Rent, which brought in tenant farmers, labourers and townspeople in hundreds of thousands. This created the first mass political movement in Irish or British history, funded petitions, newspaper publicity and lawyers to defend Catholics. O'Connell insisted on strictly legal and non-violent methods while warning that the government could not ignore millions of organised Catholics. The Association also took up tithes and the treatment of Catholics in the courts, which made it attractive to the rural poor.\n\nThe movement then turned to elections. After the government passed the Unlawful Societies Act in 1825 to suppress the Association, O'Connell re-formed it as the New Catholic Association. In the general election of 1826 the forty-shilling freeholders in County Waterford, led to the poll by their priests, elected the pro-Emancipation Protestant Henry Villiers-Stuart and defeated Lord George Beresford, whose family had dominated the county. Candidates favouring Emancipation also won in Louth, Monaghan and Westmeath. This showed that tenants could defy their landlords at the polls, and it was a political achievement that broke the landlord's control of the vote.\n\nThe political climax was the Clare election of 1828. When William Vesey-FitzGerald, a popular supporter of Emancipation, had to seek re-election on taking a post in Wellington's government, O'Connell stood against him even though as a Catholic he could not take the oath to sit. Priests marched their parishioners to the poll at Ennis in orderly columns, and O'Connell won on 5 July by 2,057 votes to 982. The government faced the likelihood that Catholics would win most Irish county seats and be unable to sit. Wellington and Peel, who had both opposed Emancipation, feared civil strife in Ireland, where many soldiers were Catholics, and Anglesey, the Lord Lieutenant, warned that Ireland was close to rebellion. Wellington overcame George IV's opposition by threatening resignation, and the Roman Catholic Relief Act received royal assent on 13 April 1829.\n\nThe religious dimension was just as strong. The Rent was collected in every parish under the supervision of the local priest, so the Church's parish network became the Association's organisation. Priests read its addresses from the altar and chaired meetings, and Bishop James Doyle of Kildare and Leighlin, who wrote as JKL, was its most influential clerical supporter. The cause itself was religious in that the disabilities existed because Catholics could not take the oath of supremacy and declaration against transubstantiation. Earlier disputes, such as the veto controversy over whether the Crown should veto appointments of Catholic bishops, showed how closely religion and politics were tied. In 1828 the repeal of the Test and Corporation Acts for Protestant dissenters also weakened the argument that the constitution had to remain exclusively Anglican. The campaign fused Catholic and national identity and gave the Church a central place in Irish politics, which also strengthened sectarian division, as the Brunswick Clubs and Orange anger at Peel showed.\n\nBoth dimensions had limits. The Act allowed Catholics to sit in Parliament and hold almost all offices, but excluded them from the posts of Lord Lieutenant and Lord Chancellor, and it restricted religious orders. Its companion measure raised the county franchise from forty shillings to £10 and cut the electorate from about 216,000 to about 37,000, so the voters who had won the victory lost their vote. The Church of Ireland remained established and Catholics still paid tithes, the grievance behind the Tithe War.\n\nIn conclusion, Emancipation was a political victory achieved through a religious movement. The political pressure of the Rent, Waterford and Clare forced the government to concede, while the clergy and the Catholic cause gave the campaign its strength and meaning, so it is right to see the two dimensions as inseparable.",
