    "model": "Charles Trevelyan and Asenath Nicholson contributed to Irish affairs in very different ways. Trevelyan, the senior Treasury official, shaped the government's response to the Great Famine and helped to make relief narrow, cheap and short-lived. Nicholson, an American teacher, gave direct relief to the poor and left some of the most vivid eyewitness accounts of Ireland before and during the Famine.\n\nTrevelyan was born in Taunton in 1807 and served in the East India Company in Bengal before becoming Assistant Secretary to the Treasury in 1840, the top permanent official in the department. From 1845 he ran the Treasury's Famine relief. Under Peel's Conservative government he controlled the secret purchase of Indian corn from America through Baring Brothers, and he oversaw the depots and the Board of Works schemes. His contribution was administrative, and it was considerable, because every shipment, wage scale and relief scheme passed through his hands.\n\nHis influence grew after the Whigs under Lord John Russell took office in June 1846. Trevelyan was an evangelical Protestant and a firm believer in laissez-faire. He feared that generous relief would make the Irish dependent on the state, so the government refused to control food prices or exports and left supply to private merchants. The Labouchere Act of August 1846 relaunched the public works with the whole cost to be repaid from local taxes, and wages were set below local rates. The works peaked at about 700,000 people in March 1847, but wages could not keep up with food prices, which more than doubled, and weakened people worked through the winter of 1846-1847 to build roads that led nowhere. Trevelyan was the civil servant most responsible for carrying out this policy, although ministers such as Russell and Charles Wood set its principles.\n\nTrevelyan also insisted that Irish property should pay for Irish poverty. When the works were closed in March 1847 the government turned to the Soup Kitchen Act, and by July about three million people a day were fed in what proved the cheapest and most effective relief of the Famine. Yet the kitchens were closed by September 1847, and relief was passed to the Poor Law Extension Act of June 1847. Its Gregory clause denied relief to anyone holding more than a quarter of an acre, so smallholders had to surrender their land to qualify. Landlords, who paid the rates on small holdings, used it to clear estates, and evictions rose sharply. Trevelyan saw the Famine as an act of Providence that would force changes in the Irish land system and blamed landlords and the character of the people. He declared the Famine over in 1848 and defended the policy in The Irish Crisis, and he was knighted that year. Irish nationalists condemned his rigidity, and Cecil Woodham-Smith's The Great Hunger made him the villain of the Famine. Robin Haines later argued that he was a scapegoat who worked hard within his orders.\n\nAsenath Nicholson made a very different contribution. Born in Vermont in 1792, she was a teacher and widow who promoted temperance and worked among the New York Irish poor. She came to Ireland in May 1844 and walked through almost every county, staying in cabins and reading the Bible to the people she met. Her book Ireland's Welcome to the Stranger (1847) described the poverty and the dependence on the potato before the blight. This gave Irish and foreign readers a sympathetic picture of the people whom officials often blamed for their own condition.\n\nShe returned in 1846 to give relief. She set up a soup kitchen in the Liberties of Dublin and then travelled to Ulster, Mayo, Erris and Arranmore, distributing food and clothing paid for by appeals in New York newspapers. Unlike the government and the large committees she acted directly and independently, and the poor welcomed her even though some officials and clergy distrusted her as a Protestant Bible reader. Her Lights and Shades of Ireland (1850) included a Famine section published as Annals of the Famine in Ireland in 1851. It condemned landlords, officials and proselytisers alike, and blamed the system rather than its victims.\n\nOverall, the two contributions were opposites. Trevelyan's was the greater in scale, because he helped to decide how the Famine was managed, and his ideology left a long legacy of bitterness towards British government. Nicholson's was small in material terms, but her relief and her eyewitness testimony were a humane counterweight and a lasting source for historians. Trevelyan shaped the policy, and Nicholson helped to record its human cost.",
