    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Thomas Davis and Cardinal Paul Cullen both did much to shape cultural and religious identity in Ireland between 1815 and 1870, but in contrasting ways. Davis defined the Irish nation through history, literature and language and hoped to unite all creeds, while Cullen built a more disciplined and Roman Catholic Church that tied Catholicism closely to Irish identity.\n\nThomas Davis was born in Mallow in 1814 into a Protestant family and educated at Trinity College Dublin. In 1842 he co-founded The Nation with Charles Gavan Duffy and John Blake Dillon. It became the most widely read paper in Ireland, passed from hand to hand and read aloud in reading rooms, and it published history, ballads and essays. Davis used it to teach that Ireland was a nation with a shared past, not just a Catholic grievance, and in doing so he gave Repeal a younger and more cultural tone than O'Connell's mass agitation.\n\nHis own songs and ballads, including A Nation Once Again and The West's Asleep, put history and patriotism into popular verse that ordinary people could remember and sing. He urged the study and teaching of Irish history and the Irish language, and he edited the Library of Ireland series to put cheap books on Irish subjects before readers. This was cultural nationalism, the belief that a nation is defined by its history, literature and language rather than by its institutions alone. Its effect was to encourage interest in manuscripts and antiquities, though it could not stop the decline of Irish, which national schools taught in English and which parents saw as a barrier to a job or emigration.\n\nDavis's most distinctive idea was that the nation should unite Catholic, Protestant and Dissenter on a shared Irish identity. This set him against the sectarian politics that grew out of the Emancipation and Repeal campaigns and the Orange response to them. His appeal to Protestants was limited, and he died of scarlet fever in September 1845, aged only 30, before he could test it. Even so, historians credit him with laying the cultural foundations of Irish nationalism, and his early death made him a model of principled youth for later generations.\n\nCardinal Paul Cullen's contribution was religious. Born in County Kildare in 1803, he studied and was ordained in Rome and became rector of the Irish College there in 1832. He was made Archbishop of Armagh and Apostolic Delegate in 1850 and presided at the Synod of Thurles that year, the first national synod of the Irish Catholic Church since Kilkenny in 1642. Its decrees condemned the Queen's Colleges as dangerous to faith and morals, forbade clergy to take posts in them, and ordered the founding of a Catholic University, which opened under Newman in 1854. It had few students and no state-recognised degrees, so this part of his policy had limited success.\n\nCullen's wider achievement was the Romanisation of the Irish Church. He championed ultramontane devotion, which stressed the pope's authority over national churches, along with strict clerical discipline, church building, religious orders, parish missions and Catholic schools. These changes belong to what the historian Emmet Larkin called the devotional revolution, which saw regular Mass attendance rise from about a third of Catholics before the Famine to the large majority by the 1870s. Archbishop of Dublin from 1852 and the first Irish cardinal in 1866, he became the dominant Catholic voice for a quarter of a century. He distrusted Young Ireland and the Fenians, opposed priests in politics, and supported Gladstone's disestablishment of the Church of Ireland. Nationalists and bishops such as MacHale resented his authority, and Protestants saw papal domination.\n\nThe two men therefore shaped identity in complementary ways. Davis offered an inclusive vision of a nation of shared culture, but it was idealistic and reached few Protestants. Cullen's influence was more lasting and more practical, because his reforms gave the Catholic majority a strong religious identity organised through the parish, the school and Rome. Cullen's Church shaped the everyday identity of most Irish people, while Davis supplied the ideas that later nationalism drew on.",
