    "label": "2",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The industrialisation of Belfast and the co-operative movement were both of major importance to Irish social and economic life between 1870 and 1914, though in different ways. Belfast became an industrial city of international standing that was tied firmly to Britain and the empire, while the co-operative movement improved the lives of ordinary farmers through self-help and trained a generation of local leaders. Each brought prosperity to a part of the country and left problems unsolved.\n\nThe industrialisation of Belfast was dramatic. From a town of about 20,000 around 1800, it grew to about 255,000 at the 1891 census, when it passed Dublin, and to 387,000 in 1911. Linen, spun and woven in mills, was the first great industry, so that Belfast was nicknamed Linenopolis, and tens of thousands of workers, mostly women and girls, were employed in it. The Belfast Ropeworks, founded in 1873, was among the largest in the world, and tobacco, engineering and distilling added further employment. The American Civil War had boosted linen by cutting cotton supplies, and power-loom mills replaced handloom weaving.\n\nShipbuilding made Belfast famous. Edward Harland bought a yard on Queen's Island in 1858 and went into partnership with Gustav Wolff in 1861. William Pirrie became a partner in 1874 and chairman after Harland's death in 1895. Harland and Wolff built the Olympic-class liners for the White Star Line, including Olympic and Titanic, which was launched on 31 May 1911 and sank on her maiden voyage on 15 April 1912. Workman Clark, founded in 1880, was a second major yard, and by 1912 Harland and Wolff alone employed about 15,000 men. The Arrol Gantry of 1908-09 allowed Olympic and Titanic to be built side by side. This industry made Belfast wealthy and gave Ulster a distinct economic interest.\n\nThe importance of Belfast's industry, however, was also political and social. Its prosperity depended on British and imperial markets and raw materials, so many Protestant workers and employers feared that Home Rule would bring tariffs and a Catholic-dominated parliament, and this tied industrial interests to unionism. Skilled shipyard jobs were mainly held by Protestants, while Catholics were largely labourers, and the city was divided between districts such as the Shankill and the Falls. Sectarian riots occurred in 1864 and 1886, and in July 1912 Protestant workers drove Catholic workers out of the shipyards. Conditions in the mills, with dust, long hours and low pay, were hard.\n\nThe co-operative movement was of a different kind. Horace Plunkett, an Anglo-Irish landowner and Unionist MP who had ranched in Wyoming, saw that Irish farmers suffered from poor marketing, dependence on shopkeepers and moneylenders, and competition from better-made Danish butter. He adopted the Danish model of farmer-owned co-operative creameries, with the first usually dated to 1889 at Dromcollogher in Limerick, and in 1894 he founded the Irish Agricultural Organisation Society to organise and inspect societies. Its motto was better farming, better business, better living. Creameries bought milk by fat content and used mechanical separators, which raised the quality of Irish butter and farm incomes, while credit societies modelled on German Raiffeisen banks reduced reliance on gombeen men. By the First World War there were close to a thousand societies with about 100,000 members, strongest in Munster and weakest in Ulster.\n\nPlunkett also brought nationalists and unionists together in the Recess Committee of 1895, which led to the Department of Agriculture and Technical Instruction in 1899, with Plunkett as its first vice-president. The movement met suspicion, as nationalists distrusted a unionist and many Ulster farmers were slow to join, and Plunkett's criticism of clerical influence in Ireland in the New Century in 1904 added to his problems. The co-operatives also did little for the poorest farmers in the west, who relied more on the Congested Districts Board.\n\nOverall, Belfast's industrialisation was the more dramatic change, making an economic powerhouse and shaping unionist opposition to Home Rule, while the co-operative movement was the more widely beneficial for ordinary farmers and left a lasting lesson in self-help. Both were important in modernising Ireland, yet neither could overcome the political and religious divisions of the period.",
