    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The GAA and the Gaelic League were both founded by cultural nationalists who believed that Ireland was being anglicised. Between them they gave Irish people games, a language and a sense of self-respect, and by the end of the period both had also fed into the political movement for independence, the GAA directly through the IRB and the League increasingly after 1910.\n\nThe GAA was founded at Hayes' Hotel in Thurles on 1 November 1884 by Michael Cusack and Maurice Davin, with Davin as president. Its cultural contribution was to rescue and organise native sport. Hurling had declined since the Famine and was played under varying local rules, while athletics was run under English amateur rules that excluded artisans and labourers. Davin drew up the first rules for hurling and Gaelic football in 1885, replacing local variations with a national code, and the GAA opened competition to all classes. It organised clubs on the parish, with county committees and a Central Council above them, a structure that put a club within reach of nearly every rural community. The first All-Ireland championships began in 1887, and the first hurling final in 1888, when Thurles beat Meelick, drew crowds from far beyond a single locality.\n\nThe GAA's political importance was just as great. Archbishop Croke of Cashel became patron in December 1884, and his letter attacking the imitation of English games and manners gave the association respectability and brought many priests into club life. Parnell and Michael Davitt also agreed to be patrons, which tied the GAA to the land and Home Rule movements. The foreign games ban kept members from cricket, rugby and soccer, and the exclusion of RIC and British army members made the association a statement against British rule. Members of the IRB joined in large numbers and fought for control of the Central Council, so that Dublin Castle described the GAA as a front for Fenianism. Cusack was removed as secretary in 1886 and Davin stepped down in 1889.\n\nThe association was almost destroyed by its own entanglement in politics. The American tour of 1888 lost money and left the championships unfinished, bishops grew uneasy about the IRB, and the Parnell split of 1890-91 divided members. By 1891 clubs had collapsed in many areas. Yet it survived, because the parish clubs could continue when the leaders quarrelled, and the appeal of hurling and football did not depend on politics. It recovered in the 1890s, and in 1908 it bought grounds at Jones's Road in Dublin, later Croke Park. The GAA was thus a mass, rural, male organisation, a school for national feeling that made the nationalist cause part of everyday life in every parish.\n\nThe Gaelic League was founded in Dublin on 31 July 1893 by Douglas Hyde, Eoin MacNeill and others, following Hyde's lecture The Necessity for De-Anglicising Ireland of November 1892. Hyde argued that Ireland was failing to be either English or Irish and pointed to the collapse of Irish as a spoken language since the Famine. The League's cultural contribution was to build a mass movement for the language. It ran Irish classes through its branches, sent travelling teachers, the timiri, into rural districts, and organised feiseanna and, from 1897, the annual Oireachtas. Its weekly paper An Claidheamh Soluis began in 1899 and Patrick Pearse edited it from 1903 to 1909. Several hundred branches existed by 1904, and, unlike the GAA, it attracted women, teachers and the urban middle class.\n\nThe League also won practical successes. Irish was allowed in primary schools under the Revised Programme of 1900, and the League won its campaign to make Irish compulsory for matriculation to the National University, decided in 1910 and applied from 1913. St Patrick's Day processions from 1903, Irish names and Irish signs spread a visible pride in Irish identity. Even so, the language did not revive as an everyday tongue, and the League's insistence on being non-political and non-sectarian was tested by D.P. Moran's narrower Irish Ireland, which defined the nation as Gaelic and Catholic.\n\nPolitically the League's contribution was real if unintended. Hyde meant the League to be non-political and welcomed unionists and Protestants, but from about 1910 growing numbers of IRB members joined and pushed it towards politics. In 1915 the League declared for Irish independence and Hyde resigned as president. Many of the men who led the Rising of 1916, Pearse and MacNeill among them, had been shaped by the League, and historians still debate whether the revival caused the revolution or merely supplied a language for politics with other causes.\n\nBoth bodies, then, contributed a great deal to the period. The GAA offered a sporting and organisational network and was political from the start, while the League offered language and education and became political late. Between them they gave nationalism a cultural base that went beyond parliamentary Home Rule, though they also stressed a Gaelic and Catholic identity that left unionists and many Protestants outside it.",
