    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Attempts to promote cultural identity were made on both sides of the border, but they took very different forms. In the South the state tried to build a Gaelic and Catholic national identity through the Irish language, the influence of the Church over schools and morality, and national cultural institutions. In the North the Unionist government protected a Protestant and British identity, while nationalists used the Catholic schools, the GAA and Irish classes to keep their own identity alive.\n\nIn the Free State the revival of Irish was the central aim. Irish was made compulsory in national schools in 1922 and many infant classes were taught entirely through it. A pass in Irish became necessary for State examinations at second level and for entry to the civil service and the Garda, so that the language was tied to jobs, and Ernest Blythe pressed civil servants to learn it. The Gaeltacht Commission of 1925 defined the Irish-speaking districts, An Gúm began publishing books in Irish in 1926, the Taibhdhearc theatre opened in Galway in 1928, and preparatory colleges trained teachers who could teach through Irish. The 1937 constitution made Irish the first official language. The results were mixed. Census returns showed those able to speak Irish rising from about 18 per cent in 1926 to about a quarter in 1936, but this came mostly from schooling, the real Gaeltacht continued to shrink through emigration and poverty, and many parents and teachers resented the compulsion. Irish became a symbol of identity rather than an everyday language.\n\nReligion and education were closely linked in the South. National schools were managed by local clergy while the state paid salaries and set the curriculum, and most secondary schools were fee-paying schools run by religious orders. The School Attendance Act of 1926 made school compulsory from 6 to 14, and the Vocational Education Act of 1930 provided technical education, but the Church kept control of the ethos of teaching. Legislation reflected Catholic teaching. A film censor was appointed in 1923, the Censorship of Publications Act of 1929 allowed books to be banned, and Samuel Beckett was among those banned. The Criminal Law Amendment Act of 1935 banned contraceptives, and the 1937 constitution banned divorce in Article 41 and recognised the special position of the Catholic Church in Article 44. Critics saw conformity enforced, and the Protestant population fell in the 1920s and 1930s.\n\nThe state also promoted identity through wider culture. The Tailteann Games were held in 1924, 1928 and 1932, the Abbey Theatre received a state subsidy from 1925, and 2RN began broadcasting in January 1926. The Irish Folklore Commission was set up in 1935 and the Schools' Collection of 1937 to 1938 had children gather local folklore. The GAA, with Rule 27 banning members from playing foreign games, strengthened a cultural boundary, and in 1938 it removed Douglas Hyde as patron after he attended a soccer international. All of this tied Irishness to a Gaelic and Catholic ideal that left many Protestants and urban people on the margins.\n\nIn Northern Ireland education was the main battleground. The Lynn Committee reported in 1923, and the Education Act of that year, known as the Londonderry Act, created county education committees and aimed at schools free of church control. Protestant churches objected to the exclusion of religious instruction, and the Act was amended in 1925 to allow Bible instruction, which gave the Orange Order and Protestant clergy a voice. The Catholic Church refused to transfer its schools to the state, so they stayed outside the system with only partial public funding, and the 1930 amendment, which favoured Protestant representation on committees of transferred schools, added to Catholic grievances. The result was a divided system that reinforced separate identities.\n\nCultural identity in the North was equally divided. Unionist culture was expressed in Orange parades on 12 July, loyalist bands and the Union Jack, and was protected by the state, with royal visits and ceremonies at Stormont. Nationalists supported the GAA, Irish classes and Catholic schools, but the Irish language received little state support. Under the Special Powers Act the authorities could ban processions and republican emblems, and nationalist parades were more likely to be banned. Craig's statement in 1934 that Northern Ireland was a Protestant state was the mirror image of the Catholic emphasis in the 1937 constitution.\n\nIn conclusion, both governments used schools and symbols to promote a single dominant identity, Gaelic and Catholic in the South and Protestant and British in the North. The southern state was the more active in promoting language and culture, but its language policy brought uneven results, while in the North the education system left the two communities more separate.",
