    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The Holy Ghost mission to Nigeria between 1945 and 1966 was one of the most successful chapters in Ireland's missionary history. Irish Spiritans, working mainly among the Igbo of south-eastern Nigeria, built a network of schools, hospitals and parishes, helped create a Catholic church under Nigerian leadership, and trained many of the people who would run an independent Nigeria. Its achievements were real and lasting, though they were qualified by the mission's links with colonial rule and by the damage of the Biafran war.\n\nThe mission's greatest achievement was in education. The Holy Ghost Fathers had begun work at Onitsha in 1885, and Bishop Joseph Shanahan, a Tipperary man who arrived in 1902, had made the school the main instrument of conversion, building village schools staffed by catechists. After 1945 this work expanded rapidly, and mission schools became the main route to education in the region, with the Catholic mission running a large share of its primary and secondary schools. Teacher training colleges prepared local teachers, which created a growing educated class. The colonial government supported the work through grants-in-aid, and when the regional government introduced free primary education in the 1950s the demand for schooling grew further. The mission did not simply teach children to read; it gave a generation of Igbo access to the qualifications on which careers in the new Nigeria depended.\n\nThe second achievement was in health care. Nigeria's rural areas had few government medical services, and the mission filled the gap. Hospitals and dispensaries run by the Holy Rosary Sisters, founded by Shanahan in 1924, and by the Medical Missionaries of Mary, founded by Mother Mary Martin in 1937, brought nursing and medical treatment to villages that would otherwise have had none. These sisters also trained Nigerian nurses and midwives, so that the benefit did not end when the missionaries left. The work also gave Irish women a leadership role in medicine that they found hard to obtain at home, and it showed that the mission was concerned with the body as well as the soul.\n\nThe mission also achieved a great deal in building the church itself. New parishes, churches and training colleges were opened across the region after 1945, and the Catholic population grew strongly. In 1950 the Catholic hierarchy was established in Nigeria, the Archdiocese of Onitsha was created with the Irish Spiritan Charles Heerey as its archbishop, and Joseph Whelan became the first bishop of Owerri. Irish recruits from seminaries and novitiates swelled the numbers of priests, brothers and sisters throughout the 1950s. This growth reflected the Irish enthusiasm for what was called Ireland's spiritual empire, and it gave the missionaries a sense of national achievement as well as religious purpose.\n\nPerhaps the most important sign of success was the growth of a Nigerian church. Local vocations to the priesthood and religious life increased. Michael Tansi, an Igbo, had been ordained in 1937 and was beatified in 1998, and in 1965 Francis Arinze became auxiliary bishop of Onitsha at the age of 32. After independence on 1 October 1960 the mission's leadership passed increasingly into Nigerian hands, a process known as localisation. Many of the educated Nigerians who served in the new state had passed through mission schools, which gave the mission a lasting influence on the country's elite. It had outlasted the colonial government that had once supported it.\n\nThe mission's final achievement came in the crisis of the Biafran war. When the eastern region declared itself the Republic of Biafra on 30 May 1967, many mission stations, schools and hospitals were damaged or abandoned, but the Holy Ghost Father Anthony Byrne helped to organise the relief airlift to Biafra. The famine moved the Irish public deeply, and the relief agency Concern was founded in 1968 in response. The missionaries had therefore also created a bond of concern between Irish people and Africa that survived the end of the mission era.\n\nThere were limits to this success. Critics argued that mission education promoted Western and Christian values at the expense of local culture and languages, and that the missionaries were paternalistic, keeping leadership in Irish hands long after Nigerians were qualified for it. Rivalry with Methodist and Anglican missions over schools and converts also divided communities. Nevertheless, the mission's schools, hospitals and parishes outlasted colonial rule and produced educated Nigerian leaders and a Nigerian-led church. On balance its achievements in education, health and church-building far outweighed its failings, and it remains the most substantial achievement of the Irish missionary movement in Africa.",
