    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Ian Paisley was one of the most influential figures in Northern Ireland from the 1960s onward. As a preacher, protest leader and founder of a church and a political party, he led the hardline unionist opposition to reform and compromise, and he shaped the course of unionist politics for more than four decades. His contribution was a divisive one: he mobilised a large section of the Protestant community, but he made agreement between unionists and nationalists much harder to reach.\n\nPaisley first became prominent as a religious leader. Born in Armagh in 1926, he founded the Free Presbyterian Church in 1951, combining strict Protestant doctrine with hostility to the Catholic Church and to any concession to nationalism. He opposed ecumenism, which he portrayed as a betrayal of the Reformation, and in 1966 he protested when the Presbyterian General Assembly considered links with the World Council of Churches. This was a lasting contribution, because it fused religion and politics and gave hardline unionism a moral and biblical language that appealed to many ordinary Protestants.\n\nIn the 1960s he became the leading critic of Terence O'Neill's policy of bridge-building. He condemned O'Neill's meeting with the Taoiseach, Seán Lemass, in January 1965, arguing that it undermined the Union and Protestant identity. From 1966 he helped lead the 'O'Neill must go' campaign, setting up the Ulster Constitution Defence Committee and the Ulster Protestant Volunteers, and he was jailed for three months in 1966 after refusing to be bound over following the Presbyterian protest. His supporters blocked a civil rights march in Armagh on 30 November 1968, and in the February 1969 election he stood against O'Neill in Bannside, which O'Neill held by only 1,414 votes. O'Neill resigned on 28 April 1969, and Paisley's rallies had helped to show that reform from above could not carry the unionist community.\n\nPaisley then moved into formal politics, which made his contribution more lasting. He won the Bannside seat at Stormont in April 1970 and the North Antrim seat at Westminster in June 1970. In 1971 he founded the Democratic Unionist Party, which he led until 2008. The DUP gave hardline unionists an organised alternative to the Ulster Unionist Party, and it opposed power sharing with nationalists and any role for Dublin in Northern Ireland's affairs. Historians argue that his campaign against O'Neill helped shift unionist politics towards the hardline position of the 1970s.\n\nHis opposition to the Sunningdale Agreement was the most decisive use of that influence. Paisley's supporters joined the United Ulster Unionist Council, formed in January 1974 by unionists opposed to power sharing, and the UUUC won eleven of the twelve Northern Ireland seats in the Westminster election of 28 February 1974. Paisley supported the Ulster Workers' Council strike that began on 15 May 1974, which cut electricity supplies and paralysed the province. The power-sharing executive led by Brian Faulkner collapsed, and the first attempt to share power between the communities ended in failure. Without Paisley's rejection of the agreement, the experiment might have lasted longer.\n\nHe continued the same role in the 1980s. After Margaret Thatcher and Garret FitzGerald signed the Anglo-Irish Agreement at Hillsborough on 15 November 1985, giving the Irish government a consultative role, Paisley worked with James Molyneaux on the 'Ulster says No' campaign. This included the large rally at Belfast City Hall on 23 November 1985. Fifteen unionist MPs resigned their seats in January 1986, but the Agreement remained in force, which showed the limits of protest. He also built a political base beyond Northern Ireland when he topped the poll in the 1979 European Parliament election, and in 1988 he was removed from the chamber in Strasbourg after shouting at Pope John Paul II.\n\nOverall, Paisley's contribution was powerful but largely negative. He gave a voice to Protestant fears, built the DUP, and brought down O'Neill's reforms and the Sunningdale executive, but he did so by refusing compromise. His supporters admired him as a defender of the Union, while his opponents condemned his rhetoric. Few figures did more to determine how unionism responded to the Troubles.",
