    "model": "Metternich's leadership of international politics between 1815 and 1848 was effective in achieving his central aim, which was to preserve the 1815 settlement and the multinational Habsburg Empire against liberalism and nationalism. He kept the great powers at peace and crushed risings for over thirty years, but his control was never complete, the Congress System broke up in the 1820s, and his whole system collapsed in March 1848.\n\nMetternich's greatest success was to shape the settlement and use it to maintain the peace. At the Congress of Vienna he worked with Castlereagh, Alexander I, Hardenberg and Talleyrand to contain France through a ring of buffer states and to restore legitimate rulers. Austria gained Lombardy-Venetia and Habsburg relatives ruled Tuscany, Modena and Parma, which made Austria the dominant power in Italy. The great powers fought no general war between 1815 and the Crimean War of 1853-56. This record counts strongly in his favour, though it owed something to exhaustion after the Napoleonic wars and to British naval power as well as to his diplomacy.\n\nHe was equally effective in turning the Concert of Europe into the Congress System. The Quadruple Alliance of 1815 and the Holy Alliance, which Metternich called empty but used to bind the eastern monarchies, gave him a framework for cooperation. At Troppau in 1820 Austria, Prussia and Russia claimed a right to intervene against revolutions elsewhere, and at Laibach in 1821 Austrian troops were authorised to crush the revolution in Naples, with the Piedmontese rising put down the same year. Austrian forces again suppressed risings in Modena, Parma and the Papal Legations in 1831. In Italy, therefore, his policy was highly effective.\n\nWithin the German Confederation Metternich was equally successful. After Karl Sand murdered the writer Kotzebue in March 1819, he drew up the Carlsbad Decrees, adopted by the Diet in September, which banned the Burschenschaften, supervised universities and imposed press censorship. After the Hambach Festival of 1832 the Six Articles and further measures banned political associations and public meetings. Austria kept its presidency of the Diet and the smaller states usually followed its lead. At home, police, informers and censorship kept the Habsburg Empire quiet. Liberal and national movements were silenced, but only silenced, since their causes were left untouched.\n\nHis leadership had serious limits, however. The Congress System collapsed when Britain refused to sign the Troppau Protocol and withdrew after Verona in 1822, leaving only the eastern autocracies. The Greek War of Independence broke the principle of non-intervention: Britain, France and Russia destroyed the Turkish-Egyptian fleet at Navarino in 1827, and Greece became independent, a result Metternich opposed. In 1830 Charles X fell in France and Belgium broke away from the Netherlands, becoming independent under Leopold in 1831. These events overturned parts of the Vienna settlement and showed that Metternich could not control events beyond Austria's reach.\n\nThe Zollverein, which came into force in 1834, was a further failure, since Prussia built a German economic union without Austria and accustomed the smaller states to look to Berlin rather than Vienna. This laid a foundation for Prussian leadership in Germany. Metternich saw the danger but could not match it, because Austria's protected industries did not fit a low-tariff union.\n\nIn the end, Metternich was highly effective in the short and medium term, preserving peace, putting down revolt in Italy and Germany and keeping the Habsburg Empire intact. But he repressed the causes of discontent rather than removing them, and the system fell within days of the Vienna demonstrations of 13 March 1848, when he was forced to resign and flee to England. His leadership was effective for a generation but not lasting.",
