    "label": "2",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Feargus O'Connor and Karl Marx both wanted to transform the position of working people in industrial society, but their visions were very different. O'Connor sought a democratic Britain in which working men had the vote and, through the land, independence, while Marx envisaged a classless society reached through the overthrow of capitalism by the working class.\n\nFeargus O'Connor, born in Co. Cork in 1796, was the leading Chartist of the 1840s. His vision of society was first political: he wanted the People's Charter of 1838, demanding universal male suffrage, secret ballots, annual parliaments, equal electoral districts, no property qualification for MPs and payment of MPs. Workers had been disappointed that the Reform Act of 1832 gave them no vote, and the hardship of the New Poor Law and the depression of 1837 added to their anger. O'Connor believed a Parliament elected by all men would act for the people. In 1837 he founded the Northern Star in Leeds, which became the most widely read radical newspaper in Britain and spread this message.\n\nO'Connor's vision was also economic and agrarian. Through the National Land Company, founded in 1845, he planned to buy estates and let subscribers draw lots for smallholdings, which would give them independence from factory employers and, by making them landholders, a vote in the counties. His ideal was a society of small, self-supporting landholders rather than factory wage-labourers. The scheme collapsed through financial mismanagement and was wound up in 1851. O'Connor pursued his vision through mass petitions in 1839, 1842 and 1848, all rejected by Parliament, and the Kennington Common demonstration of 10 April 1848, which dispersed peacefully. He chose constitutional agitation and pressure, not revolution, though he was a physical-force leader to some and quarrelled with the moral-force Chartists of William Lovett. His collapse into mental illness in 1852 ended his influence, but all the Charter's points except annual parliaments were eventually achieved.\n\nKarl Marx, born in Trier in 1818, had a far more radical vision. In the Communist Manifesto, which he wrote with Friedrich Engels and published in London in February 1848, he declared that all history was a history of class struggles and called on working men of all countries to unite. His theory of historical materialism held that economic relations and class struggle drive history, so that capitalism, in which owners profit from the labour of workers, would be overthrown by the working class. He saw the existing state as an instrument of the ruling class, so reform of the franchise alone was not enough. The aim was a society without classes in which the means of production would be owned in common.\n\nMarx developed this vision in his later writings and his political work. The first volume of Das Kapital appeared in 1867 and analysed how capitalists profit from the surplus value produced by labour. He took part in the revolutions of 1848 as editor of the Neue Rheinische Zeitung in Cologne, was expelled from Prussia in 1849 and settled in London, where he lived until his death in 1883. In 1864 he helped to found the International Workingmen's Association, wrote its inaugural address and dominated its General Council, giving his ideas an international organisation. His expectation that revolution would come first in advanced capitalist countries was not fulfilled, but his analysis of capitalism influenced later politics and economics.\n\nThe contrast between the two is clear. O'Connor sought to bring workers into the existing system through the vote and land, working within British constitutional politics, while Marx wanted to replace the capitalist system itself through class conflict. O'Connor's vision was rooted in a rural past and failed in his lifetime, whereas Marx's became far more influential in the long run, though it was not realised as he had predicted.",
