    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Baron Haussmann and Victor Hugo contributed to French affairs in different ways. Haussmann physically remade Paris under Napoleon III, tackling the problems of an overcrowded and unhealthy capital. Hugo shaped France through his writing and his politics, as a literary giant and a republican who became the voice of resistance to the Second Empire.\n\nHaussmann was appointed Prefect of the Seine in June 1853 and held the post for about seventeen years. Napoleon III wanted to improve health, create employment through public works and make the city easier to control, and the expropriation law of 1841, extended in 1852, let Haussmann buy whole blocks compulsorily. Paris was a maze of narrow medieval streets around the Île de la Cité and Les Halles, with poor drainage and about a million people. His grands travaux cut broad, tree-lined boulevards through the old city: the Rue de Rivoli was extended, the Boulevard de Sébastopol linked the Seine with the railway stations, the Boulevards Saint-Germain and Saint-Michel crossed the Latin Quarter, and the Étoile became the centre of twelve radiating avenues. Building regulations fixed the height of facades, giving Paris its uniform look. The boulevards eased traffic and, as the barricades of 1830 and 1848 had shown, allowed troops and artillery to move quickly, so they served the regime's security as well as the city's convenience.\n\nHis work on public health was arguably more important. The engineer Eugène Belgrand built aqueducts and reservoirs bringing clean spring water from the Dhuis and Vanne valleys, and an underground sewer network that carried waste away from the centre. Jean-Charles Alphand laid out parks, including the Bois de Boulogne, the Bois de Vincennes, the Buttes-Chaumont and the Parc Monceau, and Baltard's iron-and-glass pavilions at Les Halles gave the city a modern market. In 1860 the suburbs were annexed and the arrondissements grew from twelve to twenty, so that he could plan the whole urban area. The cost was high, however. Thousands of working-class families were pushed out to the east and north, rents rose in the new centre, and the division between the wealthy west and the poorer east deepened, an effect Zola satirised in La Curée. Heavy borrowing led Jules Ferry to attack the hidden accounts in 1868, and Haussmann was dismissed in January 1870 when Émile Ollivier's liberal ministry would not keep so unpopular a prefect.\n\nVictor Hugo's contribution was of a different kind. He was the leading figure of French Romanticism: his preface to Cromwell in 1827 was a manifesto for Romantic drama, and Hernani in 1830 provoked a riot between Romantic and classical supporters and marked the triumph of the new style in the theatre. Notre-Dame de Paris in 1831 revived interest in the Gothic heritage of the city and helped inspire the restoration of medieval buildings, and he was elected to the Académie française in 1841. Les Misérables in 1862, written largely in exile on Guernsey, portrayed the poor of France through the story of Jean Valjean and became one of the most widely read novels of the century.\n\nHugo was also a politician. A monarchist in the 1820s, he had become a republican by 1848, was elected to the Constituent and Legislative Assemblies, and defended freedom of the press and education while opposing restrictions on the franchise. He first backed Louis-Napoléon but broke with him, and by 1851 he was a leader of the democratic opposition. After the coup of December 1851 he left France, going first to Brussels, then to Jersey and Guernsey. His pamphlet Napoléon le Petit in 1852 and his satirical poems Les Châtiments in 1853 attacked Napoleon III and made Hugo a symbol of republican resistance. He refused the amnesty of 1859, and he returned to Paris on 5 September 1870, the day after the Third Republic was proclaimed.\n\nIn conclusion, the two men served opposite sides of the Second Empire. Haussmann made the regime's capital healthier, safer and grander, at a heavy social and financial price, while Hugo defined the moral case against that regime and gave France a lasting literary and democratic voice. Haussmann's contribution was the more physical and immediate, Hugo's the more enduring in ideas, and the lasting fame of both shows how much each mattered to French affairs.",
