    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Between 1815 and 1871 cultural nationalism developed strongly in music, art and literature. Romantic composers, painters and writers gave national movements their myths, symbols and languages, and they were most influential where a nation had no state of its own, as in Italy, Germany, Poland and the Czech lands.\n\nThe intellectual background was Romanticism, which rejected the rational order of the Enlightenment in favour of emotion, imagination and the national past. The German thinker Herder had developed the idea of a Volksgeist, a national spirit expressed in a people's language, folklore and history, and nationalists took it up. Collecting folklore and reviving medieval legends were central to the movement, because they claimed to reveal the true character of a people. Artists and writers were not simply reflecting nationalism but helping to create it.\n\nMusic was a powerful vehicle. Frédéric Chopin's mazurkas and polonaises drew on Polish dance rhythms, and after 1831 his music became a symbol of Polish national feeling in exile. Franz Liszt, born in Hungary, wrote the Hungarian Rhapsodies in the 1840s and 1850s, drawing on Hungarian and Romani music and presenting Hungary's identity to European audiences. In Italy Verdi's opera Nabucco in 1842 included the chorus Va, pensiero, sung by Hebrew exiles, which Italian audiences came to hear as a call for national liberation, and around 1859 the cry Viva VERDI was popularly said to be a coded slogan for Vittorio Emanuele Re d'Italia. Richard Wagner used German myths and Norse sagas in his operas, and his Ring cycle, first performed in full at Bayreuth in 1876, drew on the Nibelungen legend. Bedřich Smetana's The Bartered Bride in 1866 and Má vlast in 1874 to 1879 drew on Czech folk music and landscape to express Czech national feeling.\n\nArt played a similar role. Eugène Delacroix's Liberty Leading the People in 1830 showed a personification of liberty leading the Parisian crowd over the barricades during the July Revolution, giving the revolution an enduring image. Caspar David Friedrich's landscapes, such as Wanderer above the Sea of Fog of about 1818, expressed Romantic feeling for nature and the German spirit. Romantic historical painting showed scenes from national history, which gave citizens a shared image of their past and helped turn a collection of regions into a community with common memories.\n\nLiterature had the deepest effect, because it shaped languages. The Brothers Grimm published their Children's and Household Tales in 1812 and 1815, presenting German folklore as the expression of the German people, and began a great German dictionary in 1838. Alessandro Manzoni's novel I promessi sposi in 1827, revised in 1840 to 1842 in the educated Tuscan of Florence, became a model for standard Italian in a country where most people spoke local dialects. Adam Mickiewicz's Pan Tadeusz in 1834 was a national epic that celebrated the Polish past when Poland had no independent state. Pushkin wrote in a Russian literary language that became the model for later writers, Josef Jungmann's Czech dictionary of 1835 to 1839 helped revive Czech, and Elias Lönnrot's Kalevala in 1835 did the same for Finland. Standardising languages through grammars, dictionaries and schools helped create a sense of a single national community.\n\nVictor Hugo shows how literature and politics combined. Hernani in 1830 marked the victory of Romanticism in French theatre, Notre-Dame de Paris in 1831 revived interest in the Gothic past, and Les Misérables in 1862, written in exile on Guernsey, portrayed the poor of France and became one of the most widely read novels of the century. Hugo was admired as a national poet and a leader of Romanticism, although classical critics attacked his work for breaking the rules of form and taste.\n\nOverall, cultural nationalism achieved a great deal. It gave national movements symbols, languages and histories, and it helped create a feeling of belonging before states existed. It had limits, however: it often idealised the past and excluded minorities, and national myths could be used to justify conflict. Even so, music, art and literature were among the most important forces preparing the ground for national states in this period.",
