    "model": "Germany had the greater social and economic problems during the inter-war period. Britain suffered real hardship, with long-term unemployment in its older industrial regions, but Germany endured defeat, hyperinflation and dependence on foreign loans, and then a collapse in the Depression that was deeper and that destroyed its democracy.\n\nGermany began the period with burdens that Britain never faced. The Treaty of Versailles of June 1919 imposed territorial losses, military limits and reparations, and the London Schedule of 1921 set the sum at 132 billion gold marks, a figure that many Germans regarded as impossible and humiliating. Britain, a victor, had its own post-war slump, with prices collapsing in 1920 and 1921, unemployment rising above two million by mid-1921 and the Geddes Axe of 1922 cutting public spending. These problems were serious, but they were those of an economy readjusting after the war, not of a state facing bankruptcy.\n\nThe hyperinflation of 1923 has no parallel in Britain. When Germany fell behind on reparations deliveries, France and Belgium occupied the Ruhr in January 1923, and the government printed money to fund passive resistance. The mark collapsed, wiping out the savings of the middle class and of people on fixed incomes, while debtors and speculators such as Hugo Stinnes gained. The Rentenmark of November 1923, introduced under Stresemann and supported by Schacht, ended the crisis, and the Dawes Plan of 1924 and the Young Plan of 1929 eased reparations, but the middle classes were never compensated in full and lost faith in the republic. Britain's weakness in the same years was different, as the return to the gold standard in 1925 made exports dearer and led to the coal dispute and the General Strike of May 1926, but it did not destroy the savings of a whole class.\n\nThe Depression shows the difference most clearly. In Britain, unemployment reached about three million in the early 1930s, concentrated in regions such as South Wales, Tyneside and Clydeside, where it could exceed 30 per cent, and higher still in towns like Jarrow. The unemployed faced the means test of 1931, and hunger marches such as that of 1932 and books like Orwell's The Road to Wigan Pier drew attention to their poverty. Yet Britain left the gold standard in September 1931, adopted tariffs in 1932 and cheap money, and new industries in the south-east and Midlands, such as motor vehicles and electrical goods, brought rising living standards to those in work. In Germany unemployment rose from about 1.9 million in 1929 to about 6 million by early 1932, as American loans were called in and exports collapsed. The 1927 insurance scheme, designed for about 800,000 claimants, was overwhelmed, and Chancellor Brüning's deflationary decrees cut wages, pensions and benefits and deepened the slump.\n\nThe consequences were also far graver in Germany. Britain's crisis was contained within its political system, and the National Government won a landslide in October 1931 while extremist parties stayed marginal. In Germany the unemployed, small farmers and the ruined middle class turned to the Nazis, who promised jobs and national revival, and the party became the largest in the Reichstag in 1932 before Hitler was appointed Chancellor in January 1933. Economic misery brought the collapse of democracy in Germany, whereas in Britain it brought protest and hardship but not a change of regime.\n\nBritain therefore had two nations, a prosperous south and a depressed north, and its suffering was real. But Germany faced reparations, hyperinflation and the loss of savings, and then unemployment on a far larger scale, and the political consequences were far more destructive. Germany clearly had the greater social and economic problems.",
