    "label": "2",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Stalin's rule in Russia, from his rise to power in the later 1920s until his death in 1953, was marked by one-man dictatorship, a forced economic revolution, terror on a massive scale and total control over the lives and thoughts of Soviet citizens. It was a totalitarian regime in which the state tried to leave no area of life outside its reach.\n\nStalin first made himself unchallenged ruler by controlling the party machine. As General Secretary from 1922 he controlled appointments and packed the party with loyal supporters. After Lenin's death in 1924 he allied with Zinoviev and Kamenev against Trotsky, then with Bukharin against the United Opposition, and finally turned on Bukharin and the Right in 1928-29 when he adopted rapid industrialisation. Trotsky was exiled from the Soviet Union in 1929. By then Stalin had no rival, which shows that his rule rested on manoeuvre and control of the party rather than open debate.\n\nA second characteristic was the drive for rapid industrialisation through state planning. Gosplan drew up the First Five-Year Plan, launched in 1928, with heavy industry given priority over consumer goods, and later plans continued the emphasis. New cities such as Magnitogorsk rose in the Urals, and the Stakhanovite movement, named after the miner Alexei Stakhanov, was used to push workers to higher output. The cost was heavy, with poor housing, low living standards and forced labour on projects like the White Sea-Baltic Canal. Stalin's economy was one in which the state decided everything and ordinary people paid the price.\n\nThe same ruthlessness marked the collectivisation of agriculture, begun in 1929 when Stalin announced the liquidation of the kulaks as a class. Peasants were forced into collective farms under state control, and richer peasants were deported or killed. Many peasants slaughtered their animals in protest, and grain requisitions left villages without food. The result was the famine of 1932-33, which killed millions, especially in Ukraine and Kazakhstan. Collectivisation gave Stalin control of the countryside, but at the price of millions of lives.\n\nThe most notorious feature of Stalin's rule was terror. The NKVD and the Gulag network of forced-labour camps held millions of prisoners. In the Moscow show trials of 1936 to 1938 old Bolsheviks including Zinoviev, Kamenev and Bukharin confessed to treason and wrecking after interrogation and threats, and were shot. Behind the trials lay the Great Terror of 1937-38, in which hundreds of thousands were executed and many more arrested. Most victims were ordinary people, while the party and army leadership was devastated. Terror made everyone, even Stalin's closest colleagues, fear him.\n\nStalin's regime also controlled culture and ideas and built a cult of personality around him. The cult began with his fiftieth birthday in 1929, when Pravda hailed him as Lenin's heir. Towns, factories and streets were named after him, Trotsky was written out of official history and removed from photographs, and the Short Course history of the party of 1938 gave Stalin the central role in the revolution. Socialist realism became the official style in the arts from 1932, and writers and artists who did not follow the line faced censorship or arrest. The 1936 Constitution promised civil rights and a secret ballot, but the Communist Party's monopoly of power was untouched, so it was a facade.\n\nStalin's wartime leadership showed the same mixture of mobilisation and coercion. After Germany invaded in June 1941 he chaired the State Defence Committee, became Supreme Commander and moved about 1,500 factories east of the Urals. Order 227 of July 1942, \"Not one step back\", brought blocking detachments and penal battalions. Victory over Germany came at a cost of about 27 million Soviet dead, but made the USSR a superpower and strengthened Stalin's authority.\n\nStalin's rule was therefore characterised by absolute personal power, forced modernisation at enormous human cost, systematic terror and total control of society through propaganda and the cult of the leader. He made the Soviet Union an industrial and military power, but by methods that made it one of the most thoroughly totalitarian states in Europe.",
