    "model": "Anti-Semitism and the Holocaust affected Europe profoundly between 1920 and 1945. They destroyed the Jewish communities of the continent, drew states and societies into complicity with persecution and murder, and left a moral and legal legacy that Europe is still facing.\n\nThe first effect was the spread of racial anti-Semitism as political doctrine. Anti-Semitism was old in Europe, combining religious hostility, economic resentment and, from the late nineteenth century, racial theory. In Mein Kampf Hitler blamed the Jews for Germany's defeat in 1918, for communism and for the Versailles settlement, and the Nazis aimed to remove them from German life. The hatred was not confined to Germany: in 1938 Fascist Italy introduced racial laws excluding Jews from schools, the professions and the armed forces. Jews were less than one per cent of Germany's population and well integrated, so the hatred owed more to ideology than to anything Jews did.\n\nSecond, persecution became state policy in Germany and spread with German power. A boycott of Jewish shops was ordered in April 1933, and Jewish officials, lawyers and doctors were dismissed. The Nuremberg Laws of 1935 defined Jews by descent, stripped them of citizenship and banned marriage with Germans. On 9-10 November 1938 Kristallnacht saw synagogues burned, shops wrecked, Jews killed and about 30,000 men sent to concentration camps. The effect on Europe was a growing refugee crisis. The Evian Conference of July 1938 achieved little because few countries would admit more refugees, and although about 10,000 children reached Britain on the Kindertransport, most countries kept strict quotas. Europe's democracies showed that they would not take in the victims.\n\nThird, war turned persecution into mass murder across the continent. After the invasion of Poland in 1939 Jews were herded into ghettos such as Warsaw, where starvation and disease killed thousands. From June 1941 the Einsatzgruppen followed the army into the Soviet Union and shot Jews in huge numbers, as at Babi Yar in September 1941. At Wannsee in January 1942 Heydrich coordinated the Final Solution, the deportation of Jews from across Europe to camps in the east. Chelmno, Belzec, Sobibor and Treblinka were killing centres, while Auschwitz-Birkenau combined a labour camp with gas chambers using Zyklon B. In all about six million Jews were murdered, about 90 per cent of Polish Jews among them, so the Jewish civilisation of eastern Europe was virtually wiped out.\n\nFourth, the Holocaust exposed how far European societies cooperated with it or stood aside. The Vichy regime passed its own anti-Jewish statute in 1940, and in July 1942 French police arrested about 13,000 Jews in Paris, many of them sent via Drancy to Auschwitz. Authorities in other occupied countries helped in round-ups, and many neighbours profited from seized property. Allied governments knew of the killings by late 1942 and promised punishment, but did not bomb Auschwitz or the railways leading to it. Yet there were also rescuers. Denmark ferried over 7,000 Jews to Sweden in October 1943, and Oskar Schindler saved about 1,200 workers. Jews themselves resisted, notably in the Warsaw ghetto uprising of April 1943. The effect was to show both the failure of most Europeans and what a determined few could do.\n\nFinally, the Nazis extended racial murder to other groups, and the aftermath changed Europe's institutions. About 70,000 disabled patients were murdered in Aktion T4, Roma and Sinti were killed in their hundreds of thousands, and about three million Soviet prisoners of war died in captivity. When Soviet troops liberated Auschwitz in January 1945 and British and American forces reached Belsen and Dachau, the world saw the evidence. The Nuremberg trials from November 1945 judged the persecution of Jews a crime against humanity, a principle that has shaped international law since.\n\nOverall, anti-Semitism and the Holocaust affected Europe more deeply than almost any other development of 1920 to 1945. They destroyed a community that had lived on the continent for centuries, implicated many governments and peoples in crime or indifference, and left a legacy that reshaped European law, politics and memory.",
