    "model": "From my study of World War II I learned that the wartime alliances decided the outcome of the war, even though they were strained by disagreement, and that the Home Front in Britain showed how civilians could sustain a total war while changing the society that emerged from it.\n\nThe Axis powers were bound by treaty but did not act as a true alliance. The Pact of Steel of May 1939 joined Germany and Italy, and the Tripartite Pact of September 1940 added Japan. Yet there was no joint strategy, and Italy's defeats in Greece and North Africa forced Germany to divert forces to the Balkans and Africa. Against them stood the Grand Alliance of Britain, the Soviet Union and the United States, which was built by events. Britain stood alone after the fall of France in June 1940, and the Lend-Lease Act of March 1941 let the United States supply her without immediate payment. When Germany invaded the Soviet Union on 22 June 1941 Stalin was forced into the Allied camp, and when Japan attacked Pearl Harbor in December 1941 the United States entered the war. By the end of 1941 the war had become a global conflict that the Axis could not win.\n\nThe alliance was held together by shared purpose, and it produced real results. Churchill and Roosevelt issued the Atlantic Charter in August 1941, and at Casablanca in January 1943 they announced unconditional surrender. The Tehran Conference of late 1943 brought the Big Three together for the first time and set the plan for the invasion of France. Combined strength then turned the war: the Soviets won at Stalingrad in February 1943 and Kursk in July 1943, and the Normandy landings of 6 June 1944 opened a western front while the Red Army advanced from the east. Germany surrendered in May 1945.\n\nHowever, the alliance was always strained. Stalin repeatedly demanded a second front in Western Europe to relieve the Red Army, and the delay deepened his suspicion of his partners. At Yalta in February 1945 the three agreed the occupation zones of Germany, free elections in liberated Eastern Europe and Soviet entry into the war against Japan, but disputes over Poland and Soviet control of Eastern Europe foreshadowed the Cold War. The alliance defeated the Axis, but it was an alliance of convenience against a common enemy and it fell apart once that enemy was beaten.\n\nThe Home Front in Britain was the second theme I studied. The government prepared early for air attack: Operation Pied Piper began on 1 September 1939 and moved about 1.5 million people, mostly children, from cities to the countryside, although many returned during the Phoney War. The experience exposed the poverty of city slums to country hosts and reformers. Rationing began in January 1940 with bacon, butter and sugar and was later extended to meat and clothing. Most people accepted it because it seemed fair, and Dig for Victory encouraged families to grow their own vegetables.\n\nThe Blitz tested morale most severely. It began in September 1940, London was bombed on 57 consecutive nights, and Coventry was heavily bombed in November 1940. People used Anderson shelters and Underground stations, and the Home Guard and air raid wardens carried out civil defence. Churchill's broadcasts sustained resilience. Ernest Bevin as Minister of Labour directed workers to war industries, and women took up factory work and joined the ATS, WAAF and WRNS.\n\nThe Home Front also changed British society. The Beveridge Report of December 1942 named five giants of want, disease, ignorance, squalor and idleness and proposed national insurance and a health service, ideas largely carried out by the Education Act of 1944, the National Insurance Act of 1946 and the National Health Service of 1948. Shared danger and rationing had encouraged support for state planning.\n\nIn conclusion, the wartime alliances showed that a coalition of unlike partners could defeat a more tightly bound enemy, though their divisions pointed to the Cold War, while the Home Front showed that civilian sacrifice and organisation were as important as military victories and helped build the post-war welfare state.",
