    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Between 1945 and 1992 marriage, the family and the position of women in Western Europe changed profoundly, from a domestic and traditional model to one in which women had legal equality, control over childbearing and a larger place in paid work. The Second Vatican Council, which opened in 1962, renewed Catholic worship and relations with other churches, but it also exposed a widening gap between the Church and its members.\n\nThe years after the war saw a revival of the traditional family. The baby boom ran from about 1946 to the mid-1960s, people married earlier and more often, and the nuclear family became the ideal. Governments supported it through family allowances, extended in France in 1945-46, and women's legal position remained weak: in West Germany until 1977 a husband could decide whether his wife took a job, and in France married women won the right to work and open a bank account without their husband's consent only in 1965. The post-war family therefore rested on the dependence of wives.\n\nFrom the 1950s this model was challenged as more married women entered paid work, though in lower-paid jobs. Article 119 of the Treaty of Rome required equal pay for equal work, and Britain passed the Equal Pay Act of 1970, which took full effect in 1975 alongside the Sex Discrimination Act. Ideas mattered as much as law. Simone de Beauvoir's The Second Sex of 1949 argued that women's inferior position was created by history, law, religion and education and not by biology, and Betty Friedan's The Feminine Mystique of 1963 helped launch second-wave feminism. The Mouvement de liberation des femmes was formed in France in 1970 and demanded equal pay, nurseries and reproductive freedom.\n\nControl over fertility and easier divorce changed marriage itself. France legalised contraception in 1967 and Britain legalised abortion on medical grounds in the same year, and the Veil Law of 1975 legalised abortion in France in the first ten weeks. Britain's Divorce Reform Act of 1969 allowed divorce on grounds of irretrievable breakdown, and West Germany introduced no-fault divorce in 1977. Divorce rates and the number of single-parent families rose, and conservatives blamed permissiveness. The gains were real but limited, since women still did most domestic work.\n\nThe Second Vatican Council addressed a Church facing secularisation. Pope John XXIII announced it in January 1959 and spoke of aggiornamento, the updating of the Church. It opened in October 1962 with about 2,500 bishops, and at the first session the bishops rejected the drafts prepared by the Roman Curia and took control of the agenda. Paul VI carried it through to its close in December 1965. Its results were far-reaching: the liturgy was reformed so that Mass was said in local languages and lay people played a fuller part, Lumen Gentium described the Church as the people of God, and in December 1965 Paul VI and Patriarch Athenagoras lifted the excommunications of 1054. Nostra Aetate rejected the charge of collective Jewish guilt for the death of Christ and Dignitatis Humanae affirmed religious liberty.\n\nYet the Council divided Catholics. Archbishop Lefebvre rejected the reforms and founded the Society of St Pius X in 1970, while progressives felt the Council had not gone far enough. Paul VI's encyclical Humanae Vitae of 1968 reaffirmed the ban on artificial contraception even though a papal commission had favoured relaxation, and theologians and bishops' conferences in the Netherlands, Belgium and West Germany openly dissented. Mass attendance and vocations fell steeply in the 1960s and 1970s, though Protestant churches suffered the same decline without a Council, which suggests wider social causes.\n\nOverall, the period brought legal equality, reproductive choice and easier divorce, which weakened the old family model, while the Church that had once defined that model found its authority over private life shaken. Women gained far more freedom than in 1945 though not full equality, and the Council renewed Catholic worship and ecumenism without halting the decline in practice.",
