    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Marshall Aid and the 1973 Oil Crisis had very different effects on the Western European economy and society. Marshall Aid helped launch the long post-war boom and encouraged cooperation, while the Oil Crisis ended that boom and brought inflation, unemployment and a rethinking of government policy.\n\nMarshall Aid was offered by the United States in 1947 and ran as the European Recovery Programme from 1948 to 1952, providing about 13 billion dollars, mostly in grants, to sixteen states. Western Europe was short of dollars, its industry and transport were damaged, and the coal shortage of the winter of 1946-47 had slowed production. The aid paid for imports of raw materials, food and machinery, and counterpart funds were used for investment in industry and housing. Britain, France and West Germany received the largest sums. It helped restore production and confidence, and in the Cold War it was designed to prevent economic hardship from strengthening communist parties in France and Italy.\n\nThe aid also changed how Europe's economies were run. Recipients had to draw up recovery plans, and the Organisation for European Economic Co-operation, set up in April 1948, coordinated the allocation of aid; it later became the OECD in 1961. The European Payments Union of 1950 revived trade between members, and conditions encouraged states to lower trade barriers and control inflation. American productivity methods spread, and Washington gained influence by supporting Christian Democratic and social democratic parties against the communists. Some historians, such as Alan Milward, argue that Marshall Aid was a modest share of total investment and that domestic policy did much of the work of recovery, but its effect on cooperation and confidence is hard to dispute. Socially, recovery fed the growth of the 1950s and 1960s, with higher living standards, full employment and the expansion of the welfare state.\n\nThe Oil Crisis struck an economy that had grown for almost thirty years on cheap energy, with oil supplying around half of Western Europe's energy and priced at about three dollars a barrel. After the Yom Kippur War began on 6 October 1973, the Arab oil producers raised prices sharply and announced output cuts, and embargoes on the USA and the Netherlands followed, because the Netherlands supported Israel and Rotterdam was Europe's main oil port. By early 1974 prices had roughly quadrupled. Economically the result was stagflation, a combination of stagnation and rising prices which Keynesian policies struggled to address. Industrial production fell, oil-importing states ran balance of payments deficits, and heavy industries such as steel, shipbuilding and coal declined. A second shock after the Iranian revolution of 1979 pushed prices up again and caused a recession in 1980-82.\n\nThe social effects were immediate and long-lasting. Car-free Sundays were introduced in the Netherlands and West Germany, and Britain adopted a three-day working week in early 1974. Unemployment across the EEC rose from low levels to above 10 per cent by the mid-1980s, and governments abandoned the post-war commitment to full employment in favour of defeating inflation. This opened the way for Margaret Thatcher, Prime Minister from 1979, whose monetarist policies were followed by unemployment of about three million in 1982 and the deindustrialisation of the north of England, Scotland and Wales. On the other hand the crisis forced adaptation: France launched its nuclear programme under the Messmer Plan in 1974, North Sea oil made Britain self-sufficient by 1980, and the International Energy Agency was set up in 1974.\n\nIn short, Marshall Aid supported recovery, trade cooperation and rising living standards, while the Oil Crisis reversed them, bringing recession, unemployment and the end of the post-war consensus. Both left Western Europe's economies and societies permanently changed.",
