    "label": "1",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "British withdrawal had profound but very different effects on India and Palestine. In India it produced independence on 15 August 1947 together with partition, mass migration and communal slaughter, while in Palestine it left a security vacuum, a Jewish state and a war that created the Palestinian refugee problem and an unresolved Arab-Israeli conflict.\n\nIn India, the immediate effect was the end of the Raj, which had ruled directly since 1858. The Indian Independence Act of July 1947 created two dominions, India and Pakistan. Attlee's Labour government, burdened by war debts and unable to rely on the Indian Army after the INA trials and the Bombay naval mutiny of 1946, announced in February 1947 that rule would end by June 1948. Lord Mountbatten then brought the date forward to August 1947, accepting partition under the plan of 3 June. The speed of the transfer was itself an effect on India, as the army, civil service and railways had to be divided in a few weeks.\n\nThe most terrible result was partition and the violence that came with it. Cyril Radcliffe, a barrister who had never visited India, drew the boundaries through Punjab and Bengal, and his line was announced on 17 August, two days after independence, so that millions found themselves on the wrong side. Between 10 and 15 million Hindus, Sikhs and Muslims were displaced, and estimates of the dead range from about 200,000 to about 2 million. Pakistan was created in two parts, West Pakistan and East Bengal, separated by about 1,600 km of Indian territory. The violence followed the Calcutta killings after Direct Action Day in August 1946, and it showed that the British had left before communal differences were settled.\n\nIndependence also left political problems that persisted. The princely states, more than 500, had to choose between India and Pakistan, and the accession of Kashmir by its Maharaja on 26 October 1947 led to the first Indo-Pakistani war, ended by a ceasefire from 1 January 1949, which left the state divided. Gandhi, who fasted in Calcutta and Delhi against the violence, was shot dead by Nathuram Godse on 30 January 1948, and Jinnah died in September 1948. Yet there were positive effects: India became a republic on 26 January 1950 under Nehru, remained in the Commonwealth under the London Declaration of April 1949, and followed non-alignment while remaining a major trading partner of Britain. Britain's withdrawal was also notable for taking place without a colonial war of the kind France later fought.\n\nIn Palestine, withdrawal followed the collapse of Britain's attempt to reconcile the promises of the Balfour Declaration of 1917 with Arab opposition. After the Arab Revolt of 1936 to 1939 and the White Paper of 1939 limiting Jewish immigration, the Holocaust intensified Jewish demands for a state. Irgun and Lehi attacked British targets, including the King David Hotel bombing of July 1946, which killed 91 people, and Britain's turning back of the Exodus in 1947 caused world outrage. Unable to impose a settlement with about 100,000 troops, Britain referred the question to the United Nations in February 1947 and announced the end of the Mandate.\n\nThe effect was war. The UN General Assembly voted for partition on 29 November 1947 by 33 votes to 13, but the Arab states rejected it, and fighting began in December. Britain refused to implement the plan and withdrew, so that the Mandate expired on 14 May 1948, the day David Ben-Gurion proclaimed Israel. Egypt, Jordan, Syria, Iraq and Lebanon invaded the next day, and the armistices of 1949 left Israel with more territory than the UN plan gave it, while Jordan held the West Bank and Egypt administered Gaza. About 700,000 Palestinian Arabs fled or were expelled, and the refugee question remained unresolved, with Resolution 194 never implemented. About 6,000 Israelis, roughly one per cent of the Jewish population, died.\n\nOverall, British withdrawal ended imperial rule in both countries but left them with divided and violent legacies. In India the transfer was rushed and partition cost huge numbers of lives, yet a stable independent state emerged, whereas in Palestine Britain simply left a vacuum in which a new state was born in war and one of the world's longest conflicts began.",
