    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "European withdrawal from empire had deep social and economic effects on former African colonies. Independence brought pride, new schools and some real gains, but it also left states with weak economies, political divisions and a lasting dependence on their former rulers, and in places it brought war and famine.\n\nEconomically, colonial rule had built economies for export rather than for the people who lived in them. Cocoa in the Gold Coast, groundnuts in Senegal, cotton in Uganda and copper in Katanga were produced for European markets, and railways ran from mines and farms to the coast rather than linking African regions to each other. Independent states inherited this pattern. Dependence on one or two commodities left them exposed to price falls, as when copper fell in 1974-75 and cocoa and coffee fell in the 1980s, and companies such as Union Minière, Shell-BP and the United Africa Company kept control of mining, oil and trade after independence. Political freedom therefore did not mean economic freedom.\n\nThe weakness of the new states was most obvious in the Belgian Congo. Belgium had trained almost no Congolese administrators or officers, and within days of independence on 30 June 1960 the Force Publique mutinied, Belgian troops returned, and Katanga, with its copper, seceded with Belgian backing. The UN sent about 20,000 troops, Lumumba was murdered in January 1961, and Mobutu took power. Under Mobutu's Zaire the economy collapsed: Zairianisation in 1973 handed foreign-owned businesses to his associates, and the fall in copper prices made matters worse. Social life was also changed by the authenticité campaign, which banned Christian names and European dress. The Congo shows how an unprepared withdrawal damaged both society and economy.\n\nEthnic division was a second social effect. Colonial rulers had fixed ethnic identities and joined peoples together within arbitrary borders, and in Nigeria, which Lugard amalgamated in 1914, the main parties each drew support from one region and one ethnic group. The coups of 1966, the massacres of Igbo in the north and Biafra's secession in May 1967 followed. The Biafran War lasted until January 1970 and caused famine, with deaths estimated from several hundred thousand to over two million, mostly from starvation and disease. Oil, found at Oloibiri in the east, deepened the dispute. Gowon's policy of reconciliation and later oil revenue helped Nigeria rebuild, but the war showed the social cost of colonial borders.\n\nAngola suffered in a different way because Portugal refused to decolonise. A war of independence began in 1961 and lasted until the Carnation Revolution of 1974. Independence in November 1975 was followed by civil war between the MPLA, UNITA and the FNLA, each with a distinct ethnic base and foreign backers, with Cuban and Soviet help for the MPLA and South African and American support for its rivals. The war lasted until 2002. Most of the roughly 300,000 Portuguese settlers left in 1975, taking skills and capital with them, so the country had few trained administrators. Oil from Cabinda and diamonds became the main revenue, but war prevented balanced development.\n\nDebt, aid and famine added to these problems. African governments borrowed heavily after the oil price rises of 1973 and 1979, and when interest rates rose and commodity prices fell in the early 1980s the IMF and World Bank imposed structural adjustment, with devaluation and cuts in public spending that critics said hurt health and education. Aid often reinforced old links with former rulers and sometimes propped up regimes such as Mobutu's. The EEC tried to help through the Yaoundé Conventions of 1963 and 1969 and Lomé I in 1975, which gave duty-free access to European markets and the STABEX fund for falling export earnings, but dependence remained. Famine struck Biafra in 1967-70, the Sahel in the drought of the late 1960s and early 1970s, and Ethiopia in 1983-85, the last prompting Band Aid and Live Aid, while agencies such as Concern, founded in 1968 in response to Biafra, took up relief work.\n\nSome states tried to find their own path. Nyerere's Tanzania, through the Arusha Declaration of 1967, chose ujamaa, nationalising banks and industries and building village settlements. Tanzania made real gains in literacy and primary education and avoided ethnic conflict by promoting Swahili, but forced villagisation from 1973-74 disrupted farming and food had to be imported, and by 1986 his successor accepted an IMF programme. Ghana under Rawlings in 1983 showed that adjustment could work, though with hardship.\n\nOverall, the effects of withdrawal were mixed but mainly negative in the short term. Independence gave Africans control of government, schools and national identity, yet economies built for Europe, borders drawn by Europe and the rapidity of withdrawal left new states fragile. Economically most remained dependent on exports, aid and former rulers, and socially the cost was ethnic conflict, war and famine, though African leaders such as Nyerere and Gowon also shaped the outcomes.",
