    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The growth of Islam in Europe between 1945 and 1990 posed serious challenges, but they were mainly social, political and cultural rather than a threat to European states. Migrant Muslim communities faced discrimination, housing and job problems, while European societies faced difficult questions about integration, religious identity and freedom of expression.\n\nThe first challenge came from migration itself. After 1945 Western Europe needed labour, and Muslims came as workers: Algerians, Moroccans and Tunisians to France, Pakistanis and Bangladeshis to Britain, and Turkish guest workers to West Germany. France's Muslim population reached an estimated two to three million by the 1980s, making Islam its second religion. Many migrants first lived in bidonvilles such as Nanterre, and others in overcrowded housing in British cities. The state had to provide housing, schools and services for communities it had at first treated as temporary workers, especially after France suspended labour immigration in July 1974 and then allowed family reunification in 1976.\n\nSecond, racism and discrimination met these communities. In Britain the 1958 attacks on West Indian residents in Notting Hill and Nottingham, restrictive laws in 1962, 1968 and 1971 and Enoch Powell's 'rivers of blood' speech of April 1968 showed hostility to immigrants. The National Front, founded in 1967, campaigned against immigration. In France, the attack by Paris police under Maurice Papon on Algerian demonstrators on 17 October 1961 killed dozens or more, and the far right later gained support. Britain tried to respond with Race Relations Acts in 1965, 1968 and 1976, but enforcement was weak, and the 1981 riots in Brixton, Toxteth and Moss Side, which the Scarman Report linked to disadvantage, unemployment and poor policing, showed that tensions remained. Many young Muslims in banlieues and inner cities experienced exclusion.\n\nThird, the two main states had different integration models, and each created difficulties. Britain developed multiculturalism, which supported distinct cultures, and Roy Jenkins in 1966 called for equal opportunity together with cultural diversity. Muslims built mosques in Birmingham, Bradford and London in the 1960s and 1970s. France relied on assimilation and on laïcité, the secularism set out in the law of 1905, so the state did not recognise religious communities, and France did not collect statistics on ethnic origin, making discrimination harder to measure. The second-generation beurs grew up in the banlieues and became a focus of debate. The challenge for France was to reconcile secular citizenship with visible religious identity, as the headscarf question showed in 1989 when girls were excluded from school at Creil.\n\nFourth, and most dramatic, was the Rushdie affair. Salman Rushdie's The Satanic Verses was published in September 1988 and many Muslims in Britain objected to its portrayal of the Prophet Muhammad. In January 1989 copies were burned in Bradford, and on 14 February 1989 Ayatollah Khomeini issued a fatwa calling for Rushdie's death, which forced him into hiding under police protection. The affair raised the clash between religious identity and freedom of expression. It also exposed a legal question, since Britain's blasphemy law protected only Christianity, and the courts refused in 1991 to extend it to Islam, which Muslim campaigners cited as unfair. The affair also made many in Britain see Muslims as a separate challenge to the liberal order.\n\nFinally, there were broader political and cultural effects. Far-right parties, including the National Front in Britain and Le Pen's Front National in France, gained support in the 1970s and 1980s by blaming immigrants for unemployment, and many politicians treated migration as an economic and security problem while migrant communities saw discrimination as the main issue. Some Muslims also struggled to keep their identity, language and religion while their children grew up in European schools. Official support was uneven: Britain began to recognise ethnic minority needs in schools through the Rampton Report of 1981 and the Swann Report of 1985, and Black and Asian MPs entered Parliament in 1987. Muslims were not given the same protections as Christians.\n\nOverall, the main challenges posed by the growth of Islam in Europe were not caused by Islam itself but by the way European societies met it: discrimination in housing, jobs and policing, uncertainty about integration, and clashes over religious identity and free speech. Both Britain and France had to adapt their models, and by 1990 neither had resolved them.",
