    "label": "C3",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Isabella Tod, born in Edinburgh in 1836, settled in Belfast and organised the first suffrage society in Ulster in the early 1870s. She campaigned for the Married Women's Property Act of 1882, which improved women's rights over their own property. Tod helped win the inclusion of girls in the Intermediate Education Act of 1878 and in the Royal University's charter of 1879. She helped found the Ladies' Institute in Belfast in 1867, a society for girls' education, and she co-founded the Belfast Women's Temperance Association in 1874. She also campaigned against the Contagious Diseases Acts. As a Liberal Unionist she opposed Home Rule in 1886 and urged women to oppose it too.",
