    "model": "W.B. Yeats was one of the leaders of the National Literary Society, which he helped found in Dublin in 1892 with Douglas Hyde. In 1899 he co-founded the Irish Literary Theatre with Lady Gregory and Edward Martyn, staging his play The Countess Cathleen that May. The Catholic Church attacked The Countess Cathleen in 1899 as heretical, which showed the tension over who would define Irish culture. Yeats and Lady Gregory wrote Cathleen ni Houlihan, first staged in 1902 with Maud Gonne in the title role. He was one of the three directors of the Abbey Theatre, which opened in Dublin in December 1904 with funding from Annie Horniman. His early poetry drew on Irish mythology, and in 1923 he won the Nobel Prize in Literature.",
