    "label": "C4",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Seamus Heaney, born in 1939 at Mossbawn in County Derry, was the leading poet of the Troubles. In North, published in 1975, he used Iron Age bog bodies, as in the poem 'Punishment', to reflect on ritual killing in Northern Ireland. His later collections Field Work in 1979 and Station Island in 1984 explored violence, memory and the Catholic tradition. In his 1983 poem 'An Open Letter' he objected to being described as British, insisting that he was Irish. Historians see his poetry as giving expression to grief, guilt and the search for an identity beyond sectarianism. Critics such as Ciaran Carson argued that North made violence seem inevitable, but many see Heaney's work as a major cultural response to the conflict.",
