    "label": "B1",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "Ireland applied for UN membership in 1946, but the Soviet Union vetoed it, citing Ireland's neutrality in the Second World War. Ireland was finally admitted on 14 December 1955, in a package deal that admitted sixteen states together. Frank Aiken, Minister for External Affairs, followed an independent line, and Ireland and Malaya sponsored a 1959 resolution criticising Chinese suppression of human rights in Tibet. Resolution 1665 of 4 December 1961 called on all states not to transfer or acquire nuclear weapons, and Ireland was the first state to sign the 1968 Non-Proliferation Treaty. Irish troops also served in the UN force in the Congo from July 1960.",
