    "label": "B2",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "The Ireland Act 1949 declared that Northern Ireland would not leave the United Kingdom without the consent of its parliament, which gave unionists a veto. Terence O'Neill, the Northern Ireland Prime Minister, made a secret visit to Dublin to meet Seán Lemass on 14 January 1965, the first meeting of the two heads of government since partition. After Bloody Sunday in January 1972, the Irish government recalled its ambassador from London. The Sunningdale Agreement of December 1973 set up a power-sharing executive and a Council of Ireland, but it collapsed after the loyalist strike in May 1974. FitzGerald and Thatcher signed the Anglo-Irish Agreement at Hillsborough on 15 November 1985.",
