    "model": "Before the 1960s most children left school at 14, and the state left most second-level education to the churches and to parents who could pay. Investment in Education, published in 1965 after a survey commissioned by Patrick Hillery, recommended expanding second-level and technical education. Comprehensive schools, first opened in 1966, offered academic and practical subjects to pupils of all abilities. Donogh O'Malley's announcement of free second-level education took effect in September 1967. The new primary curriculum of 1971 emphasised the needs and interests of the individual child rather than memorisation.",
