    "label": "C4",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "FitzGerald led two coalitions with Labour, the first from June 1981 to March 1982 and the second from December 1982 to March 1987. In 1981 he launched a constitutional crusade to make the Republic more pluralist, but the 1986 divorce referendum was defeated. He established the New Ireland Forum in 1983, whose report offered three options for the future of Ireland. He negotiated the Anglo-Irish Agreement with Margaret Thatcher, signed at Hillsborough on 15 November 1985, which gave Dublin a consultative role in Northern Ireland. In 1985 his government allowed contraceptives to be sold without prescription to people over 18. He is credited with opening Irish politics to new ideas about pluralism, although his economic record was weak.",
