    "marks": 30,
    "model": "Sergei Witte's state-directed industrialisation of the 1890s built railways and heavy industry with foreign loans, but the cost fell on the peasantry through high indirect taxes. The industrial depression of 1900-03 caused unemployment and strikes, and the famine of 1891 weakened trust in the regime. Defeats in the First World War, together with shortages and inflation, led to the February Revolution of 1917, when women textile workers in Petrograd demanded bread. The Bolsheviks seized power in October 1917, and the Reds went on to win the civil war by late 1920.",
