    "label": "C3",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Bing Crosby became a radio singer in the early 1930s and developed a relaxed crooning style suited to the microphone, which made him the most popular entertainer of his age. His radio shows, records and films spread American popular music into Europe, including the Road series with Bob Hope from 1940. His recording of White Christmas, released in 1942 for Holiday Inn, became one of the best-selling songs of all time and was hugely popular with troops. He entertained Allied troops through radio broadcasts and USO tours, including a tour of Britain and France in 1944. Charlie Chaplin created the Tramp character, first seen in 1914, which made him the most famous film star of the silent era. His Modern Times of 1936 satirised industrial life and mechanisation, and The Great Dictator of 1940 parodied Hitler as Adenoid Hynkel.",
