    "model": "Hungary under Rakosi was a rigidly Stalinist state, with the AVH secret police, show trials and forced collectivisation, and living standards were falling. Khrushchev's secret speech of February 1956 exposed Stalin's crimes, and the Polish October encouraged Hungarian reformers and intellectuals in the Petofi Circle. Nagy, who had been removed from office in 1955, was reinstated as prime minister on 24 October 1956 and announced neutrality and withdrawal from the Warsaw Pact on 1 November. The uprising had no organised armed leadership beyond local groups, and negotiations with Soviet commanders failed to produce a lasting arrangement. Soviet forces returned in strength on 4 November and crushed the fighting, while the West did not intervene because the Suez crisis drew attention away. Moscow regarded leaving the Warsaw Pact as unacceptable to its security, so Soviet willingness to use force was decisive.",
