    "model": "Britain's exhausted economy after the war meant it could not hold a large army in India, and it had borrowed about 3.75 billion dollars from the United States in 1945-46. Clement Attlee's Labour government was committed to Indian self-rule and announced on 20 February 1947 that British rule would end by June 1948 at the latest. Congress's mass campaigns, from non-cooperation to the Quit India resolution of 1942, showed that British rule could not be maintained by force alone. The INA trials and the 1946 Royal Indian Navy mutiny made British officials doubt that the army and police could be relied on to suppress a mass rising. The Cabinet Mission of 1946 failed to win agreement between Congress and the League, and Mountbatten's plan of 3 June 1947 accepted the partition of India into two states. The settlement was the Indian Independence Act of July 1947, which made India and Pakistan independent dominions on 15 August 1947 and divided the Punjab and Bengal.",
