    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Young Ireland and Fenianism made a lasting contribution to Irish affairs, though not by winning their immediate aims. Young Ireland created a cultural and non-sectarian idea of the Irish nation and, in 1848, attempted a rising against British rule. Fenianism built a disciplined, transatlantic revolutionary organisation, kept the republican ideal alive and helped force the British government into reform. Neither achieved independence, but together they shaped the traditions of cultural nationalism and physical force that later generations drew on.\n\nYoung Ireland began as a group of young, mostly middle-class writers inside O'Connell's Repeal Association. Charles Gavan Duffy, Thomas Davis and John Blake Dillon founded The Nation in October 1842, and it became the most widely read paper in Ireland, passed from hand to hand and read aloud in reading rooms. Its idea of nationality was cultural rather than religious: a nation shared history, culture and ideally the Irish language, and it included Protestants as well as Catholics. Davis, a Protestant from Mallow, wrote ballads such as A Nation Once Again, and Duffy's Library of Ireland published cheap books on Irish history and literature. Davis died in 1845, aged only thirty, but his ideas inspired the later Gaelic revival and cultural nationalists such as Pearse.\n\nYoung Ireland also changed the politics of the national movement. Its members disagreed with O'Connell over his Whig alliance and over the Queen's Colleges, and the break came in July 1846 over the peace resolutions, when Thomas Francis Meagher's Sword Speech refused to condemn force in all circumstances. They then founded the Irish Confederation in January 1847, under William Smith O'Brien. James Fintan Lalor argued that the land question must be linked to national independence, an idea that influenced John Mitchel, who founded the United Irishman in 1848 to preach open revolution. This split the movement, and so Young Ireland weakened Repeal, but it also introduced a social and agrarian dimension to nationalism.\n\nThe rising of July 1848 was a failure. The French revolution of February 1848 encouraged the Confederates, but Mitchel was transported under the Treason Felony Act in May and habeas corpus was suspended in July. Smith O'Brien's small band besieged police in the Widow McCormack's house at Ballingarry, Co. Tipperary, on 29 July, and two rebels were killed before the rest dispersed. The rebels lacked arms and preparation, the people were starving, and the Catholic clergy opposed them. Smith O'Brien and Meagher were sentenced to death for treason, commuted to transportation to Van Diemen's Land. Yet 1848 became a link in the chain of physical force tradition, since James Stephens, wounded at Ballingarry, and John O'Mahony both escaped and later founded the Fenians.\n\nFenianism was a far better organised movement. The Irish Republican Brotherhood was founded in Dublin by James Stephens on 17 March 1858, and John O'Mahony founded the Fenian Brotherhood in New York in the same year. It was an oath-bound secret society organised in circles, recruiting artisans, clerks, shopkeepers and farmers' sons, and even soldiers in the British army. Its roots lay in the bitterness of the Famine, the failure of 1848 and disillusion with parliamentary politics after the collapse of the Independent Irish Party. The funeral of Terence Bellew MacManus in 1861 showed its strength, and its newspaper, the Irish People, edited by Luby, O'Leary and Kickham, spread the republican message. American veterans of the Civil War raised hopes of a real rising.\n\nThe Fenians failed militarily. The police raided the Irish People in September 1865 and arrested its leaders, habeas corpus was suspended in 1866 and informers compromised the plans. The rising of March 1867 was easily crushed in bad weather. Yet Fenian actions abroad kept the movement in the public eye. In Manchester in 1867 the rescue of two prisoners led to the hanging of Allen, Larkin and O'Brien, the Manchester Martyrs, who were mourned across Ireland, and the Clerkenwell explosion in December 1867 shocked British opinion. Cardinal Cullen and most bishops condemned the movement as a secret society, so it never won the Church's support.\n\nThe contribution of the two movements was therefore real. Young Ireland gave nationalism its cultural base and its ballads and history, while Fenianism gave it a revolutionary organisation and its martyrs. Many historians argue that Fenian violence prompted Gladstone to turn to Irish reform, with disestablishment of the Church of Ireland in 1869 and the Land Act of 1870, and the IRB survived to organise the Rising of 1916. They achieved little in their own day, but they shaped Irish affairs for decades.",
