    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Mother Mary Aikenhead and William Dargan both made major contributions to Irish life in the nineteenth century, one through care for the poor and the sick and the other through enterprise and the promotion of Irish industry. Aikenhead created a new kind of active religious life for women and founded institutions that shaped Irish health care. Dargan built much of the railway network and showed what Irish enterprise could achieve.\n\nMary Aikenhead was born in Cork in 1787 to a Protestant father and a Catholic mother, and became a Catholic. Guided by Archbishop Daniel Murray of Dublin, she trained at the Bar Convent in York and in 1815 founded the Religious Sisters of Charity, the first Irish Catholic order devoted to caring for the poor outside a convent. At a time when Ireland had little public provision for the poor, the Sisters visited the destitute in their homes, ran schools and nursed the sick. This was a new departure, because it gave women a public role in education and social care at a time when few other careers were open to them. In 1834 she opened St Vincent's Hospital on St Stephen's Green in Dublin, the first hospital in Ireland run by nuns, which treated the sick poor of all creeds.\n\nHer significance went beyond the hospital. She directed the order from Stanhope Street in Dublin, much of the time as an invalid, until her death in 1858, and so she led it through the cholera and fever outbreaks of the 1830s and through the Famine years, in which Sisters nursed the sick and fed the poor. Contemporaries admired her charity and her courage in running such an institution while ill. Historians see her as a pioneer of active women's religious life and of Catholic nursing, and the order she founded laid the foundations for hospitals and schools that lasted long after her death.\n\nWilliam Dargan, born near Carlow in 1799, trained as a surveyor and engineer in England and worked under Thomas Telford on the Holyhead road. He won the contract for the Dublin and Kingstown Railway, which opened in 1834, and went on to build lines including the Dublin and Drogheda and the Ulster Railway, becoming the leading contractor in Ireland. He built the Ulster Canal and dredged the Victoria Channel at Belfast, which created Queen's Island, later the Harland and Wolff site, and so helped the city's harbour and shipbuilding to grow. His work provided employment for thousands of labourers and helped to open up the country to trade and travel.\n\nDargan was also a public figure who promoted Irish industry. In 1853 he financed the Dublin Industrial Exhibition on Leinster Lawn, which drew over a million visitors and was visited by Queen Victoria. He lost heavily on it, and his reputation as an Irish industrial pioneer was his main reward. The National Gallery of Ireland, founded in 1864, was established partly as his memorial. After a fall from a horse in 1866 and the failure of some of his ventures, his career ended, and he died in Dublin on 7 February 1867. Contemporaries praised him as a self-made Catholic businessman who acted from public spirit, and historians see him as a symbol of Irish enterprise, though his work depended on railway investment more than on a wider Irish industrial base.\n\nBoth made a real contribution. Aikenhead improved the care of the poor and sick and widened the role of women, while Dargan transformed transport and showed that Irish people could lead modern industry. Their work, in different fields, changed the way Irish people lived in the nineteenth century.",
