    "label": "1",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Land agitation and land reform transformed Irish rural society between 1870 and 1914, and the co-operative movement improved the economy of the small farmer. Together they ended landlordism, made most tenants into owners and raised the quality of Irish dairy farming, although poverty in the west and the position of landless labourers remained unsolved.\n\nThe starting point was a system in which a small, often Protestant and often absentee landlord class owned the land and most farmers were tenants at will with no security. Gladstone's Land Act of 1870 gave legal force to the Ulster Custom and offered compensation for disturbance and improvements, but it did not fix rents, so landlords could raise rents and then evict for non-payment. The agricultural depression from 1877, with bad harvests and cheap American grain, turned this into a crisis. Davitt's meeting at Irishtown in April 1879 began the movement, and the Land League of October 1879, with Parnell as president, mobilised farmers across the south and west.\n\nThe social impact of the Land War was dramatic. Boycotting, begun at Ennis in 1880 and named after Captain Boycott, gave tenants a weapon that did not need violence, and showed that a whole community acting together could defeat a landlord. The Ladies' Land League under Anna Parnell kept the campaign going when the men were jailed and helped evicted families, bringing women into public life. The Land League also drew farmers into national politics and united the land and Home Rule movements. The cost was moonlighting, coercion under Forster's Act of 1881, and the jailing of hundreds of local leaders.\n\nThe economic impact of the 1881 Land Act was great. It granted the Three Fs of fair rents, fixity of tenure and free sale, with land courts setting rents. The first judicial rents cut rents by about one fifth on average, and tenants could no longer be evicted so long as they paid. The Act created dual ownership, in which landlord and tenant shared the holding, which could not last. The Arrears Act of 1882 cleared arrears for tenants rated under £30. Purchase followed. Salisbury's Ashbourne Act of 1885 advanced the full price to tenants who bought from willing landlords, and Balfour's policy of constructive unionism added the Land Purchase Act of 1891 and the Congested Districts Board, which bought and divided estates in the poor west and promoted fisheries and crafts.\n\nThe decisive step was the Wyndham Act of 1903, which came out of the Land Conference of December 1902 where landlords and tenant leaders such as Lord Dunraven, William O'Brien and John Redmond agreed terms. Tenants received loans of the full price repayable over about 68 years at low interest, and landlords received a bonus of 12 per cent to encourage them to sell. More than 200,000 tenants bought within a few years. The Birrell Act of 1909 allowed compulsory purchase where landlords refused, and the Labourers Act of 1906 provided cottages for farm labourers, with tens of thousands built by 1914. By 1914 most tenants owned or were buying their holdings, and landlordism had ended as a social and political force, so that the Protestant landed ascendancy lost its hold on local power and a Catholic farming class replaced it.\n\nThe co-operative movement addressed a different problem, that Irish farmers marketed badly, depended on shopkeepers and moneylenders and were undercut by well-made Danish butter. Horace Plunkett, an Anglo-Irish landowner who had ranched in Wyoming, took the Danish model of farmer-owned creameries, where farmers pooled milk and used mechanical separators. The first co-operative creamery is usually dated to 1889 at Dromcollogher, Co. Limerick, and in 1894 he founded the Irish Agricultural Organisation Society to organise and inspect societies under the motto better farming, better business, better living. Credit societies on the German Raiffeisen model lent small sums on the guarantee of neighbours and reduced dependence on gombeen men, and the Irish Agricultural Wholesale Society followed in 1897. By the First World War there were close to 1,000 societies with roughly 100,000 members, strongest in Munster, and standards of Irish butter had risen.\n\nPlunkett also brought about a change in government policy. His Recess Committee of 1895 brought unionists and nationalists together and recommended a department for agriculture, which led to the Department of Agriculture and Technical Instruction in 1899, with Plunkett as its first vice-president, supporting instruction, research and technical education. The movement's limits were political and geographical. Nationalists suspected a unionist, Ulster farmers were slow to join, and the western poor were helped more by the Congested Districts Board. Plunkett lost his post in 1907 after hostility from the Irish Party.\n\nOn balance, the social and economic impact was large. Land reform changed who owned the land and gave farmers security, confidence and a political voice, while co-operation improved how they farmed and sold. Neither solved rural poverty in the west or the position of the labourers, but the two movements together created a more prosperous and independent tenant-owning farming class by 1914.",
