    "label": "1",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Both the negotiations and the terms of the Anglo-Irish Treaty of 1921 were controversial because the delegates signed under pressure and without referring back to Dublin, and because what they signed fell short of the republic that many nationalists had fought for. The way the Treaty was made divided the cabinet, and its terms divided the Dáil, the army and the country, and led to the Civil War.\n\nThe negotiations were controversial first because of who went to London. The Dáil cabinet chose Arthur Griffith, Michael Collins, Robert Barton, George Gavan Duffy and Éamonn Duggan as plenipotentiaries, with Erskine Childers as secretary, while de Valera stayed in Dublin, saying that as head of state he should be held in reserve and keep the extreme republicans on side. Critics, including Collins, argued that this was a mistake, because the man with most authority and experience of Lloyd George was absent and later disowned the result. The delegates were plenipotentiaries with full power to sign, but their written instructions said they should report to Dublin on the text of a treaty before signing, and the meaning of this was disputed afterwards. Collins himself had warned that he would be a poor negotiator and was reluctant to go.\n\nThe conduct of the talks in the final days caused the most bitterness. After the plenary sessions of 11 to 24 October produced no agreement, the bargaining was done in small private sub-conferences between Griffith and Collins and the British. On 2 December Griffith privately gave Lloyd George a written assurance that he would not let the talks break on Ulster provided a Boundary Commission was set up, which republicans saw as a concession made without the cabinet's knowledge. The cabinet in Dublin on 3 December told the delegates not to accept the oath as drafted. On 5 December Lloyd George told them that there would be war unless the Treaty was signed that night, and Barton later recalled a warning of immediate and terrible war. Whether this was a bluff is still debated. Collins and Griffith argued that the alternative was war, Barton gave way, and the Treaty was signed at about 2.10 am on 6 December without the final text being sent to Dublin. Anti-Treaty opinion said the delegates had been bullied, and de Valera was angry that he had not been told. Collins said he had signed his own death warrant.\n\nThe terms were just as controversial. Article 1 gave the Irish Free State the same status as Canada, Australia, New Zealand and South Africa, so Ireland was a dominion within the Empire rather than a republic. The King was represented by a Governor-General, and members of the Free State parliament had to swear to be faithful to the Constitution and to the King in virtue of the common citizenship of Ireland with Great Britain. For republicans who had sworn loyalty to the Republic declared in 1919, this was the central betrayal. Britain also kept the use of the ports of Berehaven, Queenstown and Lough Swilly, and the Free State took on a share of the British public debt, subject to counter-claims. The Irish had hoped for external association, with the Crown recognised only as head of an association with the Commonwealth, and the Treaty went well beyond that.\n\nPartition was another source of anger. Northern Ireland could opt out of the Free State within one month, and a Boundary Commission would then settle the border in line with the wishes of the inhabitants as far as economic and geographic conditions allowed. Collins and Griffith expected that Tyrone, Fermanagh and parts of Armagh and Down would be transferred and that the North could not then survive, but opponents said that Irish unity was being given away. In fact the Commission's expected findings were leaked in November 1925 and the Tripartite Agreement of 3 December 1925 left the border unchanged, so the hope proved unrealistic.\n\nThe debate in the Dáil showed how deep the division was. The cabinet divided four to three on 8 December, with de Valera, Brugha and Stack against, and de Valera put forward his own Document No. 2, based on external association, which was never presented to the British. In the public debate from 19 December to 10 January, Collins described the Treaty as giving the freedom to achieve freedom, while opponents said that the oath and the Crown betrayed the republic, and all six women deputies voted against it. The Dáil approved it on 7 January 1922 by 64 votes to 57, de Valera resigned as President, and the IRA split in March 1922, leading to civil war in June.\n\nThe Treaty was therefore controversial on both counts. The negotiations were disputed because of the absence of de Valera, the plenipotentiary instructions and the threat of war, and the terms because of the Crown, the oath, the ports and partition. Supporters, including most newspapers, the bishops and business interests, argued that it was the best available and gave the means to build the state, and the pro-Treaty side won about three-quarters of the vote in June 1922, but the manner of its making left a bitterness that lasted for decades.",
