    "label": "2",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The Eucharistic Congress, held in Dublin from 22 to 26 June 1932, was an international Catholic gathering that combined five days of Masses and processions with a vast open-air Mass in the Phoenix Park. It contributed to Irish identity by presenting Ireland as a devout Catholic nation with a long link to St Patrick, and as a stable, independent state only ten years after the Civil War, although it also sharpened the religious division between North and South.\n\nThe Congress was the 31st in a series that had begun in 1881, and Dublin was chosen for 1932 because that year marked the 1,500th anniversary of the traditional date of St Patrick's arrival in Ireland in 432. The planning began under Cosgrave's government, but the event took place under Eamon de Valera, who became head of government on 9 March 1932 and gave it his full support. Archbishop Edward Byrne of Dublin headed the organising effort, and the Pope sent a legate, Cardinal Lorenzo Lauri. Dublin was dressed with altars, flags and illuminations, and even poorer districts were decorated. A great altar was built in the Phoenix Park, a radio station was set up at Athlone, and pilgrims arrived from the United States, Britain, Europe and Northern Ireland. The scale of the preparations showed how seriously Church and state took the occasion.\n\nThe main events unfolded in a carefully staged sequence. Cardinal Lauri was received at Dún Laoghaire on 21 June by de Valera and Archbishop Byrne, and some 36,000 schoolchildren lined the route to the Pro-Cathedral, where the Congress opened the next day. On 25 June about 100,000 children attended a Mass in the Phoenix Park. The climax came on Sunday 26 June, when the closing Mass was celebrated before a crowd often put at about a million, though that figure may be exaggerated. The tenor John McCormack sang Panis Angelicus, and a message from Pope Pius XI was broadcast live by radio from the Vatican. Afterwards four processions made their way from the park to O'Connell Bridge, where Cardinal Lauri gave the closing benediction to a crowd of up to half a million people.\n\nThe Congress shaped Irish identity first by displaying the alliance between the Catholic hierarchy and the political leadership. De Valera, known for his personal piety, stood alongside the archbishop and the papal legate, and the state treated the event as a national occasion. This closeness anticipated the influence of the Church on the 1937 constitution and on social legislation. It also fitted the Gaelic and Catholic ideal of the 1930s, in which being Irish was increasingly identified with being Catholic. The vast majority of the population was Catholic, and the crowds in Dublin showed that the Church had the loyalty of ordinary people.\n\nThe Congress also expressed the sovereignty of the new state. The Governor-General, James McNeill, who represented the Crown, was not invited to the reception for the legate at Dublin Castle, and he replied by holding his own dinner to which de Valera and his ministers were not invited. McNeill complained of discourtesy to de Valera on 7 July 1932, and the quarrel was part of the conflict that led to his dismissal in October 1932. De Valera used the occasion to present his government as Catholic and independent of the Crown at once, which strengthened a vision of Ireland as both a Catholic and a sovereign nation.\n\nInternationally, the Congress gave the Free State a confident image. Special trains, buses and sailings carried the crowds, the Garda and the Army managed them, and loudspeakers and radio let huge numbers hear the ceremonies. Foreign newspapers reported the event, and it passed off without major disorder, a strong demonstration of public order a decade after the Civil War. The Athlone station set up for the Congress later became Radio Éireann in 1938. De Valera's prestige rose at home and abroad, even though critics in Cumann na nGaedheal noted that the Congress had been planned by Cosgrave's government.\n\nIn the North the Congress was seen very differently. Catholic pilgrims travelling to and from Dublin were attacked, with trains stoned at Portadown, Lisburn and Belfast, and at Ballymena a special train was attacked and some pilgrims were injured. Unionists regarded the Congress as proof that the Free State was a Catholic state, which confirmed their belief that partition was necessary, while Northern nationalists took pride in it and felt part of an Irish Catholic nation. Irish identity was therefore strengthened for one community and made more distant for the other.\n\nOverall the Congress was a great public success whose effect was largely symbolic. It changed no laws or institutions, but it showed that the state could organise a major international event, united people across party lines, and presented Ireland as devout, stable and independent. Historians see it as the high point of the Church's public influence and a sign of the conformity of the 1930s, and its cost was a deeper distrust between North and South and between Catholic and Protestant on the island.",
