    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The Second World War, known in the South as the Emergency, affected both parts of Ireland profoundly but in different ways. In the South the impact was political and economic, as neutrality was maintained at the cost of shortages, censorship and strict state control. In Northern Ireland, which was at war from 3 September 1939, the impact was industrial and physical, bringing full employment and the Belfast Blitz, but little change to the political order.\n\nIn the South the government declared a state of emergency on 2 September 1939 and chose neutrality as the proof of independent statehood. A state that followed Britain into war would have looked like a dominion in practice, partition made an alliance with Britain impossible while Britain held the North, and the return of the Treaty ports in 1938 made neutrality possible. The army was small and poorly equipped, and the divisions of the Civil War made joining the war risky. Neutrality had wide support from Fianna Fáil, Fine Gael and Labour, though James Dillon argued for entering on the Allied side. In June 1940 Malcolm MacDonald offered de Valera a British declaration for a united Ireland in principle if Ireland joined the Allies, and de Valera rejected it in July because he doubted that Britain could deliver.\n\nThe state took wide powers to defend that policy. The Emergency Powers Act of 3 September 1939 allowed ministers to make orders with the force of law over prices, supplies, movement and censorship. Frank Aiken's department censored the press, radio and letters, weather forecasts were banned and criticism of neutrality was suppressed. The army grew from under 20,000 to about 40,000 by 1941, and the Local Defence Force was founded in June 1940 after the fall of France. The IRA, banned in 1936, raided the Magazine Fort in the Phoenix Park in December 1939, and the 1940 Offences Against the State (Amendment) Act brought internment without trial, with hundreds held at the Curragh.\n\nThe economic impact was severe. Seán Lemass at the Department of Supplies controlled imports and prices and rationed tea, sugar, butter, flour, bread and fuel. Private motoring almost stopped, compulsory tillage increased the wheat harvest, coal imports collapsed and turf was cut on a huge scale. Irish Shipping Ltd was set up in 1941 to carry essential cargoes, and its seamen died when ships were sunk despite neutral markings. The Wages Standstill Order of 1941 froze wages while prices rose, so real incomes fell. Many workers emigrated to Britain, and the poor suffered most. Neutrality also brought pressure from outside, as Churchill complained about the ports, the American Minister David Gray criticised the policy, and in February 1944 the Allies' American Note asked for the expulsion of Axis diplomats, which de Valera refused.\n\nNeutrality did not mean the South was untouched. German bombs fell on Campile in Wexford in August 1940, and on the North Strand in Dublin on 31 May 1941, killing about thirty people. In practice neutrality leaned towards the Allies, with G2 sharing intelligence with Britain, Allied airmen released, and weather reports from Blacksod Point in Mayo contributing to the decision on the date of D-Day. Tens of thousands of southerners served in the British forces. De Valera's condolence visit to the German minister after Hitler's death brought Churchill's attack in May 1945, and de Valera's reply on 16 May was widely praised at home.\n\nNorthern Ireland experienced the war as part of the United Kingdom. Unionists contrasted their loyalty with southern neutrality, which strengthened their case against unity. Conscription was not extended in the North, as the idea was dropped in 1941 after opposition from nationalists, the Catholic bishops and the Dublin government. Industry boomed, since Harland and Wolff built and repaired ships, Short Brothers made Stirling bombers, and linen and rope works made uniforms and parachutes. Unemployment fell sharply after years of mass unemployment, women entered the factories, and farmers supplied food to Britain.\n\nThe Belfast Blitz was the great shock. The government had assumed the city was out of range, so it had few shelters, few anti-aircraft guns and small fire services. The raid of 15 to 16 April 1941, Easter Tuesday, by about 180 bombers killed around 900 people, and the raid of 4 to 5 May hit the shipyards, with about 1,000 killed in all. Tens of thousands were left homeless and up to 100,000 people a night left the city as ditchers. Fire brigades from Dublin, Dún Laoghaire, Dundalk and Drogheda crossed the border with de Valera's approval. The poverty revealed among evacuees fuelled criticism of the Unionist government, and Andrews resigned in April 1943, replaced by Basil Brooke.\n\nFrom January 1942 American troops arrived, numbering well over 100,000 by 1944, and Northern Ireland became a base for the Atlantic convoys and D-Day. The war also raised expectations of welfare, and the Education Act of 1947 and the Health Services Act of 1948 came from that climate. Yet the Unionist Party kept power, sectarian divisions remained and the electoral system was unchanged.\n\nOverall, the war strengthened the South's sense of sovereignty at the price of austerity and restricted freedom, and it tied the North more closely to Britain while deepening the border between the two states. The South gained independence and unity at home, the North gained prosperity and welfare, but neither side's political order changed, and Ireland ended the war more divided than before.",
