    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Irish-American identity developed in the United States out of hardship, hostility and community building. Catholic emigrants who arrived poor and unwelcome created a distinct identity around their church, their politics and their attachment to Ireland, while the Ulster Protestant descendants known as Scotch-Irish developed a separate identity built on Presbyterian faith and Ulster ancestry. In both cases identity was shaped by contrast with others, and by the need to find a place in American society.\n\nHostility was the first shaping force. Most Irish emigrants were Catholic, poor and crowded into city slums, and Protestant Americans blamed them for disease, drink and crime and feared that their votes would be directed by the Pope. Nativism reached its height in the 1840s and 1850s. The Philadelphia riots of 1844 saw Irish Catholic churches and homes attacked, and the Know-Nothings, or American Party, won major state victories in 1854, including Massachusetts, and wanted longer waiting periods for citizenship and the exclusion of Catholics from office. Cartoonists such as Thomas Nast in the 1870s drew Irish Catholics as ape-like and corrupt. The result was that the Irish responded by closing ranks and building their own parishes, schools, newspapers and societies.\n\nThe Catholic Church was the centre of that identity. Irish-born and Irish-American clergy dominated the American hierarchy: John Hughes, born in Tyrone, became archbishop of New York in 1850, and John McCloskey became the first American cardinal in 1875. St Patrick's Cathedral in New York was dedicated in 1879. After the Third Plenary Council of Baltimore in 1884 every parish was required to run a school, and orders such as the Sisters of Mercy and the Christian Brothers staffed them. Societies such as the Ancient Order of Hibernians and the Knights of Columbus, founded in 1882 by Father Michael McGivney, gave insurance and social life, so that the parish became a social centre as well as a church. The St Patrick's Day parade in New York, dating from 1762, was a public display of this identity.\n\nPolitics was another strand. Most Irish Americans voted Democratic, since the party defended immigrants, and ward politics traded jobs and help for votes. Tammany Hall in New York was led by Irish bosses from 1872, and Richard Croker, born in Cork in 1843, ran it from the 1880s until 1902. William R. Grace became the first Irish Catholic mayor of New York in 1880 and Hugh O'Brien the first in Boston in 1885, followed by John F. Fitzgerald and James Michael Curley, and Al Smith was the Democratic presidential candidate in 1928. Irish men also filled police and fire services. This political success gave the community pride and a voice, though machine politics relied on patronage and corruption, and some historians argue it kept communities separate.\n\nAttachment to Ireland gave Irish America its hyphen. The Fenian Brotherhood was founded in New York in 1858 and Clan na Gael in 1867, and American money paid for the preparations for 1867. John Devoy, who arrived in 1871 after prison, organised the Catalpa rescue of 1876 and founded the Gaelic American in 1903. Parnell toured America in 1880 raising funds for the Land League, and Patrick Ford's Irish World sent large sums, while American money helped the 1916 Rising. After 1916 Irish-American opinion moved strongly behind the Republic. The GAA added a cultural side, with clubs in New York, Chicago and Boston and Gaelic Park in the Bronx opening in 1926.\n\nUlster-Scottish identity developed differently. Presbyterians from Ulster had settled in Pennsylvania and the Appalachians in the eighteenth century, and their descendants called themselves Scotch-Irish, a term widely used from the nineteenth century to distinguish them from the Catholic Irish, especially after the Famine. They stressed Protestant descent, loyalty to the Crown and a separate heritage, and a Scotch-Irish Society of America held its first congress in 1889. Andrew Jackson and Woodrow Wilson were claimed among their number. The Orange Order carried the rivalry to America, and the Orange parades in New York in 1870 and 1871 led to riots with Irish Catholics in which dozens died. Many Protestant emigrants assimilated quickly into American denominations, so their sense of Irishness faded sooner.\n\nIn conclusion, Irish-American identity was forged by discrimination and sustained by church, politics, nationalism and sport, and it grew stronger as the community gained power. The Ulster-Scots identity was more Protestant and more quickly absorbed into mainstream America. The two identities developed in opposition to one another as much as in reaction to American society, and the rivalry was carried from Ireland across the Atlantic.",
