    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Between 1920 and 1966 the fortunes of Irish missionaries were mixed but, in Africa especially, largely successful. Irish missionary societies expanded rapidly, supported by a vast supply of vocations and a strong sense that Ireland had a spiritual empire. Missions in China ended in expulsion after the Communist victory, whereas those in Nigeria grew into a network of schools, hospitals and parishes that lasted beyond independence, though the work also drew criticism for paternalism and cultural influence.\n\nThe background to this success was the strength of the Catholic Church at home. After the Famine, Archbishop Cullen's reforms, which Emmet Larkin called the devotional revolution, produced a surplus of priests, brothers and nuns, and seminaries such as Maynooth trained more than Ireland needed. The phrase Ireland's spiritual empire described the reach of Irish missionaries across Africa and Asia, and a country with no empire of its own could present itself as a leader in spreading the faith without the taint of colonialism. Parishes raised money through collections, missionary magazines and mission exhibitions, and Edel Quinn, a Legion of Mary envoy in East Africa who died in Nairobi in 1944, became a lay symbol of dedication.\n\nNew societies were founded in this period. The Maynooth Mission to China, founded in 1916, became the Missionary Society of St Columban with its headquarters at Dalgan Park, Navan, from 1918, and the first missionaries went to China in 1920. Bishop Edward Galvin of Cork led the mission in Hanyang, in central China, and became a bishop in 1927. The Missionary Society of St Patrick was founded at Kiltegan, Co. Wicklow, in 1932 by Father Patrick Whitney and sent priests to Nigeria and later Kenya. Joseph Shanahan founded the Holy Rosary Sisters in 1924, and Mary Martin, a Dubliner trained as a nurse and midwife, founded the Medical Missionaries of Mary in Nigeria in 1937 after a Vatican instruction of 1936 allowed sisters to practise obstetrics and surgery, with its mother house at Drogheda. Irish women thus gained a leadership role in nursing, teaching and medicine that was hard to find at home.\n\nChina brought the greatest reversal. Galvin's mission ran churches, schools, orphanages and clinics and trained Chinese catechists and priests, though few Chinese priests were ordained before the 1940s, and it survived civil conflict and the Japanese invasion, in which Hanyang was bombed. After the Communist takeover of 1949 the missionaries faced restrictions, and Galvin was placed under house arrest, tried and expelled in 1952, dying at Dalgan Park in 1956. Communist authorities treated the missionaries as agents of foreign influence, while Irish Catholics saw them as heroes of the faith. The Columbans went on working in Korea, Japan, the Philippines and Latin America.\n\nThe Holy Ghost mission to Nigeria shows the missions at their most successful. The Spiritans had begun at Onitsha in 1885, and Shanahan, born in Tipperary in 1871, arrived in 1902 and made the school the main instrument of conversion, with village schools staffed by catechists. After 1945 the mission opened new parishes, schools, hospitals and training colleges across south-eastern Nigeria. The archdiocese of Onitsha was created in 1950 with Charles Heerey as archbishop, and Mission schools became the main route to education in the region, with teacher training colleges creating an educated class, while hospitals run by the Holy Rosary Sisters and the Medical Missionaries of Mary served rural areas where government services were few. Local vocations also grew: Michael Tansi, an Igbo, was ordained in 1937, and in 1965 Francis Arinze, aged 32, became auxiliary bishop of Onitsha.\n\nThere were also limits. The mission depended on colonial grants-in-aid for its schools, which tied it to government requirements, and critics said it promoted Christian and Western values at the expense of local culture, and that Irish missionaries held leadership long after Nigerians were qualified. Rivalry with Methodist and Anglican missions over schools divided communities. Nigerian independence in 1960 forced adaptation, and leadership passed increasingly to Nigerians, a process called localisation. The Biafran war of 1967 to 1970, just after the period, damaged and closed many mission stations, though it also moved Irish people to action and led to the founding of Concern in 1968.\n\nOverall, Irish missionaries rose from small foundations in 1916 to a worldwide presence by the 1960s. China ended in expulsion, but in Nigeria the missions built schools, hospitals and a local church with African leadership that outlasted colonial rule, though they were open to criticism for paternalism, and the decline in vocations after the 1960s ended the era of large-scale Irish mission work.",
