    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The Sunningdale Agreement was a settlement reached in December 1973 between the British and Irish governments and the pro-power-sharing Northern Ireland parties, setting up a Council of Ireland and underpinning a power-sharing executive. That executive, which took office on 1 January 1974, collapsed on 28 May 1974 because most unionists rejected the Council of Ireland and power sharing, because a loyalist general strike paralysed the province, and because the British government would not confront the strikers.\n\nThe background was the suspension of Stormont and the search for a new settlement. A border poll on 8 March 1973 produced a 98.9 per cent vote to stay in the United Kingdom, but turnout was only about 59 per cent because nationalists boycotted it. The White Paper of March 1973 proposed an Assembly elected by proportional representation, an executive in which unionists and nationalists shared power, and a Council of Ireland. The Assembly election of 28 June 1973, using the single transferable vote, returned unionists, nationalists and Alliance members, but the Ulster Unionist Party was split between Brian Faulkner's supporters and those who opposed power sharing, including Paisley's Democratic Unionists and Craig's Vanguard.\n\nThe Sunningdale conference was held at Sunningdale Park in Berkshire from 6 to 9 December 1973, attended by Edward Heath, the Taoiseach Liam Cosgrave, and the Northern Ireland parties of Faulkner's Unionists, the SDLP under Gerry Fitt and John Hume, and Alliance. The agreement set up a Council of Ireland with a council of ministers, seven from each side, and a consultative assembly of 60 members drawn equally from the Dáil and the Northern Ireland Assembly. The Irish government accepted that Northern Ireland's status would not change without the consent of a majority there, while Britain stated it would support a united Ireland if a majority wished it. A Law Enforcement Commission was to examine fugitive offenders and a possible all-Ireland court, and both governments promised policing acceptable to both communities. The executive that took office on 1 January 1974 had Faulkner as Chief Executive and Fitt as Deputy, with Hume as Minister of Commerce and Oliver Napier of Alliance as Head of Legal Affairs.\n\nThe first and deepest cause of collapse was unionist rejection of the Council of Ireland. Many unionists saw it as a step towards a united Ireland, and their fears were reinforced because the Irish government did not formally change Articles 2 and 3 at Sunningdale. The Boland case, in which Kevin Boland challenged the Irish declaration on status and the government defended it as not altering the constitutional claim, badly damaged Faulkner. On 4 January 1974 the Ulster Unionist Council voted 427 to 374 to reject the Council of Ireland, and Faulkner resigned as party leader three days later. He now led an executive without the backing of most of his own community.\n\nThe second cause was the organised opposition that formed around that rejection. The United Ulster Unionist Council, formed in January 1974, won eleven of the twelve Northern Ireland seats in the Westminster election of 28 February 1974, giving opponents a powerful claim that the majority had voted against the agreement. The Assembly itself voted 44 to 28 on 14 May to support the policy, but this only showed the gap between the Assembly and unionist opinion. The Ulster Workers' Council, led by Harry Murray and backed by loyalist paramilitaries, began a general strike on 15 May 1974. It cut electricity supplies, used roadblocks and intimidation, and drew on workers in power generation and essential industries to paralyse the province.\n\nThe third cause was the weakness of the response from London. Merlyn Rees, the Secretary of State in Wilson's new Labour government, and the army did not confront the strikers directly, and the executive was unable to govern. Wilson's televised broadcast of 25 May, calling the strikers and their backers 'sponging' on British democracy, angered unionists rather than frightening them. The Dublin and Monaghan bombings of 17 May, which killed 33 people in the Republic, added to the fear and confusion. Republican violence throughout the executive's term also made moderate unionists more fearful about security and nationalist intentions.\n\nFinally, Faulkner resigned as Chief Executive on 28 May 1974 after the SDLP and Alliance colleagues rejected his proposal to delay the Council of Ireland's later stages, and the executive fell after only five months. Sunningdale collapsed chiefly because it lacked the consent of a majority of unionists and was undone by a strike that London would not break. Yet it was not wasted, since it set out principles of power sharing, consent and an Irish dimension that later settlements built on, and Seamus Mallon's later remark that the 1998 agreement was 'Sunningdale for slow learners' reflects that.",
