    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "I would argue that membership of the EEC affected the Republic of Ireland far more than membership of the United Nations. The UN gave Ireland recognition, a voice and a proud peacekeeping tradition, but the EEC reshaped the economy, farming, industry and the law, and reduced Ireland's dependence on Britain. Both mattered, but in different ways.\n\nIreland joined the UN on 14 December 1955, after the Soviet Union's veto had blocked its application in 1946 and a package deal allowed sixteen states in together. Membership gave a small state a voice in world affairs and a way of showing independence of Britain. Frank Aiken, Minister for External Affairs for most of the years from 1957 to 1969, pursued an independent line. From 1957 Ireland would not support the American-led move to block debate on the seat of China, and in 1959 it sponsored a resolution criticising Chinese suppression of human rights in Tibet. Aiken's main achievement was on nuclear weapons: Irish resolutions from 1958 led to Resolution 1665 of December 1961, which led to the Non-Proliferation Treaty of 1968, and Ireland was the first state to sign it. In 1960 Frederick Boland was elected President of the General Assembly, an honour for a country that had joined only five years earlier.\n\nThe UN's most lasting effect was in peacekeeping. Irish troops served in the Congo from 1960, and nine soldiers were killed in an ambush at Niemba in November 1960. In September 1961 A Company of the 35th Battalion under Commandant Pat Quinlan was besieged at Jadotville and surrendered after five days. About 6,000 Irish soldiers served in the Congo until 1964, and twenty-six died. Troops also served in Cyprus from 1964 and Lebanon from 1978. This service linked neutrality to practical action, gave the Defence Forces a respected role, and became a source of national pride. However, it touched the lives of relatively few people directly and had little effect on the economy or ordinary living standards.\n\nThe EEC, by contrast, touched almost everyone. Ireland applied on 31 July 1961 because Britain was the market for most of its exports, but de Gaulle's vetoes of 1963 and 1967 delayed entry until 1 January 1973, after the Treaty of Accession in January 1972 and a referendum in May 1972 in which about 83 per cent voted yes. Farmers gained most. The Common Agricultural Policy guaranteed prices for beef, milk and cereals, farm incomes rose sharply in the early years, and land values and investment rose. The gains were uneven, since small western farms benefited less, and the guarantees encouraged overproduction, which led to milk quotas in 1984.\n\nMembership also changed industry and society. Foreign firms, including American ones, set up in Ireland to sell into the EEC, drawn by IDA grants and low taxes, and electronics, pharmaceuticals and chemicals became leading sectors. Manufactured goods overtook farm produce among exports, and trade with the continent grew while the share going to Britain fell. The cost was that protected industries such as clothing, textiles and footwear lost their tariffs and many firms closed. Regional and Social Funds paid for roads, water schemes and training, and EEC directives on equal pay in 1975 and equal treatment in 1976 forced changes in Irish law, including the end of the marriage bar in the public service in 1973. Irish citizens could live and work in other member states, and Ireland joined the European Monetary System in 1979, breaking the link with sterling. The Crotty case of 1987 showed that membership raised constitutional questions, since the Supreme Court held that ratifying the Single European Act needed a referendum.\n\nIn conclusion, the UN shaped Ireland's self-image and foreign policy and gave it a respected place abroad, but the EEC had the deeper impact on its economy, law and society. Critics worried about sovereignty and neutrality, yet the size of the yes vote in 1972 showed how strongly people linked membership to hopes of prosperity, and historians generally judge that the EEC mattered more.",
