    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "City planning developed greatly between 1815 and 1871 because rapid urban growth created problems of overcrowding, disease, traffic and disorder that cities could no longer leave to chance. The most dramatic example was Haussmann's rebuilding of Paris from 1853, which became a model for other cities, but planning also developed in Vienna, Barcelona, Berlin and Britain, where sanitary reform was the main driver.\n\nThe need for planning was clear. Industrial growth brought millions into cities whose streets, water supplies and housing could not cope. Narrow medieval streets blocked traffic, unsanitary districts bred disease, and cholera struck British cities in 1831-32 and 1848-49 and Paris in 1832 and 1849. Governments also feared revolution, since barricades were easily built in narrow streets, as the risings of 1830, 1848 and the June Days had shown. Planning was also a way of displaying the prestige of the state. In Britain the first response was sanitary reform, led by Edwin Chadwick, whose report of 1842 linked poor living conditions to disease and led to the Public Health Act of 1848, with its General Board of Health and powers over drainage and water supply.\n\nParis before 1853 had about a million people, divided between the wealthy west and the crowded working-class east and north. The centre around the Île de la Cité and Les Halles was a maze of narrow streets with poor drainage. Napoleon III, who had admired London's parks and squares during his exile, wanted to improve health, create employment through public works and make the city easier to control. In June 1853 he appointed Baron Haussmann Prefect of the Seine, a post he held for about seventeen years. Haussmann had strong legal power, since the expropriation law of 1841, extended by a decree of 1852, allowed the state to buy property compulsorily and clear whole blocks.\n\nThe grands travaux cut broad straight boulevards through the old city. The Rue de Rivoli was extended and the Boulevard de Sébastopol was cut north to south, linking the Seine with the railway stations. The Boulevards Saint-Germain and Saint-Michel crossed the Latin Quarter, and the Étoile became the centre of twelve radiating avenues. Building regulations fixed the height of facades, giving Paris its uniform look. The boulevards eased traffic, opened vistas and made it easier for troops to move and for artillery to fire along the straight streets. New buildings such as Garnier's Opéra and Baltard's iron-and-glass pavilions at Les Halles, and rebuilt railway stations, completed the transformation.\n\nThe most lasting achievements were in public health and green space. Eugène Belgrand built new aqueducts bringing clean water from the Dhuis and Vanne valleys, with covered reservoirs, and an underground sewer network that carried waste away and reduced disease. Jean-Charles Alphand redesigned the Bois de Boulogne on the model of London's parks, laid out the Bois de Vincennes to give the east a counterpart, and created the Buttes-Chaumont, a former quarry and rubbish tip, which opened in 1867. The annexation of 1860 raised the arrondissements from twelve to twenty and let Haussmann plan the whole urban area.\n\nPlanning developed elsewhere too. Vienna's Ringstrasse, decreed in 1857, replaced the old city walls with a grand boulevard lined with public buildings, and its plots were sold to private developers who financed much of the building. In Barcelona Ildefons Cerdà's plan of 1859 laid out the Eixample as a regular grid of blocks. Berlin's Hobrecht Plan of 1862 organised the city's expansion, and in the 1870s Joseph Chamberlain, mayor of Birmingham, cleared slums and built Corporation Street. Haussmann's work was distinctive because a centralised authority carried it out with full powers over property.\n\nHaussmann's achievements brought real costs. Demolition displaced working-class families to the outer districts of the east and north, rents rose in the centre, and the division between west and east sharpened, an effect Zola satirised in La Curée. The works were financed by heavy municipal borrowing, and Jules Ferry attacked the hidden accounts in 1868, while Haussmann was dismissed in January 1870. Overall, city planning in this period solved many physical problems and made Paris a model for modern cities, but at the expense of the poor and in the interests of the regime and the wealthy.",
