    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Karl Marx and Charles Darwin were two of the most influential thinkers of the nineteenth century, and both changed how Europeans understood their world. Marx offered a new theory of society, history and class struggle that inspired the socialist movement, while Darwin's theory of evolution by natural selection transformed biology and challenged religious belief. Their importance lay in their ideas, which became fully influential after the period itself.\n\nKarl Marx, born in Trier in 1818, studied philosophy and in 1842 became editor of the Rheinische Zeitung in Cologne. He was expelled from Paris in 1845 and later settled in London in 1849, where he lived until his death in 1883. His importance began with the Communist Manifesto, which he wrote with Friedrich Engels and which was published in London in February 1848. It declared that all history was a history of class struggles and ended with a call for working men of all countries to unite. The Communist League, formed in London in 1847 from a society of German exiles, had commissioned its programme. In 1848 Marx took part in the revolutions as editor of the Neue Rheinische Zeitung in Cologne, but it was closed in 1849 when he was expelled from Prussia.\n\nMarx's lasting importance lay in his theory. His historical materialism held that economic relations and class struggle drive historical change, and that capitalism would be overthrown by the working class. The first volume of Das Kapital appeared in 1867 and analysed how capitalists profit from the surplus value produced by labour. He also helped to found the International Workingmen's Association in London in 1864, wrote its inaugural address and dominated its General Council, which gave socialism an international organisation. Contemporaries in the 1850s and 1860s saw him as a dangerous exile, and governments kept him under surveillance. His followers became a mass movement only after his death. His predictions of revolution in advanced capitalist countries were not fulfilled, but his analysis of capitalism influenced later economics and politics, and Engels financed him and edited the later volumes of Das Kapital.\n\nCharles Darwin, born in Shrewsbury in 1809, studied medicine at Edinburgh and divinity at Cambridge before joining HMS Beagle as naturalist. On the voyage of 1831 to 1836 he collected fossils, plants and animals in South America, the Galápagos Islands and Australia, and observed the variation of species. He developed his theory of natural selection in the years after his return, delayed publication for about twenty years, and published On the Origin of Species in November 1859, after Alfred Russel Wallace had reached the same idea. The Origin argued that species change over time through variation and natural selection, and that all living things share common ancestors. In 1871 The Descent of Man applied the theory to human origins.\n\nDarwin's ideas provoked fierce debate because they challenged the religious account of creation. Scientists such as Thomas Henry Huxley defended him, while Bishop Samuel Wilberforce and many churchmen opposed him at the Oxford debate of 1860, at which Darwin was not present. Their significance was enormous. Historians now regard Darwin as the founder of modern evolutionary biology, though his theory was later developed with genetics, which explained inheritance. He was buried in Westminster Abbey in 1882, a sign of the esteem in which he came to be held.\n\nIn judging their importance, both men were products of an age of rapid industrial and scientific change, and both challenged accepted authority, Marx the economic order and Darwin the religious understanding of nature. Darwin's work was accepted by the scientific community within his lifetime, while Marx's influence on politics grew mainly after his death. Both therefore had a lasting importance that reached far beyond their own generation, and each changed the way Europeans thought about human society and the natural world.",
