    "marks": 100,
    "model": "German industrialisation between 1871 and 1914 was of great importance, because it turned a newly united country into the leading industrial economy of continental Europe and the second industrial power in the world after the USA. It also brought deep social change and tension, and the invention of the motor car in Germany in the 1880s was part of the same story, though its larger social effects came after 1914.\n\nGermany industrialised quickly for several reasons. Its population grew from about 41 million in 1871 to about 67 million in 1914, giving labour and a large home market. The Ruhr coalfield was the heart of heavy industry, with iron ore in Lorraine, taken from France in 1871. Unification created a single market with a common currency, the mark (1873), and a central bank, the Reichsbank. Railways grew from about 21,000 km in 1871 to over 60,000 km by 1913, universal banks such as Deutsche Bank lent to industry for long periods, and technical universities trained engineers and chemists. By 1913 Germany produced more than twice as much steel as Britain, and hard coal output had risen from about 30 million to about 190 million tonnes.\n\nGermany also led the second industrial revolution of steel, chemicals and electricity. The basic Thomas-Gilchrist process of 1878 allowed the phosphoric ores of Lorraine to be turned into steel. BASF, Bayer and Hoechst led the world in dyes and pharmaceuticals, and Fritz Haber and Carl Bosch developed ammonia synthesis between 1909 and 1913. Siemens and AEG dominated electrical engineering, and cartels such as the Rhenish-Westphalian Coal Syndicate (1893) fixed prices and output. This success gave the Reich economic power and pride, and helped to fund Wilhelm II's army and navy. The Krupp works at Essen show the link: Alfred Krupp won attention with a flawless steel ingot at the Great Exhibition in 1851 and earned the name the Cannon King for his breech-loading guns, and under Bertha Krupp, who inherited the firm in 1902, the firm made the 'Big Bertha' howitzer used against Liege and Namur in 1914.\n\nIndustrial growth transformed society. By 1910 about 60 per cent of Germans lived in towns, up from about a third in 1871, and Berlin grew from under a million to about two million. Working-class families lived in overcrowded tenements, and factory work meant long hours and strict discipline, although real wages rose slowly in the 1890s and 1900s. Krupp built housing, schools and pension funds for its workers, but this paternalism expected loyalty and discouraged political activity. The Ruhr miners struck in 1889 with about 90,000 workers and again in 1905. Workers turned to the Social Democratic Party, formed at Gotha in 1875 and banned by the Anti-Socialist Law of 1878 until 1890. In 1912 it won about 4.25 million votes and became the largest party in the Reichstag, while the free trade unions had about 2.5 million members by 1913.\n\nThe state tried to answer the social question with welfare and repression. Bismarck's insurance laws created sickness insurance in 1883, accident insurance in 1884 and pensions in 1889, partly to wean workers from the SPD. Industry and the Junkers formed an alliance of 'iron and rye' behind the tariff of 1879, which raised food prices and angered workers. Workers had little political power in Prussia, where the three-class vote lasted until 1918. Historians divide on the result: Wehler argued that the dominance of the old elite blocked democratic reform, while Eley and Blackbourn replied that Germany was a confident modern society.\n\nThe motor car was also invented in Germany in these years. Nikolaus Otto built the first practical four-stroke engine in 1876, and Gottlieb Daimler and Wilhelm Maybach developed a small high-speed petrol engine in the 1880s, fitted to a carriage in 1886. Karl Benz founded Benz and Cie in Mannheim in 1883 and applied in January 1886 for a patent for his Motorwagen, regarded as the first practical car designed as a whole. In August 1888 Bertha Benz drove one from Mannheim to Pforzheim, about 106 km, which proved its usefulness and won publicity. France then led early production with Panhard et Levassor and Peugeot. Before 1914, however, cars were expensive goods for the rich, and it was Henry Ford's Model T of 1908 and moving assembly line of 1913 that made them a mass product in the USA.\n\nGerman industrialisation was therefore of major importance. It gave the country economic strength, new industries and inventions such as the car, but it also bound heavy industry to the army and the naval race, intensified class conflict, and left a modern economy under a political system that gave workers little power.",
