    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "War and revolution transformed Russia between 1900 and 1920. Defeat in the Russo-Japanese War sparked the revolution of 1905 and exposed the weakness of the autocracy, and the First World War then destroyed it in 1917, bringing first the Provisional Government and then Bolshevik rule. By 1920 Russia had lost territory, endured civil war and become a one-party state under Lenin.\n\nThe Russo-Japanese War of 1904-05 had the first major impact. Russia and Japan were rivals in Manchuria and Korea, and the interior minister Plehve is reported to have wanted a short victorious war to head off revolution. It did the opposite. Port Arthur surrendered in January 1905, the army was beaten at Mukden, and the Baltic Fleet was destroyed at Tsushima in May. By the Treaty of Portsmouth Russia gave up Port Arthur and southern Sakhalin. The defeat humiliated the regime and revealed the failure of its army, transport and administration.\n\nThat defeat helped to trigger the 1905 revolution. On Bloody Sunday, 22 January 1905, troops fired on Father Gapon's peaceful procession to the Winter Palace, and trust in the tsar as protector of his people was shattered. Strikes, peasant risings and the mutiny on the battleship Potemkin followed, and a general strike in October brought the formation of the Petersburg Soviet. Nicholas II issued the October Manifesto of 17 October, promising civil liberties and an elected Duma. The regime survived because the army stayed loyal and the opposition split, but the Fundamental Laws of 1906 kept the tsar's autocratic powers and the first two Dumas were quickly dissolved. Russia had been shaken but not reformed.\n\nThe First World War had a far greater impact. Russia mobilised the largest army, but it was short of rifles, shells and boots, and the defeats at Tannenberg and the Masurian Lakes in 1914 and the great retreat of 1915 cost millions of casualties and prisoners. At home the railways were overloaded, inflation rose as the government printed money, and by the winter of 1916-17 food and fuel were short in the cities. Nicholas II took personal command of the army in September 1915, leaving the Tsarina and Rasputin influential in government, and Rasputin's murder in December 1916 showed how isolated the regime had become. The war turned long-standing weaknesses into collapse.\n\nThe result was revolution in 1917. On 8 March, International Women's Day, women textile workers in Petrograd demanded bread, strikes became a general strike, and the garrison joined the crowds instead of firing on them. Army commanders told Nicholas to go, and he abdicated on 15 March, ending three hundred years of Romanov rule. The Provisional Government shared power with the Petrograd Soviet in what was called dual power, but it continued the war and postponed land reform. The failed Kerensky offensive of July weakened it, while Lenin's April Theses promised peace, land and bread and the Bolsheviks gained support in the Soviets. After Kornilov's attempted coup was defeated with Bolshevik help, they won majorities in Petrograd and Moscow, and on 6-7 November Bolshevik forces seized the Winter Palace.\n\nThe Bolshevik victory in turn brought more war. Lenin accepted the harsh Treaty of Brest-Litovsk in March 1918, giving up Poland, the Baltic provinces, Ukraine and Finland, a huge loss of land, population, coal and industry. The Constituent Assembly, in which the Socialist Revolutionaries had won the most votes, was dissolved by force in January 1918. The civil war of 1918-20 pitted the Reds, organised by Trotsky into the Red Army, against the Whites, a loose alliance of generals, monarchists and liberals such as Kolchak, Denikin and Wrangel, with limited foreign intervention. The Reds won because they held the central railways and industry while the Whites were scattered and divided. War Communism and the Red Terror were the price, and Wrangel's defeat in the Crimea in November 1920 ended the main fighting.\n\nOverall, war and revolution together destroyed the tsarist system and created the Soviet state, but at enormous cost. War caused the revolutions of 1905 and 1917 by exposing the failure of autocracy, and revolution led in turn to peace on harsh terms, civil war and one-party rule. Russia in 1920 was territorially smaller, economically ruined and politically transformed.",
