    "model": "Italian fascism under Mussolini was an extreme nationalist, violent and totalitarian movement built on one party, one leader and the idea that the individual existed to serve the state. Mussolini summed up its spirit in 1925 as everything within the state, nothing outside the state, nothing against the state. Its main characteristics were violent nationalism, a one-party dictatorship, the cult of the leader, the corporate state, control of society and an aggressive foreign policy.\n\nFascism was first of all nationalist and violent. Mussolini founded the Fasci di Combattimento in Milan in March 1919, and its armed squads, the squadristi, many of them ex-servicemen, attacked socialist offices, trade union halls and Catholic Popolari premises, especially in the Po valley. Fascism fed on the sense of a mutilated victory after the peace settlement gave Italy less than the Treaty of London had promised, and on fear of revolution after the Biennio Rosso of 1919 and 1920. It presented itself as the defender of nation and order against socialism, and it glorified war, youth and uniformed mass ritual, a style borrowed partly from D'Annunzio's seizure of Fiume. The March on Rome of October 1922, when Victor Emmanuel III refused to sign a state of siege, showed how violence and its threat could be turned into power.\n\nA second characteristic was the destruction of democracy and the creation of a one-party dictatorship. The Acerbo Law of 1923 and the intimidation-ridden election of April 1924 gave the Fascists a huge majority. The murder of the Socialist deputy Giacomo Matteotti in June 1924 caused a crisis, but the opposition's Aventine Secession achieved nothing because the King did not act, and on 3 January 1925 Mussolini accepted responsibility for the violence and began an open dictatorship. Opposition parties were banned, the press was censored, and the Leggi Fascistissime of 1925 and 1926 let Mussolini rule by decree and answer only to the King. A Special Tribunal and the OVRA secret police dealt with opponents, and by 1928 voters were offered a single list of candidates approved by the Fascist Grand Council. This was a totalitarian state in aim, though never in full practice.\n\nThird, the regime built a cult of the leader. Mussolini became Il Duce, shown in posters, newsreels and balcony speeches, and slogans such as Mussolini is always right were painted on walls across the country. The Ministry of Popular Culture, set up in 1937, controlled the press, radio, cinema and theatre, so that the image of a strong, tireless leader embodying the nation went almost unchallenged. All authority flowed from one man, which was central to fascism.\n\nFourth, fascism claimed to replace class conflict with the corporate state. The Rocco Law of 1926 recognised only fascist syndicates and banned strikes, and the Charter of Labour of 1927 set out the idea of employers and workers co-operating under state control. Corporations were set up in 1934. In practice the system benefited employers more than workers, and wages fell, notably after the overvalued lira of Quota 90. Mussolini also launched propaganda campaigns such as the Battle for Grain and the Battle for Births, and created the IRI in 1933, which left the state owning much of banking, shipbuilding and steel. The aim of self-sufficiency for a strong nation was thoroughly fascist, even if the results were mixed.\n\nFifth, fascism aimed to control the whole of society, especially the young, and to expand abroad. The Opera Nazionale Balilla of 1926 enrolled children in state youth groups, schools used a single state textbook from 1929, and the Dopolavoro supervised workers' leisure. Mussolini made peace with the Church in the Lateran Treaties of 1929, creating Vatican City and recognising Catholicism as the state religion, which won him Catholic support. Abroad he invaded Abyssinia in 1935 in defiance of the League of Nations, drew close to Germany in the Rome-Berlin Axis of 1936 and the Pact of Steel of 1939, and took Italy into war in June 1940.\n\nOverall, Italian fascism was a nationalist, violent and totalitarian movement built on one party and one leader, though it never achieved the complete control of Hitler's Germany, since the monarchy, the army and the Church survived beside it.",
