    "label": "2",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Hitler's foreign policy contributed to the outbreak of the Second World War to a very great extent. His racial ideology and his repeated defiance of the peace settlement made war increasingly likely, and it was his invasion of Poland on 1 September 1939 that brought Britain and France in. However, appeasement, the weakness of the League of Nations and the resentments left by Versailles all helped him, so he was the decisive cause but not the only one.\n\nHitler's aims pointed towards war from the start. In Mein Kampf he argued that Germany needed Lebensraum in Eastern Europe, to be won from the Slavs and settled by a racially pure population. At the meeting of 5 November 1937, recorded in the Hossbach Memorandum, he told his military and foreign policy chiefs that Germany's space problem could be solved only by force, probably between 1943 and 1945. Such aims could never be achieved by peaceful revision of Versailles. Rearmament reinforced this: Schacht financed the army through Mefo bills, conscription returned in 1935, and the Four Year Plan of 1936 under Göring aimed to make Germany ready for war. An economy geared to war was bound to produce one.\n\nHis early moves destroyed the restrictions of Versailles and tested the western powers. Germany left the League of Nations and the Disarmament Conference in October 1933, and in March 1935 Hitler announced conscription and an air force in open breach of the treaty. The Stresa Front condemned this but did nothing, and the Anglo-German Naval Agreement of June 1935 then undermined it by letting Germany build a navy of about 35 per cent of Britain's, without consulting France. Each success showed Hitler that the western powers would protest but not act.\n\nThe next stage was expansion. On 7 March 1936 German troops reoccupied the demilitarised Rhineland, breaching Locarno, and Britain and France offered no military resistance. In March 1938 Hitler forced the Austrian Chancellor Schuschnigg to resign and German troops entered Austria in the Anschluss. He then used Konrad Henlein's Sudeten German Party to demand the Sudetenland, and at Munich in September 1938 Britain and France agreed to hand it over, without consulting Czechoslovakia. Each of these actions broke treaties and added to German strength and strategic position.\n\nThe turning point came in March 1939. German troops entered Prague on 15 March, breaking the Munich promises and proving that Hitler's aims were not limited to German-speaking peoples. This ended appeasement, and Britain guaranteed Poland on 31 March, followed by France. Hitler pressed on, and the Nazi-Soviet Pact of 23 August 1939, with its secret protocol dividing Eastern Europe, removed the danger of a war on two fronts. He invaded Poland on 1 September, and Britain and France declared war on 3 September. The decision to attack Poland was Hitler's alone, and he gambled that the western powers would again stand aside.\n\nStill, other factors contributed. Britain and France adopted appeasement because many believed Versailles had been unfair and they were desperate to avoid another war, and their weakness made Hitler's gambles succeed. Munich removed Czechoslovakia's defences and left Hitler convinced that the West would not fight. The League had already shown its weakness in Abyssinia, and Mussolini's drift towards Germany in the Axis of 1936 added to Hitler's strength. Historians disagree: A.J.P. Taylor argued in 1961 that Hitler was an opportunist, whereas the intentionalist view sees a deliberate plan for war.\n\nOverall, these other factors made war possible, but Hitler made it happen. His ideology set the goal, his rearmament built the means, and his actions from the Rhineland to Prague and Poland drove the crisis forward, so his foreign policy was the central cause of the war.",
