    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "I largely agree that Stalin transformed the Soviet Union between 1924 and 1945. He turned a mainly agricultural country into an industrial power, destroyed independent peasant farming, created a system of terror and total party control, remade society and culture, and led the country through a war that made it a superpower. The qualification is that this came at an appalling human cost and built on foundations laid by Lenin.\n\nThe first transformation was economic. After defeating Trotsky, Zinoviev, Kamenev and then Bukharin and the Right Opposition by 1929, Stalin abandoned the New Economic Policy and launched the First Five-Year Plan in 1928, drawn up by Gosplan, with priority for coal, steel, electricity and machinery. New industrial cities such as Magnitogorsk rose in the Urals, and the Second and Third Plans continued the drive and prepared the economy for war. Official figures were inflated, wages fell, housing was overcrowded and forced labour was used on projects such as the White Sea-Baltic Canal, but the change in the economy was enormous.\n\nSecond, Stalin transformed the countryside. In late 1929 he announced the liquidation of the kulaks as a class and forced peasants into collective farms, the kolkhozy, run to state orders. Kulaks were deported or killed, and peasants responded by slaughtering livestock. The grain requisitions and harvest failure of 1932 and 1933 caused a famine that killed several million people, mainly in Ukraine and Kazakhstan, while the authorities denied it. Collectivisation ended the independent peasantry and gave the state control of the grain supply to feed the cities and pay for industry, though output was lower for several years.\n\nThird, Stalin transformed politics through terror and personal power. As General Secretary from 1922 he controlled party appointments, and after the murder of Sergei Kirov on 1 December 1934 he used the killing as a pretext for repression. The show trials of 1936 to 1938 began with Zinoviev and Kamenev, who confessed in August 1936 and were shot, and ended with the trial of Bukharin in 1938. The Great Terror under the NKVD also struck the Red Army leadership, and millions were arrested or sent to labour camps. By the end of the 1930s no rival, no independent institution and no old Bolshevik was left, and Stalin ruled as a dictator surrounded by a cult of personality.\n\nFourth, Soviet society and culture were remade. Literacy campaigns and expanded education raised reading levels and trained workers and engineers, and women were drawn into work. Against that, the League of the Militant Godless campaigned against religion and many churches were closed, and socialist realism became the official style, enforced by the Union of Soviet Writers in 1934. Writers and artists who did not follow the party line faced censorship or arrest. The regime aimed to create a new Soviet man and woman shaped by collective achievement.\n\nFifth, the Second World War showed the transformation in action. After Germany invaded in June 1941, Stalin chaired the State Defence Committee and factories were moved east of the Urals, so that tank and aircraft output rose sharply. Victory at Stalingrad in February 1943 turned the war, and the Red Army reached Berlin in 1945. Stalin ruled harshly, but he also eased pressure on the Orthodox Church and appealed to Russian nationalism in the Great Patriotic War. The Soviet losses were enormous, but by 1945 the USSR was a superpower in control of Eastern Europe.\n\nOverall, Stalin did transform the Soviet Union: its economy, its countryside, its politics and its place in the world were all changed beyond recognition. Lenin had already created the one-party state and the Cheka, so Stalin's terror extended an existing system, but the scale of industrialisation, collectivisation and the Great Terror was his own, and it justifies the view that he transformed the Soviet Union at a terrible human price.",
