    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "John Maynard Keynes and the Jarrow March of October 1936 both belong to the story of how Britain struggled with mass unemployment between the wars. Keynes mattered because he challenged the economic thinking that failed to cure the problem and offered a different remedy, while Jarrow showed what that failure meant for one ruined town. Keynes won the argument in the long run, whereas the Jarrow marchers won sympathy but no jobs.\n\nKeynes was born in Cambridge in 1883 and worked in the Treasury during the First World War. He attended the Versailles peace negotiations as a Treasury representative, but he resigned in protest at the reparations settlement. In 1919 he wrote The Economic Consequences of the Peace, which condemned the treaty as unworkable because it would cripple European recovery. This made him a famous critic of government policy early on, and it showed his habit of putting economic realities ahead of political passion.\n\nHe was equally critical of British policy at home. In April 1925 the Chancellor, Winston Churchill, returned sterling to the gold standard at the pre-war rate of about 4.86 dollars to the pound. Keynes attacked this in The Economic Consequences of Mr Churchill, arguing that the overvalued pound would make British exports dearer and uncompetitive and would force employers to cut wages. He was proved right: the coal owners demanded wage cuts and longer hours, the miners were locked out, and the General Strike of May 1926 followed. His analysis therefore explained a real crisis in the staple industries.\n\nKeynes's most lasting contribution was his answer to mass unemployment. In 1929 he and Hubert Henderson wrote We Can Conquer Unemployment for the Liberal Party, proposing large public works programmes. In 1936 his General Theory of Employment, Interest and Money argued that when private investment is low, government spending can restore demand and employment. The Treasury and the Bank of England distrusted deficit spending, so the National Government of the 1930s did not follow him. Even so, his ideas became the basis of modern economic policy. In wartime he proposed compulsory saving in How to Pay for the War (1940), and in 1944 he led the British delegation to Bretton Woods, where the International Monetary Fund and World Bank were set up.\n\nJarrow shows the human cost of the policies Keynes criticised. It was a one-industry town on the Tyne, dominated by Palmers shipyard. When world shipbuilding collapsed, Palmers launched its last ship in July 1932 and a receiver was appointed in 1933. National Shipbuilders Security bought the yard in 1934 and dismantled it, barring shipbuilding on the site for forty years. A plan for a modern steelworks was weakened by the British Iron and Steel Federation until it could not work. Well over half of insured workers were on the dole, and Ellen Wilkinson, the town's Labour MP, called Jarrow the town that was murdered.\n\nThe response was the Jarrow Crusade. About 200 men, chosen from over a thousand volunteers and medically examined, set out on 5 October 1936 with a petition of over 11,000 signatures in an oak box. The march was backed by the council and by Conservatives, Liberals and Labour alike, so it appeared as the appeal of a whole town and not a party stunt. The men walked about 290 miles, sleeping in church halls and fed by supporters, and reached Marble Arch on 31 October. Newspapers and newsreels gave them wide and often sympathetic coverage.\n\nIn terms of results, the march was a failure. Stanley Baldwin refused to meet a deputation, Wilkinson presented the petition in the Commons on 4 November but it was not debated, and the Unemployment Assistance Board reduced the marchers' benefit. No steelworks came, and Jarrow's real recovery owed far more to rearmament and war orders than to the march. As a symbol it was a success, because it kept the distressed areas before the public and shaped the belief that the 1930s were a time of government failure, which helped build support for full employment policy and the welfare state after 1945.\n\nThe two subjects fit together. Keynes diagnosed what was wrong and gave governments the tools to fix it, while Jarrow showed how much suffering followed when his advice was ignored. Keynes achieved lasting success, and Jarrow achieved a moral victory but not a practical one.",
