    "label": "1",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Both the Berlin Blockade of 1948-49 and the Hungarian Uprising of 1956 increased international tensions. The blockade was the first major confrontation of the Cold War and it hardened the division of Germany and of Europe into rival blocs, while Hungary showed that the Soviet Union would use force to hold its sphere of influence and damaged East-West relations at a sensitive moment.\n\nThe blockade grew out of Allied disagreement over Germany. The American and British zones had been merged into Bizonia in 1947, the French zone was added in 1948-49, and on 20 June 1948 the Western powers introduced the Deutsche Mark in their zones. The Soviet authorities saw this as a threat to their zone and to their wish to prevent a West German state. From June 1948 they blockaded road, rail and canal access to West Berlin, hoping to force the Western powers to abandon the currency and their plans or to leave the city. This raised tension to the point where a clash between Soviet and Western forces was possible.\n\nThe Western response made the crisis a test of will. Truman refused to withdraw, and General Lucius Clay and the Berlin mayor Ernst Reuter led the response on the spot. The airlift flew food and fuel into the city, with planes landing at times about every minute, and delivered over two million tons before the blockade was lifted on 12 May 1949. It showed Western determination without direct military confrontation, but it left deep mistrust.\n\nThe longer-term consequences were just as serious. The North Atlantic Treaty was signed on 4 April 1949 by twelve states, with Article 5 treating an attack on one member as an attack on all. The Federal Republic of Germany was founded on 23 May 1949 and the German Democratic Republic on 7 October 1949, so the blockade confirmed the division of Germany. West Germany joined NATO in 1955, which prompted the creation of the Warsaw Pact, and Berlin remained a flashpoint for the Cold War.\n\nThe Hungarian Uprising came after Khrushchev's secret speech of February 1956 denouncing Stalin and the Polish October of 1956, which encouraged reformers. On 23 October 1956 students in Budapest demanded free elections and the withdrawal of Soviet troops, the secret police fired on the crowd, and fighting spread. Imre Nagy became prime minister, formed a coalition with non-communist parties and on 1 November declared Hungary neutral and announced its withdrawal from the Warsaw Pact, which crossed a line that Moscow would not accept. On 4 November Soviet forces returned in strength in Operation Whirlwind, installed Janos Kadar and crushed the resistance. Commonly cited estimates put Hungarian dead at about 2,500 to 3,000, and about 200,000 people fled to Austria and beyond. Nagy was tried in secret and executed in June 1958.\n\nThe Western powers did not intervene. The Suez crisis, in which Britain and France attacked Egypt, distracted attention and weakened their moral position, Eisenhower ruled out military action because of the risk of a wider war, and the Soviet Union vetoed action at the UN. The West was criticised for having raised hopes and then doing nothing.\n\nThe two events contributed to tension in different ways. The blockade directly threatened war and led to NATO and a permanently divided Germany, while Hungary showed that the Soviet Union would defend its bloc by force and that the West would not roll it back, hardening Cold War attitudes. Both were important steps in the polarisation of Europe, but the blockade was the more dangerous because it risked direct conflict between the superpowers.",
