    "label": "2",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Europe was affected profoundly by both developments. Movements towards unity brought reconciliation between old enemies, rapid growth in trade and a widening Community, while the 1973 Oil Crisis ended the long post-war boom and forced governments to rethink their economic priorities. The two were also linked, since the crisis made cooperation seem more valuable.\n\nThe drive towards unity began from fear and need. After 1945 Western Europeans wanted to end the Franco-German rivalry that had caused two world wars, to contain Soviet power and to rebuild their economies, with American Marshall Aid encouraging cooperation. The Council of Europe, founded in London in May 1949, was only a forum for debate and could not make binding law, which disappointed federalists. Real change came with the Schuman Declaration of 9 May 1950, drafted by Jean Monnet, which proposed pooling French and West German coal and steel. The Treaty of Paris of 1951 created the European Coal and Steel Community, whose High Authority could set prices and quotas and fine firms without government approval. Because coal and steel were the raw materials of war, pooling them made a new Franco-German conflict hard to imagine, and Adenauer welcomed it as treating West Germany as an equal.\n\nWhen the French National Assembly rejected the European Defence Community in August 1954, integration was pushed towards economics rather than armies. The Messina Conference of 1955 relaunched the project, and the Treaties of Rome, signed on 25 March 1957, created the European Economic Community, which began on 1 January 1958 with the aim of a customs union and the free movement of goods, services, capital and workers. The effect was striking: the customs union was completed in July 1968, eighteen months ahead of schedule, trade between the six grew rapidly, and farmers gained guaranteed prices under the Common Agricultural Policy of 1962, although it soon became the largest item in the budget. Unity was not smooth, however. De Gaulle favoured a Europe of nations and vetoed British membership in January 1963 and November 1967, which also blocked Ireland, and the empty chair crisis of 1965 ended in the Luxembourg Compromise, which gave states a veto over vital national interests. Even so, the Hague summit of December 1969 agreed to enlarge the Community, and Ireland, Britain and Denmark joined on 1 January 1973, after about 83 per cent of Irish voters backed membership in May 1972.\n\nThe Oil Crisis was a very different kind of shock. Western Europe had grown for almost thirty years on cheap oil, which supplied around half of its energy at about three dollars a barrel. After Egypt and Syria attacked Israel on 6 October 1973, the Arab producers used oil as a weapon. OPEC raised the posted price by about 70 per cent on 16 October, the Arab states announced output cuts, and an embargo was placed on the USA and then on the Netherlands, which supported Israel and whose port of Rotterdam was Europe's main oil harbour. Prices roughly quadrupled within months, and although the embargo was lifted in March 1974 the higher prices stayed.\n\nThe effects on Europe were immediate and lasting. There were car-free Sundays in West Germany and the Netherlands, and Britain introduced a three-day working week in early 1974. Industrial production fell, unemployment rose from the low levels of the 1960s to over 10 per cent in the EEC by the mid-1980s, and inflation combined with stagnation to produce stagflation that Keynesian policies struggled to cure. Oil-importing states ran balance of payments deficits, and a second shock after the Iranian revolution of 1979 caused another recession in 1980 to 1982. Governments abandoned the commitment to full employment and made defeating inflation their main aim, while heavy industries such as coal, steel and shipbuilding declined, and the EEC imposed steel quotas under the Davignon Plan in 1980. Europe also sought new energy: France launched the Messmer Plan for nuclear power in 1974 and drew about 70 per cent of its electricity from it by the late 1980s, and North Sea oil made Britain self-sufficient by 1980.\n\nOverall, the two developments affected Europe in opposite ways. Unity gave Western Europe peace, growth and a growing common framework, while the Oil Crisis brought recession, unemployment and a harder economic climate, though many economists argue that inflation and falling productivity were already weakening the boom, so the shock hastened rather than caused its end. Yet the two were connected, since the crisis strengthened the case for joint action and contributed to the launch of the European Monetary System in 1979.",
