    "label": "1",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "British withdrawal from India in August 1947 was important for both countries. For India it meant independence after decades of struggle but also the tragedy of partition, while for Britain it meant the loss of the most valuable part of its empire, a change in its world role and the start of a wider process of decolonisation.\n\nFor India, the most obvious importance was the end of British rule and the achievement of self-government. The Indian National Congress, founded in 1885, had been transformed under Gandhi into a mass movement through satyagraha, from the Non-Cooperation movement of 1920-22 and the Salt March of 1930 to Quit India in 1942. Independence on 15 August 1947 ended the Raj, which had governed India directly since 1858. India became a republic on 26 January 1950 under Nehru, and the London Declaration of 1949 allowed it to stay in the Commonwealth as a republic, which showed that independence did not have to mean a total break. Nehru's non-alignment gave India an independent role in world affairs.\n\nThe second Indian consequence was partition. The Muslim League under Jinnah had called for separate states in the Lahore Resolution of March 1940 and won most Muslim seats in 1945-46. After the Cabinet Mission failed in 1946 and Direct Action Day in Calcutta in August 1946 left thousands dead, Mountbatten accepted partition in the plan of 3 June 1947 and brought the date forward to 15 August 1947. Cyril Radcliffe, who had never visited India, drew the boundaries through Punjab and Bengal, announced on 17 August, two days after independence. The consequences were huge: 10 to 15 million people were displaced, and deaths are estimated from 200,000 to 2 million. Pakistan was created in two parts 1,600 km apart, and the princely states, above all Kashmir, produced a conflict between India and Pakistan that began in 1947 and continued after the ceasefire of 1 January 1949.\n\nPartition also had political and social costs inside India. Gandhi opposed partition and fasted in Calcutta and Delhi to stop the killing, and he was shot dead by the Hindu nationalist Nathuram Godse on 30 January 1948. The new states had to divide the army, civil service and railways in a few weeks, which strained administration. Yet India also gained a lasting democracy and, in the Constitution of 1950, a commitment to equality. So the importance for India was a mixture of triumph and trauma.\n\nFor Britain, the importance was first of all economic and strategic. India was the largest and most valuable of the British possessions, and the loss of the Raj reduced Britain's role in Asia. But Britain left partly because it could no longer afford to stay. The war cost heavily, and Britain borrowed about 3.75 billion dollars from the United States in 1945-46; it also owed India over a billion pounds in sterling balances, reversing the old financial relationship. Attlee's Labour government, elected in July 1945, was committed to Indian self-rule, and the INA trials and the Royal Indian Navy mutiny of February 1946 made officials like Wavell doubt that the army could be relied on. Withdrawal was therefore a recognition of Britain's weakened position.\n\nThe withdrawal also changed Britain's imperial position and its idea of the Commonwealth. India's decision to remain in the Commonwealth as a republic established that membership did not require acceptance of the monarch as head of state, and the Commonwealth became Britain's main link with former colonies. The sterling area still tied the two economies and India remained a major trading partner. Britain's reputation did suffer from the speed of Mountbatten's timetable, which critics say left too little time to plan the boundary and policing, though his defenders argue that delay would have brought more violence. The success of a transfer without a colonial war contrasted with France in Indochina and Algeria.\n\nFinally, India set a precedent. Burma and Ceylon became independent in 1948 and Ghana in 1957, the first British colony in tropical Africa, and Macmillan spoke of a wind of change in 1960. Once India had gone, the case for holding other colonies was greatly weakened. Overall, withdrawal was a turning point for both: it gave India freedom but at the terrible cost of partition, and it showed Britain that its power in the world had shrunk and that the empire would not long survive.",
