    "label": "2",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Europe's role in the Arab-Israeli conflict between 1945 and 1990 was large but declining. European powers, above all Britain, created the conditions for the conflict through their wartime promises and Mandate rule, handed it to the United Nations when they could no longer manage it, and then lost their leading position in the region at Suez in 1956. After that Europe was a secondary actor, overshadowed by the United States and the Soviet Union.\n\nThe starting point was the British Mandate for Palestine, which came into force in 1923. Britain had issued the Balfour Declaration on 2 November 1917, supporting a national home for the Jewish people in Palestine, while also promising that the rights of the existing non-Jewish communities would not be prejudiced. These conflicting commitments were never reconciled. Jewish immigration rose sharply after the Nazis came to power in 1933, and the Jewish share of the population grew from about 11 per cent in 1922 to roughly 30 per cent by 1939. The Arab Revolt of 1936-39 was crushed by British forces, and the White Paper of May 1939 limited Jewish immigration to 75,000 over five years, which the Zionists rejected. Britain therefore entered the post-war period trusted by neither side.\n\nEurope's own history also drove the conflict after 1945. The Holocaust killed about six million Jews and greatly strengthened the demand for a Jewish state and for the admission of refugees to Palestine. Britain, with about 100,000 troops in the country, refused to admit 100,000 refugees as the Anglo-American Committee recommended in April 1946, and its policy of turning back refugee ships led to the Exodus affair of 1947, when about 4,500 survivors were sent back to Europe. The Irgun under Menachem Begin bombed the King David Hotel on 22 July 1946, killing 91 people, and later hanged two British sergeants. The cost of policing Palestine in the face of Jewish attacks and Arab hostility became too great.\n\nBritain's withdrawal was the next European contribution. In February 1947 it referred the question to the United Nations, and in November the General Assembly passed Resolution 181 by 33 votes to 13, partitioning Palestine into Arab and Jewish states. Britain abstained and refused to enforce partition, announcing that its forces would leave, and the Mandate ended on 14 May 1948. This left a security vacuum in which civil war was already under way. Ben-Gurion proclaimed Israel that day, five Arab armies invaded, and about 700,000 Palestinians fled or were expelled. The refugee problem and the lack of agreed borders, which still divide the region, were born in the collapse of European authority.\n\nThe most dramatic European intervention was the Suez Crisis of 1956. Britain had withdrawn its troops from the canal base by June 1956, and when Nasser nationalised the Suez Canal Company on 26 July, Britain and France planned to remove him. Through the secret Protocol of Sèvres, signed on 24 October, Israel would invade Sinai and Britain and France would then issue an ultimatum and occupy the canal zone as supposed peacekeepers. Israel attacked on 29 October and Anglo-French paratroopers landed at Port Said on 5 November. The plan collapsed under American financial pressure, with a run on the pound and a refusal of IMF support, and under Soviet threats. A ceasefire followed on 6-7 November, UNEF was deployed, and the canal stayed in Egyptian hands.\n\nThe results show how far Europe's role had shrunk. Eden resigned in January 1957, Nasser's prestige soared across the Arab world, and the United States announced the Eisenhower Doctrine to fill the gap left by Britain and France. The pro-British monarchy in Iraq fell in 1958. Suez also showed Israel that France was a willing supplier of support, a connection that lasted until de Gaulle's more critical line after the Six-Day War of 1967.\n\nOverall, Europe's role was decisive in creating the conflict, through the Balfour Declaration, the Mandate and the Holocaust, and decisive in ending its own control, through the withdrawal of 1948 and the humiliation of Suez. After 1956 the Cold War superpowers, not European states, set the pace in the Arab-Israeli conflict, and Europe's later influence was largely diplomatic rather than strategic.",
