    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Race relations caused serious problems in both Britain and France between 1945 and 1990. In Britain the problems took the form of discrimination, riots and restrictive laws, while in France they centred on the banlieues, the rise of the Front National and disputes over assimilation and religion. In both countries the migrants' status as citizens or former colonial subjects was undermined by prejudice, unemployment and conflict with the police.\n\nIn Britain, Commonwealth migration after the British Nationality Act of 1948 brought large numbers of people from the Caribbean and South Asia, beginning symbolically with the Empire Windrush at Tilbury on 22 June 1948. Migrants recruited for the NHS and London Transport met a colour bar in lodgings and jobs, and landlords such as Peter Rachman in Notting Hill exploited them with overcrowded lettings. Competition for housing and work, together with far-right agitation by groups such as Mosley's Union Movement, led to the Notting Hill and Nottingham riots of August and September 1958, the worst racial violence in post-war Britain up to then. The murder of Kelso Cochrane, an Antiguan carpenter, in May 1959 showed that the hostility was dangerous.\n\nBritish politics then turned against immigration. The Commonwealth Immigrants Act of 1962 introduced employment vouchers, and the 1968 Act was rushed through in a few days to limit Kenyan Asians holding British passports. In April 1968 Enoch Powell made his 'rivers of blood' speech in Birmingham, and although Heath dismissed him from the Shadow Cabinet, London dockers marched in his support. The Immigration Act of 1971 introduced patriality, and the National Front, founded in 1967, campaigned against immigration and clashed with opponents at Lewisham in 1977 and Southall in 1979, where the teacher Blair Peach died. The Race Relations Acts of 1965, 1968 and 1976 outlawed discrimination, but they were hard to enforce and did not remove disadvantage.\n\nThe tension peaked in 1981. Black residents saw the heavy use of the 'sus' law as harassment, and Operation Swamp 81 in Brixton, in which nearly a thousand people were stopped in a week, was followed by riots on 10-12 April. Riots followed in Toxteth and Moss Side in July, and CS gas was used on the British mainland for the first time. The Scarman Report of November 1981 blamed racial disadvantage, unemployment and poor policing, and it led to the Police and Criminal Evidence Act of 1984. Race relations had clearly caused deep problems for British society and for public order.\n\nIn France, migrants from Portugal, Spain, Italy, Morocco, Tunisia, Algeria and West Africa filled jobs in factories and construction after the war, and many lived in bidonvilles such as Nanterre. The Algerian war left a legacy of bitterness: on 17 October 1961 Paris police under Maurice Papon attacked an Algerian demonstration, killing dozens or more, and the harkis were housed in camps with poor conditions. After the oil shock of 1973 migrant workers lost their jobs first, and in 1974 labour immigration was suspended. The second generation, the beurs, grew up in rundown banlieue estates with high unemployment and poor relations with the police, and the unrest at Les Minguettes near Lyon in the summer of 1981 brought this to national attention.\n\nFrench race relations were also damaged by politics and by the clash between assimilation and identity. The Front National, founded by Jean-Marie Le Pen in 1972, broke through at Dreux in September 1983, won about 11 per cent in the 1984 European elections, 35 seats in the National Assembly in 1986, and Le Pen took about 14.4 per cent in the 1988 presidential election. The 1983 March for Equality and against Racism and the founding of SOS Racisme in 1984 showed a strong response, but the Pasqua laws of 1986 tightened controls and the plan to end automatic nationality was dropped only after mass protests. In October 1989 the exclusion of three Muslim girls for wearing headscarves at Creil showed the tension between laïcité and Muslim identity.\n\nOverall, race relations caused severe problems in both countries. Britain saw riots, restrictive laws and the National Front, while France experienced banlieue unrest, the Front National's electoral success and the headscarf dispute. The causes were similar, namely discrimination in housing and jobs, unemployment and policing, but Britain's colonial migrants faced a colour bar and legal restriction, while France's difficulties arose from its assimilationist model and the memory of Algeria.",
