    "label": "B4",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "Evie Hone studied art in London and Paris, and with Mainie Jellett she was one of the first Irish painters to work in a cubist style. In the 1930s she turned to stained glass, working with An Túr Gloine, the Dublin studio founded by Sarah Purser in 1903 to revive Irish stained glass. She converted to Catholicism in 1937, and much of her work was made for churches. Her window My Four Green Fields, symbolising the four provinces, was made for the Irish pavilion at the 1939 New York World's Fair and won first prize for stained glass.",
