    "label": "C1",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "The Irish Free State was to have the same constitutional status as Canada, Australia, New Zealand and South Africa, as a self-governing dominion in the Empire. The King was represented by a Governor-General, and members of the Free State parliament had to swear to be faithful to the Constitution and to the King. Britain kept the Treaty ports of Berehaven, Queenstown and Lough Swilly, and would defend the coasts. The Free State took on a share of the British public debt, subject to counter-claims, and the Free State army was limited in proportion to population. Northern Ireland could opt out of the Free State within one month, and a Boundary Commission would then settle the border. A Provisional Government would take over from the British administration until the Free State was established.",
