    "label": "C1",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "The First Programme, published in November 1958 for the years 1959 to 1963, marked the end of protection and the start of export-led growth. It removed the rule that Irish citizens hold most shares in new firms, which opened the way to foreign-owned factories. IDA grants and the Shannon Free Airport Development Company drew foreign firms to Ireland, with the best terms going to the poorer west. Real national output grew by about 4 per cent a year from 1958 to 1963, roughly double the target. Net emigration fell to about 80,000 in 1961 to 1966, from about 200,000 in 1956 to 1961. The 1966 census recorded about 2.88 million people, the first rise since the early 1950s, and confidence in the country grew.",
