    "label": "C2",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Before 1973 Irish fishing was small, with mostly old inshore boats, and foreign trawlers fished close to the coast under historic rights. The accession terms gave Ireland an exclusive six-mile zone for Irish boats and a 12-mile zone in parts of the coast until the end of 1982. The Hague agreement of 1976 extended fishing limits to 200 nautical miles from 1977, giving Ireland a large area of sea to manage. EEC and state grants financed larger vessels, and Killybegs grew into Ireland's leading fishing port. However, the Common Fisheries Policy of January 1983 gave Irish quotas that were small compared with the value of fish in its waters. In my view membership helped the industry grow, but it hurt small inshore fishermen, who often lost out to larger boats.",
