    "label": "C3",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "The marriage bar forced women in the civil service to resign when they married, and it was removed for primary teachers in 1958 and for the civil service in 1973. The Commission on the Status of Women, set up in 1970 under Thekla Beere, reported in December 1972 with 49 recommendations on equal pay and family law. In May 1971 members of the Irish Women's Liberation Movement took the contraceptive train to Belfast and brought contraceptives back to Dublin, which made contraception a public issue. In McGee v Attorney General in December 1973 the Supreme Court found the ban on importing contraceptives unconstitutional. The Anti-Discrimination (Pay) Act 1974 and the Employment Equality Act 1977 outlawed unequal pay and discrimination on grounds of sex or marital status. Married women's participation in the workforce rose from about 5 per cent in 1961 to about 17 per cent in 1981.",
