    "label": "B4",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "Victor Hugo was the leading French Romantic writer, and his play Hernani, staged in 1830, marked the victory of Romanticism in French theatre. His novels Notre-Dame de Paris and Les Miserables revived interest in the Gothic past of Paris and portrayed the poor of France. Hugo became a republican by 1848, and after Napoleon III's coup of 1851 he went into exile, where his pamphlet Napoleon le Petit and his satires made him a symbol of republican resistance. He refused the amnesty of 1859 and returned to Paris only after the Third Republic was proclaimed in 1870.",
