    "model": "Anti-Jewish prejudice grew in France after 1870, and the nationalist writer Édouard Drumont's La France juive (1886) became a bestseller that presented Jews as a threat to the nation. In October 1894 Captain Alfred Dreyfus, a Jewish artillery officer, was arrested for passing secrets to Germany and sent to Devil's Island after a secret court martial. In 1896 Colonel Picquart found that Major Esterhazy had written the leaked memorandum, but the army covered this up and Esterhazy was acquitted in January 1898. Émile Zola's open letter 'J'accuse' in January 1898 accused the army of the cover-up, and France divided into Dreyfusards and anti-Dreyfusards.",
