    "model": "Wilfred Owen was born in Oswestry, Shropshire, in 1893, and he worked as a teacher in France before the war. He enlisted in October 1915 and fought on the Somme, then was diagnosed with shell shock and sent to Craiglockhart War Hospital in June 1917. There he met Siegfried Sassoon, who advised him to write plainly, and he wrote poems such as Dulce et Decorum Est and Anthem for Doomed Youth. His poems used graphic images of gas and mud to show the suffering of the soldiers. Owen was killed on 4 November 1918, a week before the armistice, aged 25.",
