    "label": "C2",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Leo XIII, pope from 1878 to 1903, responded to the problems of industrial society mainly through Church teaching on social questions. In his encyclical Rerum Novarum of 15 May 1891 he addressed the condition of the working classes. He defended private property and rejected socialism and class war, but he upheld the right of workers to a just wage, decent conditions and to form trade unions. He called on states to protect the poor. Rerum Novarum became the foundation of modern Catholic social teaching, and it encouraged Catholic trade unions and Christian democratic parties in Germany, Belgium and Italy. This gave the Church a clear voice in the debate on workers' rights.",
