    "label": "C3",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Wilhelm II wanted a navy that matched Germany's status as a world power, and Tirpitz drew up a plan for a large battle fleet in the North Sea. The First Navy Law of 1898 planned 19 battleships, and the Second Navy Law of 1900 raised the figure to 38 battleships and 20 large cruisers. Tirpitz's risk theory argued that a German fleet strong enough to damage the Royal Navy would deter Britain from war. The British saw this as a direct threat to their naval supremacy, which they had defended with the two-power standard. The launch of HMS Dreadnought in 1906 made existing battleships obsolete, and both sides began a costly race to build more of them. Britain responded by moving its fleet to face the North Sea and by forming the Entente with France and Russia.",
