    "label": "C1",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "The Lateran Treaties of 1929 ended the dispute between the Italian state and the papacy, and Catholicism became the sole religion of the Italian state. In 1931 the regime clashed with Catholic Action over its youth groups, and Pope Pius XI protested in the encyclical Non abbiamo bisogno. The Church opposed the racial laws of 1938 in part, but it did not break with the regime. Hitler signed the Reich Concordat with the Vatican on 20 July 1933 to protect Catholic institutions, but the Centre Party dissolved itself as the agreement was concluded. The German Christians pushed for a single Protestant Reich Church under Nazi control. The Confessing Church, formed in 1934, rejected the state's control of the Church's teaching. Pope Pius XI's encyclical Mit brennender Sorge of March 1937 criticised Nazi violations of the Concordat.",
