    "label": "B2",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "Penicillin, discovered by Alexander Fleming in 1928, was developed into a usable drug by Howard Florey and Ernst Chain and mass-produced from 1943 to 1944 with American help, and antibiotics cut deaths from pneumonia and tuberculosis in the 1950s. Jonas Salk's polio vaccine was announced in 1955, and vaccination campaigns against diphtheria, polio and measles reduced child deaths across Europe. James Watson and Francis Crick published the double-helix structure of DNA in 1953, using X-ray evidence gathered by Rosalind Franklin, which opened the way to genetic engineering. The first test-tube baby, Louise Brown, was born in England in 1978 after the work of Robert Edwards and Patrick Steptoe.",
