    "label": "B3",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "Alexander Solzhenitsyn was a Soviet novelist whose writing on the Gulag made the camp system known to the world and made him the most famous dissident in the Soviet Union. His book One Day in the Life of Ivan Denisovich was published in 1962 with Khrushchev's approval and brought the camps into Soviet public discussion. After Khrushchev fell the authorities tightened censorship, so his novels The First Circle and Cancer Ward could be published only abroad, and he was expelled from the Writers' Union in 1969. He was expelled from the Soviet Union in 1974, the most famous example of the state's treatment of dissident writers.",
