    "model": "Gordimer, born at Springs near Johannesburg in 1923, was a white South African who wrote in English and examined white and black lives under apartheid. Her novel A World of Strangers (1958) was banned in South Africa for twelve years, and Burger's Daughter (1979) was also banned for a time. July's People (1981) imagined a South African revolution in which a white family takes refuge with their black servant in a village. She was awarded the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1991 and remained an opponent of censorship after apartheid ended.",
