    "label": "C1",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Ben-Gurion accepted the 1947 UN partition plan as a first step towards a Jewish state, although the Arab states rejected it and fighting began in December 1947. He proclaimed the State of Israel in Tel Aviv on 14 May 1948 and became its first prime minister, and Israel faced invasion by five Arab armies the next day. In May 1948 he oversaw the creation of the Israel Defence Forces, and in June 1948 he ordered the shelling of the Irgun ship Altalena to establish a single army. During the 1948-49 war he backed the expulsions from Lydda and Ramle in July 1948, and about 700,000 Palestinian Arabs fled or were expelled. The 1949 armistice agreements left Israel with more territory than the UN plan had allotted it. In October 1956 he attended the secret Sevres talks with Britain and France that planned the Israeli attack on Sinai during the Suez Crisis.",
