    "label": "C2",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "The Arusha Declaration of 5 February 1967 committed Tanzania to ujamaa, meaning familyhood or African socialism, self-reliance and state control of the main means of production. In 1967 the government nationalised the banks, major industries and insurance companies and introduced Education for Self-Reliance to link schooling with rural work. Ujamaa villages were voluntary cooperatives at first, but from 1973 to 1974 millions of people were moved into villages, often by force, in a policy called villagisation. Villagisation disrupted farming and food shortages followed, so the government had to import food, and the economy declined by the early 1980s. Tanzania made important gains in health and literacy, with adult literacy rising to a high proportion of the population by the early 1980s. The policy was a mixed success, because it gave Tanzania national unity and social gains but caused a collapse of farm output.",
