    "label": "C4",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Post-war labour shortages brought migrants from Portugal, Spain, Italy, Morocco, Tunisia and West Africa to work in factories, mines and construction. Many migrants lived in makeshift bidonvilles such as Nanterre near Paris, where living conditions were poor. In July 1974 the government suspended labour immigration, and in 1976 it allowed family reunification, so migrant families settled permanently. The second generation, the beurs, grew up in the banlieues, where youth unemployment was far above the national average. After unrest at the Minguettes estate in 1981, the Mitterrand government created Priority Education Zones and the Banlieues 89 project, which critics said were too limited. The March for Equality and against Racism of 1983 reached Paris with tens of thousands of supporters, and the Pasqua laws of 1986 then tightened immigration control.",
