    "label": "3(b)",
    "marks": 10,
    "model": "No, Document B is not an objective source, because McCormack was a trade unionist and human rights activist with strong opinions.\nShe uses emotive language, calling the Lockwood decision 'a blatantly political decision' and the Unionists who backed Coleraine 'modern-day Lundys' acting for 'sectarian purposes'.\nShe presents the protest as admirable, praising the people of Derry for their 'peaceful and determined revolt' against the state.\nHowever, the document does give a range of views, including the city's outrage and Desmond Boal's attack on the government, and McCormack's Protestant background shows she was not simply following the Unionist line.",
