    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Fenianism was only partly effective. At home it failed in its aim of a republic by force, since its rising was crushed and its leaders imprisoned, but it did more than any other movement of the period to shake the government and keep republican ideas alive. Abroad, its American organisation gave it money and men, and its actions in Britain and Canada won it attention, but they also cost it support. Its greatest effect was indirect, in prompting reform and leaving a legacy for later republicans.\n\nIn Ireland, Fenianism built a real organisation. The Irish Republican Brotherhood was founded in Dublin on 17 March 1858 by James Stephens as an oath-bound secret society, organised in circles with its members drawn from artisans, clerks, shopkeepers and farmers' sons. It also tried to recruit soldiers in the British army. The movement grew out of the bitterness of the Famine, the failure of the 1848 rising and the disillusion with parliamentary politics after the collapse of the Independent Irish Party in 1852. Its newspaper, the Irish People, was founded in 1863 with Luby, O'Leary and Kickham as editors, and the funeral of the 1848 rebel Terence Bellew MacManus in Dublin in 1861 showed how much support it had.\n\nHowever, the movement's military record at home was poor. In September 1865 the police raided the Irish People offices and arrested Luby, O'Leary, Rossa and Kickham, and Stephens himself was arrested in November. He escaped from Richmond Prison, but in 1866 he put off a rising he had promised, and was removed by American leaders in December. Habeas corpus was suspended in February 1866. The rising in March 1867 took place in bad weather, and the small risings in Dublin, Cork, Limerick and Tipperary were quickly crushed, since informers had compromised the plans and the Fenians lacked arms and trained leaders. Cardinal Cullen and most bishops condemned Fenianism as a secret society, so the movement was also opposed by the most powerful institution in Irish life.\n\nAbroad, the Fenian Brotherhood, founded in New York in 1858 by John O'Mahony, raised money and men and gave the movement a transatlantic reach. Many of its members were veterans of the American Civil War, whose military experience raised hopes of a rising in Ireland. However, American Fenians launched raids into Canada, at Ridgeway in June 1866 and again in 1870, which failed to win territory. The split between Stephens and the American leaders in 1866 also weakened the movement and showed the difficulty of running a revolution from two continents.\n\nIn Britain, Fenian actions were dramatic but ambivalent in effect. In September 1867 Fenians in Manchester freed two prisoners, Thomas Kelly and Timothy Deasy, from a prison van and killed a police sergeant, Charles Brett. Three men, Allen, Larkin and O'Brien, were hanged in November as the Manchester Martyrs and were mourned across Ireland, which turned them into symbols of national resistance. In December 1867 an attempt to free a prisoner at Clerkenwell prison in London killed about a dozen people and turned British opinion against the Fenians. The two events showed that Irish discontent could threaten Britain itself.\n\nThe most important result was political. William Gladstone, who became prime minister in December 1868 and said his mission was to pacify Ireland, passed the Irish Church Act in 1869, disestablishing the Church of Ireland, and the Land Act of 1870, which gave legal force to the Ulster custom and compensation for disturbance. The Fenian scare made reform politically urgent, and Fenian prisoners were released between 1869 and 1871 as the amnesty movement grew. Yet the Land Act failed to stop evictions, so the grievances behind Fenianism were not removed. Fenianism also left a legacy, because it revived physical force republicanism and built a disciplined organisation that lasted into the twentieth century and the 1916 Rising.\n\nOverall, Fenianism was a military failure both at home and abroad, but a political success in the longer term. It failed to win a republic, was crushed by informers, split and condemned by the Church, and its overseas raids won nothing. But it forced the British government to act on Irish grievances, produced martyrs who were remembered, and kept the republican idea alive for later generations. It was therefore more effective as a pressure and an inspiration than as a revolution.",
