    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Paul Cullen and Mary Aikenhead both shaped Irish life profoundly, though in different ways. Cullen reorganised the Catholic Church and made it the dominant institution of Catholic Ireland, while Aikenhead founded the Religious Sisters of Charity and pioneered Catholic nursing and care of the poor. Between them they changed how the Irish church worshipped, educated, nursed and organised itself.\n\nCullen was born in County Kildare in 1803 and spent much of his early life in Rome, where he was ordained and became rector of the Irish College in 1832. He therefore arrived in Ireland as a committed ultramontane, someone who believed in strong papal authority over national churches. He was made Archbishop of Armagh and Apostolic Delegate in 1849-50, the pope's representative with special powers, and became Archbishop of Dublin in 1852 and the first Irish cardinal in 1866. His rise gave Rome a loyal and forceful agent at the head of the Irish church for nearly thirty years.\n\nHis greatest single contribution was the Synod of Thurles, which met in Tipperary from 22 August to 9 September 1850 with Cullen presiding. It laid down uniform liturgical practice with the Roman rite, regulated clerical dress, behaviour and the conduct of the sacraments, and increased the bishops' authority over their priests. It also discouraged priests from political agitation at the altar and at election meetings. This began the Romanisation of the Irish church, replacing local variety with central discipline. Opposition came from Archbishop MacHale and from liberal Catholics who resented Roman control, but Rome backed Cullen and confirmed the decrees.\n\nCullen also drove what the historian Emmet Larkin called the devotional revolution. Under his influence new parish churches were built, religious orders and congregations multiplied, and parish missions by Redemptorists, Vincentians and Jesuits spread the rosary, novenas, benediction and devotion to the Sacred Heart and the Virgin Mary. A better-educated and better-disciplined clergy meant that Mass attendance rose from perhaps a third of Catholics before the Famine to a large majority by the 1870s. The Famine, which removed the poor who had been least regular in practice, helped this along, so historians debate how much Cullen created the change and how much he led what was already under way. Even so, he gave it direction, and Irish Catholic identity became bound up with the Church.\n\nIn education Cullen opposed the mixed Queen's Colleges, which Rome had condemned in 1847 and Thurles forbade Catholics to attend. He backed a Catholic alternative, and the Catholic University opened in Dublin in 1854 with John Henry Newman as first rector. It was a limited success, since it had no state-recognised degrees and few students, and Newman resigned in 1858. Most Catholics who wanted a degree still went to Trinity or the Queen's Colleges. Cullen's insistence on Catholic control of schooling nonetheless set the agenda for later campaigns for state-supported Catholic education.\n\nCullen's political influence was conservative. He distrusted Young Ireland, the Tenant League and priests in politics, and he condemned the Fenians as a secret society. In 1861 he refused to allow the body of Terence Bellew MacManus, the 1848 rebel, to lie in the Pro-Cathedral. He favoured co-operation with the Liberals and supported Gladstone's disestablishment of the Church of Ireland. Nationalists and some bishops resented his authority, but for a quarter of a century he was the dominant Catholic voice in public affairs.\n\nMary Aikenhead made a different but equally lasting contribution. Born in Cork in 1787 to a Protestant father and a Catholic mother, she became a Catholic and, guided by Archbishop Daniel Murray of Dublin, trained at the Bar Convent in York. In 1815 she founded the Religious Sisters of Charity, the first Irish Catholic order devoted to caring for the poor outside the walls of a convent. The Sisters visited the poor in their homes, ran schools and nursed the sick, and in the Famine years the Sisters of Charity and Mercy nursed fever victims and fed the poor in towns.\n\nIn 1834 she opened St Vincent's Hospital in St Stephen's Green, Dublin, the first hospital in Ireland run by nuns, which treated the sick poor of all creeds. She directed her order from Stanhope Street, much of the time as an invalid, until her death in 1858. Her achievement was to open active public service to Catholic women and to found institutions that shaped Irish nursing and health care.\n\nOverall, Cullen's contribution was institutional and spiritual: a disciplined, Roman-oriented church with new churches, schools and orders, though also an authoritarian one that left its university weak and alienated some Catholics and Protestants. Aikenhead's was practical and humane, and less controversial, since her hospital and schools served the poor directly. Together they made the Catholic Church the central institution of nineteenth-century Irish life.",
