    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Partition was not planned in 1912. It emerged between 1912 and 1920 because unionists in Ulster showed that they would resist Home Rule by force, because British governments failed again and again to find an all-Ireland settlement, and because nationalist politics changed so much during the war that no compromise remained. By 1920 the Government of Ireland Act gave the six north-eastern counties a parliament of their own, and the factors behind that outcome were closely connected.\n\nThe first and most important factor was the strength of unionist resistance. When Asquith introduced the Third Home Rule Bill on 11 April 1912, Ulster unionists under Edward Carson and James Craig organised mass opposition. On Ulster Day, 28 September 1912, about 237,000 men signed the Ulster Covenant pledging to resist Home Rule, and about 234,000 women signed a parallel Declaration. In January 1913 the Ulster Volunteer Force was formed and drilled tens of thousands of men. This showed that Home Rule for the whole island could only be put into effect by force, and it made some form of exclusion of Ulster the price of any settlement.\n\nThe second factor was the weakness of the British government's position. The Parliament Act of 1911 meant the Lords could only delay the Bill, so by May 1914 it could become law without their consent. But the Curragh incident of March 1914, when British cavalry officers said they would rather be dismissed than act against Ulster, showed that the army could not be relied on to coerce the unionists. The Larne gun-running of 24 to 25 April 1914, when the UVF landed about 25,000 rifles and the government did not stop it, confirmed this. Britain was therefore unwilling to impose Home Rule on a determined unionist community, and exclusion became the only way out of the crisis.\n\nThird, a series of failed negotiations made exclusion the central assumption of every proposal. In March 1914 Asquith offered county option, by which each Ulster county could vote itself out for six years. Carson rejected temporary exclusion, while Redmond accepted it only reluctantly. The Buckingham Palace Conference of 21 to 24 July 1914 broke down on the question of Tyrone and Fermanagh, where nationalists and unionists were closely balanced. Once the principle of excluding some counties had been conceded, the argument was about how much of Ulster would go and for how long, and not whether partition would happen.\n\nThe outbreak of the First World War in August 1914 was a fourth factor. The Home Rule Act received royal assent on 18 September 1914 but was suspended until the war ended, with a promise that Ulster would receive special treatment by an amending Act. This meant that the question was postponed with exclusion still built into it. The war also changed Irish politics. Redmond's call at Woodenbridge to join the British army split the Volunteers, and his party lost credibility because it had delivered only a suspended Act. The Easter Rising of 1916 and the executions that followed turned opinion towards separatism. Lloyd George's proposals of 1916 collapsed because unionist ministers in the cabinet insisted that exclusion be permanent, while nationalists had been told it would be temporary. The Irish Convention of 1917 to 1918 then failed to produce agreement.\n\nThe fifth factor was the pattern of the 1918 general election. Sinn Féin won most of the seats on the island, while unionists won 26, mostly in the north-east. Only four of the nine Ulster counties had unionist majorities, namely Antrim, Down, Armagh and Londonderry, and Tyrone and Fermanagh had narrow nationalist majorities. Unionists therefore preferred six counties to nine, because a six-county state gave them a safe majority. Northern Ireland would have about 1.25 million people, roughly two-thirds Protestant and one-third Catholic. The election showed that the island had split into two political communities, and Sinn Féin's abstention and the start of the War of Independence ruled out a negotiated all-Ireland settlement.\n\nFinally, British policy turned this division into law. Lloyd George's coalition contained a large Conservative element that was sympathetic to the unionists and unwilling to coerce Ulster. The Government of Ireland Act of December 1920 created one parliament for the six counties of Northern Ireland and one for the remaining 26 counties, with a Council of Ireland to link them. The southern parliament never worked, since Sinn Féin treated the election as one for the Dáil, but the northern one did, and it was opened in 1921.\n\nOverall, partition was the product of unionist armed resistance, which made coercion impossible, and of British reluctance to override it. Failed negotiations, the war and the Rising, and the result of the 1918 election then removed any chance of a united Ireland within Home Rule. Of these factors, the Ulster Covenant and the UVF were the most decisive, because they made exclusion the starting point of every later discussion.",
