    "label": "2",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The Ulster Unionist Party governed Northern Ireland without interruption from 1921, and between 1920 and 1945 it faced three main challenges: violence and the hostility of nationalists and republicans, the unwillingness of the minority to accept the state, and economic depression and war. It responded with emergency powers, control of the electoral system, a policy of keeping in step with Britain on welfare, and a determined wartime effort. These responses secured the survival of the state but left the Catholic minority alienated.\n\nThe first challenge was the violence of 1920 to 1922. Riots in Derry in June 1920 and in Belfast through 1920 to 1922 killed about 500 people, most of them Catholics, after thousands of Catholic and some Protestant labour workers were expelled from the shipyards. The IRA also campaigned along the border. The government answered by forming the Ulster Special Constabulary in late 1920, with A, B and C Specials recruited almost entirely from Protestants, and by replacing the RIC with the Royal Ulster Constabulary in June 1922. A third of RUC places were reserved for Catholics, but they never filled them. The Civil Authorities (Special Powers) Act of April 1922 allowed internment, curfews, the banning of meetings and arrest without warrant. It was renewed each year and made permanent in 1933. Several hundred republicans were interned in 1922, some on the prison ship Argenta. These measures defeated the IRA but gave the minority the sense of living under a partisan police and legal system.\n\nThe second challenge was the constitutional one, the threat that the border might be changed. Craig, Prime Minister from June 1921, refused to co-operate with the Boundary Commission, which the Treaty had provided for, and declined to nominate a commissioner. When the commission's expected findings were leaked to the Morning Post in November 1925, Eoin MacNeill resigned, and on 3 December 1925 the three governments agreed that the border would remain as it was. This was a diplomatic victory for the unionists, achieved by firmness rather than concession.\n\nThird, the unionists tightened their hold on the political system. Nationalist-controlled councils in Tyrone and Fermanagh that refused to recognise Belfast were dissolved and replaced by commissioners. The Local Government Act of 1922, introduced by Richard Dawson Bates, abolished proportional representation in local elections and redrew electoral areas, and proportional representation for Stormont elections was abolished in 1929, which helped the Unionists and weakened Labour and independents. Derry Corporation remained under unionist control from 1923 even though Catholics were a majority of the city's population. The party won every Stormont election by large margins, and an appeal on the border kept Protestant workers within it. Critics saw this as gerrymandering, and the minority had little voice in a state it was often reluctant to recognise. Craig's statement in April 1934 that he boasted of a Protestant parliament and a Protestant state, and Basil Brooke's urging unionists not to employ Catholics, were widely quoted by nationalists as proof of discrimination in jobs, housing and public life.\n\nThe fourth challenge was economic. Shipbuilding and linen, the main employers, fell back after the post-war boom ended in 1920 to 1921, and unemployment was around 20 per cent for much of the 1920s and about 25 per cent in the early 1930s. The government followed a policy of step by step with Britain, which kept pensions and unemployment pay in line with British levels, but it built little housing until the late 1930s and Belfast slums remained overcrowded. When Catholic and Protestant unemployed workers joined together in the Outdoor Relief strike and riots of October 1932, in which police shot dead two people, the government had to see the relief increased. This showed the danger that class discontent could cut across the religious divide on which unionist unity depended, and the Unionists were able to keep their support in part by returning to the border question.\n\nThe last test was the Second World War. Northern Ireland was at war from September 1939, and unionists treated the war effort as proof of loyalty in contrast to the neutral South. Harland and Wolff built warships, Short Brothers built Stirling bombers, farmers ploughed more land, and unemployment fell sharply. But the government's civil defence was poor. Belfast had few public shelters and little anti-aircraft cover, and in the raids of April and May 1941, especially on 15 to 16 April, about 1,000 people were killed. Criticism of the slow response and of an elderly cabinet under John Miller Andrews, who had succeeded Craig after his death in November 1940, led to Andrews's resignation in April 1943, and Basil Brooke took over.\n\nOverall, the Unionist government met its challenges successfully in the sense that the state survived violence, depression and bombing with its constitutional position secure. But it did so by emergency powers, a Protestant police force and manipulated electoral rules, and it made little attempt to win minority consent, so the response was effective for survival but left a legacy of grievance.",
