    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The Eucharistic Congress of June 1932 had a large impact on Irish life, though much of it was symbolic rather than legal. It strengthened the public standing of the Catholic Church, showed the close understanding between Church and state under the new Fianna Fáil government, gave the Free State a favourable international image and increased tension with the unionists in the North.\n\nThe Congress was an international Catholic gathering that was held in Dublin from 22 to 26 June 1932. It was chosen to mark the 1,500th anniversary of St Patrick's arrival in Ireland, and so it tied Catholicism closely to Irish national identity. It was planned under Cosgrave's government, with Archbishop Edward Byrne of Dublin at the head of the organising effort, but it took place under de Valera, who had become head of government on 9 March 1932. The Pope sent a legate, Cardinal Lorenzo Lauri, who was received by de Valera and Archbishop Byrne at Dún Laoghaire on 21 June, and some 36,000 schoolchildren lined the route to Dublin.\n\nThe most obvious impact was religious. Dublin was decorated with altars, flags and illuminations, even in the poorer districts, and parishes and thousands of volunteers organised processions and stewarding. On 25 June about 100,000 children attended a Mass in the Phoenix Park. On Sunday 26 June the closing Mass took place at an altar in the Phoenix Park before a crowd often put at around a million, though that figure may be exaggerated. John McCormack sang Panis Angelicus, and a message from Pope Pius XI was broadcast live from the Vatican. Afterwards four processions went to O'Connell Bridge, where Cardinal Lauri gave the benediction to a crowd of up to half a million. Such scenes showed how deeply Catholicism was rooted in the daily life of a population of whom the great majority were Catholic, and they confirmed the Church's place in a state where the clergy managed most schools and hospitals.\n\nSecond, the Congress showed and strengthened the alliance between Church and state. De Valera, who was known for his personal piety, gave the planning full support, and his government worked closely with the hierarchy. This relationship shaped the 1937 constitution, with its recognition of the special position of the Catholic Church and its ban on divorce, as well as social legislation in the 1930s. The Congress also made de Valera's new government look both Catholic and independent. The Governor-General, James McNeill, was not invited to the reception for the papal legate at Dublin Castle, and in response he held his own dinner and wrote to de Valera on 7 July 1932 to complain of discourtesy. The dispute helped lead to McNeill's dismissal in October 1932. The Congress therefore also served the government's wish to downgrade the Crown's representative.\n\nThird, the Congress affected the national confidence and image of the state. Only ten years after independence and a civil war, the Free State organised a huge international event without major disorder, using special trains, buses and sailings, the Garda and the Army to manage the crowds. Loudspeakers allowed vast crowds to hear the ceremonies, and a radio station was set up at Athlone for the occasion, which later became Radio Éireann in 1938. Foreign newspapers reported the event, and it gave the Free State international publicity as a stable, devout and independent country. It was a success for de Valera, whose prestige grew at home and abroad, although his opponents in Cumann na nGaedheal noted that Cosgrave's government had planned it while Fianna Fáil took the credit. The event also brought people together across party lines at a time of political change.\n\nFourth, the Congress had an impact on relations within the island. Catholic pilgrims travelling to and from Dublin were attacked in Northern Ireland, with trains stoned at places such as Portadown, Lisburn and Belfast, and at Ballymena a special train was attacked and some pilgrims were injured. Police made baton charges to protect them. Unionists saw the Congress as proof that the Free State was a Catholic state, which reinforced their case for partition, while Protestant leaders criticised the scale of the Catholic display. Northern nationalists on the other hand felt pride and a sense of belonging to an Irish Catholic nation. The Congress thus deepened the distrust between North and South and between Catholic and Protestant.\n\nIn judging its impact, it is important to see that the Congress changed no laws or institutions, and its influence was cultural and political. Contemporaries and the Catholic press regarded it as a great national triumph, whereas later historians have described it as the high point of the Church's public influence and a sign of the conformity that marked the 1930s. A moment when Church, state and nation appeared to be one shaped Irish life for a generation, and it is in that symbolic sense that the Congress had its greatest effect.",
