    "label": "2",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Domhnall Mac Amhlaigh and Maureen O'Hara both shaped how Irish emigrants and Ireland were seen abroad, one as an Irish-speaking labourer who recorded the emigrant's own experience in his own language, the other as a Dublin-born star who helped to create the film image of Ireland. Together they show the gap between the lived reality of emigration and the picture sold to the world.\n\nDomhnall Mac Amhlaigh was born in Galway in 1926 and was a native Irish speaker. Like thousands of others from the west of Ireland in the 1950s he left because there was little work at home, and he went to England in 1951. The 1950s were the decade of the emigration crisis, which led to the Commission on Emigration reporting in 1954. After the war Britain needed labour for reconstruction, and the Common Travel Area and the Ireland Act of 1949 let Irish people live and work there freely. Mac Amhlaigh was one of those who went, and he worked as a labourer, a navvy, on building sites and construction projects, and settled in Northampton, where he later died in 1989.\n\nWhat made him unusual was that he wrote about it. His best-known work, Dialann Deoraí, published in 1960 and later translated into English as An Irish Navvy, is a diary of the life of an Irish labourer in England. It describes the hard physical work, the lodgings, the pay, the company of other Irishmen and the pubs and dance halls where they met, and the pull of home. He wrote in Irish, which gave the emigrant experience a voice in a language that was itself under pressure. His book is valuable because it is a view from below. Where many official reports counted emigrants, he recorded what it felt like, including the loneliness and the exile of men who worked hard to build postwar Britain, but who were often ignored at home. It showed that the Irish in Britain, the largest emigrant group of the period, had a culture and a literature of their own.\n\nMaureen O'Hara offers a very different image. She was born Maureen FitzSimons in Ranelagh, Dublin, in 1920 and trained at the Abbey Theatre school before moving to Britain and then America. Her first Hollywood film was Jamaica Inn in 1939, directed by Alfred Hitchcock, and she went on to appear in How Green Was My Valley in 1941 and Miracle on 34th Street in 1947, which made her a major star. She became closely identified with fiery Irish heroines, and Irish-American audiences embraced her as their own.\n\nHer greatest contribution to the image of Ireland came through her work with the director John Ford, who was of Irish descent himself. They made Rio Grande in 1950 and The Quiet Man in 1952, in which she played the spirited Mary Kate Danaher opposite John Wayne. Filmed around Cong in Co. Mayo and in Connemara in 1951, with Ashford Castle as a main location, The Quiet Man won Academy Awards in 1953 for Ford's direction and for its colour cinematography. Its romantic, rural, comic Ireland, with green fields, a village and a fight, became the most influential film image of the country. Her screen image of the Irish woman as spirited and independent was partly a Hollywood creation, though her own sense of Irishness was shaped by her Dublin upbringing.\n\nThe film also had practical effects. It drew American visitors to Cong, which became a tourist destination, and its picture of Ireland suited the new tourist board, Bord Fáilte, set up in 1952. Yet Irish critics objected that it stereotyped rural people as comic and drunken, building on the old stage Irishman, and the film showed little of the Ireland of mass emigration that Mac Amhlaigh was living through. O'Hara later became a US citizen, received an honorary Academy Award in 2014 and died in 2015.\n\nIn conclusion, Mac Amhlaigh and O'Hara contributed to Irish images in different ways. Mac Amhlaigh gave a truthful, Irish-language account of the emigrant labourer's life, while O'Hara helped to create a glamorous, sentimental picture that attracted tourists and delighted Irish-American audiences. The contrast between the two reminds us that the images of Ireland created for export were often a long way from the real lives of those who left.",
