    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Joe Kennedy and John F. Kennedy had a major impact on Irish-American and Irish affairs, though in different ways. Joe showed that an Irish-American Catholic could reach the heights of American business and government, while his son, as the first Catholic president, made Irish Americans feel fully accepted and then visited Ireland in 1963 to the delight of the Irish public.\n\nJoseph P. Kennedy was born in Boston in 1888, the son of Patrick J. Kennedy, a Boston politician and businessman, and he married Rose Fitzgerald, daughter of Mayor John F. Fitzgerald. In a city where Irish Catholics had faced prejudice from the Protestant establishment, he built a fortune in banking, the stock market and the film industry. His wealth made him a symbol of Irish-American success, and it later funded his sons' political careers.\n\nHis career in government carried that success further. He was the first chairman of the Securities and Exchange Commission from 1934, set up to regulate the stock market after the crash of 1929, and in 1937 he led the US Maritime Commission. In 1938 President Roosevelt appointed him ambassador to Britain, the first Irish Catholic to hold the post. This was a major breakthrough for Irish Americans, who had long been excluded from the top diplomatic posts, and it showed how far the community had come since the days of the Know-Nothings.\n\nHowever, his impact was also controversial. As ambassador from 1938 to 1940 he supported Neville Chamberlain's policy of appeasement and doubted that Britain could win a war with Germany. He resigned in late 1940 after an interview that was widely seen as defeatist about Britain. Irish-American opinion was divided, with some proud of his success and others distrusting his conservative views and his closeness to the British government. Historians judge him a self-made businessman and an appeaser whose pessimism damaged his career, but whose ambitions shaped his sons.\n\nJohn F. Kennedy's impact on Irish Americans was greater. His great-grandfather Patrick Kennedy had left Dunganstown, Co. Wexford, in 1849, and his mother's family, the Fitzgeralds, also had Irish roots. When he was elected president in 1960 he became the first Catholic president of the United States. Many Irish Americans saw this as the acceptance of Irish Catholics in public life, a vindication after the defeat of Al Smith in 1928, when anti-Catholic feeling had played a large part. It marked the end of the long struggle against prejudice that had faced Irish emigrants since the Famine.\n\nFor Irish affairs the key event was Kennedy's visit to Ireland from 26 to 29 June 1963. He went to Dublin, Wexford and Dunganstown, Cork, Galway and Limerick, and he addressed the Oireachtas in Dublin on 28 June. The visit drew large crowds and intense press coverage and was widely celebrated as a moment of Irish pride. Kennedy's image in Ireland combined ancestry with success in American politics, and it encouraged Irish people to see their emigrant relatives as part of a shared story, after decades in which emigration had been seen as a loss. His assassination on 22 November 1963 was mourned widely, and his portrait hung in many Irish homes.\n\nHistorians argue, however, that the visit fitted the self-confidence of Seán Lemass's government as the economy opened to the world, rather than causing it. The visit celebrated the change that was already happening, and it helped Ireland to present itself to the world as modern and outward-looking, as well as the home of an emigrant people's roots. It also boosted roots tourism, which encouraged descendants of emigrants to visit the places their families had left.\n\nOverall, the Kennedys had a real but different impact. Joe's rise showed what was possible for Irish Americans and he was a controversial figure, while John's election and visit gave Irish America a sense of arrival and gave Ireland a lift in self-confidence. Their impact was greatest in symbolism and pride, rather than in changing policy.",
