    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Archbishop Daniel Mannix and Mother Mary Martin both played major roles within the Irish diaspora, though in very different fields. Mannix, as archbishop of Melbourne, was a leader of Irish-Australian Catholics and a political voice for them, while Martin, through the Medical Missionaries of Mary, carried Irish Catholic service and Irish women's professional skill into Africa. Both used the institutions of the Church to extend an Irish presence far beyond Ireland.\n\nDaniel Mannix was born at Charleville, Co. Cork, in 1864, trained at Maynooth and served as president of Maynooth College from 1903 until 1912. He was then sent to Australia as coadjutor archbishop of Melbourne in 1912 and became archbishop in 1917. In Melbourne he led a Catholic community that was largely Irish in origin and often felt itself the object of prejudice from the Protestant establishment. His role was to give that community a confident public leader who defended its schools and its interests, and who showed that Irish Catholics were a force to be reckoned with in Australian life.\n\nMannix's most famous role was political. During the First World War he took a leading part in opposing conscription in the referendums of 1916 and 1917, both of which were defeated. This brought him into open conflict with the prime minister, Billy Hughes, and with imperial loyalists who called him disloyal. For Irish Australians the campaign was a claim to be treated as equals rather than as a people to be compelled to fight in a British war, and it made Mannix the diaspora's most visible spokesman in Australia. He also clashed with the Orange Order and other Protestant organisations in Victoria, and the sectarian disputes of the period shaped Melbourne politics in his time.\n\nMannix also linked the diaspora to the cause of Ireland itself. After 1916 he criticised British policy in Ireland and supported Irish independence, which made him a target for loyalist opinion in Australia. In August 1920, on a voyage from the United States to Ireland, he was taken off his ship at sea by a British naval vessel and landed in England, and he was banned from entering Ireland. He went on to speak to Irish Catholic communities in Britain, so that the episode turned him into a symbol of the diaspora's sympathy with Irish nationalism. He remained a major figure in Australian Catholic and political life, supporting the anti-communist movement of B. A. Santamaria in the 1950s, which contributed to the Labor split of 1955, and he died in 1963 at the age of 99.\n\nMother Mary Martin played a quite different role, as the founder of an Irish missionary order that took Irish women abroad. She was born in Dublin in 1892 and trained as a nurse and midwife, and she went to Nigeria in the 1930s to work in mission medicine. Until 1936 the Catholic Church forbade religious sisters to practise obstetrics and surgery, and a Vatican instruction of that year lifted the restriction, which made her order possible. She founded the Medical Missionaries of Mary, dated to 1937 in Nigeria, with its mother house at Drogheda, Co. Louth. Her achievement was to open a new field in which Irish women could serve overseas as qualified professionals and not only as teachers or nurses under male direction.\n\nThe order trained doctors, nurses and midwives and set up hospitals, clinics and nursing schools in areas where few medical services existed. Its sisters worked in Nigeria, where the mission hospitals and nursing schools became central to its work, and the order later spread to other African countries. Martin directed this expansion and the training of its members, and she became known as Mother Mary of the Incarnation. Through the order Ireland acquired a presence in Africa that was based on healing, and the Irish who went abroad as medical missionaries became part of the wider diaspora of Irish people serving overseas. Historians present the Medical Missionaries of Mary as a pioneer of women-led medical missions, though they also ask how far medical work served colonial aims.\n\nIn Ireland, Martin and her sisters were praised by Catholic opinion as models of the mission spirit, and her work strengthened the image of Ireland as a nation that gave its sons and daughters to the service of the Church. She was the first woman to receive the Freedom of the Borough of Drogheda, in 1966, and she died in 1975.\n\nBoth figures therefore shaped the diaspora, but in contrasting ways. Mannix defended and led an established Irish Catholic community, giving it a political voice and a link to Irish nationalism, while Martin created an institution that sent Irish skill and dedication to Africa. Mannix's influence lay in politics and community leadership, Martin's in healthcare and the advancement of women in mission work, and each showed how far the Irish Catholic Church extended Ireland's reach across the world.",
