    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Archbishop John Charles McQuaid, who led the Dublin archdiocese from 1940 until 1972, was the most powerful churchman in Ireland and shaped schools, welfare, censorship and politics. Vatican II, which met from 1962 to 1965, brought reforms that he accepted cautiously and slowly, and which weakened the kind of clerical authority he represented.\n\nMcQuaid's influence on education and social provision was very large. The Church managed most primary and secondary schools, and McQuaid presided over the building of new churches and schools in the expanding Dublin suburbs. He founded charitable bodies such as the Catholic Social Service Conference, which provided welfare at a time when the state did little. Many admired his efficiency and devotion, and historians who stress this side of him argue that he built up services that the state had neglected. The result was that for much of his time in office the Church had a degree of control over the daily lives of ordinary Catholics that was rare in Western Europe.\n\nHis influence reached into politics and the relations between Church and state. He had direct access to ministers and used it. In the Mother and Child dispute of 1951 he and the bishops opposed Noel Browne's scheme of free care for mothers and children, objecting that it threatened parental rights and Church teaching on morality. The Inter-Party government dropped the scheme and Browne resigned. The affair showed that the hierarchy could determine government policy, and it left a lasting suspicion of Church interference in public affairs. The Health Act 1953 which followed introduced means-tested services instead.\n\nMcQuaid also influenced Irish culture through his support for censorship and his readiness to intervene in individual lives. Under the Censorship of Publications Acts of 1929 and 1946 thousands of books were banned, including works by leading Irish writers. In 1965 John McGahern's novel The Dark was banned, and McGahern lost his teaching job after the Archbishop of Dublin intervened. This became a symbol of the period for critics, who accused McQuaid of an authoritarian style. John Cooney's biography of 1999 portrays him as the ruler of Catholic Ireland, a figure whose methods came to seem out of date in the 1960s.\n\nVatican II then altered the Irish Church. Pope John XXIII opened the council on 11 October 1962 and it closed under Paul VI on 8 December 1965. It permitted the Mass in the vernacular instead of Latin, and the first Masses in English in Ireland were celebrated in 1965. Priests faced the congregation, laypeople could act as readers and give communion, and parish councils and lay organisations were encouraged. Seán Ó Riada's Irish-language Mass, Ceol an Aifrinn, written in 1968 for the church at Cúil Aodha, showed how the reforms and the cultural revival could join. The council also promoted ecumenism: the ban on Catholics attending Trinity College Dublin was lifted in 1970, and the first inter-church talks were held at Ballymascanlon in Co. Louth in 1973.\n\nMcQuaid attended the council and, when he came home in December 1965, assured Dubliners that it would not disturb their faith. He introduced liturgical change in Dublin, but slowly, and he was wary of ecumenism and of lay initiatives outside clerical control. In the longer term the council's declaration that religious freedom is a right weakened the claim that a Catholic state should have a special place in law, and its encouragement of independent thought on moral questions contributed to a questioning of Church authority. This questioning was increased by television, education and contact with Europe. The Late Late Show, which began in 1962, discussed marriage, contraception and the Church openly, and in 1966 an episode provoked a public condemnation from Bishop Ryan of Clonfert, while many viewers defended the programme. When Paul VI's encyclical Humanae Vitae of 1968 reaffirmed the ban on artificial contraception, many married Catholics found it hard to accept. Vocations to the priesthood and religious life began to fall from the late 1960s.\n\nOn balance, McQuaid's influence on Irish life was profound and mostly conservative: he strengthened the Church's grip on schools, welfare and public policy, and his involvement in censorship and politics later damaged its standing. Vatican II began to loosen that grip by changing worship and encouraging dialogue and independent judgement, though change was gradual. Nine in ten Catholics still attended Mass weekly in the early 1970s, and the huge crowd that greeted Pope John Paul II in the Phoenix Park in 1979 showed the Church's continuing strength. McQuaid retired in January 1972 and died in 1973, by which time his model of Church authority was already in decline.",
