    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Between 1949 and 1989 the most striking developments took place in education and in broadcasting. Education was expanded from a system for the minority into one open to nearly all, and broadcasting was transformed by the arrival of television in 1961 and the debates that followed.\n\nUntil the 1960s the state left most second-level education to the churches and to parents who could pay. Most children left school at 14 with only a primary education, since secondary schools charged fees and were mostly owned by religious orders, while vocational schools, set up under the 1930 Act, had lower status. As economic planning began after 1958 the question arose whether the country was educating enough people with the skills it needed. Patrick Hillery, Minister for Education from 1959, commissioned a survey with the help of the OECD. Its report, Investment in Education (1965), revealed wide differences in participation by social class and region and a shortage of trained teachers and technical skills, and it argued that education was an investment in growth.\n\nThe reforms followed quickly. In 1963 Hillery announced comprehensive schools, state-funded schools offering academic and practical subjects, and the first opened in 1966 in places such as Carraroe and Cootehill. In September 1966 Donogh O'Malley announced that free second-level education would begin the following year. The scheme started in September 1967, abolishing fees in schools that joined it, and was accompanied by free school transport so that rural pupils could reach a school. Enrolment rose steeply. The Primary Certificate was abolished in 1967, and the school-leaving age was raised to 15 in 1972. Community schools, usually formed by merging a secondary and a vocational school, appeared from the early 1970s, and a new child-centred primary curriculum was introduced in 1971.\n\nThird level also expanded. The Higher Education Authority was set up in 1968 and a grants scheme the same year helped students from poorer homes. Regional Technical Colleges opened from 1970 in towns such as Carlow, Dundalk, Athlone and Waterford, and the National Institute for Higher Education in Limerick opened in 1972 with an emphasis on technology and business. More women and more students from rural and working-class families reached college. There were limits, however. The churches kept control of management in most schools, many schools were overcrowded and short of qualified teachers, and free fees did not remove the cost of books, uniforms and travel, so poorer families and the west still lagged behind.\n\nIn broadcasting, radio had been run by the state since 2RN opened in 1926, but television came late. Viewers in the east and north could already receive BBC and Ulster Television, which showed that demand existed. The Broadcasting Authority Act 1960 set up the authority that became RTÉ, financed by licence fees and advertising, and Telefís Éireann began broadcasting on 31 December 1961, when President de Valera warned that television had great power for good and evil. Within a few years transmitters covered most of the country, and by the early 1970s most households had a set. Much early output was imported from Britain and the United States, but home-produced programmes drew large audiences, among them coverage of Gaelic games and The Riordans, a drama about a farming family that began in 1965. Telefís Scoile, launched in 1964, was one of the earliest schools television services in Europe.\n\nTelevision also became a forum for debate and a source of conflict with authority. The Late Late Show, presented by Gay Byrne from 1962, discussed sex, marriage, contraception and the Church, and in 1966 an item about a wife's honeymoon night caused uproar and a condemnation from Bishop Ryan of Clonfert, though many viewers defended the show. Seven Days, a current affairs series launched in 1966, investigated housing, poverty and emigration, and ministers resented its criticism. A 1969 programme on illegal moneylending led to a government-ordered inquiry and a row over RTÉ's independence, and the Troubles made coverage of the North a further source of friction. In 1971 the minister used his powers to order RTÉ not to broadcast certain material, and in 1972 the Authority was dismissed after a broadcast interview with an IRA leader. Lemass had called RTÉ an instrument of public policy, while the broadcasters believed they must be free to criticise.\n\nBroadcasting continued to expand and diversify. Raidió na Gaeltachta began in Connemara on 2 April 1972, RTÉ 2 started in 1978, pirate radio stations challenged RTÉ's monopoly in the 1970s and 1980s, and the Radio and Television Act 1988 licensed independent local radio. Overall, education opened opportunity to the whole population from 1967 onwards, while broadcasting ended the isolation of Irish homes and exposed church and state to public questioning, so that both developments helped to change the country.",
