    "model": "Robert Owen and Sir Robert Peel were both important in the way Britain responded to industrialisation, though they worked in very different ways. Owen, a mill-owner, proved at New Lanark that a factory community could be humane and profitable, and he campaigned for reform from outside government. Peel reformed the state from within, and his measures affected the whole country.\n\nOwen's first importance lay in what he achieved at New Lanark. Born in Newtown, Montgomeryshire, in 1771, he learned the cotton trade in Manchester and took over the New Lanark mills on the Clyde in 1800, after marrying the daughter of the owner, David Dale. He found a village of about 2,000 people, including some 500 pauper children, with a thirteen-hour mill day, filthy housing and widespread drunkenness. Believing that environment forms character, Owen stopped taking pauper apprentices, raised the age of employment so that children under ten left the mills, and by 1816 cut the working day to ten and three-quarter hours. He replaced fines and beatings with the Silent Monitor, a coloured block turned daily to show a worker's conduct. His reforms mattered because they showed that good treatment and profit could go together.\n\nHis second contribution was in education and community welfare. In 1816 he opened the Institute for the Formation of Character, a hall and school for adults, and an infant school for children from about one or two to six, which used songs, games and practical activities instead of rote learning. It was one of the earliest of its kind in Britain and drew visitors from home and abroad. The village store sold good goods near cost price and the sale of drink was controlled. New Lanark therefore became a reference point for later reformers, although critics said it depended on Owen's personal control and was paternalistic.\n\nOwen also mattered as a campaigner and thinker. In A New View of Society (1813-1816) he argued that circumstances make character and that society should improve them. He gave evidence to a parliamentary committee in 1816 and pressed for a law on child labour, which helped to produce the Cotton Mills and Factories Act of 1819, barring children under nine from cotton mills. The Act was weak, with no inspectors. His later schemes had mixed results, for New Harmony in Indiana, founded in 1825, collapsed within a few years and the Grand National Consolidated Trades Union of 1834 collapsed within months. Yet his writings inspired the co-operative movement, and the Rochdale Pioneers opened their store in 1844.\n\nPeel's importance was of a different order, because he used the power of government. As Home Secretary he founded the Metropolitan Police in 1829, a professional force that became a model for others. In the same year he and Wellington conceded Catholic emancipation to avoid civil conflict in Ireland. The Tamworth Manifesto of December 1834 set out a moderate reforming programme that became the basis of the modern Conservative Party. As Prime Minister from 1841 to 1846 he reintroduced income tax in 1842 to pay for tariff cuts, and the Bank Charter Act of 1844 regulated the issue of banknotes. The Factory Act of 1844 limited hours for women and children. His greatest act was the repeal of the Corn Laws in 1846, prompted by the Irish potato famine and backed by Cobden and Bright of the Anti-Corn Law League. It split the Conservative Party and Peel resigned in June 1846, but historians such as Norman Gash rank him among the greatest British statesmen of the century.\n\nOn balance, the two men were important in complementary ways. Owen worked outside government and sought co-operation and communal ownership, but his own reforms stayed largely confined to one community. Peel wanted to preserve private property and the social order while making it more efficient, and his legislation changed policing, banking, taxation and trade for the entire country. Owen's importance lay in inspiring ideas, while Peel's lay in lasting change, and the stronger claim to practical importance belongs to Peel.",
