    "marks": 100,
    "model": "I agree that France between 1815 and 1871 was both politically unstable and culturally productive. It passed through five regimes in under sixty years, none of which commanded broad agreement, yet it also produced some of the most celebrated literature, painting and drama of the age, and the two features were closely connected.\n\nThe instability began with the restored Bourbons. Louis XVIII issued the Charter in 1814, but only wealthy men who paid high taxes could vote, and his government carried out the White Terror of 1815 against Bonapartists and Revolutionaries. Charles X, who succeeded in 1824, tried to restore the power of the Church and the nobility through the sacrilege law of 1825 and compensation for the émigrés. After the liberal opposition won the elections of 1827 he appointed Polignac in 1829, and on 25 July 1830 he issued the July Ordinances dissolving the Chamber and restricting the press. Paris rose in the Three Glorious Days of 27-29 July and Charles X abdicated and went into exile in Britain.\n\nThe July Monarchy of Louis-Philippe was no more stable. The electorate reached only about 240,000 by 1848, under one per cent of the population, and Guizot, the leading minister from 1840, refused any extension of the vote. The regime faced workers' risings in Lyon in 1831 and 1834 and opposition from legitimists, Bonapartists and republicans. When Guizot banned a banquet in February 1848, fighting in Paris and the defection of the National Guard forced Louis-Philippe to abdicate on 24 February.\n\nThe Second Republic showed the instability at its most acute. Universal male suffrage, granted on 2 March 1848, raised the electorate from about 250,000 to about 9 million, but when the National Workshops for the unemployed were closed in June the workers rose in the June Days, which General Cavaignac crushed with many deaths and thousands of arrests. Louis-Napoleon Bonaparte was elected president in December 1848 with about three-quarters of the vote, and on 2 December 1851 he carried out a coup, followed by the Second Empire in 1852 as Napoleon III. The Empire brought economic growth and Haussmann's rebuilding of Paris, but it rested on one man. After defeat at Sedan on 2 September 1870 it collapsed, a republic was proclaimed on 4 September, and the Paris Commune of March to May 1871 was crushed in the Bloody Week. Each regime lacked consensus about the basis of government, and that explains the instability.\n\nCulturally, however, the period was extremely productive, above all in Romanticism. Victor Hugo's preface to Cromwell (1827) became a manifesto for Romantic drama, and his play Hernani in 1830 provoked a riot between Romantic supporters and classical critics and marked the victory of Romanticism in the French theatre. Notre-Dame de Paris (1831) revived interest in the Gothic past, and Les Misérables (1862), written in exile on Guernsey, portrayed the poor of France and became one of the most widely read novels of the century. Delacroix's Liberty Leading the People (1830) showed liberty leading the Parisian crowd over the barricades during the July Revolution. The Catholic revival, with Lamennais's L'Avenir in 1830, Ozanam's Society of St Vincent de Paul in 1833 and the apparitions at Lourdes in 1858, added a religious dimension, while Pasteur's work on fermentation and his swan-neck flask experiments of 1861 helped to establish the germ theory of disease.\n\nThe two sides were connected. Hugo was a politician as well as a writer, and the coup of 1851 drove him into exile, where Napoléon le Petit and Les Châtiments made him the symbol of republican resistance. His return to Paris on 5 September 1870, the day after the Third Republic was proclaimed, captured the same link between politics and letters. Delacroix's painting came directly out of a revolution. So the turbulence fed the creativity, and I agree with the proposition on both counts: France was unstable because no regime rested on settled consent, and it was culturally productive in literature, art, religion and science.",
