    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Mazzini and Bismarck moved their countries towards unity with very different degrees of effectiveness. Mazzini gave the Italian movement its ideal and its inspiration, but his own methods failed and the Italy of 1861 was not the republic he wanted. Bismarck, by contrast, achieved the unification of Germany within nine years of becoming Minister-President, though at the price of a Prussian-dominated, semi-authoritarian state.\n\nMazzini's greatest effect was ideological. Born in Genoa in 1805, he joined the Carbonari, was arrested and exiled in 1830, and in 1831 founded Young Italy at Marseille, a secret society, largely of young men, that aimed at a unified republic with the people as sovereign. Its motto was God and the People. He stressed duty to the nation in The Duties of Man and founded Young Europe in 1834 to link revolutionary movements. These ideas spread the notion of a united Italy beyond the secret societies of the 1820s, whose risings in Naples and Piedmont had been crushed by Austria, and they shaped the whole Risorgimento.\n\nIn practical terms, though, Mazzini's conspiracies failed. His invasion of Savoy in 1834 was easily defeated, and from 1837 he ran his network of exiles from London, which limited his control over events. In 1849 he was one of the triumvirs of the Roman Republic, defended by Garibaldi, but French troops sent by Louis-Napoleon captured Rome in July 1849 and restored the Pope. His reliance on popular insurrection, without kings or foreign help, could not defeat Austria's army or the French garrison. He rejected the monarchical Italy of 1861 as a betrayal, was arrested in Sicily in 1870 trying to start a republican rising, and died in Pisa in 1872. Unity was achieved without him, largely by methods he opposed.\n\nBismarck was far more effective because he commanded the means of a strong state. Appointed Minister-President in September 1862 to break the deadlock over Roon's army reform, he declared that the great questions would be decided by blood and iron, and he collected taxes without an approved budget. Prussia already had the Zollverein and the industry of the Ruhr and Silesia, and Bismarck exploited this advantage through Realpolitik. He gained Russian goodwill in the Alvensleben Convention of 1863 and in 1864 allied with Austria to defeat Denmark, which forced the Treaty of Vienna and gave the duchies to the two victors. The Convention of Gastein in 1865 then set up the quarrel with Austria.\n\nThe war of 1866 showed his skill. He secured an alliance with Italy in April, won Napoleon III's neutrality after meeting him at Biarritz in 1865, and fought Austria with the needle-gun, Moltke's planning and rapid rail mobilisation. The victory at Königgrätz on 3 July 1866 led to the Treaty of Prague, which dissolved the German Confederation and excluded Austria, and Prussia annexed Hanover, Hesse-Kassel, Nassau and Frankfurt. Bismarck then persuaded William I to halt at Nikolsburg to keep Austria as a future friend. The last step was the war with France. Using the Hohenzollern candidature and the edited Ems telegram, he provoked France into declaring war on 19 July 1870, which brought the southern states into the war on Prussia's side. After Sedan on 2 September, the German Empire was proclaimed at Versailles on 18 January 1871. Historians disagree whether he followed a master plan or, as A. J. P. Taylor argued, improvised, but either way the result was achieved.\n\nOverall, Bismarck was by far the more effective. His success had limits, for the Empire left Austrian Germans outside it, contained Polish, Danish and French-speaking minorities, and was dominated by Prussia, with the Chancellor answerable to the emperor and not the Reichstag. Mazzini deserves credit as the prophet who made unity an ideal and gave it a following, but he failed to deliver it. Bismarck combined diplomacy, military power and political skill to create the German Empire in 1871, even if it fell short of liberal hopes.",
