    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The churches influenced social change in both France and Great Britain, but in different directions. In France the restored Catholic Church shaped education, charity and conservative politics, and was often seen as a defender of the old order. In Britain the Anglican and Nonconformist churches responded to the problems of the industrial towns through schools, missions, reform campaigns and the temperance cause.\n\nIn France the Church had been attacked by the Revolution and Napoleon, and after 1815 it recovered its role in education and public life. Under Charles X, who came to the throne in 1824, it was closely tied to the monarchy. The sacrilege law of 1825 and the compensation of the émigrés showed a regime trying to restore the authority of Church and nobility. This alliance of throne and altar meant the Church was identified with reaction and shared in the unpopularity that helped bring down Charles X in 1830. Some Catholics tried a different path. Lamennais founded L'Avenir in 1830 and argued for the separation of Church and state and for freedom of the press and association. Pope Gregory XVI condemned these ideas in Mirari vos in 1832 and Lamennais broke with the Church in 1834, which shows that the official Church resisted liberal social change.\n\nYet the French Church also brought practical social change. In 1833 Frédéric Ozanam founded the Society of St Vincent de Paul, a lay Catholic charity whose members visited the poor and the sick in their homes. The Church also won a large role in schooling. The Falloux Law of 1850 allowed it to open secondary schools freely and gave bishops places on education councils. Popular devotion grew too, especially after the apparitions to Bernadette Soubirous at Lourdes in 1858, which created a major pilgrimage cult. At the top, Pius IX proclaimed the Immaculate Conception in 1854 and condemned liberalism in the Syllabus of Errors in 1864, protected in Rome by a French garrison under Napoleon III. The Church therefore shaped French society by influencing what children were taught and what the faithful believed, but it did so by backing order and tradition.\n\nIn Britain the churches were more directly involved in the social problems of industrialisation. Methodism, founded by John Wesley, grew rapidly among workers in the industrial towns and mining districts, giving them community and moral discipline. Sunday schools and ragged schools, run mainly by churches and charities, educated poor children before the state did much, and city missions offered preaching, relief and education in the towns. Their limits were exposed by the religious census of 1851, which revealed that large numbers of working people did not attend church. William Booth's Christian Mission in East London in 1865, later the Salvation Army, was one answer to this gap.\n\nBritish churchmen also campaigned for reform. Evangelicals such as Lord Shaftesbury led the movement for factory reform, and the Preston teetotal movement of 1832 helped launch the temperance cause, since drunkenness was seen as a cause of poverty and family breakdown. Within the Church of England, Keble's assize sermon of 1833 began the Oxford Movement, and from 1848 the Christian Socialists, F. D. Maurice and Charles Kingsley, tried to apply Christian principles to industrial society. Here the churches were pressing for change rather than defending the status quo.\n\nOverall, the churches influenced social change in both countries, but the British churches did so more through reform and the French Church more through education and charity within a conservative framework. Their influence was greatest in education and relief of the poor, and weakest where they met liberal or working-class demands, and their work did little to address the underlying causes of poverty.",
