    "label": "1",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Between 1871 and 1914 Germany's new Reich was troubled by tensions at home and abroad. Bismarck's Kulturkampf set the state against about a third of its own people, and Wilhelm II's naval policy set Germany against Britain and strained its finances. In each case a leader's confidence in power produced tensions that outweighed the gains.\n\nThe Kulturkampf arose because the Reich was about two thirds Protestant, with a large Catholic minority. After the First Vatican Council declared papal infallibility in 1870, Bismarck feared that German Catholics owed their first loyalty to Rome, and he saw the Catholic Centre Party, founded in 1870, as an enemy of the new state. He also distrusted the Polish Catholics in the east, while liberals welcomed an attack on the Church as a defence of modern society; it was the liberal Rudolf Virchow who called the campaign a Kulturkampf, a struggle for civilisation. The tension came from a Protestant, Prussian-dominated state facing a Church that claimed an authority beyond it.\n\nThe campaign was carried out through a series of laws. In 1871 the pulpit law made it an offence for clergy to criticise the state in sermons. The Jesuits were expelled in 1872 and the School Supervision Law put schools under state inspection. The May Laws of 1873, piloted by the Prussian culture minister Adalbert Falk, required priests to study at state universities and pass state examinations, and made state approval necessary for church appointments. Civil marriage became compulsory in 1875. Pope Pius IX condemned the laws, bishops were imprisoned or exiled, and by 1876 many parishes had no priest. Catholics resisted rather than submitted, and the Centre Party doubled its seats and became the second-largest party in the Reichstag. By 1878-79 Bismarck needed the Centre's support for his tariffs and against socialism, and the election of Pope Leo XIII gave him a negotiating partner. Most of the laws were repealed or relaxed in 1880-83 and 1886-87. The Kulturkampf had strengthened Catholic unity and shown the limits of Bismarck's power.\n\nWilhelm II's naval policy created a different tension, between Germany and Britain. Wilhelm admired the Royal Navy and, influenced by Mahan's The Influence of Sea Power upon History, wanted a fleet to match Germany's new status. Admiral Tirpitz, state secretary of the Navy Office from 1897, planned a battle fleet concentrated in the North Sea, and his risk theory held that a fleet strong enough to damage the Royal Navy would deter Britain from war. The Navy Laws of 1898 and 1900, the second planning 38 battleships, were backed by a Navy League with over a million members. To Britain, an island power that depended on command of the sea, this was a direct challenge.\n\nBritain reacted by competing and by seeking friends, so the fleet produced the opposite of what Tirpitz intended. Admiral Fisher concentrated the Royal Navy in home waters, and HMS Dreadnought, launched in 1906, made existing battleships obsolete and forced both sides to start again. Germany built its own dreadnoughts and speeded up its programme in 1908, and in 1909 the British public demanded more ships with the cry 'We want eight and we won't wait'. Britain ended its isolation by agreements with Japan in 1902, France in 1904 and Russia in 1907, partly because of the German fleet. The Haldane mission of 1912 failed because Germany wanted a promise of British neutrality while Britain wanted limits on shipbuilding. A possible friend had become a rival.\n\nThe fleet also caused tension inside Germany, because it cost vast sums. It contributed to the finance crisis of 1908-09 and sharpened disputes between Conservatives, who opposed an inheritance tax, and the left. In the war the fleet stayed largely in harbour apart from the indecisive battle of Jutland in 1916, and resentment among sailors led to the mutinies at Wilhelmshaven and Kiel in 1918. Overall, both policies left Germany worse off. The Kulturkampf was an attempt to impose unity that deepened division and made the Centre a permanent force, and the navy was meant to bring security and prestige but brought encirclement, debt and quarrels at home. The tensions were greater than the gains.",
