    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Between 1871 and 1920 science, technology and medicine changed how Europeans lived, travelled, communicated and fought. The key developments were germ theory and the surgical advances that followed it, the discovery of X-rays and radioactivity, the spread of electricity, the telephone and wireless, and the birth of the motor car and the aeroplane. Many of these advances saved lives, but the same science also made war deadlier.\n\nThe most important medical development was germ theory. Louis Pasteur showed in the 1860s that microbes cause fermentation and decay and argued that they cause disease. He developed vaccines against anthrax in 1881 and rabies, and in 1885 he saved the life of a nine-year-old boy, Joseph Meister, who had been bitten by a rabid dog. In Germany Robert Koch perfected the growing of bacteria in pure culture and identified the anthrax bacterium in 1876, the tuberculosis bacillus in 1882 and the cholera bacterium in 1883, winning the Nobel Prize for Medicine in 1905. Once doctors knew what caused diseases, they could fight them.\n\nGerm theory transformed surgery and treatment. Joseph Lister used carbolic acid on wounds and instruments from the mid-1860s, sharply cutting deaths from infection, and the fully sterile methods of asepsis were developed in Germany in the 1880s and 1890s. Together with anaesthetics such as chloroform, these made longer and safer operations possible. Emil von Behring's antitoxins against diphtheria and tetanus in 1890 and Paul Ehrlich's Salvarsan in 1909, the first effective drug against syphilis, extended the revolution to treatment. Medicine had moved from guesswork to science.\n\nA second great area of advance was the study of radiation. Wilhelm Röntgen discovered X-rays in Würzburg in November 1895, and doctors quickly used them to find broken bones and bullets, which won him the first Nobel Prize for Physics in 1901. Marie Curie, born in Warsaw and working in Paris, coined the term radioactivity and with Pierre Curie announced polonium and radium in 1898. She shared the Nobel Prize for Physics in 1903 and won the Chemistry prize in 1911, the first person to win Nobel Prizes in two sciences. In the First World War her mobile X-ray units, the petites Curies, brought radiography close to the front. The cost of the new knowledge was shown by her death in 1934 from an illness probably caused by radiation.\n\nTechnology transformed communications. Alexander Graham Bell patented the telephone in 1876, and Thomas Edison developed a long-lasting electric light bulb in 1879. Power stations in the 1880s brought electric lighting, trams and underground railways to cities. Guglielmo Marconi sent wireless signals from 1895-96 and in December 1901 claimed to receive a signal across the Atlantic, sharing the Nobel Prize in 1909. Wireless proved its value at sea when it was used to call for help as the Titanic sank in 1912. Distance counted for less, and news, business and rescue all moved faster.\n\nTransport was revolutionised by the internal combustion engine. Nikolaus Otto built the first practical four-stroke engine in 1876, and Gottlieb Daimler and Karl Benz, working independently, produced the first petrol vehicles in the 1880s. Benz patented his Motorwagen in 1886, and Bertha Benz's 106 km drive in 1888 proved its usefulness. Henry Ford launched the Model T in 1908 and the moving assembly line at Highland Park in 1913, cutting the time to build a chassis from over twelve hours to about ninety minutes, although mass motoring came to Europe mainly after 1920. In the air, the Wright brothers made the first powered flight at Kitty Hawk on 17 December 1903, Blériot crossed the Channel in 1909, and Alcock and Brown flew the Atlantic non-stop in 1919.\n\nOverall the greatest developments were those in medicine, which saved millions of lives, and in communications and transport, which shrank distances. But the same science served war. Aircraft, motor lorries, chemistry and wireless were all used in the First World War, producing deadlier weapons, and the benefits of medicine reached the poor only slowly. The period shows that scientific advance could serve destruction as well as healing.",
