    "label": "1",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Italy and Germany both embraced dictatorship because the First World War and its aftermath left their parliamentary systems weak, their people humiliated and frightened of revolution, and their economies in crisis. Fascist and Nazi leaders offered strength and national revival, and conservative elites helped them into power. In both countries democracy was undermined by those who should have defended it as well as by those who attacked it.\n\nThe first cause was national disillusionment after the war. Italy had suffered heavy casualties and debt, and the peace settlement gave her less than the Treaty of London of 1915 had promised. Gabriele D'Annunzio called this a mutilated victory, and his seizure of Fiume in 1919 showed the weakness of the state. In Germany the Treaty of Versailles took territory, limited the army, and in Article 231 placed war guilt on Germany. Reparations were set at 132 billion gold marks in 1921, a sum many Germans thought impossible and humiliating. The stab in the back myth blamed the politicians of 1918 for a defeat the army had not admitted. Dictators could promise to avenge these wrongs.\n\nSecond, both democracies seemed unable to govern. Italy had five prime ministers between 1919 and 1922, and proportional representation produced weak coalitions. Weimar's proportional system also fragmented the Reichstag, and Article 48 let the President rule by emergency decree. After 1930 Brüning, Papen and Schleicher governed through presidential cabinets without parliamentary majorities, so that parliamentary government had already failed before Hitler arrived. Many judges, officers and civil servants in Germany stayed hostile to the republic, and the courts treated right-wing murderers far more leniently than left-wing ones.\n\nThird, fear of communism and economic distress drove middle-class and wealthy people towards dictators. The Biennio Rosso of 1919 to 1920 brought factory occupations and land seizures in Italy, and landowners and industrialists welcomed Fascist squads that smashed socialist unions. In Germany the hyperinflation of 1923, when a dollar was worth about 4.2 trillion marks, wiped out middle-class savings. After the Wall Street Crash, unemployment reached about six million by early 1932. Brüning's deflation deepened the misery, and farmers, shopkeepers and the unemployed turned to Hitler's promises of jobs, bread and national revival. Nazi support rose from 12 seats in 1928 to 107 in September 1930 and 230 in July 1932.\n\nFourth, the dictators used violence, propaganda and the legal system to win support. Mussolini's squadristi attacked socialist and Catholic organisations from 1920, and the March on Rome of October 1922 frightened the King into refusing a state of siege and inviting Mussolini to form a government. Hitler failed with the Munich Putsch in 1923, then chose the legal road, while the SA's street violence showed strength. Goebbels ran mass rallies, posters and newspapers, and in 1932 Hitler toured the country by aeroplane. Both movements offered a strong leader and national unity, which attracted people who had lost faith in party politics.\n\nFinally, elites helped dictators to power, believing they could control them. Giolitti included Fascists in his 1921 electoral bloc, and Victor Emmanuel III chose Mussolini over confrontation. In January 1933 Papen persuaded Hindenburg to appoint Hitler Chancellor in a cabinet with only three Nazis, convinced that conservatives could hold him in check. Once in office, both leaders removed opposition quickly. Mussolini accepted responsibility for violence after the murder of Matteotti in 1924 and banned rival parties by 1926. Hitler, too, built a one-party state within months of taking office.\n\nIn short, dictatorship was embraced in both countries because wartime losses, economic collapse and fear of revolution discredited weak democracies, and because skilled leaders, supported by short-sighted conservatives, exploited this. Italy's collapse was mainly a political one, whereas Germany's turned on the Depression, but in each case dictatorship was welcomed by many people as the only answer to disorder.",
