    "label": "2",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Between 1920 and 1939 Britain faced serious economic problems, above all the decline of her staple industries and persistent mass unemployment, and these produced social problems of poverty, poor health and bitter division between regions. Britain was in many ways two nations, one prosperous and growing in the south-east and Midlands, the other trapped in decline in Wales, the north and Scotland. Governments dealt with the crises with limited success.\n\nThe first challenge was the post-war slump. A short boom in 1919 and 1920 collapsed as prices fell and export markets were lost, and unemployment rose above two million by mid-1921. The Geddes Axe of 1922 cut public spending on education, housing and the armed services, which deepened the problem. The coal industry, returned to private ownership in 1921, saw owners try to cut miners' wages. On Black Friday, 15 April 1921, the railwaymen and transport workers of the Triple Alliance refused to strike in support, and the miners were defeated. Industrial relations were poisoned for years.\n\nSecond, the staple industries of coal, cotton, shipbuilding, iron and steel, the base of Victorian prosperity, went into long decline. Lancashire cotton lost markets to Japan and India, and shipyards on the Tyne, Wear and Clyde closed as world trade collapsed. In April 1925 Churchill, as Chancellor, returned sterling to the gold standard at the pre-war rate of about 4.86 dollars. Keynes argued in The Economic Consequences of Mr Churchill that this made exports too dear and would force wages down. The coal owners demanded cuts, the miners were locked out, and the TUC called the General Strike of 4 to 12 May 1926. The government used volunteers and the Organisation for the Maintenance of Supplies, and the strike was called off after nine days. The miners stayed out for months and went back on worse terms, and the Trade Disputes Act of 1927 weakened the unions further.\n\nThird, the Depression made everything worse. After the Wall Street Crash, registered unemployment peaked at about three million, over 20 per cent of insured workers, in 1932 and 1933. The May Committee in 1931 forecast a deficit of about 120 million pounds and recommended cuts in benefit. The Labour Cabinet split over a 10 per cent cut, and Ramsay MacDonald formed a National Government in August 1931 and was expelled by his party. Britain left the gold standard on 21 September 1931. The National Government then brought in tariffs under the Import Duties Act of 1932 and imperial preference at Ottawa, and cheap money, with Bank Rate at 2 per cent from June 1932, helped recovery and house building.\n\nFourth, the social effects were severe and uneven. In distressed areas unemployment ran above 30 per cent in many towns, and higher in Jarrow and Merthyr Tydfil. J.B. Priestley's English Journey, Orwell's The Road to Wigan Pier and the Pilgrim Trust's Men Without Work described malnutrition, overcrowding and high infant mortality. The household means test of 1931, extended by the Unemployment Act of 1934, humiliated families by counting the income of every member. The National Unemployed Workers' Movement organised hunger marches to London, in 1932 presenting a petition of a million signatures that Parliament refused to receive, and the Jarrow Crusade followed in 1936. Meanwhile, in the south and Midlands real wages rose, about four million houses were built between the wars, and new industries in motor vehicles and electrical goods grew around London and Birmingham.\n\nFinally, the government response was limited. The Special Areas Act of 1934 offered grants for the worst-hit regions, but the Commissioners had little money. The abandonment of gold, cheap money and tariffs helped the south, while unemployment stayed above a million for most of the 1930s. The depressed regions did not truly recover until rearmament from 1936 created demand for steel, shipbuilding and engineering.\n\nIn conclusion, Britain's central problem was a lasting imbalance between declining old industries and growing new ones, which caused deep regional poverty beside rising prosperity. Governments kept the currency and parliamentary system stable, but they failed to relieve unemployment in the distressed areas, and that failure shaped post-war demands for full employment and the welfare state.",
