    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Stalin's leadership, in peace and in war, was characterised by a ruthless pursuit of personal power, the forced modernisation of the Soviet Union at enormous human cost, rule by terror and a carefully built cult of personality. In wartime the same authoritarian methods continued, but they were combined with patriotic appeals that won popular support and helped to secure victory.\n\nStalin first showed his skill in manoeuvring for power. As General Secretary from April 1922 he controlled party appointments and built a network of loyal supporters, even though Lenin's Testament had criticised his rudeness and called for his removal. After Lenin's death in 1924 he allied with Zinoviev and Kamenev against Trotsky, then defeated all three when they formed the United Opposition in 1926. Trotsky was expelled from the party in 1927 and exiled from the Soviet Union in 1929. Stalin then turned on the Right Opposition of Bukharin, Rykov and Tomsky, adopting the rapid industrialisation of the Left that he had once condemned. By 1929 every rival was beaten, which shows a leader who was cunning and unprincipled and who put power before consistency.\n\nSecond, Stalin pursued modernisation by force through the Five-Year Plans. Gosplan drew up the first plan, launched in October 1928, which gave priority to coal, steel, electricity and machinery over consumer goods. New industrial centres such as Magnitogorsk were built in the Urals, and steel output rose from about four million tonnes in 1928 to about eighteen million by 1940. The Stakhanovite movement, begun after the miner Alexei Stakhanov's reported record in 1935, was used to drive up output. Yet the costs were severe: wages fell, housing was overcrowded, quality was poor, official figures were inflated and forced labour was used on large projects. Stalin was willing to sacrifice living standards to build an industrial power.\n\nThird, collectivisation revealed his ruthlessness towards whole social groups. In late 1929 he announced the liquidation of the kulaks as a class, and peasants were forced into collective farms where land and livestock were pooled under state control. Kulaks were deported or killed, and peasants slaughtered their animals in protest. Excessive grain requisitions combined with poor harvests to produce the famine of 1932-33, which killed several million people, mainly in Ukraine, the North Caucasus and Kazakhstan. The regime denied the famine and restricted movement so that starving peasants could not reach the towns. Stalin treated the peasantry as a resource to be extracted from, not a people to be protected.\n\nFourth, Stalin ruled by terror and built a personality cult. The murder of Kirov in December 1934 gave him the pretext for repression. Between 1936 and 1938 the Moscow show trials saw old Bolsheviks such as Zinoviev, Kamenev and Bukharin confess to invented crimes and be shot, and the army leadership, including Marshal Tukhachevsky, was purged in 1937. The NKVD and the Gulag held millions of prisoners. At the same time propaganda, the rewritten history of the Short Course of 1938, and towns and streets named after him portrayed Stalin as the wise father of the people and the heir of Lenin. Fear and worship together gave him complete control, though the loss of so many officers and managers weakened the state.\n\nWhen war came, Stalin's leadership was at its most contradictory. The Nazi-Soviet Pact of August 1939 bought time and territory, but the Red Army, weakened by the purges, suffered heavy losses after Germany invaded on 22 June 1941. Yet Stalin recovered. He chaired the State Defence Committee, became Supreme Commander in August 1941 and stayed in Moscow during the German advance, which helped morale. About 1,500 factories were moved east of the Urals, helped by Lend-Lease aid from Britain and America. His methods remained harsh: Order 227 of July 1942, 'Not one step back', brought blocking detachments and penal battalions, and nationalities such as the Chechens and Crimean Tatars were deported on suspicion of collaboration. At the same time he eased pressure on the Orthodox Church in 1943 and called the conflict the Great Patriotic War, appealing to Russian nationalism. Victory at Stalingrad in February 1943 and Kursk in July 1943 turned the tide, and the Red Army reached Berlin in 1945, at a cost of about 27 million Soviet dead.\n\nOverall, Stalin's leadership was consistently authoritarian, ruthless and obsessed with control in peace and war alike. In peace he modernised the economy at the price of famine and terror; in war he mobilised the nation and won a great victory, again at terrible human cost, and emerged at Yalta and Potsdam as the leader of a superpower. His methods varied with circumstances, but his aim was always to preserve and extend his own power.",
