    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Margaret Thatcher, Prime Minister from 1979 to 1990, was one of the most consequential and divisive British leaders of the century. Her successes lay in defeating inflation and trade union power, in winning the Falklands War and three elections, and in her influence abroad. Her failures were mass unemployment, the decline of manufacturing, social division, and in the end a collapse in her own party over Europe and the poll tax.\n\nHer first success was her economic programme. Britain in 1979 was struggling with high inflation after the oil shocks, and Thatcher's government pursued monetarism, raising interest rates and cutting public spending. Inflation fell to low levels by the mid-1980s. She also sold state assets, including British Telecom in 1984, deregulated the City in 1986 and allowed council tenants to buy their homes from 1980. Supporters argue that she reversed Britain's relative decline and created a more flexible and enterprising economy, and these reforms were largely kept by later governments.\n\nHer second success was in taking on the trade unions. The Employment Acts of 1980 and 1982 and the Trade Union Act of 1984 restricted secondary picketing and the closed shop and required ballots. The miners' strike of March 1984 to March 1985 was the decisive test. The government had built up coal stocks and used the police, and with violent clashes at Orgreave in June 1984 and Arthur Scargill's decision to strike without a national ballot, the National Union of Mineworkers was defeated. Union power, which had troubled governments throughout the 1970s, was broken, though at the cost of bitterness in mining communities and the speeding up of pit closures.\n\nPolitically she was highly successful. The recapture of the Falklands after Argentina's invasion of 2 April 1982, completed on 14 June, restored national pride and helped her re-election in 1983. She won the general elections of 1979, 1983 and 1987 and became the longest-serving prime minister of the twentieth century. She worked closely with Reagan, backing the deployment of cruise missiles at Greenham Common, and she met Gorbachev in December 1984 and helped persuade Reagan that he was someone to do business with. In Europe she won the rebate agreed at Fontainebleau in 1984, which cut Britain's contribution to the budget.\n\nAgainst this, her economic policies carried heavy costs. Unemployment passed three million in 1982, and the early 1980s recession hit manufacturing in the north, Scotland and Wales hardest, with riots in English cities in 1981. Heavy industries like steel, shipbuilding and coal declined sharply, and critics see deindustrialisation and widening regional inequality as a lasting price. Historians also debate whether her policies were a real break with the post-war settlement or part of trends already under way, since the oil shocks had already hit industry.\n\nHer record on Europe and Northern Ireland was mixed, and it brought her downfall. She signed the Anglo-Irish Agreement with Garret FitzGerald in November 1985, giving the Irish government a consultative role in Northern Ireland, but violence continued. She accepted the Single European Act in 1986 because it advanced the single market, but in her Bruges speech of September 1988 she attacked federalism, and she opposed Delors's social policy and economic and monetary union. Britain joined the Exchange Rate Mechanism only in October 1990. Geoffrey Howe resigned in November 1990 over her European policy, Michael Heseltine challenged her, and the unpopular poll tax added to her troubles. After the first ballot gave her too narrow a lead she withdrew, resigning on 22 November 1990 and being succeeded by John Major.\n\nOverall, Thatcher's successes were real and lasting in the areas of inflation, union power and national confidence, but they were bought with high unemployment and social division, and her strengths of resolve and refusal to compromise also destroyed her. She remains a leader judged very differently by her admirers and critics.",
