    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Between 1945 and 1992 youth and popular culture, sport and the mass media gave young people in Europe money, a shared identity and a public voice as never before. The changes were real but uneven: in the West youth culture was commercial and openly rebellious, while in the East it was watched and restricted by the state.\n\nThe most basic change was the emergence of the teenager as a distinct social group. The term spread from the USA in the 1940s and 1950s, and by the 1950s young people had money to spend on records, clothes and cinema. Longer secondary schooling gave them a common identity, and the cheap 45 rpm single, teenage magazines and Radio Luxembourg created a youth media of their own. Rock 'n' roll arrived in the mid-1950s, with Bill Haley's Rock Around the Clock reaching the British charts in 1955 and Elvis Presley's first hits in 1956. Young people now had music that set them apart from their parents, and they were a market that record companies and advertisers wanted to reach.\n\nMusic and fashion became the way young people expressed rebellion and identity. Teddy boys in their Edwardian-style jackets were linked in the press with delinquency, and films such as The Wild One and Rebel Without a Cause fed adult fears. In the 1960s the Beatles became the first global pop stars: their appearance on The Ed Sullivan Show on 9 February 1964 brought Beatlemania to America, and albums such as Rubber Soul and Sgt. Pepper showed that pop could be an art form. The clashes of Mods and Rockers at Brighton, Margate and Clacton at Easter 1964 produced a moral panic, while Mary Quant's miniskirt and Carnaby Street made London a centre of youth fashion. Punk in 1976-77, with the Sex Pistols and The Clash, voiced the anger of unemployed young people in the recession. Style had become a way of declaring class and attitude.\n\nYoung people also became political. The student protests of 1968 began at Nanterre in March and moved to the Sorbonne in May, and the Night of the Barricades on 10-11 May was followed by a general strike of about ten million workers that shook de Gaulle's government. Students in West Germany, led by Rudi Dutschke, protested against the Vietnam War and the Nazi past. John Lennon used his fame for peace, with the Amsterdam bed-in of March 1969 and the anthem Give Peace a Chance. The protests overthrew no government, but they changed attitudes to authority, sexuality and the universities and influenced the women's, peace and environmental movements of the 1970s.\n\nThe mass media and sport carried these changes to millions. Television spread from a few sets in 1950 to most households in Western Europe by the 1970s, helped in Britain by the Coronation of June 1953. ITV, begun in 1955, ended the BBC's monopoly, and pirate stations such as Radio Caroline, from 1964, played the pop music the BBC would not. The Eurovision network, founded in 1954, and the Eurovision Song Contest, begun in 1956, linked audiences across borders. In June 1967 the Beatles appeared on Our World, the first live global satellite broadcast, and Live Aid in July 1985, organised by Bob Geldof, showed young people acting on a world stage to raise money for famine in Ethiopia. Sport was a shared experience too: England's World Cup win of 1966 was a mass television event and the European Cup, begun in 1955-56, made club football continental, while television money made Pelé, Johan Cruyff and George Best youth idols. Sport was also tied to the Cold War, since East Germany used a state-sponsored system, including doping revealed after 1990, to win medals, and the boycotts of Moscow in 1980 and Los Angeles in 1984 denied young athletes their chance to compete.\n\nIn the East the experience was very different. The Komsomol and East Germany's Free German Youth tried to channel young people into loyalty to the party, but many listened to Western music on Radio Luxembourg and Radio Free Europe or on hand-copied tapes. East Germany banned Western beat music in 1965 and tolerated it a little in the 1970s, the stilyagi in the Soviet Union copied Western fashion and jazz, and the arrest of the Plastic People of the Universe in 1976 helped inspire Charter 77 in Czechoslovakia. Overall, youth culture and the media gave young people money, identity, a voice and a window on the wider world, and they challenged the authority of schools, churches and, in the East, the state. Yet much of this culture was commercially packaged, and its effects varied by class, gender and country. It was nonetheless a major turning point in the lives of young people in Europe.",
