    "label": "1",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Mohandas Gandhi made the single most important contribution to Indian affairs in the first half of the twentieth century. He turned the Indian National Congress into a mass movement, gave Indian nationalism a distinctive method in non-violent resistance, and campaigned for Hindu-Muslim unity and the removal of untouchability. His limits were real, since he could not prevent partition, but his influence on how India won independence and on what independent India claimed to stand for was immense.\n\nGandhi's first contribution was the method of satyagraha, or truth-force, which he developed as a barrister in South Africa between 1893 and 1914, where he led campaigns against laws that discriminated against Indians. After returning to India in 1915 he used it in peasant campaigns at Champaran in 1917 and Kheda in 1918. This mattered because it gave ordinary Indians a way of resisting British rule that did not depend on weapons. It also gave the nationalist cause a moral authority that made repression by the British harder to justify.\n\nSecond, he transformed Congress from a small middle-class body into a mass movement. Founded in 1885, Congress had been an elite organisation that petitioned the British. After the Amritsar massacre of 13 April 1919, when British-Indian troops fired on a crowd at Jallianwala Bagh and killed hundreds, Gandhi led the Non-Cooperation movement of 1920-22, which urged Indians to boycott British goods, courts, schools and councils. He wore simple dress and promoted hand-spinning, or khadi, as a symbol of self-reliance, so that peasants and workers could identify with the cause. Although he called off the campaign after the violence at Chauri Chaura in February 1922, Congress now had popular roots that no British government could ignore.\n\nThird, Gandhi showed that British authority depended on Indian consent. The Salt March of 12 March to 6 April 1930 covered about 240 miles to Dandi, where he made salt in defiance of the British monopoly. It drew very large numbers into civil disobedience and won world attention. Twelve years later, in August 1942, Congress passed the Quit India resolution demanding British withdrawal. Gandhi and most leaders were arrested and he was imprisoned until May 1944, but the unrest showed that Britain could no longer hold India by force alone. His part was therefore central to making continued British rule politically and morally costly, which helped the Labour government under Attlee decide to leave.\n\nFourth, his contribution extended to social questions. He called untouchables Harijans and made the end of untouchability one of his stated aims. When B. R. Ambedkar won separate electorates for Dalits in the Communal Award of 1932, Gandhi fasted in Yerwada jail, and the Poona Pact of September 1932 replaced them with reserved seats in the general electorates. Critics, Ambedkar above all, argued that his achievement on caste was limited and that he denied Dalits their own political voice. Even so, he put the issue at the centre of Indian politics. In 1931 he also represented Congress alone at the London Round Table Conference, where the talks failed over separate electorates for minorities.\n\nFifth, Gandhi worked for Hindu-Muslim unity, and here his success was incomplete. He opposed the Muslim League's demand for Pakistan, set out in the Lahore Resolution of 1940, and Jinnah accused him of speaking only for Hindus. The Cabinet Mission failed in 1946, Direct Action Day on 16 August 1946 brought killings in Calcutta, and Mountbatten's plan of 3 June 1947 accepted partition. That decision was taken by the Congress and League leaders, not by Gandhi, who publicly opposed it. Yet he did all he could to limit the violence. In late 1946 he walked through the villages of Noakhali in East Bengal to calm communal killing, and on 15 August 1947 he was in Calcutta fasting rather than celebrating. He fasted again in Calcutta and Delhi in 1947-48, and was shot dead by the Hindu nationalist Nathuram Godse on 30 January 1948.\n\nOverall, Gandhi's contribution was decisive but not the whole story. Historians such as Judith Brown stress his skill in creating a mass movement, while others argue that the exhaustion of Britain after the war, the Indian National Army trials and the naval mutiny of 1946 did as much to end the Raj. He did not prevent partition, and the Gandhian method could not stop the communal killing. Nevertheless, he made non-violent mass protest the central method of Indian politics, and India became independent without a colonial war like those fought by France in Indochina and Algeria. For that reason he deserves to be seen as the man who shaped India's struggle and its moral ideals more than any other.",
