    "model": "American leaders dealt with Korea more effectively than with Cuba in the sense that Korea was a long, costly war which ended in a stalemate, whereas Cuba saw a dangerous crisis resolved on American terms. In both cases leaders achieved their central aim, which was containment, but both also showed the limits of American power and judgement. Cuba, in the end, saw the more skilful leadership because Kennedy learned from his earlier failure at the Bay of Pigs.\n\nIn Korea, Truman reacted quickly to the North Korean invasion of 25 June 1950. The Soviet delegate was absent from the Security Council, so the UN condemned the attack and recommended that members help South Korea, and Truman sent American forces under a UN flag without a declaration of war, calling it a police action. This was effective because it stopped communism taking the whole peninsula and showed that containment would be enforced by arms in Asia. The earlier error was Acheson's speech of January 1950, which left Korea outside the American defence perimeter and may have encouraged the invasion, and US troops had left in 1949.\n\nMilitary leadership was at first brilliant and then reckless. General MacArthur's landing at Inchon on 15 September 1950 cut North Korean supply lines and led to the recapture of Seoul on 28 September. But the decision to advance north of the 38th parallel in October changed the aim from containment to rolling back communism, and it brought Chinese forces into the war in large numbers from late October and November 1950, driving the UN troops back. This was a serious misjudgement, as it prolonged the war by more than two years and cost thousands of lives.\n\nTruman's handling of MacArthur showed the sound side of his leadership. When MacArthur publicly argued for attacking China, Truman dismissed him on 11 April 1951, upholding civilian control of the military and limiting the war, even though it caused a major domestic row. An armistice was signed at Panmunjom on 27 July 1953, with the frontier close to the 38th parallel, no peace treaty and about 36,000 American dead. Truman had preserved South Korea and shown American resolve, but the stalemate was a long way from victory, and Eisenhower, not Truman, ended the war. I would therefore rate the response as effective in aim but costly and flawed in execution.\n\nCuba posed a different kind of problem, ninety miles from Florida in an area the United States had long regarded as its sphere of influence. Fidel Castro took power on 1 January 1959 and nationalised American property in 1960. Eisenhower cut the sugar quota in July 1960, imposed a partial embargo in October and broke off relations in January 1961, but these measures merely pushed Cuba into the arms of the Soviet Union for trade and oil. They failed to remove Castro, and so American policy before 1961 was ineffective.\n\nKennedy's first response was a failure. The Bay of Pigs landing by CIA-trained exiles on 17 April 1961 was defeated within three days and was a humiliation for the new president, who then authorised Operation Mongoose, a covert campaign against Castro. These actions probably made Castro and Khrushchev more determined to defend Cuba, and so they helped bring on the crisis of 1962.\n\nThe missile crisis was Kennedy's best moment. After a U-2 flight on 14 October 1962 revealed Soviet missile sites, and he was shown the photographs on 16 October, Kennedy and ExComm rejected an air strike or invasion, which risked nuclear war. On 22 October he announced a naval quarantine, which was a measured response that gave Khrushchev time to back down. Soviet ships turned back on 24 October, and even after a U-2 was shot down on 27 October, killing Major Rudolf Anderson, Kennedy held back. On 28 October Khrushchev agreed to remove the missiles in return for a pledge not to invade Cuba, and Kennedy also secretly agreed to withdraw the Jupiter missiles from Turkey. The result was a hotline in 1963 and the Partial Test Ban Treaty in August of that year.\n\nOverall, American leaders were more effective over Cuba than Korea, as they removed the missiles at limited cost, though Castro stayed in power and the Bay of Pigs was a blunder. In Korea they held the line but paid heavily and ended where they started. Both cases show that the Cold War was best managed by limiting aims and avoiding direct war with a superpower.",
