    "label": "2",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Race relations in the United States between 1945 and 1989 were transformed in law but changed much less in everyday life. The key developments were the legal and direct-action campaign that ended segregation by 1965, the shift to Black Power and urban unrest after 1965, and the persistent economic inequality and fresh disputes over integration that followed.\n\nIn 1945 segregation was enforced by state law across the South, with separate schools, buses and waiting rooms, while black voters were kept off the rolls by poll taxes, literacy tests and Klan intimidation. In the North discrimination in housing and jobs was enforced by custom. The first key development was legal and federal. The NAACP led a legal campaign, and Truman's Executive Order 9981 of 1948 ordered the armed forces to desegregate, partly to answer Soviet propaganda about American racism. Then Brown v Board of Education on 17 May 1954 unanimously declared segregated public schools unconstitutional, overturning the separate but equal doctrine of Plessy v Ferguson (1896).\n\nBrown provoked resistance, and the conflict that followed was the second key development. Southern congressmen signed the Southern Manifesto in 1956, and in September 1957 Governor Faubus used the National Guard to block nine black students at Little Rock Central High School until Eisenhower sent federal troops. The murder of Emmett Till in 1955 shocked the country. In Montgomery, Alabama, a boycott of the buses after Rosa Parks's arrest in December 1955 lasted over a year and ended segregated buses. It also made Martin Luther King, a young Baptist minister, a national leader, and he founded the Southern Christian Leadership Conference in 1957 to organise church-based protest. The Civil Rights Act of 1957 was the first federal civil rights law since Reconstruction, but it did little for voting.\n\nThe third development was the non-violent direct action of the early 1960s. Four students began the Greensboro sit-ins on 1 February 1960, which spread to more than fifty cities, and SNCC was formed in April. CORE's Freedom Rides of 1961 tested interstate bus terminals, and a bus was burned near Anniston. King's Birmingham campaign of 1963 saw Bull Connor use dogs and fire hoses on demonstrators, including children, and the images shocked national and world opinion. King wrote the Letter from Birmingham Jail, and the March on Washington on 28 August 1963 drew about a quarter of a million people for his I Have a Dream speech. Kennedy called civil rights a moral issue in June 1963, and Cold War embarrassment abroad strengthened the pressure for reform.\n\nThe fourth development was the great legislation under Johnson. The Civil Rights Act of 2 July 1964, passed after a 54-day southern filibuster, banned segregation in public accommodations and created the Equal Employment Opportunity Commission. After the attack on marchers at the Edmund Pettus Bridge in Selma on 7 March 1965, Johnson called for a voting law, and the Voting Rights Act of 6 August 1965 banned literacy tests and sent federal examiners to register voters. Black registration in the South rose sharply, and the Fair Housing Act of April 1968 followed days after King was assassinated in Memphis on 4 April. Legal segregation had been ended.\n\nThe fifth development was a radical turn from 1965 and the urban violence that went with it. Malcolm X, who had criticised integration and non-violence, was assassinated in Harlem in February 1965. Stokely Carmichael popularised the slogan Black Power in 1966, and the Black Panther Party was founded in Oakland by Huey Newton and Bobby Seale that October. Riots in Watts in 1965 and in Newark and Detroit in 1967 left many dead, and the Kerner Commission warned in 1968 that the country was moving towards two societies, one black and one white, separate and unequal. It blamed white racism and poverty, but Johnson did not act on its proposals, and the War on Poverty was starved of funds by the cost of Vietnam.\n\nFrom the 1970s the issues were economic and about how to integrate. The busing of children to achieve school integration provoked riots in Boston in 1974, and affirmative action was limited but not ended by the Supreme Court's Bakke decision of 1978. Black political power grew, with Harold Washington becoming mayor of Chicago in 1983 and Jesse Jackson seeking the Democratic nomination in 1984 and 1988, and a national holiday for King was first observed in 1986. But poverty rates for black Americans stayed much higher than for whites, and in the 1980s black men were imprisoned at several times the rate of white men. Nixon's law and order slogan showed how race shaped national politics.\n\nIn conclusion, the most important development was the dismantling of legal segregation between 1954 and 1965, achieved by courts, protest and federal law working together. But the end of Jim Crow did not bring equality, as by 1989 segregation in housing and schools and deep poverty remained.",
