    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The American economy between 1945 and 1989 had real and impressive strengths, above all in the first quarter-century, but from the late 1960s these were steadily offset by weaknesses in inflation, energy, competitiveness and debt. The overall picture is one of a boom built on temporary advantages, followed by two decades of strain and uneven recovery.\n\nThe first great strength was the position the United States held in 1945. It had ended the war with its factories intact and expanded, while Europe and Japan lay devastated, and it produced roughly half of the world's manufactured goods. Wartime savings and pent-up demand for cars, houses and appliances were released once controls ended, and the GI Bill of 1944 gave veterans education and government-guaranteed home and business loans. This created a mass market for housing, which in turn fed demand for consumer goods and the construction industry.\n\nSecond, the government itself drove growth, at home and abroad. The Bretton Woods system of 1944 made the dollar the central currency, convertible to gold at 35 dollars an ounce, and the Marshall Plan of 1948 to 1952 sent about 13 billion dollars to Western Europe, much of it spent on American goods. At home the Federal-Aid Highway Act of 1956 funded around 41,000 miles of interstate, boosting steel, cars and suburban development. Defence spending, which rose from about 14 billion dollars in 1950 to about 50 billion by 1953 after the Korean War and NSC-68, supplied a stable market for aircraft and electronics firms and helped to nurture Silicon Valley. Kennedy and Johnson's Keynesian advisers, notably Walter Heller, secured the Revenue Act of 1964, which cut the top tax rate from 91 to 70 per cent and was followed by strong growth.\n\nThird, ordinary Americans shared in the prosperity, and American business spread across the world. The Treaty of Detroit in 1950 tied General Motors workers' wages to productivity and gave them pensions and health insurance, a model for other unions at a time when about a third of non-farm workers belonged to one. Meanwhile General Motors, IBM, Coca-Cola and ITT became multinationals, investing heavily in Europe after the Treaty of Rome in 1957, so strikingly that Servan-Schreiber wrote The American Challenge in 1967.\n\nHowever, even in the boom there were weaknesses. Black Americans were largely excluded from the new suburbs and faced discrimination in jobs and in GI Bill benefits, and Michael Harrington's The Other America in 1962 showed that tens of millions still lived in poverty. The boom also depended on defence spending and on the temporary dominance of the American economy, which was bound to fade as Europe and Japan recovered.\n\nFrom the late 1960s the weaknesses became dominant. Johnson financed the Vietnam War and the Great Society without raising taxes until the surtax of 1968, so deficits and inflation grew, and inflation passed 4 per cent in 1968. On 15 August 1971 Nixon ended the convertibility of the dollar into gold, ending Bretton Woods; 1971 was also the first year since 1893 that the United States imported more than it exported. The Arab oil embargo of October 1973 roughly quadrupled oil prices, and the recession of 1973 to 1975 was the deepest since the 1930s, with unemployment around 9 per cent. A second oil shock after the Iranian Revolution pushed inflation above 13 per cent in 1980, producing stagflation.\n\nForeign competition also exposed the weakness of American manufacturing. Japanese firms such as Toyota and Honda, with small fuel-efficient cars, took about a quarter of the American market by the early 1980s, and Japan overtook the United States as the largest car producer in 1980. Chrysler needed federal loan guarantees of 1.5 billion dollars in 1979, and the closure of the Youngstown Sheet and Tube mill in 1977, with about 5,000 jobs lost, became a symbol of the rust belt. Real wages stagnated from the early 1970s and the poverty rate rose from about 11 per cent in 1973 to about 15 per cent in the early 1980s.\n\nThe 1980s showed both strengths and weaknesses together. Volcker's high interest rates and Reagan's tax cuts of 1981 were followed by the sharpest recession since the Depression, with unemployment above 10 per cent in 1982, but then by a long expansion from late 1982 that created about 18 million jobs and brought inflation down. Yet defence spending and tax cuts produced huge deficits, the national debt nearly tripled to almost 3 trillion dollars, and in 1985 the United States became a net debtor nation. The Dow fell about 22 per cent on 19 October 1987 and the trade deficit reached about 150 billion dollars.\n\nOverall, the strengths were greatest between 1945 and the mid-1960s, when American industry, government spending and mass consumption produced a historic rise in living standards. After that the weaknesses outweighed them, as inflation, oil dependence, foreign competition and debt exposed how far the boom had rested on temporary advantages. The recovery of the 1980s showed the economy's resilience, but it was financed by borrowing, so the period ended with real strength alongside serious structural problems.",
