    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Betty Friedan and Billy Graham both made lasting contributions to American life, though in different spheres. Friedan changed how women saw their place in society and helped launch modern feminism, while Graham became the best-known face of American evangelical Christianity and a spiritual adviser to presidents in the Cold War era.\n\nBetty Friedan was born in Peoria, Illinois, in 1921, studied psychology at Smith College and became a magazine writer and suburban housewife. Her contribution began with The Feminine Mystique, published in 1963 and based partly on a survey of her former Smith classmates. It argued that American women were trapped by a domestic ideal, the feminine mystique, which told them that fulfilment lay only in being wives and mothers, and she described their dissatisfaction as the problem that has no name. The book sold very widely and is credited with starting the second wave of American feminism at a time when magazines, films and advertising all promoted domesticity.\n\nFriedan then turned ideas into organisation. In June 1966 she was a founder and the first president of the National Organization for Women, which campaigned for equal rights in employment and law and pressed for the Equal Rights Amendment, which passed Congress in March 1972. She organised the Women's Strike for Equality on 26 August 1970, the fiftieth anniversary of the Nineteenth Amendment, and helped found the National Women's Political Caucus in 1971. This activism formed part of a wider change that included the Equal Pay Act of 1963, Title VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964 banning sex discrimination in employment, and Title IX in 1972. Her limits should be acknowledged: the Equal Rights Amendment fell three states short of ratification by 1982, Phyllis Schlafly's STOP ERA campaign mobilised conservative women against it, and critics said she was too middle-class and too focused on white professional women. Even so, historians generally judge her a central figure in the movement, because she gave a vast number of women the language to describe what they felt.\n\nBilly Graham made his contribution in religion and public life. Born near Charlotte, North Carolina, in 1918 and trained as a Baptist minister at Wheaton College, he became a national figure through his Los Angeles crusade of 1949, which was extended from three weeks to eight after the Hearst newspapers reported it widely. He founded the Billy Graham Evangelistic Association in 1950 and the radio programme Hour of Decision, and he used mass rallies, radio and television to reach millions. He also co-founded Christianity Today in 1956, giving conservative evangelicals a voice alongside the mainstream press. His work fitted a decade in which church membership rose sharply, Congress added under God to the Pledge of Allegiance in 1954, and religion was seen as a bulwark against atheistic communism.\n\nGraham also had a political and social influence. He was a friend and adviser of Eisenhower and later of Nixon, which placed him at the centre of national life. From 1953 he refused to hold segregated crusades, an early sign of his support for integration, although he was criticised for not supporting King's protests more openly. Watergate damaged him, and he said afterwards that he had been used and would avoid partisan politics; he declined to join Falwell's Moral Majority in 1979. Abroad he preached in London in 1954 and in Moscow in 1982, and he organised the Lausanne Congress in 1974, making him a leader of world evangelicalism. Critics saw him as a tool of Cold War patriotism.\n\nOverall, Friedan challenged the postwar consensus about women's roles and gave feminism its organisational base, while Graham reinforced the religious and patriotic climate of the Cold War and made evangelicalism a major force. Both reached millions through mass media and left a legacy that is still debated, though each had limits: Friedan's focus on middle-class women and Graham's closeness to presidents.",
