    "marks": 30,
    "model": "Before 1973 Irish fishing was small, with most boats old and inshore, and foreign trawlers fished close to the coast under historic rights. Ireland's accession terms gave Irish boats an exclusive six-mile zone, and a 12-mile zone in parts of the coast until the end of 1982. The Hague agreement of 1976 extended fishing limits to 200 nautical miles from 1977. The Common Fisheries Policy of January 1983 set national quotas, but Irish fishermen said these were small compared with the value of fish in Irish waters. EEC and state grants financed larger vessels, and Killybegs grew into Ireland's leading fishing port.",
