    "label": "C1",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "T.K. Whitaker, Secretary of the Department of Finance, wrote the 1958 report Economic Development, which argued that protection had failed and that emigration was a sign of a shrinking economy. It recommended moving public investment from housing and hospitals towards productive investment in agriculture, industry and tourism. Seán Lemass, Taoiseach from June 1959, backed the First Programme for Economic Expansion, which aimed to halt emigration by creating jobs in export industry and farming. Lemass built a closer relationship with the trade unions and employers, and national wage rounds settled general pay increases. The Programme's growth of about 4 per cent a year between 1958 and 1963 was roughly double its target, and net emigration fell. Lemass also applied to join the EEC on 31 July 1961 to open markets for Irish goods and modernise industry.",
