    "label": "C2",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Telefís Éireann began broadcasting on 31 December 1961, and by the early 1970s most households had a set. Television brought national and international news and sport into every home and reduced the isolation of rural communities. The Late Late Show, presented by Gay Byrne from 1962 to 1999, discussed sex, marriage, contraception and the church. The Bishop and the Nightie episode of 1966 caused uproar and led to a public argument about morality and the limits of church authority. Telefís Scoile, which began on 4 February 1964, broadcast lessons for secondary pupils, and The Riordans gave rural viewers a drama about real farming life. The government showed its power over RTÉ when Gerry Collins dismissed the RTÉ Authority in late November 1972, after an interview with the Provisional IRA chief of staff was broadcast.",
