    "label": "C3",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "John Charles McQuaid was Archbishop of Dublin from 1940 to 1972 and was the most influential churchman in the state during the 1950s. He advised the government on the Mother and Child Scheme, and the bishops told Taoiseach Costello in 1950 and again in April 1951 that the scheme was contrary to Catholic social teaching. He objected to the principle that the state, rather than parents and the church, should decide how mothers and children were cared for. His influence rested on personal contact with ministers, notably de Valera, whom he had advised on the article on religion in the 1937 Constitution. He built churches and schools in the Dublin suburbs and founded the Catholic Social Service Conference. After Vatican II he was cautious about liturgical change and wary of ecumenism, which he regarded as a risk to Catholic doctrine.",
