    "model": "Charles Haughey became Taoiseach in December 1979 after defeating George Colley for the leadership of Fianna Fáil. In 1982 he returned to office with the support of Tony Gregory, who secured investment for inner-city Dublin. In 1987 his minority government, supported by Fine Gael under Alan Dukes through the Tallaght Strategy, cut public spending sharply under his Minister for Finance, Ray MacSharry. In October 1987 the Programme for National Recovery was agreed with the unions, employers and farmers, holding pay rises low in return for a reduction in the deficit. Historians judge that these later cuts and the programme helped to prepare the economic recovery of the 1990s. His record on public finances before 1987 was poor, and he is judged a gifted but flawed leader.",
