    "label": "C4",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Charlie Chaplin's Tramp character, first seen in 1914, made him the most famous film star of the silent era. He developed the Tramp in films such as The Kid (1921), The Gold Rush (1925) and City Lights (1931). In Modern Times (1936) he satirised industrial life and mechanisation by showing the Tramp caught in factory machinery. His first full sound film, The Great Dictator (1940), was a major commercial success in the United States, and in it he played Adenoid Hynkel, a parody of Hitler, as well as a Jewish barber. Historians judge his work a major achievement in both silent and sound cinema, although his leftist political views caused difficulties with American authorities in the 1940s and he left the United States in 1952.",
