    "label": "C1",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Hungary under Matyas Rakosi was a rigidly Stalinist one-party state with show trials, the feared AVH secret police, forced collectivisation and falling living standards, which bred deep resentment. Khrushchev's secret speech of February 1956 exposed Stalin's crimes, and the Polish October encouraged the Petofi Circle of intellectuals to demand change. The uprising began on 23 October 1956 when AVH police opened fire on a crowd outside Radio Budapest, and Imre Nagy was reinstated as prime minister. Nagy's declaration of neutrality and withdrawal from the Warsaw Pact on 1 November crossed a line that the Soviet leaders would not tolerate. The uprising failed because the Soviet Union was willing to use overwhelming force, and because it had no organised armed leadership beyond local groups. The West did not intervene, distracted by the Suez crisis, and the Soviet Union vetoed action in the UN Security Council.",
