    "model": "Britain's post-war economy was exhausted, and by 1945 it owed India over one billion pounds in sterling balances for wartime supplies. The Labour government under Clement Attlee was committed to Indian self-rule and announced on 20 February 1947 that British rule would end by June 1948. The Quit India movement of 1942 and the wider Congress campaigns showed that British rule could not be maintained by force alone. The INA trials and the 1946 naval mutiny at Bombay led officials to doubt that the army and police could be relied on. The partition of India into India and Pakistan, with Radcliffe's boundaries announced on 17 August 1947, caused millions of people to cross the new border. Communal violence in the Punjab and Bengal killed between about 200,000 and 2 million people, and the division of the army and civil service had to be done in a few weeks.",
