    "label": "C2",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Katanga was the richest mining region of the Belgian Congo, and its copper, cobalt and uranium were run by the Belgian company Union Miniere du Haut-Katanga. Moise Tshombe's Conakat party campaigned for regional autonomy so that Katanga could keep its own revenue, and it drew on the claim that Katangans had a separate identity. Belgian officers and foreign mercenaries backed the secession and led the Katanga Gendarmerie. Tshombe proclaimed the Katangan State on 11 July 1960, eleven days after independence, and no state recognised it. The UN entered Katanga on 12 August 1960, and the Kitona Declaration of 21 December 1961 followed fighting in Elisabethville, although Tshombe later repudiated it. Operation Grand Slam in December 1962 defeated the Katangan forces, and Tshombe announced the end of the secession at Kolwezi on 14 January 1963.",
