    "label": "C2",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "The boycott began on 5 December 1955, four days after Rosa Parks was arrested for refusing to give up her seat, and it was co-ordinated by the Montgomery Improvement Association. Church networks and a volunteer car pool kept about 40,000 black riders off the buses for 381 days. The city tried to divide the boycotters, arrested King and bombed his home, and indicted 89 leaders under a 1921 Alabama law against boycotts. In February 1956 Fred Gray filed Browder v Gayle in federal court, and in June a three-judge court ruled that bus segregation violated the Fourteenth Amendment. The Supreme Court upheld that ruling on 13 November 1956, and the boycott ended when the order was served on 20 December 1956. The boycott succeeded in ending bus segregation in Montgomery, but segregation in schools, housing and voting remained.",
