    "label": "C4",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Mailer's essay The White Negro in 1957 criticised the conformity of postwar America and praised the hipster who lived for the moment. Critics such as James Baldwin attacked the essay for romanticising black life. Mailer co-founded the Village Voice in New York in 1955, part of the counter-cultural press of the period. The Armies of the Night described the anti-war march on the Pentagon in October 1967 and won the Pulitzer Prize and the National Book Award. Mailer was arrested at the Pentagon march and wrote about himself in the third person in that book. Historians now treat his non-fiction, especially The Armies of the Night, as an important account of the 1960s protest movement.",
