    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Charles Trevelyan and Asenath Nicholson responded to the Famine in very different ways. Trevelyan, as Assistant Secretary to the Treasury, administered government relief according to the doctrine of laissez-faire and insisted that Irish property should pay for Irish poverty. Nicholson, an American teacher and relief worker, gave help directly to the starving poor and then wrote some of the most vivid eyewitness accounts of what she saw.\n\nTrevelyan was the senior civil servant who ran the Treasury's Famine relief from 1845. Born in Taunton in 1807 and a former East India Company official in Bengal, he was an evangelical Protestant and a firm believer in political economy. He controlled the purchase of Peel's Indian corn, the public works, the food depots and the soup kitchens. In 1845-1846 this response was fairly effective. Peel's government bought about £100,000 of maize through Baring Brothers, sold it at cost to keep prices down, and set up a Relief Commission under Sir Randolph Routh to coordinate local committees. Deaths were low in that first season, though the failure of 1845 was only partial.\n\nAfter Russell's Whigs took office in June 1846, Trevelyan carried out a much harsher policy. He opposed government interference with the food market, so the government refused to import food or control prices and left supply to private merchants, even as grain and cattle continued to leave Irish ports. The public works under the Labouchere Act of August 1846 paid wages below local rates, often late, and the cost fell on local taxes. Numbers rose to about 700,000 by March 1847, but wages could not keep up with food prices, which had more than doubled, and weakened people were set to work in the hard winter of 1846-1847. Trevelyan's policy was therefore one of limited, conditional relief that failed the most vulnerable at the worst moment.\n\nTrevelyan's views on the Famine itself explain his rigidity. He feared that generous relief would make the Irish dependent, he blamed Irish landlords and the moral character of the people, and he saw the Famine as an act of Providence that would force reform of the land system and population. When the public works collapsed, the government turned in 1847 to soup kitchens, which fed about three million people a day and were the cheapest and most effective relief of the Famine. Yet Trevelyan closed them by September 1847, and the Poor Law Extension Act of June 1847 made the Poor Law responsible for all further relief. Its Gregory clause refused relief to anyone holding more than a quarter of an acre, which pushed smallholders off their land and helped landlords clear estates. He declared the Famine over, defended his record in The Irish Crisis (1848) and was knighted that year, although nationalists and many relief workers condemned his attitude. Historians are divided: Cecil Woodham-Smith made him the villain of the Famine, while Robin Haines has argued that he worked hard within the limits set by ministers such as Russell and Charles Wood. The ideology was that of the whole Whig government.\n\nAsenath Nicholson responded as a private individual. Born in Chelsea, Vermont, in 1792, she was a widow and teacher who had worked among the poor of New York and promoted temperance. She first came to Ireland in 1844 and walked through almost every county, staying in cabins and reading the Bible to the people she met. Her account, Ireland's Welcome to the Stranger (1847), described the poverty and the dependence on the potato before the blight. This gave her unusual knowledge of the people she later tried to help.\n\nShe returned in 1846 to give relief. She opened a soup kitchen in the Liberties in Dublin, then travelled to Ulster, Mayo, Erris and Arranmore, distributing food and clothing paid for by appeals in New York newspapers. Unlike Trevelyan, she worked independently rather than through a committee and gave aid directly to people in their cabins. Her response was small in scale and she left Ireland in 1848, but it reached remote places and was quick, which official relief often was not. Some officials and clergy suspected her as a Protestant Bible reader and found her eccentric, but the poor welcomed her.\n\nNicholson's second response was to bear witness. Her Lights and Shades of Ireland (1850), whose Famine section appeared as Annals of the Famine in Ireland (1851), condemned landlords, officials and proselytisers alike, and blamed the system rather than its victims. She thus offers a direct counterpoint to Trevelyan, who blamed the Irish themselves.\n\nIn sum, Trevelyan shaped official relief through laissez-faire, public works, the closing of the soup kitchens and the Poor Law, and his rigidity cost lives. Nicholson's contribution was modest in scale but compassionate, practical and invaluable as testimony. Between them they show the gap between a government that treated the Famine as a problem of finance and principle and individuals who met it as a human catastrophe.",
