    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "James Stephens made a major contribution to Fenianism. He founded the Irish Republican Brotherhood in 1858, built it into a disciplined oath-bound organisation with a newspaper and links to America, and kept the idea of physical force republicanism alive. Yet his leadership failed at the decisive moment, because he never delivered the rising he had promised.\n\nStephens was born in Kilkenny in 1825 and took part in the 1848 rising at Ballingarry under William Smith O'Brien before escaping to Paris. His experience of failure in 1848 shaped his later belief that revolution needed careful secret organisation. He returned to Ireland in 1856 and travelled the country, assessing the mood of the people at a time of deep bitterness after the Famine and disillusion with parliamentary politics following the collapse of the Independent Irish Party.\n\nHis greatest contribution was the foundation of the Irish Republican Brotherhood in Dublin on 17 March 1858. It was an oath-bound secret society that aimed to end British rule by force and set up an Irish republic, and Stephens took the title of Chief Organiser of the Irish Republic. He organised it in circles under local leaders who reported to him. Its members were mainly artisans, clerks, shopkeepers and farmers' sons, and Stephens also tried to recruit soldiers in the British army. This structure gave Irish republicanism its first lasting revolutionary organisation.\n\nStephens also linked the movement across the Atlantic. In the same year, John O'Mahony founded the Fenian Brotherhood in New York, and Stephens worked closely with it. American Fenians sent money and supporters, and included veterans of the Civil War whose military experience raised hopes of success in Ireland. Without this transatlantic dimension Fenianism would have lacked its funds and its trained men, and it was Stephens who made the connection work.\n\nHe understood the value of publicity and public display. In 1861 the funeral of Terence Bellew MacManus, a veteran of 1848, in Dublin showed the strength of the movement, even though Cardinal Cullen refused to allow the body to lie in the Pro-Cathedral. In 1863 Stephens organised the Irish People newspaper in Dublin, with Luby, O'Leary and Charles Kickham as editors and Jeremiah O'Donovan Rossa as manager. The paper spread Fenian ideas and drew in recruits, though it also exposed the leadership to the police.\n\nStephens's contribution had serious limits. He was secretive, autocratic and over-promised. In September 1865 the police raided the Irish People offices and arrested Luby, O'Leary, Rossa and Kickham, and in November Stephens himself was arrested. He escaped from Richmond Prison within a fortnight with the help of warders, which enhanced his reputation, but he then went abroad. In 1866 he failed to hold a rising on the dates he had promised, and habeas corpus was suspended in Ireland in February 1866, allowing arrest without trial. In December 1866 the American Fenian leaders removed him.\n\nThe rising that followed in March 1867 was therefore carried out without him, in bad weather, and it was quickly crushed in Dublin, Cork, Limerick and Tipperary. Informers had compromised the plans and the Fenians lacked arms and trained leaders. Stephens's indecision and failure to launch when he said he would must take some share of the blame, since delay allowed the government to seize the initiative. He spent most of the rest of his life in Paris, returned to Ireland in 1891 and died in Dublin in 1901.\n\nThe lasting results of the movement he founded were significant. The execution of the Manchester Martyrs in 1867 and the Clerkenwell explosion that December helped to convince Gladstone that Irish discontent threatened Britain, and he introduced the Irish Church Act of 1869 and the Land Act of 1870 as part of his mission to pacify Ireland. The IRB itself survived and its legacy lasted into the revolution of 1916.\n\nOn balance, Stephens deserves credit as the organiser who founded the IRB, linked it to America, gave it a newspaper and a structure, and made republican revolution a permanent force in Irish politics. His weakness as a leader in 1866 explains the failure of the rising, but the organisation he created outlived both him and that failure.",
