    "label": "1",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Charles Stewart Parnell achieved a great deal in his political career between 1875 and 1891, to a large extent more than any other Irish leader of his time. He turned a loose group of MPs into a disciplined party, linked the land and national questions, and forced Home Rule on to the British political agenda. His achievements were real, but they stopped short of Home Rule itself, and his career ended in the split and in his early death in 1891, so his success was considerable rather than complete.\n\nParnell's first success was to break the failed methods of Isaac Butt. Elected MP for Meath in April 1875, he joined Joseph Biggar in obstruction, using long speeches and procedural objections to jam the business of the Commons. Butt's polite constitutional approach had won about 60 seats in 1874 but no concessions, as the party had no pledge, no whip and little money. By contrast, obstruction showed that a small group could make the House unworkable, and the 41-hour sitting of January-February 1881 led to the closure rule in 1882. In 1877 Parnell also replaced Butt as president of the Home Rule Confederation of Great Britain, which gave him control of the Irish vote in English cities.\n\nSecond, Parnell broadened the movement by allying it with the land question. After the New Departure of 1878-79, in which Michael Davitt and John Devoy of Clan na Gael offered support for a more active programme, Parnell became president of the Irish National Land League in October 1879. The agricultural depression made the tenants ready for a mass movement, and Parnell's speeches at Westport and Ennis encouraged them to hold their homesteads and to isolate those who took evicted farms. He raised money and publicity in America in 1880, and he won the Land Act of 1881 as a result of the agitation. This gave him a mass base and the Fenian goodwill that earlier Home Rulers lacked, although the alliance with Davitt and the IRB was always uneasy.\n\nThird, he built a party machine. He was elected chairman of the Irish Parliamentary Party in May 1880, defeating William Shaw. After the Land League was suppressed, the Irish National League was founded in 1882, with branches in nearly every parish, often led by priests, and United Ireland became the party newspaper. In 1884 candidates were required to sign a pledge to sit, act and vote with the party and were chosen at conventions. This discipline was Parnell's greatest achievement, since it made the party a bloc that British parties could not ignore. His arrest and the Kilmainham settlement of 1882 showed his pragmatism, but the Phoenix Park murders in May 1882 forced him to denounce violence, and this tested his alliance.\n\nThe high point was 1885-86. Parnell used the party's balance of power to bring down Gladstone's government in June 1885 and bargained with Lord Carnarvon, which brought the lapse of the Crimes Act and the Ashbourne Act. The extended franchise of 1884 raised the Irish electorate from about 220,000 to about 740,000, and in the 1885 election the party won 85 of 103 Irish seats, plus one in Liverpool. This gave Parnell the decisive vote in the Commons, and Gladstone introduced the First Home Rule Bill on 8 April 1886. Yet the bill was defeated on 8 June by 341 votes to 311, when about 93 Liberals led by Hartington and Chamberlain voted against it. Home Rule had become Liberal policy, but it had not been won, and Ulster's opposition, shown in the Belfast riots of 1886, remained unsolved.\n\nIn 1887-89 Parnell appeared to triumph over his enemies. The Times printed letters in April 1887 supposedly linking him to the Phoenix Park murders, but the Special Commission exposed Richard Pigott as the forger in February 1889 when he misspelled hesitancy, and Pigott fled and shot himself in Madrid. Parnell won £5,000 damages from The Times in 1890. He stood at the height of his reputation, his alliance with Gladstone strong.\n\nThe collapse that followed undid much of this. Captain O'Shea's divorce petition named Parnell as co-respondent, and after the decree of November 1890 Gladstone made it clear that he could not lead the Liberals with Parnell as leader. The Catholic bishops condemned Parnell, and in Committee Room 15 in December 1890 about 45 MPs walked out against him, leaving about 28. He lost by-elections in North Kilkenny, North Sligo and Carlow, married Katharine O'Shea in June 1891 and died at Brighton on 6 October 1891, aged 45. His refusal to step aside split the party for nearly a decade.\n\nParnell was therefore extremely successful as a political organiser and tactician: he created the disciplined party, the mass land movement and the Liberal alliance. His failure was that he never secured Home Rule, and his private life destroyed the movement he had built. His legacy was an independent Irish party and the memory of a leader who had brought Ireland closer to self-government than anyone before him.",
