    "label": "2",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Edward Carson had a decisive impact on Irish affairs between 1870 and 1914, though it was concentrated in the last few years of the period. As leader of the Irish unionists from 1910, he turned unionist opposition to the Third Home Rule Bill into a mass movement backed by the threat of force, and he made the exclusion of Ulster an issue that no British government could ignore. His impact was to prevent Home Rule from being imposed on the whole of Ireland, but at the price of beginning an armed politics that made partition more likely.\n\nCarson was an unlikely leader of Ulster. He was born in Dublin in 1854, educated at Trinity College, and became a leading barrister, Solicitor General for Ireland in 1892 and MP for Dublin University from the same year. In 1895 he cross-examined Oscar Wilde in the Queensberry libel case. He became leader of the Irish unionist party at Westminster in 1910, succeeding Walter Long, who had followed Colonel Saunderson, so he could speak for all Irish unionists, including the small southern minority, not only for Ulster.\n\nHis first impact was to organise unionism in a way it had never been organised. The Parliament Act of August 1911 meant that the Third Home Rule Bill would become law in about two years despite the Lords, and Carson worked with James Craig, who planned the campaign and was based on the Ulster Unionist Council founded in 1905. A huge demonstration was held at Craigavon in September 1911, with Carson as speaker. Thanks to this work, unionism had the Orange lodges, the Ulster Unionist Council and, from January 1911, the Ulster Women's Unionist Council, behind it. Carson also won vital British backing when Bonar Law, the Conservative leader, pledged his party at Blenheim Palace on 27 July 1912 to support Ulster in any resistance.\n\nThe most dramatic moment was Ulster Day, 28 September 1912, when Carson was the first to sign the Solemn League and Covenant at Belfast City Hall. About 237,000 men signed, pledging to use all means to defeat Home Rule, and about 234,000 women signed a separate declaration. The Covenant showed the strength of feeling and gave the campaign a religious character, with many Church of Ireland and Presbyterian ministers taking part. It convinced Liberal ministers that Ulster would not simply give way, although it did not change the Commons majority for the Bill, which passed in January 1913.\n\nCarson's second impact was to put force behind the threat. The Ulster Volunteer Force was formed in January 1913 under Sir George Richardson, and by 1914 it had perhaps 90,000 to 100,000 men drilling openly. In September 1913 the Ulster Unionist Council announced a Provisional Government of Ulster with Carson as chairman if Home Rule became law. The government did not prosecute, which Redmond warned would encourage separatists. Indeed, the Irish Volunteers were founded in November 1913 as a nationalist response, so Carson's actions began an arms race.\n\nIn 1914 this threat shaped the whole political process. When Asquith proposed on 9 March that Ulster counties might vote themselves out for six years, Carson rejected it as a stay of execution. On 20 March, in the Curragh incident, Brigadier-General Gough and many officers offered to resign rather than act against Ulster, which showed that the army could not be relied on. On 24-25 April the UVF landed about 25,000 rifles at Larne, Donaghadee and Bangor, organised by Fred Crawford, and the authorities did nothing. The nationalist gun-running at Howth and Kilcoole followed in July-August. The Buckingham Palace Conference in July 1914 failed over the exclusion of Tyrone and Fermanagh, and the Home Rule Act, which received the royal assent on 18 September 1914, was suspended for the duration of the war.\n\nHistorians differ on Carson's intentions. Some say he bluffed to protect the Union, while others, such as A.T.Q. Stewart, show how close Britain came to civil war, and argue that his threat made partition inevitable and that he abandoned southern unionists. Contemporary nationalists thought him ready to defy parliament, and Redmond denounced the threat of force.\n\nOverall, Carson's impact was greater than that of any other unionist. He defeated the plan for a Home Rule Ireland under one parliament, forced the principle of exclusion on to the table, and brought armed politics into Irish life, so that Ireland entered 1914 on the brink of civil war and his actions shaped the partition that followed.",
