    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The 1913 strike and lockout arose from the desperate conditions of Dublin's unskilled workers and from a head-on clash between James Larkin's Irish Transport and General Workers' Union and the employers led by William Martin Murphy. It was carried out through a tram strike, mass dismissals, police violence, the employers' Document and a long war of attrition kept alive by British relief. It ended in January 1914 with defeat for the workers in the short term, though the ITGWU survived and the dispute left a deep mark on Irish labour and nationalism.\n\nThe causes lay first in the poverty of the city. Dublin had few factories and a huge pool of casual, unskilled labourers hired by the day at the docks, in carting and in building, with no security and very low pay. Over 20,000 families lived in one room each, in decaying tenements in the Liberties and on the north side, often owned by landlords who charged high rents for rooms with no running water. Dublin's death rate was among the highest in the United Kingdom, with tuberculosis and infant mortality especially severe. Such misery made workers ready to follow a leader who promised to organise them, and it explains why the dispute drew such passion.\n\nThe immediate cause was the rise of a new type of unionism. Craft unions, mostly branches of British bodies, had ignored the unskilled. Larkin, who led the Belfast dockers and carters in 1907, broke with the British union and founded the ITGWU in 1909 with Liberty Hall as its headquarters. He used the sympathetic strike and refused to handle tainted goods from firms in dispute, a policy known as Larkinism. In July and August 1913 the union won gains from several firms, and Murphy, chairman of the Dublin United Tramways Company, owner of the Irish Independent and leader of the Dublin Employers' Federation, decided to destroy it before it won recognition. In mid-August he dismissed ITGWU members at the Independent and the tramways, convinced that employers had the right to manage without interference.\n\nThe strike was carried out in dramatic fashion. On Tuesday 26 August 1913, the first day of Horse Show week, tram drivers and conductors walked off their cars, causing chaos. Murphy replaced them and the police protected the replacements, so Larkin called out workers who handled tram goods. When a meeting in Sackville Street was banned, Larkin burned the proclamation and appeared in disguise on the balcony of the Imperial Hotel, Murphy's property. On 31 August the police baton-charged the crowd in what became known as Bloody Sunday, injuring hundreds, after two men had been killed in baton charges the day before. On 3 September about 400 employers agreed that all workers must sign the Document, a pledge to leave the ITGWU and not support it, and locked out those who refused. By autumn about 20,000 workers were idle, and many thousands of dependants were affected.\n\nThe struggle was sustained by outside help but also reached its limits there. British trade unions sent food and money, the first food ship, the Hare, arriving on 27 September, and relief was distributed from Liberty Hall through soup kitchens and parcels. The Askwith inquiry condemned the Document, yet the employers rejected its proposals, and George Russell (AE) attacked them in his Open Letter to the Masters of Dublin. The Save the Kiddies scheme to send strikers' children to Britain was condemned by Archbishop Walsh as a threat to their faith, and priests prevented children leaving. In November Larkin was released after seven months' sedition sentence had been cut short by a public campaign, and the Irish Citizen Army was founded on 23 November to protect pickets. Then in December a special British TUC congress refused to call a sympathetic strike or boycott Dublin goods, a decisive blow.\n\nThe outcome was a defeat in the short term. By January 1914 hunger was driving men back, the ITGWU advised a return, and the dispute formally ended around 18 January. Many men returned without signing the Document, but the union lost members, some workers were blacklisted and employers kept the right to bar ITGWU men in some firms. Larkin left for the United States in October 1914, and Connolly took over the union.\n\nYet the longer-term results were significant. The ITGWU survived and grew during the First World War, the Citizen Army under Connolly fought in the Easter Rising of 1916, and labour became a real political force in Dublin. The conditions exposed by the Church Street tenement collapse on 2 September, which killed seven, strengthened pressure for slum clearance. I would judge 1913 as a defeat that strengthened the labour movement: the employers won the lockout, but they lost public sympathy, and the workers gained a sense of solidarity and a radicalism that fed into 1916.",
