    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Between 1923 and 1945 Cosgrave and de Valera both handled Anglo-Irish relations with considerable skill, but in different ways. Cosgrave was skilful in using patient diplomacy within the Commonwealth to widen the Free State's independence without a rupture with Britain. De Valera was bolder and achieved more in removing the symbols of the Treaty, though some of his methods were costly and he relied on groundwork laid by his predecessor.\n\nCosgrave accepted the Treaty and set out to make it work from the inside. In 1924 his government registered the Treaty with the League of Nations as an international agreement, a calculated move to show that the Free State was a sovereign state and that its relationship with Britain was not a purely internal British matter. Britain objected, but the registration established the principle. The Free State had joined the League in 1923 and opened a legation in Washington in 1924, so Cosgrave was building an independent international presence from the start.\n\nHis most notable achievements came at the Imperial Conferences. At the 1926 conference Kevin O'Higgins was a principal advocate of the Balfour Declaration, which described Britain and the dominions as autonomous communities, equal in status and freely associated. This was followed by the Statute of Westminster in December 1931, which gave the dominions legislative independence and removed the British Parliament's power to override their laws. These changes transformed the Treaty position without a shot being fired, and they gave de Valera the legal tools he later used. Cosgrave's approach was therefore skilful, gradual and effective.\n\nHowever, Cosgrave had real failures. The Boundary Commission crisis of 1925 showed that nationalist hopes of a transfer of territory had been unrealistic. When the commission's expected findings were leaked to the Morning Post in November 1925, Eoin MacNeill resigned, and Cosgrave had to accept the Tripartite Agreement of 3 December 1925, which left the border unchanged. The agreement did release the Free State from its share of the imperial debt, which softened the blow, but it left partition intact. He also left the oath, the Governor-General and the Treaty ports in place, and he went on paying the land annuities to Britain.\n\nDe Valera, who became head of government in March 1932, handled relations with Britain very differently. He dismissed the Governor-General James McNeill in 1932, and in May 1933 the Removal of Oath Act ended the oath of fidelity to the Crown. The right of appeal to the Privy Council was ended later that year. When Edward VIII abdicated in December 1936 he took the opportunity to remove all reference to the Crown and Governor-General from the Constitution and to pass the External Relations Act, which left the King only a role in diplomatic matters. Britain protested but took no military or economic action, partly because the Statute of Westminster supported de Valera's argument. This was skilful use of opportunity, and the 1937 Constitution, Bunreacht na hÉireann, completed the process by declaring the state sovereign and naming it Éire.\n\nThe handling of the land annuities was more costly. De Valera stopped paying the annuities, about £3 million a year, in 1932, arguing that they were owed to the Irish government and not to Britain. Britain imposed duties on Irish cattle and other exports and Ireland retaliated, starting the Economic War, which damaged the farming economy until 1938. Yet de Valera's patience paid off in the Anglo-Irish Agreements of 25 April 1938. Britain handed over the Treaty ports at Berehaven, Cobh and Lough Swilly, the financial dispute was settled for a single payment of £10 million, far less than Britain had claimed, and the special duties were ended. The return of the ports was probably the greatest single achievement of either man, because it removed the last military restriction of the Treaty. It made neutrality possible in 1939. De Valera kept the state out of the Second World War despite pressure from Churchill, who complained about the ports, and an offer in 1940 that Britain would declare for a united Ireland in principle if Ireland joined the Allies. Neutrality was a firm assertion of sovereignty, though it left relations with Britain strained and partition untouched.\n\nOn balance, both men handled Anglo-Irish relations with skill, but de Valera achieved more in practical terms, with the oath, the Crown's representative, the ports and neutrality all secured by 1945. His success, however, rested on the Statute of Westminster and the constitutional groundwork Cosgrave had done, and neither man ended partition. Cosgrave was the more cautious and economical negotiator, while de Valera was the more daring, and the better record belongs to the man who finished what the other had started.",
