    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Irish people emigrated to Britain between 1920 and 1966 mainly because of economic weakness at home, combined with the closing of the American door and the ease of access to British jobs. Once there, they assimilated to a considerable extent in working life and language, but a distinct Catholic and Irish identity survived, so assimilation was real but only partial.\n\nThe first cause was the closing of the United States. The Emergency Quota Act of 1921 limited immigrants to 3 per cent of each nationality's foreign-born population in the 1910 census, and the Immigration Act of 1924 cut this to 2 per cent of the 1890 census. The Irish Free State received a quota of 28,567 a year, reduced to 17,853 from 1929. Emigration was therefore redirected rather than ended, and the Great Depression after 1929 reduced American demand for labour still further. Britain became the main destination because it was easy to enter, jobs were close, and the fare was low.\n\nThe second cause was the weakness of the Irish economy. Small farms, low incomes, unemployment and the lack of industry outside the north-east left young people with few prospects, and the Free State governments treated emigration as a problem they could not quickly solve and did not try to restrict it. The Commission on Emigration and Other Population Problems, which sat from 1948 and reported in 1954, traced emigration mainly to low incomes, unemployment and small farms, though it also noted the attraction of better living standards and a livelier social life abroad. Emigration peaked in the 1950s, when about 400,000 people left the state between 1951 and 1961, most of them for Britain, and the 1961 census recorded the lowest population in the history of the state, about 2.8 million.\n\nThe third cause was the pull of British labour demand. During the Emergency, travel permits were required, but Britain recruited Irish workers for war factories, building and farm work, and well over 100,000 went to work there. After the war, reconstruction created huge demand: Irish men worked as navvies on roads, building sites and power stations, while Irish women trained as nurses in the new National Health Service of 1948 or worked in factories and offices. The Ireland Act of 1949 and the Common Travel Area confirmed that Irish citizens in the United Kingdom were not aliens and could live, work and vote there freely, which made the move easy. Only the economic recovery under Whitaker's Economic Development of 1958 and Seán Lemass brought emigration down sharply in the 1960s.\n\nTurning to assimilation, the Irish integrated well in some respects. They spoke English, they could vote, and they entered British working life in large numbers, clustering in London districts such as Kilburn and Camden Town and in Birmingham, Coventry, Luton, Manchester and Liverpool. Enda Delaney has argued that Irish emigrants to Britain kept strong ties with home while integrating into British working life. Irish-speaking emigrants from the Gaeltacht tended to lose the language within a generation, which shows how strong the pressure to assimilate could be.\n\nAssimilation was held back, however, by hardship, prejudice and the Irish world that emigrants built for themselves. Many young emigrants arrived with no training and lived in lodging houses or navvy camps, where hard drinking and long hours were common, and navvy work through subcontractors paying cash in hand left men without insurance or security. Notices reading No Irish are widely remembered from lodging-house windows in the 1950s and 1960s, though historians disagree about how common they were. The Irish remained a visible Catholic working-class minority, and Catholic chaplaincies, the Legion of Mary, Irish centres and dance halls such as the Galtymore in Cricklewood and the Gresham on the Holloway Road helped to keep a distinct identity. Because Irish-born people could return home easily, many saw their stay as temporary.\n\nDomhnall Mac Amhlaigh shows both sides. He went to England in 1951 and worked as a labourer, and his diary Dialann Deoraí, published in 1960, describes the work, the lodgings, the pubs and dance halls, homesickness and the mixture of welcome and prejudice he met. He stayed in England for the rest of his life, dying in Northampton in 1989, yet he kept writing in Irish and lived within an Irish world inside English cities.\n\nOverall, Irish emigrants to Britain left because poverty at home and American quotas left them little choice, and Britain's open door made the move easy. They assimilated economically and legally to a large degree, but they remained a distinctive Catholic community with strong links to home, so their assimilation was partial rather than complete.",
