    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Boss Croker, Maureen O'Hara and John F. Kennedy each shaped the image of the Irish abroad in a different way. Croker stood for Irish success in politics, but tainted by corruption, O'Hara gave the Irish a spirited and glamorous face on the screen, and Kennedy came to symbolise acceptance and pride. Together they show an image moving from suspicion to affection.\n\nRichard Croker was born in Co. Cork in 1843 and was taken to New York as a child, where he grew up in a poor Irish district. He rose through the Fourth Avenue gang, the fire companies and the local Democratic clubs, and he held minor offices such as alderman and coroner. By the mid-1880s he was leader of Tammany Hall, the Democratic organisation that controlled New York politics, and he ran it until 1902. He used patronage, jobs in city departments and contracts to build support among immigrants and the poor, and he placed his allies in office, including Mayor Robert Van Wyck from 1898 to 1901.\n\nCroker's contribution to the image of the Irish was double-edged. To many Tammany voters he was a friend of the poor, and his career showed that an immigrant could rise from the slums to command a great city. Reformers and much of the respectable press, however, condemned him as corrupt, and the Lexow Committee of 1894 into police corruption and the Mazet Committee of 1899 exposed the system. He therefore reinforced the picture of the Irish as clannish machine politicians, even while demonstrating Irish political power. He retired to Glencairn near Dublin and bred racehorses, and his horse Orby won the Epsom Derby in 1907, a final reminder of how far he had travelled.\n\nMaureen O'Hara contributed a very different image through the cinema. Born Maureen FitzSimons in Ranelagh, Dublin, in 1920, she trained at the Abbey Theatre school before moving to Britain and then America. Her first Hollywood film was Jamaica Inn in 1939, directed by Alfred Hitchcock, and she became a major star with How Green Was My Valley in 1941 and Miracle on 34th Street in 1947. She worked repeatedly with John Ford, in films such as Rio Grande in 1950 and The Quiet Man in 1952.\n\nIn The Quiet Man she played the fiery Mary Kate Danaher opposite John Wayne, and her screen image of the Irish woman as spirited and independent became widely known. The film was shot around Cong in Co. Mayo and in Connemara, won Academy Awards at the 1953 ceremony, and drew American visitors who wanted to see the places they had seen on screen, which helped to make Cong a tourist destination. Irish-American audiences embraced her as an Irish star, though Irish critics objected that the film's rural people were comic and drunken and debated whether her roles showed a true picture of Irish women. Historians note that her image was partly a Hollywood creation. She received an honorary Academy Award in 2014 and died in 2015.\n\nJohn F. Kennedy had the greatest effect on how Irish Americans saw themselves and how Ireland saw them. His great-grandfather Patrick Kennedy left Dunganstown, Co. Wexford, in 1849, and the family's Irish origins were well known. When Kennedy was elected president in 1960 he became the first Catholic president of the United States, and many Irish Americans saw his election as acceptance of Irish Catholics in public life, after the defeat of Al Smith in 1928.\n\nKennedy visited Ireland from 26 to 29 June 1963, going to Dublin, Wexford and Dunganstown, Cork, Galway and Limerick, and on 28 June he addressed the Oireachtas in Dublin. The visit drew large crowds and intense press coverage and was celebrated as a moment of Irish pride. His image combined ancestry with success in American politics and encouraged Irish people to see their emigrant relatives as part of a shared story. His assassination on 22 November 1963 was mourned widely in Ireland, and his portrait hung in many Irish homes. Historians debate how far the visit changed Irish self-image and how far it simply celebrated it.\n\nIn conclusion, Croker gave the Irish a reputation for political power and corruption, O'Hara gave them a romantic and spirited screen image, and Kennedy gave them respectability and pride. Kennedy's contribution was the most lasting, because it presented the Irish as full participants in American life, while the other two images were more partial and more stereotyped.",
