    "label": "2",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Seán Lemass and Jack Lynch both led the state through difficult times, but they succeeded to very different degrees. Lemass tackled the problems of economic stagnation, emigration and dependence on Britain with real success, and he started to ease the poisoned relationship with Northern Ireland. Lynch handled the crisis in the North and the split in his own party with moderation and some skill, but he was far less successful in managing the economy, and he left office in 1979 with the public finances in trouble.\n\nThe problem Lemass faced most urgently was the failure of protection. By 1957 the tariffs and Control of Manufactures Acts of the 1930s had produced small factories selling only to a tiny home market, exports consisted mainly of live cattle sold to Britain, and net emigration in the 1950s was about 400,000. Lemass, who had himself built the protectionist system, was practical enough to say that it had served its purpose. He backed T.K. Whitaker's Economic Development of 1958 and the First Programme for Economic Expansion, which welcomed foreign investment, removed the rule that Irish citizens must hold a majority of shares and extended Export Profits Tax Relief. This was a major break with the past and showed how well he tackled the problem.\n\nThe results justify a high judgement. Growth ran at about 4 per cent a year between 1958 and 1963, roughly double the Programme's modest target, manufactured exports rose, and net emigration fell from about 200,000 in 1956 to 1961 to about 80,000 in 1961 to 1966. The 1966 census recorded a population of 2.88 million, the first rise since the early 1950s. There were limits: total employment grew only modestly, farming and the west lagged behind, and the Second Programme of 1963 was abandoned in 1967 after wage rises and a balance of payments deficit. Part of the growth also came from the European boom. Even so, Lemass changed the mood of the country and Fianna Fáil regained an overall majority in 1965.\n\nLemass also faced the problem of Ireland's dependence on Britain and its isolation in Europe. He applied to join the EEC on 31 July 1961, nine days before Britain, but de Gaulle's veto of 14 January 1963 halted this. He then turned to the Anglo-Irish Free Trade Agreement, signed on 14 December 1965 and in force from 1 July 1966, which removed tariffs over ten years and protected the British market for farm exports. He also prepared industry through adaptation councils and the Committee on Industrial Organisation, and he built links with unions and employers through the national wage rounds. Free trade did cause many small firms in textiles and footwear to close, but he dealt with the problem with foresight. He also broke new ground by meeting Terence O'Neill at Stormont on 14 January 1965, the first meeting of the two leaders since partition, though he left partition itself unresolved.\n\nJack Lynch, who succeeded Lemass in November 1966, faced a problem of a different kind when violence erupted in Derry and Belfast in August 1969. He declared that the state could not stand by and set up army field hospitals near the border, but he refused to send troops across it. His policy of seeking unity by consent and opposing violence kept the Republic out of the conflict. He condemned internment in 1971, recalled the ambassador after Bloody Sunday in January 1972, and met Edward Heath and Brian Faulkner at Chequers in 1971, arguing for an Irish dimension. In December 1972 he secured amendments to the Offences Against the State Act to deal with the IRA. Moderation and calm were strengths, though critics saw him as lacking firmness.\n\nLynch's most dangerous problem was inside his own party. In April 1970 he learned that ministers might be involved in a plan to import arms for Northern nationalists, and on 6 May 1970 he dismissed Charles Haughey and Neil Blaney, while Kevin Boland resigned in protest. Haughey was acquitted at the trial in October 1970, and the division it left in Fianna Fáil lasted into the 1980s. Lynch had asserted his authority and prevented the state being drawn into the Northern conflict, but critics argued that he was slow to act and that he did not heal the party.\n\nLynch fared worst on the economy. After he won the largest majority in the history of the state, 84 of 148 seats in June 1977, he cut taxes and abolished domestic rates and the car tax while increasing spending on borrowed money. With inflation, the oil shock following the Iranian Revolution and a rising national debt, the manifesto became a crisis by 1979. Two by-election defeats in Cork in November 1979 ended his authority and he resigned that December.\n\nOverall, Lemass tackled the problems he faced far more successfully. He identified the root cause of stagnation, changed policy and delivered measurable growth, falling emigration and a new openness to Europe. Lynch showed skill in keeping the South out of the Northern conflict and in managing his party, but he did not solve the Arms Crisis divisions, and his economic policy of 1977 stored up difficulties for the 1980s.",
