    "model": "1848 has been called the year of revolutions because within weeks almost every major country of continental Europe was shaken by risings with similar causes and demands. The Revolution in Germany failed because the liberals at Frankfurt had no power of their own, were divided among themselves, and depended on rulers who recovered their nerve and kept their armies.\n\nRevolution began in Palermo in January 1848 and then in Paris on 22 to 24 February, when Louis-Philippe fell and the Second Republic was declared. Within weeks there were risings in Vienna on 13 March, in Milan during the Five Days of 18 to 22 March, and in Venice, Berlin, Budapest and Prague. The causes were common to many countries. Crop failures and depression in 1846 and 1847 caused hunger and unemployment, middle-class liberals wanted constitutions, and nationalists among Germans, Italians, Hungarians and Czechs wanted national states. The example of Paris spread quickly through newspapers, railways and the telegraph. Britain and Russia largely escaped, and by 1849 most risings had been defeated by the old monarchies and their armies.\n\nIn Germany the revolution began with remarkable success. A mass meeting at Mannheim on 27 February demanded a free press, jury trial and a German parliament, and rulers in Baden, Württemberg, Saxony and Hanover appointed liberal ministers. In Berlin troops fired on a crowd on 18 March and barricade fighting left about 300 dead, after which Frederick William IV withdrew the troops and promised that Prussia would merge into Germany. The Frankfurt National Assembly opened in the Paulskirche on 18 May with about 585 deputies, mostly lawyers, civil servants and professors, so that it was nicknamed the professors' parliament, with almost no peasants or workers among them. It chose Archduke John as regent, but his Provisional Central Power had no army, civil service or tax revenue of its own.\n\nThe Assembly's weakness and divisions soon showed. It lost months debating the Basic Rights and the national question, with Grossdeutsch supporters wanting Austria included and Kleindeutsch supporters wanting a Germany under Prussia. The Schleswig-Holstein crisis exposed the lack of an army: Prussia, under British and Russian pressure, made the Malmö armistice in August 1848 without consulting Frankfurt, and the Assembly, after first rejecting it, accepted it on 16 September. Radicals then rioted and murdered two conservative deputies, and the Assembly had to be rescued by Prussian and Austrian troops, which showed its dependence on the very powers it was trying to limit.\n\nMeanwhile the old rulers recovered. Windischgrätz bombarded Prague in June, Radetzky defeated Piedmont at Custoza in July, and Vienna was retaken on 31 October. In Berlin General Wrangel's troops entered the city on 10 November, and on 5 December the king dissolved the Prussian Assembly and imposed his own constitution. The armies and bureaucracies had stayed loyal throughout. The revolutionaries were also split by class and nationality. Middle-class liberals feared a republic and social revolution more than they feared the princes, and national rivalries with Poles, Czechs and Danes divided them further.\n\nThe end came in 1849. The Assembly completed a constitution for a federal empire and on 28 March elected Frederick William IV Emperor of the Germans by 290 votes, with 248 abstentions. He refused the crown on 28 April, unwilling to accept it from an elected assembly rather than from the princes. Austria and Prussia rejected the constitution, so it never came into force. Radical risings in Saxony, the Palatinate and Baden were crushed by Prussian troops, and the rump parliament at Stuttgart was dispersed on 18 June. The old Confederation was restored in 1851.\n\n1848 was a year of revolutions because of shared causes and the speed with which Paris inspired imitation. The German revolution failed because the liberals lacked an army and unity, and the princes who held the armies had only to wait until the revolution lost its momentum.",
