    "model": "Between 1815 and 1871 agriculture and industry changed enormously in Europe. Britain led with enclosure, new methods and the first industrial revolution, while France and the German lands followed more slowly, with peasant farming and then the spread of railways, coal and iron. The gains in wealth were real, but they came at a heavy social cost.\n\nIn British agriculture the main developments were enclosure, new methods and mechanisation. Enclosure replaced open fields and common land with consolidated farms, mostly through thousands of Acts of Parliament between 1750 and 1850, and the General Enclosure Acts of 1801 and 1845 simplified the procedure. Jethro Tull's seed drill sowed in rows, Robert Bakewell's selective breeding produced better sheep and cattle, and from the 1840s fertilisers such as Peruvian guano and John Bennet Lawes's superphosphate raised yields. Threshing machines reduced the need for labour, and anger over them fed the Swing Riots of 1830. The Corn Laws of 1815 protected grain growers until Peel repealed them in 1846, a victory for free trade. Many labourers lost common rights and moved to towns or emigrated.\n\nOn the continent agriculture changed along different lines. In France the Revolution had abolished feudal dues, so that peasants owned much of the land and farming stayed small-scale. In Prussia the Stein-Hardenberg reforms from 1807 abolished serfdom in stages and allowed peasants to own land, though many had to give up part of it to the nobles. Poor harvests in 1846 and 1847 caused hunger across western and central Europe and helped create the conditions for the revolutions of 1848.\n\nIndustry was transformed first in Britain, where coal, iron and cotton were the leading industries and steam power and the factory were at the centre. James Watt improved the steam engine, Arkwright's water frame and Crompton's mule transformed cotton spinning, and Abraham Darby's coke-smelting at Coalbrookdale made iron on a larger scale. Stephenson's Rocket won the Rainhill Trials in 1829, railways expanded rapidly in the 1840s, and Bessemer's converter of 1856 made steel cheaply. The Great Exhibition of 1851 presented Britain as the workshop of the world.\n\nIndustrialisation then spread to the continent. Belgium was first, with coal in Hainaut and Liège. France was slower, building a national network under the Railway Law of 1842. In the German lands the Ruhr and Silesian coalfields grew rapidly, the first railway ran from Nuremberg to Fürth in 1835, and the Zollverein removed internal tariffs and encouraged trade and investment in Prussia and the Rhineland. Krupp's steelworks at Essen expanded from the 1850s. These developments changed working lives. In British cotton mills women and children made up a large share of the workforce, and hand workers resisted the machines, as in the Luddite attacks of 1811 to 1816. The state intervened only partly: the Factory Act of 1833 barred children under nine from most textile factories, the Mines Act of 1842 banned women and children under ten from underground work, and the Ten Hours Act of 1847 limited hours for women and young persons in textile mills.\n\nOverall the key developments were the transformation of British farming through enclosure and new methods, the very different peasant-based agriculture of France and Prussia, and the spread of coal, iron, steam and railways from Britain across the continent. They raised output and national wealth, but they drove people from the land into slums and factories, and governments responded to the costs slowly and unevenly.",
