    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Between 1848 and 1870 Napoleon III and Baron Haussmann together reshaped France. Louis-Napoleon ended the instability of the Second Republic, gave the country order, economic growth and prestige, and then lost it all through foreign failure at Sedan, while Haussmann, working under the emperor's direct authority, rebuilt Paris so completely that it became a model for modern city planning, though at a heavy social and financial cost.\n\nNapoleon III's first contribution was to restore order. Born in 1808, the nephew of Napoleon I, he had failed in coups at Strasbourg in 1836 and Boulogne in 1840 and been imprisoned at Ham, but after the bloody June Days of 1848 he won the presidential election of 10 December 1848 with about three-quarters of the vote, appealing to the peasants and to the memory of his uncle. When the Assembly refused in July 1851 to change the constitution to allow his re-election, he carried out the coup of 2 December 1851, arresting deputies and dissolving the Assembly. Plebiscites approved the coup and then the restoration of the empire, and on 2 December 1852 he became Napoleon III. The Republic was destroyed, but the order that conservatives and peasants craved had returned.\n\nThe early Empire was authoritarian: the press was censored, opponents were exiled and elections were managed through official candidates. Victor Hugo, driven into exile in Jersey and later Guernsey, attacked the regime in Napoleon le Petit and Les Châtiments. Yet the regime also delivered prosperity. The Crédit Mobilier, founded in 1852 by the Pereire brothers, financed railways and industry, and rapid growth followed. The Paris Exposition of 1855 displayed a modernising France, and the Cobden-Chevalier Treaty of 1860 opened French markets to British goods and pushed French industry to modernise. Napoleon III therefore gave France an expansion that few earlier regimes had matched.\n\nHis most visible legacy was the rebuilding of Paris. In June 1853 he appointed Haussmann Prefect of the Seine, and Haussmann held the post for about seventeen years. Napoleon III wanted a healthier city, work for the unemployed, a city easier to control after the barricade fighting of 1830 and 1848, and a showcase for the nation. Using the expropriation law, which let the state buy property compulsorily, Haussmann cut great boulevards through the old city, among them the Boulevard de Sébastopol, the Boulevard Saint-Germain and the Boulevard Saint-Michel, and made the Étoile the centre of twelve radiating avenues. The engineer Belgrand brought clean spring water into the city and built a modern sewer system, Alphand laid out parks such as the Bois de Boulogne, the Bois de Vincennes and the Buttes-Chaumont, and Baltard built the iron-and-glass pavilions of Les Halles. The annexation of the suburbs in 1860 raised the arrondissements from twelve to twenty, so that the whole city could be planned as one.\n\nThe achievement had a darker side. Demolition displaced working-class families to the east and north, rents rose in the new centre, and Paris became more sharply divided between the wealthy west and the poor east, a change Zola satirised in La Curée. The works were financed by heavy borrowing, and Jules Ferry attacked the hidden accounts in 1868. Haussmann was dismissed in January 1870 when the liberal ministry of Émile Ollivier took office. In 1871 the Commune built its barricades in the eastern districts, showing that wide boulevards had not solved the problem of the Parisian poor.\n\nNapoleon III's later years brought liberalisation and disaster. From 1860 the legislature was given more freedom, strikes were legalised in 1864 and censorship was relaxed in 1868. Abroad his record was poor: his Mexican scheme under Maximilian collapsed, with French troops withdrawn by 1867 and Maximilian executed, and Prussia's victory over Austria in 1866 damaged French prestige. He drifted into war with Prussia, was captured at Sedan on 2 September 1870, and the Empire fell two days later. Overall, Napoleon III gave France order and growth, but his regime rested on his personal authority and collapsed with his foreign policy, whereas Haussmann's Paris survived it and remains the most lasting contribution of the Second Empire.",
