    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Cultural nationalism had a profound effect on art and music between 1815 and 1871. Influenced by Romanticism, which prized emotion, the past and the spirit of a people, composers and painters gave nations without states their symbols, myths and sounds, and in doing so helped to create the national feeling that political movements then drew upon.\n\nThe intellectual background came from Herder, who argued in the late eighteenth century that each people had its own Volksgeist, expressed in its language, folklore and history. Romantics rejected the cool rationalism of the Enlightenment and looked to folk traditions and the national past for what was authentic. This mattered most where a nation lacked its own state, as in Italy, Germany, Poland and the Czech lands, because culture could create a sense of belonging where political institutions did not exist. Music and painting, which could move audiences without being censored word by word, became powerful carriers of this idea.\n\nIn music, Chopin and Liszt turned the dances and melodies of their homelands into art for European audiences. Chopin's mazurkas and polonaises drew on Polish dance rhythms, and after the Polish rising of 1830-31 failed he lived in exile, so that his music came to symbolise Polish national feeling when Poland had no government of its own. Liszt, born in Hungary, wrote the Hungarian Rhapsodies in the 1840s and 1850s using Hungarian and Romani melodies, and presented Hungary's identity to audiences across Europe. Both showed that a stateless people could assert itself through its music, and their success made national folk idioms respectable in the concert hall, so that national character became something audiences expected to hear.\n\nGiuseppe Verdi shows most clearly how music became politically charged. His opera Nabucco of 1842 contained the chorus Va, pensiero, sung by Hebrew exiles longing for their homeland, which Italian audiences living under foreign rule came to hear as a call for national liberation. Around 1859 the cry of Viva VERDI was popularly said to be a coded slogan, the letters standing for Vittorio Emanuele Re d'Italia, the king the nationalists wanted. The popularity of his operas meant that a political message could reach a mass audience that pamphlets never reached. Wagner took a different route in Germany, building operas on German myth and legend, and his Ring cycle, drawing on the Nibelungen legend and Norse sagas, was first performed in full at Bayreuth in 1876. In Bohemia, Smetana's opera The Bartered Bride of 1866 and his cycle of symphonic poems Má vlast, composed between 1874 and 1879, drew on Czech folk music and landscape to express Czech identity.\n\nPainting served nationalism in similar ways. Delacroix's Liberty Leading the People of 1830 showed a personification of liberty leading the Parisian crowd over the barricades during the July Revolution, and it gave the French a powerful image of the people as the nation itself. Caspar David Friedrich's landscapes, such as Wanderer above the Sea of Fog of about 1818, expressed the Romantic feeling for nature and the German spirit, linking national identity to the German land. Romantic historical painting, which depicted scenes from the national past, gave citizens a shared visual memory of their history, something that mattered where no common state yet existed. Together these painters made the nation visible, whether as a heroic people, a sacred landscape or a shared inheritance of past glories, and so gave nationalism an emotional appeal that argument alone could not supply.\n\nThe effects had limits. Cultural nationalism often idealised the past and could exclude minorities, and national myths could later be used to justify conflict. Music and art also reached mainly the educated and prosperous audiences of opera houses, concert halls and exhibitions, though the Verdi chorus shows that some works travelled far beyond them. Nevertheless, art and especially music did much to turn the idea of the nation into something people could feel, and so strengthened the movements that created Italy and Germany in 1861-71 and kept Polish, Hungarian and Czech aspirations alive.",
