    "model": "Between 1871 and 1920 Russia suffered problems caused by anti-Semitism, economic crisis, and war and revolution. Anti-Semitism brought violence and mass emigration, economic backwardness and the costs of forced industrialisation brought famine and discontent, and war repeatedly exposed the weakness of the autocracy, ending in the revolutions of 1917 and a devastating civil war.\n\nAnti-Semitism caused great suffering and damaged Russia itself. About five million Jews lived in the empire, the world's largest Jewish community, mostly confined to the Pale of Settlement. After Alexander II was assassinated in 1881, rumours blamed Jews and pogroms swept southern Russia, beginning at Elizavetgrad, with police and troops slow to intervene. The May Laws of 1882 banned Jews from settling in rural areas of the Pale, quotas limited their places in universities from 1887, and Jews were expelled from Moscow in 1891-92. The Kishinev pogrom of 1903 killed 49 Jews and caused international outrage. About two million Jews emigrated between 1881 and 1914, mostly to the United States, while others joined the Bund or socialist movements, so the state lost talented people and pushed some of its ablest subjects into opposition.\n\nEconomic crisis was a second problem. About 80 per cent of the population were peasants, burdened by redemption payments since 1861 and farming in the mir with outdated methods. A harvest failure in 1891 caused famine in the Volga region, followed by a cholera epidemic in 1892. Government relief was slow and volunteers such as Tolstoy and Chekhov did much of the work, so the famine weakened trust in the regime. Sergei Witte, Finance Minister from 1892, then drove rapid industrial growth with railways such as the Trans-Siberian, the gold standard of 1897 and French loans. But the cost fell on the peasants through heavy indirect taxes and grain exports even when people were hungry, workers were concentrated in huge factories such as the Putilov works, and the depression of 1900-03 brought unemployment and strikes.\n\nWar then exposed these weaknesses. Defeat by Japan in 1904-05, including the fall of Port Arthur and the destruction of the Baltic Fleet at Tsushima, humiliated the autocracy and was the immediate trigger for the revolution of 1905. On Bloody Sunday, 22 January 1905, troops fired on a peaceful procession led by Father Gapon, destroying trust in the tsar as protector. Strikes, mutinies and a general strike forced Nicholas II to issue the October Manifesto promising a Duma. The regime survived because the army stayed loyal and the opposition split, but the Fundamental Laws of 1906 kept the Tsar's autocratic powers.\n\nThe First World War was far more destructive. Defeats at Tannenberg and the Masurian Lakes in 1914 and the great retreat of 1915 cost millions of casualties, the railways were overloaded, inflation rose and by the winter of 1916-17 the cities lacked food and fuel. Nicholas took command of the army in 1915, leaving the government to the Tsarina and Rasputin, whose murder in December 1916 showed the regime's isolation. In March 1917 women textile workers in Petrograd demanded bread, a general strike followed, the garrison joined the crowds and Nicholas abdicated, ending 300 years of Romanov rule. The Provisional Government continued the war and delayed land reform, and the Bolsheviks took power in November 1917.\n\nRevolution then brought new problems. The Treaty of Brest-Litovsk in March 1918 cost Russia Poland, the Baltic provinces, Ukraine and Finland, with huge losses of land, population, coal and industry. The civil war of 1918-20 between Reds and Whites was accompanied by War Communism, which seized grain from the peasants, and by the Red Terror, and the Romanov family were shot at Ekaterinburg in July 1918. The Reds won, but only after the country had been devastated.\n\nOverall, the biggest problems were caused by war and revolution, which brought the collapse of the monarchy and years of bloodshed, but they were built on earlier weaknesses, since economic backwardness and the regime's refusal to reform made Russia vulnerable, while anti-Semitism caused terrible suffering and emigration. An autocracy that could neither modernise safely nor survive war was bound to be overturned.",
