    "model": "From my study of the First World War I learned about the generalship of Douglas Haig, the most controversial British commander of the war, and about women in the workforce, whose labour made total war possible. Haig shows how the war was fought on the Western Front and how historians still argue over its leaders, while women's work shows how the war reached into every home and altered, though only partly, women's place in society.\n\nHaig, a cavalryman who had served in the Sudan and the Boer War, succeeded Sir John French as commander-in-chief of the British Expeditionary Force in December 1915 and became field marshal in January 1917. His reputation rests on offensives against defences made strong by machine guns, barbed wire and artillery. The Somme, begun on 1 July 1916 partly to relieve Verdun, cost the British about 57,000 casualties on the first day, about 19,000 of them killed, and it wiped out the young men of communities who had joined Kitchener's Pals battalions. It gained only a few kilometres and cost over a million casualties across all armies. In 1917 Passchendaele, fought in the Ypres salient partly to clear German submarine bases, turned to mud in heavy rain and gained about eight kilometres. These battles became symbols of waste and explain why critics accuse him of throwing lives away.\n\nYet 1918 shows a different Haig. On 11 April, during the German Spring Offensive, he issued his 'backs to the wall' order, and from the Battle of Amiens on 8 August, which Ludendorff called the 'black day of the German army', he led the Hundred Days advance in which British forces played the leading part, using tanks, aircraft and artillery together. Historians divide accordingly. Denis Winter and Gerard De Groot portray a callous, unimaginative butcher, and Alan Clark's The Donkeys popularised 'lions led by donkeys', while John Terraine and Gary Sheffield argue that he learned from experience and led the army to victory. Lloyd George distrusted him, whereas King George V trusted him and many soldiers mourned him. Many historians now accept that his attritional strategy was shaped by circumstances, such as the strength of defences and the need to support France.\n\nWomen's work was the second great change. Before 1914 most women who earned wages were young and unmarried, domestic service was the largest single employer in Britain, pay was typically half that of men, and skilled trades and many unions excluded them. The shell shortage of 1915 and the loss of men to the army led the new Ministry of Munitions to turn to women. The Treasury Agreement of March 1915 and the Munitions of War Act accepted dilution, so hundreds of thousands of 'munitionettes' filled shells and fuses in places such as Woolwich Arsenal. Conditions were harsh, with shifts of 10 to 12 hours, TNT poisoning that gave the 'canary girls' their name, and explosions at Silvertown in 1917, which killed 73, and Chilwell in 1918, which killed over 130. Women also worked in banks, offices, trams and the post, formed the Women's Land Army in 1917 when the submarine campaign made food a priority, released men for the front in the Women's Army Auxiliary Corps, and served as VAD nurses near the fighting. In France women brought in the 1914 harvest and worked in shell factories, and in Germany the Auxiliary Service Law of 1916 increased pressure on women to fill gaps.\n\nThe results were mixed. Women were generally paid less than men, and unions agreed to dilution only on condition that pre-war practices returned. The Representation of the People Act of February 1918 gave the vote to women over 30 who met a property qualification, about 8 million women, and Asquith acknowledged that war work had earned it, while Constance Markievicz became the first woman elected to Westminster. In Germany all women over 20 got the vote in November 1918. But demobilisation pushed women out of their jobs, the Restoration of Pre-War Practices Act of 1919 reinstated the old trade rules, and domestic service remained a major employer in the 1920s.\n\nOverall, I learned that the war was fought with terrible loss under commanders like Haig, whose record lies between the slaughter of the Somme and Passchendaele and the victory of 1918, and that it was a total war in which women's labour was indispensable, even though the lasting change in their status was real but limited.",
