    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Between 1871 and 1920 both the motor car and medicine passed from experiment to practical reality. The car developed from the petrol engine into the mass-produced Model T, while medicine was transformed by germ theory, antiseptics, vaccines and X-rays, which together saved millions of lives.\n\nThe car depended first on the internal combustion engine. Nikolaus Otto built his four-stroke engine in 1876, burning gas, and it became the basis of the petrol engine. Gottlieb Daimler and Wilhelm Maybach developed a small, light, high-speed petrol engine in the 1880s and fitted it to a motorcycle in 1885 and a carriage in 1886. Karl Benz, working independently in Mannheim, built a three-wheeled vehicle with a single-cylinder engine in 1885 and patented his 'Motorwagen' in 1886, regarded as the first practical car designed as a whole. In August 1888 Bertha Benz drove one about 106 km from Mannheim to Pforzheim, buying fuel at a pharmacy and making repairs on the way, which proved its usefulness and won publicity. In France Panhard et Levassor and Peugeot began building cars with Daimler engines in 1890-91, and France led early production, helped by races such as Paris-Rouen in 1894. John Boyd Dunlop's pneumatic tyre of 1888 made travel smoother. In Britain the red-flag law held early vehicles to walking pace until the Act of 1896 raised the limit to 12 mph, and the Mercedes of 1901, with a lighter engine and lower body, set the pattern for later cars.\n\nThe decisive development was mass production in the United States. Early cars were handmade luxury goods, but Henry Ford founded his company in 1903 to build a cheap car for ordinary people. The Model T, launched in October 1908, was simple, strong and easy to repair, and its price fell from about 850 dollars to under 300 by the early 1920s. In 1913 Ford introduced the moving assembly line at Highland Park, cutting the time to build a chassis from over twelve hours to about ninety minutes, and in January 1914 he announced a wage of five dollars for an eight-hour day. By 1920 the USA produced most of the world's cars, and European firms copied Fordism. The First World War showed the car's value, as about 600 Paris taxis carried troops to the Marne in 1914 and lorries supplied Verdun along the 'Sacred Way' in 1916. Even so, cars remained too expensive for most Europeans by 1920.\n\nIn medicine the key development was germ theory. Louis Pasteur showed that microbes cause fermentation and decay and argued that they cause disease. He developed vaccines against chicken cholera, anthrax in 1881 and rabies, and in July 1885 treated the boy Joseph Meister, who survived after being bitten by a rabid dog. In Germany Robert Koch isolated bacteria in pure culture and identified the anthrax bacterium in 1876, the tuberculosis bacillus in 1882 and the cholera bacterium in 1883. Emil von Behring and Kitasato developed antitoxins for diphtheria and tetanus in 1890, and Paul Ehrlich's Salvarsan of 1909, the first effective drug against syphilis, was the first modern chemotherapy.\n\nSurgery and diagnosis also advanced. Joseph Lister used carbolic acid on wounds, instruments and the operating theatre from 1865-67, cutting deaths from infection, and with anaesthetics and, later, asepsis developed in Germany in the 1880s and 1890s, surgery became far safer. Wilhelm Röntgen discovered X-rays in Würzburg in November 1895, and doctors quickly used them to find broken bones and bullets. Marie Curie, who discovered polonium and radium in 1898, organised mobile X-ray units, the 'petites Curies', for field hospitals in the First World War. Clean water and sewers also reduced cholera and typhoid in cities.\n\nOverall, both fields moved from invention to lasting change between 1871 and 1920. The car's key steps were the petrol engine, Benz's Motorwagen and Ford's assembly line, and in medicine they were germ theory, antiseptic surgery and X-rays. Medicine arguably had the greater impact on ordinary lives by 1920, while the car's full social effect was still to come.",
