    "label": "1",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The fascist regimes of Europe between 1920 and 1945, above all Mussolini's Italy and Hitler's Germany, shared a set of main characteristics. They were built on extreme nationalism, rule by a single all-powerful leader, a one-party totalitarian state, violence and terror against opponents, tight control of society through propaganda and youth organisations, and an aggressive foreign policy. There were also differences between them, especially in the weight given to race.\n\nThe first characteristic was extreme nationalism combined with the leader principle. Fascists placed the nation above the individual and believed that power should be concentrated in one leader who embodied the nation. Mussolini became Il Duce, the object of a carefully built personality cult. In Germany decisions flowed down from Hitler as Führer, and his authority was reinforced after Hindenburg's death in August 1934, when he combined the offices of Chancellor and President.\n\nSecond, fascist regimes created single-party totalitarian states. Mussolini's own formula, from 1925, was 'everything within the state, nothing outside the state, nothing against the state'. After the Matteotti murder of 1924 he accepted responsibility for the violence in January 1925, banned opposition parties, censored the press and replaced elected mayors with appointed podesta. In Germany the Enabling Act of March 1933 allowed Hitler to make laws without the Reichstag, trade unions were abolished in May, and a law of July 1933 made the Nazi Party the only legal party. Gleichschaltung brought the civil service, courts, universities and cultural bodies under Nazi control. Neither state allowed any independent political life.\n\nThird, both regimes used violence and terror to gain and keep power. Mussolini's squadristi attacked socialist and trade union premises in the Po valley and Tuscany in the early 1920s, and the regime later used the OVRA secret police and a Special Tribunal for the Defence of the State. In Germany the Reichstag fire decree of February 1933 suspended civil liberties, the Night of the Long Knives of June 1934 removed Röhm and the SA leadership, and Himmler's SS and the Gestapo ran a police state. Dachau, opened in 1933, was the first of many concentration camps. Terror was central to both regimes, but it was applied on a far larger scale in Germany.\n\nFourth, the regimes sought to shape society and win consent through propaganda, youth movements and control of the economy. In Italy the Opera Nazionale Balilla enrolled children, Dopolavoro organised workers' leisure, and Minculpop controlled press, radio and cinema from 1937. The Rocco Law of 1926 banned strikes and tied workers and employers to state-recognised syndicates. In Germany the German Labour Front replaced the free unions, Strength Through Joy offered cheap holidays, and the Four Year Plan of 1936 prepared the economy for war. Both regimes made economic life serve the state and national strength.\n\nFifth, both regimes were hostile to communism and liberal democracy, and both reached an accommodation with the Church for their own purposes. Mussolini signed the Lateran Treaties in 1929, creating Vatican City and settling the Roman Question, and Hitler signed a Concordat with the Vatican in July 1933, although he later breached it. Neither tolerated real independence, and when the Church protested, as the Pope did in Mit brennender Sorge in 1937, the regime hit back.\n\nSixth, aggressive expansion and a glorification of war marked both regimes. Mussolini invaded Abyssinia in October 1935, defied League sanctions and drew close to Germany in the Rome-Berlin Axis of 1936. Hitler reintroduced conscription in 1935, remilitarised the Rhineland in 1936, annexed Austria in 1938 and pursued Lebensraum in the east. Racism was the sharpest difference: Nazism made race central, with the Nuremberg Laws and later the Holocaust, whereas Italy introduced racial laws only in 1938.\n\nIn conclusion, the main characteristics of the fascist regimes were nationalist dictatorship under a cult leader, a one-party totalitarian state, terror and propaganda, hostility to democracy and communism, and militarism and expansion. Nazi Germany was the more radical and more murderous example, but both show how fragile democracies could be destroyed and replaced by total control.",
