    "label": "2",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Josef Stalin both transformed the Soviet economy and used show trials to consolidate his power, and the two were closely linked. The Five-Year Plans and collectivisation turned a peasant country into an industrial state under central control, while the trials of 1936 to 1938 destroyed the old Bolshevik leadership and left Stalin without a rival. The economy gave him a state to command and the trials gave him a party that obeyed him.\n\nStalin first cleared the way by defeating his rivals inside the party. As General Secretary from 1922 he controlled appointments, and he used Zinoviev and Kamenev against Trotsky, then the Right Opposition of Bukharin, Rykov and Tomsky against the Left. Trotsky was expelled in 1927 and exiled in 1929. Stalin then adopted the Left's policy of rapid industrialisation and turned on Bukharin, so that by 1929 no faction could challenge him. This allowed him to scrap Lenin's New Economic Policy and launch a revolution from above.\n\nThe First Five-Year Plan, launched in October 1928 and drawn up by Gosplan, set ambitious targets for coal, steel, electricity and machinery, with heavy industry favoured over consumer goods. New cities such as Magnitogorsk in the Urals were built almost from nothing, and the Stakhanovite movement after the miner Stakhanov's reported record in 1935 was used to push up output. Steel production rose from about 4 million tonnes in 1928 to about 18 million in 1940, and the later plans continued the emphasis on heavy industry, so that the Soviet Union had a large industrial base by 1941. The costs were heavy: wages fell, housing was overcrowded, quality was poor, official figures were inflated, and forced labour was used on projects such as the White Sea-Baltic Canal.\n\nCollectivisation transformed agriculture just as radically. From late 1929 Stalin announced the liquidation of the kulaks as a class, and peasants were forced into collective farms where land and livestock were pooled under state orders. Kulaks were deported or killed, and peasants resisted by slaughtering their livestock. High grain requisitions and the harvest failures of 1932 and 1933 produced a famine that killed several million people, mainly in Ukraine, the North Caucasus and Kazakhstan, which the authorities denied. Output fell for several years, but the state gained control of the countryside and of the grain it needed to feed the towns and pay for industry.\n\nThe show trials consolidated Stalin's power in the political sphere. The murder of Sergei Kirov in Leningrad on 1 December 1934 gave him his pretext, and a decree issued that day allowed terrorism cases to be heard in days with no defence and no appeal. In August 1936 Zinoviev and Kamenev, two of Lenin's closest colleagues, were tried with fourteen others for forming a terrorist centre under Trotsky, confessed in open court and were shot. In January 1937 Pyatakov and Radek were tried for industrial wrecking, which conveniently blamed saboteurs for the failures of the plans. In March 1938 Bukharin, Rykov and the former NKVD chief Yagoda were tried and shot, which removed the last of the old opposition.\n\nThe trials were carefully stage-managed. The prosecutor Vyshinsky argued that confession was proof enough, and the NKVD extracted confessions through sleep deprivation, threats against families, false promises of leniency and, from 1937, torture. Some foreign observers accepted the evidence, but others noticed absurdities such as Pyatakov's claimed flight to Oslo, and the Dewey Commission found the charges false. The trials sat inside the wider Great Terror under Yezhov, in which about 1.6 million were arrested and some 680,000 executed in 1937 and 1938. The army was purged too, with Marshal Tukhachevsky shot in June 1937, and the old party elite was replaced by officials who owed their careers to Stalin.\n\nOverall, the economic transformation and the trials served the same end. The plans created an industrial and military power that was to help defeat Germany, and the trials ensured that nobody in the party could question the leader who had imposed them. Stalin achieved total personal power, but at the cost of famine, mass terror and a weakened army and managerial class, so his success was real but bought at enormous human cost.",
