    "model": "Mikhail Gorbachev, General Secretary from 11 March 1985, had real successes as leader of the USSR. He ended the arms race, opened Soviet society and allowed eastern Europe to go its own way without a shot being fired. Yet his failures were equally striking, for he did not revive the Soviet economy, he could not contain the nationalism his reforms released, and he lost control of the party and the union he had set out to save.\n\nHis first great success was in arms control and foreign policy. Having met Reagan at Geneva in November 1985 and Reykjavik in October 1986, where deep cuts in nuclear weapons were discussed, he signed the INF Treaty in Washington on 8 December 1987, which eliminated a whole class of US and Soviet intermediate-range missiles. In December 1988 he told the United Nations that the USSR would cut its armed forces by 500,000 men and pull divisions out of eastern Europe, and by February 1989 he had withdrawn Soviet troops from Afghanistan, ending a costly war that had begun in December 1979. These steps ended the second Cold War, won him the Nobel Peace Prize in 1990 and made him widely admired in the West, where even Margaret Thatcher had said she could do business with him.\n\nA second success was glasnost, or openness. Introduced more fully at the 27th Party Congress in February 1986 and given urgency by the concealment that followed the Chernobyl explosion of 26 April 1986, it allowed criticism of officials and of the Soviet past, and ended much censorship. Democratisation followed: the partly free elections to the Congress of People's Deputies in March 1989 were the first of their kind in the USSR, and in 1990 the party surrendered its constitutional monopoly of power. For ordinary Soviet citizens this was a genuine gain in freedom, and it was achieved without terror, a remarkable contrast with earlier Soviet leaders.\n\nHis most dramatic success lay in eastern Europe. By abandoning the Brezhnev Doctrine, in what Gennady Gerasimov jokingly called the Sinatra Doctrine in October 1989, Gorbachev let each state choose its own path. The result was the peaceful revolutions of 1989: Solidarity won almost every contested seat in Poland in June and Tadeusz Mazowiecki became prime minister in August, Hungary opened its border with Austria, Honecker fell on 18 October, the Berlin Wall opened on 9 November and Havel became president of Czechoslovakia on 29 December. Only in Romania, where Ceausescu was executed, was there serious violence. Germany was reunified on 3 October 1990 with Soviet consent. Whatever Gorbachev intended, restraint on this scale was a notable achievement, though it also meant the loss of the empire Soviet leaders had fought for since 1945.\n\nAgainst this, his economic reforms failed. His first slogan, uskorenie or acceleration, achieved little, and perestroika, which tried to mix market elements with central planning, produced the worst of both systems. Shortages, queues and inflation worsened, production fell and living standards declined, so that many Russians later blamed him for the collapse of their economy. By removing the discipline of the command system without putting a working market in its place, he left the country poorer and more discontented, and this weakened his authority at the very moment he needed it.\n\nHis second failure was nationalism and the break-up of the union. Glasnost allowed the republics to voice grievances, and Lithuania declared independence on 11 March 1990, with other republics demanding sovereignty. Conservatives within the party and the KGB, alarmed at the loss of control, staged a coup against him on 19-21 August 1991. It failed after Boris Yeltsin, president of the Russian republic since June 1991, rallied resistance in Moscow, but it left Gorbachev a spent force. The Warsaw Pact and Comecon were dissolved in 1991, and on 26 December 1991, the day after Gorbachev resigned, the Soviet Union itself was dissolved into fifteen independent republics.\n\nOverall, Gorbachev's record was one of great achievement abroad and failure at home. He ended the Cold War peacefully, freed eastern Europe and gave his people a voice, but he did not save the economy, the party or the union. Historians differ on whether he meant to end communism or to reform it, and most agree that his reforms had consequences he never foresaw. He was a remarkable reformer who unleashed forces that destroyed the system he wanted to renew.",
