    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Between 1945 and 1992 Europe was transformed by advances in the biological sciences, by nuclear power and by the computer. Medicine and genetics improved health and raised moral questions, nuclear power offered cheap energy but brought fear and accidents, and the computer changed work, society and even the balance between East and West. I will deal with each in turn.\n\nIn medicine, the impact was immediate and large. Penicillin, discovered by Fleming in 1928, was developed by Florey and Chain at Oxford into a mass-produced drug during the war and was widely used in the 1950s, cutting deaths from pneumonia and other infections. Streptomycin, found by Selman Waksman in 1943, was the first effective drug against tuberculosis. Salk's polio vaccine was announced in 1955 and Sabin's oral vaccine was in wide use from the early 1960s, while vaccination campaigns against diphtheria, measles and tuberculosis reduced child deaths. Smallpox was declared eradicated in 1980. Because the welfare state made drugs and hospital care available to all, life expectancy in Western Europe rose by over a decade between 1945 and 1990.\n\nGenetics and surgery added to this. Watson and Crick published the double-helix structure of DNA in April 1953, using X-ray evidence gathered by Rosalind Franklin and Maurice Wilkins, and the three men who lived to receive it shared the Nobel Prize in 1962. In 1973 Cohen and Boyer showed that genes could be moved between organisms, and by 1978 bacteria were producing human insulin. Louise Brown, the first IVF baby, was born in Oldham on 25 July 1978. Christiaan Barnard performed the first human heart transplant in Cape Town in 1967, and John Charnley developed the hip replacement in England in the 1960s. Advances also brought risks and debate: the thalidomide scandal of 1961 caused birth defects in thousands of children and led to stricter testing, and the Warnock Report of 1984 recommended regulating embryo research.\n\nNuclear power was promoted in the 1950s as a source of cheap energy. The Soviet Obninsk reactor supplied the grid from June 1954, and Calder Hall in Cumbria, opened in October 1956, was the first commercial-scale station. Eisenhower's Atoms for Peace speech of 1953 led to the International Atomic Energy Agency in 1957, and the Treaty of Rome created Euratom to coordinate nuclear energy among the six EEC states. After the oil crisis of 1973 governments turned to it more eagerly, and France, under the Messmer Plan of 1974, came to produce about three quarters of its electricity from nuclear plants. Nuclear science also produced weapons, and the stockpiles of both blocs lay behind the whole Cold War.\n\nThe impact of nuclear power was also negative, and opposition grew steadily. The Windscale fire of October 1957 released radioactive iodine over Britain, CND was founded in 1958 and Greenpeace in 1971. Three Mile Island in 1979 raised fears, and the explosion at Chernobyl in Soviet Ukraine on 26 April 1986 spread radioactive material across Europe, forced the evacuation of Pripyat and created a 30-kilometre exclusion zone. It also exposed Soviet secrecy, and strengthened the case for glasnost. In Ireland the plan for a plant at Carnsore Point was dropped after protests in 1978-79, Austria's Zwentendorf plant never operated after a referendum, and Italy voted in 1987 to end nuclear power.\n\nThe computer changed working life and the economy. Colossus, built at Bletchley Park in 1943-44, broke German codes, and the transistor of 1947 and the microchip of the late 1950s made machines smaller and cheaper. IBM's System/360 of 1964 set the standard for business computing, the Intel 4004 microprocessor followed in 1971, and home computers such as the Sinclair ZX Spectrum of 1982 and the IBM PC of 1981 reached offices and homes. Banks introduced cash machines from 1967 and supermarkets adopted bar codes. Automation reduced manufacturing jobs and contributed to deindustrialisation, while service jobs in banking and administration grew. In 1989 Tim Berners-Lee at CERN proposed the World Wide Web.\n\nThe computer also shaped the Cold War. Western export controls through COCOM, from 1949, kept advanced computing out of the Soviet bloc, and the communist economies fell behind in electronics, a gap that helped to discredit central planning. Taken together, these advances made Europeans healthier, richer and better connected, while raising new fears and moral questions. Medicine and the computer had the most lasting benefits, nuclear power the most divisive effects, and all three widened the technological gap between a dynamic West and a stagnant East.",
