    "model": "British withdrawal from India in 1947 was of enormous importance to both countries. For India it meant independence after nearly ninety years of direct British rule, but it came at the cost of partition and terrible violence. For Britain it meant the loss of its most valuable possession, a reduced role in Asia and the beginning of the end of empire, although the Commonwealth survived as a link with former colonies.\n\nFor India, the most important result was freedom. The Indian National Congress, founded in 1885, had moved under Gandhi from petitioning to mass civil disobedience, through the Non-Cooperation movement of 1920 to 1922, the Salt March of 1930 and the Quit India resolution of August 1942. Independence on 15 August 1947 under the Indian Independence Act ended the British Raj, which had ruled India directly since 1858. India became a republic on 26 January 1950 with Jawaharlal Nehru as prime minister, and under him it followed a policy of non-alignment in the Cold War.\n\nHowever, independence for India came with partition, which was also hugely significant. The Muslim League under Jinnah had called for separate Muslim states in the Lahore Resolution of 1940, and after the League's success in the 1945 to 1946 elections the failure of the Cabinet Mission and the violence of Direct Action Day in Calcutta convinced Mountbatten that a united India was impossible. Two dominions, India and Pakistan, were created, and Pakistan was formed in two parts, West Pakistan and East Bengal, separated by about 1,600 km of Indian territory. Cyril Radcliffe, who had never visited India, drew the boundaries through Punjab and Bengal, and the line was announced on 17 August 1947, two days after independence.\n\nThe human cost for India and Pakistan was immense. Between about 10 and 15 million people crossed the new border, Hindus and Sikhs moving into India and Muslims into Pakistan, and estimates of the dead range from about 200,000 to about 2 million. The rushed timetable, which Mountbatten had brought forward to August 1947, left little time to plan policing or the division of the army, civil service and railways. The princely states also had to choose, and the accession of Kashmir to India in October 1947 led to a war with Pakistan and a dispute that has lasted ever since. Gandhi, who opposed partition and fasted to stop the killing, was shot dead on 30 January 1948 by a Hindu nationalist.\n\nFor Britain, the loss of India was a major blow to its prestige and power. India was the largest and most valuable of the British possessions, and its loss reduced Britain's role in Asia. The decision to leave had itself been shaped by Britain's weakness after the war, since it had borrowed heavily from the United States, owed India over a billion pounds in sterling balances and could not afford a large army there. The Labour government of Clement Attlee, elected in July 1945, was also committed to Indian self-rule, and the Royal Indian Navy mutiny of 1946 and the INA trials showed that the loyalty of the Indian forces could no longer be taken for granted.\n\nWithdrawal had important results for the empire and Commonwealth. Burma and Ceylon became independent in 1948, and the Gold Coast followed as Ghana in 1957, so India set the pattern for decolonisation elsewhere. Importantly, the London Declaration of April 1949 allowed India to become a republic while remaining in the Commonwealth, which established that membership did not depend on accepting the monarch as head of state. This kept the Commonwealth alive as Britain's main continuing link with former colonies, and India remained a major trading partner within the sterling area.\n\nOverall, withdrawal was a triumph for India's nationalists but also a tragedy because of partition, while for Britain it was a necessary but painful acceptance of reduced power. The peaceful transfer of power avoided a colonial war like those France fought in Indochina and Algeria, yet the haste of the departure left a legacy of violence and conflict over Kashmir that affected both India and Britain's reputation.",
