    "label": "2",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "David Ben-Gurion and Gamal Abdul Nasser were the two most important leaders in the Middle East in the years after 1945. Ben-Gurion led the Jewish community in Palestine to statehood and became the first prime minister of Israel, while Nasser overthrew the monarchy in Egypt and became the champion of Arab nationalism after the Suez Crisis of 1956. Their opposing aims were central to the Arab-Israeli conflict.\n\nBen-Gurion's first contribution was his leadership of the Zionist movement in Palestine. Born in Plonsk in 1886, he emigrated to Palestine in 1906 and became a labour activist. From 1935 he led the executive of the Jewish Agency, and he was linked with the Haganah, the main Jewish defence force. As British policy hardened in the White Paper of 1939, which limited Jewish immigration to 75,000 over five years, and as the Holocaust intensified the demand for a Jewish homeland, he led the campaign for a Jewish state. He accepted the UN partition plan of November 1947 as a first step towards that goal, even though it gave the proposed Jewish state about 55 per cent of the land.\n\nHis most famous act was the proclamation of the State of Israel in Tel Aviv on 14 May 1948, the day the British Mandate expired. He then led Israel through the war that followed, when Egypt, Jordan, Syria, Iraq and Lebanon sent armies into former Mandate territory on 15 May. As prime minister and defence minister he oversaw the creation of the Israel Defence Forces in May 1948, and he ordered the shelling of the Irgun ship Altalena in June 1948 to establish a single army under state control. The 1949 armistice left Israel with more territory than the UN plan had allotted, but about 700,000 Palestinian Arabs had fled or been expelled, creating a refugee problem that remains unresolved. Arabs therefore saw him as the architect of a state that displaced Palestinians, while most Jews saw him as its founder.\n\nBen-Gurion also contributed to developments in the Suez Crisis. As prime minister in 1956 he attended the secret talks at Sevres with Britain and France, where the Protocol of 24 October set out an Israeli attack on Sinai followed by an Anglo-French ultimatum. Israeli forces invaded Sinai on 29 October, and Israel overran the peninsula within days. Israel's motives included stopping fedayeen raids from Gaza and ending Egypt's blockade of Israeli shipping at the Straits of Tiran, and it withdrew in early 1957 after the United States and the UN gave assurances about the Straits and the UN force.\n\nNasser's contribution began with the Free Officers' coup of July 1952, which overthrew King Farouk. He became prime minister in 1954 and president in 1956, and he pursued land reform, industrialisation and the building of the Aswan High Dam. He negotiated the Anglo-Egyptian Agreement of October 1954, under which British troops left the Suez Canal base by June 1956, ending Britain's military presence in Egypt. He opposed Western alliances such as the Baghdad Pact, attended the Bandung Conference in 1955 and bought arms from the Soviet bloc through Czechoslovakia, which made Egypt a focus of Cold War rivalry.\n\nHis defining act was the nationalisation of the Suez Canal Company on 26 July 1956, after the United States and Britain withdrew their offer to fund the Aswan High Dam. Israel, Britain and France invaded, but the United States under Eisenhower opposed the action, Britain faced a run on the pound and the Soviet Union threatened rocket attacks. The ceasefire of 6 to 7 November left the canal in Egyptian hands. Though his army was defeated in Sinai, Nasser emerged as a hero of the Arab world. Anthony Eden resigned in January 1957, and Britain and France lost their leading role in the Middle East, with the United States taking a larger part.\n\nNasser went on to promote pan-Arab unity by forming the United Arab Republic with Syria in 1958, which lasted until 1961, and he supported anti-colonial movements such as that in Algeria. However, he also suppressed the Muslim Brotherhood, ruled as a one-party leader, and led Egypt into the costly Yemen war of 1962. In 1967 he blocked the Straits of Tiran and Egypt lost Sinai in the Six-Day War. He died in 1970.\n\nOverall, both men were decisive. Ben-Gurion created and defended the Jewish state, while Nasser challenged Western control and gave Arab nationalism confidence. Together, their opposed ambitions shaped the Arab-Israeli conflict and the decline of British and French influence in the Middle East.",
