    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The withdrawal of Britain, France, Belgium and Portugal from Africa between 1960 and 1990 brought real gains in political control and some social progress, but its economic effects were largely damaging, and its social effects were mixed, with ethnic division and violence in several states. Colonial economies and colonial borders shaped what independent states could achieve, so the legacy of empire explains much of what followed.\n\nOne of the most serious social effects was the strengthening of ethnic rivalry, as seen in Nigeria. Lugard had joined the north and south into one colony in 1914, and the 1954 federation of three regions meant that each main party rested on one group, the Hausa-Fulani, the Yoruba and the Igbo. The army coup of January 1966 and the counter-coup of July brought the massacre of many thousands of Igbo in the north, and the eastern region declared the Republic of Biafra under Ojukwu in May 1967. The war lasted until January 1970, and famine and disease killed hundreds of thousands, perhaps more than two million. Gowon's policy of reconciliation, and his statement that there was no victor and no vanquished, helped Nigeria hold together, yet the war showed how colonial structures left the new state fragile.\n\nThe Congo shows the social cost of a withdrawal that was too sudden. Belgium had run the colony for profit and given little education to Africans, so that by 1960 only a handful of Congolese had a university degree and the Force Publique had no Congolese officers. Independence on 30 June 1960 came only five months after the Brussels Round Table. Within a week soldiers mutinied against their Belgian officers, Katanga seceded with Belgian backing on 11 July, and the UN had to send about 20,000 troops. Lumumba was murdered in January 1961 and Mobutu seized power in 1965. Under Mobutu, Zaire became a one-party state and authenticité banned Christian names and European dress, which shows how far politics replaced social development.\n\nThere were also real social gains, especially where independence was peaceful. In Tanganyika, Nyerere led TANU to independence in 1961 without war, made Swahili the national language and avoided ethnic parties, which kept ethnic conflict low across a state of more than a hundred groups. His government's universal primary education programme of 1974 raised school enrolment rapidly and adult literacy rose sharply by the early 1980s. International campaigns also had effect, since smallpox was eradicated by 1977. These gains show that independence could improve ordinary lives, but they depended on leaders and stability, which many states lacked.\n\nThe economic effects were in most cases harder to escape. Colonial economies were built on exporting raw materials such as cocoa, groundnuts, cotton and Katanga copper, and railways ran from mines to ports rather than linking African states. After independence these states stayed dependent on one or two commodities, so a fall in the copper price in 1974-75 wrecked Zaire's economy, and coffee and cocoa prices fell in the 1980s. Foreign firms such as Shell-BP and the Union Minière kept control of oil and mining. African leaders tried to change this, and Mobutu's Zairianisation of 1973 handed foreign businesses to his associates and made the collapse worse.\n\nDebt, aid and famine followed. Oil price rises in 1973 and 1979 raised import costs, governments borrowed heavily, and when interest rates rose and commodity prices fell in the early 1980s the IMF and World Bank made loans conditional on structural adjustment, with devaluation and cuts in public spending that reduced health and education budgets. Ethiopia's famine of 1983-85 killed hundreds of thousands and prompted Band Aid and Live Aid, and the Sahel drought of the 1970s was almost as grave. Ties with Europe continued through the Lomé Convention of 1975, which gave 46 African, Caribbean and Pacific states duty-free access to the EEC and a fund, STABEX, to cover falls in export earnings, though exports stayed concentrated on raw materials. Tanzania's ujamaa policy, launched by the Arusha Declaration of 1967, tried a different path, but forced villagisation from 1973-74 cut food output and left the country importing food by the early 1980s.\n\nOverall, the social and economic effects of withdrawal were uneven. Independence brought political control, expansion of schooling and health care, and in places national unity, but it also left states with colonial borders, ethnic divisions, narrow economies and few trained administrators. The economic effects were more damaging than the social ones, because dependence on exports and foreign lenders was hard to escape, while the social outcomes varied from Tanzania's stability to the bloodshed of Biafra and the Congo.",
