    "label": "2",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The Cuban Missile Crisis posed the greater threat to US security. The Korean War was a costly and dangerous conventional conflict fought thousands of miles from American shores, but in October 1962 Soviet nuclear missiles were sited ninety miles from Florida, and for thirteen days the United States and the Soviet Union stood closer to nuclear war than at any other time in the Cold War.\n\nThe Korean War began on 25 June 1950 when North Korean forces invaded the South, and it seemed to confirm the fears expressed in NSC-68 that communism was on the march everywhere. It came only months after Mao proclaimed the People's Republic of China on 1 October 1949 and the Soviet Union tested its own atomic bomb on 29 August 1949, so Washington read the invasion as part of a coordinated Communist offensive. Acheson's speech of January 1950, which left Korea outside the American defence perimeter, may even have encouraged the attack. This made Korea a real test of containment, and Truman sent American forces under a United Nations flag without a declaration of war.\n\nThe war carried genuine dangers. MacArthur's landing at Inchon on 15 September 1950 and the recapture of Seoul were followed by an advance north of the 38th parallel, and Chinese forces entered the war in large numbers from late October and November 1950, driving UN troops back. A direct clash with a Communist great power was now a reality, and about 36,000 Americans died before the armistice at Panmunjom on 27 July 1953. The frontier ended close to the 38th parallel and no peace treaty was signed, so the conflict was a stalemate that tied down American troops for years. It also helped to push through the huge rise in defence spending that NSC-68 had urged.\n\nYet the threat to the United States itself was limited. The fighting stayed on the Korean peninsula, American cities were never in danger, and the Soviet Union did not openly enter the war. When MacArthur publicly argued for attacking China, Truman dismissed him on 11 April 1951, showing that the President was determined to prevent the war spreading or turning nuclear. The main damage was political, in the row over MacArthur and in the pressure on Truman at home, rather than a direct menace to American territory.\n\nCuba was a different order of danger. After Castro took power on 1 January 1959 and nationalised American property in 1960, Cuba turned to the Soviet Union for trade and oil, and Eisenhower broke off relations in January 1961. The Bay of Pigs invasion by CIA-trained exiles, defeated within three days in April 1961, humiliated Kennedy, and Operation Mongoose kept up covert pressure on Castro. These events gave Khrushchev reasons to defend Cuba and to answer American Jupiter missiles in Turkey and Italy, and from September 1962 Soviet medium-range missiles were shipped to the island.\n\nWhen a U-2 flight on 14 October 1962 revealed the launch sites, the threat was immediate and direct. Missiles so close to American cities could reach much of the United States with almost no warning, which removed the thirty-minute margin that American planners had come to rely on and changed the strategic balance. Kennedy rejected an air strike or invasion, which might have triggered Soviet retaliation, and on 22 October announced a naval quarantine of Cuba. On 24 October Soviet ships turned back from the quarantine line, but on 27 October, Black Saturday, a U-2 was shot down over Cuba and its pilot, Major Rudolf Anderson, was killed. Any miscalculation could have launched a nuclear exchange.\n\nThe resolution also showed how serious the danger was. Khrushchev agreed on 28 October to remove the missiles in return for a pledge not to invade Cuba, and Kennedy secretly agreed to withdraw the Jupiter missiles from Turkey. The installation of a hotline between Washington and Moscow in 1963 was an admission by both sides that they had come too close to disaster.\n\nOverall, Korea cost far more American lives and tested containment in a long and bitter war, but it was limited in geography and the United States was never itself under direct threat. The Cuban Missile Crisis lasted less than two weeks, yet it threatened the survival of the United States and of much of the world, which makes it the greater threat to US security.",
