    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Between 1945 and 1989 there were many attempts to tackle racial inequality in the United States, from court cases and federal laws to mass protest and anti-poverty programmes. They won real legal gains and ended formal segregation, but they achieved much less against the economic and residential inequality that remained in 1989.\n\nThe first attempts came through the courts. The NAACP had led a legal campaign against segregation in education and voting in the 1930s and 1940s, challenging the doctrine of separate but equal established by Plessy v Ferguson in 1896. President Truman's Executive Order 9981 of 1948 ordered the armed forces to desegregate, partly to answer communist criticism of American racism. The great victory was Brown v Board of Education on 17 May 1954, when the Supreme Court ruled unanimously that segregated public schools were unconstitutional. Southern resistance was fierce, shown in the Southern Manifesto of 1956, and in September 1957 Eisenhower had to send federal troops to Little Rock after Governor Faubus used the National Guard to block nine black students. The Civil Rights Act of 1957 created a Civil Rights Commission, but did little for black voting.\n\nThe second attempt was mass non-violent protest. The Montgomery bus boycott of 1955 to 1956 followed the arrest of Rosa Parks and made Martin Luther King Jr. a national leader. In 1960 students sat in at a Woolworth lunch counter in Greensboro and founded SNCC, and in 1961 CORE organised the Freedom Rides to test desegregation of interstate bus terminals. King's Southern Christian Leadership Conference, founded in 1957, led the Birmingham campaign of 1963, where Bull Connor's use of dogs and fire hoses was broadcast to the nation and the world. The March on Washington on 28 August 1963, with about a quarter of a million people and King's I Have a Dream speech, built pressure on Congress, and Kennedy called civil rights a moral issue in June 1963.\n\nThe third attempt was federal legislation under Johnson, which finally used national power against Jim Crow. The Civil Rights Act of 2 July 1964 banned segregation in hotels, restaurants and theatres, allowed federal funds to be cut from segregated programmes and set up the Equal Employment Opportunity Commission. It passed after a 54-day Senate filibuster by southern senators. The Selma campaign of early 1965, with Bloody Sunday on 7 March, led to the Voting Rights Act of 6 August 1965, which banned literacy tests and sent federal examiners to register black voters, so that registration in the South rose sharply and black officials began to take office. The Fair Housing Act of April 1968, passed days after King's assassination, banned discrimination in most housing sales and rentals.\n\nA fourth approach tackled poverty, which hit black Americans hardest. Michael Harrington's The Other America in 1962 drew attention to poverty, and Johnson declared a War on Poverty in 1964, creating the Job Corps, Head Start and community action programmes. The official poverty rate fell from about 22 per cent in 1959 to about 12 per cent in 1969, helped by growth and by Medicaid and food stamps. But the programmes were underfunded as Vietnam took money, and the Kerner Commission reported in February 1968 that the country was moving towards two societies, one black and one white, separate and unequal, after riots in Watts in 1965 and Detroit in 1967.\n\nFrom the mid-1960s more radical voices sought other answers. Malcolm X criticised integration, and Stokely Carmichael popularised Black Power in 1966, demanding self-determination and community control. The Black Panthers, founded in Oakland in 1966, ran community programmes such as free breakfasts for children. In the 1970s and 1980s the focus moved to affirmative action, limited but not ended by the Bakke decision of 1978, and to school busing, which provoked riots in Boston in 1974. Black political power grew, with Harold Washington becoming mayor of Chicago in 1983 and Jesse Jackson running for the Democratic nomination in 1984 and 1988.\n\nOverall, the attempts to tackle racial inequality transformed the legal position of black Americans, ending Jim Crow and securing the vote. They were far less successful against inequality in housing, schooling and jobs, and by 1989 poverty and imprisonment rates, worsened by the 1986 Anti-Drug Abuse Act and its harsher sentences for crack, remained far higher for black Americans than for whites.",
