    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The United States achieved the Moon landing of 20 July 1969 through a decade of Cold War determination, massive funding and careful organisation, built on the shock of Soviet space successes. Its significance was great both at home, where it was a national triumph in a divided year, and abroad, where it was a Cold War victory and a global event.\n\nThe starting point was the space race with the Soviet Union. Sputnik, launched on 4 October 1957, alarmed Americans about Soviet technology and education, and led to the creation of NASA in 1958 and the National Defense Education Act. Gagarin became the first man in space on 12 April 1961, and this, following the Bay of Pigs, led Kennedy to commit the United States on 25 May 1961 to landing a man on the Moon and returning him safely before the end of the decade. At Rice University in 1962 he said the nation chose to go to the Moon because it was hard. The Moon was a field where the United States believed it could win.\n\nSuccess then depended on step-by-step technical progress and organisation. Project Mercury sent Shepard and Glenn into space in 1961 and 1962, and Gemini in 1965 and 1966 tested the rendezvous and docking needed for a lunar mission. NASA chose lunar orbit rendezvous in 1962, with a separate lunar module descending from the orbiting command module. The Saturn V, the most powerful rocket built until then, was developed under Wernher von Braun, and the Apollo Guidance Computer from MIT was one of the first to use integrated circuits. Contractors such as North American Aviation, Grumman, Boeing and IBM employed hundreds of thousands of people, directed by James Webb and from Mission Control in Houston.\n\nMoney and risk-taking were essential too. Apollo cost about 25 billion dollars, with NASA taking 4 to 5 per cent of federal spending at its peak in 1965 and 1966. The programme survived tragedy when the Apollo 1 fire on 27 January 1967 killed Grissom, White and Chaffee and forced a redesign. Apollo 8 orbited the Moon in December 1968, and Apollo 9 and 10 tested the lunar module in 1969. Apollo 11 was launched on 16 July with Armstrong, Aldrin and Collins, and the lunar module Eagle landed in the Sea of Tranquillity on 20 July. Armstrong took manual control to avoid a boulder field with very little fuel left, and the crew splashed down in the Pacific on 24 July, meeting Kennedy's deadline.\n\nAt home the landing was a moment of national pride and a triumph of American engineering and organisation. Armstrong's words about one small step for a man were heard by an audience estimated at 600 million, and Nixon spoke to the astronauts from the White House. It came in a year of Vietnam protest, and some commentators contrasted the technical triumph with troubles at home, while critics argued the money would have been better spent on poverty. It also tied the growth of Florida, Texas, Alabama and California to federal spending and fed advances in computing and electronics.\n\nAbroad, the landing was above all a Cold War victory. The Soviet N1 rocket had failed in every test and the Soviet crewed lunar programme was cancelled, and the Soviet probe Luna 15 crashed on the Moon on 21 July 1969, while Soviet media played down Apollo. The plaque left on the Moon read that the crew came in peace for all mankind, and no national claim was made because of the Outer Space Treaty of 1967. The landing showed American technology to the whole world, restoring the prestige lost to Sputnik and Gagarin, though public and political interest soon faded and Apollo 17 in December 1972 was the last mission.\n\nOverall, the landing was achieved by Kennedy's clear goal, huge spending and brilliant organisation, and its significance was greatest as a symbol, a Cold War victory abroad and a national triumph at home, rather than for any lasting space programme.",
