    "label": "B2",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "About five million Jews lived in the Russian Empire in the late 1800s, most of them confined to the Pale of Settlement set up in 1791. After Alexander II was assassinated in 1881, rumours blamed the Jews and pogroms swept southern Russia. The May Laws of 1882 banned Jews from settling in rural areas of the Pale and from owning land, and quotas limited Jewish places in schools from 1887. The Kishinev pogrom of 1903 killed 49 Jews and caused international outrage, and about two million Jews emigrated between 1881 and 1914.",
