    "label": "C1",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "The Lateran Treaties, signed on 11 February 1929 by Mussolini and Cardinal Gasparri, ended the long dispute between the Italian state and the papacy and created Vatican City. The Italian state paid the Church 1.75 billion lire in compensation for the lost papal states, and the Church recognised the Kingdom of Italy with Rome as its capital. The Concordat made Catholicism the sole religion of the Italian state and gave religious marriage civil effect, and the Church's endorsement helped legitimise Mussolini's regime among Italian Catholics. In 1931 the regime clashed with Catholic Action over its youth groups, and Pius XI protested in the encyclical Non abbiamo bisogno, after which an agreement limited Catholic Action to religious, cultural and charitable work. The racial laws of 1938 excluded Jews from schools, the professions and the armed forces, and the Church objected in part but did not break with the regime.",
