    "label": "C3",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Jarrow was a one-industry town, and when Palmers shipyard closed in the early 1930s most of its working men lost their jobs. National Shipbuilders Security bought the yard in 1934 and barred shipbuilding there for forty years, so no revival was possible. Unemployment was far above the national average, with well over half of insured workers on the dole in the mid-1930s, and the Special Areas Act of 1934 gave the town little help. The Jarrow Crusade of October 1936 was a march of about 200 men to London, carrying a petition of over 11,000 signatures for new industry, above all a steelworks. Stanley Baldwin refused to meet the deputation, and the petition was presented to the Commons on 4 November but not debated. The march did not win a steelworks or any immediate result, as recovery came only with rearmament and war, although it made Jarrow a national symbol.",
