    "label": "C4",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Penicillin was developed by Florey and Chain in Oxford and mass-produced from 1943 to 1944, mainly in the USA, and antibiotics cut deaths from tuberculosis and pneumonia in the 1950s. Jonas Salk's polio vaccine was announced in 1955, vaccination campaigns reduced child deaths, and the WHO declared smallpox eradicated in 1980. Watson and Crick published the double-helix structure of DNA in 1953, and the first test-tube baby, Louise Brown, was born in England in 1978. Calder Hall in Cumbria opened in October 1956 as the world's first commercial-scale nuclear power station, and France's Messmer Plan of 1974 later made nuclear power provide most of its electricity. The Three Mile Island accident of 1979 and the Chernobyl disaster of 1986 strengthened anti-nuclear protests across Europe. Life expectancy in Western Europe rose by over a decade between 1945 and 1990, and Italy voted to end nuclear power in 1987.",
