    "model": "The Cabinet Mission of 1946 failed to win agreement between Congress and the League on a federal India. The League's Direct Action Day of 16 August 1946 led to communal killings in Calcutta that left thousands dead. Mountbatten brought the date of independence forward to 15 August 1947, which left too little time to plan the boundary and policing. Cyril Radcliffe, a barrister who had never visited India, drew the Punjab and Bengal boundaries, and the Radcliffe Line was announced two days after independence. Millions crossed the new border as Hindus and Sikhs moved into India and Muslims moved into Pakistan, with about 10 to 15 million people displaced. Communal violence killed between about 200,000 and 2 million people, and the dispute over Kashmir led to war between India and Pakistan.",
