    "model": "The boycott began because Montgomery's bus rules forced black passengers to stand or give up their seats. Rosa Parks was arrested on 1 December 1955 for refusing to give up her seat, and the Women's Political Council called a one-day boycott for 5 December. A mass meeting that evening voted to continue, and the Montgomery Improvement Association, led by King, organised car pools that kept riders off the buses for 381 days. The initial demands were modest, such as courteous treatment and seating on a first-come, first-served basis, but the city refused to negotiate. A federal court ruled bus segregation unconstitutional in June 1956, and the Supreme Court upheld the ruling on 13 November 1956. The boycott achieved its main aim, as the buses were desegregated on 21 December 1956, although segregation remained in schools, housing and voting.",
