    "model": "Daniel O'Connell's greatest strength as a political leader was his ability to organise and discipline ordinary people in a peaceful mass movement, and this won him Catholic Emancipation in 1829. His weaknesses lay in his reliance on bluff, his willingness to trade Repeal for Whig favours and the narrowly Catholic character of his movement, and they were exposed when Repeal collapsed after 1843.\n\nHis first great strength was organisation. Born in 1775 into a Catholic gentry family in Kerry and a hugely successful barrister, O'Connell founded the Catholic Association in 1823 with Richard Lalor Sheil. In 1824 he introduced the Catholic Rent of a penny a month, which brought tenant farmers, labourers and townspeople into politics in their hundreds of thousands. It was collected through the parishes, so the Church's network became his political machine. This made the Association the first mass political movement in Irish or British history, and it paid for lawyers, petitions, newspapers and election campaigns.\n\nSecond, he was a brilliant strategist who understood how to turn popular strength into political pressure while staying within the law. After the Association was suppressed in 1825 he simply re-formed it as the New Catholic Association. In 1826 the forty-shilling freeholders, led to the poll by their priests, defeated the Beresford interest in Waterford. In 1828 he stood himself in Clare, although as a Catholic he could not take his seat, and beat the minister William Vesey-FitzGerald by 2,057 votes to 982, with the campaign passing off peacefully. Wellington and Peel feared civil war or rebellion if they refused to act, and the Relief Act of April 1829 was the result. His horror of bloodshed, shaped by the French Revolution and 1798, meant that moral force backed by an implied threat achieved what petitions had failed to do.\n\nThird, he was an unrivalled orator and a parliamentarian of real ability who used his position to win practical reforms. In the 1830s he led the Irish MPs at Westminster, and through the Lichfield House Compact of 1835 he kept Melbourne's Whigs in office in return for fair appointments. Under Thomas Drummond Catholics were given office, the police were centralised and the Municipal Corporations Act of 1840 opened up the closed Protestant corporations. He became Lord Mayor of Dublin in 1841, the first Catholic to hold the office since the reign of James II. He also championed religious liberty for Jews and dissenters and opposed slavery, which shows a breadth of principle beyond the Catholic cause.\n\nHis weaknesses, however, were considerable. His tactics depended on bluff, and in 1843 Peel called it. After the monster meetings of Repeal Year, including the vast gathering at Tara in August, the government banned the meeting planned for Clontarf the evening before it was due and brought in troops and artillery. O'Connell cancelled it at once, fearing a massacre, which preserved his principle but showed that he would never go beyond the law and that Repeal was an empty threat. He was convicted of conspiracy in 1844, though the Lords quashed the verdict, and by then he was nearly seventy, in poor health and talking confusingly of a federal alternative.\n\nHis alliance with the Whigs also revealed a willingness to put Repeal aside for favours, and his Repeal motion of 1834 had been defeated by 523 votes to 38. He controlled his movement rigidly and gave patronage to his family, and in 1846 his peace resolutions, declaring force never justified, drove Thomas Francis Meagher and Young Ireland out of the Repeal Association. His movement was also overwhelmingly Catholic, so it alienated Protestants and Ulster, and the Catholic clergy and Church became tied to nationalism in a way that deepened sectarian division. Even his triumph of 1829 had a price, since the companion Act raised the county franchise from forty shillings to £10 and cut the electorate from about 216,000 to about 37,000, disfranchising the very voters who had won the victory.\n\nOn balance, O'Connell's strengths were real and lasting. He created modern mass democratic politics and passed on a constitutional tradition that Butt and Parnell later inherited. But his weaknesses meant that his central aim after 1829, Repeal, failed completely, and that he was unable to hold his movement together as the Famine and Young Ireland closed in. He was a great agitator and organiser, but a less successful leader once Emancipation had been won.",
