    "marks": 100,
    "model": "I agree that Fenianism had both an Irish and an international dimension. It was founded in Ireland and drew on Irish grievances and traditions, but it was from the start a transatlantic movement, funded and partly led from the United States and carried abroad by actions in England and Canada.\n\nThe Irish dimension began with the founding of the Irish Republican Brotherhood in Dublin on 17 March 1858 by James Stephens, with Thomas Clarke Luby and others, as an oath-bound secret society aiming to establish an Irish republic by force. Its roots were thoroughly Irish. Stephens himself had fought at Ballingarry in 1848 under William Smith O'Brien, so the movement carried on the physical force tradition of Young Ireland. It also fed on the bitterness left by the Famine and on disillusion with parliamentary politics after the Independent Irish Party collapsed in 1852 when Keogh and Sadleir took office. Many young nationalists concluded that constitutional methods had failed.\n\nThe IRB was organised in secret circles under local leaders reporting to Stephens as Chief Organiser of the Irish Republic. Its members were mainly artisans, clerks, shopkeepers and farmers' sons, and it tried to recruit Irish soldiers in the British army. In 1861 the funeral of Terence Bellew MacManus, a 1848 rebel, in Dublin showed its strength, even though Cardinal Cullen refused to let the body lie in the Pro-Cathedral. In 1863 it founded its newspaper, the Irish People, edited by Luby, John O'Leary and Charles Kickham, whose novels also gave it a voice in rural Ireland. The government struck in September 1865, raiding the paper and arresting the leaders, and in 1866 habeas corpus was suspended. The rising of March 1867 in Dublin, Cork, Limerick and Tipperary was crushed in bad weather, undermined by informers and lack of arms. The Church was a strong opponent, as Cullen and most bishops condemned it as a secret society.\n\nThe international dimension was equally important. In 1858, the same year as the IRB, John O'Mahony founded the Fenian Brotherhood in New York, which raised money and men for the cause. Emigration after the Famine had created a large and embittered Irish population in America, and American Fenians included veterans of the Civil War whose military experience raised hopes of success in Ireland. It was American leaders who finally removed Stephens in December 1866 after he postponed a rising he had promised. The movement thus depended on the Irish diaspora for its resources, and its leadership was divided across the Atlantic.\n\nFenian action also crossed borders. American Fenians launched raids into Canada, at Ridgeway in June 1866 and again in 1870, hoping to strike at British power, though they failed to win any territory. In England, Fenians in Manchester freed Thomas Kelly and Timothy Deasy from a prison van in September 1867 and killed Sergeant Charles Brett, and William Allen, Michael Larkin and Michael O'Brien were hanged in November as the Manchester Martyrs, mourned across Ireland. The Clerkenwell explosion in London in December 1867 killed about a dozen people and turned British opinion against the Fenians.\n\nThese events had a political effect on Britain, since the Manchester executions and Clerkenwell convinced Gladstone that Irish discontent could threaten Britain itself. He became prime minister in December 1868 and spoke of his mission to pacify Ireland, passing the Irish Church Act in 1869 and the Land Act in 1870. So the international dimension, through the use of Britain and Canada as theatres of action, helped to force reform in Ireland.\n\nI therefore agree with the statement. Fenianism grew from Irish roots in 1848, the Famine and failed parliamentary politics, and it created a disciplined organisation across Ireland, but it relied on American money, American leaders and actions in England and Canada. It failed militarily in 1867, yet its two dimensions together made it the most lasting revolutionary movement of the period and kept the republican idea alive into the twentieth century.",
