    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Thomas Davis and Cardinal Paul Cullen made very different contributions to Irish identities, Davis to a cultural and non-sectarian idea of the nation and Cullen to a disciplined and strongly Roman Catholic identity. Between them they show how, in the middle of the nineteenth century, Irish people came to think of themselves in new ways.\n\nThomas Davis was born in Mallow in 1814, the son of an army surgeon, and was a Protestant educated at Trinity College Dublin who became a barrister in 1838. He joined the Repeal Association in 1841 and in 1842 co-founded The Nation with Charles Gavan Duffy and John Blake Dillon, becoming its leading writer. The paper became the most widely read in Ireland, passed from hand to hand and read aloud in reading rooms, and it gave the Repeal movement a younger and more militant tone. Davis's central idea was that a nation was a community of shared history, culture and, ideally, language, rather than of religion, and that Catholic, Protestant and Dissenter could unite in a common Irish identity.\n\nHe spread this idea through popular culture. His ballads, such as A Nation Once Again and The West's Asleep, put history and patriotism into verse that young people could sing, and he urged the study and teaching of Irish history and the Irish language, arguing that a people without a language of its own was only half a nation. He also edited the Library of Ireland, a series of cheap books on Irish history and literature. He supported mixed, non-denominational education and welcomed the Queen's Colleges in 1845, which brought him into open conflict with O'Connell. He died of scarlet fever in September 1845, aged only 30.\n\nDavis's contribution was lasting but limited. Historians see him as the founder of cultural nationalism, an inspiration to later figures such as Pearse and the Gaelic revival, and The Nation's ideas shaped the Fenians and later nationalists. But his ideal of a nation uniting Protestant and Catholic was never realised, his appeal to Protestants was small, and the Irish language continued to decline, with the 1851 census showing roughly a quarter of people still speaking it.\n\nPaul Cullen, born in Kildare in 1803, shaped Irish religious identity in a very different way. He studied in Rome, became rector of the Irish College there in 1832, and was sent back as Archbishop of Armagh and Apostolic Delegate in 1850 to carry out Roman policy. He presided at the Synod of Thurles in 1850, the first national synod of the Irish Catholic Church since Kilkenny in 1642. Its decrees laid down uniform liturgy and discipline, regulated clerical conduct, increased the bishops' authority over priests, discouraged priests from political agitation, condemned the Queen's Colleges and ordered a Catholic University, which opened in 1854 under Newman.\n\nCullen became Archbishop of Dublin in 1852 and the first Irish cardinal in 1866. He championed ultramontane devotion, church building, religious orders, parish missions and Catholic schools. Historians such as Emmet Larkin see him as the central figure in the devotional revolution, in which Mass attendance rose from perhaps a third of Catholics before the Famine to the large majority by the 1870s. The result was a Catholic identity that was more uniform, more disciplined and more closely tied to Rome and to nationality. He distrusted Young Ireland and the Tenant League, and opposed the Fenians, even refusing to let the body of MacManus lie in the Pro-Cathedral in 1861.\n\nCullen's contribution also had limits. The Catholic University attracted few students and gave no state-recognised degrees. Protestants and some liberal Catholics saw an alien, authoritarian power, and bishops such as MacHale resented his authority. Some historians argue that the changes were already under way.\n\nOverall, Davis offered a cultural, inclusive nation built on shared history and literature, while Cullen built a Catholic Church that gave most Irish people a shared religious identity. Cullen's influence was the more immediate and widespread, but Davis's ideas lived on in later nationalism, and the two together show the competing religious and cultural identities that shaped Ireland after 1830.",
