    "model": "Land legislation and land reform were of enormous importance between 1870 and 1914, because they transformed Irish rural society by moving ownership of the land from landlords to tenants, ending landlordism as a political and social force. They also showed the power of agitation, built the mass base for Parnell's movement and shaped relations between Britain and Ireland. Yet they were less successful in solving rural poverty or in settling the Home Rule question.\n\nThe starting point was the grievance of tenants who lacked security. Most farmers rented their holdings, often without a lease, from a small landlord class that was largely Protestant and often absentee. Their demands became known as the Three Fs: fair rent, fixity of tenure and free sale. Gladstone's Land Act of 1870 gave legal force to the Ulster Custom and offered compensation for improvements and disturbance, but it did not fix rents, so landlords could still raise rents beyond what tenants could pay and then evict. The purchase clauses saw little land sold. The Act's failure explains the later agitation, and it was the first step in showing that Westminster would legislate for tenants.\n\nThe Land War of 1879 to 1882 made land reform a mass movement. Falling prices and poor harvests from 1877 brought arrears and rising evictions, and Michael Davitt organised the meeting at Irishtown in April 1879 and helped found the Irish National Land League in October with Parnell as president. Tactics included boycotting, named after Captain Boycott in 1880, and the Ladies' Land League under Anna Parnell kept the campaign alive when the men were jailed. The government answered with coercion under Forster, but also with concession. The Land Act of 1881 granted the Three Fs, with fair rents set by land courts for fifteen years, reducing rents by about one fifth on average. It created dual ownership, and the Arrears Act of 1882 later cleared arrears for tenants rated under £30. The Land League also made Parnell's party a national force.\n\nThe next stage was the Plan of Campaign from 1886, launched by William O'Brien and John Dillon, in which tenants on an estate offered a reduced rent and paid it into a fund if refused. It was applied on about 100 estates, including Clanricarde's in Galway. Balfour answered with coercion in 1887, and the shooting of three people at Mitchelstown in September 1887 made him Bloody Balfour. The Holy Office condemned the Plan in 1888, and the Parnell split weakened it. Its importance lay in keeping the land issue alive and in provoking the Conservatives to adopt constructive unionism.\n\nConstructive unionism, or killing Home Rule with kindness, sought to defuse the issue by funding purchase. The Ashbourne Act of 1885 advanced the full price to tenants buying from willing landlords, Balfour's Act of 1891 extended the funds, and the Congested Districts Board of 1891 targeted poor western districts by dividing estates and supporting fisheries and crafts. Critics argued it made landlords richer while poor western farms remained a problem, and it did not stop the growth of nationalism.\n\nThe decisive change came with the Land Conference of December 1902, where Lord Dunraven, William O'Brien and John Redmond agreed a scheme. Wyndham's Land Act of 1903 gave tenants loans of the full price over about 68 years at low interest, with landlords given a bonus of 12 per cent to sell. More than 200,000 tenants bought within a few years. The Evicted Tenants Act of 1907 and the Labourers Act of 1906, which provided cottages for farm labourers, followed, and Birrell's Act of 1909 allowed compulsory purchase. By 1914 most tenants had bought or were buying their holdings.\n\nIn my judgement the land legislation was of great importance, because it ended landlordism and created a society of owner-occupying farmers, and it proved that agitation could move Westminster. But the limits were clear: the settlement was reached by compromise and state funds rather than the land nationalisation that Davitt wanted, and labourers and small western farmers gained least.",
