    "label": "2",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The Home Rule movement achieved a good deal between 1870 and 1891, but it also suffered failures that left its central aim unfulfilled. Its successes lay in building a disciplined constitutional party and in forcing Home Rule onto the agenda of British politics, while its failures were the defeat of the First Home Rule Bill and the destruction of the party in the Parnell split.\n\nThe first success was the foundation of a respectable, national movement by Isaac Butt. A Protestant barrister, he founded the Home Government Association in 1870 and the Home Rule League in 1873, which committed Home Rule MPs to act as an independent party. In the 1874 election about sixty Home Rulers were returned. This showed that a federal demand could win Catholic votes, and the secret ballot of 1872 helped tenants to vote freely. Butt's weakness, however, was a real failure. The party had no pledge, no whip and little money, his motions were rejected by huge majorities, and many MPs were Liberals in all but name, so he won no concession before his death in 1879.\n\nA second and much greater success came under Charles Stewart Parnell. Parnell and Joseph Biggar used obstruction at Westminster to make the Commons notice Irish MPs, and in 1881 a sitting of about 41 hours against the coercion bill forced the Speaker to intervene and led to the closure rule. The New Departure of 1878-79 joined Davitt's Fenian-minded land movement, Devoy's Clan na Gael and constitutional nationalists, and tied Home Rule to the land question. The Land League of 1879 gave the movement a mass base among tenant farmers, and American funds raised on Parnell's 1880 tour supported it. The Land Act of 1881 granted the three Fs, and the Arrears Act of 1882 followed the Kilmainham settlement, so agitation did bring real gains for tenants.\n\nParnell also succeeded in turning the Irish Parliamentary Party into a disciplined machine. He was elected chairman in 1880, the Irish National League replaced the suppressed Land League in 1882, and United Ireland gave the party a press. From 1884 candidates signed a pledge to sit, act and vote with the party, chosen at conventions where the clergy were prominent. The result was striking: in the 1885 election the party won 86 seats and held the balance of power in the Commons. No earlier Irish movement had made itself so indispensable to English politics.\n\nThat power produced the most dramatic success, and then a failure. In December 1885 Gladstone announced his conversion to Home Rule, and he introduced the First Home Rule Bill in April 1886. It was the first time a British government had proposed an Irish parliament, and the Liberal alliance was a real achievement for Parnell. Yet the Bill was defeated in the Commons in June 1886 by 343 votes to 313, when about ninety Liberals led by Joseph Chamberlain voted against it. Gladstone fell from power, the Conservatives governed for most of the next twenty years, and unionist resistance in Ulster began to harden. Home Rule had come close but not close enough.\n\nParnell's later career shows both sides again. In 1887 The Times printed letters linking him to the Phoenix Park murders, but they were exposed as forgeries by Richard Pigott, who collapsed under cross-examination in 1889 and shot himself. Parnell won £5,000 damages and stood at the height of his reputation. Then in November 1890 he was named as co-respondent in the O'Shea divorce case. Gladstone said he could not lead the Liberals if Parnell stayed, the Catholic bishops condemned him, and after the debate in Committee Room 15 about 45 MPs walked out in December 1890, leaving only about 28 with Parnell. This was the greatest failure of the whole period. The party was split into Parnellites and anti-Parnellites, the Liberal alliance was wrecked, and Parnell lost by-elections in Kilkenny, Sligo and Carlow before he died in October 1891.\n\nOverall, the movement's successes were considerable: a constitutional party, a mass organisation, land reform and Home Rule as a live issue for the British parties. Its failures were equally clear, since by 1891 no Home Rule Bill had passed, the leader was dead and the party was shattered. The successes were organisational and political, while the failures were in the ultimate aim, and Home Rule remained unachieved when Parnell died.",
