    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "John Redmond was the more effective political leader before 1914, because he actually delivered Home Rule onto the statute book, whereas Edward Carson, though brilliantly effective at obstruction, could only delay it and force the issue of Ulster. Carson was highly successful in his own cause, but judged by what each achieved for his goals, Redmond has the stronger case.\n\nRedmond's first achievement was rebuilding a shattered party. He had stayed loyal to Parnell and led the small Parnellite group after 1891, but when the factions reunited in January 1900 he was elected chairman. With William O'Brien's United Irish League as a grassroots machine, he restored discipline to a party that had spent the 1890s in feud. He also won concrete reform. Redmond and O'Brien took part in the Land Conference of 1902-03, which led to the Wyndham Land Act, helping tenants to buy their farms. This showed a leader who could negotiate and produce results for the people he represented.\n\nHis greatest success came after the two elections of 1910, which left the Liberals dependent on the Irish Party, which had 82 and then 84 seats. Redmond used that leverage. His party backed the Liberals after the Lords rejected the People's Budget, and the Parliament Act of 1911 removed the Lords' veto, leaving only a two-year delay. Asquith introduced the Third Home Rule Bill in April 1912, and it passed the Commons three times. It became law on 18 September 1914. Where Parnell had failed and Gladstone had been defeated in 1886 and 1893, Redmond succeeded, and that was real political effectiveness.\n\nRedmond's weakness was Ulster and the Act's suspension. He accepted the Suspensory Act for the duration of the war, and in March 1914 he conceded that Ulster counties could opt out for six years, which he had previously refused, though he rejected permanent partition at the Buckingham Palace Conference in July 1914. His reliance on Liberal goodwill left the Act unsettled, and his Woodenbridge speech in September 1914 split the Volunteers, with about 170,000 following him and about 11,000 staying with MacNeill. Even so, these costs belong largely to the war years, and by 1914 most nationalists supported him.\n\nCarson was a remarkable leader of unionism. A Dublin-born barrister, he became leader of the Irish Unionist parliamentary party in 1910, and with James Craig as organiser he built a formidable campaign. Andrew Bonar Law pledged Conservative support at Blenheim in July 1912. On Ulster Day, 28 September 1912, about 237,000 men signed the Solemn League and Covenant, with Carson the first to sign at Belfast City Hall. The Ulster Volunteer Force, formed in January 1913, had perhaps 90,000 to 100,000 men, and in September 1913 the Ulster Unionist Council announced a Provisional Government with Carson as chairman. The Curragh incident of March 1914 and the Larne gun-running of April 1914, when about 25,000 rifles were landed, showed that the government could not safely coerce Ulster.\n\nCarson therefore achieved a great deal. He rejected Asquith's six-year exclusion proposal as a stay of execution, and by 1914 the government had accepted the principle that Ulster would be treated separately. His threats made exclusion the central issue of the crisis, and the leverage he created shaped the later partition of Ireland. But he did not stop Home Rule becoming law, since the Act received the royal assent in September 1914. His achievement was bargaining power, and he did not defeat the Bill.\n\nOn balance, Redmond was the more effective leader. Carson was a brilliant agitator who made Ulster's case impossible to ignore, but he failed in the aim of preventing Home Rule becoming law. Redmond won land reform, used the Liberals' dependence on his votes to remove the Lords' veto, and saw the Third Home Rule Act passed, even if war and Ulster later undermined it. Success in the main aim of his party gives Redmond the stronger claim.",
