    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Between 1870 and 1914 the GAA and Douglas Hyde were both of lasting importance to Irish cultural life. The GAA gave Ireland a national sporting organisation open to all classes, and Hyde provided the intellectual programme and the organisation of the Irish language revival through the Gaelic League. Together they helped to shape a sense of distinct Irish identity.\n\nThe GAA was founded at Hayes' Hotel in Thurles on 1 November 1884 by Michael Cusack and Maurice Davin. At that time organised sport followed English rules and was dominated by the gentry and middle classes, and hurling had declined since the Famine. Cusack's article in United Ireland in October 1884 called for a body to revive native sports. The old athletic rules excluded artisans and labourers, so the new Association opened competition to all classes. Davin, a champion weight-thrower from Carrick-on-Suir, was elected the first president, and he drew up the first rules for hurling and Gaelic football in 1885, replacing old local variations with a national code.\n\nThe GAA gained influence quickly because it won powerful patrons. Archbishop Croke of Cashel accepted the patronage in December 1884 and urged the revival of Irish sports as an act of national self-respect, while Parnell and Michael Davitt also agreed to be patrons. Priests became club presidents and organisers, and the parish-based structure of clubs, county committees and a Central Council suited rural Ireland. The first All-Ireland championships began in 1887, and the first hurling final was played at Birr in 1888, when Thurles beat Meelick. Matches drew large crowds and gave local communities a focus. The ban on foreign games and on members of the RIC and the British forces also marked the GAA as a de-anglicising body, though the ban was divisive.\n\nThe GAA's importance was also political. Members of the IRB joined in large numbers and tried to control the Central Council, and Dublin Castle watched it as a front for Fenianism. In the late 1880s this led to quarrels, Cusack was removed as secretary in 1886, and Davin stepped down in 1889. A costly American tour in 1888 and the Parnell split of 1890 left the Association with few clubs by 1891. It survived because of its parish clubs, the appeal of the games themselves and clerical support, and it recovered in the 1890s. Its lasting importance was in creating a mass organisation that reached into nearly every parish, and it later produced many nationalist activists.\n\nDouglas Hyde was important in a different way. Born in Castlerea, the son of a Church of Ireland rector, he learned Irish from local people. On 25 November 1892 he delivered The Necessity for De-Anglicising Ireland to the National Literary Society, arguing that Ireland had been anglicised in language, names, dress, games and ideas, and that the Irish were failing to be either English or Irish. He pointed to the sharp decline of Irish since the Famine and insisted that the aim was to preserve what was distinctive, which did not require a separatist political view. His lecture gave the revival its programme.\n\nWithin a year Hyde helped to found the Gaelic League on 31 July 1893, with Eoin MacNeill and others, and he became its president. It was officially non-political and non-sectarian, welcoming Catholics, Protestants, nationalists and unionists. It ran Irish classes, sent travelling teachers to rural areas, held feiseanna and from 1897 the Oireachtas, and had several hundred branches by 1904. Its newspaper An Claidheamh Soluis began in 1899. Its successes were tangible: Irish was allowed in primary schools by the Revised Programme of 1900, and the League won the campaign to make Irish compulsory for matriculation to the National University, decided in 1910. Hyde's scholarship, including Love Songs of Connacht in 1893, gave the movement prestige.\n\nHyde's importance has limits. Younger IRB members joined the League after about 1910 and pushed it towards politics, and Hyde resigned as president in 1915 when it declared for independence. D.P. Moran thought him too tolerant of Anglo-Irish culture. Even so, the League trained many who later led the separatist movement, and Hyde became the first President of Ireland in 1938.\n\nBoth were therefore important. The GAA created a nationwide organisation open to all classes, and Hyde and the Gaelic League gave the language revival its programme and its mass following. Between them they gave Irish people a cultural pride that fed into the political changes after 1914.",
