    "label": "1",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "I agree that Ireland experienced a decade of revolutionary change between 1912 and 1922. In ten years the country moved from a Home Rule movement within the United Kingdom to a war of independence, the creation of a new Irish state and the partition of the island. The change was political, military and territorial, though it was less complete in social and economic terms.\n\nThe decade opened with the Third Home Rule Bill, introduced by Asquith in April 1912, which would have set up a parliament in Dublin while leaving the Crown, defence and foreign policy with Westminster. It provoked the first great upheaval. Edward Carson and James Craig organised unionist resistance, and on 28 September 1912 about 237,000 men signed the Ulster Covenant. The Ulster Volunteer Force was formed in January 1913, and it landed about 25,000 rifles at Larne in April 1914 without the government stopping it. Nationalists responded by forming the Irish Volunteers, and German rifles were landed at Howth in July 1914. Within two years Ireland had two armed private armies, and the Curragh incident of March 1914 showed that the British army could not be relied on to coerce Ulster. Home Rule became law in September 1914 but was suspended for the duration of the war, which ended the hope of a peaceful settlement.\n\nThe most dramatic change was the Easter Rising of 1916. The IRB's Military Council, which included Pearse, Connolly, Clarke and MacDonagh, planned a rebellion, but the loss of the Aud's arms, the arrest of Casement and MacNeill's countermand limited it. Even so, rebels seized the GPO on 24 April, and Pearse read the Proclamation of the Irish Republic. The Rising failed militarily and the Irish Parliamentary Party condemned it, but about 485 people died and much of O'Connell Street was destroyed. The British response transformed opinion. Fifteen leaders were executed between 3 and 12 May, including Connolly, who was shot seated in a chair, and about 1,800 were interned in Britain. The result was a swing in nationalist sympathy away from Redmond's party towards separatism.\n\nThis shift was confirmed by the rise of the second Sinn Féin from 1917. Count Plunkett won North Roscommon in February 1917, McGuinness won South Longford and de Valera won East Clare in July, and at the October Ard Fheis de Valera was elected president of Sinn Féin, now committed to an Irish republic. The conscription crisis of April 1918 united nationalist Ireland, with bishops, Labour and the IPP joining in the pledge against conscription and a general strike, and the arrest of Sinn Féin leaders in the German Plot strengthened the party further. In the general election of December 1918, held after the franchise had been extended to all men over 21 and women over 30, Sinn Féin won 73 of 105 seats and the Irish Parliamentary Party only six. Countess Markievicz became the first woman elected to Westminster. This was a revolution at the ballot box.\n\nThe First Dáil met on 21 January 1919 and adopted a Declaration of Independence, and the same day shots at Soloheadbeg began the War of Independence. Over the next two years the Dáil built a counter-state with its own ministries, courts and loan, while the IRA, now sworn to the Dáil, fought a guerrilla war using flying columns and Michael Collins's intelligence network. Bloody Sunday in November 1920, Kilmichael, the burning of Cork and the activities of the Black and Tans and Auxiliaries made British rule unsustainable. About 2,000 people died, and the Truce of 11 July 1921 was followed by the Treaty negotiations in London. The Treaty, signed in December 1921 and ratified by the Dáil in January 1922, created the Irish Free State as a dominion, a great change from direct rule from Westminster, though it also split the movement and led to civil war in June 1922.\n\nThe revolution also changed the map. The Government of Ireland Act of 1920 created a Northern parliament for six counties and a southern one that never functioned. Craig became Prime Minister in June 1921, and Northern Ireland opted out of the Free State on 7 December 1922. Violence in Belfast and elsewhere killed about 500 people between 1920 and 1922, mostly Catholics, and the Special Powers Act of 1922 and the new Royal Ulster Constabulary strengthened unionist control. Partition was therefore a lasting result of the decade.\n\nOverall, I agree that the decade was revolutionary. Ireland's politics, its governing parties, its armed forces and even its territory were transformed within ten years, and the old Irish Parliamentary Party was swept away. The revolution was less thorough socially, since the new state kept much of the old civil service and administration, but in political terms 1912 to 1922 was a decade of profound and lasting change.",
