    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The Eucharistic Congress of 22-26 June 1932 was the 31st international gathering of Catholics, held in Dublin to mark the 1,500th anniversary of the traditional date of St Patrick's arrival in Ireland. Its main events were the arrival of the Papal Legate, five days of Masses and processions, a children's Mass and a closing Mass in the Phoenix Park. It contributed strongly to Irish cultural identity by presenting Ireland as a Catholic nation and by uniting church, state and people, though it also deepened the divide with Northern unionists.\n\nThe Congress was planned while Cosgrave was in office, but it took place three months after de Valera became head of government in March 1932. Archbishop Edward Byrne of Dublin headed the organising effort, and the Pope sent Cardinal Lorenzo Lauri as legate. Dublin was decorated with altars, flags and illuminations, even in poorer districts, and a large altar was built in the Phoenix Park. A radio station was set up at Athlone so that the ceremonies and the Pope's message could reach the whole country. Visitors came from the United States, Britain and Europe, and thousands of pilgrims came from Northern Ireland. Volunteers acted as stewards and a national committee raised money by public subscription.\n\nThe main events began on 21 June, when de Valera and Archbishop Byrne received the legate at Dún Laoghaire, and some 36,000 schoolchildren lined the route to the Pro-Cathedral. The Congress opened on 22 June and continued with Masses in city churches and a procession of the Blessed Sacrament. On 25 June around 100,000 children attended a Mass in the Phoenix Park. On Sunday 26 June the closing Mass was held at the altar in the Phoenix Park before a crowd often put at about a million, although that figure may be exaggerated. The tenor John McCormack sang Panis Angelicus, and a message from the Pope was broadcast live by radio from the Vatican. After the Mass, processions went to O'Connell Bridge, where Cardinal Lauri gave the closing benediction to a crowd of up to half a million.\n\nThe Congress contributed to cultural identity first by showing the Catholic character of the nation. Ten years after independence and a civil war, the vast majority of the population was Catholic, and the Church managed schools and hospitals. The Congress linked that faith to the national story through St Patrick, so that being Irish and being Catholic appeared to be the same thing. The scale of public participation, with about a quarter of the population said to have attended the closing Mass, made the idea of a Catholic Ireland seem natural and was in line with the Gaelic and Catholic ideal of the 1930s.\n\nIt also showed the close alliance of church and state. De Valera, known for his personal piety, took a leading part, and his government gave the planning full support. The dispute over the Governor-General, James McNeill, who was not invited to the reception for the legate at Dublin Castle, showed de Valera presenting his government as both Catholic and independent of the Crown. This partnership between the hierarchy and political leadership foreshadowed the 1937 Constitution, with its recognition of the special position of the Catholic Church, and later historians see the Congress as the high point of the Church's public influence.\n\nThe Congress also presented the new state to the world as stable, devout and independent. It passed off without major disorder only ten years after the Civil War, and de Valera's prestige grew at home and abroad. The Athlone station that began broadcasting for it became Radio Éireann in 1938. However, in the North the Congress was seen very differently. Catholic pilgrims travelling to and from Dublin were attacked, with trains stoned at places such as Portadown, Lisburn and Belfast, and the police made baton charges to protect them. Unionists saw it as proof that the Free State was a Catholic state, which reinforced their case for partition, while Northern nationalists felt a sense of belonging to an Irish Catholic nation.\n\nOverall, the Congress was a great public success whose effect was symbolic and cultural, since it changed no laws. It strengthened an Irish identity that was Gaelic and Catholic, united people across party lines and brought credit to de Valera, but it deepened the division between North and South, which later generations judged more critically.",
