    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "John Devoy and Andrew Bonar Law both influenced developments in Ireland from outside it, one for the republicans and the other for the Ulster Unionists. Devoy, an exile in New York, sustained the physical-force tradition from the 1860s to 1916 and beyond, while Bonar Law, a Canadian-born Ulster-Scot who led the Conservatives, gave the Unionist cause the backing of a major British party during the Home Rule crisis. Their influence was real, but it was also limited and, in the case of Devoy, undermined by his later quarrels.\n\nDevoy was born in Co. Kildare in 1842 and joined the Irish Republican Brotherhood in the 1860s, recruiting Fenians in the British Army. He was arrested in 1866 and sentenced to 15 years' penal servitude for treason-felony, and he was released in 1871 on condition that he went into exile. He sailed to New York, where he became a leading figure in Clan na Gael, the secret republican society for Irish Americans founded in 1867. From America he used Irish-American newspapers to argue for an independent Irish republic, and he carried the Fenian movement across the Atlantic and kept it alive when it might have faded in Ireland.\n\nHis organising ability produced striking results. In 1876 Devoy and Clan na Gael organised the rescue of six Fenian prisoners from Western Australia, carried out by the American whaler Catalpa, a feat that raised the prestige of the republican movement worldwide. In 1878 he proposed the New Departure with Michael Davitt, a plan for Fenians to cooperate with Charles Stewart Parnell's constitutional nationalists for land reform and self-government. This linked physical-force men with the constitutional movement and helped bring American money and support to the Land League agitation of 1879 to 1882. Devoy therefore influenced not only republicanism but the main nationalist movement of the period.\n\nHis most important influence came before 1916. He founded the Gaelic American newspaper in New York in 1903 and edited it for many years to keep Irish-American opinion behind the republican cause. In 1914 he and Judge Daniel Cohalan met the German ambassador in Washington, Count Bernstorff, to ask for German help for an Irish rising, and Clan na Gael sent money to the IRB before the Rising of 1916. The Irish Race Convention in New York in March 1916 launched the Friends of Irish Freedom under Cohalan and Devoy. After the executions of the leaders Irish-American opinion moved strongly behind the Republic, and the Friends of Irish Freedom raised money and lobbied Congress. After 1919, however, Devoy and Cohalan quarrelled bitterly with de Valera over control of the American movement and its funds, and the dispute in the press weakened Irish-American unity. Devoy supported the Treaty of 1921, visited the Free State in 1924 and died in 1928.\n\nBonar Law's influence was of a different kind. Born in Kingston, New Brunswick, in 1858, the son of a Presbyterian minister from Ulster, he moved to Glasgow in 1870, entered the iron trade and became an MP in 1900. He became leader of the Conservative Party in November 1911, just as the Home Rule crisis was approaching. At Blenheim Palace in July 1912 he told a rally he could imagine no length of resistance to which Ulster could go that he would not support. This was a remarkable commitment from the leader of a major party, and it encouraged Unionists to believe that resistance to Home Rule, symbolised by the Ulster Covenant signed by about half a million people on 28 September 1912, would be backed in Britain.\n\nHis stand shaped the outcome. Through the Home Rule crisis of 1912 to 1914 his support for the Ulster Unionists made the exclusion of Ulster a Conservative goal, and it strengthened the hand of Edward Carson and James Craig against the government. Historians such as Robert Blake stress his role in shaping Conservative policy on Ulster, and they debate how far his stand made partition more likely. Later he served in the wartime coalition and as Chancellor from 1916 to 1919, and he became Prime Minister in October 1922, resigning in May 1923 because of cancer. Contemporary opinion was divided, with Liberal and nationalist papers calling him a reckless partisan and unionists praising his loyalty.\n\nOverall, both men influenced Ireland decisively from abroad. Devoy's work through Clan na Gael, the Catalpa rescue, the New Departure and the preparations for 1916 gave republicanism money, contacts and a press, though his clash with de Valera showed the limits of running a movement from America. Bonar Law gave Ulster Unionism the political weight of the Conservative Party and made exclusion of Ulster a serious possibility, which helped lead towards partition. Between them they strengthened the two opposing forces whose conflict shaped Ireland in the early twentieth century.",
