    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Irish governments between 1949 and 1957 were only partly effective. They achieved real things, such as completing the move to a republic, setting up the Industrial Development Authority and fighting tuberculosis, but they failed to solve the central problems of slow growth, unemployment and mass emigration. The decade is remembered as one of missed opportunities, although the governments were constrained by Ireland's dependence on Britain.\n\nThe most effective single act was the declaration of a republic. The First Inter-Party Government under John A. Costello, a coalition of Fine Gael, Labour, Clann na Poblachta, Clann na Talmhan and others, repealed the External Relations Act, and the Republic of Ireland Act came into force on 18 April 1949, ending Ireland's last formal link with the Crown and Commonwealth membership. Britain replied with the Ireland Act 1949, which guaranteed Northern Ireland's position until its parliament consented to change, so the act did nothing for partition. It was a symbolic success rather than a solution to the problems that ordinary people faced.\n\nThe record on health was mixed. Noël Browne led a successful campaign against tuberculosis, a major killer. New sanatoria were built, funded partly by the Hospitals Sweepstake, and BCG vaccination was begun, so that deaths fell steeply in the 1950s. This was probably the most effective social achievement of the period. However, the Mother and Child Scheme, which offered free care to mothers and to children up to 16 without a means test, collapsed in 1951. The Irish Medical Association objected, and the Catholic hierarchy, advised by Archbishop McQuaid, said it was contrary to Catholic social teaching. Browne resigned on 11 April 1951, and Costello told the Dáil that the government was bound to accept the bishops' decision. This showed that the state could not act against church and medical opposition, and the replacement Health Act 1953 was means-tested, leaving many without free care.\n\nOn the economy governments did lay some foundations. The IDA was set up in 1949, though it had little money or power, and Ireland received about 146 million dollars of Marshall Aid, much of it in loans. A large share went on land reclamation and agriculture rather than industry, which critics see as a missed chance. Fianna Fáil's Underdeveloped Areas Act 1952 gave grants for new industry in the poorer western counties, and the Capital Investment Advisory Committee was an early step towards planning public investment. Yet no government produced a long-term plan, and industry remained behind protective tariffs and the Control of Manufactures Acts, which discouraged foreign investment.\n\nThe weakest response was to the recurring balance of payments crises. MacEntee's 1952 budget cut food subsidies and raised duties, which pushed up prices and was unpopular, and Fianna Fáil lost seats in 1954. The second Inter-Party Government of 1954 to 1957, with Gerard Sweetman as Minister for Finance, faced another crisis in 1955 and 1956 and answered with higher taxes, credit restrictions and spending cuts. These reduced demand and pushed up unemployment, so deflation made the underlying problem worse. The Irish pound was tied to sterling, and the economy depended on cattle exports to Britain, with agriculture still employing about 40 per cent of the workforce.\n\nThe human cost shows the limits of government effectiveness. Net emigration was about 400,000 in the 1950s, mostly young people going to Britain, and the population fell from 2,960,593 in 1951 to 2,818,341 in 1961, while western Europe boomed. Voters punished each government in turn, with the swings between coalition and Fianna Fáil governments in 1951, 1954 and 1957 reflecting this discontent, and in 1957 Fianna Fáil won an overall majority of 78 seats after the second coalition fell when Clann na Poblachta withdrew support during the IRA Border Campaign.\n\nOverall, governments were not very effective at tackling the main problems. Historians such as J.J. Lee and Tom Garvin criticise a lack of ambition, while others stress the constraints of a small economy and the lack of planning tools, and the groundwork of the IDA and public investment that Fianna Fáil later built on. On balance, the governments solved some specific problems but not stagnation and emigration, and the real turn came only after 1957 with T.K. Whitaker's work in Finance.",
