    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Membership of the EEC from 1973 had a considerable positive impact on the Irish economy, transforming farming, bringing foreign investment and large transfers from Europe, and reducing dependence on Britain. However, the gains were uneven, protected industries suffered, and membership did not by itself prevent the high unemployment of the late 1970s and 1980s.\n\nThe first and clearest impact was on agriculture. The Common Agricultural Policy guaranteed minimum prices for beef, milk and cereals and bought surplus produce into intervention. As Irish prices rose towards EEC levels, farm incomes rose sharply in the early years of membership, especially for beef and dairy farmers. Land values rose and farmers invested in machinery, buildings and drainage with the help of grants. The benefits were uneven, since larger and more productive farms in the east and south gained most while small farms on poor land in the west gained less. Guaranteed prices also encouraged overproduction, and in 1984 the EEC introduced milk quotas limiting dairy output, so the boom did not last.\n\nSecond, membership strengthened industry through foreign investment. American and other firms set up factories in Ireland to sell into the EEC market, attracted by IDA grants, a young English-speaking workforce and low taxes, including a 10 per cent corporation tax rate on manufacturing profits from 1981. Electronics, pharmaceuticals, chemicals and engineering became leading sectors, with firms such as Digital in Galway and later Intel at Leixlip. Manufactured goods overtook farm produce in the value of exports, so the economy became more diversified and less reliant on farming.\n\nThird, Ireland received large financial support. The European Regional Development Fund, created in 1975 partly because Britain, Ireland and Italy pressed for help for poorer regions, gave Ireland a considerable share, and the European Social Fund paid for training and employment schemes, including those of AnCO. The money was spent on roads, water and sewerage, telecommunications and industrial estates. By the 1980s net payments from the EEC budget amounted to several per cent of national income, and the reform of the structural funds in 1988 doubled the money available for 1989 to 1993, with Ireland a major beneficiary. This transfer paid for infrastructure the Irish state could not have afforded itself.\n\nFourth, membership reduced economic dependence on Britain. Tariffs on trade with other members were phased out by 1977, and the share of exports going to Britain fell as trade with the continent grew. In March 1979 Ireland joined the European Monetary System, which ended the link between the Irish pound and sterling, a break that would have been unthinkable before membership. This gave Irish policy-makers new freedom and widened markets, although it also exposed the economy to wider European competition.\n\nThe costs must also be set against these gains. Protected Irish industries such as clothing, textiles and footwear lost their tariff protection, and many firms closed with heavy job losses. Unemployment rose in the late 1970s and 1980s, passing 15 per cent, and emigration returned, with about 200,000 net emigrants in the 1980s. The fishing industry became a symbol of what critics saw as lost benefits. Ireland obtained an exclusive six-mile zone for ten years, but the Common Fisheries Policy agreed in January 1983 set quotas that fishermen thought small for the value of fish in Irish waters, although grants did help ports such as Killybegs to develop.\n\nOverall, EEC membership had a largely beneficial impact on the Irish economy, especially for farmers, for exports and for infrastructure, and it ended the narrow dependence on the British market. But it was not a cure for every problem, because traditional industries collapsed, the benefits were uneven and the economy's weaknesses showed up in the 1980s, so the impact was positive on balance but far from uniform.",
