    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "From my study of culture in the Republic of Ireland I learned that both RTÉ and the reform of education changed the country profoundly between the early 1960s and the 1970s. RTÉ widened Irish horizons and opened public debate on subjects once taboo, although its independence from government proved fragile, while the reforms of the 1960s opened second-level and third-level education to children who had never had the chance before.\n\nTelevision arrived late and under state control. The Broadcasting Authority Act 1960 set up the RTÉ Authority, appointed by the government, and Telefís Éireann began broadcasting on 31 December 1961, with President de Valera warning at the opening that television had a great power for good and evil. Within a few years transmitters carried the service to most of the country and by the early 1970s most households had a set. Programmes such as Gaelic games coverage, drama and light entertainment drew huge audiences, and the effect was to end the isolation of rural communities and to bring national and international news and culture into every home.\n\nThe programmes that mattered most were those that challenged accepted attitudes. The Late Late Show, first broadcast in July 1962 and presented by Gay Byrne until 1999, became a forum for national debate on sex, marriage, contraception, unmarried mothers and the church. In 1966 the episode known as the Bishop and the Nightie, when a wife said she had not worn a nightdress on her honeymoon night, caused uproar and drew a condemnation from Bishop Thomas Ryan of Clonfert, yet many viewers defended the show. The Riordans, which began in 1965, dealt with the real issues of farming life, and Telefís Scoile, which began on 4 February 1964, was one of the earliest schools television services in Europe. RTÉ therefore entertained, informed and educated, and it speeded up a questioning of church authority that was already under way.\n\nRTÉ also showed how limited its freedom was. Seven Days, launched in 1966, investigated housing, poverty and emigration, and a programme on illegal moneylending in 1969 led to a government inquiry and a row about independence. Lemass had described RTÉ as an instrument of public policy, while its journalists believed a broadcaster could serve the public only if free to criticise. The Troubles brought the clash to a head: on 1 October 1971 the Minister for Posts and Telegraphs, Gerry Collins, issued the first order under Section 31, and after the radio programme This Week broadcast an interview with Seán Mac Stíofáin of the Provisional IRA on 19 November 1972, he dismissed the entire RTÉ Authority. That showed that the government held the real power over the broadcaster.\n\nThe second area of change was education. Until the 1960s most children left school at 14, secondary schools charged fees and were owned mainly by religious orders, and the state had no policy for expansion. Patrick Hillery, Minister for Education from 1959, commissioned a survey with the OECD, and Investment in Education, published in 1965, revealed wide differences in participation by social class and region and a shortage of skills. It argued that education was an investment in economic growth, and in 1963 Hillery had already announced comprehensive schools, the first of which opened in 1966.\n\nThe decisive step came when Donogh O'Malley announced on 10 September 1966 that free second-level education would begin the following year. The scheme started in September 1967, fees were abolished in schools that joined it and free transport allowed rural pupils to reach a school. Enrolment rose steeply. Further reforms followed: the Primary Certificate was abolished in 1967, the Higher Education Authority and a higher education grants scheme began in 1968, Regional Technical Colleges opened from 1970, community schools appeared in the early 1970s, a child-centred primary curriculum was introduced in 1971, the school-leaving age rose to 15 in 1972 and the National Institute for Higher Education opened in Limerick in the same year. More women and more students from rural and working-class homes reached university than ever before.\n\nThe reforms had limits. The churches kept control of management and the bishops were wary of comprehensive and community schools, so compromises gave them seats on boards. Many schools were overcrowded and short of teachers, free fees did not remove the cost of books, uniforms and travel, and the west still had fewer places than the east. Historians stress that the reforms were driven as much by the economic need for skills as by social ideals and that the middle classes benefited first.\n\nOverall, both RTÉ and education altered Irish society in the 1960s and early 1970s. Television changed what people knew and discussed, while education changed what they could hope to achieve. Neither change was complete, because the state controlled the broadcaster and the churches kept control of the schools, but together they ended the isolation and deference of the 1950s.",
