    "model": "Between 1815 and 1871 the Germanic lands moved from a loose Confederation of sovereign states under Austrian presidency to a Prussian-dominated Empire. The change came about through Prussia's growing economic strength, the failure of liberal nationalism in 1848, and above all the skilful diplomacy of Bismarck, who used three wars to exclude Austria and bring the southern states into a united Germany proclaimed in 1871.\n\nThe Congress of Vienna replaced the Holy Roman Empire with a German Confederation of 39 states in 1815. Its only institution was a Diet at Frankfurt of government delegates, presided over by Austria, and it existed to guard against France and revolution rather than to achieve unity. Metternich used it to suppress national feeling: after the murder of Kotzebue in 1819 the Carlsbad Decrees banned the Burschenschaften, placed universities under supervision and imposed press censorship. Yet the student movement, which had held the Wartburg Festival of 1817, showed that nationalist feeling survived. The first real step towards unity was economic. Prussia had already abolished internal tariffs in its own lands in 1818. The Zollverein took effect on 1 January 1834, and Austria stayed outside because its protected industries could not fit a low-tariff union. The union gave Prussia an economic lead and accustomed the smaller states to looking to Berlin rather than Vienna.\n\nThe revolution of 1848 raised the hope of unity from below. After the fall of Metternich in March, the Frankfurt National Assembly met in the Paulskirche in May, a parliament largely of lawyers, civil servants and professors. It drew up a constitution for a federal empire, but it had no army and depended on the princes. It divided over the Grossdeutsch and Kleindeutsch solutions, and it offered the crown to Frederick William IV of Prussia, who refused it in April 1849, scorning a crown offered by an assembly. Prussian troops crushed the remaining risings, and at Olmütz in November 1850 Prussia abandoned its Erfurt Union plan under Austrian and Russian pressure. Unity would not be achieved by liberals and speeches.\n\nIn the 1850s the balance shifted. Prussia's coal, iron and railways, the steel works of Krupp at Essen and its leadership of the Zollverein contrasted with Austria's weak finances and its defeat in Italy in 1859. In 1862 King William I, locked in conflict with the liberal Landtag over army reform, appointed Bismarck, who declared that the great questions would be decided by blood and iron and governed without an approved budget. He used the Danish War of 1864, fought jointly with Austria, to win Schleswig and Holstein, and then the quarrel over their administration to provoke Austria. Having secured an alliance with Italy and the goodwill of Russia and the neutrality of France, he won the Austro-Prussian War at Königgrätz on 3 July 1866 with the help of the needle-gun, Moltke's planning and the railways.\n\nThe Treaty of Prague dissolved the Confederation and excluded Austria from German affairs, Prussia annexed Hanover, Hesse-Kassel, Nassau, Frankfurt and Schleswig-Holstein, and the North German Confederation was formed in 1867 with the Prussian king as president, Bismarck as Chancellor and a Reichstag elected by manhood suffrage. Bismarck treated Austria leniently so that it could become a future friend, and many liberals now accepted his leadership in return for national unity. The last step was war with France. The Hohenzollern candidature for the Spanish throne alarmed France, and Bismarck edited the Ems telegram of 13 July 1870 so that William's refusal appeared an insult. France declared war, the southern states joined Prussia, and Napoleon III was captured at Sedan on 2 September. The German Empire was proclaimed at Versailles on 18 January 1871 with William I as emperor, and the Treaty of Frankfurt took Alsace and part of Lorraine from France.\n\nUnification was therefore achieved from above by Prussian arms and diplomacy, not by parliaments. The Empire excluded Austria, included Polish, Danish and French-speaking minorities, and left the Chancellor answerable to the emperor rather than the Reichstag.",
