    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Attempts to unite Italy after 1852 succeeded where earlier ones had failed because the movement acquired what it had lacked before: a strong state to lead it, skilled diplomacy, foreign support and a military force able to win. Before 1852 risings were isolated, divided and easily crushed by Austria; after it, Piedmont under Cavour combined diplomacy with volunteers and exploited favourable international circumstances.\n\nThe early attempts failed because they rested on weak and divided foundations. After 1815 Italy was split into states, with Austria ruling Lombardy and Venetia directly, Habsburg princes in Tuscany, Modena and Parma, the Pope in central Italy and the Bourbons in Naples. The Carbonari, secret societies of officers and professionals, led revolts in Naples in 1820 and Piedmont in 1821, and risings in Modena, Parma and the Papal Legations in 1831, all of which Austrian troops crushed. Mazzini's Young Italy, founded in 1831, aimed at a unified republic, but his invasion of Savoy in 1834 was easily defeated. The conspirators had little popular support and no army, and there was a gulf between republicans and those who looked to kings.\n\nThe revolutions of 1848-49 showed how much needed to change. Risings began at Palermo in January 1848 and the Five Days of Milan drove out the Austrians, while Pius IX granted a constitution. Charles Albert of Piedmont declared war on Austria but was beaten at Custoza in July 1848 and at Novara in March 1849. Pius IX withdrew from the war and later fled Rome, and the Roman Republic of Mazzini and Garibaldi fell to French troops sent by Louis-Napoleon in July 1849. Republican idealism alone, and a lone Piedmontese army, could not defeat Austria.\n\nAfter 1849 Piedmont became the natural leader. It alone kept its constitution, the Statuto Albertino, and Victor Emmanuel II accepted a liberal state. When Cavour became Prime Minister in November 1852 he promoted free trade, railways and economic modernisation, and he strengthened the army. The Italian National Society, founded in 1857 by Manin, La Farina and Garibaldi, backed Piedmontese leadership in place of Mazzini's republicanism, which healed one of the earlier divisions between republicans and monarchists.\n\nCavour's diplomacy was the second key difference. By sending about 15,000 troops to the Crimean War in 1855 he won Piedmont a seat at the Congress of Paris in 1856, where he raised the Italian question before the Great Powers. At Plombieres in July 1858 he secured Napoleon III's promise of French support against Austria in return for Nice and Savoy. Austria, goaded by Piedmont, declared war in April 1859, and the French and Piedmontese won at Magenta and Solferino. Earlier risings had faced Austria alone; now it faced a Great Power on the Italian side.\n\nThe settlement was not perfect, since Napoleon III made the armistice of Villafranca in July 1859 without consulting Cavour, leaving Venetia with Austria, but Lombardy went to Piedmont. In March 1860 Tuscany, Modena, Parma and the Romagna voted by plebiscite to join Piedmont. Then Garibaldi supplied the dynamic military force. His Thousand sailed from Quarto on 5 May 1860, landed at Marsala, won at Calatafimi and took Palermo, and he entered Naples on 7 September as the Bourbon king withdrew. Piedmontese troops moved into the Papal Marches and Umbria, and Garibaldi handed over his conquests to Victor Emmanuel II at Teano. The Kingdom of Italy was proclaimed on 17 March 1861.\n\nThe last obstacles fell through changing international circumstances. Italy allied with Prussia in 1866, and although it lost at Custoza and Lissa, Prussia's victory gave it Venetia. When France withdrew its garrison from Rome in August 1870 because of the Franco-Prussian War, Italian troops entered the city on 20 September, and Rome became the capital in 1871. Garibaldi's earlier attempts on Rome had been stopped at Aspromonte in 1862 and at Mentana in 1867.\n\nOverall, success came because Piedmont provided leadership, a constitution and a modern economy; Cavour provided diplomacy that isolated Austria and won French help; Garibaldi provided military daring; and the Great Powers were distracted at crucial moments. Unity fell short of Mazzini's popular republic, as the new state was a narrow Piedmontese monarchy, but it achieved what the earlier, isolated and divided attempts never could.",
