    "model": "German foreign policy developed from the cautious, defensive diplomacy of Bismarck, who sought security for the new Reich by isolating France and keeping the other powers friendly, to the ambitious Weltpolitik of Wilhelm II, which broke up Bismarck's system and left Germany surrounded by rivals by 1914.\n\nBismarck began from the position that Germany, created in 1871, was a 'satiated' power wanting no more territory in Europe. The Treaty of Frankfurt, which took Alsace and part of Lorraine and imposed an indemnity of 5 billion francs, left France embittered, so he made it his aim to keep France without allies and to prevent any hostile coalition. The War-in-Sight crisis of 1875, when Britain and Russia warned Germany against attacking France again, showed him how real that danger was. He encouraged French expansion in Tunisia in 1881 to distract France from Alsace-Lorraine.\n\nHis chief tool was a system of alliances. The Three Emperors' League of 1873 linked Germany, Austria-Hungary and Russia, but it was strained by the Balkans. At the Congress of Berlin in 1878, which Bismarck chaired as an 'honest broker', Bulgaria was cut down and Austria-Hungary was allowed to occupy Bosnia-Herzegovina. Russians felt cheated, and Bismarck responded with the Dual Alliance with Austria-Hungary in October 1879, a defensive treaty which lasted until 1918. In 1882 Italy, angered by France's occupation of Tunisia, joined to form the Triple Alliance. When the Three Emperors' League collapsed in 1887 over Bulgaria, he replaced it with the secret Reinsurance Treaty with Russia, promising neutrality unless Germany attacked France or Russia attacked Austria-Hungary. This complicated structure kept France isolated for twenty years, but it depended on Bismarck's personal skill. He also encouraged the Mediterranean Agreements of 1887, which tied Britain loosely to the Triple Alliance powers.\n\nWilhelm II, who became Kaiser in 1888 and forced Bismarck to resign in March 1890, wanted to rule personally and make Germany a world power. His government did not renew the Reinsurance Treaty in 1890, and Russia turned to France, helped by French loans from 1888, after Bismarck's ban on Russian bonds in 1887 had already pushed Russia towards French banks. The Franco-Russian alliance, agreed in 1892 and ratified in 1894, was the very coalition Bismarck had feared, and it ended France's isolation and threatened Germany with war on two fronts. This was the first and most damaging step in the change of policy.\n\nFrom 1897 Weltpolitik, led by Wilhelm, Bülow and Tirpitz, aimed to turn Germany into a world power through colonies, trade and a fleet. Bülow claimed 'a place in the sun' in 1897, Germany leased Kiaochow in China, and it bought Pacific islands from Spain in 1899. The Kruger telegram of 1896 outraged British opinion, and Wilhelm's visit to Constantinople in 1898 and the Baghdad Railway concession rivalled Britain and Russia in the Middle East. Attempts at an Anglo-German alliance failed in 1898-1901, and German pressure over Morocco in 1905 and 1911 gained little and pushed Britain and France closer together. The naval programme did the greatest damage. Tirpitz's Navy Laws of 1898 and 1900, which planned 38 battleships, rested on a 'risk theory' that a strong fleet would deter Britain. Instead Britain launched HMS Dreadnought in 1906, which forced Germany to start again on the same footing, and by 1909 British opinion demanded eight new dreadnoughts. Britain settled its differences with France in 1904 and Russia in 1907, creating the Triple Entente, so Germany depended increasingly on Austria-Hungary and gave it the 'blank cheque' of July 1914.\n\nOverall, German foreign policy changed from a defensive attempt under Bismarck to preserve the status quo into an assertive search for world power under Wilhelm II. Bismarck's system was complex, but it worked, whereas Wilhelm's policy, though not alone responsible for the war, turned potential friends into opponents and left Germany encircled. The decisive change was one of method: Bismarck managed rivalries with restraint, while Wilhelm and his advisers pursued prestige and left no margin for error.",
