    "label": "2",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Between 1870 and 1914 European industry was transformed. The older industries of coal, iron and textiles expanded, new industries based on steel, chemicals, electricity and the petrol engine took off, and Germany overtook Britain. This growth drew millions into the cities, created a large and organised working class and sharpened social and political conflict, to which states and employers responded with both welfare and repression.\n\nGerman industry shows the pattern most clearly. After unification in 1871 Germany combined the coal and iron of the Ruhr, Saar and Silesia and the ore of Lorraine with a population that rose from about 41 million to about 67 million by 1914. A single market with the mark as its currency, the Reichsbank, a railway network that grew from about 21,000 km to over 60,000 km, and universal banks such as Deutsche Bank that lent to industry supplied capital and transport. Hard coal output rose from about 30 million to about 190 million tonnes, and by 1913 Germany produced more than twice as much steel as Britain. Cartels such as the Rhenish-Westphalian Coal Syndicate of 1893 organised this growth, and the tariff of 1879 protected it.\n\nThe second industrial revolution gave the period its character. The Thomas-Gilchrist process of 1878 allowed the phosphoric ores of Lorraine to be made into steel. German chemical firms such as BASF, Bayer and Hoechst led the world in dyes and pharmaceuticals, and Haber and Bosch's ammonia synthesis opened the way to artificial fertilisers. Siemens and AEG spread electric lighting, trams and power stations through the cities. Industry was increasingly linked to science, with technical colleges and company laboratories training engineers and chemists.\n\nThe motor car shows this process at work. Nikolaus Otto built the first practical four-stroke engine in 1876, and Gottlieb Daimler and Wilhelm Maybach developed a light, high-speed petrol engine in the 1880s. Karl Benz patented his Motorwagen in January 1886, and in August 1888 his wife Bertha drove one about 106 km from Mannheim to Pforzheim, proving its usefulness and winning publicity. France led early production, with Panhard et Levassor and Peugeot using Daimler engines, and the Mercedes of 1901 set the pattern for later cars. Before 1914 cars were still handmade luxuries for the rich, and Henry Ford's mass production was an American development copied in Europe only later. The car nonetheless created demand for steel, rubber and petrol, and brought road improvements, traffic laws and garages.\n\nThe social impact was equally great. Millions moved from the countryside to the cities and the Ruhr, and by 1910 about 60 per cent of Germans lived in towns, compared with about a third in 1871. Berlin grew from under a million to about two million, and working families lived in overcrowded tenements with poor sanitation. Factory work meant long hours and strict discipline, and strikes such as the Ruhr miners' strike of 1889, involving about 90,000 men, showed the strains. Employers such as Alfred Krupp at Essen built housing, schools and pension funds, but expected loyalty in return, a paternalism meant to keep workers away from socialism.\n\nIndustrial change also brought organisation and political conflict. The Free trade unions had about 2.5 million members by 1913, and the Social Democratic Party, driven underground by Bismarck's Anti-Socialist Law of 1878 to 1890, won about 4.25 million votes and 110 seats in 1912, becoming the largest party in the Reichstag. The state responded with reform, through Bismarck's insurance laws of 1883 to 1889 covering sickness, accidents and old age, and with repression, through the courts and police against strikes. Tensions also grew between the Junkers and the industrialists, who united in an alliance of iron and rye even though tariffs raised food prices for workers.\n\nOverall, industry between 1870 and 1914 grew dramatically, above all in Germany, and the new industries, including the first motor cars, pointed to the future. Its effects on society were just as deep, since it created an urban working class that was better off but organised, politically assertive and in conflict with the privileged elites, while the political system failed to keep pace with economic change.",
