    "model": "Between 1871 and 1914 anti-Semitism had a profound impact in both France and Russia, though in different ways. In France it split the republic during the Dreyfus Affair, which ended in a republican victory but left the prejudice intact, while in Russia it was encouraged by the state and produced legal discrimination, pogroms and the emigration of about two million Jews.\n\nIn France, anti-Semitism grew after the humiliation of defeat in 1871, as nationalists looked for scapegoats and blamed Jews and Freemasons for the country's weakness. Edouard Drumont's La France juive of 1886 was a bestseller that portrayed Jews as a threat to France, and in 1892 he founded the newspaper La Libre Parole to spread the message. The term anti-Semitism had been coined by Wilhelm Marr in Germany in 1879, and this racial form of prejudice found a ready audience among nationalists, the army and sections of the Catholic Church.\n\nThe Dreyfus Affair turned prejudice into a national crisis. In October 1894 Captain Alfred Dreyfus, a Jewish artillery officer from Alsace, was arrested for passing secrets to Germany, convicted by a secret court martial and sent to Devil's Island. In 1896 Colonel Picquart found that the real author was Major Esterhazy, but the army suppressed the evidence, Esterhazy was acquitted in January 1898, and a forgery by Colonel Henry was later exposed. On 13 January 1898 Emile Zola published J'accuse, accusing the army of a cover-up, and was convicted of libel and forced to flee to England. France divided into Dreyfusards, mainly republicans, socialists and many intellectuals, and anti-Dreyfusards, who included the army, much of the Church, nationalists and monarchists.\n\nThe consequences were political. A second court martial at Rennes in 1899 found Dreyfus guilty again with extenuating circumstances, President Loubet pardoned him, and the court of appeals cleared him in 1906, when he was reinstated in the army. The army and the Church lost prestige, and republicans pressed on with anticlerical measures that ended in the separation of church and state in 1905. The Affair also influenced Theodor Herzl, who published Der Judenstaat in 1896. Dreyfus himself was vindicated, but anti-Semitism remained strong in French nationalist circles.\n\nIn Russia the impact was far more violent and was shaped by the state. About five million Jews lived in the empire, the largest Jewish community in the world, and most were confined to the Pale of Settlement and often lived in poverty. After the assassination of Alexander II in March 1881, rumours blamed the Jews and pogroms swept southern Russia, beginning at Elizavetgrad, while police and troops were slow to intervene. The government then responded with discrimination, not protection. The May Laws of 1882 banned Jews from settling in rural areas of the Pale, quotas limited their places in schools and universities from 1887, and Jews were expelled from Moscow in 1891 and 1892. Officials such as Pobedonostsev supported discrimination and Russification, and the Orthodox Church and nationalist groups such as the Black Hundreds spread hostile propaganda.\n\nViolence returned in the early 1900s. The Kishinev pogrom of April 1903 killed 49 Jews and injured hundreds, and caused international outrage. More pogroms followed during the revolution of 1905, and the Protocols of the Elders of Zion, a forgery claiming a Jewish plot to dominate the world, was first published in Russia in 1903. Between 1881 and 1914 about two million Jews emigrated, most to the USA and others to Britain and Palestine, while many who stayed joined the Bund, founded in 1897, or Zionism, or other socialist movements.\n\nOverall, anti-Semitism in France was a political and ideological conflict that ended in justice for one man and a victory for the republic, though prejudice persisted. In Russia it was official policy, and its impact was legal exclusion, bloodshed and mass emigration. In both countries it shaped politics profoundly, and in Russia it caused far greater suffering.",
