    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Europe faced severe social, economic and political problems immediately after the First World War. Millions were dead or disabled, economies were in debt and dislocated, empires had collapsed, new and fragile states emerged with bitter minorities, and revolution and unrest threatened governments, while the peace treaties themselves created resentment.\n\nThe social cost was enormous. About nine to ten million soldiers had died, including around 1.4 million French, 2 million German and 750,000 British, and many survivors were disabled while hundreds of thousands of women were widowed. The influenza pandemic of 1918 and 1919 killed tens of millions worldwide, striking young adults already weakened by the war. Soldiers returned to unemployment and unrest, with housing shortages and rising prices adding to the anger, and demobilisation led to strikes in 1919. Women who had worked in munitions and offices were pushed out of jobs to make way for returning men, as in Britain's Restoration of Pre-War Practices Act of 1919.\n\nThe economic problems were just as serious. States had paid for the war through borrowing and printing money, and national debts multiplied several times. Britain, once the world's greatest creditor, became a debtor to the USA, which replaced it as the leading financial power. Prices rose faster than wages and savings lost value. Germany had suffered the hunger of the Turnip Winter of 1916 and 1917 and the Allied blockade until the peace was signed, and northern France and Belgium lay devastated. Keynes warned in The Economic Consequences of the Peace that the reparations, fixed in 1921 at 132 billion gold marks, were unrealistic and would damage European recovery.\n\nPolitically, the war swept away old empires and left unstable successors. Austria-Hungary broke up in October and November 1918, producing Czechoslovakia, Yugoslavia, Austria, Hungary and Poland. The Treaty of Trianon in 1920 left over three million Hungarians outside a Hungary cut to about a third of its territory. Self-determination was applied unevenly, since about three million Germans of the Sudetenland were placed in Czechoslovakia and union between Germany and Austria was forbidden. The new states were multi-ethnic, with minorities protected only by treaties, and Germany, Hungary and Italy all wanted revision of the settlement.\n\nGermany faced the sharpest crisis. After the mutiny at Kiel and the abdication of the Kaiser in November 1918, Ebert's government faced revolution from the left, and the Spartacist rising of January 1919 was crushed by the army and Freikorps, with Luxemburg and Liebknecht murdered. A Soviet republic lasted a few weeks in Bavaria in April 1919. The republic was weakened from the start by the stab in the back myth, which falsely blamed socialists and Jews for defeat, and by the Treaty of Versailles of June 1919, which Germans condemned as a Diktat. It included the war guilt clause, the loss of about 13 per cent of territory, an army limit of 100,000 and reparations. Germans also felt the Allies had broken the Fourteen Points on which they had asked for an armistice, which gave nationalists a lasting grievance to exploit.\n\nRussia added the problem of Bolshevism. The Civil War between Reds and Whites raged after 1918, and the Polish-Soviet war of 1920 showed how disputed borders in the east could lead to fighting. Fear of communist revolution made governments wary and the new democracies fragile. Collective security was also weak, since the League of Nations, which began in January 1920, lacked the USA after the Senate rejected the treaty, and Germany and Russia were at first excluded, so Britain and France had to enforce the settlement largely alone.\n\nOverall, post-war Europe faced problems on every front, and they fed each other. Human loss and economic ruin made people desperate, the peace left resentment and unstable states, and revolutionary and nationalist politics weakened the new democracies, so the settlement began with a heavy burden of instability.",
