    "label": "1",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The Cold War in Europe turned on a series of key moments which hardened or eased the division of the continent. The Berlin Blockade of 1948-49 confirmed the split of Germany, Hungary in 1956 showed the limits of Soviet tolerance, the Berlin Wall and Cuba in 1961-62 were the high points of danger, Helsinki in 1975 was the high point of détente, and Gorbachev's reforms and the revolutions of 1989 ended the conflict. Each of these changed the course of events in a lasting way.\n\nThe first turning point was the Berlin Blockade. After the Western powers introduced the Deutsche Mark in their zones on 20 June 1948, Stalin blockaded road, rail and canal access to West Berlin, hoping to force the West to abandon its plans for a West German state or to drive it out of the city. The Western powers organised an airlift, flying in food and fuel through Tempelhof, Gatow and Tegel, and over two million tons were delivered before the blockade was lifted on 12 May 1949. The result was the opposite of what Stalin wanted. NATO was founded on 4 April 1949, the Federal Republic was created on 23 May and the German Democratic Republic followed on 7 October. Germany and Europe were now divided in a way that lasted for forty years.\n\nHungary in 1956 was the next turning point. Khrushchev's secret speech of February 1956 raised hopes of change, and after demonstrations began on 23 October, Imre Nagy formed a government that promised reform and announced Hungary's withdrawal from the Warsaw Pact. On 4 November Soviet tanks entered Budapest and crushed the uprising, and Nagy was later executed. The Western powers, distracted by the Suez crisis and unwilling to risk war, did nothing. This showed that the Soviet Union would use force to keep its satellites in the bloc, and that the West would not roll back communism. The same lesson was repeated in Czechoslovakia in August 1968, when Warsaw Pact forces ended the Prague Spring and the Brezhnev Doctrine was proclaimed.\n\nThe Berlin Wall and the Cuban Missile Crisis were the most dangerous moments. Khrushchev issued his ultimatum on Berlin in November 1958, and by 1961 about 2.7 million people had left East Germany since 1945, so the East German regime built the Berlin Wall from 13 August 1961 to stop the flow. It stabilised East Germany but became the great symbol of communist failure. In October 1962 the discovery of Soviet missiles in Cuba brought the superpowers closer to nuclear war than ever before, until Khrushchev agreed to withdraw them in return for a US pledge not to invade Cuba. The shock produced the Moscow-Washington hotline and the Partial Test Ban Treaty of 1963, and it opened the way to détente.\n\nDétente was the next turning point. Willy Brandt's Ostpolitik produced the Treaty of Moscow of 1970, the Four Power Agreement on Berlin of 1971 and the Basic Treaty of 1972 between the two German states. SALT I of May 1972 limited anti-ballistic missiles and set interim limits on strategic weapons. The Helsinki Final Act of 1 August 1975, signed by 35 states, recognised existing borders in Europe and committed signatories to human rights, a standard that dissidents such as the signers of Charter 77 later used against their own governments. Yet détente did not last. The Soviet invasion of Afghanistan in December 1979, the SS-20 missiles and NATO's decision to deploy Pershing and cruise missiles began a second Cold War, which reached a dangerous moment during the Able Archer exercise of November 1983.\n\nThe final turning point came under Gorbachev, who became General Secretary in 1985. He met Reagan at Geneva and Reykjavik and signed the INF Treaty in December 1987, and by abandoning the Brezhnev Doctrine he let eastern European states choose their own path. Solidarity won the Polish elections of June 1989, Hungary opened its border, the Berlin Wall was opened on 9 November 1989, and communist governments fell across the region. Germany was reunified on 3 October 1990, the Warsaw Pact was dissolved in 1991, and the Soviet Union itself ended on 26 December 1991.\n\nOf these, the most decisive were the Blockade, which fixed the division of Europe, and 1989, which ended it. Hungary and Prague showed the limits of reform inside the bloc, while the Wall, Cuba and Helsinki each changed how both sides behaved. Together they show that the Cold War was shaped less by a single event than by a sequence of crises and thaws, ending only when the Soviet leader decided not to use force.",
