    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The life and work of Alexander Solzhenitsyn and Simone de Beauvoir gave rise to controversy because each challenged the dominant powers and beliefs of their time. Solzhenitsyn exposed the brutality of the Soviet system and clashed with a state that treated him as a traitor, while de Beauvoir attacked traditional morality, colonial war and the treatment of women and the old, which angered conservatives in France. Both were also controversial among their own admirers, because their views did not fit neat political labels. Historians today regard Solzhenitsyn as central to understanding Soviet terror and de Beauvoir as one of the most influential critics of the twentieth century, though debate about both continues.\n\nSolzhenitsyn's controversy began with his experience of the Gulag. Born in Kislovodsk in 1918, he served as an artillery officer in the Second World War and was arrested in 1945 for criticising Stalin in private letters. He was sentenced to eight years in labour camps and then to internal exile in Kazakhstan, and he was cured of cancer in a Tashkent hospital in 1954, an episode behind Cancer Ward. One Day in the Life of Ivan Denisovich appeared in Novy Mir in 1962 with Khrushchev's approval and brought the camps into public discussion, but permission came only during the thaw.\n\nAfter Khrushchev fell, the authorities tightened censorship and Solzhenitsyn became an open opponent. The First Circle and Cancer Ward could be published only abroad in 1968, and he was expelled from the Writers' Union in 1969. He won the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1970, which the Soviet leadership regarded as a hostile political act. When the KGB seized a copy of The Gulag Archipelago in 1973 he authorised its publication in Paris, and it documented the camp system through testimony and history. He was arrested on 12 February 1974 and flown next day to West Germany. The regime saw him as a traitor, while his case became a symbol of the repression of intellectuals, alongside dissidents like Sakharov, Sinyavsky and Daniel.\n\nHis controversy did not end with exile. Living first in Switzerland and from 1976 in Vermont, he startled the West in his Harvard address of 1978 by criticising Western materialism as well as communism. Many Western admirers turned cooler, disliking his nationalist and religious views, and some historians question the reliability of parts of his testimony. After he returned to Russia in 1994 his reputation remained divided between admirers who saw him as the nation's conscience and critics who disliked his Russian nationalism and religious conservatism.\n\nDe Beauvoir's controversy was of a different kind. She and Sartre founded the review Les Temps modernes in 1945, which became the leading journal of the French left, and her work on the position of women, above all The Second Sex of 1949, challenged the traditional role of wives and mothers and provoked fierce attacks from conservative opinion. Her partnership with Sartre, a union by choice without marriage, was presented by admirers as a model of equality but condemned by critics as a privilege of the elite and a breach of conventional morality.\n\nHer politics were equally divisive. In 1960 she signed the Manifesto of the 121 supporting French conscripts who refused to fight in Algeria, and in 1962 she campaigned against torture by the French army, championing the case of Djamila Boupacha. These stands against the state in wartime were bitterly resented by patriotic opinion. Her essay The Coming of Age of 1970 attacked how society treated the elderly, and her sympathy for communism, which changed over time, drew criticism. Her novel The Mandarins, about the post-war Parisian left, won the Prix Goncourt in 1954. In short, both writers were controversial because they spoke against established power, and each unsettled their supporters as well as their opponents.",
