    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The main social effects of European withdrawal from Africa between 1945 and 1990 were ethnic conflict, political instability and some gains in education and health, while the main economic effects were continued dependence on raw materials and on Europe, then debt and famine. Independence gave Africans control of their governments, but the colonial legacy of borders, ethnic divisions and one-crop economies limited what they could achieve.\n\nThe withdrawal came quickly. Ghana became independent in March 1957, Macmillan spoke of a 'wind of change' in February 1960, and seventeen African states became independent in 1960 alone. Speed meant that many new states had few trained administrators. In the Belgian Congo, independence on 30 June 1960 followed a Round Table only five months earlier, and the colony had almost no Congolese graduates or army officers. The Force Publique mutinied within a week, Katanga seceded, and Lumumba was murdered in January 1961. Mobutu took power in 1965 and ruled Zaire as a one-party state until 1990, so the social effect of rapid withdrawal was prolonged political breakdown.\n\nEthnic division was a second major social effect. In Nigeria the British had joined north and south in 1914 and governed the north through emirs, and the parties of the 1950s each represented one region and group. The coups of 1966, the massacres of Igbo in the north and the declaration of Biafra in May 1967 led to a war that ended in January 1970 with famine and a death toll estimated from several hundred thousand to over two million. Angola shows the same pattern, since Portugal refused to leave until the Carnation Revolution of 1974, and the MPLA, FNLA and UNITA, each with a different ethnic base and foreign backers, fought a civil war lasting to 2002 while most of the 300,000 Portuguese settlers left, taking skills with them.\n\nNot all effects were negative. Tanzania under Nyerere, who led Tanganyika to independence in 1961 and united it with Zanzibar in 1964, avoided ethnic conflict by making Swahili the national language and refusing to form parties on ethnic lines. The universal primary education programme of 1974 raised enrolment rapidly, and adult literacy rose to a high level by the early 1980s. Aid agencies and missionaries ran schools, clinics and vaccination programmes, and smallpox was eradicated by 1977. So independence brought genuine social progress where government was stable.\n\nEconomically, Africa inherited economies built to export cocoa, groundnuts, cotton and copper to Europe, with railways running from mines to ports. Dependence on one or two commodities meant that falling prices were damaging, as copper prices were to Zaire in 1974-75 and cocoa and coffee prices to others in the 1980s. European companies such as Shell-BP and the Union Minière retained control of oil and mining. Ties with Europe continued formally through the Yaoundé Conventions of 1963 and 1969 and the Lomé Convention of 1975, which gave 46 African, Caribbean and Pacific states duty-free access to the EEC and a fund, STABEX, to compensate for falls in export earnings. This helped, but exports stayed concentrated on primary products.\n\nThe 1970s and 1980s brought debt and hunger. Oil price rises in 1973 and 1979 and falling commodity prices left many states unable to repay loans, and from the early 1980s the IMF and World Bank demanded structural adjustment, with devaluation and cuts in spending that hit health and education. Famine struck Biafra in 1968-70, the Sahel in the early 1970s and Ethiopia in 1983-85, where hundreds of thousands died and Band Aid and Live Aid raised about 150 million pounds. Nyerere's ujamaa programme, begun in the Arusha Declaration of 1967, tried to build self-reliance, but forced villagisation from 1973-74 disrupted farming and Tanzania ended up importing food, and a successor accepted an IMF programme in 1986.\n\nIn conclusion, the effects of European withdrawal were deep but uneven. Socially, it brought ethnic conflict and instability in the Congo, Nigeria and Angola but real gains in schooling and health in places such as Tanzania. Economically, it did little to end dependence, and debt and famine left Africa in 1990 poorer and more reliant on outside help than independence had promised.",
