    "label": "2",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Both the Suez Crisis and the secession of Katanga acquired an international dimension because each involved the interests of outside powers from the start. At Suez, Egypt's nationalisation of a canal that Western Europe depended on drew in Britain and France, and the Cold War superpowers then decided the outcome. In Katanga, the mineral wealth of the province and the Cold War turned a regional secession into a contest involving Belgium, the United Nations, the United States and the Soviet Union.\n\nThe Suez Canal was of vital international importance in itself. It had opened in 1869, Britain had bought the Khedive's 44 per cent share in 1875, and about two-thirds of Western Europe's oil imports passed through it in the 1950s. When Nasser announced the nationalisation of the Suez Canal Company on 26 July 1956, promising that the revenues would fund the Aswan High Dam, he was challenging a key Western interest. The crisis began after the United States, followed by Britain, withdrew its offer to fund the dam on 19 July. Because the canal was an international waterway owned by an Anglo-French company, Britain and France treated the move as a threat to their economies and their status, and Eden compared Nasser with the dictators of the 1930s.\n\nThe crisis also drew in Israel and the wider Middle East. Nasser's Arab nationalism, his closing of the canal and the Straits of Tiran to Israeli ships and the fedayeen raids from Gaza made Israel a willing partner. Britain, France and Israel signed the secret Protocol of Sèvres on 24 October 1956, setting out an Israeli attack on Sinai followed by an Anglo-French ultimatum. Israel invaded on 29 October, the ultimatum came on 30 October, and British and French paratroopers landed at Port Said on 5 November. Nasser's prestige as the leader of Arab nationalism meant the dispute spread beyond Egypt, and his defiance of the old colonial powers was watched across the Arab world and the newly independent states.\n\nThe Cold War superpowers made the crisis truly international. The United States under Eisenhower opposed the invasion, since it damaged the Western case against Soviet behaviour and threatened to push Arab states towards Moscow. Britain faced a run on the pound, and the Americans refused IMF support until Britain agreed to withdraw. The Soviet Union, whose forces had crushed the Hungarian rising on 4 November, threatened rocket attacks on London and Paris in a letter of 5 November and supported Egypt in the UN. The ceasefire took effect on 6-7 November, and the UN created UNEF on the proposal of Lester Pearson to supervise the withdrawal. Suez ended as a lesson that Britain and France could no longer act against the wishes of the superpowers.\n\nKatanga's secession had an international dimension from its first days for similar reasons of wealth and rivalry. Katanga was the copper-mining heart of the Congo, and the Union Minière, with Belgian shareholders, gave it a far higher revenue base than other provinces. Moïse Tshombe proclaimed secession on 11 July 1960, eleven days after independence, and he was backed by Belgian officers, advisers and mercenaries who trained the Katanga Gendarmerie. Belgium sent troops back to the Congo on 10 July, so the former colonial power was involved from the beginning, and Belgian mining interests supported Conakat's autonomy campaign.\n\nThe United Nations then became the central international actor. Lumumba asked the UN for help, and the Security Council set up the ONUC operation in July 1960, with UN troops entering Katanga on 12 August. Irish troops suffered nine dead at Niemba in November 1960, and A Company at Jadotville was besieged in September 1961 before surrendering. Resolutions 161 and 169 gradually authorised force against the secession and foreign mercenaries, and Dag Hammarskjöld died in an air crash near Ndola on 18 September 1961 while travelling to meet Tshombe. The UN's Operation Grand Slam ended the secession in January 1963.\n\nThe Cold War and African politics added further layers. Lumumba turned to the Soviet Union for aircraft and advisers in August 1960, which alarmed the United States, and Khrushchev attacked Hammarskjöld's handling of the Congo and demanded a three-person executive. Lumumba's murder in Katanga on 17 January 1961, with Belgian officers present, provoked Soviet and African protests. African states split, with the Casablanca group backing the central government and the Monrovia group less hostile to Tshombe.\n\nIn both cases the international dimension arose because strategic resources, the canal and Katanga's copper, were at stake, because former colonial powers tried to protect their interests, and because the Cold War rivalry of the United States and the Soviet Union turned a local dispute into a world crisis. Suez was an international crisis because of the great powers and the canal, while Katanga was one because of the UN and the Cold War, and both ended on terms set largely by outside forces.",
