    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Britain and France both became multi-racial societies between 1945 and 1990, but to different degrees and in different ways. Both received large numbers of migrants from former colonies and both had visible minority communities by the 1980s. Britain went further in recognising these communities and in passing laws against discrimination, whereas France's republican model insisted on assimilation, and in both countries racism, riots and far-right politics showed how incomplete integration remained.\n\nBritain's multi-racial character began with Commonwealth migration. The British Nationality Act of 1948 gave Commonwealth citizens the right to settle, and the SS Empire Windrush docked at Tilbury on 22 June 1948 with about 1,000 passengers, mostly from Jamaica. Labour shortages led Britain to recruit workers for the NHS and London Transport, while migrants from India and Pakistan, many from Punjab and Gujarat, settled in Birmingham, Leicester, Bradford and London. Migration peaked in 1961-62 as people rushed to enter before restrictions. The result was concentrated multi-racial communities in a few cities, although these communities faced a colour bar in lodgings and exploitation by landlords such as Peter Rachman in Notting Hill.\n\nBritish society was also marked by hostility, and the response of government was contradictory. The Notting Hill and Nottingham disturbances of 1958 and the murder of Kelso Cochrane in 1959 showed the strength of racism. The Commonwealth Immigrants Act of 1962 introduced employment vouchers, the 1968 Act was rushed through to limit Kenyan Asians, and the Immigration Act of 1971 introduced patriality. Enoch Powell's 'rivers of blood' speech of 20 April 1968 and the rise of the National Front, founded in 1967, showed that racism had a political voice, though the Front never won a parliamentary seat. At the same time the Race Relations Acts of 1965, 1968 and 1976 outlawed discrimination, extended the ban to housing and employment, and created the Commission for Racial Equality. Roy Jenkins's 1966 call for integration without assimilation set the tone of British policy.\n\nBy the 1980s Britain was clearly multi-cultural as well as multi-racial. Caribbean music and food shaped British life, Notting Hill Carnival grew into one of Europe's largest street festivals, and Muslim, Sikh and Hindu communities built mosques, gurdwaras and temples. Black and Asian MPs such as Diane Abbott, Paul Boateng, Bernie Grant and Keith Vaz were elected in 1987, and the Swann Report of 1985 argued that schools should recognise minority children's needs. Against this, the Brixton riots of April 1981, after Operation Swamp 81, and the riots in Toxteth and Moss Side showed the depth of unemployment, disadvantage and poor police relations, as the Scarman Report found. The Rushdie affair of 1988-89 revealed tensions between religious identity and free expression.\n\nFrance became multi-racial through labour migration from its former empire and from southern Europe. Post-war reconstruction created labour shortages, and migrants from Portugal, Spain, Italy, Morocco, Tunisia and West Africa worked in factories, mines and construction. Algerians held French nationality until 1962 and the Franco-Algerian accord of 1968 gave them preferential rights, so that by 1982 about 800,000 Algerians lived in France, the largest foreign nationality. Many lived in bidonvilles such as Nanterre and later in banlieue housing estates. The Algerian war cast a long shadow, as the police attack on Algerian demonstrators in Paris on 17 October 1961 and the poor treatment of the harkis showed, and in 1974 France suspended labour immigration while allowing family reunification from 1976. A second generation, the beurs, grew up in the banlieues.\n\nFrance's official philosophy limited how far it acknowledged itself as multi-racial. The republican model was based on universal citizenship and assimilation, with laïcité since the 1905 law, and the state did not collect ethnic statistics. The Pleven law of 1972 made incitement to racial hatred a crime, but there was no equivalent of the Race Relations Acts. The 1980s showed the strains: the Minguettes unrest of 1981, the March for Equality and against Racism of 1983, which reached Paris on 3 December with tens of thousands of supporters, and SOS Racisme, founded in 1984 with its slogan 'Touche pas à mon pote'. Meanwhile Jean-Marie Le Pen's Front National broke through at Dreux in 1983, won 35 seats in 1986 and took about 14.4 per cent in the 1988 presidential election, and the headscarf affair at Creil in 1989 exposed the tension between laïcité and Muslim identity.\n\nTo a considerable extent both countries became multi-racial in demographic terms, and by 1990 both had large and settled minority communities. Britain accepted multi-racial society more openly in law and culture, with legal protection and minority political representation, while France was multi-racial in fact but slower to accept it in principle, since it recognised only individual citizens. In neither country were minorities fully equal, as the 1981 riots in Britain and the banlieues and Front National in France demonstrate, so the answer is that both became multi-racial societies but only partly multi-racial in the sense of equal acceptance.",
