    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Gandhi was very effective in advancing India's independence, though he did not achieve it alone or on the terms he wanted, while Ho Chi Minh was highly effective in leading Vietnam to independence from France, although the country was divided and the struggle continued long after. Both turned a nationalist movement into a mass force, but Gandhi's non-violent mass campaigns contrast with Ho's use of communist organisation and armed struggle.\n\nGandhi's main contribution was to transform the Indian National Congress into a mass movement. Returning from South Africa in 1915, he became the dominant Congress leader after 1920 and developed satyagraha, or non-violent civil disobedience, first used in the peasant campaigns at Champaran in 1917 and Kheda in 1918. The Non-Cooperation movement of 1920-22 urged boycotts of British goods, courts and schools, and the Salt March of 12 March to 6 April 1930, about 240 miles to Dandi, captured world attention by defying the salt monopoly. On 8 August 1942 Congress passed the Quit India resolution and Gandhi was imprisoned until May 1944. These campaigns made British rule difficult to maintain without repression that the British public would not accept, and they gave Indians a sense of national unity.\n\nHowever, Gandhi's effectiveness was limited. Non-Cooperation was called off after Chauri Chaura in February 1922, and Congress rejected the Cripps Mission offer in 1942. Other factors helped end British rule: the Second World War exhausted Britain, the Indian National Army trials of 1945-46 and the naval mutiny of 1946 raised doubts about the army's loyalty, and Attlee's Labour government decided to leave, announcing in February 1947 that British rule would end by June 1948. Gandhi also failed to maintain Hindu-Muslim unity. Jinnah argued that Gandhi spoke only for Hindus, and Direct Action Day of 16 August 1946 brought killings in Calcutta. India was partitioned on 15 August 1947, a result Gandhi opposed, and he was in Calcutta fasting rather than celebrating. He was shot dead by Nathuram Godse on 30 January 1948. Judged on the aim of independence he was highly effective, though on unity he failed.\n\nHo Chi Minh advanced Vietnamese independence through organisation, diplomacy and war. Born in 1890, he left Vietnam in 1911, spent about thirty years abroad and petitioned the Versailles conference in 1919, an appeal the Allies ignored. In May 1941 he founded the Viet Minh, a front combining communists and non-communist nationalists, which fought the Japanese and used the famine of 1944-45 to win support. When Japan surrendered the Viet Minh seized power, and on 2 September 1945 Ho proclaimed the Democratic Republic of Vietnam in Hanoi, quoting the American Declaration of Independence. Emperor Bao Dai had abdicated on 25 August, and the Viet Minh's early seizure of power gave it legitimacy over rival groups.\n\nHo's effectiveness was proved in the long struggle against France. His appeals to Truman went unanswered and the Franco-Vietnamese agreement of 6 March 1946 broke down, with fighting breaking out in Hanoi in December 1946. After the Chinese communist victory in 1949, China supplied the Viet Minh with weapons and advisers, and under General Giap the Viet Minh overran the French garrison at Dien Bien Phu between 13 March and 7 May 1954, taking more than 10,000 prisoners. The Geneva Accords of 21 July 1954 ended French rule, but Vietnam was divided at the 17th parallel and the 1956 elections never took place. Ho had won independence for the north but not unity, and the struggle continued against the United States and Diem's South.\n\nOverall, both leaders were effective but with limits. Gandhi created the mass movement that made British rule untenable, yet independence came through partition and amid wider causes beyond his control. Ho achieved the more complete military and political defeat of the colonial power, but his success left Vietnam divided and at war. I would judge Gandhi more effective in mobilising a nation and Ho more effective in forcing the colonial power out, with neither fully achieving the united country he sought.",
