    "label": "2",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The Montgomery bus boycott took place because black people in Alabama's capital were tired of humiliating bus segregation, because a well-organised community was ready to act once Rosa Parks was arrested on 1 December 1955, and because the Supreme Court's decision in Brown had raised expectations. It advanced the cause of civil rights by ending bus segregation in the city, by creating a national leader and a lasting organisation, and by showing that disciplined non-violent mass protest could win.\n\nThe causes lay first in everyday humiliation. Montgomery's buses reserved the front rows for white passengers and the rear for black passengers, and the driver could move the dividing line back to force black riders to stand or give up seats. Black passengers had to pay at the front and then re-enter by the rear door, and drivers, who were armed and had police powers, often insulted them or drove off. Black people were the large majority of riders, many of them maids and labourers travelling to work in white homes, so the rules touched most of the black working population. The Supreme Court's Brown v Board of Education decision of May 1954 raised hopes, and in that month the Women's Political Council, led by Jo Ann Robinson, warned the mayor that a boycott was possible.\n\nA second cause was the existence of organisation and leadership ready to act. Earlier arrests, such as that of fifteen-year-old Claudette Colvin in March 1955, had not been used because local leaders like E. D. Nixon wanted a respectable adult as a test case. Rosa Parks, a seamstress and secretary of the Montgomery NAACP who had attended a workshop at the Highlander Folk School, was arrested on 1 December 1955 for refusing to give up her seat. She was no accidental protester. Nixon bailed her out and persuaded her to allow her case to be used, and that night Robinson and the Women's Political Council, with students at Alabama State College, ran off tens of thousands of leaflets calling for a one-day boycott. Black ministers announced the boycott from their pulpits, and it was almost completely observed on 5 December. That evening about 5,000 people at Holt Street Baptist Church voted to continue it.\n\nThe boycott advanced the cause first through its organisation and its method. The Montgomery Improvement Association, formed on 5 December, elected the 26-year-old Martin Luther King Jr. as president because he was new in the city and had no enemies. A car pool of volunteers and black taxi drivers charging ten cents carried about 40,000 riders to work, and churches raised money for station wagons, while many walked long distances. The boycott lasted 381 days, hit the bus company's income hard and cost city-centre shops custom. King insisted on non-violence and love for opponents, drawing on Christian teaching and Gandhi, and advisers such as Bayard Rustin helped him to apply it. Demands that began modestly, for courtesy and first-come seating, grew into a demand for desegregation as the city refused to negotiate.\n\nWhite resistance, and the way the boycotters met it, brought more advance. Police arrested car pool drivers, King was arrested on 26 January 1956 for speeding, and his house was bombed on 30 January, when he calmed an angry crowd and urged no retaliation. In February a grand jury indicted 89 leaders under an Alabama law against boycotts, and King was convicted and fined in March. This persecution brought national press attention and northern sympathy. Fred Gray filed Browder v Gayle on 1 February 1956 on behalf of four women, and a federal court ruled on 5 June that bus segregation violated the Fourteenth Amendment. The Supreme Court affirmed this on 13 November 1956, and the buses were desegregated on 21 December, when King and Ralph Abernathy rode in front of the press.\n\nThe longer term results were also important. King became a national and international figure, and in 1957 he became first president of the Southern Christian Leadership Conference, built on the model of the MIA and the black churches. The boycott showed that economic pressure and mass non-violent action could defeat segregation, a method later used in the sit-ins, in Birmingham and in Selma. It also gave publicity that mattered in the Cold War, when the Soviet Union used American racism as propaganda. Women such as Robinson and Parks proved crucial to this, although men received more recognition.\n\nIts achievements had limits. The victory applied only to buses, schools, housing and restaurants stayed segregated and black voters were still largely excluded. White violence followed, with snipers firing on buses and bombs damaging four black churches in January 1957, and some historians argue the courts, not the boycott, ended segregation. Yet Montgomery kept the legal case alive, made the ruling effective and launched the leaders, the organisation and the method that carried the movement to the Civil Rights Acts of 1964 and 1965, so it must be judged a landmark in the struggle.",
