    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Betty Friedan and Marilyn Monroe reflect the changing role of women in US society in opposite but complementary ways. Monroe embodied the 1950s ideal of glamorous, decorative femininity, together with the constraints Hollywood placed on women, while Friedan voiced the discontent with that ideal and led the organised feminist movement that challenged it from the early 1960s.\n\nThe context was a decade that promoted domesticity. Many women left wartime industrial jobs after 1945 and were encouraged by magazines, films and advice literature to return to the home. The baby boom, peaking in 1957 with about 4.3 million births, and the move to the suburbs strengthened the picture of the woman as wife and mother, while the average age of marriage fell. At the same time women's share of the paid workforce was rising, from about 30 per cent in 1950 to about 38 per cent by 1970, though mostly in low-paid clerical, teaching, nursing and sales jobs. These contradictions are the background to both women.\n\nMonroe reflected the 1950s ideal of beauty and desirability. Born Norma Jeane Mortenson in Los Angeles in 1926, she became a star in 1953 with Gentlemen Prefer Blondes and How to Marry a Millionaire, and The Seven Year Itch followed in 1955. Her screen image of a childlike, available blonde was widely copied and was closely tied to the consumer culture of the decade, appearing in advertisements and magazines that sold products through her appeal. She showed how women were expected to be attractive, and the press criticised the way Hollywood marketed her as a sex symbol rather than as a serious actress.\n\nMonroe also showed that women were beginning to resist the controls placed on them. She fought the studio over her contract and her choice of roles, founded Marilyn Monroe Productions in 1955 and studied at the Actors Studio in New York. She won critical praise for her comic performance in Some Like It Hot in 1959, directed by Billy Wilder. Her marriage to the playwright Arthur Miller in 1956 linked her with serious drama. Her death from an overdose of barbiturates on the night of 4 August 1962, after she was dismissed from Something's Got to Give, left later critics arguing that her career showed how the studio system exploited women performers. Historians treat her as a symbol of both the glamour and the constraints of the age.\n\nFriedan reflected the change that followed. Born in Peoria, Illinois, in 1921 and educated at Smith College, she became a magazine writer and suburban housewife. Her survey of former Smith classmates formed the basis of The Feminine Mystique, published in 1963. It argued that American women were trapped by a domestic ideal that denied them fulfilment, which she called the problem that has no name. The book sold very widely and is credited with helping to start the second wave of American feminism. It attacked exactly the ideal of femininity that Monroe's image had come to stand for, and it spoke for educated suburban women who felt that housework and motherhood were not enough.\n\nFriedan then turned discontent into organisation. The contraceptive pill was approved in May 1960 and the Equal Pay Act of 10 June 1963 required equal pay for equal work, so women had new tools. In June 1966 Friedan was a founder and first president of the National Organization for Women, which pressed for equal rights in employment and law. NOW campaigned for the Equal Rights Amendment, which passed Congress in March 1972 but fell short of ratification, with 35 of the 38 states needed, partly because of Phyllis Schlafly's STOP ERA campaign. Friedan organised the Women's Strike for Equality on 26 August 1970 and helped found the National Women's Political Caucus in 1971, while Title IX in 1972 banned sex discrimination in federally funded education.\n\nTogether the two women mark a transition. Monroe represents the glamour and limits of the 1950s, and in her struggles with Hollywood an early sign of dissatisfaction, while Friedan marks the move to organised demands for equality. Critics later argued that Friedan concentrated on white middle-class professional women, but she was still central to the revival of feminism, and between them they show how women's role changed from a decorative and domestic ideal in the 1950s to a campaign for equal rights by the 1970s.",
