    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The Moon landing of 1969 and Strategic Defense Initiative of 1983 were both major moments in the technological side of the Cold War. The Moon landing was a triumph that gave the United States victory in the space race and showed the world the power of American science and organisation. Star Wars was never built, but it shaped the final stage of the superpower rivalry by alarming the Soviet Union and complicating arms control.\n\nThe Moon landing had its roots in Soviet success. Sputnik, launched on 4 October 1957, and Yuri Gagarin's flight as the first man in space on 12 April 1961 convinced many Americans that the Soviet Union was ahead in technology. Sputnik led to the creation of NASA in 1958 and to the National Defense Education Act, which poured money into science teaching. After Gagarin and the humiliation of the Bay of Pigs, Kennedy told Congress on 25 May 1961 that the United States would land a man on the Moon and return him safely before the decade was out. The landing was therefore significant as an answer to Soviet challenge, a field in which America believed it could win.\n\nThe achievement itself was enormous. Apollo cost about 25 billion dollars and was the largest peacetime technological effort the country had undertaken, employing hundreds of thousands of people through contractors such as North American Aviation, Grumman and IBM. It needed the Saturn V rocket designed under Wernher von Braun, the Apollo Guidance Computer, one of the first to use integrated circuits, and the control centre at Houston. The programme survived the Apollo 1 fire in January 1967, which killed Grissom, White and Chaffee, and was carried forward by Apollo 8 orbiting the Moon in December 1968. On 20 July 1969 Neil Armstrong and Buzz Aldrin landed in the Sea of Tranquillity while Michael Collins orbited above, and Armstrong spoke of one small step for a man and one giant leap for mankind. The crew returned to the Pacific on 24 July, picked up by USS Hornet.\n\nThe Cold War significance was decisive. The Soviet Union's own Moon rocket, the N1, failed in every test and the crewed lunar programme was cancelled, while the Soviet probe Luna 15 crashed on the Moon as Apollo 11 was there. Hundreds of millions watched on television, so the landing was an image of American technology and freedom broadcast worldwide. At the same time the crew left a plaque saying they came in peace for all mankind and did not claim the Moon, in line with the 1967 Outer Space Treaty, which presented the achievement as one for humanity. Its significance was also limited. Critics such as Amitai Etzioni had called the programme a waste when poverty remained at home, and once the race was won, support faded. Apollo 17 in December 1972 was the last crewed lunar mission, and NASA's budget fell sharply in the early 1970s. The Space Shuttle, first flown in April 1981, later suffered the Challenger disaster of January 1986, which killed seven crew, showing the limits of American ambitions.\n\nStar Wars belongs to a very different moment, when Reagan had restored a hard line against Moscow. Having called the Soviet Union an evil empire in March 1983, he announced the Strategic Defense Initiative on 23 March 1983 in a television address, proposing space-based systems that could destroy incoming missiles. Critics nicknamed it Star Wars. Many scientists doubted that it was feasible and it was enormously expensive, and it was never deployed, though its research did fund work on lasers and sensors. Its significance lay mainly in its effects on the Soviets and on diplomacy rather than in what was built.\n\nSDI alarmed Soviet leaders, who feared an American technological lead that they could not match, and who saw a shield as a way to make a first strike possible. This mattered because the Soviet economy was already strained. Supporters of Reagan argue that the arms build-up and SDI forced the Soviet Union into a race it could not afford and helped create conditions for Gorbachev's reforms. It also changed negotiations. At Reykjavik in October 1986 Reagan and Gorbachev came close to agreeing deep cuts in nuclear weapons, but talks broke down when Gorbachev insisted that SDI be confined to the laboratory and Reagan refused.\n\nOverall both were highly significant, though in different ways. The Moon landing was a real and visible victory which crowned the space race and boosted American prestige at the height of the Cold War. Star Wars was never built, yet as a threat it pressured the Soviet Union and shaped the arms talks of the 1980s. One showed what American technology could do, the other what it might do, and each served American aims against the Soviet Union.",
