    "label": "B2",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "Ireland applied to join the United Nations in 1946, but the Soviet Union used its veto, and Ireland was admitted only in December 1955 in a package deal. Frank Aiken, Minister for External Affairs, pursued an independent policy, including a 1959 resolution with Malaya criticising Chinese suppression in Tibet. Irish resolutions on nuclear weapons led to Resolution 1665 in 1961, and Ireland was the first state to sign the 1968 Non-Proliferation Treaty. Irish troops served in UN peacekeeping in the Congo from 1960, where nine Irish soldiers were killed in an ambush at Niemba in November 1960.",
