    "label": "C1",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Telefís Éireann began broadcasting on 31 December 1961, and within a few years most households had a television set. The Late Late Show, first broadcast in July 1962 and presented by Gay Byrne from 1962 to 1999, discussed sex, marriage, contraception, unmarried mothers and the church, subjects that censorship had kept out of print. In 1966 the Bishop and the Nightie controversy, condemned by Bishop Thomas Ryan of Clonfert, brought the show to national attention and started a public debate about morality. Seven Days, launched in 1966, investigated housing, poverty and emigration, and its critical coverage led to complaints from ministers. The Riordans, a drama about a farming family that began in 1965, was a hit in rural Ireland and reduced the isolation of country communities. However, the Section 31 order of October 1971 and the dismissal of the RTÉ Authority in November 1972 showed that the broadcaster's independence was fragile.",
