    "label": "C2",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "The Frankfurt Assembly had no army, no money and no administration of its own, so it depended on the very governments whose power it was trying to limit. The rulers kept control of their armies and bureaucracies, which stayed loyal and retook Prague in June 1848, Vienna in October 1848 and Berlin in November 1848. Middle-class liberals were divided from radicals and workers, and they feared a social revolution and a republic more than they feared the princes. Peasants lost interest once governments abolished most feudal dues in 1848, and months of debate over Grossdeutsch and Kleindeutsch gave Austria and Prussia time to recover. Frederick William IV refused the imperial crown offered by the Assembly on 28 April 1849, so the constitution lacked the one state strong enough to enforce it. The Erfurt Union also collapsed under Austrian and Russian pressure at Olmutz in 1850.",
