    "model": "Mikhail Bakunin, born in 1814 into a Russian noble family, became a revolutionary in the 1840s. He took part in the Dresden rising of May 1849 and was sentenced to death, a sentence later commuted to life imprisonment. He argued for the abolition of the state and of all authority, a position that became known as anarchism. He rejected Marx's belief in the need for a workers' state. His rivalry with Marx split the First International, and he was expelled at the Hague Congress in 1872. Historians now treat him as a major figure in the anarchist tradition, although his influence on the mass labour movement was less than Marx's.",
