    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Martin Luther King Jr., a 26-year-old pastor of Dexter Avenue Baptist Church, was elected president of the Montgomery Improvement Association on 5 December 1955, and it co-ordinated the boycott. At the Holt Street meeting he combined a demand for justice with a commitment to non-violence and Christian love, which launched him as the movement's voice. King insisted that protesters stay non-violent, using Gandhi's methods, and he urged people not to retaliate after a bomb was thrown at his house on 30 January 1956. His arrest and conviction under an old anti-boycott law turned the court cases into a national symbol of dignity. King helped to keep the car pool and church networks running, which sustained the boycott for 381 days. After the Supreme Court ruling King rode a desegregated bus on 21 December 1956, and he later became the first president of the Southern Christian Leadership Conference.",
