    "label": "C2",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "The anti-war movement began on campuses, when the first major teach-in was held at the University of Michigan on 24 March 1965 and similar meetings spread to other universities. Students for a Democratic Society, founded in 1960, organised large anti-war marches from 1965 onwards. About 50,000 people marched on the Pentagon on 21 October 1967, an event later described by Norman Mailer in The Armies of the Night. The first draft lottery, held on 1 December 1969, made young men far more aware of the war's impact, and many burned their draft cards or fled to Canada. The Vietnam Moratorium of 15 October 1969 drew very large crowds, and the Kent State shootings of 4 May 1970 killed four students and widened opposition. In 1973 Congress passed the Case-Church amendment, which cut off funds for US combat in Indochina.",
