    "model": "Cardinal Paul Cullen shaped both Irish political attitudes and Catholic religious practice between 1850 and 1878. Politically he promoted caution, distrust of secret societies and of priests in agitation, and co-operation with the Liberals, while in religion he brought about Romanisation, discipline and uniform devotion, which helped to produce the devotional revolution.\n\nCullen was born in County Kildare in 1803, studied and was ordained in Rome, and became rector of the Irish College there in 1832. He came home as Archbishop of Armagh and Apostolic Delegate in 1850, became Archbishop of Dublin in 1852 and the first Irish cardinal in 1866. His long years in Rome made him an ultramontane, a believer in strong papal authority over national churches, and this belief shaped everything he did in Ireland.\n\nHis first religious influence came at the Synod of Thurles in 1850, the first national synod of the Irish Catholic Church since Kilkenny in 1642. Before then each bishop ran his own diocese with little central direction. The Synod, which Cullen presided over from 22 August to 9 September, laid down uniform liturgical practice and church discipline in the Roman rite and regulated clerical dress, behaviour and the conduct of the sacraments. It increased the bishops' authority over their priests, and it began the Romanisation of the Irish Church, making ultramontanism its guiding principle.\n\nCullen's second religious influence was on devotion and the institutions of the Church. He championed church building, religious orders and parish missions, with Redemptorists, Vincentians and Jesuits spreading the rosary, novenas, benediction and devotion to the Sacred Heart and the Virgin Mary. The Church ran more schools, hospitals and orphanages through congregations such as the Christian Brothers and the Sisters of Mercy and Charity. Emmet Larkin's term, the devotional revolution, describes a rise in weekly Mass attendance from perhaps a third of Catholics before the Famine to the large majority by the 1870s. Historians debate whether Cullen caused this or reflected a Famine-changed society of more prosperous and more devout farmers, but he was its central figure.\n\nIn education Cullen influenced both religion and public attitudes. He opposed the non-denominational Queen's Colleges, which Rome had condemned in 1847, and the Synod forbade Catholics to attend them. He backed the Catholic University, which opened in Dublin in 1854 under John Henry Newman. The university failed to win state-recognised degrees or many students, and most Catholics who wanted university education still went to Trinity or the Queen's Colleges, so his policy had limited success, though it showed the Church's determination to control Catholic education. He also pressed for greater Catholic control of the national schools.\n\nCullen's political influence was strongly conservative. He distrusted Young Ireland and the Tenant League and objected to priests in politics. The Synod discouraged priests from political agitation, notably at the altar and election meetings, and he disliked the Tenant League's priests, such as Fr Lavelle, who were backed by his rival, Archbishop MacHale of Tuam. He opposed Fenianism as a secret society, condemned it, and refused in 1861 to allow the body of Terence Bellew MacManus to lie in the Pro-Cathedral. Most bishops followed his lead, so the Church was a major opponent of revolutionary nationalism.\n\nHe favoured co-operation with the Liberals and supported Gladstone's disestablishment of the Church of Ireland in 1869, which shows his preference for achieving Catholic aims by constitutional, parliamentary means. Nationalists and some bishops such as MacHale resented his authority, but he was the dominant Catholic voice in public affairs for a quarter of a century.\n\nIn conclusion, Cullen's influence was enormous in both fields. Religiously he centralised and Romanised the Church and encouraged a disciplined, devotional Catholicism, while politically he encouraged caution and opposition to revolutionary movements, and a link between Catholic identity and Liberal reform, which left the Church the strongest institution of the Catholic majority.",
