    "model": "Between 1815 and 1870 Ireland saw significant developments in science and technology. Irish scientists made original contributions to astronomy, mathematics, physics and chemistry, while new technologies in transport, communications and industry began to change how Irish people travelled, communicated and worked. Some of these advances were the work of individual geniuses, others were backed by institutions, and together they showed that Ireland could contribute to the wider scientific world of the nineteenth century.\n\nThe most striking achievements were in mathematics. William Rowan Hamilton, Royal Astronomer of Ireland at Dunsink Observatory, discovered quaternions on 16 October 1843 while walking along the Royal Canal and scratched the formula onto Broom Bridge. This was a new kind of algebra that later proved fundamental to physics, mechanics and computing. A second Irish mathematician, George Boole, became the first professor of mathematics at Queen's College Cork in 1849 and published The Laws of Thought in 1854. Boolean algebra, the system of logical reasoning he created, became the basis of modern computer science. Boole was largely self-taught and Hamilton was a child prodigy, but both produced work that earned Ireland an international reputation in pure mathematics.\n\nAstronomy produced another remarkable development. William Parsons, third Earl of Rosse, built the six-foot reflecting telescope at Birr Castle, known as the Leviathan of Parsonstown, which was completed in 1845 and was the largest telescope in the world. Using it he observed the spiral structure of the galaxy later known as M51, the Whirlpool Galaxy, the first time such a structure had been seen. Rosse combined great engineering skill, since he had to cast and mount huge mirrors, with scientific vision, and Birr became a place of pilgrimage for astronomers from Britain and abroad. That a landowner in the Irish midlands built the finest instrument of its day shows how advanced Irish science could be.\n\nPhysics and chemistry also advanced. Nicholas Callan, a priest and professor of natural philosophy at Maynooth, built an early induction coil in the 1830s and developed powerful batteries, work that anticipated the transformer and showed that a Catholic college could be a centre of experimental science. Robert Kane, the first president of Queen's College Cork, wrote The Industrial Resources of Ireland in 1844, arguing that science should be applied to develop Irish agriculture and industry. Two Irish-born physicists, John Tyndall from Carlow and William Thomson, later Lord Kelvin, from Belfast, went on to win international fame, although much of their career was spent in Britain, which illustrates that Irish talent often needed to leave Ireland to flourish.\n\nTechnology changed daily life as well. The Dublin and Kingstown Railway opened in December 1834 as Ireland's first railway, and it was followed by lines to Belfast, Cork and elsewhere, so that by 1870 the railways linked most major towns, carried goods and people far more quickly and helped create a national market. Communications were transformed when the first transatlantic telegraph cable was laid in 1858 from Valentia Island in County Kerry to Newfoundland, making Ireland the European terminus of instant communication with America, with Kelvin contributing to the science behind it. In the north, the shipyard of Harland and Wolff, established on Queen's Island in Belfast in the early 1860s, built iron ships and made Belfast a leading centre of shipbuilding.\n\nThese developments were supported by institutions such as the Royal Dublin Society, the Royal Irish Academy and the new Queen's Colleges of 1845, which gave scientists teaching posts and a forum for ideas. However, the benefits were uneven. The Famine and emigration drained the country of people, and Irish industry outside the north-east remained limited, so scientific brilliance did not translate into widespread industrial growth.\n\nOverall, the years 1815 to 1870 were a period of real scientific and technological achievement in Ireland. Hamilton, Boole, Rosse and Callan made discoveries of lasting importance, while railways, the telegraph and shipbuilding changed the country's economy and links with the world. The achievements were those of a few outstanding individuals rather than a broad industrial revolution, but they gave Ireland an influence on science out of proportion to its size and its troubles.",
