    "model": "Michael Davitt was the more successful campaigner. Both men championed the poor against powerful interests, but Davitt built a mass movement that forced the state to change the law and, within a generation, transformed who owned the land of Ireland. Larkin organised the unskilled as no one had before and his union survived, yet he lost the great confrontation of his career in 1913 and left the country the following year.\n\nDavitt came to the struggle with bitter personal experience. Born in 1846 at Straide in Co. Mayo, he was evicted with his family in 1850 and grew up in Haslingden in Lancashire, where he lost his right arm in a cotton-mill accident at eleven. He joined the IRB, was arrested for arms trafficking in 1870 and served about seven years of a fifteen-year sentence, mostly at Dartmoor, before release in December 1877. His success lay in turning a Fenian background into a constitutional-agrarian alliance. Through the New Departure, linked to John Devoy and Irish America, he brought physical-force nationalists, farmers and Parnell's parliamentary party together behind the land question, something no earlier campaigner had achieved.\n\nHis greatest achievement was the Land League. A farming crisis from 1877, with poor harvests, cheap American grain and rising evictions, gave him his opening. He organised the meeting at Irishtown, Co. Mayo, on 20 April 1879, founded the Land League of Mayo that August and helped found the Irish National Land League in Dublin in October 1879, with Parnell as president. The League's branches multiplied across the south and west in 1880, funded by Irish America. Boycotting, first applied to Captain Boycott in Mayo in 1880, gave tenants a weapon of collective action. Davitt had created a mass peasant movement and made Parnell's party a national force.\n\nThe results were concrete. Under pressure from the Land War, Gladstone's Land Act of 1881 conceded the Three Fs, fair rent, fixity of tenure and free sale, and the land courts reduced rents by about one fifth on average. The Arrears Act of 1882 followed. Even more important, the agitation made the state accept that landlordism could not continue. Constructive unionism, the Ashbourne Act of 1885, the Land Conference and the Wyndham Act of 1903 all flowed from the pressure he had begun, and over 200,000 tenants bought their farms. By 1914 landlordism had effectively ended as a political and social force. There were limits: Davitt wanted land nationalisation, not peasant proprietorship, and labourers and small western farmers gained least, but few campaigners can claim such a transformation.\n\nLarkin's achievements were real but narrower. Born in Liverpool and a docker turned organiser, he led the Belfast dock and carters' strike of 1907, which united Catholic and Protestant workers for a time. After breaking with the National Union of Dock Labourers he founded the Irish Transport and General Workers' Union in 1909, with Liberty Hall as its headquarters, and the weekly Irish Worker in 1911. He brought unskilled workers into organised unionism for the first time, using the sympathetic strike and refusing to handle tainted goods. The ITGWU won disputes in Dublin and Cork, and the Irish Labour Party was founded in 1912. In a city where over 20,000 families lived in single tenement rooms, this gave hope to those at the bottom.\n\nHis campaign ended in defeat, however. In August 1913 William Martin Murphy and about 400 employers locked out workers who refused to sign the Document renouncing the union. Around 20,000 were out of work. The police batoned crowds on Bloody Sunday in O'Connell Street, Larkin was jailed for sedition, and the British TUC refused in December 1913 the sympathetic action he sought. Hunger forced men back by January 1914, many without recognition, and some were blacklisted. The Irish Citizen Army was a by-product, and the union survived and grew during the war under Connolly, but Larkin left for the United States in October 1914. His combative temper arguably made compromise harder.\n\nOn balance Davitt was the more successful campaigner. He achieved his main objective in his lifetime, secured legislation that reshaped the countryside and created an organisation whose methods later movements copied. Larkin was a brilliant and inspiring organiser who gave Dublin's poor dignity and a union that outlived him, but he lost the decisive struggle of his career, whereas Davitt's campaign ended landlordism.",
