    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Between 1870 and 1914 Unionism was highly effective in resisting Home Rule for most of the period, though not completely so. Unionists helped defeat the first two Home Rule Bills, built organisations and an armed force that made coercing Ulster impossible, and by 1914 had forced the government to accept the principle of exclusion. They could not, however, stop the Third Home Rule Bill becoming law, and southern unionists were left unprotected.\n\nUnionism was effective because it was a broad coalition with real grounds for resistance. Ulster had a slight Protestant majority, about 56 per cent in 1911, concentrated in Antrim, Down, Armagh and Londonderry, and Protestants of every class, from landlords and industrialists to Belfast workers and Presbyterian farmers, felt threatened. Their opposition rested on religion (the slogan that Home Rule is Rome Rule), on economics, since Belfast's linen, shipbuilding and engineering depended on British and imperial markets, and on a British identity. Many Presbyterians who had supported the Liberals moved over to unionism after Gladstone's conversion to Home Rule in 1886, which gave the cause a mass base.\n\nIn the 1880s and 1890s unionism scored its first successes. Gladstone's First Home Rule Bill split the Liberal Party in 1886 and was defeated in the Commons. Liberal Unionists under Joseph Chamberlain provided the margin, and Salisbury's Conservatives, in alliance with unionists, held office for most of the next twenty years. The Second Home Rule Bill of 1893 passed the Commons by 301 votes to 267, but the Lords rejected it by 419 votes to 41, and Gladstone retired. Unionist resistance was reinforced by organisation: the Irish Loyal and Patriotic Union in 1885, the revived Orange Order, and the Irish Unionist Alliance in 1891. The Ulster Convention of June 1892 in Belfast, with about 12,000 delegates, showed the strength of feeling. Unionism had therefore blocked Home Rule for twenty-eight years.\n\nThe Third Home Rule crisis after 1910 tested it more severely. The Parliament Act of 1911 removed the Lords' veto, so the bill introduced in April 1912 could not be stopped constitutionally. Unionists responded with a campaign of mass resistance. Edward Carson, the Dublin-born barrister, and James Craig organised it. Bonar Law pledged Conservative support at Blenheim Palace in July 1912. On Ulster Day, 28 September 1912, about 237,000 men signed the Solemn League and Covenant and about 234,000 women signed a separate declaration. This showed a united province ready to resist, and the Ulster Unionist Council, founded in 1905, directed it.\n\nIt was the threat of force that made unionism most effective. The Ulster Volunteer Force was formed in January 1913 and by 1914 had perhaps 90,000 to 100,000 men, while the Ulster Unionist Council announced a Provisional Government for Ulster. In March 1914 the Curragh incident, when General Gough and many officers offered to resign rather than act against Ulster, showed that the army could not be relied on. In April the UVF landed about 25,000 rifles at Larne, Donaghadee and Bangor without interference. Asquith offered the exclusion of Ulster counties for six years, and Redmond reluctantly accepted this in March 1914. Partition had become a live option because of unionist pressure, and the Buckingham Palace Conference in July 1914 failed only over Tyrone and Fermanagh.\n\nThe limits of this success are clear. Unionists could not prevent the Home Rule Act receiving royal assent on 18 September 1914. Its suspension for the duration of the war owed more to the outbreak of the First World War than to unionist strength. Southern unionists, who were about a tenth of the population outside Ulster, had no protection, and Carson later lamented that unionism had been sacrificed. The alliance with the Conservatives also left unionists exposed if that party compromised.\n\nOverall, unionism was very effective in resisting Home Rule. It defeated two bills, divided the Liberals, created organisations and a militia, and won exclusion for Ulster in principle. It failed to stop Home Rule being placed on the statute book, and it did not save the unity of the Union, but by 1914 it had made it impossible for Home Rule to be applied to the whole island against the will of Ulster.",
