    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The GAA and the Gaelic League achieved considerable success between 1884 and 1914, though in different ways. The GAA built a lasting mass organisation almost at once, despite a near collapse in 1891, while the League launched a real revival of interest in the Irish language that fell short of restoring it as a spoken language. Both did more to change Irish attitudes and identity than to achieve any immediate political goal.\n\nThe GAA was founded at Hayes' Hotel in Thurles on 1 November 1884 by Michael Cusack and Maurice Davin, to revive native games and to open athletics to all classes. The existing athletics bodies followed English amateur rules that excluded labourers and artisans, and hurling had declined since the Famine. Archbishop Croke of Cashel agreed to be patron in December 1884 and attacked the imitation of English games. Parnell and Davitt also became patrons, so the GAA gained prestige and publicity. Davin drew up the first rules in 1885, and the parish-based club structure, with county committees and a Central Council, meant that it spread rapidly. The first All-Ireland championships were held in 1887.\n\nThe GAA's early years were uneven. IRB members joined in large numbers and tried to control the Central Council, Cusack was removed as secretary in 1886, and the bishops grew uneasy. The American tour of 1888 lost money and left the championships unfinished. The Parnell split of 1890 divided members, and by 1891 clubs and membership had collapsed. It survived because its parish clubs could continue independently, because clergy and Croke's patronage gave it authority, and because hurling and football had their own appeal. Its ban on foreign games and on members of the RIC and the British forces made it a symbol of Irish identity. By 1914 it was a large national organisation with a place in almost every parish.\n\nThe Gaelic League was founded in Dublin on 31 July 1893 by Douglas Hyde, Eoin MacNeill and others, following Hyde's lecture on the necessity for de-anglicising Ireland in November 1892. Hyde argued that Ireland was failing to be either English or Irish, and the League set out to revive the language. It was officially non-political and non-sectarian, and welcomed Catholics, Protestants, nationalists and unionists. Branches ran classes and sent travelling teachers into rural areas, so that there were several hundred branches by 1904. It organised feiseanna and, from 1897, the annual Oireachtas, and An Claidheamh Soluis, edited by Patrick Pearse from 1903, spread its ideas.\n\nThe League also won concrete gains. The Revised Programme of 1900 allowed Irish in primary schools, and the campaign to make Irish compulsory for matriculation to the National University succeeded in 1910, taking effect in 1913. St Patrick's Day processions, Irish names and Irish-language signs became common. Together with D.P. Moran's Irish Ireland movement and the literary revival of Yeats and Lady Gregory, it helped form a new sense of cultural identity. Yet it did not stop the decline of Irish as a spoken language, and Moran's narrower vision of a Gaelic and Catholic nation sat uneasily with Hyde's inclusive approach.\n\nThe most striking success of both was in preparing a generation of nationalists. Many future leaders, including Pearse and MacNeill, passed through the League, and the GAA's clubs and the IRB's presence within it provided networks for later activity. From about 1910 IRB members joined the League in growing numbers and pushed it towards politics, and Hyde resigned as president in 1915 when it declared for independence. In that sense their political impact, though unplanned, was large.\n\nOverall, both bodies succeeded greatly in their cultural aims, the GAA more completely than the League. The GAA made native games popular and permanent, while the League made the language respected and taught, though not widely spoken. Both gave Irish nationalism a cultural foundation that the political movement alone could not provide.",
