    "model": "Isabella Tod and Hanna Sheehy-Skeffington both contributed greatly to social reform in Ireland, but in different ways and in different generations. Tod, a Belfast Presbyterian and unionist, worked through petitions, societies and constitutional lobbying to improve women's education, property rights and political standing in the 1860s to 1890s. Sheehy-Skeffington, a Cork-born nationalist and feminist, carried the cause into the militant phase of the early twentieth century, demanding votes for women and equality within the new Ireland.\n\nTod's first great contribution was in education. She was a founder of the Ladies' Institute in Belfast in 1867, a society for promoting girls' education, and she worked closely with Margaret Byers of the Ladies' Collegiate School, later Victoria College. Tod and her allies lobbied so that girls were included in the Intermediate Education Act of 1878, which set up public examinations for boys and girls from 1879, and in the charter of the Royal University in 1879, which admitted women to its degrees on equal terms. These reforms opened secondary and university education to Irish girls, and they gave the next generation, Sheehy-Skeffington among them, the qualifications to enter public life. Her work was therefore foundational for all later progress.\n\nTod also campaigned for the legal rights of women. She took part in the campaigns that led to the Married Women's Property Acts of 1870 and 1882, which began to end the old common law rule that a married woman's property belonged to her husband. She also campaigned against the Contagious Diseases Acts, which she saw as unjust to women, and she co-founded the Belfast Women's Temperance Association in 1874, addressing social problems caused by drink. In this way she showed that women's welfare was a public matter that deserved reform through the law.\n\nHer third contribution was political. In the early 1870s she organised a women's suffrage society in Belfast, the first in Ulster, and she became a respected public speaker. She worked alongside Anna Haslam of Dublin, who founded the Dublin Women's Suffrage Association in 1876, and the early societies used petitions, drawing-room meetings and lobbying of MPs. Their efforts helped to bring women the municipal vote in 1887 and the right to vote and stand in poor law and district council elections in 1898. Her influence was limited, however, because she was a Liberal Unionist who opposed Home Rule in 1886 and helped found the Irish Women's Liberal Unionist Association, so she was less trusted among nationalists. Even so, she was a pioneer who broke the exclusion of women from Irish public debate.\n\nHanna Sheehy-Skeffington's contribution began with the founding of the Irish Women's Franchise League in Dublin in 1908, together with Margaret Cousins and others. The League demanded the vote for women on the same terms as men, and it was more radical than the older societies. It argued that no Home Rule settlement could be legitimate without votes for women, which was a challenge to Redmond and the Irish Party, who feared that a franchise bill would complicate Home Rule and who helped defeat the Conciliation Bill in March 1912. Her achievement was to make women's votes a central issue in Irish politics at the moment when the future of Ireland was being decided.\n\nShe also used direct action and publicity. In June 1912 she was arrested with other League members for breaking windows at government buildings in Dublin, and she was imprisoned. In 1913 she was jailed again and released after a hunger strike under the Cat and Mouse Act. The League published the weekly Irish Citizen from May 1912, which she later edited, and which ran until 1920, giving women a platform that drew writers from the pacifist and trade union movements. These tactics brought much hostility, and many nationalists and trade unionists thought them counter-productive, but they kept the suffrage question in the public eye and prepared the ground for the vote granted to women over thirty in 1918.\n\nSheehy-Skeffington's wider social reform was to insist that independence must include equality. She was a graduate of the Royal University with an MA in 1902, and she married Francis Sheehy-Skeffington, a feminist and pacifist, with whom she shared the cause. She criticised the Irish Party and later Cumann na mBan, which she saw as placing women in an auxiliary role to men. After her husband was shot without trial by Captain Bowen-Colthurst in April 1916 she campaigned for an inquiry and refused compensation, showing a commitment to justice beyond the suffrage question.\n\nOverall, both women were reformers of lasting importance. Tod's contribution was the more constitutional and foundational, in education, property rights and the first suffrage organisation in Ulster, while Sheehy-Skeffington's was the more radical, bringing militant methods, a newspaper and a feminist critique of nationalism. Both were limited by the divisions of nationalism and unionism, but together their work gave women education, legal protection, political experience and, in 1918, the parliamentary vote.",
