    "label": "1",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Sinn Féin rose and Home Rule declined between 1912 and 1918 because the Home Rule party's greatest achievement was undone by events it could not control, while Sinn Féin took the separatist cause, and then the anger caused by British actions, and turned them into votes. The main factors were the suspension of Home Rule, Redmond's support for the war, the Rising and the executions, the revival of Sinn Féin in 1917, the conscription crisis and the vote of 1918. Together they moved the centre of nationalist politics from Westminster to Dublin.\n\nThe first factor was the way the Third Home Rule Bill ended. Asquith introduced it on 11 April 1912, and because the Parliament Act of 1911 had cut the Lords' veto to a two-year delay, it passed the Commons in January 1913, July 1913 and May 1914, so that it could become law without the Lords. John Redmond's Irish Parliamentary Party had achieved what nationalists had sought for a generation. But unionists, led by Carson and Craig, signed the Ulster Covenant on 28 September 1912 and formed the UVF, and the Larne gun-running showed that the government would not use force against them. Asquith offered county option and Redmond reluctantly accepted temporary exclusion, which already looked like a concession on partition. When the Home Rule Act received royal assent on 18 September 1914, a Suspensory Act postponed it until the end of the war. A party whose whole case rested on delivering Home Rule was left with a law that did not operate, and with Ulster unresolved.\n\nThe second factor was Redmond's decision to tie his party to the British war effort. At Woodenbridge on 20 September 1914 he urged the Irish Volunteers to join the British army, and the movement split: about 170,000 followed him as the National Volunteers, but about 11,000 stayed with Eoin MacNeill. The cost grew as the war went on, with Irish regiments suffering at Gallipoli and on the Somme, while prices rose faster than wages for the urban poor. Separatists had a ready argument that Redmond was spending Irish lives for a Home Rule he could not obtain, and the Volunteers who had left him formed the core of a more radical movement.\n\nThe Easter Rising of 1916 and its aftermath were the turning point in opinion. The IRB's Military Council seized the GPO on 24 April and proclaimed a republic, and about 485 people died, mostly civilians, so many Dubliners at first jeered the prisoners and the IPP condemned the rebels. What changed this was British handling. Martial law, about 3,500 arrests and the shooting of fifteen leaders between 3 and 12 May, including the wounded Connolly, turned the rebels into martyrs. Bishop O'Dwyer of Limerick publicly criticised General Maxwell, and Lloyd George's attempt at a settlement failed in the summer of 1916 when unionist ministers insisted on permanent exclusion. Redmond looked powerless, and the prisoners released from Frongoch by Christmas 1916 and the amnesty of June 1917 gave the new movement its organisers.\n\nIn 1917 Sinn Féin became the vehicle for these feelings. Arthur Griffith's small party, which the British had wrongly blamed for the Rising, won a string of by-elections: Count Plunkett in North Roscommon in February, Joseph McGuinness in South Longford in May under the slogan 'Put him in to get him out', and de Valera in East Clare in July. The death of Thomas Ashe on forcible feeding in September 1917 brought huge crowds to his funeral, where Michael Collins spoke. At the Ard Fheis of October 1917 de Valera replaced Griffith as president, and the party adopted a compromise aim of an independent republic with the people free to choose their government afterwards. This brought in ex-Volunteers, the IRB, Gaelic Leaguers and younger priests, while Redmond's death on 6 March 1918 deprived the IPP of its leader.\n\nThe conscription crisis of 1918 completed the process. When the Military Service Act of 18 April extended conscription to Ireland, the IPP, Sinn Féin, Labour and the bishops united against it. The pledge signed at church doors on 21 April and the general strike of 23 April showed how much of the country was behind resistance. Conscription was never applied, but Sinn Féin took the credit, and the arrest of about 70 of its leaders in the German Plot allowed it to appear as a martyr again. The IPP, which had left the Commons in protest, no longer had any distinctive ground.\n\nThe result was seen in the general election of 14 December 1918. The franchise had been extended to all men over 21 and women over 30, so the electorate rose from about 700,000 to about 1.9 million, many of them young first-time voters shaped by the Rising and conscription. Sinn Féin won 73 of the 105 seats, and 25 were uncontested, while the IPP fell to six seats, kept mainly through a local pact with Sinn Féin. The First Dáil then met on 21 January 1919 and declared independence.\n\nI conclude that the decline of Home Rule and the rise of Sinn Féin were two sides of one process. The suspension of the Act and Redmond's war policy discredited the constitutional party, but it was the Rising's executions, the conscription threat and the enlarged electorate that gave Sinn Féin its mandate. Without British mistakes after 1916 the party would probably have remained small.",
