    "label": "2",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Éamon de Valera's leadership was far less effective in 1921 to 1923, when his decisions contributed to a split and a civil war that he could not control, than in 1939 to 1945, when his cautious and firm direction of neutrality kept Ireland out of the war and strengthened the state. In the first period he was a divisive figure who lost; in the second he was the unquestioned leader at his strongest.\n\nIn the Treaty negotiations his decision to stay in Dublin was his most criticised act. He had met Lloyd George four times in July 1921, and his proposal of 'external association', which would make Ireland a republic linked to the Commonwealth with the Crown only as head of the association, had been a skilful attempt to avoid both the Crown and a break. But when the conference opened in October he sent Griffith, Collins, Barton, Gavan Duffy and Duggan to London as plenipotentiaries, saying that as head of state he should be held in reserve and that he had to keep the more extreme republicans on side. Collins and others thought it a mistake, since the man with the most authority and experience of Lloyd George was absent. The delegates' credentials gave them full power, but their instructions told them to report to Dublin before signing, and this contradiction allowed each side to blame the other afterwards.\n\nThe Treaty was signed on 6 December 1921, and de Valera's response deepened the problem. He had not been told in advance and was angry, and when the cabinet divided four to three he, with Brugha and Stack, opposed it and issued a press statement against it before the Dáil debated it. He then presented his own Document No. 2, based on external association, which was never put to the British and differed little in substance from the Treaty in the eyes of many. The Dáil approved the Treaty on 7 January 1922 and he resigned as President. His attempt to give the Dáil an alternative failed, and the argument had turned from policy to a question of whose authority counted.\n\nDuring the Civil War his leadership was weak and mainly symbolic. The pact he made with Collins on 20 May 1922 collapsed when Collins repudiated it before the election of 16 June, in which pro-Treaty candidates won 58 seats to 36. When fighting began after the shelling of the Four Courts on 28 June, he did not command the anti-Treaty IRA, which was led by Liam Lynch and Rory O'Connor, and the National Army, using British artillery, took the towns by the end of August. Collins and Griffith died in August, executions followed, and the war ended when Frank Aiken ordered the arms dump on 24 May 1923, with a message of support from de Valera. By then about 80 anti-Treaty prisoners had been executed, bitterness had been created for generations, and his side had been defeated.\n\nThe contrast with World War II is sharp, because there his judgement and control served the state well. Neutrality was a decision he made and held. He declared an emergency on 2 September 1939, and the Emergency Powers Act gave ministers wide control of prices, supplies and censorship, with Frank Aiken in charge of censorship. He saw neutrality as proof of sovereignty, and Britain's return of the Treaty ports in 1938, his own achievement, made it possible. Fianna Fáil, Fine Gael and Labour all accepted it, which had been unthinkable after the Civil War.\n\nHe also resisted heavy pressure. In June 1940 Malcolm MacDonald brought him an offer of a declaration of principle for a united Ireland if Ireland joined the Allies, and he rejected it in July because he doubted Britain could deliver and the Northern government had not been consulted. In February 1944 he refused the American Note asking him to expel the German and Japanese diplomats before D-Day. Churchill's victory broadcast of 13 May 1945 attacked Irish neutrality, and de Valera's reply on Radio Éireann on 16 May, which said that a great power might justify infringing a small state's rights, was widely praised. His practical management was also effective: the IRA was banned, the Offences Against the State Amendment Act of 1940 introduced internment, and the army doubled to about 40,000, with the Local Defence Force set up in June 1940. Seán Lemass's Department of Supplies organised rationing, compulsory tillage and Irish Shipping.\n\nThere were weaknesses. His condolence visit to the German minister Hempel after Hitler's death in May 1945 was criticised even by many Irish people, and censorship and the Wages Standstill Order of 1941 fell hardest on the poor, whose real incomes fell. Neutrality was also benevolent towards the Allies, with shared intelligence and the Blacksod weather reports, so it was less rigid than it looked.\n\nOverall, de Valera's leadership was ineffective in 1921 to 1923, since he failed to keep the independence movement united or to prevent civil war, but effective in 1939 to 1945, when he used his authority to protect Irish independence.",
