    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "After partition, both governments tried to promote a cultural identity that matched their own community, and education, language and religion were the main means. In the South the aim was a Gaelic and Catholic Ireland, built through compulsory Irish, church-run schools and moral legislation. In the North the Unionist government promoted a Protestant and British identity, while nationalists defended Irish and Catholic culture through their own schools and organisations, and the result in both states was to strengthen a separate identity at the expense of minorities.\n\nLanguage was the centre of cultural policy in the Free State. Irish was made compulsory in national schools in 1922, and many infant classes were taught wholly through it. A pass in Irish became necessary for State examinations at second level and for entry to the civil service and the Garda, and Ernest Blythe, the Minister for Finance, pressed the policy hard. The Gaeltacht Commission of 1925 to 1926 defined the Irish-speaking districts, An Gúm was founded in 1926 to publish books in Irish, and the Taibhdhearc theatre opened in Galway in 1928. Preparatory colleges trained teachers to teach through Irish, and the 1937 constitution declared it the first official language. The results were mixed. The number of people who claimed to speak Irish rose from about 18 per cent in 1926 to about a quarter in 1936, but this was mainly the product of schooling, the Gaeltacht continued to shrink through emigration and poverty, and many parents and teachers resented compulsion.\n\nEducation and religion were closely linked in the South. The Church managed most national schools, with clergy as managers, while the state paid salaries and set the curriculum. Secondary schools were mostly fee-paying and run by religious orders. The School Attendance Act of 1926 made school compulsory from 6 to 14 and the Vocational Education Act of 1930 provided technical education, but the state accepted the Church's role, and the 1937 constitution recognised the special position of the Catholic Church in Article 44. Catholic teaching also shaped the law. The Censorship of Films Act of 1923 and the Censorship of Publications Act of 1929 allowed the banning of films and books, and Samuel Beckett was among those banned. Divorce was blocked by the Dáil's change of standing orders in 1925, when W.B. Yeats defended the Protestant minority in the Seanad, and Article 41 of the constitution banned it. The Criminal Law Amendment Act of 1935 banned the sale of contraceptives. Critics such as Seán Ó Faoláin saw a narrow and conformist vision, and the Protestant population fell.\n\nThe state also supported culture in a wider sense. The Tailteann Games were held in 1924, 1928 and 1932, the Abbey Theatre received a state subsidy from 1925, the radio station 2RN began broadcasting on 1 January 1926, and the Irish Folklore Commission was set up in 1935, followed by the Schools' Collection of 1937 to 1938. The GAA's Rule 27 banned its members from playing foreign games and in 1938 it removed Douglas Hyde as patron for attending a soccer match, which showed how cultural nationalism enforced a boundary.\n\nIn Northern Ireland, education was the main arena. The Lynn Committee reported in 1923 and the Londonderry Act of that year set up county education committees and aimed for public elementary schools free of church control. Protestant churches objected to the exclusion of religious instruction, and the Act was amended in 1925 to allow Bible instruction, with the Orange Order and Protestant clergy gaining a voice in management. The Catholic Church refused to transfer its schools, so they stayed outside the system with only partial public funding, and the amendment of 1930 gave transferred Protestant schools a majority of transferor representatives on their committees. The outcome was a divided school system that deepened segregation.\n\nUnionist cultural identity was supported by the state, in the form of Orange parades on 12 July, the Union Jack and the royal ceremonies of Stormont. Nationalists supported the GAA, Irish classes and Catholic schools, but Irish received little state support, and the Special Powers Act allowed the authorities to ban processions and republican emblems, so that nationalist parades were more likely to be banned. Craig's declaration in 1934 that Northern Ireland was a Protestant state was the counterpart of the Catholic tone of the 1937 constitution.\n\nIn conclusion, the attempts to promote identity succeeded in giving each state a distinct character, and institutions like An Gúm, the Abbey and Radio Éireann preserved real culture. But compulsory Irish did not make it the language of daily life, and in both states the policy of promoting one tradition left a minority feeling excluded.",
