    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "From the life and work of Domhnall Mac Amhlaigh I learned that emigration in the 1950s was an economic necessity for young Irishmen with poor prospects, but that it brought hard manual work, loneliness and exile alongside wages, comradeship and a surviving Irish identity. His diary is the best-known first-hand account of Irish navvy life in post-war Britain, and it shows emigration from the inside, as a participant rather than an observer.\n\nMac Amhlaigh's own life showed the usual cause of emigration. He was born in Galway in 1926 and raised in Co. Kilkenny, and he was a native of the west who wrote in Irish. Facing poor prospects at home he went to England in 1951, which was the time when emigration from the state was heading for its peak. About 400,000 people left between 1951 and 1961, most for Britain, and the Commission on Emigration of 1954 blamed low incomes, unemployment and small farms. His story is therefore typical of a generation that left because the Irish economy offered too little, until the reforms of Whitaker and Lemass brought growth in the 1960s.\n\nHis work taught me what the emigrant's life was like. In England he worked as a labourer on building sites and roads, based in Northampton, the typical job of Irish men in Britain, where they worked as navvies on roads, building sites and power stations during post-war reconstruction. His diary of 1951 to 1957, Dialann Deoraí, was published in 1960, and Valentin Iremonger translated it as An Irish Navvy: The Diary of an Exile in 1964. It describes the work, the lodgings, the pubs and dance halls, and the company of other Irish navvies. This shows how many young men lived in lodging houses and camps with long hours and hard drinking, and often without much job security.\n\nHe also showed the emotional side of emigration. The title itself, an exile's diary, expresses homesickness and the sense of being banished from home, a view later associated with historians such as Kerby Miller. Yet the diary also records a mixture of welcome and prejudice, so it shows that the Irish met both hostility and friendliness in England, in a period when notices saying No Irish were remembered from lodging-house windows. At the same time it shows a community that held together, because Irish navvies socialised in Irish pubs and dance halls and kept their identity in an Irish world inside English cities. That fits Enda Delaney's argument that emigrants to Britain kept strong ties with home while taking part in British working life.\n\nMac Amhlaigh's life and writing also show the cultural cost of emigration. He wrote in Irish, and Irish-speaking emigrants from the Gaeltacht tended to lose the language within a generation abroad, so his diary, together with his novels and short stories such as Schnitzer Ó Sé in 1974 and his journalism, is a sign of an effort to preserve Irish culture overseas. He stayed in England for the rest of his life and died in Northampton in 1989, which shows that many emigrants who meant to return never did.\n\nFinally, his work taught me about how Irish society viewed emigration. Contemporaries in Ireland valued the diary as a frank picture of a life that official Ireland preferred not to discuss, which shows that emigration was a subject people avoided. Historians now treat it as a key source on the Irish in post-war Britain, though, as the work of a single author, it cannot stand for every emigrant's experience. Overall, I learned from Mac Amhlaigh that emigration was driven by economic need, involved hard labour, loneliness and prejudice, and yet produced a lasting and distinctly Irish community in Britain.",
