    "label": "2",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "When Seán Lemass became Taoiseach in June 1959 he faced an economy that had stagnated under protection, mass emigration, dependence on Britain, a threatening move to free trade and the unresolved problem of partition. He responded vigorously and, on the whole, effectively, since growth and confidence returned, although some problems were left unsolved and his later targets proved too ambitious.\n\nThe central problem was economic stagnation. The protection that Lemass himself had begun in the 1930s with tariffs and the Control of Manufactures Acts had created many small factories selling only in a home market too small to support efficient industry, and the rule that Irish citizens must hold most shares discouraged foreign firms. Exports were mostly live cattle and farm produce sold to Britain, so the economy depended on a single customer. Emigration was the human result: net emigration in the 1950s was about 400,000, the population was falling and western Europe was booming.\n\nLemass's response was to carry through the new policy of the First Programme for Economic Expansion. It grew out of T.K. Whitaker's Economic Development of 1958 and ran from 1959 to 1963 with the aim of raising output and halting emigration. Foreign ownership was opened up, Export Profits Tax Relief was extended, the IDA gave grants to new firms with the best terms in the west, and the Shannon Free Airport Development Company built an industrial estate to attract foreign industry. The target of about 2 per cent growth a year was deliberately modest, and the result was growth of about 4 per cent a year, double the target. Net emigration fell to about 80,000 in 1961-66 from about 200,000 in 1956-61, and the 1966 census recorded a rising population of about 2.88 million for the first time since the early 1950s. Fianna Fáil regained an overall majority in 1965. Sceptical historians point out that the European boom helped and that the Inter-Party governments had begun some measures, but the change in confidence was real.\n\nA second problem was dependence on Britain and exclusion from the new European market. Lemass applied to join the EEC on 31 July 1961, nine days before Britain, but de Gaulle's veto of British entry on 14 January 1963 stopped negotiations. Lemass then turned to Britain and signed the Anglo-Irish Free Trade Agreement on 14 December 1965, which removed tariffs over ten years and kept access for farm exports. He also set up the Committee on Industrial Organisation and adaptation councils in 1963 to prepare industries for competition, and Ireland joined GATT in 1967. These policies exposed protected firms in textiles and footwear, and many small firms closed in the following decade, so there was a cost, but he accepted it in the belief that protected industries could not survive in the long run.\n\nHe also had to manage the relationship between employers, unions and government. His practical, businesslike style produced a closer relationship with the unions and the national wage rounds, in which the parties bargained over general pay rises. Yet the Second Programme of 1963, aiming for 4 per cent growth to 1970, was abandoned in 1967 after wage rises and a balance of payments deficit, and Whitaker had been more cautious than Lemass about its targets. This shows that his response was not fully effective, since inflation and the balance of payments remained fragile and farming and the west lagged behind.\n\nThe final problem was partition and the attitude of the state to Northern Ireland. Lemass eased the old hostility: he visited Terence O'Neill at Stormont on 14 January 1965 and O'Neill came to Dublin on 9 February, the first meetings of the leaders of the two parts of Ireland since partition. He also used Telefís Éireann to explain policy and to project the image of a modern state. These meetings changed the tone but did not settle the constitutional question, and within a few years the Troubles had begun.\n\nOverall, Lemass dealt effectively with the economic problems that mattered most, ended protection, welcomed foreign investment, reversed emigration and prepared the way for Europe. Biographers rate him a modernizer who changed the tone of Irish politics, but they note that rural poverty, urban housing and partition were left unresolved, so his success was substantial rather than complete.",
