    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Television and the wider communications revolution changed Irish life profoundly between 1949 and 1989. They ended the isolation on which traditional authority had rested, opened the country to outside ideas, created a national forum for debate about sex, religion and politics, and brought the state into open conflict with a medium it could not fully control.\n\nTelefís Éireann began broadcasting on 31 December 1961 under the Broadcasting Authority Act 1960, which set up the RTÉ Authority to run radio and television. President de Valera warned at the opening that television had great power for good and evil, and within a few years transmitters carried it to most of the country. By the early 1970s most households owned a set. Television therefore reached a mass audience quickly, and it altered how people spent their evenings. They stayed at home to watch rather than go to dances or visit neighbours, and rural communities, which had been cut off from national and international life, now saw the same news, sport and entertainment as city people.\n\nThe Late Late Show, first broadcast in July 1962 and presented by Gay Byrne until 1999, became a national forum. It began as a summer filler but discussed topics rarely spoken of in public, including sex, marriage, contraception, unmarried mothers and the church. In 1966 a married woman, Eileen Fox, said on air that she had not worn a nightdress on her honeymoon night. The episode known as the Bishop and the Nightie caused uproar. Bishop Thomas Ryan of Clonfert condemned the programme, but many viewers defended Byrne. The row showed that television could provoke public argument about morality and the limits of church authority, which censorship had kept out of print. Byrne generally presented himself as a neutral host, but his willingness to air taboo subjects had a liberalising effect, and the contraceptive train of 1971 also drew attention through television coverage.\n\nTelevision also affected rural life, education and politics. The Riordans, a drama about a farming family that began in 1965, was a hit in the countryside because it dealt with real issues of farming life. Telefís Scoile, which began on 4 February 1964, broadcast lessons to secondary pupils and was among the earliest schools television services in Europe. Politicians had to learn to use the medium, and party leaders now spoke directly to voters, so campaigns began to focus on television appearances and image. The church worried about the effects of television on faith and morals, though it also used the medium to broadcast Mass, the Angelus and religious programmes. Reactions varied. Many welcomed a window on the world, while some clergy and traditionalists saw a threat to Irish values.\n\nCurrent affairs brought the medium into conflict with government. Seven Days, launched in 1966, used young producers to investigate housing, poverty and emigration, and ministers resented its criticism. A programme on illegal moneylending in 1969 led to a government-ordered inquiry and a row about independence. Lemass saw RTÉ as an instrument of public policy, while journalists held that a broadcaster served the public only if it was free to criticise. The Troubles sharpened the dispute. On 1 October 1971 Gerry Collins issued the first order under Section 31, and after the interview with Seán Mac Stíofáin on This Week in November 1972 he dismissed the whole RTÉ Authority. Section 31 restrictions lasted until 1994. Television thus raised the question of whether a state broadcaster could report freely.\n\nOther changes reinforced this. The number of cars rose several times over between 1960 and 1980, and better roads, buses and air travel reduced rural isolation. Telephone services were improved and Bord Telecom Éireann was set up in 1984. RTÉ 2 started in 1978, adding choice, and British channels reached much of the east and north. The Irish Times under Douglas Gageby from 1963 became a forum for new ideas, and Magill, founded in 1977, expanded political journalism. Pirate radio challenged RTÉ's monopoly in the 1970s and 1980s, leading to the Radio and Television Act 1988 licensing independent local radio. Computers spread in business and government, and American firms such as Digital opened plants, laying the base for an electronics industry. Radio na Gaeltachta, which began in 1972, also gave Irish speakers a service in their own language.\n\nOverall, television and the wider communications revolution transformed Irish life. They exposed people to wider ideas and lifestyles, weakened the isolation on which the authority of church and state had depended, and made public debate a national habit. Historians such as Robert Savage argue that television both reflected changes already under way and helped to speed them, alongside education, emigration and EEC membership, and that its relationship with the state was complicated. Its impact was greatest on attitudes to religion, sexuality and authority.",
