    "model": "Mary Robinson and Sylvia Meehan both contributed greatly to Irish life in the 1970s and 1980s by working to end legal and economic discrimination against women. Robinson did so as a lawyer, senator and campaigner who used the courts and the Oireachtas, and Meehan as a trade unionist who turned the equality laws into practice at the Employment Equality Agency. Together they helped create the basis for the social change of the 1990s.\n\nMary Robinson was born in Ballina, Co. Mayo, in 1944 and graduated from Trinity College Dublin. She was called to the bar in 1967, studied at Harvard and became Reid Professor of Law at Trinity at the age of 25. This gave her the legal expertise and public standing to challenge discriminatory law. In 1969 she was elected to the Seanad for the University of Dublin, and she served as an independent until 1989. She was therefore the most prominent woman in public life outside government at a time when very few women sat in the Dáil.\n\nIn the Oireachtas Robinson introduced private members' bills in the early 1970s to liberalise the law on contraception, which the Seanad refused to debate, and she later sought reform of the law on homosexuality. Neither became law at the time, but she placed the issues on the public agenda and showed that reform was possible. She campaigned for women to serve on juries, a change enacted in the Juries Act 1976. As a barrister she took cases against the State on behalf of individuals, including a tax case brought by a married couple, which the Supreme Court decided in their favour in 1980, and the case of David Norris against the law criminalising homosexual acts. She joined the Labour Party in 1976 and left it in 1985 in protest at the Anglo-Irish Agreement, and she served as a Dublin city councillor from 1979 to 1983. Conservatives and the church attacked her views, but she was respected for her persistence.\n\nSylvia Meehan was born in Dublin in 1929 and worked as a teacher. She became vice-president of the Association of Secondary Teachers, Ireland and chairperson of the women's committee of the Irish Congress of Trade Unions. Her union work gave her a platform at a time when women were paid less than men for similar work and the marriage bar forced women to resign from public service jobs on marriage. The reforms she later enforced followed the Commission on the Status of Women, chaired by Thekla Beere, which reported in 1972 with 49 recommendations including equal pay.\n\nThe Anti-Discrimination (Pay) Act 1974 required equal pay for the same or similar work, and the Employment Equality Act 1977 made it unlawful to discriminate on grounds of sex or marital status in recruitment, training and promotion. In 1977 Meehan was appointed the first chairperson of the new Employment Equality Agency, a post she held until 1992. The agency promoted equality, investigated complaints and advised the government. Under her leadership it supervised research, investigations and advice on discrimination at work, and its research into the position of girls and women in education, pay, maternity leave and childcare helped to shape later legislation such as the Maternity Protection of Employees Act 1981. She was regarded as an effective and determined advocate, although employers and some politicians resisted the agency's findings and its powers were limited.\n\nBoth women contributed in different but complementary ways. Robinson challenged discriminatory law through the courts and the Seanad, while Meehan made the new equality laws work in practice in the workplace. Their efforts had limits, since women remained scarce in politics and senior jobs, pay gaps persisted and the church kept much of its influence. Even so, historians credit Robinson with creating a legal and political basis for reform that prepared the way for the changes of the 1990s and for her election as President in 1990, and they see Meehan as a key figure in turning the equality laws of the 1970s into reality for working women.",
