    "label": "2",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Bismarck contributed more to his country's unity than Mazzini. Mazzini was the great prophet of Italian nationalism, and he inspired a generation, but he never achieved a united state by his own methods and rejected the one that emerged in 1861. Bismarck, by contrast, directed the wars and diplomacy that created the German Empire in 1871.\n\nMazzini's contribution was real, and it must be given its due. Born in Genoa in 1805, he joined the Carbonari and was arrested and exiled in 1830. In 1831 he founded Young Italy at Marseille, a society of young men who swore to work for an independent, united republic, and its motto was God and the People. He believed that the Italian people must free themselves by their own insurrection and education, not by relying on foreign powers or kings, and in The Duties of Man he stressed duty to the nation. By spreading the idea of a united Italy among students, professionals and exiles, and by founding Young Europe in 1834, he gave the Risorgimento its ideals.\n\nHowever, Mazzini's practical record was one of failure. His invasion of Savoy in 1834 was easily defeated, and his risings were crushed by Austrian troops. In 1848-49 he became one of the triumvirs of the Roman Republic, but French troops sent by Louis-Napoleon captured Rome in July 1849 and restored the Pope. His republicanism also divided the nationalist movement. Cavour and the monarchists disliked him, and from 1857 the Italian National Society backed Piedmont's leadership instead of his. When Italy was unified in 1861 under Victor Emmanuel II, it was a monarchy, which Mazzini rejected as a betrayal. He was arrested in Sicily in 1870 trying to start a republican rising, and died in 1872 with Rome already won by others.\n\nBismarck, in contrast, delivered unity in a decade. Appointed Minister-President of Prussia in September 1862 to break the deadlock between King William I and the liberals over army reform, he declared that the great questions of the day would be settled by blood and iron. He carried through Roon's army reform without an approved budget, which gave Prussia the military power to act. He then managed three wars with diplomatic skill. In 1864 Prussia and Austria together defeated Denmark and took Schleswig, Holstein and Lauenburg, and the Convention of Gastein of 1865 set up the quarrel that followed.\n\nThe decisive step was the war of 1866. Bismarck had secured Russian goodwill, French neutrality after meeting Napoleon III at Biarritz, and an alliance with Italy. Prussia's needle-gun, Moltke's planning and rapid mobilisation by rail won the battle of Königgrätz on 3 July. By the Treaty of Prague the German Confederation was dissolved and Austria excluded from German affairs, and the North German Confederation formed in 1867 under Prussian leadership with a Reichstag elected by universal male suffrage. Bismarck also persuaded the king to spare Austria humiliation, which showed his careful statecraft.\n\nThe final stage was the Franco-Prussian War. When Leopold of Hohenzollern's candidacy for the Spanish throne alarmed France, Bismarck edited the Ems telegram in July 1870 so that it seemed an insult to France, and France declared war. The southern states, Bavaria, Baden and Württemberg, joined Prussia, the French were defeated at Sedan on 2 September 1870, and the German Empire was proclaimed at Versailles on 18 January 1871 with William I as emperor. Bismarck had brought the south into a united Germany, something no nationalist movement had achieved.\n\nIt can be argued in Mazzini's defence that Bismarck's unity was imposed from above, left Austria's Germans outside and gave Prussia dominance, while Mazzini's ideals of popular nationhood were more generous. It is also true that Italy's actual unification came through Cavour, Napoleon III and Garibaldi rather than through Mazzini himself. But the question asks who contributed more to his country's move toward unity, and the answer is clear. Mazzini created an ideal and an atmosphere, but Bismarck created the state. Without Bismarck there was no German Empire in 1871, whereas Italian unity was achieved by others, and so Bismarck made the greater contribution.",
