    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Between 1815 and 1870 both agriculture and industry in Europe were transformed, though at different speeds. Britain led in both, while France and the German lands followed more slowly and in different ways. The key changes were the commercialisation of farming, the arrival of the factory, steam power and the railway, and the spread of industrialisation from Britain to the continent.\n\nIn Britain the key change in agriculture was the completion of a long agricultural revolution. Enclosure replaced open fields and common land with consolidated farms, mostly through thousands of Acts of Parliament passed between 1750 and 1850, and the General Enclosure Acts of 1801 and 1845 simplified the procedure. New methods raised output: the Norfolk four-course rotation of wheat, turnips, barley and clover kept soil fertile without fallow, Jethro Tull's seed drill sowed in rows, and Robert Bakewell's selective breeding produced better livestock. After 1815 the Corn Laws protected British grain growers from cheap imports. Farming had become a commercial business producing for growing towns.\n\nThese changes had harsh social effects. Mechanisation, notably the threshing machine, reduced the need for hand labour, and anger over it helped to cause the Swing Riots of 1830 in southern England. Many labourers lost their common rights and drifted to the towns or emigrated. From the 1840s fertilisers such as Peruvian guano and John Bennet Lawes's superphosphate, produced from 1842, raised yields further. Agriculture therefore fed a larger urban population but also released the labour that industry needed.\n\nOn the continent agricultural change followed a different path. In France the Revolution of 1789 had abolished feudal dues and divided many estates, so the peasantry owned much of the land by 1815 and farming remained small-scale. In Prussia the Stein–Hardenberg reforms from 1807 abolished serfdom in stages and let peasants become owners, though many had to surrender part of their land to the nobles, while the Junker estates of the east were modernised and grew grain for export. Yet agriculture remained vulnerable: the potato blight from 1845 and poor harvests in 1846 and 1847 caused hunger across western and central Europe and helped to create the conditions for the revolutions of 1848.\n\nIn industry the leading change was the factory system built on coal, iron, cotton and steam. James Watt's improvements to the steam engine in the 1760s and 1770s drove pumps and machinery, and Arkwright's water frame and Crompton's mule made Britain the world's leading exporter of cotton goods. Railways were the great transforming force of this period. The Liverpool and Manchester Railway opened in 1830, the first steam-hauled passenger line with a timetable, following Stephenson's Rocket at Rainhill in 1829, and the network expanded rapidly in the 1840s. Bessemer's converter of 1856 made steel cheaply, and the Great Exhibition of 1851 showed Britain as the workshop of the world.\n\nIndustrialisation then spread to the continent. Belgium was first, with the coalfields of Hainaut and Liège. France industrialised more slowly, with textiles in Alsace and Normandy and a railway network built mainly from the 1840s after the Railway Law of 1842. In the German lands the first railway ran from Nuremberg to Fürth in 1835, the Ruhr and Silesian coalfields grew rapidly, and Krupp's steel works at Essen expanded from the 1850s. The Zollverein, in effect from 1 January 1834, removed internal tariffs and gave Prussia an economic lead. The human cost was great: workers laboured twelve to fourteen hours a day in British cotton mills, Manchester grew from about 75,000 people in 1801 to about 300,000 in 1851, and the Factory Act of 1833 and Ten Hours Act of 1847 only partly eased conditions.\n\nOverall, the most important changes were the commercialisation of farming, the release of labour from the land and the rise of the factory, steam power and railways, first in Britain and then across the continent. Agriculture changed more gradually and unevenly, while industry changed the scale and pace of life across Europe by 1870.",
