    "label": "2",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Bismarck was highly effective as a leader abroad and more mixed at home between 1871 and 1890. In foreign policy he kept Germany at peace and secure for nearly twenty years and prevented the hostile coalition he feared, while at home he created a unified state and a pioneering welfare system, but his campaigns against Catholics and socialists failed and left a weak parliamentary tradition.\n\nThe Empire he governed gave him great power. The Kaiser appointed and dismissed the Chancellor, and the Chancellor was responsible to the Kaiser rather than to the Reichstag, which was elected by universal male suffrage but could not appoint or dismiss ministers. Bismarck held office from 1871 to 1890 and used his control to build a national state, with a common currency in the mark in 1873 and a central bank, the Reichsbank. Until 1878 he relied on the National Liberals, who supported free trade and unity, and this gave Germany a single national market in which industry grew rapidly.\n\nHis domestic record was weaker where he tried to defeat groups he called enemies of the Reich. The Kulturkampf against the Catholic Church began with the pulpit law of 1871 and the expulsion of the Jesuits in 1872, and the May Laws of 1873 put priests' training and appointment under state control. Bishops were imprisoned or exiled, but Catholics resisted, and the Centre Party doubled its seats and became the second largest party. Bismarck needed its votes by 1878 and 1879 and negotiated with the new Pope, Leo XIII, so most of the laws were repealed or relaxed in the 1880s. He then turned on the socialists, using the attempts on the life of Wilhelm I in 1878 to pass the Anti-Socialist Law, which banned their organisations, meetings and newspapers. Yet socialists could still stand for the Reichstag, and by 1890 the SPD had won about 1.4 million votes and 35 seats.\n\nHis economic and social policies were more successful. The tariff of 1879 protected German iron, steel and agriculture, ended his alliance with the National Liberals and brought in the Conservatives and the Centre. To win workers from socialism he introduced sickness insurance in 1883, accident insurance in 1884 and old age and invalidity insurance in 1889, making Germany the first major state with such a scheme. The measures were limited in coverage and did not reduce support for the SPD, but they were a lasting innovation that other states copied. Historians such as Wehler argue that his negative integration of the Reich against internal enemies left Germany divided and unreformed, so his domestic success was real but partial.\n\nAbroad, his achievement was greater. After the Treaty of Frankfurt in 1871 took Alsace and part of Lorraine from France, he declared Germany satisfied and set out to keep France without allies. The Three Emperors' League of 1873 linked Germany, Austria-Hungary and Russia, and when the Balkan crisis strained it Bismarck chaired the Congress of Berlin in 1878 as an honest broker and avoided a general war. He then signed the Dual Alliance with Austria-Hungary in October 1879, extended it to Italy in the Triple Alliance of 1882, and when the Three Emperors' League collapsed over Bulgaria he signed the secret Reinsurance Treaty with Russia in June 1887, so that Germany stayed on terms with Russia even as it was allied with Austria-Hungary. He also encouraged the Mediterranean Agreements of 1887 to tie Britain loosely to the alliance powers.\n\nThere were weaknesses in this system. It was complex and depended on Bismarck himself, and the Reinsurance Treaty and the Dual Alliance pulled in different directions. Russians felt cheated at Berlin in 1878, and his ban on Russian bonds in 1887 pushed Russia to borrow from France. His colonial claims in 1884 and 1885, including South West Africa, Togoland and the Cameroons, were pursued mainly for their diplomatic value, and he said his map of Africa lay in Europe. When Wilhelm II dismissed him in 1890 the Reinsurance Treaty lapsed and Russia moved towards France.\n\nOn balance Bismarck was highly effective abroad, since Germany fought no war after 1871 and avoided the two-front war he feared, but only partly effective at home, where he built a state and a welfare system but failed to break Catholics or socialists and left a weak Reichstag. His fall in March 1890 showed the limits of a system that depended on one man.",
