    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Wilfred Owen made the most important contribution to the literature of the First World War, because he turned the experience of the trenches into poetry that exposed the suffering of soldiers and rejected the patriotic view of the war. Writing as a serving officer who had suffered shell shock, he gave the war its most memorable poetic witness, although nearly all of his work was published after his death.\n\nOwen was born in Oswestry, Shropshire, in 1893, the eldest child of a railway official, and worked as a lay assistant to a vicar and as a teacher in France before the war. He enlisted in the Artists Rifles in October 1915 and was commissioned in the Manchester Regiment in 1916. He fought on the Somme and near Beaumont-Hamel in January 1917, and his own experiences, including time in a shell-hole after concussion, gave his later poems their authority. He was diagnosed with neurasthenia and sent to Craiglockhart War Hospital near Edinburgh in June 1917.\n\nCraiglockhart was the turning point in his career as a poet. There he met Siegfried Sassoon in August 1917. Sassoon, who had been sent to the hospital instead of being court-martialled for his statement against the prolongation of the war, read Owen's drafts, advised him to write plainly and introduced him to literary circles, including Robert Graves. Between autumn 1917 and 1918 Owen wrote or finished his best poems, including Dulce et Decorum Est, Anthem for Doomed Youth, Strange Meeting, Futility and Exposure.\n\nHis first contribution was the honesty with which he described what war did to men. Dulce et Decorum Est shows soldiers drunk with fatigue and a man drowning in gas, and it attacks the old Lie that it is sweet and fitting to die for one's country. This was a direct challenge to the idealism of poets such as Rupert Brooke, whose 1914 sonnets, including The Soldier, celebrated dying for England and who had been hailed as a hero when he died on the way to Gallipoli in 1915. Owen did not accept that view, and he used graphic detail of gas, mud and death to replace it with the reality of the trenches.\n\nHis second contribution was in his craft. Owen used pararhyme, the use of consonance in place of full rhyme, which gave his verse a sense of unease and incompleteness suited to his subject. Anthem for Doomed Youth took the form of a sonnet, a form linked to the traditions of English poetry, and used it to mourn the dead with the sounds of guns and rifles in place of church bells. Critics such as Jon Stallworthy have stressed his skill, and his draft preface says that the poetry is in the pity, which sums up the compassion that gives his poems their emotional power.\n\nOwen returned to the front in 1918, won the Military Cross for his actions near Joncourt in October, and was killed on 4 November 1918 in an attack on the Sambre-Oise canal, a week before the armistice, aged 25. Only a few poems appeared in his lifetime. Sassoon and Edith Sitwell published a first edition in 1919 and 1920, and Edmund Blunden produced a fuller edition in 1931. In the 1920s Owen was one of several war poets, but interest grew from the 1930s as pacifist feeling spread, and he came to be read alongside Graves and Remarque in shaping how the war was remembered.\n\nHistorians now regard Owen as the leading English voice of the war's suffering, though some note that the poets' war view of 1914 to 1918 reflects the educated officer class and that many soldiers did not see the war as he did. Even so, his contribution was decisive: he gave the war a lasting voice of protest and pity, wrote with the authority of a participant, and made poetry a means of telling the truth about it.",
