    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The Second Vatican Council and Pope John Paul II both changed the Catholic Church, but in different ways. The Council of 1962-65 reformed worship, opened the Church to other faiths and gave the laity a larger role, while John Paul II, pope from 1978, reasserted traditional teaching and gave the papacy a new international and political influence, especially in Eastern Europe.\n\nPope John XXIII, elected on 28 October 1958 at the age of 76 and seen as a caretaker, announced the Council on 25 January 1959 and spoke of aggiornamento, the updating of the Church. The Church faced falling practice in Western Europe, secularisation and division among Christians, and he wanted a pastoral Council to renew its teaching. The Council opened on 11 October 1962 with about 2,500 bishops and observers from other churches. In the first session the bishops rejected the drafts prepared by the Roman Curia, which gave the Council its own direction. John XXIII died in June 1963, but his successor Paul VI kept it going until it closed on 8 December 1965.\n\nThe most visible change was in worship. Sacrosanctum Concilium of 1963 allowed local languages in the Mass, and the new Roman Missal of 1969-70 completed the reform, so the Mass was said in the vernacular and the priest faced the congregation. Lay Catholics were encouraged to take a more active part, reading the Scriptures and serving as ministers. Lumen Gentium described the Church as the people of God and gave more weight to the laity and to the collective role of bishops, an idea known as collegiality, while Gaudium et Spes addressed the Church in the modern world, including social justice, marriage and peace.\n\nThe Council also transformed the Church's relations with others. Unitatis Redintegratio committed the Church to ecumenism, Nostra Aetate rejected the charge of collective Jewish guilt for the death of Christ and became the basis for Catholic-Jewish dialogue, and Dignitatis Humanae accepted religious liberty, reversing earlier teaching and ending the ideal of the confessional state. The results were mixed. Archbishop Lefebvre founded the Society of St Pius X in 1970 and was excommunicated in 1988, and Humanae Vitae in 1968 reaffirmed the ban on artificial contraception, which many Western Catholics rejected. Mass attendance and vocations fell steeply in later decades, though historians link this to wider social change.\n\nKarol Wojtyla, the Polish archbishop of Krakow who had contributed to Gaudium et Spes, was elected pope on 16 October 1978, the first non-Italian pope since 1523. His changes lay mainly in style and influence. He made over a hundred foreign journeys and drew huge crowds, including about a million people at Mass in the Phoenix Park during his visit to Ireland in September 1979, where he appealed at Drogheda for an end to paramilitary violence. He launched World Youth Days and apologised for historical wrongs of Christians. After the liberalising Council he reaffirmed traditional teaching on contraception, abortion and the male priesthood, opposed liberation theology, and had theologians disciplined by the Congregation for the Doctrine of the Faith under Cardinal Ratzinger.\n\nJohn Paul II also changed the Church's political role. His visit to Poland in 1979 drew enormous crowds and encouraged Poles to organise independently of the communist state, and his support for Solidarity made him a political figure whom communist governments saw as a challenge. His meeting with Gorbachev at the Vatican on 1 December 1989 showed that the Church had outlasted communist regimes, though historians disagree about how far he caused their fall. In short, Vatican II changed how the Church worshipped, how it related to other faiths and what role the laity played, while John Paul II consolidated traditional doctrine and used the papacy as a moral and political force. The Council changed the Church's structures and practice more deeply, but John Paul II changed its public standing in the world.",
