    "label": "1",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The United States was broadly successful in dealing with both Berlin and Cuba, in that it kept West Berlin free and removed Soviet missiles from Cuba without war. The success was not complete, however. In Berlin the Wall went up and America accepted it, and in Cuba the outcome rested on a secret concession and followed an earlier humiliation.\n\nThe first Berlin crisis began on 24 June 1948, when the Soviet Union blocked road, rail and canal access to the western sectors of the divided city, hoping to force the Western powers out. Truman rejected both withdrawal and an armed convoy that could have started a war, and chose the airlift. More than two million tons of food, coal and other supplies were flown into West Berlin, and the Soviets lifted the blockade on 12 May 1949 without having achieved their aim. This was a clear American success, since a risky confrontation ended with the Soviets beaten and without a shot fired.\n\nThe airlift also had wider results. It helped to persuade West Germans that the Americans were reliable protectors, it strengthened the case for NATO, and it showed the value of the policy of containment. Berlin had become a symbol of Western determination, which made the victory more significant than the city's military value.\n\nThe second Berlin crisis was less clear. In November 1958 Khrushchev demanded that the Western powers leave within six months, and he pressed Kennedy at the Vienna summit in June 1961. Well over two million East Germans had fled west between 1949 and 1961, mostly through Berlin, draining the East of skilled workers. On 13 August 1961 the East Germans began building the Berlin Wall. Kennedy did not try to tear it down, and the Americans accepted it as a means of avoiding war. It was a propaganda disaster for communism, which had to imprison its own people, and it ended the immediate crisis, but it was also a failure for the United States, which had to accept the permanent division of the city. Kennedy's visit in June 1963 and his declaration that he was a Berliner reassured West Berliners, but it could not undo the Wall.\n\nCuba tested the United States more severely. America had long treated the island as part of its sphere of influence, and after Castro's revolution Eisenhower cut the sugar quota in 1960, imposed a partial embargo and broke relations in January 1961, which pushed Cuba towards the Soviet Union. The CIA-trained exiles who landed at the Bay of Pigs on 17 April 1961 were defeated within three days, a humiliation for Kennedy and a clear failure that made Castro's position stronger and gave Khrushchev reason to think Kennedy weak.\n\nThe missile crisis was far more successful. After a U-2 photographed Soviet missile sites on 14 October 1962, Kennedy and ExComm rejected an air strike or invasion and chose a naval quarantine, announced on 22 October. On 24 October Soviet ships turned back from the line, and after the tense Black Saturday of 27 October, when a U-2 was shot down killing Major Rudolf Anderson, Khrushchev agreed on 28 October to remove the missiles in return for a pledge not to invade Cuba. Kennedy had combined firmness with restraint, and nuclear war was avoided.\n\nThe success had limits. Kennedy also secretly agreed to remove the US Jupiter missiles from Turkey within months, a deal kept private for years, and the pledge not to invade left Castro's communist regime in place ninety miles from Florida. Still, the crisis led to useful results: the hotline between Washington and Moscow in 1963 and the Partial Test Ban Treaty of August 1963.\n\nOverall the United States was largely successful. It won in Berlin in 1948 to 1949 and in Cuba in 1962, the two moments when the superpowers came nearest to war, and each time the Soviet Union backed down. The Wall and the Bay of Pigs were real failures, and the missile deal depended on a secret concession, so the success was a matter of containing the Soviets and avoiding war rather than complete victory.",
