    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The American Dream was the belief that anyone, through hard work and enterprise, could achieve prosperity, a home of their own and a better life for their children. Between 1945 and 1989 it was most fully realised for white, middle-class families in the affluent 1950s and early 1960s, but the reality of American life always fell short of the ideal for black Americans, the poor and, in different ways, women, and from the 1970s even the economic foundations of the Dream weakened.\n\nIn the years after 1945 the Dream was built on real material foundations. The United States produced roughly half of the world's manufactured goods in 1945 and wartime savings created a huge pent-up demand for cars, houses and appliances. The GI Bill of 1944 paid for veterans' education and gave government-guaranteed home loans, while Federal Housing Administration and Veterans Administration mortgages let buyers borrow with little or no deposit. The result was a mass market for housing. William Levitt began Levittown on Long Island in 1947, building about 17,000 standardised houses on production-line methods, and by 1960 more Americans lived in suburbs than in the central cities or on farms. For millions of families the Dream of owning a house and a car was no longer a slogan but a lived reality.\n\nConsumerism gave the Dream its shape. Credit made it possible to buy on instalments, and the Diners Club card of 1950 and BankAmericard of 1958 spread the habit. Television, which was in about 9 per cent of homes in 1950 and about 87 per cent by 1960, brought advertising into the living room, while shopping malls, supermarkets and dishwashers and freezers became common in middle-class homes. Hollywood and television comedies celebrated family, home and abundance, and Nixon used a model suburban kitchen in Moscow in 1959 to argue that American abundance proved the superiority of capitalism. In the Cold War the Dream was therefore also a national symbol, offered as the alternative to communism. Critics such as Galbraith, in The Affluent Society, and Vance Packard, in The Hidden Persuaders, warned that the Dream had become little more than manufactured wants.\n\nThe reality for black Americans was very different. Federal mortgage policy and private lending practice, together with restrictive covenants, kept most black families out of the new suburbs, and Levittown itself excluded black buyers. Black families continued to move to northern cities like Chicago and Detroit as white families left in what became known as white flight, leaving the inner cities poorer and more segregated. In the South, Jim Crow denied black citizens the vote and equal schooling. The civil rights movement, from Brown v Board of Education in 1954 and the Montgomery boycott to the sit-ins, Birmingham and Selma, was in essence a demand to be included in the Dream. The Civil Rights Act of 1964 and the Voting Rights Act of 1965 gave legal equality, but the riots of Watts in 1965 and Detroit in 1967, and the Kerner Report's warning of two societies, separate and unequal, showed that legal rights did not bring equal opportunity.\n\nPoverty and gender also exposed the gap. Michael Harrington's The Other America in 1962 showed that tens of millions of Americans lived in poverty, invisible to the affluent majority, and Johnson's War on Poverty from 1964 helped bring the official poverty rate down from about 22 per cent in 1959 to about 12 per cent in 1969, though programmes were underfunded once Vietnam took priority. For women the 1950s ideal was domesticity, and many left wartime jobs for the home. Betty Friedan's The Feminine Mystique of 1963 described the problem that has no name, arguing that housewives were trapped by an ideal that denied them fulfilment. Women entered the workforce in growing numbers, but into lower-paid clerical and service jobs, and they could be refused credit until the Equal Credit Opportunity Act of 1974. The founding of NOW in 1966 and the Equal Pay Act and Title VII show how women had to fight for their share.\n\nFrom 1973 the economic basis of the Dream itself weakened. The oil shocks, stagflation and the recession of 1973 to 1975 hit living standards, and real earnings of production workers stagnated, so that families needed a second income to keep up. Steel and car jobs were lost to Japanese and European competition, and the closure of the Youngstown mill in 1977 became a symbol of the rust belt. In the 1980s the tax cuts and recovery under Reagan favoured higher earners, the poverty rate rose to around 15 per cent in the early 1980s, and the crack epidemic and harsh sentencing laws fell heavily on poor black neighbourhoods.\n\nOverall, the American Dream reflected reality for a large part of the population, above all white, suburban, middle-class families in the quarter century after the war, whose standard of living was without precedent. But it was always unevenly shared by race, class and gender, and after 1973 it became harder even for those it had once served. It was a powerful ideal, and the protest movements of the period were largely attempts to make that ideal true for everyone, not rejections of it.",
