    "label": "C2",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "The GAA was founded at Hayes' Hotel in Thurles on 1 November 1884 by Michael Cusack and Maurice Davin to revive Irish games and open athletics to all classes. It was needed because English amateur rules excluded artisans and labourers, and native hurling had declined since the Famine. Archbishop Croke urged the revival of Irish sport as an act of national self-respect in his letter of December 1884 and became patron, with Parnell and Davitt. Davin drew up the first rules for hurling and Gaelic football in 1885, and the first All-Ireland championships were held in 1887. The IRB infiltrated the Association and the Central Council was contested between moderates and IRB delegates, so Cusack was removed as secretary in 1886. The American tour of 1888 lost money, the bishops grew uneasy, and the Parnell split divided members so that by 1891 membership had collapsed.",
