    "label": "C3",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Carson led the Irish Unionist parliamentary party from 1910 and was the chief unionist campaigner against the Third Home Rule Bill from 1911 to 1914. At a huge demonstration at Craigavon, outside Belfast, in September 1911, he was the main speaker. On Ulster Day, 28 September 1912, he signed the Solemn League and Covenant first at Belfast City Hall, and about 237,000 men followed him in signing it. In March 1914 Carson rejected Asquith's offer of a six-year exclusion for Ulster as a stay of execution. In September 1913 the Ulster Unionist Council announced that Carson would chair a Provisional Government of Ulster if Home Rule became law. In April 1914 unionists landed about 25,000 rifles at Larne while the police and army did not intervene.",
