    "model": "Isabella Tod organised the first sustained suffrage society in Ulster in Belfast in the early 1870s. She campaigned for the Married Women's Property Acts of 1870 and 1882, which improved the property rights of married women. She also helped found the Ladies' Institute in Belfast in 1867 to promote girls' education. Hanna Sheehy-Skeffington co-founded the Irish Women's Franchise League in Dublin in 1908 to win votes for women on the same terms as men. She was imprisoned for breaking windows in 1912 and again in 1913, and she was released after a hunger strike under the Cat and Mouse Act. She edited the weekly Irish Citizen and argued that Irish independence would be incomplete without votes for women.",
