    "label": "B1",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "Constance Gore-Booth was born in London and raised at Lissadell in Co. Sligo, and in 1900 she married the Polish count Casimir Markievicz. She joined the Irish Citizen Army and in the 1913 Lockout ran the soup kitchen at Liberty Hall. In the Rising she was second-in-command to Michael Mallin at St Stephen's Green and the College of Surgeons, and her death sentence was commuted to penal servitude for life because of her sex. In the 1918 election she won St Patrick's, Dublin while in Holloway prison, becoming the first woman elected to the House of Commons, and she served as Minister for Labour in the Dáil ministry.",
