    "label": "C1",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Collins organised the Dáil Loan, served as Minister for Finance from April 1919 and, as Director of Intelligence, built a network of informers in Dublin Castle and the police. His Squad killed detectives of the Dublin Metropolitan Police's G Division and other agents, including fourteen men on Bloody Sunday, 21 November 1920. He escaped capture throughout the war and became the most wanted man in the country. In London in 1921 he handled the military and financial issues, built a good relationship with Birkenhead and Churchill, and signed the Treaty as a first step. He was chosen as chairman of the Provisional Government in January 1922, and as commander-in-chief of the National Army he was shot dead at Béal na Bláth on 22 August 1922.",
