    "label": "C4",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "De Valera removed the oath to the King through the Constitution (Removal of Oath) Act of May 1933, and in the same year he ended appeals to the Privy Council. After the abdication of Edward VIII, the Constitution (Amendment No. 27) Act of December 1936 removed all references to the Crown and the Governor-General from the Constitution. The External Relations Act of 12 December 1936 gave the King a role only in diplomatic appointments and treaties. He drafted the 1937 constitution, Bunreacht na hÉireann, which claimed the whole island as national territory and recognised the special position of the Catholic Church. The Anglo-Irish Agreements of April 1938 returned the Treaty ports to Ireland and ended the Economic War after a payment of £10 million. He kept Ireland neutral during the Second World War and refused the American Note of 1944.",
