    "label": "B1",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "Ulster Presbyterians were the first large group of Irish emigrants to North America, sailing to the American colonies through the eighteenth century. Many settled in Pennsylvania and along the Appalachians, and Francis Makemie from Co. Donegal helped found the Presbyterian church in the colonies. Their descendants became known as Scotch-Irish, a name that set them apart from the Catholic Irish. They kept a Protestant identity and loyalty to the British Crown, and many joined American denominations quickly, so their sense of Irishness faded faster than that of Catholics.",
