    "label": "B2",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "The Apprentice Boys of Derry was founded in 1814 to commemorate the siege of 1689, and its members were mainly Protestant tradesmen and businessmen. Its main events are the Relief of Derry parade in August and the Shutting of the Gates commemoration in December. The August parade marches around the city walls with bands, banners, a cannon and crimson flags. The December commemoration includes a service at St Columb's Cathedral and the burning of an effigy of Governor Lundy. The organisation's parades past the Bogside were a source of tension, and the parade of 12 August 1969 sparked the Battle of the Bogside.",
