    "label": "C2",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Supporters of Derry argued that it was the second largest city in Northern Ireland, with a largely Catholic population, high unemployment and existing college buildings at Magee. They said a university would bring much-needed jobs to a city that had been neglected. The University for Derry campaign, chaired by John Hume, drew support from across the community, including some Protestant and business figures. Derry nationalists alleged that the decision had been made on political grounds by faceless men, and they held a motorcade to Stormont in February 1965. Supporters of Coleraine argued that it offered a central site, good access and room for expansion, and the Lockwood Committee recommended it in February 1965. The government said Derry's case had been fully considered and rejected charges of sectarian motives. Critics noted that choosing a small town weakened the claim that public investment was spread evenly across the province.",
