    "label": "C3",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "The Sunningdale conference of 6 to 9 December 1973 agreed a package of power sharing, a Council of Ireland with seven ministers from each side and a consultative assembly of 60 members. The Irish government accepted that Northern Ireland's status would not change without majority consent, and a Law Enforcement Commission was set up. The executive took office on 1 January 1974 under Brian Faulkner, with Gerry Fitt as Deputy Chief Executive. Unionists rejected the Council of Ireland as a step towards a united Ireland, and the Ulster Unionist Council voted to reject it on 4 January 1974. The United Ulster Unionist Council won eleven of the twelve Northern Ireland seats in February 1974, opposing Sunningdale. The Ulster Workers' Council strike from 15 May 1974 cut electricity and paralysed services. Britain did not confront the strikers, and Faulkner resigned on 28 May 1974, so the executive collapsed after five months.",
