    "label": "C4",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Ian Paisley founded the Free Presbyterian Church in 1951 and by the mid-1960s was the leading Protestant critic of Terence O'Neill. He opposed the Lemass meeting of 1965 and led the O'Neill must go campaign from 1966, which helped move unionism towards a hardline position. He was jailed for three months in 1966 after refusing to be bound over following a protest at the Presbyterian General Assembly. His rallies mobilised grassroots Protestant opinion and made him the voice of hardline unionism. He founded the Democratic Unionist Party in 1971 and opposed the Sunningdale power-sharing executive. He supported the Ulster Workers' Council strike of May 1974, which brought down the executive. He led protests against the Anglo-Irish Agreement of 1985, and he opposed ecumenism and dialogue with the Catholic Church.",
