    "label": "B2",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "Sergei Witte's state-directed industrialisation of the 1890s was paid for by foreign loans, high indirect taxes and grain exports, and it built the Trans-Siberian Railway and heavy industry. The rouble was put on the gold standard in 1897 to attract foreign investors. The industrial depression of 1900-03 brought unemployment and strikes in the cities. The famine of 1891 in the Volga region, together with the peasants' land hunger, caused great distress and weakened trust in the regime. By 1914 Russia was the world's fifth industrial power, but its output per head was low and it relied on foreign capital.",
