    "label": "B3",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "Industrial states needed raw materials such as rubber, cotton and copper, and they wanted protected markets when tariffs rose in Europe from the 1870s. Strategically, powers wanted naval bases and coaling stations, and Britain occupied Egypt in 1882 to protect the Suez Canal. Colonies were a mark of Great Power status, and France sought compensation overseas after its defeat in 1871 under Jules Ferry. Churches wanted to spread Christianity and end the slave trade, while Social Darwinism and the idea of a civilising mission were used to justify rule.",
