    "label": "C1",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "The naval policy of Wilhelm II caused several problems for Germany. The fleet cost vast sums, raised the national debt and taxation, and contributed to the finance crisis of 1908-09. It absorbed resources that the army needed while France and Russia threatened Germany's land frontiers. Britain was turned from a possible friend into an opponent, and it was pushed into the Entente with France and Russia. The Haldane mission of February 1912 failed because Germany wanted British neutrality and Britain wanted a slower building programme. In the war the fleet stayed largely in harbour, and the sailors' mutinies at Kiel in 1918 helped trigger the revolution.",
