    "label": "C3",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Douglas Haig commanded the British Expeditionary Force in France from December 1915 until the end of the war. He commanded the Somme offensive of 1916, the Arras offensive and Passchendaele in 1917. On the first day of the Somme the British Army suffered about 57,000 casualties, the worst single day in its history. Passchendaele was fought in heavy rain and mud and gained only about 8 km. In 1918 he issued his 'backs to the wall' order on 11 April, and he led the Allied advance in the Hundred Days, in which British forces took many prisoners. Historians disagree about him: some call him a butcher, while John Terraine argues that he adapted and led the army to victory.",
