    "label": "B1",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "The Lomé Conventions were trade and aid agreements between the EEC and African, Caribbean and Pacific (ACP) states, and Lomé I was signed in Togo on 28 February 1975. Lomé I gave ACP exports largely duty-free access to the EEC without requiring reciprocal concessions, which was a new departure in trade relations. STABEX compensated ACP countries when their earnings from specified agricultural exports fell below a set level. Lomé I also included a Sugar Protocol guaranteeing ACP sugar quotas at European prices, and Lomé II added SYSMIN to help mineral exporters.",
