    "label": "C1",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Mountbatten brought the date of independence forward to 15 August 1947, which left too little time to plan the boundary and policing. Cyril Radcliffe, a barrister who had never visited India, drew the Punjab and Bengal boundaries, and the Radcliffe Line was announced on 17 August 1947, two days after independence. Millions crossed the new border as Hindus and Sikhs moved into India and Muslims moved into Pakistan, with about 10 to 15 million people displaced. Communal violence in the Punjab and Bengal killed between about 200,000 and 2 million people. Over 500 princely states had to join India or Pakistan, and the dispute over Kashmir led to fighting between India and Pakistan in 1947. The army, civil service and railways had to be divided in a few weeks, which caused serious administrative strain.",
