    "label": "C3",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "US involvement in Vietnam grew out of containment, and Eisenhower's domino theory of 1954 warned that if Vietnam fell to communism the rest of Southeast Asia would follow. By 1954 America was paying about three quarters of the cost of the French war, and it backed Ngo Dinh Diem in South Vietnam. Johnson used the Gulf of Tonkin Resolution of August 1964 to escalate, and the bombing of North Vietnam began in March 1965. The United States withdrew because Nixon adopted Vietnamisation in 1969, handing the ground war to the South Vietnamese army while US troops fell from about 540,000 in early 1969 to under 30,000 by late 1972. The Case-Church amendment cut off funds for US combat in Indochina from August 1973, and the Paris Peace Accords were signed in January 1973.",
