    "model": "The responses to the Great Famine of 1845 to 1850 were not effective enough to prevent a catastrophe in which about a million people died and over a million emigrated. Government relief achieved most under Peel in 1845 and 1846 and with the soup kitchens of 1847, but it was undermined by laissez-faire and the principle that Irish property must pay. Private relief, though generous and often heroic, was too small to match the scale of the disaster and shrank after 1847.\n\nRobert Peel's government in 1845 and 1846 acted quickly. It secretly bought about £100,000 of Indian corn in America through Baring Brothers and sold it at cost through government depots to hold down food prices, though the yellow meal, nicknamed Peel's brimstone, was hard to grind and cook. A Relief Commission under Sir Randolph Routh coordinated hundreds of local committees, and the Board of Works ran public works to give the poor wages. Deaths were low in the first season, and these measures are usually judged the most effective of the Famine, although only part of the crop failed in 1845. Peel then repealed the Corn Laws in June 1846, but that did little for Ireland and his government fell.\n\nLord John Russell's Whig government that took office in June 1846 was committed to laissez-faire, and its policy was carried out by the Treasury official Charles Trevelyan, who feared dependence on the state. When the blight destroyed almost the entire crop in 1846, the Labouchere Act relaunched the public works with the full cost to be repaid from local taxes, and wages were set low so as not to compete with private employers. By March 1847 about 700,000 people were on the works, paid late and often too weak to labour through a hard winter. Food continued to leave Irish ports and the government did not control prices. The works were closed in 1847 when deaths and costs were rising, and the whole approach was too little, too late.\n\nThe government then changed course. The Soup Kitchen Act of February 1847 set up government kitchens that, by July 1847, fed about three million people a day, the most effective and cheapest relief of the Famine. Yet the kitchens were closed by September 1847, long before the hunger had ended. The Poor Law Extension Act of June 1847 made the poor law responsible for all relief, so that Irish property would pay for Irish poverty. Its Gregory clause refused relief to anyone holding more than a quarter of an acre, forcing smallholders to give up their land, and landlords used it to clear estates. Many western unions went bankrupt and workhouses became centres of fever. The Rate-in-Aid Act of 1849 imposed a national rate to support the bankrupt unions, which angered Ulster. The government spent about £7 million, about half as loans, which was small in relation to the need.\n\nPrivate responses filled some gaps. The Society of Friends set up its Central Relief Committee in Dublin in November 1846 and opened soup kitchens in Cork and elsewhere, giving help without regard to religion and refusing to proselytise. Quaker visitors such as William Forster and James Hack Tuke wrote reports that gave British readers trusted eyewitness accounts of the suffering. The British Relief Association, founded in January 1847 with Lionel de Rothschild among its backers, raised about £470,000, the largest private fund, helped by a letter from Queen Victoria, and fed schoolchildren in the poorest unions. Help came from America, including the warship Jamestown which brought food to Cork in April 1847, from the Choctaw Nation, and from Pope Pius IX, while Catholic and Church of Ireland clergy and the Sisters of Mercy and Charity nursed fever victims, and many died. The American Asenath Nicholson distributed food herself in Dublin, Mayo and Erris and wrote an account of what she saw.\n\nBut private relief had serious limits. Some landlords reduced rents and employed tenants, but others cleared their estates, and the evictions in Clare, Mayo and Galway were severe. Major Mahon of Strokestown paid the passage to Canada of about 1,500 tenants in 1847, and many died on the voyage. The charge of souperism, that some evangelicals gave food only in return for conversion, left lasting bitterness even though most Protestant relief was unconditional. Total private giving was a small fraction of government spending, and it fell sharply after 1847 because of donor fatigue and the belief that the crisis was over. The Quakers concluded that only the government had the resources and withdrew from mass feeding.\n\nIn conclusion, both responses were ineffective in different ways. The government had the means to save many more lives and failed because of its ideology and its insistence that Ireland pay, while private relief saved thousands, especially in 1846 and 1847, and exposed the scale of the suffering, but could never replace the state. The combined result was that relief was cut back while hunger continued, and the Famine's toll of death and emigration was not prevented.",
