    "model": "Fenianism was only partly successful. At home it failed in its stated aim of winning an Irish republic by force, since the 1867 rising was crushed within days and the organisation was riddled with informers. Abroad, however, it raised money and men in America, struck at Britain and Canada, and had a lasting impact on British policy and on Irish nationalism, so its influence was greater than its military record suggests.\n\nFenianism's first success was organisational. The Irish Republican Brotherhood was founded in Dublin on 17 March 1858 by James Stephens as an oath-bound secret society, organised in circles under local leaders. It drew in artisans, clerks, shopkeepers and farmers' sons, and even recruited among soldiers in the British army. Its roots lay in the bitterness of the Famine, the failed rising of 1848 and the disillusion that followed the collapse of the Independent Irish Party after Keogh and Sadleir took office in 1852. To have built the first lasting revolutionary organisation in Ireland, in a country wary of secret societies, was a real achievement. The funeral of Terence Bellew MacManus in Dublin in 1861 showed how much support the movement could now parade in public.\n\nThe movement also achieved a public voice. The Irish People, founded in 1863 with Luby, O'Leary and Kickham as editors and O'Donovan Rossa as manager, spread the republican message. Yet this success was also its weakness, because it exposed the leadership. In September 1865 the police raided the paper's offices and arrested Luby, O'Leary, Rossa and Kickham, and Stephens was arrested in November. Although he escaped from Richmond Prison within a fortnight, the government had struck before any rising could begin, and in February 1866 it suspended habeas corpus to complete the task. This was a serious failure, and it showed how well informed Dublin Castle was.\n\nThe rising of March 1867 confirmed the failure at home. Stephens had postponed the rising he had promised in 1866 and was removed by the American leaders in December, so the movement was divided and demoralised. When the rising came in bad weather, the scattered outbreaks at Tallaght, Cork, Limerick and Tipperary were quickly put down. The Fenians lacked arms and trained leaders, and informers had compromised the plans. Opposition from the Church also hurt. Cardinal Cullen and most bishops condemned Fenianism as a secret society and many priests denounced it, and Cullen had refused to let MacManus lie in the Pro-Cathedral. A movement that could not carry the Catholic Church or win a battle cannot be called a military success.\n\nAbroad the picture is more mixed. In New York John O'Mahony founded the Fenian Brotherhood in 1858, and it supplied money, supporters and Civil War veterans whose experience raised hopes of a successful rising. This transatlantic backing made Fenianism far stronger than earlier Irish conspiracies. But American Fenians used their strength badly. Their raids into Canada, at Ridgeway in June 1866 and again in 1870, failed to win any territory, and their leaders' decision to depose Stephens split the movement on the eve of the rising.\n\nIn England the Fenians were more effective at gaining attention. In September 1867 they freed Kelly and Deasy from a prison van in Manchester, killing Sergeant Brett, and Allen, Larkin and O'Brien were hanged in November as the Manchester Martyrs. Their executions were mourned across Ireland and turned them into heroes, even among people who disliked Fenian methods. The attempt in December 1867 to free a prisoner by blowing up the wall of Clerkenwell prison killed about a dozen people. That outrage turned British opinion against the Fenians, so the English campaign won sympathy in Ireland but lost it in Britain.\n\nThe most important success was political and long-term. Historians argue that the Manchester executions and Clerkenwell convinced Gladstone that Irish grievances had to be addressed, and his Irish Church Act of 1869 and Land Act of 1870 followed. Fenian prisoners were released between 1869 and 1871 as the amnesty movement grew, and Kickham himself was freed in 1869. The Land Act in particular fell short, but Fenianism had shown that violence could force reform and had kept physical force republicanism alive, a legacy that reached 1916.\n\nOn balance, Fenianism was a failure as a rising, both in Ireland and in its raids abroad, but a success as a movement. It built an enduring organisation, forced the British government to act, and made republicanism a permanent part of Irish politics. Its achievement was greater abroad, in America and Britain, than at home, where the Church, the informers and the police defeated it.",
