    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Between 1815 and 1870 Irish cultural and religious identities were shaped by sharp developments on several sides. The Catholic Church became more disciplined and Roman under Cardinal Cullen, Protestants strengthened their identity through evangelicalism and the Orange Order, and writers and nationalists defined the nation in cultural terms, while the Irish language went into decline. Together these developments gave Ireland the divided but strongly defined identities that later politics would draw on.\n\nThe most important religious development was the devotional revolution within Catholicism, a term used by the historian Emmet Larkin. From the 1840s the Church became more disciplined and its practice more uniform. New churches and cathedrals were built on money given by the faithful, Maynooth trained a better educated clergy, and religious orders such as the Christian Brothers, the Sisters of Mercy founded by Catherine McAuley in 1831 and the Sisters of Charity ran schools, hospitals and orphanages. Parish missions, the rosary, novenas and devotion to the Sacred Heart and the Virgin Mary spread new forms of piety. Larkin argued that regular Mass attendance rose from about a third of Catholics before the Famine to a large majority by the 1870s, helped by a Famine that had removed many of the poor who were least regular in practice.\n\nThe Synod of Thurles in 1850 was the key moment in this change. It was the first national synod of the Irish Church since Kilkenny in 1642, and it met under Paul Cullen, the Apostolic Delegate, from 22 August to 9 September. It condemned the Queen's Colleges, ordered the founding of a Catholic University, which opened in 1854 with Newman as rector, laid down uniform liturgy and discipline, and discouraged priests from political agitation. It began the Romanisation of the Irish Church under strong papal authority, which is called ultramontanism. The Catholic University was not a success, but Cullen, who became Archbishop of Dublin in 1852 and the first Irish cardinal in 1866, dominated Catholic public life for a quarter of a century. This identity was not accepted by everyone, since MacHale of Tuam and many liberal Catholics resented Roman control.\n\nProtestant identity also developed strongly. Evangelicalism, which stressed personal conversion and Bible reading, grew within the Church of Ireland and among Presbyterians and Methodists from the 1820s. The Second Reformation tried to convert Catholics through Bible societies, schools and missions, such as Edward Nangle's colony at Achill in 1834. In the Famine, Protestant soup kitchens that demanded conversion led Catholics to coin the term souperism, a bitterness that lasted for generations. The Orange Order, founded in 1795, was dissolved in 1836 and revived in the 1840s, expressing loyalty to the Crown and the Union. The clash of identities was seen at Dolly's Brae in 1849, when an Orange march met armed Ribbonmen and Catholics were killed, and in the Ulster revival of 1859. Henry Cooke worked for an alliance of Presbyterians and Anglicans against O'Connell and Catholic nationalism, so religious identity increasingly shaped political identity as well.\n\nCultural identity developed through Young Ireland and the arts. The Nation, founded in 1842 by Thomas Davis, Charles Gavan Duffy and John Blake Dillon, used poems, ballads and history to teach the idea of a nation united by shared history and culture, and Davis hoped it would include Catholic, Protestant and Dissenter alike. Scholars supported the idea. Moore's Irish Melodies were popular at home and abroad, Mangan wrote Dark Rosaleen, George Petrie showed the round towers were Christian in origin and collected traditional music, and the Ordnance Survey employed O'Donovan and O'Curry to study place names and manuscripts. William Carleton's Traits and Stories of the Irish Peasantry in 1830 recorded rural life before the Famine. Yet the Irish language declined, since O'Connell regarded English as the language of progress, the national schools taught in English and the Famine hit the Irish-speaking west hardest. The 1851 census found only about a quarter of the population reporting that they spoke Irish.\n\nOn balance, the central developments were the Catholic devotional revolution and the hardening of Protestant evangelical identity, because these tied religion firmly to community and politics. Cultural nationalism supplied a language of nationhood but could not save the Irish language or win Protestants over. By 1870 Ireland had clearly defined identities, and the divisions between them proved lasting.",
