    "label": "1",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Patrick Pearse and Countess Markievicz both made important contributions to Irish affairs between about 1900 and 1927, though in different ways. Pearse was the chief ideologue of the 1916 Rising and its leading public figure, while Markievicz was a pioneer in nationalist youth work, labour and women's participation in revolutionary politics. Pearse's contribution was the more decisive in shaping the event that changed Irish politics, but Markievicz's was broader and longer lasting.\n\nPearse first made his mark in cultural and educational life. Born in Dublin in 1879 to an English father and an Irish mother, he joined the Gaelic League in 1896 and edited its paper An Claidheamh Soluis from 1903. In 1908 he founded St Enda's, a bilingual school, and his essay The Murder Machine attacked the state education system. This work promoted the Irish language and a sense of national identity, and it helped create the cultural nationalism from which many of the revolutionary generation came. It was an important contribution, but it was a preparation for politics rather than politics itself.\n\nHis most significant contribution was in the militant movement that led to 1916. He joined the IRB in 1913, became Director of Military Organisation of the Irish Volunteers, and in 1915 joined the Military Council, which planned the Rising along with Clarke, MacDiarmada, Plunkett, Ceannt, MacDonagh and Connolly. The funeral of the Fenian Jeremiah O'Donovan Rossa on 1 August 1915 was organised as a show of strength, and Pearse used it as a platform with his graveside oration, declaring that Ireland unfree would never be at peace. His 1915 writings praised bloodshed as cleansing, which critics later called blood sacrifice. In this period he gave physical force republicanism an emotional and moral language that inspired many young men.\n\nIn 1916 Pearse was the public face of the Rising. On 24 April the rebels seized the GPO, with Pearse as President of the Provisional Government and Commandant-General, and he read the Proclamation of the Irish Republic outside it. The Proclamation addressed Irishmen and Irishwomen, guaranteed religious and civil liberty and equal rights, and gave later republicans a founding document. He surrendered on 29 April and was executed in Kilmainham Gaol on 3 May, aged 36, with his brother Willie shot the following day. The military result was a failure, since the rebels held no major target beyond their garrisons and were contained within a week, but the importance of his role lay in what followed. The executions, which at first had little public sympathy, turned opinion, so that by autumn he was honoured as a martyr and Sinn Féin gained the moral authority that Redmond's Parliamentary Party lost.\n\nMarkievicz's contribution began earlier and lasted longer. Born Constance Gore-Booth in London in 1868 and brought up at Lissadell, Co. Sligo, she joined Inghinidhe na hÉireann and Sinn Féin in 1908, and in 1909 she helped Bulmer Hobson found Fianna Éireann, a republican youth organisation that trained many who later joined the Volunteers. She joined the Irish Citizen Army and in the 1913 Lockout ran the soup kitchen at Liberty Hall, showing her commitment to labour. In the Rising she was second-in-command to Michael Mallin at St Stephen's Green and the College of Surgeons, and she was the most prominent female combatant. She was sentenced to death, but General Maxwell commuted it to penal servitude for life because of her sex, and she was released in the amnesty of June 1917.\n\nHer later career was unique. She won St Patrick's, Dublin, in the December 1918 general election while in Holloway prison, becoming the first woman elected to the House of Commons, though like all Sinn Féin members she did not take her seat. From April 1919 to January 1922 she was Minister for Labour in the Dáil ministry, the only woman in the cabinet and one of the first women in Europe to hold cabinet rank. Her practical impact was limited because she was in jail or on the run for much of the time, and some colleagues distrusted her socialism and flamboyance. She opposed the Treaty in the Dáil in January 1922.\n\nOn balance, Pearse was the more important figure because without his planning, oratory and leadership in the Rising the revolution would not have taken the path it did, and his sacrifice transformed Irish opinion. Markievicz was less central to the political outcome, but her importance as a woman in revolutionary politics, in youth organisation and in government was considerable, and historians now emphasise it. Together they illustrate the two sides of the revolution, the cult of sacrifice and the broader work of building a movement.",
