    "label": "1",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The Irish in Britain between 1840 and 1920 faced severe challenges. They arrived in large numbers, mostly poor and unskilled, and they had to survive in overcrowded slums, in badly paid and dangerous work, and in a society that often despised their religion and their origins. They also met sectarian hostility and political suspicion, especially when nationalist violence troubled Britain, though they gradually built churches, institutions and a political voice of their own.\n\nThe first challenge was poverty and the conditions in which they lived. Britain received the poorest emigrants because the crossing to Liverpool or Glasgow cost only a few shillings, and the 1841 census counted about 420,000 Irish-born people there. The Famine drove numbers far higher: about 300,000 destitute people landed at Liverpool alone in 1847, and by 1851 the Irish-born population of Britain had risen to about 730,000, with roughly a fifth of Liverpool's population Irish-born. Many settled in the poorest districts, such as Little Ireland in Manchester and St Giles in London, in crowded and unsanitary housing. Fever followed the Famine arrivals, and the Irish were blamed for outbreaks of typhus that were in fact caused by poverty and overcrowding.\n\nThe second challenge was work. Irish labourers worked as navvies on canals and railways, as dockers, as building labourers and in textile mills, and many came as seasonal harvest workers from Mayo, Donegal and Galway who returned home with their wages. These were the heaviest and least secure jobs, with low pay and little protection. Because they would accept low wages the Irish were resented by native workers, who saw them as competitors who held down pay and could be used to break strikes. Irish women went into domestic service and mill work, and their position was similarly insecure.\n\nThe third challenge was prejudice. Punch and other papers caricatured the Irish as simian, violent and backward, and writers presented them as a separate and inferior race. They were blamed for crime, drunkenness and disease, and they were over-represented in arrest figures. This hostility was shown in violence: Irish Catholic homes and churches were attacked in the Stockport riots of 1852, and the anti-Catholic lectures of William Murphy provoked the Murphy riots of 1867, which brought attacks on Irish districts in towns such as Birkenhead and Wolverhampton.\n\nReligion made matters worse, since most Irish were Catholic in a country that was overwhelmingly Protestant and suspicious of the Pope's authority. In Liverpool and Glasgow Protestant Orange lodges and Catholic Irish communities clashed, and sectarian rivalry shaped local politics and street life. The Catholic church met the challenge by building parishes and schools, with Irish-born priests serving poor congregations in Liverpool and Glasgow, and the hierarchy was restored in England and Wales in 1850. In 1887 Brother Walfrid, a Marist from Sligo, founded Celtic Football Club in Glasgow to raise money for poor Irish Catholic families, and in 1918 the Education (Scotland) Act brought Catholic schools into the state system while keeping their religious character.\n\nA further challenge was political suspicion, particularly when Irish nationalism turned violent. After the Manchester Martyrs were executed in November 1867, and the Fenian bomb at Clerkenwell prison in London that December killed about a dozen people, fears grew that all Irish people in Britain were a security threat. Irish districts were heavily policed, and nationalists complained that the whole community was treated as suspect. Yet political participation was also a response to hostility: the Irish National League of Great Britain, founded in 1883, organised Irish voters behind the Irish Parliamentary Party, and in 1885 Parnell told them to vote against the Liberals. T. P. O'Connor was elected for the Liverpool Scotland seat in 1885 and held it until 1929, and after the first Home Rule Bill of 1886 Irish voters in Liverpool and Glasgow mostly supported the Liberals, before increasingly turning to Labour from the early twentieth century.\n\nIn conclusion, the Irish in Britain faced a combination of poverty, harsh working conditions, racial and religious prejudice and political suspicion, and these challenges were most intense in the Famine years and the Fenian decade of the 1860s. By 1920 they had responded through the church, community institutions and politics, but they remained a visible and often distrusted Catholic working-class minority.",
