    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The Sunningdale Agreement was the settlement reached in December 1973 between the British and Irish governments and the pro-power-sharing parties of Northern Ireland. It set out a power-sharing executive, a Council of Ireland and a guarantee that Northern Ireland's status would not change without the consent of a majority. The executive lasted only five months and collapsed in May 1974 because unionists rejected the Council of Ireland, Faulkner's party was split, and the Ulster Workers' Council strike was never faced down by the British government.\n\nThe background to Sunningdale lay in the suspension of Stormont in 1972 and Direct Rule. In March 1973 a border poll asked whether Northern Ireland should stay in the United Kingdom or join a united Ireland. About 98.9 per cent chose to remain, but nationalists boycotted it and turnout was only about 59 per cent, so the question was not settled for them. The White Paper of March 1973 proposed an Assembly elected by proportional representation, an executive in which unionists and nationalists shared power, and a Council of Ireland linking Belfast and Dublin. The Assembly election of June 1973 used the single transferable vote and returned a mixed result, with the Ulster Unionists divided between Brian Faulkner's supporters and anti-agreement unionists such as Paisley's Democratic Unionists and William Craig's Vanguard. This division weakened the settlement before it began.\n\nThe Sunningdale conference was held at Sunningdale Park in Berkshire from 6 to 9 December 1973. It was attended by the Prime Minister Edward Heath, the Taoiseach Liam Cosgrave, and the three Northern parties willing to share power: Faulkner's Unionists, the SDLP led by Gerry Fitt and John Hume, and the Alliance Party. The agreement created a Council of Ireland with a council of ministers, seven from each side, and a consultative assembly of 60 members drawn equally from the Dáil and the Northern Ireland Assembly. The Irish government accepted that Northern Ireland's status would not change without majority consent, and Britain said it would support a united Ireland if a majority wished it. A Law Enforcement Commission was to examine fugitive offenders and a possible all-Ireland court, and both governments promised policing acceptable to both communities. The executive took office on 1 January 1974, with Faulkner as Chief Executive and Fitt as his deputy, and included Hume as Minister of Commerce and Oliver Napier of Alliance.\n\nThe most important cause of collapse was the unionist rejection of the Council of Ireland. Many unionists saw it as a first step towards a united Ireland, and their fears were reinforced by the fact that the Irish government did not change Articles 2 and 3 of its constitution. The Boland case, in which a former Fianna Fáil minister challenged the declaration on status, made things worse when the Irish government's defence suggested the constitutional claim was untouched. Faulkner was left unable to convince his own people that Sunningdale protected the Union. On 4 January 1974 the Ulster Unionist Council rejected the Council of Ireland by 427 votes to 374, and Faulkner resigned as party leader on 7 January. He was now the head of an executive that lacked the support of most of his own community.\n\nUnionist opposition then organised politically. The United Ulster Unionist Council was formed in January 1974, and in the Westminster election of 28 February 1974 it won eleven of the twelve Northern Ireland seats. This allowed opponents to argue that the agreement had been rejected by the majority at the ballot box. The change of government at Westminster, with Harold Wilson's Labour party replacing Heath's Conservatives in March 1974 and Merlyn Rees becoming Secretary of State, also reduced the authority behind the settlement. Although the Assembly voted by 44 to 28 on 14 May 1974 to support the Sunningdale policy, it was the streets and the workplaces, not the Assembly, that decided the outcome.\n\nThe decisive blow was the Ulster Workers' Council strike, which began on 15 May 1974. Supported by loyalist paramilitaries and enforced by roadblocks and intimidation, it cut electricity supplies and paralysed essential services. Rees and the army would not confront the strikers directly, and Wilson's televised broadcast of 25 May, in which he accused the strikers of sponging on Westminster, angered unionists further. Meanwhile the Dublin and Monaghan bombings of 17 May, which killed 33 people, and continuing IRA violence made moderate unionists more fearful. Faulkner proposed delaying the later stages of the Council of Ireland, but his SDLP and Alliance colleagues rejected this, and he resigned on 28 May 1974. The executive collapsed and Direct Rule returned.\n\nSunningdale failed because it was an agreement that did not have the consent of the unionist majority and relied on a British government unwilling to defend it. Yet it was not a total failure: it produced the first power-sharing government in Northern Ireland and set out the principles of consent and an Irish dimension on which later agreements were built. Seamus Mallon's remark that the Belfast Agreement of 1998 was 'Sunningdale for slow learners' captures the point that its ideas were right but its timing and support were not.",
