    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The Troubles had a heavy social and economic impact on Northern Ireland. Socially, they killed thousands, drove people from their homes, deepened segregation in housing and schooling, and left many people with lasting trauma. Economically, they deterred investment and tourism, accelerated the decline of traditional industry, and left the province dependent on public money and the security sector, with unemployment high throughout and highest among Catholics.\n\nThe most obvious social cost was loss of life. Between 1969 and 1993 about 3,000 people died, and the total to 2001 is about 3,500. The year 1972 was the worst, with nearly 500 deaths. About half of the dead were civilians, and tens of thousands were injured. The violence was concentrated in Belfast, Derry and the border counties, and especially in working-class areas such as Ardoyne, the Falls and the Shankill, where both communities lived side by side. The Royal Victoria Hospital in Belfast developed great expertise in treating gunshot and blast injuries, and the health service was placed under heavy strain. Stress and trauma were widespread among those who had lived through bombings, shootings and displacement.\n\nHousing and population movement were transformed. Intimidation drove families to areas where their own community was in the majority, and tens of thousands left their homes in Belfast between 1969 and 1973. Peace walls, built between Protestant and Catholic streets from 1969, physically separated neighbourhoods in Belfast and later in Derry. The Northern Ireland Housing Executive, set up in 1971, took public housing from the local councils and allocated it by a points system, ending the discrimination the civil rights movement had criticised. Despite this, segregated estates became more entrenched, and communities had fewer opportunities to meet. Daily life was shaped by checkpoints, searches and town centres sealed off after bombings.\n\nEducation remained divided. Most children attended either Catholic or Protestant schools, and teacher training was also separated, at St Mary's and Stranmillis. A small integrated education movement emerged in response: parents formed All Children Together in 1974, Lagan College opened in Belfast in 1981 as the first integrated secondary school, and the Education Reform Order of 1989 gave integrated schools statutory support. Such schools were few and depended on parental campaigning, so the overall pattern of segregation was little changed.\n\nEconomically, the violence discouraged outside investment. Foreign firms were reluctant to build factories where bombs and disruption were a risk, and tourism fell sharply. Belfast's city centre was ringed by security cordons, the so-called ring of steel, which harmed trade, and businesses faced high insurance premiums, with some moving to Britain or the Republic. The government's attempt to attract investment produced the De Lorean car factory at Dunmurry, which opened in 1981 with large British grants and loans and was meant to create about 2,000 jobs. It collapsed into receivership in 1982 and cost the taxpayer heavily, becoming a symbol of how hard it was to bring in investment.\n\nThe structure of the economy also changed. Manufacturing, including shipbuilding, linen and engineering, continued to decline through the 1970s and 1980s, and Harland and Wolff was taken into public ownership in 1975 and Short Brothers in 1977 to protect jobs. At the same time public-sector employment in health, education, administration and especially security grew, with the RUC, the Ulster Defence Regiment and the prison service providing many jobs. British subvention rose and kept living standards above what local output could support, but it left the province exposed to decisions on British spending and dependent on Westminster.\n\nUnemployment was the highest of any UK region for much of the period, and Catholic unemployment was consistently higher than Protestant, in some surveys roughly twice as high. West Belfast and Derry suffered the worst deprivation, and joblessness among young Catholic men in the 1980s was a factor in paramilitary recruitment. The Fair Employment Act of 1976 set up the Fair Employment Agency, and the 1989 Act created a Fair Employment Commission and required employers to monitor their workforces, while Irish-American campaigners promoted the MacBride Principles in 1984. These were real steps, but the gap between the communities was not closed.\n\nOverall, the Troubles left a society more divided and an economy more dependent on the state. The costs fell on both communities, but hardest on working-class areas, and historians see violence and inequality as feeding one another. Reforms in housing and employment showed progress, but the dominant legacy of the period was segregation, deprivation and loss.",
