    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Gerry Adams had a major influence on the affairs of Northern Ireland as the leader who moved Sinn Féin from the margins into electoral politics, as the republican voice in the dialogue that helped bring about the IRA ceasefire, and as a negotiator of the Belfast Agreement and its later implementation. His influence was also controversial: unionists and many others believed he used politics to complement the IRA's campaign, and he always denied membership of the IRA.\n\nAdams was born in 1948 in Belfast and grew up in a republican family in West Belfast. He was interned in the early 1970s, and in July 1972 he was released to take part in the secret talks between the Provisional IRA and the Secretary of State, William Whitelaw, in London. From the beginning, therefore, he was seen as a link between the republican movement and the British government. After he was elected president of Sinn Féin in November 1983, a post he held until 2018, he shaped the movement's direction for over three decades.\n\nHis first major influence was the shift to electoral politics. After the hunger strikes of 1981, in which Bobby Sands was elected MP for Fermanagh and South Tyrone before dying on 5 May 1981, republicans saw the potential of the ballot box, and Danny Morrison spoke of 'the armalite and the ballot box'. Sinn Féin won five seats in the 1982 Assembly election, and in June 1983 Adams himself took West Belfast at Westminster from Gerry Fitt, though Sinn Féin members refused to take their seats. Under his leadership the party also dropped its refusal to sit in a Dáil, in 1986. This turned Sinn Féin into a serious rival to the SDLP for nationalist votes, which altered the balance of nationalist politics. He lost the Westminster seat to the SDLP's Joe Hendron in 1992 and regained it in 1997.\n\nAdams was also a target of the state's efforts to isolate republicans. He was shot and wounded by loyalist gunmen in Belfast in March 1984, and in October 1988 the Home Secretary, Douglas Hurd, banned the broadcast of the voices of Sinn Féin spokesmen, so that broadcasters used actors' voices. The ban lasted until 1994. Even so, he remained the figure whom governments eventually had to deal with.\n\nHis most important influence on the road to peace was the dialogue with the SDLP leader, John Hume. Their talks began in 1988 and resumed in 1991, producing joint statements in 1993. The talks were bitterly criticised by unionists and by some nationalists while the IRA campaign continued, but they persuaded the Irish government under Albert Reynolds that republicans might be drawn into a peaceful process. This contributed to the Downing Street Declaration of 15 December 1993, which affirmed that a united Ireland required the consent of a majority in Northern Ireland and invited republicans to renounce violence and join talks. In 1994 the American government under President Clinton allowed Adams a visa to visit the United States, which strengthened his position within republicanism, and the IRA declared a ceasefire on 31 August 1994.\n\nThe peace process was not smooth. The IRA ended the ceasefire with the Canary Wharf bomb in February 1996, and Sinn Féin was kept out of talks until the ceasefire was restored in July 1997. Sinn Féin then joined the multi-party negotiations, and Adams was a central figure in them, which produced the Belfast Agreement of 10 April 1998. It provided for an Assembly, a power-sharing executive, North-South bodies and a principle of consent, and Adams persuaded the republican movement to accept an arrangement within Northern Ireland, a historic change from the traditional demand for British withdrawal. Decommissioning of IRA weapons proved a long obstacle and, with the IRA's formal end to its armed campaign in 2005, republicans moved towards accepting policing.\n\nThe culmination came in 2007, when Sinn Féin agreed to support the police and entered a power-sharing executive with Ian Paisley's Democratic Unionists, with Martin McGuinness as deputy First Minister. Adams's critics argue that he was a leader of the IRA, that Sinn Féin's strategy of political involvement alongside violence prolonged the conflict, and that he failed to account for IRA actions. His supporters claim that he led republicans from armed struggle to negotiation. Whatever the judgement, few individuals did more to change the course of Northern Ireland's affairs. Sinn Féin became the largest nationalist party and Adams, once banned from the airwaves, became a central participant in the settlement.",
