    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Jack Lynch and Charles Haughey were the two dominant figures of Fianna Fáil in the 1960s, 1970s and 1980s, and between them they shaped how the state responded to the Troubles, to economic crisis and to the party's own divisions. Lynch contributed steadiness and moderation, particularly on Northern Ireland, but his second government's finances were reckless. Haughey was a more divisive figure whose early record on the public finances was poor, but whose government after 1987 helped to prepare the recovery of the 1990s.\n\nJack Lynch was born in Cork in 1917, won six All-Ireland hurling medals in a row, qualified as a barrister and entered the Dáil in 1948. He served as Minister for Education, Industry and Commerce and Finance before becoming Taoiseach on 10 November 1966 as a compromise choice between rival candidates. His popularity and conciliatory manner kept the party together, and he won the 1969 general election. His greatest challenge was the outbreak of violence in Northern Ireland. In August 1969, after violence in Derry and Belfast, he declared in a broadcast that the state could not stand by, and the army set up field hospitals near the border, but he ruled out military intervention.\n\nLynch's policy was to seek unity by consent and to oppose violence. He condemned internment in August 1971 and recalled the Irish ambassador from London after Bloody Sunday in January 1972. When bombs exploded in Dublin in December 1972, his government secured amendments to the Offences Against the State Act to deal with the IRA. By keeping the state out of the conflict while protesting at British policy, he avoided drawing the Republic into the Troubles, which was his most important contribution to Irish affairs.\n\nThe Arms Crisis of 1970 tested his authority. In April 1970 the Special Branch told him that senior ministers might be involved in a plan to import arms for Northern nationalists. On 6 May 1970 he dismissed Haughey, then Minister for Finance, and Neil Blaney, and Kevin Boland resigned in protest. Haughey and others were charged with conspiracy to import arms illegally and were acquitted in October 1970. Haughey always maintained that the government had known of the plan, and who knew what remains disputed. The crisis left a lasting division between Lynch's supporters and Haughey's that continued into the 1980s, and critics said Lynch was slow to act against his ministers.\n\nLynch's second term, from 1977, was less successful. Fianna Fáil promised to abolish domestic rates and the tax on cars and to create jobs by increasing spending, and won 84 of 148 seats, the largest majority in the history of the state. The government cut taxes and increased spending, financed by borrowing, and the budget deficit and national debt rose sharply just as a second oil shock, after the Iranian Revolution of 1979, drove up costs. Ireland joined the European Monetary System in March 1979, ending the link with sterling. Two by-election defeats in Cork in November 1979 weakened Lynch, and he resigned in December 1979.\n\nCharles Haughey, born in Castlebar in 1925, trained as an accountant, married Maureen Lemass and became very wealthy. After his dismissal he rebuilt his support through the 1970s, returned as Minister for Health and Social Welfare in 1977, and defeated George Colley by 44 votes to 38 to become Taoiseach on 11 December 1979. In a television address on 9 January 1980 he told the country it was living beyond its means and called for sacrifice, yet spending and borrowing continued to rise. His first government lasted until June 1981, and he returned briefly in 1982, a year of instability that included the phone-tapping scandal and the arrest of Malcolm MacArthur in the Attorney General's apartment, which Conor Cruise O'Brien labelled GUBU.\n\nHaughey's most constructive contribution came after he returned in March 1987. His minority government, supported by Fine Gael under Alan Dukes through the Tallaght Strategy, cut public spending sharply under Ray MacSharry as Minister for Finance. The Programme for National Recovery of October 1987 with unions, employers and farmers held pay increases low in return for a commitment to reduce the deficit. These policies, whatever Haughey's motives, helped lay the ground for recovery in the 1990s. Against that, the tribunals of the 1990s and 2000s found that he had received large sums from businessmen, which badly damaged his reputation.\n\nOverall, Lynch's lasting contribution was to keep the state out of the Northern conflict and the party united, though at a cost in indecision and fiscal recklessness, while Haughey's was to dominate the 1980s and, late in his career, to impose the discipline that began Ireland's recovery. Historians see Lynch as a skilful manager and Haughey as gifted but flawed, and both left a mixed legacy.",
