    "label": "1",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The revolutions of 1848 broke out because several long-term pressures came together with a sudden economic crisis, and because the example of Paris showed that the old order could be overthrown. The main factors were economic hardship after the failed harvests of 1846 and 1847, the liberal demand for constitutions, the growth of nationalism, and the weakness of the governments set up in 1815.\n\nThe immediate trigger was economic distress. The potato blight from 1845 and poor grain harvests in 1846 and 1847 sharply raised food prices and caused hunger riots across western and central Europe. A commercial and industrial depression in 1847 followed, bringing bankruptcies and unemployment and hitting artisans already threatened by factory competition. The Silesian weavers' rising of 1844 had shown how desperate hand workers had become. These hardships were felt most by the poor, who blamed governments that did little to help them. Hunger and unemployment gave the revolutions their crowds and made ordinary people ready to join the middle-class opposition in the streets.\n\nLiberalism was a second major factor. Educated middle-class men wanted constitutions, freedom of the press and assembly, trial by jury and elected parliaments. The settlement made at Vienna in 1815 had ignored these hopes, and Metternich's system of censorship and police, backed by the Carlsbad Decrees of 1819 and the Six Articles of 1832, had suppressed them without removing them. By the 1840s opposition was growing openly, and the southern German state parliaments had become centres of liberal demands. In Prussia the United Diet of 1847 refused to vote a railway loan without constitutional concessions. When rulers faltered in 1848, liberals were ready with a programme, as at the Mannheim meeting of 27 February, which demanded a free press, jury trial, citizens' militias and a German parliament.\n\nNationalism was a third factor. Germans wanted a nation state in place of the 39 states of the German Confederation, whose Diet at Frankfurt was controlled by Austria and had no elected members. Italians wanted freedom from Austrian domination and unity, and Hungarians and Czechs wanted self-rule within the Habsburg Empire. The Zollverein of 1834 had begun to teach Germans the benefits of unity under Prussian leadership, and the student festivals at Wartburg in 1817 and Hambach in 1832 had kept the national idea alive. In the multinational Habsburg lands nationalism threatened the empire itself, so it was especially fragile once the government's authority was shaken. Peasants in the south and west of the German lands, meanwhile, still owed feudal dues, and they rose in the spring of 1848, widening the revolution beyond the middle class.\n\nThe example and weakness of the governments completed the picture. The revolution began in Palermo in January 1848 and in Paris on 22–24 February, when Louis-Philippe fell and the Second Republic was declared. Newspapers, railways and the telegraph spread the news quickly, so that each success encouraged the next. Vienna rose on 13 March, when Metternich, the main defender of the 1815 system, was forced to resign and flee, followed by Milan, Venice, Berlin on 18 March, Budapest and Prague. Rulers across the German states, including Frederick William IV after the barricade fighting in Berlin, gave way rather than risk being overthrown. The speed of the collapse shows how little real support the system had once its architect had gone, and the liberal March ministries that followed show how ready the opposition was to take power.\n\nIn conclusion, the revolutions were caused by a combination of long-term liberal and national frustration with the settlement of 1815 and a short-term economic crisis that brought hunger and unemployment. The fall of Louis-Philippe supplied the spark, and Metternich's fall removed the main support of the old order. Without the crisis of 1846 and 1847 it is doubtful that discontent would have exploded into revolution at that moment.",
