    "label": "2",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Robert Owen and Robert Peel pursued very different aims in very different ways. Owen was a mill manager who tried to prove that co-operation and good conditions could replace harsh competitive capitalism, while Peel was a statesman who sought to modernise the state and economy within the existing social order. Peel was clearly the more successful, since his reforms became permanent national policy, whereas Owen succeeded in one community and in influencing opinion but failed in his larger aim of transforming society.\n\nOwen's greatest success was at New Lanark. He became manager in January 1800 and found long hours, child labour, dirty housing and a workforce marked by drunkenness and theft. He stopped taking pauper children as apprentices, raised the minimum age of employment so that children under ten no longer worked in the mills, and by 1816 had reduced the working day to ten and three-quarter hours. He replaced punishment with the Silent Monitor, a block with four coloured sides showing each worker's conduct, and he kept paying wages during the American embargo of 1806. He improved housing and streets, opened a store selling good-quality goods near cost price and controlled the sale of drink. The Institute for the Formation of Character and the infant school, both opened in 1816, were pioneering. Visitors came from across Europe to see it. Crucially, the mills stayed profitable, so his aim of showing that welfare and profit could go together was achieved.\n\nOwen was less successful beyond New Lanark. In A New View of Society, published 1813–1816, he argued that character is formed by circumstances, and he gave evidence to a parliamentary committee in 1816. But the Cotton Mills and Factories Act of 1819, which barred children under nine from cotton mills and limited older children to twelve hours a day, had no factory inspectors and was poorly enforced. His reputation suffered when he denounced all organised religion at a public meeting in 1817. His plans for self-supporting co-operative villages were not adopted, New Harmony in Indiana, founded in 1825, collapsed within a few years, and the Grand National Consolidated Trades Union that he led in 1834 collapsed within months. New Lanark itself was hard to copy because it depended on his personal control, and he broke with his partners and left it in about 1829. Yet his writings inspired the co-operative movement, notably the Rochdale Pioneers' store of 1844.\n\nPeel, by contrast, achieved most of what he set out to do, because he worked from within government. As Home Secretary he founded the Metropolitan Police in 1829, a professional force that became a model for others. With Wellington he conceded Catholic emancipation in 1829 to avoid conflict in Ireland. The Tamworth Manifesto of 1834 set out a moderate reforming Conservatism that became the basis of the modern party.\n\nAs Prime Minister from 1841 to 1846 he reintroduced income tax in 1842 to pay for cuts in tariffs, and his budgets of 1842 and 1845 reduced duties on many imports. He passed the Bank Charter Act of 1844 regulating banknotes and the Factory Act of 1844 limiting hours for women and children. These measures stabilised finance, promoted trade and helped the British economy to grow. His repeal of the Corn Laws in 1846, prompted partly by the Irish potato famine and supported by Cobden and Bright of the Anti-Corn Law League, established free trade, but it split the Conservative Party and forced his resignation in June 1846. Disraeli attacked him as a traitor, yet historians such as Norman Gash rank him among the century's greatest statesmen.\n\nOverall, Peel was the more successful in advancing his aims, since his police, banking and tariff reforms became part of the national system. Owen proved at New Lanark that humane treatment and profit could coexist and inspired later movements, but the co-operative society he wanted never came about, and his reforms depended on his own personal control.",
