    "model": "Anti-Semitism developed in both France and Russia between 1871 and 1920, but in different ways. In France it grew from nationalist and racial prejudice and reached its peak in the Dreyfus Affair, in a republic where the law ultimately protected Jews. In Russia it was encouraged by the state through discriminatory laws and tolerated violence, and it drove mass emigration.\n\nIn France, anti-Semitism grew from the humiliation of defeat in 1871 and from economic anxiety. Nationalists blamed Jews and Freemasons for the country's weakness. The word anti-Semitism itself had been coined by Wilhelm Marr in Germany in 1879, and the new racial idea spread across Europe. In France it was given a popular voice by Édouard Drumont, whose book La France juive of 1886 became a bestseller by portraying Jews as a threat to France, and who founded the newspaper La Libre Parole in 1892. This created a climate in which Jews could be treated as outsiders.\n\nThe Dreyfus Affair turned prejudice into a national crisis. In October 1894 Captain Alfred Dreyfus, a Jewish artillery officer from Alsace, was arrested and accused of passing secrets to Germany, and a secret court martial convicted him in December and sent him to Devil's Island. In 1896 Colonel Picquart found that the real author of the leaked memorandum was Major Esterhazy, but the army suppressed the evidence and Esterhazy was acquitted in January 1898. Anti-Semitism was therefore used to protect the honour of the army, which preferred to keep an innocent Jew in prison.\n\nFrance then divided into Dreyfusards and anti-Dreyfusards. On 13 January 1898 Émile Zola published his open letter J'accuse in L'Aurore, accusing the army of a cover-up, and he was convicted of libel and fled to England. Republicans, socialists like Jaurès and many intellectuals supported Dreyfus, while the army, much of the Catholic Church, nationalists and monarchists opposed him. A second court martial at Rennes in 1899 found him guilty again, and President Loubet pardoned him, but in 1906 the court of appeals cleared him and he was reinstated in the army. The outcome showed that republican justice could defeat anti-Semitism, though the prejudice was not removed. The Affair weakened the army and the Church and led to anticlerical laws in the early 1900s, and it also influenced Theodor Herzl, who covered the case and published Der Judenstaat in 1896, leading to the First Zionist Congress at Basel in 1897.\n\nIn Russia, the position of Jews was far worse, because discrimination was official policy. About five million Jews lived in the empire, the largest Jewish community in the world, and most were confined to the Pale of Settlement, created in 1791, where many were poor artisans and traders. After Alexander II was assassinated in March 1881, rumours blamed the Jews and pogroms swept southern Russia, beginning at Elizavetgrad in April 1881, while police and troops were slow to intervene.\n\nThe state then turned to legal restriction. The May Laws of 1882 banned Jews from settling in rural parts of the Pale and from owning land, quotas limited Jewish places in schools and universities from 1887, and Jews were expelled from Moscow in 1891 and 1892. Officials such as Pobedonostsev, adviser to Alexander III and Nicholas II, supported discrimination and Russification, and the Orthodox Church and nationalist groups such as the Black Hundreds spread anti-Semitic propaganda.\n\nViolence returned in the twentieth century. The Kishinev pogrom of 6 and 7 April 1903 killed 49 Jews and injured hundreds, and caused international outrage, and more pogroms followed in 1905 and 1906 during the revolution. The Protocols of the Elders of Zion, which claimed a Jewish plot to dominate the world, was first published in Russia in 1903, and was later shown to be a forgery. Its influence spread widely after 1917. Between 1881 and 1914 about two million Jews left the empire, most for the United States, with others going to Britain and Palestine, while many who stayed joined the Bund, founded in 1897, or the Zionists.\n\nIn short, anti-Semitism in France developed from nationalism and found its climax in the Dreyfus Affair, which divided the country but ended in vindication, while in Russia it was driven by the state, with laws, pogroms and propaganda that pushed millions to emigrate. In both countries Jews were made scapegoats for wider national fears.",
