    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Douglas Haig and Woodrow Wilson both left a deep mark on war and peace in Europe, but in different ways. Haig, commander of the British Expeditionary Force from December 1915, shaped how the war was fought on the Western Front and led the British army to victory in 1918. Wilson brought the United States into the war in 1917, supplied the principles on which the armistice and peace were based, and founded the League of Nations, though he failed to bring America into it.\n\nHaig's contribution in 1916 and 1917 was costly. He commanded the Somme offensive, launched on 1 July 1916 partly to relieve the French at Verdun. The British Army lost about 57,000 casualties on the first day, about 19,000 of them killed, the worst single day in its history, and the line advanced only a few kilometres. The battle destroyed Kitchener's volunteer Pals battalions and wiped out the young men of whole communities. At Passchendaele in 1917 rain and shelling turned the ground to mud and the final gain was only about 8 kilometres. These battles made Haig a symbol of waste. Lloyd George distrusted him and withheld reinforcements in early 1918, historians such as Denis Winter and Gerard De Groot depict him as callous, and Alan Clark popularised the phrase 'lions led by donkeys'.\n\nHowever, Haig's defenders such as John Terraine and Gary Sheffield argue that he adapted and learned from experience, and his decisive contribution came in 1918. Britain first used tanks at Flers-Courcelette in September 1916, and at Cambrai in November 1917 hundreds were used together with artillery and infantry. When Ludendorff's Spring Offensive began on 21 March 1918, the British line held, and Haig issued his 'backs to the wall' order on 11 April. The Allies set up a unified command under Foch that month. From the Battle of Amiens on 8 August 1918, which Ludendorff called the black day of the German army, British forces played the leading part in the Hundred Days, using tanks, aircraft and artillery together to drive the Germans back to the Hindenburg Line. On 29 September Ludendorff told the Kaiser that Germany needed an armistice, which was signed at Compiègne on 11 November. Haig therefore helped bring the war to an end.\n\nWilson's contribution began with American entry and the war aims he set out. He kept the USA neutral from 1914, but after Germany resumed unrestricted submarine warfare and the Zimmermann Telegram was revealed, he asked Congress on 2 April 1917 to declare war so that the world might be made safe for democracy, and war was declared on 6 April. American troops under Pershing arrived in strength in 1918, bringing fresh men and resources to an Allied side that had held the Spring Offensive. On 8 January 1918 Wilson set out the Fourteen Points, which called for open diplomacy, freedom of the seas, reduced armaments, the return of Alsace-Lorraine, an independent Poland and a general association of nations. Germany asked for an armistice in October 1918 on the basis of these points, so Wilson shaped how the war ended.\n\nAt the Paris Peace Conference in 1919 Wilson, one of the Big Four with Lloyd George, Clemenceau and Orlando and the first US president to travel to Europe in office, wanted a just peace based on self-determination. He insisted that the League of Nations Covenant be part of the Treaty of Versailles, and new democratic states appeared where empires had ruled. But he compromised on reparations, Article 231 and the Saar to win agreement on the League, and self-determination was applied unevenly: the Irish case, put by Seán T. O'Kelly, was not heard. His greatest failure was at home. Senator Henry Cabot Lodge and the Republicans objected to Article 10, which could oblige the USA to defend other states. Wilson toured the country in September 1919, suffered a stroke on 2 October and refused to compromise, and the Senate rejected the treaty in November 1919 and March 1920. The USA made a separate peace with Germany in 1921, and without the strongest Western power the League was weakened.\n\nBoth men made a real contribution, but of different kinds. Haig's was military: costly and controversial in 1916 and 1917, but central to the Allied victory in 1918. Wilson's was political: he helped bring the Americans in, gave the peace its ideals and founded the League, but his own inflexibility left the settlement without the support it most needed. Haig's contribution to war was the more decisive, while Wilson's contribution to peace was the more lasting and the more flawed.",
