    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Marie Curie and women in the workforce during the First World War were both important, in different ways. Curie's work on radioactivity changed physics and medicine and made her a pioneer for women in science, while the war work of women replaced men in factories, farms and offices, showed what women could do, and helped to win some of them the vote.\n\nMarie Curie was born Maria Skłodowska in Warsaw on 7 November 1867, when Poland was under Russian rule. Women could not attend university there, so in 1891 she went to the Sorbonne in Paris to study physics and mathematics. After Becquerel found in 1896 that uranium gave off penetrating rays, she began research on them for her doctorate and coined the term 'radioactivity'. She found that pitchblende was more radioactive than the uranium it contained and concluded that it must hold new elements. In 1898 she and her husband Pierre announced polonium, named after her homeland, and radium, and by 1902 she had separated radium salt from tonnes of pitchblende. Her importance lay in founding the study of radioactivity.\n\nHer importance was recognised internationally and applied to the war. In 1903 she shared the Nobel Prize for Physics with Pierre Curie and Becquerel, the first woman to win a Nobel Prize. After Pierre was killed in 1906 she became the first woman professor at the Sorbonne, and in 1911 she won the Nobel Prize for Chemistry, the first person to win Nobel Prizes in two different sciences. Her success was not easy, since in 1911 the French Academy of Sciences rejected her membership by a narrow vote and the press attacked her, partly on xenophobic grounds, as a foreign woman in a leading scientific position. In the war she organised mobile X-ray units, the 'petites Curies', for field hospitals and trained women to run them with her daughter Irène, so X-ray equipment was brought close to the front. Her importance was scientific, medical and symbolic.\n\nWomen in the workforce during the war were important through the scale of their contribution. Before 1914 most women who earned wages were young and unmarried, working in domestic service, textiles and clothing for pay typically half or less than men's, and they were largely excluded from skilled trades. After the Shell Crisis of 1915, the Treasury Agreement of March 1915 accepted 'dilution' and the Munitions of War Act of July 1915 supported it, and hundreds of thousands of women worked as munitionettes, filling shells and fuses in factories such as Woolwich Arsenal. Women also became clerks and typists, tram and railway conductors, postal workers and farm labourers in the Women's Land Army of 1917, and served as nurses in the VADs and in the WAAC and the WRNS. In France women made up a large share of the workers in shell factories such as Renault at Billancourt, and in Germany firms such as Krupp and Siemens employed more women.\n\nThe work was hard and its rewards limited. Shifts of 10 to 12 hours were common, TNT poisoning turned skin yellow and earned the women the name 'canary girls', and explosions at Silvertown in January 1917 killed 73 and at Chilwell in July 1918 over 130. Women were paid less than men, and the unions agreed to dilution only on condition that pre-war practices were restored. When the war ended the Restoration of Pre-War Practices Act of 1919 reinstated the old trade rules, many women were dismissed from munitions, and some returned to domestic service.\n\nPolitically the importance was greater. The Pankhursts suspended the suffragette campaign in 1914 and the WSPU organised a 'Right to Serve' march in 1915, and Asquith said in 1917 that women had earned the vote by their war work. The Representation of the People Act of February 1918 gave the vote to about 8 million women over 30 who met a property qualification, and Constance Markievicz became the first woman elected to Westminster. In Germany all women over 20 got the vote in November 1918 and 37 were elected to the National Assembly. Historians such as Marwick see the war as a catalyst, while Braybon argues that the change was limited and temporary and that pre-war campaigning mattered as much. Both subjects were important, but Curie's importance was individual and lasting, in science and as an example, while women's war work was collective, changing attitudes and winning the vote even though many of the jobs were lost after 1918.",
