    "marks": 100,
    "model": "In the years 1920 to 1945 Britain faced two very different sets of problems. In peace the central problems were mass unemployment, the decline of the old staple industries, industrial conflict and a deep divide between the prosperous south and the depressed regions. In war the problems were the protection and feeding of a civilian population under air attack and the mobilisation of labour for total war. The peacetime problems proved the more deeply rooted, while the wartime ones were met more successfully.\n\nThe first peacetime problem was the collapse of the staple industries. A short post-war boom gave way to a sharp slump, and unemployment rose above two million by mid-1921 as export markets were lost. The Geddes Axe of 1922 cut public spending on education, housing and the armed services, which deepened the crisis. Coal, cotton, shipbuilding, iron and steel, the basis of the Victorian economy, declined steadily. Lancashire cotton lost markets to Japan and India, and shipyards on the Tyne, Wear and Clyde closed as world trade collapsed. Economic weakness was therefore a permanent feature of the inter-war years and not a passing difficulty.\n\nIndustrial conflict followed directly from this decline. In April 1925 Churchill, as Chancellor, returned sterling to the gold standard at the pre-war rate, and Keynes argued in The Economic Consequences of Mr Churchill that the overvalued pound would make exports uncompetitive and force wages down. The coal owners demanded wage cuts and longer hours, the miners refused, and the TUC called a General Strike from 4 to 12 May 1926. The government used volunteers and the Organisation for the Maintenance of Supplies to keep services running, and the TUC called the strike off after nine days. The miners were forced back on worse terms, and the Trade Disputes Act of 1927 banned sympathetic strikes.\n\nThe Depression that followed the Wall Street Crash brought the worst crisis and a divided country. Unemployment peaked at about three million in 1932 and 1933, and the second Labour government split over cuts in unemployment benefit in 1931. MacDonald formed a National Government in August, Britain left the gold standard on 21 September 1931, and tariffs and cheap money followed in 1932. These measures helped recovery, but not in the worst-hit areas. In the south-east and Midlands new industries such as motor vehicles and electrical goods grew, about four million houses were built and families in work bought radios and cars. In many towns in Wales, the north-east and central Scotland unemployment stayed above 30 per cent, and it was higher still in Jarrow and Merthyr Tydfil. Families on the dole faced the household means test, and the Special Areas Act of 1934 offered only modest help. Hunger marches, such as the national march of 1932, and books such as Orwell's The Road to Wigan Pier exposed the malnutrition and despair that resulted.\n\nWar brought a different threat, the bombing of civilians. Operation Pied Piper began on 1 September 1939 and moved about 1.5 million people, mostly children, out of the cities. It exposed the gap between urban poverty and rural comfort, since some host families were shocked by the hygiene and poverty of the evacuees, and many returned during the Phoney War. The Blitz began on 7 September 1940, London was bombed on 57 consecutive nights and about 40,000 civilians were killed, with Coventry, Liverpool and Birmingham also badly hit. Anderson shelters, the Underground, ARP wardens and the Home Guard were organised to cope, and V-1 and V-2 attacks followed in 1944.\n\nThe war also created shortages and a need to direct labour. Rationing of bacon, butter and sugar began in January 1940, and the Ministry of Food under Lord Woolton, with its Dig for Victory campaign, tried to keep the population healthy. Ernest Bevin as Minister of Labour directed workers into war industries, and the National Service Act of December 1941 brought single women into factories and the services. The shared hardship fed the Beveridge Report of December 1942, which named want, disease, ignorance, squalor and idleness as the five giants to be defeated. In judgement, the peacetime problems were the more deeply rooted, since mass unemployment lasted for most of two decades and the depressed regions recovered fully only when rearmament created new demand at the end of the 1930s. The wartime problems were met more successfully, because rationing, evacuation and civil defence were seen as fair and shared, and the war in effect ended mass unemployment and prepared the way for the welfare state.",
