    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Radio and cinema were the two great mass media of the years between 1920 and 1945, and together they created the first truly mass popular culture. Radio brought music, news and the voices of leaders into the home, while cinema, above all Hollywood cinema, gave millions a shared weekly entertainment and spread American style across Britain and Europe. Their contribution was to unite audiences in a common culture, to turn entertainers into world stars and, in wartime, to serve as vital instruments of morale and persuasion.\n\nRadio became a mass medium in the 1920s and 1930s and changed how people received entertainment. The British Broadcasting Company was founded in 1922 and became the British Broadcasting Corporation in 1927 under John Reith, with a public service aim to inform, educate and entertain. In the United States the networks NBC, founded in 1926, and CBS, founded in 1927, were financed by advertising and competed for audiences, which pushed them towards popular music, comedy and drama. Listeners in towns and villages alike could now share the same programmes at the same time, something no earlier medium had achieved, and this was the foundation of a national and then international popular culture.\n\nRadio also changed the relationship between leaders and people. President Franklin Roosevelt began his fireside chats in March 1933, speaking directly to Americans about the New Deal and later the war, and his calm voice built trust at a time of depression. In Britain during the war the BBC carried news, music and government information, and Churchill broadcast to the nation at key moments. The BBC's bulletins earned a reputation for trustworthy reporting, in contrast to German propaganda such as the broadcasts of William Joyce, known as Lord Haw-Haw, from 1939. Radio therefore contributed not only entertainment but also public trust and national unity in a time of crisis.\n\nRadio was also the carrier of new popular music and stars, and here Bing Crosby is the best example. He became the most popular entertainer of his age through radio shows and records in the 1930s, and his relaxed crooning style was made possible by microphone technique. His recording of White Christmas, released in 1942, became one of the best-selling songs of all time and was hugely popular with troops far from home, while the American Forces Network, broadcasting from 1942, played American popular music to a wide audience in Britain and Europe. Vera Lynn's We'll Meet Again became a symbol of separation and hope for servicemen and families. Radio thus spread musical tastes across frontiers and made entertainers into household names.\n\nCinema was the most popular mass entertainment of the 1930s and the weekly habit of millions, including working-class people and young women. The Jazz Singer in 1927 introduced synchronised sound and led to the rapid spread of the talkies. The Hollywood studio system, dominated by MGM, Paramount, Warner Brothers and Fox, produced and distributed most of the films shown in Britain and Europe, so that by the late 1930s American films filled most screens. Stars such as Charlie Chaplin were known worldwide, and his films showed that cinema could do more than entertain: Modern Times in 1936 satirised mechanised industrial life, and The Great Dictator in 1940 mocked Hitler through the parody Adenoid Hynkel and was a major commercial success. Newsreels such as Pathe and British Movietone brought world events to audiences, though Germany and Italy used them for propaganda.\n\nCinema was also central to the war effort and to the debate over Americanisation. Britain's Ministry of Information backed films such as In Which We Serve in 1942, Hollywood produced Mrs Miniver, which won the Best Picture Oscar, and Casablanca in the same year, and Frank Capra's Why We Fight series explained the war to American troops. American dominance provoked reaction: Nazi Germany restricted American films from the mid-1930s until they were in effect excluded by 1940, and the dictatorships condemned jazz and swing as degenerate. Critics in Britain worried about the effect of Hollywood and American radio on language, taste and morals, and the arrival of American troops from 1942 brought both admiration and resentment.\n\nOverall, the contribution of radio and cinema was profound. They created a shared popular culture of the same music, humour, stars and news for millions, and they became powerful tools of governments for morale and propaganda, trusted in the case of the BBC and Roosevelt and manipulated in the dictatorships. Above all they spread American popular culture across Europe, an influence that outlasted the war and made American music and film dominant in the postwar world.",
