    "label": "1",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Sovietisation meant the process by which communist parties, backed by the Red Army and Soviet security police, took full control of the states of Eastern Europe and remodelled their politics, economies and institutions on the Soviet pattern. It was carried out between 1945 and 1949 in stages, by pressure, manipulation and force, until the region became a bloc of satellite states dominated by Moscow.\n\nThe starting point was Soviet military power. By May 1945 the Red Army occupied Poland, Romania, Bulgaria, Hungary, eastern Germany and much of Czechoslovakia, so the West could do little to shape events there. Stalin wanted a buffer of friendly governments after the invasions of 1914 and 1941. Yalta had promised free elections in the Declaration on Liberated Europe, but Stalin disregarded it and the Soviet Union backed the communist-dominated Lublin government in Poland. In 1945 sixteen leaders of the Polish underground were arrested and tried in Moscow, which showed that Moscow would not tolerate genuine opposition.\n\nThe usual method in the first phase was the coalition. Communist parties governed alongside peasant, socialist and liberal parties but took care to control key posts such as the interior ministry and the police, and then used them to destroy their partners step by step. The Hungarian communist leader Mátyás Rákosi is credited with the phrase salami tactics, slicing away opponents one thin piece at a time. In Hungary the communists won only about 17 per cent in the free election of November 1945, against about 57 per cent for the Smallholders, yet by using the police and fraud in the 1947 election they became the largest party and broke their rivals. Land reform in 1945 to 1947 broke up large estates and gave land to small farmers, which helped win support.\n\nElections were rigged and opponents removed across the region. The Polish peasant leader Stanisław Mikołajczyk left the country in October 1947 after rigged elections. In Bulgaria the opposition leader Nikola Petkov was executed in 1947, and in Romania King Michael was forced to abdicate on 30 December 1947 and a people's republic was proclaimed. Czechoslovakia was the only eastern state to remain democratic until 1948. In February that year non-communist ministers resigned over police control and Klement Gottwald's communists took power without a general election. The death of the foreign minister Jan Masaryk in March 1948, in a fall from a window, shocked Western opinion.\n\nOnce in control, the communists consolidated power through terror, party mergers and economic and political ties to Moscow. Show trials and purges eliminated rivals, including the Hungarian foreign minister László Rajk, executed in 1949. Socialist parties were absorbed into communist ones, as in the Soviet zone of Germany, where the Socialist Unity Party was formed in April 1946. The Cominform, founded in September 1947, linked the communist parties of Europe under Soviet direction, and Yugoslavia's expulsion in June 1948 showed that independence from Moscow would not be tolerated, although Tito survived because his communists had come to power largely through their own wartime resistance. Marshall Aid was refused under Soviet pressure, trade was tied to Moscow through the Molotov Plan and then Comecon, set up in January 1949, and the German Democratic Republic was proclaimed in the Soviet zone in October 1949.\n\nSovietisation was therefore far more than a defensive buffer. It was the systematic imposition of one-party rule, police control and a planned economy by means that moved from coalition to coercion, and by 1949 it had divided Europe into two blocs, the Iron Curtain that Churchill had described at Fulton in 1946.",
