    "label": "2",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Marshall Aid and the 1973 Oil Crisis stand at opposite ends of the post-war boom. Marshall Aid helped launch the recovery of Western Europe and bound it politically to the United States, while the Oil Crisis brought that long boom to an end and forced governments to rethink their economic policy.\n\nThe economic impact of Marshall Aid was real. Industry, transport and ports in Germany, France and Italy had been badly damaged and Western Europe could not pay for American goods without dollars. The European Recovery Programme, announced by George Marshall at Harvard on 5 June 1947, ran from 1948 to 1952 and provided about 13 billion dollars, mostly as grants, to sixteen states, with Britain, France and West Germany receiving the largest sums. It paid for imports of raw materials and machinery, and some counterpart funds went into industry and housing. It also encouraged cooperation: the Organisation for European Economic Co-operation, set up in April 1948, allocated the aid, and the European Payments Union of 1950 revived trade between members. Historians such as Alan Milward argue that it was a modest share of total investment and that domestic policy did much of the work, so it is best seen as a stimulus rather than the sole cause of recovery.\n\nThe political impact was equally important. The plan was designed to stop economic collapse from strengthening communist parties, notably in France and Italy, and it supported Christian Democratic and social democratic parties against them. It tied Western Europe closely to the USA. The Soviet Union forbade eastern states to take part, and the Soviet delegation walked out of the Paris conference in July 1947. The plan therefore divided Europe into two economic systems, and the Soviet response was the Cominform and later Comecon. It also encouraged habits of cooperation that fed into the later moves towards European unity.\n\nThe Oil Crisis struck an economy that had grown rapidly on cheap oil, which supplied around half of Western Europe's energy at about three dollars a barrel. After the Yom Kippur War began on 6 October 1973, OPEC's Gulf members raised the posted price by about 70 per cent on 16 October, and the Arab oil-producing states announced output cuts and an embargo on the USA and the Netherlands for supporting Israel. Prices roughly quadrupled within months. The economic impact was severe. Importing states fell into balance of payments deficit, industrial production fell, and stagflation, stagnation combined with rising prices, defeated conventional Keynesian policies. Unemployment rose from the low levels of the 1960s, with the sharpest increases in heavy industry.\n\nThe political impact was also profound. Governments introduced fuel saving, such as car-free Sundays in West Germany and the Netherlands, and Britain had a three-day working week in early 1974 during a miners' dispute. The post-war commitment to full employment was weakened and the defeat of inflation became the main aim, which opened the way for monetarist policies under Margaret Thatcher, prime minister from 1979. Governments sought energy independence through North Sea oil, nuclear power and conservation, as in France's programme of 1974, and the oil-producing states gained a new role in world politics.\n\nThe two events were therefore very different in effect. Marshall Aid helped to start a period of growth and gave Western Europe political stability and a firm Atlantic alignment, while the Oil Crisis ended that growth and ushered in an era of unemployment and economic restructuring. Even so, many economists argue that inflation, the collapse of Bretton Woods in 1971 and falling productivity were already weakening the boom, so the oil shock hastened rather than caused its end.",
