    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Between 1945 and 1992 youth and popular culture in Europe were transformed. Young people emerged as a distinct group with their own spending power, music and fashion, and popular culture became a mass phenomenon carried by records, television and international sport. These developments were shaped by affluence and new media, and they spread, though in different forms, across the Iron Curtain.\n\nThe first major development was the emergence of the teenager as a consumer. By the 1950s young people had money to spend on records, clothes and the cinema, and secondary education kept them in school longer, giving them a shared identity. Rock 'n' roll arrived in Europe in the mid-1950s, with Bill Haley's Rock Around the Clock reaching the British charts in 1955 and Elvis Presley's first hits in 1956. The cheap 45 rpm single, teenage magazines, dances and stations such as Radio Luxembourg created a youth market. Older generations saw this as a threat. Teddy boys in Britain were associated with delinquency, and films such as The Wild One fed the fear, which shows how youth culture challenged traditional authority. Later subcultures followed the same pattern. Mods and Rockers clashed on English beaches at Easter 1964 and created a moral panic, Mary Quant popularised the miniskirt, and punk, with the Sex Pistols and The Clash in 1976 and 1977, expressed the anger of unemployed young people in the recession.\n\nIn the 1960s popular music became a global industry led by the Beatles. Formed in Liverpool and refined in Hamburg and at the Cavern Club, they had their first British number one in 1963, and their appearance on The Ed Sullivan Show on 9 February 1964 won them a mass American audience. Albums such as Rubber Soul and Sgt. Pepper showed that pop could be an art form, and in June 1967 they performed on Our World, the first live global satellite broadcast. John Lennon made pop political, promoting peace through the Amsterdam bed-in of 1969. Youth culture also became openly political in 1968. Student protests began at Nanterre in March and moved to the Sorbonne in May, with the Night of the Barricades on 10 and 11 May and a general strike of about ten million workers that shook de Gaulle's government. In West Germany, Rudi Dutschke led protests against the Vietnam War and the Nazi past. The protests did not overthrow governments, but they changed attitudes to authority and sexuality and influenced the women's, environmental and peace movements of the 1970s. Live Aid on 13 July 1985 later showed young people's culture raising money for famine in Ethiopia before a global television audience.\n\nTelevision and the mass media were central to popular culture. Commercial ITV began in Britain in 1955, challenging the BBC's public-service monopoly, and the Coronation of 1953 persuaded many families to buy sets. By the 1970s most households in Western Europe owned a television. The Eurovision network, founded in 1954, carried the Eurovision Song Contest from 1956 and live sport across borders, while pirate stations such as Radio Caroline, from 1964, played the pop music the BBC would not. In the East, by contrast, the party used television for propaganda, though many East Germans could receive Western broadcasts.\n\nSport was the other great area of change. The European Champion Clubs' Cup began in 1955-56, and Real Madrid won its first five editions. West Germany's win in the 1954 World Cup final boosted national morale, and England's win at Wembley on 30 July 1966 was a television event across Europe. Television money, sponsorship and advertising made stars of players such as Pelé, Johan Cruyff and George Best. Sport also reflected the Cold War. The Soviet Union entered the Olympics in 1952, East Germany used state-sponsored doping to do very well in the medal table in the 1970s and 1980s, and the Games were disrupted by the Munich killings of Israeli athletes in 1972 and the boycotts of Moscow in 1980 and Los Angeles in 1984.\n\nIn the East, young people listened to Western music on Radio Luxembourg and Radio Free Europe and on hand-copied tapes, while the authorities tried to control rock. The stilyagi copied Western fashion in the Soviet Union, and the trial of the Plastic People of the Universe in 1976 helped to inspire Charter 77. Overall, the period saw youth and popular culture move from the margins to the centre of European life. Historians debate whether this was liberation or commercial manipulation, since record companies and advertisers shaped what young people bought, but it clearly broadened tastes, challenged the authority of schools and churches and gave young people a voice.",
