    "marks": 100,
    "model": "Europe played a large and often decisive role in the Arab-Israeli conflict between 1945 and 1990. European powers created the conditions in which the conflict began, Britain handed the problem to the United Nations and withdrew, Britain and France fought alongside Israel at Suez, and later Europe acted as arms supplier and diplomatic voice, though increasingly overshadowed by the United States and the Soviet Union.\n\nThe origins lay in European decisions before 1945. The Balfour Declaration of 2 November 1917 promised a national home for the Jewish people in Palestine, and Britain held the Mandate from 1923. Jewish immigration rose after the Nazis came to power in 1933, and the Jewish share of the population grew from about 11 per cent in 1922 to roughly 30 per cent by 1939. After the Arab Revolt of 1936-39 the White Paper of 1939 limited Jewish immigration to 75,000 over five years, which the Zionists rejected. The Holocaust, in which about six million Jews were killed in Europe, then intensified the demand for a Jewish state and for the admission of refugees. Europe's persecution of the Jews therefore drove the conflict.\n\nBritain's own role between 1945 and 1948 was to try to hold the ring and fail. It kept about 100,000 troops in Palestine and refused to admit 100,000 refugees recommended by the Anglo-American Committee in April 1946 without Arab agreement. It stopped refugee ships, and in 1947 the Exodus, with about 4,500 survivors, was sent back to Europe. The Irgun under Menachem Begin bombed the King David Hotel on 22 July 1946, killing 91 people, and attacks on British targets continued. Unable to reconcile the two sides, Britain referred the question to the United Nations in February 1947. The General Assembly passed Resolution 181 for partition on 29 November 1947, Britain abstained and refused to enforce it, and the Mandate expired on 14 May 1948, the day Israel was proclaimed, leaving a security vacuum into which the first Arab-Israeli war followed. Some 700,000 Palestinians fled or were expelled.\n\nThe Suez Crisis of 1956 was the clearest case of European intervention. Britain and France disliked Nasser's Arab nationalism, his arms deal with the Soviet bloc through Czechoslovakia in 1955, and, for France, his support for the rebels in Algeria. After he nationalised the Suez Canal Company on 26 July 1956, through which about two-thirds of Western Europe's oil passed, Britain and France signed the secret Protocol of Sevres with Israel on 24 October. Israel invaded Sinai on 29 October, Britain and France issued an ultimatum, and their paratroopers landed at Port Said on 5 November. American opposition, a run on the pound and the refusal of IMF support forced a ceasefire and withdrawal. The affair showed that Britain and France could no longer act as great powers without American support, and it moved influence in the region to the superpowers.\n\nAfter Suez Europe's role became more that of an arms supplier and diplomatic observer. France became Israel's main arms supplier in the late 1950s and 1960s and helped its nuclear programme, until de Gaulle placed an embargo on arms to Israel after the Six Day War of 1967, which began a French turn towards the Arab states. Britain also gradually left its bases and protectorates in the Arab world. European states were dependent on Arab oil, and after the 1973 war and the oil embargo many of them distanced themselves from Israel. In the 1980 Venice Declaration the European Community recognised the Palestinians' right to self-determination and called for the PLO to be associated with negotiations, though the United States kept the leading diplomatic role.\n\nIn conclusion, Europe's role was greatest at the start, when the Balfour Declaration, the Mandate and the Holocaust created the conflict and Britain's withdrawal let it erupt into war, and at Suez, where Britain and France intervened and were humiliated. After 1956 Europe was a secondary player, supplying arms, buying oil and offering statements, while the United States and the Soviet Union decided events. Europe's influence, though diminished, was never negligible.",
