    "label": "3",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "France's immigration after 1945 followed a clear pattern. Labour shortages drew in workers from southern Europe, North Africa and West Africa, Algerians being the largest group, and these migrants settled permanently and produced a French-born second generation. Government policy moved from encouraging labour migration to halting it in 1974, while trying to integrate the settled population through the assimilationist republican model, and the 1980s saw this policy tested by racial tension and the rise of the Front National.\n\nThe first pattern was labour migration to rebuild the country. Post-war reconstruction created shortages of workers, and migrants from Portugal, Spain, Italy, Morocco, Tunisia and West Africa filled jobs in factories, mines and construction. The government set up the National Immigration Office in 1945 to manage recruitment. Algerians were a special case, since they held French nationality until independence in 1962 and could move freely between Algeria and France, so their migration was not legally immigration at all. The Franco-Algerian accord of December 1968 then gave Algerian nationals preferential rights to enter and work in France, which kept the flow of workers large after independence.\n\nThe second pattern was the poor housing and treatment these migrants met. Many lived in bidonvilles, makeshift shanty settlements on the edges of cities such as Nanterre near Paris, and the state responded through hostels, Sonacotra having been founded in 1956 to house Algerian workers. Hostility was sometimes violent: on 17 October 1961 Paris police under Maurice Papon attacked a demonstration by Algerians against a curfew and killed dozens or more. The harkis who came to France after 1962 were housed in camps and met hostility and poor housing. The Algerian war therefore shaped how many French people saw North African migrants for decades.\n\nThe third element was a sharp change of policy in the 1970s. After the oil shock of 1973 ended the post-war boom, migrant workers were among the first to lose their jobs, and in July 1974 the government suspended labour immigration. In 1976 it allowed family reunification for migrants already living in France, which meant that settlement continued and a second generation, the beurs, grew up in the banlieues. After 1977 the government offered payments to migrants who agreed to go home, with little effect. Policy had tried to stop a migration that had already created permanent communities.\n\nThe fourth pattern was the French model of integration. It rested on universal citizenship, so that all citizens were equal before the law and the state did not recognise ethnic or religious communities. Migrants were expected to assimilate, adopting French language and civic values, and laicite, established by the law of 1905, shaped how public religious expression was regulated. France collected no statistics on ethnic origin, which made discrimination harder to measure, and its main anti-racist law was the Pleven law of 1972, which made incitement to racial hatred a criminal offence. This differed from Britain's multiculturalism and its Race Relations Acts.\n\nIn the 1980s these patterns produced open conflict. Youth unemployment and bad policing in the banlieues led to unrest at Les Minguettes in 1981. The Socialist government under Mitterrand regularised about 130,000 undocumented migrants, created a ten-year residence card in 1984 and started regeneration projects, though his promise of local voting rights for foreign residents was never carried out. The second generation organised the March for Equality in 1983, and SOS Racisme, with its slogan Touche pas a mon pote, was founded in 1984. Against this, Jean-Marie Le Pen's Front National broke through at Dreux in 1983, won about 11 per cent in the 1984 European elections and 35 seats in 1986, and Le Pen took about 14.4 per cent in the 1988 presidential election.\n\nGovernment policy swung with the political mood. The Chirac government of 1986 under Charles Pasqua tightened controls and made expulsion easier, and its plan to end automatic nationality at 18 was dropped after mass protests. The Socialists returned in 1988 and eased some restrictions through the Joxe law of 1989, favouring the word integration over assimilation. The headscarf affair at Creil in 1989 then showed the tension between laicite and Muslim identity, with the Conseil d'Etat ruling that headscarves were allowed unless ostentatious.\n\nOverall, France's pattern was labour migration that became permanent settlement, and its policy was a republican model of assimilation combined with restriction after 1974. Neither achievement nor failure was complete. Universal citizenship gave the second generation a claim to full French nationality, but the refusal to recognise ethnic discrimination and the neglect of the banlieues left real grievances, which the Front National exploited by 1990.",
