    "label": "4",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The decolonisation of Africa had important social and economic consequences, and the three topics named show both the gains and the limits. The Lome Conventions gave African states valuable trade access to Europe but did not end their dependence on a few exports. Julius Nyerere's ujamaa brought real social gains in Tanzania but cost the country its farm output. Development workers brought practical help to rural communities but met problems that showed how hard development was.\n\nThe Lome Conventions grew out of the earlier Yaounde Conventions of 1963 and 1969, which gave 18 associated African states duty-free access to European markets. Lome I, signed in Togo on 28 February 1975 between the EEC and 46 African, Caribbean and Pacific states, was a new departure because it gave their exports largely duty-free access without requiring reciprocal concessions. It set up STABEX to compensate states when earnings from certain agricultural exports fell, and a Sugar Protocol guaranteeing quotas at European prices. Lome II added SYSMIN for mineral exporters. These were real innovations in trade policy, but most ACP exports stayed concentrated on a few primary commodities and prices fell in the 1980s, so the economic dependence inherited from colonial times continued. Later Conventions added conditions on human rights and governance.\n\nNyerere's ujamaa was the most ambitious African attempt to answer the economic legacy of colonialism. The Arusha Declaration of 5 February 1967 committed Tanzania to African socialism, self-reliance and state control of the main means of production, and banks and major industries were nationalised. Social results were impressive: Education for Self-Reliance and universal primary education, launched in 1974, raised enrolment rapidly, and literacy and health improved by the early 1980s. Nyerere also refused to rule by ethnic favour and promoted national unity.\n\nThe economic results were poor. Ujamaa villages began as voluntary cooperatives, but from 1973-74 millions of people were moved into villages, often by force, and this villagisation disrupted farming. Food shortages followed, and the oil price rise, drought and the cost of the war against Idi Amin in 1978-79 deepened the crisis. Tanzania had to import food, the economy declined, and after Nyerere stepped down in 1985 his successor Mwinyi accepted an IMF programme in 1986 that reversed many socialist policies. Supporters stress his achievements in education and unity, critics the collapse of output and the use of compulsion.\n\nDevelopment workers carried out most of the practical aid work in rural Africa from the 1960s. They came from churches, bodies such as Voluntary Service Overseas and the American Peace Corps, and NGOs such as Concern, founded in Dublin in 1968 after the Biafran famine, and Trocaire, founded in 1973. They dug wells, advised on farming, gave vaccinations and primary health care, and distributed food in famines such as that in Ethiopia in 1983-85. Their problems were poor roads and supplies, cultural misunderstandings, the danger of civil war, and the difficulty of keeping projects going after the donor left. Their reports also fed campaigns in Europe, and the shift in the 1980s towards projects planned with local people answered the criticism of top-down aid.\n\nIn judging these consequences, there were genuine gains in trade preferences, schooling, health and the eradication of smallpox by 1977. There were also serious limits in debt, structural adjustment, famine and dependence on aid. Historians now explain this mix by combining colonial legacies, falling commodity prices, Cold War pressures and the choices of African governments.",
