    "label": "1",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "The US economy prospered to a very great extent between 1945 and about 1968, when it was the strongest in the world and living standards rose for most Americans. After 1968 prosperity was much less secure: Vietnam, inflation, the oil shocks and foreign competition caused serious problems, and the recovery of the 1980s was real but uneven and built on deficits. Prosperity was therefore large but not continuous or equally shared.\n\nThe post-war boom rested on strong foundations. The United States produced roughly half of the world's manufactured goods in 1945, while its European and Japanese rivals were devastated. Wartime savings and shortages created pent-up demand for cars, houses and appliances, and the GI Bill of 1944 and government mortgage guarantees supported a mass housing market. The country also designed the international system that suited it. The Bretton Woods conference of 1944 made the dollar the central currency, the Marshall Plan of 1948 to 1952 gave about 13 billion dollars to Western Europe, much of it spent on American goods, and GATT in 1947 lowered trade barriers for the world's biggest exporter.\n\nGovernment spending was a major source of growth. The Korean War and NSC-68 raised the defence budget from about 14 billion dollars in 1950 to about 50 billion by 1953, and defence contracts kept companies like Lockheed, Boeing and General Dynamics busy for decades, so that Eisenhower warned of the military-industrial complex in 1961. The Federal-Aid Highway Act of 1956 funded about 41,000 miles of Interstate highway, boosting car production, steel and suburban building, and the space programme after Sputnik employed thousands of engineers. Kennedy and Johnson's advisers used Keynesian ideas, and the tax cut of 1964, which reduced the top rate from 91 to 70 per cent, was followed by strong growth in the mid-1960s. Large companies such as General Motors, IBM and Coca-Cola also expanded abroad as multinationals, taking advantage of the European Economic Community after 1957.\n\nThis prosperity reached many ordinary households. Union membership peaked at about a third of non-farm workers in the mid-1950s, and the Treaty of Detroit between General Motors and the United Auto Workers in 1950 tied wages to productivity and gave pensions and health insurance. Home ownership spread through suburbs like Levittown, and television, credit cards and consumer goods became common, with a large middle class emerging. Yet even in the boom there were limits. Galbraith's The Affluent Society argued in 1958 that private wealth coexisted with neglected public services, Michael Harrington's The Other America of 1962 showed tens of millions in poverty, and black Americans and many southern workers were excluded from jobs, housing and GI Bill benefits.\n\nFrom the mid-1960s the foundations began to weaken. Johnson chose not to raise taxes to pay for Vietnam while expanding the Great Society, so spending rose faster than output and inflation, about 1 per cent in 1964, passed 4 per cent in 1968, while the balance of payments worsened as dollars flowed abroad. On 15 August 1971 Nixon suspended the convertibility of the dollar into gold, ending Bretton Woods, and 1971 was the first year since 1893 that the United States imported more than it exported. The oil embargo of October 1973 roughly quadrupled oil prices, and the recession of 1973 to 1975 was the deepest since the 1930s, with unemployment about 9 per cent. A second oil shock followed the Iranian Revolution, and inflation passed 13 per cent in 1980, the combination known as stagflation.\n\nInternational competition hollowed out heavy industry. Steel imports from Japan and Europe led to mill closures, and the Youngstown Sheet and Tube closure in 1977 cost about 5,000 jobs and symbolised the rust belt. Japanese cars such as Toyota and Honda, small and fuel-efficient after the oil shocks, took a growing share from Detroit, and Chrysler needed federal loan guarantees of 1.5 billion dollars in 1979. Real earnings of production workers stagnated from the early 1970s, and the poverty rate, which fell to about 11 per cent in 1973, rose to around 15 per cent in the early 1980s.\n\nThe 1980s brought recovery, but a mixed one. Paul Volcker's high interest rates beat inflation at the price of the 1981 to 1982 recession, when unemployment passed 10 per cent. Reagan's tax cuts of 1981, deregulation and defence spending then helped growth return, and the expansion from late 1982 lasted until 1990 and created about 18 million jobs. But the national debt nearly tripled, the United States became a net debtor in 1985, the trade deficit reached about 150 billion dollars in 1987, the Dow fell about 22 per cent on 19 October 1987, and the gains went mainly to higher earners.\n\nOverall, the economy prospered greatly between 1945 and 1968 and brought rising living standards to most Americans, though not to all. After 1968 prosperity was repeatedly interrupted, and the later recovery rested on borrowing and favoured the wealthy, so that by 1989 the United States remained the largest economy but no longer enjoyed the dominance of the early post-war decades.",
