    "label": "2",
    "marks": 100,
    "model": "On balance the Cold War in Europe had the greater impact on the United States, because it shaped American strategy, alliances and economy for forty years, although the Cold War in Asia, and above all Vietnam, did more damage to American society and confidence. Europe defined what the United States became as a superpower, while Asia tested and strained it.\n\nIn Europe the Cold War turned a country that had been isolationist before 1945 into the leader of a permanent alliance system. Kennan's Long Telegram of February 1946 and his article of 1947 gave containment its name, and the Truman Doctrine of March 1947 committed the United States to aid Greece and Turkey. The Marshall Plan followed, and about 13 billion dollars was given to Western Europe between 1948 and 1952, which rebuilt trading partners and helped to block communism. The North Atlantic Treaty of April 1949 then bound the United States to the defence of Western Europe, with Article 5 treating an attack on one member as an attack on all. This was a revolution in American foreign policy, and it lasted until the end of the Cold War.\n\nEuropean crises also brought the United States closest to direct confrontation with the Soviet Union. The Berlin blockade began in June 1948 and the Western powers answered with an airlift that carried more than two million tons of supplies before the blockade ended in May 1949. Khrushchev's ultimatum of 1958 led to the building of the Berlin Wall from August 1961, and American and Soviet tanks faced each other at Checkpoint Charlie in October. Kennedy's visit to West Berlin in June 1963 showed how far American prestige was tied to the city. Here the Cold War gave the United States real success, because Western Europe was held, prosperous and secure, and the Wall was widely seen as a symbol of the failure of communism.\n\nThe Cold War in Asia, however, drew the United States into two costly wars. The fall of China to Mao in October 1949 produced the Republican charge of who lost China, and when North Korea invaded the South in June 1950 Truman sent American forces under a United Nations flag. The war ended in stalemate at the 38th parallel in July 1953, after about 36,000 American deaths and the dismissal of General MacArthur, which caused a bitter domestic row. NSC-68 and the rise in defence spending that followed Korea show that Asian events also reshaped American policy in Europe, so the two theatres cannot be wholly separated.\n\nVietnam was the Asian conflict with by far the greatest effect on American life. The domino theory of 1954 drew the United States from paying for the French war to training South Vietnam's army, and Kennedy had about 16,000 advisers there by late 1963. Under Johnson American troops rose to over half a million by 1968. The war produced the teach-ins of 1965, the Moratorium protests of 1969, the shootings at Kent State in May 1970 and the publication of the Pentagon Papers in 1971, and it deepened the divisions of the 1960s. It also fed inflation and the federal deficit, and it led Johnson not to seek re-election in 1968. About 58,000 Americans died, and the Paris Accords of January 1973 and the fall of Saigon in April 1975 left a lasting Vietnam syndrome and a reluctance to commit forces abroad. Congress reasserted itself through the War Powers Resolution of 1973.\n\nThe Asian experience altered the way the United States fought the Cold War in Europe too. Nixon and Kissinger pursued détente with the Soviet Union and China partly to escape from Vietnam, and Nixon's visits to Beijing and Moscow in 1972 produced SALT I. Yet the final stage of the Cold War was decided in Europe. Reagan's arms build-up and the Strategic Defense Initiative of 1983, his Berlin speech of June 1987 and the INF Treaty of December 1987 led to the collapse of communist governments in Eastern Europe in 1989, which was the triumph of containment.\n\nIn conclusion, the Cold War in Asia did the greater harm to American society, dividing the people and costing tens of thousands of lives, but the Cold War in Europe had the greater and more lasting impact on the United States as a whole. It determined its alliances, its military posture and its sense of mission, and it ended in American success, while Asia brought two wars and a defeat that changed attitudes but not the central strategy.",
