    "label": "B3",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "Mary Martin was born in Dublin in 1892, trained as a nurse and midwife, and went to Nigeria in the 1930s. A Vatican instruction of 1936 lifted the ban on religious sisters practising obstetrics and surgery, which made her order possible. She founded the Medical Missionaries of Mary in Nigeria in 1937, with its mother house at Drogheda, Co. Louth. The order trained doctors, nurses and midwives and set up hospitals and clinics where few medical services existed. She was the first woman to receive the Freedom of the Borough of Drogheda in 1966, and she died in 1975.",
    "model_source": "haiku",
    "answerStatus": "written",
    "type": [
     "long-answer"
    ],
