    "label": "B2",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "Seamus Heaney was born in 1939 at Mossbawn, near Castledawson in County Derry, into a Catholic farming family. His collection North, published in 1975, used Iron Age bog bodies to reflect on the violence in Northern Ireland. He moved to County Wicklow in the Republic in 1972 and was awarded the Nobel Prize for Literature in 1995. He was a director of the Field Day Theatre Company, founded in 1980 by Brian Friel and Stephen Rea. Historians see his work as a key cultural response to the Troubles, one that expressed grief, guilt and the search for an identity beyond sectarianism.",
