    "label": "B4",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "Foreign investors were reluctant to build factories because of the risk of bombing, and tourism fell sharply. Businesses faced high insurance premiums, and some companies moved their operations to Britain or the Republic. Manufacturing employment kept falling in the shipyards, linen and engineering through the 1970s and 1980s. The public sector grew, and the province became more dependent on British subvention. The De Lorean factory at Dunmurry, opened with government grants in 1981, went into receivership in 1982, a costly failure for taxpayers.",
