    "label": "C3",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Bernadette Devlin was a student leader in People's Democracy, formed in October 1968 at Queen's University Belfast, which organised the Belfast to Derry march. She was a prominent speaker in the civil rights campaign in 1968 and 1969. She was elected MP for Mid Ulster at the by-election of April 1969, aged twenty-one, and became the youngest woman elected to Westminster at that time. Her election brought the civil rights movement into Westminster politics. She was convicted of incitement to riot after the Battle of the Bogside in August 1969, and on 31 January 1972 she struck the Home Secretary, Reginald Maudling, in the House of Commons. Nationalists saw her as a symbol of youth and defiance, while unionists regarded her as a dangerous agitator.",
