    "model": "T.K. Whitaker was born in 1916, joined the civil service in 1934 and became Secretary of the Department of Finance in 1956. In May 1958 he published Economic Development, which argued that protection had failed to produce growth or jobs. He recommended moving public investment away from housing and hospitals towards productive investment in agriculture, industry and tourism. He welcomed foreign capital and technology, broke with the Control of Manufactures Acts and argued for export-led growth. His work led to the First Programme for Economic Expansion, which set a target of about 2 per cent growth a year for 1959 to 1963 and achieved about 4 per cent. He later served as Governor of the Central Bank from 1969 to 1976.",
