    "label": "C4",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Jack Lynch faced the Arms Crisis of 1970, when the Special Branch told him that senior ministers might be involved in a plan to import arms for Northern nationalists. He dismissed Charles Haughey and Neil Blaney from the cabinet on 6 May 1970, which left a lasting division in Fianna Fáil. From 1969 he faced violence in Northern Ireland, and he said the state could not stand by but ruled out military intervention, keeping the state out of the conflict. He condemned internment in August 1971 and recalled the Irish ambassador from London after Bloody Sunday in January 1972. After bombs in Dublin in December 1972, he secured amendments to the Offences Against the State Act to deal with the IRA. His 1977 manifesto spending, financed by borrowing, turned the economy into crisis by 1979, and he resigned in December 1979 after losing two by-elections in Cork.",
