    "label": "B1",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "About five million Jews lived in the Russian Empire in the late 1800s, the largest Jewish community in the world, and most were confined to the Pale of Settlement set up in 1791. After Alexander II was assassinated in 1881, rumours blamed the Jews and pogroms swept southern Russia. The May Laws of 1882 banned Jews from settling in rural areas and from owning land, and quotas limited their places in schools and universities from 1887. The Kishinev pogrom of 1903 killed 49 Jews and caused international outrage. Between 1881 and 1914 about two million Jews emigrated from the Empire, most of them to the USA.",
