    "label": "B1",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "In his 1925 pamphlet The Economic Consequences of Mr Churchill, Keynes criticised the return of sterling to the gold standard at the pre-war rate, arguing that it would make British exports uncompetitive and force down wages. He had resigned from the Treasury in protest at the Versailles reparations settlement and condemned it in The Economic Consequences of the Peace (1919). In his General Theory of Employment, Interest and Money of 1936 he argued that government spending could restore demand and employment when private investment was low. At the Bretton Woods conference in 1944 he argued for an international clearing union, and the agreement that followed set up the International Monetary Fund and World Bank.",
