    "model": "Charlie Chaplin's Tramp character, first seen in 1914, made him the most famous film star of the silent era. He developed the Tramp in films such as The Kid (1921), The Gold Rush (1925) and City Lights (1931). In Modern Times (1936) he satirised industrial life and mechanisation by showing the Tramp caught in factory machinery. In The Great Dictator (1940), his first full sound film, he played Adenoid Hynkel, a parody of Hitler, and a Jewish barber, and it was a major commercial success in the United States. Contemporaries admired his comic genius, but his leftist sympathies caused difficulties with American authorities in the 1940s. Historians judge his work a major achievement in silent and sound cinema, and he left the United States in 1952.",
