    "label": "B2",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "In 1953 President Eisenhower's Atoms for Peace speech promoted civil nuclear power as a source of cheap energy. The Soviet Union's Obninsk reactor, the first nuclear power station connected to a public electricity network, began supplying electricity in June 1954. Calder Hall in Cumbria opened in October 1956 as the world's first nuclear power station to supply electricity on a commercial scale. The Messmer Plan of 1974 committed France to building many reactors, and by the 1980s nuclear power supplied most of its electricity.",
