    "label": "C2",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "The Yom Kippur War, which began on 6 October 1973, gave Arab oil producers the chance to use oil as a weapon, and they placed an embargo on the USA and the Netherlands for supporting Israel. OPEC's Gulf members raised the price of oil by about 70 per cent in October, and prices roughly quadrupled in the following months. Governments introduced fuel-saving measures such as car-free Sundays in the Netherlands and West Germany, which changed daily life, and households were asked to turn down their heating. Britain introduced a three-day working week in early 1974, and industrial production fell as unemployment rose, especially in shipbuilding, steel and coal. Inflation rose sharply, producing stagflation, and governments abandoned their commitment to full employment. Oil-importing states also fell into balance of payments deficit, as they had to pay far more for imports.",
