    "label": "C3",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Jacques Delors, a French socialist, became president of the European Commission in January 1985 and held the post until 1995, the longest tenure in its history at that time. He drove the Single European Act of 1986, which set a 1992 deadline for completing a single market without internal border controls. The Delors Commission's 1985 White Paper listed nearly 300 measures to remove barriers to trade between member states. He chaired the committee whose 1989 report set out a three-stage route to economic and monetary union, which became the basis of the Maastricht Treaty. The Maastricht Treaty of 1992 created the European Union and committed member states to a single currency. Historians credit him with reviving the Community in the 1980s, although Margaret Thatcher opposed his federalist ideas and social policy.",
