    "label": "C2",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Katanga's copper, cobalt and uranium mines were run by the Belgian-owned Union Minière, which gave the province a far higher revenue base than other provinces. Moïse Tshombe's Conakat party campaigned for regional autonomy and argued that Katanga should keep its own revenue. Tshombe proclaimed the Katangan State on 11 July 1960, and the secession was supported by Belgian officers and mercenaries. The United Nations operation ONUC entered Katanga on 12 August 1960, and Secretary-General Dag Hammarskjöld died in an air crash near Ndola in September 1961 while trying to negotiate with Tshombe. Tshombe announced the end of the secession at Kolwezi on 14 January 1963, which removed the largest threat to central authority. Mobutu nationalised the Union Minière's Katangan assets in 1966-67 and set up Gécamines, so the province's mineral wealth became the central state's main source of revenue.",
