    "label": "C3",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "The FLN launched its rising on 1 November 1954, and by its peak the French government had about 400,000 troops fighting in Algeria. In 1957 French paratroopers under General Massu broke the FLN's urban network in Algiers through arrests and torture, which was described in Henri Alleg's book La Question. The French bombing of the Tunisian village of Sakiet in February 1958 damaged France's reputation with the United States and the UN. Settlers and army officers seized government buildings in Algiers on 13 May 1958, and this revolt brought de Gaulle back to power. In April 1961 a group of generals seized Algiers to stop independence, but the putsch failed after four days. The OAS carried out bombings and assassinations in Algeria and France, and about 800,000 settlers left Algeria in 1962 for a France that had to absorb them into a strained economy.",
