    "label": "C4",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Chinua Achebe was born on 16 November 1930 at Ogidi in eastern Nigeria and studied at University College Ibadan. His novel Things Fall Apart, published in 1958, portrayed an Igbo community before and during the arrival of British colonial rule, and it became one of the most widely read African novels in English. He followed it with No Longer at Ease (1960), Arrow of God (1964) and A Man of the People (1966), which examined corruption and the failures of the new independent states. As the first editorial adviser of Heinemann's African Writers Series from 1962, he helped publish many other African writers. In his 1965 essay he argued that English could be remade to carry African experience. He supported Biafra during the Biafran War and travelled abroad to advocate its cause.",
