    "label": "B1",
    "marks": 30,
    "model": "The Second World War left the United States with the strongest industrial economy in the world, producing roughly half of the world's manufactured goods in 1945. The GI Bill of 1944 paid for veterans' education and offered government-guaranteed home loans, which created a mass market for housing and consumer goods. The Bretton Woods system made the dollar the central currency, convertible to gold at 35 dollars an ounce. The Federal-Aid Highway Act of 1956 funded an Interstate Highway System of about 41,000 miles, which opened up suburban development and boosted car production.",
