    "label": "C1",
    "marks": 40,
    "model": "Joe McCarthy, a Republican senator for Wisconsin from 1946, became controversial after his Wheeling speech of 9 February 1950, in which he claimed to hold a list of communists working in the State Department. He made his name at a time of fear, when Klaus Fuchs's arrest as a spy, the Hiss conviction and the loss of China had made communist subversion a national worry. His methods of guilt by association, unnamed lists and personal smears were so harsh that the cartoonist Herblock coined the term McCarthyism in 1950. He attacked officials in the State Department and, in 1954, the army, and the Army-McCarthy hearings were televised. The lawyer Joseph Welch's question Have you no sense of decency, sir? turned opinion against him. The Senate voted 67 to 22 to censure him in December 1954, and his influence collapsed after that vote.",
