    "model": "As president of the Montgomery Improvement Association, elected on 5 December 1955, Martin Luther King Jr. became the public voice of the boycott. At the Holt Street meeting he argued for justice while insisting on non-violence and Christian love. He held firmly to non-violence, drawing on Gandhi's methods, and in February 1956 Bayard Rustin and Glenn Smiley came to advise him on the approach. After his arrest in January 1956 and the bombing of his house, he urged people not to retaliate. His own conviction under an old anti-boycott law made him a symbol of dignity, and the car pool he helped to organise sustained the boycott for 381 days. King rode a desegregated bus on 21 December 1956 and went on to lead the Southern Christian Leadership Conference.",
