    "model": "In The Feminine Mystique (1963) Betty Friedan criticised the domestic ideal that trapped American women, a view based on her survey of former Smith College classmates. She described the unhappiness of these educated housewives as the problem that has no name. Friedan attacked the expectation that women should find fulfilment only in home and family, an ideal promoted in magazines, films and advice literature. In June 1966 she founded the National Organization for Women to challenge discrimination in employment and law. NOW pressed for the Equal Rights Amendment, which passed Congress in March 1972 but failed to win ratification by 1982. On 26 August 1970 Friedan organised the Women's Strike for Equality, protesting that women still lacked equal rights fifty years after winning the vote.",
